Batch #	Guidance #	WordID	Word	IPA	Part of Speech	Definition	Synonym(s)	Example Sentence	Note(s)	Category	Source
1	1	1	irrepressible	/ˌɪ.ɹəˈpɹɛ.sə.bəl/	adj	Impossible to control or restrain.	unstoppable, uncontainable, exuberant	Her irrepressible laughter filled the entire room. / Her irrepressible enthusiasm lifted the whole team. / He has an irrepressible sense of humor.	Common with positive energy and emotion: irrepressible enthusiasm, spirit, energy, laughter, smile. It suggests something keeps bursting out despite attempts to restrain it.		
1	2	2	abrogate / abrogated	/ˈæ.bɹəˌɡeɪ.tɪd/	verb; past / past participle: abrogated	1. To formally repeal, abolish, or cancel a law, agreement, right, or rule. 2. To fail to fulfill or deliberately cast aside a duty, responsibility, or obligation.	repeal, abolish, annul, revoke, rescind, invalidate; shirk, evade, renounce (a responsibility/duty, context-dependent)	The government moved to abrogate the treaty. / Congress cannot simply abrogate constitutional protections. / Critics accused the board of abrogating its responsibilities to shareholders. / The old rule was formally abrogated.	The legal/official sense is the core one: abrogate a law, treaty, agreement, right, or provision. With duties or responsibilities, it means to cast them aside or fail to discharge them. 'Abrogated' is commonly passive or adjectival in formal writing: 'the provision was abrogated.' Do not confuse with arrogate = claim or seize for oneself.		
1	3	3	adversely	/ædˈvɝs.li/	adv	In a way that prevents success or causes harm.	negatively, harmfully, unfavorably	The medication can adversely affect your blood pressure. / The delay adversely affected our travel plans. / Higher rates could adversely impact small businesses.	Very common collocations: adversely affect, adversely impact. Contrast adverse = harmful/unfavorable with averse = opposed/unwilling ('averse to risk').		
1	4	4	arcane	/ɑɹˈkeɪn/	adj	Secret, mysterious, or known only to a few people who have special knowledge or understanding. It's often used to describe things that are complex, obscure, or esoteric. 	obscure, mysterious, esoteric	The professor specialized in arcane legal theory. This argument may seem arcane to those not closely involved in the world of finance. / The manual is full of arcane rules that only longtime users seem to understand.	"Examples:
Arcane rituals, arcane knowledge, or arcane rules can all be described as things that require a degree of specialized understanding to comprehend. Already a strong early card. Useful combinations: arcane knowledge/rules/procedures/details. Arcane often implies specialized and obscure rather than literally secret."		
1	5	5	arresting	/əˈɹɛs.tɪŋ/	adj	Striking or eye-catching; demanding attention.	striking, impressive, captivating	Her arresting beauty left everyone speechless. / The film opens with an arresting image. / She has an arresting voice that immediately commands attention.	Arresting = striking enough to stop and hold your attention. Common with image, appearance, design, voice, opening, contrast. Not related to being arrested by police in this use.		
1	6	6	austere	/ɑˈstɪɚ/	adj	1. Severe, strict, or uncompromising in manner or discipline. 2. Plain, sparse, and without comfort or decoration. 3. Economically severe or restrictive, especially in 'austere measures/policies.'	stern, strict, severe, ascetic, spare, stark, unadorned; stringent, restrictive (economic context)	The headmaster had an austere manner. / The room was austere, with bare walls and almost no furniture. / The government introduced austere spending measures after the crisis.	Austere can describe a person, lifestyle, design, or policy. The common thread is severity and lack of indulgence. 'Austere beauty' can be positive: simple, restrained, and striking rather than luxurious.		
1	7	7	belie / belied	/bɪˈlaɪd/	verb; past / past participle: belied	1. To give a false or misleading impression of something. 2. To show that a statement, appearance, or belief is false or inconsistent with the facts.	misrepresent, disguise, mask, conceal; contradict, disprove, give the lie to	His calm voice belied how angry he really was. / Her youthful appearance belied her age. / The latest figures belie the claim that demand is falling.	Two closely related patterns matter: 'X belies Y' can mean X hides Y ('his smile belied his anxiety') or X proves Y false ('the evidence belies that claim'). The subject is the thing creating the misleading appearance or contradiction.		
1	8	8	bloviate	/ˈbloʊ.viˌeɪt/	verb	To talk at length in a pompous or boastful way.	rant, lecture, pontificate	The politician bloviated for over an hour without saying much. / He spent ten minutes bloviating about a topic he barely understood.	Usually humorous or critical, often about politicians, pundits, or self-important speakers. Bloviate emphasizes pompous, inflated talk more than ordinary rambling.		
1	9	9	brink	/bɹɪŋk/	noun	The edge or threshold of something, often a dramatic change.	edge, verge, threshold	The company was on the brink of bankruptcy. / The country stood on the brink of war. / One mistake pushed the project to the brink of collapse.	Teach the fixed frame 'on the brink of X'. Literal brink = edge; figurative brink = threshold of an extreme state. Related: brinkmanship.		
1	10	10	brown-nosing / brown-nose	/ˈbɹaʊn ˌnoʊ.zɪŋ/	noun / verb; informal, disapproving	Brown-nosing is trying to gain favor from someone in authority through excessive, insincere flattery or servile behavior. To brown-nose means to behave this way.	sucking up, kissing up, bootlicking, fawning, toadying, currying favor; flatter excessively	Everyone could see that his constant compliments were just brown-nosing. / He keeps brown-nosing the manager because he wants a promotion. / I don't want to suck up to anyone just to get ahead.	Usually hyphenated as brown-nosing; the verb is brown-nose. Strongly informal and insulting. 'Suck up to' is the most natural everyday equivalent. 'Curry favor with' is more formal and does not necessarily sound as crude.		
2	11	11	capstone	/ˈkæpˌstoʊn/	noun	1. A finishing stone placed at the top of a structure. 2. Figuratively, the culminating or crowning part of a project, course of study, career, or achievement.	culmination, crowning achievement, finishing touch, pinnacle, climax	The capstone completed the stone arch. / Her thesis was the capstone of her graduate program. / The final project serves as a capstone experience for the entire course.	Very common in education: capstone course/project/experience. Figuratively it suggests the final piece that completes and summarizes what came before.		
2	12	12	carve out	/ˈkɑɹ.vɪŋ aʊt/	phrasal verb	1. To create or establish a role, niche, career, reputation, or position through sustained effort. 2. To set aside or obtain a portion of time, space, territory, or resources.	establish, create, build, forge; set aside, make room for, secure	She's carved out a successful career in animation. / The channel carved out a niche for itself among older viewers. / I need to carve out an hour tonight to study. / They carved out a small workspace in the corner.	The image is of cutting a distinct piece out of something larger. Common patterns: carve out a niche/career/role/reputation; carve out time/space. 'Carve out a niche' is especially frequent.		
2	13	13	check in with yourself	/tʃɛk ɪn wɪð jɚˈsɛlf/	verb phrase	To pause and reflect on your thoughts, emotions, or needs.	self-reflect, evaluate, assess	Take a moment to check in with yourself before making a decision. / Before replying, I checked in with myself and realized I was more frustrated than angry.	Modern self-reflection phrase rather than a rigid idiom. Compare check in with someone = contact them / see how they are doing.		
2	14	15	debacle	/dɪˈbɑ.kəl/	noun	A sudden and complete failure.	disaster, fiasco, collapse	The product launch was a total debacle. / The rollout became a public-relations debacle after the site crashed on launch day.	Debacle usually suggests a conspicuous, often embarrassing collapse or failure. Close to fiasco; debacle can sound slightly more formal.		
2	15	16	debilitating	/dɪˈbɪ.ləˌteɪ.tɪŋ/	adj	Making someone weak or unable to function.	weakening, exhausting, crippling	She suffered from a debilitating illness for years. / Chronic pain can be physically and mentally debilitating. / The uncertainty had a debilitating effect on morale.	Common with illness, pain, anxiety, injury, debt, fear, effects. Stronger than tiring: it seriously reduces someone's ability to function.		
2	16	17	defiant	/dɪˈfaɪ.ənt/	adj	Showing resistance or bold disobedience.	rebellious, uncooperative, bold	His defiant tone made it clear he wouldn't back down. / She remained defiant even after repeated warnings. / He gave the interviewer a defiant stare.	Common collocations: defiant tone/look/gesture/stance. Better synonyms than 'bold': rebellious, resistant, insubordinate, unyielding.		
2	17	18	aptitude	/ˈæp.tə.tuːd/	noun	A natural ability to do something.	talent, skill, knack	She showed great aptitude for mathematics at an early age. / He has a natural aptitude for languages. / The test measures spatial and numerical aptitude.	Aptitude is natural potential/capacity to learn or perform well, not simply an acquired skill. Common: aptitude for, aptitude test. Skill can be trained; aptitude is underlying ability.		
2	18	19	bugbears	/ˈbʌɡˌbɛɹz/	noun (plural)	Things that cause persistent annoyance or irritation.	pet peeves, irritants, nuisances	One of his biggest bugbears is people who chew loudly. / Late trains are one of my biggest bugbears. / Bureaucratic delays remain a major bugbear for small businesses.	Especially common in British English. A bugbear can be a pet peeve or a persistent troublesome problem, not only a personal annoyance.		
2	19	20	cerebral	/səˈɹi.bɹəl/	adj	1. Relating to the brain or cerebrum. 2. Intellectual, analytical, or abstract rather than emotional, instinctive, or physical.	intellectual, analytical, cerebral, brainy, thought-provoking; neurological/brain-related (literal context)	The scan showed reduced cerebral blood flow. / It's a very cerebral film—fascinating, but emotionally distant. / Their conversation was more cerebral than personal.	In reviews, 'cerebral' often means intellectually demanding or idea-driven. It can be praise ('thought-provoking') or mild criticism ('too detached or cold'). Literal medical uses relate to the brain.		
2	20	21	coffers	/ˈkɑ.fɚz/	noun (plural)	Funds or financial reserves of an organization or government.	treasury, funds, reserves	The city's coffers were depleted after years of overspending. / Tax increases helped refill the government's coffers. / The settlement put millions into the company's coffers.	Literal coffer = strongbox/chest. Plural coffers is very common figuratively for the treasury or financial reserves of a government/organization.		
3	21	22	compliance	/kəmˈplaɪ.əns/	noun	The act of conforming to rules or standards.	obedience, adherence, conformity	The company was fined for non-compliance with safety regulations. / The system was redesigned to ensure compliance with privacy rules. / All staff must act in compliance with the policy.	High-frequency frames: compliance with; in compliance with; regulatory/legal compliance. Compliance is conformity with a requirement, not necessarily enthusiastic agreement.		
3	22	23	dope	/doʊp/	noun / adjective; informal/slang; meanings vary by context	1. Drugs or an illegal drug, especially in older/informal use. 2. Information or inside knowledge ('the dope on...'), somewhat dated. 3. A foolish person, informal and dated. 4. Slang adjective: excellent, cool, impressive.	drugs, narcotics; information, inside scoop; fool, idiot; awesome, cool, sick, fire (slang, sense-dependent)	Police found dope in the car. / Give me the inside dope on what happened. / Don't be a dope—read the instructions first. / That new track is dope; I've had it on repeat all day.	'Dope' is highly polysemous, so context is essential. The positive adjective 'That's dope' is common slang. The drug sense is older and still widely understood. Never merge the different senses into one synonym list without labels.		
3	23	24	glitches	/ˈɡlɪ.tʃɪz/	noun (plural)	Small faults or malfunctions, especially in software or systems.	bugs, hiccups, errors	The software update caused several unexpected glitches. / A temporary network glitch knocked the app offline for a few minutes.	A glitch is usually a small, temporary, unexpected malfunction. A bug is often a reproducible flaw in code/design; everyday speakers may use them loosely.		
3	24	25	indelible	/ɪnˈdɛ.lə.bəl/	adj	Not able to be forgotten or removed.	unforgettable, lasting, permanent	That trip left an indelible mark on her memory. / The experience left an indelible impression on me. / The image is indelibly associated with the event.	Common: indelible mark/impression/memory. Literal sense: ink/mark that cannot be erased; figuratively, an effect that cannot be forgotten.		
3	25	26	integer	/ˈɪn.tə.dʒɚ/	noun	A positive or negative whole number, including zero; a number with no fractional part: ..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ...	whole-valued number (descriptive); no exact everyday synonym	-7, 0, and 42 are integers, but 3.5 and 2/3 are not. / The program accepts only integer values for this field.	Do NOT use digit or numeral as synonyms. A digit is one symbol from 0–9; a numeral is a written representation of a number; an integer is a mathematical type of number. 'Whole number' is sometimes used for nonnegative integers only, so it is not always an exact synonym.		
3	26	27	notch	/nɑtʃ/	noun	1. A small cut, indentation, or mark, often used for counting or fitting. 2. A level or degree on a scale. 3. Figuratively, a small step in quality, intensity, status, or achievement.	indentation, cut, mark; level, degree, step	He cut a notch in the wood to mark the height. / This restaurant is a notch above the others nearby. / She took her performance up a notch in the finals. / The company notched up another record year.	Useful patterns: a notch above/below; take/turn/kick something up a notch; notch up a win/success (verb). The figurative sense is extremely common.		
3	27	28	permeate	/ˈpɝ.miˌeɪt/	verb	To spread or pass through every part of something, often by penetrating into its material, space, or members.	penetrate, saturate, diffuse through, seep through, spread throughout, infuse	The smell of garlic permeated the entire kitchen. / Water had permeated the walls. / A sense of optimism gradually permeated the organization.	Compared with pervade, permeate more readily suggests entering or penetrating through a substance or structure, though both can be figurative. Common subjects: smell, moisture, light, ideas, attitudes.		
3	28	29	pervade	/pɚˈveɪd/	verb	To be present, felt, or noticeable throughout a place, situation, group, or period.	permeate, suffuse, fill, spread throughout, run through	A sense of unease pervaded the room. / Optimism pervaded the campaign. / The smell of smoke pervaded the building.	Pervade is especially natural with atmospheres, feelings, attitudes, smells, and qualities. Compared with permeate, it focuses more on widespread presence than on physical penetration.		
3	29	30	rear	/ɹɪɚ/	noun / adjective / verb	1. Noun: the back part of something. 2. Adjective: at or toward the back. 3. Verb: to raise and care for children or animals. 4. Verb: to rise up, especially on the hind legs.	back, hind part; raise, bring up; rise up	The engine is mounted at the rear of the vehicle. / Please use the rear entrance. / She reared three children on her own. / The horse reared when it heard the explosion.	Rear is highly polysemous. 'Rear a child/animal' = raise. 'A horse rears' = rises on its hind legs. In some varieties, 'rear' as a noun can also mean buttocks, usually informal.		
3	30	31	shrewd	/ʃɹuːd/	adj	Having sharp judgment or practical intelligence.	astute, clever, insightful	She made a shrewd investment that doubled in value. / It was a shrewd move to negotiate before prices rose. / She's a shrewd judge of character.	Shrewd is practical, sharp, often unsentimental judgment. It can sound admiring but may imply calculating cleverness.		
4	31	32	smattering	/ˈsmæ.tɚ.ɪŋz/	noun	1. A small, scattered amount or number of something. 2. A slight or superficial knowledge of a subject or language.	sprinkling, handful, scattering, trace; rudimentary knowledge, a little knowledge	There were only a smattering of people in the audience. / The article contains a smattering of technical jargon. / I know a smattering of Italian, but nowhere near enough to hold a real conversation.	Usually appears as 'a smattering of ...'. The knowledge sense is especially useful: 'a smattering of French/history/coding.' The plural 'smatterings' exists but the singular headword is much more useful.		
4	32	33	desultorily	/ˈdɛs.əl.tɚ.li/	adv	In a disconnected or aimless manner.	randomly, erratically, fitfully	He wandered desultorily through the exhibit without focus. / He worked desultorily, jumping from one unfinished task to another.	Desultory/desultorily suggests movement from one thing to another without clear plan, continuity, enthusiasm, or purpose. Formal/literary.		
4	33	34	deviate	/ˈdi.viˌeɪt/	verb	To depart from an established course or norm.	diverge, stray, veer	Try not to deviate from the original plan. / The results deviated significantly from the forecast. / We had to deviate from the original route.	Core grammar: deviate from a path, plan, norm, standard, expected result. Deviation is the noun.		
4	34	36	drench	/dɹɛntʃ/	verb	To soak something thoroughly.	soak, saturate, flood	The rain completely drenched his clothes. / We got drenched in the sudden storm. / The fries were drenched in sauce.	Common patterns: be drenched in/with rain, sweat, blood, sauce; drench something with liquid. Stronger than simply wet.		
4	35	37	eclectic	/ɪˈklɛk.tɪk/	adj	Deriving ideas or style from a broad and diverse range of sources.	varied, diverse, wide-ranging	Her music taste is eclectic, ranging from jazz to metal. / The café has an eclectic playlist—jazz, metal, K-pop, and old soul.	Eclectic means selecting or combining elements from many different sources/styles, often deliberately. More specific than merely 'diverse'.		
4	36	38	eclecticism	/ɪˈklɛk.təˌsɪ.zəm/	noun	The practice of combining elements from different styles or ideas.	variety, synthesis, diversity	His art reflects an eclecticism rarely seen in contemporary work. / The interior reflects deliberate eclecticism, mixing industrial furniture with antique art.	Eclecticism is the approach or practice behind eclectic choices. Common in art, design, philosophy, architecture, and intellectual method.		
4	37	39	enigma	/ɪˈnɪɡ.mə/	noun	A person or thing that is mysterious or difficult to understand.	mystery, puzzle, riddle	Her sudden departure remains an enigma. / Even after years of interviews, the actor remains something of an enigma.	Pattern: an enigma to someone / remain an enigma. An enigmatic person is difficult to read or understand.		
4	38	40	ennui	/ɑnˈwi/	noun	A weary feeling of boredom, listlessness, and dissatisfaction caused by having nothing interesting or meaningful to do, often prolonged rather than momentary.	boredom, listlessness, tedium, world-weariness, languor (context-dependent)	After months of doing the same routine, a deep sense of ennui set in. / The novel captures the ennui of wealthy characters who have everything except a sense of purpose.	Ennui is more literary/formal than boredom and often implies weary dissatisfaction or emptiness, not simply 'I'm bored for ten minutes.' 'Melancholy' can overlap emotionally but is not a close synonym.		
4	39	41	epitomize	/ɪˈpɪ.təˌmaɪz/	verb	To be a perfect example of something.	embody, represent, exemplify	She epitomizes grace and professionalism. / Her calm response epitomized the professionalism of the whole team.	Epitomize = embody the defining qualities of something, not merely 'be an example'. Noun: epitome.		
4	40	42	eradicate	/ɪˈɹæ.dəˌkeɪt/	verb	To destroy or remove completely.	eliminate, wipe out, abolish	The program aims to eradicate poverty in rural areas. / The campaign aims to eradicate polio worldwide. / It's almost impossible to eradicate every trace of mold.	Very strong verb: remove completely. Common with disease, pests, poverty, corruption, weeds, errors. Stronger than reduce or control.		
5	41	43	every alternate day / every other day	/ˈɛ.vɚ.i ˈɔl.tɚ.nət deɪ/	adverbial phrase	On one day, then not the next, repeating every two days.	every other day, on alternate days, every second day	He takes the medication every other day. / The clinic is open on alternate days. / In some varieties of English, 'every alternate day' is also used for the same schedule.	'Every other day' is the most natural everyday AmE form. 'On alternate days' is broadly standard. 'Every alternate day' is used in some varieties, especially South Asian English, but may sound less idiomatic to many AmE speakers.		
5	42	44	exonerate	/ɪɡˈzɑ.nɚˌeɪt/	verb	To clear someone from blame, fault, responsibility, or an accusation, especially when evidence shows they were not responsible.	clear, absolve, vindicate, acquit (legal context only), clear of blame, clear of wrongdoing	New DNA evidence could exonerate the suspect. / The investigation exonerated the company of wrongdoing. / Nothing in the report exonerates management from responsibility for the failure.	Typical active patterns: evidence/investigation/report exonerates someone; exonerate someone of wrongdoing/blame; exonerate someone from responsibility/liability. 'Acquit' is specifically a legal judgment in a criminal case; 'vindicate' can also mean prove a decision or belief was right.		
5	43	45	exonerated	/ɪɡˈzɑ.nɚˌeɪ.tɪd/	past tense / past participle; often passive or adjectival	Cleared from blame, accusation, wrongdoing, or responsibility; the past/past-participle form of exonerate, very often used in passive constructions.	cleared, absolved, vindicated; acquitted (only when a court verdict is meant), cleared of wrongdoing	She was exonerated after DNA evidence identified another suspect. / He was fully exonerated of any wrongdoing. / The exonerated defendant later sued the city. / Years later, the evidence finally exonerated him.	KEEP separately because the usage profile is useful for learners: 'was exonerated', 'exonerated of wrongdoing', and 'the exonerated defendant' are common passive/adjectival patterns that differ from the active dictionary form 'exonerate someone'. This is more than simply memorizing a past-tense spelling.		
5	44	46	face value / at face value	/feɪs ˈvæl.ju/	noun phrase / idiom	1. The stated or printed monetary value of a coin, banknote, ticket, bond, etc. 2. In 'take/accept something at face value': accept its apparent meaning or truth without questioning or looking for hidden factors.	nominal value, stated value; apparent meaning, surface meaning (figurative)	The ticket has a face value of $80, but resale prices are much higher. / Don't take his promises at face value—he often exaggerates. / At face value, the proposal sounds reasonable.	The literal financial meaning is important. The idiom is usually 'take X at face value'. 'Face value' does not itself mean 'superficial meaning' in every context.		
5	45	47	faff	/fæf/	verb (British informal)	To waste time doing unnecessary or unproductive tasks.	dither, dawdle, mess around	Stop faffing and get to work already! / Booking this trip has been such a faff. / Stop faffing about and pick one.	Strongly British informal. Verb: faff / faff about/around = waste time fussing. Noun: a faff = an annoying hassle requiring unnecessary effort.		
5	46	48	fiasco	/fiˈæs.koʊ/	noun	A complete and embarrassing failure.	disaster, debacle, flop	The dinner party turned into a total fiasco. / What was meant to be a simple launch turned into a complete fiasco.	Fiasco = conspicuous, embarrassing failure. Very close to debacle WID 15; keep both but cross-reference rather than duplicate identical notes.		
5	47	49	foolhardy	/ˈfuːlˌhɑɹ.di/	adj	Recklessly bold or rash.	reckless, rash, impulsive	It was foolhardy to go hiking alone in that storm. / Trying to cross the river during the flood would be foolhardy.	Foolhardy is always negative: recklessly daring despite obvious danger. Brave/bold can be positive; foolhardy means the risk is foolish.		
5	48	50	garb	/ɡɑɹb/	noun	Clothing or distinctive style of dress.	attire, outfit, costume	The priest wore traditional ceremonial garb. / The performers appeared in elaborate ceremonial garb.	Formal/literary word for distinctive clothing, especially ceremonial, traditional, religious, military, or occupational dress.		
5	49	51	garrulous	/ˈɡɛɹ.ə.ləs/	adj	Excessively talkative, especially on trivial matters.	chatty, talkative, loquacious	The garrulous host dominated the conversation all evening. / The otherwise quiet meeting became exhausting once the garrulous guest took over.	Garrulous usually implies excessive, sometimes tiresome talkativeness. Loquacious is formal and can be more neutral.		
5	50	52	go/come to the brink of; bring/take to the brink	/ɡoʊ tu ðə bɹɪŋk/	idiomatic phrase family	To approach, or cause someone/something to approach, an extreme and usually dangerous point such as war, collapse, bankruptcy, or disaster.	come close to, verge on, approach the edge of, nearly reach; push to the edge	The crisis brought the two countries to the brink of war. / The company came to the brink of bankruptcy. / Negotiators went to the brink before finally reaching an agreement.	The most useful pattern is 'the brink of + noun': the brink of war/collapse/disaster/bankruptcy. 'Go to the brink' can occur, but teach it as part of the wider brink phrase family rather than as an isolated fixed idiom.		
6	51	54	in defiance of	/ɪn dɪˈfaɪ.əns ʌv/	prepositional phrase	In open resistance or bold disobedience to something.	against, despite, disregarding	She continued working in defiance of her doctor's advice. / They marched in defiance of the ban. / The structure seems to stand in defiance of gravity.	'In defiance of' implies deliberate/open resistance, stronger than simple despite. Common with orders, law, warnings, expectations, gravity.		
6	52	55	inertia	/ɪˈnɝ.ʃə/	noun	1. Physics: the tendency of an object to remain at rest or keep moving at a constant velocity unless acted on by an external force. 2. Figuratively, a tendency to remain inactive or resist change once a situation is established.	resistance to change, inactivity, stagnation, sluggishness (figurative); no exact synonym in physics	A moving object continues because of inertia unless another force acts on it. / Organizational inertia made even small reforms difficult. / After a week off, pure inertia kept me from getting back into my routine.	Do not equate inertia directly with apathy. Apathy is lack of interest or feeling; inertia is resistance to changing the current state, which may happen even when people care.		
6	53	56	infantile	/ˈɪn.fənˌtaɪl/	adj	Childish or immature.	juvenile, immature, babyish	His infantile behavior annoyed everyone at the meeting. / The argument was embarrassingly infantile. / The study focuses on infantile developmental reflexes.	Literal/formal sense relates to infants; figurative sense = childish/immature and is disparaging.		
6	54	57	ingenuous	/ɪnˈdʒɛn.ju.əs/	adj	Innocent, candid, and free from deceit or calculation; sometimes naively trusting or straightforward.	guileless, candid, innocent, sincere, artless, naive, trusting	Her ingenuous questions made it clear she genuinely wanted to understand. / He gave an ingenuous account of what had happened, without trying to make himself look better.	High-value contrast: ingenuous = innocent/candid; ingenious = clever/inventive. They look similar but are unrelated in meaning. 'Ingenuous' can be positive (refreshingly candid) or suggest naivety.		
6	55	58	inquisitive	/ɪnˈkwɪ.zə.tɪv/	adj	Curious or eager for knowledge.	curious, probing, questioning	Her inquisitive nature made her a great journalist. / She was inquisitive about how the machine worked. / The neighbor became uncomfortably inquisitive about my private life.	Can be positive (curious, eager to learn) or negative (prying, nosy), depending on context.		
6	56	59	interred	/ɪnˈtɝd/	verb (past tense)	Buried a dead body in a grave or tomb.	buried, laid to rest, entombed	He was interred in the family cemetery. / The remains were interred in a private ceremony.	Formal word for burial. High-value contrast: interred = buried; interned = detained/confined, often during wartime.		
6	57	60	jejune	/ʒɪˈʒun/	adj	1. Dull, uninteresting, or lacking substance. 2. Naive, simplistic, superficial, or immature in thought.	dull, insipid, tedious; naive, simplistic, shallow, immature	The lecture was so jejune that even specialists struggled to stay interested. / The critic dismissed the argument as jejune and overly simplistic.	Formal/literary word with two established senses. Context decides whether the criticism is 'boring/insubstantial' or 'naive/superficial'. Avoid reducing it to only 'childish'.		
6	58	61	justly	/ˈdʒʌst.li/	adv	In a fair or morally correct way.	fairly, rightfully, righteously	She was justly praised for her honesty and dedication. / The book was justly praised as one of the year's best. / He wanted everyone involved to be treated justly.	Two common ideas: fairly/according to justice; deservedly/rightly ('justly famous', 'justly criticized').		
6	59	62	let alone	/ˈlɛt əˈloʊn/	phrase	Used to emphasize that something is even less likely or possible than another thing.	much less, not to mention, still less	He can't afford a bike, let alone a car. / She can barely walk to the store, let alone run a marathon. / I don't have time to read the article, let alone the whole book.	'Let alone B' presents B as even less possible/likely than A. It can also work in positive contexts when B is an even stronger claim.		
