7	61	64	mores	/ˈmɔˌɹeɪz/	noun (plural)	The essential customs and values of a community.	customs, traditions, norms	Social mores vary widely from one culture to another. / Changing social mores have reshaped attitudes toward marriage and work.	Usually plural. Mores are established social/moral customs regarded as important to a community. More value-laden than mere habits.		
7	62	65	non sequitur	/nɑn ˈsɛ.kwɪ.tɚ/	noun	A statement that does not logically follow from the previous one.	illogical conclusion, random remark, misconnection	His response was a complete non sequitur. / His answer was a complete non sequitur. / 'It's raining, so the movie must be bad' is a non sequitur.	In logic, a non sequitur is a conclusion that does not follow from the premises; in conversation it can also mean an irrelevant or random remark. Latin non sequitur literally means 'it does not follow.' Related term: sequitur literally means 'it follows' and can mean a logical consequence that follows from what came before. Standalone English use of sequitur is uncommon and is often technical, playful, or used specifically as a contrast to 'non sequitur'.		
7	63	66	note	/noʊt/	noun / verb	Noun: 1. a short written message or reminder; 2. an annotation or brief written record; 3. a particular quality or tone ('a note of sadness'); 4. a musical sound/symbol. Verb: to notice, observe, mention, or write something down.	message, memo, annotation, remark; observe, notice, record, mention (sense-dependent)	She left a note on the fridge. / I made a note of the address. / There was a note of irritation in his voice. / Please note that the schedule has changed. / The report notes several serious risks.	This card was too narrow. High-frequency patterns: make/take a note of; note that...; please note...; a note of concern/sarcasm; musical note. Because 'note' is very polysemous, examples should signal the sense clearly.		
