7	68	72	pet peeve	/ˈpɛt ˌpiːv/	noun	Something that especially annoys or irritates a person.	annoyance, irritation, bugbear	Loud chewing is one of his biggest pet peeves. / One of my pet peeves is people who block the whole sidewalk.	A pet peeve is a particular, often idiosyncratic annoyance. Very common frame: 'One of my pet peeves is...'. Cross-reference bugbear WID 19.		
7	69	73	pick out curtains	/pɪk aʊt ˈkɝ.tənz/	phrase	Used humorously to refer to planning a life together or making domestic choices.	settle down, plan a future, nest-build	We're not picking out curtains just yet it's only our third date. / Relax—we've only had two dates. I'm not picking out curtains yet.	Humorous romantic shorthand for acting as if a relationship is already serious/domestic. Often appears as 'We're not picking out curtains yet.' Not a rigid dictionary idiom.		
7	70	74	pique	/piːk/	verb / noun	Verb: to arouse or stimulate interest, curiosity, or attention. Noun: a feeling of irritation or wounded pride, especially in 'in a fit of pique.'	arouse, spark, stimulate, whet, provoke (interest); irritation, resentment, wounded pride (noun)	The trailer piqued my curiosity. / That detail immediately piqued his interest. / She left in a fit of pique after her suggestion was ignored.	Common confusion: pique interest/curiosity, not 'peak' or 'peek'. Peak = highest point; peek = quick look; pique = stimulate or irritate.		
