8	80	84	off the cuff / speak off the cuff	/ˈspi.kɪŋ ɑf ðə kʌf/	idiom / adverbial phrase	Without preparation, rehearsal, or advance planning; improvised spontaneously.	impromptu, extemporaneously, spontaneously, unrehearsed, improvised	She spoke off the cuff for twenty minutes and still sounded polished. / His off-the-cuff remark ended up becoming the headline. / I can explain the idea off the cuff, but I'd want notes for a formal presentation.	'Off the cuff' can function adverbially ('speak off the cuff') or adjectivally before a noun ('an off-the-cuff comment'). The original headword 'speaking off and cuff' was nonstandard.		
9	81	85	staggering	/ˈstæ.ɡɚ.ɪŋ/	adjective; related verb: stagger	Extremely surprising, shocking, or impressive, especially because of very large size, amount, or degree.	astonishing, astounding, breathtaking, overwhelming, enormous, eye-watering (informal, amount/cost)	The project cost a staggering $2 billion. / The number of people affected is staggering. / She made a staggering recovery after the accident.	The adjective comes from stagger = move unsteadily or be shocked/overwhelmed. Common collocations: staggering amount/cost/number/figure/scale. The verb has separate senses: 'He staggered home' and 'The news staggered me.'		
