10	94	98	vindicate	/ˈvɪn.dɪˌkeɪt/	verb	1. To clear someone from blame, criticism, or suspicion by showing that they were right or not responsible. 2. To show that a decision, belief, claim, or course of action was justified or correct.	clear, exonerate, absolve; justify, validate, confirm, prove right	The new evidence vindicated her. / The final results vindicated his decision to change strategy. / She felt vindicated when the investigation confirmed her account.	'Vindicate her innocence' is awkward because vindicate already carries the idea of clearing/justifying. Compared with exonerate, vindicate more readily applies to decisions, beliefs, reputations, or claims—not only accusations of wrongdoing.		
