11	103	107	arrest / arresting / arrested	/əˈrɛ.stɪŋ ˈædʒ.ɪk.tɪv/	verb / adjective / past participle	1. arrest (verb): to take someone into legal custody. 2. arrest (verb, formal): to stop, check, or slow the progress or development of something. 3. arresting (adjective): striking, captivating, or capable of immediately catching and holding attention. 4. arrested (past participle/adjective): stopped or halted at a particular stage, as in arrested development.	arresting: striking, stunning, captivating, eye-catching, attention-grabbing; arrest (stop sense): halt, stop, check, impede; arrested: halted, stopped, checked	The police arrested the suspect outside the station. / Researchers hope the treatment can arrest the progression of the disease. / The film opens with an arresting image of the empty city. / The condition can lead to arrested physical development.	KEEP as a distinct usage-cluster card rather than treating it as a trivial inflection. The adjective arresting has a lexicalized meaning ('striking/captivating') that is not predictable from the police sense of arrest. The formal verb sense 'stop/check the progress of' explains combinations such as arrest decay, arrest disease progression, and arrested development. 'Arrested development' broadly means development that has stopped or been impeded; in clinical contexts, use the specific field's terminology rather than assuming one universal psychological diagnosis.		
