12	114	119	consummate / consummated	/ˈkɑn.səˌmeɪ.tɪd/	verb; adjective (different pronunciation)	Verb /ˈkɒnsəmeɪt/: 1. to complete or bring a transaction, agreement, or process to completion; 2. traditionally/legal-historically, to complete a marriage by having sexual intercourse. Adjective consummate /ˈkɒnsəmət/: supremely skilled, accomplished, or complete.	verb: complete, finalize, conclude; marriage sense: complete/consummate the marriage; adjective: accomplished, superb, masterful	The companies consummated the merger after months of negotiations. / In traditional legal language, a marriage may be described as consummated after the spouses have sexual intercourse. / She is a consummate professional.	High-value pronunciation/part-of-speech contrast: the verb ends in /-meɪt/, while the adjective ends in /-mət/. 'Consummated' is common in formal business/legal writing. The marriage sense is established but can sound formal or legalistic.		
