13	121	126	inception	/ɪnˈsɛp.ʃən/	noun	the establishment or starting point of an institution or activity	beginning, start, commencement	The company has grown significantly since its inception in 2010.	Depth-pass addition: Common frame since/from its inception; formal; inception = beginning/establishment, not the process of developing.		
13	122	127	inculcate	/ˈɪn.kəlˌkeɪt/	verb	to instill an idea or habit by persistent instruction	instill, implant, ingrain	The teacher worked to inculcate respect and discipline in the students.	Depth-pass addition: Inculcate values/habits/ideas in someone / inculcate someone with (less preferred); repeated instruction; formal, sometimes authoritarian nuance.		
13	124	129	laxity	/ˈlæk.sə.ti/	noun	lack of strictness or care	negligence, carelessness, looseness	Laxity in enforcing the rules led to a chaotic environment.	Depth-pass addition: Add laxity in security/enforcement/standards; distinguish laxity (insufficient strictness) from laziness.		
13	125	130	mundane	/mʌnˈdeɪn/	adjective	1. Ordinary, routine, or lacking excitement or interest. 2. Formal/literary: relating to the earthly or worldly realm rather than the spiritual or heavenly.	ordinary, routine, everyday, humdrum, prosaic; worldly, earthly (second sense)	Most of the job consists of mundane administrative tasks. / The novel contrasts spiritual aspirations with mundane concerns.	The everyday sense is by far the most common. 'Mundane' does not necessarily mean bad; it often simply means ordinary or routine.		
