13	130	136	semantic	/səˈmæn.tɪk/	adjective	Relating to meaning in language, symbols, logic, or other representational systems.	meaning-related, relating to meaning	The two sentences are grammatically similar but have an important semantic difference. / The word underwent a semantic shift over time.	'Linguistic' is much broader and is not a synonym. Common terms: semantic meaning, semantic difference, semantic shift, semantic field, semantic memory. Contrast semantic (meaning) with syntactic (structure/grammar).		
14	131	137	succinctly	/səkˈsɪŋkt.li/	adv	in a brief and clearly expressed manner	concisely, briefly, clearly	She succinctly summarized the entire project in two sentences.	Depth-pass addition: Add succinct = brief without sacrificing clarity; succinctly put; contrast terse (can sound abrupt) and concise.		
14	132	138	unabashed	/ˌʌn.əˈbæʃt/	adj	not embarrassed, disconcerted, or ashamed	unashamed, shameless, bold	He was unabashed about his love for cheesy rom-coms.	Depth-pass addition: Add unabashed enthusiasm/fan/supporter and 'unabashedly'; unlike shameless, often positive/playful because no embarrassment.		
14	133	139	unadulterated	/ˌʌn.əˈdʌl.tɚ.eɪ.tɪd/	adj	pure and complete; not mixed or diluted with anything else	pure, absolute, untainted	The documentary captured the unadulterated joy of childhood.	Depth-pass addition: Literal not diluted/mixed/contaminated; figurative complete/pure ('unadulterated joy/nonsense'). Contrast adulterated.		
14	134	140	unwavering	/ʌnˈweɪ.vɚ.ɪŋ/	adj	steady or resolute; not changing or becoming weaker	steadfast, firm, resolute	She remained unwavering in her support of the team through tough times.	Depth-pass addition: Add unwavering support/commitment/gaze/voice; steady despite pressure; contrast unwavering vs unchanging.		
14	135	141	denominator	/dɪˈnɑ.məˌneɪ.tɚ/	noun	the number below the line in a fraction; also used metaphorically to refer to a shared trait, characteristic, or factor that connects multiple things or situations. It can also be used to describe something of low quality or appeal designed to reach a broad but undemanding audience. 	divisor, common factor, base number, attribute, feature, manner	"In the fraction 3/4, the number 4 is the denominator. The common denominator for their success was hard work. The reality show was designed to appeal to the lowest common denominator, sacrificing depth for cheap laughs.""
"	"Common Denominator (figurative):
Something shared by all elements in a group.
e.g., A shared sense of responsibility was the common denominator among the team’s top performers.
Despite their different backgrounds, kindness was the common denominator that brought them together.
In every successful project we've led, clear communication was the common denominator.

Lowest Common Denominator (figurative):
A shared trait at the most basic or superficial level — often used to criticize something for being dumbed down.
e.g., The show caters to the lowest common denominator of public taste. They lowered the content to the lowest common denominator just to get more clicks. Depth-pass addition: Preserve current literal + common denominator + lowest common denominator coverage; only clean formatting/quote artifact if needed."		
14	136	142	discerning	/dɪˈsɝ.nɪŋ/	adj	having or showing good judgment and insight	perceptive, insightful, astute, sagacious	She's a discerning reader who prefers complex character development.	Depth-pass addition: Discerning = able to notice fine distinctions and judge quality; common discerning reader/customer/palate; can imply selectiveness.		
14	137	143	disconcert / disconcerted	/ˌdɪs.kənˈsɝ.tɪd/	past tense of 'disconcert'	unsettled or confused, typically due to an unexpected event	unsettled, rattled, flustered	He was disconcerted by the sudden change in her tone.	Depth-pass addition: Use disconcert / disconcerted; disconcerted = unsettled, confused, thrown off; add be disconcerted by/at and contrast embarrassed.		
14	138	144	expository	/ɪkˈspɑ.zɪˌtɔr.i/	adj	intended to explain or describe something	explanatory, descriptive, interpretive	The textbook is filled with expository passages that clarify key concepts.	Depth-pass addition: Expository writing/text/essay explains or informs rather than narrates/persuades; distinguish exposition/expository from explanatory.		
14	139	145	fertility drugs	/fɚˈtɪ.lə.ti drʌɡz/	noun (phrase)	medications used to stimulate ovulation or increase fertility	ovulation stimulants, reproductive medicine	The doctor prescribed fertility drugs to improve her chances of conception.	Depth-pass addition: Use neutral medical wording; note umbrella term for medications used to induce/regulate ovulation or support fertility treatment; avoid treating all as identical.		
14	140	146	fertility issues	/fɚˈtɪ.lə.ti ˈɪʃ.uːz/	noun (phrase)	problems or challenges related to becoming pregnant or reproducing	infertility, reproductive difficulties, conception problems	They sought medical help after struggling with fertility issues for years.	Depth-pass addition: Note broad/nontechnical wording; fertility issues can affect any partner and do not automatically equal a diagnosis of infertility.		
15	141	147	without further ado	/ˈfɝ.ðɚ əˈdu/	noun (phrase)	used to indicate the immediate continuation of an activity, often after a delay	delay, ceremony, fuss (in negative form: 'without further ado')	Without further ado, let's begin the presentation.	Depth-pass addition: Normalize headword; ado = fuss/delay/activity; 'without further ado' = without more delay or ceremony. Ado alone is uncommon outside set phrases.		
