15	143	150	gauge	/ɡeɪdʒ/	verb	to measure, estimate, or judge something	measure, assess, evaluate	It's hard to gauge the team's performance from just one game.	Depth-pass addition: Gauge = measure/estimate/judge; noun instrument/measure/standard; gauge reaction/interest/distance; note common misspelling 'guage'.		
15	144	151	general store	/ˈdʒɛn.rəl stɔr/	noun (phrase)	a retail store in a small town or rural area that sells a wide variety of goods	country store, corner shop, local shop	We stopped by the general store to grab some snacks and supplies.	Depth-pass addition: General store traditionally rural/small-community shop selling varied everyday goods; distinguish convenience store and department store.		
15	145	152	greatest glory in living lies not in never falling but in rising every time we fall	/ˈɡreɪ.təst ˈɡlɔr.i ɪn ˈlɪ.vɪŋ laɪz nɑt ɪn ˈnɛ.vɚ ˈfɑ.lɪŋ bət ɪn ˈraɪ.zɪŋ ˈɛv.ri taɪm wi fɔl/	quotation / proverb-like saying	A motivational saying expressing the idea that real achievement or dignity lies not in never failing, but in recovering and continuing after failure.	resilience, perseverance, getting back up, bouncing back (idea-level equivalents)	After the project failed, she reminded the team that the real test was whether they could learn from it and rise again.	Keep as a quotation card only if its source context is useful to the collection. Online versions are frequently attributed to different historical figures, so do not attach an author without reliable source verification. The learning value is the resilience idea and the wording, not an uncertain attribution.		
15	146	153	incantation	/ˌɪn.kænˈteɪ.ʃən/	noun	a series of words said as a magic spell or charm	chant, spell, invocation	The wizard recited an incantation to unlock the ancient gate.	Depth-pass addition: Add figurative repetitive formula/ritual words if useful; distinguish incantation, invocation, chant, spell.		
15	147	154	incubation	/ˌɪn.kjuˈbeɪ.ʃən/	noun	1. The process of keeping eggs, embryos, or organisms under conditions that allow development. 2. Medicine: the period between infection with a pathogen and the appearance of symptoms. 3. Figuratively, a period in which an idea, project, or plan develops before becoming visible or active.	development, gestation (figurative/biological context); incubation period (medical)	The virus has an incubation period of several days. / The eggs require careful incubation. / The idea spent years in incubation before becoming a real company.	Do not reduce incubation to 'development' in every context. In infectious disease, incubation period specifically runs from infection to symptom onset; it is not automatically the same as a latent or infectious period.		
15	148	155	instill	/ɪnˈstɪl/	verb	To gradually but firmly introduce an idea, value, feeling, habit, or quality into someone's mind or character.	implant, inculcate, imbue, inspire, foster, impress upon	Her parents instilled a strong work ethic in her. / The coach tries to instill confidence in every player. / Constant warnings can instill fear in children.	Canonical card for WID 128 'instills'. Core pattern: instill something in/into someone. Common objects: confidence, fear, discipline, values, habits, respect. Compare inculcate: more formal and often emphasizes repeated teaching or instruction.		
15	149	156	keystone species	/ˈkiː.stoʊn ˈspiː.ʃiz/	noun	A species whose effect on the structure and functioning of an ecosystem is disproportionately large relative to its abundance; removing it can cause major ecological changes.	no exact synonym; ecologically pivotal species (descriptive)	Sea otters are often described as a keystone species because their predation on sea urchins can help maintain kelp forests.	'Central species' or 'ecosystem anchor' can help explain the idea but are not technical synonyms. Keystone species can be predators, ecosystem engineers, mutualists, or other organisms; the defining idea is disproportionate ecological impact.		
15	150	157	lad	/læd/	noun	a boy or young man, often used informally or affectionately	boy, youngster, fellow	The lad ran off to join his friends at the park.	Depth-pass addition: Especially BrE/Irish informal; 'the lads' can mean male friends/team; sometimes jocular. Contrast lad vs boy/guy.		
16	151	158	leverage	/ˈlɛ.vɚ.ɪdʒ/	noun / verb	1. Mechanical advantage gained by using a lever. 2. Influence or bargaining power that helps someone obtain a desired result. 3. Finance: use of borrowed money or debt to increase potential returns, also increasing risk. 4. Verb: to use a resource, advantage, information, or position effectively to achieve a result.	mechanical advantage; influence, bargaining power, clout; financial gearing (BrE, finance); use, exploit, capitalize on (verb, context-dependent)	A longer handle gives you more leverage. / The union used the deadline as leverage in negotiations. / Highly leveraged companies can be vulnerable when interest rates rise. / We can leverage the existing data instead of starting from scratch.	Keep the senses separate. Business-verb 'leverage X to do Y' is extremely common but can sound corporate or jargon-heavy. Financial leverage magnifies both gains and losses.		
16	152	159	linear	/ˈlɪn.i.ɚ/	adjective	1. Arranged in or resembling a straight line. 2. Proceeding in a direct, sequential order. 3. Mathematics: involving a relationship represented by a straight line or satisfying linearity properties. 4. Figuratively, progressing in a simple step-by-step way.	straight-line, sequential, direct; proportional (only in some mathematical contexts)	The story is told in a mostly linear sequence. / Recovery isn't always linear—you can improve for a while and then have a bad week. / The graph shows a roughly linear relationship between the variables.	Contrast WID 164 nonlinear. 'Linear' does not automatically mean continuous, constant, or easy; its exact technical meaning depends on the field.		
16	153	160	die-in / lie-in protest	/ˈlaɪ.ɪŋ daʊn ˈmuːv.mənt/	noun	A form of protest in which participants lie down in a public place, often symbolically representing dead or affected people or physically occupying a space to draw attention to a cause.	die-in, lie-in, lying-down protest (descriptive)	Activists staged a die-in outside the building to protest preventable deaths. / Students organized a lie-in in the lobby as a peaceful demonstration.	Normalize from the vague/nonstandard headword 'lying down movement'. A die-in usually has symbolic associations with death or casualties; a lie-in can more broadly mean a protest involving people lying down. Do NOT confuse this with China's 'lying flat' (tang ping) movement in WID 161.		
16	154	161	lying flat / lying flat movement (tang ping, 躺平)	/ˈlaɪ.ɪŋ flæt ˈmuːv.mənt/	noun / social-cultural term	A Chinese social attitude and trend, widely discussed from 2021 onward, in which people reject or disengage from intense work pressure, hypercompetition, and the relentless pursuit of conventional material success.	tang ping; opting out (partial idea-level equivalent); low-desire lifestyle/passive resistance (descriptive, not exact synonyms)	For some young people, lying flat means refusing the expectation that they must work extreme hours simply to keep competing. / The phrase tang ping became shorthand for stepping back from the conventional race for status and material success.	Chinese: 躺平 (tǎng píng), literally 'lie flat'. The idea became especially associated with frustration over high-pressure work culture, competition, housing and living costs, and limited perceived rewards from relentless striving. It can range from a minimalist/low-desire lifestyle to symbolic passive resistance; it does not necessarily mean literally refusing all work. It is distinct from a die-in or lie-in protest (WID 160). Related Chinese slang such as 摆烂 (bǎi làn, roughly 'let it rot') can express a more resigned 'give up trying to fix it' attitude, but the terms are not identical.		
16	155	162	moonlight / moonlighting	/ˈmuːnˌlaɪ.tɪŋ/	present participle of 'moonlight'	working a second job in addition to one's main employment, typically at night	side hustling, freelancing, holding a second job, working extra	She's been moonlighting as a graphic designer while working full-time in finance. She's moonlighting as a bartender to pay off student loans.	Depth-pass addition: Prefer moonlight / moonlighting; second paid job in addition to primary job, often secretly/without employer knowledge in some contexts; moonlight as + role.		
16	156	163	moonshine	/ˈmuːnˌʃaɪn/	noun	1. Illegally or informally distilled liquor, especially homemade high-proof spirits. 2. Literally, moonlight (older/literary). 3. Dated figurative use: nonsense or foolish talk.	illicit liquor, homemade spirits; moonlight (older sense); nonsense (dated figurative)	The family had once made moonshine in the mountains. / In older writing, moonshine can simply mean the light of the moon. / He dismissed the whole scheme as moonshine.	The illicit-liquor sense is dominant in modern American English. A person who makes illicit spirits may be called a moonshiner. Do not assume the dated 'nonsense' sense from modern context without cues.		
16	157	164	nonlinear / non-linear	/nɑnˈlɪn.i.ɚ/	adjective	1. Not forming or following a straight line. 2. Not proceeding in a simple sequential order. 3. Mathematics/science: not satisfying a linear relationship or linearity conditions. 4. Figuratively, involving irregular changes, feedback, jumps, or multiple paths.	nonsequential, irregular, complex; non-linear (variant spelling)	The film uses a nonlinear narrative that jumps between different periods. / Language learning is nonlinear: progress can come in bursts. / The system exhibits nonlinear behavior.	Standard closed spelling 'nonlinear' is common, though 'non-linear' is also accepted in many styles. Contrast WID 159 linear.		
