16	160	167	offbeat	/ˈɔfˌbit/	adj	unconventional or unusual in style or behavior	quirky, eccentric, unorthodox	He has an offbeat sense of humor that not everyone understands.	Depth-pass addition: Offbeat = unconventional/quirky, often appealing; also musical offbeat noun/adjective sense if useful; distinguish odd/eccentric.		
17	161	168	outthink	/ˌaʊtˈθɪŋk/	verb	to surpass someone in intelligence, strategy, or problem-solving	outsmart, outwit, outmaneuver	The chess champion managed to outthink every opponent in the tournament.	Depth-pass addition: Outthink = defeat by thinking/planning better; often strategy/problem-solving. Contrast outsmart/outwit/outmaneuver.		
17	162	169	podium	/ˈpoʊ.di.əm/	noun	1. A raised platform on which a speaker, conductor, athlete, or other person stands. 2. Especially in American usage, also commonly used for the lectern—the stand that holds a speaker's notes or microphone.	platform, dais; lectern (common AmE usage, though traditionally distinct)	The conductor stepped onto the podium. / She walked to the podium and placed her notes on it.	Traditional distinction: podium = raised platform you stand on; lectern = stand you stand behind and place notes on. In modern AmE, 'podium' is very commonly used for the lectern too, so treating that use as simply 'wrong' would be misleading.		
17	163	170	price gouging	/praɪs ˈɡeɪ.dʒɪŋ/	noun	Charging prices viewed as excessively or unfairly high, especially by sharply raising prices for essential goods or services during shortages, emergencies, or sudden spikes in demand.	excessive pricing, exploitative pricing, profiteering (overlapping, not always legally identical)	The store was accused of price gouging after tripling the price of bottled water during the emergency.	Correct the error 'price gauging' → 'price gouging'. Ordinary price increases or dynamic/surge pricing are not automatically price gouging. Legal definitions and restrictions vary by jurisdiction.		
17	164	171	propaganda	/ˌprɑ.pəˈɡæn.də/	noun	Information, messaging, or material deliberately shaped and spread to influence attitudes or behavior in favor of a political cause, ideology, organization, or authority, often through selective or emotionally persuasive presentation.	persuasive messaging, ideological messaging; agitprop (specific context)	The regime used posters, films, and radio broadcasts as propaganda. / Both sides accused the other of spreading wartime propaganda.	Misinformation is false or inaccurate information, whether intentional or not; disinformation is deliberately false or deceptive information. Propaganda is defined primarily by persuasive purpose and framing, so it can contain true, false, misleading, or selectively presented material. 'Spin' overlaps but is usually narrower and less systematic.		
17	165	172	takeaway	/ˈteɪ.kəˌweɪ/	noun	a key point or main message taken from a discussion, experience, or event	lesson, insight, conclusion	Her biggest takeaway from the workshop was the importance of active listening.	Depth-pass addition: Add takeaway = key lesson/message; food bought to eat elsewhere (especially BrE; AmE takeout); takeaway point. Keep senses separated.		
17	166	173	retrieve	/rɪˈtriːv/	verb	to get or bring something back from a place	recover, fetch, reclaim	He ran back to retrieve his forgotten backpack.	Depth-pass addition: Retrieve object/data/information; retrieve a ball; regain/recover; computing information retrieval. Contrast fetch (go and bring) vs recover.		
17	167	174	rhetoric	/ˈrɛ.t̬ɚ.rɪk/	noun	1. The art or practice of using language effectively and persuasively. 2. A particular style of persuasive language. 3. Often disapproving: impressive-sounding language that is seen as lacking substance, sincerity, or action.	oratory, persuasive language, discourse; bombast, empty words (only in negative sense)	She studied classical rhetoric and public speaking. / The campaign's rhetoric became increasingly aggressive. / Voters were tired of rhetoric and wanted concrete action.	Do not define rhetoric only as empty talk. The neutral academic sense is central. Related: rhetorical device, rhetorical question, rhetorical strategy.		
17	168	175	rocky intertidal zone / rocky shore intertidal zone	/ˈrɑ.ki ˈtaɪ.dəl zoʊn/	noun	The rocky coastal area between the usual high- and low-tide marks, alternately exposed to air and submerged by seawater as tides rise and fall.	rocky intertidal, rocky shore (broader/near-equivalent)	Tide pools in the rocky intertidal zone support organisms that must tolerate both exposure and submersion.	'Coastal reef' is not a synonym. Intertidal specifically refers to the zone affected by the regular rise and fall of tides.		
17	169	176	simile	/ˈsɪm.ə.li/	noun	A figure of speech that explicitly compares one thing with another, typically using words such as 'like' or 'as'.	explicit comparison, figurative comparison	'Her smile was like sunshine' is a simile. / 'He fought like a lion' uses a simile to make the description more vivid.	A metaphor is related but not a synonym: metaphor directly identifies one thing as another ('Time is a thief'), while a simile makes an explicit comparison ('Time is like a thief'). An analogy is usually a more extended comparison used to explain or reason about something.		
17	170	177	stumble upon	/ˈstʌm.bəl əˈpɑn/	phrasal verb	to find or encounter something by chance	come across, discover, happen upon	I stumbled upon an old photo album while cleaning the attic.	Depth-pass addition: Add stumble across/on/upon and figurative chance discovery; distinguish literally stumble over something.		
18	171	178	termite / termites	/ˈtɝ.maɪts/	noun	A social insect that lives in colonies and feeds primarily on cellulose; some species can cause serious damage to wooden structures and other plant-based material.	no exact everyday synonym	The inspector found termite damage in several wooden beams. / Not every termite species is a household pest.	'Destroyers' is not a synonym. Termites are ecologically important decomposers in many environments even though some species are major structural pests.		
18	172	180	voyeur	/ˈvɔɪ.jɚ/	noun	1. A person who secretly watches others engaged in private or intimate activity, especially for sexual pleasure. 2. Figuratively, a person or audience deriving fascination from observing other people's private lives, distress, or intimate experiences.	Peeping Tom (informal, sexual/privacy sense); prying observer (figurative/descriptive)	The man was accused of acting as a voyeur by secretly filming people in a private space. / Some critics said the documentary turned viewers into voyeurs of the family's grief.	Ordinary observer/onlooker is too neutral to be a synonym. The core idea involves intrusive observation, privacy, and often sexual gratification in the primary sense.		
18	173	181	whistleblower	/ˈwɪs.əlˌbloʊ.ɚ/	noun	A person who reports or exposes suspected wrongdoing, illegality, danger, fraud, or misconduct within an organization or system, often using information gained through their position or access.	internal reporter, wrongdoing reporter (descriptive); informant, leaker (overlapping but not equivalent)	The whistleblower reported the accounting irregularities to regulators. / She blew the whistle on unsafe practices at the company.	An informant provides information to authorities but need not be exposing wrongdoing from within an organization; a leaker discloses information, which may or may not concern wrongdoing. Legal definitions and whistleblower protections vary by jurisdiction.		
18	174	182	get hooked on	/ɡɛt hʊkt/	idiom / phrasal pattern; informal	To become addicted to something or strongly absorbed in, enthusiastic about, or attached to it.	become addicted to, get addicted to; get really into, become engrossed in (non-drug sense)	I got hooked on the show after two episodes. / He got hooked on nicotine as a teenager. / Once I started the game, I was completely hooked.	The severity depends on the object: 'hooked on a show' is casual enthusiasm, while 'hooked on a drug' can mean genuine addiction. Common adjective pattern: be hooked on.		
18	175	183	turn to	/tɝn tu ˈsʌmˌθɪŋ ˈsʌmˌwʌn/	phrasal verb	1. To seek help, advice, comfort, or support from someone or something. 2. To begin using something as a solution, resource, occupation, or coping method. 3. To direct attention to a new topic or task. 4. To move to a specified page or section.	seek help from, rely on, resort to; switch to, move on to; consult (context-dependent)	When I'm stuck, I turn to a friend for advice. / Some people turn to alcohol when they're under stress. / Let's turn to the next issue on the agenda. / Turn to page 42.	Do not reduce 'turn to' to 'rely on'. High-frequency patterns include turn to someone for help/advice, turn to something as a solution, turn to a new topic, and turn to page X. 'Resort to' often carries a stronger sense of using a last or undesirable option.		
18	176	184	salve	/sæv/	noun	a soothing ointment for wounds or pain; also used metaphorically for emotional relief	balm, ointment, comfort	Laughter can be a salve for the soul in tough times.	Depth-pass addition: Literal medicinal ointment/balm; figurative comfort/remedy for emotional pain. Add salve a conscience/wound? Note verb salve exists but is less common.		
