19	182	190	geriatric	/ˌdʒɛr.iˈæt.rɪk/	adjective / noun	Relating to older adults, especially their medical care, health, and diseases; as a noun, an older patient/person, though noun use can sound clinical or insensitive.	geriatric-medicine-related, relating to older adults; elderly/older (not exact synonyms)	She specializes in geriatric medicine. / The hospital expanded its geriatric care services.	In medical contexts, geriatric is neutral and technical. Informal use to mean extremely old, decrepit, or outdated can be insulting when applied to people. Prefer 'older adult' in ordinary respectful reference to a person.		
19	183	191	phlebotomist	/fləˈbɑ.tə.mɪst/	noun	a medical professional trained to draw blood for tests, transfusions, or donations	blood technician, medical technician	The phlebotomist was quick and gentle during the blood draw.	Depth-pass addition: Phlebotomist specifically trained to collect blood, usually venipuncture; not generic medical technician. Add phlebotomy.		
19	184	192	antipsychotic	/ˌæn.ti.saɪˈkɑ.tɪks/	noun / adjective	A medication used primarily to treat psychotic symptoms and disorders, and also used in some other psychiatric conditions depending on the drug and clinical situation.	neuroleptic (older/related term, not always perfectly interchangeable); antipsychotic medication	The psychiatrist discussed the potential benefits and side effects of the antipsychotic medication.	'Tranquilizer' and generic 'psychiatric medication' are not synonyms. Antipsychotics are commonly grouped into first-generation and second-generation classes, but individual drugs differ substantially in effects and side-effect profiles.		
19	185	193	boxed warning (black box warning)	/blæk bɑks ˈwɔr.nɪŋ/	noun; U.S. regulatory/medical term	A prominent warning in U.S. prescription-drug labeling used to call attention to serious or life-threatening risks associated with a medication or its use.	boxed warning; black box warning (common informal name)	The prescribing information carries a boxed warning about a serious risk associated with the drug.	'Boxed warning' is the official FDA terminology; 'black box warning' is a very common informal name. A boxed warning does not mean a drug is banned or never appropriate—it signals that prescribers and patients need to consider a particularly serious risk.		
