20	191	199	delirium	/dɪˈlɪr.i.əm/	noun	An acute, usually fluctuating disturbance in attention, awareness, and cognition that develops over a relatively short period and is often triggered by illness, medication, intoxication/withdrawal, surgery, or other medical causes.	acute confusional state (clinical descriptive term); confusion (too broad for exact synonym)	The infection caused sudden delirium, and his attention and awareness fluctuated throughout the day.	Delirium is typically acute and fluctuating, unlike many dementias, which are usually more chronic and progressive. It is also not the same as psychosis, although hallucinations or delusions can occur during delirium. Medical evaluation is important because delirium often reflects an underlying physical cause.		
20	192	200	seizure	/ˈsi.ʒɚ/	noun	1. Medicine: a sudden episode of abnormal electrical activity in the brain that can cause changes in movement, sensation, awareness, behavior, or consciousness. 2. The act of taking possession of property or goods by legal or official authority. 3. The sudden taking of power, control, territory, or an opportunity.	medical: epileptic seizure (when epilepsy-related), fit (informal/variety-dependent); legal: confiscation, taking; power sense: takeover, capture	Not every seizure involves convulsions or loss of consciousness. / Customs officials announced the seizure of illegal goods. / The coup resulted in the seizure of power by the military.	Keep senses separate. A convulsion is a type of involuntary movement and can occur during some seizures, but seizure and convulsion are not synonyms. A single seizure does not by itself establish a diagnosis of epilepsy.		
