23	223	232	It was not until ... that ... / It is not until ... that ...	/ɪt wəz nɑt ənˈtɪl ðæt	emphatic grammatical construction	An emphatic construction used to say that something happened, became true, or was realized only at a particular time or after a particular event.	only when, only after, not before	It was not until I got home that I noticed my wallet was missing. / It wasn't until years later that I understood what she meant. / It's not until the end of the month that we'll know the final result.	Past framing normally uses 'It was/wasn't until ... that ...'; present/future framing can use 'It is/isn't until ... that ...'. Related fronted construction: 'Not until I got home did I notice ...'—this requires subject–auxiliary inversion.		
