33	326	343	rack / wrack (confusable forms)	/ræk	verb; usage note	rack: to cause severe physical or mental suffering, especially in patterns such as be racked with guilt/pain and nerve-racking. wrack survives in some spellings and in fixed/historical uses, but rack is the safer modern choice for torment/strain.	torment, torture, afflict, agonize	He was racked with guilt after the accident. / Waiting for the results was nerve-racking. / The storm-wracked coast suffered extensive damage.	High-confusion card. Prefer racked with guilt/pain and nerve-racking. Wrack is related to ruin/destruction and appears in forms such as storm-wracked; dictionaries/style guides vary on some rack/wrack spellings.		
