39	389	411	innuendo	/ˌɪnjuˈɛndoʊ/	noun	An indirect or subtle reference, often negative or suggestive.	insinuation, suggestion	His comments were full of innuendo about her personal life.	Depth-pass guidance: Add sexual innuendo, innuendo about, by innuendo; distinguish insinuation and euphemism.		
39	390	412	put down	/pʊt daʊn/	phrasal verb	To criticize or belittle someone or something.	belittle, criticize	He was quick to put down anyone who disagreed with him.	Depth-pass guidance: Deepen put someone down (belittle), put something down (place/write/pay deposit/euthanize animal); if card is only insult sense, label clearly and cross-reference noun put-down.		
40	391	413	doublespeak	/ˈdʌbəlˌspiːk/	noun	Language used to obscure or mislead, especially in politics or business.	doubletalk, gobbledygook	The politician's doublespeak made it hard to understand his true intentions.	Depth-pass guidance: Add Orwellian/business euphemistic language nuance; distinguish doublespeak from double-talk and doublethink.		
40	392	414	disparity / disparities	/dɪsˈpærətiz/	noun	A noticeable, important, and often unfair difference or inequality between people, groups, outcomes, or conditions.	gap, inequality, imbalance, discrepancy, difference	The report highlights racial disparities in health outcomes. / There is a large disparity between urban and rural incomes.	Merge plural WID414 and singular WID415 into one productive card. Common patterns: disparity between A and B; disparity in income/outcomes/access; economic/health/racial disparities.		
40	394	416	repercussions	/ˌrɛpərˈkʌʃənz/	noun	Consequences or outcomes, especially negative ones.	consequences, outcomes	The repercussions of his actions were felt by everyone in the company.	Depth-pass guidance: Add 'repercussions for/from', usually negative and often delayed/ripple effects; distinguish consequences (neutral).		
40	395	417	insofar	/ɪnˈsoʊfɑːr/	conjunction	To the extent or degree that something is true or applicable.	to the extent, as far as	This argument is valid insofar as it applies to the current situation.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize 'insofar as' as full conjunction; 'insofar' alone is incomplete in most learner contexts. Also 'in so far as' variant.		
40	396	419	pit someone/something against someone/something	/pɪt iːtʃ ˈʌðər/	verb phrase	To set people, groups, ideas, or competitors against one another so that they compete, fight, or are compared as opponents.	set against, turn against, make compete, play off against	The format pits two teams against each other. / The policy risks pitting younger workers against older ones. / The article pits tradition against innovation.	Normalize away from unnatural 'pit each other'. Core construction: pit A against B. Reciprocal: be pitted against each other.		
40	397	420	toon bag / cartoon-illusion bag	/tuːn bæɡz/	noun phrase (informal/descriptive)	A real bag designed with bold outlines, flat colors, and visual perspective so that it appears like a two-dimensional cartoon drawing. In other contexts, 'toon bag' can simply mean a bag featuring cartoon characters, so the intended meaning depends on context.	cartoon bag, 2D bag, cartoon-illusion bag, pop-art bag (context-dependent)	Her toon bag looked like a flat cartoon drawing until she opened it and put her laptop inside. / The shop sells brightly outlined cartoon bags that create a 2D illusion.	KEEP, but replace the fabricated animation-studio definition. The useful fashion sense is a descriptive/informal label rather than one tightly standardized dictionary term; brands such as JumpFromPaper popularized the 2D/cartoon-illusion style. In other contexts 'toon bag' may refer generically to cartoon-character merchandise, and an isolated occurrence could also be a typo/mishearing of another bag term. Preserve context sensitivity.		
40	398	421	succumbed	/səˈkʌmd/	verb	To give in to a force or overwhelming influence.	yield, surrender	He succumbed to the pressure and agreed to their terms.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize succumb/succumbed; patterns succumb to pressure/temptation/injuries/disease. Often implies inability to resist; with illness/injuries can mean die.		
40	399	422	utilitarian	/ˌjuːtɪlɪˈtɛriən/	adjective	Focused on utility or practicality, often at the expense of aesthetics.	practical, functional	The utilitarian design of the building prioritized function over form.	Depth-pass guidance: Separate everyday utilitarian = practical/function-first from philosophical Utilitarian/utilitarianism; capitalization/context.		
40	400	423	oxymoronic	/ˌɒksɪmɔːˈrɒnɪk/	adjective	A contradiction in terms, combining two seemingly opposite ideas.	contradictory, paradoxical	The phrase 'jumbo shrimp' is an example of an oxymoronic expression.	Depth-pass guidance: Teach oxymoron/oxymoronic; distinguish paradox and contradiction; 'deafening silence' etc. are conventional examples.		
41	401	424	insurmountable	/ˌɪnsərˈmaʊntəbl/	adjective	Too great to be overcome or dealt with.	unbeatable, invincible	The mountain was insurmountable, and they turned back.	Depth-pass guidance: Add insurmountable obstacle/problem/difficulty/odds; not literally 'unbeatable' in all contexts.		
41	402	425	bumptiousness	/ˈbʌmptʃəsnəs/	noun	The quality of being self-important or arrogantly assertive.	arrogance, conceit	His bumptiousness made him unpopular among his colleagues.	Depth-pass guidance: Rare/formal word; teach bumptious/bumptiousness with register note. Synonyms pushy, self-important, officious depending context.		
41	403	426	canonize	/ˈkænənaɪz/	verb	To officially declare someone or something as sacred or worthy of honor.	sanctify, glorify	The church decided to canonize the bishop for his devotion to the faith.	Depth-pass guidance: Cover Catholic/Orthodox formal saint declaration plus figurative canonize a cultural figure/work; distinguish canon/canonical.		
41	404	427	temper / tempered	/ˈtɛmpərd/	verb; adjective	temper (v.): to moderate, soften, or balance something by adding a counteracting influence. tempered (adj.): moderated or restrained. In 'tempered glass', tempered means specially heat- or chemically-treated to increase strength and safety.	moderate, soften, mitigate, restrain, balance; toughened (glass)	Her optimism was tempered by caution. / The criticism was tempered with praise. / The door is made of tempered glass.	Fix definition/example mismatch. 'Tempered glass' is a technical material sense, not simply 'moderated glass'. Common pattern: temper A with B / be tempered by B.		
41	405	428	misshapes	/ˈmɪʃeɪps/	verb	To form or shape in a flawed or improper way.	distort, deform	The artist misshapes the clay into abstract figures.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize misshape/misshapen. 'misshapes' is uncommon retrieval form; useful adjective misshapen likely higher value.		
41	406	429	aberrant	/æbəˈreɪtɪd/	adjective	Departing from what is normal, typical, expected, or accepted.	abnormal, atypical, anomalous, deviant, irregular	The researchers treated the result as an aberrant data point. / His behavior that night was highly aberrant.	Replace rare/nonstandard-looking 'aberrated' adjective with productive modern 'aberrant'. Related noun: aberration. Avoid 'deviant' as a blanket synonym when it adds moral/social judgment.		
41	407	430	demented	/dɪˈmɛntɪd/	adjective	Mentally unstable or insane.	insane, irrational	The demented man wandered the streets, confused and lost.	Depth-pass guidance: Update usage: demented can mean wildly irrational/crazy informally but referring to a person with dementia as 'demented' is dated/insensitive. Add figurative 'demented plan/laugh' with register caution.		
41	408	431	avarice	/ˈævərɪs/	noun	Extreme greed or selfishness.	greed, cupidity	His avarice led him to make unethical decisions in business.	Depth-pass guidance: Add avaricious; literary/formal stronger greed, especially wealth/money.		
41	409	432	eschewing	/ɪsˈtʃuɪŋ/	verb	To avoid or deliberately abstain from.	avoiding, shunning	She was eschewing the traditional path to follow her own dreams.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize eschew; transitive without 'from': eschew tradition/alcohol/violence. Formal/literary.		
41	410	433	angst	/æŋɡst/	noun	A deep feeling of anxiety or dread.	fear, anxiety	The film's themes of existential angst resonated with many viewers.	Depth-pass guidance: Add existential/teenage angst; borrowed German but ordinary English; distinguish anxiety (clinical/general) from angst (brooding unease).		
42	411	434	atomistic	/ætəˈmɪstɪk/	adjective	Relating to or emphasizing individual elements over the whole.	fragmented, individual	The atomistic approach broke the issue into smaller, manageable parts.	Depth-pass guidance: Explain technical/philosophical/social-science use: treating entities as independent units rather than relations/whole; contrast holistic/systemic.		
42	412	435	Pleistocene	/ˈplaɪstəsin/	noun	The geological epoch marking the last ice age, around 2.6 million years ago.	ice age	The Pleistocene epoch was marked by the last ice age.	Depth-pass guidance: Correct geological precision: Pleistocene ~2.58 million to 11,700 years ago; not simply 'the last ice age'. Mention epoch capitalization.		
42	413	436	worked up	/wɜːrkt ʌp/	verb phrase	To become agitated or upset.	agitated, upset	She was so worked up about the situation that she could hardly think straight.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize get/be worked up about/over; emotionally agitated/excited, often negative. Also 'work someone up'.		
42	414	437	go berserk	/ɡoʊ bərˈzɜrk/	verb phrase	To lose control of one's temper and behave violently or irrationally.	rage, explode	He went berserk when he found out what had happened.	Depth-pass guidance: Add go berserk over/when; strong informal expression for losing control, not necessarily literal violence.		
42	415	438	wet market	/wɛt ˈmɑrkɪt/	noun	A market selling fresh food, particularly meat, fish, and produce.	fresh market, food market	The wet market was bustling with vendors selling fresh fish and produce.	Depth-pass guidance: Clarify wet market = market selling fresh meat/fish/produce and other perishables, historically contrasted with dry goods; not inherently exotic or unsanitary.		
42	416	439	perishable	/ˈpɛrɪʃəbl/	adjective	Subject to decay or spoilage, especially in a short period of time.	decaying, spoilable	Milk is a perishable item that needs to be refrigerated.	Depth-pass guidance: Add perishable goods/food; noun plural perishables; contrast nonperishable/shelf-stable.		
42	417	440	imminent	/ˈɪmɪnənt/	adjective	About to happen; forthcoming.	impending, forthcoming	The storm's imminent arrival caused panic in the coastal towns.	Depth-pass guidance: Add imminent danger/arrival/collapse; distinguish eminent/immanent. Forthcoming is not always imminent.		
42	418	441	posit	/ˈpɒzɪt/	verb	To assume or assert the existence or truth of something. to put forward as a fact or as a basis for argument	assert, propose, suggest, hypothesize, postulate, presuppose	He posited that the data would support his hypothesis. The theory posits that all matter is composed of tiny particles.	Depth-pass guidance: Already relatively strong; add formal/academic register and posit X / posit that; distinguish hypothesize/postulate/assume.		
42	419	442	proprietary	/prəˈpraɪəˌtɛri/	adjective	Relating to or owned by a specific individual or company, often legally protected.	exclusive, controlled	The proprietary technology gave the company a competitive edge.	Depth-pass guidance: Add proprietary software/data/formula/technology and ownership sense; distinguish confidential/trade secret/open-source.		
42	420	443	propriety	/prəˈpraɪəti/	noun	The state or quality of being proper, correct, or acceptable.	correctness, decency	She acted with propriety during the formal event.	Depth-pass guidance: Add propriety of doing something, sense of propriety; distinguish proprietary (common confusable).		
43	421	444	decorum	/dɪˈkɔːrəm/	noun	Behavior that is appropriate or conforming to established conventions.	etiquette, politeness	He maintained a sense of decorum even in stressful situations.	Depth-pass guidance: Add maintain/observe decorum, breach/lack of decorum; formal social behavior, not exactly etiquette.		
43	422	445	pertain	/pərˈteɪn/	verb	To be relevant or related to something.	relate, concern	The details pertain to the original agreement.	Depth-pass guidance: Add pertain to only; formal. Distinguish appertain if useful, and 'pertinent' adjective.		
43	423	446	demise	/dɪˈmaɪz/	noun	The end or death of something, especially life or a system.	death, end	The demise of the company was due to poor management.	Depth-pass guidance: Add demise of person/company/system; formal/euphemistic for death; verb demise is specialized and not needed.		
43	424	447	bright eyed and bushy tailed	/braɪt aɪd ənd ˈbʊʃi teɪld/	adjective	Having a cheerful, energetic, or optimistic attitude.	energetic, enthusiastic	She was bright eyed and bushy tailed, ready to start the day.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize hyphens 'bright-eyed and bushy-tailed'; cheerful, alert, eager, often humorous/ironic especially early morning.		
43	425	448	nooks and crannies	/nʊks ənd ˈkræniz/	noun	Hidden or small, often in places that are hard to find or reach.	crevices, corners	The old house was full of nooks and crannies, hiding all sorts of treasures.	Depth-pass guidance: Add every nook and cranny / search the nooks and crannies; small recesses/corners, often figurative exhaustive search.		
43	426	449	dissertation	/ˌdɪsərˈteɪʃən/	noun	A long, formal written work, usually for an academic degree.	thesis, treatise	She wrote a dissertation on modern architecture for her PhD.	Depth-pass guidance: Distinguish dissertation vs thesis across US/UK academic usage; PhD dissertation (US) vs thesis terminology varies.		
43	427	450	petulantly	/ˈpɛtʃələntli/	adverb	In a childish or sulky manner, often in response to something unfair.	irritably, grumpily	He petulantly stomped out of the room after hearing the news.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize petulant/petulantly; childishly sulky/irritable especially when thwarted; add petulant tone/response.		
43	428	451	felicitate	/fəˈlɪsɪteɪt/	verb	To congratulate or wish success to someone.	congratulate, praise	I want to felicitate you on your recent success.	Depth-pass guidance: Rare/formal and much more common in Indian English/formal registers than everyday AmE/BrE; ordinary choice congratulate.		
43	429	452	buttress	/ˈbʌtrəs/	verb; noun	verb: to support, strengthen, or reinforce an argument, structure, position, or system. noun: a projecting support built against a wall; figuratively, a source of support.	support, reinforce, strengthen, bolster, shore up	The new evidence buttressed her argument. / Additional beams were added to buttress the wall. / Flying buttresses support the cathedral walls.	Current POS says noun while definition/example use the verb. Teach both. Figurative verb 'buttress an argument/claim' is especially useful.		
43	430	453	refute	/rɪˈfjuːt/	verb	To prove something to be false or incorrect.	disprove, contradict	She refuted all the claims made against her.	Depth-pass guidance: Refute means show/prove claim false, not merely deny/contradict. Add refute claim/argument/allegation; distinguish rebut and deny.		
44	431	454	discern	/dɪsˈɜːrn/	verb	To perceive or recognize something clearly.	perceive, recognize	He could discern the truth in her eyes.	Depth-pass guidance: Add discern difference/pattern/meaning; discern A from B; formal, often difficult-to-perceive nuance; discerning adjective separate sense.		
44	432	455	incipient	/ɪnˈsɪpɪtənt/	adjective	Beginning to develop or exist; at an early stage, often before becoming fully formed or obvious.	emerging, developing, beginning, nascent	Doctors detected signs of incipient disease. / The government moved quickly to contain the incipient rebellion.	Correct typo 'incipitent' → incipient. Common collocations: incipient disease, crisis, movement, rebellion, change.		
44	433	457	denial	/dɪˈnaɪəl/	noun	A refusal to accept, acknowledge, believe, or admit something; also a statement that something is not true. In psychology, denial can refer to avoiding an uncomfortable reality.	refusal, rejection, repudiation, disavowal	He issued a firm denial of the allegations. / She was still in denial about how serious the problem had become.	Remove obsolete note '(duplicate of #456)' because WID456 is absent. Useful patterns: denial of allegations/facts; be in denial; deny wrongdoing.		
44	434	458	unwarranted	/ʌnˈwɔːrəntɪd/	adjective	Not justified or authorized by the circumstances.	unjustified, unnecessary	The unwarranted criticism was hurtful and unnecessary.	Depth-pass guidance: Add unwarranted assumption/attack/intrusion/confidence; unjustified, not necessarily unauthorized.		
44	435	459	self-denial	/sɛlf dɪˈnaɪəl/	noun	The deliberate refusal or restriction of one's own desires, pleasures, or comforts, often for moral, religious, practical, or disciplinary reasons.	self-restraint, abstinence, self-discipline, renunciation	Saving that much money required years of self-denial. / The tradition emphasizes fasting and self-denial.	Standard spelling is usually self-denial. Distinguish from denial of one's identity/feelings and from healthy self-control; context determines whether self-denial is praised or criticized.		
44	436	460	hold someone accountable for something / hold someone to something	/hoʊld əˈkaʊntəbl tuː ɔːr fɔːr/	verb phrases	hold someone accountable for something: require them to accept responsibility for an action, decision, or outcome. hold someone to something: insist that they keep a promise, agreement, standard, or stated position.	hold responsible, answer for; enforce, insist on, bind to	We need to hold officials accountable for their decisions. / You said you'd finish by Friday, and I'm going to hold you to it. / The company should be held to the same standards as its competitors.	Split the two constructions because 'accountable to' and 'hold someone to' are not interchangeable. A person can also be accountable to a boss/body (= answerable to them).		
44	437	461	abdicate	/ˈæbdɪkeɪt/	verb	To formally give up a position of power or responsibility.	resign, relinquish	The king decided to abdicate the throne after much pressure.	Depth-pass guidance: Add abdicate throne + abdicate responsibility; formal; distinguish resign, renounce, relinquish.		
44	438	463	model-agnostic / algorithm-agnostic	/æɡˈnɒstɪk əˈproʊtʃ ɪn məˈʃiːn ˈlɜːnɪŋ/	adjective (technical)	Designed to work without depending on one particular model, algorithm, architecture, or implementation. In machine learning, a model-agnostic method can be applied across different models rather than being tied to one specific model type.	model-independent, algorithm-independent, architecture-agnostic, implementation-independent	Permutation feature importance is often described as model-agnostic because it can be applied to many different fitted models. / We wanted an algorithm-agnostic evaluation framework.	Redesign vague 'agnostic approach in machine learning'. In technical compounds, -agnostic means 'not tied to / independent of a particular X', not 'open-minded'. The exact compound should match what is being abstracted away: model-agnostic, framework-agnostic, platform-agnostic, etc.		
44	440	465	agility	/əˈdʒɪləti/	noun	The ability to move quickly and easily.	dexterity, flexibility	Her agility on the dance floor impressed everyone at the competition.	Depth-pass guidance: Expand physical agility + mental/business/organizational agility; agile/agility; distinguish dexterity.		
45	441	467	iterative	/ˈɪtəˌreɪtɪv/	adjective	Describes a process or method that is done repeatedly or in cycles.	repetitive, cyclical	The iterative approach ensured that improvements were made after each cycle.	Depth-pass guidance: Add iterate/iteration/iteratively; iterative design/process/development; distinguish repetitive (can imply mere repetition without learning).		
45	442	468	intrinsic	/ɪnˈtrɪnsɪk/	adjective	Belonging naturally; essential or inherent.	inherent, innate	The intrinsic value of the art lies in its cultural significance.	KEEP as a distinct usage-profile card. Deepen intrinsic around intrinsic value/motivation/property and the sense of belonging to the essential nature of something or arising from within. Cross-reference WID480 inherent, but do not merge merely because meanings overlap.		
45	443	469	Luddite	/ˈluːdɪt/	noun; adjective (often informal)	A person who opposes, distrusts, or is reluctant to adopt new technology or technological change; historically, a member of the early-19th-century English Luddite movement.	technophobe, technology skeptic (context-dependent)	I'm not a Luddite, but I don't need every appliance in my house connected to the internet. / He jokingly calls himself a Luddite because he still uses a flip phone.	Correct typo 'lludite' → Luddite. Capitalized from the historical Luddites. Modern use is often humorous or critical and can oversimplify the historical movement; it does not simply mean 'old/outdated'.		
45	444	470	gibbous	/ˈɡɪbəs/	adjective	Describes the phase of the moon when more than half of it is illuminated.	luminous, crescent	The moon appeared gibbous, almost full but not quite.	Depth-pass guidance: Correct moon terminology: waxing gibbous vs waning gibbous; more than half illuminated but less than full. 'luminous/crescent' are not synonyms.		
45	445	471	scowl	/skaʊl/	verb	To make a facial expression indicating displeasure or disapproval.	frown, glare	He scowled at the proposal, clearly displeased with the idea.	Depth-pass guidance: Add scowl at someone / scowl in disapproval; noun a scowl; stronger/darker than frown.		
45	446	472	convene	/kənˈviːn/	verb	To come together or assemble, typically for a formal meeting.	assemble, gather	The committee will convene tomorrow to discuss the new policies.	Depth-pass guidance: Transitive and intransitive: convene a meeting/committee; committee convenes. Formal/institutional.		
45	447	473	equity	/ˈɛkwɪti/	noun	Fairness or justice in the way people are treated or resources/opportunities are distributed, often taking differing needs or circumstances into account. Also has important legal/financial senses.	fairness, justice; ownership interest (finance); equity (law)	The program aims to improve equity in access to healthcare. / The company has $2 million in shareholders' equity. / She built substantial equity in her home.	Keep separate because equity has major finance/legal senses beyond the social-policy contrast with equality. Cross-reference WID475 for equity vs. equality.		
45	448	474	equality	/ɪˈkwɒləti/	noun	The state of being equal in status, rights, opportunities, treatment, quantity, or value.	equal status, parity, equal treatment, equivalence (context-dependent)	The law guarantees equality before the law. / The organization campaigns for gender equality. / Equality of opportunity does not necessarily produce equality of outcome.	Keep as a productive standalone concept. Cross-reference WID475; do not define equality merely as 'uniformity'.		
45	449	475	equity vs. equality	/ˈɛkwɪti vɪz ˈɪkwɒləti wɒts ðə ˈdɪfrəns/	comparison card	Equality generally focuses on people having equal status, rights, opportunities, treatment, or shares; equity focuses on fairness and may take different needs or starting conditions into account. The contrast is context-dependent, and the words are not simple opposites.	equal treatment vs. fairness; parity vs. needs-sensitive fairness	Giving every student the same device is an equality-based approach; providing additional accessibility tools to students who need them is an equity-based measure. / The debate involves both equality of opportunity and equity in access.	Keep as comparison card because it teaches selection between two highly confusable words. Avoid oversimplified meme-style formulas such as 'equality = same, equity = fair' as universal definitions. Equity also has finance/legal senses not covered by this contrast.		
45	450	476	have reservations about	/hæv rɛzərˈveɪʃən/	verb phrase	To have doubts, concerns, or misgivings about a plan, idea, person, or course of action.	have doubts, have concerns, have misgivings, be hesitant about	I have some reservations about signing a five-year contract. / She had serious reservations about the proposal but agreed to hear them out.	Usually plural reservations in this sense: have reservations about something. Singular 'a reservation' more often means a booking or a specific reserved condition/right, though 'I have one reservation' can mean one specific concern.		
46	451	477	quaking	/ˈkweɪkɪŋ/	verb	To tremble or shake, often due to fear or anxiety.	tremble, shiver	The ground was quaking as the earthquake hit the city.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize quake/quaking; physical shaking, ground quake, quake with fear; distinguish quiver/tremble.		
46	452	478	deity	/ˈdiːəti/	noun	A god or goddess, especially in a polytheistic religion.	god, divinity	The deity of the sun was worshipped by the ancient civilization.	Depth-pass guidance: Add deity vs god/goddess/divinity; deity can be gender-neutral and formal; 'a deity associated with the sun'.		
46	453	479	directive / directive meaning	/dəˈrɛktɪv miːn/	noun; adjective	directive (n.): an official instruction or order. directive (adj.): intended to direct, guide, or control. In linguistics/pragmatics, a directive is an utterance intended to get someone to do something.	instruction, order, command, mandate, guideline (depending force)	The agency issued a directive requiring new safety checks. / 'Please close the door' can function as a directive even though it is phrased politely.	Replace broken headword 'directive mean'. If original intent was 'What does directive mean?', the vocabulary item should simply be directive. Distinguish binding directives/orders from softer guidance.		
46	454	480	inherent	/ɪnˈhɪərənt/	adjective	Belonging naturally; essential or inherent.	innate, natural	Trust and compassion are inherent qualities of a good leader.	KEEP as a distinct usage-profile card. Deepen inherent around 'inherent in', inherent risk/problem/limitation/quality: a feature inseparable from or characteristic of something. Cross-reference WID468 intrinsic and teach the selection contrast rather than merging on semantic similarity alone.		
46	455	481	conundrum	/kəˈnʌndrəm/	noun	A difficult or confusing problem or question.	puzzle, mystery	The conundrum of the puzzle baffled the team for hours.	Depth-pass guidance: Fix circular example 'conundrum of the puzzle'; difficult problem/dilemma, sometimes riddle historically. Add pose/face/solve a conundrum.		
46	456	482	pasture	/ˈpɑːstʃər/	noun	A piece of land used for grazing or farming animals.	meadow, field	The cows were grazing peacefully in the lush pasture.	Depth-pass guidance: Add pasture vs meadow/field; grazing land; put/turn animals out to pasture; figurative 'put out to pasture' if useful.		
46	457	483	graze	/ɡreɪz/	verb	To feed on grass or other vegetation, typically by animals.	feed, pasture; scrape, brush, skim (contact sense)	Sheep grazed on the hillside all afternoon. / The bullet grazed his shoulder without causing a serious wound. / My bag grazed the wall as I squeezed past.	Two major verb senses: animals feed on growing vegetation; something lightly touches/scrapes a surface or skin. The feeding sense is especially common with cattle/sheep; the contact sense often implies only slight damage. | Depth-pass guidance: Already enriched in earlier merge; KEEP unless later duplicate reconciliation finds another graze card.		
46	458	484	lush	/lʌʃ/	adjective	Covered in rich, green vegetation, or growing abundantly.	verdant, abundant	The lush green landscape was breathtaking after the rain.	Depth-pass guidance: Add lush vegetation/landscape + rich/luxurious 'lush production' and informal lush hair; avoid overexpanding noun 'lush' unless useful.		
46	459	485	big fast one	/bɪɡ fæst wʌn/	contextual noun phrase; related idiom / pronoun usage	A context-dependent phrase whose interpretation comes from 'one' plus its modifiers. It can mean a big, fast instance of a previously mentioned countable thing ('a big, fast one' = e.g. a big, fast car); in context it can refer to a notably fast case, process, approval, application, or other event under discussion ('this/that one was fast'); and it should also be learned alongside the separate idiom 'a fast one / pull a fast one', meaning a trick or deception.	a big/fast case or instance (context-dependent); a fast one / pull a fast one = trick, deception, con	Let's buy a new car. — Yeah, a big, fast one! / Wow, this one was fast. / That was a really big, fast one, huh? / That I-485 approval was incredibly fast—this one moved unusually quickly. / Don't let them pull a fast one on you.	KEEP the original headword 'big fast one'; do not collapse it into a single interpretation. Usage cluster 1: one/ones substitution—'a big, fast one' can replace a previously mentioned countable noun such as car. Usage cluster 2: contextual one = the case/process/application/event currently under discussion; fast describes unusually quick completion or processing. Preserve the immigration/I-485-type context supplied by Jin as an example of this contextual reading: an unusually fast approval/application case can be described conversationally with 'this/that one was fast' or a modified 'fast one'. Do not claim 'big fast one' is an official USCIS term or a fixed immigration idiom. Usage cluster 3: separately, 'a fast one / pull a fast one on someone' is an established informal idiom meaning a trick/deception; 'big' can contextually intensify the scale. Punctuation depends on structure: 'a big, fast one' naturally coordinates adjectives modifying one.		
46	460	486	serfs	/sɜːfs/	noun	Peasants or laborers who are bound to the land and work for a feudal lord.	laborers, peasants	The serfs worked the land for the lord, never owning anything themselves.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize serf/serfs/serfdom; tied to land under feudal system, not simply any peasant/laborer.		
47	461	487	onerous	/ˈəʊnərəs/	adjective	Burdening or causing difficulty, especially in terms of obligations or tasks.	burdensome, difficult	The onerous task of cleaning the entire building took all day.	Depth-pass guidance: Add onerous burden/task/requirement/obligation; formal, burdensome because demanding, costly, or restrictive.		
47	462	488	Glyphosate	/ˈɡlɪfəʊseɪt/	noun	A chemical compound used as a herbicide to kill weeds.	weedkiller, pesticide	Glyphosate is widely used in agriculture to control weeds.	Depth-pass guidance: Capitalize generically as glyphosate unless sentence-initial; define herbicide, not pesticide synonym exactly. Avoid safety claims beyond vocabulary scope.		
47	463	489	herbicide	/ˈhɜːbɪsaɪd/	noun	A chemical substance used to kill unwanted plants (weeds).	pesticide, weedkiller	Herbicide is sprayed on fields to prevent the growth of weeds.	Depth-pass guidance: Herbicide is a type of pesticide targeting plants; weedkiller informal. Add herbicidal.		
47	464	490	dubbed	/dʌbd/	verb	To give a name or label to someone or something.	named, titled	She was dubbed 'The Queen of the Day' for her excellent performance.	Depth-pass guidance: Expand dub someone/something X = give nickname/title; dub a film = replace dialogue with translated/other-language audio; distinguish subtitle.		
47	465	491	sought-after	/sɔːt ˈɑːftər/	adjective	Greatly desired or in high demand.	in-demand, coveted, desirable, highly prized	She's one of the most sought-after designers in the industry. / Apartments in that neighborhood are highly sought after.	Use hyphen before a noun: a sought-after expert/product. Usually no hyphen after a linking verb: the tickets are highly sought after.		
47	466	492	salivating	/ˈsælɪveɪtɪŋ/	adjective	To be drooling or showing excessive interest in something, usually in anticipation.	drooling, eager	The dog was salivating at the sight of the food.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize salivate/salivating; literal produce saliva + figurative salivate over/at prospect = eager/desirous, often humorous.		
47	467	493	run of the mill	/rʌn əv ðə mɪl/	idiom	Ordinary, not special or unique.	average, typical	The restaurant was run of the mill; nothing special, just basic food.	Depth-pass guidance: Hyphenate attributive/adjectival idiom 'run-of-the-mill'; ordinary/unexceptional; examples before noun and predicate.		
47	468	494	round up	/raʊnd ʌp/	phrasal verb	To gather people, animals, or things together; to find and collect what is needed. Also, in mathematics, to increase a number to the next specified place/value.	gather, collect, assemble, corral	Can you round up everyone for a quick meeting? / I need to round up the receipts before I file the claim. / Round 3.76 up to the nearest tenth.	Fix POS from noun to phrasal verb. Noun is 'roundup/round-up' depending sense/style, e.g. a news roundup. Be cautious with law-enforcement 'round up people', which can sound coercive.		
47	469	495	maladies	/ˈmælədiz/	noun	Diseases or health conditions, often used in a general sense.	diseases, conditions	The doctor treated several maladies, including a persistent cough and fever.	Depth-pass guidance: Normalize malady/maladies; somewhat formal/old-fashioned for illness/problem; figurative social/economic maladies.		
47	470	496	chalk it up to something	/ʧɔːk ɪt ʌp/	verb phrase	To attribute an event, result, or situation to a particular cause or explanation, often casually or provisionally.	attribute to, put down to, ascribe to, blame on (negative contexts)	I chalked the mistake up to inexperience. / Let's chalk it up to bad luck and move on.	Normalize to the productive construction 'chalk it up to X'. Existing related cards should be checked during later global duplicate reconciliation; this WID is currently useful as the early canonical card in this range.		
48	472	498	pro-life	/prəʊˈlaɪf/	adjective; noun modifier	Describing a position or movement that opposes abortion and generally supports legal protections for fetuses/unborn life; terminology is politically contested and may be self-identifying.	anti-abortion (more descriptive in some contexts), abortion-opposing	She describes herself as pro-life. / The bill drew support from several pro-life organizations.	Use neutral descriptive framing. 'Pro-birth' is usually a critical label, not a neutral synonym, so remove it from synonyms. Cross-reference pro-choice WID311. Exact policy positions vary among people/groups using either label.		
48	473	499	adverse	/ædvɜːs/	adjective	Having a negative effect, harmful, or unfavorable.	harmful, negative	The adverse effects of the drug became evident after a few weeks.	Depth-pass guidance: Add adverse effect/reaction/conditions/weather; distinguish averse (to) and adversity.		
48	474	500	conspire	/kənˈspaɪə/	verb	To plot or scheme, usually with others, to achieve something secretly or illegally.	plot, scheme	They conspired to create a plan that would help them achieve their goal.	Depth-pass guidance: Add conspire with someone to do / conspire against; also events/circumstances conspire to produce outcome (figurative, no secret plot).		
48	475	501	double whammy	/ˈdʌbl ˈwæmi/	noun phrase	A situation in which two separate problems, disadvantages, or blows occur together or in close succession, making the overall effect worse.	double blow, two setbacks at once, one-two punch (context-dependent)	Losing my job and then having my rent raised was a double whammy. / The business was hit by the double whammy of rising costs and falling demand.	Usually negative. Common pattern: the double whammy of A and B / be hit by a double whammy. It can also be used more loosely for two simultaneous strong effects.		
48	476	502	twofold	/ˈtuːfəʊld/	adjective; adverb	Having two parts, aspects, or purposes; or by a factor of two / to twice the degree.	dual, two-part, two-pronged; twice, two times (adverbial sense)	The benefit is twofold: it saves time and reduces costs. / Sales increased twofold over the decade.	Very common pattern: 'The reason/benefit/problem is twofold: ...'. Distinguish 'twofold' = two aspects or twice as much from 'double' in constructions where only one is idiomatic.		
48	477	504	rife	/raɪf/	adjective	Very common, widespread, or abundant, especially something undesirable.	widespread, prevalent, rampant, abundant	The industry was rife with rumors. / The report found a system rife with corruption.	Usually predicative and followed by with: be rife with rumors/corruption/speculation. Often negative. 'Rampant' is close but not interchangeable everywhere.		
48	478	505	deduction	/dɪˈdʌkʃən/	noun	1) A conclusion reached by reasoning from evidence or premises. 2) The process of deductive reasoning. 3) An amount subtracted, especially from pay, tax, or a total.	inference, conclusion; subtraction, reduction, allowance (financial contexts)	From those clues, she made a reasonable deduction. / Taxes and other deductions are taken from my paycheck.	Important polysemy: reasoning vs subtraction/financial deduction. Cross-reference deduct/deductive. 'Inference' is broader than deduction.		
48	479	506	plausible	/ˈplɔːzəbl/	adjective	Seeming reasonable, believable, or possible, especially on the basis of available facts, even if not proven true.	believable, credible, reasonable, conceivable, possible	That's a plausible explanation, but we still need evidence. / The ending felt plausible given what the character had been through.	Plausible ≠ proven or necessarily likely. A story/explanation can be plausible yet false. Common: plausible explanation/scenario/argument.		
48	480	507	expenditure	/ɪksˈpɛndɪtʃər/	noun	The act or amount of spending money, time, energy, or other resources; in finance, an amount spent.	spending, outlay, expense, disbursement	The company cut capital expenditure this year. / The trip required a considerable expenditure of time and money.	Formal. Countable for specific amounts/items; uncountable for the act of spending. Common: government/public/capital expenditure; expenditure on X; expenditure of time/energy.		
49	481	508	explicitly	/ɪksˈplɪsɪtli/	adverb	In a clear, direct, and unambiguous way; stated rather than merely implied.	clearly, directly, expressly, specifically, unambiguously	The rules explicitly prohibit outside food. / She never explicitly said no, but her meaning was obvious.	Contrast explicitly with implicitly. 'Expressly' often means specifically and deliberately stated.		
49	482	509	imprudent	/ɪmˈpruːdənt/	adjective	Not showing good judgment about risks or consequences; unwise or inadvisable.	unwise, ill-advised, rash, injudicious	It would be imprudent to spend all our savings at once. / The company made an imprudent investment.	Formal. Stronger and more judgment-oriented than merely careless. Direct contrast with prudent.		
49	483	510	prudent	/ˈpruːdənt/	adjective	Showing careful, sensible judgment, especially by considering future risks and consequences.	wise, sensible, judicious, cautious, advisable	It would be prudent to keep an emergency fund. / She took the prudent step of getting a second opinion.	Often used for risk/finance/decisions: prudent decision, prudent investment, prudent to do. Contrast imprudent; prudent does not simply mean timid/cautious.		
49	484	511	slash / slashing	/ˈslæʃɪŋ/	verb; adjective	slash (v.): to cut violently or deeply; to reduce something sharply; to strike with a sweeping cutting motion. slashing (adj.): cutting or sharply forceful; also used in compounds such as slashing attack.	cut, gash, slice; cut/reduce drastically	The company slashed prices by 30%. / He slashed the rope with a knife. / The budget faces another round of slashing cuts.	Redesign because current 'slashing winds' example is unnatural for the intended lexical core. High-value figurative use: slash prices/costs/jobs/budgets. Related noun: slash.		
49	485	512	discern	/dɪˈsɜːnz/	verb	To perceive, detect, recognize, or distinguish something, especially when it is subtle, unclear, or difficult to notice.	perceive, detect, recognize, distinguish, make out, identify	I could barely discern a figure in the fog. / She quickly discerned a pattern in the data. / It's difficult to discern what he really wants from that answer.	KEEP as the productive verb entry. Core patterns: discern something; discern A from B; discern that...; discern a pattern/difference/meaning. Do not merge merely because WID454 also contains discern-related material. Master-wide principle approved by Jin: discern and discerning should both exist as distinct, depth-rich learning entries. 'Discerning' should focus on good judgment/taste and the ability to notice subtle quality differences, while 'discern' focuses on the act of perceiving/distinguishing.		
49	487	514	come to terms with	/kʌm tə tɜːmz wɪð/	idiom	To gradually accept and learn to deal with a difficult, painful, or unwanted reality.	accept, reconcile oneself to, make peace with, adjust to	It took him years to come to terms with the loss. / I'm still coming to terms with the fact that the project is over.	Current card confuses this with 'come to terms' = reach an agreement. Keep the 'accept a difficult reality' idiom as headword. Separately, 'come to terms (with someone)' can mean reach agreement, but the constructions differ.		
49	488	515	coerce / coerced	/kəʊˈɜːst/	verb	To force or pressure someone into doing something by threats, intimidation, manipulation, or other strong pressure.	force, compel, pressure, intimidate	They coerced him into signing the document. / She said the confession had been coerced.	Normalize to coerce / coerced. Core pattern: coerce someone into doing something. Stronger than persuade or pressure; often implies lack of genuine consent.		
49	489	516	obsequious	/əbˈsiːkwɪəs/	adjective	Excessively eager to obey, flatter, or please someone more powerful, usually in a way that seems servile or insincere.	servile, fawning, sycophantic, ingratiating, deferential (too weak in some contexts)	The waiter was almost comically obsequious toward the celebrity. / His obsequious praise made everyone uncomfortable.	Build contrast cluster with servile, sycophantic, fawning: obsequious emphasizes excessive submissive attentiveness toward a superior.		
49	490	517	servile	/ˈsɜːvaɪl/	adjective	Excessively submissive or willing to obey and serve others, especially in a degrading or self-abasing way.	submissive, obsequious, slavish, fawning	He adopted a servile tone whenever the director entered the room. / The novel rejects blind, servile obedience.	Not a synonym of itself. Compared with obsequious, servile emphasizes low/subordinate submissiveness; compared with sycophantic, it need not imply strategic flattery for gain.		
50	491	518	sycophantic	/ˌsɪkəˈfæntɪk/	adjective	Excessively flattering or praising someone powerful in order to gain advantage or favor.	fawning, obsequious, ingratiating, toadying, flattering	The interview was so sycophantic that no difficult questions were asked. / He surrounded himself with sycophantic advisers.	Emphasizes self-interested flattery toward power. Related noun: sycophant. Contrast servile = submissive; fawning = excessive display of admiration/affection.		
50	492	519	fawn over/on someone	/ˈfɔːnɪŋ/	verb phrase	To give someone excessive, often insincere attention, praise, or affection in order to please or impress them.	flatter, gush over, ingratiate oneself with, suck up to (informal)	Everyone was fawning over the famous actor. / He fawned on the boss all evening.	Normalize from adjective 'fawning' to productive verb pattern. Fawning can be adjective: fawning praise. Contrast sycophantic (motive of gaining favor) and obsequious (submissive manner).		
50	493	520	reap	/riːp/	verb	Literally, to cut and gather a crop; figuratively, to receive a result, reward, or consequence produced by earlier actions.	harvest, gather; gain, receive, derive	Farmers reap the wheat in late summer. / Years of training finally allowed her to reap the benefits. / He reaped the consequences of his own decisions.	Common figurative collocations: reap the benefits/rewards/profits; reap what you sow. Not all outcomes are positive.		
50	494	521	champion	/ˈʧæmpɪən/	noun; verb	noun: a winner in a competition, or a strong advocate/supporter of a cause. verb: to actively support, defend, or promote a person, idea, or cause.	winner, titleholder; advocate, defender, supporter; promote, support, defend	She became world champion at 19. / He has long been a champion of free speech. / The organization champions accessible design.	Major missing verb/advocate sense. 'Champion of X' and 'champion a cause/idea' are highly productive.		
50	495	522	flawed	/flɔːd/	adjective	Containing a fault, weakness, defect, or error; imperfect in a way that may undermine quality or validity.	defective, imperfect, faulty, unsound, problematic	The study was based on flawed assumptions. / He's a flawed but sympathetic character.	Common with argument, logic, methodology, design, character. 'Faulty' often suits mechanisms/reasoning; 'flawed' can describe people/characters without meaning worthless.		
50	496	523	dogmatic	/dɒɡˈmætɪk/	adjective	Expressing beliefs or principles as unquestionably true and refusing to seriously consider alternatives or evidence.	doctrinaire, rigid, inflexible, uncompromising	He became dogmatic about his preferred teaching method. / The book is opinionated without being dogmatic.	Usually negative. Related dogma/dogmatism. Stronger than merely confident or principled.		
50	497	524	supposition	/ˌsʌpəˈzɪʃən/	noun	A belief or idea accepted or considered as possibly true without firm proof; an assumption used as a basis for reasoning.	assumption, hypothesis, conjecture, presumption	The argument rests on the supposition that demand will remain stable. / That's only supposition; we don't know what happened.	Formal. Compare conjecture: a conjecture is often a tentative conclusion/speculation formed from incomplete evidence; supposition emphasizes something supposed/assumed.		
50	498	525	conjecture	/kənˈdʒɛktʃər/	noun; verb	noun: an opinion or conclusion formed from incomplete evidence. verb: to form or express such a tentative conclusion.	speculation, guess, hypothesis, supposition; speculate, surmise	At this point, the cause is pure conjecture. / Historians have conjectured that the building was used for ceremonies.	Often formal/academic. In mathematics, a conjecture is a proposition believed true but not yet proved. Distinguish from hypothesis depending field.		
50	499	526	pertinent	/ˈpɜːtɪnənt/	adjective	Directly relevant or applicable to the matter being considered.	relevant, applicable, material, to the point	Please include only information pertinent to the investigation. / She asked several pertinent questions.	Common: pertinent to. Slightly formal. Cross-reference germane WID527; pertinent often means directly useful/relevant to the immediate matter.		
50	500	527	germane	/dʒɜːˈmeɪn/	adjective	Closely and appropriately relevant to the subject being discussed or considered.	relevant, pertinent, applicable, material	That question isn't germane to the issue we're discussing. / The judge ruled that the evidence was germane to the case.	Formal; normally germane to. Very close to pertinent, but often emphasizes relevance to the subject/topic. Keep both as a confusable pair rather than merge automatically.		
51	501	528	test	/tɛst/	noun; verb	noun: an examination, trial, procedure, or challenge used to measure knowledge, ability, quality, condition, or truth. verb: to examine, measure, try, or challenge.	exam, assessment, trial, check; examine, evaluate, try	I have a math test tomorrow. / The lab tested the water for contaminants. / The crisis will test their leadership.	Current card is too narrow. Add medical/lab/product/figurative senses and verb. Common patterns: test for, test whether, put to the test.		
51	502	529	chasm	/ˈkæzəm/	noun	A deep fissure or gorge; figuratively, a very large difference or division between people, groups, views, or conditions.	gorge, abyss, ravine; gulf, divide, gap	A narrow bridge crossed the chasm. / A political chasm has opened between the two groups.	Figurative 'chasm between A and B' is common and high-value. Stronger/more dramatic than gap.		
51	503	530	albeit	/ɔːlˈbeɪɪt/	conjunction	Although; even though. Used to introduce a concessive phrase, adjective, adverb, or subordinate idea, often in formal writing.	although, though, even though	The room was small, albeit comfortable. / He agreed, albeit reluctantly.	Fix example: 'Albeit challenging, the project...' is possible but less learner-friendly. Albeit often introduces a reduced phrase rather than a full finite clause: albeit slowly/expensive/with difficulty.		
51	504	531	form factor	/fɔːm ˈfæktər/	noun	The physical size, shape, layout, and configuration of a device or component, especially as constrained by a design standard.	physical format, size and shape, hardware format	The smaller form factor makes the laptop easier to carry. / ATX and Mini-ITX are different motherboard form factors.	Technical term. Not just generic 'shape': often refers to standardized dimensions/layout or overall physical configuration.		
51	505	532	pejorative	/pɪˈdʒɒrətɪv/	adjective; noun	adjective: expressing contempt, disapproval, or a negative judgment. noun: a pejorative word or expression.	derogatory, disparaging, insulting, demeaning	The term can be pejorative in that context. / He rejected the label as a pejorative.	Current definition says 'a word' while POS is adjective. Teach both adjective and noun. Contrast pejorative with slur: not every pejorative is a slur.		
51	506	533	brazen hussy	/ˈbreɪzən ˈhʌsi/	noun phrase (dated/insulting)	A dated, insulting expression for a woman regarded as shamelessly bold, sexually improper, or socially brazen.	shameless woman, impudent woman (descriptive paraphrases)	In the old comedy, the scandalized aunt calls her a 'brazen hussy.'	KEEP as a historical/literary insult, not a neutral description. 'Hussy' is dated and derogatory; using it seriously today can sound sexist or theatrical. Useful mainly for recognizing older dialogue.		
51	507	534	brazen	/ˈbreɪzən/	adjective	Bold and shameless, especially in openly doing something wrong, offensive, or audacious without embarrassment.	shameless, audacious, blatant, impudent	It was a brazen attempt to cheat the system. / The thieves carried out the robbery in broad daylight with brazen confidence.	Common: brazen lie/attack/theft/attempt. 'Bold' alone can be positive; brazen is usually disapproving.		
51	508	535	rapport	/ræˈpɔː/	noun	A comfortable, trusting, mutually understanding relationship that makes communication easy.	connection, mutual understanding, affinity, good relationship	She quickly established rapport with the students. / The interviewer built a good rapport with him.	Usually uncountable: build/establish/develop rapport with someone; have a good rapport. Pronunciation is important: final t is silent.		
51	509	536	take someone/something for ...	/teɪk fɔː/	verb phrase	To believe or assume that someone or something is a particular person, thing, or type, sometimes mistakenly; also in 'take A for B' = mistake A for B.	regard as, assume to be, mistake for	I took him for a doctor because of the white coat. / Don't take my silence for agreement. / What do you take me for?	Current example 'I take you for someone who...' is possible but not the most central pattern. Teach take A for B, take something for granted separately, and rhetorical 'What do you take me for?'		
51	510	537	appropriate	/əˈprəʊprɪət/	adjective; verb	adjective: suitable or proper for a particular situation. verb (formal): to take something for one's own use, often without permission; or to allocate money/resources for a purpose.	suitable, fitting, proper; seize, commandeer; allocate	Wear something appropriate for the interview. / The regime appropriated private property. / Congress appropriated funds for disaster relief.	Major polysemy. Verb pronunciation/stress may differ in careful speech. Distinguish appropriate (suitable) from inappropriate and from cultural appropriation/appropriate funds.		
52	511	539	tactile	/ˈtæktaɪl/	adjective	Relating to touch or the sense of touch; perceptible or designed to be experienced through physical contact.	touch-related, haptic, tangible (sometimes), textured	The controls provide tactile feedback when pressed. / I like the tactile feel of printed books. / The exhibit is highly tactile and encourages visitors to touch it.	'Touchable/perceivable' are weak synonyms. Common: tactile sensation/feedback/experience/surface. Haptic is especially technical.		
52	512	540	proponent	/prəˈpəʊnənt/	noun	A person who publicly supports, advocates, or argues in favor of an idea, policy, theory, or course of action.	advocate, supporter, backer, champion	She's a leading proponent of nuclear energy. / Proponents argue that the reform will lower costs.	Common: proponent of. Contrast opponent. Often used in balanced debate/reporting.		
52	513	541	fenestrated	/fɛnɪˈstreɪtɪd/	adjective (technical)	Provided with openings, holes, windows, or perforations; used in architecture, anatomy, medicine, and technical design.	perforated, windowed, apertured	The façade is heavily fenestrated. / The surgeon used a fenestrated drape with an opening over the operative site.	Technical/formal. Related fenestration. In medicine/anatomy, a fenestration is an opening; 'fenestrated' does not necessarily mean literal glass windows.		
52	514	542	oscillate / oscillating	/ɒsɪˈleɪtɪŋ/	verb; adjective	To move repeatedly back and forth around a position; figuratively, to alternate between states, opinions, or values. Oscillating describes such repeated movement.	swing, vibrate, fluctuate, alternate, waver	The fan oscillates from side to side. / His mood oscillated between optimism and despair. / We bought an oscillating fan.	Normalize to verb family. Distinguish oscillate (regular/repeated alternation) from fluctuate (irregular changes) and vacillate (indecision).		
52	515	543	requisition	/ˌrɛkwɪˈzɪʃən/	noun; verb	noun: an official request or demand for supplies, equipment, services, or action. verb: to formally request, obtain, or take something for official use.	request, order, demand; commandeer (some verb contexts)	Submit a purchase requisition before ordering the equipment. / The army requisitioned vehicles for emergency use.	Formal/administrative. A requisition is not merely any request. Verb can imply official authority, especially requisition property/supplies.		
52	516	544	haphazardly	/ˌhæpˈhæzədli/	adverb	In a way that lacks order, planning, method, or care; randomly or irregularly.	randomly, chaotically, carelessly, unsystematically	Boxes were piled haphazardly in the hallway. / The policy had been implemented haphazardly.	Related adjective haphazard. 'Randomly' can be neutral/statistical; haphazardly usually suggests poor organization.		
52	517	545	erratic	/ɪˈrætɪk/	adjective	Unpredictable, irregular, or inconsistent in movement, behavior, performance, or pattern.	unpredictable, irregular, inconsistent, unstable	The car was moving erratically. / His attendance became increasingly erratic. / The stock has shown erratic price movements.	Useful across behavior/motion/performance/data. Does not necessarily mean mentally unstable.		
52	518	546	fixate / be fixated on	/fɪkˈseɪtɪd/	verb; verb phrase	To focus one's attention intensely or excessively on something; be fixated on = be unable or unwilling to stop focusing on it.	obsess over, dwell on, become preoccupied with	Don't fixate on one small mistake. / He became fixated on proving that he was right.	Normalize grammar: current 'fixated' definition says 'to become'. Common construction be/get/become fixated on. Often negative, but intensity varies.		
52	519	547	discernment	/dɪsˈɜːnmənt/	noun	The ability to judge, perceive, or distinguish things clearly and intelligently, especially subtle differences or what is good/appropriate.	judgment, insight, discrimination, perceptiveness	Choosing reliable sources requires discernment. / She showed excellent discernment in hiring the new manager.	Related discern/discerning. Often implies good judgment, not merely perception. 'Discrimination' is a technical synonym only in the sense of distinguishing, and can mislead learners.		
52	520	548	elusive	/ɪˈluːsɪv/	adjective	Difficult to find, catch, achieve, remember, define, or understand.	hard to find, evasive, unattainable, hard to pin down	The suspect remained elusive for months. / A simple solution has proved elusive. / The exact meaning of the phrase is elusive.	Broad productive pattern: elusive goal/answer/quality/suspect. 'Evasive' usually describes deliberate avoidance, so not a general synonym.		
53	521	549	inscrutable	/ɪnˈskruːtəbl/	adjective	Very difficult or impossible to understand, interpret, or read, especially a person's expression, motives, or a complex mystery.	mysterious, unfathomable, impenetrable, enigmatic	She gave me an inscrutable look. / His motives remained inscrutable.	Formal/literary. Stronger than merely unclear. Avoid stereotyped uses about ethnic groups/cultures.		
53	522	550	inglorious	/ɪnˈɡlɔːrɪəs/	adjective	Shameful, dishonorable, or lacking glory, distinction, or success.	shameful, dishonorable, humiliating, undistinguished	The campaign came to an inglorious end. / After an inglorious defeat, the team returned home quietly.	Common phrase: inglorious end/defeat. Can mean merely uncelebrated/undistinguished as well as disgraceful.		
53	523	551	devious	/ˈdiːvɪəs/	adjective	Using indirect, secretive, or dishonest methods to achieve a goal; also following a winding or indirect route (less common literal sense).	underhanded, scheming, crafty, dishonest; circuitous	They used a devious scheme to hide the payments. / The path took a devious route through the hills.	Usually negative for people/plans. 'Cunning' can be admiring or neutral; devious strongly suggests dishonesty or indirectness.		
53	524	552	dichotomy	/daɪˈkɒtəmi/	noun	A division or contrast between two categories or alternatives, especially when they are treated as sharply opposed.	division, contrast, binary opposition	The debate creates a false dichotomy between security and privacy. / The book explores the dichotomy between public image and private life.	High-value collocation: false dichotomy. A dichotomy need not mean the two things truly exhaust all possibilities; speakers often criticize an imposed binary.		
53	525	553	incompatible	/ɪnˈkəmˈpætɪbəl/	adjective	Unable to coexist, be combined, or work together successfully; mutually inconsistent or unsuitable.	inconsistent, conflicting, irreconcilable, mismatched	The software is incompatible with older devices. / Their goals turned out to be incompatible. / The two medications may be incompatible.	Remove self-synonym. Common: incompatible with. Covers technology, goals, beliefs, people, substances.		
53	526	554	wrought	/rɔːt/	past participle / adjective	Past participle of work in certain formal/literary expressions, meaning made, created, shaped, or brought about; also used in fixed compounds such as wrought iron.	caused, produced, created, fashioned	The destruction wrought by the storm was enormous. / Years of neglect had wrought lasting damage. / The gate is made of wrought iron.	Teach as irregular/historical form mainly surviving in formal 'damage/change wrought by X' and compounds. Not the ordinary past tense of work today (worked).		
