53	528	556	abysmal	/əˈbɪzməl/	adjective	Extremely bad, poor, or inadequate.	terrible, dreadful, atrocious, woeful	The service was abysmal. / The team had an abysmal record last season.	Often about quality/performance/conditions, not 'severe' generally. Stronger than poor; informal-to-neutral evaluative register.		
53	529	557	chafe	/ʧeɪf/	verb	To irritate or make skin sore through rubbing; to rub against something; figuratively, to feel irritated or resentful under a restriction or situation.	rub, irritate, abrade; resent, bridle at	The backpack straps chafed my shoulders. / He chafed under the new restrictions.	Important figurative pattern: chafe at/under restrictions, rules, authority. Related chafing.		
53	530	558	inexhaustible	/ɪnɪɡˈzɔːstəbl/	adjective	So abundant or enduring that it seems impossible to use up, exhaust, or deplete.	limitless, boundless, unfailing, seemingly endless	She has an inexhaustible supply of energy. / His curiosity seems inexhaustible.	Can be literal or figurative. Avoid asserting that a real-world resource is literally inexhaustible when 'renewable' or 'vast' is more accurate.		
54	531	559	joie de vivre	/ʒwɑː viːvrə/	noun phrase (French borrowing)	A lively, exuberant enjoyment of life; a cheerful delight in living.	zest for life, exuberance, love of life	Her curiosity and joie de vivre were infectious. / Even in his eighties, he retained a remarkable joie de vivre.	Correct form is joie de vivre, not 'joie vivre'. French borrowing used in English; pronunciation varies by speaker.		
54	532	560	gaiety	/ˈɡeɪəti/	noun	Cheerful, lively enjoyment or festivity; brightness or merriment, often with a somewhat literary or old-fashioned tone.	merriment, cheerfulness, festivity, high spirits	Music and laughter added to the gaiety of the evening. / The room was decorated with festive gaiety.	Somewhat literary/old-fashioned in everyday AmE. Historical 'gay' senses are separate; do not assume modern sexual-orientation meaning from gaiety.		
54	533	561	buoyant	/ˈbɔɪənt/	adjective	Able to float or rise in a fluid; cheerful and optimistic; or strong/rising in economic or market contexts.	floating; cheerful, upbeat, optimistic; robust	The material is naturally buoyant. / She remained buoyant despite the setback. / Consumer demand has stayed buoyant.	Three useful senses: physical buoyancy, mood/personality, economics/markets. Related buoyancy.		
