54	536	564	infrangible	/ɪnˈfrændʒəbl/	adjective (rare/formal)	Impossible or very difficult to break; inviolable or not to be violated.	unbreakable, inviolable, indestructible	The author describes the bond as infrangible. / They treated the principle as an infrangible rule.	Rare word. Current example is possible but stiff. More useful for recognition than active everyday use. Distinguish intangible and infringe/infringement.		
54	537	566	progeny	/ˈprəʊdʒəni/	noun (usually plural/collective)	A person's, animal's, or plant's offspring or descendants; figuratively, things produced by something.	offspring, descendants, issue (formal), descendants	The stallion's progeny won several races. / Many of her artistic progeny adopted the same style.	Formal. Often collective and can take singular or plural agreement depending style/context. Not limited to royal families.		
54	538	569	revoke	/rɪˈvəʊk/	verb	To officially cancel, withdraw, or make no longer valid a right, license, permission, law, offer, or decision.	cancel, withdraw, rescind, annul	His driver's license was revoked. / The company revoked her access privileges.	Usually formal/official. Common objects: license, permission, visa, access, offer. Distinguish rescind (contracts/decisions) and repeal (laws).		
54	539	570	gap year	/ɡæp jɪə/	noun	A period, often about a year, taken between stages of education or before work to travel, work, volunteer, or pursue other experiences.	year out (BrE), break from study	She took a gap year before university and worked in Japan. / He's considering a gap year after graduation.	Not necessarily exactly one calendar year. 'Sabbatical' is not a close synonym; sabbatical usually refers to an established leave from a job/academic post.		
