55	541	572	discourse	/ˈdɪskɔːs/	noun	Connected spoken or written communication; discussion or debate about a subject; in linguistics/social theory, language and communication considered in a broader context.	discussion, debate, communication, conversation; language use	Public discourse around the issue has changed. / The course examines political discourse and media language.	Broader and more formal than conversation. Can mean extended connected language in linguistics, or a body/mode of discussion in academic fields.		
55	542	573	discourse marker	/ˈdɪskɔːs ˈmɑːkər/	noun phrase	A word or phrase that helps organize, connect, manage, or signal relationships in spoken or written discourse, often without contributing much propositional content.	connector, pragmatic marker, linking expression (overlapping terms)	Well, I think we should leave. / However, the results were different. / You know can function as a discourse marker in conversation.	Examples include well, so, anyway, you know, I mean, however, therefore, depending framework. 'Connector' is only partial synonym; discourse markers also manage interaction, stance, topic shifts.		
55	543	574	get itchy feet / have itchy feet	/ɡɛt ˈɪtʃi fiːt/	idiom	To become restless and strongly want to travel, move somewhere new, or change one's situation.	feel restless, get wanderlust, want a change of scene	After two years in the same city, I started getting itchy feet. / She always gets itchy feet after a few months at home.	Especially common BrE but widely understood. Usually about travel/moving, sometimes broader restlessness. Do not confuse with literal itchy feet.		
55	544	575	thirst for adventure	/θɜːst fɔːr ædˈvɛntʃər/	noun phrase	A strong desire for exciting, unfamiliar, or challenging experiences.	sense of adventure, wanderlust, craving for adventure	His thirst for adventure took him across South America. / I still have a thirst for adventure, but I'm more careful now.	Productive metaphor: thirst for knowledge/power/revenge/adventure. Useful to teach 'thirst for X' rather than only this fixed combination.		
55	545	576	bull's-eye / bullseye	/bʊlz aɪ/	noun	The center of a target; figuratively, an exact or highly successful hit, answer, or assessment.	center of the target, dead center; exact hit	Her arrow landed in the bull's-eye. / Your diagnosis was right on the bullseye.	Correct spelling from 'bulls eye'. Both bull's-eye and bullseye occur; style varies. Common: hit the bull's-eye; right on the bullseye.		
55	546	577	get a lemon / buy a lemon	/ɡɛt ðə ˈlɛmən/	idiom	To end up with a defective or unsatisfactory product, especially a car; a lemon is something that proves faulty or disappointing.	get a dud, buy a defective product, end up with a dud	The used car looked great, but I realized I'd bought a lemon. / If the laptop keeps failing, you may have gotten a lemon.	Current 'get the lemon = fail' is inaccurate. In AmE, a lemon is especially a defective vehicle/product. Also lemon law. 'When life gives you lemons...' is a separate expression.		
55	547	578	negligible	/ˈnɛɡlɪdʒəbl/	adjective	So small, slight, or unimportant that it can reasonably be ignored for a particular purpose.	insignificant, minimal, trivial, inconsequential	The difference in cost is negligible. / The risk is small enough to be negligible.	Often technical/analytical: negligible effect/difference/risk/amount. Means negligible relative to context, not literally zero.		
