57	561	593	treacherous	/ˈtrɛtʃərəs/	adjective	Dangerously unstable, hazardous, or difficult; or likely to betray trust and act disloyally.	dangerous, hazardous, perilous; traitorous, disloyal	The icy road was treacherous. / They crossed treacherous mountain terrain. / He discovered that his supposed ally was treacherous.	Two major senses: hazardous conditions/terrain and betrayal/disloyalty. 'Harmful' is too broad.		
57	562	594	bristle / bristling	/ˈbrɪslɪŋ/	verb; adjective	bristle: for hair/fur to stand stiffly upright; to react with anger or defensiveness; to be densely filled with projecting things. bristling: full of such projections, tension, anger, or abundance.	stand on end; stiffen, take offense; teem with, be full of	The dog bristled when the stranger approached. / She bristled at the suggestion that she had cheated. / The fortress was bristling with weapons.	Current 'bristling attitude' is unnatural. High-value patterns: bristle at a remark/accusation; bristle with weapons/energy.		
