57	567	599	plight	/plaɪt/	noun	A difficult, distressing, or dangerous situation, especially one that arouses concern or sympathy.	predicament, hardship, difficult situation, distress	The documentary drew attention to the plight of migrant workers. / Their plight worsened as winter approached.	Common: the plight of X; draw attention to/highlight someone's plight. Usually serious and sympathy-evoking, unlike neutral predicament.		
57	568	600	predicament	/prɪˈdɪkəmənt/	noun	A difficult, awkward, or unpleasant situation from which it is hard to find a good solution or escape.	dilemma, difficult situation, bind, quandary	I found myself in a predicament when both meetings were scheduled for the same time. / How did you get into this predicament?	Often less severe than plight and more focused on being stuck in a difficult problem. Common: be/find oneself in a predicament.		
57	569	601	quandary	/ˈkwɑndɚi/	noun	A difficult state of uncertainty about what to do, especially when the available choices are problematic or competing.	dilemma, predicament, bind, uncertainty	I'm in a quandary about whether to accept the offer or stay where I am. / The discovery left researchers in a quandary.	Common: be in a quandary; a quandary over/about whether... Compared with dilemma, quandary emphasizes uncertainty/perplexity; predicament emphasizes being stuck in a difficult situation.		
57	570	602	crunch time	/krʌntʃ taɪm/	noun phrase	A critical period when a deadline or decisive moment is close and intense effort or pressure is required.	critical period, deadline pressure, make-or-break period	It's crunch time—we have three days to finish the project. / The team usually performs well when crunch time arrives.	KEEP despite earlier WID318 'crunch time' because current workflow does not mechanically delete repeated forms if a later global reconciliation may choose the stronger card. Core: at crunch time / when it comes to crunch time.		
58	571	603	bite the bullet	/baɪt ðə ˈbʊlɪt/	idiom	To force yourself to face or do something difficult, unpleasant, or painful that can no longer reasonably be avoided.	face it, tough it out, endure, take the plunge (some contexts)	I hate going to the dentist, but I'll have to bite the bullet and make an appointment. / We finally bit the bullet and replaced the old furnace.	Often means accepting an unavoidable unpleasant action/cost, not simply showing courage. Historical origin claims are disputed; avoid relying on folk etymology.		
58	572	604	respondent	/rɪˈspɑndənt/	noun	A person who responds to a survey, questionnaire, research study, petition, appeal, or other formal inquiry; in law, the party responding to a proceeding or appeal.	survey participant, interviewee (some studies), answering party	Most respondents said they preferred the new design. / The respondent filed a brief opposing the appeal.	Do not equate generally with defendant: legal meaning depends on proceeding. Survey respondent is especially common. Contrast participant: not every participant is necessarily a respondent.		
58	573	605	luminary / luminaries	/ˈluːməˌnɛriz/	noun	A prominent, influential, or highly respected person in a particular field.	leading figure, notable, star, eminent person	The conference attracted luminaries from science and technology. / Several literary luminaries attended the ceremony.	Normalize singular + plural. Usually complimentary and somewhat elevated. Original literal sense relates to a source of light; modern common sense is figurative.		
58	574	606	expat / expatriate	/ˈɛkˌspæt/	noun; adjective	A person living outside their country of origin, often for work, lifestyle, or long-term residence; expatriate can also be an adjective.	expatriate, person living abroad, overseas resident	She's a British expat living in Tokyo. / The company provides housing for expatriate employees.	Expat is informal shortening of expatriate. Not a precise legal immigration category. It overlaps with immigrant/emigrant/migrant, but social usage differs and can carry class/cultural connotations; choose terms according to context rather than treating them as exact synonyms.		
58	575	607	hearty	/ˈhɑrti/	adjective	Warm and enthusiastic; substantial and satisfying, especially food; strong, vigorous, or robust.	warm, enthusiastic; substantial, filling; robust, vigorous	They gave us a hearty welcome. / We ate a hearty breakfast before the hike. / He let out a hearty laugh.	Productive collocations: hearty welcome/laugh/congratulations; hearty meal/breakfast; hale and hearty. Do not reduce to 'warm' only.		
58	576	608	cut corners	/kʌt ˈkɔrnɚz/	idiom	To save time, money, or effort by omitting steps or doing something less thoroughly, often at the cost of quality, safety, or standards.	take shortcuts, skimp, economize improperly	They cut corners on construction, and the defects appeared within a year. / I don't mind saving money, but I won't cut corners on safety.	Usually disapproving when quality/standards suffer. A shortcut is not necessarily bad; cutting corners often implies improper compromise.		
58	577	609	expend	/ɪkˈspɛnd/	verb	To use, spend, or consume a finite resource such as energy, effort, time, money, or ammunition.	use, spend, consume, deploy	She expended a lot of energy worrying about something she couldn't control. / Considerable effort was expended on the redesign.	Formal. Common: expend energy/effort/resources; expend something on/in doing something. Related expenditure, expendable.		
58	578	610	skimp / skimp on	/skɪmp/	verb	To spend, provide, or use less than is needed or advisable, especially to save money, time, or effort.	scrimp, economize, cut back, stint	Don't skimp on sleep before the exam. / They skimped on materials and the product felt cheap.	Very common pattern skimp on something. Scrimp often appears in scrimp and save and emphasizes extreme economy; skimp emphasizes insufficient quantity/quality.		
58	579	611	skimpy	/ˈskɪmpi/	adjective	Too small, meager, or insufficient in amount; of clothing, very small or revealing.	meager, scant, inadequate; revealing, scanty	The report gave only a skimpy explanation. / We were served a pretty skimpy portion. / The costume was deliberately skimpy.	Two clusters: insufficient amount/details/portion and revealing clothing. Register informal. Do not make clothing the only salient sense.		
58	580	612	on the spur of the moment	/spɝ ðə ˈmoʊmənt/	idiom	Suddenly and without advance planning; as an impulsive decision.	spontaneously, impulsively, on impulse	We booked the trip on the spur of the moment. / On the spur of the moment, I decided to call her.	Correct malformed headword 'spur the moment'. Fixed expression is on the spur of the moment. Related: spur-of-the-moment decision/purchase, with hyphens before a noun.		
59	581	613	spur	/spɝ/	noun; verb	noun: something that encourages or prompts action; also a pointed riding device and a projecting branch/route. verb: to stimulate, encourage, or prompt action or development.	stimulus, incentive, impetus; encourage, prompt, drive	The criticism gave her an extra spur to improve. / The announcement spurred investors to act. / Rising prices spurred demand for cheaper alternatives.	High-value pattern: spur someone to do something; spur growth/action/change; spur someone on. Distinguish spur = stimulus from spurn = reject.		
59	582	614	premeditation	/ˌpriːˌmɛdəˈteɪʃən/	noun	Advance planning or deliberate thought before an action, especially before a crime; in criminal law, a concept whose exact requirements depend on jurisdiction.	forethought, advance planning, deliberation	Prosecutors argued that the messages showed premeditation. / The attack appeared to have been carried out with premeditation.	Especially legal. Avoid oversimplifying murder categories across jurisdictions. Related premeditate/premeditated; common phrase with premeditation.		
59	583	615	extemporize / extemporized	/ɪkˈstɛmpəˌraɪzd/	verb	To speak, perform, compose, or devise something without advance preparation, using what is available at the moment.	improvise, ad-lib, speak off the cuff	When the slides failed, she extemporized for ten minutes. / The musicians extemporized a short ending.	Normalize lemma. More formal than improvise. Can mean improvise a speech/performance or devise a temporary solution. Related extemporaneous.		
59	584	616	hush-hush	/ˈhʌʃ ˌhʌʃ/	adjective (informal)	Secret, confidential, or deliberately kept from public knowledge.	secret, confidential, under wraps, clandestine	The negotiations were very hush-hush. / They're working on a hush-hush project.	Hyphenated. Informal and often mildly playful/suspicious. Usually predicative or attributive: keep it hush-hush; a hush-hush meeting.		
59	585	617	raise the bar	/ˈhaɪɚ ðə bɑr/	idiom	To increase the standard, level of performance, or expectations that others are measured against.	set a higher standard, increase expectations, set a new benchmark	Her performance raised the bar for everyone else. / The new safety rules raise the bar for the entire industry.	Correct malformed headword 'higher the bar'. Opposite: lower the bar. 'Set the bar high/low' is related but structurally different.		
59	586	618	vicissitude / vicissitudes	/vɪˈsɪsɪˌtudz/	noun (usually plural)	An unwelcome, unpredictable, or difficult change in circumstances; vicissitudes = the changing fortunes and ups and downs of life or a situation.	ups and downs, changes of fortune, fluctuations, reversals	They survived the vicissitudes of war and economic crisis. / The business has weathered many vicissitudes over the decades.	Formal/literary and most common in plural. Often: the vicissitudes of life/history/fortune. Not merely any neutral variation.		
59	587	619	lighthearted / light-hearted	/ˈlaɪtˌhɑrtɪd/	adjective	Cheerful, playful, and not serious or burdensome in tone.	cheerful, playful, carefree, breezy	The movie is a lighthearted comedy. / I was only making a lighthearted comment.	Usually positive; can contrast with serious/heavy. Spelling lighthearted is common AmE; light-hearted also occurs.		
59	588	620	divest / divest oneself of	/daɪˈvɛstɪŋ/	verb	To sell or dispose of assets or investments; to remove or rid oneself/someone of something; in finance, to divest from a company or sector.	sell off, dispose of, shed, relinquish	The company plans to divest several non-core businesses. / The fund divested from fossil-fuel companies. / He divested himself of most of his possessions.	Normalize from divesting. Finance/business use is common. Patterns: divest assets; divest a business of X; divest oneself of X; divest from X.		
59	589	621	depersonalization	/diˌpɝsənələˈzeɪʃən/	noun	A state or experience of feeling detached from oneself, one's thoughts, emotions, body, or actions, sometimes as though observing oneself from outside.	self-detachment, sense of unreality (partial paraphrases)	The article describes depersonalization as a feeling of being detached from oneself. / The character says the experience made everything about himself feel strangely unreal.	Keep definition descriptive rather than diagnosing the learner. Distinguish depersonalization (detachment from self) from derealization (surroundings feel unreal). Clinical interpretation belongs to qualified medical contexts.		
59	590	622	upkeep	/ˈʌpˌkiːp/	noun	The work, maintenance, or expense required to keep a building, vehicle, property, system, or standard in good condition.	maintenance, care, maintenance costs	The house is beautiful, but the upkeep is expensive. / Regular upkeep prevents small problems from becoming major repairs.	Usually uncountable. Common: cost of upkeep; maintenance and upkeep. Can occasionally refer to maintaining a standard/appearance.		
60	591	623	caregiver vs. caretaker	/ˈkɛrˌɡɪvɚ/ vs /ˈkeɚˌteɪkɚ/	noun comparison	Caregiver usually means a person who provides ongoing personal care or assistance to someone who needs it. Caretaker can mean a person responsible for a property/place and, in some varieties and contexts, can also mean caregiver.	caregiver: carer, care provider; caretaker: custodian, property keeper; caregiver (some contexts)	Her father became her primary caregiver after the surgery. / The caretaker lives on the grounds and maintains the property.	Preserve regional nuance. In AmE, caregiver strongly suggests personal care; caretaker often property/temporary responsibility, but caretaker can also mean a person caring for another person. BrE often uses carer. Avoid presenting the distinction as absolute.		
60	592	624	discreet	/dɪˈskriːt/	adjective	Careful, tactful, and unobtrusive, especially in order to avoid embarrassment, attention, or disclosure of private information.	tactful, circumspect, unobtrusive, diplomatic	She made a discreet inquiry about the problem. / The hotel has a discreet side entrance.	Key confusable: discreet = tactful/private; discrete = separate/distinct. Common: discreet inquiry, discreet service, be discreet about something.		
60	593	625	protégé / protege	/ˈproʊtəˌʒeɪ/	noun	A less experienced person whose career, skills, or development are guided and supported by an influential mentor.	mentee, pupil, apprentice (some contexts)	She was once the protégé of a famous director. / He helped his young protégé build a research career.	Standard spelling protégé preserves accents, though protege is common in plain English text. A protégé is not necessarily an apprentice/student in a formal program; emphasis is sponsorship/mentorship.		
60	594	626	entrenched	/ɪnˈtrɛntʃt/	adjective	So firmly established in a position, habit, system, belief, or power structure that change is difficult.	deep-rooted, ingrained, embedded, firmly established	Long working hours are deeply entrenched in the company culture. / Both sides became more entrenched in their positions.	From military trench imagery. Common: deeply entrenched; entrenched interests/attitudes/inequality/position. Can describe people becoming less willing to compromise.		
60	595	627	entrenchment	/ɪnˈtrɛntʃmənt/	noun	The process or state of becoming firmly established and resistant to change; also, literally, defensive fortification by trenches.	institutionalization, embedding, consolidation; fortification	The policy contributed to the entrenchment of existing inequalities. / Political polarization can lead to further entrenchment on both sides.	Related entrench/entrenched. Abstract use often concerns power, inequality, attitudes, positions. Do not use generic 'establishment' as if fully synonymous.		
60	596	629	indiscipline	/ɪnˈdɪsəplən/	noun	Lack of discipline, self-control, or adherence to rules, especially in a group, institution, or organization.	lack of discipline, disorder, unruliness, misconduct	The coach blamed the defeat partly on tactical indiscipline. / Repeated indiscipline disrupted the classroom.	More common in BrE/institutional contexts than everyday AmE, where 'lack of discipline' is often more natural. Can describe behavior, finances, tactics, organizations.		
60	597	630	disciplined	/ˈdɪsəplɪnd/	adjective	Showing strong self-control, orderly habits, or adherence to a method, training, or rules.	self-controlled, methodical, orderly, rigorous	She's very disciplined about studying every day. / The team played a disciplined defensive game.	Not merely 'well-behaved'. Can describe a disciplined approach, investor, athlete, team. Related self-disciplined.		
60	598	631	hit the bull's-eye / hit the bullseye	/hɪt ðə ˈbʊlz ˌaɪ/	idiom; literal phrase	To hit the exact center of a target; figuratively, to be exactly right or achieve precisely the intended result.	hit the mark, nail it, be spot-on	The arrow hit the bull's-eye. / Your explanation hit the bullseye—you identified the real problem.	Related to WID576 bull's-eye/bullseye. KEEP if retrieval value of the full expression is useful; later global duplicate reconciliation can decide whether noun + idiom should remain separate.		
60	599	632	Catch-22 / catch-22	/kætʃ twɛntiˈtu/	noun	A no-win situation in which contradictory or mutually dependent rules or conditions prevent a solution or escape.	no-win situation, impossible bind, paradoxical trap	It's a Catch-22: I need experience to get the job, but I need a job to get experience. / Small businesses can face a funding catch-22.	Correct punctuation and broken example. Originates from Joseph Heller's novel Catch-22; generic use often lowercase. More specific than any dilemma: the conditions themselves trap you circularly.		
60	600	633	mixed blessing	/mɪkst ˈblɛsɪŋ/	noun phrase	Something that brings both benefits and disadvantages, so its overall value is ambiguous.	double-edged sword, trade-off, blessing and curse	Working from home has been a mixed blessing: I save commuting time, but I miss seeing colleagues. / Fame can be a mixed blessing.	Compare double-edged sword: mixed blessing often emphasizes something beneficial that also has drawbacks; double-edged sword emphasizes a factor that can help and harm.		
61	601	634	double-edged sword	/ˌdʌbəl ˌɛdʒd ˈsɔrd/	noun phrase / idiom	Something that has both beneficial and harmful effects, or an advantage that also creates a corresponding risk or disadvantage.	mixed blessing, two-sided effect, trade-off	Social media can be a double-edged sword: it connects people but can also amplify misinformation. / Her perfectionism was a double-edged sword.	Correct hyphenation. Often 'a double-edged sword'. Not identical to mixed blessing, though they overlap strongly.		
61	602	635	wolf in sheep's clothing	/wʊlf ɪn ʃiːps ˈkloʊðɪŋ/	idiom	A dangerous, malicious, or untrustworthy person or thing disguised as harmless, friendly, or beneficial.	deceiver, hidden threat, false friend	The investment looked safe, but it turned out to be a wolf in sheep's clothing. / He warned that the charming newcomer was a wolf in sheep's clothing.	Can describe people, policies, products, ideas—not only a person. Biblical/fable-like imagery; common enough for active recognition/use.		
61	603	636	toss-up	/ˈtɔsˌʌp/	noun (informal)	A situation or contest in which either of two or more outcomes seems about equally likely; a choice that is difficult because options are closely matched.	coin toss, close call, fifty-fifty proposition	It's a toss-up between the two restaurants. / The election is still a toss-up.	Common patterns: it's a toss-up whether...; a toss-up between A and B. Hyphenated noun. 'Coin flip' is informal close synonym.		
61	604	637	catcall / catcalls	/ˈkætˌkɔlz/	noun; verb	A whistle, shout, sexual remark, jeer, or mocking call directed at someone in public; as a verb, to direct such calls at someone.	wolf whistle, jeer, heckle; harass verbally	She ignored the catcalls from across the street. / Several audience members catcalled the speaker.	Broaden carefully: historically/general catcall can be a jeer or disapproving whistle, while contemporary use often means unsolicited sexual comments/whistles, especially toward women. Do not reduce it solely to one gender.		
61	605	638	mixed bag	/mɪkst bæg/	noun phrase (informal)	A collection, situation, or result containing a varied mixture, often including both good and bad elements.	mixture, assortment, uneven mix, mixed results	The reviews were a mixed bag: some were glowing and others were brutal. / My first week at the new job was a mixed bag.	Not always exactly 50/50 good and bad; can simply mean varied/uneven. Common: a mixed bag of X.		
