62	616	649	relish / relishing	/ˈrɛlɪʃɪŋ/	verb; noun	verb: to enjoy or look forward to something greatly. noun: enjoyment/appetite for something; also a chopped or pickled condiment.	savor, delight in, enjoy greatly; condiment	She relished the chance to prove them wrong. / I'm not relishing the thought of another six-hour meeting. / He added pickle relish to the hot dog.	Normalize lemma and include common negative progressive 'not relishing the prospect/thought'. Food noun is a separate major sense.		
62	617	650	poster boy / poster girl / poster child	/ˈpoʊstɚ bɔɪz/	noun phrase (informal/figurative)	A person who is seen as a highly visible, representative, or emblematic example of a quality, movement, problem, or trend.	symbol, emblem, representative example, public face	He became the poster boy for reckless spending. / She was once described as the poster child for the reform movement.	Normalize singular and broaden productive family. Can be positive, negative, or ironic. 'Poster child' is gender-neutral in many contexts and extremely common figuratively.		
62	618	652	mull / mull over	/mʌl/	verb / phrasal verb	To think carefully about something for a period of time before deciding or forming an opinion.	ponder, consider, think over, reflect on	I need a day to mull it over. / She's mulling over whether to accept the offer.	Mull over is very common; pronoun placement: mull it over. Plain mull can also be transitive in some usage. Separate unrelated mulled wine sense of mull if encountered.		
62	619	653	induce / inducing	/ɪnˈduːsɪŋ/	verb	To cause something to happen; to persuade or lead someone to do something; in medicine, to initiate a physiological process such as labor or anesthesia.	cause, bring about, trigger; persuade, prompt	The medication may induce drowsiness. / Nothing could induce him to change his mind. / Doctors decided to induce labor.	Normalize lemma. Patterns: induce X; induce someone to do; induce labor/sleep/anesthesia. Related induction. Not interchangeable with 'introduce'.		
62	620	654	disquieting	/dɪsˈkwaɪɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Causing unease, anxiety, or concern; troubling in a subtle or persistent way.	unsettling, troubling, disturbing, alarming	There was something disquieting about his sudden silence. / The report contains several disquieting findings.	Often more restrained/literary than alarming. Related disquiet (noun/verb) and disquieted.		
63	621	655	incongruous	/ɪnˈkɑŋɡruəs/	adjective	Out of place or not in harmony with its surroundings, context, style, or other elements.	out of place, mismatched, inconsistent, discordant	The modern glass tower looked incongruous beside the medieval church. / His cheerful tone seemed incongruous with the bad news.	Common pattern incongruous with. Related incongruity. Focus is mismatch, not simply 'inappropriate'.		
63	622	656	phew / whew	/fjuː/	interjection	An exclamation expressing relief, exhaustion, being impressed/overwhelmed, or reaction to something intense.	whew, wow (some contexts), thank goodness (relief paraphrase)	Phew, that was close! / Phew—I'm exhausted after that climb. / Whew, that's a lot of money.	Phew and whew overlap; spelling/pronunciation vary. 'Whoa' is not a general synonym. Tone determines relief vs exhaustion vs amazement.		
63	623	657	crowning	/ˈkraʊnɪŋ/	adjective	Representing the highest, final, or most impressive achievement or feature of something.	supreme, culminating, greatest, defining	Winning the championship was the crowning achievement of her career. / The tower is the crowning feature of the building.	High-value collocation: crowning achievement/glory. 'Crowning moment' also possible. Related crown (v.) = complete gloriously / put a crown on.		
63	624	659	rev / rev up	/rɛv/	verb; noun	verb: to increase an engine's speed; figuratively, rev up = increase activity, excitement, energy, or preparation. noun: an engine revolution, especially in plural revs.	race an engine, accelerate; ramp up, energize	He revved the engine at the light. / The campaign is revving up ahead of the election. / Keep the revs low until the engine warms up.	Add productive phrasal verb rev up. Rev ≠ simply accelerate a vehicle; it specifically concerns engine speed in literal use.		
63	625	660	unsnarl	/ʌnˈsnɑrl/	verb	To untangle something physically or to resolve a complicated blockage, traffic jam, administrative problem, or tangled situation.	untangle, disentangle, sort out, clear	She patiently unsnarled the knotted cord. / Police worked to unsnarl traffic after the crash. / Negotiators tried to unsnarl the dispute.	Less common than untangle/disentangle but vivid and useful. Particularly natural with traffic/logistics/bureaucratic complications.		
63	626	661	disentangle	/ˌdɪsənˈtæŋɡəl/	verb	To free something from a physical tangle; figuratively, to separate interconnected issues, relationships, causes, or oneself from a complicated involvement.	untangle, unravel, separate, extricate	I spent ten minutes disentangling my headphones. / It's difficult to disentangle cause from correlation. / The company is trying to disentangle itself from the partnership.	Productive figurative patterns: disentangle A from B; disentangle oneself from. Compare unsnarl: often resolve a jam/mess; disentangle emphasizes separating intertwined elements.		
