63	628	663	reek	/riːk/	verb; noun	verb: to smell very strongly and unpleasantly; figuratively, to strongly suggest an undesirable quality. noun: a strong unpleasant smell (less common than verb).	stink; smack of, be redolent of (register differs); stench	The room reeked of cigarette smoke. / The deal reeks of corruption. / A reek of chemicals hung in the air.	Very common pattern reek of X, literal and figurative: reek of desperation/corruption/hypocrisy. Noun is less common in everyday AmE.		
63	629	664	left-hand path	/lɛft hænd pæθ/	noun phrase (religion/esotericism)	A label used in some Western esoteric and occult traditions for approaches emphasizing individual autonomy, antinomian or taboo-breaking practices, self-deification, or other departures from conventional religious norms; meanings vary by tradition.	Left-Hand Path, LHP (specialist abbreviation)	The author contrasts Left-Hand Path traditions with what he calls the Right-Hand Path. / Different occult groups define the left-hand path differently.	Hyphenate left-hand. Avoid defining it simply as 'dark' or immoral: the label is tradition-dependent and contested. In Western occultism it has specialized meanings; historical South Asian left/right-hand classifications have different contexts.		
63	630	665	altruist / altruists	/ˈæltruɪsts/	noun	A person motivated by concern for others' welfare, especially someone willing to benefit others without expecting equivalent personal gain.	selfless person, humanitarian (some contexts), benefactor (not exact)	Even committed altruists have to decide where their help can do the most good. / The group attracted idealists and altruists.	Normalize singular. Philanthropist/benefactor specifically imply giving resources and are not exact synonyms. Related altruism/altruistic.		
64	631	666	angle	/ˈæŋɡəl/	noun; verb	noun: a geometric angle; a particular perspective, approach, or way of presenting a subject; a strategic motive or 'angle.' verb: to position or present something at an angle or from a particular perspective.	perspective, viewpoint, approach, slant; position	Let's look at the problem from another angle. / What's his angle—why is he offering this for free? / The article angles the story toward younger readers.	Current figurative sense is useful but too narrow. High-value: from a different angle; what's your/his angle?; camera angle; angle for something (informal, seek).		
64	632	667	right-hand path	/ˈraɪt hænd pæθ/	noun phrase (religion/esotericism)	A label used in some Western esoteric frameworks for traditions emphasizing alignment with a transcendent/divine order, conventional ethical constraints, or union with a higher principle; definitions vary widely by tradition.	Right-Hand Path, RHP (specialist abbreviation)	The book uses 'right-hand path' for traditions oriented toward union with a higher spiritual order. / Not all occultists accept the left-hand/right-hand path distinction.	Hyphenate right-hand. Avoid simplistic equation with 'good/moral' versus left-hand = 'bad'. These are contested specialist categories, and historical South Asian uses differ from modern Western occult classifications.		
64	633	668	bad actor	/bæd ˈæktɚz/	noun phrase (informal)	A person, organization, state, account, or system participant that acts dishonestly, maliciously, irresponsibly, or in bad faith.	wrongdoer, malicious actor, bad-faith participant, threat actor (cybersecurity-specific)	The platform is trying to prevent bad actors from abusing the system. / A few bad actors can undermine trust in the whole market.	Normalize singular. Common in policy, business, cybersecurity, geopolitics. 'Threat actor' is narrower cybersecurity/security jargon.		
64	634	669	blockade	/blɑˈkeɪd/	noun; verb	noun: an organized action that prevents people, goods, traffic, or supplies from entering or leaving a place. verb: to impose or create such an obstruction.	siege (not exact), barrier, obstruction; seal off, obstruct	The navy imposed a blockade on the port. / Protesters blockaded the entrance. / Aid deliveries were delayed by the blockade.	A blockade is not identical to a siege: siege involves surrounding/attacking to force surrender; blockade focuses on preventing movement/supplies. Can be military, political, or protest-related.		
64	635	670	bootstrap / bootstrap something	/ˈbuːtˌstræp/	verb; noun	verb: to start, build, or improve something using one's own limited resources rather than substantial outside support; in computing/statistics, bootstrap has specialized meanings. noun: a loop/strap on a boot and several derived technical concepts.	self-fund, build from scratch, start with limited resources	She bootstrapped the company without outside investors. / The system uses a bootstrap process to load its core components.	Business sense comes from 'pull oneself up by one's bootstraps,' originally an impossible feat later reinterpreted as self-reliance. Computing/statistics senses deserve context-specific treatment; do not collapse them into business self-funding.		
64	636	671	teeter	/ˈtiːtɚ/	verb	To move or balance unsteadily as if about to fall; figuratively, to be in an unstable situation close to a major change or failure.	wobble, totter, sway; hover on the brink	The glass teetered on the edge of the table. / The company was teetering on the brink of bankruptcy.	High-value figurative construction teeter on the brink/edge of X. Compare totter = unsteady walking/movement; teeter emphasizes precarious balance.		
64	637	672	break it to someone	/breɪk ɪt tu ˈsʌmwʌn/	idiom	To tell someone unpleasant, disappointing, or surprising news, often carefully or reluctantly.	tell, reveal, inform gently, let someone know	I don't know how to break it to him that the trip was canceled. / Who's going to break the news to her?	Pattern: break it to someone that... / break the news to someone. The phrase does not inherently mean the delivery itself is gentle, though speakers often try to be tactful.		
64	638	673	breakage	/ˈbreɪkɪdʒ/	noun	The act or process of breaking; damage caused by breaking; the quantity or cost of items that are broken.	damage, broken items, loss through breakage	Fragile items should be packed carefully to prevent breakage. / The store allows for a small amount of breakage.	Usually uncountable. Common in shipping/retail/materials. Also technical senses such as hair breakage.		
64	639	674	buddy-buddy	/ˈbʌdi ˌbʌdi/	adjective (informal)	Very friendly or familiar, often suddenly, conspicuously, or more intimately than the speaker thinks is natural or sincere.	chummy, overly familiar, palsy-walsy	Why are you two suddenly so buddy-buddy? / He got buddy-buddy with the manager after the promotion was announced.	Often mildly suspicious, teasing, or disapproving—not simply 'close friends'. Common get/be buddy-buddy with.		
64	640	675	charley horse	/ˈtʃɑrli hɔrs/	noun (informal, chiefly North American)	A sudden painful muscle cramp or spasm, especially in the leg or calf.	muscle cramp, leg cramp, muscle spasm	I woke up with a charley horse in my calf. / Stretching helped the charley horse ease up.	Informal North American expression. Usually a cramp, though sports usage historically can include muscle stiffness/injury. Spelling charley horse is standard; Charlie horse also appears.		
65	641	676	chaste	/tʃeɪst/	adjective	Abstaining from sexual activity according to a moral/religious ideal; sexually pure in traditional usage; also restrained, simple, or unadorned in style.	celibate (not exact), sexually abstinent; pure, restrained, austere	He had taken a vow to remain chaste. / The building's design is elegant and chaste, with almost no ornament.	Do not equate chaste automatically with celibate: celibacy often means unmarried/abstinent by commitment/status; chastity depends on ethical/religious framework. Important nonsexual aesthetic sense = restrained/unornamented.		
