66	660	699	lay off	/leɪ ɔf/	phrasal verb	To terminate someone's employment, typically because a position is eliminated or an employer reduces staff rather than because of individual misconduct; also informally, to stop bothering/criticizing/using something.	make redundant (BrE), dismiss due to downsizing, let go; leave alone (other sense)	The company laid off 200 employees after sales fell. / Lay off him—he's had a rough day. / I'm trying to lay off the caffeine.	Important multiple senses. Employment layoff differs from being fired for misconduct, though everyday usage can blur. Noun: layoff. Separable: lay someone off.		
