70	699	741	fishing haul / haul	/ˈfɪʃɪŋ hɔl/	noun phrase	The quantity or collection of fish or other catch taken during a fishing trip, netting, or period; haul more broadly means an amount obtained, carried, bought, or seized.	catch, take, yield	The crew brought in a large fishing haul that morning. / Today's haul included tuna and mackerel.	Could later merge with WID740 if global duplicate consolidation favors one deep haul card. For now KEEP because source phrase may aid retrieval; explicitly link to general noun haul.		
70	700	742	enshrine / enshrined	/ɛnˈʃraɪnd/	verb	To preserve, protect, or establish an idea, right, principle, or rule as especially important or authoritative; literally, to place or preserve something in a shrine.	embed, establish, protect, preserve, codify (some contexts)	Freedom of expression is enshrined in the constitution. / The agreement enshrined the principle in law. / The relic was enshrined in the temple.	Normalize lemma. Very common passive: be enshrined in law/the constitution. 'Codify' overlaps only when formally written into a code.		
71	701	743	pungent	/ˈpʌndʒənt/	adjective	Having a very strong, sharp smell or taste; figuratively, sharply expressive, biting, or penetrating in language or criticism.	sharp, acrid, strong-smelling; biting, incisive	The kitchen was filled with the pungent smell of garlic. / The cheese has a pungent flavor. / She offered a pungent critique of the policy.	Strong smell/taste can be pleasant or unpleasant. Figurative pungent wit/commentary is less common but useful. Compare acrid = usually harsh/unpleasant.		
71	702	744	have a bump on one's eyelid / eyelid bump	/hæv ə bʌmp ɑn maɪ ˈaɪˌlɪd/	descriptive phrase	To have a small lump or swelling on the eyelid; everyday description only, not the name of a specific condition.	eyelid lump, eyelid swelling, bump on the eyelid	I've got a small bump on my eyelid. / She noticed a tender lump near the edge of her eyelid.	Keep as productive everyday symptom-description language, but remove diagnostic speculation from the definition. Specific causes require medical evaluation; vocabulary card should not imply every bump is an infection or stye.		
71	703	745	stye / sty; styes	/staɪz/	noun	A localized, usually painful red lump near the edge of an eyelid caused by inflammation/infection of an eyelid gland or follicle.	hordeolum (medical term), eyelid stye	I woke up with a painful stye on my upper eyelid. / She gets styes occasionally when her eyelids are irritated.	Normalize singular/plural. Stye and sty are accepted spellings; stye avoids confusion with pigsty. Distinguish from chalazion, which is typically a blocked gland and often less acutely painful.		
71	704	746	edgy middle schooler	/ˈɛdʒi ˈmɪdl ˈskuːlɚ/	informal descriptive noun phrase	A middle-school-aged person who deliberately adopts provocative, dark, shocking, cynical, or rebellious attitudes to seem bold, mature, or unconventional.	provocative kid, try-hard edgelord (strong slang/context-dependent)	That joke has the energy of an edgy middle schooler trying to shock everyone. / The character sounds less dangerous than like an edgy middle schooler.	KEEP as source-driven descriptive/slang phrase, not a standardized lexical item. 'Edgy' itself has several meanings: tense/on edge; bold/innovative; deliberately provocative. Here the third cluster is intended.		
71	705	747	anti-establishment view / attitude	/æn ˌæntaɪ ɪˈstæblɪʃmənt vju/	noun phrase	A view or attitude that distrusts, challenges, or opposes established political, social, cultural, or economic institutions and power structures.	anti-system stance, anti-institutional attitude, oppositional view	The movement attracted voters with a strongly anti-establishment message. / His anti-establishment attitude shaped his criticism of both major parties.	Hyphenate anti-establishment. It does not specify left/right ideology; movements across the political spectrum can be anti-establishment.		
71	706	748	heedful	/ˈhiːdfəl/	adjective	Carefully attentive to advice, warnings, risks, needs, or circumstances.	mindful, attentive, observant, careful	Be heedful of the warning signs. / A good editor is heedful of both tone and context.	Somewhat formal/literary and much less common than mindful/aware. Pattern heedful of. Related heed (v./n.) and heedless.		
71	707	749	arguably	/ˈɑrɡjuəbli/	adverb	Used to indicate that a claim can reasonably be argued or supported, while acknowledging that others may disagree.	it can be argued that, plausibly, perhaps (weaker)	This is arguably the best episode of the season. / She is arguably one of the most influential writers of her generation.	Useful hedging device in analytical writing and conversation. It does not mean 'definitely'; nor does it necessarily mean the speaker personally doubts the claim.		
71	708	750	confer	/kənˈfɝː/	verb	1. To discuss or consult together. 2. To formally grant or bestow a degree, title, honor, right, status, or benefit.	consult, deliberate; grant, bestow, award	The lawyers conferred before answering. / The university conferred an honorary degree on her. / Citizenship confers certain rights.	Two distinct constructions: confer with someone = consult; confer something on/upon someone = formally grant. Very useful contrast with bestow and award.		
71	709	751	relinquish	/rɪˈlɪŋkwɪʃ/	verb	To voluntarily or reluctantly give up, release, surrender, or stop holding a possession, right, claim, position, control, or responsibility.	give up, surrender, yield, renounce, release	He refused to relinquish control of the company. / She relinquished her claim to the property. / The champion was forced to relinquish the title.	Formal. Relinquish emphasizes ceasing to hold/control something. Contrast waive = intentionally forgo a right/requirement; renounce = formally reject/abandon; surrender can imply yielding under pressure.		
71	710	752	waive / waived	/weɪvd/	verb	To intentionally give up, forgo, or choose not to enforce a right, claim, requirement, fee, rule, or privilege.	forgo, relinquish, give up, dispense with (some contexts)	You may be asked to waive your right to a hearing. / The airline waived the change fee. / The requirement was waived for returning students.	Normalize lemma. Key confusable wave. Common legal/admin patterns: waive a right/fee/requirement; waive enforcement. Unlike relinquish, waive often concerns a right or rule that could otherwise be invoked.		
72	711	753	honorary degree	/ˈɑnɚˌɛri dɪˈɡriː/	noun phrase	An academic degree awarded by an institution as an honor, usually recognizing achievement or contribution, without the recipient completing the normal degree requirements.	honorary doctorate, honorary academic degree	The university awarded her an honorary degree for her contributions to public health. / He received an honorary doctorate.	An honorary degree is not the same as an earned academic qualification. Institutions differ in naming and conventions. Common verbs: award/confer/bestow an honorary degree.		
72	712	754	bestow	/bɪˈstoʊ/	verb	To formally give, present, or confer an honor, title, gift, right, quality, or benefit, often ceremonially or in elevated language.	confer, grant, award, present	The foundation bestowed its highest honor on her. / The title was bestowed upon him after the ceremony.	Formal/elevated. Pattern bestow something on/upon someone. Compare confer: confer also means consult; bestow only has the giving/granting cluster.		
72	713	755	insolent	/ˈɪnsələnt/	adjective	Boldly rude, disrespectful, or contemptuous, especially toward someone in authority or someone considered deserving of respect.	impudent, impertinent, disrespectful, brazen	The officer considered his reply insolent. / She gave the teacher an insolent stare.	Stronger/more formal than rude. Often implies defiant disrespect. Compare impudent/impertinent: close, with register and nuance differences.		
72	714	756	slip up	/slɪp ʌp/	phrasal verb (informal)	To make a usually small or accidental mistake, especially after trying to be careful; noun: slip-up.	make a mistake, mess up, make an error	I slipped up and sent the email to the wrong person. / One small slip-up cost them the game.	Often implies an accidental lapse rather than incompetence. Noun is hyphenated: a slip-up. Compare screw up = stronger/more informal.		
72	715	757	receptacle	/rɪˈsɛptəkəl/	noun	A container, holder, or place designed to receive, contain, or collect something; also a technical term in botany/electrical contexts.	container, holder, vessel, bin	Please put used batteries in the designated receptacle. / The wall receptacle needs to be replaced.	Formal/generic. In North American electrical usage, receptacle can mean an electrical outlet/socket component. Context determines sense.		
72	716	758	volatile	/ˈvɑlətəl/	adjective	1. Liable to change rapidly and unpredictably, especially in price, mood, politics, or conditions. 2. Easily evaporated at ordinary temperatures. 3. Potentially explosive or suddenly violent in behavior/situation.	unstable, unpredictable, turbulent; evaporative; explosive, hot-tempered	Markets have been extremely volatile this week. / Gasoline contains volatile compounds. / He has a volatile temper.	Major polysemy: finance/politics, chemistry, temperament. Volatile does not inherently mean 'bad', though rapid unpredictability often creates risk.		
72	717	759	run off	/rʌn ɔf/	phrasal verb; noun runoff (related)	To leave or flee suddenly; to make/print copies quickly; to flow or drain away; to cause someone/something to leave. Related noun runoff = water or an additional election round.	flee, dash away; print; drain; chase off	He ran off before I could ask his name. / I'll run off a few copies. / Rainwater runs off the roof into the gutter. / The dog ran the deer off.	Current card mixes verb with noun-like drainage. Keep phrasal verb senses distinct and note runoff is one word as a noun. Also 'run off with' = steal/elope with, a separate productive extension.		
72	718	760	call off	/kɔl ɔf/	phrasal verb	To cancel a planned event, activity, search, attack, or arrangement; also to order a person/animal to stop attacking or pursuing.	cancel, cancel plans, abort; recall, restrain	They called off the game because of the storm. / The police called off the search at midnight. / Call off your dog!	Separable: call it/the meeting off. 'Call off your dog' preserves literal order-to-stop sense. Compare put off = postpone, not cancel.		
72	719	761	abort / aborted	/əˈbɔrtɪd/	verb; adjective/past participle	To stop a process, mission, plan, or operation before completion, often because continuing is unsafe or impossible; in medicine, abort has specialized pregnancy-related meanings requiring context.	terminate, cancel, halt, abandon	The crew aborted the landing because of strong crosswinds. / The launch was aborted at the last second. / The program aborted after encountering a fatal error.	Normalize lemma. Common technical/aviation/computing sense is important. Medical meanings are context-sensitive; avoid collapsing spontaneous pregnancy loss, induced abortion, and general 'stop early' into one vague definition.		
