73	721	763	ineffectual	/ˌɪnəˈfɛktʃuəl/	adjective	Not producing the intended or needed effect; lacking the ability, force, or competence to achieve results.	ineffective, futile, powerless, feeble	The response was slow and ineffectual. / He was portrayed as an ineffectual leader who couldn't control his own cabinet.	Often more personal/evaluative than ineffective: an ineffectual person/leader can imply weakness or inability to act effectively. Do not confuse with ineffective, which is more neutral/general.		
73	722	764	fester / festering	/ˈfɛstərɪŋ/	verb	Literally, for a wound or sore to become infected or produce pus; figuratively, for resentment, conflict, problems, or grievances to worsen because they are ignored or unresolved.	worsen, rankle, smolder, become infected	Don't let the resentment fester—talk to her about it. / The dispute had been festering for years. / The untreated wound began to fester.	Normalize lemma and preserve literal + figurative. Figurative collocations: festering resentment/grievance/conflict/problem.		
