74	737	779	reap what you sow	/riːp wʌt ju soʊ/	proverb / idiom	To experience consequences that correspond to one's earlier actions or choices, whether good or bad.	face the consequences, get what you deserve (often negative), actions have consequences	If you treat people badly for years, eventually you may reap what you sow. / The proverb can also be positive: invest patiently now and reap what you sow later.	Agricultural metaphor: sow seeds → later reap the crop. Often used negatively, but the logic itself can apply to positive outcomes too.		
74	738	780	corrosive	/kəˈroʊsɪv/	adjective	Able to chemically eat away or damage materials; figuratively, gradually destructive to relationships, institutions, trust, morale, or character.	caustic, erosive, destructive, damaging	The chemical is highly corrosive to metal. / Constant suspicion can have a corrosive effect on a relationship.	Literal chemistry + figurative social/emotional use. Corrosive ≠ poisonous; a substance can be corrosive because it damages tissue/material.		
74	739	781	septic	/ˈsɛptɪk/	adjective	Relating to infection or sepsis; contaminated by harmful microorganisms. Also used in septic tank/system, where it refers to an on-site sewage-treatment system rather than 'infected.'	infected, infection-related; septic-system (separate sense)	Doctors were concerned that the wound had become septic. / The house uses a septic system instead of a municipal sewer.	Current definition is too narrow and risks conflating septic with sepsis. Add extremely common septic tank/system sense. Medical use should remain descriptive, not diagnostic.		
