75	750	793	stubble	/ˈstʌbəl/	noun	Short, stiff hair that has grown back after shaving or cutting, especially facial hair; also short stalks left in a field after crops are harvested.	short beard growth, five-o'clock shadow (specific); crop stubble	He had two days of stubble on his face. / The fields were covered in wheat stubble after harvest.	Add agricultural sense. Usually uncountable. Five-o'clock shadow is a specific light facial-stubble appearance, not exact synonym for all stubble.		
76	751	794	become second nature / be second nature	/ɪt bɪˈkʌmz ˈsɛkənd ˈneɪtʃɚ/	idiom	To become so familiar through repetition or practice that an action feels automatic, effortless, or instinctive.	become automatic, become instinctive, become habitual	After a few months, using the shortcuts became second nature. / Checking mirrors before changing lanes is second nature to her.	Normalize from full sentence 'it becomes second nature'. Common: become second nature; be second nature to someone; doing X is second nature.		
