76	756	799	angsty	/ˈæŋsti/	adjective (informal)	Full of anxiety, dissatisfaction, emotional tension, brooding, or existential frustration, often in a way associated with adolescence, art, or subculture.	brooding, anxious, moody, emotionally turbulent	I listened to a lot of angsty music as a teenager. / The character is intense and angsty without being completely humorless.	Informal derivative of angst. Can be teasing/dismissive, not a clinical description. Often collocates with teen/teenager/music/lyrics/character.		
76	757	800	angsty teen / angsty teenager	/ˈæŋsti tin/	informal descriptive noun phrase	A teenager characterized—often stereotypically or humorously—as brooding, anxious, emotionally intense, dissatisfied, or rebellious.	brooding teenager, moody teen, troubled teen (not exact)	The protagonist starts out as a classic angsty teen but becomes more self-aware. / I was an angsty teenager who wrote dramatic journal entries.	KEEP as a source-driven phrase if retrieval value matters, but link explicitly to WID799 angsty. Do not define ordinary adolescent emotion as pathology. Possible later merge candidate if global reconciliation decides adjective + phrase are redundant.		
76	758	801	chūnibyō / chuunibyou (中二病)	/ˈtʃuːniˌbjoʊ/	noun (Japanese slang)	A Japanese slang term, literally 'second-year middle-school syndrome,' for adolescent-style grandiosity, affected edginess, fantasies of hidden powers or special destiny, or behavior seen as trying too hard to appear unique, mature, dark, or mysterious.	eighth-grader syndrome (translation), adolescent grandiosity, edgy fantasy persona (contextual)	The character acts like a classic chūnibyō, inventing secret powers and dramatic titles. / I went through a mild chuunibyou phase where everything had to sound mysterious.	KEEP as Japanese cultural/slang vocabulary. The term is often humorous or teasing, not a clinical diagnosis. Anime/manga usage strongly features delusions of supernatural power, but real-world usage can be broader: pretentious, self-dramatizing adolescent behavior.		
76	759	802	multimodal	/ˌmʌltiˈmoʊdəl/	adjective	Using, involving, or combining multiple modes, channels, types of data, or forms of communication.	multi-channel, multi-mode, cross-modal (technical overlap)	The course uses a multimodal approach combining text, video, discussion, and hands-on work. / The model can perform multimodal reasoning across text and images.	Broader than AI. Used in communication, education, transportation, medicine, interfaces, and machine learning. The exact 'modes' depend on domain.		
76	760	803	multimodal AI	/ˌmʌltiˈmoʊdəl ˌeɪˈaɪ/	noun phrase (technology)	Artificial intelligence that can work with more than one data modality—such as text, images, audio, video, or sensor data—whether by understanding, generating, or connecting information across those modalities.	multimodal model, multimodal system, vision-language model (narrower)	A multimodal AI system might analyze an image while answering a spoken question about it. / Some multimodal models can accept text, images, and audio as input.	Do not define multimodal AI as necessarily supporting every modality or every input/output combination. 'Vision-language model' is a narrower subtype involving vision and language.		
77	761	805	ulterior	/ʌlˈtɪriɚ/	adjective	Existing beyond what is openly stated or immediately apparent; usually used of a hidden additional purpose or motive.	hidden, underlying, undisclosed, concealed	I wondered whether there was some ulterior purpose behind the offer. / The question seemed innocent, but it may have had an ulterior aim.	In modern everyday English, ulterior is strongly associated with motive/motives, purpose, agenda, reason. It does not simply mean 'secret' in every context.		
77	762	806	ulterior motive	/ʌlˈtɪriɚ ˈmoʊtɪv/	noun phrase	A hidden or undisclosed reason for doing something, especially one that is self-interested, manipulative, or different from the reason presented openly.	hidden agenda, secret motive, underlying motive	She offered to help with no ulterior motive. / He suspected that their sudden generosity came with an ulterior motive.	Very common phrase. Often negative, but 'no ulterior motive' is also frequent. Distinguish motive (reason for action) from intention (what one plans to do).		
77	763	807	crack the whip	/kræk ðə wɪp/	idiom	To use authority, pressure, or strict supervision to make people work harder, faster, or more obediently.	push hard, pressure, drive, lay down the law (some contexts)	The manager started cracking the whip as the deadline approached. / We need someone to crack the whip and keep the project moving.	Usually figurative today and can sound joking or critical. Implies forceful management, not merely encouragement.		
77	764	808	neck and neck	/nɛk ənd nɛk/	idiom / adjective phrase	So close in a race, contest, poll, or competition that neither side has a clear lead.	even, tied, too close to call, running level	The two runners were neck and neck entering the final stretch. / The candidates are still neck and neck in the polls.	Common: be/run neck and neck; a neck-and-neck race (hyphenated before noun). Origin is horse-racing imagery.		
77	765	809	slug	/slʌɡ/	noun; verb	noun: a shell-less terrestrial mollusk; a heavy punch; a small amount/shot of liquor; a projectile or various technical objects. verb: to hit hard; to move laboriously; in baseball, to hit for power.	slug (mollusk); punch, blow; hit hard	There was a slug on the garden path. / He took a slug to the shoulder. / She slugged the ball over the fence. / He took a slug of whiskey.	Highly polysemous. For active vocabulary, prioritize animal + hard punch/slug someone + baseball slug. 'Slug of liquor' is informal. Avoid treating all technical senses as one concept.		
77	766	810	tragus	/ˈtreɪɡəs/	noun (anatomy)	The small cartilaginous projection of the outer ear immediately in front of the ear canal.	ear cartilage, auricular projection (descriptive)	The earbud rests near the tragus. / She got a tragus piercing.	Anatomical term. Plural can be tragi or traguses depending register. Common everyday context: tragus piercing.		
77	767	811	on a lark	/ɑn ə lɑrk/	idiom (informal)	For amusement, adventure, or curiosity, especially as a spontaneous or unserious impulse rather than a carefully planned decision.	on a whim, for fun, impulsively, just for kicks	We entered the contest on a lark and somehow won. / On a lark, they drove to the coast after dinner.	Often implies playful spontaneity. Compare on a whim: spontaneous but not necessarily fun/playful.		
77	768	813	perch / perched	/pɝtʃt/	verb; noun	verb: to sit, rest, or be positioned on a high, narrow, or precarious place; noun: a place/position for sitting or a type of fish.	sit, balance, roost; vantage point (some contexts)	A bird perched on the fence. / She was perched on the edge of the sofa. / The village is perched high above the valley.	Normalize lemma. Very productive passive/adjectival pattern be perched on/above/atop. Also noun perch and fish sense, though contextual priority is positional verb.		
77	769	814	outlandish	/aʊtˈlændɪʃ/	adjective	Strikingly strange, bizarre, extravagant, or implausible, often beyond what seems normal or reasonable.	bizarre, outrageous, extravagant, far-fetched, eccentric	He showed up in an outlandish costume. / At first the theory sounded outlandish, but part of it proved correct.	Can describe appearance, behavior, claims, prices, plans. Not simply 'unfamiliar'; usually noticeably extreme or bizarre.		
77	770	815	orifice	/ˈɔrɪfɪs/	noun	An opening or aperture, especially a natural or artificial opening in the body, a machine, vessel, pipe, or other structure.	opening, aperture, hole, outlet	The valve controls flow through a small orifice. / In anatomy, the term can refer to a bodily orifice.	Formal/technical. Common in anatomy and engineering. In casual speech, bodily-orifice usage can sound clinical or deliberately comic.		
