78	772	817	stick it out	/stɪk ɪt aʊt/	idiom (informal)	To continue enduring a difficult, unpleasant, or discouraging situation until it ends or improves instead of quitting.	persevere, tough it out, hang in there, see it through	The first month was rough, but I decided to stick it out. / If you can stick it out until Friday, things should calm down.	Often intransitive 'stick it out' or 'stick something out'. Emphasizes endurance despite wanting to quit.		
78	773	818	smudge / smudging	/ˈsmʌdʒɪŋ/	noun; verb	A smudge is a blurred, smeared, or dirty mark; to smudge is to make such a mark or blur something. Smudging can also refer to smoke-based ceremonial practices in some Indigenous and other spiritual contexts.	smear, blur, stain, mark	I smudged the ink before it dried. / There was a dark smudge on the screen. / The article discusses ceremonial smudging in a specific cultural context.	Normalize lemma and separate ordinary physical sense from ceremonial use. Avoid presenting diverse smoke-cleansing traditions as one generic practice; terminology and cultural significance vary.		
