78	775	820	deterrent	/dɪˈtɝrənt/	noun; adjective	Something that discourages or prevents an action by making it less attractive, more difficult, or more costly; also attributively, having a discouraging effect.	disincentive, discouragement, preventive measure	The high fine is intended to act as a deterrent. / Visible security can have a deterrent effect.	Common: deterrent to/against; act as a deterrent; deterrent effect. Deterrence is the strategy/process. A deterrent does not guarantee prevention.		
78	776	821	lose sleep over	/luːz sliːp ˈoʊvɚ/	idiom	To worry a great deal about something, especially enough that it occupies one's thoughts; often used negatively to say something is not worth worrying about.	worry about, fret over, agonize over	I wouldn't lose sleep over one bad review. / She was losing sleep over the upcoming exam.	Often figurative; actual insomnia is not required. Very common negative: 'I'm not going to lose any sleep over it.'		
78	777	823	forgo	/fɔrˈɡoʊ/	verb	To choose to do without, give up, or abstain from something that one could otherwise have or enjoy.	go without, give up, abstain from, waive (some rights/benefits)	We decided to forgo dessert and head home. / She forgone? No—she forwent the bonus to keep more staff employed.	Irregular forms are rare but useful: forgo–forwent–forgone. Often writers avoid the awkward past by rephrasing. Distinguish forego, which historically can mean 'go before'; modern usage often confuses the spellings.		
78	778	826	writhe / writhing	/ˈraɪðɪŋ/	verb	To twist, squirm, or move the body repeatedly, especially because of pain, discomfort, strong emotion, or physical restraint.	squirm, twist, contort, thrash	He was writhing in pain after the cramp. / The snake writhed in the grass. / She writhed with embarrassment at the memory.	Normalize lemma. Common: writhe in pain/agony; writhe with embarrassment. Stronger/more dramatic than simply squirm.		
