78	780	829	disintegrate	/dɪˈsɪntəˌɡreɪt/	verb	To break apart into smaller pieces or components; figuratively, for an organization, relationship, plan, order, or situation to collapse or lose coherence.	break apart, crumble, fall apart, decompose; collapse	The old paper disintegrated when I touched it. / Their alliance began to disintegrate under pressure.	Both physical and figurative. In science, disintegration can have specialized meanings. Compare dissolve: may become incorporated into liquid or disappear gradually, not necessarily fragment.		
79	781	830	condensation	/ˌkɑndənˈseɪʃən/	noun	The process by which a gas or vapor becomes liquid; the resulting moisture droplets; also, in writing/science, the act of making something more compact or concentrated.	moisture, water droplets; compression, abridgment (other sense)	Condensation formed on the cold window. / Water vapor undergoes condensation as it cools. / The summary is a condensation of a much longer argument.	Add less-common abstract sense. Common everyday pattern: condensation on windows/glass.		
79	782	831	polestar / pole star	/ˈpoʊlˌstɑr/	noun	A star near a celestial pole, especially Polaris in the Northern Hemisphere; figuratively, a guiding principle, ideal, or central point of reference.	North Star, guiding star; guiding principle, lodestar	Accuracy should be our polestar when we evaluate the data. / Polaris has long served as a pole star for navigation.	Spelling pole star is common for astronomical literal sense; polestar is common figuratively. Lodestar is a close figurative synonym.		
79	783	832	snitch on someone	/snɪtʃ ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb (informal)	To inform an authority or other person about someone's wrongdoing, rule-breaking, or secret behavior, usually with a negative implication of betrayal.	rat someone out, tell on, inform on	Who snitched on us to the teacher? / He said he wouldn't snitch on his friends.	Compare WID714 rat someone out. Both are informal and negative; snitch can also be noun 'a snitch'. Later global reconciliation may retain both because they are independently common slang.		
