79	787	838	sting / words sting	/wɝdz stɪŋ/	verb; phrase	To cause a sharp physical pain; figuratively, for words, criticism, rejection, or memories to cause sudden emotional pain.	hurt, wound, smart, burn	Her comment stung more than I expected. / Those words still sting years later. / My eyes stung from the smoke.	Current phrase becomes a productive sting card while preserving 'words sting'. Past stung. Useful patterns: criticism stings; it stung to hear...; still stings.		
79	788	839	grovel / grovel for mercy	/ˈɡrɑvəl fɔr ˈmɝsi/	verb / phrase	To behave in an excessively submissive, self-abasing, or pleading way, especially to gain forgiveness, favor, or mercy.	beg, plead, abase oneself, crawl (figurative)	I'm not going to grovel for forgiveness over a minor mistake. / The villain expected his enemies to grovel for mercy.	Normalize lemma. Grovel can be literal 'lie/crawl low' historically but modern use is mainly figurative humiliation/subservience. Common: grovel to/before someone; grovel for forgiveness/mercy.		
79	789	840	bring someone/something to their/its knees	/brɪŋ ˈsʌmwʌn tu ðɛr niz/	idiom	To weaken, defeat, overwhelm, or cripple a person, organization, economy, system, or country so severely that it can barely function or resist.	cripple, devastate, defeat, break, overwhelm	The cyberattack brought the company to its knees. / Years of war brought the economy to its knees.	Broaden beyond 'humble someone'. Often used for systems/economies/businesses, not just people. Strong dramatic metaphor.		
