80	794	846	turn on someone	/tɝn ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb	To suddenly become hostile toward, attack, criticize, or betray someone who was previously tolerated, supported, or trusted.	attack, betray, turn against, lash out at	The crowd that had cheered him began to turn on him. / Even his closest allies eventually turned on him.	Do not confuse with turn someone on = excite/interest/sexually arouse, which has different object placement. Here: turn on someone = become hostile.		
80	795	847	room to maneuver / manoeuvre	/rum tu məˈnuːvɚ/	noun phrase	Freedom, flexibility, or available options to adjust one's actions, plans, position, or strategy as circumstances change.	leeway, latitude, flexibility, wiggle room	The tight budget leaves us very little room to maneuver. / She wants more room to manoeuvre in the negotiations.	AmE maneuver; BrE manoeuvre. Can be literal physical room, but common figurative sense is strategic flexibility. Compare latitude and elbow room.		
80	796	848	latitude	/ˈlætəˌtud/	noun	1. Geographic distance north or south of the equator, measured in degrees. 2. Freedom or discretion to act, decide, interpret, or vary within limits.	leeway, discretion, freedom; geographic coordinate	Managers have considerable latitude in how they schedule staff. / New York City lies at roughly 40.7 degrees north latitude.	Major polysemy. Figurative pattern give/allow someone latitude; have considerable latitude. Not identical to room to maneuver: latitude often emphasizes permission/discretion.		
