81	807	860	bare / baring	/ˈbɛrɪŋ/	verb; adjective	verb: to uncover, reveal, or expose something; adjective: uncovered, minimal, empty, or lacking usual covering. Baring is the -ing form of bare.	expose, reveal, uncover; naked, minimal	The dog bared its teeth. / She was baring her soul in the interview. / The room had bare walls and a bare wooden floor.	Normalize lemma and distinguish bare from bear. Productive figurative: bare one's soul, bare one's teeth. Baring ≠ bearing.		
81	808	861	beat the dickens out of someone / the dickens	/bit ðə ˈdɪkənz/	idiom / euphemistic expression	The dickens is an old euphemistic exclamation meaning roughly 'the devil' or used for emphasis. In 'beat the dickens out of someone/something,' it intensifies the idea of beating, exhausting, or forcefully affecting.	the devil, the heck; beat badly (phrase)	That noise scared the dickens out of me. / The rough road beat the dickens out of the old car.	Current definition is too narrow. 'The dickens' appears in scare the dickens out of, hurt like the dickens, what the dickens, etc. Old-fashioned/mildly humorous euphemism.		
81	809	862	beat the fuck out of someone/something	/bit ðə fʌk aʊt ʌv ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	idiom (vulgar)	To beat, hit, damage, or overpower someone/something extremely severely; figuratively, it can also mean to outperform something by a huge margin in constructions like 'X beats the fuck out of Y.'	beat badly, thrash, pummel; vastly outperform (figurative)	He threatened to beat the fuck out of the guy. / This cheap setup beats the fuck out of my old one for convenience.	Very vulgar and aggressive. Preserve recognition value but flag register strongly. Object can be a person or thing; figurative comparative use exists. Not appropriate in polite/professional contexts.		
81	810	863	bedazzle / bedazzled	/bɪˈdæzəld/	verb	To impress, enchant, or overwhelm someone with brilliance, glamour, or spectacle; also to decorate something with sparkling rhinestones, gems, or similar ornaments.	dazzle, impress, enchant; embellish with rhinestones	The audience was bedazzled by the elaborate production. / She bedazzled the jacket with rhinestones.	Normalize lemma. Modern decorative sense was popularized by the BeDazzler craft tool/brand but is now used generically as a verb. Figurative sense is older.		
82	811	865	beef up / beefing up	/ˈbifɪŋ ʌp/	phrasal verb (informal)	To make something stronger, more substantial, more effective, larger, or better equipped.	strengthen, reinforce, bolster, expand	The company is beefing up security. / We need to beef up the examples in this section. / They beefed up the staff before the holiday rush.	Normalize base form. Very productive with security, staff, defenses, résumé, argument, content. Informal but common.		
82	812	866	behoove / behove	/bɪˈhuvz/	verb (formal)	To be appropriate, advisable, necessary, or in someone's interest; most commonly in 'it behooves someone to do something.'	be advisable for, be wise for, be incumbent on (stronger/formal)	It behooves us to check the numbers before making a claim. / It would behoove you to keep a copy of the receipt.	Normalize from behooves. AmE behoove; BrE behove also occurs. Fixed impersonal construction is far more common than ordinary conjugated use.		
82	813	867	benevolence	/bəˈnɛvələns/	noun	The quality or disposition of wishing to do good and act kindly toward others; also charitable generosity or a benevolent act/policy in formal contexts.	kindness, goodwill, generosity, charity	He liked to present his philanthropy as an act of pure benevolence. / The ruler's supposed benevolence did not eliminate the power imbalance.	Formal. Benevolence can describe kind intention, but in discussions of paternalism/power it may be critically framed. Related benevolent.		
82	814	868	birthright	/ˈbɝθˌraɪt/	noun	A right, privilege, status, possession, or expectation regarded as belonging to someone by virtue of birth, ancestry, citizenship, or inherited tradition.	inherited right, hereditary privilege, entitlement by birth	He regarded access to the land as his birthright. / Citizenship by birth can confer rights that vary by country.	Can be literal/legal, cultural, rhetorical, or biblical. Avoid implying a claimed birthright is necessarily legally valid.		
82	815	869	blanket	/ˈblæŋkɪt/	noun; adjective; verb	noun: a large covering for warmth. adjective: applying broadly to all cases without distinction. verb: to cover something completely or extensively.	covering; comprehensive, across-the-board; cover	I grabbed an extra blanket. / The company imposed a blanket ban on personal devices. / Snow blanketed the city overnight.	Major polysemy. Productive adjective: blanket ban/rule/statement/coverage. Verb: blanket an area in snow/smoke/advertising.		
82	816	870	blatantly	/ˈbleɪtəntli/	adverb	In an obvious, conspicuous, and often shameless or offensive way, especially when violating a rule, truth, or expectation.	flagrantly, obviously, brazenly, shamelessly	The claim is blatantly false. / He was blatantly ignoring the rules.	Usually negative. Common: blatantly obvious/false/unfair/illegal. 'Openly' is more neutral and not always equivalent.		
82	817	871	blue blood / blue-blooded	/ˈbluːˌblʌd/	noun; adjective	blue blood: aristocratic or noble ancestry, or a person of such ancestry; blue-blooded: aristocratic by birth.	aristocrat, noble, patrician	She came from an old blue-blood family. / The novel mocks a circle of wealthy blue bloods.	Normalize morphology. Often historical, humorous, or critical. Plural noun 'blue bloods'. Hyphen usually in adjective blue-blooded.		
82	818	872	blunder	/ˈblʌndɚ/	noun; verb	A serious or embarrassing mistake caused by carelessness, poor judgment, or ignorance; as a verb, to make such a mistake or move clumsily.	mistake, error, gaffe, botch; stumble	The announcement was a major political blunder. / I blundered by sending the draft before checking it. / He blundered into the room in the dark.	Noun often stronger than ordinary mistake. Verb also has physical 'move clumsily' and 'blunder into' figurative accidental-entry senses.		
82	819	873	boilerplate / boilerplate approach	/ˈbɔɪlɚˌpleɪt əˈproʊtʃ/	noun; adjective	Standardized text, language, code, or a formulaic method reused with little change; a boilerplate approach applies a generic template rather than tailoring to the situation.	template, standard wording, stock language; cookie-cutter approach	The contract contains several pages of boilerplate. / A boilerplate approach won't work for every client. / The generated code included a lot of framework boilerplate.	Broaden beyond approach. Common in law, writing, software, business. Boilerplate is not necessarily bad; it becomes a criticism when generic reuse replaces needed customization.		
82	820	874	buff	/bʌf/	noun; adjective; verb	noun: an enthusiast or knowledgeable fan of a subject. adjective: muscular and physically fit; pale yellow-brown in color. verb: to polish; in games, to strengthen a character/item/ability.	enthusiast, aficionado; muscular, ripped; polish; strengthen (gaming)	He's a history buff. / The actor got buff for the role. / Buff the surface with a soft cloth. / The patch buffed several weaker characters.	Major polysemy and modern gaming sense. As noun, film/history/train buff is common. Contrast nerf in gaming = weaken.		
83	821	875	butt in / butt into	/bʌt ˈɪntu/	phrasal verb (informal)	To interrupt or interfere in a conversation, activity, or situation without being invited or welcomed.	interrupt, intrude, barge in, meddle	Sorry to butt in, but I think there's a mistake in that number. / He kept butting into conversations that didn't involve him.	Base expression butt in is more general; butt into + conversation/business also occurs. Often softened with 'Sorry to butt in, but...'		
83	822	876	carry out	/ˈkæri aʊt/	phrasal verb	To perform, conduct, execute, or complete a task, plan, order, experiment, investigation, threat, or procedure.	perform, conduct, execute, implement	Researchers carried out three experiments. / The order was carried out immediately. / He never carried out the threat.	Highly productive formal-neutral phrasal verb. Carry out a plan/task/order/survey/investigation/repair. Distinguish carryout/takeout food in some regional noun usage.		
83	823	877	cash and equity	/kæʃ ənd ˈɛkwəti/	noun phrase (finance/business)	Two different forms of value or compensation: cash is money available in liquid form; equity is an ownership interest in a company or asset whose value and liquidity can vary.	cash compensation and ownership stake, cash plus shares/stock (context-dependent)	The startup offered a compensation package of cash and equity. / The acquisition was financed with a mix of cash and equity.	Current definition overgeneralizes equity as merely a less-liquid financial asset. Equity can mean ownership/net value in multiple contexts. In compensation, it may involve shares, options, or other equity awards; details matter.		
83	824	878	caveat	/ˈkæviˌæt/	noun	A warning, qualification, limitation, or condition that should be kept in mind before accepting a statement, plan, or conclusion.	warning, qualification, proviso, limitation	The results look promising, with one important caveat: the sample was very small. / I'd recommend it, but there are a few caveats.	Very common analytical phrase: with the caveat that...; one caveat is... Not necessarily a deal-breaker—often simply a limitation/qualification.		
83	825	879	climactic	/klaɪˈmæktɪk/	adjective	Relating to or forming the climax—the most intense, important, or decisive point of a story, event, process, or sequence.	culminating, peak, decisive, dramatic	The final battle is the movie's climactic scene. / The negotiations reached a climactic moment late that night.	Key confusable: climactic = relating to climax; climatic = relating to climate. Not every 'important' event is climactic; it usually marks a culmination/peak.		
83	826	880	clubby	/ˈklʌbi/	adjective	Characterized by a close-knit, socially comfortable, insider-oriented atmosphere; often implying exclusivity, informality among members, or difficulty for outsiders to enter.	cliquish, insular, exclusive, chummy	The industry still has a clubby culture dominated by personal connections. / The small restaurant has a warm, clubby atmosphere.	Can be positive (cozy, sociable) or critical (exclusive/insular). Context determines tone.		
83	827	881	cohort	/ˈkoʊˌhɔrt/	noun	A group of people sharing a characteristic or experience, especially a group studied or tracked together; more broadly, a group/companion set. Historically, a Roman military unit.	group, class, generation, study group	The study followed a cohort of 2,000 adults for ten years. / This year's graduate cohort is unusually large.	Common in research, education, demographics, business. Avoid using 'cohort' as a fancy synonym for any random group when shared timing/characteristic is absent.		
83	828	882	come rain or shine / rain or shine	/kʌm reɪn ɔr ʃaɪn/	idiom	Regardless of weather or other difficulties; no matter what happens.	whatever happens, no matter what, without fail	I'll be there at six, rain or shine. / She goes for a walk every morning, come rain or shine.	Both forms common. Often emphasizes reliability/commitment. Literal weather origin but mostly figurative.		
83	829	883	compromise	/ˈkɑmprəˌmaɪz/	noun; verb	noun: an agreement in which sides make concessions; verb: to reach such an agreement. Also, to weaken, damage, endanger, or expose something such as security, integrity, health, or a position.	middle ground, settlement, concession; weaken, jeopardize, expose	We reached a compromise after hours of negotiation. / Both sides had to compromise. / Reusing passwords can compromise your security. / The leak compromised the investigation.	Critical polysemy: negotiated concession vs impair/endanger. 'Compromise one's principles' can mean abandon/weaken them. Do not teach only agreement sense.		
