84	833	888	cop out / cop-out	/kɑp aʊt/	phrasal verb; noun (informal)	verb: to avoid a responsibility, difficult choice, or commitment, often by making an excuse. noun: an avoidance/easy escape, or a person/answer regarded as evasive.	duck out, evade, shirk; excuse, easy way out	Don't cop out just because the conversation is uncomfortable. / Saying 'both sides are the same' felt like a cop-out.	Hyphen noun/adjective cop-out; verb cop out. Often implies cowardly/evasive avoidance, not merely changing one's mind.		
84	834	889	coveted	/ˈkʌvətɪd/	adjective	Strongly desired, sought after, or prized, especially because something is scarce, prestigious, valuable, or difficult to obtain.	sought-after, prized, desirable, prestigious	She won a coveted spot in the program. / The award is one of the industry's most coveted honors.	Usually before noun. Related covet (verb) can carry stronger/envying or biblical moral overtones; coveted itself is often neutral-positive.		
84	835	890	cruft	/krʌft/	noun (computing slang)	Unnecessary, outdated, redundant, awkward, or accumulated code, files, configuration, features, or system debris that makes software harder to maintain.	technical debt (overlap), legacy clutter, dead code, junk	The refactor removed years of cruft from the codebase. / Old configuration files had accumulated as deployment cruft.	Computing slang with flexible scope. Cruft overlaps with technical debt but is not identical: debt emphasizes future maintenance cost; cruft emphasizes accumulated unnecessary/messy residue.		
