84	838	893	div	/dɪv/	noun (UK slang, insulting)	A foolish, stupid, or incompetent person; a mild-to-moderate British insult whose force varies by region, age, and tone.	idiot, fool, prat, muppet (UK slang; nuance differs)	You absolute div—you left the keys inside. / He called himself a div for missing the obvious clue.	British slang. Can be playful among friends or genuinely insulting. Etymology claims are uncertain/varied, so avoid presenting a single folk origin as fact.		
84	839	894	drift	/drɪft/	verb; noun	To move slowly without firm control or a fixed course; to gradually change position, state, topic, relationship, or opinion; noun: such movement/change, or the general meaning/tendency of what someone says ('get the drift').	float, wander, stray; gradual shift, trend	The boat drifted away from shore. / We gradually drifted apart after college. / The conversation drifted toward politics. / I think I get your drift.	Major literal + figurative family. Productive: drift off (sleep/move away), drift apart, drift into, get the drift. Avoid overloading card with every phrasal extension but note them.		
