85	843	900	exalted	/ɪɡˈzɔltɪd/	adjective	Highly honored, elevated in rank/status, noble or lofty in character, or grand/elevated in tone; sometimes used ironically for an overly elevated status or opinion.	elevated, prestigious, lofty, illustrious	He rose to an exalted position in the organization. / The essay uses an exalted style that can feel overly grand. / Even people in exalted positions make ordinary mistakes.	Related exalt (verb) = praise highly/elevate. Can be sincerely reverential or ironic. Do not reduce to merely 'powerful'.		
85	844	901	flagrantly	/ˈfleɪɡrəntli/	adverb	In a glaringly obvious and seriously wrong, offensive, or unlawful way.	blatantly, brazenly, egregiously, shamelessly	The company was flagrantly violating the rules. / The referee ignored a flagrantly obvious foul.	Stronger and more condemnatory than merely openly/obviously. Common with violate, disregard, breach, unfair, illegal. Compare blatantly: both imply obviousness, but flagrantly strongly foregrounds offensiveness or serious wrongdoing.		
