85	849	907	get around	/ɡɛt əˈraʊnd/	phrasal verb	1. To travel or move from place to place. 2. To find a way around a rule, obstacle, or problem. 3. For news/information to become widely known. 4. To persuade or manage someone, in some contexts.	get about, travel; circumvent, bypass; spread	It's easy to get around the city by subway. / They found a way to get around the restriction. / Word soon got around that the store was closing.	Major polysemy. Also 'get around to doing something' = finally find time to do it, which is a separate productive extension worth recognizing.		
