87	861	920	in flux	/ɪn flʌks/	phrase	In a state of ongoing change, uncertainty, transition, or instability, with the final form or outcome not yet settled.	changing, unsettled, fluid, in transition	The schedule is still in flux, so don't book anything yet. / The industry has been in flux for years.	Very common with plans, rules, organizations, identity, markets, technology. Usually implies unsettled change rather than smooth predictable movement.		
87	862	921	in kind / in-kind	/ɪn kaɪnd/	adverbial phrase; adjective	In kind: in the same form or manner, or with goods/services rather than money depending on context. In-kind before a noun commonly means non-cash goods, services, or contributions.	non-cash, goods-and-services; in the same manner (other sense)	The charity received $20,000 in in-kind donations. / Instead of paying cash, the company contributed equipment in kind. / She returned the favor in kind.	Important form distinction: adjective 'in-kind donation/contribution'; adverbial 'paid/contributed in kind'. Also 'respond/repay in kind' = in the same manner, sometimes retaliation.		
87	863	923	inferior	/ɪnˈfɪriɚ/	adjective; noun	Lower in quality, rank, status, position, or performance; anatomically, situated below another structure. As a noun, a person of lower rank/status, now often formal or hierarchical.	substandard, lower-grade, lesser, subordinate	The cheaper material is clearly inferior to the original. / He hated being treated as an inferior. / In anatomy, inferior means lower or below.	Pattern: inferior to, not 'inferior than'. Technical anatomical opposite is superior. Social noun use can sound hierarchical or insulting.		
