87	865	925	iron out / ironed out	/ˈaɪɚnd aʊt/	phrasal verb	To resolve, remove, or smooth out difficulties, disagreements, inconsistencies, or practical details.	resolve, sort out, smooth out, work out	We still need to iron out a few details before signing. / They ironed out their differences over lunch.	Normalize base form. Very common: iron out problems/details/kinks/differences. Metaphor from smoothing wrinkles with an iron.		
87	866	926	judicious	/dʒuˈdɪʃəs/	adjective	Showing sound, careful judgment, especially in choosing, using, or deciding something in a measured way.	wise, prudent, sensible, discerning, well-judged	A judicious use of humor can make the presentation easier to follow. / She made a judicious choice to wait for more evidence.	Often means not merely 'wise' but appropriately selective/measured: judicious use/choice/mix. Compare judicial = relating to courts/judges.		
87	867	927	leeward	/ˈliːwɚd/	adjective; adverb; noun	On, toward, or being the side sheltered from or away from the direction the wind is coming from.	downwind, sheltered side	We anchored on the leeward side of the island. / The smoke drifted leeward.	Contrast windward = facing/toward the wind. Technical/navigation/geography vocabulary. Pronunciation and exact directional interpretation depend on wind reference, not compass direction.		
