93	929	994	uncharitable	/ʌnˈtʃærɪtəbəl/	adjective	1. Ungenerous or lacking kindness. 2. Unfairly harsh, unsympathetic, or inclined to interpret someone in the least favorable way.	unkind, ungenerous, harsh, unsympathetic	It would be uncharitable to assume he did it deliberately. / Her review was witty but unnecessarily uncharitable.	Very useful figurative pattern 'an uncharitable interpretation/reading'. Often about judgment rather than literal charity.		
93	930	995	unequivocal	/ˌʌnɪˈkwɪvəkəl/	adjective	Clear, definite, and leaving no room for doubt, ambiguity, or multiple interpretations.	unambiguous, explicit, categorical, definite	She gave an unequivocal no. / The evidence is not unequivocal enough to support that conclusion.	Common: unequivocal support/rejection/statement/evidence. Opposite equivocal = ambiguous/uncertain, not 'equal'.		
94	931	996	vet / vets	/vɛts/	noun; verb	noun: short for veterinarian or veteran, depending on context. verb: to examine, investigate, or evaluate someone/something carefully before approval or acceptance.	veterinarian, veteran; screen, check, investigate, evaluate	Ask the vet whether the dog needs a booster. / Several military vets attended the ceremony. / We need to vet the source before citing it.	Current card misses very common verb. Context disambiguates veterinarian vs veteran. Vetting is common in hiring, security, sourcing, politics.		
94	932	997	volition	/voʊˈlɪʃən/	noun	The power or act of making a conscious choice or decision; one's own will or deliberate choice.	will, choice, free will, intention	She left of her own volition. / He joined the project voluntarily, entirely of his own volition.	Formal. Extremely common fixed phrase 'of one's own volition'. Volition is the capacity/act of willing, not merely any decision outcome.		
94	933	998	BART	/bɑrt/	proper noun; acronym	Bay Area Rapid Transit, the rapid-transit rail system serving the San Francisco Bay Area. 'Bart' separately can be a personal name, but that is not the same lexical item.	Bay Area Rapid Transit	We took BART from the airport into San Francisco. / BART service was delayed during the evening commute.	FIX scope: uppercase BART is primarily the transit-system acronym in this card. Do not combine it with the unrelated given name Bart as though they were senses of one word.		
94	934	999	whereby	/wɛrˈbaɪ/	relative adverb / conjunction-like connector	By which, through which, or in accordance with which; used to introduce the method, arrangement, or mechanism by which something happens.	by which, through which, under which	They created a system whereby users can recover deleted files. / The parties reached an agreement whereby payment would be made in stages.	Formal. Very useful pattern 'a system/process/arrangement whereby...'. Often more natural in formal writing than conversation.		
94	935	1000	wiggle room	/ˈwɪɡəl ruːm/	noun (informal)	A small amount of flexibility, discretion, extra time, space, or freedom to adjust a decision, rule, schedule, price, or interpretation.	leeway, flexibility, latitude, breathing room, room to maneuver	Is there any wiggle room on the price? / The deadline gives us almost no wiggle room. / The wording leaves some wiggle room for interpretation.	Informal and highly productive. Compare WID847 room to maneuver (strategic flexibility), WID848 latitude (discretion), WID849 elbow room (space/freedom), WID850 slack (looseness/spare capacity).		
94	936	1001	withering	/ˈwɪðɚɪŋ/	adjective	Severely scornful, contemptuous, or humiliating; also, literally causing something to wither or lose vitality.	scathing, devastating, contemptuous, crushing, blistering	She gave him a withering look. / The proposal received withering criticism from reviewers.	High-value collocations: withering look/glance/criticism/attack. Figurative sense is much more common than literal in everyday prose. Compare scathing: harshly critical; withering often suggests making the target shrink or feel small.		
94	937	1002	woe / woes	/woʊz/	noun	Serious trouble, sorrow, distress, or misfortune; plural woes often means a person's or group's continuing problems.	trouble, sorrow, distress, hardship, misfortune	The company blamed its financial woes on falling demand. / Woe is me! is a deliberately dramatic or old-fashioned expression.	Normalize singular/plural. Modern everyday use strongly favors plural in compounds such as financial woes, legal woes, injury woes. Singular woe is literary or fixed-expression-heavy.		
94	938	1003	zap / zapped	/zæpt/	verb; adjective (informal)	To strike, destroy, remove, heat, transmit, or affect suddenly with energy or speed; zapped can also mean exhausted or depleted.	blast, hit, destroy, nuke (contextual); exhausted, drained	I zapped the leftovers in the microwave. / The bug zapper zapped another mosquito. / After that flight, I was completely zapped.	Broaden beyond destroy/kill. Informal and highly context-dependent: microwave something, erase/kill, transmit electronically, suddenly affect; 'I'm zapped' = exhausted.		
94	939	1004	clash	/klæʃ/	verb; noun	To come into conflict or disagreement; to be incompatible in color/style; or to collide with a sharp metallic sound. As a noun, a conflict, confrontation, or mismatch.	conflict, collide, disagree, conflict with, confrontation	The two groups clashed over funding. / That tie clashes with the shirt. / We heard the clash of metal from the hallway.	Major clusters: people/ideas clash over/on; colors/styles clash; objects clash together. Noun: clash between/with, personality clash.		
94	940	1005	crash	/kræʃ/	verb; noun	To collide violently; to fall or fail suddenly; for a computer/system to stop working; informally, to sleep somewhere or enter an event without invitation. A crash is such a collision, failure, or sudden decline.	collide, smash, fail, collapse; sleep over (informal)	The car crashed into a barrier. / My browser crashed again. / Stock prices crashed after the announcement. / Can I crash at your place tonight?	Very polysemous. Productive: crash into, system crashes, market crash, crash a party, crash at someone's place, crash course (separate compound).		
95	941	1006	crush	/krʌʃ/	verb; noun	To press or break by force; to defeat overwhelmingly; to suppress or overwhelm; noun: a strong romantic attraction, especially an early or informal one.	squash, compress, pulverize; defeat, overwhelm; infatuation	Crush the garlic before adding it. / Their team crushed us 8–0. / She had a crush on him in high school. / The workload was crushing me.	Major physical, competitive, emotional, and romantic clusters. 'Crushing' can mean devastating/overwhelming. A crush is not necessarily temporary, though it often implies informal or unreciprocated attraction.		
95	942	1007	preempt / pre-empt	/priˈɛmptɪŋ/	verb	To act before someone or something else in order to prevent, forestall, replace, or gain priority over it.	forestall, head off, anticipate, supersede	The company preempted criticism by publishing the data first. / The emergency broadcast preempted the scheduled program.	Normalize from pre-empting. AmE commonly preempt; BrE also pre-empt. Meanings include preventive action and replacing scheduled content. Related preemptive.		
