95	946	1011	indefeasible	/ˌɪndɪˈfiːzəbəl/	adjective (law/formal)	Not capable of being annulled, defeated, or made void under the relevant legal conditions; often used of rights, titles, or interests.	unalterable, irrevocable (contextual), not defeasible	The statute grants an indefeasible title once the registration requirements are met.	Technical legal term. Do not gloss as universally 'permanent': legal rights can still be subject to specific statutory exceptions or conditions. Opposite defeasible.		
95	947	1012	heir apparent	/ˌɛr əˈpɛrənt/	noun	An heir whose right to inherit a title or position cannot normally be displaced by the birth of another person with a superior claim; figuratively, a person widely expected to succeed a leader.	expected successor, designated successor (figurative overlap)	The prince is the heir apparent to the throne. / She is widely viewed as the CEO's heir apparent.	Important contrast: heir apparent vs heir presumptive, whose claim can be displaced by a later-born heir with a stronger right. Figurative use is common in politics/business.		
