106	1052	1125	sardonically	/sɑrˈdɑnɪkli/	adverb	In a grimly mocking, scornful, cynical, or bitterly humorous way.	mockingly, cynically, scornfully, dryly	'Well, that went beautifully,' she said sardonically after everything failed.	Sardonic humor is typically darker, more bitter, or contemptuous than ordinary sarcasm.		
106	1053	1126	carry over / be carried over	/ˈbiːɪŋ ˈkɛrid ˈoʊvɚ/	phrasal verb	To continue, transfer, or extend something from one period, place, context, account, or situation to another.	transfer, continue, roll over, extend	Unused vacation days can carry over to next year. / The tension from the meeting carried over into dinner.	Normalize active/passive family. Common in accounting, scheduling, sports, emotions, and effects.		
106	1054	1127	incidentally	/ˌɪnsɪˈdɛntəli/	adverb	Used to introduce a related or secondary point: 'by the way'; also, in a manner that is incidental rather than central.	by the way, in passing, parenthetically	Incidentally, I saw your brother yesterday. / The policy also incidentally reduced paperwork.	Usually not a synonym for accidentally. Related incidental = secondary or occurring as a minor consequence.		
106	1055	1128	break into	/ˈbreɪkɪŋ ˈɪntu/	phrasal verb	To enter forcibly or illegally; to enter a career/market/field; to suddenly begin an activity such as laughing, singing, or running.	force entry, enter; establish oneself; burst into	Someone broke into the office overnight. / She's trying to break into publishing. / He broke into a grin.	Major polysemy. Productive patterns break into a house, industry, song, laughter, sweat, run.		
106	1056	1129	deter	/dɪˈtɝ/	verb	To discourage or prevent someone from doing something by making it seem difficult, costly, risky, or undesirable.	discourage, dissuade, inhibit	The fine is intended to deter people from parking there. / Bad weather didn't deter us from going out.	Pattern deter someone from doing something. Fear can be involved but is not required.		
106	1057	1130	opt for	/ˈɑptɪd fɔr/	phrasal verb	To choose one option, course, or alternative rather than another.	choose, select, go with	We opted for the cheaper plan. / She opted for staying home instead of traveling.	Normalize from opted for. Related opt to do something; opt in/out are separate productive patterns.		
106	1058	1131	lay off someone / lay off	/leɪ ɔf ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb (informal)	To stop criticizing, bothering, pressuring, or attacking someone; separately, to dismiss employees because work is unavailable or costs are being reduced.	back off, ease up, stop bothering; dismiss, make redundant	Lay off him—he's trying his best. / The company laid off 200 workers.	Related-family with WID699. Keep both senses explicit; this card's source meaning is the 'stop bothering' sense.		
106	1059	1132	chow time	/tʃaʊ taɪm/	informal phrase	Mealtime; time to eat, especially in military, camp, or joking contexts.	mealtime, time to eat, grub time	All right, everyone—chow time! / The kids came running when I called chow time.	Casual/slangy and somewhat military/camp-flavored. Recognition/use card rather than a formal phrase.		
106	1060	1133	chow	/tʃaʊ/	noun; verb (informal)	Food, especially casual or hearty food; as a verb in 'chow down', to eat enthusiastically.	food, grub, eats	We grabbed some chow after the game. / Let's chow down before the movie starts.	Informal. 'Chow down' is the common verb phrase; plain transitive chow is less common.		
107	1061	1134	chug	/tʃʌɡ/	verb; noun	To drink quickly in large gulps; to move or operate with a repeated heavy sound; a chug is such a gulping action or rhythmic sound.	guzzle, gulp; throb, putter	He chugged the entire bottle of water. / The old train chugged up the hill.	Broaden beyond drinking. Also productive in gaming/party contexts: chug a drink.		
107	1062	1135	bring about / bring about misery	/brɪŋ əˈbaʊt ˈmɪzəri/	phrasal verb / phrase	To cause something to happen or exist; 'bring about misery' means cause suffering or unhappiness.	cause, produce, lead to, result in	The reforms brought about major changes. / The war brought about widespread misery.	Teach productive phrasal verb 'bring about' rather than treating the whole source phrase as a fixed expression.		
107	1063	1136	be through with someone/something	/ˈbiːɪŋ θru wɪð ju/	phrase (informal)	To be finished with, no longer willing to deal with, or no longer wanting involvement with someone or something.	be done with, have had enough of, finish with	I'm through with this argument. / After the betrayal, she told him, 'I'm through with you.'	Normalize away from 'being through with you'. Can refer to relationships, tasks, habits, or situations.		
107	1064	1137	ferret out	/frɛt aʊt/	phrasal verb	To discover, uncover, or find something hidden or difficult to locate through persistent searching or investigation.	dig out, uncover, track down, root out	The reporter ferreted out several important details from old records.	CORRECT likely error: current 'fret out' with the meaning 'work out/solve through persistent effort' is not standard. 'Ferret out' matches the intended meaning. 'Fret over/about' is a separate expression meaning worry.		
107	1065	1138	reel	/ril/	verb; noun	verb: to stagger, lose balance, or feel mentally shocked; to wind or draw in with a reel. noun: a spool/cylinder for winding line, film, cable, etc.	stagger, lurch; wind in; spool	He reeled backward from the blow. / I was still reeling from the news. / She reeled in the fish.	Major literal + figurative family. Common: reel from shock/news; reel in a fish; film reel.		
107	1066	1139	dog days	/dɔɡ deɪz/	plural noun / idiom	The hottest, sultriest period of summer; figuratively, a sluggish, stagnant, or difficult period.	hottest part of summer, summer doldrums; slump	The dog days of August made everyone lethargic. / The team is trying to escape the dog days of its season.	The original expression is tied to Sirius, the Dog Star. Figurative use can mean a languid or declining period, but context matters.		
