107	1069	1142	have it your way	/hæv ɪt jʊr weɪ/	idiom (informal)	Used to concede that someone may do or decide something as they wish, often with resignation or irritation.	suit yourself, fine, do what you want	I still think it's a bad idea, but have it your way.	Tone is important: it often sounds exasperated or reluctant rather than generous.		
107	1070	1143	skive / skive off	/skaɪv/	verb (BrE informal)	To avoid work, school, or a duty, especially by staying away, pretending to be ill, or doing something else instead.	shirk, bunk off, skip, play hooky	He skived off work to watch the match. / We used to skive school on Friday afternoons.	Normalize family. Chiefly British. Related to WID981 skive off; likely duplicate-family.		
