108	1075	1148	brethren	/ˈbrɛðrən/	plural noun	Fellow members of a religious, fraternal, professional, or other close-knit group; historically also an old plural of brother.	brothers, fellow members, comrades	The preacher addressed his brethren. / He called on his legal brethren to support the reform.	Formal, religious, fraternal, or old-fashioned. Not simply an archaic synonym for biological brothers.		
108	1076	1150	off the charts	/ɔf ðə tʃɑrt/	idiom (informal)	Extremely high, strong, successful, intense, or exceptional—beyond the scale normally measured or expected.	extreme, extraordinary, exceptional, through the roof	Demand is off the charts. / Her energy level was off the charts that day.	NORMALIZE: plural 'off the charts' is the standard idiom. Singular 'off the chart' can occur literally or in less fixed usage.		
108	1077	1151	zeitgeist	/ˈzaɪtˌɡaɪst/	noun	The characteristic spirit, mood, ideas, attitudes, and cultural climate of a particular period.	spirit of the age, cultural mood, ethos	The film captured the zeitgeist of the early 2000s. / The product fit the political zeitgeist perfectly.	German loanword literally 'spirit of the time'. Often used for broad cultural/intellectual atmosphere, not just a trend.		
108	1078	1152	chortle	/ˈtʃɔrtəl/	verb; noun	To laugh or chuckle with gleeful, pleased, or sometimes smug amusement; a chortle is such a laugh.	chuckle, laugh, snicker, guffaw (stronger)	He chortled at his own joke. / She gave a satisfied chortle.	Coined by Lewis Carroll from chuckle + snort. Often playful/literary.		
108	1079	1153	elementary	/ˌɛləˈmɛntəri/	adjective	Basic, fundamental, or introductory; relating to elementary school; in technical contexts, relating to basic indivisible components such as elementary particles.	basic, fundamental, rudimentary, introductory	The mistake was elementary. / She teaches elementary school. / The course introduces elementary statistics.	Broaden beyond 'early learning'. Famous phrase 'Elementary, my dear Watson' is culturally associated with Sherlock Holmes, though not used in exactly that form in Conan Doyle's original stories.		
108	1080	1154	granted	/ˈɡræntɪd/	adverb / conjunction-like concession marker	Used to acknowledge that a point is true before introducing a qualification, contrast, or counterargument; 'granted that...' means assuming/admitting that something is true.	admittedly, to be sure, even accepting that	Granted, the plan is risky, but it may still be our best option. / Granted that he's talented, he still lacks discipline.	Already a strong card. Core rhetorical function is concession. Do not treat 'nevertheless' as a direct synonym; nevertheless typically introduces the counterpoint rather than the conceded point.		
109	1081	1155	spread the gospel / spreading the gospel	/ˈsprɛdɪŋ ðə ˈɡɑspəl/	phrase	Literally, to preach or share the Christian gospel; figuratively, to enthusiastically promote or advocate an idea, method, product, or cause.	evangelize, preach; promote enthusiastically	He spent years spreading the gospel abroad. / She travels widely spreading the gospel of open-source software.	Add figurative extension. In literal religious use, gospel is often capitalized by style when referring to the Christian message or specific Gospel books.		
