109	1090	1164	frugal	/ˈfruɡəl/	adjective	Careful and economical in spending or using resources, avoiding unnecessary waste.	thrifty, economical, prudent, sparing	She's frugal with money but still spends on things she values.	Usually neutral or positive. Contrast cheap/stingy, which criticize unwillingness to spend appropriately.		
110	1091	1165	spurt	/spɝt/	noun; verb	A sudden short burst of liquid, speed, activity, growth, effort, or output; to gush or increase suddenly for a short time.	burst, gush, surge	Water spurted from the pipe. / The runner put on a final spurt. / The company had a brief growth spurt.	Literal liquid + figurative short burst. Related growth spurt.		
110	1092	1166	have/hit a growth spurt	/hɪt jʊr ɡroʊθ spɝt/	phrase	To experience a relatively sudden period of rapid physical growth, especially in childhood or adolescence; figuratively, rapid expansion can be called a growth spurt.	grow rapidly, shoot up, expand quickly	He hit a growth spurt at fourteen and grew several inches. / The startup went through a growth spurt last year.	Both 'have a growth spurt' and 'hit a growth spurt' occur; 'have/go through' are broadly natural.		
