110	1095	1169	catatonic	/ˌkætəˈtɑnɪk/	adjective (medical; also figurative)	Relating to catatonia, a serious psychomotor syndrome that can involve reduced movement/responsiveness, rigidity, agitation, mutism, or other symptoms; figuratively, extremely unresponsive or inert.	unresponsive, immobile (partial/contextual)	The patient showed catatonic symptoms and required medical assessment. / Figuratively: I was practically catatonic after the overnight flight.	CORRECT medical oversimplification. Catatonia is not simply 'lack of movement due to mental illness or shock'; it can occur with psychiatric, neurologic, medical, or drug-related conditions and has multiple possible presentations.		
