110	1099	1174	exec / execs	/ɪɡˈzɛks/	noun (informal)	Short for executive: a person in a senior managerial or decision-making role in an organization.	executive, senior manager, higher-up	Several company execs attended the meeting.	Informal abbreviation common in business/media. Exact seniority depends on context.		
110	1100	1175	cut someone some slack	/kʌt ˈsʌmwʌn səm slæk/	idiom (informal)	To be less strict, critical, or demanding with someone; to allow extra tolerance because of circumstances.	go easy on, give someone a break, be lenient	Cut her some slack—she just started the job.	Related-family with WID850 slack. This card teaches the fixed idiom, so KEEP independently if retrieval value is useful.		
111	1101	1176	AFK	/ˌeɪ ɛf ˈkeɪ/	abbreviation / internet-gaming slang	Away from keyboard; temporarily not available or not actively present online, even when literally using a phone rather than a keyboard.	away, temporarily unavailable, inactive	I'm AFK for ten minutes—back soon. / Sorry, I was AFK when you messaged me.	Originally desktop/gaming slang; now used more broadly in online communication. Usually written uppercase, though lowercase afk is common informally.		
111	1102	1177	accrue	/əˈkru/	verb	To accumulate, increase, or be earned gradually over time; also, for a right, benefit, cost, or obligation to arise or become due.	accumulate, build up, amass, earn	Interest accrues daily. / Employees accrue vacation time each month. / Additional costs may accrue if payment is late.	Common in finance, accounting, employment, and law. Can be intransitive ('interest accrues') or transitive ('accrue benefits').		
111	1103	1178	conniving	/kəˈnaɪvɪŋ/	adjective; present participle	Secretly scheming, plotting, or manipulating in order to gain advantage, often dishonestly.	scheming, calculating, manipulative, devious	The story's conniving adviser manipulates everyone around him.	Strongly negative. Related connive = secretly cooperate in wrongdoing or scheme; exact verb sense varies by context.		
111	1104	1179	doting	/ˈdoʊtɪŋ/	adjective	Extremely loving, attentive, and affectionate, especially toward a child, partner, or family member.	adoring, devoted, affectionate, indulgent	He's a doting grandfather who never misses a birthday.	Usually warm/positive; it can imply overindulgence, but excessive affection is not required.		
111	1105	1180	ramp	/ræmp/	noun; verb	noun: a sloped surface connecting different levels. verb: to increase or decrease something, especially in 'ramp up/down'; also to move via a ramp.	incline, slope; increase, scale up, intensify	They installed an access ramp. / The company is ramping up production. / We'll ramp spending down next quarter.	Major noun + phrasal-verb family. 'Ramp up' is far more common than plain transitive ramp in figurative business use.		
111	1106	1181	ram	/ræm/	noun; verb	noun: an adult male sheep; a heavy device for striking; verb: to hit, force, or drive something violently into/through something.	batter, crash into, force; male sheep	The truck rammed the gate. / He rammed the box into the trunk. / A ram stood beside the flock.	FIX POS and priorities. Vulgar sexual use exists but is not a core everyday sense and should be treated as explicit slang if retained. Also distinguish RAM (computer memory acronym), a separate lexical item.		
111	1107	1182	EWO / EWO-ing (GTA Online)	/ˈjuːɪŋz/	gaming slang / abbreviation	In GTA Online slang, EWO refers to using 'Easy Way Out'—historically an in-game self-kill option—or otherwise deliberately killing one's own character to reset or deny an opponent a kill/advantage.	Easy Way Out, self-kill/reset tactic (game-specific)	Players accused him of EWO-ing to avoid giving the opponent the kill.	CORRECT corrupted current definition. 'Exit Without Option' is not the intended expansion. This is game-specific slang whose exact mechanics/availability can vary by game version and community usage.		
