111	1109	1184	yours truly	/jʊərz ˈtruːli/	phrase	1. A conventional closing in correspondence. 2. Informally or humorously, a way to refer to oneself: 'me.'	sincerely (closing only); me, myself (self-reference)	Yours truly, Alex Morgan. / And who had to clean up the mess? Yours truly.	Current synonyms mix two different senses. The self-reference use is often playful, ironic, or mildly self-important.		
111	1110	1185	deprecating / self-deprecating	/ˈdɛprɪˌkeɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Deprecating = expressing disapproval or belittling something; self-deprecating = modestly or humorously criticizing oneself.	disparaging, critical; self-mocking, self-effacing	He made a self-deprecating joke about his cooking. / The reviewer used a deprecating tone toward the proposal.	Important distinction: 'deprecating' alone does not mean humble/self-critical. In everyday English, 'self-deprecating' is the common phrase for humor directed at oneself.		
112	1111	1186	facetious	/fəˈsiʃəs/	adjective	Treating a serious subject with deliberately flippant, joking, or inappropriate humor; not meant literally or seriously.	flippant, joking, tongue-in-cheek, unserious	I was being facetious—I don't actually think we should cancel the whole project.	Often misunderstood as simply funny. Facetiousness can be inappropriate because the subject is serious.		
112	1112	1187	full house	/fʊl haʊs/	noun phrase	1. A venue filled to capacity. 2. In poker, a hand containing three cards of one rank and two of another. 3. Informally, a home/room with all expected people present.	sellout crowd; poker hand	The theater played to a full house. / She won the pot with a full house.	Add major poker sense. 'A full house' is countable; 'the concert was a full house' is natural, though 'played to a full house' is especially idiomatic.		
112	1113	1188	quibble	/ˈkwɪbəl/	verb; noun	To argue or object over a minor detail; a quibble is a small objection or criticism.	nitpick, haggle, carp; minor objection	We can quibble over the exact wording later. / I have one small quibble with the proposal.	Common pattern quibble over/about. Less hostile than bicker; often intellectual/detail-focused.		
112	1114	1189	co-opt / co-opted	/ˈkoʊˈɑptɪd/	verb	To bring someone into a group or system, often to neutralize opposition or gain support; to appropriate an idea, movement, symbol, or practice for a different purpose; formally, to appoint someone into a group.	appropriate, absorb, enlist, incorporate, commandeer	The movement's language was co-opted by advertisers. / The committee co-opted two additional members.	Current 'take over' definition is too narrow. Often the original group still exists; the issue is absorption, appropriation, or neutralization.		
