112	1116	1191	indiscriminate	/ˌɪndɪsˈkrɪmɪnət/	adjective	Done without careful selection, distinction, or discrimination; affecting targets broadly rather than selectively.	random, sweeping, unselective, haphazard	The indiscriminate use of antibiotics can create problems. / The attack was condemned as indiscriminate.	Not always literally random; key idea is lack of appropriate distinction/selectivity.		
112	1117	1192	dish out	/dɪʃ aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal)	To distribute or serve something; figuratively, to give out criticism, punishment, abuse, praise, advice, etc., often freely or forcefully.	serve, hand out, dole out, mete out	She dished out soup to everyone. / The coach dished out harsh criticism after the loss.	Literal food-serving origin + figurative distribution. Common phrase 'dish it out but can't take it' = criticize others but cannot handle criticism.		
112	1118	1193	dispense	/dɪsˈpɛns/	verb	To distribute, provide, or issue something in measured/organized fashion; to give advice or justice; in 'dispense with', to do without or get rid of.	distribute, issue, provide; do without (dispense with)	The machine dispenses tickets. / The pharmacist dispensed the medication. / We can dispense with the formalities.	Important separate pattern dispense with. Formal-neutral, common in medicine, machines, law, and institutional contexts.		
112	1119	1195	unrequited	/ˌʌnrɪˈkwaɪtɪd/	adjective	Not returned, reciprocated, or answered, especially of love or affection.	unreturned, unreciprocated, one-sided	He wrote poems about unrequited love. / Her affection remained unrequited.	Most strongly associated with love/affection. Can occasionally modify other feelings or efforts, but not broadly interchangeable with 'unanswered'.		
112	1120	1196	unrequited love	/ˌʌnrɪˈkwaɪtɪd lʌv/	noun phrase	Romantic love or deep affection that is not returned by the person loved.	one-sided love, unreturned love	She spent years trying to move on from unrequited love.	Useful phrase card independent from adjective if retrieval value is desired. Compare WID1195 lexical adjective.		
113	1121	1197	pester	/ˈpɛstər/	verb	To annoy, bother, or pressure someone repeatedly, especially with repeated questions, requests, or interruptions.	bother, nag, badger, hassle	Stop pestering me with questions. / The kids pestered him for another story.	Duplicate-family with WID1037, where the lemma has already been normalized/enriched. Recommend later consolidation unless source/retrieval value justifies both.		
113	1122	1198	flustered	/ˈflʌstərd/	adjective	Agitated, confused, embarrassed, or nervous because of pressure, surprise, or too much happening at once.	rattled, agitated, confused, nervous	She got flustered when the microphone stopped working. / He looked visibly flustered by the rapid questions.	FIX grammar: adjective meaning, not 'to be'. Related fluster verb = make someone agitated/confused.		
113	1123	1199	lackey	/ˈlækɪ/	noun	A servant, subordinate, or follower who is regarded as excessively obedient, servile, or willing to do another person's bidding.	minion, henchman, toady, underling	The dictator surrounded himself with obedient lackeys.	Strongly disparaging. Not a neutral synonym for employee/assistant.		
113	1124	1200	gofer	/ˈɡoʊfɚ/	noun (informal)	A junior worker or assistant whose job involves running errands and doing routine or menial tasks for others.	errand runner, junior assistant, runner	He started as a gofer, fetching coffee and delivering scripts.	From 'go for' this/go for that. Informal and sometimes belittling. Avoid gendered 'errand boy' as the primary synonym.		
113	1125	1201	menial	/ˈmiːniəl/	adjective	Relating to low-status, routine, repetitive, or undemanding work, often involving little autonomy and traditionally regarded as requiring little specialized skill.	lowly, routine, mundane, unskilled, tedious	He was stuck doing menial tasks like filing papers and cleaning. / I don't mind menial work if it frees everyone else to focus on the difficult part.	Often carries a dismissive/status judgment about the work, not merely 'boring'. Common: menial task/job/work.		
113	1126	1202	boorish menial	/ˈbʊrɪʃ ˈmiːniəl/	noun phrase (source-dependent / literary)	A rude, uncouth, or insensitive low-status servant or worker.	rude servant, uncouth underling, ill-mannered subordinate	The character dismisses him as a 'boorish menial,' combining contempt for his manners with contempt for his status.	Not a standard fixed expression. Analyze compositionally: boorish = rude/uncouth; menial as a noun = a servant or low-status worker, now dated/disparaging.		
113	1127	1203	look in on someone	/lʊk ɪn ɑn/	phrasal verb	To make a short visit to someone, especially to check how they are doing or whether they need anything.	check on, drop in on, call in on, stop by to see	I'll look in on Grandma after work to see how she's doing. / Could you look in on the cat while I'm away?	Usually implies a brief welfare/checking visit. Compare look into = investigate; look in = make a brief visit, often followed by on + person.		
113	1128	1204	fine feathers make fine birds	/faɪn ˈfɛðɚz meɪk faɪn bɝdz/	proverb	Attractive or expensive clothes and outward appearance can make a person seem more impressive, refined, or important.	clothes make the man, appearances influence impressions	He dressed impeccably for the interview—fine feathers make fine birds, as the saying goes.	Traditional proverb, often used wryly. It comments on the power of appearance; depending on context it can endorse dressing well or criticize superficial judgment.		
113	1129	1205	chad	/tʃæd/	noun; slang noun	1. A small piece of paper/card punched out to make a hole, famously in punched-card or ballot contexts. 2. Internet slang: a stereotypically confident, attractive, socially or sexually successful man.	paper punch-out; stereotypical alpha male, attractive confident guy	The recount focused on hanging chads on punch-card ballots. / In the meme, the 'Chad' character is exaggeratedly confident.	The slang sense is meme/internet culture and can be admiring, mocking, or ironic. 'Hanging chad' became especially associated with the 2000 U.S. election.		
