119	1185	1265	amanuensis	/əˌmænjuˈɛnsɪs/	noun (formal/literary)	A person who writes from another's dictation, copies manuscripts, or serves as a literary/secretarial assistant.	scribe, copyist, secretary, literary assistant	The author dictated portions of the memoir to his amanuensis.	Rare/formal. Historically broader than a modern secretary; often encountered in literary or historical contexts.		
119	1186	1266	cantankerous	/kænˈtæŋkərəs/	adjective	bad-tempered, argumentative, and uncooperative	irritable, grumpy, quarrelsome	The cantankerous old man yelled at kids for walking on his lawn.			
119	1187	1267	fractious	/ˈfrækʃəs/	adjective	Irritable, quarrelsome, unruly, or difficult to manage; also used of groups/situations marked by internal conflict.	unruly, irritable, quarrelsome, restive, troublesome	The fractious toddler refused to settle down. / The leader struggled to unite a fractious coalition.	Broaden beyond individuals. Common with children, animals, political parties, coalitions, meetings.		
119	1188	1268	impertinence	/ɪmˈpɝːtənəns/	noun	rude or disrespectful behavior; lack of respect	insolence, rudeness, audacity	The teacher was shocked by the student's impertinence.			
119	1189	1269	occlusion	/əˈkluːʒən/	noun	A blockage, closing, or obstruction of a passage; in dentistry, the contact/alignment relationship between upper and lower teeth; in meteorology, an occluded front/process.	blockage, obstruction, closure; bite relationship	The scan showed an arterial occlusion. / The dentist checked her occlusion.	Major technical polysemy. Medical occlusion and dental occlusion are especially common; exact meaning depends strongly on field.		
119	1190	1270	petulant	/ˈpɛtʃələnt/	adjective	childishly sulky or bad-tempered	irritable, moody, whiny	She became petulant when her requests weren't immediately granted.			
120	1191	1271	get on with it	/ɡɛt ɑn wɪð ɪt/	idiom / phrasal expression	To start, resume, or continue doing something without further delay; often used impatiently to tell someone to proceed.	get going, proceed, carry on, get to it	Stop stalling and get on with it. / After the interruption, we got on with the meeting.	BrE especially common, but widely understood. Tone ranges from practical to impatient.		
120	1192	1272	eh, lass / ey up, lass (regional)	/eɪ læs/	regional expression / address	A dialectal or regional way of getting a girl's/woman's attention or addressing her; exact form and meaning depend heavily on region and source.	hey, lass; hello, girl (context-dependent)	'Eh, lass, you dropped your scarf!'	SOURCE/REGIONAL CAUTION: 'lass' is well established in northern England/Scotland and elsewhere, but 'eh lass' should not be presented as one standardized Northern UK formula. If the source actually says 'ey up, lass', that is a different regional greeting.		
120	1193	1273	wintry	/ˈwɪntɚi/	adjective	relating to or characteristic of winter; cold or bleak	cold, frosty, bleak	The wintry wind cut through his jacket like a knife.			
120	1194	1274	make of someone/something	/meɪk ʌv ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrase	To interpret, understand, or form an opinion about someone or something, especially when it is puzzling or ambiguous.	think of, interpret, understand, judge	I don't know what to make of him. / What do you make of the ending?	Broaden to someone/something. Very common frame: 'What do you make of X?' = What is your interpretation/opinion?		
120	1195	1275	flex	/flɛks/	verb; noun; slang verb/noun	Literal: to bend/tighten a muscle or joint. Slang: to show off or conspicuously display status, possessions, ability, or success; noun slang can mean an act/thing used to show off.	show off, flaunt, brag; bend, tense	He flexed his arm for the photo. / Posting the first-class ticket was a deliberate flex.	Broaden beyond slang. 'Weird flex, but OK' is a well-known internet formula reacting to an odd boast/display.		
120	1196	1276	beguilement	/bɪˈɡaɪlmənt/	noun	the act of charming or enchanting someone, often in a deceptive way	enchantment, seduction, deception	Her beguilement of the crowd was effortless and mesmerizing.			
120	1197	1277	strand / be stranded	/strænd/	verb; noun	verb: to leave someone or something stuck, isolated, or unable to continue; noun: a single thread/fiber, a shore, or a component/thread of a larger whole.	maroon, leave stuck; thread, fiber, shore	The storm stranded hundreds of travelers. / A strand of hair fell across her face. / The course has several thematic strands.	Major polysemy. Current card covers only the verb; noun senses are common and useful.		
120	1198	1278	menacing	/ˈmɛnəsɪŋ/	adjective	suggesting the presence of danger or threat	threatening, intimidating, ominous	The dog gave a low, menacing growl as they approached the gate.			
120	1199	1279	steadfast	/ˈstɛdfæst/	adjective	firmly loyal or determined; not changing or quitting	unwavering, loyal, resolute	Her steadfast belief in justice never wavered.			
120	1200	1280	jumpy	/ˈdʒʌmpi/	adjective	nervous or easily startled	anxious, edgy, skittish	He was so jumpy after the movie that he flinched at every noise.			
121	1201	1281	slippery customer	/ˈslɪpəri ˈkʌstəmɚ/	noun phrase (informal, chiefly BrE)	A person who is difficult to pin down, catch, understand, trust, or deal with, often because they are evasive or cunning.	evasive person, tricky character, dodgy customer	He's a slippery customer—you'll have trouble getting a straight answer out of him.	'Customer' here means person/character, not necessarily a buyer. Informal and especially British.		
121	1202	1282	a whole heap of something	/hoʊl hip əv ˈʌðɚ ˈɑpʃənz/	informal phrase	A large amount or number of something; 'a whole heap of other options' means many other choices.	a lot of, loads of, tons of, a whole bunch of	We've got a whole heap of other options. / That created a whole heap of problems.	Teach productive frame rather than freezing the source sentence. Very informal.		
121	1203	1283	I'm game	/aɪm ɡeɪm/	idiom (informal)	I'm willing, interested, or ready to participate in a suggested activity, including something challenging or adventurous.	I'm in, count me in, I'm up for it, I'm down	'Want to try bungee jumping?' 'Sure, I'm game.'	Game as adjective = willing/ready. Related 'game for something' and 'game to do something'.		
121	1204	1284	not by a long shot	/nɑt baɪ ə lɔŋ ʃɑt/	idiom	Not at all; not even close; by no means.	not even close, by no means, absolutely not	Is the job finished? Not by a long shot. / He's not the best player—not by a long shot.	Often used as a standalone emphatic reply. Contrast WID1285 positive 'by a long shot'.		
121	1205	1285	by a long shot	/baɪ ə lɔŋ ʃɑt/	idiom	By a large margin or to a considerable degree, usually in comparisons; much less commonly, 'at all' in certain negative constructions.	by far, easily, by a wide margin	She was the best candidate by a long shot.	Most natural in comparisons. The negative idiom 'not by a long shot' is especially common and semantically stronger.		
121	1206	1286	pushover	/ˈpʊʃˌoʊvɚ/	noun (informal)	A person who is easily persuaded, dominated, or taken advantage of; also an opponent, task, or challenge that is very easy to defeat or accomplish.	easy mark, soft touch, doormat; easy win, piece of cake	She's kind, but she's no pushover. / Everyone expected the match to be a pushover, but it wasn't.	Broaden beyond person. 'No pushover' is a common positive formulation meaning not easily defeated or manipulated.		
121	1207	1287	hereafter	/ˌhɪrˈæftɚ/	adverb; noun (formal)	From this point onward, especially in formal/legal writing; as a noun with 'the', the life or existence after death.	henceforth, from now on; afterlife	Hereafter, all requests must be submitted in writing. / Some traditions describe rewards in the hereafter.	Separate grammatical senses: adverb = from now on; noun 'the hereafter' = afterlife. Formal/old-fashioned outside legal/religious contexts.		
121	1208	1288	pull one's weight	/pʊl wʌnz weɪt/	idiom	to do one's fair share of work or duties	contribute, carry your load, do your part	Everyone on the team has to pull their weight to succeed.			
121	1209	1289	have at someone/something	/hæv æt ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrase (informal)	To attack, criticize, tackle, or energetically begin dealing with someone or something; 'Have at it!' can mean go ahead and start/try.	go at, attack, tackle; go ahead	The two critics had at each other for an hour. / 'Can I try fixing it?' 'Sure—have at it.'	Current example 'had at the guy' is awkward. High-value expression is 'Have at it!' and reciprocal 'have at each other'.		
121	1210	1290	ward off	/tə wɔrd ɔf/	phrasal verb	To prevent, repel, or keep away something harmful or unwanted, such as danger, illness, an attack, hunger, or criticism.	fend off, stave off, repel, keep at bay	She took a walk to ward off drowsiness. / Vaccination can help ward off certain infections.	Normalize without infinitive 'to'. Garlic-and-mosquito example is distracting; use realistic collocations. Compare stave off = delay/prevent temporarily.		
122	1211	1291	auricle	/ˈɔrɪkəl/	noun (anatomy)	1. The visible external part of the ear (pinna). 2. An ear-like appendage of an atrium of the heart; historically, 'auricle' was also used for an atrium.	pinna, external ear; atrial appendage	The auricle helps collect sound and direct it toward the ear canal.	CORRECT anatomy precision: in modern cardiac anatomy, auricle usually means the ear-shaped appendage of an atrium, not simply 'an upper chamber of the heart'.		
122	1212	1292	oracle	/ˈɔrəkəl/	noun	A person, shrine, or medium through which divine prophecy/advice is believed to be given; the prophecy itself; figuratively, a person or source treated as highly authoritative.	seer, prophet, prophetic source, authority	Ancient Greeks consulted the oracle at Delphi. / Everyone treated him as an oracle on the subject.	Broaden beyond 'person or medium'. Computing/database proper-name uses are separate and should not be conflated with the common noun.		
122	1213	1293	pilfer	/ˈpɪlfɚ/	verb	To steal, especially small amounts or relatively low-value items, often repeatedly or opportunistically.	steal, filch, swipe, pinch	Someone had been pilfering supplies from the storeroom. / Tourists kept pilfering towels from the hotel.	Often suggests petty theft, but the stolen thing need not literally be worthless.		
122	1214	1294	pulverize / pulverise	/ˈpʌlvəˌraɪz/	verb	To crush or grind into very fine particles; figuratively, to defeat, destroy, or criticize overwhelmingly.	crush, grind, powder; demolish, annihilate	The machine pulverized the concrete into dust. / Their team pulverized us in the second half.	Literal + emphatic figurative sense. AmE pulverize; BrE also pulverise.		
122	1215	1295	bite the dust	/baɪt ðə dʌst/	idiom (informal)	To die; to fail, break down, be defeated, or come to an end.	die, perish; fail, collapse, go under	Another business idea bit the dust. / My old laptop finally bit the dust.	Often humorous/light when used of objects, plans, or companies; can sound flippant if used about a real person's death.		
122	1216	1296	music to one's ears	/ˈmjuzɪk tə wʌnz ɪrz/	idiom	something that is very pleasant or gratifying to hear	delightful news, pleasing sound	Her praise was music to his ears after a long day.			
122	1217	1297	upstage	/ˌʌpˈsteɪdʒ ˈsʌmwʌn/	verb	To draw attention away from someone else and make them seem less impressive; in theater, literally to move upstage in a way that can force another actor to turn away from the audience.	outshine, overshadow, steal the spotlight	The supporting actor upstaged the lead. / The surprise announcement upstaged the product launch.	Normalize lemma. Modern figurative sense is much more common outside theater.		
122	1218	1298	talk someone out of something / doing something	/tɔk ˈsʌmwʌn aʊt ʌv ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	phrasal verb	To persuade someone not to do something, not to choose something, or to abandon an intended action.	dissuade, persuade against, convince not to	She talked him out of quitting his job. / Can I talk you out of driving in this weather?	Productive patterns: talk someone out of doing something; talk someone out of a decision/purchase. Opposite: talk someone into something.		
122	1219	1299	perturbed	/pɚˈtɝbd/	adjective	Worried, unsettled, disturbed, or agitated; in technical/scientific contexts, altered from a normal state by an external influence.	troubled, unsettled, disturbed, agitated	He seemed perturbed by the sudden change of plans. / The object followed a slightly perturbed orbit.	Everyday sense is moderately formal. Technical sense appears in physics/mathematics/astronomy via perturb/perturbation.		
122	1220	1300	altar	/ˈɔltɚ/	noun	A raised table, platform, or structure used in religious worship, sacrifice, offerings, or ritual; also the ceremonial focal point in many weddings.	sacred table, shrine (not exact), ritual structure	The couple stood before the altar to exchange vows. / Offerings were placed on the temple altar.	Do not use 'sanctuary' as a direct synonym: a sanctuary is a sacred place/area, not the altar itself. Common confusable: altar vs alter.		
