123	1225	1305	evasive	/ɪˈveɪsɪv/	adjective	Avoiding a direct answer, commitment, capture, or clear statement; deliberately or habitually tending to evade.	noncommittal, indirect, elusive, cagey, slippery	He gave an evasive answer when asked where the money had gone. / The suspect proved evasive and difficult to track.	For answers/people, often implies avoidance rather than simple vagueness. Distinguish elusive = difficult to find/catch/define; evasive = actively avoiding.		
123	1226	1306	sleeper / sleeper hit	/ðə ˈsliːpɚ/	noun / noun phrase	A person, product, work, team, or other thing that attracts little attention at first but later proves unexpectedly successful, effective, or important; 'sleeper hit' is especially common for entertainment/products.	dark horse, surprise success, sleeper hit	The film became a sleeper hit through word of mouth. / That inexpensive player was the sleeper of the draft.	'Sleeper' has many unrelated senses (a person who sleeps, a railroad tie in BrE, sleeping car, etc.). This card targets the 'unexpected success' sense.		
123	1227	1307	snowflake	/ˈsnoʊˌfleɪk/	noun; slang noun (derogatory)	1. A single crystal/flakelike cluster of snow. 2. Slang insult for a person portrayed as overly sensitive, fragile, self-important, or easily offended.	flake of snow; oversensitive person, fragile person (slang)	No two snowflakes are exactly alike. / He dismissed the critics as 'snowflakes.'	The slang sense is pejorative and politically/culturally charged. It is often used to mock sensitivity rather than neutrally describe it.		
123	1228	1308	convalescence	/ˌkɑːnvəˈlɛsəns/	noun	A period or process of gradually recovering health and strength after illness, injury, or medical treatment.	recovery, recuperation, recovery period	She spent several weeks in convalescence after surgery.	More formal than recovery/recuperation. 'Rehabilitation' can overlap but often refers specifically to therapy or retraining, so it is not always a direct synonym.		
123	1229	1309	put someone over one's knee	/pʊt ˈsʌmwʌn ˈoʊvɚ wʌnz niː/	idiomatic expression	To place someone across one's knee to spank them; figuratively or jokingly, to threaten old-fashioned physical discipline.	spank, discipline, punish	The line 'I'll put you over my knee' is an old-fashioned threat of spanking.	Often old-fashioned, comic, or threatening; can sound inappropriate or infantilizing when used toward an adult. Meaning depends strongly on context.		
123	1230	1310	chivalry	/ˈʃɪvəlri/	noun	1. The medieval ideals, customs, and conduct associated with knights. 2. Courteous, protective, or gallant behavior, especially a traditional style of conduct by men toward women.	gallantry, courtesy, knightly code	The story romanticizes medieval chivalry. / He offered his seat out of old-fashioned chivalry.	Modern use can be approving, ironic, or critical, especially when the behavior is strongly gendered or paternalistic.		
124	1231	1311	conjurer / conjuror	/ˈkʌndʒɚɚ/	noun	A performer who does magic tricks or illusions; in older, fantasy, or occult contexts, a person said to summon spirits or supernatural forces.	magician, illusionist; sorcerer (context-dependent)	The conjurer amazed the audience with sleight of hand. / In the fantasy novel, the conjurer summons spirits.	'Conjurer' is the more common modern spelling; 'conjuror' is also used. The stage-magician sense and supernatural/fantasy sense should be separated by context.		
