124	1233	1313	be / find oneself at a loose end	/faɪnd wʌnˈsɛlf æt ə luːs ɛnd/	idiom (chiefly BrE)	To have nothing in particular to do, or to feel uncertain about how to occupy oneself.	be idle, be unoccupied, have time on one's hands	I found myself at a loose end after the meeting was canceled.	Chiefly British. In AmE, 'at loose ends' is more common and can also suggest feeling unsettled or without direction.		
124	1234	1314	elude	/ɪˈluːdɪŋ/	verb	To escape from, avoid, or remain out of the grasp of someone; figuratively, to remain difficult to remember, understand, achieve, or obtain.	evade, escape, dodge; baffle, escape one's memory	The suspect eluded police for weeks. / Her name eludes me. / A clear solution continued to elude the team.	Normalize from 'eluding'. High-value figurative patterns: 'the answer eludes me,' 'success eluded him.' Distinguish evade = actively avoid; elude can describe something that remains out of reach.		
124	1235	1315	conch	/kɑːŋk/	noun	A large marine gastropod or its spiral shell; a conch shell can also be used as a horn or ceremonial instrument.	sea snail, shell, conch shell	He blew into a conch shell to signal the start of the ceremony.	Pronunciation varies by region and word tradition; /kɑŋk/ is common in AmE. The word may refer to the animal, shell, or shell used as a horn.		
124	1236	1316	tome	/toʊm/	noun	A large, heavy, or scholarly book, often one that seems serious or imposing.	volume, hefty book, scholarly work	He pulled a dusty tome on medieval history from the shelf. / I don't want to read a 900-page tome on tax law.	Often slightly literary or humorous; it emphasizes size/weight/seriousness more than merely 'book'.		
124	1237	1317	get/be hooked on	/ɡɛt hʊkt ɑn/	phrasal adjective/verb phrase (informal)	To become, or be, strongly attracted or addicted to something; used for substances, habits, games, shows, hobbies, etc.	get addicted to, get into, become obsessed with, be captivated by	I got hooked on the series after one episode. / He became hooked on nicotine as a teenager.	Can range from harmless enthusiasm ('hooked on a show') to genuine addiction ('hooked on opioids'); context controls seriousness.		
124	1238	1318	evasion	/ɪˈveɪʒən/	noun	The act or practice of avoiding, escaping, or sidestepping something, especially a duty, question, rule, payment, or responsibility.	avoidance, circumvention, dodging, evasion	His answers were a form of evasion. / Tax evasion is illegal.	Important contrast: tax evasion = illegal nonpayment/concealment; tax avoidance usually refers to legally arranging affairs to reduce tax.		
124	1239	1319	sulfur / sulphur	/ˈsʌlfɚ/	noun	Chemical element S, a yellow nonmetal used in industry and present in many minerals and compounds.	chemical element S, brimstone (archaic/literary)	Sulfur burns with a blue flame. / The spring smelled of hydrogen sulfide, not elemental sulfur.	CORRECTION: elemental sulfur itself has little to no characteristic 'rotten egg' smell; that odor is strongly associated with hydrogen sulfide and some other sulfur compounds. 'Sulfur' is U.S./IUPAC spelling; 'sulphur' remains common in BrE.		
124	1240	1320	askew	/əˈskjuː/	adjective / adverb	Crooked, tilted, or out of alignment; figuratively, out of order or not quite right.	crooked, tilted, lopsided, misaligned; awry	His glasses sat askew on his face. / The whole arrangement looked slightly askew.	Often physical/visual. Figurative use exists, but 'go awry' is generally more idiomatic for plans and events that go wrong.		
125	1241	1321	awry	/əˈraɪ/	adverb / predicative adjective	Wrong, off course, or contrary to what was intended or expected; also literally crooked/out of position in some contexts.	wrong, off course, amiss, askew	Something went awry with the launch. / Our carefully planned schedule went awry by noon.	Especially common in 'go awry'. Less often used before a noun. Compare askew = visually crooked; awry = plans/events turning out wrong.		
125	1242	1322	brimstone	/ˈbrɪmˌstoʊn/	noun	An old/literary word for sulfur, especially in the religious expression 'fire and brimstone,' evoking divine punishment or hellfire.	sulfur; hellfire (figurative context)	The preacher delivered a fire-and-brimstone sermon.	Rare as an everyday chemistry term. Mostly encountered in literary, biblical-style, or dramatic language and in 'fire and brimstone'.		
125	1243	1323	act up	/ækt ʌp/	phrasal verb (informal)	1. To misbehave. 2. To malfunction or behave unpredictably. 3. Of a body part or medical problem, to start causing trouble or pain.	misbehave, malfunction, play up, give trouble	The kids started acting up in the restaurant. / My laptop is acting up again. / My knee has been acting up lately.	Very productive informal phrase. The subject can be a person, machine, system, or body part.		
125	1244	1324	deduce	/dɪˈduːs/	verb	To arrive at a conclusion by reasoning from known facts, evidence, or premises.	infer, conclude, derive, reason out	From the footprints, she deduced that someone had entered through the window.	Common pattern: deduce X from Y. In everyday English, infer is often close; technically, deduce may imply reasoning from premises while infer is broader.		
125	1245	1325	venture a guess	/ˈvɛntʃɚ ə ɡɛs/	phrase	To offer a guess despite uncertainty, often politely or cautiously.	hazard a guess, take a guess, speculate	Would you care to venture a guess at how much it cost?	Often used in questions/requests: 'Care to venture a guess?' Slightly more polished than simply 'guess'.		
125	1246	1326	equivocally	/ɪˈkwɪvəkli/	adverb	In an ambiguous, noncommittal, or deliberately unclear way, leaving room for more than one interpretation.	ambiguously, evasively, noncommittally	She answered equivocally, refusing to say whether she supported the proposal.	Related: equivocal (ambiguous/uncertain). Opposite: unequivocally = clearly and without ambiguity. 'Equivocal' does not always imply deliberate deception.		
125	1247	1327	punditry	/ˈpʌndɪtri/	noun (usually uncountable)	Commentary and opinion offered by pundits, especially in politics, sports, or mass media; sometimes used critically for confident analysis with limited accountability.	commentary, analysis, opinion, talking-head commentary	Political punditry often focuses more on strategy than policy.	Can be neutral or mildly dismissive. Related: pundit = commentator/expert figure; pundit class/punditocracy are more openly critical terms.		
125	1248	1328	candidly	/ˈkændɪdli/	adverb	Frankly, openly, and without trying to hide one's true opinion, feelings, or assessment.	frankly, honestly, openly, forthrightly	She spoke candidly about the project's failures. / Candidly, I don't think the plan will work.	Can modify a verb ('spoke candidly') or introduce a frank statement ('Candidly, ...').		
125	1249	1329	nightly drink	/ˈnaɪtli drɪŋk/	noun phrase (compositional)	A drink consumed every night; depending on context, often an alcoholic drink taken as part of an evening routine.	evening drink, nightcap (if taken before bed)	He had developed a habit of having a whiskey every night.	Not a fixed idiom. 'Nightcap' is a more lexicalized word for a final drink, often alcoholic, taken before bed. 'Nightly drink' simply means a drink occurring every night.		
125	1250	1330	bag on someone	/bæɡ ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb (informal, regional)	To criticize, mock, tease, or put someone down, often repeatedly.	mock, roast, rag on, put down, make fun of	They kept bagging on him for being late. / Quit bagging on your brother.	Regional and informal; familiarity varies by dialect. It can be playful among friends or genuinely insulting depending on tone.		
126	1251	1331	accolade / accolades	/ˈækəˌleɪdz/	noun	An award, honor, or expression of praise and recognition for an achievement.	honor, award, praise, recognition, tribute	The film received critical accolades. / She accepted the accolade on behalf of the team.	Often plural in modern use: 'receive/win accolades'. Singular 'accolade' can mean a specific honor or strong praise.		
126	1252	1332	uber- / über-	/ˈuːbɚ/	informal combining form / intensifier	Used informally to mean extremely, super-, or an outstanding/extreme example of something.	super-, ultra-, extremely, very	He's an uber-fan of sci-fi. / The restaurant is uber-trendy right now.	From German über ('over/above'). Usually attached to another word (uber-rich, uber-geek) or used informally as an intensifier. Distinguish the common word-forming use from the company name Uber.		
126	1253	1333	JK / jk	/ˌdʒeɪˈkeɪ/	abbreviation (informal, texting/internet)	Short for 'just kidding'; used to signal that the preceding statement was a joke or not meant seriously.	just kidding, joking, kidding	I totally failed the test—jk, I got an A.	Very informal digital shorthand. Capitalization varies. In speech, people may literally say 'jay-kay' for comic effect.		
126	1254	1334	one bit / not one bit	/wʌn bɪt/	noun phrase / emphatic expression	'One bit' means even a small amount or degree; in the very common negative 'not one bit,' it means not at all.	in the slightest, at all, even a little	I don't regret it one bit. / She wasn't one bit surprised.	CORRECTION: 'one bit' itself does not inherently mean 'not at all'; the negative construction creates that meaning. Common frame: not ... one bit.		
