126	1256	1336	subservient	/səbˈsɝːviənt/	adjective	Excessively willing to obey or serve others; or lower in rank, importance, or function and made subordinate to something else.	servile, submissive, deferential, subordinate	He resented being treated as a subservient assistant. / The design should be subservient to function, not the other way around.	Pattern: subservient to. Often negative when describing a person because it suggests servility; the 'secondary/subordinate' sense can be more neutral.		
126	1257	1337	kick the bucket	/kɪk ðə ˈbʌkɪt/	idiom (informal, humorous)	To die.	die, pass away, croak (slang)	The old computer finally kicked the bucket. / He joked about traveling the world before he kicked the bucket.	Usually humorous, irreverent, or euphemistic. Can sound insensitive when referring to a recent or serious real-world death.		
126	1258	1338	the cat is out of the bag	/kæt aʊt əv ðə bæɡ/	idiom	The secret has been revealed or is no longer secret.	the secret is out, the truth is revealed	Once she saw the decorations, the cat was out of the bag.	Normalize from the fragment 'cat out of the bag'. Related active idiom: let the cat out of the bag = reveal the secret.		
126	1259	1339	let the cat out of the bag	/lɛt ðə kæt aʊt əv ðə bæɡ/	idiom	To reveal a secret, often accidentally or earlier than intended.	spill the beans, reveal, disclose, give away the secret	He let the cat out of the bag about the surprise party.	Often implies accidental/premature disclosure, though deliberate revelation is also possible. Pairs naturally with 'the cat is out of the bag'.		
126	1260	1340	erroneous	/ɪˈroʊniəs/	adjective (formal)	Wrong or incorrect, especially because of an error in facts, reasoning, assumptions, data, or belief.	incorrect, mistaken, inaccurate, faulty	The report was based on erroneous assumptions. / The database contained several erroneous entries.	More formal than wrong. Common: erroneous belief/assumption/information/data/conclusion.		
127	1261	1341	hunker down	/ˈhʌŋkɚ daʊn/	phrasal verb (informal)	1. To take shelter or settle in securely, especially during danger or bad weather. 2. To settle in and concentrate on work or endure a difficult period.	take shelter, hole up, dig in, buckle down	We hunkered down in the basement during the storm. / I need to hunker down and finish this report tonight.	Broader than simply 'prepare for a long stay'. Can be physical sheltering or figurative concentrated effort/endurance.		
127	1262	1342	fivefold	/faɪv foʊld/	adjective / adverb	Five times as great, numerous, or large; by a factor of five.	five times, quintuple	Sales increased fivefold, from 100 units to 500. / There was a fivefold increase in demand.	SPELLING/QUANTITATIVE FIX: normally one word, fivefold. 'Increased fivefold' means became five times the original level (a 400% increase), not 'increased by five times' in the loose sense.		
127	1263	1343	spew	/spjuː/	verb; noun (informal/figurative uses common)	To eject or pour out forcefully in large amounts; to vomit; figuratively, to produce or express a stream of words, abuse, smoke, data, etc.	gush, pour out, eject, vomit; spout	The volcano spewed ash into the sky. / The pipe was spewing water. / The account kept spewing hateful nonsense.	Often vivid and negative. Figurative 'spew hate/nonsense' suggests uncontrolled or excessive output.		
127	1264	1344	smear	/smɪɚ/	verb; noun	1. To spread a substance messily across a surface. 2. To blur or stain by rubbing. 3. To damage someone's reputation with accusations or insinuations; noun: a mark/stain or a reputational attack.	smudge, spread, stain; slander, vilify, defame	She smeared butter on the toast. / The ink smeared. / The campaign launched a smear against its opponent.	Major literal + figurative polysemy. Related-family overlap exists elsewhere in the Master; retain here for now because this card combines noun/verb and reputational sense.		
127	1265	1345	spitball / spitballing	/ˈspɪtˌbɔlɪŋ/	verb; noun; informal	To toss out rough ideas for discussion without committing to them; noun 'spitball' also literally means a small wad of chewed/wet paper thrown through a tube.	brainstorm, toss around ideas, kick ideas around	We're just spitballing ideas for the title. / Let me spitball a few possibilities.	Normalize from participle-only headword. In business/creative talk, 'spitball ideas' = brainstorm casually; literal schoolroom sense is separate.		
127	1266	1346	alcove	/ˈælˌkoʊv/	noun	A small recessed area opening off a room, hallway, wall, garden, or other space.	nook, recess, niche	She turned the window alcove into a reading nook.	Usually a partly enclosed/recessed architectural space. Nook is similar but need not be architecturally recessed.		
127	1267	1347	dispute	/dɪˈspjuːt/	noun; verb	noun: a disagreement, controversy, or conflict. verb: to challenge the truth, validity, ownership, or correctness of something, or to argue about it.	disagreement, controversy, argument; challenge, contest	The neighbors are in a dispute over the boundary. / The company disputes the allegations.	Important verb pattern: dispute a claim/fact/allegation. 'Debate' is not always a synonym because dispute often means challenge/deny.		
127	1268	1348	bargain	/ˈbɑːrɡɪn/	noun; verb	noun: 1. something bought or offered at a favorable price; 2. an agreement or deal. verb: to negotiate over price or terms.	good deal, deal, agreement; haggle, negotiate	I got the coat for a bargain price. / They struck a bargain. / We bargained with the seller over the price.	High-value collocations: bargain price, strike a bargain, bargain with/for, drive a hard bargain. Also 'more than I bargained for' = more than expected.		
127	1269	1349	illusive	/ɪˈluːsɪv/	adjective (rare)	Based on or producing illusion; deceptive or unreal in appearance.	deceptive, illusory, misleading	The apparent simplicity was illusive; the problem was actually complex.	IMPORTANT CONFUSABLE: illusive is rare. 'Illusory' is usually the natural word for based on an illusion, while 'elusive' means difficult to find/catch/understand. Many apparent uses of illusive are spelling confusions.		
