128	1271	1351	based on	/beɪst ɑn/	phrase	Using something as the foundation, evidence, source, model, or inspiration for something else.	founded on, grounded in, derived from, inspired by	The film is based on a true story. / The decision was based on the available evidence.	Broad high-frequency phrase. 'Based off (of)' is common in informal AmE, but 'based on' remains the safest standard form.		
128	1272	1352	empirical	/ɛmˈpɪrɪkəl/	adjective	Based on observation, measurement, experiment, or real-world evidence rather than purely on theory, intuition, or deduction.	evidence-based, observational, experimental, data-driven	There is little empirical evidence to support the claim. / The researchers tested the theory empirically.	Common collocations: empirical evidence/data/research/findings. Empirical does not automatically mean scientifically conclusive; evidence can still be weak or badly collected.		
128	1273	1353	fathom	/ˈfæðəm/	verb; noun	verb: to understand or figure out something difficult or puzzling. noun: a unit of depth equal to six feet (1.8288 m), traditionally used at sea.	grasp, comprehend, make sense of; six-foot depth unit	I can't fathom why he refused the offer. / The water here is twenty fathoms deep.	The verb is especially common in negatives/questions: 'I can't fathom...' The nautical noun sense explains expressions such as 'fathomless'.		
128	1274	1354	decipher	/dɪˈsaɪfɚ/	verb	To decode something written in cipher; more broadly, to work out the meaning of writing, speech, signals, behavior, or information that is difficult to understand.	decode, interpret, make out, unravel	I could barely decipher his handwriting. / Analysts are trying to decipher what the announcement really means.	Broader than cryptography. Common with handwriting, codes, signals, motives, and ambiguous messages.		
