130	1299	1379	thrust in the sickle / thrust in your sickle and reap	/θrʌst ɪn jʊɚ ˈsɪkəl ənd riːp/	biblical/literary allusion	A scriptural-style harvesting expression meaning to begin reaping when the harvest is ripe; figuratively, it can evoke decisive action when the time has come.	reap, harvest, seize the moment (figurative)	The phrase evokes the biblical image of putting in the sickle when the harvest is ripe.	SOURCE-DEPENDENT: treat this primarily as a biblical/literary allusion, not a standard everyday idiom. Exact wording varies by translation and source; avoid inventing a modern productive phrase around it.		
