131	1307	1388	Episcopalian	/ɪˌpɪskəˈpeɪliən/	noun; adjective	A member of the Episcopal Church, especially the Episcopal Church in the United States; relating to Episcopalians or that church tradition.	Episcopal Church member; Anglican-tradition Christian (broader relation)	She was raised Episcopalian. / The family attended an Episcopalian parish.	PRECISION: Episcopalian is related to the Anglican tradition but is not simply synonymous with Anglican in every context. 'Episcopal' is the official adjective in the name 'The Episcopal Church'.		
131	1308	1389	ugly duckling	/ˈʌɡli ˈdʌklɪŋ/	idiom / noun phrase	A person, project, place, or thing that initially seems unattractive, awkward, or unpromising but later develops into something admired, successful, or beautiful.	late bloomer, unlikely success, diamond in the rough	The neglected neighborhood became the city's ugly duckling success story after years of redevelopment.	From Hans Christian Andersen's tale. Can refer to appearance, talent, commercial potential, or broader transformation; avoid awkward phrasing like 'he's a total ugly duckling story'.		
131	1309	1390	duckling	/ˈdʌklɪŋ/	noun	A young duck.	young duck, baby duck	A line of ducklings followed their mother across the path.	Simple high-frequency sense is sufficient. 'Duckling' also appears figuratively in 'ugly duckling'.		
