132	1311	1392	dubious	/ˈduːbiəs/	adjective	1. Doubtful, uncertain, or unconvinced. 2. Questionable in truth, quality, legality, or trustworthiness.	doubtful, skeptical, questionable, suspect	I'm dubious about that explanation. / The site makes several dubious health claims.	Two related senses: a person can be dubious = doubtful/skeptical; a claim/deal can be dubious = questionable/suspicious. Not every dubious thing is proven false.		
132	1312	1393	tear into someone / tear into something	/tɔrn ˈɪntu ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb (informal)	To attack or criticize someone fiercely; also to begin eating or attacking something with great energy.	rip into, berate, lambaste; dig into	She tore into him for missing the deadline. / The kids tore into the pizza as soon as it arrived.	NORMALIZE from past-participle 'torn into someone'. Broaden beyond criticism: 'tear into food' is common and positive/neutral in context.		
