132	1314	1395	egalitarian	/ɪˌɡælɪˈtɛriən/	adjective; noun	Believing in, advocating, or characterized by social, political, or economic equality; noun: a person who supports such equality.	equality-minded, equalitarian (rare), leveling (context-dependent)	They wanted a more egalitarian workplace. / She describes herself as an egalitarian.	CORRECTION: 'democratic' and 'fair' are related ideas but not direct synonyms. Egalitarian specifically concerns equality in status, rights, opportunities, or distribution.		
132	1315	1396	inoculation	/ɪˌnɑːkjəˈleɪʃən/	noun	1. The introduction of material into the body to induce immunity or resistance; historically includes practices distinct from modern vaccination. 2. The introduction of microorganisms/cells into a culture medium. 3. Figuratively, preparation intended to build resistance to an idea or influence.	vaccination (overlapping, not always identical), immunization; seeding (lab context)	Historically, smallpox inoculation predated modern vaccination. / The media-literacy lesson was designed as an inoculation against misinformation.	PRECISION: inoculation and vaccination overlap but are not perfect historical/technical synonyms. 'Inoculation' is also common in microbiology and in figurative 'inoculation theory'.		
