132	1319	1401	concha	/ˈkɔntʃə/	noun (Spanish; Mexican food term)	1. In Mexican Spanish/English food contexts, a concha is a round sweet bread (pan dulce) with a shell-patterned sugary topping. 2. Spanish concha more broadly means shell and has other regional meanings.	Mexican sweet bread, pan dulce; shell (Spanish)	I picked up a chocolate concha from the panadería for breakfast.	For the food sense, 'Mexican sweet bread with a shell-patterned topping' is more precise than merely 'roll shaped like a shell'. Spanish concha has additional regional meanings, including slang, so context matters.		
132	1320	1402	fluctuating criteria	/ˈflʌktʃuˌeɪtɪŋ kraɪˈtɪriə/	noun phrase (compositional)	Criteria or standards that change over time, especially repeatedly or unpredictably.	changing criteria, shifting standards, variable requirements	Applicants struggled to keep up with the fluctuating criteria for approval.	Not a fixed idiom. 'Shifting criteria/standards' may sound more natural when criticizing inconsistent requirements. Fluctuating emphasizes repeated movement/change.		
