133	1323	1405	get a grip / get a grip on something	/ɡɛt ə ɡrɪp/	idiom / verb phrase	1. To regain control of one's emotions or behavior. 2. To gain control over, understand, or manage a situation or subject.	pull oneself together, regain composure; get control of, get a handle on	Get a grip—you’re panicking over nothing. / We need to get a grip on our spending.	'Get a grip!' can sound blunt or unsympathetic. Productive extension: get a grip on something = understand/control/manage it.		
133	1324	1406	Whac-A-Mole / whack-a-mole	/ˈwæk ə ˌmoʊl/	proper noun; figurative noun/adjective	Originally an arcade game in which players hit toy moles as they pop up; figuratively, a situation where solving one problem is immediately followed by another similar problem appearing elsewhere.	endless cycle, recurring problem, reactive problem-solving	Fighting spam can become a game of whack-a-mole. / The team was stuck in whack-a-mole mode, fixing bugs one by one.	The commercial game name is Whac-A-Mole; lowercase 'whack-a-mole' is common figuratively. The metaphor stresses reactive, recurring problems rather than simply any never-ending task.		
