137	1366	1451	stooge	/stuːdʒ/	noun	1. A person used by another as a subordinate, puppet, accomplice, or fall guy. 2. In comedy, a performer whose role is to feed lines or be the butt of jokes for another performer.	puppet, lackey, dupe, underling; straight man (not exact)	Critics called him a stooge for the regime. / The comedian used a stooge from the audience as part of the act.	Often disparaging. A stooge is not simply a fool: the key idea is serving or being used by someone else; comedy has a distinct performance sense.		
