138	1372	1457	sappy	/ˈsæpi/	adjective (informal)	1. Excessively or cloyingly sentimental. 2. Literally full of or resembling plant sap.	sentimental, mawkish, cheesy, corny; sap-filled	The ending was a little sappy, but I still liked it. / Fresh-cut branches can be sticky and sappy.	Usually mildly negative/teasing in the sentimental sense, but speakers may affectionately call something sappy.		
138	1373	1458	gag me with a spoon	/ɡæɡ mi wɪð ə spuːn seɪɪŋ/	catchphrase / slang expression (dated, humorous)	An exaggerated expression of disgust, revulsion, or contempt: roughly 'that's disgusting/gross'.	gross, disgusting, gag me, barf	He said matching outfits were romantic. Gag me with a spoon.	Associated especially with 1980s U.S. 'Valley Girl' speech/pop culture and now sounds dated, campy, or deliberately retro. Remove unnecessary word 'saying' from headword.		
138	1374	1459	deus ex machina	/ˈdeɪəs ɛks ˈmɑːkɪnə/	noun (literary/dramatic term)	A person, event, or device introduced suddenly to resolve an apparently insoluble plot problem, especially when the resolution feels insufficiently prepared or artificial.	contrived rescue, sudden plot solution, plot device	The last-minute rescue felt like a deus ex machina because nothing earlier in the story had set it up.	Not simply any plot twist or lucky event. From Latin 'god from the machine', referring to ancient theatrical machinery used to bring a god onto the stage.		
138	1375	1460	stupendous	/stjuːˈpɛndəs/	adjective	Extremely impressive, remarkable, large, or excellent.	tremendous, extraordinary, amazing, magnificent	The restoration team did a stupendous job. / The project required a stupendous amount of work.	Enthusiastic and somewhat grand/old-fashioned compared with amazing. Can emphasize size/degree as well as excellence.		
138	1376	1461	wheelbarrow	/ˈwiːlbæroʊ/	noun	A small hand-propelled cart, typically with one wheel at the front and two handles, used to carry soil, tools, construction materials, etc.	barrow, handcart (broader)	He filled the wheelbarrow with soil and pushed it across the garden.	Simple concrete sense. A handcart is broader and may have two or more wheels, so it is not exact.		
138	1377	1462	racketeering	/ˌrækɪˈtɪərɪŋ/	noun (law/crime)	Participation in an organized pattern or enterprise involving specified illegal activities; in U.S. federal law, 'racketeering activity' has a defined statutory meaning under RICO.	organized criminal activity, criminal enterprise activity	Prosecutors charged several defendants with racketeering.	LEGAL PRECISION: not simply any organized illegal activity, extortion, or bribery. Exact elements depend on the statute/jurisdiction; U.S. RICO uses specifically defined predicate acts and enterprise/pattern concepts.		
138	1378	1463	racketeering conspiracy	/ˌrækɪˈtɪərɪŋ kənˈspɪrəsi/	noun phrase (law)	A conspiracy charge alleging an agreement to participate in or further racketeering activity or a racketeering enterprise; exact elements depend on the governing law.	RICO conspiracy (U.S. context), criminal conspiracy involving racketeering	The indictment included a racketeering-conspiracy count.	LEGAL PRECISION: avoid defining this generically as merely 'a criminal plot'. In U.S. federal cases, RICO conspiracy is a specific statutory theory; requirements vary by jurisdiction/statute.		
138	1379	1464	debauchery	/dɪˈbɔːtʃəri/	noun	Excessive indulgence in sensual pleasures, especially sex, alcohol, partying, or other behavior portrayed as morally dissolute.	dissipation, excess, licentiousness, revelry	The novel depicts a weekend of drunken debauchery.	Strongly judgmental/old-fashioned or literary in many contexts. It describes perceived moral excess; 'indulgence' alone is much milder.		
138	1380	1465	be no stranger to something	/noʊ ˈstreɪndʒɚ/	idiom	To be familiar with, experienced in, or accustomed to something, often something difficult, notable, or recurring.	be familiar with, be accustomed to, know well	She's no stranger to criticism. / The region is no stranger to severe storms.	NORMALIZE to productive pattern. Often used with hardships or challenges, but can take neutral/positive experiences too.		
139	1381	1466	complacent	/kəmˈpleɪsənt/	adjective	Too self-satisfied or unconcerned about risks, weaknesses, or the need for improvement, often because things are currently going well.	self-satisfied, smug, unworried, overconfident	After three easy wins, the team became complacent and stopped preparing carefully.	Important nuance: complacent is negative; it is not simply content/satisfied. It implies insufficient concern or vigilance.		
139	1382	1467	the least tight thing / least tight	/liːst taɪt θɪŋ/	source-dependent slang phrase	In a context where 'tight' means cool, impressive, or excellent, 'the least tight' means especially uncool, lame, or objectionable.	uncool, lame, weak, not cool	Showing my poem to everyone without asking? That's the least tight thing you could do.	SOURCE-DEPENDENT/pop-culture phrasing rather than a standard slang expression. Preserve because of source value; teach productive slang 'tight' = cool/excellent separately. Meaning depends on the quoted context.		
139	1383	1468	moniker	/ˈmɒnɪkər/	noun (informal)	A name, nickname, alias, or identifying label, especially one by which someone or something is popularly known.	nickname, name, alias, sobriquet	The singer performs under the moniker 'Night Owl.'	Often used for nicknames/stage names/labels, but can simply mean name. Slightly journalistic/playful tone.		
139	1384	1469	Quasimodo	/ˌkwɑːzɪˈmoʊdoʊ/	proper noun	The central character of Victor Hugo's novel 'The Hunchback of Notre-Dame', a bell-ringer of Notre-Dame who is socially marginalized and treated cruelly because of his appearance.	—	Quasimodo is the bell-ringer at the center of Victor Hugo's novel.	CORRECTION/CAUTION: treat primarily as a proper literary name, not a common noun meaning 'outcast/misfit'. Using 'Quasimodo' to mock someone's appearance or disability can be insulting.		
139	1385	1470	quasi- / quasi	/ˈkwɑːzaɪ/	prefix / adjective	Seemingly, partly, or almost, but not fully or genuinely; having some resemblance or functional similarity.	semi-, pseudo-, near-, almost	The study used a quasi-experimental design. / The committee has quasi-judicial powers.	Very productive formal combining form: quasi-legal, quasi-scientific, quasi-governmental. 'Pseudo-' often implies falseness more strongly, so it is not always interchangeable.		
139	1386	1471	retaliate	/rɪˈtæliˌeɪt/	verb	To respond to an injury, attack, insult, punishment, or adverse action by taking harmful or punitive action in return.	strike back, get back at, take revenge, respond in kind	They threatened to retaliate if the sanctions were imposed. / He was fired after complaining and alleged that the company had retaliated against him.	Patterns: retaliate against someone; retaliate for something; retaliate by doing something. 'Avenge' normally takes an object (avenge a death/wrong) and is not a direct grammatical substitute.		
139	1387	1472	standoffish	/ˈstændɔːfɪʃ/	adjective (informal)	Distant, reserved, aloof, or unfriendly in manner, especially in a way that discourages closeness.	aloof, distant, reserved, unapproachable, cool	She seemed standoffish at first, but she warmed up once we started talking.	Describes social manner, not necessarily actual dislike. Can reflect shyness/reserve as perceived by others.		
139	1388	1473	standoff / stand-off	/ˈstændɔːf/	noun	A confrontation or deadlock in which neither side is willing or able to give way or make decisive progress.	deadlock, impasse, confrontation	Police negotiated for hours during the standoff. / The talks ended in a political standoff.	Broader than 'neither side can win': it can be an armed confrontation, negotiation deadlock, or strategic impasse. Spelling 'standoff' is standard AmE; 'stand-off' also occurs.		
139	1389	1474	prig	/prɪɡ/	noun (disapproving, somewhat old-fashioned)	A self-righteously proper, moralistic, or pedantic person who annoyingly displays their correctness or superiority.	prude (not exact), pedant, goody-goody, self-righteous person	He comes across as a pompous prig whenever he lectures everyone about etiquette.	Dated/literary flavor. 'Puritan' has historical/religious meanings and is not a safe direct synonym.		
139	1390	1475	quicksilver	/ˈkwɪksɪlvər/	noun; adjective (literary)	noun: the old/common name for mercury. adjective/figurative: rapidly changing, lively, mercurial, or difficult to pin down.	mercury; mercurial, changeable, volatile	Mercury was once commonly called quicksilver. / She has a quicksilver wit that jumps from idea to idea.	Figurative use is literary and derives from mercury's mobile appearance. Avoid vague synonym 'fast-moving' alone; personality/mood/intellect can be quicksilver.		
140	1391	1476	weary	/ˈwɪəri/	adjective; verb	adjective: very tired, especially after prolonged effort, stress, or experience; also tired of something. verb: to make or become tired.	tired, fatigued, exhausted; fed up	We were weary after the long hike. / I'm weary of having the same argument every week.	High-value pattern: weary of + noun/gerund = tired/fed up with. Often more literary than tired.		
140	1392	1477	coax / coaxing	/ˈkəʊksɪŋ/	verb; noun/adjective participial use	To gently or patiently persuade someone to do something; also to obtain or bring something out gradually through careful effort.	cajole, persuade, wheedle; draw out	She coaxed him into trying one bite. / He coaxed a smile out of the shy child. / We coaxed the old engine back to life.	Normalize to base verb. Productive patterns: coax someone into doing; coax something out of someone; coax something into/out of a state.		
140	1393	1478	prune	/pruːn/	noun; verb	noun: a dried plum. verb: to cut back branches/growth; figuratively, to remove unnecessary parts, reduce, or trim a list/system.	dried plum; trim, cut back, pare down	She pruned the rose bushes in spring. / We pruned several outdated items from the list.	Existing card is good; add productive figurative reduction sense. Related-family overlap with earlier WID961 should be retained for later duplicate-family review.		
140	1394	1479	exultant	/ɪɡˈzʌltənt/	adjective	Triumphantly or intensely joyful, especially after a victory, success, or piece of good news.	jubilant, triumphant, elated, ecstatic	The exultant crowd erupted when the final whistle blew.	Often conveys outward, triumphant joy rather than quiet happiness. Related verb: exult in/over.		
140	1395	1480	exhilarated	/ɪɡˈzɪləˌreɪtɪd/	adjective	Thrilled, energized, and intensely excited or happy, often after an exciting, risky, fast, or physically stimulating experience.	thrilled, elated, invigorated, euphoric	I felt exhilarated after the roller-coaster ride. / She was exhausted but exhilarated after the race.	Often combines excitement with heightened energy. Compare elated = very happy; exhilarated more strongly suggests stimulation/energy.		
140	1396	1481	monogram	/ˈmɒnəɡræm/	noun; verb	noun: a design made by combining or arranging initials/letters as a personal or organizational mark. verb: to decorate or mark with a monogram.	initials, emblem; personalize with initials	She had her initials monogrammed on the cuff. / The towels bear the hotel's monogram.	A monogram may combine/interlock letters; it is not simply any written initials or a signature.		
140	1397	1482	cross	/krɔːs/	noun; verb; adjective	Highly polysemous: noun: intersecting lines/symbol, Christian cross, hybrid, burden/trial in some expressions. verb: go from one side to another, intersect, oppose, betray, cross out, etc. adjective (especially BrE): angry/annoyed.	intersection/symbol; traverse, intersect, oppose; annoyed (BrE)	We crossed the street. / Cross that name off the list. / Don't cross him—he holds grudges. / Mum was cross with me for being late.	Current card is too compressed for a very high-frequency polysemous word. Important phrases include cross someone's mind, cross paths, cross out/off, cross the line, and BrE 'be cross with'.		
