141	1404	1489	daffodil / daffodils	/ˈdæfəˌdɪlz/	noun	A spring-flowering plant of the genus Narcissus, typically with a trumpet/cup-shaped corona and yellow, white, or orange flowers.	Narcissus; jonquil (a type/group, not exact synonym)	Daffodils were blooming along the path in early spring.	Normalize singular/plural. 'Narcissus' is the genus/broader botanical grouping; 'jonquil' refers to particular Narcissus types, not every daffodil.		
141	1405	1490	crocus	/ˈkrəʊkəs/	noun	A small flowering plant of the genus Crocus, with cup-shaped flowers; different species bloom in spring or autumn. Saffron comes from Crocus sativus.	Crocus flower; saffron crocus (specific species)	Purple crocuses emerged through the late-winter snow. / Saffron is harvested from Crocus sativus.	CORRECTION: 'autumn crocus' commonly refers to Colchicum, which is not a true Crocus, so it should not be a generic synonym. Saffron is a product of one Crocus species, not a synonym for crocus.		
141	1406	1491	annul / annulled	/əˈnʌld/	verb	To declare something legally or officially invalid, void, or without effect; commonly used of marriages, laws, contracts, decisions, and elections.	invalidate, void, cancel, nullify	The court annulled the marriage. / The decision was later annulled on procedural grounds.	Normalize to base verb while retaining source form. CORRECTION: a marriage is not ordinarily annulled merely because of 'irreconcilable differences'; annulment generally depends on a legal ground showing the marriage was invalid/voidable under applicable law.		
141	1407	1492	alone together	/əˈloʊn təˈɡɛðər/	phrase / paradoxical expression	Physically together while each person remains separate, private, emotionally distant, or engaged in their own activity; can be negative, neutral, or intimate depending on context.	together but separate, companionable solitude (context-dependent)	We spent the evening alone together, each reading our own book. / They sat alone together, emotionally miles apart.	Not a fixed idiom with only the negative meaning 'emotionally distant'. It can also describe comfortable shared solitude or simply two people alone with each other.		
