141	1409	1494	keep someone company	/kiːp ˈsʌmwʌn ˈkʌmpəni/	idiom / verb phrase	To stay with or spend time with someone so they are not alone, often while they wait, work, travel, recover, etc.	accompany, stay with, sit with, spend time with	I'll keep you company while you wait. / I stayed up to keep her company while she finished packing.	Natural high-frequency expression. 'Company' here means companionship, not a business.		
141	1410	1495	brim / brimming with	/ˈbrɪmɪŋ/	verb; adjective/participle	To be filled to the edge or overflowing; figuratively, to be full of an emotion, quality, energy, or ideas.	overflow, be full of, teem with	The cup was brimming with coffee. / She was brimming with confidence and ideas.	Normalize beyond participle-only form. High-value figurative pattern: be brimming with confidence/excitement/ideas. 'Loaded' is not a general synonym.		
142	1411	1496	clobber / clobbering	/ˈklɑːbərɪŋ/	verb (informal); noun (other senses)	To hit hard; figuratively, to defeat decisively, damage severely, or impose a heavy cost/impact on someone or something.	hit, wallop, batter; crush, thrash, hammer	He got clobbered in the first round. / Rising costs are clobbering small businesses. / We clobbered them 8–1.	Normalize to base verb. Very productive figurative use for sports, economics, criticism, and severe impact. BrE noun 'clobber' can also mean clothes/equipment, a separate sense.		
142	1412	1497	clobbered	/klɒbd/	verb / adjective (informal)	Past/past participle of clobber: hit hard, defeated decisively, or badly affected/damaged.	hit, walloped; crushed, hammered	He got clobbered over the head. / Our team got clobbered 7–0. / The stock was clobbered after the earnings report.	CORRECTION: 'clobbed' is nonstandard/likely an error for 'clobbered' in this context. Keep separate only if source retrieval value matters; otherwise this belongs to the clobber family.		
142	1413	1498	spaz / spazz / spaz out	/spæz/	noun; verb (slang, potentially offensive)	Slang historically derived from 'spastic'. Depending on dialect, it has been used for clumsiness, overexcitement, losing control, or 'freaking out'; it can be an ableist slur and is especially offensive in British usage.	freak out, lose it, flail, overreact (context-dependent)	In older U.S. slang you may hear 'I totally spazzed out,' but it is safer to say 'I freaked out.'	REGISTER/SAFETY: offensiveness varies geographically but the disability-derived history is important. Avoid using it as a label for a person. Spellings include spaz and spazz; recognition is more useful than active production.		
