142	1416	1501	scratch someone's eyes out / scratch your eyes out	ʃi wɪl skrætʃ jɚ aɪz aʊt	idiom / hyperbolic expression	To attack someone viciously, especially by scratching at the face/eyes; often used hyperbolically for intense anger, jealousy, or hostility.	claw at, attack, go for, lash out at	If she catches you flirting with him, she'll scratch your eyes out. / They were joking that the two rivals would scratch each other's eyes out.	Often hyperbolic rather than a literal threat. Traditionally stereotyped in descriptions of fights between women, so avoid presenting the gender stereotype as part of the core definition.		
142	1417	1502	Raggedy Ann	ˈræɡədi æn	proper noun	A red-yarn-haired rag doll character created by Johnny Gruelle and featured in American children's books and related dolls.	—	She still keeps her childhood Raggedy Ann doll on the shelf.	Capitalize as a proper name. More precise than defining it as merely a generic doll; Raggedy Ann is a specific character/design with a long U.S. children's-book/toy history.		
142	1418	1503	raggedy	ˈræɡədi	adjective (informal)	Ragged, shabby, tattered, worn-looking, or untidy; sometimes used dismissively of a person's appearance or possessions.	shabby, ragged, tattered, scruffy, worn-out	He showed up in the same raggedy old coat. / The fence looked pretty raggedy after the storm.	Informal extension of ragged. Can sound judgmental when applied to a person. Common in AmE speech.		
