143	1421	1506	easy-pour spout	ˈiːzi pɔr spaʊt	noun phrase (descriptive/product language)	A spout or opening designed to make liquid easier to pour cleanly and with better control.	pour spout, dispensing spout, easy-pour opening	The carton has an easy-pour spout that helps prevent spills.	Not a fixed lexical expression; common descriptive/product wording. Hyphenate easy-pour before a noun.		
143	1422	1507	goodie / goodies	ˈɡʊdiz	noun (informal)	A pleasant, desirable, or tasty item; plural 'goodies' commonly means treats, snacks, gifts, prizes, or other desirable things.	treat, sweet, gift, prize, desirable item	The kids came home with bags of Halloween goodies. / The deluxe edition includes a few extra goodies.	Broader than food. Singular goodie can mean a desirable item; 'the goodies' can mean treats or attractive extras. Separate 'goody' spellings/usages exist.		
143	1423	1508	indignantly	ɪnˈdɪɡnəntli	adverb	In a way that shows anger or offense at something perceived as unjust, insulting, improper, or undeserved.	resentfully, offendedly, angrily	She indignantly denied cheating on the exam. / 'How dare you?' he replied indignantly.	Core emotion is moral/offended anger, not generic annoyance. Related adjective WID1535 indignant.		
143	1424	1509	fretful	ˈfrɛtfəl	adjective	Restlessly worried, uneasy, irritable, or difficult to soothe.	anxious, restless, irritable, querulous	The baby grew fretful after missing her nap. / He spent a fretful night worrying about the results.	Often used of babies/children or anxious sleep, but adults can be fretful too. Combines worry with restlessness/irritability.		
143	1425	1510	trifle	ˈtraɪfəl	noun; verb	noun: 1. Something of little importance or value. 2. A layered dessert, especially BrE, traditionally made with sponge/cake, fruit, custard, and cream. verb: to treat something as unimportant or waste time; 'trifle with' = treat lightly/play with.	trivial matter, small thing; dessert; toy with	Don't worry about such a trifle. / We had strawberry trifle for dessert. / Don't trifle with people's feelings.	Major missing common BrE dessert sense and verb. 'Knickknack' is not a general synonym for trifle in the 'minor matter' sense.		
143	1426	1511	unscrupulous	ʌnˈskruːpjələs	adjective	Willing to act dishonestly, unfairly, or unethically because one lacks moral scruples.	dishonest, unethical, corrupt, unprincipled	Unscrupulous sellers sometimes prey on inexperienced buyers.	Strongly negative. Often collocates with dealer, employer, businessman, tactic, practice. Opposite concept: scrupulous = careful about ethics/principles (and also very thorough).		
143	1427	1512	ascetic	əˈsɛtɪk	adjective; noun	adjective: practicing strict self-denial and abstention from comforts or pleasures, often for spiritual/religious reasons. noun: a person who follows such a disciplined way of life.	austere, self-denying; monk/hermit (context-dependent, not exact)	He adopted an ascetic lifestyle with few possessions. / The ascetic spent years in prayer and fasting.	Can be religious/philosophical or figurative. Do not equate every ascetic with a monk/hermit; those are context-dependent roles.		
143	1428	1513	ravenous	ˈrævənəs	adjective	Extremely hungry; figuratively, intensely eager or voracious for something.	famished, starving, voracious; insatiable	After hiking all day, we were ravenous. / She has a ravenous appetite for new information.	Very strong hunger word. Figurative use occurs with appetite/desire, but 'ravenous for X' is less universal than 'ravenous appetite'.		
143	1429	1514	ecstasy	ˈɛkstəsi	noun	1. Overwhelming joy, delight, or emotional rapture. 2. Ecstasy/MDMA: a common name for the psychoactive drug MDMA, though illicit tablets sold under that name may contain other substances.	rapture, bliss, elation, euphoria; MDMA (drug sense)	The fans were in ecstasy after the winning goal. / The report discussed risks associated with drugs sold as ecstasy.	Important polysemy. Drug sense is common enough for recognition. Do not imply that every illicit 'ecstasy' tablet is chemically pure MDMA.		
143	1430	1515	snapdragon	ˈsnæpˌdræɡən	noun	A flowering plant of the genus Antirrhinum, named for blossoms that can resemble a dragon's mouth when squeezed.	Antirrhinum	Snapdragons added bright pink and yellow color to the garden.	'Dragon flower' is not a standard everyday synonym in English. Antirrhinum is the botanical genus; snapdragon is the normal common name.		
