145	1448	1534	get knocked up	ɡɛt nɑkt ʌp	slang phrasal verb	To become pregnant or get someone pregnant; informal and potentially crude/disrespectful depending on context.	get pregnant, become pregnant	She joked that she got knocked up right after the wedding.	Register warning: casual/slang and can sound crude, especially about someone else's pregnancy. In older BrE, 'knock someone up' historically also had unrelated senses such as waking someone by knocking.		
145	1449	1535	indignant	ɪnˈdɪɡnənt	adjective	Angry or offended because one believes something is unjust, insulting, improper, or undeserved.	outraged, offended, resentful	She was indignant at being accused of cheating. / He gave an indignant reply.	More specific than generic angry/annoyed: indignation involves a sense of injustice or affront. Related adverb WID1508 indignantly.		
145	1450	1536	run something by someone	rʌn ˈsʌmθɪŋ baɪ ju	phrasal verb (informal)	To tell, show, or explain an idea, plan, question, or proposal to someone in order to get their reaction, opinion, or approval.	check with, consult, get someone's take on	Can I run something by you before I send this email?	NORMALIZE productive pattern: run something by someone. Often casual workplace/conversation English.		
146	1451	1537	trample / trampled	ˈtræmpəld	verb	To step heavily or repeatedly on and crush/damage something; to injure people by stepping on them in a crowd; figuratively, to disregard or violate rights, feelings, or principles.	stomp on, tread on, crush; violate, ride roughshod over	The crowd trampled the flowers. / Several people were injured in the crush. / The policy was criticized for trampling on basic rights.	Normalize to base verb while retaining source form. Important figurative pattern: trample on/over rights, feelings, traditions.		
