147	1465	1551	put someone off / put something off	pʊt ˈsʌmwʌn ɔf	phrasal verb	1. put someone off: cause dislike, discomfort, loss of interest, or reluctance. 2. put something off: postpone or delay it. 3. BrE: distract someone ('Don't put me off').	repel, deter, discourage; postpone, delay	His rude manner really put me off. / We had to put the meeting off until Friday.	Major polysemy: person-object and thing-object constructions differ. 'Put someone off doing something' = discourage/deter them from doing it.		
