150	1500	1588	disposition	ˌdɪspəˈzɪʃən	noun	1. A person's usual temperament or tendency of mood/behavior. 2. The arrangement or positioning of things. 3. The action/result of disposing of, transferring, or resolving something, especially in legal/business contexts.	temperament, nature; arrangement; disposal/resolution (context-dependent)	She has a cheerful disposition. / The report describes the disposition of the assets.	Highly polysemous formal word. Everyday personality sense is common, but legal/business 'disposition of a case/assets' and arrangement senses are important.		
151	1501	1589	no kidding	noʊ ˈkɪdɪŋ	idiom / interjection (informal)	1. Used to show surprise or ask whether something is really true: 'Really?' 2. Used to strongly agree with an obvious statement: 'Seriously / tell me about it.' 3. Literally, 'I'm not joking.'	really?, seriously, you're telling me, I'm not joking	'She quit yesterday.' 'No kidding?' / 'This traffic is awful.' 'No kidding.' / No kidding—I actually won.	Intonation changes function. Can express genuine surprise, emphatic agreement, or insistence on truth.		
151	1502	1590	one Mississippi, two Mississippi	wʌn ˌmɪsɪˈsɪpi tu ˌmɪsɪˈsɪpi	counting phrase	A traditional English-language way of approximating seconds by saying 'one Mississippi, two Mississippi...' at a steady pace.	counting seconds, informal timing	Count 'one Mississippi, two Mississippi' before you start chasing us.	Approximate, not an accurate timing method. Common in children's games and informal timing; other regional counting words exist.		
151	1503	1591	well, duh / duh	wɛl dʌ	interjection (informal, sarcastic)	Used to say that something is extremely obvious, often with sarcasm or mild mockery.	obviously, of course, no kidding	'You need a password to log in.' 'Well, duh.'	'Well, duh' adds conversational emphasis to duh. Can sound dismissive or rude. Related WID1230/1516 family should be considered in later duplicate-family QA.		
151	1504	1592	Bullwinkle	ˈbʊlwɪŋkəl	proper noun (pop-culture reference)	Bullwinkle J. Moose, the good-natured but dim-witted cartoon moose from 'The Rocky and Bullwinkle Show' franchise.	—	The joke compares him to Bullwinkle, the cartoon moose.	CORRECTION: treat as a proper character reference, not a common noun meaning 'goof'. Any implication of clumsiness/goofiness comes from the comparison/context and should not be universalized.		
151	1505	1593	I can't even send back soup	aɪ kænt ˈivən sɛnd bæk suːp	source-dependent humorous phrase	A self-deprecating line suggesting extreme reluctance to complain, assert oneself, or confront someone—even in a low-stakes situation such as returning unsatisfactory food.	I'm too nonconfrontational, I hate complaining, I'm not assertive	Confront my boss? I can't even send back soup when it's cold.	Not a standard idiom with a fixed dictionary meaning. Preserve as source/contextual humor. It evokes nonconfrontational behavior or low assertiveness; do not label it clinically as 'social anxiety' without context.		
151	1506	1594	crotchety	ˈkrɑːtʃɪti	adjective (informal)	Bad-tempered, irritable, and prone to complaining or snapping at people.	grumpy, cranky, irascible, cantankerous	The crotchety neighbor complained whenever the kids played outside.	Often stereotypically used of older people, but age is not part of the definition. Can be humorous or insulting.		
151	1507	1595	peevish	ˈpiːvɪʃ	adjective	Irritable, petulant, or easily annoyed, often over small matters.	irritable, testy, petulant, fretful	He grew peevish after waiting for an hour. / She answered in a peevish tone.	Somewhat literary/formal. Often suggests petty or childish irritability more than general anger.		
151	1508	1596	unrelenting	ˌʌnrɪˈlɛntɪŋ	adjective	Not becoming less severe, intense, demanding, or persistent; not giving way or showing mercy.	relentless, persistent, remorseless, unyielding	The unrelenting heat lasted for weeks. / She faced unrelenting criticism.	Can describe weather, pressure, pain, criticism, pace, effort, etc. 'Uncompromising' overlaps mainly for a person's stance, not all uses.		
151	1509	1598	it's like cocktails in Appalachia	ɪts laɪk ˈkɑkˌteɪlz ɪn ˌæpəˈleɪʃə	source-dependent cultural simile	A source-specific comparison whose intended joke or implication depends on its original context; it should not be treated as an established English idiom.	—	The speaker joked, 'It's like cocktails in Appalachia,' using the contrast for comic effect.	CORRECTION/CAUTION: not a standard idiom meaning simply 'an unlikely combination'. The line may rely on cultural/class/regional stereotyping of Appalachia, so preserve the source wording but do not generalize or recommend it as a neutral expression.		
151	1510	1599	boisterous	ˈbɔɪstərəs	adjective	Loud, energetic, lively, and somewhat rough or rowdy.	rowdy, noisy, exuberant, rambunctious	A boisterous crowd spilled out of the stadium. / The children became boisterous after dessert.	Not necessarily cheerful: the core is noisy, energetic, exuberant/rowdy behavior. Can be positive, neutral, or mildly disapproving.		
152	1511	1600	frisky	ˈfrɪski	adjective (informal)	1. Playful, lively, and energetic, especially of an animal. 2. Informally, sexually playful, flirtatious, or aroused.	playful, lively, spirited; flirtatious, amorous	The puppy was feeling frisky and raced around the yard. / He was getting a little frisky after a couple of drinks.	Important second sense is stronger than merely 'a hint of flirtation'. Context matters; sexual sense is informal and can imply unwanted advances depending on behavior.		
