154	1535	1624	spell something out / spell it out	ˈspɛlɪŋ ɪt aʊt	phrasal verb	1. To say or write the letters of a word one by one. 2. Figuratively, to explain something very clearly, explicitly, or in detail.	spell, explain explicitly, make clear, lay out	Could you spell your last name out for me? / Do I really have to spell it out?	Major literal + figurative senses. Normalize from gerund-only form. Figurative 'spell it out' can sound impatient when the point should be obvious.		
154	1536	1625	plunk someone/something down	plʌŋk ˈsʌmwʌn daʊn	phrasal verb (informal)	To put, drop, set, or seat someone/something down abruptly, heavily, or casually; intransitively, to sit oneself down.	plop down, drop, set down	He plunked the groceries down on the counter. / She plunked herself down on the sofa.	Broader than sitting someone down. Objects and oneself are very common. 'Plunk down money' can also mean pay a sum, especially readily.		
