156	1559	1648	play hardball	pleɪ ˈhɑːrdˌbɔl	idiom (informal)	To use tough, uncompromising, aggressive, or highly competitive tactics to get what one wants, especially in negotiation, politics, business, or disputes.	take a hard line, use tough tactics, be uncompromising	If they refuse to negotiate, we may have to play hardball.	Does not necessarily mean ruthless/illegal; it means abandoning a soft/cooperative approach for tougher tactics. From baseball imagery.		
156	1560	1649	mistletoe	ˈmɪsəlˌtoʊ	noun	A group of parasitic or hemiparasitic plants that grow on trees; in Western Christmas tradition, sprigs are hung as decorations and kissing beneath them is customary.	—	They hung mistletoe over the doorway. / The couple kissed under the mistletoe.	The kissing custom is cultural, not an inherent botanical property. Mistletoe refers to multiple related plant groups/species.		
157	1561	1650	revel / revel in	ˈrɛvəl	verb; noun (less common)	verb: to take great pleasure or delight in something ('revel in'); also to celebrate noisily or festively. noun: lively celebration or festivity, especially in plural/revels.	delight in, relish; celebrate, carouse	She reveled in the attention. / Fans reveled in the streets after the victory.	Major productive pattern is 'revel in something' and need not involve drinking/dancing. Past/participle: reveled/revelled depending spelling convention.		
157	1562	1651	talk someone out of something / talk someone out of doing something	tɔk ˈsʌmwʌn aʊt əv ɪt	phrasal verb	To persuade someone not to do something or not to pursue a plan, purchase, decision, etc.	dissuade, persuade against, convince not to	She talked me out of quitting. / Can you talk him out of buying that car?	Normalize from fixed 'it' to productive construction. Contrast talk someone into something = persuade them to do it.		
157	1563	1652	emphatic	ɛmˈfætɪk	adjective	Expressed or done with force, certainty, clarity, or strong emphasis; leaving little doubt.	forceful, definite, unequivocal, insistent	She gave an emphatic no. / The team responded with an emphatic 4–0 victory.	Can describe speech/gestures and, in sports/journalism, decisive results. 'Assertive' describes a person's communication style and is not always a synonym.		
157	1564	1654	inutile	ɪˈnjuːtaɪl	adjective (rare/formal)	Useless, ineffective, or serving no practical purpose.	useless, ineffective, futile, unavailing	The committee concluded that further debate would be inutile.	Very rare in modern everyday English; often literary, learned, or deliberately unusual. 'Useless' is the normal everyday choice.		
157	1565	1655	fertile	ˈfɝːtəl	adjective	1. Biologically capable of reproducing. 2. Of soil/land, able to support abundant plant growth. 3. Figuratively, productive or rich in ideas, possibilities, imagination, etc.	fruitful, productive; fecund (formal); rich (soil)	The valley has fertile soil. / The discussion proved fertile ground for new ideas.	Highly polysemous. Common figurative collocation: fertile ground for. 'Rich' applies well to soil but not every sense.		
157	1566	1656	infertile	ɪnˈfɝːtəl	adjective	1. Not biologically able to reproduce successfully, or having impaired fertility. 2. Of land/soil, unable to support much growth; unproductive.	sterile (context-dependent), barren (land/context-dependent), unproductive	The soil is too infertile for most crops. / Some medical conditions can make a person infertile.	PRECISION: infertility in people is a medical concept and does not necessarily mean absolute impossibility of conception. 'Sterile' can imply complete inability and is not always interchangeable.		
157	1567	1657	deduce / deduced	dɪˈduːst	verb	To reach a conclusion by reasoning from known facts, evidence, or premises.	infer, conclude, reason out	From the wet footprints, she deduced that someone had just come inside.	Normalize to base verb while retaining source form. Pattern: deduce X from Y. This overlaps WID1324 and should be handled as a usage-family duplicate later rather than silently deleted here.		
157	1568	1658	periodontist	ˌpɛriəˈdɑːntɪst	noun	A dentist specializing in periodontology: prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of diseases affecting the gums and other supporting structures of the teeth, and often placement/management of dental implants.	gum specialist (informal), periodontal specialist	My dentist referred me to a periodontist for advanced gum treatment.	More precise than generic 'dental surgeon'. Periodontists are dental specialists, not simply anyone who treats gums.		
157	1569	1659	periodontitis	ˌpɛriəˌdɑnˈtaɪtɪs	noun (medical/dental)	A chronic inflammatory disease of the tissues supporting the teeth, associated with microbial dental plaque and progressive loss of periodontal attachment and supporting bone.	advanced gum disease, periodontal disease	Untreated periodontitis can damage the bone supporting the teeth and lead to tooth loss.	PRECISION: more than a generic 'dental infection'. It is a specific inflammatory periodontal disease; gingivitis is a related but distinct, less advanced condition.		
157	1570	1660	pass on / passed on (death euphemism)	pæst ɑn	phrasal verb (euphemistic; one of several senses)	In one euphemistic sense, to die. 'Pass on' also commonly means transmit/give something to another person or decline an opportunity, so context is essential.	die, pass away, depart (euphemistic)	Her grandfather passed on peacefully. / This card targets the death euphemism; elsewhere 'pass on a message' means transmit it.	Do not define the whole phrasal verb only as death. The death sense is gentler/euphemistic and less common than 'pass away' in some varieties.		
158	1571	1661	pass away / passed away	pæst əˈweɪ	phrasal verb (euphemistic)	To die; a common gentle or polite euphemism used when speaking about someone's death.	die, pass on, depart (euphemistic)	Her grandmother passed away peacefully last night.	Very common euphemism. 'Deceased' is normally adjective/noun, not a direct verb synonym.		
158	1572	1662	whack job / whackjob	wæk dʒɑb	noun (slang, derogatory)	A strongly insulting label for a person regarded as bizarre, irrational, extreme, or dangerously unpredictable.	nutcase, kook, crank, extremist (context-dependent)	The character dismisses him as a 'whack job.'	REGISTER WARNING: disparaging and can stigmatize mental illness; better treated as recognition vocabulary than a neutral way to describe a person. Spelling varies.		
158	1573	1663	boff	bɔf	verb; noun (slang, meanings vary)	Slang with several meanings depending on era/dialect: to hit/strike; to have sex with; noun uses can include a blow or other specialized senses.	hit, thump; have sex with (slang)	The old comic scene has one character boff another on the head. / In some slang, 'boff' means have sex with someone.	Register and meaning are highly context-dependent and somewhat dated. Do not assume the sexual sense in every occurrence.		
158	1574	1664	devil-may-care	ˈdɛvəl meɪ kɛr	adjective	Cheerfully carefree, unconcerned about consequences, and sometimes recklessly bold.	carefree, insouciant, reckless, nonchalant	He had a devil-may-care attitude toward money.	Hyphenate before a noun/adjectivally: devil-may-care attitude. It can sound admiring (bold/free) or critical (reckless).		
158	1575	1665	Clydesdale	ˈklaɪdzˌdeɪl	noun / proper breed name	A large, powerful breed of draft horse originating in Scotland, traditionally used for heavy work and known for feathering on the lower legs.	draft horse (broader category)	A team of Clydesdales pulled the wagon through the parade.	Capitalize breed name. 'Horse' and 'draft horse' are categories, not exact synonyms.		
158	1576	1666	you swell	ju swɛl	source-dependent / likely malformed phrase	As written, 'you swell' is not a standard modern compliment meaning 'you look great'. The source may involve 'swell' as an old-fashioned adjective ('You're swell') or a transcription/context issue.	you're swell, you look great (if intended)	You're swell—you really helped me out. / If the source literally says 'you swell', preserve it as source wording until context confirms the intended meaning.	CORRECTION/CAUTION: do not teach 'You swell in that suit!' as standard English. 'Swell' can be an old-fashioned adjective meaning excellent/fine: 'You're swell.' It is also a verb meaning become enlarged.		
