158	1578	1668	floss / flossing	ˈflɔsɪŋ	verb; noun	To clean between teeth with dental floss; noun floss = thin thread used for this purpose. 'Flossing' also names a dance move in pop culture, a separate sense.	clean between the teeth; dental floss	I floss every night before bed. / The dentist showed me a better flossing technique.	CORRECTION: brushing is not a synonym for flossing; they clean different tooth surfaces. Normalize to base verb while retaining source form.		
158	1579	1669	tushie / tushy	ˈtʊʃi	noun (informal, euphemistic/childlike)	The buttocks or rear end.	butt, bottom, backside, tush	The toddler fell on his tushie and started laughing.	Cute/child-directed euphemism. Spellings tushie and tushy both occur. Related WID1677 tush.		
158	1580	1670	julienne / cut something into julienne strips	du ˈdʒʊliˌɛn	verb; noun/adjective (cooking)	To cut food, especially vegetables, into thin matchstick-like strips; noun/adjective 'julienne' refers to the cut or food prepared that way.	cut into matchsticks, shred finely (not exact)	Julienne the carrots and bell peppers. / Cut the vegetables into thin julienne strips.	CORRECTION: 'do julliene' is misspelled and unnatural. Standard culinary term is julienne (one l after ju-), used as a verb or noun/adjective.		
