160	1594	1685	sow one's wild oats	soʊ jʊər waɪld oʊts	idiom (old-fashioned)	To engage in youthful, reckless, pleasure-seeking, or sexually adventurous behavior before settling into a more conventional life.	have a wild phase, live it up, play the field (sexual context)	He spent his early twenties sowing his wild oats before settling down.	Old-fashioned and historically gendered, often applied to young men. Can carry moral judgment or a double standard; use with awareness.		
160	1595	1686	wild oats	waɪld oʊts	plural noun; idiomatic element	Literally, wild oat plants; idiomatically best known from 'sow one's wild oats', meaning youthful reckless or sexually adventurous behavior.	youthful excesses, wild phase (figurative)	The phrase usually appears as 'sow your wild oats,' rather than 'a wild oats phase.'	CORRECTION: 'wild oats' by itself does not normally mean 'the period of life'. The established idiom is 'sow one's wild oats'. Keep this card as the component/family form.		
