161	1604	1695	flame-retardant / flame retardant	fleɪm rɪˈtɑːdnt	adjective; noun	adjective: designed or treated to slow ignition or the spread of flame. noun: a chemical/substance used to reduce flammability.	fire-retardant, flame-resistant (related)	The curtains are made from flame-retardant fabric. / Some products contain added flame retardants.	CORRECTION: flame-retardant does not mean fireproof or nonflammable. Hyphenate adjective before noun; noun usually open: flame retardant.		
161	1605	1696	retardant	rɪˈtɑːdnt	noun; adjective	noun: a substance that slows or inhibits a process, especially fire retardant. adjective: slowing or inhibiting a process.	inhibitor, slowing agent, suppressant (context-dependent)	Aircraft dropped fire retardant ahead of the wildfire. / The coating has flame-retardant properties.	Broader than fire, though fire retardant is the most familiar phrase. 'Deterrent' acts by discouraging behavior and is not a general synonym.		
