162	1611	1702	Boggle tournament	/ˈbɑɡəl ˈtʊrnəmənt/	noun phrase / proper game name	A competitive event in which players play Boggle, the word game where they find words in a grid of lettered dice/letters under time pressure.	word-game tournament, Boggle competition	He placed second in a Boggle tournament last weekend.	Capitalize Boggle when referring to the branded game. 'Boggle tournament' is compositional rather than a fixed idiom.		
162	1612	1703	barnacle	/ˈbɑrnəkəl/	noun	A marine crustacean that, as an adult, typically lives attached to rocks, ship hulls, whales, docks, or other surfaces; figuratively, a clingy person/thing in some contexts.	marine crustacean; hanger-on (figurative)	The hull was covered in barnacles. / The comedy portrays him as a barnacle who won't leave his friend alone.	Add figurative cling-on sense for recognition. Not all barnacles are literally 'permanently' fixed throughout every life stage.		
162	1613	1704	chickenpox	/ˈtʃɪkən ˌpɑks/	noun	A highly contagious viral disease caused by varicella-zoster virus, typically producing an itchy blister-like rash and fever.	varicella	She had chickenpox as a child. / Vaccination greatly reduces the risk of severe varicella.	CORRECTION: standard spelling is one word, chickenpox. Medical precision: caused by varicella-zoster virus; severity varies.		
