170	1692	1785	abridgment / abridgement	/əˈbrɪdʒmənt/	noun	A shortened version of a book, speech, recording, or other work that omits material while preserving the main content; also the act of shortening, and in formal contexts a reduction/curtailment of a right.	condensation, shortened version; curtailment	This audiobook is an abridgment of the novel. / The law was challenged as an abridgment of free speech.	Both spellings occur; abridgment is common in AmE, abridgement common in BrE. Add important formal 'abridgment of rights' sense.		
