170	1696	1789	butt naked	/bʌt ˈneɪkɪd/	adjective phrase (informal/slang)	Completely naked; wearing no clothes.	buck naked, stark naked, completely naked	He ran through the sprinklers butt naked.	Common informal variant alongside buck naked (WID1729). 'Butt-naked' is also written with a hyphen in some styles.		
170	1697	1790	constitute / constituting	/ˈkɑnstəˌtutɪŋ/	verb	To form, compose, or make up a whole; to amount to or be regarded as; formally, to establish/create something.	make up, form, compose; amount to; establish	Women constitute a majority of the staff. / That could constitute a breach of contract. / The committee was formally constituted in May.	CORRECTION: normalize to base verb and replace unnatural progressive example 'are constituting the foundation'. Stative/classificatory uses often prefer simple tense.		
170	1698	1791	cutoff / cut-off / cut off	/ˈkʌtˌɔf/	noun/adjective; verb phrase	Noun: a limit, deadline, threshold, stopping point, or route shortcut; adjective: isolated or truncated. Verb 'cut off' is separate and has many senses.	deadline, limit, threshold; cutoff point	The application cutoff is Friday. / The cutoff score was 80. / The village was cut off by snow.	Spelling depends on function/style: verb two words 'cut off'; noun/adjective commonly 'cutoff' in AmE, 'cut-off' also common. Also 'cutoffs' = shorts made by cutting trousers.		
170	1699	1792	dainty	/ˈdeɪnti/	adjective; noun (plural 'dainties', dated)	Delicately small, pretty, graceful, or refined; also small/light in movement or appetite. Can carry stereotypically feminine or faintly critical connotations.	delicate, petite, elegant, refined	She wore a dainty gold necklace. / He took dainty little bites.	Not simply fragile. Tone can be admiring or patronizing depending context.		
170	1700	1793	you bet	/ju bɛt/	informal phrase	Used to express strong agreement, certainty, willingness, or a friendly response to thanks; roughly 'certainly', 'absolutely', or 'you're welcome' depending context.	absolutely, certainly, of course, you're welcome	'Can you help me?' 'You bet.' / 'Thanks!' 'You bet!'	Important pragmatic range: affirmative answer and response to thanks. Related 'you betcha' is more folksy/regional.		
