174	1732	1827	Where am I giving birth—in a hospital or a big box under the stairs?	/wɛr æm aɪ ˈɡɪvɪŋ bɜrθ ɪn ə ˈhɑspɪtəl ɔr ə bɪɡ bɑks ˈʌndɚ ðə stɛrz/	source-specific rhetorical question / joke	A source-specific sarcastic comparison contrasting an appropriate place for childbirth with an absurdly inadequate one, used to complain about poor arrangements or treatment.	what kind of conditions are these?, this is ridiculous	Where am I giving birth—in a hospital or a big box under the stairs?	Do not generalize this exact wording into a standard idiom. Learning value comes from the rhetorical pattern 'Where am I doing X—in A or absurd B?' and the comic contrast. Preserve the source quote.		
174	1733	1828	bejesus	/bəˈdʒiːzəs/	noun/interjection element (informal, euphemistic)	Used mainly in emphatic expressions such as 'scare/beat/annoy the bejesus out of someone', where it intensifies the action; also appears as an exclamation.	daylights (in similar idioms), hell (stronger)	That noise scared the bejesus out of me. / They beat the bejesus out of the old machine.	KEY CONSTRUCTION is not standalone 'bejesus' so much as 'the bejesus out of someone'. Historically a euphemistic alteration associated with 'by Jesus'; informal and comic.		
174	1734	1829	the score is X–Y in favor of someone / in someone's favor	/skɔr ɪz ɪn ˈfeɪvɚ əv/	sports phrase	Used to state that a team/person is leading by a particular score; more generally, 'in someone's favor' means to that person's advantage.	be leading, be ahead, favor	The score is 3–1 in favor of the home team. / The ruling went in her favor.	Natural sports order is often 'The score is 3–1 to the home team' (BrE) or 'The home team leads 3–1' (AmE). Teach broader collocation 'in someone's favor'.		
174	1735	1830	rile someone up / get riled up	/raɪld ʌp/	phrasal verb / adjective phrase (informal)	To make someone angry, agitated, excited, or emotionally stirred; 'get/be riled up' describes that state.	agitate, provoke, stir up, get worked up	Don't rile him up before the meeting. / She got riled up about the decision.	KEY FORMS: 'rile someone up'; 'get/be riled up about/over something'. Usually anger/agitation, though excited stirring is possible.		
174	1736	1831	dibs / call dibs on something	/dɪbz/	plural-form noun / informal expression	An informal claim to have or use something before others, usually established by saying 'dibs' or 'I call dibs'.	claim, first claim, first pick	I call dibs on the front seat. / Dibs on the last slice!	KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'call dibs on + thing' or simply 'Dibs on X!' Normally treated as plural-form 'dibs', not singular 'a dib'.		
