180	1798	1896	faun	/fɔn/	noun (mythology)	A Roman mythological woodland/rustic deity or spirit, typically represented in later art/literature as partly human and partly goatlike; often compared with Greek satyrs.	woodland spirit; satyr (related, not exact)	Mr. Tumnus is a faun in The Chronicles of Narnia.	PRECISION: fauns and Greek satyrs became heavily conflated in later tradition but are not simply exact equivalents in all historical mythology.		
180	1799	1897	unassuming	/ˌʌnəˈsumɪŋ/	adjective	Not pretentious, boastful, conspicuous, or eager to attract attention; modest in manner or appearance.	modest, low-key, unobtrusive, humble	Despite his success, he remained quiet and unassuming. / The restaurant looks unassuming from outside.	Can describe people, buildings, places, objects—not only personality. 'Reserved' means restrained/socially quiet and is not always synonymous.		
180	1800	1898	blah / feel or seem blah	/ˈsʌmwʌn ɪz blɑː/	adjective-like informal expression; interjection/noun	Informally, dull, uninteresting, low-energy, vaguely unwell, or emotionally flat; 'blah blah blah' can represent boring/irrelevant talk.	meh, dull, low-energy, uninspired	I feel kind of blah today. / The room looked a little blah, so we added some plants.	NORMALIZE beyond 'someone is blah'. Common frames: feel/seem/look blah; 'blah' can describe mood, appearance, food, design, etc.		
181	1801	1899	be a hoot / someone is a hoot	/ˈsʌmwʌn ɪz ə huːt/	informal expression	To be very funny, entertaining, amusing, or enjoyably eccentric; 'a hoot' can also mean a very enjoyable time/event.	a riot, hilarious, a character; great fun	Your grandmother is a hoot. / The party was an absolute hoot.	Broaden beyond people: 'The trip was a hoot.' Usually affectionate/positive.		
181	1802	1900	yesteryear	/ˈjɛstɚˌjɪr/	noun (literary / nostalgic)	The past, especially an earlier era recalled nostalgically or as having a distinctive old-fashioned character.	days gone by, bygone days, the old days	The town still has the charm of yesteryear.	Usually uncountable/singular and literary/nostalgic. Common phrase: 'the ___ of yesteryear'.		
181	1803	1901	flaky	/ˈfleɪki/	adjective (informal); adjective (literal)	Informally, unreliable, inconsistent, or prone to canceling/forgetting commitments. Literally, consisting of or breaking into thin flakes/layers.	unreliable, inconsistent, undependable; layered, flaking	He's flaky about plans and cancels at the last minute. / The pastry was light and flaky.	Important literal + figurative senses. For a person, 'flaky' often suggests unreliability or scattered behavior, not simply eccentricity.		
181	1804	1902	eulogy	/ˈjuːlədʒi/	noun	A speech or piece of writing praising and remembering someone, especially one delivered at or around a funeral; more broadly, formal praise.	tribute, memorial speech, encomium (formal)	She delivered a moving eulogy for her grandfather.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: give/deliver/write a eulogy; a eulogy for/to someone. It is usually associated with the dead, but the broader historical sense is praise.		
181	1805	1903	Friends don't let friends + verb	/frɛndz doʊnt lɛt frɛndz juz snoʊ kloʊnz/	snowclone / productive meme template	A humorous warning/advice template modeled on 'Friends don't let friends drive drunk': 'Friends don't let friends + undesirable action.' The source 'Friends don't let friends use snowclones' is itself a meta-snowclone.	meme template, formulaic slogan, phrasal template	Friends don't let friends use Comic Sans. / 'Friends don't let friends use snowclones' jokingly uses the very template it criticizes.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'Friends don't let friends + bare verb'. A snowclone is a recognizable, adaptable phrase template whose slots can be replaced. Repair mojibake in current synonym.		
181	1806	1904	anthropomorphic	/ˌænθrəpəˈmɔːrfɪk/	adjective	Having or being represented with human form, traits, emotions, intentions, speech, or behavior despite being nonhuman.	humanlike, personified, human-shaped (context-dependent)	The film features an anthropomorphic fox who talks and wears clothes. / People often use anthropomorphic language about computers that 'want' something.	Related noun/process: anthropomorphism; verb: anthropomorphize. Not limited to physical shape—mental/emotional human traits count too.		
181	1807	1905	Fruit Roll-Ups	/fruːt roʊl ʌps/	brand / plural product name	A branded U.S. fruit-flavored chewy snack sold as a thin flexible sheet that can be unrolled and eaten.	fruit snack	Do we have any Fruit Roll-Ups?	Brand/cultural-reference card. Keep capitalization and plural product name. Ingredient formulations can change, so avoid defining the product by one specific ingredient list.		
181	1808	1906	frat / fraternity	/fræt/ /frəˈtɜrnəti/	noun (informal abbreviation); noun	'Frat' is an informal abbreviation for a college fraternity. A fraternity is an organized brotherhood/association; in U.S. campus culture, it often means a Greek-letter social organization, traditionally male, though the broader word has other uses.	fraternity, Greek-letter organization; brotherhood, association	He joined a fraternity in college. / There's a party at the frat house tonight.	KEY RELATION: frat ← fraternity. 'Frat house' = house associated with a fraternity. Do not define fraternity only by parties; campus fraternities vary, and 'fraternity' also has broader association/brotherhood senses.		
181	1809	1908	blindside / be blindsided	/ˈblaɪndˌsaɪdɪd/	verb; adjective/participle	To hit, attack, criticize, or surprise someone from an unexpected direction or without warning; figuratively, to catch someone completely unprepared.	catch off guard, surprise, ambush	The announcement blindsided the staff. / I was completely blindsided by the breakup.	Normalize to productive verb + passive. KEY FRAME: 'blindside someone with/by...' / 'be blindsided by...'. Figurative surprise sense is extremely common.		
181	1810	1909	take a hint	/teɪk ə hɪnt/	idiom	To understand and respond appropriately to an indirect suggestion, signal, rejection, or request rather than requiring it to be stated explicitly.	get the message, catch on, read the room	I've stopped replying—why won't he take a hint? / Take the hint and give them some privacy.	KEY COLLOCATION: 'take the/a hint'; often negative or imperative: 'can't/won't take a hint', 'Take a hint.' Can sound impatient.		
182	1811	1910	I wanna say... / I want to say...	/aɪ ˈwɑnə seɪ/	conversational discourse marker	Used before a tentative answer or recollection to signal that the speaker thinks something is true but is not fully certain; 'want to say' also has its ordinary literal meaning of wishing to express something.	I think, if I remember right, I believe	I wanna say it was around 2003, but I'm not sure. / I want to say her name was Rachel.	KEY CONSTRUCTION in recall contexts: 'I want/wanna say + tentative answer'. 'Wanna' represents informal speech and should not be used in formal writing. Distinguish literal 'I want to say something'.		
182	1812	1911	luck out / lucked out	/lʌkt aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal, especially AmE)	To have unexpectedly good luck or obtain a fortunate outcome.	get lucky, catch a break, strike it lucky (BrE)	We really lucked out with the weather. / I lucked out and found the last available ticket.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAME: 'luck out with/on...' and 'luck out and + verb'. In modern AmE it normally means GOOD luck.		
182	1813	1912	be overdue / that's overdue	/ðæts ˌoʊvɚˈduː/	adjective / conversational phrase	To be later than the expected, scheduled, or appropriate time; of something desirable or necessary, 'That's overdue' means it should have happened earlier.	late, past due; long overdue, about time	The payment is overdue. / An apology is long overdue. / He finally apologized—about time; that was overdue.	Broaden beyond the source sentence. Common collocations: overdue payment/book/bill; long-overdue reform/apology/change. 'Finally' is not a synonym by itself.		
182	1814	1913	let someone feel someone up	/lɛt ˈsʌmwʌn fiːl ʌp/	sexual slang construction	To allow someone to touch/grope another person's body sexually. The core phrasal verb is 'feel someone up'; 'let' adds permission: 'let + person + feel + person + up'.	allow someone to grope/fondle someone	She couldn't believe he expected her to let him feel her up on the first date.	CORRECTION: current example 'let him feel up' is incomplete/unnatural for the intended transitive sense. KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'feel + person + up'; with let: 'let him feel me/her/you up'. Consent must not be inferred merely from the phrase outside explicit context.		
182	1815	1914	maul / mauled	/mɔld/	verb	To attack and seriously injure by tearing, crushing, biting, or battering; figuratively, to defeat or criticize very severely.	mangle, savage, tear into; crush, thrash	The hikers were mauled by a bear. / Their team got mauled in the second half.	Normalize base + source form. Sports/figurative 'maul' is strong informal imagery. 'Attacked' is broader and does not necessarily imply severe injury.		
182	1816	1915	fake out / fake someone out	/feɪk aʊt/	phrasal verb; noun/adjective element (informal)	To deceive or misdirect someone with a feint, false move, or misleading appearance, especially in sports; a 'fake-out' can be the deceptive move itself.	feint, trick, fool, misdirect	She faked the defender out with a quick step. / The apparent ending was a fake-out.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'fake + person + out'; also 'fake-out' as noun/adjective. Sports feints are prototypical, but narrative/prank uses occur.		
182	1817	1916	hold someone/something back	/hoʊld ˈsʌmwʌn ˈsʌmθɪŋ bæk/	phrasal verb	To restrain physically; prevent progress/development; suppress emotion/information; or keep someone from advancing to the next level/grade.	restrain, hinder, impede, suppress	Fear was holding her back. / He struggled to hold back tears. / The guards held the crowd back.	Major constructions: hold someone back; hold back tears/laughter/information; hold something back from someone. The object and context determine the sense.		
182	1818	1917	mooch off/from someone	/ˈmuːtʃɪŋ frəm/ɔf ʌs/	phrasal verb / slang	To obtain food, money, housing, favors, etc. from someone repeatedly or opportunistically without contributing fairly; also 'mooch something off someone'.	freeload, sponge off, bum something off	He's been mooching off his parents for months. / Can I mooch a ride off you?	KEY FRAMES: 'mooch off someone' = live/take advantage of their generosity; 'mooch something off someone' = obtain a specific thing/favor. 'Mooch from' occurs, but 'mooch off' is especially idiomatic.		
182	1819	1919	patch things up / patch up a relationship	/pætʃ θɪŋz ʌp/	phrasal verb / idiom	To repair a damaged relationship, disagreement, or situation and restore friendlier relations; literally, 'patch up' can make a temporary repair.	reconcile, make up, mend fences; repair	They patched things up after the argument. / I hope you two can patch up your friendship.	KEY METAPHOR: literal patching repairs a tear/damage; figuratively 'patch things up with someone' repairs a relationship. Also literal 'patch up a hole/wound'.		
182	1820	1920	blow someone off	/bloʊ ʌs ɔf/	phrasal verb (informal)	To ignore, dismiss, avoid, or fail to meet/respond to someone, often casually or disrespectfully; also to disregard an obligation or invitation.	ignore, brush off, ditch, stand up (specific context)	He blew us off and never showed up. / She keeps blowing off my messages.	KEY FRAME: 'blow + person/thing + off'. Can blow off a person, meeting, class, responsibility. 'Stand someone up' specifically means fail to appear for an arranged meeting/date.		
183	1821	1921	freeze someone out	/friːz ˈsʌmwʌn aʊt/	phrasal verb / idiom	To deliberately exclude someone from a group, relationship, discussion, information flow, market, or opportunity, often by cold or uncooperative treatment.	exclude, ostracize, shut out, ice out	They froze him out of the meetings. / Smaller competitors were being frozen out of the market.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'freeze + person/group + out (of something)'. Metaphor of social/economic coldness; broader than merely refusing to speak.		
183	1822	1922	stifle someone / stifle something	/ˈstaɪfəl ˈsʌmwʌn/	verb	To make someone feel unable to act, grow, breathe, or express themselves freely; to suppress an emotion, sound, idea, debate, or development.	suppress, smother, restrain, inhibit	She felt stifled by the rules. / He stifled a laugh. / Excessive control can stifle creativity.	This overlaps the stifle family already reviewed at WID1713. Preserve for now; teach productive objects: stifle a laugh/yawn/cry, stifle debate/innovation, feel stifled.		
