184	1834	1936	heave / heaved	/hiːvd/	verb; noun	To lift, pull, push, or throw with great effort; to rise/fall strongly; to breathe/sigh convulsively; to retch or vomit. Noun: a strong pull/effort or upward movement.	haul, hoist, hurl; retch	He heaved the suitcase into the trunk. / Her chest heaved as she ran. / He leaned over the sink and heaved.	Normalize base + source form. Important patterns: heave a sigh; chest/stomach heaves; heave something over/into; dry-heave.		
184	1835	1937	visit a town a little south of Throw Up	/ˈvɪzɪt ə taʊn ə ˈlɪtl saʊθ əv θroʊ ʌp/	source-specific humorous euphemism	A source-specific comic metaphor for being extremely nauseated or close to vomiting, imagining 'Throw Up' as a place and one's condition as geographically near it.	be nauseated, be close to throwing up, feel sick	After the ride, I felt like I was visiting a town just south of Throw Up.	Do not teach as a standard idiom. WORDPLAY: 'south of X' literally means below X on a map; here the speaker turns 'Throw Up' into a fictional place to locate their nausea just short of vomiting.		
