187	1861	1964	qualitative	/ˈkwɑːlɪˌteɪtɪv/	adjective	Relating to qualities, characteristics, meanings, or kinds rather than numerical quantity; in research, involving non-numerical data/interpretive analysis, though qualitative work can still use counts or structured methods.	descriptive, interpretive; non-quantitative (context-dependent)	The researchers conducted qualitative interviews about patient experiences. / There was a qualitative change in the tone of the debate.	Contrast quantitative. Important broader sense: 'a qualitative difference/change' = difference in kind or character, not merely research methodology.		
187	1862	1965	increment / increments	/ˈɪŋkrəmənts/	noun; verb (technical)	A small increase or step in a series; the amount by which a variable changes. As a verb, especially technical/computing, to increase by a specified amount.	step, increase, addition	Increase the dose in small increments. / The counter increments by one each time.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: in increments of X; small/regular increments; increment a counter/value. Normalize singular/plural.		
187	1863	1966	attenuate / attenuated	/əˈtɛnjueɪtɪd/	verb; adjective/participle	To reduce the strength, intensity, effect, concentration, or amplitude of something; in microbiology, to weaken a pathogen's virulence. 'Attenuated' = weakened/reduced.	weaken, reduce, diminish, dampen	Walls attenuate the wireless signal. / Some vaccines use live attenuated organisms.	Normalize base + source form. Technical collocations: attenuate a signal/sound/effect; attenuated virus/vaccine. 'Dilute' is only appropriate in some contexts.		
187	1864	1967	roll off	/roʊl ɔf/	phrasal verb; noun/adjective 'roll-off'	To roll and fall/move off a surface; to come off a production line; in audio/electronics, for response/level to decrease progressively beyond a frequency or point.	roll away/off; decline, attenuate	The bottle rolled off the table. / High frequencies begin to roll off above 10 kHz. / New cars rolled off the assembly line.	KEY technical sense: frequency response 'rolls off'; noun 'roll-off' = rate/character of attenuation. Add productive 'roll off the assembly line'.		
187	1865	1968	FWIW	/ɛf ˈdʌbəlju aɪ ˈdʌbəlju/	initialism / internet-text abbreviation	For what it's worth; used to introduce an opinion, fact, or suggestion while modestly acknowledging that it may have limited relevance or value.	for what it's worth, if it helps, just my two cents	FWIW, I think the first version reads more naturally.	Register: common in texts/forums/email, often read as the full phrase rather than spoken letter-by-letter in conversation. It can soften a contribution.		
