190	1893	1997	bootstrap / bootstrapped	/ˈbuːtˌstræpt/	verb; adjective/participle (business/computing)	In business, to start/build a venture using founders' own resources and operating revenue rather than substantial outside funding. More broadly/technically, to initiate a process using minimal existing resources; computing 'bootstrap' underlies 'boot'.	self-fund, build from scratch (context-dependent)	They bootstrapped the company for three years before raising outside capital. / The system uses a bootstrap process to load the operating environment.	Normalize base + source form. Business meaning is not simply 'without outside help'—a bootstrapped firm may use customers, loans, small grants, etc.; core idea is limited external equity/funding. Related noun: bootstrapping.		
