191	1910	2015	restitution	/ˌrɛstəˈtuːʃən/	noun	The restoration of something to its rightful owner or condition, or payment/compensation ordered or made for loss, damage, or wrongdoing; legal meaning varies by context.	restoration, compensation, reparation	The court ordered restitution to the victims. / The museum agreed to restitution of the looted artwork.	KEY FRAMES: make/pay restitution; restitution to victims; restitution of property. Not identical to reimbursement, which usually repays expenses.		
192	1911	2016	imbue / be imbued with	/ɪmˈbjud/	verb; passive collocation	To fill, permeate, or strongly influence someone/something with a quality, feeling, idea, or value.	infuse, permeate, instill	Her speech was imbued with hope. / The novel imbues ordinary objects with symbolic meaning.	Normalize base + source form. KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'imbue X with Y'; passive 'X is imbued with Y'. 'Instill' typically takes a different frame: instill Y in/into X.		
192	1912	2017	badger / badgers	/ˈbædʒɚz/	noun; verb	noun: a burrowing mammal of the mustelid family/group. verb: to repeatedly pester, pressure, or nag someone, especially with requests or questions.	pester, nag, harass; badger (animal)	He kept badgering me to lend him the car. / Badgers live in underground setts.	Normalize singular/base. KEY VERB FRAME: badger someone about/for/into something. Animal species differ by region.		
