193	1921	2026	sequester / sequestered	/sɪˈkwɛstɚd/	verb; adjective/participle	To isolate or keep apart; especially to isolate a jury from outside influence. In law/finance, to seize, set aside, or restrict property/funds depending context.	isolate, segregate, set apart; impound (specific legal sense)	The judge sequestered the jury. / The cabin was sequestered deep in the woods.	Normalize base + source form. 'Secluded' overlaps place sense but not legal jury/property senses. Technical meanings vary by jurisdiction.		
