193	1927	2032	wear something out / wore out my first copy — Friends	/aɪ wɔr aʊt maɪ fɝst ˈkɑpi wɛn aɪ wəz wɪð ju/	phrasal verb + source quote	To use something so much that it becomes worn, damaged, or unusable; also to exhaust a person. In the Friends line, Rachel's 'I wore out my first copy when I was with you' is a sexual joke implying very heavy use of the book while she and Ross were together.	use up, wear down; exhaust	I wore out my first pair of running shoes. / The kids wore me out. / Rachel: 'I wore out my first copy when I was with you.'	KEY CONSTRUCTION: 'wear + object + out'; passive 'be worn out'. For objects = deteriorate through use; for people = exhaust. The source joke depends on the ordinary phrasal verb plus sexual context; preserve that context rather than making the quote itself an idiom.		
