196	1955	2061	touché	/tuːˈʃeɪ/	interjection	Used to acknowledge that another person has made a clever, effective, or fair point against you, especially in witty verbal exchange; originally a fencing term meaning 'touched/hit'.	fair point, well played, you got me	A: You complain about being late, but you were late yesterday. B: Touché.	CORRECTION: spelling takes accent in standard presentation: touché (though touche appears informally). The fencing origin explains the metaphor of conceding a verbal 'hit'.		
