203	2029	2143	troll / trolling	/ˈtroʊlɪŋ/	verb; noun / internet slang	To deliberately provoke, bait, disrupt, or deceive people online for reaction/amusement; 'troll' can mean the person or the act. Broader online use sometimes labels insincere provocative behavior.	bait, provoke, antagonize; troll	He's trolling the replies for attention. / Don't feed the trolls.	Modern usage is broader than simply posting 'off-topic' messages. KEY phrase: 'Don't feed the trolls' = don't reward provocation with engagement.		
203	2030	2144	play off / playoff / play off against	/pleɪ ɔf wɪð/	verb phrase; noun	In sports, teams/players may 'play off' or 'play off against each other' to decide a tie/qualification; noun 'playoff' = deciding postseason/tiebreak competition. 'Play X off against Y' also means manipulate rivals against each other.	play a decider, face off; pit against	The teams will play off for the final spot. / The manager played the two departments off against each other.	CORRECTION: 'play off with each other' is unnatural for the intended sports sense. Distinguish verb 'play off' from noun/adjective 'playoff'.		
