209	2088	2205	nothing to write home about	/ɪt ɪz ˈnʌθɪŋ tə raɪt hoʊm əˌbaʊt/	idiom	Not especially good, impressive, unusual, or noteworthy; merely ordinary or mediocre.	unremarkable, mediocre, nothing special	The food was fine, but nothing to write home about.	Common frame: 'X is/was nothing to write home about.' Often mildly negative rather than strongly critical.		
209	2089	2206	push through / push through it	/pʊʃ θru ɪt/	phrasal verb	To continue through difficulty, resistance, fatigue, discomfort, or an obstacle until reaching the other side or completing something; transitively, also to force a proposal/process through.	persevere, power through, press on; force through	I was tired, but I pushed through and finished the workout. / They pushed the bill through despite opposition.	KEY FORMS: push through; push through it; push X through. CAUTION: pain/illness is not always something one should simply 'push through'; source example with fever was poor general advice.		
209	2090	2207	moon someone / moon at someone	/mun ɪn ˈdraɪvɪŋ/	verb (slang, informal)	To expose one's bare buttocks to someone as a prank, insult, or act of provocation, sometimes from a moving vehicle.	bare one's buttocks, flash (broader)	A passenger leaned out and mooned the car behind them.	CORRECTION: 'moon in driving' is not a natural English construction. Productive forms are 'moon someone', 'moon at someone', or describe 'mooning from a car'.		
210	2091	2208	double jeopardy	/ˈdʌbəl ˈdʒɛpɚdi/	noun (legal term)	Being prosecuted or punished again for the same offense after a legally significant prior proceeding; the precise protection and when it attaches vary by legal system.	second prosecution for the same offense (descriptive)	The defense argued that a second trial would violate the rule against double jeopardy.	PRECISION: do not state a universal rule across all legal systems. In U.S. constitutional law, the Double Jeopardy Clause has specific requirements/exceptions; 'repeat trial' is not a true synonym.		
210	2092	2209	wouldn't put it past someone (to do something)	/pʊt ɪt pæst ju/	idiom	Used to say you believe someone is quite capable of doing something surprising, sneaky, dishonest, extreme, etc.	wouldn't be surprised if, consider someone capable of	I wouldn't put it past him to fake the whole thing. / I wouldn't put that past her.	CORRECTION: core idiom is normally NEGATIVE: 'I wouldn't put it past someone (to...)'. 'Put it past you' alone does not mean the same thing.		
210	2093	2210	loose end / tie up loose ends	/luːs ɛnd/	idiom / noun phrase	An unresolved detail, unfinished task, or remaining problem; 'tie up loose ends' = finish or resolve the remaining details.	unfinished business, unresolved detail, outstanding matter	I have a few loose ends to tie up before I leave. / Let's tie up the loose ends before launch.	Teach the productive idiom, not only the noun. Singular 'a loose end'; plural is especially common in 'tie up loose ends'.		
210	2094	2211	sorghum	/ˈsɔrɡəm/	noun	A cereal grass (genus Sorghum) cultivated for grain, animal feed, syrup, biofuel, and other uses; also the grain/product itself.	cereal grain, grain crop	Sorghum is drought-tolerant and widely grown in hot, dry regions.	CORRECTION: remove mojibake/garbled text. Sorghum is not simply a synonym of millet; they are different cereal crops.		
210	2095	2212	sass / sass someone	/sæs/	noun; verb (informal)	Bold, cheeky, or disrespectful talk/attitude; verb: to speak back to someone in an impudent or cheeky way.	backtalk, cheek, lip; talk back to	Don't give me any sass. / He got in trouble for sassing his teacher.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: give someone sass; sass someone; sassy. Tone can range from playful boldness to disrespect depending context.		
210	2096	2213	wreak havoc (on/with something)	/rikt ˈhævək/	idiom / verb collocation	To cause extensive damage, disruption, confusion, or disorder.	devastate, disrupt badly, cause chaos	The storm wreaked havoc on the coastline. / The bug wreaked havoc with the database.	Normalize from past tense. KEY COLLOCATION: WREAK havoc—not 'wreck havoc'. Common prepositions: wreak havoc on/with.		
210	2097	2214	cull	/kʌl/	verb; noun	To select and remove members/items from a group, especially animals, plants, data, or material; often to reduce numbers or remove unwanted cases. Noun: the act or selected group removed.	thin out, remove selectively, weed out	Officials culled infected animals from the herd. / We culled duplicate entries from the list.	Broaden beyond 'inferior or surplus': disease/control/data selection may motivate culling. 'Cull' can also mean select/gather choice items in older/formal use.		
210	2098	2215	don't break your arm patting yourself on the back	/doʊnt breɪk jʊɚ ɑrm ˈpætɪŋ jʊɚˈsɛlf ɑn ðə bæk/	sarcastic idiom / humorous expression	A sarcastic warning not to congratulate or praise yourself too enthusiastically.	don't be so self-congratulatory, don't get smug	You fixed one typo—don't break your arm patting yourself on the back.	KEY BASE IDIOM: 'pat yourself on the back' = congratulate yourself. This expression exaggerates it into an injury for sarcasm.		
210	2099	2216	TBD	/tiː biː ˈdiː/	abbreviation	To be determined (and sometimes contextually 'to be decided'): used as a placeholder when a detail has not yet been settled.	pending, undecided, not yet determined	The venue is TBD. / Start time: TBD.	Common in schedules, project plans, event listings, tables. Distinguish TBA = to be announced and TBC = to be confirmed/continued depending context.		
210	2100	2217	sandbox	/ˈsændˌbɑks/	noun; verb (computing)	A box/area of sand for play; in computing/security, an isolated environment in which code, files, or changes can run with restricted effects on the wider system. Verb: to isolate in such an environment.	test environment, isolated environment, playground	Run the untrusted code in a sandbox. / The app sandboxes third-party processes.	Broaden beyond merely 'safe testing': isolation/containment is the key computing idea. Also used metaphorically for experimentation.		
211	2101	2218	appraiser	/əˈpreɪzɚ/	noun	A person qualified or engaged to estimate the monetary value of property, assets, art, jewelry, etc.	valuer, valuation expert, assessor (context-dependent)	We hired an appraiser to value the house before refinancing.	KEY WORD FAMILY: appraise = estimate value; appraisal = valuation. An 'assessor' may have a specific tax/official role and is not always interchangeable.		
211	2102	2219	immunity	/ɪˈmjuːnəti/	noun	Protection from or reduced susceptibility to disease; exemption/protection from a legal duty, prosecution, suit, or consequence; more broadly, freedom from an effect.	resistance; exemption, protection	Vaccination can produce immunity to a disease. / The witness received immunity from prosecution.	KEY FRAMES: immunity to/from disease/effect; immunity from prosecution/liability. Legal forms include diplomatic, sovereign, qualified immunity, etc., with distinct rules.		
211	2103	2220	liability	/ˌlaɪəˈbɪləti/	noun	Legal responsibility for harm/debt; a financial obligation/debt; or a person/thing that creates disadvantage, risk, or burden.	legal responsibility, obligation, debt; drawback, burden	The company denied liability for the accident. / The loan appears as a liability on the balance sheet. / His temper became a political liability.	Important legal + accounting + figurative senses. Contrast asset vs liability in accounting.		
211	2104	2222	shortchange / shortchanged	/ʃɔrtʧeɪndʒd/	verb	Literally, to give someone less change than they are owed; figuratively, to give someone less than they deserve, expect, or were promised.	cheat, underpay, deprive, give short shrift to (different construction)	The cashier accidentally shortchanged me. / The adaptation shortchanged the supporting characters.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAME: shortchange someone (on something). Figurative use is common for inadequate attention, credit, value, etc.		
211	2105	2223	ultimatum	/ˌʌltəˈmeɪtəm/	noun	A final demand or set of terms whose rejection will trigger stated or implied consequences.	final demand, take-it-or-leave-it demand	She gave him an ultimatum: apologize or leave. / The union rejected the company's ultimatum.	Repair punctuation. KEY COLLOCATIONS: issue/give/deliver an ultimatum; ultimatum to someone; deadline may accompany it but is not required.		
211	2106	2224	sorority	/səˈrɔrəti/	noun	A social organization for women students/alumnae, especially in U.S. colleges and universities; some are part of Greek-letter systems, while organizations and membership practices vary.	women's Greek-letter organization, sisterhood (broader)	She joined a sorority during her first year of college.	'Female fraternity' can describe historical naming but is not a clean everyday synonym. Sororities may be social, professional, service-based, etc.		
211	2107	2226	walk on eggshells / be walking on eggshells	/ˈwɑkɪŋ ɑn ˈɛɡˌʃɛlz/	idiom	To behave extremely carefully around someone or a sensitive situation because a small mistake may cause anger, conflict, or trouble.	tread carefully, tiptoe around, be extremely cautious	Everyone was walking on eggshells around him after the argument.	Normalize to productive base/state. KEY FRAME: walk on eggshells around + person; metaphor = fragile surface that may crack.		
211	2108	2227	engulf / be engulfed in/by	/ɪnˈɡʌlft/	verb	To flow over, surround, cover, or overwhelm completely; figuratively, emotions, darkness, chaos, etc. can engulf someone/something.	swallow up, envelop, overwhelm, consume	The building was engulfed in flames. / She was engulfed by panic.	Normalize base + passive. KEY PATTERNS: engulf X; be engulfed in flames/smoke/darkness; engulfed by emotion.		
211	2109	2228	crisscross	/ˈkrɪsˌkrɔs/	verb; noun; adjective/adverb	To cross back and forth or form a network/pattern of intersecting lines; a crossing pattern or movement.	cross, intersect, zigzag, traverse	Power lines crisscross the neighborhood. / We crisscrossed the city all afternoon.	Broaden grammar. 'Crisscross the country/city' = travel repeatedly across it; not necessarily a literal visible X pattern.		
211	2110	2229	prescriptive	/prɪˈskrɪptɪv/	adjective	Giving rules or recommendations about how something should be done; in language, prescribing 'correct' usage rather than merely describing actual usage.	normative, rule-setting, directive	The guide is deliberately prescriptive about punctuation. / Prescriptive grammar tells speakers how they ought to use language.	Important contrast: prescriptive vs descriptive, especially linguistics. 'Dictatorial' is too strong as a general synonym.		
212	2111	2230	wing it	/wɪŋ ɪt/	idiom (informal)	To perform, speak, decide, or handle something with little or no preparation, relying on improvisation.	improvise, ad-lib, make it up as one goes	I didn't prepare a speech, so I'll just wing it.	Repair punctuation. KEY FRAME is fixed 'wing it'; the object is usually the pronoun 'it'.		
212	2112	2231	bop / bopping	/ˈbɑpɪŋ/	verb; noun (informal)	To dance or move rhythmically, especially casually to music; to move along briskly/casually; to hit lightly. Noun: a bop can be a dance/movement, and slang 'a bop' can mean a catchy/good song.	dance, bounce, groove; tap, hit	We were bopping along to the music. / I'll bop over to the store. / That song is a bop.	Normalize base + source form. Modern slang noun 'a bop' = enjoyable/catchy song is highly useful.		
212	2113	2232	OB-GYN / ob-gyn	/ˌoʊbiːˈdʒiːwaɪɛn/	noun; adjective (abbreviation)	An obstetrician-gynecologist, or the medical specialty/practice of obstetrics and gynecology; can also modify clinic/appointment/care.	obstetrician-gynecologist, obstetrics and gynecology	I have an OB-GYN appointment next week. / She works in OB-GYN.	CORRECTION: standard styling commonly OB-GYN, ob-gyn, or OB/GYN—not 'obgyn' only. It can refer to the doctor or specialty/context.		
212	2114	2233	scrotum	/ˈskroʊtəm/	noun (anatomy)	The external sac of skin and associated tissues that contains the testes and helps regulate their temperature.	scrotal sac (descriptive)	The testes are located within the scrotum.	PRECISION: 'testicle sac' is informal/nontechnical. Temperature regulation involves scrotal muscles and vascular mechanisms; avoid implying the skin alone does all regulation.		
212	2115	2234	innocuous	/ɪˈnɑkjuəs/	adjective	Not harmful, dangerous, offensive, or likely to cause trouble; seemingly harmless.	harmless, inoffensive, benign	It looked like an innocuous question, but it revealed a major problem.	Often 'seemingly innocuous' when something later proves consequential. 'Benign' overlaps medical/general harmlessness but has technical senses.		
212	2116	2236	allegiance	/əˈliːdʒəns/	noun	Loyalty, commitment, or duty of support to a person, group, country, cause, institution, etc.	loyalty, fidelity, commitment	The soldiers swore allegiance to the crown. / His political allegiances changed over time.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: pledge/swear allegiance to; allegiance to a country/cause. Can be formal or figurative.		
212	2117	2237	fealty	/ˈfiːəlti/	noun (historical/formal)	Loyalty owed or pledged to a lord or superior, especially in a feudal relationship; figuratively, devoted loyalty.	allegiance, fidelity, loyalty	The vassal swore fealty to his lord. / Critics accused the board of fealty to the CEO.	Historical sense is specific to feudal obligation/homage. Figurative modern use is elevated and often critical.		
212	2118	2238	bend the knee to someone / bent the knee	/bɛnt ðə niː tə ˈsʌmwʌn/	idiom / historical-ceremonial phrase	Literally, to kneel as an act of submission, homage, or allegiance; figuratively, to submit to someone's authority or dominance.	submit to, bow to, yield to, swear allegiance to	He refused to bend the knee to the new ruler. / Eventually, the rival faction bent the knee.	Normalize base + source past. KEY CONSTRUCTION: bend the knee to + person/power. Strong fantasy/historical resonance in modern usage.		
212	2119	2239	sway someone / sway someone's opinion	/tə sweɪ ˈsʌmwʌn/	verb construction	To influence or change someone's opinion, decision, feelings, or course of action; literally, to move slowly from side to side.	influence, persuade, move; rock	The evidence swayed several undecided voters. / Her argument didn't sway my opinion. / The trees swayed in the wind.	Normalize from 'to sway someone'. Add literal motion. KEY frames: sway someone; sway someone's opinion/vote; be swayed by.		
212	2120	2240	penchant	/ˈpɛnʃənt/	noun	A strong, noticeable, or habitual liking/tendency for something.	fondness, inclination, predilection, weakness for	He has a penchant for elaborate metaphors.	KEY FRAME: a penchant for + noun/gerund. Slightly formal; 'predilection' is similarly formal.		
213	2121	2241	snap / snapped	/snæpt/	verb	To break suddenly; make a sharp sound/movement; speak sharply; or suddenly lose emotional self-control under pressure.	break, crack; lash out, lose it	The branch snapped. / She snapped at me. / After weeks of stress, he finally snapped.	CORRECTION: current card captured only one sense. KEY distinctions: snap at someone = speak irritably; snap = lose control; object can physically snap.		
213	2122	2242	insolvent	/ɪnˈsɑlvənt/	adjective	Unable to pay debts as they fall due and/or having liabilities exceeding assets under an applicable legal/accounting test; exact definition varies by jurisdiction.	unable to pay debts, financially insolvent	The company became insolvent after months of cash-flow problems.	PRECISION: 'bankrupt' is not a universal synonym; bankruptcy is a legal status/process, while insolvency is a financial condition/test.		
213	2123	2243	infantry	/ˈɪnfəntri/	noun	Soldiers trained and organized primarily to fight on foot, including modern infantry that may be transported by vehicles or aircraft.	foot soldiers, ground combat troops	The infantry advanced after the artillery barrage.	PRECISION: 'fight on foot' describes combat role, not necessarily how troops travel. Types include light, mechanized, motorized, airborne infantry.		
213	2124	2244	wrap one's head around something	/ræp wʌnz hɛd əˈraʊnd/	idiom (informal)	To understand, mentally grasp, or come to terms with something difficult, complex, surprising, or emotionally hard to accept.	grasp, comprehend, get one's mind around, process	I'm still trying to wrap my head around how the system works. / I can't wrap my head around the ending.	KEY FRAME: wrap my/your/his... head around + noun/clause. Can mean intellectual comprehension or emotional acceptance.		
213	2125	2245	frazzled	/ˈfræzəld/	adjective (informal)	Mentally or emotionally exhausted, stressed, disorganized, or agitated, especially after too many demands.	stressed out, harried, rattled, worn out	I was completely frazzled after answering messages all afternoon.	Not simply physically 'exhausted'. Often combines stress + scattered attention. 'Fraught' is not a synonym for a person in this sense.		
213	2126	2246	curl up with something/someone	/kɝl ʌp wɪð/	phrasal verb	To settle into a curled, comfortable position with a book, blanket, drink, pet, person, etc., often for relaxation.	snuggle up with, cozy up with, settle in with	I curled up with a novel and a cup of tea. / The cat curled up with me on the couch.	KEY FRAME: curl up with + companion/object. 'Curl up' itself describes body position; 'with' adds what accompanies the cozy activity.		
213	2127	2247	torpedo	/tɔrˈpiːdoʊ/	noun; verb	Noun: a self-propelled underwater weapon. Verb: to attack with a torpedo; figuratively, to ruin, sabotage, or abruptly destroy a plan, deal, career, chances, etc.	sabotage, sink, derail, wreck	One careless comment torpedoed the deal. / The submarine fired a torpedo.	Add literal noun/verb behind the metaphor. Figurative collocations: torpedo a deal/bill/career/chances.		
213	2128	2248	disseminate	/dɪˈsɛmɪˌneɪt/	verb (formal)	To spread or distribute information, ideas, knowledge, or material widely among people or places.	circulate, spread, distribute, propagate	The agency disseminated the guidance to local offices.	Usually transitive: disseminate information/findings/results. More formal than 'spread'.		
213	2129	2249	finagle	/fəˈneɪɡəl/	verb (informal)	To obtain, arrange, or maneuver something through cleverness, persuasion, manipulation, or sometimes trickery.	wangle, maneuver, scheme, finesse	She finagled us a better table. / He finagled his way into the sold-out event.	KEY FRAMES: finagle + object; finagle one's way into/out of X. Trickery is possible but not required; can be resourceful persuasion.		
213	2130	2250	probe / probed	/proʊbd/	verb; noun	To investigate, examine, or question deeply and systematically; physically, to explore with an instrument. Noun: an investigation or an exploratory instrument/device.	investigate, examine, scrutinize; inquiry	Reporters probed the allegations. / The doctor gently probed the wound. / The agency opened a probe into the incident.	Normalize base + source form. Add noun and physical/technical senses.		
214	2131	2251	mishap	/ˈmɪshæp/	noun	An unlucky accident, mistake, or minor unfortunate incident, usually not catastrophic.	accident, minor accident, unfortunate incident	Aside from a small mishap with the cake, everything went smoothly.	Often mildly euphemistic/light in tone. A serious disaster would usually not be called merely a 'mishap'.		
214	2132	2252	ire	/aɪɚ/	noun (formal/literary)	Anger, especially strong anger provoked by something; commonly used in journalism/formal writing.	anger, wrath, fury, indignation	The decision drew the ire of local residents.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: draw/raise/provoke someone's ire; incur the ire of. 'Indignation' specifically includes perceived injustice/wrongness.		
214	2133	2253	talking head / talking-head segment	/ˈtɔkɪŋ hɛd ˈsɛɡmənt/	noun / media phrase	A person shown speaking directly to camera, especially an expert/commentator/interview subject; a 'talking-head segment' is a video/TV section dominated by such on-camera speech rather than action or illustrative footage.	on-camera commentator, interview segment, direct-to-camera segment	The documentary cuts from archival footage to a talking-head interview.	Teach underlying term 'talking head'. It can be mildly dismissive when referring to TV pundits, but is also a neutral production term.		
214	2134	2254	take someone's breath away	/teɪk jʊr brɛθ əˈweɪ/	idiom	To cause intense awe, surprise, beauty, excitement, or other strong emotion; literally, something can also momentarily make breathing difficult.	breathtakingly impress, astonish, stun, awe	The view took my breath away. / The sudden impact briefly took his breath away.	Normalize possessive: take my/your/her... breath away. Figurative awe is common, but literal physical sense also exists.		
214	2135	2255	PB&J / PB and J	/piː biː ənd dʒeɪ/	noun (informal, AmE)	Peanut butter and jelly, especially a peanut-butter-and-jelly sandwich.	peanut butter and jelly sandwich	I packed a PB&J for lunch.	CORRECTION: remove speculative Pam Beesly/Jim material from the definition. In ordinary English, PB&J means peanut butter and jelly. Any The Office wordplay would be source-specific, not the lexical meaning.		
214	2136	2256	cartwheel	/ˈkɑrtˌwiːl/	noun; verb	A sideways gymnastic movement in which the body rotates with hands and feet contacting the ground in sequence; verb: to perform or move like a cartwheel.	gymnastic turn, hands-over-feet rotation	She did a cartwheel across the lawn. / The car cartwheeled down the slope after the crash.	Add figurative/extended verb for objects tumbling end-over-end/sideways. A cartwheel is not simply a 'flip'.		
214	2137	2257	flake / flake on someone	/fleɪk/	noun; verb (informal slang)	Noun: an unreliable person who often fails to follow through. Verb phrase 'flake on someone' = cancel, fail to show, or abandon a commitment, often at the last minute.	unreliable person; bail on, stand up, cancel on	He's a total flake. / Don't flake on me again tonight.	KEY FAMILY: be a flake; flake out; flake on + person/plan. 'Quitter' is not a clean synonym.		
214	2138	2258	go haywire	/ˈɡoʊɪŋ ˈheɪˌwaɪɚ/	idiom (informal)	To malfunction, become chaotic, behave unpredictably, or go badly out of control.	malfunction, go wrong, go crazy, act up	My phone went haywire after the update. / The schedule went haywire when three flights were canceled.	Normalize from progressive. Used for machines, systems, plans, emotions/body processes figuratively.		
214	2139	2259	springy	/ˈsprɪŋi/	adjective	Elastic, resilient, or bouncy; tending to spring back after pressure; also light and energetic in movement.	bouncy, elastic, resilient, buoyant	The mattress is firm but springy. / She walked with a springy step.	Broaden beyond material property. Common: springy texture/hair/turf/step.		
214	2140	2260	the green-eyed monster	/ˌɡriːn aɪd ˈmɑnstɚ/	idiom / literary allusion	Jealousy personified, especially possessive or romantic jealousy.	jealousy, envy	The green-eyed monster appeared when he saw his ex with someone else.	Usually appears with article 'the'. Shakespearean allusion from Othello; modern use can be playful/literary. Jealousy and envy overlap but are not identical.		
215	2141	2261	zero in on something	/ˈzɪəroʊ ɪn ɑn/	phrasal verb	To direct attention, effort, investigation, or targeting increasingly precisely toward a specific person, issue, location, cause, etc.	focus on, home in on, target, pinpoint	The investigation zeroed in on one account. / Let's zero in on the main problem.	KEY FRAME: zero in on + target. 'Home in on' is a close established alternative; 'hone in on' is common but historically contested.		
215	2142	2262	perverse	/pɚˈvɝs/	adjective	Deliberately or unreasonably contrary to what is expected, sensible, or desirable; showing obstinate wrongheadedness; also, in some contexts, morally/sexually deviant in an older or judgmental sense.	contrary, obstinate, willfully unreasonable, distorted	He took a perverse pleasure in proving everyone wrong. / The rule created a perverse incentive to waste money.	Important collocations: perverse incentive, perverse pleasure, perverse outcome. Sexual/moral labeling can be dated or stigmatizing; context matters.		
215	2143	2263	flaunt / flaunts	/flɔnts/	verb	To display something conspicuously or ostentatiously, often to attract admiration, envy, or attention.	show off, parade, display ostentatiously	He loves to flaunt his wealth online. / She flaunted the award jokingly.	Normalize base + source form. Commonly confused with 'flout' = openly disregard a rule/law.		
215	2144	2264	worth a damn	/wɝθ ə dæm/	idiom (informal, mildly vulgar)	Having any real value, quality, usefulness, competence, or importance, especially in negative statements/questions.	any good, worthwhile, useful, decent	None of these batteries are worth a damn. / Is that advice worth a damn?	Especially common under negation: not worth a damn. Related 'give a damn' concerns caring, not value.		
215	2145	2265	scrub / can't scrub out; scrub (gaming slang)	/kænt skrʌb/	verb; noun/slang — context-dependent	Standard 'scrub' = clean by rubbing hard; 'can't scrub X out/off' = cannot remove a stain/mark by scrubbing. Separately, 'scrub' can be an insulting slang noun for an inexperienced/bad player or incompetent person in some gaming/sports communities.	clean, scour; noob, bad player (slang)	I can't scrub the stain out. / He called the new player a scrub.	CORRECTION: 'can't scrub' is not itself an established slang phrase meaning an incompetent person, and 'he's a can't scrub' is unnatural. Separate the standard verb construction from slang noun 'a scrub'.		
215	2146	2266	back burner / put something on the back burner	/ˈbækˌbɝnɚ/	idiom / noun phrase	A lower-priority state in which something is postponed while more urgent matters receive attention.	postpone, deprioritize, shelve, put aside	We put the redesign on the back burner until next month.	CORRECTION: standard idiom is normally two words, 'back burner'. KEY FRAME: put X on the back burner; X is on the back burner.		
215	2147	2267	iteration	/ˌɪtəˈreɪʃən/	noun	One repetition/cycle of a process; a particular version produced through repeated development; in computing/math, one execution of a repeated procedure.	version, cycle, repetition, revision	This is the third iteration of the design. / Each iteration of the loop updates the value.	Clean duplicated definition/example. KEY PRODUCTIVE USE: next/current/earlier iteration of a product/design; iterative = repeated refinement.		
215	2148	2268	disruption / disruptions	/dɪsˈrʌpʃənz/	noun	An interruption or disturbance that prevents something from continuing normally; also a major change that unsettles an established system/industry.	interruption, disturbance; upheaval	The outage caused major disruptions. / Streaming created major disruption in the media business.	Normalize singular/plural and add innovation/industry sense. 'Disruption' can be temporary interruption or structural upheaval.		
215	2149	2269	dish it out / dish something out	/dɪʃ ɪt aʊt/	phrasal verb / idiom (informal)	To give or distribute something; especially to hand out criticism, insults, punishment, advice, etc. freely. In 'can dish it out but can't take it', someone attacks others but cannot tolerate the same treatment.	hand out, dole out; criticize, attack	He can dish it out, but he can't take it. / The coach dished out harsh criticism.	Broaden underlying construction: dish out + food/criticism/punishment. The famous idiom uses pronoun 'it' for harsh treatment.		
215	2150	2270	disposal	/dɪˈspoʊzəl/	noun	The act/process of getting rid of something; also availability/control for use in the idiom 'at someone's disposal'.	discarding, removal; availability, command	Safe disposal of batteries is important. / We have several tools at our disposal.	Important second construction: at one's disposal = available for one to use/control. Also garbage disposal = kitchen appliance in AmE.		
216	2151	2271	tank / tank a game; tank it	/tæŋk ɪt/	verb (informal/slang)	To perform very badly or fail; transitively, to deliberately lose/underperform, especially in sports for strategic advantage; also of prices/ratings, to fall sharply.	bomb, flop, collapse; deliberately lose	The movie tanked at the box office. / Critics accused the team of tanking for a better draft position.	CORRECTION: 'tank it' is possible but not the core idiom. Distinguish ordinary fail badly from deliberate sports tanking.		
216	2152	2272	draw lots / draw lots to decide...	/lɛts drɔ lɑts tə dɪˈsaɪd hu slips wɛɚ/	idiom / decision method	To choose among people/options randomly, traditionally by drawing marked slips, objects, straws, etc.	choose by lot, decide by chance, randomize	We drew lots to decide who would sleep where. / If nobody volunteers, we'll draw lots.	Normalize from full source sentence. KEY FRAME: draw lots to decide + wh-clause; draw lots for + role/order.		
216	2153	2273	station in life	/ˈsteɪʃən ɪn laɪf/	noun phrase (formal/old-fashioned)	A person's social position, rank, role, or circumstances in society, especially in older class-conscious language.	social position, standing, rank	The novel treats everyone as bound by their station in life.	Often historical/literary and can imply a fixed social hierarchy. Not simply current income.		
216	2154	2274	I'm toast / be toast	/aɪm toʊst/	idiom (informal)	To be doomed, defeated, ruined, caught, or in serious trouble.	be doomed, be finished, be done for	If she sees the broken screen, I'm toast. / Without that contract, the project is toast.	Normalize productive 'be toast'; subjects can be people, plans, businesses, objects. Often humorous exaggeration.		
216	2155	2275	itinerant	/aɪˈtɪnɚənt/	adjective; noun	Traveling from place to place, especially for work or as part of one's way of life; noun: an itinerant worker/preacher/etc.	roving, traveling, peripatetic, nomadic	He worked as an itinerant musician. / Itinerant traders moved between villages.	'Nomadic' can imply a broader lifestyle/community pattern; itinerant often emphasizes repeated travel for occupation.		
216	2156	2276	alakazam	/ˌæləˈkæzæm/	interjection / magic word	A conventional nonsense magic word used theatrically to accompany a trick, transformation, or magical act.	abracadabra, presto	The magician waved the wand and cried, 'Alakazam!'	Recognition/cultural word. 'Presto' can announce a result but also has a separate musical meaning.		
216	2157	2277	coy	/kɔɪ/	adjective	Deliberately or affectedly shy, modest, or reluctant to reveal information, often playfully/flirtatiously; can also mean evasively noncommittal.	demure, teasingly shy, evasive, reticent	She gave him a coy smile. / The company was coy about the release date.	Important non-romantic use: be coy about details/plans = deliberately avoid saying much. 'Reserved' may be genuine rather than affected.		
216	2158	2278	vis-à-vis	/ˌviːz ə ˈviː/	preposition; adverb; noun (formal)	In relation to or compared with; facing/opposite; noun historically can mean a person/object situated face-to-face.	regarding, in relation to, compared with, opposite	We need to clarify our position vis-à-vis the new rules.	CORRECTION: standard spelling is 'vis-à-vis' (accents/hyphens often retained). Modern common use = 'in relation to/regarding', somewhat formal.		
216	2159	2279	conquistador / conquistadors	/kɒnˈkwiːstədɔrz/	noun (historical)	A Spanish conqueror/explorer involved in the conquest and colonization of parts of the Americas, especially in the 16th century.	Spanish conqueror (descriptive)	Spanish conquistadors overthrew major Indigenous states and helped establish colonial rule.	Normalize singular/plural. 'Explorer' alone understates the conquest role; term is historically specific.		
216	2160	2280	cubby / cubbyhole	/ˈkʌbi/	noun	A small compartment, recess, or enclosed storage space, often one of a set for personal belongings.	compartment, cubbyhole, small storage space	Each child has a cubby for a backpack and coat.	'Cubicle' is generally a larger partitioned workspace and is not a close synonym. Common in schools/daycare/entryways.		
217	2161	2281	speckled	/ˈspɛkəld/	adjective	Covered or marked with many small spots, flecks, or patches of contrasting color.	spotted, flecked, dotted	The bird has a speckled breast. / Sunlight speckled the floor through the leaves.	Can describe naturally spotted surfaces or figurative/light patterns. Related verb: speckle.		
217	2162	2282	that's a stretch / be a stretch	/ðæts ə strɛtʃ/	idiom (informal)	Used to say a claim, interpretation, comparison, or expectation goes beyond what seems reasonable, plausible, or supported.	far-fetched, doubtful, an exaggeration	Calling that tiny mistake 'fraud' is a stretch. / Winning from here would be a stretch.	Broaden productive frame: 'That's a stretch'; 'X is a bit of a stretch'; 'It would be a stretch to say...'.		
217	2163	2283	cringeworthy / cringe-worthy	/ˈkrɪndʒˌwɝði/	adjective (informal)	So awkward, embarrassing, tasteless, or uncomfortable that it makes one metaphorically cringe.	embarrassing, awkward, wince-inducing	The dialogue is painfully cringeworthy in places.	Both closed and hyphenated spellings occur; 'cringeworthy' is common modern styling. Related slang adjective 'cringe' is more informal.		
217	2164	2285	rhombus	/ˈrɑmbəs/	noun (geometry)	A quadrilateral with all four sides equal in length; under standard definitions, a square is a special rhombus.	equal-sided quadrilateral	A square is a rhombus with four right angles.	CORRECTION: 'diamond' is an informal visual label, not a precise geometry synonym; a rhombus need not be oriented like a diamond.		
217	2165	2286	trapezoid	/ˈtræpəˌzɔɪd/	noun (geometry; AmE)	A quadrilateral defined in many U.S. texts as having at least one pair of parallel sides; some curricula use an exclusive definition requiring exactly one pair. BrE terminology differs, often using 'trapezium'.	quadrilateral with parallel side(s) (definition-dependent)	Under the inclusive definition, every parallelogram is also a trapezoid.	CORRECTION: current definition says 'at least one pair' but example says 'only one pair', an internal contradiction. Flag inclusive vs exclusive conventions.		
217	2166	2287	quadrilateral	/ˌkwɑdrəˈlætərəl/	noun; adjective (geometry)	A polygon with four sides (and therefore four vertices/angles); adjective: having four sides.	four-sided polygon, tetragon	Squares, rectangles, rhombuses, and many trapezoids are quadrilaterals.	Add adjective. 'Tetragon' is technical/rare. Exact trapezoid subclass relation depends on convention noted at WID2286.		
217	2167	2288	for a stretch	/fɚ ə strɛtʃ/	phrase (informal)	For a continuous period of time, often a noticeable but unspecified span.	for a while, for a period, for a spell	I worked nights for a stretch before switching schedules.	KEY COMPONENT: 'a stretch' = an uninterrupted period. Related: 'for long stretches'; distinct from 'That's a stretch' (WID2282).		
217	2168	2289	geometric shape / geometric shapes	/ˌdʒiːəˈmɛtrɪk ʃeɪps/	noun phrase	A mathematically describable figure/form such as a circle, triangle, polygon, polyhedron, etc., in two or more dimensions depending context.	geometric figure, shape	The lesson introduces basic geometric shapes such as circles and triangles.	CORRECTION: 'polygons' are only one subset of geometric shapes; circles are not polygons. Normalize singular/plural.		
217	2169	2290	in all	/ɪn ɔl/	adverbial phrase	Altogether; in total, used to state the complete number or amount after counting components.	altogether, in total, all told	There were fifteen people in all. / The repairs cost $800 in all.	Often appears after the quantity or at clause end. 'Collectively' is not always interchangeable.		
217	2170	2291	concurrent	/kənˈkɝrənt/	adjective	Happening, existing, or operating during the same period; in law, concurrent sentences run at the same time rather than one after another.	simultaneous, overlapping, parallel	The conference has three concurrent sessions. / He received concurrent sentences.	Connect to WID2167 'concurrently'. In computing, concurrency can involve overlapping progress without literal same-instant execution.		
218	2171	2292	pediatric / paediatric	/ˌpiːdiˈætrɪk/	adjective	Relating to the medical care, health, or diseases of infants, children, and adolescents.	children's medical, child-health	She works in pediatric medicine. / The hospital has a pediatric emergency department.	AmE pediatric; BrE paediatric. Remove awkward pseudo-synonyms such as 'pediatrician's'.		
218	2172	2293	give-and-take / give and take	/ɡɪv ənd teɪk/	noun / idiom	Mutual compromise, exchange, adjustment, or reciprocal concession between people; also the back-and-forth exchange of conversation/interaction.	compromise, reciprocity, back-and-forth	A good partnership requires give and take. / I enjoyed the give-and-take of the debate.	As noun before another noun often hyphenated: give-and-take relationship. Not necessarily equal concessions.		
218	2173	2294	level out / level off	/ˈlɛvəlz aʊt/	phrasal verb	To become stable after rising/falling or to make something even/flat; 'level off' is especially common for a trend that stops rising/falling.	stabilize, flatten out, even out, plateau	Prices rose quickly, then leveled off. / The road levels out after the hill.	Normalize from third-person. Distinguish physical flattening and trend stabilization; 'level off' often more idiomatic for graphs/rates.		
218	2174	2295	plasticity	/plæˈstɪsəti/	noun	Capacity to be shaped, deformed, or changed; in neuroscience/biology, capacity of systems/tissues—especially the nervous system—to adapt structurally or functionally in response to experience or conditions.	malleability, adaptability, flexibility	Neural plasticity allows the brain to change with learning and experience. / Clay has high plasticity when wet.	Important technical meanings differ by field (materials science, neuroscience, developmental biology). 'Flexibility' is broader.		
218	2175	2296	willy-nilly	/ˈwɪli ˈnɪli/	adverb; adjective (informal)	Without order, planning, discrimination, or control; haphazardly. Historically it can also mean whether one wants to or not.	haphazardly, indiscriminately, randomly	Don't add features willy-nilly. / Files were scattered willy-nilly across the folders.	CORRECTION: standard styling commonly hyphenated 'willy-nilly'. Add older 'whether willing or unwilling' origin/sense for recognition.		
218	2176	2297	dud	/dʌd/	noun; adjective (informal)	Something that fails to work, explode, succeed, or meet expectations; a person/thing considered ineffective or disappointing. Attributive adjective: dud check, dud product, etc.	failure, flop, lemon (product), nonstarter	The firework was a dud. / The sequel turned out to be a dud.	Broaden beyond malfunction. 'Dud' originally/commonly covers ammunition/explosives that fail, then figurative failures.		
218	2177	2298	compulsory	/kəmˈpʌlsəri/	adjective	Required by law, rule, authority, or circumstances; mandatory rather than optional.	mandatory, obligatory, required	Attendance is compulsory for all first-year students.	'Enforced' is not a direct adjective synonym. Common: compulsory education/service/attendance.		
218	2178	2299	bum bag / bumbag	/ˈbʌm bæɡ/	noun (BrE informal)	A small pouch worn around the waist or hips; AmE commonly 'fanny pack'.	fanny pack (AmE), waist bag, belt bag	I kept my passport in a bum bag while sightseeing.	Styling varies: 'bum bag' is common; 'bumbag' also occurs. Register note: 'fanny' has a vulgar anatomical meaning in BrE, making the AmE term amusing/awkward there.		
218	2179	2300	minge	/mɪndʒ/	noun (BrE slang, vulgar/offensive)	A vulgar British slang term for the vulva/female genital area; can also occur in insulting sexualized language.	vulva (neutral anatomical referent); pussy, twat (vulgar, not exact in tone/anatomy)	The word 'minge' is vulgar and would be inappropriate in most formal settings.	PRECISION: avoid presenting other slurs as straightforward synonyms. This is recognition-focused British vulgar slang; exact anatomical reference in slang can be loose.		
218	2180	2302	slag someone off / slag off	/slæɡ mi ɔf/	phrasal verb (BrE slang, informal)	To criticize, insult, or speak disparagingly about someone or something.	badmouth, trash-talk, diss, run down	She slagged me off in front of the whole team. / They were slagging off the new policy.	Normalize from source pronoun. KEY FRAME: slag someone/something off. Strongly British; informal and often fairly harsh.		
219	2181	2303	lax	/læks/	adjective	Not sufficiently strict, careful, controlled, or attentive to standards or duties.	slack, negligent, careless, lenient	Security was surprisingly lax. / The company was lax about enforcing the rule.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: lax security/standards/enforcement; be lax about/in doing something. 'Lenient' focuses permissiveness; 'negligent' can be stronger/legal.		
219	2182	2304	spearhead / spearheading	/ˈspɪrˌhɛdɪŋ/	verb; noun	Verb: to lead, organize, or initiate an important effort, campaign, project, or movement. Noun: the leading force/person; literally, the pointed head of a spear.	lead, head, champion, drive	She is spearheading the campaign. / He spearheaded the redesign.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAME: spearhead a campaign/initiative/effort. Usually implies active leadership, not merely participation.		
219	2183	2305	boil down to / boil something down	/bɔɪlz daʊn/	phrasal verb	To reduce to the essential point or result; transitively, to condense information or literally reduce liquid by boiling.	come down to, amount to; condense, reduce	It all boils down to trust. / Can you boil the report down to three points?	KEY DISTINCTION: X boils down to Y = Y is the essence; boil X down to Y = reduce X into Y. Literal cooking sense underlies metaphor.		
219	2184	2306	dour	/daʊr/	adjective	Stern, severe, gloomy, or persistently unsmiling in manner, appearance, or mood.	stern, grim, gloomy, unsmiling	He wore a dour expression throughout the meeting. / The film has a deliberately dour tone.	Often describes expression/personality/tone. Pronunciation varies: /dʊr/ and /daʊr/ occur.		
219	2185	2307	disintegrate / disintegrated	/dɪˈsɪntɪˌɡreɪtɪd/	verb	To break apart into small pieces or components; figuratively, to lose cohesion, structure, or existence.	break apart, crumble, fall apart, collapse	The material began to disintegrate. / Their alliance disintegrated within months.	Normalize base + source form. Can be physical or figurative; not necessarily decay.		
219	2186	2308	bawl / bawling	/ˈbɔlɪŋ/	verb; noun	To cry or shout very loudly; 'bawl one's eyes out' = cry intensely.	wail, sob loudly, yell	The child was bawling after the fall. / I bawled my eyes out at the ending.	Normalize base + source form. Bawl can mean loud crying OR loud shouting; common construction 'bawl someone out' = scold loudly is distinct.		
219	2187	2309	undermine	/ˌʌndɚˈmaɪn/	verb	To weaken, damage, or make less effective gradually or indirectly; literally/historically, to erode or excavate beneath a foundation.	weaken, erode, impair, sabotage	Constant criticism can undermine confidence. / The leak undermined the foundation.	KEY OBJECTS: undermine confidence/authority/credibility/efforts. 'Sabotage' often implies deliberate action; undermine need not.		
219	2188	2310	talking head	/ˈtɔkɪŋ hɛd/	noun / media term	A person shown speaking on camera, especially an interview subject, expert, commentator, or pundit; sometimes used dismissively for media commentators perceived as repetitive or superficial.	on-camera commentator, pundit, interview subject	The documentary alternates archival footage with talking-head interviews.	CORRECTION: lack of insight is not inherent in the term. It is also a neutral production term. Overlaps WID2253, retained as a core-term recognition card.		
219	2189	2312	intrinsically	/ɪnˈtrɪnsɪkli/	adverb	By or because of a thing's inherent nature; in itself, rather than because of external circumstances or relationships.	inherently, essentially, by nature	The task isn't intrinsically difficult; it just takes practice. / Gold is intrinsically valuable in some arguments, though value claims depend on context.	KEY CONTRAST: intrinsically vs extrinsically/instrumentally. Often used with valuable, motivated, interesting, good/bad.		
219	2190	2313	boob	/buːb/	noun (informal/slang)	A foolish or inept person (dated/mild); also informal slang for a breast.	fool, dope, dummy; breast (slang)	I felt like a boob for forgetting the appointment. / The comedy uses juvenile jokes about boobs.	IMPORTANT POLYSEMY: person-insult sense can sound dated; plural 'boobs' commonly means breasts. Register is informal.		
220	2191	2314	put one's foot in one's mouth	/pʊt jʊr fʊt ɪn jʊr maʊθ/	idiom	To say something tactless, embarrassing, insensitive, or inappropriate, usually accidentally.	say the wrong thing, make a verbal blunder, misspeak	I really put my foot in my mouth when I asked about the party she wasn't invited to.	Normalize possessives. KEY IMAGE: an awkward verbal mistake one wishes one could take back. BrE also 'put one's foot in it'.		
220	2192	2315	my ride bailed me out / bail someone out	/maɪ raɪd beɪl mi aʊt/	source phrase + phrasal verb	In the source phrase, 'my ride' means the person/vehicle providing transportation, and 'bailed me out' means rescued me from an awkward or difficult situation. More broadly, bail someone out = rescue from trouble, including financially or by posting legal bail.	rescue, get someone out of trouble, save	Thankfully, my ride bailed me out of the awkward conversation. / My brother bailed me out when I couldn't cover the bill.	CORRECTION: current headword is ungrammatical ('my ride bail me out'). Teach underlying 'bail someone out' and source-specific 'my ride bailed me out'.		
220	2193	2316	can't stomach something / stomach something	/kænt ˈstʌmək/	idiom / verb	To be unable to tolerate, accept, watch, hear, or endure something because it is disgusting, upsetting, offensive, or otherwise unbearable.	can't stand, can't bear, be unable to tolerate	I can't stomach cruelty to animals. / She couldn't stomach watching the surgery scene.	KEY FRAME: can't/couldn't stomach + noun/gerund. Figurative verb comes from physical stomach/nausea; stronger than simple dislike.		
220	2194	2317	invigorating	/ɪnˈvɪɡəˌreɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Making someone feel energized, refreshed, stimulated, or more alive; also intellectually/emotionally stimulating.	energizing, refreshing, stimulating, revitalizing	The cold morning air was invigorating. / We had an invigorating discussion.	Broaden beyond physical health/strength. Related verb invigorate; noun invigoration.		
220	2195	2318	testament / a testament to	/ˈtɛstəmənt/	noun; collocation	Evidence, proof, or a strong sign of a quality or fact, especially in the construction 'a testament to X'; also a will/testament or a biblical division/title in other contexts.	evidence, proof, tribute, demonstration	Her recovery is a testament to her persistence. / The building is a testament to the architect's skill.	KEY FRAME: be a testament to + quality/person/effort. 'Proof' can be stronger than the figurative rhetorical use. Add legal/religious recognition senses.		
220	2196	2319	charisma black hole	/kəˈrɪzmə blæk hoʊl/	creative metaphor / informal expression	A vivid, non-fixed metaphor for a person or presence perceived as absorbing or draining a room's energy, charm, or social momentum rather than generating it.	energy drain, charisma vacuum, buzzkill (context-dependent)	The character is written as a charisma black hole who drains every scene of energy.	SOURCE/CREATIVE: not a standard dictionary idiom. Teach composition: charisma + black hole → metaphorically 'absorbs' social energy. Can be harshly judgmental.		
220	2197	2320	widow's peak	/ˈwɪdoʊz piːk/	noun	A V-shaped point of the hairline descending toward the center of the forehead.	V-shaped hairline	He has a pronounced widow's peak.	Common appearance term. The name comes from historical associations with widow's hoods and later folklore; it does not imply anything about actual widowhood.		
220	2198	2322	pec / pecs	/pɛks/	noun (informal)	Short for pectoral muscle(s), especially the chest muscles; usually plural 'pecs' in everyday fitness/body talk.	pectorals, chest muscles	Bench presses work the pecs. / He strained his left pec.	Normalize singular/plural. 'Chest' is broader anatomy, not an exact synonym. Informal but very common in fitness contexts.		
220	2199	2323	drape / drapes	/dreɪps/	noun; verb	Noun: a curtain or hanging fabric, especially a long/heavy window covering; verb: to hang, arrange, or fall loosely over something.	curtain, drapery; hang, arrange loosely	She chose velvet drapes for the room. / A blanket was draped over the chair.	Add productive verb. In 'does the carpet match the drapes?', drapes metaphorically refers to head hair; see WID2325.		
220	2200	2324	crude	/kruːd/	adjective	Rough, basic, or not refined; offensively vulgar; or in a natural/unprocessed state, as in crude oil.	rough, rudimentary, unrefined; vulgar, coarse	They built a crude shelter. / He made a crude joke. / Crude oil prices rose.	Major polysemy: crude design/tool, crude language, crude oil/material. 'Offensive' is only one branch.		
221	2201	2325	does the carpet match the drapes?	/dʌz ðə ˈkɑrpɪt mætʃ ðə dreɪps/	slang question / sexual double entendre (vulgar/invasive)	A crude sexualized question asking whether someone's pubic-hair color ('carpet') matches their head-hair color ('drapes').	no neutral synonym; sexualized hair-color joke	He made the crude 'carpet and drapes' joke, and everyone looked uncomfortable.	KEY METAPHOR: carpet = pubic hair; drapes = head hair. Often invasive/objectifying; recognition-focused rather than recommended usage. Repair punctuation.		
221	2202	2326	keg party	/kɛɡ ˈpɑrti/	noun (informal, AmE)	A party at which beer is served from one or more kegs, stereotypically associated with U.S. college/fraternity culture but not limited to it.	beer party, kegger (slang)	They hosted a keg party after the game.	Related slang 'kegger'. 'Rager' means a wild party generally, not specifically a keg party.		
221	2203	2327	demarcation	/ˌdiːmɑːrˈkeɪʃən/	noun	The act/process of marking or establishing a boundary, or the boundary/distinction itself.	boundary, dividing line, delineation, separation	There's a clear demarcation between public and private space.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: line of demarcation; clear/sharp demarcation between X and Y. Can be physical, conceptual, organizational.		
221	2204	2328	get the axe / give someone the axe	/ɡɛts ði æks/	idiom (informal)	To be fired, dismissed, canceled, or eliminated; 'give someone/something the axe' = terminate or cut it.	get fired, be cut, be dropped; fire, cancel	Several employees got the axe. / The network gave the show the axe after one season.	Normalize from third-person. Broader than jobs: shows/projects/features can get the axe.		
221	2205	2329	be under someone / report to someone	/hi ɪz ˈʌndɚ mi/	literal/organizational phrase; context-dependent	In an organizational hierarchy, 'be under someone' can mean be lower-ranking or under that person's supervision, though 'report to someone' is often clearer. Literally, 'under' can describe physical position; sexual double entendres depend on context.	report to, work under, be subordinate to	I report to Maya; two analysts work under me. / The phrase can sound suggestive in the wrong context.	CORRECTION: not a stable sexual-dominance expression. Teach hierarchy use plus why physical 'under' can create contextual double entendre.		
221	2206	2330	blow chunks / blowing chunks	/ˈbloʊɪŋ tʃʌŋks/	idiom (slang, graphic)	To vomit, especially forcefully; a vivid, juvenile/informal expression.	throw up, puke, barf, vomit	He got carsick and started blowing chunks.	Normalize base + source form. Not inherently alcohol-related. Graphic/comic register; avoid formal contexts.		
221	2207	2331	cap / no cap	/kæp/	noun/slang; interjectional phrase	In contemporary slang, 'cap' = a lie, exaggeration, or false claim; 'that's cap' = that's false; 'no cap' = honestly/for real/no lie.	lie, BS; for real, honestly	He says he ran a four-minute mile—that's cap. / No cap, that was the best episode yet.	Useful productive frames: that's cap; stop capping; no cap. Slang is strongly informal and generational; usage can shift.		
221	2208	2332	remittance	/rɪˈmɪt(ə)ns/	noun	Money sent to a person or organization, especially payment or money sent by migrants/workers to family in another place/country; also the act of remitting payment.	money transfer, payment, funds sent	She sends a monthly remittance to her family abroad. / Please include the invoice number with your remittance.	Broaden beyond mail. In economics, migrant remittances are important cross-border household transfers.		
221	2209	2333	intervention	/ˌɪntərˈvɛnʃən/	noun	The act of becoming involved to change, improve, stop, or influence a situation; also a planned confrontation/support meeting concerning harmful behavior, and a treatment/action in medicine or research.	involvement, action, mediation; treatment	Early intervention can improve outcomes. / The family staged an intervention about his drinking.	Highly polysemous: medical intervention, military intervention, government intervention, crisis intervention, family 'intervention'. 'Interference' can be negatively loaded.		
221	2210	2334	append / appended	/əˈpɛndɪd/	verb	To add or attach something to the end of a document, list, file, string, or other sequence.	add, attach, affix	She appended a note to the report. / The program appends each new record to the file.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAME: append X to Y. In computing, append specifically adds at the end.		
222	2211	2335	contiguous	/kənˈtɪɡjuəs/	adjective	Touching along a boundary or directly adjoining; forming an unbroken connected area or sequence.	adjacent, adjoining, continuous (context-dependent)	The contiguous United States excludes Alaska and Hawaii. / The files occupy a contiguous block of memory.	Important technical use: contiguous memory/data. 'Neighboring' does not necessarily mean touching; 'continuous' is not always interchangeable.		
222	2212	2336	keyboard spamming / key mashing	/ˈkiːbɔrd ˈspæmɪŋ/	informal internet/gaming phrase	Repeatedly pressing keys or sending excessive/repetitive keyboard input, often randomly, rapidly, or disruptively.	key mashing, input spamming, flooding	The chat filled with keyboard spam after everyone started mashing keys.	'Keyboard spamming' is descriptive rather than a tightly fixed idiom. Distinguish key mashing (rapid/random keystrokes) from message/chat spamming (repeated messages).		
222	2213	2337	audacious	/ɔˈdeɪʃəs/	adjective	Bold, daring, or surprisingly ambitious, sometimes impressively so and sometimes recklessly or shamelessly so.	bold, daring, gutsy, brazen (negative contexts)	It was an audacious plan, but it worked. / The audacious claim drew criticism.	Like 'audacity', polarity can be admiring or disapproving. Not automatically 'fearless'.		
222	2214	2338	apparently	/əˈpɛrəntli/	adverb	According to what one has heard or learned, or judging from available evidence; sometimes marks that the speaker is not personally confirming the fact.	seemingly, evidently, reportedly, it seems	Apparently, the meeting has been canceled. / He was apparently unaware of the change.	NUANCE: often means 'I hear/it seems that...', not simply visual appearance. 'Allegedly' has stronger legal/skeptical implications; 'evidently' can sound more inferential.		
222	2215	2339	the apple of someone's eye	/ˈæpəl əv ˈsʌmwʌnz aɪ/	idiom	A person or thing that someone cherishes especially dearly or regards with great affection/pride.	darling, beloved, favorite, treasure	Her youngest grandson is the apple of her eye.	Usually takes 'the'. Can refer to a person or occasionally a prized thing. Strong affectionate tone.		
222	2216	2340	second-guess / second-guess oneself	/ˌsɛkənd ˈɡɛs/	verb	To question, reconsider, or doubt a decision/judgment, often after it has been made; also to try to anticipate another person's actions or reasoning.	doubt, reconsider, question; anticipate	Don't second-guess yourself—you made the best call you could. / Analysts tried to second-guess the central bank.	Standard verb often hyphenated 'second-guess'. Add less-common 'anticipate/guess in advance' sense.		
222	2217	2341	second a motion / second	/ˈsɛkənd/	verb; noun	In a formal meeting, to formally support another person's motion so it can be considered or debated; noun: an act/person giving such support. More broadly/informally, 'I second that' = I agree/support that.	support, endorse, back	'I move that we adjourn.' 'I second the motion.' / 'We need coffee.' 'I second that.'	KEY MEETING CONSTRUCTION: move/make a motion → another member seconds it. Informal 'I second that' is common outside parliamentary procedure.		
222	2218	2342	ROFL	/ˈrɑːfəl/	initialism / internet slang	'Rolling on the floor laughing'; used in text/online speech to signal that something is extremely funny, often hyperbolically.	LOL, LMAO, dying laughing	That clip had me ROFL.	Usually written as letters/online text; pronunciations vary and many users simply read the letters or treat it visually. Somewhat older internet slang but still recognizable.		
222	2219	2343	snuggle up / snuggle up to/with	/ˈsnʌɡəl ʌp/	phrasal verb	To settle closely into a warm, comfortable position, often beside or against someone/something.	cuddle up, nestle, cozy up	She snuggled up to him on the couch. / I snuggled up with a blanket and a book.	KEY FRAMES: snuggle up to + person/object; snuggle up with + companion/object. 'Huddle' often emphasizes grouping for warmth/protection.		
222	2220	2344	in spirit / be there in spirit	/ɪn ˈspɪrɪt/	idiom	Emotionally, symbolically, or in intention despite not being physically present; also 'in spirit' can mean in underlying character rather than literal form.	symbolically, emotionally, in essence	I can't attend, but I'll be there in spirit. / The remake is faithful to the original in spirit.	Broaden beyond absence-at-event. KEY: there in spirit; similar/faithful in spirit.		
223	2221	2345	hold on to someone/something	/hoʊld ɑn tu mi/	phrasal verb	To grip and not let go; figuratively, to keep, retain, preserve, or remain emotionally attached to someone/something.	cling to, keep hold of, retain	Hold on to me while we cross the ice. / She held on to the old letters for years.	Normalize away from fixed 'hold on to me'. KEY FRAME: hold on to + person/object/hope/memory. Distinguish 'hold on' = wait.		
223	2222	2346	perspire	/pɚˈspaɪɚ/	verb (formal/euphemistic)	To sweat; to release perspiration through the skin.	sweat	He began to perspire during the workout.	More formal/euphemistic than everyday 'sweat'. Common humorous saying contrasts 'horses sweat, men perspire, ladies glow'—dated gendered joke, not usage guidance.		
223	2223	2347	sniffle	/ˈsnɪfəl/	verb; noun	To repeatedly inhale through the nose with short audible sounds, often because of a cold, crying, or allergies; noun: such a sound or a mild cold ('the sniffles').	sniff, snuffle; the sniffles	She sniffled through the sad scene. / I've had the sniffles all week.	'Whimper' and 'sob' are different vocal crying sounds, not synonyms. Useful noun plural: have the sniffles.		
223	2224	2348	tongue-in-cheek	/ˌtʌŋ ɪn ˈtʃiːk/	adjective; adverb	Intended or expressed ironically, playfully, or not entirely seriously, often with a straight-faced appearance.	ironic, wry, playful, facetious	The article makes a tongue-in-cheek proposal to ban Mondays. / He said it tongue in cheek.	FORM: attributive adjective usually hyphenated 'tongue-in-cheek'; adverbial phrase can appear 'tongue in cheek'. Not necessarily sarcastic.		
223	2225	2349	every step of the way	/ˈɛvri stɛp əv ðə weɪ/	idiom	At every stage throughout a process, journey, development, or difficult experience; often emphasizing continuous involvement or support.	throughout, from start to finish, all along	She supported me every step of the way. / We documented the process every step of the way.	Not inherently 'providing support'; the phrase itself means throughout every stage. Support is a common context.		
223	2226	2350	keep something intact / remain intact; love remained intact	/lʌv ɪnˈtækt/	collocation + source phrase	To keep something whole, undamaged, unchanged, or unbroken; 'love remained intact' creatively applies the ordinary adjective to a relationship/feeling that survived difficulty.	preserve, remain whole, stay undamaged; endure	Despite the argument, their friendship remained intact. / Years apart couldn't keep their affection entirely intact.	CORRECTION: 'love intact' alone is not a fixed expression. Teach productive 'keep/remain intact' construction and retain love as a figurative source application. Overlaps WID2007 intentionally.		
223	2227	2351	crack	/kræk/	verb; noun	To break or split; make a sharp sound; solve a difficult problem/code; tell a joke; lose composure under pressure; noun: a narrow break, an attempt ('have a crack'), a witty remark, and other context-specific senses.	split, break; solve; joke; buckle	The glass cracked. / She cracked the code. / He cracked a joke. / He cracked under pressure.	CORRECTION: 'succeed under pressure' was wrong; 'crack under pressure' means FAIL/lose composure. Highly polysemous card; teach collocations rather than one slang gloss.		
223	2228	2352	aha moment / a-ha moment	/ɑːˈhɑː ˈmoʊmənt/	noun phrase (informal)	A sudden moment of insight, realization, or understanding when something becomes clear.	epiphany, realization, breakthrough	I had an aha moment when I saw how the two ideas connected.	Both 'aha moment' and 'a-ha moment' occur. Related interjection 'Aha!' signals discovery/recognition.		
