223	2230	2355	give someone the cold shoulder / get the cold shoulder	/koʊld ˈʃoʊldɚ ˈtriːtmənt/	idiom	To deliberately ignore, snub, or treat someone with unfriendly indifference; 'get the cold shoulder' = receive that treatment.	snub, ignore, freeze out	She gave me the cold shoulder after our argument. / I got the cold shoulder all evening.	NORMALIZE: 'cold-shoulder treatment' is understandable but less idiomatic than productive give/get the cold shoulder.		
224	2231	2356	charm someone's socks off	/tʃɑrm wʌnz sɑks ɔf/	idiom (informal)	To charm, delight, or win someone over extremely effectively.	charm completely, win over, captivate	He can charm your socks off when he wants to.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: verb + someone's socks off is an intensifying humorous pattern; compare 'knock/blow someone's socks off' with different meanings.		
224	2232	2357	mixer	/ˈmɪksɚ/	noun	A social event designed to help people meet and mingle; also a machine/tool for mixing, a person/device that mixes audio, and a nonalcoholic drink combined with spirits.	social gathering; mixing machine; audio mixer; mixed-drink ingredient	They met at a freshman mixer. / Use an electric mixer for the batter. / Tonic water is a common mixer.	Major polysemy missing. Context determines social, kitchen, audio, or beverage sense.		
