224	2234	2359	contention	/kənˈtɛnʃən/	noun	A claim or position asserted in an argument; disagreement/conflict; or the state of competing for something, as in 'in contention'.	claim, assertion; dispute; competition	Her main contention is that the rule is unfair. / The issue remains a point of contention. / The team is still in contention for the title.	IMPORTANT THREE USES: contention that...; point of contention; in contention for. Repair repetitive examples.		
224	2235	2360	impostor / imposter	/ɪmˈpɑstɚ/	noun	A person who falsely pretends to be another person or to have an identity, role, status, or qualifications they do not possess.	fraud, pretender, fake	The impostor posed as a doctor. / She worried that colleagues would discover she was an imposter.	Both spellings are standard; 'impostor' is traditional and 'imposter' common. Related 'impostor syndrome' is a psychological experience, not actual deception.		
224	2236	2361	reiterate / reiterating	/riˈɪtəˌreɪtɪŋ/	verb	To state, say, or emphasize something again, usually to make it clear or reinforce it.	repeat, restate, reaffirm	Let me reiterate the main point. / She reiterated that the deadline had not changed.	Normalize base + source form. 'Reiterate again' is usually redundant because re- already means again.		
224	2237	2362	retaliation	/rɪˌtæliˈeɪʃən/	noun	Action taken in response to an injury, attack, criticism, or perceived wrong, especially to punish or get even; in employment/law, adverse action for protected reporting/activity has specific legal meanings.	reprisal, revenge, retribution, payback	He feared retaliation after reporting the misconduct. / The strike prompted military retaliation.	KEY FRAME: retaliation for/against/in response to. Legal 'retaliation' is context-specific; not every negative response meets a legal definition.		
224	2238	2363	warm and fuzzy / warm-and-fuzzy	/wɔrm ənd ˈfʌzi/	idiom / adjective phrase	A pleasantly comforting, affectionate, sentimental feeling; can describe something that produces such feelings. Literal 'warm and fuzzy' can also simply describe temperature/texture.	comforting, heartwarming, cozy, sentimental	The reunion gave me a warm and fuzzy feeling. / The ad has a warm-and-fuzzy tone.	FORM: 'warm and fuzzy' predicative/noun phrase; hyphenation may appear attributively. Often playful and sometimes mildly ironic.		
224	2239	2364	confabulate / confabulation	/kənˈfæbjəˌleɪtɪd/	verb; noun	To converse/chat (older/formal sense); in clinical neuropsychology, to produce false or distorted memories without conscious intent to deceive, often filling memory gaps.	chat, converse; produce confabulated memories	The patient confabulated details she genuinely believed were true. / The older sense means to converse informally.	CORRECTION: clinical confabulation is not simply 'fabricating imaginary experiences as compensation' and does not imply deliberate lying. Normalize base and noun.		
224	2240	2365	Have you ever pooped a balloon?	/hæv ju ˈɛvɚ puːpt ə bəˈluːn/	source-specific humorous question / medical-context joke	A bizarre humorous question whose meaning depends on source context. Literally it evokes expelling a balloon rectally; in a medical context this can allude to the balloon expulsion test (BET), used in evaluating defecatory disorders. It is not an established figurative English idiom.	balloon expulsion test (medical context); absurd joke	The unexpected question, 'Have you ever pooped a balloon?', may be a comic way of referring to a balloon expulsion test.	CORRECTION: remove unsupported metaphorical senses such as 'release tension' or 'shatter illusions'. Preserve as source-specific humor; medical BET connection only when context supports it.		
225	2241	2366	doe / DOE	/doʊ/	noun; abbreviation	A doe is an adult female deer and, for some species, a female rabbit/goat/antelope or similar animal. Separately, DOE is an abbreviation used by multiple organizations, including the U.S. Department of Energy.	female deer; Department of Energy (context-specific abbreviation)	A doe and her fawn crossed the trail. / The U.S. DOE released the report.	CORRECTION: lowercase lexical word and uppercase abbreviation are unrelated homographs. Do not fold DOE into the animal definition without distinction.		
