225	2248	2373	laissez-faire	/ˌlɛseɪ ˈfɛr/	noun; adjective	An economic doctrine favoring minimal government interference in markets; more broadly, a hands-off, noninterventionist attitude or style.	nonintervention, hands-off, permissive (context-dependent)	The manager takes a laissez-faire approach to scheduling. / They debated laissez-faire economics.	CORRECTION: standard spelling hyphenated 'laissez-faire'. General-use adjective can imply relaxed/noninterfering, sometimes excessively so.		
