227	2264	2390	MIA	/ˌɛm.aɪˈeɪ/	initialism; adjective-like slang	Military: missing in action. Informally, unexpectedly absent, unreachable, or not showing up/communicating for a period.	missing, unaccounted for, absent, off the radar	He went MIA for three days and didn't answer anyone. / The soldier was listed as MIA.	Keep military origin distinct from casual figurative use. Usually pronounced as letters.		
227	2265	2391	valedictorian	/ˌvælɪdɪkˈtɔriən/	noun (chiefly North American education)	A graduating student selected for the valedictory role/honor, often—but not universally—the student with the highest academic rank; selection rules vary by school.	top-ranked graduate (often), graduation speaker (often)	She was named valedictorian and delivered the graduation address.	CORRECTION/PRECISION: not universally defined solely as highest GPA, and the speech role can vary. Contrast salutatorian in systems that use the term.		
227	2266	2392	get some Z's / catch some Z's	/ɡɛt səm ziːz/	idiom (informal)	To get some sleep, usually a modest amount or a needed rest.	sleep, get some shut-eye, catch some sleep	You look exhausted—go get some Z's. / I'm going to catch a few Z's before the flight.	Repair punctuation. Z represents the conventional written sound of snoring/sleep. Common variants get/catch some Z's.		
227	2267	2393	contrive	/kənˈtraɪv/	verb	To devise, arrange, or bring about something through ingenuity or planning; often implies artificiality, manipulation, or implausible construction, but not always deceit.	devise, engineer, arrange, concoct	They contrived a way to fit everyone into one car. / The plot feels contrived.	Important adjective 'contrived' = artificial/forced. Verb can be clever/ingenious without wrongdoing; current tax-evasion example overweights deceit.		
227	2268	2394	stipulate / stipulate that	/ˈstɪpjəˌleɪt/	verb (formal)	To specify or require something explicitly as a condition, term, or provision; in legal procedure, parties may also formally agree to a fact/point.	specify, require, state as a condition; agree formally	The contract stipulates that payment is due within 30 days. / The parties stipulated to the authenticity of the document.	KEY FRAME: stipulate that + clause; stipulate X as a condition. Legal 'stipulate to' = formally agree/admit for purposes of proceeding.		
