227	2270	2396	I won't state our stances so as not to skew the results / withhold one's stance to avoid biasing results	/aɪ woʊnt ˈstɪpjəˌleɪt aʊɚ ˈstænsɪz æz tu nɑt skju rɪˈzʌlts/	source sentence / reasoning construction	A naturalized way to say the speaker will not reveal participants' positions/opinions because doing so might influence the outcome. The original use of 'stipulate our stances' is unnatural for simply 'state/reveal our positions'.	withhold one's position, avoid biasing the results, remain neutral	I won't state our stances beforehand so as not to skew the results. / Let's withhold our opinions until everyone has answered.	CORRECTION: 'stipulate' normally means specify as a condition or formally agree, not merely 'state an opinion'. KEY PURPOSE FRAME: so as not to + verb = in order not to.		
228	2271	2397	keep one's head held high / hold one's head high	/kip jʊɚ hɛd hɛld haɪ/	idiom	To maintain dignity, confidence, or self-respect despite criticism, failure, embarrassment, or hardship.	hold one's head up, stand tall, keep one's dignity	Even after the loss, she held her head high. / Keep your head held high—you handled it well.	More idiomatic base is 'hold your head high'; 'keep your head held high' is also natural. Physical posture metaphor → dignity/pride.		
228	2272	2398	in the face of adversity	/ɪn ðə feɪs əv ədˈvɝsəti/	phrase / collocation	While confronting hardship, misfortune, opposition, or difficult circumstances; despite such adversity.	despite hardship, amid difficulty, when facing adversity	She remained optimistic in the face of adversity.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: in the face of + danger/opposition/criticism/adversity = when confronted by/despite. Connects to WID2134 adversity.		
228	2273	2399	illustrator	/ˈɪləˌstreɪtɚ/	noun	A person who creates illustrations—images made to explain, decorate, interpret, or accompany text/content, especially for books, magazines, advertising, games, and digital media.	illustration artist, visual artist (broader)	The children's-book illustrator gave each character a distinctive look.	'Designer' is a different role and 'drawer' is not a natural professional synonym. Related: illustrate, illustration.		
228	2274	2400	empty rhetoric	/ˈɛmpti ˈrɛtərɪk/	noun phrase	Language that sounds persuasive, grand, principled, or impressive but is regarded as lacking substance, concrete action, evidence, or sincere commitment.	hollow rhetoric, empty words, hollow promises, hot air (informal)	Voters dismissed the promise as empty rhetoric without a concrete plan.	KEY COMBINATION: rhetoric + empty/hollow = persuasive language judged substantively vacant. Often political, corporate, or promotional; evaluative phrase.		
228	2275	2401	political rhetoric	/pəˈlɪtɪkəl ˈrɛtərɪk/	noun phrase	Language and persuasive techniques used in political argument, campaigning, public debate, or messaging; the term itself is neutral, though context may criticize rhetoric as manipulative, inflammatory, or lacking substance.	political discourse, political messaging, campaign language	The candidate's political rhetoric became sharper as the election approached. / Critics said the speech offered rhetoric rather than concrete policy.	CORRECTION: 'rhetoric' does not inherently mean insincere or empty language. Compare WID2400 'empty rhetoric', which explicitly adds that negative judgment.		
228	2276	2402	fluff / filler / empty talk	/ˈseɪɪŋ flʌf/	noun; informal expressions	Content or language that adds little substance, information, or value; 'fluff' can refer to padding in writing/media, while 'filler' is material used mainly to fill space/time.	fluff, filler, padding, empty talk, filler language	Cut the fluff and get to the main point. / The article has useful information, but there's a lot of filler.	CORRECTION: 'saying fluff' is not a natural fixed English expression. Teach productive nouns/phrases such as 'fluff', 'filler', 'filler language', or 'empty talk'.		
228	2277	2403	downy	/ˈdaʊni/	adjective	Covered with, made of, or resembling soft fine down—small soft feathers or fine hair/fuzz; by extension, very soft and fluffy.	soft, fluffy, feathery, fuzzy	The chick was covered in downy feathers. / A downy layer of hair covered the fruit.	KEY WORD FAMILY: down (soft feathers) → downy. Do not confuse with Down syndrome; unrelated word/history.		
228	2278	2404	communal	/kəˈmjunəl/	adjective	Shared, used, owned, or experienced by members of a group or community rather than privately by one person; relating to community life.	shared, collective, common, community	The hostel has a communal kitchen. / Meals were eaten at a communal table.	Common collocations: communal kitchen/bathroom/space/living/dining. 'Public' is not always equivalent: communal may be restricted to members of a particular group.		
228	2279	2405	savvy	/ˈsævi/	adjective; noun; verb (informal, verb less common)	Having practical knowledge, good judgment, or shrewd understanding, especially in a particular area; noun: practical know-how. Historically/colloquially, verb 'savvy?' can mean understand.	knowledgeable, shrewd, astute; know-how	She's financially savvy. / He has a lot of business savvy. / You need to be more media-savvy.	KEY PATTERNS: tech-/media-/business-savvy; have the savvy to do X. 'Smart' is broader; savvy emphasizes practical understanding.		
228	2280	2406	antithetical to	/ˌæntɪˈθɛtɪkəl/	adjective + preposition	Directly opposed to or fundamentally incompatible with something in principle, character, or effect.	opposed to, contrary to, incompatible with	That approach is antithetical to the values the organization claims to uphold.	KEY FRAME: X is antithetical to Y. More formal/stronger than simply 'different from'. Related noun: antithesis.		
229	2281	2407	brusque	/brʌsk/	adjective	Abrupt, curt, and lacking social warmth or politeness in manner or speech, sometimes unintentionally.	curt, abrupt, blunt, terse	His brusque reply sounded ruder than he intended.	Brusque describes manner, not necessarily hostility. A person can be efficient/direct but come across as brusque.		
229	2282	2408	lean in / lean into something	/liːn ɪn/	phrasal verbs / expressions	Literally, 'lean in' = incline one's body toward something. Figuratively, 'lean in' can mean engage more actively or assertively; 'lean into X' commonly means embrace, commit to, exploit, or deliberately intensify a challenge, role, quality, or tendency.	engage, commit, embrace, press into, take initiative	She leaned in to hear him better. / Instead of hiding the weirdness, the show leans into it. / He decided to lean into the challenge.	CORRECTION: simplify garbled note. 'Lean In' was popularized as a leadership/workplace slogan by Sheryl Sandberg, but figurative uses are broader. KEY CONTRAST: lean in (often engagement) vs lean into + object (actively embrace/use X).		
229	2283	2409	meteoric rise	/ˌmiːtiˈɔrɪk raɪz/	collocation / figurative phrase	An extremely rapid, striking rise in fame, success, status, popularity, value, etc.	rapid ascent, sudden rise, spectacular rise	The singer's meteoric rise from local clubs to stadium tours took only two years.	KEY COLLOCATION: meteoric rise to fame/prominence/success. Despite the image, meteors visually fall through the sky; the phrase conventionally means spectacularly rapid ascent.		
229	2284	2410	prone to	/proʊn tu/	adjective phrase	Likely or inclined to experience, suffer from, or do something, especially something undesirable, recurring, or characteristic.	susceptible to, liable to, inclined to, likely to	This road is prone to flooding. / I'm prone to overthinking when I'm tired.	KEY FRAME: prone to + noun/gerund. Not restricted to innate tendencies and not always negative, though negative contexts are especially common.		
229	2285	2411	goofball	/ˈɡuːfbɔl/	noun (informal, usually affectionate)	A silly, goofy, playful, or mildly ridiculous person, often used affectionately rather than as a serious insult.	goof, clown, joker, dork	You're such a goofball—you had the whole room laughing.	Tone is often warm/playful. 'Dork' can overlap but has different social/awkward connotations.		
229	2286	2412	titillate / titillated	/ˈtɪtəˌleɪtɪd/	verb	To excite, stimulate, or arouse interest—often in a superficial, sensational, or mildly sexual way.	excite, tantalize, stimulate, arouse	The tabloid story was designed to titillate readers. / The audience was titillated by the scandalous details.	Normalize base + source form. Often carries a slightly disapproving sense of cheap/sensational stimulation; sexual undertone is possible but not required.		
229	2287	2413	enthrall / enthralled	/ɪnˈθrɔl/	verb	To hold someone's attention completely; fascinate or captivate intensely.	captivate, fascinate, mesmerize, spellbind	The story enthralled me from the first page. / The children sat enthralled by the performance.	KEY ACTIVE/PASSIVE: X enthralls someone; someone is enthralled by/with X. Stronger than merely 'interest'.		
229	2288	2414	beguile	/bɪˈɡaɪl/	verb	To charm, delight, or fascinate someone, sometimes in a way that distracts or deceives; older/literary uses can mean deceive or trick.	charm, enchant, captivate; deceive, trick	The old town beguiled us with its narrow streets. / He beguiled investors with promises that proved false.	IMPORTANT: deception is possible but not inherent in every modern use. Can be positively charming or negatively deceptive depending context.		
229	2289	2415	catenate / concatenate	/ˈkætəˌneɪt/	verb (technical)	To link or join things into a chain or series. 'Concatenate' is the much more common computing/data term for joining strings or sequences end-to-end; 'catenate' is rarer/technical.	link, chain, concatenate, join	The program concatenates the first and last names into one string. / In technical writing, 'catenate' can mean link into a chain.	USAGE: retain catenate for recognition, but for programming/data, 'concatenate' is normally the word learners will encounter and use.		
229	2290	2416	geminate / geminate consonant	/ˈdʒɛmɪˌneɪts/	noun; adjective; verb (linguistics)	A geminate is a consonant that is phonologically or phonetically long/doubled relative to a singleton consonant; adjective 'geminate' describes it; verb 'geminate' means make/become geminate in linguistic analysis.	long consonant, doubled consonant (orthographic approximation)	Italian contrasts singleton and geminate consonants; for example, consonant length can distinguish words.	CORRECTION: gemination is not simply 'two identical letters' and does not generally refer to vowels in the same way. Spelling may signal gemination in some languages, but the linguistic concept concerns sound/phonological length.		
230	2291	2417	halve	/hæv/	verb	To divide something into two equal or roughly equal parts; or to reduce an amount, number, rate, distance, etc. by half.	cut in half, divide by two, reduce by half	She halved the apple. / The new process halved the time needed to finish the task.	Broaden productive use: halve costs/time/risk/number. Pronounced /hæv/; the 'l' is silent.		
230	2292	2419	take a fall / take the fall	/teɪk ə fɔl/	idiom / phrase	'Take the fall' = accept or receive blame/punishment, often for others or as a scapegoat. 'Take a fall' can also literally mean fall down and, in some contexts, deliberately lose or suffer a setback.	take the blame, take the hit, be the scapegoat	He took the fall for the accounting scheme. / She took a nasty fall on the stairs.	CORRECTION: for the blame/cover-up sense, the standard idiom is usually 'take THE fall', not 'take A fall'. Preserve 'take a fall' for literal/contextual uses.		
230	2293	2420	plough / plow	/plaʊ/	noun; verb	A farm implement used to turn or break soil; verb: to turn soil with a plough/plow. Figuratively, to move or work through something forcefully, steadily, or with difficulty.	till, cultivate; push through, forge through	They ploughed the field before planting. / She plowed through three chapters in one evening.	CORRECTION: repair garbled definition. BrE 'plough'; AmE 'plow'. Productive figurative forms: plough/plow through work, traffic, snow, a crowd.		
230	2294	2421	percolate	/ˈpɝːkəˌleɪt/	verb	To filter or pass gradually through a porous substance; of liquid, to circulate/filter in coffee-making; figuratively, for ideas, information, feelings, or changes to spread or develop gradually.	filter, seep, trickle; spread gradually, develop	Water percolates through the soil. / The idea took time to percolate through the organization. / Let that thought percolate for a while.	Useful figurative patterns: percolate through + group/system; idea percolates = gradually develops/sinks in.		
230	2295	2422	josh / josh around	/ˈdʒɑʃɪŋ əˈraʊnd/	verb (informal)	To joke with or tease someone playfully; 'josh around' = engage in lighthearted joking or teasing.	joke, tease, kid, mess around	Relax—I was just joshing you. / We were joshing around after dinner.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAMES: josh someone; josh around. Repair punctuation in source example.		
230	2296	2423	probation	/proʊˈbeɪʃən/	noun	A period of supervised release in the community imposed in criminal justice under specified conditions; more broadly, a trial/evaluation period at work, school, or an organization.	supervised release; trial period, provisional period	He was sentenced to probation. / New employees have a three-month probation period.	IMPORTANT: legal probation is not simply 'testing after a mistake'; it is a formal legal status whose rules vary by jurisdiction. Common frames: on probation; probation period.		
230	2297	2424	cop out / cop out of something	/kɑp aʊt əv/	phrasal verb (informal)	To avoid a responsibility, commitment, challenge, or difficult action, often by making an excuse; noun 'cop-out' = an evasive excuse or avoidance.	duck, evade, back out, shirk	Don't cop out of the presentation just because you're nervous. / Calling it 'too complicated' felt like a cop-out.	KEY FORMS: cop out; cop out of + noun/gerund; a cop-out (noun, hyphenated).		
230	2298	2425	gallivant / gallivanting (also galavant)	/ˈɡæləˌvæntɪŋ/	verb (informal)	To roam, travel, or go around from place to place for pleasure, amusement, or social activity, often carefree and sometimes with a teasing implication of neglecting responsibilities.	roam, gad about, traipse around, wander	He spent the summer gallivanting around Europe. / Where have you been gallivanting off to?	CORRECTION/NORMALIZE: 'gallivant' is the standard spelling; 'galavant' is a recognized variant. The flirtation/courting association is historical/occasional, not required in modern use.		
230	2299	2426	fondle / fondling	/ˈfɑndlɪŋ/	verb	To touch or stroke someone or something gently and repeatedly; often affectionate, but frequently sexual when referring to a person's body.	caress, stroke, touch; grope (more aggressive/sexual)	She fondled the cat's ears absentmindedly. / In sexual contexts, 'fondle' can describe intimate touching.	Normalize base + source form. Current hair example is possible, but the word often has a sexual connotation with people; 'caress' is usually warmer/less clinical.		
230	2300	2427	leprechaun / leprechauns	/ˈlɛprəˌkɑnz/	noun	A small supernatural being in Irish folklore, typically portrayed as a solitary male fairy associated with shoemaking, trickery, and hidden treasure; modern popular imagery often includes green clothing and pots of gold.	Irish fairy, folkloric trickster	Stories about leprechauns have changed substantially in popular culture. / The children set up a playful 'leprechaun trap' for St. Patrick's Day.	Normalize singular/plural. 'Guarding pots of gold' is popular-culture shorthand, not a complete folklore definition.		
231	2301	2428	rile / riled / rile someone up	/raɪld/	verb; adjective/participle	To irritate, anger, or agitate someone; 'rile someone up' can intensify this and may also mean excite/stir a group into agitation.	annoy, irritate, anger, agitate, work up	The comment really riled him. / Don't rile the crowd up. / She was still riled about the decision.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAMES: rile someone; get/be riled; rile someone up.		
231	2302	2429	butthurt	/ˈbʌt.hɝt/	adjective; noun (slang, vulgar/derogatory)	Offended, resentful, or upset—often with the speaker implying the reaction is excessive or petty. As a noun, 'butthurt' can mean such resentment.	overly offended, resentful, salty (slang), thin-skinned (judgmental)	He was still butthurt about losing the argument.	Register: crude and dismissive; calling someone butthurt judges their reaction rather than neutrally describing emotion. Avoid in formal/respectful contexts.		
231	2303	2430	seal something up / seal it up	/siːl ɪt ʌp/	phrasal verb	To close or secure something tightly so air, liquid, people, etc. cannot pass; figuratively/contextually, to finish or secure a result, though 'seal the deal' is more idiomatic for completing an agreement.	close tightly, secure, seal off; finalize (context-dependent)	Seal the bag up before you put it in the freezer. / They sealed up the opening with tape.	CORRECTION: do not overteach 'seal it up' as a generic idiom meaning wrap up a project. Core construction is literal/productive 'seal X up'; compare fixed 'seal the deal'.		
231	2304	2431	berate / berated	/bɪˈreɪtɪd/	verb	To scold or criticize someone angrily and severely, often at length.	scold, rebuke, reprimand, lambaste	The manager berated him for ignoring the safety procedure. / She was berated in front of her coworkers.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAME: berate someone for + noun/gerund. Stronger than simply criticize.		
231	2305	2432	backlash	/ˈbækˌlæʃ/	noun	A strong adverse reaction by a group or the public against a change, decision, trend, person, or action.	pushback, adverse reaction, resistance, outcry	The policy triggered a backlash from local residents. / The company faced backlash over the ad.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: face/provoke/trigger/spark a backlash; backlash against/over X. Usually negative reaction, often collective.		
231	2306	2433	mediocrity	/ˌmiːdiˈɑkrəti/	noun	The state or quality of being mediocre—only average in quality, ability, or achievement; can also refer disparagingly to a mediocre person or thing.	averageness, ordinariness, middling quality	He feared settling for mediocrity. / The critic accused the studio of rewarding mediocrity.	Often strongly evaluative: 'average' can be neutral, whereas 'mediocrity' usually implies disappointing lack of excellence.		
231	2307	2434	cryogenics	/ˌkraɪəˈdʒɛnɪks/	noun	The science and technology of producing, maintaining, and studying very low temperatures and their effects on materials and systems.	low-temperature science, cryogenic engineering	Cryogenics is essential for technologies that use liquefied gases and superconducting systems.	PRECISION: cryogenics is not the same as cryonics, the controversial preservation of human bodies/brains after legal death in hopes of future revival.		
231	2308	2435	disreputable	/dɪsˈrɛpjətəbəl/	adjective	Having a bad reputation or not considered respectable, trustworthy, or socially acceptable.	shady, discreditable, dishonorable, seedy	He was associated with several disreputable businesses. / They stayed in a disreputable-looking hotel.	Can describe people, businesses, places, practices. 'Infamous' means famous for something bad and is not always interchangeable.		
231	2309	2436	out of line / be out of line	/ˈbiɪŋ aʊt əv laɪn/	idiom / adjective phrase	Beyond acceptable limits of behavior, authority, propriety, or fairness; inappropriate, disrespectful, or presumptuous.	inappropriate, unacceptable, over the line, presumptuous	That comment was completely out of line. / I was out of line to speak to you that way.	Normalize from 'being out of line'. KEY FRAME: be out of line; tell someone they're out of line. Also literal 'out of line' = not aligned/in sequence.		
231	2310	2438	jug / jugs	/dʒʌɡz/	noun; slang noun	A container, typically with a handle and narrow opening, for holding/pouring liquids. Plural 'jugs' is also vulgar/dated slang for breasts.	pitcher/container (context-dependent); breasts (vulgar slang)	She filled a jug with water. / The comedy used a crude joke about 'jugs'.	Normalize singular/plural. Slang sense is objectifying and somewhat dated; recognition-focused. A pitcher and jug overlap but regional/object differences exist.		
232	2311	2439	martyr / martyr oneself	/ˈmɑrtɚ/	noun; verb	A person who is killed or suffers severely because of a belief, cause, or identity; figuratively, someone who conspicuously suffers or sacrifices and may seek sympathy. Verb: to kill/persecute as a martyr, or figuratively 'martyr oneself'.	sufferer for a cause; self-sacrificer (figurative), victim (not exact)	She was remembered as a martyr for the movement. / Stop martyring yourself and ask someone else to help.	Do not make attention-seeking part of the core historical/religious definition. Figurative 'play the martyr' often criticizes performative self-sacrifice.		
232	2312	2440	holier-than-thou	/ˈhoʊliɚ ðæn ðaʊ/	adjective (informal, disapproving)	Self-righteously convinced of or displaying one's moral superiority over others.	self-righteous, sanctimonious, preachy, morally superior	His holier-than-thou tone made a reasonable point sound unbearable.	Standard fixed form is 'holier-than-thou'. Usually attributive/predicative adjective: a holier-than-thou attitude; don't get holier-than-thou with me.		
232	2313	2441	goody-goody	/ˈɡʊdi ˌɡʊdi/	noun; adjective (informal, disapproving/teasing)	A person regarded as excessively well-behaved, virtuous, rule-following, or eager to win approval; adjective can describe such behavior.	goody two-shoes, teacher's pet (specific), excessively virtuous	He was teased for being a goody-goody who never broke a rule.	Repair punctuation. 'Prude' specifically concerns sexual/modesty standards and is not a general synonym. Compare WID2034 goody two-shoes.		
232	2314	2442	an undeniable pull between people / feel a pull toward someone	/ˌʌndɪˈnaɪəbəl pʊl bɪˈtwin ʌs/	romantic/figurative collocation	A strong sense of attraction, emotional draw, or compelling interest between/toward people; 'pull' metaphorically conceptualizes attraction as a force drawing someone closer.	mutual attraction, chemistry, magnetic pull, draw	There was an undeniable pull between them. / I felt a strange pull toward her from the start.	Teach productive construction: feel a pull toward X; there is a pull between X and Y. 'Undeniable' intensifies but is not part of a fixed idiom.		
232	2315	2443	breathe someone/something in	/briːð ˈsʌmwʌn ɪn/	verb construction / poetic expression	Literally, to inhale air, scent, smoke, etc.; poetically, 'breathe someone/something in' can mean deeply take in and savor a person's presence, scent, atmosphere, or moment.	inhale; take in, savor, absorb	She stepped outside and breathed in the cold air. / He held her close and breathed her in.	KEY CONSTRUCTION: breathe X in. Romantic use is creative/poetic rather than a fixed idiom; literal inhalation is the base meaning.		
232	2316	2444	reverently	/ˈrɛvɚəntli/	adverb	With deep respect, awe, solemn regard, or religious devotion.	respectfully, devoutly, solemnly, with reverence	She reverently touched the old manuscript. / The congregation listened reverently.	Word family: revere (verb), reverence (noun), reverent (adj), reverently (adv). Can be religious or secular.		
232	2317	2445	cup someone's face / cup something in one's hands	/kʌp wʌnz feɪs/	verb construction / gesture	To hold something gently in curved hands as if forming a cup; 'cup someone's face' means gently hold the sides of their face, often affectionately or reassuringly.	cradle, hold gently	She cupped his face in her hands. / He cupped the tiny bird in his palms.	Teach productive verb 'cup + object', not only romantic phrase. Common: cup one's hands, cup someone's cheek/face.		
232	2318	2446	kip / kip down	/kɪp daʊn/	verb; noun (BrE informal)	To sleep, especially briefly or somewhere temporary/informal; 'kip down' = settle down to sleep. Noun: a sleep/nap.	sleep, crash, bed down, nap	You can kip on the sofa tonight. / We kipped down in the spare room. / I need a quick kip.	Broaden to core BrE 'kip'. 'Kip down' is possible, but simply 'kip' and 'have a kip' are very common.		
232	2319	2447	saw wood / saw logs	/sɔ wʊd/	idiom (informal, humorous)	To snore, especially loudly; the sound is humorously compared to sawing wood/logs.	snore, saw logs	He was sawing logs before the movie even ended.	'Saw logs' is especially common in AmE; 'saw wood' also occurs. 'Wheeze' is not a synonym for snoring.		
232	2320	2448	cop some Z's / get/catch some Z's	/kɑp səm ziːz/	idiom (informal)	To get some sleep or take a nap.	get some sleep, catch some Z's, get some shut-eye, snooze	I'm exhausted—I'm going to cop some Z's. / Try to catch a few Z's on the plane.	'Cop some Z's' is less common than get/catch some Z's but understandable, especially AmE slang. Z's represent sleep/snoring.		
233	2321	2450	a paragon of	/ə ˈpærəˌɡɑn ʌv/	noun phrase / collocation	A person or thing regarded as an outstanding model or perfect example of a particular quality.	model, exemplar, embodiment, ideal	She remained a paragon of patience even during the delay. / The building was once considered a paragon of modern design.	KEY FRAME: a paragon of + virtue/quality/type. Often elevated or slightly ironic depending context.		
233	2322	2451	catch forty winks / catch some winks; '10,000 winks' (playful hyperbole)	/kæʧ tɛn ˈθaʊzənd wɪŋks/	idiom + creative variant	To take a short sleep or nap. 'Forty winks' is an established idiom; 'catch some winks' is also natural. 'Catch 10,000 winks' is a playful exaggeration, not a standard idiom.	take a nap, get some sleep, catch some Z's, get some shut-eye	I'm going to catch forty winks before dinner. / After that week, I feel like I need 10,000 winks.	CORRECTION: normalize the established expressions and preserve '10,000 winks' only as transparent humorous hyperbole. Number formatting standardized.		
233	2323	2452	superannuate / superannuated	/ˌsuːpərˈænjuˌeɪt/	verb; adjective/participle (formal, chiefly BrE/Commonwealth contexts)	To retire someone because of age or eligibility for a pension; by extension, to make/consider something obsolete through age. 'Superannuated' can mean retired or antiquated/outdated.	retire, pension off; obsolete, antiquated	He was superannuated after decades of service. / The office finally replaced its superannuated equipment.	Current machinery example is possible figuratively but uncommon as active verb. Recognition-focused formal word; 'superannuation' is a pension/retirement term in several countries.		
233	2324	2453	buffoon	/bəˈfuːn/	noun	A person who behaves in a ridiculous, foolish, or clownish way, often making themselves an object of amusement or contempt.	clown, fool, oaf, jester (historical role)	He made himself look like a buffoon by interrupting everyone with bad jokes.	Can be comic or insulting. 'Idiot' emphasizes stupidity; buffoon emphasizes ridiculous/clownish behavior.		
233	2325	2454	that won't fly / something won't fly	/ˈsʌmθɪŋ wɪl nɑt flaɪ/	idiom (informal)	Used to say that an excuse, idea, behavior, proposal, or arrangement will not be accepted, tolerated, believed, or succeed.	won't be accepted, won't work, won't pass, won't be tolerated	That excuse won't fly with the professor. / Charging extra after we agreed on the price isn't going to fly.	KEY FRAME: X won't fly; X won't fly with + person/group. Meaning depends on context: unacceptable, implausible, or unworkable.		
233	2326	2455	put one's foot in it / put one's foot in one's mouth	/ju pʊt jɚ fʊt ɪn ɪt/	idiom	To say or do something tactless, embarrassing, or socially awkward, especially accidentally.	say the wrong thing, make a faux pas, blunder	I really put my foot in it when I asked why she wasn't married. / In AmE, 'put my foot in my mouth' is especially common.	Normalize source sentence to productive forms. BrE commonly 'put one's foot in it'; AmE commonly 'put one's foot in one's mouth'.		
233	2327	2456	my foot!	/maɪ fʊt/	interjection / dismissive expression (informal, somewhat old-fashioned)	An exclamation expressing disbelief, scorn, or emphatic rejection of what was just said—roughly 'nonsense!' or 'yeah, right!'	nonsense!, yeah right!, no way!, rubbish!	'He's an expert.' 'Expert, my foot! He started yesterday.'	CORRECTION: remove invented 'someone stepping on your toes' exasperation sense. This expression is figurative/dismissive, not normally a literal complaint about one's foot.		
233	2328	2457	succumb to something / get someone to give in to something	/ɡɛt ˈsʌmwʌn tu səˈkʌm tu/	verb construction / causative paraphrase	'Succumb to X' means yield to a force, temptation, pressure, illness, injury, etc.; if another person causes the yielding, English usually says 'persuade/get someone to give in' rather than treating 'get someone to succumb to' as a fixed phrase.	give in to, yield to, surrender to; persuade, wear down	He finally succumbed to temptation and bought it. / She eventually got him to give in and take a vacation.	CORRECTION: teach the productive lexical frame 'succumb to + noun' separately from causative 'get someone to give in'. 'Succumb' can also mean die from an illness/injury.		
233	2329	2458	siphon off	/ˈsaɪfən ɔf/	phrasal verb	To divert or remove part of a liquid, money, resources, people, attention, etc. from a larger supply or intended destination, literally or figuratively; it may be secret/illegal but need not be.	divert, drain off, draw away, skim off (often illicit)	The pipe siphons off excess water. / The scheme siphoned off public funds. / The new service siphoned customers off from its rivals.	CORRECTION: illegality/secrecy is common in financial contexts but not inherent. KEY FRAME: siphon off + resource / siphon X off from Y.		
233	2330	2459	crunch numbers / number-crunching	/krʌnʧ ˈnʌmbɚz/	idiom / informal collocation	To perform calculations or analyze quantitative data, especially to reach a decision, estimate, forecast, or comparison.	calculate, analyze the figures, run the numbers	Let's crunch the numbers before deciding whether the move is affordable. / The analysts spent the afternoon number-crunching.	Not necessarily 'complex' calculations. Related noun/adjective: number cruncher; number-crunching.		
234	2331	2460	apiarist	/ˈeɪpiəˌrɪst/	noun (formal/technical)	A person who keeps and manages honey bees; a beekeeper.	beekeeper	The apiarist inspected the hives for signs of disease.	'Beekeeper' is the normal everyday word; 'apiarist' is more formal/technical. Commercial activity is not required.		
234	2332	2461	mutiny	/ˈmjuːtəni/	noun; verb	An organized revolt or refusal to obey lawful authority by members of a military force or a ship's crew, especially against commanding officers; figuratively, a group rebellion against leadership. Verb: to mutiny.	rebellion, revolt, insurrection (broader)	The sailors staged a mutiny against the captain. / The crew threatened to mutiny over the conditions.	Add verb. Historically/legal definitions can be specific; figurative use is common for humorous workplace/team rebellion.		
234	2333	2462	shrill	/ʃrɪl/	adjective; verb	High-pitched, piercing, and often unpleasantly sharp in sound; figuratively, strident or aggressively insistent in tone. Verb: to make a shrill cry/sound.	piercing, screeching, high-pitched; strident	A shrill alarm woke us. / The debate became increasingly shrill.	CAUTION: figurative 'shrill' can be a loaded/subjective criticism, especially of a person's voice or advocacy. Describe tone precisely when possible.		
234	2334	2463	keep it up	/kip ɪt ʌp/	phrasal verb / expression	To continue doing something at the same level or in the same way. Often encouraging ('You're doing well—keep it up'), but can also be a warning ('Keep it up and you'll get in trouble').	continue, maintain it, carry on	Your pronunciation is improving—keep it up! / Keep it up and I'm leaving.	IMPORTANT POLARITY: not always praise. Tone/context determines encouragement vs threat/warning.		
234	2335	2464	scuttlebutt	/ˈskʌtəlˌbʌt/	noun (informal)	Rumor, gossip, or informal information circulating among a group, especially workplace, military, or organizational talk.	gossip, rumor, buzz, word	What's the scuttlebutt about the reorganization?	Origin: nautical 'scuttlebutt' was a cask of drinking water, where sailors gathered and exchanged gossip—roughly analogous to 'water-cooler talk'.		
234	2336	2465	scuttlebutt has it	/ˈskʌtəlˌbʌt hæz ɪt/	playful phrase / analogical expression	A playful formation modeled on 'rumor has it', meaning informal gossip suggests that something is true.	rumor has it, word has it, the scuttlebutt is that	Scuttlebutt has it that the office is moving downtown.	USAGE: understandable and playful, but less established than 'rumor has it'. More ordinary: 'The scuttlebutt is that...' or 'According to the scuttlebutt...'.		
234	2337	2466	vapid	/ˈvæpɪd/	adjective	Lacking liveliness, interest, intelligence, flavor, or stimulating substance; dull and insipid.	insipid, dull, bland, lifeless, empty	The interview was full of vapid celebrity chatter. / The drink tasted strangely vapid.	Often used for conversation, entertainment, remarks, personalities; literal 'flat/tasteless' sense is older/less common.		
234	2338	2467	colonoscopy	/ˌkɑːləˈnɑːskəpi/	noun (medical)	An endoscopic examination of the colon, typically using a flexible colonoscope inserted through the rectum; used for screening, diagnosis, surveillance, and sometimes treatment such as polyp removal.	endoscopic examination of the colon, colon screening (when used for screening)	She scheduled a screening colonoscopy after discussing it with her doctor.	PRECISION: not merely 'a flexible camera'; the instrument is a colonoscope. Not every colonoscopy is routine screening; indications vary.		
234	2339	2468	mock up / mock-up	/mɑk ʌp/	verb; noun	Verb: to make a model, draft, or preliminary representation of something for evaluation, demonstration, design, or testing. Noun 'mock-up': that model/draft.	prototype, model, draft, sample	Can you mock up a new homepage layout? / They showed us a mock-up of the packaging.	FORM: verb 'mock up' two words; noun/adjective commonly 'mock-up' hyphenated. A mock-up may be nonfunctional, unlike some prototypes.		
234	2340	2469	sanctimonious	/ˌsæŋktəˈmoʊniəs/	adjective (disapproving)	Making an exaggerated, hypocritical, or irritating display of moral superiority, piety, or righteousness.	self-righteous, holier-than-thou, preachy, smugly moralizing	His sanctimonious lecture about honesty was hard to take seriously after he lied.	Stronger/more negative than simply 'moral'. Compare WID2440 holier-than-thou.		
235	2341	2471	Erntedankfest	/ˈɛʁntəˌdaŋkˌfɛst/	noun (German cultural term)	A German harvest-thanksgiving festival or observance ('harvest thanksgiving festival'), generally celebrated in autumn in churches and communities; customs and timing vary by region.	German harvest thanksgiving, harvest festival	The church decorated the altar with produce for Erntedankfest.	CORRECTION: capitalize German noun. It is comparable to harvest-thanksgiving traditions but is not simply a German version of U.S./Canadian Thanksgiving.		
235	2342	2472	be in the same boat	/bi ɪn ðə seɪm boʊt/	idiom	To be in the same situation or predicament as another person/group, often—but not necessarily—a difficult one.	be in the same situation, share the same predicament	Neither of us knows the answer, so we're in the same boat.	Commonly negative/difficult context, but core meaning is shared circumstances. KEY FRAME: be in the same boat as + person.		
235	2343	2473	be on the same page	/bi ɑn ðə seɪm peɪdʒ/	idiom	To share the same understanding, expectations, interpretation, or plan; often used to check alignment before acting.	be aligned, agree, share an understanding, see eye to eye	Before we start, let's make sure we're on the same page about the deadline.	Not necessarily complete agreement on every opinion; often means shared understanding of the current plan/facts.		
235	2344	2474	chicken out / chicken out of	/ˈʧɪkən aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal)	To decide not to do something because of fear, nervousness, or lack of courage.	back out, lose one's nerve, wimp out, bottle out (BrE)	I was going to try the ride, but I chickened out. / He chickened out of asking her.	KEY FORMS: chicken out; chicken out of + noun/gerund. More specifically fear-based than generic 'back out'.		
235	2345	2475	lack remorse / show no remorse	/læk rɪˈmɔrs/	verb phrase / collocation	To feel or display little or no deep regret or guilt about wrongdoing or harm one has caused.	be unremorseful, be unrepentant, show no regret	The court heard testimony that he showed no remorse for the harm he caused.	CORRECTION: 'cold-hearted' is an inference, not a synonym. Distinguish internal 'lack/feel no remorse' from observable 'show no remorse'.		
235	2346	2476	creep someone out / creep me out	/krip mi aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal)	To make someone feel uneasy, disturbed, disgusted, or frightened in an eerie or unsettling way.	unsettle, freak out, give the creeps, disturb	That empty hallway creeps me out at night. / The doll really creeped her out.	Normalize productive frame: X creeps someone out. Related: give someone the creeps. Usually eerie discomfort rather than ordinary fear alone.		
235	2347	2477	avail / to no avail / avail oneself of	/əˈveɪl/	noun; verb (formal)	Noun 'avail' = use, benefit, or advantage, now especially in fixed phrases such as 'to no avail' (= without success). Verb: to help/benefit; reflexive 'avail oneself of X' = make use of X.	use, benefit, advantage; make use of	We tried everything, but to no avail. / She availed herself of the free legal advice.	KEY CONSTRUCTIONS are more useful than bare word: to no avail; of little avail; avail oneself of. Bare transitive verb is formal/less common.		
235	2348	2480	crush / crushed	/krʌʃt/	verb; adjective/participle	To press/squeeze so something is damaged or deformed; defeat overwhelmingly; emotionally devastate. 'Crushed' can mean physically compressed or deeply disappointed/heartbroken.	squash, compress; defeat; devastate	The box was crushed in transit. / Our team crushed them 6–0. / I was crushed when the trip was canceled.	Normalize base + source form. Add common competitive sense 'crush someone/team' = defeat decisively.		
235	2349	2481	one's ass off	/æs ɔf/	slang intensifier pattern (vulgar)	A productive vulgar intensifier meaning to an extreme degree or with great effort, used after many verbs: laugh/work/dance/run/freeze one's ass off, etc.	extremely, intensely, very hard	I laughed my ass off. / She worked her ass off to finish on time. / We froze our asses off outside.	Teach as construction, not standalone 'ass off'. Meaning varies by verb: intense action/effort or extreme state. Pronoun must agree with subject.		
235	2350	2482	bumpkin	/ˈbʌmpkɪn/	noun (informal, derogatory)	An insulting term for a supposedly unsophisticated, awkward, or naive person from the countryside.	hick, yokel, country bumpkin, rustic (less insulting in some contexts)	They dismissed him as a country bumpkin because of his accent.	Register matters: stereotypes rural people as unsophisticated. 'Country folk' is neutral/plural and not a synonym.		
236	2351	2483	rube / be a rube	/ʃiːz ˈkaɪnd əv ə ruːb/	noun (informal, derogatory, somewhat old-fashioned)	A naive, unsophisticated, or gullible person, traditionally stereotyped as being from the countryside.	gullible person, simpleton, bumpkin, sucker (gullibility focus)	He thought I was some rube he could scam. / She's kind of a rube when it comes to online ads.	Normalize from full source sentence while preserving usable frame. Often emphasizes easy-to-fool naivety more than literal rural origin.		
236	2352	2484	contrition	/kənˈtrɪʃən/	noun (formal)	Sincere remorse, guilt, and regret for having done wrong, often accompanied by a desire to make amends.	remorse, penitence, repentance, regret	He expressed genuine contrition for the harm he had caused.	Stronger/more formal than ordinary regret. Common: show/express contrition; genuine/deep contrition.		
236	2353	2485	not hold a candle to someone/something	/ˈdʌzənt hoʊld ə ˈkændl tu ju/	idiom	To be clearly inferior to someone or something in quality, ability, attractiveness, importance, etc.	not compare to, pale in comparison with, be nowhere near as good as	The remake doesn't hold a candle to the original. / He says that singer is good, but she doesn't hold a candle to you.	Normalize productive negative frame. Historically connected to the lowly task of holding a candle for a skilled worker/performer, though exact origin stories vary.		
236	2354	2486	TCBY	/ˈtiː ˈsiː ˈbiː ˈwaɪ/	proper noun / brand initialism	A U.S.-origin frozen-yogurt brand and chain. The initials currently expand to 'The Country's Best Yogurt'; the company originally used a different name before adopting TCBY.	frozen-yogurt chain (descriptive)	We stopped at TCBY for frozen yogurt.	Brand/reference card. Avoid treating 'dessert place' as a lexical synonym. Corporate branding/history can change, so core learning value is recognizing the chain/initialism.		
236	2355	2487	tire fire	/ˈtaɪɚ ˌfaɪɚ/	noun; figurative slang	Literally, a fire involving tires, which can be difficult and polluting; figuratively, a disastrously chaotic, dysfunctional, or badly managed situation.	disaster, dumpster fire, train wreck, mess	The rollout turned into a complete tire fire. / Firefighters struggled to contain the tire fire.	Add literal source image. Figurative use is vivid/informal; 'dumpster fire' is a close modern metaphor.		
236	2356	2488	testify	/ˈtɛstəˌfaɪ/	verb	To give evidence or make a formal statement as a witness, especially under oath in a legal proceeding; more broadly, to declare/attest to something, including religious usage.	give evidence, bear witness, attest, declare	She testified that she saw the car leave. / Several witnesses testified in court.	KEY FRAMES: testify that + clause; testify about/to X; testify against/for someone. 'Swear' is not a synonym; witnesses may be sworn in before testifying.		
236	2357	2489	bear witness (to something)	/bɛr ˈwɪtnəs/	verb phrase / idiom	To witness, observe, or attest to something; also for evidence or facts to demonstrate/confirm something.	witness, attest to, testify to, demonstrate	She bore witness to the destruction firsthand. / These records bear witness to decades of change.	KEY FRAME: bear witness to + event/fact. Can mean personally witness OR serve as evidence. Past tense 'bore witness'.		
236	2358	2490	totter	/ˈtɑtɚ/	verb	To stand or move with unsteady, wavering steps as if likely to fall; figuratively, a system/institution can totter on the brink of collapse.	wobble, stagger, teeter	The toddler tottered across the room. / The regime was tottering on the brink of collapse.	Remove duplicate example. KEY: totter toward/across; totter on the brink of X. Often evokes weakness, infancy, age, or instability.		
236	2359	2491	garble / garbled	/ˈɡɑrbəl/	verb; adjective/participle	To reproduce, transmit, arrange, or report words/data in a distorted, confused, or unintelligible form; 'garbled' describes the result.	mangle, distort, scramble, jumble	The bad connection garbled his voice. / The message arrived garbled and incomplete.	Normalize base + source form. Can be speech/audio, text, data, or retelling.		
236	2360	2492	contraction	/kənˈtrækʃən/	noun	The act/process of becoming smaller, shorter, tighter, or reduced; a shortened linguistic form such as 'can't'; a tightening of muscle, including uterine contractions; and in economics, a decline in economic activity.	shortening, shrinking, tightening; contracted form	'Can't' is a contraction of 'cannot.' / She felt another contraction. / The economy entered a sharp contraction.	Major polysemy missing: grammar, muscles/childbirth, economics, physical reduction. 'Abbreviation' is broader and not the same as grammatical contraction.		
237	2361	2493	creatine	/ˈkriːətiːn/	noun	A naturally occurring compound made from amino-acid precursors and stored largely in skeletal muscle as phosphocreatine, helping rapidly regenerate ATP during high-intensity activity; creatine monohydrate is also a widely used dietary supplement.	creatine monohydrate (common supplement form), phosphagen-system compound (descriptive)	Creatine helps supply rapid energy during short bursts of high-intensity exercise. / He uses creatine monohydrate as a supplement.	CORRECTION/PRECISION: creatine is not itself an amino acid; it is synthesized from amino acids. Supplement effects/appropriate use depend on context; avoid promising muscle growth or recovery as guaranteed outcomes.		
237	2362	2494	base / baser	/ˈbeɪsɚ/	adjective; comparative (formal/literary)	Morally low, dishonorable, selfish, or ignoble; 'baser' is the comparative, especially common in phrases such as 'baser instincts' or 'baser motives'.	ignoble, low, vile, dishonorable	The speech appealed to the crowd's baser instincts. / He suspected a baser motive behind the generosity.	Normalize base + comparative. This moral sense of 'base' is formal/literary and distinct from base = foundation, military base, chemical base, etc.		
237	2363	2495	ignoble	/ɪɡˈnoʊbəl/	adjective (formal)	Not honorable, noble, or worthy in character, motive, action, or purpose; morally base or shameful.	dishonorable, base, unworthy, shameful	Jealousy was an ignoble motive, but at least he admitted it.	Common: ignoble motive/action/end. Contrast noble. Slightly elevated/literary.		
237	2364	2496	cater for / cater to	/ˈkeɪtɚ fɔr/	phrasal verbs	To provide food, services, facilities, or what a group needs/wants. BrE commonly uses 'cater for' a group/event; 'cater to' is especially common in AmE for serving needs/tastes, and can imply excessive accommodation in some contexts.	provide for, serve, accommodate	The hotel caters for families with young children. / The app caters to beginners. / A local company catered the wedding.	Broaden dialect distinction. Also transitive 'cater an event' = provide food/service. 'Cater to someone's whims' can be mildly critical.		
237	2365	2497	whim	/wɪm/	noun	A sudden, often passing desire, idea, or change of mind that is not based on a strong reason or plan.	impulse, fancy, caprice, sudden urge	We drove to the beach on a whim. / He changes the rules on a whim.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: on a whim; at someone's whim; whim of fate. 'Caprice' is more formal and often stresses unpredictability.		
237	2366	2498	foible	/ˈfɔɪbəl/	noun	A minor weakness, eccentricity, or mildly annoying peculiarity in someone's character or behavior.	quirk, minor flaw, idiosyncrasy, weakness	His habit of labeling everything is one of his harmless foibles.	Usually relatively minor and often spoken of tolerantly. 'Shortcoming' can be more serious.		
237	2367	2499	whimsy	/ˈwɪmzi/	noun	Playful, fanciful, imaginative, or charmingly odd quality, humor, design, or behavior; sometimes an individual fanciful notion.	playfulness, fancifulness, quirkiness, caprice	The hand-drawn signs add a touch of whimsy to the café.	KEY COLLOCATION: a touch/sense of whimsy. Unlike 'whim', whimsy often describes an aesthetic or playful imaginative quality.		
237	2368	2500	inflexible	/ɪnˈflɛksəbəl/	adjective	Not capable of bending; figuratively, unwilling or unable to change, adapt, negotiate, or make exceptions.	rigid, unbending, unyielding, uncompromising	The material is strong but inflexible. / The policy is too inflexible for unusual cases.	Add literal physical sense. Can describe schedules/rules/systems as well as people; 'stubborn' specifically describes a person/stance and is not always equivalent.		
237	2369	2501	rigid	/ˈrɪdʒɪd/	adjective	Stiff and not able to bend; figuratively, fixed, strict, or unwilling/unable to adapt.	stiff, inflexible, unyielding, strict	The frame is rigid. / The schedule is too rigid to accommodate last-minute changes.	Important literal + figurative senses. Compare WID2500 inflexible: rigid often suggests structural stiffness or strict fixedness.		
237	2370	2502	a little bit of apprehension / feel apprehensive	/ə ˈlɪtəl bɪt əv ˌæprɪˈhɛnʃən/	noun phrase; adjective construction	A mild degree of anxiety or uneasiness about something expected or possible; 'apprehensive' is the corresponding adjective.	slight unease, mild anxiety, nervousness	I felt a little bit of apprehension before the interview. / I'm apprehensive about driving in the storm.	KEY FRAMES: apprehension about/over X; feel/be apprehensive about X. 'Apprehension' can also mean arrest or understanding in other contexts.		
238	2371	2503	eschew / eschewed	/ɪsˈtʃuːd/	verb (formal)	To deliberately avoid, reject, or abstain from something, especially as a matter of choice or principle.	avoid, shun, abstain from, steer clear of	She eschews social media. / He eschewed flashy effects in favor of a simple design.	Normalize base + source form. Usually transitive: eschew + noun/gerund. Formal; unlike 'avoid', often suggests deliberate preference/principle.		
238	2372	2504	scruffy	/ˈskrʌfi/	adjective	Untidy, shabby, or somewhat dirty in appearance; can describe people, hair, clothing, animals, or places.	unkempt, shabby, disheveled, bedraggled	He showed up looking scruffy and unshaven. / The little dog looked scruffy after the rain.	Often mildly negative but can be affectionate. 'Shabby' can focus worn condition; 'disheveled' focuses disorder.		
238	2373	2505	underscore	/ˌʌndɚˈskɔr/	verb; noun	Verb: to emphasize or make the importance of something especially clear. Noun: the '_' character, especially in writing/computing.	emphasize, highlight, stress; underscore character	The results underscore the need for better testing. / Use an underscore instead of a space in the filename.	Add major noun/computing sense. Figurative verb comes from literally underlining text.		
238	2374	2506	mitigate	/ˈmɪtɪˌɡeɪt/	verb (formal)	To reduce the severity, harmfulness, seriousness, or impact of something; not necessarily eliminate it.	alleviate, lessen, reduce, moderate	Trees can help mitigate urban heat. / The apology did little to mitigate the damage.	KEY FRAME: mitigate + risk/damage/impact/effects. Contrast 'prevent': mitigation reduces consequences or likelihood rather than guaranteeing prevention.		
238	2375	2507	read back / read something back	/riːd bæk/	phrasal verb	To repeat by reading aloud information that has just been written, received, or recorded, especially to verify accuracy.	repeat back, recite, confirm	Could you read the address back to me? / Air-traffic controllers may require a pilot to read back an instruction.	KEY FRAME: read X back (to someone). 'Repeat back' overlaps, but read back specifically implies reading/reciting recorded or received wording.		
238	2376	2508	bring down	/brɪŋ daʊn/	phrasal verb	To cause something/someone to fall or collapse; reduce a level, price, rate, or number; defeat/remove a government or powerful person; or make someone feel sad.	lower, reduce, topple, defeat; depress	The new supply helped bring prices down. / The scandal brought down the government. / Don't let one bad comment bring you down.	Highly polysemous. KEY PATTERNS: bring X down; bring down X. Add emotional and political senses.		
238	2377	2510	disingenuous	/ˌdɪsɪnˈdʒɛnjuəs/	adjective	Not candid or fully sincere; presenting oneself, an argument, or a question in a misleadingly innocent, incomplete, or strategic way.	insincere, misleading, evasive, not candid	It's disingenuous to pretend you had no idea what the rule meant.	CORRECTION: not limited to 'pretending to know less'. Often describes arguments/questions that conceal relevant knowledge, motives, or implications.		
238	2378	2511	manipulative	/məˈnɪpjələtɪv/	adjective	Seeking to influence or control people or situations, especially through indirect, unfair, deceptive, or emotionally exploitative means.	controlling, scheming, calculating, exploitative	The message felt manipulative because it tried to make customers feel guilty.	Usually negative. 'Persuasive' is neutral; manipulative implies problematic methods or concealed control.		
238	2379	2512	insufferable	/ɪnˈsʌfɚəbəl/	adjective	So unpleasant, irritating, arrogant, tedious, or extreme that it is very difficult to tolerate.	unbearable, intolerable, obnoxious	His smugness became insufferable. / The heat was insufferable by midday.	Can describe people/behavior or conditions. Often hyperbolic in everyday speech.		
238	2380	2513	lousy snitch	/ˈlaʊzi snɪtʃ/	slang phrase / insult	A contemptuous way to call someone a bad/despicable informer or tattletale. 'Lousy' intensifies the insult; 'snitch' is the core noun.	snitch, rat, tattletale, informer	He called me a lousy snitch for telling the teacher what happened.	Teach composition: lousy = contemptible/very bad + snitch = person who informs. Informal and hostile; 'informer' is more neutral/formal.		
239	2381	2514	rowdy	/ˈraʊdi/	adjective	Noisy, boisterous, and difficult to control, often because a group is excited, unruly, or disorderly.	boisterous, unruly, disorderly, rambunctious	The bar got rowdy after the game. / A rowdy group was shouting in the hallway.	Often group/crowd behavior. 'Boisterous' can be cheerful without being disorderly; rowdy more strongly suggests loss of control.		
239	2382	2515	stake out / stake something out; stakeout	/steɪkt aʊt/	phrasal verb; noun	To watch a place secretly over time, especially for police/investigative purposes; also to mark or claim an area/position. Noun 'stakeout' = surveillance operation.	surveil, watch; mark out, claim	Detectives staked out the warehouse. / We staked out a spot near the stage. / The police conducted a stakeout.	Normalize base + source form and add non-police sense. Verb two words; noun usually one word.		
239	2383	2516	Eustachian tube / auditory tube	/juˈsteɪʃən tuːb/	noun (anatomy)	A passage connecting the middle ear with the nasopharynx; it helps ventilate the middle ear and equalize pressure across the eardrum.	auditory tube, pharyngotympanic tube	Swallowing can help the Eustachian tube open during a flight.	Anatomical term; often capitalized Eustachian, though lowercase also occurs in medical style. Pressure equalization is one important function.		
239	2384	2517	temporomandibular joint (TMJ)	/ˌtɛmpəroʊˌmænˈdɪbjəlɚ dʒɔɪnt/	noun (anatomy)	Either of the paired joints connecting the mandible (lower jaw) to the temporal bone of the skull, enabling jaw movement.	TMJ, jaw joint	Pain around the temporomandibular joint can make chewing uncomfortable.	TMJ names the joint; TMD/temporomandibular disorders refers to disorders affecting the joint/masticatory system. Do not use TMJ as if it inherently means a disorder.		
239	2385	2518	hyperacusis	/ˌhaɪpɚəˈkuːsɪs/	noun (medical/audiological)	Abnormally reduced tolerance to ordinary sounds, so sounds that are comfortable to most people may feel excessively loud, uncomfortable, or painful.	decreased sound tolerance, sound sensitivity	Someone with hyperacusis may find ordinary dishes clattering painfully loud.	PRECISION: not simply 'good hearing' or generic dislike of noise. Related sound-tolerance conditions/terms are not interchangeable.		
239	2386	2519	characterize / be characterized by	/ˈkɛrɪktəˌraɪzd/	verb	To describe the distinctive features or nature of something; in passive construction, 'be characterized by X' = have X as a typical or defining feature.	describe, define, typify, distinguish	The author characterizes him as cautious. / The region is characterized by dry summers.	Normalize base + source form. KEY FRAMES: characterize X as Y; X is characterized by Y.		
239	2387	2520	might as well	/maɪt əz wɛl/	modal idiom	Used when there is no strong reason not to do something or no better alternative; also used with resigned acceptance when circumstances make an option effectively inevitable.	may as well, there's no reason not to, we may as well	We're already here, so we might as well look around. / If nobody else is coming, we might as well leave.	Preserve two key axes: practical 'no reason not to' and resigned 'given the situation, this is what remains'.		
239	2388	2521	planking	/ˈplæŋkɪŋ/	noun / gerund	The exercise of holding a plank position to train core stability; also a late-2000s/early-2010s internet fad involving lying face-down rigidly in unusual places.	plank exercise; planking meme	We did three sets of planking. / Old photos show people planking on benches and signs.	Separate fitness and meme senses. In fitness, 'plank' is more commonly the exercise noun: do/hold a plank.		
239	2389	2522	disrupt / disrupted	/dɪsˈrʌptɪd/	verb	To interrupt, disturb, or seriously interfere with the normal operation, continuity, activity, or development of something.	interrupt, disturb, interfere with, derail	The storm disrupted train service. / The protest disrupted the meeting.	Normalize base + source form. 'Disrupt' often implies more substantial interference than a brief interruption.		
239	2390	2523	retain	/rɪˈteɪn/	verb	To keep, continue to have, preserve, or remember something; also to hire/engage a professional's services, especially a lawyer.	keep, preserve, maintain, remember; engage	The company retained control of the patent. / I retained very little from the lecture. / They retained an attorney.	Add memory and professional-services senses. KEY: retain information; retain ownership/control; retain counsel.		
240	2391	2524	volatility	/ˌvɑlətɪlɪti/	noun	The tendency to change rapidly or unpredictably; in finance, the degree of variation in prices/returns; also emotional/political instability or a substance's tendency to vaporize.	instability, variability, turbulence; price variability	Market volatility increased sharply. / The region's political volatility worried investors.	CORRECTION: change is not necessarily 'for the worse'. Technical meanings vary by domain; finance volatility measures variation, not direction.		
240	2392	2525	pugnacious	/pʌɡˈneɪʃəs/	adjective	Eager or quick to argue, quarrel, or fight; aggressively combative in manner.	combative, belligerent, aggressive, quarrelsome	His pugnacious style turned every discussion into a fight.	Formal/literary flavor. Can describe rhetoric/attitude as well as people.		
240	2393	2526	wanderlust	/ˈwɑndɚˌlʌst/	noun	A strong desire or impulse to travel, roam, and experience unfamiliar places.	urge to travel, travel bug, desire to roam	After a year at home, her wanderlust came roaring back.	Borrowed from German and fully established in English. Usually positive/romanticized; not the same as general restlessness.		
240	2394	2527	ovulation is cyclical / ovulation occurs in cycles	/ˌɑvjəˈleɪʃən ɪz ˈsɪklɪkəl/	source sentence / biological statement	A statement that ovulation is part of a recurring reproductive cycle. In humans, timing and cycle length vary substantially; ovulation is not guaranteed to occur once every calendar month.	ovulation occurs cyclically, recurring ovulation	Ovulation occurs as part of the menstrual cycle, but its timing can vary from cycle to cycle.	CORRECTION: remove overgeneralized 'typically monthly/once a month in most women'. Preserve the language-learning point while keeping biological wording accurate.		
240	2395	2528	grow tired of / have grown tired	/ɡroʊn ˈtaɪɚd/	verb construction	To gradually become physically tired or, more commonly with 'of', bored, dissatisfied, or fed up over time.	become weary of, tire of, get fed up with	I've grown tired of hearing the same excuse. / By evening, we had grown tired from walking.	KEY FRAME: grow tired of + noun/gerund = gradually lose patience/interest. 'Have grown tired' is present perfect; source form alone is incomplete.		
240	2396	2529	don't be coy / be coy	/doʊnt bi kɔɪ/	adjective construction / imperative	To be deliberately reserved, evasive, or teasing about one's intentions, knowledge, or feelings; sometimes flirtatiously shy.	be evasive, play shy, be teasingly reserved	Don't be coy—just tell me what you think. / She was coy about whether she'd accept the offer.	Repair punctuation. Coy can be flirtatious, but also strategically evasive/noncommittal; not simply genuine shyness.		
240	2397	2530	say something outlandish / outlandish	/seɪ ˈsʌmθɪŋ aʊtˈlændɪʃ/	phrase; adjective	To say something strikingly bizarre, extreme, implausible, or unconventional; 'outlandish' is the productive adjective.	outrageous, bizarre, far-fetched, extravagant	He said something outlandish just to get a reaction. / The rumor sounded completely outlandish.	Teach the adjective beyond the source phrase: outlandish claim/outfit/idea/behavior.		
240	2398	2531	send out / send something out	/sɛnd aʊt/	phrasal verb	To dispatch, distribute, transmit, or emit something outward or to multiple recipients; can also send a person somewhere for a purpose.	distribute, dispatch, issue, transmit, emit	We'll send out invitations tomorrow. / The antenna sends out a signal. / They sent a technician out to inspect it.	Broaden beyond mail/email. KEY FRAME: send X out; send out X.		
240	2399	2532	nobody knows how to drive in the rain	/ˈnoʊˌbɑdi noʊz haʊ tə draɪv ɪn ðə reɪn/	colloquial complaint / hyperbole	A common hyperbolic complaint that other drivers seem unusually slow, reckless, or incompetent when it rains.	people drive badly in the rain, rainy-day traffic gripe	Every time traffic slows in a storm, somebody says, 'Nobody knows how to drive in the rain.'	Not a factual universal claim or fixed idiom; preserve as conversational hyperbole. 'Nobody knows how to...' is a productive exaggerated complaint pattern.		
240	2400	2534	roads are slickest when rain first begins / first-rain slickness	/ju noʊ ðə roʊdz ɑr ˈslɪkəst ɪn ðə fɜrst hæf ˈaʊɚ əv reɪn/	driving-safety statement	Road surfaces can become especially slippery near the beginning of rainfall when water mixes with accumulated oil, dust, and debris; conditions vary by surface, rainfall, traffic, and maintenance.	early-rain road slickness, wet-road hazard	Be extra careful when rain first starts; residue on the road can make the surface unusually slick.	CORRECTION: avoid presenting 'the first half hour' as a universal fixed rule. Teach 'slick' + driving context and the underlying caution accurately.		
241	2401	2535	the plants are going to love this / plants will love the rain	/ðə plænts ɑr ˈɡənə lʌv ðɪs wɛn ɪt reɪnz/	colloquial personification	A casual, personifying way to say that rain will be beneficial for plants.	the rain will be good for the plants, the garden needs this	Finally some rain—the plants are going to love this.	KEY DEVICE: 'X is going to love this' personifies plants and is common casual speech. Source wording 'when it rains' is redundant if spoken during/just before rain.		
241	2402	2536	I sleep better when it rains	/aɪ ˈækʧuəli sliːp ˈbɛtɚ wɛn ɪt reɪnz/	personal statement / comparative construction	A natural personal statement that one's sleep tends to be better during rainy weather; the reason is left to context.	rain helps me sleep, I find rain soothing	I actually sleep better when it rains—the sound relaxes me.	Teach productive comparative frame: sleep better/worse when + condition. This is personal preference, not a general claim about sleep quality.		
241	2403	2537	curl up with a good book / curl up with	/ðɪs ˈwɛðɚ meɪks mi wɑnt tə steɪ æt hoʊm, kɜrld ʌp wɪð ə ɡʊd bʊk/	idiom-like collocation	To settle into a comfortable curled or tucked position with a book, person, blanket, drink, etc., usually for relaxation.	cozy up with, settle in with, snuggle up with	This weather makes me want to stay home and curl up with a good book. / I curled up with a blanket and watched a movie.	Normalize to productive construction. KEY FRAME: curl up with + book/person/object; also curl up on/in + place.		
241	2404	2538	dock	/dɑk/	verb; noun	Verb: to deduct money from pay; bring a ship/spacecraft to a dock or connect vehicles/devices; cut short/removal in some contexts. Noun: a structure for boats/loading, or a docking location.	deduct, penalize; berth, connect	They docked his pay for the missed shift. / The ferry docked at noon. / Put the laptop back on the dock.	Major polysemy: current card only has wage deduction. 'Fine' is not exact because docking specifically subtracts an amount/benefit.		
241	2405	2539	cookie-cutter world / cookie-cutter	/ˈkʊki ˌkʌtɚz wɝld/	creative phrase; adjective	'Cookie-cutter' describes things made to the same standardized pattern and lacking individuality; 'a cookie-cutter world' is a creative application to a conformist environment.	formulaic, standardized, generic, uniform	He hated the cookie-cutter houses in the development. / The film imagines a cookie-cutter world where everyone is expected to fit the same mold.	CORRECTION: standard modifier is 'cookie-cutter', not possessive 'cookie cutter's'. Teach productive adjective; 'world' is source-specific application.		
241	2406	2540	sick burn	/sɪk bɝn/	slang phrase	An especially sharp, clever, funny, or devastating insult/comeback; often used jokingly to praise a roast.	roast, diss, zing, savage comeback	'Even your GPS gives up on you.' 'Ooh, sick burn.'	Internet/youth slang; can be sincere or ironic. 'Burn' is the insult; 'sick' intensifies positively = impressive.		
241	2407	2541	back at you / right back at you	/bæk æt ju/	informal expression	Used to return a greeting, compliment, wish, insult, gesture, or sentiment to the person who just directed it at you.	same to you, likewise, you too, right back at you	'Have a great weekend!' 'Back at you!' / 'You're amazing.' 'Right back at you.'	CORRECTION: repair garbled example. Can return positive or negative sentiment; 'right back at you' is emphatic.		
241	2408	2542	fluctuate	/ˈflʌktʃuˌeɪt/	verb	To rise and fall or vary irregularly in level, amount, value, condition, or intensity over time.	vary, oscillate, waver, shift	Prices fluctuate throughout the year. / Her energy level fluctuated from day to day.	Usually intransitive: X fluctuates; fluctuate between A and B; fluctuate around a level.		
241	2409	2543	ratify	/ˈrætəˌfaɪ/	verb (formal/legal/political)	To formally approve, confirm, or give official validity to an agreement, treaty, decision, or action through the required authority/process.	formally approve, confirm, endorse	The legislature voted to ratify the agreement.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: ratify a treaty/agreement/amendment. Ratification is formal approval, not merely informal support.		
241	2410	2544	interjection	/ˌɪntɚˈdʒɛkʃən/	noun (grammar)	A word or short expression used as an independent exclamation, reaction, greeting, hesitation, etc., often grammatically separate from the surrounding sentence.	exclamation, discourse expression	'Wow!', 'ouch!', and 'hey!' can function as interjections.	CORRECTION: interjections do not have to express 'strong emotion'; they can signal hesitation, response, greeting, attention, and more.		
242	2411	2545	hit the mark	/hɪt ðə mɑrk/	idiom	To achieve the intended effect, be accurate, or satisfy the relevant standard/goal.	be accurate, succeed, land well, be on target	Her criticism hit the mark. / The joke didn't quite hit the mark with this audience.	Literal target metaphor → figurative effectiveness/accuracy. Useful negative: miss the mark.		
242	2412	2546	alley	/ˈæli/	noun	A narrow passage or lane, especially between/behind buildings; in bowling, the long lane down which the ball is rolled; also appears in compounds/idioms such as alleyway and 'right up my alley'.	lane, passageway, alleyway; bowling lane	We cut through the alley behind the theater. / She rolled the ball straight down the bowling alley.	Clarify bowling sense: 'bowling alley' can mean the venue or lane depending context. Related idiom 'up one's alley' = suited to one's interests/skills.		
242	2413	2547	dismal / dismal days	/ˈdɪzməl deɪz/	adjective; collocation	Dismal = gloomy, depressing, bleak, or very poor/unsatisfactory. 'Dismal days' can mean gloomy-weather days or a bleak period.	gloomy, bleak, dreary, miserable	It was a dismal rainy afternoon. / The team ended a dismal season with another loss.	Teach productive adjective beyond source phrase: dismal weather/performance/results/prospects.		
242	2414	2548	sever	/ˈsɛvɚ/	verb	To cut or break something completely; figuratively, to end a connection, relationship, communication channel, or tie.	cut off, detach, break, terminate	The cable was severed. / The company severed ties with the supplier.	KEY COLLOCATION: sever ties/relations/connections. Stronger/more complete than merely weaken.		
242	2415	2549	share-out / share out	/ˈʃɛɚ aʊts/	noun; phrasal verb (chiefly BrE)	Noun 'share-out' = a distribution/division of money, resources, etc., or sometimes the resulting shares; verb 'share out' = divide and distribute among people.	distribution, division, allocation; divide up	They agreed on a share-out of the profits. / The money was shared out equally among the partners.	FORM: noun often hyphenated 'share-out'; verb 'share out' two words. Chiefly British.		
242	2416	2550	outlast	/ˌaʊtˈlæst/	verb	To continue, survive, remain effective, or endure longer than another person or thing.	last longer than, survive, outlive, endure beyond	This battery outlasted the others. / She outlasted every opponent in the tournament.	Transitive comparison is built into the verb: X outlasts Y. Not limited to people.		
242	2417	2551	make someone squat / squat	/meɪk mi skwɑt/	causative phrase; verb	'Make someone squat' literally means cause/require someone to lower into a squat. The verb 'squat' also means crouch, occupy property without permission, or reside temporarily in such a place. Unrelated slang 'not do/know squat' means nothing.	make someone crouch; squat, crouch	The coach made us squat and hold the position. / They squatted in the abandoned building. / He doesn't know squat about cars.	CORRECTION: remove unsupported metaphor 'force into a powerless situation'. Separate causative exercise phrase, property sense, and 'squat = nothing' slang.		
242	2418	2553	highfalutin / highfalutin'	/ˌhaɪfəˈlutən/	adjective (informal, often humorous/disapproving)	Pretentiously grand, fancy, sophisticated, or important-sounding, especially in language, ideas, tastes, or behavior.	pretentious, pompous, grandiose, fancy	Skip the highfalutin jargon and explain it plainly.	Often playful rather than severe. Variant spellings include highfalutin and highfalutin'.		
242	2419	2554	get pouty / pouty	/ˈɡɛtɪŋ ˈpaʊti/	adjective construction	To become sulky or visibly displeased, often with a protruding-lip expression associated with pouting.	sulky, petulant, moody	He gets pouty when he loses. / She was getting pouty after being told no.	Normalize to productive adjective/construction. 'Pouty' can describe facial appearance or behavior; often mildly teasing.		
242	2420	2555	brewery	/ˈbruːəri/	noun	A place or business where beer is brewed; can range from a small craft brewery to a large industrial operation.	brewing company, brewhouse (context-dependent)	We toured a local craft brewery. / The brewery produces several seasonal beers.	'Beer factory' is understandable but not the normal synonym. Related: brewer, brew, brewing.		
243	2421	2556	impressionable	/ɪmˈprɛʃənəbəl/	adjective	Easily influenced, shaped, or affected by other people, ideas, experiences, media, or surroundings.	susceptible, easily influenced, receptive	Young viewers can be especially impressionable. / He was impressionable and eager to fit in.	Not equivalent to naive: someone can be impressionable without lacking intelligence. Often used for children/young people but not exclusively.		
243	2422	2557	workaround / workarounds	/ˈwɝkəˌraʊndz/	noun	An alternative method used to bypass or cope with a problem, limitation, bug, or unavailable feature without fixing the underlying cause.	temporary solution, bypass, stopgap, hack	We found a workaround for the login bug. / These workarounds keep the system usable until the patch arrives.	Normalize singular/plural. A workaround can be temporary or long-lived; unlike a true fix, it usually leaves the root issue in place.		
243	2423	2558	take delivery / on taking delivery	/ɑn ˈteɪkɪŋ dɪˈlɪvəri/	verb phrase; formal time phrase	To officially receive goods, property, a vehicle, securities, etc. 'On taking delivery' = when/upon receiving them. In some markets, taking delivery can mean receiving the underlying asset rather than settling otherwise.	receive, accept delivery, take possession	Payment is due on taking delivery of the equipment. / She took delivery of her new car on Friday.	KEY FRAME: take delivery of + item; on/upon taking delivery = at the time of receipt. Formal/logistical/business usage.		
243	2424	2559	as to	/æz tu/	prepositional phrase (formal)	Regarding, concerning, or with respect to a topic; also used in constructions expressing uncertainty or questions about a detail.	regarding, concerning, with respect to	As to the budget, we need another estimate. / There was some confusion as to who was responsible.	Not specifically 'business' English. Formal/written; productive frames include as to whether/how/why/who.		
243	2425	2560	as such	/æz sʌʧ/	discourse phrase	In the exact capacity, category, or sense just mentioned ('as such'); sometimes used to draw a consequence from that status, but it does not simply mean 'therefore' in all contexts.	in that capacity, in itself, strictly speaking	He's not an employee as such; he's an independent contractor. / She is the team lead and, as such, approves final changes.	CORRECTION: 'therefore/accordingly' is only licensed when it follows from a previously established role/status. Not merely a generic transition.		
243	2426	2561	in the event of / in the event that	/ɪn ði ɪˈvɛnt/	formal conditional phrase	Used to introduce a possible future contingency: 'in the event of + noun' or 'in the event that + clause', roughly 'if' or 'if it happens that'.	in case of, if, should	In the event of a power failure, use the emergency exit. / In the event that the server fails, traffic will be redirected.	Teach grammar explicitly. Formal/legal/business style; 'in case' may imply precaution and is not identical in every sentence.		
243	2427	2562	make up / make up for	/meɪk ʌp/ vs /meɪk ʌp fɔr/	phrasal verbs	'Make up' has many senses: invent, constitute, reconcile, apply cosmetics, complete missed work/time, etc. 'Make up for X' specifically means compensate for a loss, deficiency, mistake, or disadvantage.	invent, constitute, reconcile; compensate for	We need to make up the missed hours. / Women make up half the team. / She apologized to make up for the delay.	CORRECTION: broaden highly polysemous 'make up'. KEY CONTRAST: make up X (e.g. missed time) vs make up for X (compensate for a problem).		
243	2428	2563	protocol	/ˈproʊtəˌkɑl/	noun	An established system of rules, procedures, conventions, or technical standards governing conduct, communication, operations, or data exchange.	procedure, rules, standard, convention	Follow the lab's safety protocol. / HTTPS is a protocol for secure web communication.	Add computing/network sense. Also diplomatic protocol = formal rules of official conduct.		
243	2429	2564	culture of accountability	/ˈkʌltʃɚ əv əˌkaʊntəˈbɪləti/	organizational collocation	An organizational environment in which people are expected to own commitments, explain decisions/results, follow through, and address failures rather than routinely deflect responsibility.	accountability culture, ownership culture	Clear roles and follow-through can help build a culture of accountability.	Evaluative management phrase, not a technical guarantee of performance or ethics. 'Accountability' involves answerability/ownership; implementation varies.		
243	2430	2565	cathartic	/kəˈθɑrtɪk/	adjective	Providing or associated with a sense of emotional release, relief, or cleansing; also historically/medically related to purging.	emotionally releasing, relieving, cleansing	Writing the letter was surprisingly cathartic. / The final scene felt cathartic after so much tension.	Everyday use often means emotionally relieving. Do not assume emotional expression is clinically therapeutic merely because it feels cathartic.		
244	2431	2566	catharsis	/kəˈθɑrsɪs/	noun	A feeling or process of emotional release or relief, especially after intense emotion; in literary theory, famously associated with emotional effect in tragedy.	emotional release, relief, purgation	The confession gave him a sense of catharsis. / The ending provides emotional catharsis for the audience.	Related adjective cathartic. Modern psychology does not make every 'venting' episode beneficial; lexical meaning is broader than a clinical claim.		
244	2432	2567	cut someone some slack	/kʌt ˈsʌmwʌn slæk/	idiom	To be less strict, critical, or demanding toward someone; allow extra tolerance or leeway because of circumstances.	go easy on, give someone a break, show leniency	Cut her some slack—it's her first day.	CORRECTION: standard idiom normally includes 'some': cut someone some slack. Repair punctuation.		
244	2433	2568	incomparable	/ɪnˈkɑmp(ə)rəbəl/	adjective	So exceptional, distinctive, or extreme that no adequate comparison/equal is available; in some contexts, simply not meaningfully comparable.	matchless, peerless, unequaled; not comparable	The view from the summit was incomparable. / The two datasets are incomparable because they use different definitions.	IMPORTANT POLYSEMY: laudatory 'without equal' vs literal/technical 'cannot fairly be compared'. Context decides.		
244	2434	2570	subreddit	/ˈsʌbˌrɛdɪt/	noun	A topic-focused community on Reddit, conventionally identified by an r/ name such as r/AskHistorians.	Reddit community, forum (broader)	She posted the question in a photography subreddit.	Platform-specific term. A subreddit is not merely any discussion group; it is a community within Reddit.		
244	2435	2571	alliteration	/əˌlɪtəˈreɪʃən/	noun	Repetition of the same or similar initial consonant sound in nearby words, especially for stylistic effect.	initial-sound repetition, sound patterning	'Peter Piper picked...' is a familiar example of alliteration.	CORRECTION: fundamentally about sounds, not necessarily the same written letter. Some definitions extend the term more broadly, but initial consonant-sound repetition is the core teaching sense.		
244	2436	2572	comeback	/ˈkʌmˌbæk/	noun	A return to success, popularity, health, competition, or prominence after decline/absence; also a quick reply or retort, especially a witty one.	return, resurgence, recovery; retort, comeback line	The singer made a comeback after years away. / I couldn't think of a good comeback until five minutes later.	CORRECTION: remove '(literal)'—the success-return sense is itself figurative/established, and the retort sense is major.		
244	2437	2573	backpedal / backpedaling	/ˈbækˌpɛdəlɪŋ/	verb; noun/gerund	Literally, to pedal a bicycle backward; figuratively, to retreat from, soften, reverse, or qualify a previous statement, promise, or position.	retreat, reverse course, walk back, retract	He started backpedaling after the comment drew criticism. / The company backpedaled on its earlier promise.	Normalize base + source form. Figurative use often implies reaction to pressure or realizing a position is untenable.		
244	2438	2574	let someone in / let someone in on something	/ju lɛt mi ɪn/	phrasal verb	Literally, allow someone to enter; figuratively, allow emotional access/trust; 'let someone in on X' = share a secret, plan, or information with them.	admit, open up to; confide in, tell	You really let me in when you told me how scared you were. / Can you let me in on the plan?	Normalize source phrase to productive constructions. Distinguish let someone in (access) from let someone in on + information.		
244	2439	2575	member	/ju ɑr ði ˈʌðɚ ˈmɛmbɚ/	noun	A person or entity belonging to a group, organization, body, or set; also a body part/limb in formal or old usage, and euphemistically the penis ('member').	participant, constituent; limb (formal), penis (euphemistic)	She's the newest member of the committee. / In older or euphemistic writing, 'member' can refer to a body part or penis.	CORRECTION: remove unsupported claim that 'the other member' is established Hong Kong Cantonese slang for a gay man. Preserve attested English group/body-part/euphemistic senses.		
244	2440	2576	tiptoe around something / someone	/ˈtɪpˌtoʊ əˈraʊnd/	phrasal verb / figurative construction	Literally, move quietly on one's toes; figuratively, avoid addressing a sensitive issue directly or behave extremely carefully around a person/topic to avoid conflict.	avoid, sidestep, tread carefully around	Everyone kept tiptoeing around the real problem. / I feel like I have to tiptoe around him when he's angry.	Add literal base and productive frames. Compare 'walk on eggshells around someone' for stronger ongoing caution.		
245	2441	2577	mora	/ˈmɔrə/	noun (linguistics)	A unit of phonological timing/weight used in analyses of syllable structure and rhythm; a syllable may contain one or more morae depending on the language.	timing unit, unit of syllable weight	In Japanese, rhythm is often analyzed in morae: 'Nippon' has more morae than syllables.	CORRECTION: 'each kana represents one mora' is a useful rough teaching shortcut but has exceptions/orthographic complications. Plural: morae or moras.		
245	2442	2578	impediment	/ɪmˈpɛdəmənt/	noun	Something that hinders, obstructs, or slows progress, action, movement, or communication; in 'speech impediment', a condition affecting speech production/fluency.	obstacle, hindrance, barrier, obstruction	Cost was the biggest impediment to expansion. / The character is described as having a speech impediment.	KEY FRAME: impediment to + noun/gerund. Broader than physical barrier.		
245	2443	2579	false friend / false friends	/fɔls frɛndz/	noun (linguistics/language learning)	A word/expression in one language that resembles a word in another language but differs significantly in meaning or usage, creating a risk of mistaken interpretation.	deceptive cognate (sometimes), misleading cross-language lookalike	English 'actual' and Spanish 'actual' ('current') are classic false friends.	Normalize singular/plural. Not all false friends are true cognates, so 'deceptive cognate' is not universally exact.		
245	2444	2580	dissent	/dɪˈsɛnt/	noun; verb	Disagreement with an established, majority, official, or authoritative view/decision; verb: to express or hold such disagreement.	disagreement, objection, opposition; disagree	Several judges dissented from the majority opinion. / The proposal passed despite strong dissent.	KEY FRAMES: dissent from + opinion/decision; dissenting opinion. More formal than ordinary disagree.		
245	2445	2581	flounder	/ˈflaʊndɚ/	verb; noun	Verb: to struggle clumsily or ineffectively, physically or figuratively. Noun: a type/group of flatfish.	struggle, falter, thrash; flatfish	I floundered through the interview. / The fish market sells fresh flounder.	Add major unrelated noun sense. Do not confuse with 'founder' = fail/sink/collapse; the verbs are commonly confused.		
245	2446	2582	markedly	/ˈmɑrkɪdli/	adverb	To a clearly noticeable, distinct, or significant degree.	noticeably, distinctly, significantly, appreciably	Her pronunciation improved markedly over the semester.	Often modifies comparative/change language: markedly different/better/lower/increased. 'Significantly' can have statistical meaning that markedly does not inherently carry.		
245	2447	2583	forthright	/ˈfɔrθˌraɪt/	adjective	Direct, open, and honest in expressing opinions or information, without unnecessary evasion.	frank, candid, straightforward, direct	She was forthright about the project's weaknesses.	Generally positive/neutral, though forthrightness can feel blunt. 'Blunt' more strongly suggests lack of tact.		
245	2448	2584	interlocutor / interlocutors	/ˌɪntɚˈlɑkjətɚz/	noun (formal)	A person participating in a conversation, dialogue, discussion, or exchange.	conversation partner, participant, speaker	Each interlocutor interpreted the silence differently.	Normalize singular/plural. Formal/academic term, useful in linguistics, philosophy, discourse analysis.		
245	2449	2585	backchanneling / backchannel cues	/ˈbækˌʧænəlɪŋ/	noun (conversation analysis/linguistics)	Listener responses that signal attention, understanding, agreement, surprise, etc. without normally taking over the conversational floor, such as 'uh-huh', 'mm-hm', nods, or brief reactions.	listener feedback, continuers, backchannel cues	Frequent 'mm-hm' responses can function as backchanneling while the other person keeps speaking.	PRECISION: backchannels are not simply 'active listening' and need not indicate agreement; they often signal continued attention or receipt.		
245	2450	2586	reticence	/ˈrɛtəsəns/	noun	Reluctance or restraint in speaking, revealing thoughts/feelings, or taking action; reserve.	reserve, reluctance, restraint, unwillingness to speak	His reticence about the incident made the interview difficult.	Not identical to shyness: reticence can be deliberate/context-specific. KEY: reticence about/to do something.		
246	2451	2587	communication apprehension	/kəˌmjunəˈkeɪʃən ˌæprɪˈhɛnʃən/	noun (communication studies)	Fear or anxiety associated with actual or anticipated communication with another person or people, potentially across interpersonal, group, meeting, or public-speaking contexts.	communication anxiety, speaking anxiety	Her communication apprehension was strongest during unplanned group discussions.	PRECISION: broader than public-speaking anxiety. A recognized construct in communication research; not itself a diagnosis.		
246	2452	2588	utterance	/ˈʌtɚəns/	noun (linguistics/general)	Something spoken or vocalized; in linguistics, a stretch of speech produced in a particular context, not necessarily equivalent to a grammatical sentence.	spoken expression, speech segment, remark	'Uh-huh' can be a complete utterance even though it isn't a sentence.	Important distinction: sentence is an abstract grammatical unit; utterance is an actual instance of language use in context.		
246	2453	2589	recalcitrance	/rɪˈkælsɪtrəns/	noun (formal)	Stubborn resistance to authority, control, discipline, instruction, or change.	defiance, resistance, obstinacy, noncompliance	The agency struggled with the contractor's recalcitrance.	Related adjective recalcitrant. Stronger/more formal than simple reluctance; often implies persistent resistance.		
246	2454	2590	impede	/ɪmˈpid/	verb (formal)	To hinder, obstruct, delay, or make progress/movement more difficult.	hinder, obstruct, hamper, slow	Heavy snow impeded traffic. / Poor documentation can impede debugging.	Transitive: impede + progress/process/person. Often means slow/hinder, not necessarily completely prevent.		
246	2455	2591	hierarchical	/ˌhaɪəˈrɑrkɪkəl/	adjective	Arranged in a hierarchy of ranked levels, categories, or authority; relating to such a hierarchy.	ranked, tiered, stratified	The company has a strongly hierarchical management structure. / The files are organized hierarchically into folders.	Broaden beyond authority: data/classification structures can also be hierarchical.		
246	2456	2592	homogeneity	/ˌhoʊməˈdʒinɪti/	noun	The quality or state of being relatively uniform or similar in composition, character, properties, or membership.	uniformity, sameness, similarity	The sample's homogeneity made subgroup comparisons difficult.	CORRECTION: homogeneity does not imply 'fewer internal conflicts'. That is a separate causal claim. Contrast heterogeneity.		
246	2457	2593	training in a vacuum / practice in isolation	/ˈtreɪnɪŋ ɪn ə ˈvækjuəm ɪˈfɛkt/	descriptive metaphor / phrase	Training or practicing a skill without enough realistic context, interaction, variability, feedback, or transfer conditions; a metaphorical criticism that practice is isolated from real-world use.	decontextualized practice, isolated training	Pronunciation drills can feel like training in a vacuum if they never connect to real conversation.	CORRECTION: 'training in a vacuum effect' / 'vacuum effect' is not established here as a standard named effect. Teach productive metaphor 'in a vacuum' = in isolation from relevant context.		
246	2458	2594	throw someone off / be thrown off	/bi θroʊn ɔf/	phrasal verb	To confuse, distract, surprise, unsettle, or disrupt someone's concentration, rhythm, expectation, or judgment.	disorient, distract, unsettle, confuse	The unexpected question threw me off. / I was thrown off by how quickly she changed the subject.	KEY ACTIVE/PASSIVE: X throws someone off; someone is thrown off by X. Also literal 'throw off' has other senses such as remove/cast off.		
246	2459	2595	monolithic	/ˌmɑnəˈlɪθɪk/	adjective	Literally, formed from or resembling a single massive block; figuratively, very large, unified, uniform, or treated as if lacking internal diversity.	massive, unitary, uniform, undifferentiated	The temple contains a monolithic stone column. / It's misleading to treat the entire generation as monolithic.	CORRECTION: resistance to change is possible contextually but not inherent. Important critical use: 'not monolithic' = internally diverse.		
246	2460	2596	glomp / *glomps you*	/ɡlɑmps ju/	verb / internet-fandom slang	To give someone an exuberant, often sudden tackle-like hug; in role-play text, '*glomps you*' narrates that action.	tackle-hug, pounce-hug, hug enthusiastically	She ran over and glomped her friend. / '*glomps you*' is old-school fandom-style action text.	Normalize base + source form. Internet/anime-fandom slang, especially associated with older online fandom culture; playful and nonstandard.		
247	2461	2597	modular vs. single-unit / integrated design	/ˈmɑdʒələr vɚs ˈsɪŋɡəl ˈjunɪt/	comparison phrase	A contrast between systems built from separable/interchangeable modules and systems designed as one integrated unit whose components are less independently replaceable or rearrangeable.	modular vs integrated, component-based vs unitary	A modular laptop may allow easier part replacement, while a highly integrated design can prioritize compactness.	CORRECTION: neither design is inherently more solid, flexible, scalable, or better; those are engineering trade-offs depending on implementation.		
247	2462	2598	module	/ˈmɑdʒul/	noun	A self-contained or functionally distinct unit that forms part of a larger system, course, structure, program, or device.	unit, component, section	The course has six modules. / This software module handles authentication.	Common in education, software, engineering, hardware. A module need not be physically detachable.		
247	2463	2599	modular	/ˈmɑdʒələr/	adjective	Designed, organized, or constructed from distinct modules that can be combined, replaced, rearranged, developed, or understood as units.	component-based, modularized, sectional	The modular architecture lets teams replace one service without rewriting the entire system.	Benefits such as flexibility depend on design; 'modular' describes structure, not guaranteed scalability.		
247	2464	2600	scalability	/ˌskeɪləˈbɪləti/	noun	The ability of a system, process, organization, or design to accommodate increased or decreased scale—users, data, workload, production, scope, etc.—while remaining workable and meeting relevant performance/cost requirements.	capacity to scale, expandability, growth handling	The prototype works for ten users, but we still need to test its scalability to millions.	CORRECTION: scalability does not guarantee growth 'without compromising performance'; trade-offs and resource increases may be required. Related adjective: scalable.		
247	2465	2601	microservices / microservice architecture	/ˈmaɪkroʊˌsɝvɪsɪz/	plural noun; software architecture term	An architectural style in which an application is composed of relatively small, independently deployable services organized around distinct capabilities and communicating through defined interfaces.	service-oriented architecture (broader), independently deployable services	The team split the monolith into several microservices that can be deployed independently.	PRECISION: microservices are not automatically scalable, maintainable, or loosely coupled in practice; those are design goals/trade-offs. Singular: microservice.		
247	2466	2602	decentralized	/diˈsɛntrəˌlaɪzd/	adjective	Organized so that control, decision-making, processing, or authority is distributed among multiple locations or participants rather than concentrated in one center.	distributed, noncentralized, dispersed	The organization uses a decentralized decision-making structure. / The network is designed to operate in a decentralized way.	Broader than blockchain. Contrast centralized. Decentralization can concern governance, computing, operations, finance, etc.		
247	2467	2603	go all in / go all out	/ˈɑl ɪn/ vs /ˈɑl aʊt/	comparison of idiomatic constructions	Go all in = commit fully, often putting all available resources or one's stake behind a choice; go all out = use maximum effort, intensity, or resources to achieve something.	commit fully; give it everything, spare no effort	She went all in on the new business. / We went all out to make the final event memorable.	Teach VERB constructions, not just adjectives. Poker gives 'all in' its risk/stake image. 'All-out' also works attributively: an all-out effort/attack.		
247	2468	2604	much work / a lot of work	/mʌʧ wɝk/	noun phrase / quantifier pattern	'Much work' means a large amount of work, but in ordinary affirmative conversation 'a lot of work' is usually more natural. 'Much' is especially common in negatives, questions, formal writing, and fixed/formal patterns.	a lot of work, a great deal of work	There's still much work to be done. / This is a lot of work for one person. / We don't have much work left.	CORRECTION: 'This is much work for one person' is grammatical in some styles but unnatural in ordinary conversation. Teach countability: work is normally uncountable here.		
247	2469	2605	all in / go all in	/ˈɑl ɪn/	adjective/adverbial phrase; idiom	Fully committed or invested; in poker, having bet all one's remaining chips. 'Go all in on X' = commit completely to a choice, project, person, strategy, etc.	fully committed, fully invested, bet everything	He went all in on the poker hand. / The company is going all in on electric vehicles.	KEY FRAME: go all in on + noun. Distinguish from 'all-inclusive' pricing, which is also sometimes shortened to 'all-in' in specific contexts.		
247	2470	2606	all-out	/ˈɑl aʊt/	adjective; in 'go all out', adverbial idiom	Using the greatest possible effort, intensity, or resources; 'go all out' = make the maximum effort or do something on a very large scale.	maximum, full-scale, wholehearted; give it everything	They launched an all-out effort to find the missing files. / We went all out for her birthday.	FORM: attributive adjective usually hyphenated 'all-out'; verbal idiom 'go all out' is not hyphenated.		
248	2471	2608	exasperation	/ɪɡˌzæspəˈreɪʃən/	noun	A feeling of intense irritation, frustration, or annoyance, especially after repeated difficulty or provocation.	frustration, irritation, aggravation	He threw his hands up in exasperation when the printer jammed again.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: in/with exasperation; a sigh/look of exasperation. Related adjective: exasperated; verb: exasperate.		
248	2472	2609	persiflage	/ˈpɜrsəˌflɑʒ/	noun (formal/literary)	Light, witty, often teasing or slightly mocking conversation; bantering talk that may deliberately avoid seriousness.	banter, raillery, badinage, teasing repartee	Their dinner conversation drifted into witty persiflage.	Rare/formal recognition word. 'Contemptuous' is too strong as a necessary feature; tone can be playful and light.		
248	2473	2610	pleasantry / pleasantries	/ˈplɛzəntri/	noun	A polite, friendly, or lightly humorous remark used in social interaction; plural 'pleasantries' often means conventional polite small talk.	nicety, polite remark, small talk	After exchanging pleasantries, they got down to business.	Normalize singular/plural. 'Banter' can be more playful/teasing and is not a clean synonym.		
248	2474	2611	badinage	/ˈbædɪˌnɑʒ/	noun (formal/literary)	Light, playful, witty teasing or banter in conversation.	banter, repartee, persiflage, jesting	Their easy badinage made the long wait more enjoyable.	Rare/formal recognition word. Usually uncountable. Compare persiflage, which can carry a slightly more mocking/superficial flavor.		
248	2475	2612	be taken up with/by something / take up time or space	/ˈteɪkən ʌp baɪ ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	phrasal constructions	'Be taken up with/by X' can mean be occupied or absorbed by X; separately, X can 'take up' time, space, attention, etc. Passive 'be taken up by' can also mean space/time is occupied.	be absorbed in, preoccupied with; occupy, consume	She's been completely taken up with the new project. / Meetings take up most of my morning. / Half the desk is taken up by the monitor.	CORRECTION: distinguish mental preoccupation from passive occupancy. 'Taken up by work' can be ambiguous; 'taken up with work' often emphasizes preoccupation.		
248	2476	2613	mouthful	/ˈmaʊθfəl/	noun	An amount of food/drink that fills the mouth; figuratively, a long, difficult, or cumbersome word/name/phrase to say.	bite, gulp; cumbersome expression	He swallowed a mouthful of soup. / 'Temporomandibular' is a bit of a mouthful.	Clean duplicated definition. Common frame: 'That's a mouthful!' Can also mean a lot to say/express, depending context.		
248	2477	2614	sink in	/sɪŋk ɪn/	phrasal verb	To become fully understood, realized, or emotionally absorbed, often gradually or after a delay; literally, to penetrate or descend into something.	register, dawn on, be absorbed; penetrate	It took a moment for the news to sink in. / Let the moisturizer sink in before applying makeup.	Add literal/material extension. KEY: let it sink in = give someone time to absorb/realize it.		
248	2478	2615	play small	/pleɪ smɔl/	informal/self-development expression	To hold back from pursuing, expressing, or using one's abilities, ambitions, influence, or opportunities, often because of fear or self-doubt.	hold back, limit oneself, underplay one's potential	She realized she'd been playing small at work and started applying for bigger roles.	USAGE: common in coaching/self-help/business rhetoric rather than a neutral technical idiom. Often carries an evaluative assumption that greater ambition is desirable.		
248	2479	2616	take someone up on something / take you up on that	/teɪk ʌp ɑn ðæt/	idiom	To accept someone's offer, invitation, challenge, or suggestion.	accept, say yes to, take up an offer	Thanks for offering to help—I may take you up on that. / I'll take you up on your offer of coffee.	KEY FRAME: take + PERSON + up on + OFFER/that. Do not omit the person in the full productive construction.		
248	2480	2617	stoked	/stoʊkt/	adjective (informal/slang)	Very excited, pleased, or enthusiastic about something.	thrilled, pumped, psyched, excited	I'm stoked to see the concert tonight.	KEY FRAME: stoked about/for/to do X. Informal, especially associated with surfing/skating and broader casual AmE.		
249	2481	2618	ticked off / tick someone off	/tɪkt ɔf/	adjective phrase; phrasal verb (informal)	Ticked off = annoyed or angry. Tick someone off = annoy/anger them; in BrE, 'tick someone off' can also mean reprimand/scold.	annoyed, irritated, angry; annoy, reprimand	I was really ticked off about the delay. / That comment ticked her off.	Normalize state + active construction. In AmE, 'ticked off' commonly means angry; register is milder than 'pissed off'.		
249	2482	2619	nothing to write home about	/ˈnʌθɪŋ tə raɪt hoʊm əˌbaʊt/	idiom	Not especially good, impressive, unusual, or noteworthy; ordinary or mediocre.	unremarkable, nothing special, mediocre	The hotel was clean, but the breakfast was nothing to write home about.	KEEP concept but deepen usage. Fixed frame: X is/was nothing to write home about. Usually mildly negative rather than harsh.		
249	2483	2620	have no spine / be spineless	/doʊnt θɪŋk hiz ɡɑt ə spaɪn tə hɪm/	idiom / adjective	To lack courage, resolve, or willingness to stand up for oneself or one's principles.	be cowardly, weak-willed, lack backbone	He never challenges anyone in authority—he has no spine. / I don't think he's got much of a spine.	CORRECTION: 'he's got a spine to him' is not the standard productive idiom. Teach 'have no spine', 'lack a spine/backbone', and 'spineless'; preserve source-like variant only as contextual speech.		
249	2484	2621	when it counts	/wɛn ɪt kaʊnts/	idiom	At the moment or in the situation where performance, action, or support matters most.	at the crucial moment, when it matters, in the clutch	She stays calm when it counts. / He always shows up when it counts.	Productive clause-final phrase. 'In the clutch' is sports-derived and more informal/AmE.		
249	2485	2622	not half bad	/nɑt hæf bæd/	idiom (informal)	Quite good or better than expected, despite its superficially negative wording.	pretty good, surprisingly decent, better than expected	That homemade pizza is not half bad.	Litotes/understatement: often warmer praise than the literal words suggest. Tone can be dry or playful.		
249	2486	2623	twofold / the reason is twofold	/ɪts ˈtuˌfoʊld/	adjective; adverb	Having two parts, aspects, effects, or reasons; also 'by two times' in some quantitative/formal uses.	two-part, dual; twice	The reason is twofold: cost and time. / The change produced a twofold increase.	Normalize from 'it's twofold'. Distinguish 'twofold' = two aspects from quantitative 'a twofold increase' = twice as much.		
249	2487	2624	minor conflict / low-stakes disagreement	/ˈkæʒuəl ˈkɑnflɪkt/	descriptive phrase	A relatively small disagreement or interpersonal conflict with limited stakes or consequences.	minor disagreement, mild friction, small dispute	They had a minor disagreement over where to eat lunch.	CORRECTION: 'casual conflict' is not a standard collocation for this meaning and can sound contradictory/unclear. Use minor conflict, low-stakes disagreement, mild friction, etc.		
249	2488	2625	call on a student / call on students	/ˈkɔlɪŋ ɑn ˈstudənts/	verb construction (education)	For a teacher or discussion leader to select or invite a particular student to answer, speak, or participate.	ask a student to respond, select a student	The professor called on students at random. / She called on me even though my hand wasn't raised.	KEY FRAME: call on + person = invite/select them to speak. Distinguish from call on someone = visit/request help in other contexts.		
249	2489	2626	cold-call / cold-calling	/koʊld ˈkɔlɪŋ/	verb; noun/gerund	To contact a person or organization without a prior relationship or expressed interest, especially for sales/recruiting; in education, to call on a student who has not volunteered.	unsolicited outreach, prospecting call; call on an unraised hand	The recruiter cold-called several candidates. / The professor uses cold-calling so everyone prepares to participate.	CORRECTION/EXPANSION: add important classroom sense, especially relevant next to WID2625. Hyphenation varies by style; 'cold call' also works as noun.		
249	2490	2627	take up / take something up	/ˈteɪkɪŋ ʌp/	phrasal verb	Highly polysemous: begin a hobby/activity; occupy time/space; accept an offer/challenge; discuss/handle a matter; shorten a garment by raising its hem, among other senses.	begin, occupy, accept, address; shorten/hem	She took up tennis. / This sofa takes up too much space. / The tailor took the trousers up an inch.	CORRECTION: source card captured only two senses. For clothing, 'take trousers/skirt up' = shorten by moving the hem upward.		
250	2491	2628	sew up / sew the hem / hem a garment	/ˈsoʊɪŋ ʌp ðə hɛm/	verb constructions	To close, fasten, repair, or finish something by sewing. For a garment edge, the usual specific verb is 'hem': fold/finish the edge and stitch it.	stitch, mend, hem	I need to hem these trousers. / She sewed up the tear in the sleeve.	CORRECTION: 'sewing up the hem' is possible in a particular repair context but not the normal general term for altering a hem. Teach sew up vs hem.		
250	2492	2629	adjust the length of a garment / alter the hem	/əˈʤʌstɪŋ ðə lɛŋkθ ʌv ðə ˈɡɑrmənt/	tailoring phrase	To make clothing longer or shorter so it fits or hangs as desired, commonly by altering the hem.	hem, shorten, lengthen, alter	The tailor adjusted the length of the trousers by taking up the hem.	Useful tailoring language: take up/raise the hem = shorten; let down/lower the hem = lengthen when fabric allowance permits.		
250	2493	2630	put someone down	/pʊt ˈsʌmwʌn daʊn/	phrasal verb	To belittle, insult, or criticize someone in a way that makes them feel inferior; also has other senses such as place something down and euthanize an animal.	belittle, demean, insult, disparage	He keeps putting his coworkers down to make himself look better. / We had to have our old dog put down.	Important polysemy: interpersonal insult sense vs euthanasia. KEY FRAME: put someone down; put down + object.		
250	2494	2631	wind someone up	/waɪnd ˈsʌmwʌn ʌp/	phrasal verb (especially BrE)	To tease, provoke, or deliberately annoy someone, often for amusement; also literally to wind a mechanism and figuratively to make someone tense/excited.	tease, provoke, rile, get a rise out of	Ignore him—he's just winding you up. / The suspense really wound me up.	BrE teasing sense is especially common. Pronunciation: wind /waɪnd/ here. Related 'wind-up' = joke/tease or mechanical action.		
250	2495	2632	put the wind up someone	/pʊt ðə wɪnd ʌp ˈsʌmwʌn/	idiom (BrE informal)	To frighten, alarm, or make someone nervous.	scare, alarm, unnerve, put the fear into	That letter from the tax office really put the wind up him.	Distinct from WID2631 wind someone up. Fixed BrE frame: put the wind up + person.		
250	2496	2633	giveaway	/ˈɡɪvəˌweɪ/	noun; adjective	Something given free; a contest/promotion in which prizes are given; a clue or sign that reveals something, often unintentionally. Attributively, 'giveaway' can mean revealing.	freebie, prize promotion; clue, telltale sign	They're running a ticket giveaway. / His accent was a dead giveaway that he wasn't local.	Major collocation: dead giveaway = unmistakable clue. Distinguish verb 'give away' (two words) from noun/adjective 'giveaway'.		
250	2497	2634	take away from something	/teɪk əˈweɪ frəm/	phrasal verb	To reduce or detract from the quality, value, impact, enjoyment, or importance of something.	detract from, diminish, lessen	The awkward editing takes away from an otherwise strong episode.	KEY FRAME: X takes away from Y. Contrast 'take X away from Y' = physically remove.		
250	2498	2635	sling / slinging	/ˈslɪŋɪŋ/	verb; informal/slang uses	To throw, hurl, hang, or move something casually/forcefully; informal compounds can mean sell/serve something repeatedly, including drugs ('sling dope'), drinks, food, or insults.	throw, hurl, toss; sell, deal, dish out	He slung his bag over his shoulder. / The bartender was slinging drinks all night.	Normalize base + source form. Drug-dealing sense is one slang use, not the only 'selling' extension.		
250	2499	2636	joint	/dʒɔɪnt/	noun; slang noun	A place/establishment in informal speech; a marijuana cigarette; prison/jail in slang; plus standard meanings such as a body joint or connection between parts.	place, establishment; marijuana cigarette; prison/jail	Let's get out of this joint. / He spent two years in the joint. / She rolled a joint.	CORRECTION/EXPANSION: 'joint' slang is much broader than prison. Card should make context decide among place, cannabis cigarette, prison, etc.		
250	2500	2637	out of line / be out of line	/aʊt əv laɪn/	idiom / adjective phrase	Beyond acceptable limits of behavior, authority, propriety, or fairness; inappropriate or presumptuous. Literally, also not aligned or not in the expected sequence.	inappropriate, unacceptable, over the line, presumptuous	That remark was out of line. / One tile is slightly out of line with the others.	Duplicate concept retained because source location may matter; teach figurative + literal. Not specifically about authority.		
251	2501	2638	jeopardy / Jeopardy!	/ˈdʒɛpɚdi/	noun; proper noun	Jeopardy = danger or risk of harm/loss. Jeopardy! = the U.S. quiz show known for presenting clues and requiring responses in question form.	danger, risk, peril	The delay put the project in jeopardy. / On Jeopardy!, contestants respond to clues in the form of questions.	Separate lexical noun from proper-name cultural reference. KEY FRAME: put/place X in jeopardy; be in jeopardy.		
251	2502	2639	by the skin of one's teeth	/baɪ ðə skɪn əv jʊr tiθ/	idiom	Only just; by the narrowest possible margin, especially when escaping failure/danger or barely succeeding.	barely, narrowly, by a hair	I caught the train by the skin of my teeth.	Possessive varies: my/your/his... teeth. Biblical origin; modern meaning is simply 'barely'.		
251	2503	2640	dismissive	/dɪsˈmɪsɪv/	adjective	Showing that one considers someone or something unworthy of serious attention, respect, or consideration.	disdainful, contemptuous, offhand, rejecting	She gave a dismissive shrug and changed the subject.	KEY: dismissive of/about/toward. 'Indifferent' lacks the active sense of brushing something aside.		
251	2504	2641	matronly	/ˈmeɪtrənli/	adjective	Resembling a conventional stereotype of a mature, respectable woman in appearance or manner; often used for clothing/body/style in a way that can imply unfashionable, older-looking, or stout.	motherly, mature-looking; dowdy (more negative)	The costume designer deliberately gave the character a matronly look.	CAUTION: age/gender-loaded and potentially insulting. 'Motherly' can be warm/behavioral and is not a clean synonym.		
251	2505	2642	unsolicited	/ˌʌnsəˈlɪsɪtɪd/	adjective	Not asked for, requested, invited, or sought.	unrequested, uninvited, unasked-for	He kept giving me unsolicited advice. / The company sent unsolicited marketing emails.	Common collocations: unsolicited advice, email, call, proposal, bid. Not inherently unwanted, though often carries that implication.		
251	2506	2644	solicit	/səˈlɪsɪt/	verb	To ask for, seek, or try to obtain something such as money, support, business, information, opinions, or submissions; in legal contexts, can also mean seek to obtain certain illegal services/acts.	request, seek, canvass, ask for	The charity is soliciting donations. / The editor solicited feedback from readers.	Broaden beyond money/support. KEY: solicit donations/business/advice/feedback/bids. Legal/sexual-service sense is context-specific.		
251	2507	2645	mio caro / mia cara	/ˈmiːoʊ ˈkɑːroʊ/  /ˈmiːə ˈkɑːrə/	Italian phrase / term of address	Italian for 'my dear' or 'my darling' with grammatical gender agreement: mio caro for a male addressee, mia cara for a female addressee.	my dear, darling	'Grazie, mia cara,' she said warmly.	Language/cultural recognition card. These are Italian expressions that may appear in English dialogue, not ordinary productive English vocabulary.		
251	2508	2646	be upfront about something	/biɪŋ ʌpˈfrʌnt əˈbaʊt ɪt/	adjective construction (informal)	To be open, honest, and direct about a fact, intention, limitation, cost, problem, etc., especially from the beginning.	be candid, transparent, straightforward	He was upfront about the extra fees before we agreed to anything.	Normalize source progressive. KEY FRAME: be upfront with someone about something.		
251	2509	2647	brush someone/something off	/brʌʃ ɔf/	phrasal verb	To dismiss, ignore, or treat someone/something as unimportant; literally, to remove dust/dirt from a surface with brushing movements.	dismiss, shrug off, ignore; dust off	She brushed off my concern. / He brushed the sand off his jacket.	KEY FRAME: brush X off. Compare 'brush it off' = refuse to let a setback/insult bother you.		
251	2510	2648	hold down the fort	/hoʊld daʊn ðə fɔrt/	idiom (informal)	To keep things operating, under control, or looked after while someone else is absent or unavailable.	keep things running, take care of things, cover for someone	Can you hold down the fort while I'm at lunch?	Often workplace/home use; humorous military metaphor. Variant 'hold the fort' also occurs and is older/more literal.		
252	2511	2649	don't burn down the house	/doʊnt bɜrn daʊn ðə haʊs/	literal/humorous warning; source expression	Literally, a warning not to cause a house fire; humorously, something one might say when leaving someone in charge to mean 'don't cause a disaster'. It is not a standard general idiom for avoiding disproportionate solutions.	don't cause a disaster, behave yourself (contextual)	I'm going out for an hour—don't burn down the house while I'm gone.	CORRECTION: remove invented broad idiomatic rule 'don't overreact to a small problem'. A sentence like 'don't burn down the house to kill a spider' is a creative analogy, not a fixed idiom.		
252	2512	2650	catch you on the flippity-flip	/kæʧ ju ɑn ðə ˈflɪpɪti flɪp/	playful catchphrase / farewell	A deliberately goofy way to say 'see you later', strongly associated with Michael Scott in The Office and used humorously outside it.	catch you later, see you on the flip side, later	All right, I'm heading out—catch you on the flippity-flip.	Normalize punctuation/hyphenation. Pop-culture catchphrase rather than general slang; likely riffs on 'catch you on the flip side'.		
252	2513	2651	run/work one's ass off	/rʌn maɪ æs ɔf/	vulgar slang construction	To run extremely hard/fast or, with other verbs, do something with extreme effort or intensity. Part of the productive 'VERB one's ass off' pattern.	work extremely hard, hustle, exert oneself	I've been working my ass off all week. / I ran my ass off trying to catch the bus.	Connect to WID2481 productive pattern. Meaning comes from verb + intensifier; 'run my ass off' does not necessarily mean work metaphorically.		
252	2514	2652	the small of one's back / put a hand on the small of someone's back	/hænd ɪz ɑn ðə smɔl ʌv maɪ bæk/	anatomical phrase; gesture construction	The 'small of the back' is the lower back/waist area. Putting a hand there can guide, steady, comfort, or signal intimacy depending on context.	lower back; guide/touch (context-dependent)	He placed a hand on the small of her back as they moved through the crowd.	CORRECTION: do not encode flirtation as the default meaning. Gesture interpretation depends on relationship, pressure, duration, setting, and consent.		
252	2515	2653	pleasantries	/ˈplɛzəntriz/	plural noun	Polite, friendly remarks or conventional social small talk exchanged before or around more substantive conversation.	niceties, polite remarks, small talk	They exchanged pleasantries before discussing the contract.	CORRECTION: 'humorous' is not required. Common collocation: exchange pleasantries.		
252	2516	2654	thicket	/ˈθɪkɪt/	noun	A dense growth or cluster of shrubs, bushes, small trees, or undergrowth; figuratively, a dense or complicated mass of things/ideas.	brush, undergrowth, dense shrubbery	The rabbit vanished into a thicket. / The rule is buried in a thicket of regulations.	Add useful figurative extension: a thicket of rules/details/arguments.		
252	2517	2655	co-opt	/koʊ ˈɑpt/	verb	To take over, appropriate, absorb, or recruit a person, idea, movement, symbol, or organization for one's own purposes; historically/formally, to elect or appoint someone into a group by existing members.	appropriate, absorb, take over, recruit	The slogan was co-opted by advertisers. / The committee co-opted two new members.	PRECISION: not necessarily 'without permission'. Modern critical use often implies appropriation/neutralization; formal governance sense is different.		
252	2518	2656	rail at/against someone or something; rail (sexual slang)	/reɪl mi/	verb constructions; vulgar slang	Standard figurative 'rail at/against X' = criticize or complain about X angrily. Separately, vulgar sexual slang 'rail someone' = have vigorous/rough sex with them.	rant against, attack verbally; have sex with (vulgar slang)	He railed against the new policy for an hour. / In vulgar online slang, 'rail someone' can be sexual.	CORRECTION: 'rail me' does NOT normally mean 'harshly criticize me'; criticism uses rail at/against. Keep the unrelated sexual transitive slang clearly separated.		
252	2519	2657	bamboozle	/bæmˈbuːzəl/	verb (informal)	To trick, deceive, confuse, or outwit someone, often through cleverness, fast talk, or misleading tactics.	dupe, hoodwink, trick, deceive	Don't let the flashy presentation bamboozle you.	Playful/old-fashioned flavor in some contexts. Can emphasize confusion as well as deception.		
252	2520	2658	mother of none	/ˈmʌðɚ ʌv nʌn/	literal/creative phrase	Literally, a woman who is a mother of no children. It is not a standard idiom or established label; any emotional, literary, or identity-related nuance comes from context.	woman with no children (descriptive)	The poem describes its speaker as 'mother of none,' using the phrase for contrast with 'mother of many.'	CORRECTION: avoid presenting 'non-mother' as a normal synonym. Treat as compositional/source-dependent wording, not a lexicalized expression.		
253	2521	2659	disbar / be disbarred	/dɪsˈbɑrd/	verb; passive construction (legal)	To remove a lawyer from the bar and revoke their authorization to practice law, typically through a disciplinary process under the relevant jurisdiction.	remove from legal practice, revoke a law license	The court disbarred the attorney for serious misconduct. / He was disbarred after the disciplinary hearing.	Normalize base + source passive. Legal procedures/grounds vary by jurisdiction.		
253	2522	2660	arbitrary	/ˈɑrbɪˌtrɛri/	adjective	Based on individual discretion, whim, or an unsupported choice rather than a clear, relevant, consistent principle; in some contexts, simply selected without a particular reason.	capricious, discretionary, random (sometimes)	The cutoff seemed arbitrary because nobody could explain why that number was chosen.	CORRECTION: arbitrary does not always mean random or unplanned. Legal/policy use often emphasizes lack of reasonable principle or consistency.		
253	2523	2661	favoritism	/ˈfeɪvərɪˌtɪzəm/	noun	Unfair preferential treatment of a person or group, especially because of personal liking or connection rather than relevant merit/rules.	partiality, preferential treatment, bias	Employees complained about favoritism in promotions.	'Preference' alone can be neutral; favoritism is normally negative/unfair. Related: nepotism specifically favors relatives.		
