255	2543	2683	get at someone / what are you getting at?	/ˈɡɛtɪŋ æt ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb / idiomatic constructions	'Get at someone' can mean repeatedly criticize, nag, or pressure them (especially BrE); 'what are you getting at?' asks what someone is implying or trying to say.	pick on, nag, criticize; imply, mean	Why are you always getting at me? / What exactly are you getting at with that question?	CORRECTION: separate PERSON-target sense from implication sense. Repair source punctuation.		
