256	2560	2700	knock something loose / knock one loose	/nɑk wʌn lus/	verb construction; source-dependent phrase	To strike, shake, or jolt something so that it becomes loose or dislodged. 'Knock one loose' means dislodge one item whose identity is understood from context; metaphorical emotional/memory uses are creative rather than a standard idiom.	dislodge, loosen, shake free	The impact knocked a screw loose. / Hit the tray gently and see if you can knock one loose.	CORRECTION: remove unsupported fixed meaning 'stir up emotions or memories'. Note separate idiom 'have a screw loose' = be eccentric/crazy; 'knock something loose' is productive literal causative wording.		
257	2561	2701	shimmy	/ˈʃɪmi/	verb; noun	To shake or wiggle the body quickly, especially the shoulders/hips in dance; also to move through a narrow space with small side-to-side movements.	wiggle, shake, sway, squeeze	She did a little shimmy on the dance floor. / I shimmied between the table and the wall.	Add productive movement sense beyond dance. Noun: do a shimmy.		
257	2562	2702	just another Tuesday	/dʒʌst əˈnʌðɚ ˈtuzdeɪ/	ironic phrase	A humorous way to treat something chaotic, bizarre, or unusually dramatic as routine or unsurprising.	business as usual, typical day, nothing new	The fire alarm went off during the flood drill—just another Tuesday around here.	Not a fixed universal idiom; Tuesday is replaceable in creative variants. The humor comes from extreme event + mundane 'ordinary day' framing.		
257	2563	2703	the road not taken	/roʊd nɑt ˈteɪkən/	literary reference / figurative phrase	A path, choice, or life possibility not chosen; a reference to Robert Frost's poem 'The Road Not Taken'.	unchosen path, alternative path, what might have been	Sometimes I wonder about the road not taken—what if I'd moved abroad sooner?	CORRECTION: the poem is often simplified as celebrating unconventional choice, but its treatment of choice and later storytelling is more ambiguous. Use primarily as cultural/figurative reference.		
