264	2633	2776	respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)	/ˈrɛspɪrəˌtɔri sɪnˈsɪʃəl ˈvaɪrəs/	noun (medical)	A common respiratory virus that usually causes cold-like illness but can cause serious lower-respiratory disease, particularly in infants, some older adults, and other higher-risk people.	RSV	RSV can cause bronchiolitis or pneumonia in vulnerable patients.	CORRECTION: 'lung infection' and 'bronchiolitis virus' are not synonyms. Standard capitalization is usually lowercase common name + uppercase initialism.		
