270	2694	2839	for lack of a better word	/fɚ læk əv ə ˈbɛtɚ wɝd/	hedging phrase	Used before or after a word/description to acknowledge that it is imperfect, approximate, or not quite the term one wants.	so to speak (not exact), roughly speaking, for want of a better term	The ending felt, for lack of a better word, hollow.	Not equivalent to 'if you will' in all contexts. It explicitly comments on word choice.		
270	2695	2840	out of place	/aʊt əv pleɪs/	adjective/adverbial phrase	In the wrong position; unsuitable or incongruous in a setting; or socially uncomfortable/not belonging.	incongruous, inappropriate, misplaced; uncomfortable	That modern lamp looks out of place in the old room. / I felt out of place at the formal dinner.	Three useful patterns: object is out of place; something looks/seems out of place; person feels out of place.		
270	2696	2841	ride the wave / ride a wave of something	/ˈraɪdɪŋ ðə weɪv/	idiom / figurative construction	To benefit from, continue with, or adapt to a period of momentum, popularity, enthusiasm, change, or other strong trend while it lasts.	capitalize on momentum, go with the trend, surf the wave	The band is riding a wave of renewed popularity. / Let's ride the wave while interest is high.	Normalize productive base. Not always passive 'going with the flow'; can imply strategically exploiting momentum.		
270	2697	2842	a mood/energy/vibe lingers	/ˈɛnɚdʒi ɔr vaɪb ɪz ˈlɪŋɚɪŋ/	productive collocation	A feeling, atmosphere, impression, smell, sound, or other effect continues to be present after its source/event has passed.	remain, persist, hang in the air	Tension lingered in the room after the argument. / The good vibe lingered long after the party ended.	CORRECTION: teach productive verb 'linger' rather than lexicalizing 'energy or vibe is lingering' as a fixed modern expression.		
270	2698	2843	expedite	/ˈɛkspəˌdaɪt/	verb (formal/business)	To make a process, action, delivery, decision, etc. happen sooner or proceed more quickly.	speed up, accelerate, fast-track, hasten	Could you expedite the shipment? / We changed the workflow to expedite approval.	Common transitive frame: expedite + process/order/shipment/request. Does not mean skip required steps.		
270	2699	2844	address	/tu əˈdrɛs/	verb	To deal with, respond to, or give attention to an issue/question; to speak to a person/audience; to direct remarks to someone; or to write an address on an item.	deal with, tackle; speak to, direct; label	We need to address the underlying problem. / She addressed the audience. / Address the envelope clearly.	Normalize from infinitive 'to address' and add major senses/constructions.		
270	2700	2845	deplete / depleted	/dɪˈplitɪd/	verb; adjective/participle	To reduce the amount or supply of a resource substantially; depleted = greatly reduced or drained. Informally, a person can feel depleted of energy.	drain, exhaust, diminish, use up	The drought depleted the reservoir. / After the trip, I felt mentally depleted.	Normalize base + source adjective. Not necessarily completely exhausted/empty.		
271	2701	2846	expand	/ɪkˈspænd/	verb	To become or make larger in size, amount, range, scope, detail, or influence; 'expand on/upon X' = give more detail.	grow, enlarge, broaden, extend; elaborate	The company expanded into Europe. / Could you expand on that point?	Add important intransitive/transitive and 'expand on' construction.		
271	2702	2847	hoarse	/hɔrs/	adjective	Having a rough, harsh, breathy, or strained voice, commonly from irritation, illness, overuse, or other causes.	raspy, rough-voiced, husky	My voice was hoarse after shouting at the game.	Avoid implying sore throat/overuse are the only causes. Common: become/go hoarse.		
271	2703	2848	take it down a notch / take something down a notch	/teɪk ɪt daʊn ə nɑtʃ/	idiom	To reduce the intensity, volume, aggressiveness, ambition, emotional heat, or degree of something by a small amount.	tone it down, dial it back, ease off	The music is too loud—take it down a notch. / We may need to take the rhetoric down a notch.	Productive object variant. 'A notch' metaphorically means one step/degree.		
271	2704	2849	withhold	/wɪðˈhoʊld/	verb	To deliberately refrain from giving, providing, disclosing, granting, or paying something that could otherwise be given.	hold back, retain, keep back, refuse to disclose	The company withheld part of his pay. / She withheld the information until the investigation ended.	CORRECTION: source example 'withhold judgment' was wrong; standard idiom is 'withhold judgment' = refrain from forming/expressing judgment, not a judge ordering a company to do so.		
271	2705	2850	insolence	/ˈɪnsələns/	noun	Boldly rude, disrespectful, or contemptuous behavior or speech, especially toward someone in authority or entitled to respect.	impudence, disrespect, impertinence, cheek	The teacher would not tolerate open insolence in class.	Related adjective: insolent. Often stronger/more openly defiant than ordinary rudeness.		
271	2706	2851	faff around / faff about	/ˈfæfɪŋ əˈraʊnd/	phrasal verb (BrE informal)	To waste time doing unimportant things, proceed inefficiently, or fuss with something instead of getting on with the task.	mess around, dawdle, fiddle around	Stop faffing around and get your shoes on. / I spent an hour faffing about with the settings.	Normalize base. 'Faff' can also be noun: 'What a faff!' = what a hassle/fuss.		
271	2707	2852	put one's foot in one's mouth / put one's foot in it	/pʊt fʊt ɪn maʊθ/	idiom	To say something tactless, embarrassing, or inappropriate, usually accidentally.	say the wrong thing, make a faux pas, blunder	I put my foot in my mouth when I asked about a job she'd just lost.	Normalize full possessive construction. AmE strongly uses 'put one's foot in one's mouth'; BrE often 'put one's foot in it'.		
271	2708	2853	be slow to reply / take a while to reply	/ˈsʌmwʌn ɪz sloʊ tə rɪˈplaɪ/	productive phrase	To take longer than expected or desired to respond to a message, email, question, etc.	reply slowly, take a while to respond, be a slow responder	Sorry I'm slow to reply when work gets hectic. / He sometimes takes a while to reply to texts.	Not an idiom; motive is not encoded. Teach natural variants and 'reply to + message/person'.		
271	2709	2854	whack / wack	/wæk/	adjective/slang; noun/verb (other senses)	In slang, bad, unfair, strange, ridiculous, or uncool. Spelling 'wack' is especially common for this adjective, while 'whack' is also seen and is the standard spelling for hit/strike senses.	lame, messed up, ridiculous, bad	That rule is wack—it punishes people who did nothing wrong.	PRECISION: slang spelling varies. Keep distinct from 'whack' = hit/strike and 'out of whack' = out of order/alignment.		
271	2710	2855	outraged	/ˈaʊtreɪdʒd/	adjective	Extremely angry, shocked, or offended because something is perceived as seriously wrong, unfair, immoral, or insulting.	incensed, furious, indignant, appalled	Residents were outraged by the sudden closure.	Often implies moral offense/indignation, not merely high-intensity anger. Related noun/verb: outrage.		
272	2711	2856	visceral	/ˈvɪsərəl/	adjective	Literally relating to the internal organs (viscera); figuratively, felt deeply and immediately at a bodily/gut level rather than through detached analysis.	gut-level, instinctive, raw, deeply felt	I had a visceral reaction to the scene. / The film creates a visceral sense of danger.	Add literal origin/sense. Figurative visceral can describe fear, disgust, excitement, impact, experience—not only emotion.		
272	2712	2857	cold open	/koʊld ˈoʊpən/	noun (TV/radio/media term)	An opening segment that begins before the title sequence, opening credits, or standard introduction, often to hook the audience or set up the episode.	pre-title sequence, teaser (overlapping)	The episode starts with a cold open before the theme song.	PRECISION: not limited to TV/film and not necessarily comedic. 'Teaser' overlaps but production terminology varies.		
272	2713	2858	have the hots for someone	/hæv ðə hɑts fɔr/	idiom (informal/slang)	To feel strong romantic or sexual attraction toward someone.	be attracted to, be into, have a crush on	He's had the hots for her for months.	KEY FRAME: have the hots for + person. More overtly physical/sexual than 'have a crush on' in many contexts.		
272	2714	2859	supply closet	/səˈplaɪ ˈklɑzɪt/	noun	A closet or small storage room used for supplies, commonly in offices, schools, workplaces, or institutions.	storage closet, supply room	Extra paper and toner are in the supply closet.	A utility closet often houses building equipment and is not a clean synonym.		
272	2715	2860	wreck someone / wreck something	/rɛk ˈsʌmwʌn/	verb construction; slang extension	To badly damage/destroy something; defeat someone decisively; or emotionally/physically overwhelm someone.	destroy, devastate, crush, overwhelm	The storm wrecked the roof. / Their team wrecked us. / That ending absolutely wrecked me.	Broaden from person-only slang to core verb. Online 'that wrecked me' often means emotionally devastated or overwhelmed.		
272	2716	2861	Invictus / invictus	/ɪnˈvɪktəs/	proper noun; Latin adjective/reference	Latin 'unconquered/unconquerable'; especially the title of William Ernest Henley's poem 'Invictus', widely associated with endurance and self-mastery.	unconquered, undefeated; Henley poem	The final lines of 'Invictus' are often invoked as a statement of resilience.	Distinguish Latin word from poem title. Avoid making 'resilient' a literal translation.		
272	2717	2862	let things unfold	/lɛt θɪŋz ʌnˈfoʊld/	verb construction / figurative phrase	To allow events or a situation to develop over time without trying to control every step or force an immediate outcome.	let things develop, wait and see, allow events to play out	We don't have enough information yet, so let's let things unfold.	Built from unfold = develop/reveal gradually. Does not necessarily mean passive acceptance of everything.		
272	2718	2863	kill the vibe	/kɪl ðə vaɪb/	informal/slang construction	To spoil, abruptly change, or drain the enjoyable mood/atmosphere of a social situation.	spoil the mood, ruin the atmosphere, bring everyone down	Don't bring up the argument right now—you'll kill the vibe.	Productive: kill the mood is more established/general; vibe is casual contemporary wording.		
272	2719	2864	lose face / save face	/luːz feɪs/	idioms	Lose face = suffer humiliation or loss of social standing/dignity; save face = avoid or reduce such humiliation.	be humiliated, lose standing; preserve dignity	He refused to back down because he feared losing face. / The compromise let both sides save face.	Useful pair. 'Face' concerns socially recognized dignity/reputation, often especially salient in interpersonal/cultural contexts.		
272	2720	2865	deflection	/dɪˈflɛkʃən/	noun	A change in direction; figuratively, a tactic or response that redirects attention away from a question, criticism, blame, or uncomfortable issue.	diversion, redirection, evasion (contextual)	Instead of answering the question, he used a joke as deflection.	Add literal/general direction sense. In conversation/politics, 'deflection' often implies avoidance but motive should be inferred from context.		
273	2721	2866	trifecta	/traɪˈfɛktə/	noun	Originally a bet in which the first three finishers are selected in exact order; figuratively, a set or combination of three notable things, often three successes/advantages.	triple, trio, three-part combination	The restaurant hit the trifecta: great food, fair prices, and friendly service.	CORRECTION: figurative trifecta need not be three things happening 'at once', though it is often positive. Add horse-racing origin.		
273	2722	2867	tell people/things apart / tell X from Y	/tɛl ˈsʌmθɪŋ əˈpɑrt/	verb constructions	To distinguish similar people or things by recognizing differences.	distinguish, differentiate, tell the difference	I can't tell the twins apart. / Can you tell the fake from the real one?	CORRECTION: productive frame is usually plural/object pair: tell X and Y apart, tell them apart, or tell X from Y—not generic singular 'tell something apart'.		
273	2723	2868	rack up	/ræk ʌp/	phrasal verb (informal)	To accumulate, incur, score, or achieve a relatively large amount/number of something over time.	accumulate, amass, run up, chalk up	She racked up thousands of miles. / He racked up a huge phone bill. / The team racked up five wins.	Can be positive or negative: points/wins/awards vs debt/charges/penalties.		
273	2724	2869	box someone/something in / be boxed in	/bɑkst ɪn/	phrasal verb; adjective/passive	To surround or restrict someone/something so movement or options are limited; figuratively, to constrain choices or freedom.	trap, hem in, corner, constrain	A truck boxed my car in. / The contract left us feeling boxed in.	Normalize active + passive. Literal spatial restriction motivates figurative limited-options sense.		
273	2725	2870	marvel at	/ˈmɑrvəl æt/	verb construction	To feel or express wonder, surprise, or admiration at something.	wonder at, be amazed by, admire	We marveled at how quickly the city had changed.	KEY FRAME: marvel at + noun/wh-clause. Wonder can be admiration or astonishment.		
273	2726	2871	lead to a tangent / go off on a tangent	/lid tə səm ˈtændʒənts/	conversation constructions	A remark/topic can lead to a tangent, causing discussion to move away from the main subject; a speaker can 'go off on a tangent'.	digress, go off-topic, wander	That question led us off on a tangent about old movies. / I went off on a tangent and forgot my original point.	CORRECTION: normalize the productive idiom 'go off on a tangent' and causative variants; 'lead to some tangents' is merely compositional.		
273	2727	2872	a pantheon of / the pantheon of	/ə ˈpænθiˌɑn əv/	noun construction	Originally, the collective gods of a religion or a temple dedicated to them; figuratively, an elite or highly revered group of people/things in a field.	elite group, canon, hall of fame (informal analogy)	She belongs in the pantheon of great jazz singers.	KEY FRAME: in/join the pantheon of + celebrated group. 'Hall of fame' is only an analogy, not a synonym in all uses.		
273	2728	2873	rascal	/ˈræskəl/	noun	A mischievous, cheeky, or troublesome person, often a child; can be affectionate, humorous, mildly scolding, or historically more negative.	scamp, rogue, mischief-maker	You little rascal—you hid my keys again!	Tone matters. 'Rogue' can be affectionate similarly but has broader meanings.		
273	2729	2874	get behind / get behind on	/ɡɛt bɪˈhaɪnd/	phrasal constructions	Get behind X = support or endorse X; get behind on X = fall behind schedule/progress with tasks, payments, work, etc.; literal spatial senses also occur.	support, back; fall behind, be overdue	I can get behind that idea. / I got behind on my email after vacation.	CORRECTION: preposition changes the construction: get behind + cause/idea vs get behind on + work/payments.		
273	2730	2875	now you're talking / now you're talking my language	/naʊ jʊr ˈtɔkɪŋ maɪ ˈlæŋɡwɪdʒ/	idiom	Used to show enthusiastic approval because a suggestion or topic strongly matches one's preferences or interests.	that's more like it, I like the sound of that	Pizza and a movie? Now you're talking my language.	'Now you're talking!' is the shorter common form; 'my language' adds 'that's exactly my kind of thing'.		
274	2731	2876	conjure up	/ˈkɑndʒɚ ʌp/	phrasal verb	To cause something to appear as if by magic; or evoke/create a mental image, memory, feeling, explanation, idea, or resource, sometimes with effort.	summon, evoke, call up, dream up	The smell conjured up memories of childhood. / Somehow she conjured up a solution before the deadline.	Broaden beyond memory/image. 'Conjure' without up is also common.		
274	2732	2877	Romance language	/ˈroʊˌmæns ˈlæŋɡwɪdʒ/	noun (linguistics)	A language descended from varieties of Vulgar Latin; major examples include Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian, and Romanian, alongside many others.	Latin-descended language	Romanian is a Romance language even though it is geographically separated from many western Romance languages.	CORRECTION: capitalize Romance. 'Latin family' is imprecise; Romance is a branch of the Indo-European family.		
274	2733	2878	back on the books / get something back on the calendar	/bæk ɑn ðə bʊk/	idiomatic/productive scheduling phrases	To restore something to an official schedule, calendar, roster, or record after cancellation/removal. For appointments, 'back on the calendar/books' is natural; singular 'back on the book' is generally nonstandard for this meaning.	reschedule, put back on the calendar, restore to the schedule	Let's get the session back on the calendar for Friday. / The event is back on the books for next month.	CORRECTION: rebuild nonstandard 'back on the book'. 'On the books' can also mean officially recorded/existing, but bookkeeping uses require context-specific wording.		
274	2734	2879	swirl with / be swirling with	/ˈswɝlɪŋ wɪð/	verb construction	To move in a twisting/circling way; figuratively, a place, mind, or situation can be full of rapidly moving thoughts, emotions, rumors, questions, activity, etc.	teem with, be full of; churn with	My mind was swirling with questions. / Rumors were swirling around the office.	Teach both 'X is swirling with Y' and 'Y is swirling around/in X'. Not inherently overwhelming.		
274	2735	2880	more than meets the eye	/mɔr ðən mits ði aɪ/	idiom	More complex, significant, interesting, capable, or mysterious than is apparent at first glance.	deeper than it looks, not as simple as it seems	There is more to this case than meets the eye.	CORRECTION: most productive full pattern is 'there's more to X than meets the eye' or 'X is more than meets the eye' in some contexts.		
274	2736	2881	strike a chord with someone	/ˈsʌmθɪŋ straɪks ə kɔrd/	idiom	To resonate emotionally or intellectually with someone, especially because something feels familiar, meaningful, relevant, or true.	resonate with, hit home, connect with	Her story struck a chord with viewers who had gone through the same thing.	KEY FRAME: X strikes/struck a chord with + person/group. 'Touch a nerve' can be negative/sensitive and is not a clean synonym.		
274	2737	2882	keep rolling	/kip ˈroʊlɪŋ/	informal phrase	To continue moving, working, recording, progressing, or proceeding without stopping; exact sense depends on context.	keep going, carry on, continue	We're making good time, so let's keep rolling. / The camera kept rolling after the mistake.	Add literal/media sense. Informal encouragement; not a fixed idiom with one meaning.		
274	2738	2883	bust a move	/bʌst ə muv/	slang expression	To start dancing or show a dance move; more broadly and less consistently, to take action or make a move.	dance, make a move, get moving	When the music started, he jumped up and busted a move.	Dance sense is the core established slang use. General 'take action' is contextual/extended.		
274	2739	2884	give off	/ɡɪv ɔf/	phrasal verb	To emit, release, radiate, or produce something perceptible such as heat, light, smell, gas, sound, or an impression/vibe.	emit, release, radiate, exude	The heater gives off a lot of heat. / He gives off a calm, confident vibe.	KEY FRAME: X gives off Y. For impressions, 'give off a ... vibe' is especially productive.		
274	2740	2885	tangy	/ˈtæŋi/	adjective	Having a pleasantly sharp, lively, acidic, or piquant taste; sometimes extended to smell.	zesty, tart, sharp, piquant	The sauce is creamy but tangy from the lemon and yogurt.	CORRECTION: tangy is not simply 'slightly sour'; it can involve sharp/pungent flavor more broadly. Usually positive/neutral.		
275	2741	2886	speculate	/ˈspɛkjəˌleɪt/	verb	To form theories/guesses without firm evidence; or to take financial risk in hopes of profit, especially by buying/selling assets.	conjecture, theorize, guess; invest speculatively, gamble	We can only speculate about why she left. / He speculated in property during the boom.	KEY FRAMES: speculate about/on + question; speculate that + clause; speculate in + asset/market (financial).		
275	2742	2887	strain / straining	/ˈstreɪnɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle/adjective	To exert great effort; stretch or injure through excessive force; place pressure on a relationship/resource; filter through a strainer; noun has related physical/injury/pressure/type senses.	struggle, overexert, tax, stress; filter	I strained to hear her. / The workload is straining the team. / He strained his back lifting the box.	Normalize base + source form and add major senses. 'Straining' as adjective is less central than participle.		
275	2743	2888	it happens to the best of us	/ɪt ˈhæpənz tə ðə bɛst əv ʌs/	idiom	A consoling phrase meaning that even capable, careful, or experienced people sometimes make mistakes or suffer ordinary mishaps.	everyone makes mistakes, it can happen to anyone	You sent it to the wrong address? It happens to the best of us.	Often minimizes embarrassment kindly; can also be lightly humorous.		
275	2744	2889	expanse	/ɪkˈspæns/	noun	A wide, continuous area or extent, especially of land, water, sky, space, or another visible surface.	stretch, sweep, extent, spread	A vast expanse of desert stretched to the horizon.	CORRECTION: abstract 'expanse of thought/imagination' is possible but uncommon/poetic; concrete spatial uses are central.		
275	2745	2890	electrolyte	/ɪˈlɛktrəˌlaɪt/	noun (chemistry/physiology)	A substance that produces mobile ions when dissolved or molten and can conduct electric current; in physiology, electrolytes such as sodium and potassium ions help regulate fluid balance, nerve signaling, muscle function, etc.	ionic solute, electrolyte ion (contextual)	Sweat contains water and electrolytes such as sodium and chloride.	CORRECTION: electrolytes are not defined by being 'essential for hydration'; that's a physiological application. 'Ion solution' is not a synonym.		
275	2746	2891	jasper	/ˈdʒæspɚ ˈdʒɛmˌstoʊn/	noun (mineral/gemstone)	An opaque, microcrystalline variety of silica/chalcedony, often patterned or colored by impurities and used as an ornamental stone/gem material.	ornamental chalcedony, gemstone material	The pendant was carved from red jasper.	CORRECTION: normalize headword to jasper. Often described as an opaque variety of chalcedony; avoid oversimplifying it as simply 'quartz'.		
275	2747	2893	reminisce / reminisce about	/ˌrɛməˈnɪs/	verb	To think, talk, or write about past experiences, often with nostalgia or pleasure.	look back, recall, remember fondly	We spent the evening reminiscing about college. / He reminisced with old friends.	KEY FRAME: reminisce about + past topic; reminisce with + person. Fondness is common but not logically required in every use.		
275	2748	2894	canvass / canvassing	/ˈkænvəsɪŋ/	verb; noun/gerund	To approach people systematically to seek votes, support, opinions, orders, or information; also to examine/discuss a subject thoroughly. 'Canvas' is the fabric; 'canvass' is the verb.	campaign, solicit, survey; examine	Volunteers canvassed the neighborhood for votes. / The committee canvassed several options.	Normalize base + source form. 'Proselytize' specifically seeks converts to beliefs and is not a general synonym.		
275	2749	2895	enunciate	/ɪˈnʌnsieɪt/	verb	To pronounce speech clearly and distinctly; also, more formally, to state or formulate a principle, idea, or doctrine clearly.	pronounce clearly, articulate; state, formulate	Please slow down and enunciate. / The report enunciates three guiding principles.	CORRECTION: 'enunciate words more clearly' is redundant but natural; distinguish pronunciation sense from formal 'state clearly' sense.		
275	2750	2896	unearthly	/ʌnˈɝθli/	adjective	Seeming not of this world: supernatural, eerie, extraordinarily strange, or exceptionally intense/beautiful in a way that feels beyond ordinary experience.	otherworldly, eerie, uncanny, supernatural	An unearthly howl came from the woods. / The singer had an unearthly beauty to her voice.	Can be frightening or positively awe-inspiring. Common with sound, silence, glow, beauty.		
276	2751	2897	possum / opossum	/ˈpɑsəm/	noun	In North American informal usage, 'possum' commonly means an opossum (family Didelphidae). In Australia and nearby regions, 'possums' are different marsupials in the suborder Phalangeriformes.	opossum (North American colloquial use); possum (Australasian animals)	A Virginia opossum wandered across the yard. / Australian possums are not the same animals as American opossums.	CORRECTION: original card wrongly grouped American and Australasian animals as one animal known for playing dead. Playing dead is especially associated with opossums.		
276	2752	2898	on the edge of one's seat / keep someone on the edge of their seat	/ɑn ði ɛdʒ əv ðə sit/	idiom	Extremely interested, excited, anxious, or suspensefully engaged while waiting to see what happens.	in suspense, riveted, intensely engaged	The finale kept me on the edge of my seat.	CORRECTION: standard possessive construction is 'on the edge of my/your/their seat', not generic 'the seat'.		
276	2753	2899	Urfa biber / isot pepper	/ˈʊrfə ˈbiːbɚ/	noun / food term	A dried Turkish chili pepper/flaked seasoning associated with Şanlıurfa, known for dark color and a complex smoky, earthy, raisin-like flavor with moderate heat.	isot pepper, Urfa pepper	I sprinkled Urfa biber over roasted vegetables for smoky heat.	Food/cultural term. Flavor and heat vary by product; capitalize Urfa. 'Biber' is Turkish for pepper.		
276	2754	2900	antics	/ˈæntɪks/	plural noun	Playful, silly, mischievous, attention-getting, or sometimes foolish/outrageous behavior or actions.	shenanigans, hijinks, capers, clowning	The kids' antics kept everyone laughing. / His online antics eventually annoyed his coworkers.	Usually plural. Can be affectionate/amused or critical depending context; not necessarily intended to amuse.		
276	2755	2901	gullible streak	/ˈɡʌləbəl striːk/	noun phrase	A tendency or recurring trait in someone that makes them more easily deceived or tricked.	naive side, trusting nature, susceptibility	He's usually skeptical, but he has a gullible streak when it comes to online deals.	Productive pattern: have a + ADJECTIVE + streak = have a recurring side/tendency (a stubborn streak, a competitive streak). Here gullible modifies streak; it is not a fixed idiom.		
276	2756	2902	in contrast vs. by contrast	/ɪn ˈkɑntræst/ vs. /baɪ ˈkɑntræst/	phrase (comparison markers)	Both mark contrast. 'In contrast' commonly stands alone or appears in 'in contrast to/with X'; 'by contrast' is especially natural as a sentence-level connector meaning 'when compared with what was just mentioned'.	conversely, on the other hand, however	In contrast to her brother, she's very quiet. / The first version is cluttered. By contrast, the revised one is easy to scan.	Do not reduce the distinction to sentence position. KEY FRAME: in contrast to/with + noun; By contrast, + clause/sentence. Both can connect discourse, with overlapping use.		
276	2757	2903	porthole	/ˈpɔrt hoʊl/	noun	A small window in the side of a ship or other vessel, traditionally round; the term can also be used for similar small round windows elsewhere.	round window, cabin window	I looked through the porthole and saw nothing but ocean.	CORRECTION: standard spelling is one word, 'porthole'. It is especially associated with ships; 'aircraft window' is not a useful defining synonym.		
276	2758	2904	ladder truck	/ˈlædɚ trʌk/	noun	A fire apparatus equipped with an aerial ladder and related equipment for access, rescue, ventilation, and other firefighting operations; exact apparatus terminology varies by department/region.	aerial ladder truck, aerial apparatus, truck company apparatus	The ladder truck arrived quickly to help rescue people trapped on the upper floors.	Avoid treating every ladder truck as simply a fire engine with a ladder. Fire-service classifications and equipment configurations vary.		
276	2759	2906	wolf down / wolf something down	/wʊlf aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal)	To eat food very quickly and hungrily.	devour, gobble down, scarf down, inhale (slang)	He wolfed down his sandwich in less than a minute.	CORRECTION: standard expression is 'wolf down' / 'wolf something down', not normally 'wolf out'. KEY FRAME: wolf + FOOD + down.		
276	2760	2907	squiggly	/ˈskwɪɡli/	adjective	Having irregular, wavy, or curly lines or shapes; often used to describe handwriting, lines, or motion.	wavy, curly, loopy, zigzag, twisted	Her squiggly signature was hard to read but full of personality. He drew a squiggly line to decorate the edge of the page.	Common collocations: squiggly line, squiggly handwriting, squiggly shape. Informal/descriptive; suggests repeated bends/waves rather than a precise geometric pattern.		
277	2761	2908	idyllic	/aɪˈdɪ.lɪk	adjective	extremely peaceful, picturesque, and pleasant, often in an idealized or romanticized way.	blissful, serene, pastoral, perfect, idealized	They spent the weekend at an idyllic cottage by the lake. They spent their honeymoon in an idyllic village by the sea.	Often describes places/scenes/lifestyles as attractively peaceful and picturesque, sometimes idealized: idyllic village, setting, childhood, life.		
277	2762	2909	famished	/ˈfæmɪʃt/	adjective	Extremely hungry; very hungry in ordinary modern use.	starving, ravenous, extremely hungry	After the long hike, we were absolutely famished.	'Famished' is commonly hyperbolic and does not literally imply starvation. Stronger than hungry; compare WID1843 peckish = slightly hungry.		
277	2763	2910	extraneous	/ɪkˈstreɪniəs/	adjective	Irrelevant or unnecessary; not essential to the matter at hand.	unrelated, unnecessary, superfluous, nonessential	Try to remove any extraneous details from your report to make it more concise.	Common: extraneous detail/information/noise/factor/material. Means unnecessary or irrelevant to the purpose; not necessarily false.		
277	2764	2911	perk up / perk	/pɝk/	phrasal verb; verb	'Perk up' = become or make more lively, cheerful, alert, or energetic. Bare 'perk' also occurs, especially in related senses, but 'perk up' is the central everyday construction here.	brighten, liven, energize, cheer up	She perked up when she heard they were getting ice cream. / A cup of coffee might perk me up.	KEY FRAME: PERSON perks up; THING perks PERSON up. Keep separate from noun 'perk' = benefit.		
277	2765	2912	perk (noun)	/pɝk/	noun	A non-wage benefit or extra advantage offered as part of a job, membership, or situation.	benefit, bonus, fringe benefit, incentive	One of the perks of working here is unlimited coffee.	Common frame: a perk of + job/situation; job perks. A perk can be formal compensation (insurance, discounts) or an informal advantage ('a perk of living downtown').		
277	2766	2913	perk / percolate (coffee)	/pɝk/	verb; noun (dialectal/informal coffee use)	As a verb, 'perk' can mean percolate or make coffee in a percolator. Noun uses referring to percolated coffee/percolator are dialectal or uncommon and should not be treated as general standard English.	percolate, brew (coffee)	Let the coffee perk for a few minutes.	CORRECTION: prioritize the established verb use. 'Fresh perk' as a noun is regional/uncommon; do not teach it as a general synonym for brewed coffee.		
277	2767	2915	atypical	/ˌeɪˈtɪpɪkəl/	adjective	Not representative of a type, group, or norm; unusual or irregular.	uncharacteristic, unusual, irregular, uncommon	It was an atypical response from someone usually so calm.	KEY FRAME: atypical of + person/group; atypical for + situation/person. Means not typical, not necessarily abnormal or bad.		
277	2768	2916	ostensibly	/ɑːˈstɛn.sə.bli/	adverb	Apparently or purportedly, but perhaps not actually; used to express something that seems true on the surface.	seemingly, apparently, allegedly, supposedly	He was ostensibly on vacation, but people suspected he was still working.	Often carries a gap between stated/apparent purpose and suspected reality: ostensibly to do X; ostensibly about X. It does not itself prove deception.		
277	2769	2917	overegg / overegg the pudding	/ˌoʊvɚˈɛɡɪŋ/	verb; idiom (chiefly BrE informal)	To overdo, overembellish, or exaggerate something so that the effect becomes excessive; from the expression 'overegg the pudding'.	overdo, exaggerate, embellish too much	The final scene overegged the emotion. / Don't overegg the pudding with too many dramatic effects.	Normalize base form. Chiefly British. Can apply to claims, performances, descriptions, decoration, drama, etc.		
277	2770	2918	burgeon / burgeoning	/ˈbɝdʒənɪŋ/	verb; adjective/participle	To begin growing or expanding rapidly; burgeoning = growing/flourishing quickly.	flourishing, developing, thriving, rising	A small scene began to burgeon around the club. / The city's burgeoning tech sector attracted investors.	Normalize base + source form. Often: burgeoning industry/market/population/interest. It emphasizes vigorous growth, not merely existence.		
278	2771	2919	tease out / tease something out	/tiːzd aʊt/	phrasal verb	To gradually extract, identify, clarify, or reveal subtle information, distinctions, implications, or threads through careful effort.	extract, reveal, unravel, deduce	The interviewer teased out the differences between the two accounts.	Normalize base. KEY FRAME: tease X out / tease out X. Common in analysis, research, interviews, interpretation.		
278	2772	2920	taut	/tɔt/	adjective	1. Stretched or pulled tight, with no slack. 2. (figuratively) Showing tension, discipline, or emotional strain.	tight, tense, firm, controlled	She wrapped the rope taut around the pole. / His taut expression revealed the pressure he was under.	Literal: taut rope/wire/skin = tightly stretched. Figurative: taut prose/plot = tightly controlled with little excess; taut nerves/expression = tense. 'Tight' is broader.		
278	2773	2921	on tenterhooks / tenterhooks	/ˈtɛntɚˌhʊks/	idiom; plural noun	'On tenterhooks' means in a state of anxious suspense or nervous anticipation. Tenterhooks were hooks used to hold cloth stretched on a tenter.	edge of the seat, suspense, nervous tension, high alert	We were on tenterhooks waiting for the results.	KEY FORM is 'on tenterhooks', not simply tenterhooks = suspense. Historical literal origin is a useful memory hook.		
278	2774	2923	gauntlet	/ˈɡɔnt.lət/	noun	A protective glove, historically part of armor; figuratively in established expressions such as 'run the gauntlet' = endure a series of attacks, criticisms, tests, or difficulties. 'Throw down the gauntlet' = issue a challenge.	glove, ordeal, challenge, trial	The knight wore armored gauntlets. / The candidate ran the gauntlet of hostile questions. / She threw down the gauntlet to her rival.	Add two major idioms. Note historical spelling/etymological overlap around 'gantlet/gauntlet'; modern 'run the gauntlet' is standard.		
278	2775	2924	IBS	/ˌaɪ.biːˈɛs/	noun (medical acronym)	Irritable bowel syndrome: a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by recurrent abdominal pain associated with changes in bowel habits, without a single structural lesion explaining the symptoms.	irritable bowel syndrome	IBS can involve constipation, diarrhea, or both, along with abdominal pain.	Medical precision: symptoms and treatment vary. Avoid treating 'gut sensitivity' as a synonym or implying one universal management plan.		
278	2776	2925	coerce	/koʊˈɝs/	verb	To compel or pressure someone to act through force, threats, intimidation, or other coercive pressure.	force, pressure, intimidate, bully	They coerced him into signing the agreement. / She said the confession had been coerced.	KEY FRAME: coerce + PERSON + into + gerund/noun. Stronger than persuade or pressure; lack of genuinely free choice is central.		
278	2777	2926	appease	/əˈpiːz/	verb	To pacify, calm, or satisfy someone, often by yielding to their demands.	calm, placate, soothe, mollify	The manager tried to appease the angry customer with a refund.	Common: appease a person/group/critics; appease anger/fears (less central). Can be neutral 'calm/satisfy' or critical when concessions are seen as excessive. Historical/political 'appeasement' carries special associations.		
278	2778	2927	disruptive	/dɪsˈrʌptɪv/	adjective	Causing interruption, disorder, or major change to normal activity; in business/technology, 'disruptive' can describe innovations that significantly alter established markets or practices.	disorderly, unsettling, radical, groundbreaking	His behavior in class was disruptive. / The new app is seen as a disruptive force in the taxi industry.	Do not use 'groundbreaking' as a blanket synonym: disruptive describes effect on an existing system, not simply novelty.		
278	2779	2928	single-handedly	/ˌsɪŋɡəlˈhændɪdli/	adverb	Without help from anyone else; independently.	alone, independently, solo, unaided	She single-handedly organized the entire fundraiser event.	Can be literal credit for doing something alone, or hyperbolic emphasis: 'That one scene single-handedly saved the episode.' Hyphenated.		
278	2780	2929	trickle down	/ˈtrɪkəl daʊn/	phrasal verb	To pass, spread, or filter gradually from one level/group to another, especially from higher levels to lower ones; strongly associated with debates over 'trickle-down' economics but usable more broadly.	filter down, diffuse, spread slowly	The policy changes eventually trickled down to local offices.	Separate neutral phrasal verb from political/economic label 'trickle-down economics', whose effectiveness is a contested empirical/policy question.		
279	2781	2930	empty nest syndrome	/ˈɛmpti nɛst ˈsɪnˌdroʊm/	noun (psychological term)	A nonclinical popular term for sadness, loss, loneliness, or adjustment difficulties some parents/caregivers may experience after children leave home.	empty-nest adjustment, feelings of loss after children leave home	After her last child went to college, she struggled with empty nest syndrome.	CORRECTION: not a formal psychiatric diagnosis and not equivalent to depression or 'parental separation anxiety'. Experiences vary widely.		
279	2782	2931	cut back / cut back on	/kʌt bæk/	phrasal verb	To reduce spending, consumption, activity, production, or another amount/extent; 'cut back on X' is the common frame for reducing one's use of something.	reduce, scale down, trim, curtail	We're cutting back on eating out. / The company cut back production.	KEY FRAMES: cut back on + noun/gerund; cut back + production/spending. Contrast 'cut down on' (very similar in consumption contexts).		
279	2783	2932	quid pro quo	/ˌkwɪd proʊ ˈkwoʊ/	noun (Latin phrase)	Something given or done in exchange for something else; a reciprocal arrangement. In legal/political contexts, the phrase may describe an exchange relevant to bribery, contracts, harassment, etc., but is not inherently illegal.	exchange, reciprocal arrangement, something for something	They agreed to a quid pro quo: technical support in exchange for access to the data.	Latin literally 'something for something'. CORRECTION: 'trade-off' is not always equivalent; a trade-off is a compromise between competing benefits/costs.		
279	2784	2933	exasperate	/ɪɡˈzæspəˌreɪt/	verb	To irritate or anger someone intensely.	infuriate, enrage, provoke, aggravate	Her constant lateness exasperated the team leader.	Transitive: X exasperates PERSON; PERSON is exasperated by/with X. Means intensely irritate/frustrate, often after repeated difficulty rather than explosive rage.		
279	2785	2934	vex	/vɛks/	verb	To annoy, frustrate, puzzle, or worry; often literary/formal. Also used in 'vexed question/issue' for a difficult, much-debated problem.	irritate, bother, disturb, provoke	The inconsistency vexed the researchers. / It remains a vexed question.	Broaden beyond small/repeated annoyance. 'Vexed' has an important adjectival use = difficult/controversial.		
279	2786	2935	peeve / pet peeve	/piːv/	verb; noun phrase	Verb 'peeve' = annoy or irritate. A 'pet peeve' is a particular thing that especially annoys someone; bare noun 'peeve' exists but is much less common than 'pet peeve'.	annoyance, pet peeve, irritate, bug	It peeves me when people block the doorway. / Loud chewing is one of my pet peeves.	CORRECTION: prioritize common noun construction 'pet peeve'. Verb is transitive: X peeves someone.		
279	2787	2936	revamp / revamping	/ˌriːˈvæmpɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle	To renovate, revise, or give a new and improved form to something.	renovating, updating, reworking, modernizing	The company is revamping its website to attract more users.	Normalize base + source form. Verb: revamp a site/system/process. Noun: a revamp. Often modernization/substantial redesign, not a guarantee of improvement.		
279	2788	2937	debunk	/diˈbʌŋk/	verb	To show that a claim, myth, theory, or belief is false or unsupported, typically by evidence or argument.	disprove, refute, expose as false, challenge successfully	The scientist debunked the popular but incorrect health myth.	'Invalidate' is broader/technical; 'debunk' often targets widely believed myths/claims. Avoid claiming a debunk succeeds unless the evidence actually establishes it.		
279	2789	2938	dread / be dreading	/ˈdrɛdɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle	To anticipate something with strong fear, anxiety, or reluctance; noun 'dread' = strong apprehension/fear.	fearing, anticipating with anxiety, worrying about	I'm dreading the meeting tomorrow. / She felt a sense of dread as the deadline approached.	Normalize base + source form. Dread can include strong reluctance without literal terror.		
279	2790	2939	delve into	/dɛlv ˈɪntu/	phrasal verb	To investigate, explore, or examine something deeply and thoroughly.	explore, investigate, probe, examine	The journalist delved into the company's financial records.	KEY FRAME: delve into + topic/question/records/history. Slightly formal; implies going beneath the surface, though 'deeply and thoroughly' can be contextual rather than guaranteed.		
280	2791	2940	disenfranchised / disenfranchise	/ˌdɪsɪnˈfrænˌtʃaɪzd/	adjective; verb	Deprived of the right to vote (core political/legal sense); more broadly, deprived of rights, power, or meaningful participation.	marginalized, oppressed, excluded, silenced	The law disenfranchised many eligible voters. / Some residents felt politically disenfranchised.	CORRECTION: voting-rights sense is central and precise. Broader figurative use exists, but 'marginalized/oppressed/silenced' are not exact synonyms.		
280	2792	2941	coercive	/koʊˈɝsɪv/	adjective	Using force, threats, pressure, or other means that constrain a person's free choice or compel compliance.	compelling, force-based, threatening, pressuring	The regime was accused of using coercive tactics to silence opposition.	Related verb coerce. 'Manipulative' may overlap but is not necessarily coercive; coercion specifically involves constrained choice/pressure.		
280	2793	2942	dalliance	/ˈdæliəns/	noun	A brief, casual, or not-very-serious involvement, especially a romantic/sexual affair; also a temporary flirtation with an activity, idea, or pursuit.	fling, flirtation, brief affair; brief involvement	His dalliance with acting ended when he realized it wasn't his true calling.	KEY FRAME: a dalliance with + person/activity/idea. 'Dabble' is a verb and not a noun synonym.		
280	2794	2943	miss out on someone/something	/mɪst aʊt ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn ˈsʌmˌθɪŋ/	phrasal verb	To fail to experience, obtain, enjoy, or take advantage of something; with a person, to lose the opportunity for a relationship/experience with them depending context.	lost the chance, passed up, didn't get to experience	I missed out on the concert. / If you never talk to her, you might miss out on getting to know her.	Normalize base. KEY FRAME: miss out on + noun/gerund. Distinguish 'miss someone' = feel their absence.		
280	2795	2944	op-ed	/ˈɑpˈɛd/	noun	A signed opinion/commentary article, traditionally associated with the page opposite a newspaper's editorial page; today used broadly for contributed opinion pieces.	opinion piece, guest commentary, opinion essay	She wrote an op-ed criticizing the government's climate policy.	CORRECTION: an op-ed need not be in a magazine/newspaper nor written by someone outside editorial staff in every modern usage. 'Editorial' usually represents an editorial board/institutional position and is not synonymous.		
280	2796	2945	blinker	/ˈblɪŋkɚ/	noun	A flashing vehicle turn signal/indicator (especially AmE). Separately, 'blinkers' in BrE can mean horse blinders; figurative limited-perspective uses usually come from 'blinkers/blinkered', not the vehicle light.	turn signal, indicator	Use your blinker before changing lanes. / His blinkered view ignored the alternatives.	CORRECTION: separate vehicle 'blinker' from figurative 'blinkers/blinkered' = restricted outlook.		
280	2797	2946	clutch / clutching	/ˈklʌtʃɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle	To grasp or hold tightly, often suddenly or from fear/urgency; noun 'clutch' can mean a tight grasp and has important mechanical/other senses.	gripping, grasping, holding onto	The child clutched her teddy bear during the storm. / He clutched the railing as the boat rocked.	Normalize base + source form. Emotional motivation is common but not required.		
280	2798	2947	ally	/ˈælaɪ/	noun / verb	Noun: a person, group, or country that cooperates with/supports another, especially toward a shared goal. Verb: to join or associate with another party for a purpose.	partner, supporter, collaborator, comrade	They became allies during the campaign. / The group allied itself with local organizers.	KEY VERB FRAMES: ally oneself with X; be allied with X. In social-justice contexts, 'ally' can specifically mean a supporter outside a marginalized group, but that is one contextual sense.		
280	2799	2948	ostracize	/ˈɑstrəˌsaɪz/	verb	To exclude someone from a group, society, or social circle.	shun, exile, expel, cast out	He was ostracized by his former friends after the scandal.	KEY FRAME: ostracize + person/group; be ostracized from/by. Social exclusion is central; 'exile/expel' can imply formal/physical removal and are not exact.		
280	2800	2949	diatribe / diatribes	/ˈdaɪəˌtraɪbz/	noun	A bitter, forceful, often lengthy verbal or written attack on someone or something.	tirades, rants, harangues, denunciations	He launched into a diatribe against the new policy.	Normalize singular/plural. Common: launch into a diatribe; a diatribe against/about X. Stronger and more sustained than ordinary criticism.		
281	2801	2950	prank	/præŋk/	noun / verb	A mischievous trick or practical joke, usually intended to surprise or amuse, though pranks can also be harmful or unwelcome; verb = play such a trick.	joke, gag, stunt, practical joke	They pulled a prank by covering her office in balloons.	Intent to amuse does not guarantee the target finds it funny. Common: play/pull a prank on someone; prank someone.		
281	2802	2951	swipe	/swaɪp/	verb; noun	To move a finger across a touchscreen; steal/take quickly (informal); strike with a sweeping motion; noun 'a swipe' can be such a motion or a critical attack/remark ('take a swipe at').	steal, snatch, jab, dig, roast, tap	Swipe left to delete it. / Someone swiped my umbrella. / The columnist took a swipe at the mayor.	CORRECTION: consolidate major senses rather than label all of 'swipe' as slang. KEY IDIOM: take a swipe at + person/idea.		
281	2803	2952	willy-nilly	/ˌwɪliˈnɪli/	adverb / adjective	Adverb/adjective: in a haphazard, random, or uncontrolled way; also, whether willing or unwilling / inevitably, especially in older/formal use.	randomly, aimlessly, unavoidably	He tossed clothes into the suitcase willy-nilly.	Two historically related senses remain: haphazardly and willy-nilly = whether one likes it or not. Context usually disambiguates.		
281	2804	2953	legislative	/ˈlɛdʒɪˌsleɪtɪv/	adjective	Relating to legislation or a legislature—the making of laws or the lawmaking branch/body.	lawmaking, statutory, parliamentary	The legislative branch debated the bill for over three weeks.	Common: legislative branch/body/process/power/agenda. 'Statutory' means established/required by statute and is not a general synonym.		
281	2805	2954	chew the fat	/tʃu ðə fæt/	idiom	To engage in a casual, leisurely conversation.	chat, shoot the breeze, gab, jaw	We stayed up all night chewing the fat about high school memories.	Informal/old-fashioned flavor in some varieties. Means chat at length in a relaxed way; not necessarily gossip. Compare shoot the breeze.		
281	2806	2955	chew the cud	/tʃu ðə kʌd/	verb phrase (literal zoological)	For a ruminant, to regurgitate partially digested material from the foregut and chew it again as part of rumination.	ruminate (zoological)	The cow lay in the field chewing the cud as the sun set.	Literal biological construction. 'Digest again' is not a synonym; chewing cud is one stage in the ruminant digestive process.		
281	2807	2956	chew the cud / chew over	/tʃu ðə kʌd/	idiom (chiefly BrE, dated/less common)	Figuratively, to think or talk reflectively at length; this use is less common than literal 'chew the cud'. 'Chew over X' is a more productive modern expression for consider/discuss carefully.	mull over, ponder, contemplate, reflect	He sat quietly chewing the cud over what had happened. / Let's chew the idea over before deciding.	Register caution: figurative 'chew the cud' can sound old-fashioned or deliberately humorous. Do not overstate it as a common British idiom.		
281	2808	2957	cud	/kʌd/	noun	Partially digested food that a ruminant regurgitates from the foregut to the mouth and chews again.	regurgitated feed (ruminant context)	The cow lay in the field chewing its cud.	CLEANUP: remove corrupted text and overlong anatomy dump. Figurative 'chew the cud' belongs on the separate idiom card.		
281	2809	2958	ruminant	/ˈruːmɪnənt/	noun / adjective	Noun: a mammal that digests plant material through foregut fermentation and rumination, typically using a multi-compartment stomach; adjective: relating to such animals or, figuratively, deeply contemplative.	ruminating mammal; contemplative (figurative adjective)	Cattle, sheep, goats, and deer are ruminants. / He had a slow, ruminant manner of thinking.	PRECISION: avoid reducing the class to 'herbivore with a four-chambered stomach'; camelids are ruminating foregut fermenters but are not true ruminants. Figurative person-noun use is rare.		
281	2810	2959	ruminate	/ˈruːməˌneɪt/	verb	To think repeatedly or deeply about something; in psychology, often to engage in repetitive, difficult-to-disengage thinking, frequently about distress. Zoology: to regurgitate and rechew cud.	ponder, brood, obsess, dwell on	She kept ruminating on the mistake. / Cattle spend hours ruminating.	Important nuance: everyday/literary ruminate can be reflective; psychological rumination often has repetitive negative content/process. KEY FRAME: ruminate on/about X.		
282	2811	2960	ruminative	/ˈruːmɪnətɪv/	adjective	Characterized by sustained, reflective thought; in psychological contexts, involving repetitive rumination, which may be negative or self-focused.	reflective, contemplative, brooding (contextual), introspective	His ruminative gaze lingered on the empty street. / The novel had a quiet, ruminative tone that made it linger in your mind. / She grew more ruminative after the loss of her mentor.	Do not build depression into the lexical definition. Common: ruminative thinking/style/mood/tone.		
282	2812	2961	regurgitate	/rɪˈɡɝːdʒəˌteɪt/	verb	To bring swallowed material back up to the mouth; in some animals this is a normal feeding/digestive behavior. Figuratively, to repeat information or ideas mechanically without meaningful processing.	bring back up; repeat mechanically, parrot	Birds regurgitate food to feed their young. / He just regurgitated the talking points without understanding them.	CORRECTION: regurgitation is not simply synonymous with vomiting; vomiting is a distinct forceful physiological process. Figurative 'regurgitate facts/talking points' is strongly negative.		
282	2813	2962	musing / musings	/ˈmjuːzɪŋ/	noun	A thought, reflection, or period of reflective/speculative thinking; plural 'musings' often means informal reflections on a subject.	reflection, thought, meditation, pondering	The essay is a collection of musings on memory and travel.	CORRECTION: 'musing' as adjective is uncommon compared with 'musing' noun and 'musingly' rare; prioritize noun/plural. Related verb: muse on/about/over.		
282	2814	2964	somber	/ˈsɑːmbɚ/	adjective	Dark, gloomy, or dull in tone or mood. Often associated with sadness, melancholy, or seriousness.	gloomy, melancholic, grave, mournful	The movie had a somber tone throughout. / He wore a somber expression at the funeral.	AmE spelling 'somber'; BrE also 'sombre'. Can describe mood, expression, colors, music, atmosphere, occasion. Means serious/gloomy/subdued, not always explicitly sad.		
282	2815	2965	supercalifragilisticexpialidocious	/ˌsuːpərkælɪˌfrædʒɪˌlɪstɪkˌɛkspiˌælɪˈdoʊʃəs/	adjective	A famously long, playful invented word popularized by Disney's 1964 film Mary Poppins, used humorously to suggest something extraordinarily wonderful or to revel in the sound of the word.	fantastic, wonderful, amazing, excellent	The concert was simply supercalifragilisticexpialidocious!	Cultural/reference word rather than ordinary productive adjective. Capitalization usually lowercase unless title/context requires. Do not treat it as a precise synonym for 'excellent'.		
282	2816	2966	tooltip / tooltips	/ˈtuːlˌtɪps/	noun (UI)	A small contextual information box that appears near a user-interface element, commonly on hover, focus, or other interaction, to explain or label it.	hints, help pop-ups, UI prompts	Hover over the icon to display its tooltip.	Normalize singular/plural. Modern touch interfaces may trigger tooltip-like help without mouse hover, so hover is common but not definitional.		
282	2817	2967	cour	/kʊr/	noun (context-specific)	In Japanese television/anime scheduling, a roughly three-month broadcast block, commonly around 10–13 weekly episodes; a series may run for one or multiple cours.	broadcast cour, seasonal broadcast block	This show is getting a second cour starting next summer.	Loanword from French 'cours' via Japanese industry/fandom usage. CORRECTION: a cour is not exactly synonymous with 'season'; English-language fandom may distinguish a multi-cour season from separate seasons.		
282	2818	2968	strap in / be strapped in	/stræpt ɪn/	phrasal verb; passive/adjective	To secure oneself/someone with straps or a safety restraint; figuratively, 'strap in' can mean prepare yourself for an intense, surprising, or eventful experience.	buckled up, fastened, ready	Strap in before the ride starts. / Strap in—this story gets wild.	Normalize active + passive. Figurative imperative 'strap in' is related to 'buckle up' and is informal.		
282	2819	2969	beside	/bɪˈsaɪd/	preposition	Next to or at the side of someone or something.	next to, alongside, adjacent to	She sat beside him during the meeting.	Contrast beside = next to with besides = in addition to/except for (depending construction). Common: beside me, beside the bed; figurative 'beside the point' is a separate idiom.		
282	2820	2970	travail	/trəˈveɪl/	noun / verb	Noun (formal/literary): painful, arduous labor, hardship, or suffering. Verb (rare/archaic/literary): to toil or suffer laboriously.	hardship, toil, ordeal, labor	After years of travail, the project finally succeeded.	Modern active vocabulary is mainly the noun. The verb is rare; do not encourage routine productive use.		
283	2821	2971	tentative vs provisional	/ˈtɛntətɪv/ vs /prəˈvɪʒənəl/	adjectives	Tentative emphasizes uncertainty, hesitation, or lack of final commitment; provisional emphasizes temporary/conditional status pending a final arrangement or confirmation.	tentative: uncertain, cautious, hesitant; provisional: temporary, interim, conditional	We made tentative plans for Saturday. / The committee gave provisional approval pending inspection.	Useful contrast: a tentative date may be uncertain; a provisional date/status is temporarily adopted. They overlap when something is both uncertain and temporary.		
283	2822	2972	legend	/ˈlɛdʒənd/	noun	A traditional story handed down over time, often with a historical basis but not reliably verifiable; also an exceptionally famous/admired person, especially in a field or community; map/chart keys are another major sense.	traditional tale; icon, great; map key	The story became a local legend. / She's a jazz legend. / Check the map legend for the symbols.	CORRECTION: add major cartographic sense. Distinguish legend (person/story/key), legendary (famous or relating to legend), legacy (what is left behind).		
283	2823	2973	be nuts / that's nuts	/ˈsʌmwʌn ɪz nʌts/	informal/slang expression	To be crazy, irrational, extreme, or astonishing; often humorous/hyperbolic rather than a literal statement about mental illness.	crazy, insane, out of their mind	You'd be nuts to drive through that storm. / They doubled the price? That's nuts.	Normalize productive forms. Register caution: calling a person 'nuts' can be insulting/ableist in sensitive contexts; 'that's nuts' often simply means unbelievable/extreme.		
283	2824	2974	eternal legacy	/ɪˈtɝːnəl ˈlɛgəsi/	noun phrase	A legacy described rhetorically as enduring forever or across generations.	enduring legacy, lasting legacy, immortal legacy (rhetorical)	Her humanitarian work left an eternal legacy that inspired generations.	Not a fixed idiom and usually hyperbolic; 'lasting/enduring legacy' is more ordinary. A legacy itself already implies continuing impact after an event/person.		
283	2825	2975	timeless legends	/ˈtaɪmləs ˈlɛdʒəndz/	noun phrase	A rhetorical phrase for legendary stories/figures whose fame or appeal seems to endure across generations.	enduring legends, legendary figures/stories	Timeless legends like King Arthur continue to inspire modern writers.	Compositional rather than a fixed expression. 'Timeless' means not seeming tied to or diminished by a particular era; whether something is timeless is evaluative.		
283	2826	2976	encompass	/ɪnˈkʌmpəs/	verb	To include comprehensively within a scope or range; also literally/formally to surround or enclose.	include, cover, surround, contain	The course encompasses history, politics, and economics. / Mountains encompass the valley.	Common transitive frame: X encompasses A, B, and C. 'Comprise/include' overlap; encompass often stresses breadth/comprehensiveness.		
283	2827	2977	potboiler	/ˈpɑtˌbɔɪlər/	noun	A book, play, artwork, etc. produced primarily to earn money, traditionally implying formulaic or lower artistic ambition; the judgment can be dismissive.	commercial work, formulaic commercial work	The detective novel was criticized as a predictable potboiler.	Historical image: work produced to 'keep the pot boiling'. CORRECTION: speed/low quality are common implications, not definitional requirements.		
283	2828	2978	tentatively qualified / provisionally qualified	/ˈtɛntətɪvli ˈkwɑləˌfaɪd/	adjective phrase	Judged or treated as qualified for now, subject to confirmation, additional evidence, or later requirements; wording is context-specific rather than a fixed idiom.	conditionally certified, provisionally eligible	She was provisionally qualified pending verification of her final grades.	'Provisionally qualified' is often clearer for formal temporary status; 'tentatively qualified' can imply uncertainty in the evaluator's judgment. Choose based on intended nuance.		
283	2829	2980	surmise	/sərˈmaɪz/	verb / noun	(verb) To suppose or guess something based on limited evidence. (noun) A conjecture or assumption made without strong evidence.	guess, infer, suppose, conjecture	From the clues, the detective surmised the culprit's identity.	Verb: surmise that + clause / surmise from + evidence. Noun: a surmise. Slightly formal; unlike infer, surmise highlights uncertainty/limited evidence.		
283	2830	2981	defiance	/dɪˈfaɪəns/	noun	Open, bold resistance or refusal to obey an authority, rule, expectation, or opposing force.	rebellion, resistance, disobedience, noncompliance	She spoke in defiance of the ban. / He folded his arms in open defiance.	KEY COLLOCATIONS: in defiance of; act of defiance; open defiance. 'Noncompliance' can be neutral/technical and lacks the bold oppositional tone.		
284	2831	2982	reverence	/ˈrɛvərəns/	noun	Deep respect, honor, or awe, especially toward something regarded as sacred, venerable, morally important, or profoundly worthy.	respect, veneration, admiration, awe, honor	She spoke with reverence about her teacher. / The shrine is treated with great reverence.	KEY FRAMES: reverence for + person/thing; with reverence. Stronger/more solemn than ordinary admiration.		
284	2832	2983	truncate	/ˈtrʌŋˌkeɪt/	verb	To shorten by cutting off or omitting an end/portion; in computing/data display, to cut content to a specified length; in mathematics, to drop digits beyond a chosen place without rounding.	shorten, cut, abbreviate, condense	The interface truncates long filenames. / Truncate 3.14159 to two decimal places and you get 3.14.	Important contrast: truncate vs round. Also 'truncated' = cut short/abbreviated.		
284	2833	2984	asset	/ˈæsɛt/	noun	Something valuable/useful; in accounting/finance, a resource with economic value owned or controlled by an entity; more broadly, a person/skill/quality that is advantageous.	resource, advantage, benefit, strength	Her language skills are a major asset. / Cash and equipment appear among the company's assets.	Add technical financial sense. Opposite accounting category: liability (not always an everyday antonym).		
284	2834	2985	wobble / flip-flop	/ˈwɑbəlz ənd ˈflɪpˌflɑps/	nouns; verbs; figurative political/decision language	Wobble = instability, hesitation, or a temporary weakening/change; flip-flop = a conspicuous reversal of position or decision. Together they can describe inconsistency, but they are not a fixed lexical pair.	instability, vacillation, inconsistency	The candidate wobbled under pressure and later flip-flopped on the policy.	CORRECTION: 'wobbles and flip-flops' is compositional/source wording, not a standard noun phrase. Teach the distinct productive words.		
284	2835	2986	soft and floaty	/sɔft ənd ˈfloʊti/	adjective phrase	A compositional description meaning soft in texture/feel and light, airy, loose, or seemingly floating in movement/appearance.	airy, delicate, buoyant, lightweight	She chose a soft, floaty dress for the beach.	Not a fixed idiom. 'Floaty' is informal and common for fabric/clothing/hair/visual style; it can also describe a dreamy/light sensation.		
284	2836	2987	bite off more than one can chew	/doʊnt baɪt ɔf mɔr ðæn ju kæn ʧu/	idiom	To take on more work, responsibility, difficulty, or commitment than one can realistically handle.	overcommit, overreach, overextend	I bit off more than I could chew by agreeing to run both projects.	Normalize reusable base form; imperative warning 'Don't bite off more than you can chew' is one common realization. Literal eating metaphor → overcommitment.		
284	2837	2988	uniformly	/ˈjunəˌfɔrmli/	adverb	In the same or consistent way throughout a group/area/time; or to the same degree in every case.	consistently, evenly, homogeneously	The rule was applied uniformly across all departments. / The surface was uniformly smooth.	Does not necessarily mean 'identically' in every detail; often means consistently/evenly. 'Uniformly negative' = negative across the board.		
284	2838	2989	comeback	/ˈkʌmˌbæk/	noun	A return to success, popularity, activity, or a previous position after decline/absence; also a quick, effective reply, especially to an insult or criticism.	return, resurgence; retort, rejoinder	The singer made a comeback after five years away. / She had a sharp comeback ready.	CORRECTION: restore major return/resurgence sense; this card had been narrowed to retort only. Compare WID2572.		
284	2839	2990	bring down the house	/brɪŋ daʊn ðə haʊs/	idiom	To delight an audience so much that it responds with very loud applause/laughter/approval.	bring the crowd to its feet, wow the audience, steal the show	The comedian brought down the house with his final joke.	Performance idiom. It describes audience reaction, not literally damaging a building. 'Steal the show' means be the outstanding performer and is related but not identical.		
284	2840	2991	NSFW	/ɛn ɛs ɛf ˈdʌbəlju/	abbreviation / adjective (Internet slang)	Initialism for 'not safe for work': an online warning that content may be inappropriate to view in a workplace/public setting, commonly because of sexual/nude, graphic, or otherwise sensitive material.	inappropriate, explicit, sensitive content, adult content, warning label	That link is marked NSFW, so don't open it on the office screen.	Usually uppercase NSFW. What counts as NSFW varies by community/context; it is not synonymous with pornography or 'adult content' alone.		
