286	2856	3009	down to	/daʊn tu/	multiword expression	Has several constructions: 'down to the last X' = including the smallest/final detail; 'down to X' = reduced to/remaining; 'be down to someone' = be their responsibility (especially BrE); 'put X down to Y' = attribute X to Y; informal 'be down to do X/for X' = be willing.	up to the last detail; reduced to; attributable to; willing	We're down to three choices. / It's down to me to decide. / I put the mistake down to fatigue. / I'm down to try it.	Do not fold 'get down to work' into the same lexical construction; that is a separate phrasal pattern.		
