288	2871	3026	lean in / lean into	/liːn ˈɪn.tu/ vs /liːn ɪn/	phrasal verbs / figurative constructions	Literally, move the body closer/forward. Figuratively, 'lean into X' commonly means embrace, emphasize, or engage fully with a situation/quality; 'lean in' can mean engage more actively, but its figurative career/leadership use is context- and culture-dependent.	embrace, engage with, commit to	She leaned into the awkwardness and made it part of the joke. / When the discussion got difficult, he leaned in and listened closely.	CORRECTION: the original contrast was too rigid. The expressions overlap; 'lean into + noun' is the clearer productive frame. Add literal source.		
