294	2937	3093	take something home / take that paddle home	/ˈteɪkɪŋ ə ˈpædəl hoʊm/	productive phrase; source-specific line/innuendo	Ordinarily, 'take something home' means bring it home, keep it, or leave with it; in competition contexts, 'take home + prize/trophy' means win it. In the Friends Barbados ping-pong scene, 'We're taking that paddle home, mister' literally refers to taking the ping-pong paddle home, with a playful trophy/souvenir-like reading available on the surface.	bring something home; take home a prize/trophy; keep as a souvenir	We're taking that paddle home, mister. / She took home the trophy after winning the tournament.	SOURCE CONTEXT: Friends, 'The One in Barbados.' First preserve the literal reading: paddle here is the table-tennis paddle, and Phoebe says they are taking it home. The line can play like keeping the paddle as a trophy/souvenir after the impressive display. There is also a strong sexual subtext: Phoebe is visibly turned on and shortly afterward says, 'It's like watching porn!', so the paddle line can additionally suggest kinky/spanking play at home. Do not treat that innuendo as the only literal meaning, and do not teach 'take that paddle home' as an established idiom. Productive learner pattern: take + THING + home; take home + prize/trophy/award.		
294	2938	3094	Japanese sea bream / madai (red sea bream)	/ˈdʒæpəˌniz briːm/	noun / food-fish term	In Japanese food/cultural contexts, 'tai' can refer broadly to sea-bream-like fishes; the especially important celebratory fish is madai, the red sea bream (Pagrus major).	red sea bream, madai, tai (contextual Japanese term)	Grilled madai is served for celebratory occasions in Japan.	CORRECTION: 'Japanese bream' is vague. Do not imply all 'tai' is one species or specifically a New Year dish; sea bream is associated broadly with auspicious celebrations.		
294	2939	3095	postmortem / post-mortem	/poʊstˈmɔrtəm/	noun; adjective	A medical examination after death to determine cause/pathology; figuratively, a retrospective analysis after a project/event to learn what happened and why. Also adjective: postmortem examination/analysis.	autopsy; retrospective, after-action review	The coroner ordered a postmortem. / We held a project postmortem to identify what to improve.	Spelling varies by dialect/style. A project postmortem need not focus only on failure; it can examine successes and lessons too.		
294	2940	3096	airtight / air-tight	/ɛr taɪt/	adjective	Sealed so air cannot readily enter or escape; figuratively, extremely strong, complete, or difficult to challenge, especially an argument, case, alibi, or plan.	sealed, hermetic; watertight, solid, unassailable	Store it in an airtight container. / The prosecutor thought the timeline was airtight.	CORRECTION: standard modern spelling is usually one word 'airtight'. Figurative use means hard to challenge, not literally flawless.		
295	2941	3097	dote on/upon	/doʊt/	verb construction	To show very great, often indulgent or adoring affection toward someone/something.	adore, lavish affection on, fuss over	She dotes on her grandchildren. / He absolutely dotes on that dog.	KEY FRAME: dote on/upon + person/animal. 'Fawn over' often implies excessive/flattering attention and is not a clean synonym.		
295	2942	3099	tail twitch / tail twitching	/teɪl ˈtwɪʧɪŋ/	noun phrase / participial phrase	A small quick movement of an animal's tail. Its meaning depends heavily on species, individual, and context and may reflect arousal, attention, irritation, excitement, hunting focus, etc.	tail flick, tail movement	The cat's tail began twitching as it watched the bird.	CORRECTION: do not assign a universal emotional meaning to tail twitching across animals. Treat behavioral interpretation as context/species dependent.		
295	2943	3100	to no avail	/tu noʊ əˈveɪl/	idiom / prepositional phrase	Without achieving the desired result; unsuccessfully, despite an attempt or effort.	in vain, unsuccessfully, fruitlessly	I restarted the app several times, but to no avail.	Common pattern: try/search/argue/etc., but to no avail. Clean definition punctuation.		
295	2944	3101	futile	/ˈfjuːtəl/ or /ˈfjuːtaɪl/	adjective	Incapable of producing the desired or useful result; ineffective or pointless because it is unlikely to succeed.	fruitless, vain, ineffective, pointless, useless	It was futile to argue with someone who refused to listen. / Their rescue attempts proved futile.	Common frames: futile attempt/effort/gesture; it is futile to + verb. 'Vain' overlaps in 'in vain' but has other unrelated senses. Remove duplicate synonym.		
295	2945	3103	tag (comedy / narrative)	/tæɡ/	noun	In stand-up, an additional punchline or short joke following the main punchline, extending the laugh. In TV/story structure, 'tag' can also mean a short closing scene/beat after the main action; terminology varies by medium.	follow-up punchline, button, stinger (contextual)	The punchline got a laugh, and the comic added two quick tags. / The episode ends with a short comedic tag.	Keep comedy and narrative senses distinct. 'Stinger' overlaps with post-credit/closing material but is not always identical.		
295	2946	3104	ending gag	/ˈɛndɪŋ ɡæɡ/	noun	A humorous scene, line, or event that concludes a story or performance, often providing a final laugh or twist.	final joke, closing joke, closing gag	The movie ended with a clever ending gag that left the audience laughing. Every episode of the sitcom featured an ending gag during the credits.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
295	2947	3105	subversion	/səbˈvɜrʒən/	noun	The act or process of undermining, destabilizing, or overturning an established system, authority, convention, or expectation; also the result/strategy of doing so.	undermining, destabilization, overthrow (contextual), inversion (artistic/contextual)	The regime accused the group of subversion. / The novel plays with a subversion of the traditional hero narrative.	Capitalize only sentence-initially. In politics the word can imply attempts to undermine authority; in art/storytelling, 'subversion of expectations/tropes' is broader and need not imply rebellion or sabotage.		
295	2948	3106	em dash	/ɛm dæʃ/	noun	The punctuation mark —, longer than an en dash, used to mark a strong break, interruption, parenthetical insertion, or emphatic shift in a sentence.	dash, punctuation mark	I was going to call—but then I changed my mind. / The answer—surprisingly—was yes.	CORRECTION: show the actual em dash (—), not an apostrophe-like placeholder. Style conventions differ on spacing around it.		
295	2949	3107	fall for an illusion / fall for something	/ˈfɔlən fɔr ən ɪˈluʒən/	verb construction	To be deceived or taken in by something false, misleading, or merely apparent. 'Fall for + trick/lie/scam' is the productive idiomatic frame; with a literal magician's illusion, 'fall for' can mean be fooled by it.	be fooled by, be taken in by, believe	I can't believe I fell for that trick. / The investors fell for the illusion of easy money.	Normalize reusable construction: fall for + trick/lie/scam/illusion. Distinguish 'fall for someone' = become romantically attracted.		
295	2950	3108	rev up	/rɛv ʌp/	phrasal verb	To increase speed, intensity, or activity, energy, excitement; originally refers to increasing an engine's speed.	accelerate, intensify, energize, stimulate	The runners revved up their pace during the final lap. The manager's speech revved up the entire sales team. The company is revving up production for the holiday season.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
296	2951	3109	knock it off / knock something off	/nɑk ðæt dɔɡ ɔf hɜr hɛd/ ; /nɑk ɪt ɔf/	phrasal verb; source-specific phrase	'Knock it off!' is an informal command meaning stop doing something annoying or unacceptable. 'Knock X off Y' literally means cause X to fall/be removed from Y by a blow or force.	stop it, cut it out; knock/remove off	Knock it off—you two are making too much noise. / The ball knocked the hat off her head.	CORRECTION: 'knock that dog off her head' is not itself a standard vulgar idiom. Treat it as a literal/source-specific construction if that wording occurred; teach the reusable 'knock it off' and 'knock X off Y' patterns.		
296	2952	3110	glitzy	/ˈɡlɪtsi/	adjective	Bright, flashy, and showy in a way that is intended to attract attention but may lack depth or substance.	flashy, showy, ostentatious	The awards ceremony was full of glitzy outfits and dazzling lights. Despite the glitzy exterior, the restaurant's food was disappointing.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
296	2953	3111	glam	/ɡlæm/	adjective, noun (informal)	Glamorous or stylish, especially in a dazzling or flashy way.	glamorous, stylish, dazzling	She looked totally glam in her shimmering dress. The party had a glam theme, with everyone dressed to impress.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
296	2954	3112	con	/kɑn/	noun; verb	Noun: a fraud, scam, trick, or dishonest scheme; also a disadvantage in 'pros and cons'. Verb: to deceive someone, often to obtain money or cooperation.	scam, swindle, fraud; deceive, trick	He conned investors out of thousands of dollars. / One con of the apartment is the long commute.	KEY FRAME: con PERSON out of MONEY/THING; con PERSON into doing X. Add separate 'pro/con' sense.		
296	2955	3113	long con / long-con	/lɔŋ kɑn/	noun	An elaborate confidence scheme carried out over an extended period, often by building trust before the final deception or payoff.	long-term scam, confidence scheme	The plot revolves around a long con that takes months to set up.	Usually noun 'long con'; hyphenation may appear attributively ('a long-con setup'). Related: con, confidence trick.		
296	2956	3114	top off / top something off	/tɑp ɑf/	phrasal verb	To fill something to the top or add a final amount; figuratively, to complete an experience or sequence with a final addition.	fill up, finish, complete, cap off	Could you top off my coffee? / We topped off the evening with dessert.	KEY FRAMES: top off + container; top X off; top off an event/meal with + final addition. Compare 'cap off', which more strongly suggests a culminating finish.		
296	2957	3115	become a significant thread / a thread running through something	/bɪˈkʌm ə sɪɡˈnɪfɪkənt θrɛd/	productive metaphor	A 'thread' can metaphorically be a recurring theme, idea, relationship, or line of development connecting parts of a larger work/discussion. Something can 'become a significant thread' as it grows in importance.	theme, strand, through-line, recurring element	Her fear of failure becomes a significant thread in the story. / Memory is a thread running through the whole novel.	Not a fixed idiom. Teach productive THREAD metaphor: a thread in/through X; a thread running through X; pick up/follow a thread.		
296	2958	3116	a dash of	/ə dæʃ ʌv/	phrase	A small quantity of something, especially added to improve flavor, interest, or variety.	a little bit, a small amount, a touch of	Add a dash of cinnamon to enhance the flavor. The play included a dash of humor to lighten the serious theme.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
296	2959	3117	tongue-in-cheek / tongue in cheek	/tʌŋ ɪn ʧik/	adjective/adverbial idiom	Not meant to be taken completely seriously; humorously ironic, playful, or mock-serious.	ironic, facetious, playful, wry	It was a tongue-in-cheek apology. / He said, tongue in cheek, that he deserved a statue.	Hyphenate before a noun ('a tongue-in-cheek remark'); usually unhyphenated in adverbial 'with tongue in cheek'. Not necessarily sarcastic.		
296	2960	3118	emotions overflow / overflow with emotion	/ɪˈmoʊʃənz ˈoʊvɚfloʊ/	productive metaphor	For emotions to become so strong that they are expressed freely or seem impossible to contain; 'overflow with + emotion' is a common productive metaphor.	be overwhelmed with emotion, be overcome, well up	She overflowed with joy when she heard the news. / At the reunion, emotions overflowed and several people cried.	Not a fixed idiom. Teach CONTAINER metaphor: overflow with joy/gratitude/love; emotions spill/overflow. 'Emotional flooding' is a more specialized term and not a simple synonym.		
297	2961	3119	irk / irks	/ɜrks/	verb	To annoy or irritate someone.	annoy, irritate, bother, vex	It irks me when people leave the door open. / His constant interruptions irk everyone.	Normalize base + source form. Transitive frame: THING irks PERSON. Related adjective: irksome.		
297	2962	3120	at a stretch	/æt ə strɛʧ/	idiom / phrase	For a continuous period without interruption ('for hours at a stretch'); or, in a different use, only with difficulty or by stretching what is plausible/possible ('at a stretch').	continuously, in one go; at most, with difficulty (contextual)	She can work for four hours at a stretch. / At a stretch, we could fit six people in the car.	Separate the two constructions. For plausibility, 'that's a stretch' is often more natural than 'believe it at a stretch'.		
297	2963	3121	stink eye	/stɪŋk aɪ/	noun (slang)	A dirty or disapproving look given to someone to show anger, distrust, resentment, disapproval, annoyance, or irritation.	dirty look, glare, scowl	She gave him the stink eye after he cut in line. He shot me a stink eye when I brought up the embarrassing story.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
297	2964	3122	skeeters	/ˈskiːtɚz/	noun (slang, plural)	A slang term for mosquitoes, especially in southern or rural American English.	mosquitoes, bugs	The camp was swarming with skeeters by sunset. Don't forget the bug spray ' the skeeters are vicious this time of year. The skeeters were out in full force after the rainstorm.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
297	2965	3123	not do squat / do squat	/doʊnt du skwɑt/	slang expression	In negative contexts, to do nothing or almost nothing; 'squat' here means anything of value/amount and commonly appears after negatives.	do nothing, accomplish nothing	He doesn't do squat around the house. / I studied all afternoon and still didn't get squat done.	KEY PATTERN: don't/doesn't/didn't do squat; know squat = know nothing. Informal and mildly crude.		
297	2966	3124	break one's arm patting oneself on the back	/breɪk wʌnz ɑrm ˈpætɪŋ jɔrˈsɛlf ɑn ðə bæk/	sarcastic idiom	A humorous exaggeration used to mock excessive self-congratulation or boasting.	pat oneself on the back, congratulate oneself, brag	Don't break your arm patting yourself on the back—you had plenty of help.	Normalize pronouns consistently: break your arm patting yourself... / break one's arm patting oneself.... Built on 'pat oneself on the back' = congratulate oneself.		
297	2967	3125	crisscross / criss-cross	/krɪs krɔs/	verb; noun; adjective	To cross back and forth in different directions; a pattern/network of intersecting lines or paths.	cross, intersect, zigzag, weave	Trails crisscross the park. / The fabric has a crisscross pattern.	Standard modern spelling often 'crisscross' in AmE; 'criss-cross' also occurs. Avoid unhyphenated two-word 'criss cross' as the main headword.		
297	2968	3126	jitter / jittering	/ˈdʒɪtərɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle	To shake or move in small rapid irregular movements; noun 'jitter' can mean such variation/movement, especially technically in electronics/networking. 'The jitters' = nervousness.	tremble, shake, fluctuate	The image jittered on the screen. / Too much coffee gives me the jitters.	CORRECTION: 'jittering with nerves' is less natural than 'jittery' or 'have the jitters'. Add technical movement/timing sense; normalize base.		
297	2969	3127	round out vs. cap off	/raʊnd aʊt/ vs /kæp ɔf/	phrasal-verb comparison	'Round out X' = make X fuller, more balanced, or complete by adding something. 'Cap off X' = conclude X, often with a final notable event/addition.	complete, fill out; conclude, crown	A salad rounded out the meal. / Fireworks capped off the festival.	Useful contrast: round out focuses on completeness/balance; cap off focuses on the culminating ending. They overlap but are not interchangeable everywhere.		
297	2970	3128	cap off / cap something off	/kæp ɔf/	phrasal verb	To bring something to a conclusion, often with a final event, achievement, or addition that serves as a culmination.	conclude, crown, finish off, top off	They capped off the trip with a sunset cruise.	Not necessarily 'satisfying' or positive; a final disaster can cap off a terrible day. KEY FRAME: cap off X with Y.		
298	2971	3129	tentative	/ˈtɛntətɪv/	adjective	Not certain or definite, fixed, or confident; done hesitantly or subject to change.	uncertain, provisional, hesitant	We scheduled a tentative meeting for next week. Her tentative smile suggested she was still unsure. They made a tentative plan to meet next week.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
298	2972	3130	apocalypse	/əˈpɑkəlɪps/	noun	A catastrophic event resulting in widespread destruction or devastation; also refers to the end of the world or final judgment in religious contexts.	catastrophe, end of the world, disaster, doomsday	The movie depicted a zombie apocalypse wiping out humanity. Some ancient texts describe an apocalypse as a final reckoning between good and evil. The movie depicts an apocalypse brought on by climate change.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
