298	2974	3132	carry that weight / carry the weight	/ˈkæri ðæt weɪt/	phrase; source/reference expression	To bear an emotional burden, responsibility, consequence, or pressure. 'Carry That Weight' is also the title/lyric phrase of a Beatles song, which can add an allusive layer.	bear the burden, shoulder the responsibility, live with the weight	He carried the weight of that decision for years. / The phrase 'carry that weight' can evoke the Beatles song in the right context.	Teach productive metaphor 'carry/bear the weight of X'. 'That' is source/context-specific rather than required.		
298	2975	3133	pop the balloon / burst someone's bubble	/ˈpɑpɪŋ ðə bəˈluːn/	literal/productive metaphor; idiom comparison	'Pop a/the balloon' literally means burst it. Figuratively it can be used creatively for abruptly ending excitement or momentum, but the established idiom for disappointing someone by destroying an optimistic belief/hope is 'burst someone's bubble'.	burst someone's bubble, deflate, dash hopes	I hated to burst her bubble, but the tickets were already sold out. / His comment popped the balloon of excitement—a creative metaphor.	CORRECTION: do not teach 'popping the balloon' as a standard idiom equivalent to 'burst someone's bubble'. Preserve as creative/source wording.		
298	2976	3134	steal away	/stiːl əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To leave secretly or quietly, often to avoid attention.	sneak away/out, slip away, sneak off, leave quietly	They decided to steal away during the wedding reception for a private moment. The child tried to steal away without saying goodbye. He managed to steal away during the commotion without anyone noticing.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
298	2977	3135	expulsion	/ɪkˈspʌlʃən/	noun	The act of forcing or officially requiring a person/group to leave an institution, organization, place, or country; also the act of forcing a substance/object out.	ejection, removal, dismissal, deportation (contextual)	His expulsion from school shocked everyone. / The test measures expulsion of the balloon.	Avoid 'international expulsion' without a specific organization/context. Add physical expulsion sense, useful with the earlier balloon-expulsion card.		
298	2978	3137	lean into	/lin ˈɪntu/	phrasal verb / figurative construction	Literally, incline or press one's body into/toward something. Figuratively, embrace, emphasize, or engage fully with a situation, quality, role, or difficulty.	embrace, commit to, play up, engage with	The actor leaned into the character's awkwardness. / She leaned into the challenge.	Overlaps WID3026 comparison card; retain because this card gives focused active-use practice. KEY FRAME: lean into + noun/gerund.		
298	2979	3138	surefire / a surefire sale	/ˈʃʊrˌfaɪr seɪl/	adjective; compositional phrase	'Surefire' means very likely or certain to succeed or produce a result. 'A surefire sale' is a compositional use meaning a sale/deal considered highly likely, not a fixed expression.	certain, reliable, near-certain, foolproof (contextual)	A big discount is a surefire way to attract attention. / The agent thought the house would be a surefire sale.	Normalize around productive adjective 'surefire': surefire way/method/hit/success. 'Infallible' is too absolute in many contexts.		
298	2980	3139	thee	/ðiː/	pronoun (archaic/poetic)	Archaic second-person singular object form of 'thou', used as the object of a verb or preposition; survives in older literature, hymns, prayers, and stylized speech.	you (object form, archaic)	I give this gift unto thee. / How do I love thee?	PRECISION: 'thee' is specifically objective case; 'thou' is traditionally subject, 'thy/thine' possessive forms. Modern 'you' replaced these distinctions.		
