299	2982	3141	tell someone off	/tɛl ˈsʌmwʌn ɔf/	phrasal verb (informal)	To speak angrily or severely to someone because you think they have done something wrong.	scold, reprimand, rebuke, give someone a dressing-down	She told him off for being rude.	KEY FRAME: tell PERSON off for + noun/gerund. 'Lash out' is broader and need not involve corrective scolding.		
299	2983	3142	you can say that again	/ju kən seɪ ðæt əˈɡɛn/	idiom (phrase)	An informal way to express strong agreement with what someone has just said.	absolutely, totally agree, no kidding	"A: ""This traffic is terrible!"" B: ""You can say that again!"" A: ""That movie was amazing."" B: ""You can say that again."""	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
299	2984	3143	dead lie / outright lie	/dɛd laɪ/	source/nonstandard phrase; noun phrase	'Dead lie' is not a broadly standard modern idiom for 'blatant lie'. In some source/dialectal wording it may intensify 'lie'; for general active use, 'outright lie', 'blatant lie', 'barefaced/bald-faced lie', or 'flat-out lie' are natural.	outright lie, blatant lie, bald-faced lie, flat-out lie	That was an outright lie, and everyone knew it.	CORRECTION: retain 'dead lie' for recognition/source fidelity only; do not teach it as a normal general-purpose idiom without source/dialect context. Clean corrupted punctuation.		
299	2985	3144	draw a line in the sand / a line in the sand	/laɪn ɪn ðə sænd/	idiom	To establish a firm boundary, limit, or position beyond which one will not compromise or permit something.	set a boundary, draw the line, set a limit	She drew a line in the sand: no more unpaid overtime.	Teach full productive construction 'draw a line in the sand'. The noun phrase alone names the boundary.		
299	2986	3145	snowclone	/ˈsnoʊˌkloʊn/	noun (linguistics/internet culture)	A recognizable phrase template with one or more variable slots, reused to create new expressions, often from a familiar quotation or cliché.	phrasal template, formulaic template	'X is the new Y' is a classic snowclone: 'Orange is the new black,' 'Quiet is the new loud,' etc.	CORRECTION/CLEANUP: repair mojibake and corrupted example. A snowclone is not simply any cliché; its reusable variable template is central.		
299	2987	3146	eat crow	/it kroʊ/	idiom (phrase)	To admit one's mistake or defeat, especially in a way that is humiliating or humbling.	admit fault, swallow pride, confess defeat	After losing the bet, he had to eat crow and apologize. The CEO ate crow after the product failed miserably.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
299	2988	3148	say that / I would say that / to say that...	/seɪ ðæt/	verb construction / discourse patterns	Bare 'say that + clause' reports or states a proposition. 'I would say that...' softens/presents an opinion; 'to say that X is Y would be...' is a separate evaluative construction.	state, remark, argue; I think (contextual)	I would say that the second version is clearer. / To say that she was surprised would be an understatement.	CORRECTION: 'say that' alone is too broad to have one discourse function. Preserve the useful source patterns explicitly rather than treating them as one idiom.		
299	2989	3149	sobering	/ˈsoʊbərɪŋ/	adjective	Causing a person to become more serious, reflective, or realistic; reducing feelings of excitement or idealism.	serious, unsettling, thought-provoking	The sobering news of the disaster made everyone quiet. Hearing about the risks was a sobering experience.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
299	2990	3151	that said	/ðæt sɛd/	phrase (idiom)	Used to introduce a contrasting point or qualification to what was just said; similar to 'however' or 'nevertheless.'	nevertheless, however, even so	The car is very old. That said, it still runs perfectly. The hike was exhausting. That said, the view at the top was worth it.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
300	2991	3152	wear out	/wɛr aʊt/	phrasal verb	To use something until it becomes damaged, unusable, or exhausted. To tire someone completely.	exhaust, fray, deteriorate, fatigue	After years of hiking, his boots finally wore out. Long meetings wear me out mentally.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
300	2992	3153	that being so / with that said / having said that	/ðæt ˈbiɪŋ soʊ/ , /wɪð ðæt sɛd/ , /ˈhævɪŋ sɛd ðæt/	discourse markers	'That being so' means given that this is true and often introduces a consequence. 'Having said that' and 'that said' introduce a qualification or contrast. 'With that said' is a common variant, especially AmE, with similar qualifying/transition use.	given that; that said, nevertheless, even so	The road is closed. That being so, we'll take the train. / The plan is expensive. Having said that, it may still be our best option.	CORRECTION: these are not interchangeable transition markers. Separate consequence ('that being so') from qualification/contrast ('having/with that said').		
