300	2999	3162	fall back asleep	/ˈfɔlˌbæk əˈsliːp/	verb phrase	To return to sleep after having awakened.	go back to sleep, drift back to sleep, doze off again	I woke up at six but fell back asleep and missed my alarm.	CORRECTION: three words 'fall back asleep', not 'fallback asleep'. 'Fallback' is a noun/adjective in other contexts.		
300	3000	3163	a second nap / take another nap	/ˈsɛkənd næp/	noun phrase / productive phrase	A second period of daytime sleep or dozing; ordinary compositional wording rather than a fixed expression.	another nap, an extra nap, nap again	I took a second nap after lunch. / I was still tired, so I took another nap.	Not an idiom. 'Second nap' means literally another nap; after briefly waking during nighttime sleep, 'fall back asleep' is usually more natural.		
301	3001	3164	get a bit more sleep	/ɡɛt ə bɪt mɔr sliːp/	productive phrase	To obtain some additional sleep, usually because one is tired or has time to sleep longer.	get some more sleep, catch a little more sleep, sleep a little longer	I'm going back to bed to get a bit more sleep.	Does not inherently mean recovery from exhaustion. Natural related phrases: get some sleep; catch a few more hours/winks.		
