301	3006	3171	paradigm	/ˈpærəˌdaɪm/	noun	A model, pattern, or framework; especially a broad conceptual framework or accepted way of understanding/approaching a field or problem.	framework, model, pattern, worldview (contextual)	The discovery helped create a new paradigm for understanding the disease.	Avoid using 'prototype' as a blanket synonym. Common: paradigm shift; within a paradigm; a paradigm for/of X.		
301	3007	3172	archetype	/ˈɑrkɪˌtaɪp/	noun	A recurring, prototypical model or pattern of a character, image, situation, or concept; also an original model/type from which similar examples are understood.	prototype, type, recurring model, embodiment (contextual)	The wise mentor is a familiar story archetype.	'Perfect example' is not always central; in literary/psychological discussion, recurring fundamental pattern/type is important.		
301	3008	3173	paragon of something	/ˈpærəˌɡɑn əv ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	noun construction	A person or thing regarded as an outstanding or near-perfect example of a particular quality.	model, exemplar, epitome	She was regarded as a paragon of patience.	KEY FRAME: a paragon of + virtue/quality. Often elevated/formal and can be ironic.		
301	3009	3175	ode to	/oʊd tu/	noun construction / figurative phrase	An ode is traditionally a lyric poem of praise or elevated reflection. Figuratively, a film, album, design, etc. can be 'an ode to X' when it lovingly celebrates, evokes, or pays tribute to X.	tribute, homage, celebration	The film is an ode to 1980s action movies.	KEY FRAME: an ode to + person/place/era/style. 'Eulogy' is not a synonym; it specifically praises/commemorates, often after death.		
301	3010	3176	boilerplate	/ˈbɔɪlɚˌpleɪt/	noun; adjective	Standardized, reusable text or language used with little change, especially in contracts, policies, press releases, or software/templates; adjective: boilerplate language/code.	standard text, stock language, template text	The contract contains standard boilerplate on liability. / I replaced the boilerplate introduction with something specific.	Can be neutral or mildly critical. In programming, 'boilerplate code' is repetitive setup code, not necessarily copied blindly.		
