308	3075	3245	nestle	/ˈnɛsəl/	verb	To settle, place, or lie snugly and comfortably in/against something; also for a place/object to sit sheltered or closely surrounded.	snuggle, settle, tuck, lie snugly	She nestled into the couch. / The village nestles in a valley between two mountains.	Add important non-affectionate spatial use. Frames: nestle into/against/between; be nestled in/among.		
308	3076	3246	concoction	/kənˈkɑkʃən/	noun	A mixture made by combining ingredients, often unusual or improvised; figuratively, an invented story, explanation, plan, or fabrication.	mixture, brew, blend; fabrication, invention	He made a strange concoction of fruit and spices. / The alibi sounded like an elaborate concoction.	Literal mixture and figurative invented-story/scheme senses are both useful. Tone can be playful, skeptical, or neutral.		
308	3077	3248	express permission	/ɪkˈsprɛs pɚˈmɪʃən/	noun phrase (formal/legal)	Permission that is explicitly stated or communicated, rather than merely implied by circumstances or conduct.	explicit permission, explicit consent, stated authorization	You may not reproduce the material without express permission from the copyright holder.	LEGAL/FORMAL contrast: express permission/consent vs implied permission/consent. 'Express' here means explicitly stated, not fast.		
308	3078	3250	frolic / frolicking	/ˈfrɑlɪkɪŋ/	verb; noun; participle	To play, move, or behave in a lively, carefree, playful way; noun 'a frolic' can mean playful activity or, in some formal/legal contexts, a personal deviation from assigned duties.	romp, cavort, play about	The dogs were frolicking in the snow. / The children frolicked along the beach.	Normalize base + source form. Everyday sense is playful movement; 'cavort' is close but can sound more literary.		
