310	3098	3275	hearsay	/ˈhɪrˌseɪ/	noun; legal term	Ordinarily, information heard from another person rather than known directly. In law, 'hearsay' has a specific rule-based definition concerning out-of-court statements offered to prove the truth of what they assert, subject to exclusions/exceptions depending jurisdiction.	secondhand information, rumor (everyday); hearsay evidence (legal)	That's just hearsay—I didn't hear it from her directly. / The judge considered whether the statement was inadmissible hearsay.	CORRECTION: legal hearsay is not simply 'unsubstantiated information,' and it is not always inadmissible. Keep everyday and legal senses distinct.		
310	3099	3277	rizz / rizz up	/rɪz/	noun; verb phrase (slang)	Slang for romantic charm or ability to attract/flirt successfully; 'rizz up + person' means charm or flirt with them successfully. 'Unspoken rizz' refers to charisma conveyed without much talking.	game, charm, charisma	He's got rizz. / She rizzed him up in about five minutes. / People joked about his 'unspoken rizz.'	Modern internet/youth slang. Often linked to 'charisma' as a clipping/derivation in popular accounts. Tone can be playful/ironic; not limited to smooth talking.		
