311	3103	3281	put away	/pʊt əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To return/store something in its place; eat/drink a surprisingly large amount; imprison; defeat/finish off; euphemistically kill/euthanize in some contexts; save money can appear in 'put money away'.	store, eat/drink, imprison, defeat; save (money)	Put the dishes away. / He can put away a whole pizza. / The criminal was put away for years. / She puts a little money away each month.	Polysemy hub. Meaning is strongly object/context dependent. 'Put an animal down' is much more common than 'put away' for euthanasia.		
311	3104	3282	schema	/ˈskiːmə/	noun	A structured framework or representation. In cognitive psychology, an organized mental framework for knowledge/expectations; in databases/computing, a formal structure describing how data is organized and related.	framework, structure, model	A restaurant schema helps us predict what normally happens when dining out. / The database schema defines tables, fields, and relationships.	Plural can be 'schemas' (common) or 'schemata' (technical/traditional). Piaget-related assimilation/accommodation is one theoretical context, not the definition of schema itself.		
