311	3106	3284	out of context / take something out of context	/aʊt əv ˈkɑnˌtɛkst/	phrase / idiom	Without enough surrounding information to understand something properly; 'take/quote X out of context' means present it separated from context, sometimes causing a misleading impression.	without context, decontextualized; misrepresent (when misleading)	That sentence sounds terrible out of context. / The clip was taken out of context.	CORRECTION: being out of context does not automatically mean distortion or intentional misrepresentation. Teach productive take/quote X out of context.		
311	3107	3285	L&D (Learning and Development)	/ˌɛl ənd ˈdiː/	initialism / business-HR term	Learning and Development: the organizational function/practice concerned with employee learning, skills, capability development, training, and professional growth.	learning and development, employee development, workplace learning, training	She works in L&D and designs leadership-training programs.	Normalize capitalization. L&D may refer to a function/team/field, not merely a single 'initiative'.		
311	3108	3286	obliquely	/əˈbliːkli/	adverb	Indirectly or not straightforwardly in expression; also at a slanting/diagonal angle in literal or technical contexts.	indirectly, allusively, elliptically; diagonally	She referred obliquely to the disagreement without naming anyone. / The line crosses the axis obliquely.	Keep figurative and literal senses. 'Evasively' implies avoidance and is not always appropriate.		
311	3109	3287	there goes the shirt	/ðɛr ɡoʊz ðə ʃɝt/	source-specific / productive construction	'There goes + noun' is an ordinary productive construction used to observe that something is going, disappearing, being lost, or predictably happening. 'There goes the shirt' can therefore work literally as a playful/resigned meta-comment when a character predictably removes or loses his shirt; in Galaxy Quest, the line jokes about the heroic captain ending up shirtless again.	there goes + noun; there it goes; there goes X again	There goes the shirt—right on cue. / There goes our chance of catching the train. / There goes the neighborhood.	SOURCE/CONSTRUCTION NOTE: Keep this as source-specific humor, not a general fixed idiom meaning 'lose money,' 'lose dignity,' or 'get angry.' The reusable pattern is THERE GOES + NOUN, often used to comment wryly, playfully, or resignedly when an expected event happens or something is lost/gone. In Galaxy Quest, 'And there goes the shirt...' functions as a literal/meta running-gag observation about the heroic captain taking off/losing his shirt, parodying the familiar shirtless sci-fi-hero trope. Do not confuse it with separate idioms 'lose your shirt' = lose a great deal of money and 'keep your shirt on' = calm down / be patient.		
311	3110	3290	shoot up / shot up	/ʃɑt ʌp/	phrasal verb; past form	To rise/increase very rapidly; grow taller quickly; attack a place/person with gunfire ('shoot up'); or inject a drug, especially illicitly (informal). 'Shot up' is the past form in all these senses.	soar, spike, surge; grow rapidly; fire on; inject	Prices shot up overnight. / David has really shot up since last year. / The building was shot up. / The character was shown shooting up heroin.	Normalize base + source past. Distinguish intransitive increase/growth from transitive gunfire and drug-use senses. Register/safety context varies.		
312	3111	3291	cumulative	/ˈkjuːmjələtɪv/	adjective	Increasing, resulting, or calculated through successive additions over time; representing the total accumulated amount/effect up to a point.	accumulated, aggregate, total-to-date, compounding (contextual)	The cumulative effect of several small delays was significant. / Her cumulative GPA includes all completed terms.	Remove 'collective' as loose synonym and stray comma. 'Cumulative' does not necessarily mean continuously increasing; it concerns accumulation.		
312	3112	3292	ellipsis	/ɪˈlɪpsɪs/	noun	The punctuation mark '...' used to indicate omitted material or, in informal/literary writing, a pause/trailing off; in grammar/rhetoric, omission of words that are understood from context.	omission; three-dot mark	'I'm not sure...' she whispered. / In 'I ordered coffee and she tea,' the omitted 'ordered' is an example of ellipsis.	CORRECTION/CLEANUP: repair corrupted ellipsis character/example. Distinguish punctuation ellipsis from grammatical ellipsis.		
312	3113	3293	not bat an eye / bat an eye	/bæts æn aɪ/	idiom	To show a visible reaction, especially surprise, concern, or hesitation; overwhelmingly common in the negative 'not bat an eye' = show no reaction.	not flinch, remain unfazed; react	She didn't bat an eye when the price doubled.	Normalize around the common negative construction. Literally 'bat' here = blink/move the eyelid, not hit.		
312	3114	3294	jump the shark	/dʒʌmp ðə ʃɑrk/	idiom / pop-culture expression	For a TV series, franchise, creator, product, etc. to make a conspicuous move seen as gimmicky, implausible, or desperate, often taken as a sign that its creative peak has passed.	go downhill, become gimmicky, show creative desperation	Some fans said the series jumped the shark when it introduced the bizarre twist.	Origin: Happy Days scene in which Fonzie jumps over a shark on water skis. It refers to a perceived turning point/sign of decline, not simply 'the point quality begins declining' in an objective sense.		
312	3115	3295	burn that bridge when we get to it	/bɝn ðæt brɪdʒ wɛn wi ɡɛt tu ɪt/	jocular altered idiom	A humorous blend/alteration of 'cross that bridge when we get to it' and 'burn one's bridges', joking that one will deal with a future problem by making it worse or destroying options.	self-sabotage later (jocular), darkly humorous variant	We haven't even decided whether to move yet—let's burn that bridge when we get to it.	Explain the wordplay: cross that bridge = handle problem later; burn one's bridges = destroy routes/options. The humor comes from combining them.		
312	3116	3296	one and the same	/wʌn ənd ðə seɪm/	phrase	Identical; referring to the exact same person or thing.	identical, the very same, indistinguishable	The CEO and the anonymous donor turned out to be one and the same.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
312	3117	3297	by the seat of one's pants / fly by the seat of one's pants	/baɪ ðə siːt əv ðə pænts/	idiom	To do something mainly by instinct, feel, experience, or improvisation rather than a detailed plan, data, or formal guidance.	wing it, improvise, go by feel	We didn't have time for a full plan, so we ran the event by the seat of our pants. / The pilot was said to fly by the seat of his pants.	Normalize possessive. Aviation-origin story is commonly associated with flying by bodily feel before/relying less on instruments; avoid overclaiming a single precise origin.		
312	3118	3298	eggcorn	/ˈɛɡkɔrn/	noun (linguistics)	A nonstandard reinterpretation of a word/phrase based on a similar sound that produces a semantically plausible expression, e.g. 'for all intensive purposes' for 'for all intents and purposes'.	plausible mishearing/reanalysis	'Old-timers' disease' for 'Alzheimer's disease' is often cited as an eggcorn-like reanalysis.	KEEP. Distinguish from malapropism: an eggcorn is typically a plausible reanalysis of a fixed expression, not merely substitution of a similar-sounding word.		
312	3119	3299	malapropism	/ˈmæləˌprɒpɪzəm/	noun	The mistaken use of a word in place of a similar-sounding one, often producing an absurd or humorous result.	word substitution, verbal blunder	'He's the pineapple of politeness' for 'pinnacle of politeness' is a classic malapropism.	KEEP. Named after Mrs. Malaprop in Sheridan's The Rivals. Contrast eggcorn: malapropism need not create a semantically plausible reinterpretation.		
312	3120	3300	guise / under the guise of	/ɡaɪz/	noun / construction	An outward appearance, form, or manner of presentation. In the common construction 'under/in the guise of X', something is presented as X, often while its true nature or purpose is different.	appearance, form, pretense, disguise (contextual)	He entered under the guise of a consultant. / The policy was criticized as censorship in the guise of safety.	KEY FRAME: under the guise of + noun/gerund. Deception is common in this construction but 'guise' itself can simply mean outward form/appearance.		
313	3121	3301	raunchy	/ˈrɔːntʃi/	adjective	Vulgar, sexually explicit, or crude in a way that's intended to be humorous or provocative.	lewd, obscene, bawdy, vulgar	The comedian's raunchy jokes weren't suitable for a family audience.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
313	3122	3302	slovenly	/ˈslʌvənli/	adjective	Messy, untidy, or careless in appearance, habits, or work.	unkempt, disheveled, sloppy	His slovenly handwriting made the report hard to read.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
313	3123	3303	grubby	/ˈɡrʌbi/	adjective	Dirty or grimy, especially in a way that's unpleasant or unclean.	filthy, grimy, soiled	The kids came back from the park looking grubby and exhausted.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
313	3124	3304	slay / it slayed	/ɪt sleɪd/	verb; slang use	In modern slang, 'slay' means perform exceptionally well, look especially impressive, or succeed brilliantly; 'it slayed' means a performance/look/work was extremely effective or impressive.	kill it, crush it, nail it, be amazing	Her performance absolutely slayed. / That outfit slays. / The finale slayed.	Normalize reusable base + source form. Traditional 'slay' = kill; modern praise use is informal and can be playful/hyperbolic.		
313	3125	3305	beg the question / begging the question	/ˈbɛɡɪŋ ðə ˈkwɛstʃən/	logical fallacy; phrase	In its traditional logical sense, to assume in the premises what the argument is supposed to prove—circularly presupposing the conclusion. In everyday modern use, 'begs the question' is also widely used to mean 'raises/prompts the question', though this is distinct from the logical sense.	reason circularly, assume the conclusion; raise the question (modern separate use)	Saying 'This policy is best because it's superior to all alternatives' begs the question if 'superior' is never independently established. / The delay raises the question of whether the deadline is realistic.	Important distinction: logical 'beg the question' ≠ simply raise a question. However, the 'raises the question' use is widespread in contemporary English, so recognize it while using 'raises the question' when precision matters.		
313	3126	3306	modus operandi	/ˈmoʊdəs ˌɑːpəˈrændi/	noun	A particular way or method of doing something, especially one that is characteristic or habitual (typical or well-established)	method, approach, system, procedure	The thief's modus operandi involved disabling security cameras before breaking in. His modus operandi is to tackle the hardest task first thing in the morning.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
313	3127	3307	intent vs. intention	/ɪnˈtɛnt/ vs /ɪnˈtɛnʃən/	noun comparison	Both mean a purpose, aim, or plan. 'Intent' often sounds more formal, focused, or legally significant and commonly appears in 'intent to do X / intent on doing X'. 'Intention' is common in everyday language for plans or purposes and in 'intention to do X / intention of doing X'.	purpose, aim, plan	His intent was to deceive the buyer. / I had every intention of calling you back. / She is intent on finishing tonight.	Avoid an overly rigid formal-vs-everyday split: the words overlap substantially. Also distinguish noun 'intent' from adjective 'intent on' = determined/focused.		
313	3128	3308	cut short	/kʌt ʃɔrt/	phrasal verb	To end or interrupt something before it was expected to finish.	interrupt, halt, stop early	The meeting was cut short due to a fire alarm.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
313	3129	3309	faux slip-up / staged slip-up	/foʊ ˈslɪp ʌp/	source/creative phrase	A 'slip-up' is an unintentional mistake. 'Faux slip-up' is understandable creative wording for an apparently accidental mistake that is actually staged or deliberate, but it is not a standard fixed idiom. It should not be used to mean an ordinary social faux pas.	staged mistake, deliberate 'accident', planted gaffe	The supposedly accidental remark looked more like a staged slip-up for publicity.	CORRECTION: 'faux' = false/artificial, so 'faux slip-up' naturally suggests a fake/staged mistake. Do not merge this with 'faux pas', which means a social blunder. For active use, 'staged slip-up' or 'deliberate gaffe' is clearer.		
313	3130	3310	be looking to do something	/bi ˈlʊkɪŋ tu du ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	verb construction (informal/business)	To be planning, hoping, seeking, or intending to do something, often in the near or foreseeable future.	plan to, aim to, intend to, hope to	We're looking to hire two more people this fall. / I'm looking to replace my laptop soon.	KEY FRAME: be looking to + verb. Depending context it can sound less definite than 'plan to' and may express a goal rather than a fixed plan.		
314	3131	3311	take a crack at it	/teɪk ə kræk æt ɪt/	idiom	To attempt or try something, especially something difficult or new.	give it a shot, try, attempt	I've never fixed a leaky faucet before, but I'll take a crack at it.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
314	3132	3312	listen for	/ˈlɪsən fɔr ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	verb construction	To listen attentively in order to detect or notice a particular sound, word, signal, or other audible event.	keep an ear out for, listen out for (esp. BrE), be alert for	Listen for your name to be called. / I lay awake listening for the sound of the door.	CORRECTION: not a phrasal verb in the narrow sense; productive verb + preposition construction. Contrast listen to X = direct attention to X already audible; listen for X = try to detect X.		
314	3133	3313	mainstay	/ˈmeɪnˌsteɪ/	noun	A person or thing that acts as a chief support or part of something.	pillar, backbone, foundation	Farming is the mainstay of the local economy.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
314	3134	3314	stagehand	/ˈsteɪdʒˌhænd/	noun	A person who works backstage or behind the scenes in a theater to move scenery, props, or handle technical aspects.	crew member, backstage worker	The stagehands quickly changed the scenery between acts.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
314	3135	3316	barge / barge into	/bɑrdʒ/	noun; verb	Noun: a broad, often flat-bottomed boat used especially for transporting goods. Verb: move clumsily or forcefully; 'barge into + place/conversation' = enter or interrupt abruptly and intrusively.	boat; push in, burst in, intrude	A barge carried cargo down the river. / He barged into the room without knocking. / Sorry to barge into your conversation.	Teach productive 'barge into + room/conversation' and 'barge past/through'. The verb does not literally mean 'shove' in every use.		
314	3136	3317	-monger / monger	/ˈmʌŋɡɚ/	combining form; noun	Historically, a dealer or trader in a specified commodity; in modern compounds, often a person who promotes, spreads, or traffics in something, frequently something viewed negatively: warmonger, fearmonger, scaremonger, rumormonger.	dealer, trader; promoter/spreader (compound-dependent)	Critics accused him of fearmongering. / 'Warmonger' and 'fishmonger' show the two major patterns.	Better learned as a productive compound element. Not all -monger words are negative: 'fishmonger' is simply a seller of fish. Hyphenation varies by compound; fearmonger is commonly one word.		
