314	3139	3320	hummina / hummina hummina	/ˈhʌmɪnə/	interjection / comic vocalization	A comic, exaggerated vocalization representing being flustered, speechless, excited, or especially overwhelmed by someone's attractiveness.	uh...; wow; hubba hubba (related comic reaction)	Hummina hummina... I completely forgot what I was going to say.	Cartoon/comedy register; deliberately inarticulate rather than a normal lexical interjection. Clean mojibake. 'Shwing' is a separate pop-culture slang expression, not a clean synonym.		
314	3140	3321	fallback	/ˈfɔlˌbæk/	noun; adjective	Something or someone relied on when the preferred option fails or is unavailable.	backup, alternative, substitute	We planned a picnic, but our fallback is the museum in case it rains.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
315	3141	3322	cilantro	/sɪˈlɑnˌtroʊ/	noun	The leaves of the coriander plant, used as an herb in cooking, especially in Mexican and Asian cuisines.	coriander leaves (British English)	I love the fresh flavor cilantro adds to salsa and tacos.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
315	3142	3324	compound a problem / compounding issues	/ˈkɑmˌpaʊnd ˈɪʃuz/	verb construction; participial phrase	Verb 'compound' means make a problem, difficulty, or situation worse by adding another factor. 'Compounding issues/problems' are problems that add to or intensify one another; 'compound issues' can also simply mean complex/multi-part issues depending context.	worsen, aggravate, exacerbate; mutually reinforcing problems	A staffing shortage compounded the delays. / The team faced several compounding problems at once.	CORRECTION: prioritize productive verb frame COMPOUND + problem/difficulty/error. 'Compound issues' is ambiguous and not a fixed term meaning interacting problems.		
