315	3144	3326	affliction	/əˈflɪkʃən/	noun	A condition of pain, suffering, or distress caused by illness or misfortune.	ailment, hardship, disorder	Chronic back pain is a common affliction among office workers.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
315	3145	3327	vouch for	/vaʊtʃ fɔr/	verb construction	To state from personal knowledge or confidence that a person or thing is trustworthy, genuine, reliable, good, or true.	attest to, endorse, stand behind, guarantee (contextual)	I can vouch for her reliability. / I haven't tried that restaurant, so I can't vouch for it.	KEY FRAME: vouch for + person/thing. Usually implies personal basis/confidence; it is not always a formal guarantee.		
315	3146	3328	reminiscence	/ˌrɛmɪˈnɪsəns/	noun	The act/process of recalling past experiences, or a remembered story/recollection from the past, often but not necessarily nostalgic.	recollection, memory, recollection of the past	He published reminiscences of his years abroad. / The photo prompted a reminiscence about their first apartment.	Distinguish 'reminiscence' (a recollection or act of recalling) from 'nostalgia' (a feeling of longing/sentiment for the past).		
315	3147	3329	exemplify	/ɪɡˈzɛmplɪˌfaɪ/	verb	To serve as a typical or excellent example of something.	illustrate, demonstrate, represent	His calm response exemplified true leadership under pressure.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
315	3148	3330	unsheathe	/ʌnˈʃiːð/	verb	To draw or remove a blade or other object from a sheath; figuratively, can be used creatively for bringing out something sharp/aggressive, but literal weapon/tool use is core.	draw, remove from its sheath	The knight unsheathed his sword. / She unsheathed the knife carefully.	Productive pair: sheath / unsheathe. Do not overstate metaphorical use; 'reveal/expose' is only contextual.		
315	3149	3331	throwback / a throwback to	/ˈθroʊˌbæk/	noun	A person, thing, style, or feature resembling or evoking an earlier time or type; also, in biology, a reversion-like resemblance to an ancestral type (less common everyday use).	retro reminder, relic, callback (contextual), atavistic feature (technical/contextual)	Her jacket is a throwback to the 1990s. / The diner feels like a throwback to another era.	KEY FRAME: a throwback to + era/style. 'Nostalgia' is a feeling, not a direct synonym. Social-media 'Throwback Thursday' is a related usage.		
315	3150	3332	atavism	/ˈætəˌvɪzəm/	noun	The reappearance of an ancestral characteristic after it has been absent for generations; more broadly, a reversion to an older or more primitive-seeming trait, practice, or style.	reversion, ancestral throwback; throwback (figurative)	The trait was described as an atavism. / Critics called the policy an atavistic throwback to an earlier era.	Technical biological sense + broader figurative use. Avoid treating 'regression' as an exact biological synonym.		
316	3151	3333	revert / revert to	/rɪˈvɝt/	verb	To return to a previous state, condition, practice, version, or subject. In technical workflows, 'revert a change/commit' can also mean undo it, though standard general-English syntax is usually 'revert to X'.	return, go back, roll back (technical/contextual), undo (technical/contextual)	After a week, I reverted to my old routine. / We reverted to the previous version. / The developer reverted the faulty commit.	KEY FRAME: revert to + state/version. Note Indian/South Asian business English 'revert' = reply/respond is regionally common but not the usual meaning in broader international English.		
