316	3153	3336	bail / bail on / bail out	/beɪl/	noun; verb; phrasal constructions	Noun: money/security used to secure temporary release from custody pending proceedings. Informal verb 'bail' = leave abruptly; 'bail on + person/plan' = abandon/cancel; 'bail out' = leave an aircraft/boat, rescue financially, or get someone out of difficulty.	leave, ditch, back out; bond/security (legal)	I bailed after the second episode. / He bailed on our plans. / The judge set bail at $5,000. / The government bailed the company out.	Polysemy hub. Do not equate bail with 'jail release money' only; legal details vary by jurisdiction. Distinguish bail on vs bail out.		
316	3154	3337	guilt-free	/ˌɡɪltˈfriː/	adjective; adverbial use	Without causing or involving guilt; often used to frame a choice as something one need not feel guilty about. Common in marketing and casual lifestyle language.	without guilt, guiltlessly (less common), without feeling bad	I dropped the show guilt-free after four episodes. / The ad promises 'guilt-free' snacking.	CORRECTION: 'innocent' and 'blameless' are not direct synonyms. Marketing uses can imply health/morality claims rather than literally describing nutrition.		
316	3155	3338	phone in / phoned in	/foʊnd ɪn/	phrasal verb; participial adjective	To do or deliver something with little effort, enthusiasm, or engagement, as though merely going through the motions; 'phoned-in' before a noun is the adjective form.	go through the motions, half-ass (vulgar), give minimal effort	The actor phoned in the performance. / It felt like a phoned-in sequel.	Normalize verb + adjective forms. Origin is associated with doing something remotely/by telephone rather than showing up, later extended to low-effort performance; avoid claiming a single precise origin without source.		
316	3156	3339	swing by	/swɪŋ baɪ/	phrasal verb	To briefly stop at a place, often casually or without a long stay.	drop by, pop in, stop over	I'll swing by your place after work to drop off the book.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
316	3157	3341	point-blank / point blank	/pɔɪnt blæŋk/	adjective; adverb	Literally, at very close range, especially of a shot. Figuratively/adverbially, directly, bluntly, and without evasion: ask/tell/refuse point blank.	directly, bluntly, flatly, straight out	She asked him point blank whether he had lied. / The shot was fired at point-blank range.	Hyphenate attributively: point-blank range/refusal; often unhyphenated adverbially: asked point blank. Literal firearms sense is about very close range, not one exact universal distance.		
316	3158	3342	paranoid / being paranoid	/ˈbiɪŋ ˈpærəˌnɔɪd/	adjective; colloquial phrase	In everyday speech, excessively or unreasonably suspicious, fearful, or worried that something bad is happening. In clinical contexts, 'paranoid' has more specific mental-health meanings and should not be inferred casually.	overly suspicious, mistrustful, fearful; worried (contextual)	I'm probably being paranoid, but did I lock the door? / He became increasingly suspicious of everyone's motives.	For everyday learner use, note that people often say 'I'm being paranoid' hyperbolically. Do not use the casual card to diagnose a person or equate ordinary overthinking with a clinical condition.		
316	3159	3343	performative / performative behavior	/pɚˈfɔrmətɪv bɪˈheɪvjɚ/	adjective; noun phrase	In common contemporary social commentary, 'performative' describes actions presented publicly in a way seen as more concerned with appearance, signaling, or audience reaction than substantive commitment. In linguistics/philosophy, 'performative' also has technical senses unrelated to insincerity.	for-show, symbolic, image-focused (contextual)	The company was accused of making a performative gesture without changing its practices.	PRECISION: 'performative' does not objectively prove insincerity; it often expresses a critic's interpretation. 'Virtue signaling' is a more loaded related term, not a neutral synonym. Separate from technical 'performative utterance'.		
316	3160	3344	exasperated	/ɪɡˈzæspəˌreɪtɪd/	adjective	Intensely irritated and frustrated.	frustrated, annoyed, aggravated	She gave an exasperated sigh and walked away.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
317	3161	3345	affricate	/ˈæfrɪkət/	noun (phonetics)	A consonant produced with a complete closure followed by a fricative-like release at the same or a nearby place of articulation, functioning as a single consonantal unit; English /tʃ/ in 'chip' and /dʒ/ in 'judge' are common examples.	stop-plus-fricative consonant (descriptive)	The /tʃ/ in 'church' is a voiceless affricate, and /dʒ/ in 'judge' is voiced.	Technical precision: an affricate is not merely any stop followed by any fricative; the coordinated release and single-segment analysis matter.		
