317	3163	3347	bail guilt-free	/beɪl ˌɡɪltˈfriː/	creative/compositional phrase	To leave, cancel, drop, or opt out without feeling guilty. This is understandable casual wording built from slang 'bail' + adverbial 'guilt-free', not a fixed idiom.	bail without feeling guilty, opt out guilt-free	If a show isn't working for me after a few episodes, I'll bail guilt-free.	SOURCE/COMPOSITION NOTE: teach the components rather than treating the whole string as an established idiom. Related cards: bail; guilt-free.		
317	3164	3349	methodical	/məˈθɑdɪkəl/	adjective	Done in an orderly, systematic, and deliberate way.	organized, structured, precise	Her methodical approach made the project a success.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
317	3165	3350	concession	/kənˈsɛʃən/	noun	Something yielded or granted in response to demands/negotiation; an acknowledgment of a point in argument; also a special right/privilege, a reduced-price category ('concession' in some varieties), or a concession stand/business selling refreshments.	compromise, allowance, acknowledgment; grant (contextual)	They made a concession on the deadline. / His statement was a concession that the original plan had flaws. / We bought popcorn at the concession stand.	Polysemous. A concession is not always 'surrender'. Common frames: make a concession to X; concession on X; concession that + clause.		
317	3166	3351	performative remorse	/pɚˈfɔrmətɪv rɪˈmɔrs/	noun phrase (critical/social-commentary)	Remorse displayed in a way a speaker judges to be more about appearances, reputation, or audience expectations than genuine regret or corrective action.	show of remorse, image-management apology (contextual), insincere regret (if established)	Critics dismissed the statement as performative remorse because it offered no concrete change.	Do not define the person's inner sincerity as fact unless evidence supports it. 'Performative' here marks a critical interpretation; 'virtue signaling' is not a clean synonym.		
317	3167	3352	social blunder	/ˈsoʊʃəl ˈblʌndɚ/	noun phrase	An embarrassing or tactless act or remark in a social situation.	faux pas, slip-up, misstep	Forgetting her name at the party was a major social blunder.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
317	3168	3353	push away	/pʊʃ əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To create emotional or physical distance from someone, often due to fear, anger, or discomfort.	alienate, reject, distance oneself	She tends to push people away when they get too close.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
317	3169	3354	homage / homages	/ˈɑmɪdʒɪz/ or /ˈoʊmɪdʒɪz/	noun; plural	A public or artistic expression of respect, admiration, or tribute; especially a work, scene, style, or detail deliberately honoring/invoking an earlier creator or work.	tribute, salute, nod, tribute/reference	The film pays homage to classic horror. / It contains several homages to silent cinema.	Normalize singular + plural. Common frame: pay homage to. Pronunciations with /h/ and without /h/ occur; usage varies by speaker and region.		
317	3170	3355	misspeak / misspoke	/ˌmɪsˈspiːk/	verb	To say something incorrectly or unintentionally express oneself wrongly; sometimes used by speakers as a softer way to characterize a verbal error.	say incorrectly, misstate, make a verbal slip	I misspoke earlier—the meeting is Thursday, not Tuesday. / She said she had misspoken during the interview.	KEY FORMS: misspeak–misspoke–misspoken. It does not inherently mean 'misleading'; whether euphemistic depends on context.		
318	3171	3356	go-ahead	/ˈɡoʊ əˌhɛd/	noun	Official permission to proceed with a project or plan.	green light, approval, authorization	We finally got the go-ahead to launch the campaign.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
318	3172	3357	slay / let's slay it	/lɛts sleɪ ɪt/	slang verb; motivational source phrase	Modern slang 'slay' = perform exceptionally well, look impressive, or succeed brilliantly. 'Let's slay it' is an understandable motivational use, though 'Let's crush it' / 'Let's kill it' may be more idiomatic for a task depending speaker/context.	crush it, kill it, nail it, do great	Let's crush this presentation. / You absolutely slayed that performance. / Okay—let's slay it.	Keep source phrase but teach productive SLAY. Cultural note: strongly visible in drag/LGBTQ+ and Black/online/pop-culture speech, then spread broadly; avoid presenting it as belonging exclusively to one group.		
318	3173	3358	strain up someone	/ˈstreɪn ʌp ˈsʌmwʌn/	nonstandard / source-dependent phrase	A nonstandard construction that may suggest physically straining/lifting someone or mentally/physically taxing someone. For the common stress/pressure sense, standard English normally uses 'put a strain on someone/something,' 'put someone under strain,' or 'tax someone.'	tax someone, wear someone down, put pressure on someone; cf. put a strain on someone, put someone under strain	Nonstandard/source wording: The constant criticism was straining everyone in the group. / Standard: The constant criticism was putting a strain on everyone in the group. / The workload put the whole team under considerable strain.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
318	3174	3359	snark	/snɑrk/	noun (informal)	A style of mocking, cutting, or sarcastic commentary, often witty and dismissive; also the remarks themselves.	sarcasm, mockery, biting wit, snide commentary	The review is full of snark, but it still makes a serious point.	Related adjective 'snarky'. Snark is not identical to sarcasm: it often emphasizes a cutting, smart-alecky tone.		
318	3175	3360	normie / normies	/ˈnɔrmiːz/	noun (internet/subculture slang)	A person regarded as ordinary, mainstream, or outside a particular subculture/community; often dismissive, ironic, or self-aware.	mainstream person, outsider (contextual), average person (contextual)	The meme escaped the niche community and reached the normies.	Normalize singular + plural. Pejorative force varies; often used as in-group internet slang rather than a neutral demographic label.		
318	3176	3361	disjointed	/dɪsˈdʒɔɪntɪd/	adjective	Lacking a clear connection or order; incoherent or fragmented.	incoherent, disconnected, jumbled	The report was disjointed and hard to follow.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
318	3177	3362	uninviting	/ˌʌnɪnˈvaɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Not welcoming, attractive, or pleasant.	unappealing, bleak, unfriendly	The cold, gray room felt uninviting.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
318	3178	3363	disagreeable	/ˌdɪsəˈɡriːəbəl/	adjective	Unpleasant, objectionable, or difficult to deal with; of a person, inclined to be unpleasant or uncooperative.	unpleasant, objectionable, difficult, cantankerous (person/contextual)	The smell was disagreeable. / He became disagreeable when the discussion turned to money.	Remove duplicate synonym. Can describe experiences, conditions, tastes/smells, tasks, or people—not merely grumpiness.		
318	3179	3364	palatable	/ˈpælətəbəl/	adjective	Pleasant or acceptable to the taste; figuratively, acceptable or easier to accept/consider.	tasty, appetizing; acceptable, tolerable, agreeable	The sauce made the vegetables more palatable. / They softened the proposal to make it more palatable to voters.	KEY figurative frame: make X more palatable to PERSON/group. Often means acceptable rather than actively appealing.		
318	3180	3365	unsavory / unsavoury	/ʌnˈseɪvəri/	adjective	Unpleasant, morally questionable, disreputable, or offensive, especially of people, activities, details, or reputations. Historically/literally can mean unpleasant to taste or smell, but figurative moral/social use is especially common.	disreputable, shady, distasteful, unpleasant	The investigation uncovered some unsavory business practices. / He was linked to several unsavory characters.	Do not imply food sense is equally common in modern use; 'unappetizing' is usually more natural for food. BrE spelling: unsavoury.		
319	3181	3366	unappetizing	/ˌʌnˈæpəˌtaɪzɪŋ/	adjective	Not appealing to the appetite or senses.	unappealing, disgusting, off-putting	The leftovers looked cold and unappetizing.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
319	3182	3367	Dundee	/ˌdʌnˈdiː/	proper noun	A city on the east coast of Scotland, on the Firth of Tay; known for universities, design/culture, publishing, and the video-game industry.	—	Dundee is one of Scotland's major cities and has a notable game-development history.	Clean corrupted Crocodile Dundee punctuation. Do not conflate Dundee, Scotland with the fictional Australian character Crocodile Dundee; the shared name is incidental.		
319	3183	3368	sidekick	/ˈsaɪdˌkɪk/	noun	A close companion or assistant who accompanies a more central or prominent person, especially in fiction, comedy, adventure, or informal descriptions.	companion, assistant, partner, right-hand person (contextual)	The detective and her sidekick solved the case together. / Robin is famously portrayed as Batman's sidekick.	'Partner-in-crime' can be playful but is not a clean synonym. Sidekick often implies secondary status relative to the main figure.		
319	3184	3369	cue	/kjuː/	noun; verb	Noun: a signal or prompt to begin/say/do something; a hint/indication; also a billiards stick. Verb: give a signal or prompt, or prepare/start media/action at the right moment.	signal, prompt, indication; prompt, signal	That was my cue to leave. / Cue the music. / The stage manager cued the actor. / He chalked his pool cue.	Polysemy card. Distinguish cue from queue (line/list). Productive discourse phrase: 'That's my cue to...'.		
319	3185	3370	repellent / repellant	/rɪˈpɛlənt/	adjective; noun	Adjective: causing strong dislike/disgust, or tending to repel/resist something. Noun: a substance used to repel insects, water, etc. 'Repellent' is the standard spelling; 'repellant' also occurs, especially for the noun/adjective in some usage.	disgusting, repulsive; deterrent, repellent substance	His behavior was repellent. / Apply insect repellent before hiking. / The jacket has a water-repellent coating.	Separate evaluative and functional senses. 'Deterrent' is related but not identical: a deterrent discourages; a repellent drives/keeps away.		
319	3186	3371	outrage	/ˈaʊtˌreɪdʒ/	noun; verb	Noun: intense anger/indignation caused by something seen as wrong or offensive; also an act/event regarded as grossly offensive or shocking. Verb: to cause such anger or shock.	indignation, fury; enrage, offend, shock	The decision sparked public outrage. / The remarks outraged many viewers. / They called the attack an outrage.	Remove 'wrath' as awkward synonym. Useful collocations: spark/provoke outrage; outrage over/at X; be outraged by/at.		
319	3187	3372	atop	/əˈtɑp/	preposition; adverb	On the top of; at or on a higher position.	on, above, over	A flag waved atop the building.	Core meaning retained. Depth QA: strengthen natural collocations, grammatical frame, register, and reusable literal/figurative patterns where useful.		
