320	3191	3378	function vs. formula (spreadsheets)	/ˈfʌŋkʃən vɚsəs ˈfɔrmjələ/	concept comparison	In spreadsheets, a formula is an expression entered to calculate a result, usually beginning with '='; a function is a predefined operation such as SUM or IF that can be used inside a formula. A formula may contain functions, operators, references, constants, or combinations of them.	formula = calculation expression; function = predefined operation	Formula example: =A1+A2 uses no function. / Formula example: =SUM(A1:A2) uses the SUM function.	CORRECTION: a formula is not simply 'user-defined' and a function is not the opposite of a formula. This card is spreadsheet-specific; mathematical 'function' and 'formula' have broader meanings.		
320	3192	3379	quadratic function	/kwɑˈdrætɪk ˈfʌŋkʃən/	noun (mathematics)	A polynomial function of degree 2, commonly written f(x) = ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 0. Its graph is a parabola.	degree-two polynomial function	f(x) = 2x² - 3x + 1 is a quadratic function. / The graph opens upward when a > 0 and downward when a < 0.	CORRECTION/CLEANUP: repair corrupted 'a ≠ 0'. If a = 0, the function is not quadratic.		
320	3193	3380	addendum	/əˈdɛndəm/	noun	An additional item or section added to a document, contract, report, book, etc., especially to supply information, corrections, terms, or updates after the main text.	supplement, addition, appendix (overlapping), rider (legal/contextual)	The contract includes an addendum covering remote-work terms.	Plural traditionally 'addenda'; 'addendums' also occurs. An appendix is usually planned supplementary material and is not always interchangeable with addendum.		
320	3194	3381	integer vs. whole number	/ˈɪntɪdʒɚ vɚsəs hoʊl ˈnʌmbɚ/	mathematical comparison	Integers are ..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ... . In common school usage, whole numbers are the nonnegative integers 0, 1, 2, 3, ... . Terminology can vary somewhat by educational source.	integer, whole number, nonnegative integer	Zero is both a whole number and an integer; -3 is an integer but not a whole number under the common school definition.	Technical precision: 'whole number' is less universally standardized than 'integer', so preserve the convention being used.		
320	3195	3382	natural number	/ˈnætʃɚəl ˈnʌmbɚ/	noun (mathematics)	A member of the counting-number set; depending convention, natural numbers are {1,2,3,...} or {0,1,2,3,...}.	counting number (often), nonnegative/positive integer (convention-dependent)	Seven is a natural number under every common convention. / Check whether a text includes 0 in ℕ.	The inclusion of zero is convention-dependent; good mathematical writing defines the convention when ambiguity matters.		
320	3196	3383	speech bubble / speech balloon	/spiːʧ ˈbʌbəl/	noun	A graphic shape in comics/cartoons containing a character's spoken words, usually with a tail pointing toward the speaker. Thought bubbles/balloons are related but conventionally distinct.	speech balloon, dialogue balloon	The character's words appear in a speech bubble above her head.	CORRECTION: a speech bubble normally represents speech, not thoughts; thoughts are typically shown with a thought bubble/cloud or other conventions.		
320	3197	3384	malnutrition	/ˌmælnuˈtrɪʃən/	noun (medical/public health)	A condition resulting from deficiencies, excesses, or imbalances in a person's intake of energy and/or nutrients; it includes undernutrition as well as overweight/obesity and diet-related noncommunicable-disease dimensions in broad public-health usage.	undernutrition (narrower), nutrient deficiency (narrower), poor nutritional status	Malnutrition can result from too little food, an imbalanced diet, impaired absorption, or other factors.	Precision: 'malnutrition' is broader than 'undernourishment'. Avoid making a diagnostic claim from appearance alone.		
320	3198	3385	epithelial tissue / epithelium	/ˌɛpəˈθiːliəl ˈtɪʃuː/	noun phrase; noun (biology/anatomy)	Tissue composed of closely arranged epithelial cells that covers body surfaces, lines cavities and organs, and forms many glands; functions include protection, absorption, secretion, filtration, and sensation depending on type/location.	epithelium, epithelial lining	The intestinal epithelium is specialized for absorption. / Skin contains a protective stratified squamous epithelium.	CORRECTION: it does not simply 'protect internal organs from infection'. Major classifications include simple/stratified and squamous/cuboidal/columnar; glandular epithelium is a functional category.		
320	3199	3386	square a number / squaring a number	/ˈskwɛrɪŋ ə ˈnʌmbɚ/	verb phrase	To multiply a number or quantity by itself; equivalently, raise it to the second power.	raise to the second power	Squaring 5 gives 25: 5² = 5 × 5 = 25.	Normalize reusable verb form. For algebraic quantities, square X means X².		
320	3200	3387	raise something to the second power / square something	/reɪz ˈsʌmθɪŋ tu ðə ˈsɛkənd ˈpaʊɚ/	mathematical verb construction	To use 2 as the exponent of a number/expression: x². For a number x, this equals x × x.	square, raise to the power of two	Raise 4 to the second power: 4² = 16. / Squaring (a+b) gives (a+b)².	KEY FRAME: raise X to the nth power. 'Exponentiate' is broader and more technical, not an exact synonym for specifically squaring.		
321	3201	3388	residual	/rɪˈzɪdʒuəl/	adjective; noun	Adjective: remaining after the main part/process has ended or been removed. Noun: a remainder; in statistics/regression, an observed value minus its model-predicted value; in entertainment, a residual is a repeat-use/reuse payment under applicable agreements.	remaining, leftover; remainder; residual payment	The medication had residual effects. / The model's residuals were small. / The actor receives residuals when the show is reused under the contract.	Technical senses differ by field. 'Residual error' is sometimes used, but a statistical residual is specifically observed minus fitted/predicted value and is not identical to an unobservable theoretical error.		
321	3202	3391	half-baked	/ˌhæfˈbeɪkt/	adjective (informal)	Insufficiently thought through, developed, prepared, or completed; poorly conceived.	ill-conceived, underdeveloped, poorly thought-out, harebrained (stronger)	The plan was half-baked and ignored several obvious risks. / The plot twist felt half-baked.	Informal metaphor from something not fully baked. 'Half-assed' is more vulgar and often emphasizes low effort rather than poor development.		
321	3203	3392	phone in / phoned-in	/ˌfoʊndˈɪn/	phrasal verb; adjective (informal)	To perform or produce something with little effort or enthusiasm; adjective 'phoned-in' describes such work/performance.	go through the motions, perfunctory, half-hearted, low-effort	Season two felt completely phoned-in. / The actor seemed to phone in several scenes.	Related to WID3338; this card can serve adjective-focused recognition. Hyphenate before a noun/adjectivally: a phoned-in performance.		
321	3204	3393	full-on	/ˈfʊl ɑn/	adjective/adverb (informal)	At full intensity; complete, extreme, or unabashedly intense. Can modify nouns or function informally after verbs/with descriptions.	intense, extreme, all-out, outright (contextual)	By the finale, it was full-on chaos. / The argument turned full-on hostile.	Usually hyphenated. Meaning depends on collocation: full-on attack, full-on panic, full-on weird, etc. Not always negative.		
321	3205	3394	zero payoff / no payoff	/ˈzɪəroʊ ˈpeɪˌɔf/	compositional noun phrase	No satisfying result, reward, resolution, or return after effort, setup, investment, or narrative buildup. 'Zero payoff' is emphatic compositional wording, not a fixed idiom.	no reward, no return, no resolution, no satisfying outcome	The mystery got ten episodes of buildup and zero payoff. / All that work had almost no payoff.	Teach productive PAYOFF: payoff for buildup/setup; emotional/narrative payoff; payoff from investment/effort. 'Payoff' also has financial and bribery-related senses.		
321	3206	3395	nail it / nails it	/neɪlz ɪt/	informal verb phrase	To do, perform, express, identify, or reproduce something extremely well or exactly right.	ace it, crush it, get it right, hit the mark	That confession scene nails it. / She nailed the pronunciation on her first try.	Normalize base + source form. Productive objects include performance, answer, impression, landing, timing, pronunciation.		
321	3207	3396	fall flat / falls flat	/fɔlz flæt/	idiom / verb phrase	To fail to produce the intended effect, response, humor, excitement, or emotional impact.	flop, fail to land, miss the mark, bomb (performance/contextual)	The final joke falls flat. / His attempt at reassurance fell flat.	Normalize base + source form. Often used of jokes, speeches, reveals, campaigns, gestures, or dramatic moments.		
321	3208	3397	dumpster fire	/ˈdʌmpstər ˈfaɪr/	noun (US slang)	A situation, project, event, or process regarded as disastrously bad, chaotic, and difficult to fix.	disaster, train wreck, fiasco, shitshow (vulgar)	That rollout turned into a complete dumpster fire.	Informal, vivid, strongly negative. Often hyperbolic; calling something a dumpster fire expresses a judgment rather than an objective category.		
321	3209	3398	mid	/mɪd/	adjective/slang	Mediocre, unimpressive, or merely average, especially relative to hype or expectations.	mediocre, average, meh, unremarkable	Honestly, the new series is kind of mid.	Contemporary slang, often dismissive. It can mean 'not very good' rather than a mathematically neutral midpoint; tone/context matter.		
321	3210	3399	burnout	/ˈbɝnˌaʊt/	noun	A state of severe exhaustion and reduced functioning/engagement associated with prolonged stress or demands; in occupational-health usage, burnout specifically concerns chronic workplace stress, while everyday speech uses the word more broadly for exhaustion from sustained activity/repetition.	exhaustion, depletion, fatigue; occupational burnout (specific)	I'm getting anime burnout after watching too many similar shows in a row. / She took time off after months of workplace burnout.	PRECISION: everyday 'burnout' is broad, but WHO's occupational concept is work-specific and not classified as a medical condition. Do not equate any tiredness with clinical/occupational burnout.		
322	3211	3402	chef's kiss	/ʃɛfs kɪs/	noun phrase / approving gesture (informal)	A gesture—kissing the fingertips and opening the hand outward—or the phrase describing it, used to express that something is excellent, perfect, or especially satisfying.	perfect, exquisite, flawless, just right; perfection	The final shot of the episode was chef's kiss. / That tiny musical cue at the end? Absolute chef's kiss.	Common internet/spoken patterns: 'X is chef's kiss', 'absolute chef's kiss', or simply 'chef's kiss' as a reaction. It is informal and often playful; the expression refers to the stereotyped approving chef gesture.		
322	3212	3403	overstay one's welcome	/ˌoʊvɚˈsteɪ ɪts ˈwɛlkəm/	verb phrase / idiom	To stay or continue longer than people want, so that one's presence—or an event, joke, scene, trend, etc.—becomes unwelcome or tiresome.	stay too long, drag on too long, wear out one's welcome, linger too long, become tiresome	I didn't want to overstay my welcome, so I left after dinner. / The beach arc was fun at first, but it overstayed its welcome.	Productive frame: PERSON/THING + overstay + one's/its welcome. Literal with guests; figurative with scenes, jokes, trends, sequels, story arcs, and anything that continues past the point of enjoyment.		
322	3213	3404	cop-out	/ˈkɑpˌaʊt/	noun; attributive adjective (informal)	An avoidance of a difficult responsibility, decision, or challenge; an easy or unsatisfactory way out. Attributively, 'a cop-out ending/excuse/answer' describes something evasive or disappointingly easy.	easy way out, evasion, dodge, escape, lame excuse (contextual)	Calling it 'just a dream' felt like a cop-out. / That ending was a total cop-out.	Usually negative. Distinguish noun 'a cop-out' from verb 'cop out (of something)' = avoid doing something difficult or responsible.		
322	3214	3405	gaslight	/ˈɡæsˌlaɪt/	verb	To manipulate someone over time into doubting their own memory, perception, judgment, or grasp of reality. In looser modern use, it is sometimes used for persistent denial or distortion, but not every lie, disagreement, or misleading narrative is gaslighting.	psychologically manipulate, make someone doubt their perception, distort or deny someone's reality	He kept denying conversations that had happened and tried to gaslight her into doubting her own memory. / Saying a story 'gaslights the audience' is a loose figurative use, not the core interpersonal meaning.	Core frame: gaslight + PERSON; gaslight someone into doubting/believing X. The term is often overextended in casual speech, so use it carefully when precise psychological manipulation is meant.		
322	3215	3407	plot armor	/plɑt ˈɑrmɚ/	noun (informal media criticism)	Apparent narrative protection that lets an important character survive, escape, or succeed despite dangers that would realistically defeat or kill them, because the story needs that character to continue.	narrative immunity, protagonist immunity, story protection	He survives three impossible attacks in a row because the plot armor is doing overtime. / Fans complained that the villain suddenly lost only because the hero's plot armor kicked in.	Usually critical or joking. It is a meta-narrative term: the 'armor' is not literal protection inside the story but perceived protection supplied by the writer/plot.		
322	3216	3408	hit different / hits different	/hɪts ˈdɪfɚənt/	informal slang expression	To feel, affect, or resonate with someone in a noticeably different—often stronger or more emotional—way than usual or than before.	land differently, feel different, resonate differently, affect someone more strongly	That scene hits different once you know what happens later. / This song hit different after I moved away from home.	Common frame: X hits different (when/after/because...). Highly informal. The expression became widespread through internet and youth speech; 'different' functions adverbially in this slang construction.		
322	3217	3409	keep someone in the loop	/kiːp ju ɪn ðə luːp/	idiom	To keep someone informed about relevant developments, decisions, or progress so they remain included in the flow of information.	keep informed, keep updated, keep posted, keep apprised	I'll keep you in the loop if the schedule changes. / Please keep me in the loop on anything that affects the launch.	Productive frame: keep + PERSON + in the loop; often followed by 'on/about + topic'. Opposite: leave/keep someone out of the loop.		
322	3218	3410	pastoral	/ˈpæs.tər.əl/ or /ˈpɑː.stər.əl/	adjective; noun (specialized)	1. Relating to the countryside, shepherds, or rural life, often in an idealized, peaceful way. 2. Relating to the care or guidance provided by a pastor or clergy member. As a noun in art/literature/music, a work depicting idealized rural life.	rural, rustic, bucolic, idyllic (sense 1); clerical, ministerial (sense 2)	The film opens with a peaceful pastoral landscape. / The school offers pastoral care to students who need personal support.	Do not reduce it only to 'countryside'. Common combinations: pastoral landscape/scene/poetry; pastoral care/counseling/ministry. 'Bucolic' is close for the rural-literary sense.		
322	3219	3411	double down / double down on	/ˈdʌb.əl daʊn/	phrasal verb	To strengthen one's commitment to a position, strategy, claim, or course of action, especially after it is challenged or becomes risky.	recommit, intensify, dig in, stand firm, press ahead	Instead of apologizing, he doubled down on the claim. / After the first version succeeded, the studio doubled down and ordered two sequels.	Key frame: double down on + NOUN/GERUND. From blackjack, where doubling down increases the wager under specific rules; figuratively it means committing more strongly rather than backing off.		
322	3220	3412	hunker down / bunker down	/ˈbʌŋ.kər daʊn/	phrasal verb; variant expression	'Hunker down' is the standard idiom meaning take shelter, settle in, or prepare to endure a difficult period. 'Bunker down' is also used, especially when literal shelter or bunker imagery is salient, but it is less established as the general idiom.	take shelter, shelter in place, settle in, dig in, brace oneself	We hunkered down for the night as the storm moved in. / The team bunkered down in the basement during the warning.	NORMALIZATION: teach 'hunker down' as the default reusable idiom. 'Bunker down' is understandable and attested but often sounds like a blend with 'bunker'.		
323	3221	3413	weigh A against B / weigh A and B	/weɪ eɪ ənd biː/	verb construction	To compare competing options, factors, costs, or benefits carefully before deciding; literally, 'weigh' means determine weight, while figuratively it means evaluate relative importance.	compare, evaluate, balance, weigh up, consider	We need to weigh the risks against the potential benefits. / She weighed both options carefully before deciding.	Most explicit comparison frame: weigh A against B. Also natural: weigh A and B, weigh the pros and cons, weigh one's options. Do not treat literal weighing and figurative evaluation as unrelated words.		
323	3222	3415	side job	/saɪd dʒɒb/	noun	A job or paid work done in addition to one's main job, usually to earn extra income.	second job, extra job, secondary job, moonlighting (activity)	He has a side job at a café on weekends. / I picked up a side job to cover some extra expenses.	Usually means additional employment. Contrast 'side hustle', which often suggests a self-directed gig, freelance activity, or small business and may carry a more entrepreneurial tone.		
323	3223	3416	side hustle	/saɪd ˈhʌs.əl/	noun (informal)	Paid work, a gig, freelance activity, or small business pursued alongside one's main job or primary commitments, often for extra income and sometimes with entrepreneurial ambitions.	side gig, freelance gig, extra work, small venture, secondary income stream	Her side hustle selling prints online turned into a full-time business. / I'm looking for a side hustle I can do on weekends.	More informal and entrepreneurial than 'side job'. A side hustle may be self-employed or irregular rather than conventional employment.		
323	3224	3417	Without further ado	/wɪˈðaʊt ˈfɜː.ðər əˈduː/	phrase	Used to introduce something without any delay or further talk.	without delay, let's begin, let's proceed	Without further ado, let's begin the ceremony.	Often used in formal events or scripted introductions.		
323	3225	3419	on point	/ɒn pɔɪnt/	adjective phrase (informal)	Exactly right, accurate, appropriate, stylish, or impressively well-executed.	spot-on, excellent, sharp, accurate, just right	Your analysis was on point. / Her outfit was completely on point for the event.	Informal and flexible: can praise accuracy, timing, style, performance, or presentation. Usually predicative ('was on point'), though attributive uses also occur informally.		
323	3226	3420	put someone down	/dəʊnt pʊt hɪm daʊn/	phrasal verb	To belittle, insult, or criticize someone in a way that makes them feel inferior. 'Don't put him down' is the negative imperative form.	belittle, demean, disparage, insult, run down	Don't put him down just because he made a mistake. / She constantly puts herself down even when she does well.	Key frame: put + PERSON/oneself + down. This card targets the 'belittle' sense. 'Put down' also has unrelated senses such as place something on a surface, write something down, suppress, and euthanize an animal.		
323	3227	3421	feudalism	/ˈfjuː.dəl.ɪ.zəm/	noun	A term for social, political, and economic arrangements associated especially with medieval Europe in which landholding, lord-vassal relationships, obligations, and localized authority played major roles. Historians debate how broadly and precisely the label should be applied.	feudal system; lord-vassal system (partial description)	Feudalism is often used as a broad label for medieval European systems of landholding and obligation. / Historians disagree about whether 'feudalism' is too broad a term for the varied institutions of the period.	PRECISION: avoid reducing feudalism to 'peasants worked for nobles in exchange for protection'. That describes only part of a much more complex set of relationships, and 'manorialism' specifically concerns the economic organization of estates/manors.		
323	3230	3424	anime pilgrimage / anime location pilgrimage	/ˈæn.ɪ.meɪ ˈpɪl.ɡrɪ.mɪdʒ/	noun phrase (fan-culture)	A fan trip to real-world places associated with an anime, manga, game, or other work—especially locations depicted in or used as models for scenes.	anime location visit, location pilgrimage, seichi junrei (Japanese cultural term)	We made an anime pilgrimage to the town that inspired the series' setting. / Fans travel there to photograph the same viewpoints shown in the anime.	Related Japanese term: 聖地巡礼 (seichi junrei), literally 'pilgrimage to sacred sites', widely used for visiting real-life locations connected with media works. In English, 'anime pilgrimage' is natural fan-community wording; 'holy land of anime' is usually a literal/playful translation rather than a fixed English expression.		
324	3232	3426	filming-location tour / location tour	/ləʊˈkeɪ.ʃən tʊə/	noun phrase	A tour or trip focused on visiting places where a film, TV series, anime, or other production was filmed, set, or modeled.	filming-location tour, film-location tour, set-location tour, location visit	We joined a filming-location tour that stopped at several places used in the movie. / Fans made their own location tour of the drama's most recognizable spots.	'Location tour' is understandable but broad; 'filming-location tour' or 'film-location tour' is clearer when the point is visiting screen locations. For anime/model locations, 'anime pilgrimage' may be more culturally specific.		
324	3233	3427	peerage	/ˈpɪə.rɪdʒ/	noun	1. The system or rank of hereditary or life peers and their titles, especially in Britain. 2. The body of people who hold such titles. 3. A particular peerage title or dignity.	nobility, titled nobility, aristocracy (overlapping); peerage system	He was elevated to the peerage and took a seat in the House of Lords. / The British peerage includes ranks such as duke, marquess, earl, viscount, and baron.	A 'peer' is a holder of a peerage title. 'Peerage' can mean the institution/body or an individual title. Count is the continental equivalent of British 'earl', not a standard British peerage rank name.		
324	3234	3428	fresh off	/frɛʃ ɔf/	prepositional-style phrase / informal construction	Having just come from, completed, experienced, or achieved something; very recently following a place, event, success, failure, trip, or other experience.	just back from, straight from, newly returned from, immediately after	She was fresh off the plane and already heading to a meeting. / The team came in fresh off a big win. / He's fresh off finishing a six-month project.	Productive frame: fresh off + NOUN/GERUND. Common combinations: fresh off the plane/boat, fresh off a win/loss, fresh off doing something. 'Fresh off the boat' can be neutral literally but has also been used as a stereotype/pejorative about immigrants, so context matters.		
324	3235	3429	patriarchy	/ˈpeɪ.tri.ɑː.ki/	noun	A social system or pattern of organization in which men disproportionately hold authority, power, property, or leadership roles, especially in family, political, economic, or institutional life.	male-dominated system, male-dominant social order	The novel critiques the patriarchal rules governing inheritance and marriage. / Scholars debate how patriarchy operates differently across societies and institutions.	More precise than simply 'a government controlled by men'. The term may refer to family structure, institutions, cultural norms, or broader social systems. Adjective: patriarchal.		
324	3236	3430	chūnibyō / eighth-grader syndrome	/eɪtθ ˈɡreɪ.dər ˈsɪn.droʊm/	noun (Japanese pop-culture term)	A Japanese humorous label for adolescent behavior in which someone acts as though they possess special powers, secret knowledge, a dark past, or an unusually dramatic identity; often translated as 'eighth-grader syndrome'.	chuunibyou, adolescent delusions of grandeur (rough description)	The character's eyepatch and talk about forbidden powers are classic chūnibyō. / The show lovingly makes fun of 'eighth-grader syndrome'.	NORMALIZATION: repair corrupted Japanese text. Japanese: 中二病 (chūnibyō; also romanized chuunibyou). The English translation is explanatory, not a medical diagnosis.		
324	3237	3431	edgelord	/ˈɛdʒ.lɔːd/	noun (internet slang, derogatory)	A person who deliberately uses shocking, offensive, nihilistic, or provocative statements or imagery to seem edgy, transgressive, or impressively unconcerned with social norms.	provocateur, shock poster, try-hard contrarian (contextual), troll (overlapping)	Posting the most offensive take in every thread just makes him look like an edgelord. / The villain's dialogue sometimes slips into edgelord territory.	Usually mocking or insulting. Not every provocative person is a troll: 'edgelord' specifically targets performative edginess or shock value.		
324	3238	3432	leeway	/ˈliː.weɪ/	noun	Flexibility or freedom to make decisions or act within limits.	latitude, flexibility, room to maneuver, margin	The boss gave us some leeway with the project deadline. The boss gave me some leeway in how I manage the team.	Common in business, planning, and negotiations.		
324	3239	3433	iridescent	/ˌɪr.ɪˈdɛs.ənt/	adjective	Showing luminous colors that appear to change as the viewing angle or lighting changes, like soap bubbles, oil films, some feathers, shells, or certain fabrics.	opalescent, pearlescent, shimmering, rainbow-colored (approx.)	The beetle's shell had an iridescent blue-green sheen. / The fabric looked purple from one angle and green from another because of its iridescent finish.	CORRECTION: the old example treated 'iridescent' as if it meant ambiguous or 'どっちつかず'. That is not its normal meaning. Figurative uses can suggest shifting/multifaceted color or quality, but ambiguity itself is not the definition.		
324	3240	3434	equivocal	/ɪˈkwɪv.ə.kəl/	adjective	Open to more than one interpretation, uncertain, or deliberately noncommittal; often suggesting that a speaker is avoiding a clear answer or commitment.	ambiguous, noncommittal, evasive, hedged, unclear	His answer was deliberately equivocal, so neither side could claim his support. / The evidence is equivocal and does not clearly favor either explanation.	Two useful patterns: equivocal answer/statement = ambiguous or evasive; equivocal evidence/results = inconclusive or mixed. Opposite: unequivocal = clear and unambiguous.		
325	3241	3435	express milk / express breast milk	/ɪkˈsprɛs mɪlk/	verb phrase	To remove breast milk from the breast by hand or with a pump, usually for feeding, storage, comfort, or milk-supply management.	pump milk, hand-express milk, remove breast milk	She expressed milk before work and stored it in the refrigerator. / Some parents hand-express a small amount of breast milk for relief.	Key frame: express + milk/breast milk. In this sense 'express' means press or squeeze out a liquid. CORRECTION: remove the typo 'xpress' and avoid implying that 'express air from a container' is a standard everyday extension.		
325	3242	3436	windfall	/ˈwɪnd.fɔːl/	noun	An unexpected gain, advantage, or piece of good fortune, especially a sudden amount of money or profit that was not earned or anticipated in the usual way.	unexpected gain, unexpected profit, bonanza, stroke of luck, lucky break	The company received a tax windfall after the rule changed. / Selling the old property gave them an unexpected windfall.	Originally 'windfall' referred to fruit or wood blown down by the wind and therefore obtained unexpectedly. Common modern combinations: financial windfall, tax windfall, windfall profit/gain.		
325	3243	3437	godsend	/ˈɡɒd.send/	noun	A person or thing that arrives at exactly the right time and is extremely helpful or welcome, as if sent by providence.	blessing, lifesaver, boon, stroke of luck, timely help	That portable charger was a godsend during the trip. / The extra childcare turned out to be a godsend.	Often strongly appreciative but not necessarily literally religious. Common pattern: X was/is a godsend.		
325	3244	3438	pennies from heaven	/ə ˈpɛn.iz frəm ˈhɛv.ən/	idiom / cultural phrase	Unexpected money or good fortune that seems to arrive without effort, like a small blessing from above.	windfall, unexpected bonus, lucky break, godsend	The refund felt like pennies from heaven after an expensive month. / To them, the surprise royalty payment was pennies from heaven.	CORRECTION: no article—'pennies from heaven', not 'a pennies from heaven'. The phrase is associated with the 1936 song and later works, giving it a somewhat nostalgic or whimsical flavor.		
325	3245	3439	chaff	/tʃæf/	noun	1. The dry husks or other light plant material separated from grain during threshing or winnowing. 2. Figuratively, material regarded as worthless, trivial, or less valuable than what matters.	husks, debris; worthless material, dross (figurative)	The wind carried the chaff away from the grain. / We had to separate the useful evidence from the chaff.	Important idiom: separate the wheat from the chaff = distinguish what is valuable/good from what is worthless/poor. Do not treat 'chaff' simply as generic waste in every context.		
325	3246	3440	edgeways / get a word in edgeways	/ˈedʒ.weɪz/	adverb; idiom (especially British English)	'Edgeways' literally means with the edge first or sideways. In 'get a word in edgeways', it means manage to say something when another person is talking continuously.	edgewise (AmE); manage to speak, get a chance to speak (idiom)	She talked for twenty minutes and I couldn't get a word in edgeways. / In American English, 'get a word in edgewise' is more common.	CORRECTION: 'interrupt' is not a synonym for edgeways. The fixed conversational expression is chiefly BrE 'get a word in edgeways'; AmE commonly uses 'edgewise'.		
325	3247	3441	lost cause	/lɒst kɔːz/	noun phrase / idiom	A person, effort, situation, or objective considered hopeless, beyond saving, or no longer worth pursuing because success is extremely unlikely.	hopeless case, no-win situation, dead end, futile effort	Repairing that water-damaged laptop is probably a lost cause. / After months of arguing, he decided changing their minds was a lost cause.	Common frame: X is a lost cause. It can be harsh when applied to a person, because it implies the person is beyond help or improvement.		
325	3248	3442	throw someone for a loop	/θrəʊ ˈsʌm.wʌn ə luːp/	idiom	To surprise, confuse, shock, or unsettle someone so that they momentarily do not know how to respond.	catch off guard, bewilder, stun, unsettle, take aback	Her sudden resignation threw everyone for a loop. / That plot twist really threw me for a loop.	CORRECTION: standard form is 'throw someone for a loop', not 'throw someone a loop'. Pattern: X threw + PERSON + for a loop.		
325	3249	3443	divine retribution	/dɪˈvaɪn ˌrɛ.trɪˈbjuː.ʃən/	noun phrase	Punishment from a higher power or deity for wrongdoing.	divine punishment, karmic justice	He believed the storm was divine retribution for their wrongdoing.	Appears in religious or mythological contexts; often implies moral consequence.		
325	3250	3444	whimsical	/ˈwɪm.zɪ.kəl/	adjective	Playfully imaginative, fanciful, or charmingly unusual; sometimes also changeable or unpredictable in a way driven by whim.	fanciful, playful, quirky, imaginative, capricious (in the less-charming sense)	The film has a whimsical visual style full of tiny magical details. / She decorated the room with whimsical illustrations and mismatched lamps.	Often positive with art, design, stories, humor, or personality. 'Whimsical' can also shade toward capricious/unpredictable, but the common modern descriptive use is playful and fanciful.		
326	3251	3445	laxative	/ˈlæk.sə.tɪv/	noun; adjective	A substance or medicine that promotes bowel movements and is used to relieve constipation; as an adjective, relating to or having this effect.	constipation medicine, bowel stimulant (specific type), stool softener (specific type, not exact synonym)	The doctor recommended a mild laxative for temporary constipation. / Some laxatives work by drawing water into the bowel, while others stimulate intestinal movement.	CORRECTION: 'stool softener' is one type of constipation treatment, not a perfect synonym for all laxatives. Laxatives treat constipation rather than generally 'helping digestion'.		
326	3252	3446	guardian deity/protector of children / Jizō	/ˈɡɑː.di.ən ˈdiː.ə.ti əv ˈtʃɪl.drən/	noun phrase / cultural-religious term	A religious protector associated with children; in Japanese Buddhism, Jizō (地蔵, Jizō Bosatsu) is especially associated with protecting children, travelers, and the vulnerable.	protector of children; Jizō Bosatsu	Small Jizō statues are often dressed with bibs or hats and may be associated with prayers for children.	CORRECTION: repair mojibake 'JizÅ''. Jizō is a Buddhist bodhisattva (Kṣitigarbha), not simply a generic Shinto 'guardian god'. The exact religious meaning depends on cultural context.		
326	3253	3447	head mound / burial mound for severed heads (kubizuka)	/ˈber.i.əl maʊnd fər ˈsɛv.ərd hɛdz/	noun phrase / historical-cultural term	A burial or memorial mound associated with severed heads, especially in historical Japanese contexts; Japanese 首塚 (kubizuka) literally means 'head mound'.	head mound, memorial mound, burial mound (contextual)	Local tradition identifies the site as a kubizuka, a mound associated with the burial or memorialization of severed heads.	CORRECTION: repair corrupted Japanese text. This is a descriptive historical/cultural phrase, not a common everyday English lexical item; preserve the Japanese term when source context matters.		
326	3254	3448	when unreasonableness prevails, reason retreats / 無理が通れば道理が引っ込む	/wɛn ʌnˈriː.zən.ə.bəl.nəs prɪˈveɪlz ˈriː.zən rɪˈtriːts/	translated Japanese proverb / source expression	A Japanese saying meaning that when unreasonable force or demands are allowed to prevail, sound reason and proper principles are pushed aside.	when force overrides reason; might overrules reason; reason gives way to power	The saying 無理が通れば道理が引っ込む can be translated roughly as 'When unreasonableness prevails, reason retreats.'	SOURCE-SPECIFIC: do not present this English wording as an established native English proverb. It is a translation of 無理が通れば道理が引っ込む. Depending context, natural paraphrases include 'reason gives way to force' or 'when might makes right, reason loses out.'		
326	3255	3449	might makes right	/maɪt meɪks raɪt/	proverb / aphorism	The cynical idea that those with superior power or force can impose their will and define what is treated as 'right', regardless of moral or legal justice.	power determines right, the strong rule, force over justice	The regime operated on the principle that might makes right. / The phrase is usually used critically, not as an endorsement.	'Might' here = power/strength, not the modal verb. The expression often condemns the substitution of raw power for justice or moral legitimacy.		
326	3256	3450	where might is master, justice is servant	/weə maɪt ɪz ˈmɑː.stər ˈdʒʌs.tɪs ɪz ˈsɜː.vənt/	aphoristic / source-style expression	An aphoristic way of saying that when raw power dominates a society or system, justice becomes subordinate to those who hold power.	power subordinates justice, might overrules fairness	The line 'where might is master, justice is servant' presents power and justice as master and servant to criticize an unjust system.	SOURCE CAUTION: treat this as an aphoristic sentence or quotation-like wording, not as a standard fixed English proverb unless a specific source establishes one. The reusable idea is closely related to 'might makes right'.		
326	3257	3451	when might is right, then justice there is none	/wɛn maɪt ɪz raɪt ðen ˈdʒʌs.tɪs ðeər ɪz nʌn/	aphoristic / source-style expression	A rhetorical statement arguing that if power alone determines what is considered right, genuine justice disappears.	if power defines right, justice disappears; might over justice	The line 'When might is right, then justice there is none' condemns systems in which force substitutes for justice.	SOURCE CAUTION: retain as a quotation-like or aphoristic expression rather than teaching it as a common English proverb. Compare the established phrase 'might makes right'.		
326	3258	3452	diaphragm	 /ˈdaɪ.ə.ɡræm/	noun	1. The dome-shaped muscle beneath the lungs that plays a major role in breathing. 2. A thin flexible membrane or partition in various devices. 3. A dome-shaped contraceptive barrier placed over the cervix.	breathing muscle (sense 1); membrane, partition (sense 2); contraceptive diaphragm (sense 3)	The diaphragm contracts and moves downward when you inhale. / A contraceptive diaphragm is used with spermicide and placed over the cervix.	Multiple senses share the idea of a separating membrane or sheet-like structure. In photography/optics, a diaphragm controls the aperture/opening. Replace the old placeholder definition/example.		
326	3259	3453	personnel shift	/ˌpɜː.sənˈel ʃɪft/	noun phrase	A change in staffing, personnel allocation, roles, or assignments within an organization; the exact scale can range from a few reassignments to a broader staffing change.	staffing change, personnel change, workforce reallocation, staff reorganization	The department announced a personnel shift that moved several employees to new teams. / The personnel shift was meant to align staff with changing priorities.	CORRECTION: remove encoding garbage. 'Personnel shift' is a broad descriptive phrase, not a highly fixed HR term; use more specific terms such as reassignment, transfer, reshuffle, or reorganization when the type of change is known.		
326	3260	3454	staff reshuffle	/stɑːf ˈriː.ʃʌf.əl/	noun phrase	Reorganization of roles within an organization.	role shuffle, position shake-up	There was a staff reshuffle after the new manager took over.	Often triggered by leadership changes or internal restructuring.		
327	3261	3455	staff reassignment	/stɑːf ˌriː.əˈsaɪn.mənt/	noun phrase	The transfer or reallocation of an employee or employees to different duties, roles, teams, departments, or locations within the same organization.	employee reassignment, internal transfer, role reassignment, staff reallocation	The staff reassignment moved two analysts from finance to operations. / Temporary staff reassignments helped the hospital cover the busiest unit.	Usually focuses on where existing staff are moved rather than on hiring or firing. A reassignment may be temporary or permanent and may or may not involve a change in rank or pay.		
327	3262	3456	furlough	/ˈfɜː.ləʊ/	noun; verb	A temporary authorized absence from work or duty. In employment contexts, especially in the U.S., a furlough often means a temporary period of unpaid leave or reduced work imposed by an employer while the employment relationship continues.	temporary unpaid leave, temporary layoff (overlapping), leave of absence; furlough someone	The company furloughed hundreds of workers during the shutdown. / Federal employees were placed on furlough when funding lapsed.	Distinguish furlough from permanent layoff/termination: a furlough is intended to be temporary, though details vary by employer and jurisdiction. The word also has military/prison-leave senses in some contexts.		
327	3263	3457	feel someone out about/on something	/ˈfiːl ˈsʌmwʌn aʊt/	phrasal verb construction (informal)	To approach someone indirectly or cautiously in order to discover their opinion, feelings, intentions, or willingness before making a direct proposal or commitment.	sound someone out, gauge someone's reaction, probe gently, test the waters	Could you feel her out about joining the project? / I asked a friend to feel Joe out on how he felt about the gift.	Core pattern: feel + PERSON + out, optionally about/on + TOPIC or followed by a clause. Similar to 'sound someone out'. It implies tactful probing rather than bluntly asking.		
327	3264	3458	feel out the room / read the room	/ˈfiːl ˈaʊt ðə ɹum/	informal expression / comparison	'Feel out the room' means cautiously gauge the people or atmosphere before acting; 'read the room' is the more established idiom for noticing the mood, reactions, and social atmosphere and adjusting accordingly.	gauge the room, read the room, test the waters, sense the mood	Let's feel out the room before we pitch the idea. / He should have read the room before making that joke.	'Feel out' is productive with people/situations ('feel someone out', 'feel out the situation'). 'Read the room' is the idiomatic default when the focus is perceiving the group's mood or social cues.		
327	3265	3459	pitch an idea / pitch ideas	/pɪtʃ aɪˈdiəz/	verb construction	To present an idea, proposal, product, project, or story persuasively in order to win approval, support, funding, or interest.	propose, present, sell, make a pitch, pitch a proposal	We need to pitch the idea to the client tomorrow. / She pitched three concepts to the producers.	Productive frame: pitch + THING + to + PERSON/GROUP. Also: pitch someone an idea. 'Pitch' emphasizes persuasive presentation, not merely mentioning or suggesting an idea.		
327	3266	3462	crux	/krʌks/	noun	The most important or decisive point of an issue or argument.	essence, core, heart, gist, nucleus	The crux of the matter is whether the new policy will benefit small businesses. / He missed the crux of her argument entirely.	よく「the crux of the matter」で使われる。「essence」と似ているが、重要な決定点のニュアンスが強い。		
327	3267	3463	fossilization	/ˌfɑːsələˈzeɪʃən/	noun	1. The process by which organic remains become fossils. 2. In second-language acquisition, the stabilization of non-target or incomplete linguistic forms in a learner's interlanguage so that they become resistant to further change.	fossil formation; stabilization, entrenchment (linguistics, contextual)	Fossilization can preserve hard biological material over geological time. / In language learning, repeated non-target forms may become fossilized even in otherwise fluent speakers.	PRECISION: linguistic fossilization is not simply 'incorrect usage becoming a bad habit'; it is a technical concept about persistent interlanguage features that resist further development.		
327	3268	3464	equilibrium	/ˌiːkwɪˈlɪbriəm/	noun	A state of balance in which opposing forces, influences, reactions, or tendencies are equal or stable; also a figurative state of emotional or systemic stability.	balance, stability, steady state, poise (figurative)	The chemical reaction eventually reached equilibrium. / It took her a few days to regain her emotional equilibrium after the shock.	Common in physics, chemistry, economics, biology, and figurative writing. Equilibrium does not necessarily mean nothing is happening; in dynamic equilibrium, opposing processes continue at equal rates.		
327	3269	3467	agnostic	/æɡˈnɑːstɪk/	noun; adjective	1. A person who holds that the existence or nature of God or the ultimate cannot be known, or who does not claim knowledge on the question. 2. More broadly, not committed to a particular view, system, platform, or solution because the evidence or preference is not decisive.	undecided, noncommittal, uncommitted (extended sense); skeptic (overlapping, not exact)	He describes himself as an agnostic rather than an atheist. / The software is platform-agnostic, so it can run across several operating systems.	CORRECTION: 'nonbeliever' is not an exact synonym; agnostic concerns knowledge/commitment, while atheist concerns belief in gods. Extended compounds such as platform-agnostic or vendor-agnostic mean not tied to one option.		
327	3270	3468	conflate	/kənˈfleɪt/	verb	To combine two or more distinct things, ideas, issues, or categories into one, often in a way that obscures important differences between them.	merge, combine, collapse together, blur together, confuse (resulting effect)	Don't conflate popularity with quality. / The article conflates two separate legal issues.	Key frame: conflate A with B. Unlike simple 'confuse', it emphasizes treating distinct things as if they were one or blending them together.		
328	3271	3469	tizzy	/ˈtɪzi/	noun	A state of nervous excitement or confusion.	agitation, fluster, panic, fuss, commotion	She was in a tizzy over what to wear to the party. / The news sent the office into a tizzy.	くだけた口語表現。主に米英で日常的に使われる。		
328	3272	3470	spark a wave of jealousy / ever-sparking...	/ˈɛvər ˌspɑːrkɪŋ ən ɪnˈɛksplɪkəbl weɪv əv ˈʤɛləsi/	productive verb construction / source-style phrase	'Spark' can mean cause something to begin suddenly; 'spark a wave of jealousy' means trigger a spreading or repeated burst of jealous reactions. 'Ever-sparking...' is a creative modifier suggesting something continually causes such reactions.	trigger, ignite, set off, provoke, cause	Her sudden success sparked a wave of jealousy among her rivals. / In the source-style wording, 'ever-sparking' presents the cause as continually triggering that reaction.	SOURCE-SPECIFIC: 'ever—sparking an inexplicable wave of jealousy' is not a fixed English phrase. Teach the reusable pattern spark + reaction/event: spark outrage, debate, interest, controversy, jealousy.		
328	3273	3471	demure	/dɪˈmjʊr/	adjective	Quiet, modest, reserved, or decorously restrained in manner, appearance, or dress; sometimes used playfully or ironically for an understated style.	reserved, modest, decorous, restrained, shy (contextual)	She gave a demure smile and let the others take the spotlight. / The dress is elegant and relatively demure compared with the rest of the collection.	Traditionally associated especially with modest or reserved feminine behavior/appearance. 'Demure' is not identical to timid: it can describe deliberate restraint or style rather than fear.		
328	3274	3472	brat	/bræt/	noun (informal, usually derogatory)	A badly behaved, spoiled, demanding, or immature child; by extension, a person acting selfishly, petulantly, or immaturely.	spoiled child, pest, troublemaker, little monster (informal); immature person	That spoiled brat threw a tantrum when he didn't get the toy. / Stop acting like a brat and talk it through.	Often insulting; tone ranges from playful teasing to genuine contempt. Common collocation: spoiled brat. Do not confuse with 'brat' as a shortening of bratwurst in some American food contexts.		
328	3275	3473	dote on/upon someone	/doʊt ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn/	phrasal/prepositional verb	To show someone a great deal of love, fondness, and attentive affection, sometimes to the point of indulgence.	adore, lavish affection on, cherish, fuss over, indulge (sometimes)	He absolutely dotes on his granddaughter. / She dotes on her rescue dog and buys him new toys constantly.	Key frame: dote on/upon + PERSON/ANIMAL. 'Dote' is rarely used without 'on/upon' in this sense. The affection can be warm and positive, though it may imply excessive indulgence.		
328	3276	3475	inductive	/ɪnˈdʌktɪv/	adjective	1. In reasoning, moving from particular observations or cases toward a broader generalization, pattern, or probable conclusion. 2. Relating to electromagnetic induction or induction-based processes.	empirical, generalizing, bottom-up (rough analogy); induction-based	The researchers used inductive reasoning to build a theory from repeated observations. / An inductive sensor detects objects through electromagnetic effects.	Inductive conclusions are generally supported to a degree rather than guaranteed by the premises. Contrast deductive reasoning, where a valid argument makes the conclusion necessary if its premises are true.		
328	3277	3476	deductive	/dɪˈdʌktɪv/	adjective	Relating to reasoning that derives a conclusion from stated premises or general principles; in a valid deductive argument, true premises guarantee the conclusion.	logical, syllogistic, top-down (rough analogy), demonstrative	The proof uses deductive reasoning from a small set of axioms. / A valid deductive argument cannot have true premises and a false conclusion.	Contrast inductive reasoning, which supports conclusions probabilistically rather than guaranteeing them. 'Deductive' does not mean merely 'smart detective-style reasoning'.		
328	3278	3477	convergent	/kənˈvɝdʒənt/	adjective	Moving or tending toward the same point, result, value, or solution. In mathematics, a convergent sequence or series approaches a finite limit; in broader use, separate paths or ideas become more alike or come together.	coming together, merging, approaching a limit, aligning	Several convergent lines of evidence support the same explanation. / The sequence is convergent and approaches 2.	Contrast divergent = moving apart, differing, or failing to approach a finite limit. In psychology, 'convergent thinking' narrows possibilities toward a single solution.		
328	3279	3478	imbue	/ɪmˈbju/	verb	To fill, permeate, or inspire someone or something with a quality, feeling, idea, or character so that it becomes deeply present.	infuse, instill, permeate, suffuse, inspire	The film is imbued with a quiet sense of loss. / Her parents imbued her with a strong sense of responsibility.	Key frames: imbue + THING/PERSON + with + QUALITY; be imbued with + QUALITY. 'Infuse' is close; 'imbue' often suggests deep or pervasive influence.		
328	3280	3479	élan	/eɪˈlɑn/	noun	Energetic style and enthusiastic self-assurance; a distinctive flair.	verve, flair, panache, zest, vigor, dash	He delivered the presentation with remarkable élan. / The restaurant’s décor adds élan to an otherwise plain space.	フレンチ由来。上品な勢い・華を示す語で、単なる“energy”より洗練度が高い。		
329	3281	3480	cowabunga	/ˌkaʊwəˈbʌŋɡə/	interjection	A playful exclamation expressing excitement, triumph, or enthusiasm (surf culture, pop-culture発)。	woo-hoo, yippee, heck yeah, radical (slang), gnarly (slang)	Cowabunga! We finally landed the trick shot. / The kids shouted “cowabunga” as they ran into the waves.	砕けたポップ表現。フォーマル文脈では不適。		
329	3282	3481	dookie	/ˈduki/	noun (slang)	Slang for feces or a piece of feces; by extension, sometimes used dismissively for something regarded as bad, worthless, or trash.	poop, crap, turd; garbage, junk (extended insult)	Watch your step—there's dog dookie on the sidewalk. / He called the broken old printer 'a piece of dookie.'	Very informal and juvenile/crude. Spelling also appears as 'dooky'. Avoid in formal settings. The extended 'this is dookie' type of use is slangy and context-dependent.		
329	3283	3482	self-deprecating	/ˌsɛlf ˈdɛprɪˌkeɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Making light of one’s own abilities or flaws in a modest or humorous way.	self-effacing, modest, humble, wry, understated	Her self-deprecating jokes put the audience at ease. / A bit of self-deprecating humor can soften a bold claim.	self-deprecating vs. self-defeating → 前者は「控えめ・ユーモラス」、後者は「自分の成功を妨げる」ネガティブ。		
329	3284	3483	weeb / anime weeb	/ˈænəˌmeɪ wib/	noun (internet slang, often teasing or derogatory)	A person, especially a non-Japanese fan, perceived as excessively obsessed with anime, manga, or Japanese pop culture, sometimes with stereotyped or superficial ideas about Japan.	weeaboo (full/original slang form); anime obsessive (descriptive); Japanophile (much more neutral, not exact)	He jokingly calls himself a weeb because half his vacation plans revolve around anime locations. / Calling someone a weeb can be playful among friends or insulting depending on tone.	NORMALIZATION: 'weeb' is the reusable headword; 'anime weeb' is an emphatic/common combination but somewhat redundant. 'Otaku' and 'Japanophile' overlap only partly and differ in cultural tone and scope.		
329	3285	3484	ingratiating	/ɪnˈɡreɪʃiˌeɪtɪŋ/	adjective	Designed or tending to win favor, approval, or affection, often through excessive friendliness, flattery, or attentiveness; frequently implies insincerity or obsequiousness.	fawning, obsequious, sycophantic, flattering, unctuous	His ingratiating smile made her wonder what he wanted. / The interviewer found the candidate's constant flattery a little too ingratiating.	Related construction: ingratiate oneself with + PERSON = deliberately try to gain that person's favor. 'Ingratiating' can sometimes be neutral ('pleasantly charming'), but modern use often carries suspicion of calculated charm.		
329	3286	3487	peter out	/ˈpiːtər aʊt/	phrasal verb	To gradually decrease in strength, size, or intensity until it stops or disappears.	dwindle, taper off, diminish, fade away, fizzle out	The party started lively but petered out around midnight. / His enthusiasm for the project slowly petered out.	非フォーマルな日常表現。比喩的にも物理的にも使われる。		
329	3287	3488	cleat / cleats	/klits/	noun	1. A projecting stud on the sole of a sports shoe, or in North American English, a shoe fitted with such studs ('cleats'). 2. A fitting with projecting arms used to secure a rope, especially on a boat or dock.	stud, spike (sports); sports shoe with studs (AmE); mooring cleat (nautical)	He changed into his soccer cleats before practice. / Wrap the rope around the cleat on the dock.	In AmE, plural 'cleats' commonly means the shoes themselves. In BrE, 'football boots' and 'studs' are more typical. Nautical cleats are a separate but common physical sense.		
329	3288	3489	you bet your bippy	/aɪ bɛt jər ˈbɪpi/	catchphrase / slang expression (dated, humorous)	A playful emphatic expression meaning 'you can be absolutely sure of it' or 'definitely'.	you bet, absolutely, for sure, you can count on it	Will they complain about the price? You bet your bippy they will.	CORRECTION: the well-known catchphrase is 'You bet your (sweet) bippy', popularized by Rowan & Martin's Laugh-In. 'I bet your bippy' may be understandable wordplay but is not the standard form. 'Bippy' is a comic euphemistic slang word, often interpreted as referring to the backside.		
329	3289	3490	attrition	/əˈtrɪʃən/	noun	A gradual reduction in number, strength, or effectiveness through continued pressure, losses, wear, or departures. In organizations, employee attrition is the reduction of staff as people leave and are not necessarily replaced.	wearing down, erosion, depletion, gradual loss; staff loss, workforce shrinkage	The campaign became a war of attrition. / The company reduced headcount partly through natural attrition rather than layoffs.	Common combinations: war of attrition, employee/staff attrition, customer attrition. Attrition differs from turnover: turnover counts departures/replacements, while attrition often emphasizes net shrinkage as positions are left unfilled.		
329	3290	3491	out of whack	/aʊt əv wæk/	idiom	Not working properly; not in proper order or alignment.	malfunctioning, broken, askew, out of order, out of alignment	My phone’s battery is out of whack. / His sleep schedule has been out of whack lately.	非フォーマル。米口語で機械・健康・バランスなど多様に使う。		
330	3291	3496	be equal to	/bi ˈikwəl tu/	phrase / adjective construction	1. To be the same in amount, value, rank, or effect as something. 2. To have enough ability, strength, courage, or resources to meet a task, challenge, or responsibility.	be equivalent to, match (sense 1); be up to, be capable of, be fit for (sense 2)	Ten millimeters is equal to one centimeter. / She proved equal to the challenge. / I don't think this old laptop is equal to the task.	For capability, common frame: be equal to + task/challenge/job/occasion. This sense is more formal than 'be up to'. Do not omit the ordinary equality/equivalence sense.		
330	3292	3497	be up to	/bi ʌp tu/	multi-use phrase	1. Be doing or occupied with something ('What are you up to?'). 2. Be secretly planning or doing something, often suspicious ('They're up to something'). 3. Be capable/well enough for a task ('I'm not up to it'). 4. Be someone's responsibility or choice ('It's up to you'). 5. Reach a maximum amount or level ('up to 50 people').	be doing, be engaged in; be capable of; be one's choice/responsibility; as many/much as	What are you up to this weekend? / The kids are up to something. / I'm not up to a long hike today. / It's up to you which route we take. / The room holds up to 50 people.	Highly polysemous and context-dependent. For capability, 'be up to + noun/gerund' often means have enough energy/ability. 'Up to' meaning maximum ('up to 50') and responsibility ('up to you') are separate core patterns worth recognizing.		
330	3293	3498	have what it takes	/hæv wʌt ɪt teɪks/	idiom	To possess the qualities, skills, or determination needed to succeed.	be capable, be qualified, be fit for, measure up	She has what it takes to be a great leader. / Not everyone has what it takes to run a marathon.	非フォーマル。肯定・否定どちらの形でも頻出。		
330	3294	3499	up to scratch	/ʌp tə skrætʃ/	idiom	Meeting the required standard; satisfactory.	adequate, acceptable, sufficient, up to par, fit for purpose	The work isn’t up to scratch—we’ll need to redo it. / His performance is finally up to scratch.	英式でも使われる。否定文と共に多く出る。		
330	3296	3501	it makes no never mind	/ɪt meɪks noʊ ˈnɛvər maɪnd/	idiom (regional/colloquial)	A regional/old-fashioned colloquial expression meaning 'it makes no difference to me' or 'it doesn't matter.'	it doesn't matter, it makes no difference, no matter, makes no odds (BrE)	It makes no never mind to me which restaurant we pick. / If they want to leave early, it makes no never mind to him.	Chiefly U.S. regional/older colloquial speech. The double negative-looking form is idiomatic rather than literal. More neutral modern alternatives: 'It makes no difference to me' / 'It doesn't matter to me.'		
330	3297	3502	rote memorization / rote learning	/roʊt ˌmɛmərəˈzeɪʃən/	noun phrase	Learning or memorizing material mainly through repetition, often with limited emphasis on understanding, reasoning, or meaningful context.	rote learning, learning by repetition, mechanical memorization, drilling	Rote memorization can help with basic vocabulary, but it works better when you also use the words in context. / The class relied heavily on rote learning of formulas.	Rote practice is not automatically useless: repetition can support automatic recall. The criticism is usually that repetition is used without enough understanding, application, or context.		
330	3298	3504	expedient	/ɪkˈspiːdiənt/	adjective, noun	(adj.) Useful, convenient, or effective for achieving an immediate purpose, sometimes chosen because it is practical rather than principled or ideal. (n.) A practical means or device adopted to achieve an end, often temporarily.	practical, convenient, pragmatic, advantageous; stopgap, makeshift measure, means	It was politically expedient to postpone the decision, even if it solved nothing. / Using the lobby as a classroom was a temporary expedient.	Important nuance: 'expedient' can be neutral ('practical in the circumstances') but often suggests short-term convenience, compromise, or questionable principle. Common: politically expedient; temporary expedient.		
330	3299	3506	forthcoming	/ˈfɔrθˌkʌmɪŋ/	adjective	1. Planned or expected to happen, appear, or be published soon. 2. Willing to provide information openly and readily; candid or cooperative in answering.	upcoming, approaching, impending; candid, open, communicative, cooperative	More details will appear in the forthcoming report. / He wasn't very forthcoming about why he had resigned.	Two distinct common senses. In the information sense, frequent pattern: be forthcoming about/with information. 'Not forthcoming' often means evasive or unwilling to tell you much.		
330	3300	3507	divulge	/daɪˈvʌldʒ/	verb	To reveal or disclose information that was secret, private, confidential, or previously withheld.	disclose, reveal, make known, give away; spill (informal)	The company refused to divulge the details of the agreement. / I can't divulge who told me that.	Usually takes information as its object: divulge a secret/details/information/name. More formal than 'tell' or 'spill'. Avoid 'divulge about'; normally divulge + WHAT.		
331	3301	3508	the upper hand	/ˈʌpər hænd/	idiom / noun phrase	A position of greater power, control, or advantage over another person, group, or situation.	advantage, edge, leverage, dominance, stronger position	Once they controlled the supply route, they had the upper hand. / She gained the upper hand in the negotiation after the new data came out.	Usually with the article: have/get/gain/hold the upper hand; often 'the upper hand over someone' or 'in a situation'.		
331	3302	3510	CMS (content management system)	/ˌsiː.emˈɛs/	noun / initialism	A software system used to create, organize, edit, manage, and publish digital content, especially website content, often through an administrative interface rather than direct editing of source files.	content management system, web content management system (more specific)	We update the site's articles through the CMS instead of editing the HTML manually. / WordPress is a widely used CMS.	CORRECTION: avoid time-sensitive superlative claims such as 'the most popular CMS in the world' on a vocabulary card. CMS can be broader than websites; WCMS specifically refers to web content management.		
331	3303	3511	enamored (of/with)	/ɪˈnæmərd/	adjective	Filled with strong liking, admiration, fascination, or romantic affection for someone or something.	smitten with, infatuated with, captivated by, charmed by, taken with, fond of	She became enamored of the city's old architecture. / He's completely enamored with the new camera.	Common constructions: enamored of and enamored with; both are established. Often figurative and need not imply romantic love.		
331	3304	3512	slapstick	/ˈslæpˌstɪk/	noun, adjective	(n.) Comedy based on deliberately clumsy actions, exaggerated physical humor, and pratfalls; (adj.) relating to such comedy.	physical comedy, farce, buffoonery, clowning	The film is full of slapstick humor and sight gags. / They staged a slapstick chase scene through the market.	映画・舞台芸でのジャンル用語。サイレント映画時代からの伝統。		
331	3305	3513	simp	/sɪmp/	noun (slang, often derogatory)	Internet slang for a person—often stereotypically a man—seen as giving excessive attention, devotion, praise, money, or deference to someone they desire, especially without reciprocation; also used more loosely as an insult for perceived over-admiration.	doormat (overlap), overly devoted admirer, sycophant (different context)	People called him a simp just because he defended his girlfriend online. / The word gets thrown around so loosely that ordinary kindness can be mislabeled as 'simping.'	Often derogatory and gendered; meaning varies by community. Verb: simp for/over someone. Etymology/history is more complicated than simply 'short for simpleton', so avoid presenting that as a certain origin.		
331	3306	3514	cinch	/sɪntʃ/	noun, verb	(n.) 1. Something very easy to do: 'a cinch.' 2. A strap or girth used to secure a saddle. (v.) 1. To tighten or secure something firmly. 2. To make an outcome certain or secure.	breeze, snap, piece of cake; girth; tighten, secure; clinch (sense 2, close)	Once you know the shortcut, the setup is a cinch. / She cinched the belt tighter around her waist. / The final win cinched a playoff spot.	CORRECTION: remove unsupported sanitary-product claim. Common idiom: 'It's a cinch' = it's easy. Verb 'cinch' can mean tighten/secure and, especially AmE, make certain; compare 'clinch', which is often more common for securing a victory/deal.		
331	3307	3515	desensitized	/diːˈsɛnsɪtaɪzd/	adjective	Made less sensitive or less responsive to something, often due to repeated exposure.	numb, unfeeling, indifferent, habituated, inured	Over time, viewers can become desensitized to violent images in the media. / After years of city living, she was desensitized to the noise.	心理学・医療・日常的文脈で使用。ポジティブにもネガティブにも使われる。		
331	3308	3516	bury	/ˈbɛri/	verb	1. Put a dead body in the ground or a tomb. 2. Put, cover, or hide something deeply under or inside something else. 3. Press or hide part of the body in something ('bury your face in your hands'). 4. Immerse oneself deeply in work/activity or suppress a feeling/memory. In SRS software such as Anki, temporarily hide a card from the current study session/day.	inter, entomb; cover, conceal; immerse oneself in; suppress; temporarily hide (SRS)	They buried the box under the tree. / She buried herself in work after the breakup. / In Anki, you can bury a related card so it doesn't appear again that day.	Useful frames: bury X in/under Y; bury one's face/head in; bury oneself in work/a book; bury feelings/memories. SRS 'bury' is a specialized software sense distinct from deleting or suspending a card.		
331	3309	3517	heckler	/ˈhɛklər/	noun	A person who interrupts a performer, speaker, or event with derisive or challenging comments.	interrupter, disruptor, antagonist, protester, detractor	The comedian handled the heckler with quick wit. / Security removed a heckler from the political rally.	heckle（ヤジを飛ばす）の実行者。特に演説・舞台・試合などでの妨害者。		
331	3310	3519	no skin off my/your nose	/noʊ skɪn ɔf maɪ noʊz/	idiom (informal)	Used to say that something causes no loss, trouble, or concern to the speaker/person mentioned; it makes no difference to them.	no concern of mine, no problem for me, makes no difference to me	If you want to take the earlier train, that's no skin off my nose. / It's no skin off his nose if we change the plan.	Common variants include 'no skin off my nose' and especially AmE 'no skin off my back'. Informal and sometimes dismissive.		
332	3311	3520	by the skin of one's teeth	/skɪn əv maɪ tiːθ/	idiom	Only just; by the narrowest possible margin, especially when escaping failure or danger.	barely, narrowly, by a hair, by the narrowest margin	I passed the exam by the skin of my teeth. / They escaped by the skin of their teeth.	CORRECTION: the full idiom normally includes 'by': by the skin of one's teeth. Biblical origin (Book of Job). 'Teeth' is already plural; the possessive changes with the subject.		
332	3312	3521	rebut / rebutted	/rɪˈbʌtɪd/	verb	To respond to a claim, argument, accusation, or evidence by presenting reasons or evidence intended to show that it is wrong, misleading, or unpersuasive.	counter, answer, refute (if successfully disproved), contradict, contest	The defense rebutted the allegation with phone records. / She rebutted his argument point by point.	NORMALIZATION: headword to base form 'rebut'. Important distinction: rebut = argue/evidence against; refute traditionally implies successfully prove false, though everyday usage sometimes overlaps. Noun: rebuttal.		
332	3313	3522	nouveau riche / cocky nouveau riche	/ˈkɑːki ˌnuːvoʊ ˈriːʃ/	noun; adjective / source phrase	'Nouveau riche' refers to people who have recently become wealthy, often with a disparaging implication that they display wealth without established upper-class taste or status. 'Cocky nouveau riche' adds the separate adjective 'cocky' = arrogantly self-confident.	newly rich, new money; parvenu (literary/derogatory), upstart (contextual)	The old aristocratic families looked down on the nouveau riche. / The source phrase 'cocky nouveau riche' paints the newly rich person as both showy and arrogant.	Teach the reusable lexical item 'nouveau riche'; 'cocky nouveau riche' is a compositional/source phrase, not a fixed idiom. French-derived expression; can function collectively or adjectivally.		
332	3314	3523	head-scratcher	/ˈhɛd ˌskrætʃər/	noun (informal)	A question, problem, event, or situation that is puzzling, surprising, or difficult to explain.	puzzle, mystery, conundrum, puzzler	Why the app fails only on Tuesdays is a real head-scratcher. / That ending was a bit of a head-scratcher.	Often hyphenated 'head-scratcher'. Related literal gesture: scratch one's head = show puzzlement.		
332	3315	3524	props / major props	/ˈmeɪdʒər prɑps/	plural noun (slang)	Respect, credit, or recognition given to someone for something they did well; 'major props' intensifies the praise.	credit, respect, kudos, recognition, praise	Props to you for admitting the mistake. / Major props to the animation team for pulling that sequence off.	Common frame: props to + PERSON + for + action. Informal slang. Often explained historically as related to 'proper respect', but avoid treating that expansion as certain etymology.		
332	3316	3525	volatile / find someone or something volatile	/ˈvɑːlətl/	adjective / construction	1. Liable to change rapidly and unpredictably, especially in mood, conditions, prices, or situations. 2. Liable to erupt into violence or conflict. 3. In chemistry, evaporating readily at ordinary temperatures.	unstable, unpredictable, changeable, explosive, combustible (contextual); readily evaporating (chemistry)	The market has been extremely volatile this week. / I find him volatile when he's under pressure. / Gasoline is highly volatile.	'Find X volatile' is not a fixed phrase: it is the ordinary construction find + OBJECT + COMPLEMENT, meaning judge/perceive X as volatile. Teach 'volatile' as the headword.		
332	3317	3526	scoop up	/skuːpt ʌp/	phrasal verb	1. Pick up or gather something quickly, often with a scooping motion or into one's arms. 2. Acquire, buy, or take available things eagerly or quickly.	pick up, gather up, grab; snap up, acquire	She scooped up the child and carried him inside. / Fans scooped up the limited-edition tickets within minutes.	Common physical frame: scoop X up / scoop up X. Figurative acquisition overlaps with 'snap up', especially for bargains, shares, property, tickets, etc.		
332	3318	3527	capisce? / capiche? / capeesh?	/kəˈpiʃ/	interjection / question (informal, stylized)	A stylized expression meaning 'Do you understand?' or 'Got it?', often used after an instruction, threat, or emphatic statement.	understand?, got it?, you follow?	Nobody touches that folder, capisce? / Be back here by nine, capiche? / Nobody messes with my family, capeesh?	From Italian capisci? 'do you understand?' English has several stylized spellings, including capisce, capiche, and capeesh. They are strongly associated with stereotyped Italian-American or mob-movie dialogue and can sound theatrical or caricatured. MERGED VARIANT: WID3528 'capeesh' was folded into this card rather than kept as a separate spelling-only card.		
332	3320	3529	coefficient	/ˌkoʊɪˈfɪʃənt/	noun	A numerical or constant quantity placed before and multiplying a variable in an algebraic expression; in general use, a factor influencing a result.	factor, constant, multiplier, ratio	In the equation 3x, 3 is the coefficient of x. / The coefficient of friction determines how easily two surfaces slide against each other.	数学や物理の分野で頻出。		
333	3321	3530	coefficient of variation (CV)	/ˌkoʊɪˈfɪʃənt əv ˌvɛriˈeɪʃən/	noun phrase (statistics)	A dimensionless measure of relative dispersion, commonly calculated as the standard deviation divided by the mean, often multiplied by 100% and reported as a percentage.	CV, relative standard deviation (often closely related/used synonymously)	Dataset A has a larger standard deviation, but its coefficient of variation is lower because its mean is much larger.	Useful for comparing relative variability across data with different scales or units. Caution: CV is problematic or not meaningful when the mean is zero or near zero, and interpretation can be inappropriate for scales without a meaningful ratio zero.		
333	3322	3531	be lost on someone	/lɔst ɑn ˈsʌmwʌn/	idiom	1. Fail to be understood or noticed by someone. 2. Fail to have the intended emotional, humorous, aesthetic, or persuasive effect on someone.	go over someone's head, fail to register, be missed by, have no effect on	The sarcasm was completely lost on him. / The beauty of the quiet ending wasn't lost on me.	Important negative pattern: 'X is not lost on me' = I fully notice/appreciate/understand X. Common subjects: joke, irony, significance, subtlety, meaning, beauty.		
333	3323	3532	fly right past someone / it flew right past me	/ɪt flu raɪt pæst mi/	informal figurative expression	To pass unnoticed or fail to be understood or registered by someone, as though the information moved past them before they could catch it.	go right over someone's head, pass someone by, escape someone's notice, fail to register	The reference flew right past me the first time. / His sarcasm went right over my head.	Natural but not as fixed as 'go over someone's head'. Productive pattern: X flew/went right past + PERSON. Can refer to something literally passing as well, so context determines the figurative meaning.		
333	3324	3533	fleeting whiff	/ˈflitɪŋ wɪf/	noun phrase	A very brief, faint smell; figuratively, a momentary hint, trace, or suggestion of something.	faint whiff, brief scent, passing hint, trace	I caught a fleeting whiff of coffee as the door opened. / For a moment, he felt a fleeting whiff of nostalgia.	Core noun 'whiff' = a brief/light smell or puff; figuratively 'a whiff of scandal/danger/nostalgia' = a slight indication. 'Fleeting whiff' is a compositional phrase, not a fixed idiom.		
333	3325	3534	call something to mind / vaguely call to mind	/ˈveɪɡli kɔld tə maɪnd/	verb construction	To cause someone to remember or think of something; adding 'vaguely' means the resemblance or recollection is faint or indistinct.	bring to mind, evoke, remind someone of, recall	The melody called to mind an old lullaby. / His voice vaguely called to mind a teacher I once had.	NORMALIZATION: reusable construction is call/bring X to mind. 'Vaguely called to mind' is compositional rather than a fixed idiom. Subject can be a sight, sound, person, idea, etc.		
333	3326	3535	inheritor	/ɪnˈhɛrɪtɚ/	noun	A person who inherits property, money, a title, position, tradition, responsibility, or legacy from a predecessor; also figuratively, a successor who carries forward ideas or influence.	heir, successor, beneficiary (legal overlap), legatee (will-specific)	She became the principal inheritor of the estate. / The director is often described as an inheritor of the silent-film tradition.	'Heir' is generally more common for inheritance of property/title. 'Inheritor of a tradition/legacy' is useful figuratively. Beneficiary and legatee are legal terms with narrower meanings and are not exact synonyms.		
333	3327	3536	tainted	/ˈteɪntɪd/	adjective	1. Contaminated, spoiled, or made unsafe by contact with something harmful. 2. Damaged in reputation, credibility, purity, or moral standing by corruption, suspicion, or association.	contaminated, polluted; sullied, stained, compromised, marred	The water supply may have been tainted by chemicals. / Evidence obtained improperly can be seen as tainted. / His victory was tainted by the cheating scandal.	Common frames: tainted food/water/blood; tainted evidence; a tainted reputation/victory/legacy. Verb: taint X; be tainted by/with X.		
333	3328	3538	on a whim	/ɑn ə wɪm/	idiom, adverbial phrase	Because of a sudden wish or idea, without planning or careful thought.	impulsively, spontaneously, on impulse, spur-of-the-moment	We booked tickets to Kyoto on a whim. / He shaved his head on a whim after the game.	軽い衝動性・気まぐれのニュアンス。フォーマルでは避けることも。		
333	3329	3539	taxonomy	/tækˈsɑnəmi/	noun	The practice or science of classification, particularly of organisms; more broadly, any systematic classification.	classification, categorization, typology, systematics	Modern taxonomy relies heavily on genetic data. / We need a clear taxonomy for our product features.	生物学が原義だがビジネス文脈でも「分類体系」の意味で常用。		
333	3330	3540	cartography	/kɑrˈtɑɡrəfi/	noun	The science or practice of drawing maps and charts.	mapmaking, mapping, geospatial charting	Advances in satellite imagery have transformed cartography. / She studied cartography to support her work in urban planning.	GISや測位技術と関連。		
334	3331	3541	mail it in	/meɪl ɪt ɪn/	idiom / phrasal verb (informal)	To perform a task with little effort, energy, care, or commitment, especially when better performance is expected.	phone it in, go through the motions, do the bare minimum, coast	The actors seemed to mail it in during the final episode. / Don't mail it in just because the deadline is tomorrow.	Very close to 'phone it in'. The expression implies perfunctory effort rather than literally sending something by mail; literal 'mail it in' is of course also possible from context.		
334	3332	3542	patter / patter out (contextual)	/ˈpætɚ aʊt/	verb / creative phrasal use	'Patter' means make a rapid series of light tapping sounds, especially rain or footsteps. 'Patter out' can contextually describe those tapping sounds gradually dying away, but it is not a standard general-purpose equivalent of 'peter out'.	patter; die away, fade, peter out (for gradual ending)	Rain pattered against the window all night. / The light tapping gradually faded away as the shower passed.	NORMALIZATION: do not teach 'patter out' as a broadly established phrasal verb. For something gradually diminishing until it stops, use 'peter out' (already WID3487). Keep 'patter' for the sound itself.		
334	3333	3543	go the distance	/ɡoʊ ðə ˈdɪstəns/	idiom	1. Continue through an entire difficult course, contest, period, or undertaking without quitting. 2. In boxing, last until the scheduled end of the bout; by extension in sports, complete the full contest or duration.	see it through, last to the end, persevere, stick it out, endure	I wasn't sure the series could go the distance for twelve episodes, but it did. / The pitcher went the distance and threw a complete game.	Origin/strong association with boxing: a fighter who 'goes the distance' lasts all scheduled rounds. Productive figurative use for relationships, projects, businesses, endurance, etc.		
334	3334	3544	baffling	/ˈbæflɪŋ/	adjective	Extremely confusing or hard to understand.	perplexing, puzzling, confounding, bewildering, mystifying	The judge’s decision was baffling to many observers. / The device failed for some baffling reason.	“confusing” より強めの当惑ニュアンス。		
334	3335	3545	hotshot / hot shot	/ˈhɑt ʃɑt/	noun; adjective (informal)	A person regarded as exceptionally skilled, successful, important, or confident; often admiring, but it can imply arrogance or showiness. As an adjective, describes such a person or high-performing status.	ace, whiz, star, standout; big shot (importance-focused), show-off (negative overlap)	They hired a hotshot lawyer from New York. / He came in acting like a hot shot who already knew everything.	Both 'hotshot' and 'hot shot' occur; one-word 'hotshot' is common as noun/adjective. Tone ranges from genuine praise to sarcastic criticism.		
334	3336	3546	basket weaving	/ˈbæskət ˈwiːvɪŋ/	noun	The craft of making baskets by interlacing materials; colloquially, a joking reference to an “easy” college elective (esp. underwater basket weaving).	wickerwork, plaiting, handicraft	She teaches basket weaving at the community center. / He joked that his major was underwater basket weaving.	文化参照（米カレッジの冗談表現）あり。		
334	3337	3547	seasoned	/ˈsiːzənd/	adjective; past participle	1. Experienced and skilled through long practice. 2. Flavored with salt, spices, herbs, etc. 3. Prepared or matured for use, as with dried wood or a seasoned cast-iron pan.	experienced, veteran, practiced; flavored, spiced; matured, dried	She's a seasoned litigator who has handled dozens of complex cases. / The vegetables were well seasoned. / Cook it in a seasoned cast-iron skillet.	NORMALIZATION: remove question-form headword. Common pattern 'seasoned professional/veteran/traveler'. For cast iron, 'seasoned' means a protective polymerized-oil layer has been built up, not simply flavored.		
334	3338	3548	rag on someone	/ræɡ ɑn/	phrasal verb (informal)	To repeatedly criticize, tease, mock, or give someone a hard time, often over a particular fault or behavior.	give someone a hard time, pick on, ride, tease, criticize, hassle	They kept ragging on me about my old phone. / Don't rag on him for his accent.	Usually frame: rag on + PERSON, sometimes rag on someone about/for + TOPIC. Can be friendly teasing or genuinely hostile depending on tone.		
334	3339	3549	subdued	/səbˈduːd/	adjective	(1) Quiet and rather reflective or depressed; (2) (Color/light/sound) Soft, muted, not bright or loud; (3) Brought under control.	muted, restrained, low-key, toned-down, hushed, somber	The mood in the office was subdued after the announcement. / The restaurant’s subdued lighting created a calm atmosphere.	感情・雰囲気・色調・音量などに広く適用。		
334	3340	3550	purse one's lips	/pɝs/	verb phrase	To press and tighten the lips into a small rounded or compressed shape, often showing disapproval, thought, concentration, restraint, or uncertainty.	pucker one's lips, tighten one's lips, compress one's lips	She pursed her lips and looked at the bill again. / He pursed his lips, trying not to smile.	Core construction: purse + one's lips. The verb 'purse' here is unrelated in meaning to the noun 'purse' = handbag/wallet, though spelled the same.		
335	3341	3551	stifling	/ˈstaɪflɪŋ/	adjective	(1) Very hot or stuffy, making it hard to breathe; (2) Restricting freedom or expression; oppressive.	suffocating, oppressive, airless, smothering, constricting	The room was stifling without any ventilation. / A stifling bureaucracy can kill innovation.	物理的「息苦しさ」から比喩的「抑圧」へ拡張。		
335	3342	3552	no real stakes / low stakes	/noʊ ˈriːəl steɪks/	noun phrase / informal expression	A situation in which little of importance can be gained or lost and the consequences of success or failure are minor.	low stakes, little on the line, minimal consequences, little risk	It's only a practice round, so there are no real stakes. / The conversation felt easier because it was low-stakes.	Core noun 'stakes' = what may be won, lost, risked, or affected by an outcome. Common contrasts: high stakes, low stakes, raise the stakes, have a lot at stake.		
335	3343	3553	upperclassman	/ˌʌpɚˈklæsmən/	noun (US)	In U.S. usage, a student in the later years of high school or college, traditionally a junior or senior; contrasted with an underclassman.	junior or senior (depending context), upper-year student, upper-level student	As an upperclassman, she mentors first-year students. / Most of the club officers are upperclassmen.	CORRECTION: it does not simply mean any 'senpai' or anyone in a higher grade than you; it conventionally groups juniors and seniors in a four-year U.S. school/college system. Gender-neutral alternatives such as 'upper-year student' are also used.		
335	3344	3554	YOLO	/ˈjoʊloʊ/	interjection / acronym / slang	'You only live once'; said to encourage or jokingly justify doing something bold, spontaneous, indulgent, or risky.	you only live once, live a little, seize the day, carpe diem (roughly)	We bought the concert tickets at the last minute—YOLO. / He shouted 'YOLO' before trying the ridiculous stunt.	Strongly associated with 2010s internet/pop culture and often used ironically or self-mockingly. It can imply reckless justification, unlike the broader and more reflective 'carpe diem'.		
335	3345	3556	drab	/dræb/	adjective, noun	(adj.) Lacking brightness or interest; dull, monotonous. (n.) A dull, light brownish-gray color.	dull, lackluster, monotonous, boring, faded, muted	The apartment was clean but drab. / He wore a drab uniform that blended into the background.	色や雰囲気両方に使える。		
335	3346	3558	from the vault	/frʌm ðə vɔlt/	idiomatic media phrase	Taken or released from stored archives, especially older, rare, unreleased, or previously unavailable material.	from the archives, archival, previously unreleased, unearthed	The anniversary edition includes three tracks from the vault. / Here's an old photo from the vault.	The 'vault' is figurative storage/archives. In music and entertainment, 'vault track/material' often means recorded or preserved material not previously released. The phrase can also be used playfully for one's personal archives.		
335	3347	3559	cut someone some slack	/kʌt səm slæk/	idiom (informal)	To be less strict, demanding, or critical toward someone; allow them extra tolerance or flexibility because of the circumstances.	give someone a break, go easy on someone, be lenient, ease up on someone	Cut me some slack—I've only been doing this for two days. / We should cut her some slack after the week she's had.	Productive frame: cut + PERSON + some slack. Negative: 'They didn't cut him any slack.' The literal noun 'slack' refers to looseness; figuratively it is extra room/tolerance.		
335	3349	3561	shy of	/ʃaɪ əv/	prepositional phrase	1. Slightly less than or just short of a number, amount, age, distance, or target. 2. Short of achieving or reaching something. In a separate construction, 'shy away from' means avoid.	just under, short of, less than, just below	The episode runs just shy of an hour. / We finished two points shy of the target.	Useful quantitative pattern: just shy of + NUMBER/AMOUNT. Do not confuse with adjective 'shy' = timid or 'shy away from' = avoid.		
335	3350	3562	vice versa / or vice versa	/ɔr ˌvaɪs ˈvɝsə/	adverbial phrase	With the order or relationship reversed; used to say that the reverse of the preceding statement is also true.	the other way around, conversely, in reverse	Parents can learn from children, and vice versa. / You can move the files from A to B, or vice versa.	NORMALIZATION: core expression is Latin 'vice versa'; 'or vice versa' is one common frame, not the headword itself. Pronunciation varies in English.		
336	3351	3563	nope out / nope out of	/noʊp aʊt/	phrasal verb (internet slang, informal)	To leave, withdraw from, or refuse to engage with a situation because it seems frightening, unpleasant, awkward, absurd, or otherwise unacceptable.	bail, get out, back out, duck out, walk away	I saw the huge spider and immediately noped out of the room. / Halfway through the creepy video, she noped out.	Built humorously from 'nope' as a verb. Common pattern: nope out / nope out of + PLACE/SITUATION. Strongly casual and often comedic.		
336	3352	3564	make the cut / make the cut for	/meɪk ðə kʌt/	idiom / construction	To meet the required standard or survive a selection process and therefore be included, accepted, retained, or allowed to continue. With 'for + group/event/purpose', it specifies what someone or something qualifies for.	qualify, be selected, be accepted, pass muster, earn a place/spot, qualify for	Only twelve applicants made the cut. / She made the cut for the national swimming team. / Only the best players make the cut for the finals. / That scene was good, but it didn't make the final cut of the episode.	Common in auditions, sports, hiring, rankings, and editing. Productive frame: make the cut for + TEAM/FINALS/PROGRAM/EVENT. 'The cut' can be a selection threshold; in film/editing, 'final cut' has a separate industry sense. MERGED CONSTRUCTION: WID3565 'make the cut for' was folded into this card rather than discarded as a bare duplicate.		
336	3354	3567	truncate / truncated	/ˈtrʌŋkeɪtɪd/	verb; adjective / past participle	To shorten something by cutting off or omitting a part, especially the end. In mathematics/computing, to discard digits, characters, data, or a portion rather than preserve the full value/content.	shorten, cut off, clip, abbreviate; chop (informal)	The app truncated the title after 50 characters. / The displayed decimal was truncated rather than rounded. / A truncated cone has its top cut off.	NORMAL lexical family: truncate (verb), truncated (adj./past participle), truncation (noun). In numeric contexts, truncation differs from rounding: discarded digits do not cause the retained digit to round up.		
336	3355	3569	cascade / cascading structure	/ˈkæskeɪd ˈstrʌktʃɚ/	noun; adjective / descriptive technical phrase	A 'cascade' is a sequence in which one stage, event, effect, or level leads into or triggers another. A 'cascading structure' is a descriptive term for such a hierarchical or sequential arrangement.	cascade, cascading sequence, hierarchy, sequential chain, tiered structure	One failure triggered a cascade of downstream errors. / The approval process has a cascading structure in which each level feeds into the next.	CORRECTION: 'cascade structure' is not one universal technical term across engineering, programming, organizations, and CSS. In CSS, 'the cascade' specifically describes how competing style declarations are resolved; do not define that as merely a sequential structure.		
336	3356	3570	flat list / flat enumeration	/flæt ɪˌnuməˈreɪʃən/	noun phrase (technical/descriptive)	A non-hierarchical, single-level listing in which items are presented at the same level rather than nested into parent/child groups.	flat list, single-level list, non-hierarchical enumeration	For only ten categories, a flat list is easier to scan than a nested menu. / The specification presents the values as a flat enumeration.	'Flat enumeration' is understandable technical/descriptive wording, but 'flat list' is often the more general reusable term. In programming, 'enumeration/enum' can also mean a defined set of named constants, so context matters.		
336	3357	3571	not worth doing / not worthy of	/nɑt ˈwɝði əv ˈduːɪŋ ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	construction / contrast	'Not worth doing' means an action does not justify the time, effort, cost, or trouble. 'Not worthy of + noun' means not deserving of a person, status, attention, respect, etc.; 'worthy of being + past participle' is possible but more formal.	not worth the effort, not worthwhile; undeserving of, unworthy of	That movie isn't worth watching twice. / The claim isn't worthy of serious consideration. / He felt the behavior was unworthy of a professional.	CORRECTION: 'not worthy of doing something' is generally not the natural equivalent of Japanese ～する価値がない. Prefer 'not worth doing'. Compare: worthy of praise/attention/consideration; worth watching/reading/trying.		
336	3358	3572	chevron	/ˈʃɛvrən/	noun	A V-shaped or inverted-V-shaped mark, pattern, molding, or device; used in military rank insignia, road signs/markings, heraldry, architecture, and design.	V-shaped mark, V-pattern; rank stripe (contextual)	The sergeant wore chevrons on his sleeve. / Black-and-white chevrons warned drivers of the sharp bend.	Do not equate chevron with herringbone: herringbone is a repeated broken zigzag pattern made from V-like elements, while a chevron can be a single V-shaped form.		
336	3359	3573	rhombus	/ˈrɑmbəs/	noun (geometry)	A quadrilateral with all four sides equal in length; equivalently, a parallelogram with four equal sides. Its angles need not be right angles, though a square is a special rhombus.	lozenge (shape, not always strict geometric synonym); diamond shape (informal visual label)	A square satisfies the definition of a rhombus because all four sides are equal. / The logo uses a diamond-shaped rhombus.	CORRECTION: saying 'but not necessarily with right angles' is fine, but do not imply a rhombus excludes squares. In everyday English, 'diamond' describes orientation/appearance, not a precise separate geometric class.		
336	3360	3574	besting	/ˈbɛstɪŋ/	verb (present participle of best)	Defeating or surpassing someone in a contest or competition.	outdoing, outperforming, beating, surpassing, prevailing over	She kept besting her rival in every race. / The underdog ended up besting the champion.	“best” は動詞で「打ち負かす」の意味がある。		
337	3361	3575	evaluation	/ɪˌvæljuˈeɪʃən/	noun	The process or result of systematically assessing the quality, value, effectiveness, performance, condition, or significance of someone or something.	assessment, appraisal, review, analysis, judgment	The program will undergo an independent evaluation next year. / Her performance evaluation focused on both results and teamwork.	Common frames: evaluation of + THING/PERSON; evaluation by + EVALUATOR; evaluation against/based on + CRITERIA. CORRECTION: 'evaluation on performance' is not a general rule; usually say 'evaluation of performance' or 'performance evaluation'.		
337	3362	3576	slanted	/ˈslæntɪd/	adjective	1. Positioned or running at an angle rather than straight, level, or vertical. 2. Presented from a biased, one-sided, or deliberately angled perspective.	tilted, inclined, angled; biased, skewed, one-sided	The roof is slightly slanted to let rain run off. / Critics said the documentary presented a slanted account of the dispute.	Physical pattern: slanted at/toward/to one side. Figurative: slanted coverage/report/account; often 'slanted toward/in favor of/against' a viewpoint. Verb 'slant' = angle physically or present with a bias.		
337	3363	3577	glam up	/ɡlæm ʌp/	phrasal verb (informal)	To make someone or something look more glamorous, stylish, polished, or showy, especially through clothing, makeup, decoration, or presentation.	dress up, spruce up, doll up, jazz up, make glamorous	She glammed up the simple dress with bold jewelry. / They glammed up the venue for the premiere.	Productive frame: glam + PERSON/THING + up; also 'get glammed up' = dress/style oneself glamorously. Informal and common in fashion/entertainment contexts.		
337	3364	3579	keep things modular	/kiːp θɪŋz ˈmɑdʒələr/	phrase (technical)	To design or organize a system as separate, self-contained components so that parts can be changed, tested, reused, or replaced with minimal impact on the rest.	maintain modularity, separate concerns, decouple components, use modular design	Keep the parser modular so we can replace it without rewriting the UI. / The course is modular, so individual units can be updated separately.	Not a fixed idiom but a productive design principle. 'Modular' applies beyond software: hardware, architecture, curricula, furniture, and organizational systems.		
337	3365	3580	glitch out	/ɡlɪtʃ aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal/slang)	To malfunction, behave erratically, or display a temporary technical error; sometimes used figuratively for a person momentarily freezing or acting strangely.	malfunction, bug out, freeze, crash, act up	The app glitched out and showed the same screen three times. / I totally glitched out for a second and forgot what I was saying.	Usually technology/gaming slang. Do not assume it means intentionally exploiting a glitch; that is better expressed as 'use/exploit a glitch' or 'glitch through/out of' something in gaming context.		
337	3366	3581	wobbly	/ˈwɑbli/	adjective	1. Moving or standing unsteadily; liable to shake or tip. 2. Figuratively unstable, uncertain, weak, or shaky, including a voice, argument, plan, or recovery.	shaky, unsteady, unstable, rickety; uncertain, weak	The chair is wobbly because one leg is loose. / Her voice went wobbly when she started talking about the farewell.	Useful extensions: wobbly table/legs; wobbly voice; wobbly economy/plan. Informal but common.		
337	3367	3582	chilling / chill	/ˈtʃɪlɪŋ/	adjective; verb / slang	'Chilling' as an adjective means frightening or disturbing in a way that causes a figurative chill. Separately, 'chill/chilling' in informal slang can mean relax or spend time casually.	frightening, eerie, disturbing; relaxing, hanging out (slang sense)	The documentary ends with a chilling warning. / We're just chilling at home tonight.	FIX: these are distinct uses, not one blended meaning. 'A chilling story' = frightening; 'we're chilling' comes from slang verb 'chill' = relax.		
337	3368	3583	flat-out	/ˌflæt ˈaʊt/	adverb; adjective (informal)	1. At maximum speed, effort, or intensity. 2. Completely or absolutely, especially to emphasize a judgment or refusal. 3. As an adjective, direct and unqualified.	at full speed, all-out; outright, completely, absolutely	I ran flat-out to catch the train. / That's flat-out wrong. / He gave me a flat-out refusal.	Common contrast: 'work flat-out' = work as hard/fast as possible; 'flat-out deny/refuse/wrong' = outright/completely.		
337	3369	3585	echelon	/ˈɛʃəˌlɑn/	noun	1. A level or rank within an organization, profession, society, or hierarchy. 2. A military formation in which units are arranged diagonally or in successive offset positions.	rank, level, tier, stratum; echelon formation	Only people in the upper echelons of management knew about the merger. / The aircraft flew in echelon formation.	Often plural in hierarchy sense: the upper/higher echelons of government, management, society. More formal than 'level' or 'tier'.		
337	3370	3586	obsidian	/əbˈsɪdiən/	noun	A dark, glasslike volcanic rock formed from rapidly cooled lava.	volcanic glass	The ancient tools were made of sharp obsidian. / The pendant was carved from black obsidian.	装飾品・武器・ゲーム内アイテムとしても登場。		
338	3371	3587	consummate a marriage / the marriage was consummated	/ˈfɪzɪkli ˈkɑnsəˌmeɪtɪd/	verb construction	To complete or make a marriage fully established through sexual intercourse; more broadly, 'consummate' can mean complete or finalize something.	complete, finalize; sexually consummate a marriage	The marriage was never consummated. / The agreement was consummated with the signing of the final documents.	NORMALIZATION: 'physically consummated' is understandable but not the reusable headword. In marriage context, 'consummate the marriage' / 'the marriage was consummated' strongly implies sexual intercourse. In business/legal prose, consummate can mean complete a transaction.		
338	3372	3589	shrouded / be shrouded in	/ˈʃraʊdɪd/	adjective / past participle; verb construction	Covered or wrapped so that something is hidden or obscured; figuratively, surrounded by secrecy, uncertainty, mystery, or gloom.	veiled, concealed, obscured, hidden, cloaked	The mountain was shrouded in fog. / The circumstances of his disappearance remain shrouded in mystery.	Common productive frame: be shrouded in + mist/fog/darkness/mystery/secrecy. Base verb 'shroud' = cover or conceal.		
338	3373	3590	transcendental	/ˌtrænsɛnˈdɛntl/	adjective	1. In philosophy, relating to conditions or concepts beyond ordinary empirical experience; especially associated with Kant and other philosophical traditions. 2. Relating to a spiritual or mystical realm beyond ordinary experience. 3. In mathematics, describing a number that is not algebraic—that is, not a root of any nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients.	transcendent, metaphysical (contextual); non-algebraic (math description)	Kant uses 'transcendental' in a specialized philosophical sense. / π and e are transcendental numbers.	FIX: mathematical definition should not say 'not solvable as roots of algebraic equations' loosely; precise sense is not a root of any nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients. 'Transcendental' and 'transcendent' overlap but are not interchangeable in all fields.		
338	3374	3591	auspicious	/ɔˈspɪʃəs/	adjective	Conducive to success; favorable; suggesting a positive outcome.	favorable, promising, propitious, advantageous	They chose an auspicious date for the wedding. / The sunny morning was an auspicious start to the trip.	反意語は inauspicious。		
338	3375	3592	awe / overwhelming awe	/ˌoʊvɚˈwɛlmɪŋ ɔː/	noun; compositional phrase	'Awe' is a powerful feeling of wonder, reverence, admiration, and sometimes fear in response to something vast, sublime, or extraordinary. 'Overwhelming awe' simply intensifies that feeling.	wonder, reverence, amazement, astonishment	I stood in awe of the mountains. / The scale of the cathedral filled her with overwhelming awe.	NORMALIZATION: 'overwhelming awe' is not a fixed expression. Reusable frames: be in awe of; fill someone with awe; inspire awe; awe-inspiring.		
338	3376	3595	zone out	/zoʊn aʊt/	phrasal verb (informal)	To stop paying attention and become mentally disengaged from one's surroundings or the current activity, often because of boredom, fatigue, distraction, or daydreaming.	space out, tune out, drift off mentally, lose focus	I zoned out halfway through the lecture. / Sorry—what did you say? I completely zoned out.	'Zone out' focuses on attention drifting away. 'Tune out' can also mean deliberately ignore; 'doze off' means actually begin to sleep.		
338	3377	3596	refactoring	/ˌriːˈfæktərɪŋ/	noun (software engineering)	The process of changing the internal structure of existing code while preserving its externally observable behavior, usually to improve readability, maintainability, extensibility, or design.	code restructuring, code cleanup, structural improvement	We refactored the parser before adding the new feature. / The refactoring reduced duplication without changing what the program does.	FIX: performance improvement can result, but it is not the defining goal of refactoring. Refactoring differs from adding features or fixing behavior-changing bugs.		
338	3378	3597	unbeknownst to someone	/ˌʌnbɪˈnoʊnst/	preposition-like adjective/adverbial expression	Without the knowledge of a particular person; unknown to them.	without someone's knowledge, unknown to, without someone knowing	Unbeknownst to me, they had already changed the schedule. / The decision was made unbeknownst to most of the staff.	Most natural construction: unbeknownst to + PERSON. 'Unbeknown' is an older/less common variant. Slightly literary or humorous in modern speech.		
338	3379	3598	trope	/troʊp/	noun	1. In modern storytelling/media discussion, a recurring narrative pattern, convention, character type, situation, or device. 2. In rhetoric, a figure in which a word or expression is used in a nonliteral or shifted sense.	narrative convention, recurring motif, stock device; figure of speech (rhetoric)	The reluctant hero is a familiar fantasy trope. / In classical rhetoric, metaphor is one kind of trope.	Important: 'trope' does not inherently mean cliché or overused. A trope can be common without being stale. In rhetoric, the term is older and more technical than its modern media-analysis sense.		
338	3380	3599	trope vs. metaphor	/wʌt ɪz ə troʊp ənd haʊ dʌz ɪt ˈdɪfɚ frʌm ə ˈmɛtəˌfɔr/	concept contrast / learning card	A trope is a broad term that can refer either to a recurring narrative convention in modern media discussion or, in classical rhetoric, to a figurative turn of expression. A metaphor is one specific figure of speech that describes one thing in terms of another. Thus, a metaphor can be a rhetorical trope, but many narrative tropes are not metaphors.	trope, metaphor, figure of speech, narrative convention	“Time is a thief” is a metaphor. “Star-crossed lovers” is a narrative trope, not a metaphor. / A metaphor can be a trope in the rhetorical sense, but 'trope' is broader.	INTENTIONAL-DUPLICATE VALUE: keep separately from WID3598 because this card tests a useful contrast rather than merely defining 'trope'. Also distinguish trope from meme: a meme is cultural content/pattern that spreads; a trope is a recurring narrative or rhetorical form.		
339	3381	3601	materialistic	/məˌtɪriəˈlɪstɪk/	adjective	Excessively concerned with money, possessions, consumption, or material comfort, especially at the expense of nonmaterial values such as relationships, ideals, or spirituality.	money-oriented, acquisitive, consumerist, possession-focused	He became more materialistic as his income rose. / The film criticizes a materialistic culture obsessed with status symbols.	FIX: 'practical, realistic, pragmatic, utilitarian, matter-of-fact' are not synonyms of materialistic. Also distinguish philosophical 'materialist/materialistic' relating to materialism, though everyday use usually concerns possessions and wealth.		
339	3382	3602	hit home	hit home	idiom	To affect someone deeply or make the truth, significance, or personal relevance of something suddenly feel real and clear.	strike a chord, resonate, sink in, have a strong impact	The warning really hit home after a friend had the same problem. / Her comment hit home because I'd been thinking the exact same thing.	Common pattern: X hit home; X hit home for/with someone. Often combines emotional impact with realization. Also literal baseball use exists separately.	感情や認識への影響を表す。	
339	3383	3603	after-hours / NSFW	after hours (NSFW)	adjective/adverb; internet label	'After-hours' means occurring outside normal business or operating hours. 'NSFW' ('not safe for work') is a separate internet label warning that content may be inappropriate to view in a workplace, often because of sexual, graphic, or otherwise sensitive material.	after closing, outside business hours; not safe for work	The museum held an after-hours event on Friday. / The link was marked NSFW because it contained explicit images.	NORMALIZATION: do not teach 'after hours' as inherently sexual. An event may be both after-hours and NSFW, but the two ideas are independent.	NSFW は “Not Safe For Work” の略で、成人向けや職場で不適切な内容を示す。	
339	3384	3605	steam: lose steam / let off steam / build up steam	a bit of steam	idiom family / noun	'Steam' is used figuratively for energy, momentum, pressure, or pent-up emotion. 'Lose steam' = lose energy/momentum; 'let off steam' = release tension; 'build/gather steam' = gain momentum.	lose momentum, run out of energy; vent; gain momentum	The campaign lost steam after the first week. / I went for a walk to let off steam. / The project is finally gathering steam.	NORMALIZATION: 'a bit of steam' alone is not the most useful reusable phrase. Teach the productive idioms built around steam. Literal steam remains the vapor produced by boiling water.	“blow off steam” の変形としても用いられる。	
339	3385	3606	gloss over	/ɡlɔs ˈoʊvər/	phrasal verb	To mention or deal with something only briefly or superficially, especially in order to avoid focusing on an awkward, serious, or inconvenient detail.	skim over, skate over, downplay, brush aside, pass quickly over	The report glosses over the cost overruns. / He tried to gloss over why he had missed the deadline.	FIX: correct typo/etymology note—base word is 'gloss', not 'loss'. 'Gloss over' historically relates to putting a gloss/interpretation or smooth surface over something; synchronically, learn it as 'treat superficially or conceal difficulty'.		
339	3386	3607	hustle vs. hassle	hustle /ˈhʌsəl/, hassle /ˈhæsəl/	verb/noun contrast	'Hustle' commonly means move or work quickly and energetically, or engage in energetic effort; in some contexts it can mean pressure, scam, or pursue money/work aggressively. 'Hassle' means a nuisance/difficulty, or to bother, pressure, or harass someone.	hustle: hurry, work hard, bustle; hassle: bother, inconvenience, harass	We had to hustle to catch the train. / Getting the refund was a hassle. / Stop hassling me about the paperwork.	Useful pronunciation contrast: hustle /ˈhʌsəl/ vs hassle /ˈhæsəl/. Their meanings are unrelated despite similar spelling/sound.		
339	3387	3611	runner-up	/ˌrʌnər ˈʌp/	noun	A person, team, or entry that finishes immediately behind the winner; most commonly second place, though in some contests multiple runners-up may be recognized.	second-place finisher, finalist, silver medalist (when applicable)	She was runner-up in last year's tournament. / The two runners-up received smaller prizes.	Plural: runners-up. FIX: remove distracting/rare 'proxime accessit' and unsupported election-duty claim. 'Runner-up' does not automatically inherit the winner's role.		
339	3388	3612	ewe	ewe	/juː/	noun	An adult female sheep.	female sheep	The farmer keeps several ewes for breeding. / The ewe watched over her lambs in the field.	発音は “you” と同じ。	
339	3389	3613	buffer space	buffer space	noun phrase	Extra space deliberately left between objects, regions, values, or activities to reduce interference, prevent collisions/overlap, absorb variation, or provide a safety margin.	safety margin, clearance, separation gap, reserve space	Leave some buffer space around the text so it doesn't touch the edge. / The trucks need a buffer space between them when parking.	Compositional phrase based on 'buffer'. In layout/UI work it means extra spacing; in physical planning it can mean clearance or separation; context determines the exact kind of margin.	コンピュータや輸送安全分野での使用が多い。関連語 “buffer” も参照。	
339	3390	3614	buffer	buffer	noun; verb	(n.) Something that cushions, separates, protects, or reduces the effect of a shock/change; in computing, a temporary storage area used while data is being transferred or processed. (v.) To cushion or reduce impact; in computing, to store data temporarily.	cushion, shield, safeguard, reserve; temporary storage	Emergency savings give us a buffer against unexpected bills. / The video buffers a few seconds of data before playback. / Trees can buffer the neighborhood from traffic noise.	Productive uses: buffer against/from + RISK; financial/emotional/safety buffer; data buffer; buffering video. Chemistry also uses 'buffer' for a system resisting changes in pH.	“buffer space” は物理的な間隔を指すが、“buffer” は抽象的な保護や一時保存にも使える。	
340	3391	3615	debug	debug	/diˈbʌɡ/	verb (computing)	To identify and remove errors or problems from software or hardware.	troubleshoot, correct, fix, repair	The engineers are debugging the new application. / She spent hours debugging the program to find the syntax error.	ソフトウェア開発に必須の工程。	
340	3392	3616	media query	media query	/ˈmiːdiə ˈkwɛri/	noun (computing)	In web development, a CSS technique used to apply styles based on device characteristics such as screen size, resolution, or orientation.	responsive design rule, CSS condition	The website uses media queries to adjust its layout for smartphones. / Media queries make responsive design possible.	モバイルフレンドリーなサイト作成の基礎。	
340	3393	3618	copy someone in / copy in data	copy in	verb constructions	1. 'Copy someone in (on a message/thread)' = include them as a recipient so they receive the communication. 2. 'Copy in data/text' = copy and insert it into a destination.	CC someone, include someone; paste/copy data into	Please copy me in on your reply to the client. / Copy the new values into the spreadsheet.	NORMALIZATION: distinguish the two constructions. For email, especially BrE: copy someone in / copy someone in on X; AmE often simply 'CC someone'. For data, 'copy X into Y' is usually more natural than 'copy in X into Y'.	主に電子メールやデータ管理で使用される。	
340	3394	3619	squint / squinting	squinting	verb; noun; participle	To partly close or narrow the eyes, typically to see more clearly, protect them from bright light, or examine something closely.	narrow one's eyes, peer (related), screw up one's eyes	I had to squint to read the tiny text. / She was squinting into the bright sunlight.	FIX: 'glaring' is not a synonym; glare involves an angry/intense look or strong light. Squinting can sometimes accompany suspicion or scrutiny, but the core action is narrowing the eyes.	名詞 “squint” は「斜視」の意味もある。	
340	3395	3620	forge / forged	forged	verb; adjective / past participle	1. Shape metal by heating and hammering or pressing it. 2. Create or build something through effort, such as a relationship, path, alliance, or identity. 3. Make a fraudulent imitation of a document, signature, currency, etc.	shape, hammer; build, form, create; counterfeit, falsify	The smith forged the blade from steel. / The crisis forged a strong bond between them. / Someone forged her signature.	NORMALIZATION: use base form 'forge'. The senses 'create through effort' and 'counterfeit' are both very common; context distinguishes them.	意味は文脈で大きく異なる。	
340	3396	3621	blob / BLOB	blob	noun; computing acronym/term	1. A small shapeless lump, drop, or mass, especially of something soft or thick. 2. In computing/database contexts, BLOB ('binary large object') is data stored as a single binary object, such as an image or file.	lump, glob, drop, mass; binary large object	A blob of paint landed on the floor. / The database stores the image in a BLOB column.	In ordinary English, 'blob' is descriptive and informal. In computing, BLOB is often expanded as 'binary large object'; capitalization depends on style/context.	ITでは Binary Large Object の略語としても使われる。	
340	3397	3622	air / be aired (TV, radio, streaming)	it will air or it will be aired (anime, tv show)	verb / passive construction	For a program, episode, interview, or broadcast: 'X airs at/on...' means it is broadcast/shown; 'X is aired by/on...' is the passive form and emphasizes the broadcaster or act of transmission.	broadcast, be broadcast, be shown, be transmitted	The new episode will air on Friday. / The interview will be aired on national television.	Both are grammatical, but active intransitive 'the episode airs...' is especially natural for schedules/announcements in AmE. 'Air' can also mean broadcast something transitively: 'The network will air the finale.'	アニメや番組表では 'will air' が自然。受け身は出来事を強調する場合に使う。	
340	3398	3624	click with someone / click for someone	it clicked with me so well	idiom / phrasal-style construction	1. If an idea 'clicks with/for someone', it suddenly makes sense or feels intuitively right. 2. If two people 'click', they connect easily and get along well.	make sense, fall into place, resonate; hit it off (people)	That explanation really clicked with me. / Once I saw the diagram, it finally clicked for me. / We clicked right away when we met.	FIX: 'it clicked me' is not standard. Use 'it clicked with me', 'it clicked for me', or simply 'it clicked'. 'Click with someone' can also mean two people have natural rapport.	原形は 'click with someone'。 'it clicked me' は非標準。	
340	3399	3625	harbor / harboring	harboring	verb	1. Give shelter or refuge to someone, especially someone being sought. 2. Keep or hold a thought, feeling, suspicion, resentment, hope, etc., often privately and for some time.	shelter, hide; hold, entertain, nurse, foster	They were accused of harboring a fugitive. / She still harbors doubts about the plan. / He harbored a grudge for years.	NORMALIZATION: use base form 'harbor'. Common abstract objects: doubts, suspicion, resentment, feelings, hope, a grudge. BrE spelling: harbour.	感情・犯罪者など、物理的/抽象的対象に使える。	
340	3400	3626	dip into	something dips into	phrasal verb	1. Put part of something briefly into a liquid or other substance. 2. Use some of a stored resource, especially money or savings. 3. Read, sample, or explore something briefly rather than continuously.	draw on, tap into, use some of; sample, browse	We had to dip into our emergency savings. / I like to dip into that book whenever I need an example. / She dipped the brush into the paint.	NORMALIZATION: teach the reusable phrasal verb 'dip into', not 'something dips into'. Common objects: savings/funds, a book, a subject, a pool/liquid.	'dip into' は比喩的にも物理的にも使える。	
341	3401	3627	hijinks	hijinks	/ˈhaɪˌdʒɪŋks/	noun (plural)	Playful, mischievous, or rowdy activities.	antics, mischief, pranks, shenanigans	The movie is full of holiday hijinks. / The kids got into some hijinks after school.	常に複数形扱い。	
341	3402	3628	contrived	contrived	/kənˈtraɪvd/	Created or arranged deliberately in a way that feels artificial, forced, implausible, or too conveniently engineered rather than natural or convincing.	forced, artificial, labored, manufactured, implausible	The misunderstanding felt contrived because nobody asked the obvious question. / The ending relies on a contrived coincidence.	Often negative in reviews of plots, dialogue, situations, or explanations. A thing can be deliberately constructed without being 'contrived'; the negative word implies the construction shows.	否定的ニュアンスが多い。	
341	3403	3629	lackadaisical	lackadaisical	/ˌlækəˈdeɪzɪkəl/	Showing little energy, enthusiasm, care, or effort; lazily indifferent or halfhearted.	lethargic, listless, halfhearted, careless, apathetic	His lackadaisical attitude toward the deadline frustrated the team. / They played a lackadaisical first half and fell behind.	Usually critical. Describes attitude, effort, manner, or performance rather than simply physical tiredness.	ややフォーマルまたは文学的。	
341	3404	3630	slapdash	slapdash	adjective; adverb	Done quickly and carelessly, with little planning, precision, or attention to quality.	careless, hasty, sloppy, rough-and-ready, makeshift	The report looked slapdash, with missing citations and inconsistent formatting. / They repaired it slapdash and had to redo the work later.	Stronger than merely 'quick'. The key idea is haste plus poor care/quality.	名詞としてはあまり使われない。	
341	3405	3633	tremble with/from something	tremble with	verb construction	To shake involuntarily or slightly, often because of fear, cold, weakness, anger, excitement, or another strong physical/emotional cause.	shake, quiver, shiver, quake	She trembled with fear. / His hands were trembling from the cold. / He trembled with excitement before going onstage.	FIX: not 'tremble of something'. Common frames: tremble with + EMOTION; tremble from + CAUSE; tremble at + thought/prospect; tremble for + someone's safety (less common).	'tremble of something' は詩的または文学的表現として、感情や物理的原因を示すときに使われることがある（例：a tremble of anticipation）。	
341	3406	3634	unapologetic	unapologetic	/ˌʌnəˌpɑləˈdʒɛtɪk/	Not sorry or regretful about one's actions, beliefs, identity, style, or position; openly refusing to apologize or back down.	unrepentant, unashamed, defiant, unabashed	She remains unapologetic about criticizing the policy. / The film is unapologetically sentimental.	Not limited to wrongdoing. It can be positive/admiring: 'unapologetically nerdy/bold'. Adverb: unapologetically.	ポジティブに『信念を曲げない』意味でも使われる。	
341	3407	3635	scalpel	scalpel	/ˈskælpəl/	noun	A small and extremely sharp bladed instrument used for surgery or dissection.	surgical knife, lancet	The surgeon picked up the scalpel to make the first incision.	比喩的に『鋭い分析』にも使われることがある。	
341	3408	3636	pickaxe	pickaxe	/ˈpɪkˌæks/	noun	A tool with a heavy metal head having a pointed end and a chisel end, used for breaking up hard ground or rock.	mattock, miner's tool	The workers used pickaxes to break through the rocky soil.	鉱業や建設で使用。ゲーム文化でも採掘道具として登場する。	
341	3409	3637	CTA styles / call-to-action styling	CTA styles	noun phrase (design/marketing)	The visual treatment and design choices used for calls to action—such as buttons, links, banners, or prompts—to make the desired next action clear and noticeable.	call-to-action design, CTA button styling, conversion-focused UI styling	We tested two CTA styles: a filled button and a simple text link. / Consistent call-to-action styling makes the next step easier to spot.	NORMALIZATION: CTA = call to action. This is a compositional design phrase, not a fixed lexical item. Useful related terms: CTA button, CTA copy, primary/secondary CTA, conversion.	CTAは『ユーザーに行動を促す要素』。色・形・配置・文言などが成果に直結する。	
341	3410	3638	pitfall	pitfall	/ˈpɪtˌfɔl/	A hidden, easily overlooked, or unexpected danger, difficulty, or source of failure in a plan, process, choice, or situation.	trap, hazard, hidden danger, catch, potential problem	One pitfall of automating the process is that bad input can spread quickly. / The guide explains common pitfalls for first-time visitors.	Common frame: the pitfalls of + ACTIVITY/APPROACH; avoid a pitfall; common/potential pitfall. More specific than generic 'drawback' because a pitfall is something you may fall into or fail to foresee.	元々は『落とし穴』の意味。	
342	3411	3639	drawback	drawback	/ˈdrɔˌbæk/	A disadvantage, negative feature, or limitation that makes an option, plan, product, or situation less attractive or useful.	disadvantage, downside, limitation, shortcoming, minus	The main drawback of the apartment is the long commute. / One drawback to working from home is the lack of casual office interaction.	Common frames: drawback of/to + NOUN/GERUND. Contrast pitfall = hidden/unexpected risk; drawback = known negative feature.	ポジティブな面とセットで議論されることが多い。	
342	3412	3640	snag	snag	/snæɡ/	(n.) 1. A small unexpected problem or obstacle. 2. A tear/pull caused when fabric catches on something. (v.) 1. Catch or tear on something. 2. Obtain or secure something quickly or successfully, often informally.	hitch, obstacle, complication; catch, tear; grab, score, land	We hit a snag when the supplier canceled. / My sleeve snagged on a nail. / She snagged the last ticket.	Useful idiom: hit a snag = encounter an unexpected problem. Informal acquisition sense: snag a deal/ticket/job/table.	カジュアルな場面で使われることが多い。	
342	3413	3641	jack of all trades	jack of all trades	idiom / noun phrase	A person competent at many different kinds of work or skills rather than specializing narrowly in one.	generalist, all-rounder, versatile person, multiskilled person	He's a jack of all trades who can fix the plumbing, edit video, and build a website.	The familiar longer saying is 'jack of all trades, master of none', which can be mildly critical; the shorter phrase alone can be complimentary. 'Jack' is generic here, not necessarily referring to a man.	フル表現は『jack of all trades, master of none』で、器用貧乏の意味合いも含む。	
342	3414	3643	jag	/dʒæɡ/	noun; verb	1. A sharp projection, notch, or uneven point; as a verb, to cut or pierce with such a point (rare). 2. Informally, a period or bout of intense activity, emotion, or indulgence, as in 'a crying jag' or 'a spending jag'.	sharp point, notch; bout, spree, binge	The torn metal had a dangerous jagged edge. / She went on a crying jag after the call.	FIX: modern 'jag' is most useful in expressions like 'crying jag', 'spending jag', etc. The intoxication sense is dated/less common and need not be foregrounded. Related adjective: jagged.		
342	3415	3644	hitch	/hɪtʃ/	noun, verb	(n.) 1. A temporary fastening/knot or a device for attaching something. 2. A minor unexpected problem or delay. (v.) Fasten or attach; obtain a ride ('hitch a ride').	fastening, attachment; snag, minor problem; attach, fasten	We hitched the trailer to the truck. / The event went off without a hitch. / Can I hitch a ride with you?	High-value expressions: without a hitch = without problems; hit a hitch = encounter a problem; hitch a ride = get a lift.		
342	3416	3645	loopy	/ˈluːpi/	adjective	Informally, somewhat crazy, silly, eccentric, confused, or mentally foggy, often temporarily from fatigue, medication, stress, etc.	goofy, kooky, spaced-out, foggy, eccentric	I get a little loopy when I'm badly sleep-deprived. / The medication made him feel loopy.	Usually light/informal, but describing another person's mental state as 'loopy' can sound dismissive. Also literal senses related to loops exist in technical contexts.		
342	3417	3646	signboard / sign	/ˈsaɪnˌbɔːrd/	noun	A board or panel displaying a sign, notice, name, direction, or advertisement. In everyday AmE, 'sign' is often more natural for storefront and roadside signage.	sign, sign panel, placard; billboard (large advertising sign)	The old wooden signboard above the shop had faded lettering. / The sign outside says the café closes at six.	'Signboard' is standard English but can sound more literal/formal than simply 'sign'. A billboard is specifically a large outdoor advertising display, not a synonym in every context.		
342	3418	3648	iteration	/ˌɪtəˈreɪʃənz/	noun	One repetition or version in a repeated process of testing, revising, or developing something; also the act/process of repeating.	version, cycle, pass, repetition, revision round	The third iteration of the design finally solved the navigation problem. / We improved the script through several iterations.	Common in software, design, engineering, math, and product development. 'Iterate on X' = repeatedly refine X.		
342	3419	3649	parchment	/ˈpɑːrtʃmənt/	noun	1. A writing material traditionally made from specially prepared animal skin. 2. A document or surface resembling it. 'Parchment paper' is a separate modern heat-resistant paper used in cooking/baking.	prepared animal skin; vellum (related, not always identical); baking parchment/parchment paper	The medieval text was copied onto parchment. / Line the tray with parchment paper before baking.	FIX: parchment paper is not the same material as historical parchment. 'Vellum' traditionally refers to a finer kind of prepared skin, though terminology varies historically.		
342	3420	3650	breadcrumbs / breadcrumb navigation	/ˈbrɛdˌkrʌmz fɔːr ˈwɛbˌpeɪdʒ/	noun (plural, web design)	A navigation trail showing the user's current location within a site's hierarchy, typically as a sequence such as Home > Category > Page.	breadcrumb trail, hierarchical navigation path	Breadcrumbs let users jump back to the parent category without using the Back button.	NORMALIZATION: standard web term is 'breadcrumbs' or 'breadcrumb navigation', not 'breadcrumbs for webpage'. Inspired by the trail motif from Hansel and Gretel. Useful for orientation and hierarchical navigation, but not literally 'essential' on every site.		
343	3421	3651	personal manifesto	/ˈpɜːrsənl ˌmænɪˈfɛstoʊ/	noun phrase	A written declaration of one's core beliefs, principles, values, goals, or intended way of acting.	statement of principles, personal creed, declaration of intent, values statement	She wrote a personal manifesto describing the principles she wanted to follow in her creative work.	Compositional phrase rather than a fixed genre with one required format. Can be private/self-development oriented or public/activist/creative depending context.		
343	3422	3652	loom	/luːm/	verb; noun	(v.) Appear as a large, often indistinct shape; or seem imminent and potentially threatening/important. (n.) A machine or frame used for weaving cloth.	appear, tower, emerge; approach, threaten; weaving frame/machine	A dark shape loomed through the fog. / The deadline is looming. / She wove the fabric on a hand loom.	High-value figurative use: deadline/problem/crisis looms = it is approaching and feels significant or threatening. Common frame: loom over + PERSON/PLACE.		
343	3423	3653	line break / break a line / start a new line	/breɪk ðə laɪn/	noun; verb constructions (text/formatting)	A 'line break' is the point where text ends on one line and continues on the next. To create one, you can 'insert a line break', 'start a new line', or in some technical contexts 'break the line'. Automatic movement at the margin is 'line wrapping'.	newline, line break; start a new line, insert a newline; wrap	Insert a line break after the heading. / The editor wraps long lines automatically. / In HTML, <br> inserts a line break.	FIX: 'break the line' is not the best general headword for 改行する. Distinguish manual line break/newline from automatic line wrapping.		
343	3424	3654	serve/act as a buffer	/æz ə ˈbʌfər/	verb construction	To function as something that cushions, separates, protects, mediates, or reduces the impact between two sides, forces, risks, or systems.	serve as a cushion, act as a shield, provide a buffer, mediate, absorb impact	Emergency savings can act as a buffer against unexpected expenses. / A neutral colleague served as a buffer between the two managers. / Extra memory can serve as a buffer during bursts of data.	INTENTIONAL-CONSTRUCTION VALUE: keep separately from WID3614 'buffer' because this card teaches the productive frame serve/act as a buffer + against/between. WID3614 teaches the lexical senses; this card teaches how to deploy the noun naturally in a construction.		
343	3425	3655	attribution	/ˌætrɪˈbjuːʃən/	noun	The act of identifying or crediting the source, creator, author, or cause of something; also, the process of assigning responsibility or causation.	credit, acknowledgment, source identification; assignment of cause	The article reproduced the image with proper attribution. / Researchers disagreed about the attribution of the effect to one particular cause.	Broader than copyright credit alone. Common areas: source/author attribution; causal attribution in psychology; attribution models in marketing; attribution of a quotation/artwork.		
343	3426	3656	to the brim / brim with	/ðə brɪm wɪð/	idiom / verb construction	'To the brim' means completely full up to the edge of a container. Figuratively, 'filled/full to the brim with X' means completely full of X. 'Brim with X' means be full of a feeling, quality, or thing.	completely full, overflowing, packed; be full of	The glass was filled to the brim. / Her eyes were brimming with tears. / The episode is brimming with clever visual jokes.	FIX: not 'the brim with' as a standalone phrase. Useful frames: full/filled to the brim with; brim with; be brimming with.		
343	3427	3657	duality / dualities	/duːˈælətiːz/	noun	A state, concept, or structure involving two contrasting, complementary, or interrelated aspects; plural 'dualities' refers to multiple such pairs or two-sided relationships.	two-sidedness, polarity, opposition, paired contrast; dichotomy (not always exact)	The novel explores the duality of freedom and responsibility. / Its characters embody several moral dualities.	'Duality' does not always imply a strict either/or split; 'dichotomy' more strongly suggests division into two contrasting categories.		
343	3428	3658	stand-in for	/stænd ɪn fɔːr ðə ˈmæsɪz/	noun phrase / construction	A substitute or representative that takes the place of another person or thing; figuratively, something used to represent a larger group, idea, or category.	substitute, proxy, surrogate, representative	The actor used a stand-in while the lights were being set. / The character functions as a stand-in for the audience. / In the essay, one family becomes a stand-in for the masses.	Reusable frame: X is/serves as a stand-in for Y. Literal entertainment-industry sense and figurative representative/proxy sense are both common.		
343	3429	3659	the humanities	/hjuːˈmænɪtiz/	plural noun	Academic disciplines concerned with human culture, thought, expression, and interpretation, such as literature, philosophy, history, languages, religious studies, and many of the arts.	humanistic disciplines, arts and humanities (contextual), liberal arts (broader category)	She studied philosophy and literature in the humanities. / Funding for the humanities includes support for archives, languages, and historical research.	FIX: social sciences are generally distinguished from the humanities, though boundaries can overlap. 'Liberal arts' is broader and may include humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, and mathematics depending institution. Humanities ≠ simply Japanese 文系.		
343	3430	3660	science / STEM	/ˈsaɪəns/	noun; acronym/category	'Science' is the systematic investigation of the natural and social world through observation, evidence, theory, and testing, depending on the field. 'STEM' is a broader educational/professional grouping: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics.	natural science, scientific disciplines; STEM fields	She studied physics, one of the natural sciences. / The scholarship supports students across STEM, including engineering and mathematics.	FIX: science and STEM are not synonyms. STEM includes fields beyond science, while 'science' itself can include natural and social sciences depending context. Japanese 理系 is culturally broader/different and should not be treated as an exact translation.		
344	3431	3661	sulky	sulky	/ˈsʌlki/	adjective	Bad-tempered or resentful, typically in a silent and withdrawn way.	moody, sullen, petulant	He sat in the corner with a sulky expression after losing the game.	Describes a mood of silent resentment or pouting. 日本語では「ふてくされた」「不機嫌な」にあたる。	
344	3432	3662	vine	/vaɪn/	noun	A plant with long flexible stems that trail along the ground or climb/support themselves on other structures; some vines use tendrils, twining stems, roots, or other mechanisms.	climbing plant, trailing plant, creeper (contextual)	Grapes grow on vines trained along wires. / A vine climbed the fence and covered most of the wall.	Do not assume every vine has tendrils. 'Vine' is also used in compounds such as grapevine. Figurative uses exist but are less central than the botanical sense.		
344	3433	3663	nested	/ˈnɛstɪd/	adjective / past participle	Placed, organized, or contained inside another structure of the same or related kind, often at multiple hierarchical levels.	embedded, contained, hierarchical, layered	The folders are nested three levels deep. / The function contains a nested loop. / The HTML elements are incorrectly nested.	Common in computing: nested folders, loops, functions, conditions, elements, data structures. A nested structure is specifically inside another, not merely 'layered'.		
344	3434	3664	mind you	/maɪnd juː/	discourse marker (informal/conversational)	Used to add a qualification, contrast, reminder, or important afterthought to what was just said—roughly 'though', 'however', or 'I should add'.	though, however, that said, I should add	The room is tiny. Mind you, the rent is surprisingly low. / He's difficult to work with—mind you, he's brilliant at the technical side.	Often parenthetical and conversational. It does not literally mean 'pay attention to me'; it signals a qualifying comment or counterpoint.		
344	3435	3665	blurb	/blɝbz/	noun; verb	A short descriptive or promotional piece of text, especially on a book cover/jacket, webpage, film page, product page, etc.; also, a brief endorsement quote.	promotional copy, short description, endorsement, jacket copy	The back-cover blurb gives away too much of the plot. / The publisher asked several authors to provide blurbs.	'Blurb' can mean neutral short descriptive copy or explicitly promotional praise depending context. Verb: blurb a book = provide promotional copy/endorsement (less common).		
344	3436	3666	money well spent	/ˈmʌni ˌwɛl ˈspɛnt/	phrase	Money used in a way that proves worthwhile, useful, enjoyable, or good value.	worthwhile expense, good investment, good value	The tickets were expensive, but it was money well spent.	FIX: usually written without a hyphen after the noun: 'money well spent'. Hyphenation may appear attributively in other constructions, but this fixed phrase is normally open.		
344	3437	3667	well worth the price	/wɛl wɝːθ ðə praɪs/	phrase	Clearly worth paying the stated price because the quality, benefit, experience, or result justifies the cost.	worth the cost, worth every penny, good value	The tour is expensive, but it's well worth the price. / For the build quality alone, the upgrade is well worth the cost.	'Well worth' intensifies 'worth'. Related frames: well worth the money/time/effort; worth every penny. 'Worth the price' does not necessarily mean cheaper than alternatives—only that the value justifies the cost.		
344	3438	3668	contemplative	/kənˈtɛmpleɪtɪv/	adjective	Deeply thoughtful, reflective, or meditative; encouraging or characterized by sustained inward thought.	reflective, meditative, pensive, introspective, thoughtful	The film becomes quieter and more contemplative in its final act. / He sat in contemplative silence by the window.	Can describe a person, mood, artwork, practice, or atmosphere. 'Pensive' can carry more sadness/worry; 'contemplative' emphasizes sustained reflection.		
344	3439	3670	wisp	/wɪsp/	noun	A small, thin, light strand, tuft, streak, or trace of something, especially smoke, cloud, hair, grass, or a faint sign of a feeling/quality.	strand, tendril, trace, streak, tuft	A wisp of smoke rose from the candle. / A few wisps of hair fell across her face. / There was a wisp of a smile.	Common frame: a wisp of + smoke/cloud/hair. Conveys thinness, lightness, and small quantity.		
344	3440	3671	tendril	/ˈtɛndrəl/	noun	A slender, curling plant organ that coils around a support and helps a climbing plant hold on; figuratively, a thin curling strand or extension resembling one.	clasping shoot, curling stem; curl, strand (figurative)	The pea plant's tendrils wrapped around the wire. / Tendrils of smoke drifted toward the ceiling.	Botanically, a tendril is a specialized climbing structure, not simply any vine shoot. Figurative uses often describe smoke, hair, mist, or branching shapes.		
345	3441	3672	wispy	/ˈwɪspi/	adjective	Thin, light, fine, delicate, or sparse in a way that resembles wisps.	fine, feathery, filmy, faint, sparse	Wispy clouds stretched across the sky. / He had a few wispy strands of hair over his forehead.	Common with clouds, smoke, hair, beard, fabric, and thin shapes. Can imply attractive delicacy or insufficient thinness/sparseness depending context.		
345	3442	3673	snollygoster	/ˈsnɑːliˌɡɑːstər/	noun (rare/humorous, chiefly historical U.S.)	A shrewd, unprincipled person, especially a politician motivated by personal advantage rather than principle.	opportunist, schemer, unscrupulous politician, political operator	The columnist jokingly dismissed the candidate as an old-fashioned snollygoster.	Rare, colorful 19th-century U.S. slang that is occasionally revived for humorous political commentary. Not a normal modern everyday word; register is intentionally quirky/archaic.		
345	3443	3674	brouhaha	/ˈbruːhɑːhɑː/	noun (informal)	A noisy fuss, uproar, or excited public reaction, often over something relatively minor or overblown.	uproar, commotion, hubbub, hullabaloo, fuss	The casting announcement caused a huge brouhaha online.	Often humorous or mildly dismissive: calling something a 'brouhaha' can imply people are making a big fuss.		
345	3444	3675	kerfuffle	/kərˈfʌfəl/	noun (informal, humorous)	A fuss, commotion, or minor disturbance, often caused by disagreement, confusion, or a small conflict.	fuss, commotion, ruckus, flap, brouhaha	There was a brief kerfuffle over who had reserved the room.	Light/humorous tone and especially associated with British/Commonwealth usage, though widely understood elsewhere. Usually less serious-sounding than 'uproar'.		
345	3445	3676	jazzed about / be jazzed	/aɪm nɑt ˈsuːpər ˈdʒæzd əˈbaʊt ɪt/	adjective / informal slang	Very excited, enthusiastic, or pleased about something. 'I'm not super jazzed about it' softens a negative reaction to mean 'I'm not especially enthusiastic about it.'	excited about, enthusiastic about, pumped about, thrilled about	I'm pretty jazzed about the trip. / I'm not super jazzed about changing the plan again.	NORMALIZATION: teach reusable 'be jazzed about + NOUN/GERUND'. Informal, especially North American. The sentence-form source expression is a softened negative use of the same construction.		
345	3446	3677	runner-up	/ˌrʌnər ˈʌp/	noun	A person, team, or entry that finishes immediately behind the winner; most commonly second place.	second-place finisher, finalist (broader), silver medalist (when applicable)	She was runner-up in the national spelling bee. / Two runners-up received smaller prizes.	INTENTIONAL-DUPLICATE CHECK: WID3611 already teaches runner-up in greater depth. Keep this card for now rather than deleting automatically; normalize spelling to hyphenated 'runner-up' and preserve position. Plural: runners-up.		
345	3447	3678	aria / ARIA	/ˈɑːriə/	noun; web accessibility acronym	1. An aria is an extended solo vocal piece, especially in opera, usually with instrumental accompaniment. 2. ARIA in web development stands for Accessible Rich Internet Applications, a WAI specification whose roles/states/properties help expose interface semantics to assistive technologies.	operatic solo; Accessible Rich Internet Applications	The soprano sang a famous aria in the second act. / The button uses an ARIA label so a screen reader can announce its purpose.	FIX: distinguish unrelated terms by capitalization/context. ARIA attributes do not automatically make a site accessible; native semantic HTML is generally preferred when available, with ARIA supplementing semantics where needed.		
345	3448	3680	purposely vs. purposefully	/ˈpɜːrpəsli/	adverb contrast	'Purposely' means intentionally or on purpose. 'Purposefully' can also mean intentionally, but often emphasizes acting with determination, resolve, or a clear purpose.	purposely: intentionally, deliberately; purposefully: deliberately, with purpose, resolutely	He purposely left the door unlocked. / She walked purposefully toward the stage.	The two words overlap, but 'purposely' is the clearest choice for simply 'intentionally'; 'purposefully' often adds manner/resolve.		
345	3449	3681	tend / tending	/ˈtɛndɪŋ/	verb	1. Care for, look after, or attend to someone/something. 2. Be likely or inclined to behave or develop in a particular way.	care for, look after, attend to; be inclined, be likely	She tends the garden every morning. / I tend to overthink small decisions.	NORMALIZATION: use base form 'tend'. Important frames: tend + OBJECT = care for; tend to + VERB = be inclined/likely to.		
345	3450	3682	riff on/off	/rɪf ɒf/	phrasal verb / creative expression	To improvise, elaborate, or create variations based on an existing idea, theme, joke, style, or work.	improvise on, build on, play off, vary, elaborate on	The episode riffs on classic detective movies. / The comedian riffed off a comment from the audience.	'Riff on' is especially common for creative variations on a theme; 'riff off' also occurs, particularly when emphasizing the source one is building from. Originates in music, where a riff is a recurring phrase.		
346	3451	3683	learning curve	/ˈlɜrnɪŋ kɜrv/	noun	A relationship between learning/practice and performance over time; in everyday speech, the amount or difficulty of learning needed to become competent at something.	learning progression, rate of learning, learning difficulty (informal sense)	There's a steep learning curve when you first start using the software. / After a few weeks, the learning curve flattened as the team became proficient.	FIX/NUANCE: technically, a steep learning curve can mean rapid improvement with experience, but popular usage often uses 'steep learning curve' to mean 'difficult to learn'. Be aware of the mismatch between technical and colloquial interpretations.		
346	3452	3684	SEO (search engine optimization)	/ˌɛs.iːˈoʊ/	noun / initialism	The practice of improving a website or content so it can be discovered and rank appropriately in unpaid/organic search results.	search engine optimization, organic search optimization	We improved the page titles, content structure, internal links, and technical setup as part of the site's SEO work.	Includes on-page/content work (search intent, keywords/topics, titles, content quality), technical/site-structure work (crawlability, performance, indexing, internal linking), and authority/link-related work such as backlinks. Distinguish SEO from paid search advertising/SEM. MERGE NOTE: useful details from WID3769 are folded here, so WID3769 can be removed without losing learning value.		
346	3453	3685	fluff	/flʌf/	noun; verb (informal, polysemous)	1. Soft, light material or loose fibers. 2. Superficial or nonessential content added without much substance. 3. A mistake in a performance/line. As a verb, make something fuller/fluffier or make a mistake in delivery.	lint, soft fibers; filler, padding; mistake, flub	The article has useful data but a lot of fluff. / She fluffed the pillows before the guests arrived. / The actor fluffed one line and recovered quickly.	Do not assume all 'fluff' is negative: literal fluff is neutral, while content 'fluff' often means low-substance filler. Related informal verb 'flub' is common for a mistake.		
346	3454	3686	fever dream	/ˈfiːvər driːm/	noun	Literally, a vivid or disturbing dream experienced during a fever; figuratively, an experience, work, or memory that feels bizarre, surreal, chaotic, or unreal.	surreal experience, bizarre vision, delirious dream	The movie plays like a fever dream full of abrupt cuts and impossible imagery. / I barely remember the trip—it feels like a fever dream now.	The figurative use is common in reviews and internet speech and does not imply an actual fever.		
346	3455	3687	farce	/fɑːrs/	noun	1. A comedy built around exaggerated, improbable situations, physical humor, misunderstandings, and rapid complications. 2. Figuratively, an event or process so badly handled, absurd, or insincere that it seems ridiculous.	comedy, slapstick comedy (overlap); travesty, sham, absurdity	The play is a fast-paced bedroom farce. / The hearing turned into a farce when nobody could answer the basic questions.	Figurative 'farce' is strongly negative and implies a supposed serious process has become absurd or meaningless.		
346	3456	3688	baffle	/ˈbæfəl/	verb; noun	(v.) To confuse, puzzle, or make something difficult to understand. (n.) A plate, wall, or device used to control, redirect, or reduce the flow of sound, fluid, light, etc.	bewilder, perplex, puzzle, mystify; barrier/deflector (technical noun)	The sudden error baffled the developers. / Acoustic baffles help reduce echoes in the room.	The technical noun and the 'confuse' verb are separate common senses. Adjective: baffling; past participle: baffled.		
346	3457	3689	undercut	/ˌʌndərˈkʌt/	verb; noun/adjective (other senses)	1. Offer goods/services at a lower price than a competitor. 2. Weaken, undermine, or reduce the effectiveness/credibility of something. 3. Cut away from beneath; also refers to an undercut hairstyle.	underprice, undersell; undermine, weaken, erode	The discount chain undercut local shops on price. / His later comments undercut the credibility of his earlier argument.	High-value figurative pattern: undercut an argument/claim/position/authority. Haircut sense is separate.		
346	3458	3690	miscellaneous	/ˌmɪsəˈleɪniəs/	adjective	Consisting of various kinds or types, often without clear category.	varied, assorted, mixed, diverse	The box contained miscellaneous items from the attic.	Common in labeling for uncategorized content. 日本語では「種々雑多な」。		
346	3459	3692	disciple vs. apprentice	/dɪˈsaɪpəl/ or /əˈprɛntɪs/	noun contrast	'Disciple' is a devoted follower or student of a teacher, leader, religion, philosophy, or school of thought. 'Apprentice' is a learner who receives practical training in a skilled trade, craft, or profession under an experienced practitioner.	disciple: follower, adherent, pupil; apprentice: trainee, learner, protégé (overlap)	He became a disciple of the philosopher. / She trained as an apprentice carpenter for three years.	Both can translate loosely as 弟子, but the relationship differs: disciple emphasizes following teachings/ideas; apprentice emphasizes hands-on vocational training.		
346	3460	3693	in doing so	/ɪn ˈduːɪŋ soʊ/	adverbial phrase	By performing the action just mentioned; as part of that action, with the following result or consequence.	by doing that, thereby, in the process	He admitted the mistake and, in doing so, took responsibility for the delay.	Useful cohesion device in formal and everyday writing. 'In doing so' normally refers back to a previously stated action.		
347	3461	3694	low-key	/loʊ kiː/	adjective; adverb (informal/slang)	As an adjective, restrained, understated, or not intense. As informal adverbial slang, somewhat/quietly, or sometimes privately/without making a big deal of it.	understated, subdued, discreet; kind of, quietly, mildly	We had a low-key dinner at home. / I'm low-key excited about the new season.	'High-key' exists as slang contrast but is less neutral/common. Do not assume low-key always means 'secretly'; often it simply softens intensity.		
347	3462	3695	the quintessence of	/kwɪnˈtɛsəns/	noun phrase / construction	The purest, most perfect, or most characteristic embodiment of a quality, style, or type.	epitome, embodiment, essence, perfect example	The village is the quintessence of rural charm. / To him, that performance was the quintessence of elegance.	Common frame: the quintessence of + ABSTRACT QUALITY/TYPE. More literary/formal than 'perfect example'.		
347	3463	3696	on-brand / on brand	/ɑn brænd/	adjective phrase (informal)	Consistent with a brand's established image or, figuratively, with a person's recognizable habits, personality, or public persona.	in character, consistent with the image, fitting, characteristic	The minimalist packaging is very on-brand for them. / Forgetting his umbrella again is completely on brand for him.	Marketing origin, now widely used jokingly about people. Hyphenation varies; predicative 'that's on brand' is common.		
347	3464	3697	flinch	/flɪntʃ/	verb; noun	To make a sudden small movement away or tense involuntarily in response to pain, fear, shock, or surprise; figuratively, to hesitate or recoil from something difficult.	wince, recoil, shrink back, jerk back; hesitate	She didn't flinch when the needle went in. / He never flinched from making the unpopular decision.	Useful frames: flinch at/from + THING; not flinch = show no visible fear/hesitation. 'Wince' more specifically involves a facial/body reaction to pain or embarrassment.		
347	3465	3698	overcome	/ˌoʊvərˈkʌm/	verb	1. Successfully deal with, control, or defeat a problem, obstacle, fear, or opponent. 2. In passive form, be strongly affected or overwhelmed by an emotion, smoke, heat, etc.	surmount, conquer, defeat; overwhelm, overpower	She overcame her fear of flying. / He was overcome with emotion after the announcement.	Key contrast: overcome + OBJECT = defeat/manage it; be overcome by/with + FEELING/force = be overwhelmed by it.		
347	3466	3699	tabby	/ˈtæbi/	noun; adjective	A cat with a characteristic coat pattern of stripes, spots, swirls, or bands; also the pattern itself/adjective. 'Tabby' describes coat pattern, not a specific breed.	tabby cat; striped/spotted cat (descriptive)	Their orange tabby sleeps on the windowsill every afternoon.	FIX: foreground the cat-pattern sense. The historical textile sense is rare today. Do not confuse with 'tubby' = short/fat/rounded. A tabby can be many colors and breeds.		
347	3467	3700	squeal	/skwiːl/	verb; noun	To make a high-pitched cry, shriek, or screeching sound; used for people, pigs and some animals, tires/brakes/machinery, etc. Informally, 'squeal on someone' means inform on or betray them.	shriek, screech, yelp; inform on, snitch on	The brakes squealed as the car stopped. / The kids squealed with excitement. / He refused to squeal on his friends.	Cats can sometimes make a sound someone might describe as a squeal, especially in distress, but their typical vocalizations are meow, yowl, hiss, caterwaul, etc.		
347	3468	3702	serve a purpose	/sɜrvz ə ˈpɜrpəs/	verb phrase	To have a useful function, role, or reason for existing, even if it is not ideal or generally useful.	have a function, fulfill a role, be useful for something	The old script is ugly, but it still serves a purpose. / Every section should serve a clear purpose.	Common singular/plural: serve a purpose; serve no purpose; serve several purposes. Often implies functional adequacy rather than excellence.		
347	3469	3703	hold off / hold off on	/hoʊld ɔf/	phrasal verb	1. Delay or postpone an action, especially 'hold off on doing X'. 2. Prevent someone/something from approaching, attacking, or succeeding for a time. 3. Wait before starting because conditions are not yet right.	postpone, delay, wait; fend off, keep back	Let's hold off on buying it until the price drops. / The defenders held off the attack until help arrived.	Important frame: hold off on + NOUN/GERUND. Transitive defensive sense: hold off + attacker/threat.		
347	3470	3704	common denominator / denominator (figurative)	/dɪˈnɒmɪˌneɪtər/	noun phrase	Figuratively, a feature, condition, or factor shared by different people or things; 'common denominator' is the usual expression.	common factor, shared element, shared trait	Curiosity was the common denominator among all the successful applicants.	NORMALIZATION: teach 'common denominator' as the common figurative phrase. 'Denominator' alone can occasionally be figurative but is less natural. Distinguish 'lowest common denominator', which carries a separate negative evaluative sense.		
348	3471	3705	lowest common denominator	/ˈloʊɪst ˈkɒmən dɪˈnɒmɪneɪtər/	idiom / noun phrase	Figuratively, the simplest, broadest, or least demanding level of taste, intelligence, standards, or appeal shared by a large audience, often used critically.	lowest standard, broadest basic appeal, lowest level of common taste	The show relies on jokes aimed at the lowest common denominator.	From mathematics. Figurative use is usually negative and often implies sacrificing quality or sophistication to maximize mass appeal.		
348	3472	3706	squeal (animals, including cats?)	/skwiːl/	usage note / verb	'Squeal' describes a high-pitched cry or sound. It is strongly associated with pigs, children, brakes, and some small animals. A cat may occasionally make a sound described as a squeal, but more typical cat verbs are meow, yowl, hiss, caterwaul, or shriek depending on the sound.	shriek, screech, yelp; meow/yowl/hiss (cat-specific alternatives)	The kitten gave a brief squeal when startled, but an adult cat in distress is more often described as yowling or shrieking.	INTENTIONAL-DUPLICATE VALUE: keep separately from WID3700 because this card answers a specific usage/confusability question about animal-sound verbs.		
348	3473	3709	gritty	/ˈɡrɪti/	adjective	1. Containing grit or having a rough, sandy texture. 2. Showing courage and determination. 3. In art/media, realistic in a harsh, raw, or unglamorized way.	grainy, sandy, rough; tough, determined; raw, hard-edged, realistic	The sauce tasted gritty. / It was a gritty comeback after a terrible first half. / The series has a gritty, street-level tone.	FIX: 'gritty' does not mean 'core/essential'; that association belongs to 'nitty-gritty', a separate expression meaning practical/basic details.		
348	3474	3710	jaded	/ˈdʒeɪdɪd/	adjective	Bored, tired, cynical, or no longer impressed because of too much experience, repetition, or exposure.	world-weary, cynical, blasé, disillusioned, weary	After reviewing hundreds of nearly identical shows, he became jaded. / The city's constant nightlife eventually left her jaded rather than excited.	Usually emotional/attitudinal fatigue from overexposure, not simply physical tiredness.		
348	3475	3712	show someone up	/ʃoʊ ˈsʌmwʌn ʌp/	phrasal verb	To embarrass or humiliate someone, especially by making their mistake, weakness, or inferiority obvious; sometimes by outperforming them conspicuously.	embarrass, humiliate, outshine, make someone look bad	I didn't mean to show him up in front of the whole team. / Her flawless performance showed up the more experienced players.	Different from 'show up' = arrive/appear. Construction: show + PERSON + up.		
348	3476	3714	pull in	/pʊl ɪn/	phrasal verb	1. Arrive and stop, especially a vehicle. 2. Attract or draw people, attention, business, etc. 3. Earn or receive money. 4. Move something inward or closer.	arrive; attract, draw; earn, bring in; move inward	The train pulled in at noon. / The festival pulled in thousands of visitors. / The film pulled in $50 million worldwide.	Highly polysemous; context decides. Compare 'bring in' for revenue and 'draw' for attracting crowds.		
348	3477	3715	finesse	/fɪˈnɛs/	noun; verb	(n.) Delicate skill, tact, subtlety, or refined technique in handling a difficult situation. (v.) To handle, maneuver, or achieve something skillfully and subtly, sometimes by finding a clever workaround.	tact, dexterity, subtlety, skill; maneuver, manage deftly	She handled the awkward question with finesse. / We finessed the layout by reducing the spacing slightly.	Can be admiring or slightly skeptical if it implies clever maneuvering around a problem rather than solving it directly.		
348	3478	3717	scalable	/ˈskeɪləbəl/	adjective	Able to increase or decrease in size, capacity, workload, or scope effectively; especially, a system/business can grow without requiring disproportionate cost or losing unacceptable performance.	expandable, extensible, growth-capable, scale-ready	The architecture is scalable enough to handle ten times the current traffic. / They want a business model that is scalable beyond one city.	Not simply 'can be resized'. In computing/business, scalability concerns how well something handles growth in demand, users, data, operations, etc.		
348	3479	3718	lmao	/ɛl.em.eɪˈoʊ/	internet slang	Laughing My Ass Off; used online to express strong amusement.	LOL, hahaha	That joke had me lmao.	Informal chat/SNS expression; stronger than 'LOL'. 日本語では「爆笑した」。		
348	3480	3719	roflmao	/ˈrɒfəl.meɪ.oʊ/	internet slang	Rolling On the Floor Laughing My Ass Off; used online to express extreme amusement.	ROFL, hysterical laughter	That video had me roflmao.	Combines ROFL and LMAO; stronger laughter intensity than 'lmao'. 日本語では「床を転げ回るほど爆笑」。		
349	3481	3720	chronicler	/ˈkrɒnɪklər/	noun	A person who records, recounts, or documents events, often over time and sometimes in a formal historical chronicle.	recorder, historian, annalist, documentarian (contextual)	She became the unofficial chronicler of the local music scene.	Can be literal/historical or figurative for a writer/journalist/artist who persistently documents a place, era, movement, or community.		
349	3482	3721	walk the walk / talk the talk	/ˈwɔːkɪŋ ðə wɔːk/	idiom	To act in a way that proves one's stated beliefs, claims, or promises; 'walk the walk' is often contrasted with 'talk the talk', meaning speak convincingly without necessarily acting.	practice what you preach, back up words with actions, follow through	If the company really cares about sustainability, it needs to walk the walk, not just talk the talk.	NORMALIZATION: base idiom 'walk the walk'; WID3744 is another related card. Retain both for now because one may serve as an inflected/source-form reinforcement rather than deleting automatically.		
349	3483	3722	interpretive	/ɪnˈtɜːrprətɪv/	adjective	Concerned with explaining, presenting, or expressing the meaning/significance of something rather than merely stating facts.	interpretative, explanatory, meaning-focused	The museum uses interpretive panels to explain the site's history. / Her performance is highly interpretive rather than literal.	Common combinations: interpretive display/signage, interpretive dance, interpretive framework, interpretive reading. 'Interpretative' is a variant.		
349	3484	3723	choose/pick a day / choose that day	/tʃuːz ðæt deɪ/	verb construction	To select a particular day or date. 'Choose that day' is grammatical when a specific previously mentioned day is being selected, but it is not a fixed scheduling expression.	pick a day, choose a date, settle on a day, set a date	Let's pick a day that works for everyone. / We looked at the calendar and chose that day for the kickoff.	FIX: teach the productive construction rather than treating 'choose that day' as a phrase. For scheduling, 'pick a date', 'settle on a day', and 'set a date' are often more natural.		
349	3485	3724	balm	/bɑːm/	noun	1. A soothing or healing ointment/preparation. 2. Figuratively, something that comforts, calms, or relieves emotional pain or distress.	ointment, salve; comfort, solace, relief	Use lip balm before going out in the cold. / Her message was a balm after a terrible week.	Common figurative frames: a balm to/for + pain/soul/nerves; soothing balm. Related adjective 'balmy' has different senses and should not be inferred directly.		
349	3486	3725	slog	/slɒɡ/	verb; noun	To work, walk, or continue through something with sustained effort when it is difficult, tiring, or tedious; as a noun, a long exhausting task/journey.	toil, grind, trudge, plow through; arduous task	We slogged through three hours of paperwork. / The last mile was a real slog.	Focuses on sustained arduous effort. Compare 'plod' = move/proceed slowly and heavily; 'slog' = labor through difficulty.		
349	3487	3726	plod	/plɒd/	verb; noun	To walk with slow, heavy steps; figuratively, to continue working or progressing slowly and laboriously.	trudge, lumber, trample along; grind away	We plodded through the mud. / I kept plodding through the report one section at a time.	Emphasizes slow, heavy progress more than overall hardship. Compare slog = work through something arduous.		
349	3488	3727	brothel	/ˈbrɒθəl/	noun	A house where people engage in sexual activity with sex workers.	bordello, house of prostitution	The district was once known for its brothels before the reforms.	Historical/legal term; registers vary by region and era (e.g., red‑light district). 日本語では「売春宿」。		
349	3489	3728	newt	/njuːt/	noun	A small salamander in the family Salamandridae; many species are semiaquatic or aquatic as adults. In some species, the terrestrial juvenile stage is called an eft.	salamander (broader category), eft (juvenile stage in some species)	We found a newt under a damp log near the pond.	FIX: newts are salamanders, but not all salamanders are newts. Japanese イモリ usually corresponds to newt, while ヤモリ is a gecko (a reptile), so they should not be conflated.		
349	3490	3731	average Joe	/ˈæv(ə)rɪdʒ dʒoʊ/	noun phrase (informal, chiefly U.S.)	An ordinary, typical, non-specialist person, traditionally framed as an ordinary man.	ordinary person, regular guy, person in the street, everyday person	The article explains the tax change in terms the average Joe can understand.	Casual and gendered/traditional. 'Average Jane' exists as a playful female counterpart; in neutral/formal writing prefer 'ordinary person', 'average person', or 'member of the public'.		
350	3491	3732	perverted	/pərˈvɜːrtɪd/	adjective; past participle	1. Sexually deviant in a way the speaker condemns—strongly judgmental/derogatory. 2. Distorted, corrupted, or diverted from an original proper purpose or meaning.	depraved, sexually deviant (sensitive); distorted, corrupted, warped	The original goal was perverted into a tool for personal gain. / Calling a person 'perverted' is a harsh sexual/moral judgment.	Use with care. In nonsexual abstract contexts, 'perverted' can mean twisted/corrupted, but 'distorted' or 'corrupted' is often clearer and less loaded.		
350	3492	3733	excess	/ˈɛksɛs, ɪkˈsɛs/	noun; adjective	(n.) An amount beyond what is necessary, normal, desirable, or allowed; also excessive behavior/indulgence. (adj.) More than needed or allowed.	surplus, overage, overabundance; extravagance; extra	Trim the excess from the budget. / He regretted the excesses of the weekend. / Airlines charge for excess baggage.	Insurance: BrE 'excess' often corresponds to AmE 'deductible'. Common plural 'excesses' = immoderate behavior. Pronunciation/stress differs between noun/adjective in some usage.		
350	3493	3734	magnate	/ˈmæɡneɪt/	noun	A very wealthy, powerful, and influential person in a particular industry or field of business.	tycoon, mogul, business baron, industrialist	A media magnate bought the newspaper chain.	Common compounds: media magnate, oil magnate, shipping magnate, tech magnate. Stronger than simply 'rich person' because influence/power is central.		
350	3494	3735	excessive / over-the-top person (source idea)	/ˈsʌmwʌn ɪn ɪkˈsɛs/	descriptor / usage note	English does not normally use 'someone in excess' to mean a person who habitually overdoes things. More natural choices are 'an excessive person' in limited contexts, 'over-the-top', 'too much', 'extra' (slang), or a specific description such as 'overly dramatic'.	over-the-top, excessive, too much, extra (slang), overly dramatic	He's a bit over-the-top—every small problem becomes a crisis. / She's so extra about birthday parties.	NORMALIZATION: 'someone in excess' is not a standard reusable phrase for this meaning. Choose the adjective that names the excess: overly dramatic, extravagant, excessive, intense, etc.		
350	3495	3736	latch / latch onto	/lætʃ/	noun; verb / phrasal verb	(n.) A fastening device for a door, gate, lid, etc. (v.) Fasten with a latch. 'Latch onto' means grasp, attach oneself to, become strongly interested in, or seize on an idea/opportunity.	catch, fastener; fasten; seize on, cling to, attach to	She latched the gate. / The press quickly latched onto the rumor. / The child latched onto his mother's arm.	Productive distinction: literal latch; phrasal 'latch onto + idea/person/opportunity'.		
350	3496	3737	emotionally stunted	/ɪˈmoʊʃənəli ˈstʌntɪd ˈpɜːrsən/	adjective phrase / descriptor	Describes someone whose emotional development, expression, coping, or relational skills are perceived as unusually limited or immature.	emotionally immature, emotionally underdeveloped, emotionally restricted	The character is emotionally stunted and avoids vulnerability by turning everything into a joke.	Can sound judgmental or quasi-clinical; it is descriptive, not a formal diagnosis. Prefer specific behavior descriptions when precision or sensitivity matters.		
350	3497	3738	stunned	/stʌnd ˈpɜːrsən/	adjective / past participle	Temporarily shocked, dazed, or unable to react normally because of surprise, emotion, or a physical blow.	shocked, dazed, dumbfounded, bewildered	She stood there stunned after hearing the result. / He was briefly stunned by the impact.	NORMALIZATION: 'stunned person' is a compositional phrase; teach the reusable adjective. Can be physical or emotional.		
350	3498	3739	emotionally stunned / stunned by emotion	/ɪˈmoʊʃənəli stʌnd ˈpɜːrsən/	adjective phrase	So overwhelmed by an emotional event that one is temporarily speechless, dazed, or unable to respond normally.	emotionally overwhelmed, shocked, speechless, numb	He was emotionally stunned by the sudden announcement and couldn't answer at first.	INTENTIONAL-NUANCE CHECK: retain separately from general WID3738 because this card isolates the emotional-overwhelm sense rather than merely repeating the adjective.		
350	3499	3741	beyond words	/bɪˈjɑːnd wɜːrdz/	idiomatic phrase	So intense, extreme, moving, beautiful, terrible, etc. that ordinary language seems inadequate to express it.	indescribable, inexpressible, ineffable, impossible to put into words	I was grateful beyond words. / The destruction was horrific beyond words.	Works with positive or negative emotion. Common patterns: be beyond words; + adjective beyond words ('beautiful beyond words').		
350	3500	3742	translate X from A into/to B	/ˌprɛpəˈzɪʃənz trænzˈleɪt frɒm ɛks tuː/ˈɪntuː waɪ/	verb construction / usage contrast	For languages, 'translate X from A into B' is a classic explicit source→target construction; 'translate X from A to B' is also common. Beyond language, 'translate into' often emphasizes transformation into a result/form, while 'translate to' often emphasizes correspondence, effect, or applicability.	render, convert, turn into	She translated the novel from Japanese into English. / That advantage doesn't always translate to higher sales. / The idea translated well into animation.	Do not overstate a rigid rule: both 'into' and 'to' occur in language translation. Productive patterns differ by context: translate into results/action/value; translate to another audience/context/medium.		
351	3501	3744	walk the walk	/wɔːk ðə wɔːk/	idiom	To demonstrate through action that one's stated beliefs, promises, or claims are genuine; to practice what one preaches.	practice what you preach, back up words with actions, follow through	It's easy to promise better service; now the company has to walk the walk.	INTENTIONAL-RELATED CARD CHECK: WID3721 teaches the paired contrast 'walk the walk / talk the talk'; retain this card as the clean headword/retrieval card rather than deleting it.		
351	3502	3745	pagination	/ˌpædʒɪˈneɪʃən/	noun	The process or system of dividing content into pages; in publishing, arranging/numbering pages, and in web/software interfaces, splitting result sets/content into separate navigable pages.	page division, page numbering, paged navigation	The search results use pagination with 20 items per page. / The editor checked the pagination before the book went to print.	Web pagination commonly contrasts with infinite scroll or 'load more'. APIs may also use pagination to return large result sets in chunks.		
351	3503	3746	deploy	/dɪˈplɔɪ/	verb	1. Position or distribute troops, equipment, people, or resources so they are ready for use. 2. In software, release/install a build or service into a target environment where it can run.	position, station, allocate; release, roll out, put into production	The company deployed extra staff during the launch. / We'll deploy the new version to production tonight.	Computing frame: deploy X to + environment (production/staging/cloud). Broader business use: deploy capital/resources/personnel.		
351	3504	3747	humbled	/ˈhʌmbəld/	adjective / past participle	Made more modest or less self-important by an experience; also commonly used to express gratitude when receiving praise, support, or recognition. In harsher contexts, 'humble' as a verb can mean defeat or lower someone's pride/status.	made modest, grateful, chastened; defeated (contextual verb sense)	I was humbled by how many people offered to help. / The heavily favored team was humbled by the underdogs.	FIX: 'humiliated' is not a normal synonym for the positive 'I feel humbled' sense. Humbled can be appreciative/modest without shame.		
351	3505	3748	shaken	/ˈʃeɪkən/	adjective / past participle	Emotionally disturbed, shocked, frightened, or unsettled; also the past participle of physically shake.	rattled, unnerved, disturbed, shocked, upset	He was visibly shaken after the near miss. / She sounded shaken but said she was okay.	Common emotional frame: be shaken by + EVENT. 'Shaken up' often emphasizes temporary emotional disturbance.		
351	3506	3749	delimit / delimited	/dɪˈlɪmɪtɪd/	verb; adjective / past participle	To set or mark the boundaries or limits of something. In computing/data, to separate fields or items using a delimiter such as a comma, tab, or pipe.	bound, define, mark off; separate with delimiters	The fields are delimited by commas. / The study area was delimited by the river and two highways.	NORMALIZATION: use base verb 'delimit'. Related terms: delimiter, delimited text, comma-delimited.		
351	3507	3750	repository	/rɪˈpɒzɪtəri/	noun	A place, system, or container where things are stored or accumulated; in computing, a managed storage location for source code, packages, artifacts, data, etc.	storehouse, archive, storage location; repo (informal tech)	The documents are kept in a digital repository. / Push the change to the Git repository.	A Git repository is a specific version-control repository, not simply any central folder. 'Repo' is common informal tech shorthand.		
351	3508	3751	interleave / interleaved	/ˌɪntərˈliːvd/	verb; adjective / past participle	To arrange things in alternating order or insert layers/items between other layers/items.	alternate, intersperse, insert between, intermingle	The book interleaves historical chapters with present-day scenes. / The pages were interleaved with protective tissue.	Computing uses include memory/data interleaving. NORMAL lexical family: interleave (verb), interleaved (adj./past participle).		
351	3509	3752	rote memorization / rote learning	/roʊt ˌmɛmərəˈzeɪʃən/	noun phrase	Memorizing material primarily through repetition, often with limited emphasis on understanding or application.	rote learning, mechanical memorization, drilling	Rote memorization can help with forms and vocabulary, but it should be combined with meaningful use.	INTENTIONAL-DUPLICATE CHECK: WID3502 already covers this phrase in depth. Retain rather than delete automatically because this later card may reflect intentional reinforcement; if a future global dedupe pass is desired, compare provenance/order before removal.		
351	3510	3753	rote / by rote	/roʊt/	noun; phrase	'Rote' is mechanical repetition used for learning; 'by rote' means by repeated memorization, often without deep understanding.	mechanical repetition, drill, memorization	We learned the multiplication table by rote. / He could recite the speech by rote but couldn't explain the argument.	Usually appears in 'by rote', 'rote learning', or 'rote memorization'. Rarely used as a free-standing countable noun.		
352	3511	3754	john (customer of prostitution)	/dʒɒn/	slang noun	Slang for a man who pays for sexual services.	sex buyer, client (of a sex worker)	The police arrested several johns during the sting operation.	US/Canada slang; can also mean ‘toilet’ in US slang. 日本語では「買春客」。		
352	3512	3755	go out on a limb / reach out	/riːtʃ aʊt ɒn ə lɪm/	idiom contrast / source blend	'Go out on a limb' means put oneself in a risky or vulnerable position, often to support a belief or person. 'Reach out' means contact, offer support, or extend help. 'Reach out on a limb' is a blend/creative combination, not a standard idiom.	take a risk, stick one's neck out; contact, offer help	I'll go out on a limb and say the sequel will be better. / She reached out to him after hearing the news.	FIX: do not teach 'reach out on a limb' as a conventional phrase. If a source deliberately blends the two images, preserve it as wordplay and explain the standard expressions behind it.		
352	3513	3756	shammer	/ˈʃæmər/	noun (rare)	A person who shams—someone who pretends, feigns, or presents something false; a faker or pretender.	faker, pretender, fraud, impostor	The old text dismisses him as a shammer and a fraud.	Rare/dated compared with 'faker', 'fraud', 'impostor', or 'phony'. Useful mainly when encountered in older or unusual sources.		
352	3514	3757	reckon with / reckoning with	/ˈrɛkənɪŋ wɪð/	phrasal verb	To confront, acknowledge, and deal seriously with a difficult fact, problem, consequence, history, or powerful force.	confront, come to terms with, face up to, grapple with, address	The industry is reckoning with years of poor labor practices. / She had to reckon with the consequences of her decision.	NORMALIZATION: base form 'reckon with'. Common in moral/social contexts: reckon with the past, consequences, legacy. Separate sense 'a force/person to be reckoned with' = formidable.		
352	3515	3758	laggy	/ˈlæɡi/	adjective (informal tech/gaming)	Noticeably delayed, slow to respond, or affected by lag, especially in games, video calls, interfaces, networks, or devices.	lagging, delayed, sluggish, unresponsive	The controls feel laggy whenever the frame rate drops. / The video call got really laggy.	Informal but very common in tech/gaming speech. Depending cause, 'slow', 'high-latency', 'stuttering', or 'low frame rate' may be more precise.		
352	3516	3759	zinger	/ˈzɪŋər/	noun (informal)	A sharp, witty, surprising, or memorable remark, especially a punchy joke, comeback, or criticism; more broadly, a striking surprise or twist.	quip, barb, one-liner, comeback, punch line; surprise	She ended the speech with a zinger that got the biggest laugh. / The final zinger is that the supposedly cheap option costs more overall.	Tone can be admiring ('great line') or critical if the remark is cutting.		
352	3517	3760	ramp up	/ræmps ʌp/	phrasal verb	To increase the level, rate, scale, intensity, production, staffing, effort, or activity gradually or deliberately.	increase, scale up, step up, intensify, boost	The factory ramps up production before the holidays. / We need to ramp up testing before launch.	NORMALIZATION: use base form 'ramp up'. Can be transitive or intransitive: ramp up production; production ramps up.		
352	3518	3761	mental itch	/ˈmɛntəl ɪtʃ/	metaphorical noun phrase	A nagging unresolved thought, curiosity, question, or sense of incompleteness that keeps demanding attention.	nagging thought, unresolved curiosity, cognitive itch	That unexplained detail gave me a mental itch I couldn't stop thinking about.	Creative/productive metaphor rather than a fixed idiom. Often pairs naturally with 'scratch the itch' = satisfy the curiosity or urge.		
352	3519	3762	forward-looking	/ˈfɔːrwərd ˌlʊkɪŋ/	adjective	Focused on the future and on anticipating, planning for, or shaping what comes next rather than only reacting to the present or past.	future-oriented, proactive, progressive, farsighted	They adopted a forward-looking policy on energy and infrastructure.	Common in business, policy, strategy, investing, and design. Does not automatically mean 'good'; it describes orientation toward future conditions.		
352	3520	3763	rob someone of something	/rɒb ˈsʌmwʌn əv ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	verb construction	To deprive someone of something valuable, desirable, or important, either literally through robbery or figuratively by causing them to lose an opportunity, quality, experience, time, dignity, etc.	deprive someone of, strip someone of, take away	The injury robbed her of the chance to compete. / Constant worry robbed him of sleep.	Productive frame: rob + PERSON + of + THING. Often figurative and does not require literal theft.		
353	3521	3764	country cousin	/ˈkʌntri ˈkʌzən/	noun phrase (dated/informal)	A person from the countryside viewed in contrast with more urban, sophisticated people; can be gently humorous or patronizing depending tone.	rural relative, provincial person; country bumpkin (much more insulting)	He joked that he felt like a country cousin at the fashionable city party.	Somewhat dated. 'Country bumpkin' is more openly derogatory and implies ignorance/clumsiness; do not treat the two as equal-strength synonyms.		
353	3522	3765	buzzkill	/ˈbʌzˌkɪl/	noun (slang)	A person, event, fact, or comment that ruins or sharply reduces a good mood, excitement, fun, or enthusiasm.	downer, killjoy, spoilsport, mood-killer	The rain was a buzzkill, but we stayed outside anyway. / Don't be a buzzkill—let them celebrate.	Can refer to a person or thing. 'Killjoy' focuses more on a person; 'downer' can be a depressing event/topic.		
353	3523	3766	dinosaur	/ˈdaɪnəˌsɔːr/	noun	1. A member of the extinct dinosaur groups of reptiles. 2. Figuratively, a person, organization, technology, or practice considered old-fashioned, obsolete, or resistant to change.	obsolete relic, fossil (figurative), old-timer (person, softer)	That software is a dinosaur by modern standards. / He jokes that he's a dinosaur who still prints every document.	Figurative use can be teasing or insulting. Literal zoological sense is far broader than 'large extinct reptile'; dinosaurs ranged greatly in size.		
353	3524	3767	fuddy-duddy	/ˈfʌdi ˌdʌdi/	noun (informal, humorous)	A person viewed as old-fashioned, conventional, fussy, or resistant to new ideas, fashions, or fun.	old fogey, stick-in-the-mud, square (dated), traditionalist (more neutral)	Don't be such a fuddy-duddy—try the new place with us.	Usually teasing/humorous rather than vicious, but still potentially insulting. Often evokes age/conservatism stereotypes.		
353	3525	3768	anthology vs. compilation album		music/media terminology contrast	A 'compilation album' is any album assembled from previously released or otherwise separately sourced tracks rather than conceived as one original album. An 'anthology' is a type of curated collection, often broader or more archival/comprehensive in scope, but the terms can overlap and are not separated by a strict rule.	collection, compilation, retrospective, collected works	The label released a compilation of the band's singles. / The box-set anthology includes hits, B-sides, demos, and rare live tracks.	FIX: do not define anthology as necessarily covering an entire career or compilation as necessarily hits/theme-based. In music marketing, usage varies; 'anthology' often signals breadth/retrospective prestige, while 'compilation' is the general category.		
353	3527	3770	at one's own risk	/æt wʌnz oʊn rɪsk/	idiom / warning phrase	With the understanding that the person choosing to proceed accepts the possibility of harm, loss, failure, or other consequences.	at your own peril, taking responsibility for the risk, proceed at your own risk	Use the unofficial patch at your own risk. / Visitors enter the trail at their own risk.	Common warning wording: 'Proceed/use/enter at your own risk.' Legal effect varies by jurisdiction/context; the phrase itself does not automatically eliminate another party's legal responsibility.		
353	3528	3771	off the list	/ɒf ðə lɪst/	phrase	No longer included on a literal list or, figuratively, removed from consideration, eligibility, selection, or priority.	removed, excluded, no longer under consideration	After the price increase, that hotel is off the list. / His name was taken off the candidate list.	Compositional phrase. Common verbs: be off the list, take/remove X off/from the list, cross X off the list.		
353	3529	3772	breakthrough	/ˈbreɪkˌθruː/	noun; adjective	A major advance, discovery, or achievement that overcomes an important barrier or creates significant new progress; adjectivally, 'breakthrough' can describe such a development.	major advance, discovery, leap forward, milestone	The treatment was hailed as a medical breakthrough. / Researchers reported breakthrough results.	Stronger than ordinary 'progress'; implies crossing a barrier or achieving something previously difficult/unavailable.		
353	3530	3773	the ties that bind	/ðə taɪz ðæt baɪnd/	idiom / literary phrase	The relationships, loyalties, obligations, experiences, or shared values that connect people or groups and keep them attached to one another.	bonds, connections, shared ties, social bonds	Despite their arguments, family history remained one of the ties that bind them.	Often has a literary/emotional tone and may imply that bonds can both support and constrain.		
354	3531	3774	strike a chord with someone	/straɪk ə kɔːrd wɪð miː/	idiom	To cause a strong feeling of recognition, agreement, sympathy, or emotional resonance in someone.	resonate with, hit home, connect with, speak to	That scene struck a chord with me because I'd gone through something similar.	Productive frame: strike a chord with + PERSON/AUDIENCE. Music metaphor. Compare 'hit home' = often makes truth/significance feel personally real.		
354	3532	3777	faker / fraud / impostor / charlatan / pretender		noun contrast	Several nouns can describe someone who fakes or falsely presents something, but they differ: a faker pretends or produces something false; a fraud is a deceptive person or thing; an impostor pretends to be another person or to hold an identity/status they do not have; a charlatan falsely claims special knowledge or skill; a pretender claims a role, title, or quality without full legitimacy.	faker, fraud, impostor, charlatan, pretender, phony	He's a fraud who lied about his credentials. / The impostor pretended to be a doctor. / The so-called healer was exposed as a charlatan.	NORMALIZATION: replace the question-form headword with a reusable contrast card. Standard spelling is 'impostor' or 'imposter'; both occur. Choose the noun according to what is being faked.		
354	3533	3778	instill	/ɪnˈstɪl/	verb	To gradually but firmly establish an idea, feeling, or habit in a person's mind.	implant, impart, inculcate	Parents try to instill good values in their children.	"Nuance differences:
- instill: gradual, steady introduction of values or attitudes (日本語: 徐々に植え付ける)
- implant: put deeply into (often physical or strong emotional idea) (日本語: 埋め込む)
- impart: communicate or bestow (knowledge/information) (日本語: 伝える)
- inculcate: teach persistently by repetition (日本語: 繰り返し教え込む)
Examples:
• The teacher instilled confidence in her students.
• The coach implanted a sense of discipline in the team.
• The mentor imparted wisdom to the new employees.
• The parent inculcated table manners in their children."		
354	3534	3779	beveled edge / bevel	/ˈbɛvəl ɛdʒ/	noun phrase; noun/verb	A beveled edge is an edge cut or shaped at an angle rather than at 90 degrees to the main faces. A bevel is the angled surface/cut itself; as a verb, to bevel means create such an angled edge.	angled edge, sloped edge; chamfer (related)	The mirror has a beveled edge. / The carpenter beveled the edge of the tabletop.	NORMALIZATION: 'beveled edge' is more natural than 'bevel edge' as the descriptive phrase. A chamfer is a specific flat beveled transition, so the terms overlap but are not always identical.		
354	3535	3780	a cascade of / trigger a cascade of	/meɪk ə kæsˈkeɪd əv ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	noun phrase / verb construction	A 'cascade of X' is a series of events, effects, messages, failures, etc. that follow one another, often because one triggers the next. To cause this, say 'trigger/set off/cause a cascade of X'.	chain reaction, series, succession; trigger a chain of	The announcement triggered a cascade of reactions online. / One server failure caused a cascade of errors.	FIX: 'make a cascade of' is not the usual reusable construction. The waterfall image motivates the figurative sense.		
354	3536	3781	follow your plan to the letter	/ˈfɒloʊ jɔːr plæn tə ðə ˈlɛtər/	idiom	To follow instructions or a plan exactly as written or intended.	follow precisely, adhere strictly	She followed her doctor’s advice to the letter.	Emphasizes strict, literal compliance. 日本語では「一字一句忠実に従う」。		
