354	3538	3783	braced for disappointment	/breɪst fər ˌdɪsəˈpɔɪntmənt/	verb phrase	Prepared mentally or emotionally for an expected letdown.	prepared for letdown, steeled oneself	I was braced for disappointment after hearing the rumors.	From brace oneself = prepare for impact/difficulty. 日本語では「失望に備える」。		
354	3539	3784	serve someone up / serve up someone	/sɜrvd ʌp ˈsʌmwʌn fər ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	phrasal verb (figurative)	To present, hand over, expose, or make someone available to another person/group, sometimes for criticism, punishment, exploitation, or some unwanted role.	hand over, offer up, deliver, sacrifice, expose	The scandal served up an easy scapegoat for the press. / They practically served him up to the critics.	Context determines the implication. It can suggest treating a person as something to be consumed/used, but it is not a fixed phrasal verb meaning only 'make someone a scapegoat'.		
