360	3595	4640	might as well	/maɪt əz wel/	modal idiom	Used to say that, given the circumstances, there is no good reason not to do something, or that it is the easiest, most logical, or best available course when no better alternative is apparent. It can also say that one situation is practically equivalent to another.	may as well, there's no reason not to, might just as well, practically/effectively (in comparison use)	"The movie is free, so we might as well watch it.
We're stuck here until six, so we might as well get some work done.
It's so quiet here, we might as well be on an empty island."	The core idea is circumstance-driven choice: there is no strong reason not to do it, or no better alternative. Depending on tone, it can sound matter-of-fact, lightly enthusiastic, indifferent, or resigned ('Well, we might as well...'). In the comparison pattern 'might as well be X,' it can mean the present situation is practically/effectively the same as X. This is why learners sometimes interpret it as 'almost': e.g. 'We might as well be finished' can imply that the situation is so close to or effectively equivalent to being finished that the distinction barely matters. But might as well itself does not mean 'almost.'		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: might as well means almost
360	3596	4473	you shall know a word by the company it keeps	/juː ʃæl noʊ ə wɝːd baɪ ðə ˈkʌm.pə.ni ɪt kiːps/	linguistics quotation / principle	The meaning and behavior of a word can be understood from the other words and contexts that regularly occur around it.	distributional principle, contextual meaning	Corpus linguistics often illustrates the idea that you shall know a word by the company it keeps.	Associated with linguist J. R. Firth. 'Company' means neighboring linguistic context/collocations, not literal companions.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: I'll tell you what a word means by the company that it keeps
360	3597	4734	page someone / paging	/peɪdʒ/	verb	To call or summon someone over a public-address system or by an electronic paging system; in computing, paging is a memory-management technique that moves fixed-size blocks of data between memory and storage.	call, summon; memory paging	Dr. Lee was paged to the emergency room.	The everyday verb and computing noun are distinct senses. 'Paging Dr. X' is also used humorously to call attention to someone associated with a topic.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: paging meaning
360	3598	4557	kink	/kɪŋk/	noun; verb	A twist or bend that obstructs something; figuratively, a small problem or flaw that needs adjustment. It can also refer to an unconventional sexual preference.	snag, wrinkle, glitch	We're still working out a few kinks in the new system.	Common phrase work out the kinks. The sexual sense is context-dependent and distinct from the problem/flaw sense.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define kinks
