361	3601	5457	trip up on something		phrasal verb	To stumble physically on something, or figuratively to make a mistake or get caught by a difficult point.	stumble on, get caught by, make a mistake on	A lot of learners trip up on articles.	Related transitive pattern: 'That question tripped me up.' Very useful for language learning: trip up on articles, prepositions, pronunciation, exceptions, etc.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
361	3602	4492	implicit vs. explicit	/ɪmˈplɪs.ɪt/ vs. /ɪkˈsplɪs.ɪt/	contrast	Implicit information is suggested or understood without being directly stated; explicit information is stated clearly and directly.	implied vs. stated, indirect vs. direct	Her criticism was implicit, but his was completely explicit.	Implicit does not mean vague: something can be strongly implied without being directly said.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: implicit vs explicit meaning
