361	3607	5281	You don't want to know.	/juː doʊnt wɑːnt tə noʊ/	pragmatic expression	A response meaning the answer is so unpleasant, complicated, embarrassing, shocking, or absurd that the listener would supposedly be better off not hearing it.	Don't ask; It's better you don't know	“How bad was the meeting?” “You don't want to know.”	Often not literal: the speaker may actually be inviting curiosity while emphasizing how bad, messy, strange, or embarrassing the answer is. Tone can be humorous, ominous, weary, or dramatic.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: you don't want to know
