362	3611	5378	comeuppance	/kəˈmʌp.əns/	noun	A deserved punishment, defeat, or negative consequence, especially one that feels satisfying or ironic.	just deserts, reckoning, deserved punishment	After years of cheating people, he finally got his comeuppance.	Almost always implies the person deserved what happened. Stronger moral satisfaction than a neutral 'consequence.'		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
362	3612	5080	talk yourself into something / doing something	/tɔːk jɚˈself ˈɪn.tuː/	verb phrase	To persuade yourself to do or believe something, often by reasoning your way past doubts; sometimes despite suspect motives or better judgment.	convince yourself, persuade yourself	I talked myself into buying it even though I didn't really need it.	Raw search adds 'for the wrong reason': the phrase itself is neutral; context can imply rationalizing or convincing yourself for bad reasons. Opposite: talk yourself out of something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: talk myself into for the wrong reason meaning
