362	3614	4681	be in the market for something	/biː ɪn ðə ˈmɑːr.kɪt fɔːr/	idiom	To be actively interested in buying or obtaining something; not be in the market means one is not currently looking to buy or acquire it.	be shopping for, be looking for	I'm not in the market for a new car right now.	Usually followed by for + noun. Can extend figuratively to opportunities or relationships, but purchasing is the core sense.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: not in the market meaning
