383	3828	5342	opah	/ˈoʊ.pə/	noun	A large, colorful, deep-bodied ocean fish of the genus Lampris, also called a moonfish, found in open oceans and sometimes caught commercially for food.	moonfish	The restaurant occasionally serves opah caught in deep offshore waters.	Opah are distinctive round-bodied ocean fish with reddish fins. 'Moonfish' is a common name, though common fish names can overlap across species. Preserve as niche zoological/food vocabulary.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
383	3829	5350	KPI (key performance indicator)	/ˌkeɪ.piːˈaɪ/	noun	A measurable value chosen to show how effectively a person, team, organization, or process is progressing toward an important objective.	performance metric, key metric	Customer retention is one of the team's core KPIs this quarter.	Not every metric is a KPI: KPI implies that the measure is 'key' to evaluating a strategic or operational objective. Common collocations: define/set KPIs, track/monitor KPIs, hit/meet/miss a KPI or target.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
383	3830	5275	camera wowo	/ˌkæmərə ˈwoʊwoʊ/	internet meme / mondegreen	A humorous English-language mishearing/meme rendering of Lionel Messi's Argentine Spanish '¿Qué mirás, bobo?' ('What are you looking at, fool?'), said to Wout Weghorst after Argentina's 2022 World Cup quarterfinal against the Netherlands. 'Camera wowo' is not the actual Spanish phrase.	Qué mirás, bobo? (source phrase); mondegreen, mishearing (category/related terms)	People online jokingly wrote 'camera wowo' after hearing Messi say '¿Qué mirás, bobo?' / 'Camera wowo' became a meme precisely because it approximated what some non-Spanish speakers thought they heard.	NORMALIZED from the earlier vague headword 'wowo (slang; context-dependent)'. The relevant use is the Messi meme/mishearing 'camera wowo,' not a general English slang word wowo. Unrelated online uses of 'wowo' exist, so they should not be merged into this card.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: wowo meaning
384	3831	4888	reproducibility	/rɪˌprɑː.də.səˈbɪl.ə.t̬i/	noun	The degree to which a result can be obtained again under specified conditions, especially by repeating an analysis or experiment.	repeatability, replicability	The researchers released their code to improve reproducibility.	Terminology differs across scientific fields, so note the field-specific distinction rather than assuming one universal definition.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: reproducibility | reproducibility meaning
384	3832	4408	AI hallucination	/ˌeɪˈaɪ həˌluː.səˈneɪ.ʃən/	noun	An output generated by an AI system that presents false, unsupported, or fabricated information as if it were reliable or factual.	fabrication, confabulation, unsupported output	The model produced an AI hallucination and cited a paper that did not exist.	Technical/AI usage; not a literal sensory hallucination.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define hallucinations ai
384	3833	5130	tinfoil hat / tin-foil hat		noun; modifier, informal	A symbol or label for paranoid or implausible conspiracy thinking.	conspiracy-minded, conspiratorial; tinfoil-hat thinking, conspiracy-theory thinking	That's getting into tinfoil-hat territory.	Often jocular or derisive: tinfoil-hat theory; put on my tinfoil hat.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: tin foil hat meaning
384	3834	4738	pan out	/pæn aʊt/	phrasal verb	To develop, happen, or turn out in a particular way, especially successfully after uncertainty.	work out, turn out, succeed	The job offer didn't pan out, so she kept looking.	Often negative: didn't pan out. Historically connected with washing gravel in a pan to find gold.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: panned out meaning
384	3835	5481	jock	/dʒɑːk/	noun, informal, chiefly North American	A person strongly identified with sports and athletic ability, especially a male high-school or college athlete; also a familiar social stereotype.	athlete, sports guy	In the movie, the jocks sit together at lunch.	A classic U.S. school-archetype label. Depending on context it can imply athletic popularity, masculinity, or a stereotypically sports-focused personality.		Supplemental from approved batch 4662–4711
384	3836	4960	emoji sentiment	/ɪˈmoʊ.dʒi ˈsen.tə.mənt/	noun phrase, NLP/social media	The positive, negative, or neutral emotional orientation associated with an emoji in context, especially when used in sentiment analysis.	emoji polarity, affective value	The model uses emoji sentiment as one signal for classifying posts.	Emoji meanings are context-dependent; sentiment lexicons estimate tendencies rather than one fixed emotion for every use.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0105 | searches: sentiment emoji meaning
384	3837	5078	take the cake	/teɪk ðə keɪk/	idiom	To be the most extreme, remarkable, absurd, annoying, or impressive example among several things.	top them all, be the most extreme	I've heard some bad excuses, but that one takes the cake.	Often negative or sarcastic, though historically/occasionally positive.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: take the cake meaning
384	3838	4724	out the window	/aʊt ðə ˈwɪn.doʊ/	idiomatic phrase	Out of existence, use, consideration, or practical relevance; especially in go out the window, meaning to stop being used, followed, or possible, and throw something out the window, meaning to deliberately discard or stop considering it.	discarded, abandoned, gone, no longer applicable	"Once we had kids, our sleep schedule went out the window.
When the crisis started, the old rules went out the window.
We can throw that idea out the window."	Core family: be out the window = be gone/no longer relevant; go out the window = cease to apply or be used; throw something out the window = deliberately discard/ignore it. Typical subjects include plans, rules, logic, caution, privacy, budgets, schedules, and expectations. Some user-generated slang sources give 'out the window' idiosyncratic senses such as being out of touch with reality, but that is not the central general-English idiom. Also do not confuse this idiom with literal looking/throwing out a window or with window-based meme templates; see the supplemental Boardroom Suggestion and Squidward Looking Out the Window cards.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: out the window meaning
384	3839	3928	belle	/bel/	noun	A notably attractive and socially admired woman, especially the most admired woman at a particular social event or in a group.	beauty	She was considered the belle of the ball.	Somewhat old-fashioned/literary. Classic phrase: belle of the ball.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: belle meaning
384	3840	4553	keep something bottled up	/kiːp ˈsʌm.θɪŋ ˌbɑː.t̬əld ˈʌp/	idiom	To suppress feelings, worries, anger, or thoughts instead of expressing or discussing them.	suppress, repress, hold in	He kept his frustration bottled up until he finally snapped.	Common passive/state form: keep your feelings bottled up / emotions are bottled up.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: keep bottled up meaning
385	3841	4533	irony	/ˈaɪ.rə.ni/	noun, literature / rhetoric	A contrast between what is said, expected, or apparently true and what is actually meant or happens, often creating humor, criticism, or dramatic effect.	incongruity, contrast	It's dramatic irony when the audience knows the character is walking into a trap but the character does not.	Major literary types include verbal irony, situational irony, and dramatic irony. Irony is not simply any unfortunate coincidence.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106, voca_0107 | searches: irony meaning in literature
385	3842	4751	patronizing	/ˈpeɪ.trəˌnaɪ.zɪŋ/	adjective	Speaking or behaving toward someone in a way that seems kind or helpful but reveals a belief that they are less intelligent, capable, experienced, or important.	condescending, belittling	Her patronizing tone made the explanation more irritating than helpful.	The problem is the implied superiority, not merely politeness or simplification. BrE spelling patronising.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define patronizing
385	3843	4484	be onto someone	/biː ˈɑːn.tuː ˈsʌm.wʌn/	informal phrase	To know or suspect what someone is really doing, especially when they are trying to hide or deceive you.	see through, suspect, have figured out	He thinks nobody knows about the scam, but the police are onto him.	This is the opposite of being fooled by someone: if you're onto them, you've begun to figure them out.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: if you are onto someone, you are fooled by someone?
