385	3845	4234	enticing	/ɪnˈtaɪ.sɪŋ/	adjective	Attractive or tempting in a way that makes someone want something or want to do something.	tempting, appealing, alluring	The restaurant's lunch special looked enticing.	Direct adjective from entice; often used for offers, smells, prospects, images, or possibilities.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107, voca_0108 | searches: enticing synonym
385	3846	4090	cope	/koʊp/	verb	To manage or deal successfully enough with a difficult situation, feeling, or demand.	manage, deal with, handle	She's still learning how to cope with the workload.	Usually: cope with something. Unlike solve, cope can mean managing a problem that still exists.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099, voca_0100 | searches: cope mening
385	3847	4510	inch right up to something	/ɪntʃ raɪt ʌp tuː ˈsʌm.θɪŋ/	phrase	To move or progress very gradually until extremely close to a physical point, limit, threshold, deadline, or outcome.	creep up to, edge toward, approach gradually	The temperature kept inching right up to the record high.	Inch as a verb emphasizes slow, small increments; right up to intensifies how close the endpoint is.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: inching right up to meaning
385	3848	5019	squint	/skwɪnt/	verb; noun	To partly close the eyes, often to see more clearly, protect them from bright light, or express suspicion/confusion.	narrow one's eyes, peer	She squinted at the tiny text on the screen.	Can describe the facial expression as well as an eye condition in some varieties of English.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: squint meaning
