389	3883	4601	lore	/lɔːr/	noun	Traditional knowledge, stories, beliefs, or background information associated with a culture, subject, community, or fictional world.	tradition, mythology, backstory	The game has surprisingly deep lore.	Modern fandom/gaming use often means the accumulated canonical backstory and world-building of a fictional universe.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: lore meaning
389	3884	4287	finish strong	/ˈfɪn.ɪʃ strɔːŋ/	verb phrase	To perform especially well near the end of an activity, period, competition, or project.	end well, close strongly	We had a rough start, but we're determined to finish strong.	Common in sports, school, work, sales, and motivational language.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: finish strong meaning
389	3885	4580	leave something to someone	/liːv ˈsʌm.θɪŋ tuː ˈsʌm.wʌn/	phrase	To entrust a task, decision, responsibility, or item to another person to handle.	entrust, delegate, put in someone's hands	I'll leave the glasses to you—you know which ones we need.	The raw search appears to be a contextual example rather than an idiom. Core pattern: leave X to someone = let that person handle X.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: leave glasses to you meaning
389	3886	4763	fazed by	/feɪzd baɪ/	phrase	Disturbed, unsettled, or made nervous by something.	bothered by, rattled by	She wasn't fazed by the criticism.	The raw 'phased by' is usually a spelling confusion. Faze = disturb; phase = stage or introduce gradually.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104 | searches: phased by meaning
389	3887	4416	harp on something	/hɑːrp ɑːn ˈsʌm.θɪŋ/	phrasal verb, informal	To keep talking about the same subject, especially in an annoying or repetitive way.	dwell on, go on about, belabor	He keeps harping on the one mistake I made.	Often followed by about as well: harp on about something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: harp on
389	3888	5025	step on someone's toes	/step ɑːn ˈsʌm.wʌnz toʊz/	idiom	To offend, upset, or interfere with someone by intruding on their role, authority, interests, or responsibilities.	offend, encroach, tread on toes	I don't want to step on anyone's toes, but this needs to be fixed.	Often used diplomatically when discussing boundaries at work or in groups.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: stepping on toes meaning
389	3889	5339	Foucauldian	/fuːˈkoʊ.di.ən/	adjective	Relating to the ideas, methods, or analytical framework associated with French philosopher Michel Foucault, especially analyses of discourse, power, knowledge, institutions, discipline, and subject formation.	Foucault-inspired, Foucauldian analysis	The paper offers a Foucauldian analysis of how institutions define and regulate normal behavior.	Common in humanities and social-science academic writing. It does not mean merely 'complicated' or 'philosophical'; it specifically signals a connection to Foucault's concepts or methods.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
389	3890	5057	suprasegmental	/ˌsuː.prə.seɡˈmen.t̬əl/	adjective; noun, linguistics	Relating to speech features that extend over more than one individual sound segment, such as stress, rhythm, intonation, tone, or sometimes length.	prosodic, nonsegmental	Intonation is a suprasegmental feature of speech.	Useful contrast: segmental = individual consonants/vowels; suprasegmental/prosodic = patterns operating across segments.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: suprasegmental
390	3891	4588	liberally	/ˈlɪb.ɚ.ə.li/	adverb	In large or generous amounts; freely or broadly rather than strictly or sparingly.	generously, freely, abundantly, broadly	Apply the sauce liberally to both sides.	Can also mean interpreting something broadly: interpret a rule liberally. Not necessarily political.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: define liberally
390	3892	5200	vaudeville	/ˈvɔːd.vɪl/	noun	A form of stage entertainment, especially popular in North America in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, featuring a variety bill of comedy, music, dance, acrobatics, and other acts.	variety theater, variety show	The comedian's style drew on old vaudeville routines.	Culturally useful historical entertainment term; related to the variety-show tradition.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: define vaudeville
390	3893	3836	absorb	/əbˈzɔːrb/	verb	To take in a liquid, substance, energy, information, impact, or cost so that it is incorporated or lessened.	soak up, take in, assimilate	"The towel absorbed the water.
