390	3896	4992	slop	/slɑːp/	noun; verb; internet slang	Wet, messy, low-quality food/material; more broadly something carelessly made. Online, 'slop' disparagingly labels cheap, formulaic, mass-produced or low-effort content, including 'AI slop.'	low-quality content, junk, mush, swill; mess	The pigs were fed a bucket of slop. / My feed is filling up with generic AI slop. / Calling a show 'slop' suggests it was produced or consumed with little care, not merely that you disliked it.	Current internet sense is especially useful for media/AI criticism: cheap, formulaic, mass-produced or low-effort content, including 'AI slop.' Broader 'content slop' predates the AI label. Strongly dismissive. Literal senses include wet/messy food or material.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: define slop
390	3897	4591	lilting	/ˈlɪl.tɪŋ/	adjective	Having a pleasant, gently rising and falling rhythm or musical quality, especially a voice, accent, or melody.	rhythmic, musical, sing-song	She spoke with a soft, lilting accent.	Usually positive. Lilt can be a noun or verb; often describes speech prosody and music.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102, voca_0103 | searches: lilting
390	3898	4934	salvage	/ˈsæl.vɪdʒ/	verb; noun	To save or recover something from damage, loss, failure, or destruction; as a noun, recovered property/material.	save, recover, rescue	We managed to salvage useful data from the damaged drive.	Figurative: salvage a relationship, reputation, deal, season, situation.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: salvage meaning
390	3899	5252	irk → irritation / annoyance (noun)	/ɝːk/	word-family note	The verb irk means annoy or irritate. English normally uses irritation or annoyance as the corresponding noun rather than a common everyday noun *irk*.	irritation, annoyance	His constant interruptions were a source of irritation rather than merely something that irked me.	The Master already has irk as a verb (WordID2130). This card preserves the specific search intent: what noun do I use? 'Irksomeness' exists but is rare and means the quality of being irksome.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: what is the noun form of irk
390	3900	5007	sound-dampening	/ˈsaʊnd ˌdæm.pən.ɪŋ/	adjective/noun modifier	Designed to reduce the strength, transmission, echo, or perceived loudness of sound.	noise-reducing, sound-absorbing	They installed sound-dampening panels in the studio.	Dampen = reduce intensity. Related terms soundproofing and sound absorption are not always technically identical.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: sound-dampening
391	3901	4217	elegy	/ˈel.ə.dʒi/	noun	A poem, song, or other work that mourns a person who has died or reflects sadly on loss.	lament, dirge	The poem is an elegy for a generation lost to war.	Often literary; elegiac is the related adjective.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: elegy meaning
391	3902	5008	sous-	/suː/	prefix, from French	Under, below, or subordinate; encountered in English especially in sous-chef, the chef directly below the head/executive chef.	deputy, subordinate	She worked as a sous-chef before opening her own restaurant.	As a standalone English word, sous is uncommon; learning value is mainly recognizing the French-derived prefix in compounds such as sous-chef.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: sous meaning
391	3903	4101	crowd out	/ˌkraʊd ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To displace, squeeze out, or reduce the space or opportunity available to something else.	displace, squeeze out	Higher housing costs are crowding out smaller local businesses.	Common in economics: government borrowing can crowd out private investment. Also general figurative use.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104 | searches: crowding out meaning
391	3904	4169	dither	/ˈdɪð.ɚ/	verb / noun	To be indecisive or waste time because you cannot decide what to do.	hesitate, vacillate	We can't keep dithering—we need to make a decision today.	Often mildly critical; BrE especially common. As a noun: stop dithering.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define dither
391	3905	4685	thorough	/ˈθɝː.oʊ/	adjective	Careful, complete, and detailed, with all important parts considered or dealt with.	comprehensive, meticulous, exhaustive	The review was useful, but it wasn't very thorough.	Normalize the raw negative phrase to the reusable adjective thorough. Not very thorough = incomplete, superficial, or insufficiently detailed depending on context.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: not very thorough
391	3906	5056	supersede	/ˌsuː.pɚˈsiːd/	verb	To replace something older, less effective, or no longer valid, taking its place or authority.	replace, supplant, override	The new policy supersedes the previous guidelines.	Spelling trap: supersede, not supercede. Often used for laws, versions, rules, technologies.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104, voca_0106, voca_0107 | searches: supersede | supersede meaning
391	3907	5170	twist the knife	/twɪst ðə naɪf/	idiom	To make an already painful, embarrassing, or upsetting situation feel even worse, often by adding a hurtful remark or reminder.	rub salt in the wound, make matters worse	After rejecting his proposal, she twisted the knife by praising his rival's version.	Also twist the knife in the wound; raw 'twisting knife' normalized to the idiom.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: twisting knife meaning
391	3908	4221	emotional stakes	/ɪˈmoʊ.ʃən.əl steɪks/	noun phrase	The degree of emotional importance, risk, or personal investment involved in a situation.	emotional investment, personal stakes	The emotional stakes are much higher once the characters' families become involved.	Common in storytelling/criticism: raising the emotional stakes makes outcomes matter more emotionally.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104 | searches: emotional stakes
391	3909	3831	a whiff of	/ə wɪf əv/	noun phrase	A faint smell of something; figuratively, a slight trace, hint, or suggestion of something.	hint, trace, faint smell	"There was a whiff of smoke in the hallway.
