399	3982	5133	turtles all the way down	/ˈtɝː.t̬əlz ɔːl ðə weɪ daʊn/	expression	An expression for infinite regress: a chain of reasoning, support, or explanation in which each level requires another beneath it, so no final foundation or endpoint is reached.	infinite regress, endless regress	Every justification depended on another assumption beneath it—it was turtles all the way down.	The phrase evokes the anecdote of the world resting on a turtle and, when asked what supports that turtle, the answer being more turtles indefinitely. It is used in philosophy, science, and everyday discussion for explanations that never reach an ultimate foundation. It can also evoke the unsettling feeling of endlessly spiraling questions, or the relief of accepting that there may be no final bottom. It is also widely recognizable in popular culture, including John Green's title Turtles All the Way Down.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: tiny turtles... phrase
