402	4017	5477	whale (gaming)	/weɪl/	noun, gaming/internet slang	A player or customer who spends unusually large amounts of money on a game or digital product, especially through gacha, microtransactions, or other in-app purchases.	high spender, big spender	The game makes most of its money from a relatively small number of whales.	Originally related to casino/high-roller terminology and now common in free-to-play/mobile gaming. It can be neutral industry jargon or critical when discussing systems designed to milk or exploit heavy spenders.		Supplemental from approved batch 4612–4661
402	4018	3924	be no slouch	/biː noʊ ˈslaʊtʃ/	idiom	To be quite capable, skilled, or impressive, despite perhaps not being the very best.	be highly capable	He's no slouch on the tennis court.	Usually understated praise: someone is no slouch at/in something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: being no slouch meaning
402	4019	4275	favor for	/ˈfeɪ.vɚ fɔːr/	noun phrase / pattern	Support, preference, or approval directed toward a person, option, or idea, especially in constructions such as show favor for or have a favor for.	preference for, support for, partiality toward	The policy shows clear favor for large companies over smaller competitors.	Not normally a standalone idiom. More natural patterns include favor for, in favor of, show favor to/toward, and favor A over B.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: favor for
402	4020	4659	naivete / naïveté	/naɪˌiːvəˈteɪ/	noun	Lack of experience, sophistication, or worldly judgment, often resulting in excessive trust or simplicity.	innocence, gullibility, ingenuousness	His early optimism now seems like political naivete.	Also spelled naïveté or naiveté. Can imply innocent freshness or criticize unrealistic lack of experience.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: naivete
