419	4184	5300	small differences, completely different meanings: thing for/about; plans/a plan; an only child/only a child; had fun/was fun		contrast / usage cluster	A contrast set showing how a small change in preposition, number, article placement, or grammatical structure can produce a substantially different meaning: 'have a thing for X' expresses a liking or attraction, while 'have a thing about X' signals a particular issue, fixation, sensitivity, or hang-up about X; 'I have plans' commonly means I already have arrangements, while 'I have a plan' means I have a particular plan/strategy; 'I am an only child' means I have no siblings, while 'I am only a child' means I am merely/still a child; 'I had fun' means I enjoyed myself, while 'I was fun' describes me as enjoyable, entertaining, or good company.	have a thing for/about; have plans/have a plan; an only child/only a child; had fun/was fun	I have a thing for dogs, but she has a thing about dogs. / Sorry, I have plans tonight. / I have a plan for how we can fix this. / I am an only child. / I am only a child—how was I supposed to know? / I had fun at the party. / I was fun at the party, apparently; everyone kept laughing at my stories.	Preserve this as a multi-contrast learning card rather than the generic YouTube title. (1) 'Have a thing for someone/something' normally means have a liking, attraction, or special fondness. 'Have a thing about something' does NOT automatically mean 'hate/dislike it'; it means have a particular concern, fixation, sensitivity, hang-up, or strong feeling about it, which can be negative depending on context. Thus 'I have a thing about dogs' could suggest discomfort or an issue with dogs, but context is needed. (2) 'I have plans' is the idiomatic way to say you already have arrangements/commitments, especially when declining an invitation. 'I have a plan' means one particular plan, idea, or strategy. The plural is common for 'arrangements,' but it is not a grammatical rule that one must always use plural whenever one has a scheduled event. (3) 'an only child' is the fixed noun phrase for a person with no siblings; 'only a child' has focusing 'only' modifying 'a child' = merely/still a child. (4) 'I had fun' reports the speaker's experience of enjoyment; 'I was fun' predicates 'fun' of the speaker, meaning the speaker was enjoyable/entertaining to be with. The difference is not literally an -ed/-ing contrast; it is 'have fun' versus 'be fun.'		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: ちょっとした違いで意味が全然違う英語！ - YouTube
419	4185	4864	rebuttal	/rɪˈbʌt̬.əl/	noun	A response or argument that challenges or answers a claim, criticism, or opposing argument.	counterargument, response	The article included a detailed rebuttal of the criticism.	Noun corresponding to rebut.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: rebuttal
419	4186	4493	impostor syndrome	/ɪmˈpɑː.stɚ ˌsɪn.droʊm/	noun	A persistent feeling that one's achievements are undeserved and that one may be exposed as less competent than others believe.	fraud feelings, self-doubt	Despite years of experience, she still sometimes feels impostor syndrome.	Impostor is the traditional spelling; imposter is also common. The term describes a psychological experience, not necessarily a clinical diagnosis.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102, voca_0103 | searches: imposter syndrome
