427	4261	5267	garden-path sentence (why 'garden path'?)	/ˈɡɑːr.dən pæθ ˌsen.təns/	linguistics term / etymology note	A sentence that initially leads the reader or listener toward a plausible but incorrect grammatical interpretation, forcing them to go back and reinterpret it.	temporarily ambiguous sentence	'The old man the boats' sends many readers down the wrong interpretive path at first.	The name draws on the idiom 'lead someone down the garden path,' meaning to mislead or deceive someone. A garden-path sentence similarly leads the parser toward an initially attractive interpretation that turns out to be wrong. This is a deliberate deep-dive companion to the existing garden-path sentence card (WordID 4336), preserving the user's specific 'why is it called that?' search.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: why garden path sentence is called garden path sentence?
