428	4275	5273	worth vs worthwhile vs worthy		contrast / usage	A contrast among three related forms: worth commonly expresses value ('worth the money', 'worth doing'); worthwhile means worth the time/effort because it is useful or rewarding; worthy means deserving respect, attention, support, or a particular honor.	valuable; beneficial; deserving	The book is worth reading; the trip was worthwhile; and the charity is a worthy cause.	'Worth' is typically followed by a noun/pronoun or -ing form: worth $20, worth the effort, worth seeing. 'Worthwhile' is an adjective: a worthwhile project / It was worthwhile. 'Worthy' often means deserving: worthy of praise, a worthy opponent, a worthy cause.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: worth vs worthwhile vs worthy
