430	4296	5024	steel yourself	/stiːl jɚˈself/	verb phrase	To mentally prepare yourself to face something difficult, unpleasant, frightening, or painful.	brace yourself, fortify yourself	She steeled herself for the difficult conversation.	Common pattern: steel yourself for something / to do something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: Steel yourself
430	4297	4204	duck out	/dʌk aʊt/	phrasal verb, informal	To leave a place quietly or quickly, often to avoid attention or responsibility; also to avoid doing something.	slip away, sneak out, avoid	I ducked out of the meeting a few minutes early.	Common patterns: duck out of a room; duck out of an obligation.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: ducks out meaning
430	4298	5221	viscount	/ˈvaɪ.kaʊnt/	noun	A British nobleman ranking below an earl and above a baron.	nobleman, peer	The viscount inherited the title from his father.	Pronounced VY-count; the s is silent. Feminine title: viscountess.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: viscount meaning
430	4299	5209	vet	/vet/	verb; noun	As a verb, to examine someone or something carefully for suitability, accuracy, safety, or reliability. As a noun, short for veterinarian or veteran.	screen, check, evaluate	We need to vet the sources before citing them.	The verb is especially common for candidates, sources, proposals, suppliers, and information; noun meaning depends on context.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: vet meaning
