431	4302	3950	bod	/bɑːd/	noun (informal)	A person's body, especially when discussing physical appearance or build.	body, physique	He's in great shape and has a pretty athletic bod.	Informal shortening of body. Common in phrases such as dad bod, beach bod, hot bod.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099 | searches: bod
431	4303	4566	lack agency	/læk ˈeɪ.dʒən.si/	phrase	To have little ability, power, or perceived freedom to make one's own choices and act independently or influence what happens.	lack autonomy, lack control, be powerless	The character feels passive because she lacks agency in the story.	Builds on existing agency card, but this negative collocation is useful in criticism, psychology, sociology, and discussions of autonomy.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: lacking agency
431	4304	3865	analogous	/əˈnæl.ə.ɡəs/	adjective	Similar or comparable in certain important respects, though not identical.	comparable, similar, parallel	The role of a CPU is roughly analogous to the role of a brain in this simplified comparison.	Common pattern: analogous to. Focuses on functional or structural similarity rather than exact sameness.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099 | searches: analogous
431	4305	5303	A dry air advisory has been issued from Houston to tuna.		nonsensical / context-specific line (Journal with Witch)	A deliberately nonsensical weather-report-like sentence heard by Asa in Journal with Witch when ordinary adult speech stops registering as meaningful language. The words remain audible, but their semantic connections have broken down, creating the sense of hearing sounds without truly hearing the meaning.	deliberate nonsense, semantic disconnection, words without meaning	“A dry air advisory has been issued from Houston to tuna.” The sentence sounds like a weather bulletin, but “Houston to tuna” makes no literal sense.	Preserve this exact line because the nonsense is the point. In the scene, Asa has just lost her parents and is surrounded by relatives discussing, in a practical and emotionally cold way, who will take responsibility for her. Their speech is rendered to her as scrambled, semantically incoherent language: she can hear the sounds, but cannot emotionally process the words as meaningful communication. The weather-report syntax makes the line sound superficially normal while the content is impossible ('from Houston to tuna'), dramatizing psychological distance/dissociation from the adults' conversation. The image of a 'dry air advisory' also resonates with the dry/desert-like emotional imagery associated with Asa's isolation. Raw Japanese search: ヒューストンからマグロに乾燥注意報. User specifically wants this personally memorable anime line retained. More specifically, the keyword 'dry' also functions as important foreshadowing: it leads into the later desert-like inner landscape that visualizes Asa's loneliness. The apparently random 'dry air advisory' therefore does double work—it expresses her present inability to process the adults' words and quietly anticipates the dry, isolated emotional imagery that follows.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: ヒューストンからマグロに乾燥注意報
431	4306	4592	lineage	/ˈlɪn.i.ɪdʒ/	noun	A line of descent from an ancestor; figuratively, a historical chain of influence, development, or tradition.	ancestry, descent, heritage	The technique has a lineage stretching back centuries.	Useful figuratively for intellectual, artistic, technological, or linguistic traditions.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: lineage
431	4307	4793	preach to the choir	/priːtʃ tə ðə ˈkwaɪr/	idiom	To argue for a view to people who already agree with it, so persuasion is unnecessary.	preach to the converted	You don't need to convince me—you're preaching to the choir.	Very common AmE; BrE often 'preach to the converted.'		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: preaching to the choir
431	4308	3893	attentive	/əˈten.tɪv/	adjective	Paying close attention; also considerate of another person's needs or comfort.	alert, observant, considerate	"The students were unusually attentive during the demonstration.
The staff were attentive without being intrusive."	Two useful shades: mentally paying attention, and caring/considerate service or behavior.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: define attentive
431	4309	4124	deictic	/ˈdaɪk.tɪk/	adjective (linguistics)	Relating to words or expressions whose interpretation depends on context such as speaker, place, or time.	context-dependent, indexical	Words like “here,” “now,” “I,” and “you” are deictic expressions.	Related noun: deixis. Useful categories include person, place, time, discourse, and social deixis.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108, voca_0109 | searches: deictic
431	4310	4883	echoing / repeating the user	/ˈek.oʊ.ɪŋ/	conversation/AI term	Repeating or closely restating what a user has just said instead of adding substantive information, analysis, or progression.	parroting, mirroring, restating	The response felt unhelpful because it mostly echoed the user's point.	Mirroring can be useful for confirmation or empathy, but excessive repetition can feel mechanical or add no value. Raw search likely concerned this conversational behavior.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: repeating the user meaninig
432	4311	4201	dry hump	/draɪ hʌmp/	verb / noun, informal	To rub one's genitals against another person or object through clothing, without penetration.	grind	They were joking about dry humping at the party.	Dry humping is the common gerund/noun form. Informal sexual term.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: dry humping meaning
432	4312	4488	Imagism	/ˈɪm.əˌdʒɪz.əm/	noun, literary movement	An early-20th-century poetic movement favoring precise imagery, clear language, and economy of expression over ornament and abstraction.	Imagist movement	Imagism helped shape modernist poetry through its emphasis on direct, concrete images.	Associated with poets including Ezra Pound, H.D., and Richard Aldington.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: IMAGISM Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster
432	4313	4417	haughty	/ˈhɔː.t̬i/	adjective	Arrogantly proud and unfriendly, as if other people are beneath you.	arrogant, disdainful, aloof	She gave him a haughty look and walked away.	Usually negative; often describes a person's expression, manner, or attitude.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define haughty
432	4314	4574	laugh one's head off	/læf wʌnz hed ɔːf/	idiom, informal	To laugh very hard or for a long time.	laugh hysterically, crack up	I laughed my head off at that scene.	Hyperbolic and very common; comparable to laugh one's ass off, which is more vulgar/informal.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: laugh my head off
432	4315	4589	like someone doing something	/laɪk ˈsʌm.wʌn ˈduː.ɪŋ ˈsʌm.θɪŋ/	grammar pattern	To like, enjoy, approve of, or be comfortable with a person performing a particular action.	enjoy someone doing, approve of someone doing	I like you asking questions—it keeps the conversation moving.	Object + -ing is very common in informal English. A possessive form ('I like your asking questions') is possible but markedly more formal.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: like me doing something meaning
