432	4317	5319	the ick vs 蛙化現象		cross-cultural contrast	'The ick' is a sudden feeling of aversion or loss of attraction toward someone, often triggered by a behavior or detail. Japanese 蛙化現象 originally referred more specifically to developing strong aversion when a person one liked begins to reciprocate that affection; modern Japanese usage has broadened toward sudden turn-offs and can overlap with 'the ick.'	the ick, sudden turn-off, loss of attraction	She got the ick after watching him behave rudely to the waiter—but that is closer to the modern broad use of 蛙化 than its original psychological sense.	Do not treat 'the ick' as an exact translation of every 蛙化現象. Original academic 蛙化: the other person's reciprocation itself triggers aversion. Modern popular 蛙化: attraction suddenly collapses after some small behavior, which is much closer to 'the ick.' The existing 'the ick' card gives the English term; this card preserves the cross-cultural distinction.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: 蛙化現象　英語で
