432	4319	4272	false equivalence	/ˌfɔːls ɪˈkwɪv.ə.ləns/	noun	A misleading comparison that treats two things as equivalent even though important differences make the comparison invalid or disproportionate.	false comparison, misleading equivalence	Treating a minor mistake as equivalent to deliberate fraud creates a false equivalence.	Common in argument analysis, journalism, and political discussion.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: false equivalence
432	4320	5114	therapized	/ˈθer.ə.paɪzd/	informal adjective / participle	Shaped by therapy or therapy-style language and habits.	therapy-speak-influenced, therapy-shaped, steeped in therapy language (informal paraphrases)	The dialogue feels heavily therapized, with everyone naming their boundaries and triggers.	Recent informal formation; often humorous, skeptical, or critical rather than clinical.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: therapized
433	4321	5208	vested interest	/ˌves.tɪd ˈɪn.trəst/	noun phrase	A personal, financial, institutional, or other stake in an outcome that may influence someone's actions or judgment.	stake, personal interest, self-interest	The company has a vested interest in keeping the standard unchanged.	Often implies the person's position is not neutral because they benefit from a particular outcome.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: vested interest meaninng
433	4322	4967	treat someone to something	/triːt ˈsʌm.wʌn tuː/	verb pattern	To pay for or provide something enjoyable, special, or pleasant for someone; by extension, to give or present someone with a notable experience, sight, smile, performance, or other pleasurable display.	buy/provide for, give, present with	"I'll treat you to lunch.
She treated her friends to a round of drinks.
She treated him to a coy, inviting smile."	Core everyday use: treat someone to food, drinks, entertainment, or a gift means you pay for/provide it as a pleasurable gesture; treat yourself to something means buy/do something nice for yourself. The raw sentence uses the natural extended construction 'treat someone to a smile/look/view/performance': no literal payment is involved—the person is figuratively 'given' something pleasant or notable. Coy = deliberately shy or modest, often flirtatiously. A 'smile of invitation' signals welcome or romantic/sexual interest.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106, voca_0107 | searches: she treated him to a coy smile of invitation
433	4323	5051	subterfuge	/ˈsʌb.tɚ.fjuːdʒ/	noun	Deception or a deceptive strategy used to conceal intentions, evade a difficulty, or achieve something indirectly.	deception, trickery, ruse	They gained access through a carefully planned subterfuge.	Usually uncountable, though a subterfuge can mean a particular trick or device.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: define subterfuge | subterfuge meaning
433	4324	4047	circumvent	/ˌsɝː.kəmˈvent/	verb	To avoid or get around a rule, restriction, obstacle, or problem, often cleverly or indirectly.	bypass, evade, get around	They found a way to circumvent the restriction.	Often implies avoiding the intended effect of a rule or obstacle.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: circumvent
433	4325	4758	pedometer	/pəˈdɑː.mə.t̬ɚ/	noun	A device or app that counts the number of steps a person takes.	step counter	My phone's pedometer says I walked ten thousand steps today.	Traditional pedometers are dedicated devices; phones and watches often provide the same function via motion sensors.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: pedometer meaning
