437	4361	4725	outlier	/ˈaʊtˌlaɪ.ɚ/	noun	A person, thing, or data point that differs markedly from the rest of a group or falls outside the usual pattern.	exception, anomaly, aberration	That unusually high score may be an outlier.	In statistics, an outlier is an unusually distant observation; everyday use extends to atypical people, cases, or results.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099 | searches: outlier meaning
437	4362	5277	write-up	/ˈraɪt.ʌp/	noun	A written article or report; in U.S. workplace usage, also a formal disciplinary record documenting an employee's misconduct or rule violation.	report, article, disciplinary notice	The magazine published a short write-up of the event. / He got a write-up for repeated lateness.	Distinguish the noun 'write-up' from the verb 'write up.' 'Get a write-up' in a workplace or school context often means receive formal disciplinary documentation.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108, voca_0109 | searches: write-up meaning
437	4363	3883	assiduously	/əˈsɪdʒ.u.əs.li/	adverb	With persistent, careful, and sustained effort.	diligently, industriously	She worked assiduously to build the archive over several years.	Formal. Related adjective: assiduous.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: assiduously
437	4364	4675	there's no such thing as bad publicity	/ðerz noʊ sʌtʃ θɪŋ æz bæd pʌbˈlɪs.ə.t̬i/	saying	The idea that any public attention, even negative attention, can be beneficial because it increases awareness or fame.	all publicity is good publicity	The scandal boosted sales, reviving the old claim that there's no such thing as bad publicity.	Bad press is understandable, but the established saying is usually 'there's no such thing as bad publicity' or 'all publicity is good publicity.'		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: no such thing as bad press meaning
