437	4369	4555	kick something down the road	/kɪk ˈsʌm.θɪŋ daʊn ðə roʊd/	idiom	To postpone dealing with a problem or decision, leaving it to be handled later.	put off, defer, postpone	The temporary fix just kicks the real problem down the road.	Often critical: postponement avoids the issue rather than solving it. Related: kick the can down the road.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: kick something down the road meaning
437	4370	5426	gnarly	/ˈnɑːr.li/	adjective, informal/slang	Knotted, twisted, rough, or weathered; in slang, extremely impressive/cool or extremely difficult, nasty, severe, or gross, depending on context.	rough, twisted; awesome; nasty	The old tree had gnarly branches. / That was a gnarly wave. / He had a gnarly injury.	A classic context-dependent slang word. Positive surf/skate use ('awesome/intense') coexists with negative 'nasty/severe/gross.' The older literal sense refers to knots, twists, and roughness.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
438	4371	4428	hell hath no fury like a woman scorned	/hel hæθ noʊ ˈfjʊr.i laɪk ə ˈwʊm.ən skɔːrnd/	proverbial expression	A person who has been rejected or betrayed—traditionally a woman in this saying—may react with intense anger or revenge.	a scorned person can be fiercely vengeful	After the breakup turned ugly, his friend joked, 'Hell hath no fury like a woman scorned.'	A famous adaptation of a line by William Congreve; often used jokingly or dramatically. The traditional wording is gendered.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: hell hath no fury like a woman scorned meaning
438	4372	4335	galoot	/ɡəˈluːt/	noun, informal	A clumsy, awkward, or foolish man, often used with humorous rather than severe contempt.	oaf, lout, big clumsy guy	Move over, you big galoot.	Old-fashioned/playful in many contexts.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define galoot
438	4373	4019	carnage	/ˈkɑːr.nɪdʒ/	noun	The killing or violent injury of many people; informally, severe destruction, chaos, or damage.	slaughter, devastation, chaos	The storm left carnage across the coastline.	Strong word. Informal hyperbole is common for messy destruction or disastrous situations.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: carnage
