439	4386	4976	shut-in	/ˈʃʌtˌɪn/	noun; adjective	A person who rarely or cannot leave home, traditionally because of illness or disability; more loosely, someone highly socially withdrawn or homebound.	homebound person, recluse	He had become something of a shut-in and rarely went outside.	Traditional meaning is homebound, not simply introverted. Modern informal usage can overlap loosely with recluse/hikikomori-like descriptions, but they are not exact equivalents.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: shut-ins
439	4387	3849	adversary	/ˈæd.vɚ.ser.i/	noun	An opponent or enemy, especially in a conflict, contest, or struggle.	opponent, enemy, rival, antagonist, opposing party	The two lawyers were adversaries in court but friendly afterward. / A good strategy starts by understanding your adversary.	More formal than 'opponent' in many contexts and can imply sustained conflict. Common in military, legal, political, cybersecurity, strategic, and competitive contexts; not necessarily a personal enemy.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: adversary meaningh
439	4388	5504	I am an only child vs I am only a child		contrast / word-order pattern	'I am an only child' means I have no siblings. 'I am only a child' means I am merely/still a child, often emphasizing youth, limited responsibility, experience, or power.	no siblings vs merely/still a child	I'm an only child, so I never had to share a bedroom with a sibling. / I'm only a child—how am I supposed to handle all of this?	In 'an only child,' 'only' belongs inside the noun phrase and identifies a child with no siblings. In 'only a child,' focusing 'only' means 'merely/just.' A tiny shift in article/word position therefore changes the structure and meaning completely.	contrast card	Supplemental from Guidance #5051 video-content recovery
439	4389	4025	mitosis	/maɪˈtoʊ.sɪs/	noun (biology)	The process in which a cell nucleus divides to produce two genetically similar daughter nuclei, usually followed by cell division.	cell division	Mitosis allows organisms to grow and replace damaged cells.	Use mitosis as the headword. The original search phrase “cellular mitosis” is understandable and may occur descriptively, but cellular is normally redundant because mitosis is inherently a cellular/nuclear-division process. Compare karyokinesis and cytokinesis.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104 | searches: cellular mitosis
