441	4407	5299	obokoi → innocent / naive / childlike		Japanese-to-English nuance	Japanese おぼこい describes someone as innocent, naive, unsophisticated, or childlike—often with a sense of inexperience or untouched simplicity rather than stupidity.	innocent, naive, unsophisticated, childlike	She has an innocent, almost childlike quality about her.	There is no single English equivalent for every context. 'Innocent' is often warm/positive; 'naive' can suggest lack of worldly experience or judgment; 'unsophisticated' emphasizes lack of polish/worldliness; 'childlike' is usually more positive than 'childish.' Preserve the Japanese nuance rather than forcing one translation.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: おぼこいとは
441	4408	5332	wonky	/ˈwɑːŋ.ki/	adjective, informal	Not working properly, unreliable, crooked, unstable, or somehow 'off'; in another informal sense, excessively detailed or technical about policy or a specialist subject.	faulty, shaky, crooked, off; nerdy/technical	The table is a little wonky, and the payment system has been acting wonky too.	Very flexible informal word. Physical: crooked/unsteady. Systems/technology: malfunctioning or unreliable. Reasoning/data: questionable or not quite right. In political/policy contexts, 'wonky' can mean highly technical or characteristic of a 'wonk.' Context determines the sense.		voca_0099–0109 | Review Similar | confirmed KEEP
