442	4414	4749	patchy	/ˈpætʃ.i/	adjective	Uneven, inconsistent, or occurring only in some places or periods rather than continuously or uniformly.	uneven, inconsistent, spotty	Mobile coverage is patchy in the mountains.	Works for physical distribution (patchy fog/hair) and figurative quality or knowledge (patchy service, patchy understanding).		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104 | searches: patchy
442	4415	4530	irk someone	/ɝːk ˈsʌm.wʌn/	verb	To annoy, irritate, or bother someone.	annoy, irritate, vex	It really irks me when people leave without saying anything.	Irk means to annoy, irritate, bother, or make someone feel mildly angry, especially through a persistent or petty nuisance: 'It really irks me when people leave without saying anything.' 'Irked out' is a nonstandard but understandable colloquial slip/variation. Speakers may add out by analogy with common expressions such as freaked out, stressed out, tired out, or bugged out. In standard edited English, use irked / be irked or the verb pattern something irks someone rather than irked out.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: irk out meaning
