448	4473	5502	have a thing for someone/something vs have a thing about someone/something		contrast / idiomatic pattern	'Have a thing for X' usually means to be attracted to, fond of, or especially interested in X. 'Have a thing about X' means to have a particular fixation, sensitivity, hang-up, concern, or strong feeling about X; it can be negative, but does not automatically mean dislike.	be into / be fond of vs have an issue/fixation about	I have a thing for dogs—I absolutely love them. / She has a thing about dogs and gets tense whenever one comes near.	'For' points toward liking/attraction: 'I've always had a thing for redheads.' 'About' marks X as the subject of a particular psychological concern or fixation: 'He has a thing about germs.' Context decides whether that 'thing about' is fear, dislike, fussiness, obsession, etc. Therefore the contrast can produce a near-opposite reading in a suitable context, but 'about = dislike' is not a fixed equation.	contrast card	Supplemental from Guidance #5051 video-content recovery
448	4474	5107	that way	/ðæt weɪ/	adverbial phrase	In that manner; by that method; in that direction; or, depending on context, in a particular condition or attitude just mentioned.	like that, by doing that, in that direction	Do it that way and you'll save time.	Very context-dependent. 'Don't be that way' means don't behave/act like that; 'I didn't mean it that way' = with that meaning/interpretation.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: that way meaning
448	4475	5195	nerp	/nɝːp/	slang / playful expression	A playful slang form that can function as a goofy variation of 'nope'; in some uses it can also resemble 'derp' as a label for silliness, a silly mistake, or a foolish person.	nope; derp-like expression	“Want to go out tonight?” “Nerp.”	The meaning is not standardized, so context matters. One attested slang use is a playful 'no/nope'; other user-created uses treat it more like 'derp' or a label for a silly person/mistake. Do not assume every occurrence has the same meaning. Separately, NERP is the fictional quirky lifestyle/wellness company in Modern Family, owned by Nicole Rosemary Page; that proper-name use is not the same thing as the slang word.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: Urban Dictionary: nerp
