453	4526	4755	payload capacity	/ˈpeɪ.loʊd kəˈpæs.ə.t̬i/	noun phrase	The maximum weight or amount of cargo, passengers, equipment, or other useful load that a vehicle, aircraft, spacecraft, or machine is designed to carry.	load capacity, carrying capacity	The truck's payload capacity is 1,500 pounds.	Payload generally excludes the vehicle's own empty weight; exact definitions vary by vehicle/system specifications.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: payload capacity meaning
453	4527	4526	invigorate	/ɪnˈvɪɡ.ə.reɪt/	verb	To give someone or something renewed energy, strength, vitality, or enthusiasm.	energize, revitalize, refresh	The cold morning air invigorated me.	Invigorated = feeling energized or strengthened; invigorating = causing that feeling.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: invigorated meaning
453	4528	4355	get someone back on	/ɡet ˈsʌm.wʌn bæk ɑːn/	phrasal construction	To cause or arrange for someone to return to a program, activity, schedule, treatment, platform, or other previously understood context.	bring someone back, put someone back on	We should get her back on the show for another interview.	Meaning depends heavily on the following/understood object: back on the show, team, schedule, medication, track, etc.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: get someone back on meaning
453	4529	4848	raise the stakes	/reɪz ðə steɪks/	idiom	To increase the risk, importance, cost, reward, or intensity of a situation or competition.	up the ante, increase the risk	The lawsuit raised the stakes for both companies.	From gambling stakes; opposite idea: lower the stakes.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: raise the stakes meaning
