455	4547	4122	degradation	/ˌdeɡ.rəˈdeɪ.ʃən/	noun	The process of becoming worse, weaker, damaged, or lower in quality/status; also humiliating treatment.	deterioration, decline, humiliation	The study measured soil degradation over several decades.	Useful across environment, materials, image/audio quality, biology, and social treatment. Context determines whether it means physical deterioration or humiliation/loss of status.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0104, voca_0106, voca_0107 | searches: define degradation | degradation
455	4548	4091	corollary	/ˈkɔːr.ə.ler.i/	noun	A result, consequence, or proposition that follows naturally from something already established.	consequence, implication	One corollary of remote work is a reduced need for office space.	In formal logic/math, a proposition following readily from a theorem. General use = natural consequence.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: corollary
455	4549	5063	sweep something under the rug	/swiːp ˈsʌm.θɪŋ ˈʌn.dɚ ðə rʌɡ/	idiom	To hide, ignore, or avoid dealing with a problem, mistake, or embarrassing fact instead of addressing it openly.	cover up, conceal, hush up, brush aside, bury	They tried to sweep the complaint under the rug. / The company kept sweeping small safety problems under the rug until one became a crisis.	US wording; British English commonly has 'sweep something under the carpet.' Implies deliberate avoidance/concealment rather than simply forgetting. Do not confuse the idiom with literal 'slip on the rug.'		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: sweeping under the rug meaning
