456	4552	5231	wallow on one's own	/ˈwɑː.loʊ ɑːn wʌnz oʊn/	informal phrase / contextual use	To spend time by yourself dwelling in or fully experiencing sadness, disappointment, self-pity, or another difficult feeling, rather than immediately trying to distract yourself, solve the problem, or move on.	wallow alone, sit with one's feelings, mope by oneself	After the breakup, I just wanted to go home and wallow on my own for a while.	The core verb wallow is normally used with 'in' when the feeling itself is named: wallow in self-pity, sadness, misery, regret, etc. In 'wallow on one's own,' however, 'on one's own' tells us that the wallowing is being done alone; it is not replacing the usual 'in + feeling' complement. The phrase can therefore imply something like 'wallow in my feelings on my own.' Depending on tone, wallow often carries a mildly critical sense of dwelling excessively on negative feelings, but in context a speaker may use it more sympathetically or self-awarely to mean giving oneself some private time to feel bad before moving on. Preserve this searched construction because the 'on one's own' component was part of the original learning context.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101, voca_0102 | searches: wallowing on my own
