477	4768	5503	I have plans vs I have a plan		contrast / usage	'I have plans' normally means I already have arrangements or commitments. 'I have a plan' means I have one particular plan, idea, or strategy for achieving something.	have arrangements vs have a strategy	“Want to get dinner Friday?” “Sorry, I have plans.” / “How are we going to fix this?” “Don't worry—I have a plan.”	The plural 'plans' is highly idiomatic for scheduled arrangements and is especially common when declining an invitation. 'A plan' foregrounds a specific scheme/strategy. This is a meaning-and-collocation contrast, not a rule that every single scheduled event must grammatically be plural.	contrast card	Supplemental from Guidance #5051 video-content recovery
