490	4899	5226	wait for vs await	/weɪt fɔːr/; /əˈweɪt/	contrast	Both can mean remain until something happens or arrives, but wait normally needs for before an object, while await takes the object directly and is more formal.	wait for, await	We're waiting for the results. / We await the results.	Do not say 'await for the results.' Await is transitive and common in formal/written language; wait is far more common in everyday speech.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: waiting vs awaiting
