494	4940	5320	蛙化現象: coined in 2004 → popularized from around 2019		etymology / usage history	The term 蛙化現象 is traced to a 2004 Japanese psychology conference presentation by Shinsuke Fujisawa; it later spread beyond the original academic sense, with online explanatory articles/videos appearing around 2019 and much broader youth/pop-culture use by the early 2020s.	coined in 2004, popularized later	The term was already used academically in 2004, long before it became a widely recognized youth-culture expression.	The 2004 presentation was titled 『女子が恋愛過程で遭遇する蛙化現象』. The original sense concerned aversion arising when one's romantic interest is reciprocated. Later popular usage broadened toward suddenly losing attraction because of a small behavior or perceived flaw. Thus 'when did people start using it?' has two answers: academic coinage/use in 2004, and wider internet/youth diffusion much later, around 2019 onward, with major mainstream visibility in the early 2020s.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: 蛙化現象はいつ頃から使われるようになったか
