500	4997	5305	get back at someone	/ɡet bæk æt ˈsʌm.wʌn/	phrasal verb	To do something to hurt, annoy, embarrass, or disadvantage someone in return for something they did to you; to take personal revenge on them.	revenge; payback; retaliate; get revenge on someone; take revenge on someone	She embarrassed him in front of everyone, so he tried to get back at her by spreading a rumor.	Very common and conversational for personal 仕返し. The pattern is 'get back at someone (for something)': 'He wanted to get back at them for humiliating him.' It often suggests a personal, sometimes petty or emotionally motivated act of revenge. Compare: 'revenge' is the broad noun ('He wanted revenge') and can sound heavier or more serious; 'payback' is informal and can mean the retaliatory act or its consequence ('This is payback'), ranging from playful to hostile; 'retaliate' is a more formal verb and is especially natural for responding to attacks, punishment, measures, or actions between groups/institutions ('They retaliated after the attack'). 'Get back at' is therefore often the most natural everyday choice when 仕返し means doing something back to a particular person because of what they did.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: 仕返し 英語
