504	5039	4663	nerf	/nɝːf/	verb; noun, gaming slang	To weaken a character, weapon, ability, strategy, or other game element, usually through a developer's balance change; a nerf is such a reduction in power.	weaken, downgrade, reduce power	They nerfed the weapon in the latest patch.	Opposite: buff. Now sometimes extended metaphorically beyond games to mean deliberately weaken or limit something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: nerf
504	5040	4613	ludicrous vs. ridiculous	/ˈluː.də.krəs/; /rɪˈdɪk.jə.ləs/	adjective contrast	Both mean extremely unreasonable, absurd, or deserving ridicule. Ludicrous often emphasizes something so absurd that it is laughable; ridiculous is broader and more common in everyday speech.	absurd, preposterous, laughable	Charging $80 for a basic sandwich is ridiculous—almost ludicrous.	Ridiculous can also mean comically excessive ('a ridiculous amount'). Ludicrous often sounds slightly stronger or more formal.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: ludicrous vs ridiculous
505	5041	5045	stunner	/ˈstʌn.ɚ/	noun, informal	A strikingly attractive or impressive person or thing; when referring to a woman, an informal way to say she is exceptionally beautiful.	beauty, knockout, head-turner	Everyone agreed she was an absolute stunner.	Raw 'stunner girl' is not the normal fixed phrase; simply 'She's a stunner' or 'an absolute stunner.' Can describe things too: a stunner of a goal/view/dress.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: stunner girl meaning
