505	5044	4778	pleonasm	/ˈpliː.əˌnæz.əm/	noun, linguistics/rhetoric	The use of more words than necessary to express a meaning, sometimes redundantly and sometimes for emphasis.	redundancy, tautological wording	'Free gift' is often cited as a pleonasm.	Not every pleonasm is an error; some are conventional or rhetorically useful.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: define pleonasm
505	5045	4920	rumble vs. ramble	/ˈrʌm.bəl/; /ˈræm.bəl/	word contrast	Rumble usually refers to a low continuous sound, shaking movement, or an informal fight; ramble means wander without a fixed route or talk/write at length without clear focus.	roar/shake/fight vs. wander/digress	"Thunder rumbled in the distance.
He rambled for ten minutes without answering the question."	Similar spelling/sound, unrelated core meanings.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0106 | searches: rumble and ramble difference
505	5046	5467	hoist	/hɔɪst/	verb; noun	To raise, lift, or haul something to a higher position, especially something heavy or awkward, often with ropes, cables, pulleys, or mechanical equipment. It can also mean to lift or pull a person or oneself upward. As a noun, a hoist is a device or mechanism used for lifting.	lift, raise, haul up, heave, elevate	"The workers hoisted the steel beams onto the roof.
She hoisted herself onto the counter.
At sunrise, they hoisted the flag."	Core image: moving something upward, often with effort or mechanical help. Common collocations include hoist a flag, hoist a sail, hoist cargo, and hoist oneself up. In the idiom hoist with one's own petard / hoisted by one's own petard, hoist is part of a historical expression meaning to be harmed or defeated by one's own trap or scheme. Do not confuse the everyday verb hoist with the full idiom's figurative meaning.		Supplemental deep-dive from Guidance #4191 (hoist with one's own petard)
505	5047	5283	you gotta do you	/juː ˈɡɑː.tə duː juː/	informal expression	You have to act in the way that is right or authentic for you, even if other people would choose differently.	be yourself, do what works for you	I wouldn't make that choice, but you gotta do you.	Very informal. 'You do you' can be supportive ('be yourself') or mildly distancing/dismissive ('I disagree, but it's your choice'). 'Gotta' adds a sense of necessity.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: you gotta do you meaning
505	5048	4280	feel it in one's bones	/fiːl ɪt ɪn wʌnz boʊnz/	idiom	To have a strong intuitive feeling or certainty about something without clear evidence.	sense deeply, have a gut feeling	I can feel it in my bones—something big is about to happen.	Common personal form: I feel it in my bones.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: feel it in my bones meaning
505	5049	4719	oppositional defiant disorder (ODD)	/ˌɑː.pəˈzɪʃ.ən.əl dɪˈfaɪ.ənt dɪsˌɔːr.dɚ/	noun, psychology	The established diagnostic term for a persistent pattern involving angry or irritable mood, argumentative or defiant behavior, and vindictiveness.	ODD	The phrase he was looking for was oppositional defiant disorder, or ODD.	The raw search was 'oppositional accusation disorder.' That is not the standard diagnostic name. It may have come from a joke, fictional line, mishearing, or deliberately pseudo-clinical wording—the source context may have been Community—but the reusable real term is oppositional defiant disorder. Keep any pop-culture reading separate from the medical term unless the exact source is confirmed.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: oppositional accusation disorder meaning
505	5050	4435	hire out	/haɪr aʊt/	phrasal verb	To let someone use something in return for payment; or to make yourself or another person's services available for paid work.	rent out, lease; contract out	They hire out bikes by the hour.	Compare hire someone to do a job. Hire oneself out means work for others for pay.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: hire out
