506	5054	4251	exchange A for B	/ɪksˈtʃeɪndʒ eɪ fɔːr biː/	verb pattern	To give up or hand over A and receive B in return.	trade A for B, swap A for B	I exchanged the damaged shirt for a new one.	A is what you give/replace; B is what you receive.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: exchange a for b
506	5055	4050	clincher	/ˈklɪn.tʃɚ/	noun	A final fact, argument, event, or achievement that settles a matter or makes a decision certain.	decisive factor, final proof	The lower price was the clincher for me.	Also used in sports for a decisive score or event that secures a win.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define clincher
506	5056	5263	bleed for something / have bled for something	/bliːd fɔːr ˈsʌm.θɪŋ/	figurative expression	To have struggled, sacrificed, suffered, or invested oneself deeply for something, so that one's connection to it feels hard-earned rather than casual.	sacrifice for, suffer for, fight for	When you're someone who's bled for this language, casual dismissals of the effort can hit differently.	Figurative and emotionally intense. 'Bleed' turns effort into an image of pain, sacrifice, and personal cost. 'I've bled for this' can imply years of struggle, humiliation, discipline, or sacrifice—not necessarily literal injury.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0105, voca_0106 | searches: when you're someone who’s bled for this language,
506	5057	5487	nerd / geek	/nɝːd/; /ɡiːk/	noun, informal	Labels for people strongly associated with intellectual, technical, fandom, or niche interests; historically school-status stereotypes, now often neutral or proudly self-applied.	brainy person, enthusiast	The old movie treats the nerds and jocks as rival groups.	Both have shifted greatly toward neutral/positive self-identification. Nerd often foregrounds intellectual/academic intensity; geek often foregrounds specialized enthusiasm, though they overlap heavily.		Supplemental from approved batch 4662–4711
506	5058	5450	glitchy	/ˈɡlɪtʃ.i/	adjective, informal	Prone to glitches, intermittent malfunctions, or odd technical behavior.	buggy, unstable, flaky	The app is still a little glitchy after the update.	'Buggy' suggests software contains bugs; 'glitchy' often emphasizes intermittent, unpredictable, or weird behavior rather than a consistently reproducible defect.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
506	5059	3910	bane	/beɪn/	noun	A persistent source of harm, trouble, annoyance, or misery.	scourge, curse, nuisance	Spam calls are the bane of my existence.	Very common phrase: the bane of someone's existence/life. Historically bane could mean a cause of death or poison.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: define bane
506	5060	5259	whatnot	/ˈwʌt.nɑːt/	pronoun / noun, informal	Used after examples to mean 'and other similar things'; as a noun, it can also mean miscellaneous small items.	and so on, and the like, miscellaneous things	We talked about movies, games, music, and whatnot.	Very conversational. A whatnot can also historically mean a light shelf/stand for ornaments, but that sense is much less common.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: whatnot meaning
507	5061	4346	get behind an idea	/ɡet bɪˈhaɪnd ən aɪˈdiː.ə/	phrase	To support, endorse, or commit to an idea or proposal.	support, back, endorse	I could get behind that idea if we keep the budget realistic.	Natural pattern: get behind a person/plan/idea/cause.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: get behind the idea meaning
507	5062	4498	VN vs. LN	/ˌviːˈen/ vs. /ˌelˈen/	abbreviation contrast	In Japanese-learning and otaku contexts, VN usually means visual novel and LN means light novel.	visual novel vs. light novel	Some learners use VNs and LNs as sources of extensive Japanese reading practice.	A VN is an interactive, primarily text-and-image digital work; an LN is a prose book format, typically with some illustrations.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: in japanese learning, what is vn and ln
507	5063	3966	boxy	/ˈbɑːk.si/	adjective	Shaped like a box; broad, square, and lacking curves or tapering.	square, blocky	The jacket has a deliberately boxy cut.	Common for cars, clothing, furniture, and body shapes.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: boxy
507	5064	4168	dissonance	/ˈdɪs.ən.əns/	noun	A lack of harmony or agreement; in psychology, an uncomfortable conflict between beliefs, attitudes, or behavior.	discord, inconsistency, conflict	He felt cognitive dissonance after acting against his own values.	Also used in music for combinations of notes that sound tense or unresolved.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102, voca_0106 | searches: define dissonance | dissonance
507	5065	4011	call the shots	/ˌkɔːl ðə ˈʃɑːts/	idiom	To be the person who makes the important decisions and is in control.	be in charge, make the decisions	She's the one who really calls the shots around here.	Informal. Often implies practical decision-making power, whether or not the person has the formal title.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100, voca_0101 | searches: call the shots meaning
507	5066	3987	a brush with	/ə ˈbrʌʃ wɪð/	noun phrase	A brief encounter or experience, especially with danger, trouble, illness, or the law.	encounter, close call	He had a brief brush with fame in his twenties.	Common: a brush with death/the law/fame/danger.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099 | searches: brush with
507	5067	5038	the straw that broke the camel's back	/ðə strɔː ðæt broʊk ðə ˈkæm.əlz bæk/	idiom	The final small problem or burden that causes a situation already under strain to collapse or become intolerable.	the last straw, final trigger	The extra fee was the straw that broke the camel's back.	Closely related shorter idiom: the last straw.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0100 | searches: straw camel back
507	5068	4076	construe	/kənˈstruː/	verb	To interpret a word, action, statement, or situation in a particular way.	interpret, understand, read	His silence could be construed as agreement.	Very common passive pattern: be construed as something.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: define construed
507	5069	4993	slump over / be slumped over	/slʌmp ˈoʊ.vɚ/	verb phrase	To bend, collapse, or sit with the upper body drooping forward, often from exhaustion, unconsciousness, defeat, or poor posture.	hunch over, droop, collapse	He was slumped over his desk asleep.	Slumped over describes posture/state; slump can also mean a decline in performance/economy.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: slumped over meaning
507	5070	3916	bau5	/baʊs/	adjective / slang	Slang meaning excellent, impressive, or 'boss-like,' especially in older Deadmau5-related internet usage.	boss, awesome	That track is totally bau5.	Niche/source-dependent internet slang associated with Deadmau5-style leetspeak, where 'mau5' replaces 'mouse' and similar 5-for-S wordplay can appear. Treat recognition as the goal; do not assume 'bau5' is broadly current slang outside that context.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107 | searches: bau5 meaning
508	5071	3829	a notch on one's bedpost	/ə nɑːtʃ ɑːn wʌnz ˈbed.poʊst/	idiom	A sexual conquest, especially one treated as merely one more in a series.	sexual conquest	He seemed to treat every new relationship as another notch on his bedpost.	Often objectifying and somewhat dated. The image is of recording conquests by cutting notches into a bedpost.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: a notch on the bedpost
508	5072	4721	ore	/ɔːr/	noun	Rock or earth containing enough metal or another valuable mineral for it to be economically extracted.	mineral-bearing rock	The region contains large deposits of iron ore.	Ore is the raw mineral-bearing material; metal is extracted from ore through processing.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0103 | searches: ore meaning
508	5073	5268	wig someone out	/wɪɡ ˈsʌm.wʌn aʊt/	informal phrasal verb	To make someone feel disturbed, freaked out, uneasy, confused, or mentally thrown off.	freak someone out, unsettle, disturb	That uncanny smile really wigs me out.	Informal. Related 'wig out' means become extremely agitated, excited, or freaked out. The expression is well established in American informal English and is also understood/used elsewhere; it is not specifically more Australian than American. The exact emotional shade can range from creeped out to overwhelmed or startled.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: wig me out meaning
508	5074	5382	come out ahead		phrase	To finish in a better position than before or than others, especially financially or competitively.	end up better off, profit, gain	After all the expenses, we still came out ahead.	Often financial, but also broader: after trade-offs, conflict, negotiation, or competition, the final result leaves you advantaged.		voca_0099–0109 | reviewed improved card
508	5075	4887	replicant	/ˈrep.lɪ.kənt/	noun, science fiction	An artificial human-like being; especially, the bioengineered humanoids called replicants in the Blade Runner franchise.	android, synthetic human	The film asks whether a replicant can be meaningfully distinguished from a human.	Strongly associated with Blade Runner; not the normal general word for robot/android.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: replicant meaning
508	5076	4290	fizzle out	/ˈfɪz.əl aʊt/	phrasal verb	To gradually lose strength, energy, interest, or momentum and come to an unimpressive end.	peter out, fade away, die out	Their early enthusiasm fizzled out after a few weeks.	Often used for relationships, plans, protests, trends, conversations, or efforts.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: fizzled out
508	5077	4134	demoralizing	/dɪˈmɔːr.ə.laɪ.zɪŋ/	adjective	Causing someone to lose confidence, hope, enthusiasm, or morale.	discouraging, dispiriting	Repeated setbacks can be incredibly demoralizing.	Related verb demoralize = destroy or weaken morale/confidence.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0108 | searches: demoralizing
508	5078	5132	tinker with something		phrasal verb	To make small changes, adjustments, or experiments with something, often informally.	fiddle with, mess with, tweak, adjust, experiment with	I've been tinkering with the layout all afternoon.	Can imply curious experimentation or unhelpful minor adjustment.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101 | searches: tinkering meaning
508	5079	4549	jest	/dʒest/	noun; verb	A joke or playful remark; to speak or act jokingly rather than seriously.	joke, quip, joke around	I said it in jest—I wasn't actually criticizing you.	Common phrase: in jest = as a joke, not seriously.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: define jest
508	5080	4829	pull back	/pʊl bæk/	phrasal verb	To move away or retreat; to reduce involvement, spending, support, or intensity; or to reveal a wider view.	retreat, withdraw, scale back	Investors pulled back from riskier assets.	Transitive/intransitive patterns vary: pull back troops; pull back from a commitment.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: pull back something
509	5081	4038	chip away	/ˌtʃɪp əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To remove small pieces gradually; figuratively, to reduce, weaken, or make progress on something little by little.	erode, whittle away	"We're slowly chipping away at the backlog.
Years of criticism chipped away at his confidence."	Core pattern: chip away at something = gradually reduce, weaken, erode, or work through it. Literal use is also possible. This general card supports the more specific shell metaphor in #3790.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0107, voca_0108 | searches: chipping away meaning
509	5082	5055	superfluous	/suːˈpɝː.flu.əs/	adjective	More than is needed or useful; unnecessary because it is extra.	unnecessary, redundant, excessive	Cut any superfluous details from the introduction.	Not merely 'extra': usually implies the extra part serves no necessary purpose.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0101, voca_0102 | searches: superfluous definition
509	5083	4962	set in stone	/set ɪn stoʊn/	idiom	Fixed, final, and difficult or impossible to change.	fixed, finalized, immutable	The schedule isn't set in stone yet.	Very common in negatives: nothing is set in stone; it isn't set in stone.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0099 | searches: set in stone
509	5084	5064	sweltering	/ˈswel.tɚ.ɪŋ/	adjective	Extremely and uncomfortably hot.	boiling, scorching, oppressively hot	It was sweltering inside the apartment.	Often describes weather, a room, or conditions: sweltering heat.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0102 | searches: sweltering
509	5085	4296	flip out on someone	/flɪp ˈaʊt ɑːn ˈsʌm.wʌn/	phrasal verb (informal)	To suddenly direct intense anger, panic, or emotional agitation at another person.	blow up at, lose it on, lash out at	He flipped out on me over a tiny mistake.	Adds a target/person to flip out; often specifically means angrily confronting that person.		voca_0099–0109 | unmatched | folders: voca_0109 | searches: flip out on someone meaning
509	5086	6517	I'm expecting	/aɪm ɪkˈspektɪŋ/	euphemistic phrase	Without an object, 'I'm expecting' can mean 'I'm pregnant / expecting a baby,' especially when context makes pregnancy clear. In other contexts, expect normally needs an object/complement.	I'm pregnant, I'm expecting a baby	'We have some news—I'm expecting.' / 'I'm expecting a package' is ordinary expect and has nothing to do with pregnancy.	A gentle/euphemistic pregnancy expression. Batch21's pregnancy synonym card maps the register; this card preserves the exact elliptical phrase searched.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: I'm expecting meaning
509	5087	6876	detached	/dɪˈtætʃt/	adjective	Separated physically; emotionally uninvolved or not strongly affected; objective/dispassionate; in housing, not joined to another building.	separate, disconnected; unemotional, dispassionate, aloof	The cable had become detached from the wall. / He sounded strangely detached while describing the event. / They live in a detached house.	Broader than aloof. Detached emotional tone can be neutral/analytical rather than socially unfriendly. BrE 'detached house' = freestanding house.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: detached
509	5088	6404	be seconded / go on secondment	/biː sɪˈkɑːndɪd/; /ɡoʊ ɑːn səˈkɑːndmənt/	workplace verb / noun, chiefly BrE/international business	To be temporarily assigned or transferred from one's normal job/organization to work in another department, office, organization, or location while retaining an underlying employment relationship.	be temporarily assigned, be transferred temporarily, be posted; go on assignment	He was seconded to the New York office for two years. / She went on secondment to a partner company. / 日本企業の文脈の「出向」は、雇用関係を残した一時的な配置なら be seconded / go on secondment がかなり近い。	Japanese 出向 has institution-specific legal/employment nuances, so no single English term always maps perfectly. Secondment is especially natural in BrE/international corporate English; AmE may prefer temporary assignment/transfer depending on arrangement. 'Be posted to' often fits overseas/government assignments.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: 出向する 英語で
509	5089	6579	shuffle the functions of every hole	/ˌʃʌfəl ðə ˈfʌŋkʃənz əv ˌevri ˈhoʊl/	source-specific comic/threat line	A grotesque comic phrase meaning to rearrange or confuse the bodily functions associated with a person's various openings; the humor comes from deliberately absurd anatomical menace.	rearrange bodily functions (comic paraphrase)	'How about I shuffle the functions of every hole?' works as an absurd, threatening joke because it leaves the imagined result unspecified.	Preserve exact search wording because the line itself is the memorable unit. Not an established idiom; meaning is compositional and intentionally bizarre/crude.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: how about i shuffle the functions of every hole
509	5090	5827	redacted	/rɪˈdæktɪd/	adjective / past participle	Edited so that sensitive, confidential, or restricted information has been removed or obscured before release.	censored, blacked out, removed	The report was released with several names redacted.	Redact originally also means edit/prepare text, but modern public-record use strongly evokes hiding information.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: redacted meaning
510	5091	5538	dumpy	/ˈdʌmpi/	adjective, informal	Short and somewhat fat, or unattractively squat in shape; also used of a place that looks shabby, drab, or poorly maintained.	squat, shabby, frumpy	The motel looked a little dumpy from the outside.	Informal and potentially unkind when used about a person's body or appearance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: dumpy meanng
510	5092	6174	peacock	/ˈpiːkɑːk/	noun / verb	Strictly, an adult male peafowl, famous for its colorful train; colloquially often used for the species generally. As a verb, to display oneself proudly or ostentatiously.	male peafowl; show off, strut, preen	The peacock spread his train into a huge fan. / He peacocked around the party in a bright designer suit, clearly hoping to be noticed.	Female = peahen; collective/species = peafowl. Figurative verb and 'peacocking' emphasize conspicuous display, sometimes in fashion/dating contexts. 'Peacock feathers' technically come from males.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: peacock meaning
510	5093	6591	epistemology	/ɪˌpɪstəˈmɑːlədʒi/	noun	The branch of philosophy concerned with knowledge: what knowledge is, how we know things, what justifies belief, and the limits/sources of knowledge.	theory of knowledge	Epistemology asks questions such as what counts as knowledge and when a belief is justified. / The disagreement was partly epistemological: they trusted different kinds of evidence.	Raw search misspelled epistimology. Related: epistemic = relating to knowledge; epistemological.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: epistimology meaning
510	5094	6128	pelt	/pelt/	noun / verb	As a noun, an animal's skin with fur/hair attached. As a verb, to throw things at someone/something repeatedly or forcefully; rain/hail can pelt down, and someone can pelt along = run fast (especially BrE).	hide, fur; bombard, shower, hurl; dash, race	The trapper sold animal pelts. / Kids pelted the fence with snowballs. / Rain was pelting against the windows. / He came pelting down the stairs when he heard the news.	Several distinct senses. 'Pelt someone with X' = bombard them with thrown X. Weather pelts = strikes hard. BrE informal 'pelt along/down' = run quickly. Do not confuse with pellet.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: pelt meaning
510	5095	5527	take the floor	/ˌteɪk ðə ˈflɔːr/	idiom	To begin speaking to a group or assembly; also, to move onto a dance floor and begin dancing.	begin speaking, address the room	When the chair invited questions, Maya took the floor.	Floor historically refers to the speaking area of an assembly; context can also give the dance sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: take the floor
510	5096	5808	catfish someone	/ˈkætˌfɪʃ/	verb, internet slang	To deceive someone online by pretending to be a different person, often using a false identity or stolen photos.	impersonate, deceive with a fake identity	He realized he'd been catfished when the photos turned out to belong to someone else.	Past: catfished. A catfish can also be the deceiver; unrelated literal fish sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: catfished meaning
510	5097	6420	tell it like it is	/ˌtel ɪt laɪk ɪt ˈɪz/	idiom	To describe a situation directly and frankly as one sees it, without softening, euphemizing, flattering, or avoiding unpleasant truths.	be frank, speak plainly, call it as you see it, be straight with someone	I don't always agree with her, but she tells it like it is. / He'll tell you like it is even when the answer isn't what you want to hear.	Often approving self-image ('I'm just telling it like it is'), but can also excuse tactlessness or present subjective judgment as objective truth. 'Call it like I see it' explicitly foregrounds personal perspective.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: tell it like it is meaning
510	5098	5619	register	/ˈredʒɪstər/	verb / noun	To record officially or sign up; to show or express; to be noticed or understood; as a noun, an official list, record, range, or device that records/controls something.	enroll, record; notice, sink in	I registered for the course online, but his warning didn't register until later.	Highly polysemous. Useful verb senses include register for, register a complaint, emotion registered on her face, and it didn't register = it wasn't noticed/understood.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: register meaning
510	5099	6550	PSA	/ˌpiː es ˈeɪ/	initialism	Most commonly in everyday media: public service announcement—a message intended to inform the public about health, safety, civic, or social issues. Online, 'PSA:' can jokingly introduce useful advice or a warning.	public service announcement, public notice	PSA: back up your files before updating the app. / The station aired a road-safety PSA. / In medicine, PSA can also mean prostate-specific antigen.	Highly context-dependent acronym. Everyday/internet 'PSA' often = public service announcement; medical PSA = prostate-specific antigen. Don't assume one expansion across domains.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: psa meaning
510	5100	6918	body count	/ˈbɑːdi ˌkaʊnt/	noun / slang	Literally, the number of people killed in a conflict/disaster/crime. Slang: the number of sexual partners someone has had. Context determines the meaning.	death toll; number of sexual partners (slang)	The report gave a provisional body count after the attack. / Online, people sometimes ask about someone's 'body count' to mean sexual-partner count.	The sexual sense can sound objectifying, judgmental, or provocative and is common in internet/dating discourse. Do not assume that sense in news/war/crime contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: body count meaning
511	5101	6587	conscientious / conscientiousness (not *consciousful)	/ˌkɑːnʃiˈenʃəs/	adjective / correction	Careful, responsible, diligent, and guided by a desire to do one's duties properly; *consciousful is not the standard word for this meaning.	diligent, responsible, scrupulous, thorough, careful	She's a conscientious editor who checks every citation twice. / If you meant 'responsible and careful,' use conscientious, not *consciousful.	Existing WID6123 has conscientious; this card preserves the raw malformed search as a correction/memory hook. Conscious = awake/aware; conscience = moral sense; conscientious = careful/dutiful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: consciousful
511	5102	6537	jingling keys / constant dopamine hits	/ˌdʒɪŋɡlɪŋ ˈkiːz/	internet metaphor / critique	A dismissive metaphor for flashy, rapidly changing, attention-grabbing stimuli used to keep an audience engaged, likened to jingling keys in front of a baby; often paired online with talk of 'constant dopamine hits.'	cheap stimulation, attention bait, sensory bait, constant novelty	The critic complained that the editing felt like jingling keys—cuts, captions, sound effects, and constant novelty every few seconds. / Calling it 'dopamine hits' is a loose popular metaphor, not a precise measurement of dopamine release.	Preserve the combined search context. The criticism is about attention capture/overstimulation. Popular 'dopamine hit' language often oversimplifies neuroscience: dopamine is involved in motivation, learning, salience, reward prediction, and more, not simply a pleasure chemical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: jingling keys / constant dopamine hits
511	5103	5965	imperil	/ɪmˈperəl/	verb	To put someone or something in danger or at serious risk.	endanger, jeopardize, threaten, put at risk	A prolonged strike could imperil the launch schedule. / The character's impulsive decision imperils everyone in the group.	More formal/literary than endanger or put at risk. Jeopardize often focuses on chances, plans, careers, success; imperil vividly suggests exposure to danger.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: imperil meanig
511	5104	6254	appellate	/əˈpelət/	adjective, legal	Relating to appeals or to a court that reviews decisions made by a lower court.	appeals-related, reviewing-court (paraphrase)	The case moved from the trial court to the appellate court. / Appellate judges generally review alleged legal errors rather than retrying the entire case from scratch.	Common collocations: appellate court, appellate jurisdiction, appellate review, appellate judge. Contrast trial court. Exact scope/standard of review depends on the legal system and issue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: appellate
511	5105	6637	get on someone's case	/ˌɡet ɑːn ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈkeɪs/	idiom, informal	To criticize, nag, pressure, question, or repeatedly bother someone about something they should do or have done.	nag, hassle, ride, pressure, give someone a hard time	My boss got on my case about the late report. / Don't get on his case—he already apologized.	Especially AmE. 'Be on someone's case' can mean already be nagging/pressuring them; 'get on' emphasizes starting to do so.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: get on your case meaning
511	5106	6688	ledge	/ledʒ/	noun	A narrow horizontal surface or projection, especially one sticking out from a wall, cliff, window, or other vertical surface.	shelf, projection, sill, rim	The cat was sitting on the window ledge. / They rested on a narrow rock ledge halfway up the cliff.	Contrast ledger /ˈledʒər/. Window ledge overlaps with windowsill, though sill is specifically the bottom horizontal part of a window opening/frame.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ledge meaning
511	5107	5817	detoxify	/diːˈtɑːksɪˌfaɪ/	verb	To remove or neutralize toxic substances from something; in general/wellness language, to rid the body or environment of supposed toxins.	detox, cleanse, purify	The liver helps detoxify substances in the bloodstream.	In medical/scientific contexts the object and mechanism matter; commercial 'detox' claims can use the word much more loosely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: detoxify synonym
511	5108	5648	positively grim	/ˌpɑːzətɪvli ˈɡrɪm/	adjective phrase	Extremely or unmistakably grim; here positively functions as an intensifier meaning definitely or downright.	downright grim, decidedly grim	By late afternoon, the weather looked positively grim.	Positively does not mean 'optimistically' here. It intensifies a negative adjective, often with slightly emphatic or literary flavor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: positively grim meaning
511	5109	6151	tingle	/ˈtɪŋɡəl/	verb / noun	To feel a light prickling, buzzing, stinging, or pins-and-needles sensation; figuratively, to experience a thrill of excitement, fear, anticipation, etc.	prickle, buzz, pins and needles; thrill	My fingers started to tingle after being out in the cold. / I felt a tingle of excitement when the opening music started. / The spicy sauce left my lips tingling.	Physical sensation can have many causes and is not itself a diagnosis. Figurative 'a tingle of excitement/fear' is common. Tingle is lighter/finer than throb or ache.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: tingle
511	5110	6298	bring something on oneself	/ˌbrɪŋ ˌsʌmθɪŋ ɑːn wʌnˈself/	idiom-like construction	To cause one's own problem, punishment, criticism, misfortune, or other consequence through one's actions or choices.	bring it upon oneself, cause one's own trouble, ask for it (more blaming), have oneself to blame	He brought the criticism on himself by making promises he couldn't keep. / I feel bad for her, but some of this trouble was brought on by her own decisions.	Usually negative and can sound blame-oriented. 'Bring it on yourself' is conversational; 'bring it upon yourself' is slightly more formal/dramatic. Avoid using it to dismiss harm where responsibility is complex.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: bring something on yourself meaning
512	5111	6683	retrieval	/rɪˈtriːvəl/	noun	The act/process of getting something back or accessing stored information; in memory, bringing stored information into active use; in computing, finding and returning stored data.	recovery, recall, access, fetching	Flashcards strengthen retrieval because you have to produce the answer from memory. / Search indexing speeds information retrieval. / The dog was trained for game retrieval.	Important learning-science sense: retrieval practice = actively recalling information, not merely rereading it. Also computing/information-retrieval and physical recovery senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: retrieval
512	5112	6239	fakecel	/ˈfeɪkˌsel/	noun, internet/incel-community slang	A mocking label for someone accused of claiming to be an incel ('involuntarily celibate') despite supposedly having realistic romantic/sexual opportunities, prior experience, or other traits that disqualify them in the speaker's view.	fake incel, pretend incel (paraphrase)	Forum users called him a fakecel because they believed he could date if he actually tried. / The label reflects community gatekeeping about who counts as 'truly' involuntarily celibate.	Incel-community jargon, not neutral social-science vocabulary. The criteria are unstable and ideological. -cel is highly productive online: fakecel, volcel, femcel, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: fakecel meaning
512	5113	5532	it never occurred to me	/ɪt ˈnevər əˈkɜːrd tə miː/	expression	Used to say that an idea or possibility never entered your mind or was never considered.	I never thought of it, it didn't cross my mind	It never occurred to me that she might already know.	Pattern: it occurs to someone that... = the thought comes into that person's mind.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: it never occurred to me meaning
512	5114	6744	approach to doing something	/əˈproʊtʃ tə ˈduːɪŋ/	grammar / collocation	When approach is a noun meaning method or way of dealing with something, use approach to + noun/gerund: 'an approach to learning,' not normally 'an approach to learn.' As a verb, approach can take a direct object.	method of doing, way of dealing with	I like your approach to learning vocabulary. / We need a different approach to solving the problem. / We approached the problem cautiously.	Here 'to' is a preposition, so it takes a noun or -ing form. Compare 'plan to do,' where to is infinitival.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: approach to doing or do
512	5115	6476	Spyderco	/ˈspaɪdərkoʊ/	proper noun / brand	An American brand known for folding knives and knife-design features; the name in a text usually refers to a Spyderco-branded knife rather than a generic knife type.	knife brand, folding-knife brand	The character pulled out a Spyderco—here the brand name identifies the knife, not a special generic category called a 'spyderco.'	Proper brand name. Preserve because source context may use it metonymically ('a Spyderco' = a knife made by the brand). No need to learn product specifications for vocabulary purposes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: spyderco knife
512	5116	6434	sapient being / sapient person	/ˈseɪpiənt ˈbiːɪŋ/	noun phrase	A being characterized as sapient—wise or, especially in speculative fiction, possessing advanced reasoning and intellectual capacities comparable to or beyond humans.	intelligent being, rational being, advanced-thinking being	The treaty recognizes every sapient species as a legal person. / In fantasy and science fiction, 'sapient beings' may include humans, aliens, androids, or magical species.	'Sapient person' is grammatically possible but uncommon in ordinary speech; 'sapient being/species' is more natural in SF/worldbuilding. Do not use sapient as a simple synonym for sentient.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: sapient person
512	5117	5791	shove something down someone's throat	/ˌʃʌv ˌsʌmθɪŋ daʊn ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈθroʊt/	idiom, informal	To force an idea, message, belief, product, or content on someone aggressively or repeatedly, leaving little room to refuse or ignore it.	force on, ram down someone's throat	Viewers complained that the message was being shoved down their throats.	Strongly negative metaphor; often used hyperbolically about media, advertising, ideology, or unwanted advice.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: shove it down their throat meaning
512	5118	6249	Indigenous	/ɪnˈdɪdʒənəs/	adjective; sometimes collective identity term	Originating in and having deep historical ties to a particular land or region; especially, describing peoples who inhabited a place before colonization or later dominant settlement and maintain distinct cultural/social identities.	native to, aboriginal (context-sensitive), First Peoples (context-specific)	The museum worked with Indigenous communities to revise the exhibit. / This plant is indigenous to the region. / Capitalization is common when referring to Indigenous peoples as a broad identity category.	For plants/animals, indigenous ≈ native to a place. For peoples, terminology is political, historical, and region-specific; prefer the community's own name when known. 'Aboriginal' is appropriate in some official/local contexts but not universally interchangeable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: indigenous meaning
512	5119	6416	a handful of vs a mouthful of	/ə ˈhændfʊl əv/; /ə ˈmaʊθfʊl əv/	contrast / quantifier phrases	A handful of = a small number/amount, literally enough to fit in a hand; a mouthful of = an amount of food/drink that fills the mouth, and is not normally a general small-number quantifier.	a few, a small number; one bite/sipful	Only a handful of people stayed until the end. / He took a mouthful of water before answering. / Don't use 'a mouthful of people' to mean a few people.	Handful has productive figurative quantity use and also 'someone is a handful' = difficult to manage. Mouthful is about what fits in the mouth or, separately, a long/difficult word/phrase.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: a handful of vs a mouthful of
512	5120	6449	concede a point	/kənˌsiːd ə ˈpɔɪnt/	verb collocation	To admit that a particular argument, observation, or part of another person's case is valid, often while continuing to disagree with the larger conclusion.	grant a point, admit a point, acknowledge a point	I'll concede that point: the first season was paced better. But I still think the sequel has stronger characters. / She conceded the point without abandoning her overall argument.	Excellent debate phrase because concession can be partial. Conceding a point ≠ conceding the whole argument. Common pattern: 'I concede that..., but...'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: concede a point meaning
513	5121	5997	sentience	/ˈsenʃəns/	noun	The capacity to have subjective experiences or feelings—especially sensations such as pleasure and pain; definitions vary somewhat across philosophy, ethics, neuroscience, and AI discussion.	capacity for feeling, conscious experience (overlapping), phenomenal experience	Animal-ethics arguments often treat sentience—the capacity to suffer or enjoy—as morally important. / Debates about AI sometimes blur sentience, consciousness, intelligence, and self-awareness even though they are not identical.	Sentience is often narrower than consciousness: roughly capacity for felt experience. Sapience = wisdom/intelligence (especially humanlike higher reasoning); self-awareness = awareness of oneself. Existing sentientism cards assume this concept but do not define it standalone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: sentience meaning
513	5122	6685	ranty	/ˈrænti/	adjective, informal	Sounding or feeling like a rant: overly angry, prolonged, complaint-heavy, heated, or uncontrolled in tone.	rant-like, tirade-like, angry, preachy, rambling	I want the review to be critical without being ranty. / He gets a little ranty whenever that topic comes up.	'Not being ranty' means avoiding a rant-like tone, not necessarily avoiding criticism. Informal productive adjective from rant + -y.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: not being ranty
513	5123	6487	warm blanket	/ˌwɔːrm ˈblæŋkɪt/	noun phrase / metaphor	Literally, a blanket that provides warmth; figuratively, something comforting, familiar, safe, reassuring, or emotionally cozy.	comfort, security blanket, cozy familiarity, reassurance	I wrapped myself in a warm blanket and watched an old anime. / Rewatching that series feels like a warm blanket after a stressful week.	The figurative metaphor is common in reviews: a familiar show/book/game can 'feel like a warm blanket.' 'Security blanket' more specifically suggests a source of emotional reassurance/dependence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: warm blankets meaning
513	5124	6314	vexation	/vekˈseɪʃən/	noun, formal/literary	The state of being annoyed, frustrated, worried, or troubled; something that causes such irritation or distress.	annoyance, irritation, frustration, exasperation, chagrin	To my great vexation, the same error returned after the update. / He sighed in vexation when the train doors closed just before he reached them.	More formal/literary than annoyance. Can convey a blend of irritation and frustrated distress. Related verb vex = annoy, trouble, puzzle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: vexation meaning
513	5125	6608	life is like climbing a mountain	/ˌlaɪf ɪz laɪk ˌklaɪmɪŋ ə ˈmaʊntən/	metaphor	A common metaphor framing life as an upward journey involving effort, obstacles, stages, setbacks, perspective, and goals/summits.	life is a journey, uphill climb, climb toward a goal	He described life as climbing a mountain: you struggle upward, stop to catch your breath, and see the landscape differently as you rise.	Not a single fixed proverb; meaning depends on speaker. Preserve as conceptual metaphor rather than inventing a canonical quotation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: someone says life is something like a climbing a mountain
513	5126	6172	shriek	/ʃriːk/	verb / noun	To give a loud, high-pitched, piercing cry, often from fear, excitement, pain, or laughter.	scream, screech, squeal, cry out	She shrieked when the spider landed on her sleeve. / The kids shrieked with laughter on the ride. / The brakes gave a metallic shriek.	Shriek is high-pitched and piercing. Scream is broader; screech can describe harsh human/animal/mechanical sound; squeal may be excitement or a high-pitched sound.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: shriek meaning
513	5127	6932	suck-up	/ˈsʌk ˌʌp/	noun, informal, disparaging	A person who flatters or ingratiates themselves with someone in authority to gain approval or advantage.	brown-noser, sycophant, bootlicker, toady	Everyone thought he was a suck-up because he praised the boss no matter what. / Don't be such a suck-up.	Hyphenated noun. Strongly negative. Compare suck up to someone (verb). 'Teacher's pet' overlaps in school contexts but is not identical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: suck-up meaning
513	5128	5767	bona fide	/ˌboʊnə ˈfaɪd/	adjective / adverb	Genuine, real, authentic, and made or acting in good faith.	genuine, authentic, legitimate	She is now a bona fide expert in the field.	Distinct from existing bona fides (plural noun = credentials/evidence of legitimacy). In AmE the adjective is often pronounced BOH-nuh fide.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: bona fide meaning
513	5129	6215	fuck-shit-brah / fuckshit, brah	/ˈfʌkʃɪt brɑː/	internet / stylized slang; uncertain phrase	A highly informal, profanity-heavy expression whose exact meaning depends on source/community; it may function as an exclamation, stylized persona-speech, or a compound around 'fuckshit' (nonsense, bad behavior, chaotic stuff) plus 'brah/bro.'	bullshit/nonsense, chaotic stuff, bro/brah (contextual)	The phrase reads more like stylized internet speech than a standard idiom. / 'What is this fuckshit, brah?' would roughly convey irritated disbelief at some nonsense or chaos.	Raw search preserved because spelling/segmentation is uncertain. 'Brah' is a bro-like address associated with surf/skater/internet speech. Do not assign a precise fixed definition without the original source context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: fuck shit bra meaning
513	5130	6588	converge on something	/kənˈvɜːrdʒ ɑːn/	verb phrase	To come together toward the same place, result, conclusion, target, or solution.	meet, come together, home in on, agree on	Several lines of evidence converge on the same explanation. / The roads converge on the town center.	Physical and abstract. Contrast diverge = move/develop apart.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: converge on something meaning
514	5131	6569	bring/call something into question	/ˌbrɪŋ ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˌɪntu ˈkwestʃən/	idiom	To create doubt about the truth, validity, reliability, legitimacy, wisdom, or acceptability of something.	cast doubt on, call into question, undermine, raise doubts about	The missing data brings the conclusion into question. / His inconsistent account called his credibility into question.	'Call X into question' is more established/common; 'bring X into question' also occurs. Different from 'bring up a question' = raise a question for discussion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: bring into question meaning
514	5132	6369	plunder	/ˈplʌndər/	verb / noun	To steal goods or valuables by force, especially during war, invasion, piracy, or disorder; as a noun, stolen goods or wealth taken by such force. Figuratively, to exploit or strip resources.	loot, pillage, ransack, despoil, rob; booty	Pirates plundered the merchant ship. / The invading force carried off its plunder. / Critics accused the company of plundering the region's natural resources.	Close to pillage, but plunder works especially well for taking valuables/resources and can be a noun. Pillage often foregrounds violent destructive looting of a place. Loot is broader and more everyday.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define plunder
514	5133	5889	flattening (anime/animation production)	/ˈflætənɪŋ/	contextual industry/criticism term	In animation/anime discussion, flattening can refer to reducing visual depth, dimensionality, tonal variation, character complexity, or production distinctions; the exact technical meaning depends on the source context.	simplification, loss of depth, homogenization, leveling	The compositing gives the scene a flattening effect, making foreground and background feel less separated. / Critics sometimes talk about adaptation flattening when a nuanced character becomes much more one-note.	The raw search says 'flattening in anime industry' but does not preserve the original source sentence. Do not pretend it names one standardized anime-industry process. Keep as contextual recognition vocabulary and check surrounding context when encountered.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: flattening in anime industry meaning
514	5134	6327	out of bounds	/ˌaʊt əv ˈbaʊndz/	adjective/adverb phrase	Outside the permitted playing area; outside an allowed physical area; figuratively, beyond what is acceptable, appropriate, permissible, or relevant.	off-limits, prohibited, beyond the line, unacceptable	The ball went out of bounds. / That part of the building is out of bounds to visitors. / Asking about her medical history in a job interview felt completely out of bounds.	Sports sense is literal boundary. Figurative use is common for topics/behavior. Contrast 'off-limits' (explicitly prohibited) and 'out of line' (behavior socially unacceptable).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0117 | search: define out of bounds
514	5135	5784	ingenuity	/ˌɪndʒəˈnuːəti/	noun	Cleverness, inventiveness, and skill in designing, solving, or creating something.	inventiveness, resourcefulness, creativity, cleverness, originality	The repair showed remarkable ingenuity. / It took real ingenuity to build a working prototype from spare parts. / I admire the ingenuity of a simple solution that removes three unnecessary steps.	Ingenuity = clever inventiveness in solving/creating, often under constraints. Important contrast: 'ingenious' = cleverly inventive; 'ingenuous' = innocent/candid; 'disingenuous' = insincere/misleading. Those look similar but are different word families in modern use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: ingenuity
514	5136	6690	balance the ledger (in a relationship)	/ˌbæləns ðə ˈledʒər/	financial metaphor / relationship phrase	To treat favors, sacrifices, mistakes, harms, or obligations as if they were credits and debits that should be made even; in relationships, often implies scorekeeping or restoring perceived fairness.	even the score, settle accounts, keep score, restore balance	Healthy reciprocity doesn't require balancing the ledger after every tiny favor. / He apologized partly because he felt he needed to balance the emotional ledger.	Not a single fixed relationship idiom, but a natural accounting metaphor. Can criticize transactional scorekeeping or describe a genuine desire to restore fairness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: balancing the ledger in relationships
514	5137	6668	buffalo hugs	/ˌbʌfəloʊ ˈhʌɡz/	source-specific / playful phrase	A context-dependent playful phrase rather than a broadly fixed English idiom. It can literally or jokingly evoke hugs involving a buffalo and may also occur as a community-specific saying, title, nickname, or proper-name expression.	context-dependent; big/tight hug (possible playful reading), hugs from afar (contextual/proper-name use)	They ended the message with 'buffalo hugs,' using it as an affectionate in-group sign-off. / Without the original source, ask what 'buffalo hugs' refers to rather than assuming a fixed slang meaning.	KEEP, but preserve uncertainty. Possible contexts include communities using 'High, Low, and Buffalo' check-ins, playful associations with a large/tight buffalo hug, and proper-name/business uses such as 'Buffalo Hugs & Heart.' These associations do not establish one universal dictionary meaning. The verb 'buffalo' can mean intimidate, bewilder, or bamboozle, but that does not by itself prove a fixed idiom 'buffalo hug.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: buffalo hugs
514	5138	6413	altered state (of consciousness)	/ˌɔːltərd ˈsteɪt/	noun phrase	A condition in which ordinary consciousness, perception, awareness, thought, emotion, or sense of self is significantly changed, whether through sleep, meditation, substances, illness, trance, etc.	altered state of consciousness, nonordinary state, trance-like state	The documentary discusses meditation as one route to an altered state of consciousness. / The character seems to enter an altered state after the ritual.	Broad descriptive term, not automatically drug-related and not itself a diagnosis. In psychology/neuroscience, definitions and boundaries vary; always use context to identify the cause/type.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: altered state meaning
514	5139	5717	come across vs come through vs come off	/kʌm əˈkrɔːs; kʌm ˈθruː; kʌm ˈɔːf/	contrast card	Come across can mean encounter/find or give an impression; come through can mean survive, arrive/be delivered, or succeed when needed; come off can mean detach, succeed, or give a particular impression.		I came across an old photo. / She came through when we needed help. / His joke came off as rude.	Preserve because the original search explicitly compared three confusable phrasal verbs. Existing come off card covers one member, but the contrast itself is useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: come across vs come through vs come off
514	5140	6765	conceited	/kənˈsiːtɪd/	adjective	Having an excessively high opinion of oneself, one's abilities, appearance, or importance.	vain, arrogant, self-important, egotistical, full of oneself	He comes across as conceited because he constantly talks about how talented he is. / Confidence isn't the same as being conceited.	Usually negative. Conceited emphasizes inflated self-opinion; arrogant additionally emphasizes superiority and often dismissive behavior toward others.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: conceited meaning
515	5141	6480	pry something out	/ˌpraɪ ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˈaʊt/	phrasal construction	To force something out using leverage or effort; figuratively, to extract information, an admission, a secret, or a response from someone with difficulty.	lever out, wrench out; extract, drag out, coax out	I pried the nail out with a tool. / It took twenty minutes to pry the story out of him. / She wouldn't volunteer the answer, so I had to pry it out of her.	Physical pry = use leverage to force apart/open/remove. Figurative pattern often 'pry information/the truth out of someone.' Do not confuse with pry into = intrude curiously into private matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: pry it out meanig
515	5142	6846	detractor	/dɪˈtræktər/	noun	A person who criticizes, disparages, or expresses a negative view of someone or something.	critic, opponent, naysayer, disparager	The film has passionate fans and equally vocal detractors. / Even his detractors admitted the presentation was effective.	Unlike adversary, a detractor need not be actively competing against you; the core is criticism/disparagement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define detractors
515	5143	6501	run someone off	/ˌrʌn ˌsʌmwʌn ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To force, chase, intimidate, or drive someone away from a place; in driving, 'run someone off the road' means force their vehicle off the roadway.	drive away, chase off, scare off, force out	The locals ran the trespassers off the property. / The truck nearly ran us off the road. / He felt as if the hostile atmosphere was designed to run newcomers off.	Often run someone off + place/property/road. Distinct from 'run off' = leave suddenly, print copies, drain liquid, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: run someone off meaning
515	5144	6402	scamper	/ˈskæmpər/	verb / noun	To run or move quickly with light, short steps, often playfully, nervously, or like a small animal/child.	scurry, skip/run quickly, dash, skitter	The children scampered across the playground. / A mouse scampered behind the cabinet. / The puppy scampered over when it heard the treat bag.	Compare scuttle = hurried short steps, often furtive/crablike; scamper often feels lighter/playful and is common with children/small animals. As a noun: 'a quick scamper.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: scamper
515	5145	6697	conjure / conjuring	/ˈkɑːndʒər/; /ˈkɑːndʒərɪŋ/	verb / noun-gerund	To perform magic or seemingly summon something by magic; figuratively, to bring an image, memory, feeling, or idea vividly to mind; conjuring = the act/practice of doing so.	summon, evoke, call up, invoke; magic	The magician seemed to conjure a coin from nowhere. / That soundtrack conjures images of late-night city streets. / 'The Conjuring' uses the magical/supernatural sense in its title.	Common phrase conjure up. Figurative evoke sense is very useful. Pronunciation often /ˈkʌndʒər/ in BrE and /ˈkɑːndʒər/ in AmE.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: conjuring meaning
515	5146	5845	yapper	/ˈjæpər/	noun, slang	A person who talks a lot, especially continuously, loudly, or about trivial things.	chatterbox, nonstop talker	I'm a certified yapper once I get comfortable around people.	From yap = talk noisily/at length. Contemporary internet use is often playful and self-deprecating.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: yapper meaning
515	5147	5780	desecrate	/ˈdesɪˌkreɪt/	verb	To treat a sacred, respected, or solemn place or object with violent disrespect or irreverence.	defile, profane, violate	The vandals were accused of desecrating the memorial.	Related noun desecration already existed in prior review/merge history; base verb is independently useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112, voca_0113 | search: desecrate meaning
515	5148	5918	euphemism	/ˈjuːfəˌmɪzəm/	noun	A mild, indirect, or less offensive expression used instead of one considered harsh, embarrassing, unpleasant, taboo, or too direct.	softened expression, indirect term, polite substitute	'Passed away' is a euphemism for 'died.' / Calling layoffs a 'workforce adjustment' can sound like a corporate euphemism designed to soften what happened.	A euphemism changes how something is framed, not necessarily the underlying fact. Compare understatement (deliberately presents something as less extreme), circumlocution (wordy indirectness), and doublespeak (language that obscures/manipulates meaning, usually critical).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: euphemism meaning
515	5149	6187	command	/kəˈmænd/	noun / verb	An authoritative order; authority/control over people or a situation; mastery of a skill or language. As a verb, to order authoritatively, control, lead, or deserve/attract something such as respect or attention.	order, directive; control, authority; mastery; order, lead, dominate	The officer gave the command to stop. / She has an impressive command of English. / The hill commands a view of the harbor. / His calm competence commands respect without him demanding it.	Key patterns: command someone to do X; be in command; have a good command of X; command respect/attention/a high salary. In 'command respect,' command = naturally inspire or merit it, not literally order people to respect you.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define command
515	5150	6165	sobriety	/səˈbraɪəti/	noun	The state of not being intoxicated; especially, sustained abstinence from alcohol or drugs. More formally, it can also mean seriousness, moderation, or clear-headed restraint.	abstinence, being sober, recovery (not identical); seriousness, temperance	He celebrated five years of sobriety. / Early sobriety changed how she organized her social life. / The report was praised for the sobriety of its tone.	In recovery contexts, sobriety can be personally defined and may involve abstinence from particular substances. 'Recovery' is broader than simply not using. Formal 'sobriety of tone/judgment' = seriousness and restraint.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sobriety meaning
516	5151	5892	knock someone off their feet	/ˌnɑːk ˌsʌmwʌn ɔːf ðer ˈfiːt/	idiom	Literally, to make someone fall over; figuratively, to overwhelm, astonish, strongly impress, or suddenly weaken someone.	floor, bowl over, blow away, overwhelm, sweep off one's feet (romantic nuance)	The wave knocked me off my feet. / Her performance completely knocked me off my feet. / That flu knocked him off his feet for nearly a week.	Compare sweep someone off their feet: especially romantic enchantment. Knock someone off their feet can be physical, impressive, shocking, or illness-related.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: knock off one's feet
516	5152	6710	ascertain whether ...	/ˌæsərˈteɪn ˈweðər/	formal construction	To find out or determine whether something is true, has happened, or meets a condition, usually through investigation, checking, or evidence.	determine whether, establish whether, find out whether, verify whether	We need to ascertain whether the two files contain the same records. / The doctor tried to ascertain whether the symptoms had changed.	Formal. Whether introduces the yes/no uncertainty being determined. In everyday speech, 'find out whether/if' is usually more natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ascertain whether meaning
516	5153	6426	rekindle	/ˌriːˈkɪndəl/	verb	Literally, to light a fire again; figuratively, to revive or renew a feeling, relationship, interest, hope, memory, conflict, or enthusiasm that had faded.	revive, reignite, renew, reawaken, resurrect, spark again	The trip rekindled my interest in photography. / They met again years later and rekindled their friendship. / The documentary rekindled an old debate.	Re- + kindle. Often positive with romance/interest/hope, but can also rekindle conflict, resentment, controversy. 'Reignite' is close and preserves the fire metaphor more transparently.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: rekindle meaning
516	5154	6850	identify with	/aɪˈdentəˌfaɪ wɪð/	phrasal verb	To feel a personal connection, similarity, sympathy, or shared perspective with a person, character, group, or experience.	relate to, connect with, see oneself in, empathize with	I identified with the character's difficulty fitting in. / Many viewers identify with her frustration even if they disagree with her choices.	Different from identify as = state one's identity/category, and identify someone/something = recognize/name.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: identify with definition
516	5155	6385	lexicographer	/ˌleksɪˈkɑːɡrəfər/	noun	A person who writes, edits, researches, or compiles dictionaries.	dictionary writer, dictionary editor, lexicology professional (overlapping)	Lexicographers track how words are actually used before deciding how to describe them in a dictionary. / Modern lexicographers often work with huge searchable corpora.	Lexicography = practice/study of dictionary making. Lexicology = study of words/vocabulary more broadly. A lexicographer does not simply 'invent definitions'; evidence of usage is central.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define lexicographers
516	5156	6783	institutional clout	/ˌɪnstəˈtuːʃənəl ˈklaʊt/	noun phrase	Influence or power that comes from an institution's established status, resources, authority, reputation, networks, or position within a system.	institutional influence, organizational power, establishment leverage	The small group had good ideas but lacked the institutional clout to change policy. / A university's name can carry institutional clout beyond the work of any one professor.	Builds on clout = influence. Institutional clout is structural/organizational, not merely personal popularity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: institutional clout meaning
516	5157	5880	skeletons in the closet	/ˌskelətənz ɪn ðə ˈklɑːzət/	idiom	Embarrassing, shameful, scandalous, or damaging secrets from someone's past that they want to keep hidden.	dirty secrets, hidden scandals, buried secrets	Every family has a few skeletons in the closet. / The documentary starts digging into the politician's old skeletons in the closet.	BrE commonly says skeletons in the cupboard. The image is of a hidden secret that would be shocking if discovered.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: skeletons in the closet meaning
516	5158	6755	you can see up my nose	/ju kən ˌsiː ʌp maɪ ˈnoʊz/	conversational phrase	The camera/viewing angle is so low or close that the inside of my nostrils is visible—usually said self-consciously about an unflattering shot.	the camera is looking up my nose, low-angle close-up	Can you move the camera higher? You can see up my nose from that angle. / I hate this webcam position—it looks straight up my nose.	Natural 'see up someone's nose.' Useful for camera/video calls. 'Look up someone's nose' can describe the viewing direction; unrelated to 'look down one's nose at' = act superior.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: you can see up my nose meaning
516	5159	5876	obsess over/about something	/əbˈses/	verb	To think about, worry about, or focus on someone or something persistently and excessively; also to dominate someone's thoughts.	fixate on, dwell on, preoccupy oneself with, be consumed by, get hung up on	I spent way too long obsessing over one awkward sentence in the review. / Fans are still obsessing over that tiny detail in the final scene. / Don't obsess about getting every word perfect when you're speaking.	Common patterns: obsess over/about X; be obsessed with/by X. Obsess often implies disproportionate or hard-to-stop attention, stronger than simply focus on.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113, voca_0116 | search: obsess
516	5160	5526	sit someone down	/ˌsɪt ˈsʌmwʌn daʊn/	phrasal verb	To have someone sit, often so you can speak to them seriously, explain something, or deal with an issue directly.	have a talk with, speak seriously to	I sat him down and explained exactly what had happened.	Often implies a deliberate, focused conversation rather than merely causing someone to sit physically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: sit someone down meaning
517	5161	6368	yeoman	/ˈjoʊmən/	noun / attributive adjective	Historically, a free man/small landholder or a servant/official of a particular rank; in modern phrases, 'yeoman's work/service' means diligent, valuable, hard work.	small landholder (historical), attendant; diligent worker (figurative)	The volunteers did yeoman's work sorting thousands of records. / In historical England, yeoman referred to a social rank below the gentry, though its exact status changed over time.	Modern learners most often meet 'yeoman's work/service,' praising dependable hard labor. Also appears in naval ranks/titles and historical institutions such as Yeomen Warders.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: yeoman
517	5162	6092	jerk one's head	/ˌdʒɜːrk wʌnz ˈhed/	verb phrase	To move one's head suddenly and sharply, often to look, signal, recoil, or indicate a direction.	snap one's head, whip one's head, nod sharply, motion with one's head	She jerked her head toward the door, silently telling me to leave. / He jerked his head around when he heard his name.	Jerk emphasizes abrupt sharp motion. 'Jerk one's head toward X' can function as a quick directional gesture; 'snap/whip one's head around' emphasizes sudden turning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: jerk one's headmeaning
517	5163	6474	shiv	/ʃɪv/	noun / verb, slang	A makeshift or improvised knife or stabbing weapon, especially associated with prisons; as a verb, to stab someone with such a weapon.	improvised knife, prison knife; stab	The prison drama shows an inmate hiding a homemade shiv. / In crime fiction, 'shiv' immediately evokes a crude concealed stabbing weapon.	Slang with strong prison/crime association. This is descriptive vocabulary, not a technical category: improvised weapons vary widely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: shiv
517	5164	6581	it didn't come out right	/ɪt ˌdɪdənt kʌm ˌaʊt ˈraɪt/	conversational phrase	What I said, wrote, made, cooked, recorded, printed, etc. did not turn out or get expressed the way I intended; often used after wording accidentally sounds wrong/offensive.	that came out wrong, it didn't turn out right, I didn't express that well	Sorry, that didn't come out right—I meant that I was surprised, not disappointed. / The photo didn't come out right, so I took another one.	Come out can refer to words being expressed, a result/product emerging, photos developing/printing, information becoming public, etc. In conversation 'that came out wrong' is especially common for misphrasing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: it didn't come out right meaning
517	5165	5975	boggle someone's mind / something boggles someone	/ˌbɑːɡəl ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈmaɪnd/	idiom / verb pattern	To astonish, overwhelm, or confuse someone because something is extremely surprising, complex, or hard to comprehend.	astound, bewilder, blow someone's mind, be mind-boggling	It boggles my mind that they made the same mistake three times. / The sheer number of possible combinations boggles me. / The scale of the project is mind-boggling.	Existing boggle WID? has 'mind began to boggle'; this card preserves the searched argument pattern. Most idiomatic: it boggles the mind / it boggles my mind; 'X boggles me' is grammatical but less formulaic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: something boggles you meaning
517	5166	6401	adapt	/əˈdæpt/	verb	To change oneself or something so it becomes suitable for new conditions, purposes, audiences, or environments; to modify a work into another form such as a film adaptation.	adjust, modify, acclimate, tailor, convert, transform	It took me a while to adapt to living in a different country. / We adapted the workflow after the first few batches. / The novel was adapted into an animated series.	Patterns: adapt to X (oneself changes); adapt X for Y / from X / into Y (modify something). Contrast adopt = take/accept/use as one's own. Existing 'adopt vs adapt' card compares them; standalone adapt adds full constructions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define adapt
517	5167	6522	damage control	/ˈdæmɪdʒ kənˌtroʊl/	noun	Actions taken to limit, contain, repair, or reduce harm after a mistake, scandal, accident, leak, failure, or other damaging event.	containment, crisis management, mitigation, cleanup, reputation management	After the disastrous interview, the PR team went into damage-control mode. / We spent the next hour doing damage control after the bad update went live.	Originally literal emergency control of damage, especially ships; now extremely common figuratively in PR/work/relationships. 'Do damage control'; 'damage-control mode.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: damage control
517	5168	6823	gamify	/ˈɡeɪməˌfaɪ/	verb	To add game-like mechanics or incentives—points, levels, streaks, badges, challenges, competition, feedback—to a non-game activity to increase engagement or motivation.	turn into a game, add game mechanics, game-ify	The app gamifies vocabulary review with streaks and levels. / You can gamify chores, but rewards can also distract from the underlying goal.	Noun gamification. Does not necessarily mean creating a full game; usually means applying selected game-design elements.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: gamify meaning
517	5169	5622	blow someone/something out of the water	/ˌbloʊ aʊt əv ðə ˈwɔːtər/	idiom, informal	To defeat, outperform, disprove, or impressively surpass someone or something by a very large margin.	crush, demolish, outclass	Their new prototype blew the competition out of the water.	Also used of arguments, expectations, records, or previous versions—not only direct competitors.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: blow out of water
517	5170	6129	stammer / stammering	/ˈstæmər/	verb / noun / adjective-participle	To speak with involuntary pauses, repetitions, or difficulty producing sounds/words; also, in nonclinical description, to speak haltingly because of nervousness or shock.	stutter, falter, stumble over one's words, speak haltingly	He stammered out an apology when everyone turned to look at him. / She began stammering with nerves during the presentation. / A persistent stammer can be a speech disorder, not simply nervousness.	Stammer and stutter overlap heavily; regional/preference differences exist, and clinical terminology may favor stuttering. Avoid using speech disfluency as shorthand for stupidity or dishonesty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: stammering meaning
518	5171	6726	box jellyfish	/ˈbɑːks ˌdʒelifɪʃ/	noun	A cnidarian of class Cubozoa, named for its roughly box-shaped bell; some species have extremely potent venom.	cubozoan, sea wasp (some species/common names)	Box jellyfish live mainly in warm coastal waters. / Some box jellyfish species can cause severe envenomation.	Not one single species. 'Sea wasp' may refer to particular dangerous species and is not a perfect synonym for every box jellyfish.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: Box jellyfish
518	5172	6251	weeb out	/ˌwiːb ˈaʊt/	internet slang / phrasal verb	To enthusiastically indulge in, gush over, or display one's anime/Japanese-pop-culture fandom, often self-deprecatingly or playfully.	geek out over anime/Japan, nerd out, go full weeb	Give me ten minutes to weeb out over this ridiculously good transformation sequence. / We spent the whole train ride weebing out about old anime openings.	Productive slang built from weeb + out, modeled on geek out/nerd out. Usually playful among fans, but weeb can also be insulting depending on speaker/context. Does not literally imply the harsher stereotype every time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: weeb out meaning
518	5173	6297	it's all on you	/ɪts ˌɔːl ɑːn ˈjuː/	conversational phrase	You have full responsibility, control, or choice in the matter; depending on tone, it can mean 'the decision is yours' or 'you will bear the consequences.'	it's up to you, it's your responsibility, the burden is on you	I've explained the options; from here, it's all on you. / If you ignore the backup warning and lose the file, that's all on you. / The final call is all on you.	More responsibility-heavy than neutral 'it's up to you.' Tone can be supportive ('I trust you') or accusatory ('don't blame me'). Context/prosody matter.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: it's all on you meaning
518	5174	6037	vigilant	/ˈvɪdʒələnt/	adjective	Carefully watchful and alert for possible danger, wrongdoing, problems, or changes.	watchful, alert, attentive, wary, on guard	Security staff remained vigilant throughout the event. / You have to be vigilant about small changes in the data when the sample is this noisy.	Often vigilant about/against/for. Wary emphasizes suspicion/caution; vigilant emphasizes active sustained watchfulness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: vigilant meaning
518	5175	5948	labile verb	/ˈleɪbaɪl vɜːrb/	noun, linguistics	A verb that can be used both transitively and intransitively without changing its form, with a systematic relationship between the arguments.	ambitransitive verb, ergative verb (in some English-grammar uses)	The chef boiled the water. → The water boiled. / I moved the chair. → The chair moved.	Labile is broader terminology than the English-teaching use of ergative. Not every transitive/intransitive alternation behaves identically: compare 'I ate the cake / I ate' (object omission) with 'I broke the glass / the glass broke' (affected object becomes intransitive subject).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: labile verbs
518	5176	5726	tenuously	/ˈtenjuəsli/	adverb	In a weak, slight, uncertain, or only loosely connected way.	weakly, loosely, precariously, faintly, only marginally	The theory is only tenuously connected to the evidence. / The sequel is only tenuously connected to the original story. / He was still tenuously holding on to hope.	From tenuous = thin/weak/slight. Common with connected/linked/related, supported, held. Often signals that a claimed relationship or evidential link exists, but only weakly. 'Precariously' fits physical/security senses better than logical connection.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: tenuously
518	5177	5955	spastic	/ˈspæstɪk/	adjective / noun; medical history / offensive slang	Relating to muscle spasticity in medical contexts; historically a noun/adjective for people with cerebral palsy or motor disabilities. In British English especially, slang uses meaning clumsy/stupid are highly offensive ableist insults.	spasticity-related; spaz (offensive derivative)	The clinician described increased spastic muscle tone in the patient's leg. / In British conversation, calling someone 'spastic' as an insult is strongly offensive and should be avoided.	Important regional difference: the insult has long been especially severe in the UK because of its disability history. U.S. 'spaz' historically became more casual, but it shares the same ableist origin and can still offend. Existing WID1414 notes this link.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: spastic meaning
518	5178	6158	a bad egg	/ə ˌbæd ˈeɡ/	idiom / noun phrase	A person regarded as dishonest, troublesome, unreliable, or morally bad.	bad apple, troublemaker, shady character, rotten person	His uncle warned him that the new business partner was a bad egg. / The character looks respectable at first, but you gradually realize he's a bad egg.	Old-fashioned but still readily understood, often playful. 'Bad apple' is more common and can imply one corrupting a group; bad egg simply labels the person.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: A bad egg
518	5179	6659	sponge (slang/figurative)	/spʌndʒ/	noun / verb, informal	A person who habitually lives off others or takes their money/help without giving back; as a verb, to obtain things by freeloading or absorb information readily ('sponge it up').	freeloader, moocher, scrounger; absorb	He's a sponge who never pays for anything himself. / Kids can sponge up new expressions surprisingly fast.	Freeloader sense is disparaging. Literal sponge absorbs liquid, motivating 'sponge up information.' Distinct from the animal/material senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: sponge in slang
518	5180	5512	brain fart	/ˈbreɪn fɑːrt/	noun, informal	A brief mental lapse in which you forget something obvious, say the wrong thing, or momentarily fail to think clearly.	mental lapse, momentary blank, slip	I knew her name, but I had a total brain fart when I introduced her.	Very informal and mildly vulgar; usually humorous and self-deprecating.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: brain fart meaning
519	5181	6803	quick/slow on the uptake	/ˌkwɪk ɑːn ði ˈʌpteɪk/	idiom	Quick/slow to understand, notice, or grasp what is being implied or explained.	quick-witted, catch on quickly; slow to catch on	She was quick on the uptake and understood the joke immediately. / I'm a little slow on the uptake before coffee.	Existing WID6411 covers 'slow on the uptake.' This card preserves the broader productive pattern, especially the positive 'quick on the uptake.' Uptake here = mental reception/understanding.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: on the uptake meaning
519	5182	6226	effeminate	/ɪˈfemənət/	adjective	Describing a man or boy as having mannerisms, appearance, voice, or qualities culturally stereotyped as feminine.	feminine-presenting (not exact), unmanly (dated/pejorative), delicate (contextual)	The actor was mocked as effeminate simply because of his voice and gestures. / What one culture labels 'effeminate' may be completely ordinary masculine behavior in another.	Often judgmental or pejorative because it relies on gender stereotypes; use carefully when describing a person. It does not imply sexual orientation. 'Feminine' is more neutral and not restricted to men.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define effeminate
519	5183	6718	let slip	/ˌlet ˈslɪp/	verb phrase / idiom	To reveal information accidentally or carelessly, often something intended to remain secret; also literally allow something to escape one's grasp/control.	blurt out, accidentally reveal, give away, disclose inadvertently	He let slip that they were planning a surprise party. / She accidentally let the name slip during the interview.	Very common pattern: let slip that... / let a detail/name slip. Existing 'slip out' card mentions it, but this construction is independently useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: let slip meaning
519	5184	6888	put off doing something	/ˌpʊt ˈɔːf ˈduːɪŋ/	grammar / phrasal verb pattern	To postpone or delay an action. In this sense, put off is followed by a noun or gerund, not an infinitive.	postpone, delay, defer	I kept putting off replying to the email. / Don't put off studying until the night before the test.	Existing put off card covers postpone and repel senses; this card preserves the gerund complementation: put off doing, not *put off to do.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: put off doing something
519	5185	6635	polymath	/ˈpɑːlimæθ/	noun	A person with substantial knowledge, learning, or expertise across many different subjects or fields.	Renaissance person, universal scholar, multidisciplinary thinker	Leonardo da Vinci is often described as a polymath. / He wants to be a polymath, not merely dabble in ten hobbies.	Raw 'polymas' appears to be a misspelling/mishearing. Polymath is the standard word; plural polymaths.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: polymas meaning
519	5186	5868	give someone their due	/ˌɡɪv ˌsʌmwʌn ðer ˈduː/	idiom	To acknowledge the credit, ability, good qualities, or achievement someone deserves, often despite criticism or dislike.	give credit where it's due, give someone credit, acknowledge someone's merits	I disagree with him on almost everything, but I'll give him his due: he knows how to hold an audience. / Give the animators their due—the action scenes look fantastic even when the story drags.	Often concessive: 'I don't like X, but to give X their due...' Similar 'give credit where credit is due' emphasizes fair recognition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: give someone one's due meaning
519	5187	6135	wary vs weary	/ˈweri/ vs /ˈwɪri/	contrast card	Wary = cautious, watchful, or suspicious because danger/problem may exist. Weary = tired, exhausted, or fed up after prolonged effort or experience.	cautious vs tired; guarded vs exhausted	I'm wary of links from unknown senders. / I'm weary after answering emails all day. / She grew weary of the argument but remained wary of trusting him.	Classic spelling/pronunciation confusable. Memory hook: wary = beware; weary = wearied/tired. Existing standalone cards remain useful; contrast card targets the common mix-up.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: wary vs weary
519	5188	5591	throw someone a bone	/ˌθroʊ ˌsʌmwʌn ə ˈboʊn/	idiom, informal	To give someone a small concession, reward, or token benefit, often to placate them while withholding something more important.	give a token concession, toss someone a crumb	The company wouldn't raise salaries, but it threw employees a bone by adding an extra day off.	Raw search included 'a formidable foe'; formidable already exists in Master. The idiomatic learning target is throw someone a bone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: throw a formidable foe a bone meaning
519	5189	5523	limit (core idea)	/ˈlɪmɪt/	noun / verb	A boundary or point beyond which something cannot or should not go; as a verb, to keep something within such a boundary.	boundary, cap, restriction; restrict	There is a limit to how much information I can absorb at once.	Core image: a boundary marking 'this far, but no farther.' Useful for understanding limited, limitation, off-limits, speed limit, etc.		"voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: core idea of the word ""limit"""
519	5190	6446	concede (sports)	/kənˈsiːd/	verb, sports	To allow the opposing side to score a goal, point, run, etc.; in some sports/competitions, also to admit defeat or surrender a hole/match without completing play.	allow, give up, let in; surrender	They conceded two goals in the final ten minutes. / The defense has conceded only six goals all season. / In match play golf, a player can concede a putt or hole.	Especially common in football/soccer: concede a goal / goals conceded. AmE sports may prefer 'give up/allow' in many contexts. Related but distinct from concede = reluctantly admit a point.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: conceded meaning in sports
520	5191	6646	pad a scene / padding	/ˌpæd ə ˈsiːn/	verb / noun, storytelling	To lengthen a scene, episode, story, or runtime with material that adds little essential plot/information, often to meet length requirements or slow pacing.	filler, stretch out, bulk out, pad the runtime	The adaptation pads the scene with repeated reaction shots. / That entire subplot feels like padding added to reach twelve episodes.	Padding is often critical, but extra material can still add atmosphere/character even if not plot-essential. Also pad = cushion/protect or add false expenses/numbers.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: padding the scene
520	5192	6258	garner	/ˈɡɑːrnər/	verb	To obtain, collect, or gradually attract something, especially attention, support, praise, votes, information, or awards.	gain, gather, attract, accumulate, earn	The video garnered millions of views within a week. / Her performance garnered praise even from critics who disliked the film. / The proposal failed to garner enough support.	Common with abstract/countable results rather than ordinary physical possession: garner attention/support/praise/votes/interest. Slightly more formal/journalistic than get.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: garner meaning
520	5193	6838	home of a melon	/ˌhoʊm əv ə ˈmelən/	usage contrast / context-dependent phrase	Literally, the place where one particular melon belongs or originates; unlike 'home of the melon,' it does not naturally sound like a slogan for a region famous for melons.	place where a melon comes from	If you're describing a farming region, 'home of the melon' or 'home to famous melons' is more natural than 'home of a melon.'	Search-intent contrast. Article choice changes interpretation: a = one nonspecific melon; the = a known/type-symbolic melon. Exact intended source remains uncertain.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: home of a melon meaning
520	5194	5897	Lady and the Tramp: 'Tramp'	/ˌleɪdi ən ðə ˈtræmp/	title/context card	In Disney's Lady and the Tramp, Tramp is the male stray dog's name; the word evokes a roaming, unattached drifter, contrasting with Lady's sheltered, respectable identity.	stray, wanderer, drifter (contextual)	The title itself sets up the contrast: Lady belongs to a comfortable home, while Tramp lives as a free-roaming stray.	The raw search asks for quotes, but without the original quote there is no supported exact line to preserve. This card keeps the likely vocabulary reason behind the search without inventing copyrighted dialogue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: lady and the tramp quotes
520	5195	6437	go nuclear	/ˌɡoʊ ˈnuːkliər/	idiom, informal	To react or escalate in an extremely aggressive, drastic, or all-out way; to use the most severe option available. Literal contexts can refer to adopting/using nuclear power or weapons.	go ballistic, go scorched-earth, escalate drastically, use the nuclear option	He went nuclear when he found out the files had been deleted. / The company went nuclear in the lawsuit and demanded every available remedy.	Hyperbolic figurative expression. 'Nuclear option' often means an extreme procedural/strategic measure. Avoid reading literal nuclear meaning unless context supports it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: go nuclear meaning
520	5196	5631	headstrong	/ˈhedstrɔːŋ/	adjective	Determined to do what one wants despite advice, objections, or consequences; stubbornly self-willed.	willful, stubborn, obstinate	He was talented but headstrong and rarely listened to advice.	Can suggest admirable determination or frustrating stubbornness, but usually leans critical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: headstrong definition
520	5197	6181	I dare you to do something	/aɪ ˌder ju tə ˌduː ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	challenge construction	A direct challenge urging someone to do something risky, difficult, embarrassing, or confrontational, often implying they may be afraid or unwilling.	I challenge you to, go on—do it, bet you won't (slang)	I dare you to watch that scene without laughing. / 'Say that again. I dare you.' can sound genuinely threatening depending on tone. / We dared each other to try the ridiculous dessert.	Dare can be playful or hostile. Patterns: dare someone to do X; I dare you; don't you dare do X (strong prohibition). 'How dare you!' expresses outrage, not a challenge.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: i dare you to do something meaning
520	5198	6271	pesky	/ˈpeski/	adjective, informal	Annoying, troublesome, persistent, or inconvenient, usually in a relatively light or playful way.	annoying, bothersome, troublesome, irritating, pesky little	I finally fixed that pesky formatting bug. / Mosquitoes are especially pesky around sunset. / One pesky little detail keeps ruining the whole plan.	Usually lighter/cuter than infuriating or obnoxious. Common with small recurring problems, insects, details, errors, chores, etc. Can sound playfully minimizing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: pesky meaning
520	5199	5742	rotoscoping	/ˈroʊtəˌskoʊpɪŋ/	noun / gerund	An animation or visual-effects technique in which artists trace over or isolate elements from live-action footage frame by frame.	frame-by-frame tracing	The film used rotoscoping to give the animated movement a realistic quality.	Raw search 'lotoscoping' normalized to rotoscoping. Modern VFX also uses rotoscoping to create masks/mattes around moving subjects.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111, voca_0112 | search: lotoscoping
520	5200	6779	I'm vibing with it	/aɪm ˈvaɪbɪŋ wɪð ɪt/	slang, informal	I'm enjoying it, connecting with it, feeling comfortable with it, or responding positively to its mood/style/energy.	I'm into it, I like the vibe, it's clicking with me, I'm feeling it	I wasn't sure about the new art style at first, but now I'm vibing with it. / This playlist is exactly what I needed—I'm vibing with it.	Modern casual slang. 'Vibe with' can mean like/get along with a person or resonate with an idea/style. Very register-sensitive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: i'm vibing with it meaning
521	5201	6544	if the offer still stands	/ɪf ði ˌɔːfər stɪl ˈstændz/	phrase	If an earlier invitation, proposal, promise, or opportunity is still available and has not been withdrawn or expired.	if the offer is still open, if that's still available	If the offer still stands, I'd love to come with you this weekend. / I know it's been a month, but if your offer still stands, I could use that help.	An offer 'stands' when it remains valid/in force. Natural polite way to return to an earlier invitation after some time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: if the offer still stands meaning
521	5202	5762	titular otaku	/ˌtɪtʃələr oʊˈtɑːkuː/	noun phrase	The otaku named or identified in a work's title; titular modifies the title-linked role, not necessarily 'main' by itself.	title-character otaku	The titular otaku is introduced before the rest of the cast.	Contextual application of titular. Keep because the original search likely tested what titular means when attached to a role/category such as otaku.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: titular otaku meaning
521	5203	6853	clemency	/ˈklemənsi/	noun, formal/legal	Mercy or leniency shown by a person or authority toward someone subject to punishment, especially reduction, commutation, pardon, or relief from a sentence.	mercy, leniency, pardon, commutation (specific forms)	The prisoner petitioned the governor for clemency. / The campaign asked for clemency rather than claiming the conviction never happened.	Umbrella concept; exact mechanisms differ by legal system. A pardon, commutation, or reprieve may be forms of executive clemency, but they are not interchangeable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: clemency definition
521	5204	6623	cry wolf	/ˌkraɪ ˈwʊlf/	idiom	To repeatedly raise a false or exaggerated alarm, causing people to stop believing you when a real danger/problem occurs.	raise false alarms, sound a false alarm	If you keep crying wolf, people won't believe you when something serious actually happens. / He cried wolf so many times that nobody reacted to the real emergency.	From Aesop's 'The Boy Who Cried Wolf.' The idiom focuses on loss of credibility through false alarms.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: If you keep crying wolf, people won’t believe you when something serious actually happens.
521	5205	6290	OC (original character / original content)	/ˌoʊ ˈsiː/	internet/fandom initialism	Depending on context, 'original character'—a character created by a fan/artist rather than belonging to an existing canon—or 'original content'—material created by the poster rather than reposted.	original character, original content	She drew her OC interacting with characters from the series. / The post was tagged OC to show that the photographer had taken the picture themself.	Very context-dependent. Fandom/art spaces strongly favor original character; Reddit/social platforms often use OC = original content. Other fields have many unrelated expansions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: oc meaning
521	5206	5590	dig in	/ˌdɪɡ ˈɪn/	phrasal verb	To begin eating eagerly; to prepare to resist or defend a position; or to settle in for sustained effort.	tuck in, entrench oneself, get stuck in	Dinner's ready—dig in. / The two sides dug in and refused to compromise.	Several senses share the image of getting deeply engaged or entrenched. Context determines whether it means eat, resist, or work with determination.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: dig in meaning
521	5207	5871	fill your boots	/ˌfɪl jər ˈbuːts/	idiom, chiefly British informal	Help yourself freely; take, eat, drink, use, or enjoy as much as you want or can.	help yourself, have at it, go for it, knock yourself out	There are plenty of leftovers in the fridge—fill your boots. / If you want to spend three hours reading every review before buying it, fill your boots.	Often permissive, jocular, or slightly dismissive: 'If that's what you want, go ahead.' Literal boot-filling is not normally intended.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: fill your boots
521	5208	6870	carcinoma	/ˌkɑːrsəˈnoʊmə/	noun, medicine	A malignant cancer arising from epithelial cells—the cells that line or cover body surfaces, organs, and glands.	epithelial cancer, malignant epithelial tumor	The pathology report identified the tumor as a carcinoma. / Many common cancers are carcinomas, but not every cancer is a carcinoma.	Medical lexical card only. Carcinoma is a category of cancer; contrast sarcoma (connective/supporting tissues), lymphoma, leukemia, etc. Diagnosis/subtype depends on pathology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: carcinoma
521	5209	5678	religiously	/rɪˈlɪdʒəsli/	adverb	In a religious manner; figuratively, with very regular, conscientious, or devoted consistency.	devotedly, faithfully, unfailingly	She checks the forecast religiously every morning.	Figurative religiously does not simply mean enthusiastically. It emphasizes faithful regularity or strict consistency, though enthusiasm may accompany it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: not religiously meaning not enthusiastically?
521	5210	6520	tormentor	/tɔːrˈmentər/	noun	A person who repeatedly causes someone pain, distress, fear, harassment, or suffering.	persecutor, bully, abuser, harasser, torturer (stronger)	Years later, he confronted one of his childhood tormentors. / The cat finally escaped its tiny tormentor—the neighbor's overexcited puppy.	From torment. Can be serious (abuse/persecution) or exaggerated/playful depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: tormentor
522	5211	6879	How far are you with ...?	/haʊ ˈfɑːr ɑːr ju wɪð/	conversational question	A question asking how much progress someone has made on a task, book, project, process, etc. Natural in many varieties, though 'How far along are you with...?' is often more idiomatic in AmE.	How far along are you?, how much have you done?, where are you with...?	How far are you with the report? / How far along are you with the new deck? / How far are you in the book?	Preposition depends on object: with a project/task; in/into a book, show, route; along with a process/project. Preserve search as natural but note AmE preference.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: how far are you with meaning
522	5212	6304	vitriol	/ˈvɪtriəl/	noun	Extremely bitter, cruel, angry, or hateful criticism or language directed at someone or something.	venom, bile, hostility, abuse, invective	The actor was shocked by the vitriol directed at her online. / You can criticize the policy without pouring vitriol on everyone who supports it.	Originally a chemical term associated with sulfuric acid/metal sulfates; modern figurative sense evokes corrosive language. Stronger than criticism or anger: vitriol suggests poisonous hostility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define vitriol
522	5213	5928	rap	/ræp/	noun / verb	A style of rhythmic spoken music; a sharp knock; informal criticism/blame or a criminal charge; as a verb, to perform rap music, knock, or criticize/talk sharply depending on context.	hip-hop vocal style; knock; blame, charge	She started rapping over the beat. / There was a rap at the door. / He took the rap for a mistake the whole team made.	Highly polysemous. Useful phrases: take the rap = accept blame/punishment; beat the rap = escape conviction (WID5849); bad rap = unfairly bad reputation. 'Rap sheet' = criminal record.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: rap meaning
522	5214	6382	barf me out / barf someone out	/ˌbɑːrf mi ˈaʊt/; /ˌbɑːrf ˈsʌmwʌn aʊt/	slang phrase; chiefly dated/Valley Girl-style	To disgust or gross someone out; 'barf me out' can function as an emphatic reaction meaning roughly 'that disgusts me / gross me out.' Separately, literal constructions with barf ... out can describe vomiting or ejecting something, depending on context.	gross someone out, disgust, revolt, nauseate; make someone want to puke	That totally barfs me out—it's disgusting. / Ugh, barf me out! / Literal/contextual: The creature swallowed him and then barfed him back out.	KEEP + FIX. 'Barf someone out' is attested slang associated especially with 1980s Valley Girl/Valspeak-style speech, meaning 'disgust/gross someone out.' Modern speakers are more likely to say 'gross me out'; literal barf ... out is also compositionally understandable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: barf me out meaning
522	5215	6700	razor-wire fence	/ˈreɪzər waɪr ˌfens/	noun	A fence topped with or made from razor wire—security wire fitted with sharp blades/barbs designed to deter crossing.	security fence, razor-wire barrier, concertina-wire fence (not always identical)	The compound was surrounded by a tall razor-wire fence.	Razor wire is a high-security barrier material. Concertina wire describes coiled wire form and may be barbed or razor-type; terms overlap in casual use but aren't exact synonyms.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: razor wire fence
522	5216	6008	touch grass	/ˌtʌtʃ ˈɡræs/	internet slang / imperative	A mocking or playful way to tell someone to leave the online world, reconnect with ordinary reality, and gain perspective.	go outside, get offline, get some perspective, log off	You've been arguing with strangers about fictional power levels for six hours—go touch grass. / I realized I was getting way too invested in the discourse and needed to touch grass.	Usually teasing or dismissive; can be genuinely hostile. Literal image = go outdoors and physically reconnect with the real world. Often aimed at someone perceived as terminally online.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: touch grass
522	5217	5904	lost to time vs lost in time	/ˌlɔːst tə ˈtaɪm/; /ˌlɔːst ɪn ˈtaɪm/	contrast card	Lost to time means forgotten, destroyed, or no longer recoverable because time passed. Lost in time means stranded/displaced in another time, or figuratively absorbed/disconnected from the present.	forgotten by history vs temporally stranded/absorbed	The original ending is lost to time; no complete copy survives. / The time traveler is lost in time with no way home. / Walking through the old village, I felt almost lost in time.	Preposition changes the image: TO time = time is what took/erased it; IN time = located or trapped within time. 'Lost in the mists of time' is another phrase for something historically obscure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: lost to time vs lost in time
522	5218	5612	vogue	/voʊɡ/	noun	The state of being fashionable, popular, or widely accepted at a particular time.	fashion, trend, popularity	Minimalist interiors came into vogue again.	Common patterns: be in vogue; come into vogue. Also the name of the fashion magazine, distinguished by context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define vogue
522	5219	6817	ditch	/dɪtʃ/	noun / verb, informal senses	Noun: a long narrow channel beside land/roads for drainage. Verb: abandon, discard, leave, skip, or get rid of someone/something; also make an emergency landing on water.	channel, drain; abandon, dump, skip, get rid of	The car slid into a roadside ditch. / We ditched the original plan and started over. / He ditched class to go to the beach.	Very productive informal verb: ditch a plan, date, class, friend. Aviation 'ditch' = intentionally land an aircraft on water in an emergency.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: define ditch
522	5220	5560	listless	/ˈlɪstləs/	adjective	Lacking energy, enthusiasm, or interest; sluggish and unmotivated.	lethargic, languid, apathetic	He felt listless all afternoon and couldn't focus on anything.	Related noun: listlessness. The 'list' here is an old word for desire or inclination, not a written list.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: listless meaning
523	5221	5961	indoctrination	/ɪnˌdɑːktrəˈneɪʃən/	noun	The process of teaching beliefs or ideology so that they are accepted uncritically, especially while discouraging questioning or alternative views.	brainwashing, ideological conditioning, inculcation, propaganda	The documentary examines allegations of political indoctrination in the school system. / Whether something counts as education or indoctrination often depends on how much room there is for evidence, disagreement, and questioning.	Noun of indoctrinate. Strongly evaluative: calling education 'indoctrination' alleges a method or power relationship, not merely that values are being taught.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: indoctrination meaning
523	5222	5652	ethnographic	/ˌeθnəˈɡræfɪk/	adjective	Relating to the systematic study and description of people, cultures, and social practices, especially through close observation and fieldwork.	anthropological, fieldwork-based	The researchers conducted ethnographic interviews in the community.	Related noun ethnography; common in anthropology, sociology, design research, and qualitative research.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define ethnographic
523	5223	6830	chance it / chancing	/ˈtʃæns ɪt/	verb, informal	To take a risk and proceed despite uncertainty about the outcome or possible trouble.	risk it, take a chance, gamble	The forecast looked bad, but we chanced it and went hiking anyway. / I wouldn't chance driving that far on an empty tank.	Usually 'chance it' or 'chance doing something.' Chancing = taking a risk. Different from 'by chance' = accidentally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: chancing meaning
523	5224	5534	not have the slightest/faintest idea	/nɑːt hæv ðə ˈslaɪtəst ˈaɪdiə/	idiom	To have absolutely no knowledge or understanding of something.	have no clue, have no idea, not have the foggiest, be completely in the dark	I didn't have the faintest idea where he had gone. / I haven't the slightest idea why that button stopped working. / She had no idea—literally not the faintest clue—what they were referring to.	Slightest and faintest intensify the negative: not even the smallest trace of an idea. Common patterns: 'I don't have the slightest/faintest idea,' 'I haven't the faintest,' and 'not the slightest clue.' 'Faintest' can sound slightly more idiomatic/BrE-flavored in the elliptical form.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: I didn't have the slightest/faintest idea
523	5225	5916	bombshell	/ˈbɑːmˌʃel/	noun	A shocking or highly surprising piece of news/revelation; also, dated/informal, a very attractive glamorous woman ('blonde bombshell').	shocker, revelation, bomb; knockout (person, dated)	The documentary drops a bombshell halfway through: the witness had known him for years. / Her resignation was a complete bombshell. / 'Blonde bombshell' is an older glamour stereotype.	Common: drop a bombshell, bombshell revelation/report. Figurative news sense is very common. Person sense can sound dated/objectifying.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: bombshell meaning
523	5226	6894	Transcendentalism	/ˌtrænsenˈdentəlɪzəm/	proper/common noun, philosophy/literature	A 19th-century American philosophical and literary movement associated especially with Ralph Waldo Emerson and Henry David Thoreau, emphasizing intuition, individual conscience/self-reliance, nature, and spiritual reality beyond purely empirical/material experience.	American Transcendentalism	Emerson and Thoreau are central figures in American Transcendentalism. / The movement treated nature as a source of spiritual and moral insight.	Existing cards cover Transcendentalist (person/adjective); this preserves the movement itself, which is distinct. Do not confuse broadly with Kant's technical use of 'transcendental.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: transcendentalism definition
523	5227	5518	high-octane	/ˌhaɪ ˈɑːkteɪn/	adjective	Very energetic, intense, exciting, or powerful.	high-energy, intense, turbocharged	It is a high-octane action movie from start to finish.	Literally refers to fuel with a high octane rating; figuratively suggests power, speed, and intensity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: high octane meaning
523	5228	6354	doth	/dʌθ/	archaic verb	Archaic third-person singular present form of do: 'does.' Used with he/she/it or a singular subject in Early Modern English and modern imitation of archaic/biblical speech.	does (modern English)	'The lady doth protest too much' uses doth where modern English would use does. / Fantasy dialogue sometimes throws in doth simply to sound old-fashioned.	Pronounced roughly 'duth.' Do not combine modern -s with it: 'he doth know,' not 'he doth knows.' Modern parody often overuses doth for mock-Shakespearean flavor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: doth meaning
523	5229	6621	confabulation	/kənˌfæbjəˈleɪʃən/	noun	In clinical neuropsychology, the production of false or distorted memories without conscious intent to deceive; more broadly/older, conversation or fabrication depending on context.	false-memory production; fabrication (broader), chat (older)	A patient may confidently report an event that never happened because of confabulation, not deliberate lying. / In AI discussion, the word is sometimes borrowed metaphorically for invented outputs.	Clinical confabulation is not simply lying: the person is not necessarily consciously deceiving. In AI, 'hallucination' is more established terminology, though both are metaphors.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: confabulation meaning
523	5230	6191	fold to someone / something	/ˈfoʊld tə/	verb phrase; slang/contextual	To give in, surrender, or abandon one's position under pressure from someone or something; in poker, to fold rather than continue against an opponent.	give in to, cave to, yield to, surrender to, back down against	He talked tough online but folded the moment his boss pushed back. / I wasn't going to fold to peer pressure. / In poker, she folded to his large raise.	General slang is an extension of poker/card-game fold = withdraw from the hand. 'Fold under pressure' is more established; 'fold to X' is common in gaming/internet speech but can sound informal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: fold to someone meaning
524	5231	5554	toe-to-toe / go toe-to-toe with someone	/ˌtoʊ tə ˈtoʊ/	idiom / adjective / adverb	Directly opposing, competing, or fighting someone in a determined, evenly matched way.	head-to-head, face-to-face	The smaller company is willing to go toe-to-toe with the industry leader.	Raw search 'toe by toe' is most likely a misremembering of toe-to-toe.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: toe by toe meaning
524	5232	5825	liquidity	/lɪˈkwɪdəti/	noun, finance / general	The ease with which an asset can be converted into cash without greatly affecting its price; also the availability of cash or readily tradable assets.	marketability, cash availability	The company kept extra cash to maintain liquidity during the downturn.	In markets, high liquidity also implies assets can be bought/sold readily with relatively low transaction impact.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: liquidity meaning
524	5233	6384	compile	/kəmˈpaɪl/	verb	To collect information, material, or items from multiple sources and organize them into a list, book, report, dataset, etc.; in computing, to translate source code into another form that can be executed or processed.	collect, assemble, gather, collate; build/translate code	I compiled the bookmark searches into one candidate list. / The editor compiled the essays into a single volume. / The program won't run because the code fails to compile.	Two major senses: assemble information; computing translation/build process. A compiler performs the latter. 'Compile a list/report/data' is very common outside programming.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define compile
524	5234	6682	contrastive stress	/kənˈtræstɪv ˈstres/	linguistics / prosody term	Extra stress or prominence placed on a word to contrast it with another possibility, correct an assumption, or highlight a particular distinction.	contrastive emphasis, emphatic stress, corrective stress	I said the BLUE one, not the green one. / 'SHE borrowed it' contrasts the borrower with someone else.	Raw 'contrasive' repaired. Often realized through a combination of pitch accent, duration, intensity, etc., not just loudness. Closely related to contrastive focus.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: contrasive stress
524	5235	5577	put oneself alongside someone/something	/pʊt wʌnˈself əˌlɔːŋˈsaɪd/	phrase	To place or present oneself as comparable to, aligned with, or on a similar level to someone or something.	compare oneself with, align oneself with	He was reluctant to put himself alongside writers he had admired since childhood.	Not a fixed idiom in every context; the metaphorical sense comes from placing two people or things side by side for comparison or alignment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: put oneself alongside someone else meaning
524	5236	6340	mystery breadcrumbs	/ˈmɪstəri ˌbredkrʌmz/	storytelling phrase	Small clues, hints, details, or unanswered questions deliberately scattered through a story so the audience can gradually follow a mystery toward later revelations.	clues, hints, planted details, trail of clues, narrative breadcrumbs	The first episode drops mystery breadcrumbs about the brother's past without explaining them. / Good mystery breadcrumbs feel meaningful in retrospect rather than like random trivia.	Creative extension of breadcrumb trail/Hansel-and-Gretel metaphor. In storytelling, breadcrumbs guide audience inference. Distinguish from web-navigation breadcrumbs and dating 'breadcrumbing.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: mystery breadcrumbs
524	5237	6851	pluck up	/ˌplʌk ˈʌp/	phrasal verb, chiefly in fixed phrase	To summon or gather an inner quality, especially courage; most common as 'pluck up the courage.'	muster, summon, gather	I finally plucked up the courage to ask. / She plucked up enough nerve to call him back.	Most natural modern use is 'pluck up the courage.' Bare 'pluck up' without an object is uncommon. BrE flavor; 'muster the courage' is broadly natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pluck up definition
524	5238	6774	query letter	/ˈkwɪri ˌletər/	noun, publishing	A concise pitch letter sent to a literary agent or publisher to interest them in a book/manuscript, typically including the premise, key story information, author details, and requested materials according to submission guidelines.	book pitch, submission query	She revised her query letter before contacting literary agents. / A strong query should hook the agent without summarizing every scene.	Publishing-specific. Requirements vary by agent/market; a query letter is not the manuscript itself and differs from a synopsis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: query letter meaning
524	5239	6880	So, that is 2 p.m. on a Tuesday.	/soʊ ðæt ɪz ˌtuː piːˈem ɑːn ə ˈtuːzdeɪ/	discourse example / time framing	A sentence that converts or restates prior information into a concrete time, often emphasizing how ordinary, inconvenient, or contextually significant that time is.	in other words, that works out to, so that's	The livestream starts at 19:00 UTC—so, that is 2 p.m. on a Tuesday for me.	Preserve exact search because likely source/context mattered. 'On a Tuesday' can mean a typical/unspecified Tuesday or emphasize weekday context; for a specific date, 'on Tuesday' is usually enough.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: so, that is 2 p.m. on a Tuesday meaning
524	5240	6318	vile booty	/ˌvaɪl ˈbuːti/	contextual / playful phrase	A deliberately comic or insulting phrase combining vile ('disgusting, morally repulsive, extremely unpleasant') with booty ('buttocks' in slang, or loot/treasure in another sense). Exact joke depends on source context.	disgusting butt, awful booty; nasty loot (contextual)	The line 'vile booty' sounds like exaggerated comic disgust if booty means someone's butt. / In a pirate joke, it could instead play on booty meaning stolen treasure.	Not a fixed idiom. Preserve the exact wording because the humor likely comes from the collision of dramatic 'vile' with slangy 'booty,' and possibly from booty’s double meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: vile booty
525	5241	6411	pregnant: common synonyms and euphemisms	/ˈpreɡnənt/	usage / synonym set	Pregnant is the neutral standard adjective. Alternatives vary strongly by register: expecting / expecting a baby (gentle/common), with child (old-fashioned/literary), carrying a baby (descriptive), knocked up (very informal, can be crude), up the duff (BrE slang), in the family way (old-fashioned euphemism).	expecting, expecting a baby, with child, carrying a baby, knocked up, up the duff, in the family way	She's pregnant. / They're expecting their first child in June. / 'Knocked up' may sound joking or disrespectful depending on speaker/context. / 'With child' sounds deliberately old-fashioned today.	No synonym is perfectly interchangeable because register matters. 'Pregnant' is usually the safest neutral word. Existing 'knocked up' card covers one informal expression; this card maps the broader register spectrum prompted by the synonym search.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: pregnant synonym
525	5242	5938	pillage	/ˈpɪlɪdʒ/	verb / noun	To violently rob a place, especially during war or conquest; figuratively, to exploit or strip something of valuable resources or ideas.	plunder, loot, ransack, raid, despoil	The invading army pillaged the town. / The studio was accused of pillaging its old catalog for recognizable ideas instead of developing new ones.	Pillage/plunder often imply large-scale violent looting. Loot can be verb/noun and is broader. Figurative pillage is vivid and critical: pillage resources, archives, intellectual property, the past.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: pillaging meaning
525	5243	6091	jerk	/dʒɜːrk/	noun / verb	As a noun, an annoying, inconsiderate, or unpleasant person. As a verb, to move or pull suddenly and sharply, or cause something to do so. Also has other technical/slang senses.	asshole (stronger/vulgar), idiot (not exact); yank, twitch, jolt	Don't be a jerk—just tell her the truth. / The bus jerked forward and everyone grabbed the rail. / He jerked the door open when he heard the noise.	Person-insult sense is common and moderately rude. Motion sense = sudden sharp movement. 'Jerk off' is separate vulgar sexual phrasal verb; physics 'jerk' = rate of change of acceleration. Context is crucial.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: jerk meaning
525	5244	5953	pompous	/ˈpɑːmpəs/	adjective	Self-important and excessively grand, formal, or dignified in a way that feels affected or ridiculous.	self-important, pretentious, grandiose, high-flown, stuffy	He gave a pompous speech about how nobody else understood real cinema. / The phrase is grammatically fine, but it sounds a little pompous for a casual text.	Pompous usually criticizes inflated self-importance. Pretentious emphasizes trying to seem more cultured/important than one is; grandiose emphasizes extravagant scale/style; stuffy emphasizes excessive formality/conservatism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114, voca_0115 | search: pompous
525	5245	6638	evaporative cooler / portable air cooler	/ɪˈvæpəˌreɪtɪv ˈkuːlər/	noun / translation	A device that cools air by evaporating water; Japanese 冷風機 may also refer more loosely to a portable air cooler, so the best English term depends on the mechanism/product.	swamp cooler (informal AmE), air cooler, portable air cooler	An evaporative cooler works best in relatively dry air. / If the Japanese product is simply marketed as 冷風機 without true evaporative cooling, 'portable air cooler' may be safer.	Do not automatically translate 冷風機 as air conditioner: many units do not use refrigeration/compressors and cool much less than AC. 'Swamp cooler' is common informal AmE for evaporative cooler.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: 冷風機 英語で
525	5246	6642	flop	/flɑːp/	noun / verb, informal	A complete or conspicuous failure, especially a movie, show, product, event, joke, or performance; as a verb, to fail badly. Also literally, to fall/drop loosely or heavily.	failure, bomb, dud, fiasco; fail, bomb	The movie was a box-office flop. / His first product launch flopped, but the second did well. / I flopped onto the couch after dinner.	Entertainment/business sense is very common. Contrast 'flip-flop' and 'flop' physical movement. Online, people may call media a flop based on sales, reception, or simply as an insult.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define flop
525	5247	6606	Crux (constellation)	/krʌks/	proper noun	A small but prominent constellation in the southern sky, commonly called the Southern Cross.	Southern Cross	Crux is one of the best-known constellations of the Southern Hemisphere. / Its stars have long been useful for orientation in the southern sky.	Distinct from ordinary crux = central/decisive point. Crux is Latin for cross. It appears on several national flags.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: what is crux constellation
525	5248	6120	breadcrumbing	/ˈbredˌkrʌmɪŋ/	noun / gerund, dating/internet slang	Giving someone intermittent small signs of romantic interest or attention—messages, likes, vague plans—enough to keep them engaged without committing to a real relationship or meaningful progress.	stringing someone along, leading someone on, giving mixed signals (broader), keeping someone on the hook	He sends a flirty message every few weeks but never agrees to meet; it feels like breadcrumbing. / Breadcrumbing can keep someone emotionally invested through tiny bursts of attention.	Metaphor of leaving a trail of breadcrumbs. Often discussed in dating, but can extend to employers/brands offering tiny hints/promises. Ghosting = abruptly disappearing; breadcrumbing = intermittent contact that sustains hope.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115, voca_0121 | search: breadcrumbing meaning
525	5249	6776	trench	/trentʃ/	noun / verb	A long, narrow ditch cut into the ground, especially for drainage, utilities, defense, or warfare; as a verb, to dig or form such a trench.	ditch, channel, excavation	Workers dug a trench for the new cable. / Soldiers sheltered in trenches during the fighting.	Basis of figurative 'in the trenches.' Also ocean trench = very deep elongated depression in the seafloor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: trench meaning
525	5250	6064	pedantry	/ˈpedəntri/	noun	Excessive concern with minor rules, details, technicalities, or displays of learning, especially when they distract from the real point.	nitpicking, hair-splitting, punctiliousness, excessive technicality	Correcting one typo is helpful; derailing the whole conversation over it can become pedantry. / The review is knowledgeable without slipping into academic pedantry.	Usually negative. Precision is not inherently pedantry: the criticism is that the detail is disproportionate, irrelevant, showy, or rigidly enforced. Pedantic = adjective; pedant = person.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: pedantry
526	5251	6724	two plus two equals four	/ˌtuː plʌs ˌtuː ˌiːkwəlz ˈfɔːr/	literal statement / rhetorical symbol	Literally a basic arithmetic truth; rhetorically, it can symbolize an obvious fact, objective reality, or something so self-evident that denying it seems absurd.	an obvious fact, basic truth, self-evident reality	You don't need an expert to prove that two plus two equals four. / In arguments about truth, '2 + 2 = 4' is often used as shorthand for an objective fact.	Meaning depends on discourse. Often contrasted with 'two plus two equals five' as truth versus imposed falsehood.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: two plus two equals four meaning
526	5252	6505	creeped out	/ˌkriːpt ˈaʊt/	adjective phrase, informal	Feeling uneasy, disturbed, disgusted, or frightened because someone or something seems creepy, uncanny, intrusive, or threatening.	spooked, unnerved, freaked out, disturbed, weirded out	That empty hallway really creeped me out. / I got creeped out when he knew details I'd never told him. / The doll is cute in daylight but kind of creepy at night.	Usually 'be/get creeped out' or 'X creeps me out.' More specifically uneasy/creepy than general freaked out, which can mean panic, anger, or excitement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: creeped out meaning
526	5253	6492	umbilical cord	/ʌmˈbɪlɪkəl ˌkɔːrd/	noun	The flexible cord connecting a developing fetus to the placenta, containing blood vessels that carry oxygen/nutrients toward the fetus and waste products away through placental circulation.	navel cord (informal/older), cord	The umbilical cord connects the fetus to the placenta. / After birth, the cord is clamped and cut, leaving the umbilical stump.	Usually contains two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein. Figuratively, 'cut the umbilical cord' can mean end excessive dependence, though 'cut the cord' is more common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: umbilical cord
526	5254	5753	wish fulfillment	/ˈwɪʃ fʊlˌfɪlmənt/	noun	The imagined or narrative satisfaction of a desire, especially one that is difficult or impossible to realize in ordinary life.	fantasy gratification, desire fulfillment	The story works partly as wish fulfillment for anyone who has dreamed of starting over.	Used in psychology and especially in criticism of fiction/media.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: wish fulfillment meaning
526	5255	5998	revolt	/rɪˈvoʊlt/	noun / verb	An organized rebellion against authority; as a verb, to rebel. Also, to disgust or repel someone strongly.	rebellion, uprising, insurrection; rebel; disgust, repulse	The soldiers revolted against the regime. / The tax increase sparked a revolt. / The cruelty of the scene revolted her.	Two major verb senses: revolt against authority vs X revolts someone = disgusts them. 'Revolting' commonly means disgusting in BrE/general English, not merely rebellious.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: revolt meaning
526	5256	6741	talk the talk and walk the walk	/ˌtɔːk ðə ˈtɔːk ən ˌwɔːk ðə ˈwɔːk/	idiom	To both say the right things/claim certain values and actually behave consistently with those claims. Often contrasted: it's easy to talk the talk; harder to walk the walk.	practice what you preach, back up words with actions	He talks the talk about work-life balance, but does he walk the walk with his employees? / She doesn't just talk the talk—she walks the walk.	Existing WID3501 covers walk the walk; this full paired construction preserves the rhetorical contrast the search asked for.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: walk the walk talk the talk meaning
526	5257	5535	have no idea	/hæv noʊ aɪˈdiːə/	expression	To not know something at all; also used emphatically to say someone cannot imagine the extent of something.	have no clue, not know	I had no idea the meeting had been moved.	Very common conversational expression. 'You have no idea how...' can be emphatic rather than literally about knowledge.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: I had no idea meaning
526	5258	6910	larynx	/ˈlærɪŋks/	noun, anatomy	The organ in the neck between the pharynx and trachea that contains the vocal folds and is involved in breathing, voice production, and protecting the airway.	voice box	The vocal folds are located in the larynx. / Inflammation of the larynx can make the voice hoarse.	Commonly called the voice box. Related: laryngeal /ləˈrɪndʒiəl/; laryngitis = inflammation of the larynx.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: larynx meaning
526	5259	6801	packaged nuggets	/ˌpækɪdʒd ˈnʌɡɪts/	metaphor / informal phrase	Small, neatly packaged pieces of information, advice, insight, or content designed to be easily consumed, remembered, or shared, sometimes at the cost of context or nuance.	bite-sized insights, digestible takeaways, snippets, sound bites	The course turns complex research into packaged nuggets of advice. / Social media rewards packaged nuggets, but the missing context can matter more than the takeaway.	Not a fixed idiom. Nugget already metaphorically means a small valuable piece of information; packaged emphasizes pre-shaped, consumable presentation and may sound mildly critical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: packaged nuggets metaphor meaning
526	5260	6263	goober	/ˈɡuːbər/	noun, informal	A silly, goofy, awkward, or foolish person, often used affectionately rather than harshly; regionally/historically, also a peanut.	goofball, dork, silly person, doofus; peanut (regional)	You absolute goober—you left your keys in the fridge. / The character is a lovable goober who somehow keeps saving the day. / 'Goober pea' is an old regional term for peanut.	Tone is highly context-dependent but often warm/playful. Less harsh than idiot when affectionate. Peanut sense is associated especially with Southern U.S. usage/history.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define goober
527	5261	5951	bludgeon	/ˈblʌdʒən/	verb / noun	To hit repeatedly with a heavy object; figuratively, to force, overwhelm, or batter someone into accepting something through pressure, repetition, or heavy-handed argument.	club, batter, beat; browbeat, hammer, force	He was bludgeoned with a heavy object. / The film doesn't trust the audience; it bludgeons you with the message in every scene. / They tried to bludgeon the committee into accepting the proposal.	Figurative bludgeon is vivid and strongly negative: bludgeon someone with statistics/a message; bludgeon someone into submission/agreement. Compare hammer home = emphasize repeatedly, which can be neutral/positive; browbeat = intimidate through bullying words.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define bludgeoned
527	5262	6842	pry into someone's life	/ˌpraɪ ˌɪntuː ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈlaɪf/	phrase	To intrude inquisitively into someone's private affairs, relationships, history, or personal information.	nose into, snoop into, meddle in, probe into	I don't want to pry into your personal life, so you don't have to answer. / The tabloids kept prying into her family life.	Pry into = investigate intrusively/curiously. Distinct from pry information out of someone = extract it with persistent effort.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pry into someone's life meaning
527	5263	5853	wellard / well hard	/ˌwel ˈhɑːrd/	British slang	Very tough, hard, intimidating, or impressive; well functions as an intensifier. 'Wellard' can represent a fused/nonstandard spelling or a name depending on context.	very tough, really hard	He was acting well hard in front of his mates.	The raw search 'wellard' is ambiguous: in British speech/writing, well hard can sound/fuse like wellard; Wellard is also known as a proper name. Preserve the likely slang target without erasing ambiguity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: wellard meaning
527	5264	6926	put one's finger on it	/ˌpʊt wʌnz ˈfɪŋɡər ɑːn ɪt/	idiom	To identify, recognize, or articulate exactly what something is—especially a subtle problem, feeling, difference, cause, or quality that is hard to describe.	pinpoint, identify, nail down, articulate	Something about his delivery feels off, but I can't quite put my finger on it. / You just put your finger on the real problem.	Often negative 'can't quite put my finger on it.' Related 'put your finger on X' = identify precisely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: put my finger on it meaning
527	5265	6678	prominence	/ˈprɑːmənəns/	noun	The state of being important, noticeable, salient, or standing out; in speech/prosody, the perceptual highlighting of a syllable, word, or phrase through stress, pitch, duration, etc.	importance, salience, conspicuousness; prosodic emphasis	The issue rose to national prominence. / In speech, contrastive focus can give a word extra prominence.	Broad general noun plus important linguistics/prosody sense given surrounding searches. Related prominent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: prominence
527	5266	6874	impartiality	/ɪmˌpɑːrʃiˈæləti/	noun	The quality of treating sides or people fairly without favoritism, bias, or improper preference.	neutrality, fairness, objectivity, even-handedness	Judges are expected to maintain impartiality. / Complete impartiality is difficult when you are personally involved.	Impartial does not necessarily mean having no values/opinions; it means not improperly favoring one side in the relevant judgment/process.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: Impartiality
527	5267	6035	point of entry	/ˌpɔɪnt əv ˈentri/	noun phrase	The place, route, stage, or conceptual starting point through which someone or something enters a system, location, subject, market, body, etc.	entry point, access point, gateway, starting point	Investigators found the burglar's point of entry at a rear window. / That short film is a good point of entry into the director's work. / The skin can be a point of entry for infection.	Very flexible literal/figurative phrase. 'Entry point' is often more idiomatic in computing/markets; 'point of entry' is common in security, medicine, travel, and formal prose.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: at the point of entry meaning
527	5268	6941	wallow	/ˈwɑːloʊ/	verb / noun	Literally, to roll or lie about in mud, water, etc., especially animals. Figuratively, to indulge deeply and often excessively in an emotion/state, especially self-pity, misery, nostalgia, or luxury.	roll about; indulge, luxuriate, brood	The pigs wallowed in the mud. / I gave myself one evening to wallow in self-pity, then moved on. / She wallowed in nostalgia while rewatching old shows.	Common patterns: wallow in self-pity/misery/guilt/luxury. Figurative use often implies excessive or self-indulgent immersion. Related personal phrase 'wallow on one's own' can emphasize doing it alone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define wallowing
527	5269	6535	fetchingly	/ˈfetʃɪŋli/	adverb, somewhat old-fashioned/literary	In an attractive, charming, appealing, or becoming way.	attractively, charmingly, appealingly, becomingly	She smiled fetchingly at him across the table. / The hat was tilted fetchingly to one side.	From fetching = attractive/charming. 'Smile fetchingly' has an old-fashioned romantic/narrative flavor and may sound deliberately quaint today.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: smiles so fetchingly meaning
527	5270	5543	verbal tic	/ˈvɜːrbəl tɪk/	noun phrase	A word, phrase, sound, or speech habit that a person repeats frequently, often automatically or without noticing.	speech habit, filler habit, repeated mannerism	Saying 'you know' every few seconds had become a verbal tic.	Can refer to habitual fillers/catchphrases; not necessarily a clinical tic disorder.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: verbal tic
528	5271	6815	gut it out	/ˌɡʌt ɪt ˈaʊt/	idiom, informal	To endure and push through pain, fatigue, difficulty, or adversity through determination rather than quitting.	tough it out, grind it out, push through, soldier on	My legs were dead for the last mile, but I gutted it out. / We were exhausted, but we gutted out the final hour of work.	Raw 'gutting out' points to gut it out / gut out something. Especially sports/endurance AmE. 'Grit it out' is a related expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120, voca_0121 | search: gutting out meaning
528	5272	6077	high water / high-water mark	/ˌhaɪ ˈwɔːtər/; /ˌhaɪ ˈwɔːtər mɑːrk/	noun / phrase	High water is the state/level of a tide or body of water near its maximum. A high-water mark is the highest level reached, figuratively the peak or greatest achievement.	high tide, peak level; apex, zenith, peak	The trail disappears at high water. / That album remains the high-water mark of the band's career.	Also appears in 'come hell or high water' = regardless of difficulties (existing related card). 'High-water mark' is common figuratively; 'high water' alone is usually literal unless context supplies an idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: high water meaning
528	5273	6324	scuttle	/ˈskʌtəl/	verb / noun	To move quickly with short hurried steps; to deliberately sink a ship by making holes/openings in it; figuratively, to deliberately ruin, abandon, or prevent a plan. As a noun, a hurried run or a container such as a coal scuttle.	scurry, scamper; sink; sabotage, scrap, derail	A crab scuttled under the rock. / The crew scuttled the ship to prevent its capture. / The deal was scuttled at the last minute by a funding dispute.	Three memorable senses: hurried movement, sink one's own ship, kill a plan. Do not confuse with scuttlebutt = rumor/gossip, though historically scuttlebutt has nautical roots.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: scuttle
528	5274	6443	latch on to someone	/ˌlætʃ ˈɑːn tə/	phrasal verb	To attach oneself closely to someone physically, socially, or emotionally; often to begin following, depending on, befriending, or clinging to them, sometimes more persistently than they want.	cling to, attach oneself to, stick with, glom onto (informal), become dependent on	The shy kid latched on to me during the first week of camp. / Once he found someone who shared his obsession, he latched onto him immediately.	Can be neutral/affectionate or mildly negative depending on unwanted dependency. Existing base latch card notes latch onto ideas; this search specifically motivates the person/social sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: latching on to you meaning
528	5275	5609	dissimilar	/dɪˈsɪmələr/	adjective	Not alike; noticeably different.	different, unlike	The two games look similar at first but are actually quite dissimilar.	Often followed by to or from depending on dialect/style: dissimilar to/from.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: dissimilar meaning
528	5276	6252	track meet	/ˈtræk miːt/	noun, chiefly AmE	An organized athletics competition consisting of running races and usually jumping/throwing events on or around a track.	athletics meet, track-and-field meet	Our school hosted a track meet on Saturday. / She ran the 800 meters and the relay at the same track meet.	AmE track meet; BrE often athletics meeting/meet. Track and field includes track races plus field events such as long jump, high jump, shot put, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: track meet meaning
528	5277	6291	booner	/ˈbuːnər/	noun; regional Australian slang / hunting slang	1. Australian slang, especially associated with Canberra: a disparaging or humorous label for a working-class, uncultivated, or 'bogan'-type person. 2. In North American hunting slang, a trophy animal large enough to qualify for, or evoke, Boone and Crockett record-book standards.	bogan, yobbo (Australian sense; approximate); trophy animal, record-book animal (hunting sense)	The term 'booner' has been used in Canberra slang for a stereotypically rough or uncultivated person. / The hunter called the enormous buck a booner.	KEEP + FIX. This is not merely an undefined internet coinage. Meanings are regional/domain-specific, so context matters. The Australian social label can be disparaging; the hunting sense derives from Boone and Crockett trophy/record-book standards.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: booner meaning
528	5278	5840	not have the foggiest (idea)	/nɑːt hæv ðə ˈfɑːɡiəst/	idiom, informal	To have absolutely no idea or knowledge about something.	have no clue, not have the faintest idea, not have the slightest idea, be clueless	I haven't the foggiest where he put the keys. / I don't have the foggiest idea what that acronym stands for. / 'How does this setting work?' 'Haven't the foggiest.'	Informal idiom, especially associated with BrE: 'I haven't the foggiest' can omit 'idea' because it is understood. Equivalent to 'not have the faintest/slightest idea.' In AmE, 'I don't have the foggiest' also occurs.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: i haven't the foggiest meaning
528	5279	5956	rudiment	/ˈruːdəmənt/	noun	A basic principle, element, or early undeveloped form of something; usually plural rudiments for the fundamental skills or facts of a subject.	basic, fundamental, element, foundation, first principle	She taught me the rudiments of music theory before we moved on to composition. / He understands the rudiments of editing but not the more technical workflow yet.	Common: the rudiments of X. Rudimentary (existing WID1925) = basic/undeveloped. Singular rudiment is less common in everyday speech and can also mean a vestigial/undeveloped structure in biology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: rudiment
528	5280	6510	bum something off someone	/ˌbʌm ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˈɔːf ˌsʌmwʌn/	informal verb phrase	To get or ask for something from someone for free, especially a small item such as a cigarette, ride, food, or money.	mooch, cadge (BrE), sponge, scrounge, borrow/get for free	Can I bum a cigarette off you? / He was always bumming rides off his friends. / I bummed a charger off someone at the café for ten minutes.	Informal, especially AmE. Pattern 'bum X off someone' or simply 'bum X.' It can imply casual freeloading but is often lighthearted for small favors/items.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: bum off you meaning
529	5281	5705	pigmentation	/ˌpɪɡmənˈteɪʃən/	noun	The natural coloring of skin, hair, eyes, tissue, or another material caused by pigments.	coloration, coloring	The condition can affect skin pigmentation.	General biological/descriptive term; pigment is the coloring substance, pigmentation the coloring or pattern/process.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: pigmentation meaning
529	5282	5595	congregate	/ˈkɑːŋɡrɪˌɡeɪt/	verb	To gather together in a group or crowd.	gather, assemble, cluster	Fans began to congregate outside the theater before the doors opened.	Often used of people gathering in a particular place.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: congregate meaning
529	5283	6214	Americaboo	/əˈmerɪkəˌbuː/	noun, internet slang	A playful or mocking coinage modeled on 'weeaboo,' referring to a non-American person seen as excessively idealizing, imitating, or obsessing over U.S. culture.	America-obsessed person, U.S.-culture fan (neutral paraphrase), reverse-weeb (joking)	His friends jokingly called him an Americaboo because he romanticized diners, pickup trucks, college football, and every American cliché. / The term is usually internet wordplay, not a stable identity label.	Productive -boo formation modeled on weeaboo; meanings and tone vary. Usually humorous/pejorative and often critiques idealization of a country through media stereotypes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: americaboo meaning
529	5284	6190	get / be brought up to speed	/ˌɡet ʌp tə ˈspiːd/	idiom	To become fully informed about the current situation, recent developments, procedures, or knowledge needed to participate effectively.	get caught up, get updated, get briefed, become current	Give me ten minutes to get up to speed on what happened yesterday. / Can you bring Maya up to speed before the meeting? / It took me a week to get up to speed with the new workflow.	Raw 'caught up to speed' blends 'caught up' and 'up to speed.' Standard: get/be up to speed; bring someone up to speed. 'Catch someone up' is also natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: caught up to speed
529	5285	6620	no wonder / it's no wonder that	/ˌnoʊ ˈwʌndər/	idiom	It is not surprising; the preceding facts make the result easy to understand.	unsurprisingly, naturally, it's hardly surprising	He slept three hours—no wonder he's exhausted. / It's no wonder that people were confused by the instructions.	Raw 'there's no wonder that' is generally less idiomatic for this meaning. Prefer 'it's no wonder that...' or simply 'no wonder...'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: there's no wonder that meaning
529	5286	5760	bite me	/ˈbaɪt mi/	expression, rude/slang	A defiant or dismissive retort meaning roughly 'leave me alone,' 'screw you,' or 'I don't care what you think.'	screw you, get lost, kiss my ass, go to hell	You don't like my haircut? Bite me. / 'You can't do that.' 'Bite me.' / Among close friends it can be mock-defiant, but to a stranger it will usually sound hostile.	Confrontational and rude, though sometimes playful among friends. It rejects criticism, authority, or unwanted advice rather than literally inviting biting. Tone is crucial; it is much stronger than 'whatever.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: bite me meaning
529	5287	6028	give the impression that	/ˌɡɪv ði ɪmˈpreʃən ðæt/	phrase	To cause someone to form a particular belief, perception, or feeling, intentionally or unintentionally.	make it seem that, create the impression that, suggest, convey the impression that	The trailer gives the impression that she's the main character, but she disappears after episode two. / I didn't mean to give the impression that I was angry; I was just tired.	Useful when separating appearance from fact. 'Give the impression of being X' is another pattern. Can be intentional or accidental; 'pretend' specifically implies intention.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: give the impression that meaning
529	5288	5781	let up vs chill out vs stop	/let ˈʌp; tʃɪl ˈaʊt; stɑːp/	contrast card	Let up means become less intense or stop pressing/harassing; chill out means relax or calm down; stop is the broad neutral verb for ceasing an action.	ease off; relax; cease	The rain finally let up. / Chill out—it's not a big deal. / Stop shouting.	Raw search said 'chill up'; normalized to the idiomatic chill out. Preserve as a contrast because the three are not interchangeable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: let up vs chill up vs stop
529	5289	5756	bad to the bone	/ˌbæd tə ðə ˈboʊn/	idiom, informal	Thoroughly tough, rebellious, dangerous, or impressively 'bad' all the way through; sometimes admiring rather than condemning.	hard-core, thoroughly bad, badass	The character walks in like he's bad to the bone.	Popularized strongly in modern culture by the George Thorogood song/title; bad may carry a swaggering, admiring sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: bad to the bone meaning
529	5290	6019	leave someone hanging	/ˌliːv ˌsʌmwʌn ˈhæŋɪŋ/	idiom, informal	To leave someone waiting, uncertain, unsupported, or without the response/completion they expect; also to fail to return an offered high-five.	keep someone waiting, leave someone in suspense, abandon someone, leave someone high and dry (stronger/contextual)	You can't say 'I have huge news' and then leave me hanging. / She never replied, so I was left hanging for three days. / Don't leave me hanging—high five!	Meaning depends on situation: unanswered message/question, unresolved information, lack of support, or literal social gesture. Leave high and dry usually implies more serious abandonment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: leave someone hanging meaning
530	5291	6636	pull a fast one on someone	/ˌpʊl ə ˈfæst wʌn ɑːn/	idiom, informal	To trick, deceive, or cheat someone, especially through a clever maneuver or by exploiting their inattention.	trick, fool, deceive, con, put one over on	Don't try to pull a fast one on me—I checked the numbers already. / They pulled a fast one by changing the terms at the last minute.	Usually implies crafty deception rather than a huge elaborate fraud. Common: 'Are you trying to pull a fast one on me?'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: pulling a fast one on me meaning
530	5292	5684	not off the table	/nɑːt ˌɔːf ðə ˈteɪbəl/	idiom	Still possible, available, or under consideration; not ruled out.	still an option, still possible	A later launch is not off the table if testing takes longer.	Off the table = removed from consideration; not off reverses that.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: it's not off the table meaning
530	5293	5911	corporal punishment	/ˌkɔːrpərəl ˈpʌnɪʃmənt/	noun	Punishment that deliberately inflicts physical pain on a person's body, traditionally including practices such as spanking or caning.	physical punishment, physical discipline	Corporal punishment is banned in schools in many places. / The debate was not about ordinary discipline but specifically about whether physical punishment should ever be permitted.	Corporal here means bodily/physical, not the military rank corporal. Distinguish capital punishment = execution/death penalty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: corporal punishment
530	5294	6531	have one's fill of someone/something	/ˌhæv wʌnz ˈfɪl əv/	idiom	To have as much of something as one wants or can tolerate; depending on context, to be satisfied by abundance or to be thoroughly tired/fed up with it.	get enough of, have enough of, be satiated with; be fed up with	At the buffet, you can have your fill of both dishes. / I've had my fill of pointless online arguments for one day. / 'I can have my fill of both of you' could be positive, sensual, playful, or hostile depending on source context.	Literal origin is eating/drinking until satisfied, then figurative abundance. Tone is highly context-dependent with people; preserve ambiguity rather than forcing one reading.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: I can have my fill of both of you meaning
530	5295	6373	pushback	/ˈpʊʃˌbæk/	noun	Resistance, opposition, criticism, or negative reaction against a proposal, change, claim, policy, request, or pressure.	resistance, opposition, backlash, objections, resistance to change	The new policy faced immediate pushback from employees. / I expected some pushback when I suggested changing the workflow. / The claim received pushback from other researchers.	Pushback is often direct resistance to a specific move; backlash can suggest a broader/stronger public reaction after something happens. Very common in work, politics, media, and discussion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define pushback
530	5296	6898	servile obedience	/ˌsɜːrvəl oʊˈbiːdiəns/	noun phrase	Excessively submissive, deferential, or slave-like obedience to authority, implying loss of independence, dignity, or critical judgment.	slavish obedience, abject submission, unquestioning obedience, subservience	The essay criticizes servile obedience to authority rather than ordinary cooperation. / He expected servile obedience from everyone beneath him.	Servile is strongly negative and historically relates to servitude/slavery. Stronger than obedient; suggests degrading subordination or fawning compliance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: servile obedience
530	5297	6312	sweeping harmful generalization	/ˌswiːpɪŋ ˌhɑːrmfəl ˌdʒenərəlaɪˈzeɪʃən/	critical phrase / concept	A broad claim about a whole group that ignores important differences and can reinforce prejudice, stigma, or harmful assumptions.	overgeneralization, blanket statement, harmful stereotype, broad-brush claim	Saying that an entire generation is lazy is a sweeping harmful generalization, not a useful description of millions of individuals. / The criticism identifies both problems: the claim is sweeping, and the stereotype can cause harm.	Not a fixed dictionary idiom, but the searched phrase captures a useful distinction: 'sweeping' describes overbreadth; 'harmful' describes consequences/content. A broad generalization is not automatically false, but needs evidence and qualification.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sweeping harmful generalization meaing
530	5298	5624	tidbit	/ˈtɪdˌbɪt/	noun	A small, interesting piece of information or a small tasty piece of food.	morsel, snippet, nugget	She shared a few interesting tidbits about how the film was made.	Also spelled titbit in British English.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: tidbits meaning
530	5299	5887	sit in the corner and think about what you did	/ˌsɪt ɪn ðə ˈkɔːrnər ən ˌθɪŋk əˌbaʊt wʌt ju ˈdɪd/	mock-scolding expression	A humorous or exaggerated command treating someone like a misbehaving child who should go away, reflect on their behavior, and feel ashamed.	go to the corner, think about what you've done, go sit in time-out	That pun was terrible. Go sit in the corner and think about what you did. / After recommending that awful movie to me, you can sit in the corner and think about what you've done.	Evokes old-fashioned child discipline/time-out. Often playful online or among friends, not a fixed formal idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: sit in the corner and think about what you did meaning
530	5300	5556	as in vs as to vs as for	/æz ˈɪn/; /æz ˈtuː/; /æz ˈfɔːr/	contrast set	As in introduces an example or intended sense; as to means regarding/about or introduces an indirect question; as for shifts the topic to a particular person or thing.		'Cold' as in unfriendly. / There is doubt as to whether it will work. / As for the price, we can discuss it later.	Preserve as a contrast card because the original search explicitly compared three similar-looking structures.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: as in vs as to vs as for
531	5301	6119	plagued by / plagued with	/ˈpleɪɡd baɪ/; /ˈpleɪɡd wɪð/	adjective / passive verb phrase	Repeatedly or persistently troubled, harmed, or beset by something unpleasant, as though by a plague.	beset by, afflicted by, dogged by, troubled by, riddled with	The production was plagued by delays and budget problems. / He was plagued with doubts before the interview. / The game launched with servers plagued by crashes.	Stronger sense of persistent/recurrent trouble than simple 'affected by.' Plagued by is broadly common; plagued with often emphasizes being full of/repeatedly experiencing a problem. Existing examples use plagued but no standalone productive card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: plagued
531	5302	6459	trunk	/trʌŋk/	noun	A large box/chest; the enclosed luggage compartment of a car (AmE; BrE boot); the main stem of a tree; an elephant's long flexible nose; the main body/torso; also various main channels/lines.	car boot (BrE), chest, tree stem, elephant proboscis, torso	Put the suitcase in the trunk. / The elephant lifted its trunk. / The tree trunk was too wide to wrap my arms around. / The nerves branch from the main trunk.	Highly polysemous. For cars: AmE trunk vs BrE boot. 'Swimming trunks' = men's swim shorts, usually plural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: trunk
531	5303	6148	bolt	/boʊlt/	noun / verb	A metal fastener; a sliding door lock; a flash of lightning; a projectile for a crossbow. As a verb, to fasten, suddenly run away, move abruptly, or swallow food very quickly.	fastener, latch; dash, flee, make a run for it; gulp	The horse bolted when it heard the explosion. / He bolted the door before going upstairs. / A bolt of lightning struck nearby. / Don't bolt your food—you'll make yourself sick.	Highly polysemous. 'Bolt from the blue' is a separate idiom already in the Master. Motion sense implies sudden rapid escape. Food sense = swallow/eat too quickly.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: bolt meanig
531	5304	6881	lost time	/ˌlɔːst ˈtaɪm/	noun phrase	Time that was unavailable, wasted, missed, or taken away from normal activity/work; also time one later tries to recover or compensate for.	wasted time, missed time, downtime (contextual), time lost	We worked late to make up for lost time. / The injury resulted in several weeks of lost time at work.	Common fixed collocation in 'make up for lost time.' In employment/safety contexts, lost time can have technical meanings such as absence caused by injury.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: lost time meaning
531	5305	6273	kryptonite	/ˈkrɪptəˌnaɪt/	noun / figurative pop-culture reference	In Superman stories, a fictional substance that weakens Superman; figuratively, a person's particular weakness, temptation, vulnerability, or thing they find unusually hard to resist or handle.	Achilles' heel, weakness, downfall, weak spot	Chocolate is my kryptonite—I can ignore most desserts, but not that. / Public speaking is basically his kryptonite even though he's confident one-on-one.	The figurative use is extremely common and often playful. Unlike Achilles' heel, kryptonite can be a temptation ('cheesecake is my kryptonite') as well as a vulnerability.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: kryptonite
531	5306	6093	waddle	/ˈwɑːdəl/	verb / noun	To walk with short steps while swaying from side to side, like a duck or penguin; can be humorous or insulting when used of a person.	toddle, shuffle (not exact), sway along	The penguins waddled toward the water. / After putting on three layers of winter clothes, I could barely do anything except waddle.	Often cute/humorous for animals/children, but describing an adult's body or gait as waddling can sound mocking. Motion combines short steps + side-to-side sway.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: waddle meaning
531	5307	5915	anorexic	/ˌænəˈreksɪk/	adjective / noun	Relating to or affected by anorexia, especially anorexia nervosa; historically also meaning causing or characterized by loss of appetite.	having anorexia, affected by anorexia nervosa	The article describes the difficulties faced by anorexic patients during recovery. / Calling a merely thin person 'anorexic' is inaccurate and can be hurtful.	In ordinary speech, anorexic is sometimes misused as a casual synonym for extremely thin; medically, anorexia nervosa is an eating disorder and cannot be inferred from appearance alone. 'Person with anorexia' is often preferable when discussing an individual.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define anorexic
531	5308	5839	take a stab at something	/ˌteɪk ə ˈstæb æt/	idiom, informal	To make an attempt at doing, solving, answering, or guessing something, especially without certainty of success.	have a go at, try, take a shot at	I'll take a stab at explaining why the code fails.	Can also be 'take a stab in the dark' for a guess made with little information.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: take a stab meaning
531	5309	6546	Jeremy Bearimy	/ˌdʒerəmi bəˈraɪmi/	proper noun / fictional concept	In The Good Place, the deliberately absurd name for the afterlife's nonlinear conception of time: its timeline loops and curves in a shape resembling the written words 'Jeremy Bearimy.'	nonlinear afterlife time, Jeremy Bearimy timeline	Michael explains that time in the afterlife doesn't move in a straight line; it moves in a 'Jeremy Bearimy.' / The joke lets the show hand-wave impossible chronology while making the explanation itself part of the comedy.	Search provenance: 'good place michael's explanation jeremy.' This is a show-specific cultural reference rather than ordinary English vocabulary. The dot over the i is itself part of the joke and represents especially inexplicable temporal states.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: good place michael's explanation jeremy
531	5310	6376	preen / preening	/priːn/	verb / noun-gerund	Of a bird, to clean and arrange its feathers with its beak; of a person, to groom oneself carefully or behave with visible self-satisfaction/pride.	groom, primp; congratulate oneself, strut, bask	The parrot spent several minutes preening its feathers. / He preened in front of the mirror before the interview. / The politician was preening over the favorable headlines.	Human figurative sense often carries mild criticism: visibly pleased with oneself. 'Preen oneself on X' = take self-satisfied pride in X. Bird sense is literal grooming.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define preening
532	5311	5625	tyke	/taɪk/	noun, informal	A small child, especially an energetic or mischievous one; historically, it has also had other regional or insulting senses.	kid, youngster	The little tyke had already figured out how to open the gate.	Common affectionate/informal child sense. Historical senses include dog and a rude/uncouth person; Yorkshire 'Tyke' can also be a regional nickname.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define tyke
532	5312	6940	trot	/trɑːt/	noun / verb	A two-beat gait faster than a walk and slower than a canter/gallop, especially of horses; by extension, to move at a brisk, short-stepped pace.	jog, brisk gait	The horse broke into a trot. / The dog trotted over to greet us. / She trotted down the stairs to answer the door.	Also figurative phrasal 'trot something out' = repeatedly present an argument/excuse; existing card covers that separately.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: trot meaning
532	5313	6720	at the helm	/ət ðə ˈhelm/	idiom	In charge of or directing an organization, project, government, team, or other undertaking.	in charge, in control, leading, at the controls, running the show	The company changed dramatically with a new CEO at the helm. / Who was at the helm when the project began?	Helm is literally the steering position/control of a ship. Figurative leadership metaphor. Related recent card 'in the driver's seat' emphasizes control; at the helm often emphasizes leadership/management.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: at the helm meaning
532	5314	6536	hammer something in / hammer home	/ˌhæmər ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˈɪn/	phrasal construction	Literally, to drive something in by striking it; figuratively, to make an idea, lesson, rule, or fact stick through forceful emphasis or repetition. 'Hammer home' is especially idiomatic for emphasizing a point.	drill in, drive home, pound in, impress upon, reinforce	The teacher hammered the rule into us until nobody forgot it. / The final scene hammers home the cost of his decision. / He hammered the nail in with three blows.	For ideas, 'hammer home a point/message' and 'hammer something into someone' are very natural. 'Hammer in' alone is more literal/compositional.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: hammer in
532	5315	6217	PvP (player versus player)	/ˌpiː viː ˈpiː/	gaming initialism / noun-adjective	Gameplay in which human players compete directly against other human players, as opposed to fighting only computer-controlled enemies.	player-versus-player, competitive multiplayer; PvE (contrast)	I mostly play PvE, but my friend lives for PvP. / The patch completely changed the PvP meta. / Some games let you toggle PvP on or off.	Contrast PvE = player versus environment. PvP can describe a mode, combat, server, build, meta, etc. Exact rules vary by game.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0119 | search: pvp meaning
532	5316	6319	riffraff	/ˈrɪfˌræf/	noun, collective; informal/disparaging	People regarded as disreputable, undesirable, low-class, or socially inferior.	rabble, undesirables, lowlifes, scum (stronger), hoi polloi (different nuance)	The snobbish character complained that the neighborhood was filling up with riffraff. / The bouncer was told to keep the 'riffraff' out—a deliberately contemptuous way to describe people.	Disparaging and often reveals the speaker's class prejudice/snobbery. Usually collective and uncountable: 'the riffraff,' not normally 'a riffraff.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: riffraff
532	5317	5879	vicar	/ˈvɪkər/	noun, Christianity / chiefly British	An Anglican parish priest; more broadly in some usage, a clergy member serving a parish or acting as a representative/deputy in ecclesiastical contexts.	parish priest, clergyman, minister (not exact)	The village vicar conducted the wedding. / In an old British comedy, the local vicar is often a recognizable social character.	Do not treat priest, vicar, rector, pastor, and minister as exact synonyms; titles depend on denomination and church structure. Vicar is strongly associated with the Church of England/Anglican context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113, voca_0114 | search: vicar
532	5318	6051	cockily / in a cocky way	/ˈkɑːkəli/	adverb / phrase	In an overly self-confident, smug, or brash manner, especially when confidence seems unjustified or irritating.	smugly, arrogantly, brashly, overconfidently, swaggeringly	He cockily predicted a sweep before the first game had even started. / She leaned back and said, a little too cockily, 'I've got this.'	Cocky is informal and usually negative, though playful admiration is possible. Confident is neutral/positive; cocky adds excessive self-assurance and often showiness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: in a cocky way meaning
532	5319	5851	Coventry	/ˈkʌvəntri/	proper noun	A city in the West Midlands of England; also appears in the idiom 'send someone to Coventry,' meaning deliberately ignore or socially ostracize someone.		The factory was based in Coventry. / After the argument, they practically sent him to Coventry.	Preserve both place recognition and the culturally specific idiom likely to arise in British contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: Coventry
532	5320	5537	prolonged	/prəˈlɔːŋd/	adjective	Continuing for a long time or longer than usual or expected.	extended, lengthy, protracted, drawn-out, long-lasting	The region suffered a prolonged period of drought. / Prolonged eye contact can start to feel awkward. / After a prolonged silence, he finally answered.	Often suggests that the duration is notable, burdensome, or longer than desirable. Common collocations: prolonged period, exposure, illness, absence, silence, conflict, use. 'Protracted' is more formal and often stresses troublesome length; 'extended' is more neutral.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: prolonged
533	5321	6826	deify / deified	/ˈdiːəˌfaɪ/; /ˈdiːəˌfaɪd/	verb	To treat or worship someone/something as a god; figuratively, to idolize or elevate a person to an unrealistically exalted status.	worship, idolize, glorify, exalt	Some rulers were deified after death. / Fans sometimes deify creators and then react harshly when they turn out to be ordinary people.	Strong word. Figurative deify goes beyond admire: it suggests near-worship or placing someone above normal criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: deified meaning
533	5322	5722	warranted	/ˈwɔːrəntɪd/	adjective	Justified, reasonable, or supported by the circumstances or evidence.	justified, called for, defensible	Given the evidence, further investigation is warranted.	Opposite of existing unwarranted. Common formal pattern: concern/action is warranted.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define warranted
533	5323	5873	infamy	/ˈɪnfəmi/	noun	The state of being famous for something extremely bad, shameful, or notorious; an infamous reputation.	notoriety, disgrace, disrepute, bad fame	The attack became a date that would live in infamy. / The disastrous finale achieved a kind of internet infamy.	Infamy is stronger and darker than ordinary notoriety. Famous = widely known; notorious/infamous = widely known for something bad.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: define infamy
533	5324	6227	soyboy / soy boy	/ˈsɔɪˌbɔɪ/	noun, internet insult	A derogatory internet label for a man portrayed as weak, unmasculine, overly sensitive, submissive, or politically/culturally progressive, based on a pseudoscientific stereotype linking soy consumption with feminization.	weak man, unmanly man (insult), beta male (related insult)	The comment section dismissed anyone who disagreed as a 'soyboy' instead of answering the argument. / The insult relies on a culture-war stereotype, not a serious nutritional diagnosis.	Pejorative manosphere/culture-war slang. The soy→feminization premise grossly misrepresents the effects of dietary phytoestrogens. Useful for understanding rhetoric, not as a neutral description.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: soyboy
533	5325	5924	run that by me again	/ˌrʌn ðæt baɪ mi əˈɡen/	expression, informal	Ask someone to repeat or explain what they just said, often because it was unclear, surprising, complicated, or hard to believe.	say that again, go over that again, repeat that, walk me through that again	Sorry, run that by me again—are you saying the meeting moved to tomorrow? / Run that last part by me again; I think I missed the distinction.	Productive construction: run something by someone = tell/show them an idea or plan to get their reaction: 'Can I run something by you?' With again, it often means repeat/explain.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: run that by me again meaning
533	5326	6132	Cretaceous	/krɪˈteɪʃəs/	proper adjective / noun	The geologic period from about 145 to 66 million years ago, the final period of the Mesozoic Era, ending with the mass extinction that eliminated non-avian dinosaurs.	Cretaceous Period, late Mesozoic (broader context)	Tyrannosaurus and Triceratops lived near the end of the Cretaceous. / Flowering plants diversified dramatically during the Cretaceous.	Comes after Jurassic and before Paleogene. 'Late Cretaceous' is especially common in dinosaur contexts. Existing cards mention Cretaceous incidentally; standalone period is useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: cretaceous meaning
533	5327	6435	letdown	/ˈletˌdaʊn/	noun	A disappointment: someone or something that fails to meet expectations, hopes, or promises; the feeling/result of being disappointed.	disappointment, anticlimax, disappointment after hype, bust	After all that hype, the finale was a bit of a letdown. / The hotel looked amazing online, so the tiny room was a real letdown.	Usually countable: a letdown. 'Anticlimax' specifically emphasizes a weak/disappointing culmination after buildup. 'Let someone down' is the related verb phrase.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: letdown meaning
533	5328	6210	bromance	/ˈbroʊˌmæns/	noun, informal	A very close, affectionate, emotionally significant friendship between men, framed playfully with the language of romance but usually understood as nonsexual/nonromantic.	close male friendship, male bonding, buddy relationship	Their off-screen bromance became almost as popular with fans as the show itself. / The movie is basically a road-trip bromance disguised as an action comedy.	Blend of bro + romance. Can normalize male affection, but sometimes carries an assumption that intimacy between men needs a joking heterosexual frame. Context/tone varies.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: bromance meaning
533	5329	6056	exert	/ɪɡˈzɜːrt/	verb	To apply or bring to bear force, effort, influence, authority, pressure, or another power; reflexively, to make a strong effort.	apply, exercise, use, bring to bear; strain oneself, make an effort	You don't need to exert much force to close the latch. / The studio exerted enormous pressure on the director to shorten the film. / He rarely exerts himself unless the deadline is immediate.	Common collocations: exert force/pressure/influence/control/authority/effort; exert oneself. Exercise influence/authority is close; exert often emphasizes active force or pressure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: exert meaning
533	5330	6836	fishmonger	/ˈfɪʃˌmʌŋɡər/	noun	A person or business that sells fish and seafood, especially at a shop or market.	fish seller, seafood seller, fish dealer	The fishmonger recommended a whole sea bass for dinner. / We bought fresh scallops from the local fishmonger.	Traditional occupational compound. -monger historically means dealer/trader; in modern figurative compounds such as fearmonger it can be disparaging.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: fishmonger meaning
534	5331	5668	ascension	/əˈsenʃən/	noun	The act or process of rising to a higher position, level, rank, or spiritual realm.	rise, ascent, elevation	Her rapid ascension to the top job surprised the industry.	Often more elevated/formal than rise. Capitalized Ascension has specific Christian religious meanings.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define ascension
534	5332	5802	distraught	/dɪˈstrɔːt/	adjective	Extremely worried, upset, distressed, or emotionally agitated.	distressed, devastated, frantic, beside oneself, deeply upset	She was distraught after realizing her dog was missing. / He sounded distraught on the phone and could barely explain what happened. / The parents were understandably distraught while they waited for news.	Strong emotional word: more intense than 'upset' and often suggests someone is overwhelmed by worry, grief, fear, or shock. Common collocations: distraught mother/parent/family, visibly distraught, become distraught.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define distraught
534	5333	5547	whirlwind	/ˈwɜːrlwɪnd/	noun / adjective	A rapidly rotating column of air; figuratively, a fast, intense burst of activity, events, or emotion.	tornado; rush, flurry	The week after the announcement was a whirlwind of interviews and meetings.	Common figurative patterns: a whirlwind of activity/emotions; a whirlwind romance; a whirlwind tour.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: whirlwind meaning
534	5334	6012	tut / tut-tut	/tʌt/	interjection / verb / noun	A clicking sound made with the tongue to express disapproval, annoyance, impatience, or mild reproach; to make that sound.	tsk, tsk-tsk, click one's tongue	She tutted when she saw the muddy footprints on the floor. / 'Tut, tut,' he said, pretending to be scandalized.	Especially associated with British English; U.S. speakers more often write 'tsk.' Can be genuinely disapproving or theatrically/playfully judgmental.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tut meaning
534	5335	6100	flick something away	/ˌflɪk ˌsʌmθɪŋ əˈweɪ/	phrasal pattern	To remove or send something away with a quick, light, sharp movement, usually of a finger or hand.	brush away, swat away, flip away, knock away	She flicked the bug away from her sleeve. / He flicked away the notification without reading it. / The character casually flicks the blade away as if it weighs nothing.	Can be literal or figurative/digital. Flick away emphasizes a small quick dismissive motion; brush away is often gentler; swat away suggests a broader/stronger strike.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: flick away  meaning
534	5336	6391	introvert	/ˈɪntrəˌvɜːrt/	noun / adjective	A person who tends to prefer lower-stimulation environments, solitude, or smaller-scale social interaction and who may restore energy through time alone; not simply a shy, antisocial, or socially unskilled person.	introverted person, inward-oriented person (rough); opposite: extrovert	I'm an introvert, but I still enjoy long conversations with people I know well. / Being introverted doesn't automatically mean being shy—you can be socially confident and still need a lot of alone time afterward.	Everyday personality label, not a diagnosis. Introversion concerns temperament/energy/stimulation preferences more than fear of social judgment. Ambivert is commonly used for people showing a mix of introverted/extroverted tendencies.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: introvert
534	5337	5531	rub off on someone	/ˌrʌb ˈɔːf ɑːn/	phrasal verb	For a quality, habit, attitude, or influence to be transferred from one person to another through close contact or association.	influence, spread to, transfer to	Her optimism started to rub off on the rest of the team.	Raw search was 'rub it off on someone'; the idiomatic form for transferred influence is rub off on someone. Do not confuse with literally rubbing something off a surface.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: rub it off on someone
534	5338	6515	follow in someone's footsteps	/ˌfɑːloʊ ɪn ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈfʊtsteps/	idiom	To pursue a similar career, role, lifestyle, achievement, or course of action as someone who came before you.	follow someone's example, emulate, take the same path	She followed in her mother's footsteps and became a doctor. / The sequel tries to follow in the original's footsteps without simply copying it.	Usually about a life/career path or precedent, not literal walking. Can be admiring or neutral. 'Follow someone's lead' is more immediate/situational.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: follow in someone's footsteps
534	5339	5820	when pigs fly	/wen ˌpɪɡz ˈflaɪ/	idiom, humorous	Used to say that something will never happen or is extremely unlikely.	never, not in a million years, when hell freezes over, fat chance (related)	He'll apologize when pigs fly. / 'Do you think he'll voluntarily wake up at 6 a.m.?' 'Yeah, when pigs fly.' / She joked that she'd give up coffee when pigs fly.	Built on an obviously impossible image and normally means 'never' or 'extremely unlikely.' Often sarcastic/playful rather than a neutral probability statement. 'When hell freezes over' is stronger/rougher; 'fat chance' expresses skepticism rather than a time clause.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: when pigs fly meaning
534	5340	5740	cranky	/ˈkræŋki/	adjective, informal	Irritable, bad-tempered, or easily annoyed; also sometimes not working smoothly, especially of a machine or system.	grumpy, irritable, grouchy	I'm always cranky when I haven't slept enough.	Common conversational American English. Machine/system sense is less central but useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: cranky
535	5341	6923	have prestige attached to it	/hæv preˈstiːʒ əˌtætʃt tuː ɪt/	phrase	To carry social status, esteem, cachet, or perceived distinction because people associate it with an institution, role, label, product, tradition, etc.	carry prestige, have status, confer cachet, be prestigious	The degree had a lot of prestige attached to it in that industry. / Certain job titles have prestige attached to them even when the work is similar.	Natural construction: X has prestige/status/stigma/value attached to it. 'Attached' emphasizes socially associated rather than inherent quality.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: had prestige attached to it
535	5342	5836	make an example of someone	/ˌmeɪk ən ɪɡˈzæmpəl əv/	idiom	To punish someone conspicuously or severely so that others will be deterred from doing the same thing.	punish as a warning, set an example through punishment	The school made an example of the students who organized the prank.	Usually negative/coercive; distinct from 'set a good example.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: make an example of someone
535	5343	6112	vetting	/ˈvetɪŋ/	noun / gerund	The process of carefully checking a person's background, qualifications, reliability, security risk, claims, or suitability before approval, hiring, publication, selection, etc.	screening, background checking, due diligence, verification, review	Every candidate goes through a fairly extensive vetting process. / The article was published without enough vetting of its sources. / I do a little vetting before recommending a channel I barely know.	Vet = examine/check carefully. Background check is one kind of vetting; due diligence is broader and common in business/legal contexts. 'Vetting' often implies filtering before trust/access/selection is granted.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: vetting
535	5344	6321	iteratively	/ˈɪtəˌreɪtɪvli/	adverb	By repeating a process in successive cycles, using each result or round to refine, improve, or advance the next one.	repeatedly in cycles, incrementally, through iteration	We improved the flashcard pipeline iteratively: test a batch, inspect the problems, then adjust the next batch. / The model is refined iteratively rather than designed perfectly in one pass.	Important distinction: iterative = repeated cycles; incremental = built in small additions. A process can be both, but they are not identical. Common in software, design, optimization, and research.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Iteratively
535	5345	5700	familiarity	/fəˌmɪliˈerəti/	noun	Knowledge or recognition gained from experience or repeated exposure; also an easy, informal manner arising from closeness.	acquaintance, knowledge, intimacy	Her familiarity with the software made the task much faster.	Common patterns: familiarity with; breed familiarity; undue familiarity. Related adjective familiar.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: familiarity
535	5346	5610	be flattering on someone	/bi ˈflætərɪŋ ɑːn/	adjective pattern	To make someone's appearance look especially attractive or favorable, especially when said of clothing, colors, lighting, or a style.	suit, look good on	That shade of blue is really flattering on you.	Also simply 'That dress is flattering.' Compare flatter as a verb meaning praise someone, often excessively.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: something is flattering on me meaning
535	5347	6441	I don't have time vs I don't have the time	/aɪ ˌdoʊnt hæv ˈtaɪm/	usage contrast	I don't have time = I lack enough available time. I don't have the time can mean the same with more emphasis, or 'I don't know what time it is' in certain contexts; 'the time' may also refer to a specific required amount/opportunity.	I have no time; I don't have enough time	I don't have time to watch a two-hour video tonight. / I don't have the time to argue about this right now. / 'Do you have the time?' can mean 'Do you know what time it is?'	Article changes nuance rather than creating a simple correctness rule. For ordinary lack of availability, no article is extremely common. 'Don't have the time for X' can sound emphatic/dismissive: I won't devote my time/energy to it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: I don't have time vs I don't have the time
535	5348	6270	auditorily	/ˌɔːdəˈtɔːrəli/	adverb	In a way relating to hearing or the sense of sound; by means of auditory perception.	by ear, aurally, through hearing	The two consonants are hard for me to distinguish auditorily, even though I can see the difference on a spectrogram. / The scene is easy to follow visually but confusing auditorily.	Raw 'auditorially' is a plausible formation but auditorily is the more standard dictionary adverb from auditory. 'Aurally' is shorter and often more natural. Do not confuse auditory (hearing) with auditor (person who examines accounts).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: auditorially
535	5349	6622	dysregulated	/dɪsˈreɡjəˌleɪtɪd/	adjective	Poorly regulated or unable to maintain normal control/stability; used of emotions, physiology, behavior, biological systems, markets/processes, etc.	poorly regulated, unstable, out of balance	After the argument he felt emotionally dysregulated and had trouble calming down. / Dysregulated immune signaling can contribute to disease.	Broad technical/clinical adjective. Emotional dysregulation has specific psychological uses, but casual internet use can be looser. Avoid treating it as a diagnosis by itself.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: dysregulated
535	5350	6583	scanlation	/skæˈneɪʃən/	noun, fandom/publishing slang	A fan-made scanned and translated version of a comic, especially manga/manhwa/manhua, typically produced and distributed without the official publisher's authorization.	fan translation, scanlated manga, fan-translated comic	I first read the series through an old scanlation before an official English edition existed. / Scanlation quality can vary widely between groups.	Blend of scan + translation. Scanlation groups may handle scanning, cleaning, typesetting, translation, proofreading, etc. Legal/copyright status differs from licensed translation; the term itself describes the fan-production practice.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: scanlation
536	5351	5588	sneak into something	/ˌsniːk ˈɪntuː/	phrasal-verb pattern	To enter a place secretly, quietly, or without permission or notice.	slip into, creep into, infiltrate	They sneaked into the theater through a side door.	Both sneaked and snuck are common past-tense forms, with regional/style variation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: sneaked into meaning
536	5352	5571	stick-on	/ˈstɪk ɑːn/	adjective / noun	Designed to attach to a surface by means of adhesive; as a noun, something attached this way.	adhesive, self-adhesive	She used stick-on labels to organize the storage boxes.	Usually before a noun: stick-on label, patch, decoration, hook. Hyphenated as an attributive adjective.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: stick-on meaning
536	5353	5830	blackpilling / blackpill	/ˈblækˌpɪlɪŋ/	noun / verb, internet slang	Adopting or promoting an extremely pessimistic, fatalistic worldview in which a problem is seen as largely hopeless or beyond meaningful change.	fatalism, extreme pessimism	Hours of doom-focused content left him blackpilled about the future.	Internet metaphor derived from red-pill language. Meaning varies by community and can occur in misogynistic/incel spaces as well as broader political/cultural pessimism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: blackpilling
536	5354	6773	hands down	/ˌhændz ˈdaʊn/	idiom, informal	Easily, unquestionably, or by a clear margin; used to say something wins or is true without serious competition/doubt.	easily, unquestionably, without a doubt, by far	That's hands down the best episode of the season. / She won hands down.	From winning so easily one need not exert full effort (often linked to horse racing). Common intensifier before superlatives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: hands down meaning
536	5355	6869	conciseness vs concision vs succinctness	/kənˈsaɪsnəs/; /kənˈsɪʒən/; /səkˈsɪŋktnəs/	contrast	All refer to expressing much in few words. Conciseness is the ordinary noun from concise; concision is a more formal/literary synonym and can sound stylistic; succinctness emphasizes compact, clear expression without unnecessary words.	brevity, compactness, terseness (can be negative)	I value conciseness, but not if it removes useful nuance. / The editor praised the essay's concision. / Her succinctness made the instructions easy to follow.	'Succinct' is adjective, 'succinctness' noun. Concision is not 'being cut' in ordinary writing usage. Terse can imply abruptness; brevity only means shortness and need not imply clarity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: conciseness vs concision vs succinct
536	5356	6162	emotionally volatile	/ɪˌmoʊʃənəli ˈvɑːlətəl/	adjective phrase	Prone to rapid, intense, or unpredictable shifts in emotion or emotional reactions.	emotionally unstable, mercurial, reactive, unpredictable, labile (technical/contextual)	The character is emotionally volatile, switching from affection to rage within seconds. / The conversation became difficult because both of them were exhausted and emotionally volatile.	Volatile emphasizes rapid/unpredictable change and potential explosiveness. Existing volatile card is broad; Batch10 'labile vs volatile' notes that labile can be technical/clinical while volatile is more everyday and often more negative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: emotionally volatile
536	5357	5561	for once	/fər ˈwʌns/	idiom	Used to say that something happens on this occasion even though it usually does not.	for a change, this time, on this occasion	For once, the train arrived early. / For once, I went to bed before 2 a.m. / Could you, for once, just answer the question directly?	Marks a contrast with the usual pattern: this occasion is exceptional. Depending on tone it can express relief/surprise ('For once, it worked') or irritation/reproach ('For once, listen to me'). 'For a change' is close but often carries stronger impatience.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0115 | search: for once meaning
536	5358	6714	in the driver's seat	/ɪn ðə ˈdraɪvərz ˌsiːt/	idiom	In control of a situation, decision, organization, negotiation, or one's own direction/future.	in control, calling the shots, at the helm, in charge	After paying off the debt, she finally felt back in the driver's seat financially. / The early goal put the home team in the driver's seat.	Driving metaphor: the driver controls direction. Common 'put someone in the driver's seat' and 'be back in the driver's seat.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: in the driver's seat meaning
536	5359	5935	low-key my type	/ˌloʊˈkiː maɪ ˈtaɪp/	slang phrase	A casual way to admit, often somewhat understatedly or sheepishly, that someone is the kind of person one finds attractive.	kind of my type, secretly my type, actually my type	I'm not usually into that look, but he's low-key my type. / That sarcastic, bookish character is low-key exactly my type.	Slang low-key can mean somewhat/subtly, secretly/without making a big deal of it, or—through semantic bleaching—simply 'actually/kinda.' The exact strength depends on speaker/context. 'My type' = the kind of person I'm usually attracted to.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: lowkey my type meaing
536	5360	5999	amorphous	/əˈmɔːrfəs/	adjective	Without a clearly defined shape, structure, boundaries, or organization; in science, lacking a crystalline structure.	shapeless, formless, ill-defined, unstructured, diffuse	The creature begins as an amorphous black mass. / The project was still an amorphous idea with no clear scope or owner. / Glass is an amorphous solid rather than a conventional crystal.	Very useful figuratively for vague groups, ideas, fears, plans, movements. 'Vague' concerns lack of clarity; amorphous suggests lack of definite form/structure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define amorphous
537	5361	6436	cascading effect	/kæˈskeɪdɪŋ ɪˈfekt/	noun phrase	A chain reaction in which one change, failure, or event triggers further effects that spread through connected parts of a system.	domino effect, ripple effect, chain reaction, cascade	One delayed shipment had a cascading effect on production, staffing, and the launch date. / A small continuity error can have cascading effects if later scenes build on it.	Cascade emphasizes successive propagation through dependencies. Domino effect is close; ripple effect often suggests effects spreading outward rather than one failure directly triggering the next.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: cascading effect meaning
537	5362	6455	entrust	/ɪnˈtrʌst/	verb	To give someone responsibility for taking care of a person, task, object, secret, or duty because you trust them.	trust someone with, commit to someone's care, hand over, assign	I entrusted her with the only copy of the key. / They entrusted the project to a small independent team. / He was entrusted with protecting the child.	Two core patterns: entrust someone with something; entrust something/someone to someone. Stronger trust/responsibility nuance than merely assign or give.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define entrusting
537	5363	5910	reparation	/ˌrepəˈreɪʃən/	noun	Something done or paid to repair harm or make amends for a wrong; reparations often refers to compensation for large-scale historical, wartime, or institutional harm.	compensation, restitution, redress, amends, recompense	The government agreed to pay reparations to victims of the abuse. / An apology alone may not feel like adequate reparation for years of damage.	Often plural in political/historical contexts: war reparations, slavery reparations. Compensation focuses on payment for loss; restitution often restores what was taken; redress is broader remedy; make amends is personal/moral.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: reparation meaning
537	5364	6334	supplant	/səˈplænt/	verb	To take the place of someone or something, especially by displacing, replacing, or superseding what was previously established.	replace, displace, supersede, oust, take the place of	Streaming gradually supplanted DVD rental as the default way many people watched films at home. / The new theory did not completely supplant the older one; both remained influential.	More formal than replace and often implies one thing gains the position previously occupied by another. Supersede emphasizes becoming newer/more authoritative; oust often involves people/power.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define supplant
537	5365	5735	birdbath / bird bath	/ˈbɜːrdˌbæθ/	noun	A shallow basin, usually outdoors, filled with water for birds to drink from and bathe in.	bird basin, bird-bathing basin	Sparrows gathered around the birdbath in the garden. / We put a shallow birdbath near the window so we could watch the birds. / The birdbath froze overnight.	Both 'birdbath' and 'bird bath' occur; the closed compound is common. It is typically a shallow outdoor basin providing water for drinking and bathing. Not the same as a bird feeder, which supplies food.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: bird bath meaning
537	5366	5835	scapegoat	/ˈskeɪpˌɡoʊt/	noun / verb	A person or group unfairly blamed for problems caused by others or by broader circumstances; to place such blame on them.	fall guy, whipping boy; blame	Immigrants were used as scapegoats for a much more complicated economic problem.	Existing 'sacrifice someone on the altar of...' notes scapegoating, but this core word merits its own card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: scapegoat
537	5367	6631	It carries at least as much weight as a feather—maybe even two feathers.	/ɪt ˌkæriz ət ˌliːst əz ˌmʌtʃ ˈweɪt əz ə ˈfeðər/	humorous mini-dialogue / wordplay	A teasing reassurance built around the idiom 'carry weight': first pretending the person's opinion barely matters, then revealing the joke and saying it matters a lot.	your opinion matters; playful mockery	'Come on, your opinion always counts. It carries at least as much weight as a feather. Maybe even two feathers. I'm kidding—of course it counts for a lot.'	Preserve the full joke because the learning value is pragmatic: 'your opinion counts' is sincere reassurance, while feather/two feathers temporarily flips it into mock-insult before the explicit repair.		"voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: Come on, your opinion always counts. You know that.
It carries at least as much weight as a feather.
Maybe even two feathers.
I’m kidding. Of course it counts for a lot."
537	5368	5783	candor	/ˈkændər/	noun	The quality of being open, honest, and straightforward in expression.	frankness, honesty, openness	I appreciated her candor about the project's problems.	AmE spelling candor; BrE candour.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112, voca_0115 | search: define candor
537	5369	5872	out of one's depth	/ˌaʊt əv wʌnz ˈdepθ/	idiom	In a situation too difficult, complex, or demanding for one's current knowledge, skill, or experience; literally, in water too deep to stand safely.	in over one's head, overwhelmed, beyond one's competence	I was completely out of my depth when the conversation turned to tax law. / He looked confident at first, but the technical questions quickly showed he was out of his depth.	Literal water image → figurative competence. In over one's head is a close conversational synonym and can also imply excessive commitments/debt.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: out of his depth
537	5370	6694	summation	/səˈmeɪʃən/	noun	The act/result of adding things together; a concise summary or final assessment; in mathematics, the addition of a sequence/series of terms.	sum, total, addition; summary, recap	The final paragraph is a neat summation of her argument. / The formula uses summation notation to add all the values.	Formal relative to summary. In legal contexts, summation can mean a lawyer's concluding argument, especially AmE.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: summation
538	5371	6873	hold back / be held back	/ˌhoʊld ˈbæk/	phrasal verb	To restrain, prevent progress, suppress, delay, or keep something/someone from advancing; also refrain from expressing an emotion/opinion or withhold information.	restrain, hinder, impede, suppress, withhold	Fear of making mistakes held me back from speaking. / She held back tears. / The teacher felt he was being held back by outdated rules.	Multiple productive patterns: hold someone back; hold back tears/information; hold back from doing. 'Held back' can also mean required to repeat a school grade in AmE.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: held back meaning
538	5372	6323	twitch	/twɪtʃ/	verb / noun	To make a small, sudden, involuntary or nervous jerking movement; to cause something to jerk briefly. As a noun, one such movement.	jerk, spasm, flick, tic (not identical)	His eyelid twitched from lack of sleep. / The cat's tail twitched when it heard the bird. / Her mouth twitched as if she was trying not to laugh.	Usually smaller and briefer than jerk. Body-part collocations: eye/eyelid, mouth, finger, muscle, tail. A tic is recurrent; a twitch can be a single movement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define twitched
538	5373	6111	humane	/hjuːˈmeɪn/	adjective	Showing compassion, kindness, and concern for suffering, especially in the treatment of people or animals; designed to minimize unnecessary pain or distress.	compassionate, merciful, kind, benevolent, considerate	They argued for a more humane way of treating prisoners. / The shelter uses humane traps that capture animals without injuring them. / Even necessary criticism can be delivered in a humane way.	Do not confuse human = relating to people, or humanitarian = concerned with human welfare/relief. Humane specifically evaluates compassion and avoidance of cruelty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: humane meaning
538	5374	6909	periphrasis	/pəˈrɪfrəsɪs/	noun, linguistics/rhetoric	The use of a longer or indirect expression instead of a shorter, simpler, or inflected form; in linguistics, expressing grammatical meaning with multiple words rather than morphology.	circumlocution, roundabout expression; analytic construction	Saying 'the man who is in charge' instead of a shorter title can be periphrastic. / English 'more beautiful' is a periphrastic comparative.	Plural periphrases /pəˈrɪfrəsiːz/. In rhetoric, often circumlocution; in grammar, periphrastic constructions are not necessarily wordy or bad—they are structural alternatives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define periphrasis
538	5375	5865	deplete	/dɪˈpliːt/	verb	To use up or greatly reduce the amount, supply, strength, or resources of something.	exhaust, drain, diminish, use up, run down	The long trip depleted most of my savings. / Constant stress can deplete your energy surprisingly fast. / Years of overfishing depleted the stock.	Existing depleted WID2806 is the adjective/past-participle result ('completely depleted'). Keep the base verb for the productive pattern deplete + resource/supply/reserves/energy.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: define deplete
538	5376	5635	no hassle	/ˌnoʊ ˈhæsəl/	informal phrase	With no trouble, inconvenience, argument, or unnecessary difficulty.	no trouble, no fuss, hassle-free	The return was quick and no hassle at all.	Existing Master has hassle inside a hustle-vs-hassle contrast, but this searched chunk is useful as a common standalone expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: no hassle meaning
538	5377	5926	apotropaic	/ˌæpətrəˈpeɪɪk/	adjective	Intended to avert, repel, or protect against evil, misfortune, harmful spirits, or the evil eye.	protective, evil-averting, warding, talismanic	The grotesque face carved above the doorway may have had an apotropaic function. / Scholars interpret the symbol as apotropaic rather than merely decorative.	Specialized anthropology/art-history/religion vocabulary. From Greek idea of 'turning away.' Apotropaic objects, gestures, symbols, and rituals ward off harm; talismanic may overlap but a talisman can attract good fortune as well as repel evil.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define apotropaic
538	5378	6708	manifest in / manifest itself in	/ˈmænəˌfest ɪn/	verb phrase	To become visible, apparent, or expressed in a particular form, behavior, symptom, result, or context.	appear in, show up in, be expressed in, reveal itself in	The tension manifests itself in small arguments. / The same bias can manifest in different ways across cultures.	Manifest can be transitive ('manifest symptoms') or intransitive/reflexive-like ('manifest in/as', 'manifest itself in'). Separate from modern spiritual 'manifesting' = trying to bring desires into reality.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: manifest in meaning
538	5379	5885	throw everything into one pot	/ˌθroʊ ˈevriθɪŋ ˌɪntuː wʌn ˈpɑːt/	metaphorical phrase	To combine different things, categories, issues, or ingredients together without keeping useful distinctions between them.	lump together, mix together, bundle together, put in the same bucket	You can't throw every kind of online criticism into one pot and call it harassment. / The recipe is basically: throw everything into one pot and let it simmer.	Not a strongly fixed idiom in English; for abstract categorization, lump everything together / put everything in the same bucket is usually more idiomatic. Literal cooking use is perfectly natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: throw something into one pot meaning
538	5380	6143	giddy-up / giddyap	/ˌɡɪdi ˈʌp/	interjection / informal noun-like expression	A command traditionally used to urge a horse to start moving or go faster; figuratively, an energetic 'let's get moving / hurry up.'	go!, get moving!, let's go!, come on!	He clicked his tongue and shouted, 'Giddy-up!' / We've wasted enough time—giddy-up, let's get this thing finished.	Associated with horseback riding and cowboy speech; modern use is often playful, theatrical, or figurative. Spellings include giddy-up, giddyup, and giddyap.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define giddy up
539	5381	6063	descriptivist vs prescriptivist	/dɪˈskrɪptɪvɪst/ vs /prɪˈskrɪptɪvɪst/	contrast card / linguistics	A descriptivist describes how speakers actually use language and analyzes patterns without treating one variety as inherently correct; a prescriptivist promotes or enforces rules about how language ought to be used, especially in a particular standard or context.	descriptive linguistics vs prescriptive grammar	A descriptivist might ask why speakers increasingly use the form; a prescriptivist might ask whether a style guide accepts it. / Good editing can use prescriptive conventions without pretending nonstandard dialects are linguistically defective.	Not a simple good-vs-bad binary. Linguistics is primarily descriptive, while prescription is useful for agreed standards in formal writing, editing, teaching, etc. A person can describe language scientifically and still recommend a house style for a context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: descriptivist vs prescriptivist
539	5382	5686	not an option	/nɑːt ən ˈɑːpʃən/	phrase	Impossible, unacceptable, or not available as a choice.	out of the question, impossible, unacceptable, unavailable, off the table	Giving up is not an option. / Skipping the integrity check is not an option if we want a reliable Master. / With the last train gone, going home by rail was no longer an option.	Can mean literally unavailable/impossible or rhetorically unacceptable. Often stronger than 'I don't prefer it': the speaker removes that choice from consideration. Compare 'not on the table' = not currently being considered.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: this is not an option meaning
539	5383	6889	demand doing vs demand to do	/dɪˈmænd/	grammar contrast	Demand can take a noun, that-clause, or -ing form in constructions where something requires action ('the problem demands addressing'). A person can demand to do something when insisting on being allowed to do it ('he demanded to speak to the manager').	require doing; insist on doing	The issue demands addressing immediately. / She demanded to speak to the supervisor. / They demanded that the policy be changed.	Meaning and subject structure differ. 'X demands doing' ≈ X needs/requires to be done (more BrE/formal). 'Person demands to do' = insists on performing the action.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: demand doing or to do
539	5384	6211	aneurysm (hyperbolic slang)	/ˈænjəˌrɪzəm/	hyperbolic slang use	Used jokingly/exaggeratedly in phrases such as 'this is giving me an aneurysm' to mean something is intensely frustrating, confusing, infuriating, or mentally overwhelming—not a literal medical event.	this is driving me insane, my brain is melting, this is killing me (hyperbolic)	Trying to follow that timeline is giving me an aneurysm. / The comment section makes me feel like I'm going to have an aneurysm.	Hyperbole built from a serious medical condition; not medically accurate and may sound insensitive in contexts involving actual illness. Separate from the literal medical card WID6184.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: aneurysm meaning slang
539	5385	5855	bloke	/bloʊk/	noun, chiefly British informal	A man; an ordinary male person.	guy, man, fellow, chap	He's a nice bloke once you get to know him. / Some bloke at the station asked me for directions. / He's the sort of bloke who'll help you move house without making a fuss.	Very common informal word in British English and also common in Australian English; roughly 'guy/man.' Usually neutral and casual, but modifiers can add evaluation ('weird bloke,' 'poor bloke'). Less natural as ordinary everyday vocabulary in American English.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: bloke meaning
539	5386	5989	evil twin	/ˌiːvəl ˈtwɪn/	noun / trope	A villainous or morally opposite double of a character; more broadly, a counterpart that looks similar to something familiar but has harmful, distorted, or opposite qualities.	dark double, sinister counterpart, doppelgänger (not necessarily evil), mirror counterpart	The episode introduces an evil twin who looks identical but works for the enemy. / You could describe a manipulative imitation of a helpful technology as its 'evil twin.'	Common fiction trope, sometimes literal twin and sometimes duplicate/alternate-universe counterpart. Doppelgänger only means look-alike/double and need not be evil.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: evil twin meaning
539	5387	5964	fraught	/frɔːt/	adjective	Filled with tension, anxiety, difficulty, or potential problems; historically/less commonly 'fraught with X' = full of or accompanied by X.	tense, troubled, stressful, difficult, loaded, perilous	The conversation became increasingly fraught once money came up. / Their relationship with the studio has always been fraught. / The decision is fraught with legal and ethical problems.	Modern standalone 'a fraught relationship/situation' usually means tense/troubled. 'Fraught with danger/problems' emphasizes being full of them. It does not simply mean exhausted, despite occasional loose synonym lists.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define fraught
539	5388	6087	subservience	/səbˈsɜːrviəns/	noun	The state or behavior of being excessively willing to obey, submit to, serve, or defer to someone more powerful.	servility, submissiveness, deference, obsequiousness, docility	The relationship demanded obedience bordering on subservience. / Respect for expertise doesn't require intellectual subservience.	Usually negative: loss of independence/status. Deference can be appropriate/respectful; subservience implies excessive or subordinate submission. Obsequiousness emphasizes fawning eagerness to please.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define subservience
539	5389	6917	Did you want ...? vs Do you want ...?	/dɪd ju ˈwɑːnt/ vs /duː ju ˈwɑːnt/	politeness / pragmatic contrast	Both can ask about a present desire. 'Do you want...?' is direct and neutral. 'Did you want...?' uses past tense for pragmatic distance/softening and can sound more tentative, service-oriented, or like checking a previously implied intention.	Would you like...?, were you wanting...? (regional/contextual)	Do you want a receipt? / Did you want a bag as well? / Did you want me to send that today?	The past form need not refer to past desire. This is modal remoteness/politeness. Tone and setting matter: 'Did you want...?' is common in service encounters and gentle check-ins in North American English.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: did you want vs do you want in a polite sense
539	5390	6811	the wee hours	/ðə ˌwiː ˈaʊərz/	idiom	The very early hours after midnight and before dawn.	the small hours, early morning hours, middle of the night	We ended up talking until the wee hours of the morning. / I was still editing cards in the wee hours.	Often 'the wee hours of the morning.' Wee = very small; phrase has a slightly cozy/old-fashioned flavor but remains common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: wee hours
540	5391	6782	beddy-bye	/ˈbedi ˌbaɪ/	noun/interjection, baby talk / playful	A childish or playful way of saying bedtime, bed, or 'go to bed.'	bedtime, night-night, sleepy time	Okay, kids, it's beddy-bye. / I'm exhausted—beddy-bye for me.	Also beddy-byes/beddy-bye time. Intentionally cutesy/babyish and often used jokingly by adults.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: beddy-bye meaning
540	5392	6442	transliteration	/trænzˌlɪtəˈreɪʃən/	noun	Representing words written in one writing system with the characters of another, aiming to map spelling/sounds rather than translate meaning.	romanization, transcription (overlapping), script conversion	Tokyo is a romanized form of 東京; romanization is a type of transliteration. / Transliteration tells you how a name is represented in another script, while translation tells you what it means.	Translation transfers meaning; transliteration transfers written representation across scripts. Transcription often focuses more directly on pronunciation. Real systems may mix these goals, so boundaries are not always perfect.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: transliteration
540	5393	6378	dish it back	/ˌdɪʃ ɪt ˈbæk/	informal expression	To respond to teasing, criticism, insults, or aggressive treatment by giving the same kind of treatment back rather than simply taking it.	give it back, hit back, fire back, return the favor, dish it out in return	If your friends tease you, you have to be able to dish it back sometimes. / She didn't get offended—she laughed and dished it right back.	Related fixed expression: 'dish it out but can't take it.' Dish it back is understandable/productive but less dictionary-fixed; it emphasizes reciprocating the same verbal/social energy.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: dish it back meaning
540	5394	5811	absolve	/əbˈzɑːlv/	verb	To formally or morally free someone from blame, guilt, responsibility, or an obligation.	exonerate, acquit, clear	The new evidence did not completely absolve him of responsibility.	Common pattern: absolve someone of/from blame, guilt, responsibility, duty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define absolve
540	5395	6913	AP	/ˌeɪ ˈpiː/	abbreviation, context-dependent	An abbreviation with many meanings. Common U.S. education: Advanced Placement. News/media: Associated Press. Other fields use AP for numerous technical terms, so context is essential.	Advanced Placement; Associated Press; other expansions by context	She took AP Biology in high school. / The report was published by the AP. / In a technical document, check what AP is defined to mean.	Do not assign one expansion without context. In U.S. school talk, AP often means Advanced Placement courses/exams; in journalism, 'the AP' commonly means Associated Press.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: what is AP?
540	5396	5898	if you sound mental, you're bigger	/ɪf ju ˌsaʊnd ˈmentəl jʊr ˈbɪɡər/	uncertain contextual phrase	An exact remembered/search fragment whose intended meaning cannot be recovered confidently from the wording alone.		Original search: 'if you sound mental you're bigger meaning.' Preserve the fragment rather than fabricating a confident dictionary meaning.	This is not a recognizable standard English expression as written. 'Mental' in BrE informal speech can mean crazy/extreme/intense; 'bigger' could depend on missing context. If the original source is recovered later, this card should be revised around that context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: if you sound mental you're bigger meaning
540	5397	6655	it can only do so much	/ɪt kən ˌoʊnli duː soʊ ˈmʌtʃ/	conversational phrase	A tool, method, treatment, rule, or intervention has limited power/effectiveness and cannot solve everything by itself.	it has limits, it can only help to a point	Spell-check can catch typos, but it can only do so much. / Exercise helps, but it can only do so much if you never sleep.	Productive extension of 'can only do so much.' Useful for distinguishing partial usefulness from total solution.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: it can only do so much meaning
540	5398	6124	keep one foot out the door	/ˌkiːp wʌn ˈfʊt aʊt ðə ˌdɔːr/	idiomatic variation / less-established phrase	To deliberately remain partly disengaged or ready to leave a job, relationship, plan, or commitment. It is an attested variation built on the much more established idiom 'have one foot out the door.' Do not confuse it with 'get/have/keep a foot in the door,' which means gaining or maintaining an opportunity or access.	stay half-committed, keep an exit open, remain ready to leave; cf. have one foot out the door; get/have/keep a foot in the door	He never fully committed to the project; he always seemed to keep one foot out the door. / She already has one foot out the door and is interviewing elsewhere. / An internship can help you get a foot in the door.	KEEP. 'Keep one foot out the door' is less established than 'have one foot out the door' but is attested and understandable. Master already contains the standard 'have one foot out the door' as WID 6125. No separate 'foot in the door' card was found in this Master snapshot, so the contrasting standard expression is retained here: get/have/keep a foot in the door = gain or maintain a small opening, opportunity, or access.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: keep one's feet out of the door meaning
540	5399	5822	maritime	/ˈmerəˌtaɪm/	adjective	Relating to the sea, ships, shipping, navigation, or coastal activity.	marine, nautical, seafaring, ocean-related, shipping-related	The city has a long maritime history. / Maritime trade shaped the region's economy for centuries. / The dispute involves maritime boundaries and shipping routes.	Broad adjective covering the sea, shipping, navigation, ports, coastal affairs, and maritime law/security. 'Nautical' more specifically concerns ships/sailing/navigation; 'marine' is broader and also heavily used for ocean life/environment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: maritime meaning
540	5400	5847	profane	/prəˈfeɪn/	adjective / verb	Not sacred or religious; irreverent toward sacred things; containing offensive or obscene language; as a verb, to treat something sacred with disrespect.	secular; irreverent, obscene; desecrate	The broadcast removed profane language. / They were accused of profaning the shrine.	Do not confuse profane with profound. As a verb it overlaps desecrate; adjective senses are independently common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: define profane
541	5401	5661	fare thee well	/ˌfer ðiː ˈwel/	archaic / literary farewell	An old-fashioned expression meaning goodbye, with a wish that the other person fare well.	farewell, goodbye	Fare thee well, my friend, until we meet again.	Thee is the old singular object form of thou. Modern use is literary, theatrical, humorous, or deliberately archaic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: fare thee well
541	5402	6884	provocation	/ˌprɑːvəˈkeɪʃən/	noun	An act, statement, event, or stimulus that provokes a reaction—especially anger, conflict, retaliation, or a response; also the act of provoking.	incitement, goading, trigger, stimulus, challenge	He refused to respond to the provocation. / The attack was described as a deliberate provocation.	Can be interpersonal, political/military, legal, or general. 'Without provocation' = without being provoked. Legal significance varies by jurisdiction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: provocation
541	5403	6947	in the nick of time	/ɪn ðə ˌnɪk əv ˈtaɪm/	idiom	Just before it would have been too late; at the last possible or crucial moment.	just in time, at the last moment, at the eleventh hour (often more dramatic)	I caught the train in the nick of time—the doors closed right behind me. / We found the bug in the nick of time, just before pushing the update.	Stronger/more dramatic than neutral 'just in time': it highlights a very narrow escape from being too late. Historical 'nick' can mean a precise point or critical moment. This card is intentionally restored as a normal learning card; an older Web-only version was not part of the finalized Anki/Master set.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: in the nick of time
541	5404	5893	pull someone under	/ˌpʊl ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌndər/	verb phrase / metaphor	Literally, to drag someone beneath water or another surface; figuratively, to overwhelm or ruin someone emotionally, financially, socially, or through another person's problems.	drag under, overwhelm, sink, bring down	The current nearly pulled him under. / I wanted to help, but I couldn't let his problems pull me under too. / One bad investment was enough to pull the small company under.	Related pull a business under = cause it to fail. Figurative image is drowning/being submerged by pressure or another person's crisis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: pull someone under means
541	5405	5976	age like milk	/ˌeɪdʒ laɪk ˈmɪlk/	idiom / internet slang	To become embarrassingly wrong, inappropriate, unattractive, or unfortunate as time passes—the opposite of aging well/like fine wine.	age badly, not age well, become dated, backfire in hindsight	That confident prediction aged like milk within two weeks. / Some jokes from the series have aged like milk, while others still work perfectly.	Milk spoils with age, hence the metaphor. Common online retrospective phrase: 'This aged like milk.' Opposite: age like fine wine = improve or look better with time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: aged like milk
541	5406	6159	a good egg	/ə ˌɡʊd ˈeɡ/	idiom / noun phrase	A kind, decent, reliable, or likable person.	good person, decent sort, stand-up person, good guy	She drove across town just to return my wallet—she's a good egg. / He can be awkward, but he's a genuinely good egg.	Warm, mildly old-fashioned, often British-flavored or playful. Usually affectionate rather than formal praise.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: A good egg
541	5407	6204	slip out	/ˌslɪp ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To leave quietly or unnoticed; to escape accidentally from a position/container; or, of words/information, to be said accidentally without intention.	sneak out, quietly leave; escape; blurt out, let slip	I slipped out before the speeches ended. / The phone slipped out of my hand. / His real opinion slipped out before he could stop himself.	Three useful frames: person slips out = leaves quietly; object slips out of X = escapes/falls free; words/secret slips out = is accidentally revealed. 'Let something slip' is especially common for information.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: slipped out meaning
541	5408	5708	androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS)	/ˈændrədʒən ɪnˌsensəˈtɪvəti ˈsɪndroʊm/	noun	A group of genetic conditions in which the body's cells respond partly or not at all to androgens, affecting sexual development in people with an XY karyotype.	AIS	Androgen insensitivity syndrome can affect the development of reproductive anatomy and secondary sex characteristics.	Medical term. Complete, partial, and mild forms are described according to degree of androgen response. Keep definition informational rather than diagnostic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: what is androgen insensitivity syndrome
541	5409	6372	dunderhead	/ˈdʌndərˌhed/	noun, old-fashioned/humorous insult	A stupid, foolish, or slow-witted person.	fool, idiot, blockhead, dunce, numbskull	You dunderhead—you mailed the envelope without putting the letter inside. / The villain calls his henchmen dunderheads in an intentionally old-fashioned comic style.	Recognizable but old-fashioned/comic rather than a normal modern insult. Existing 'dunderheadedness' is the abstract noun; this is the much more usable person-noun/base form.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: dunderhead
541	5410	5807	frayed	/freɪd/	adjective / past participle	Worn or unraveling at the edges; figuratively, strained, exhausted, or increasingly irritable.	worn, tattered; strained	By the end of the meeting, everyone's nerves were frayed.	Raw 'freyed' normalized to frayed. Common figurative collocation: frayed nerves/tempers.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: freyed
542	5411	5618	bum (person)	/bʌm/	noun, informal, potentially derogatory	A person regarded as lazy, idle, or irresponsible; in older/informal use, a homeless or itinerant person, roughly overlapping with tramp.	idler, loafer; tramp (dated)	The old movie uses bum for a drifter with nowhere permanent to live.	Meaning and offensiveness depend strongly on context. In AmE bum can be a contemptuous label for a lazy/useless person or an older term for a homeless drifter. It also has unrelated senses such as buttocks and borrow/get for free ('bum a cigarette').		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: bum means tramp?
542	5412	6024	only to + verb	/ˈoʊnli tə/	construction	Used after an action or expectation to introduce a later result that is surprising, disappointing, ironic, or contrary to what was intended.	but then, only for X to happen, and then unexpectedly	I walked all the way back to the store, only to find it had already closed. / She spent years searching for the answer, only to realize she'd had it from the beginning.	Common narrative construction: X did A, only to do/discover/find B. Often signals frustration or irony, but the result can simply be surprising. Different from 'only' meaning merely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: only to be meaning
542	5413	6887	give rise to	/ˌɡɪv ˈraɪz tuː/	idiom, formal	To cause, produce, lead to, or create something, especially a situation, effect, problem, idea, or development.	cause, lead to, result in, produce, generate	The ambiguity gave rise to several different interpretations. / Rapid growth can give rise to new problems.	'To' is a preposition: give rise to + noun/gerund. More formal than cause/lead to.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: give rise to
542	5414	6053	swaggering	/ˈswæɡərɪŋ/	adjective / participle	Walking, behaving, or speaking with conspicuous self-confidence, bravado, or arrogance.	strutting, cocky, boastful, brash, self-assured	He makes a swaggering entrance like he already owns the place. / The movie's swaggering confidence is fun until the plot starts falling apart.	From swagger. Can be admiring ('bold, stylish confidence') or critical ('arrogant bravado'). Existing swagger card covers the noun; this preserves the descriptive participial use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define swaggering
542	5415	5803	poke fun at someone/something	/ˌpoʊk ˈfʌn æt/	idiom	To make jokes about someone or something, usually in a light or teasing way.	tease, make fun of, rib	The show pokes fun at superhero clichés without being mean-spirited.	Usually gentler than ridicule, though tone/context can make it hurtful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: poke fun at meaning
542	5416	5732	discounted	/ˈdɪsˌkaʊntɪd/	adjective / past participle	Sold or offered at a reduced price; also treated as less important, less credible, or unlikely to be true.	reduced-price; disregarded, devalued	Students can buy discounted tickets. / His explanation was quickly discounted.	Two major senses derive from discount: reduce the price, or reduce the weight/credibility given to something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: DISCOUNTED definition in American English | Collins English Dictionary
542	5417	6296	sniff	/snɪf/	verb / noun	To draw air through the nose in a short audible breath, especially to smell something or because of a runny nose/crying; also to smell or investigate something by sniffing. As a noun, one such breath or smell.	smell, inhale through the nose, snuffle; scent	The dog sniffed my bag for food. / She sniffed the coffee before taking a sip. / He kept sniffing because of his cold. / One sniff was enough to tell that the milk had gone bad.	Contrast sniffle: repeated small nasal inhalations, often from a cold or crying. 'Sniff at' can mean smell experimentally or figuratively show contempt; 'sniff out' = detect/discover.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sniff meaning
542	5418	6149	sling / slung	/slɪŋ/; /slʌŋ/	verb; past tense/past participle	To throw or fling something, hang/carry something loosely, or place it casually over/around something. Slung is the past tense and past participle of sling.	throw, fling, toss; hang, drape, shoulder	She slung her bag over one shoulder and headed out. / He slung an arm around his friend's shoulders. / Mud was slung across the side of the car.	Common constructions: sling/slung something over one's shoulder; sling an arm around someone; sling mud = make insulting accusations. 'Slung' is irregular: sling–slung–slung.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: slung
542	5419	6799	invasive vs pervasive	/ɪnˈveɪsɪv/ vs /pərˈveɪsɪv/	contrast	Invasive = intruding, entering, spreading into, or violating a space/body/privacy where it is unwanted; pervasive = spread throughout an area/system and present almost everywhere. Something can be both.	intrusive/encroaching vs widespread/ubiquitous	Constant tracking can feel invasive because it intrudes on privacy, and pervasive because it appears across nearly every app. / The plant is invasive; the smell is pervasive.	Key distinction: invasive describes intrusion/encroachment; pervasive describes extent/distribution. Medical 'invasive procedure' and ecological 'invasive species' are major senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: invasive and pervasive meaning
542	5420	6833	tie-in	/ˈtaɪ ɪn/	noun / adjective	A connection or coordinated product/promotion linked to another work, event, campaign, or franchise; e.g. a tie-in novel, toy, ad, or episode.	cross-promotion, related product, companion work, linkage	The movie got a tie-in game and a line of toys. / The episode was a promotional tie-in with the new film.	Hyphenated noun/adjective tie-in. Verb 'tie in with' = connect/coordinate with. In media, often commercial/franchise linkage.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: tie-in meaning
543	5421	6097	sham	/ʃæm/	noun / adjective	Something presented as genuine, legitimate, or serious that is actually false, fraudulent, hollow, or merely for show; as an adjective, fake or bogus.	fraud, fake, pretense, charade, bogus, phony	The election was denounced by critics as a sham. / Their supposed investigation was a sham designed to make it look as though action was being taken. / He called the apology a sham.	Strongly accusatory: not merely low-quality, but falsely presented as real/legitimate. 'Charade' emphasizes performance/pretense; 'scam' usually involves deception for gain; sham can describe institutions, marriages, trials, investigations, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: shammeaning
543	5422	6822	on the grind	/ɑːn ðə ˈɡraɪnd/	slang	Working hard and persistently, especially toward money, career, fitness, study, or another goal; often evokes hustle culture and repetitive effort.	grinding, hustling, working hard, putting in work	I've been on the grind all week trying to finish the project. / His posts are full of 'rise and grind' hustle rhetoric.	Can be sincere, self-mocking, or critical of hustle culture. Related grind = sustained hard work; 'the daily grind' is more weary/routine.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: on the grind meaning
543	5423	6732	go on a crawl	/ˌɡoʊ ɑːn ə ˈkrɔːl/	activity phrase	To visit a sequence of places of the same type, usually socially or recreationally, moving from one stop to the next; the specific kind is named, e.g. pub crawl, bar crawl, food crawl.	do a crawl, make the rounds	We went on a taco crawl and tried four places in one afternoon. / They're going on a pub crawl after the conference.	'Crawl' here means a multi-stop outing, not moving on hands and knees. Usually needs context/modifier: pub crawl, bar crawl, gallery crawl, food crawl.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: going on a crawl
543	5424	6693	animation cel / cel	/ˌænəˈmeɪʃən ˈsel/	noun / translation	A transparent sheet, historically usually cellulose-based, on which characters/objects were painted for traditional hand-drawn animation and photographed over backgrounds.	cel, animation cell (misspelling), production cel	The museum displayed an original animation cel from the film. / Traditional anime production used painted cels before digital coloring became standard.	Japanese セル画 = animation cel / cel. Spelling cel has one l, shortened from celluloid. In collector contexts, distinguish production cels from reproduction/sericels.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: セル画 英語で
543	5425	6125	have one foot out the door	/ˌhæv wʌn ˈfʊt aʊt ðə ˌdɔːr/	idiom	To be partly disengaged from a job, relationship, group, or commitment and already considering or preparing to leave.	be halfway out, be ready to leave, be mentally checked out, be on one's way out	By the final season, you could tell the actor already had one foot out the door. / It's hard to repair a relationship when one person has one foot out the door.	Metaphor: body still inside, one foot already outside. Usually implies reduced commitment. Contrast foot in the door = initial opportunity/access, nearly opposite image.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: have one foot out the door
543	5426	6854	reprieve	/rɪˈpriːv/	noun / verb	A temporary delay or cancellation of punishment, especially execution; more broadly, a temporary relief or break from something unpleasant or difficult.	respite, temporary relief, stay, postponement	The court granted a temporary reprieve. / The cool weather gave us a brief reprieve from the heat.	Key nuance: usually temporary relief/delay, unlike a full pardon. Figurative reprieve is very common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define reprieve
543	5427	6399	rung	/rʌŋ/	noun	One of the horizontal bars forming a step on a ladder; figuratively, a level or position in a hierarchy, career, social structure, or progression.	ladder step, crossbar; level, tier, stage	He climbed onto the top rung of the ladder. / She started on the lowest rung of the company and worked her way up. / Each promotion felt like another rung on the career ladder.	Plural rungs. Strong literal→figurative metaphor: ladder rung → level in hierarchy. 'A rung above/below' is common for comparative status.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define rungs
543	5428	6018	get a word in edgewise	/ˌɡet ə ˌwɜːrd ɪn ˈedʒwaɪz/	idiom	To manage to say something during a conversation dominated by someone who talks continuously or interrupts.	get a chance to speak, get a word in, squeeze a word in	She talked for ten minutes straight and I couldn't get a word in edgewise. / When those two start debating, nobody else can get a word in.	AmE edgewise; BrE often 'get a word in edgeways.' Usually used negatively: can't/couldn't/hardly get a word in edgewise.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: get a word in edgewise
543	5429	6935	impel	/ɪmˈpel/	verb, formal	To drive, urge, or cause someone/something to act or move, especially through an internal motive, strong force, pressure, or circumstance.	drive, urge, propel, prompt, spur	Curiosity impelled her to keep investigating. / A sense of duty impelled him to speak up.	Usually 'impel someone to do.' More formal than push/drive. Often suggests a motivating force rather than direct coercion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define impel
543	5430	5655	hit a brick wall	/ˌhɪt ə ˌbrɪk ˈwɔːl/	idiom	To reach a point where progress becomes impossible or extremely difficult.	reach an impasse, get stuck	We hit a brick wall when the archive had no records from that year.	Vivid metaphor for suddenly encountering an obstacle that stops progress.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: hit a brick wall meaning
544	5431	6877	self-contained	/ˌself kənˈteɪnd/	adjective	Complete and able to function independently without needing outside parts, information, or support; also reserved and not inclined to share feelings; of accommodation, having its own facilities.	independent, standalone, autonomous, complete; reserved	Each lesson is self-contained, so you can study them in any order. / He's quiet and self-contained. / They rented a self-contained studio.	Several senses share independence/boundedness. Compare modular: a modular unit is designed to combine/rearrange with others; self-contained emphasizes completeness on its own.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: self-contained meaning
544	5432	6110	sublimate	/ˈsʌbləˌmeɪt/	verb	In psychology, to redirect an unacceptable, troubling, or powerful impulse into a socially acceptable or productive activity; in chemistry, for a solid to change directly into gas (or cause it to do so) without becoming liquid first.	channel, redirect, transform; sublime (chemistry)	He sublimated his anger into obsessive training and eventually became an excellent boxer. / She seems to sublimate anxiety into work. / Dry ice sublimates directly from solid carbon dioxide into gas.	Two very different senses. Psychological sublimation is associated with psychoanalytic theory: redirecting impulses rather than merely suppressing them. Chemistry: sublimate/sublimation = solid ↔ gas transition (reverse is deposition). Personal context / memory hook: originally searched in a discussion comparing Japanese and Western approaches to dating/relationships. The intended figurative idea was to sublimate elements of both traditions—not merely mix or compromise between them, but transform what each contains into a more refined or higher-order form while shedding less useful aspects. This is a useful conceptual bridge to '昇華': transformation into another form, while remaining distinct from the technical chemistry sense (solid → gas without passing through the liquid state).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115, voca_0119 | search: sublimate
544	5433	6433	sapient vs sentient	/ˈseɪpiənt/ vs /ˈsentiənt/	contrast	Sentient primarily means capable of sensing/feeling or having subjective experience; sapient means wise and, especially in speculative fiction, capable of advanced reasoning/intellectual understanding.	feeling/aware vs wise/reasoning (rough contrast)	A dog is clearly sentient, but science-fiction writers might reserve 'sapient' for beings capable of language, abstract reasoning, and civilization. / An AI could be highly intelligent without that alone proving it is sentient.	Useful SF vocabulary contrast, not a universally settled scientific taxonomy. Sentience, consciousness, self-awareness, intelligence, and sapience are related but distinct concepts and are debated across disciplines.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: saptient vs sentient
544	5434	5790	localization discourse	/ˌloʊkələˈzeɪʃən ˈdɪskɔːrs/	noun phrase	Discussion and debate about how translated media should be adapted for a target audience, including fidelity, cultural references, censorship, rewriting, and translator/localizer choices.	localization debate	The localization discourse around the game focused on how much adaptation was appropriate.	Contextual media/internet phrase built on existing localization. Preserve because the original search targets the debate itself, not merely the process.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: localization discourse
544	5435	6849	entail	/ɪnˈteɪl/	verb	To necessarily involve, require, or bring something as a consequence or essential part.	involve, require, necessitate, imply as a consequence	Updating the whole deck would entail checking thousands of rows. / The job entails frequent travel.	Common pattern entail + noun/gerund: 'entails working weekends.' In logic, entail has a stricter technical sense: if A entails B, B must be true if A is true.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: entail definition
544	5436	6121	reps	/reps/	plural noun, informal abbreviation	Short for repetitions in exercise: individual repeated movements in a set. Also short for representatives in business/politics, and informally repeated practice experiences ('get your reps in').	repetitions; representatives; practice repetitions	Do three sets of ten reps. / Sales reps will contact customers next week. / The only way to get comfortable speaking is to get more reps in.	Fitness sense is extremely common: sets × reps. Figurative 'get reps in' treats repeated practice as training. Context distinguishes repetitions from representatives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: reps meaing
544	5437	6379	affectation	/ˌæfekˈteɪʃən/	noun	A deliberately adopted manner, style, behavior, pronunciation, or habit intended to impress or create a particular image, especially when it seems unnatural or insincere.	pretense, pose, mannerism, artificiality, pretension	His exaggerated British accent sounded like an affectation. / The character's elaborate politeness is partly an affectation hiding how insecure he is.	Usually critical: something put on rather than natural. Compare mannerism = characteristic habit, not necessarily fake; pretension = claim/display of importance/culture; affectation is the adopted feature itself.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: affectation meaning
544	5438	6729	let her cook	/ˌlet hər ˈkʊk/	internet slang	Let her continue what she is doing or saying because she may be onto something, has a plan, or deserves a chance to finish before being judged.	let her continue, hear her out, give her a chance, let her work	That idea sounds ridiculous, but let her cook—I want to see where she's going with it. / Everyone laughed at first, then somebody said, 'Let her cook.'	From 'let him cook,' popular internet/sports slang. Cook = perform/create/work successfully. Can be sincere, ironic, or used before an anticipated disaster.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: let her cook meaning
544	5439	5540	roll off the tongue	/ˌroʊl ɔːf ðə ˈtʌŋ/	idiom	To be easy, smooth, or pleasing to say aloud.	be easy to say, flow naturally, trip off the tongue, sound smooth	The name is unusual, but it rolls off the tongue. / 'Mia-chan' rolls off the tongue more easily than the full formal name. / The slogan is catchy because the words just roll off the tongue.	Used for words, names, phrases, or lines whose sound/rhythm feels effortless and pleasant. It concerns pronunciation/phonetic flow, not whether the meaning is easy. Related 'trip off the tongue' is less common and can sound literary.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: roll off one's tongue
544	5440	6248	eugenics	/juːˈdʒenɪks/	noun, historical/social-science term	A discredited movement and set of policies aimed at influencing human reproduction to increase traits judged 'desirable' and reduce traits judged 'undesirable,' historically tied to coercive sterilization, racism, ableism, and Nazi racial policy.	selective-breeding ideology, racial hygiene (historical related term)	Eugenics movements influenced forced-sterilization laws in several countries. / Modern genetics is not the same thing as eugenics; the term specifically carries a history of ranking people and controlling reproduction.	Historically important and ethically loaded. Eugenic programs often dressed social prejudice in claims of biological science. Distinguish genetic counseling or disease research from coercive population-level judgments about whose traits/lives are desirable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define eugenics
545	5441	6699	horrid	/ˈhɔːrɪd/	adjective	Extremely unpleasant, nasty, dreadful, or horrible; historically/less commonly, causing horror.	awful, horrible, dreadful, nasty, ghastly	The room smelled horrid. / We had horrid weather all weekend. / What a horrid thing to say.	Especially common in BrE and can sound somewhat old-fashioned/childlike/dramatic depending on speaker. Usually less literally horrifying than horrific.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: define horrid
545	5442	5576	belong in the same conversation as someone/something	/bɪˈlɔːŋ ɪn ðə ˌseɪm ˌkɑːnvərˈseɪʃən æz/	idiom-like phrase	To deserve comparison with, or consideration in the same category or level as, someone or something else.	be comparable to, be in the same league as	This film belongs in the same conversation as the director's best work.	Often used in ranking/evaluation: saying X does or doesn't belong in the same conversation as Y judges whether the comparison is legitimate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: belong in the same conversation as meaning
545	5443	5529	prodigious	/prəˈdɪdʒəs/	adjective	Remarkably great in size, amount, degree, or ability; impressive or enormous.	enormous, immense, remarkable	She has a prodigious memory for names and dates.	Despite the spelling, not semantically related to prodigal in modern use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: prodigious
545	5444	6610	pheasant	/ˈfezənt/	noun	A medium-to-large game bird, typically with a long tail; males of many species have colorful plumage.	game bird	A pheasant flew out of the grass beside the road. / Male ring-necked pheasants are much more brightly colored than females.	Raw search plural 'pheasants.' Do not confuse peasant = poor/rural agricultural laborer historically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define pheasants
545	5445	5867	muck up	/ˌmʌk ˈʌp/	phrasal verb, chiefly British informal	To spoil, mishandle, confuse, or make a mistake in something; to mess something up.	mess up, screw up, botch, bungle, foul up	I mucked up the dates and booked the hotel for the wrong weekend. / Don't muck around with the settings—you'll muck something up.	Often milder/less vulgar than screw up. Transitive: muck something up; intransitive/contextual: I mucked up.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: muck up meaning
545	5446	6302	own up to one's mistakes	/ˌoʊn ˈʌp tə wʌnz mɪˈsteɪks/	phrasal-verb collocation	To openly admit and accept responsibility for one's mistakes instead of denying, hiding, or shifting blame for them.	admit one's mistakes, take responsibility, acknowledge fault, come clean	I respect people who can own up to their mistakes without making excuses. / The company eventually owned up to its mistake and corrected the report.	A highly common collocation worth preserving beyond the base verb because it packages the accountability meaning naturally. Often paired with apologize, take responsibility, learn from.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: owning up to mistakes
545	5447	5752	schlocky	/ˈʃlɑːki/	adjective, informal	Cheap, trashy, low-quality, or artistically crude, often in an exaggeratedly sentimental or sensational way.	cheesy, tacky, trashy	The movie is schlocky, but its over-the-top effects are part of the fun.	From schlock = cheap/inferior merchandise or entertainment. Can be affectionate when describing enjoyable B-movie excess.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: schlocky meaning
545	5448	6057	exertion	/ɪɡˈzɜːrʃən/	noun	Physical or mental effort, especially effort that requires energy or strain; also the act of applying force or influence.	effort, strain, labor, exercise, application of force	Even mild exertion left him out of breath. / After hours of mental exertion, I stopped absorbing what I was reading. / The mechanism moves with very little exertion.	Often physical: strenuous exertion, avoid exertion. But mental exertion is normal. Effort is broader; exertion often foregrounds energy expenditure/strain.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: exertion meaning
545	5449	6460	cuff / cuffs	/kʌf/	noun / verb	The end part of a sleeve around the wrist; a band around an ankle/wrist; cuffs can mean handcuffs; as a verb, to strike lightly with an open hand or put handcuffs on someone.	sleeve end, wristband; handcuffs; slap (verb)	The shirt cuffs were frayed. / The officer put him in cuffs. / She gave him a playful cuff on the shoulder. / 'I have a cuff' by itself is not a standard fixed expression; context is needed.	Raw 'I have a cuff' is ambiguous. Existing 'off the cuff' is unrelated idiomatically (= without preparation). Preserve base word rather than inventing a slang sense unsupported by source context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: i have a cuff meaning
545	5450	5970	carpal tunnel syndrome	/ˌkɑːrpəl ˈtʌnəl ˌsɪndroʊm/	noun, medical	A condition caused by compression of the median nerve as it passes through the carpal tunnel in the wrist, often causing numbness, tingling, pain, or weakness in the hand.	CTS, median nerve compression	She gets tingling in her thumb and first two fingers and is being evaluated for carpal tunnel syndrome. / Long computer sessions can aggravate hand symptoms, but not every wrist or hand pain is carpal tunnel.	Distinct from tendonitis/tenosynovitis: CTS is primarily a nerve-compression syndrome. Diagnosis and causes are more specific than simply 'typing too much.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: carpal tunnel syndrome
546	5451	5582	PB / Pb (context-dependent abbreviation)	/ˌpiː ˈbiː/	abbreviation	An abbreviation whose meaning depends heavily on context; in sports, PB commonly means personal best, while Pb is the chemical symbol for lead and PB can have other field-specific meanings.	personal best (sports)	She ran a new PB in the 10K.	The raw search was only 'pb meaning', so preserve the ambiguity rather than pretending one context is certain. In international sport PB commonly = personal best; capitalization matters for chemical Pb = lead.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: pb meaning
546	5452	5814	write someone/something off	/ˌraɪt ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To dismiss someone or something as unimportant, hopeless, or unlikely to succeed; also to cancel a debt/loss for accounting purposes or declare a damaged asset beyond repair.	dismiss, give up on; deduct, declare a total loss	Don't write her off just because her first attempt failed.	Multiple common senses: write someone off as..., write off a debt/expense, write off a wrecked car.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: write someone off meaning
546	5453	6224	virility	/vəˈrɪləti/	noun	Traditionally, the quality of masculine strength, vigor, sexual potency, or fertility; more broadly/literarily, robust energy or forcefulness.	masculinity, vigor, potency, manliness (traditional), vitality	The old advertisement absurdly equated owning the car with virility. / The character is obsessed with proving his virility through displays of strength and sexual conquest.	Culturally loaded and often tied to traditional masculinity. Virility does not objectively measure someone's worth or masculinity. In biological/medical contexts it may refer more specifically to male sexual/reproductive capacity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define virility
546	5454	6444	latch onto something	/ˌlætʃ ˈɑːntuː/	phrasal verb	To seize on, become strongly interested in, understand, adopt, or cling to an idea, detail, opportunity, explanation, trend, etc.	seize on, pick up on, grab onto, cling to, fixate on	Fans latched onto one line in the trailer and built an entire theory around it. / She quickly latched onto the main idea. / Don't latch onto the first explanation before checking the evidence.	Existing latch card mentions this sense, but the explicit search merits a productive standalone construction. Spelling both latch on to and latch onto is seen; onto is natural when the particle+preposition function together.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: latching onto meaning
546	5455	6250	homogeneous: pronunciation	/ˌhoʊməˈdʒiːniəs/	pronunciation / usage note	The standard modern pronunciation stresses the third syllable: ho-muh-JEE-nee-us. Variation exists in the first vowel and in careful regional speech, but pronunciations modeled on 'homogenous' are often treated as a separate/contested form rather than a historical replacement of homogeneous.	ho-muh-JEE-nee-us; uniform (meaning cue)	I used to hesitate over homogeneous, but the stress lands on JEE: ho-muh-JEE-nee-us. / Heterogeneous follows the same -geneous pattern: het-er-uh-JEE-nee-us.	Raw search asked whether pronunciation 'has changed.' The safer learning point is current standard stress and the frequent confusion with homogenous. Homogeneous derives from homo- + Greek genos 'kind'; dictionaries record accent/first-vowel variation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pronunciation of homogeneous has changed?
546	5456	5841	I only understand train station	/aɪ ˌoʊnli ˌʌndərˈstænd ˈtreɪn ˌsteɪʃən/	contextual / learner-humor phrase	A deliberately limited-English statement meaning that 'train station' is the only part or phrase the speaker understands.		He laughed and said, 'I only understand train station,' after catching just those two words.	Not a standard idiom. Preserve the exact memorable search wording because its humor/context—not a dictionary headword—is likely why it was bookmarked.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: I only understand train station m
546	5457	6530	prologue	/ˈproʊlɔːɡ/	noun	An introductory section of a literary, dramatic, film, game, or other narrative work that comes before the main story; also an event/action that serves as a prelude to something larger.	preface, introduction, prelude, opening	The game opens with a short prologue set twenty years before the main story. / The political crisis proved to be a prologue to a much larger conflict.	Epilogue comes after the main work. A prologue is part of the work/story structure; a preface is often authorial nonfiction/commentary outside the narrative proper.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0120 | search: prologue meaning
546	5458	5759	grave	/ɡreɪv/	noun / adjective	A place where a dead body is buried; as an adjective, very serious, solemn, or causing serious concern.	tomb; serious, solemn, dire	The doctor gave him a grave look and explained the risks.	Polysemous. Grave danger/concern = very serious; grave expression = solemn. Pronunciation /ɡreɪv/ for these senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: grave meaning
546	5459	6816	make a dent in something	/ˌmeɪk ə ˈdent ɪn/	idiom	To make noticeable progress in reducing, using up, completing, or affecting something, even if much remains.	make headway, make progress, chip away at, reduce	I studied for two hours and finally made a dent in the backlog. / The payment barely made a dent in the debt.	Metaphor from leaving a visible dent in a surface. Common with work, debt, workload, savings, supply, problem.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: make a dent meaning
546	5460	6277	boulder	/ˈboʊldər/	noun	A very large, naturally rounded or irregular rock, generally too large to move easily by hand.	large rock, rock mass, stone	A huge boulder blocked the mountain road. / Sisyphus is commonly pictured pushing a boulder up a hill, though ancient accounts may simply say a massive stone.	Boulder is larger than an ordinary rock/stone in everyday use, though geology uses size classifications more precisely. Also Boulder is a city name, so capitalization matters in proper-name contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: boulder meaning
547	5461	5937	cleavage	/ˈkliːvɪdʒ/	noun	The visible space/line between a person's breasts when emphasized by clothing; more generally, a division or split between groups, ideas, minerals, cells, etc.	décolletage (not exact); division, split, fissure	The dress had a low neckline that showed some cleavage. / The election exposed a deep generational cleavage in the party.	Highly polysemous. Body sense is common everyday usage. Figurative/social-science sense = major division. Geology/mineralogy: tendency to split along planes. Biology: early cell division. Context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: cleavage meaning
547	5462	5804	realm	/relm/	noun	A kingdom; or a field, domain, sphere, or area of activity, knowledge, thought, or possibility.	domain, sphere, field	That question belongs more to the realm of philosophy than science.	Common figurative pattern: in/within the realm of; beyond the realm of possibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: realm meaninig
547	5463	5515	aqueduct	/ˈækwədʌkt/	noun	A structure or channel built to carry water from one place to another, often across a valley or uneven ground.	water channel, conduit	The Roman aqueduct carried fresh water into the city.	Can refer to the whole water-conveyance system, not only the iconic bridge-like structure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: aqueduct meaning
547	5464	6405	dart down the ladder	/ˌdɑːrt daʊn ðə ˈlædər/	verb phrase	To move very quickly and suddenly down a ladder; a compositional phrase using dart for rapid directional movement.	dash down the ladder, scramble down quickly, shoot down	He heard someone coming and darted down the ladder before they reached the roof. / The child darted down the ladder so fast that I told him to slow down.	Not a fixed idiom; preserve because it concretely anchors the motion sense of dart. Compare scamper down stairs (light quick steps) and scramble down (hurried, using hands/feet, often awkward terrain).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: dart down the ladder
547	5465	6325	jerk / jerked	/dʒɜːrk/	verb / noun	To move or pull suddenly with a short sharp motion; to cause something to do so. As a noun, a sudden sharp movement; separately, jerk is a common insult for an obnoxious person.	jolt, yank, twitch, snap, pull sharply	His head jerked toward the door when he heard his name. / She jerked her hand back from the hot pan. / The train started with a sudden jerk.	This card centers the motion verb searched as 'jerked,' distinct from existing insult usage. Compare twitch = smaller often involuntary movement; yank = forceful pull; jerk can be intransitive or transitive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: jerked
547	5466	6800	lived experience	/ˌlɪvd ɪkˈspɪriəns/	noun phrase	Knowledge and perspective grounded in what a person has actually experienced in their own life, especially as distinct from abstract theory, secondhand description, or institutional assumptions.	firsthand experience, personal experience, experiential knowledge	A textbook can explain immigration law, but lived experience shows what navigating the system actually feels like. / Vocabulary divorced from lived experience can remain technically known but hard to use.	Common in social science, advocacy, education, and discussion of identity/institutions. Can be used sincerely or criticized as rhetorical authority; the phrase itself does not make a claim automatically correct.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: lived experience meaning
547	5467	6728	don't hate	/ˌdoʊnt ˈheɪt/	conversational phrase	An informal appeal meaning don't criticize, resent, or judge me/it for this—often playful, defensive, or teasing rather than literally about hatred.	don't judge, don't knock it, don't be a hater	Don't hate—I actually like pineapple on pizza. / Don't hate until you've tried it.	Often appears in playful internet/casual speech. Related 'don't hate the player, hate the game,' but 'don't hate' alone is broader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: don't hate meaning
547	5468	6632	smoking gun	/ˌsmoʊkɪŋ ˈɡʌn/	idiom / noun	A piece of evidence that strongly or conclusively proves someone's responsibility, wrongdoing, or a disputed causal claim.	conclusive evidence, decisive proof, damning evidence	The email wasn't just suspicious; investigators treated it as the smoking gun. / Researchers found no single smoking gun explaining the decline.	Very common metaphor from the image of catching someone with a recently fired gun. Often used negatively: 'there is no smoking gun.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: smoking gun meaning
547	5469	6180	meat and potatoes	/ˌmiːt ən pəˈteɪtoʊz/	idiom / adjective phrase	The basic, essential, substantial, or most practical part of something; as an adjective, plain, traditional, hearty, or no-frills.	basics, core, fundamentals, bread and butter (overlapping), nuts and bolts	Let's skip the intro and get to the meat and potatoes of the argument. / It's a meat-and-potatoes action movie: simple story, solid fights, no pretension.	From a stereotypically plain, filling meal. 'Bread and butter' often means main source of income/core activity; meat and potatoes emphasizes basic substance/fundamentals.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: meat and potatoes meaning
547	5470	5589	run-in (with someone)	/ˈrʌn ɪn/	noun, informal	A brief argument, confrontation, or unpleasant encounter with someone, especially an authority figure or opponent.	clash, altercation, confrontation	He had a run-in with his manager over the schedule.	Usually in the pattern have a run-in with someone. Hyphenated as a noun.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: run-in meaning
548	5471	5795	crash and burn	/ˌkræʃ ən ˈbɜːrn/	idiom, informal	To fail suddenly, dramatically, and completely.	fail spectacularly, flame out	The product launched with huge hype and then crashed and burned.	Can also be literal in accident contexts; figurative use is common for careers, plans, relationships, performances.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: crash and burn
548	5472	6812	shake it off	/ˌʃeɪk ɪt ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb / idiom	To recover from or stop dwelling on a setback, bad feeling, embarrassment, pain, criticism, etc.; literally, remove something by shaking.	brush it off, get over it, shrug it off, recover	The mistake bothered me for an hour, but I eventually shook it off. / The dog shook the water off its coat.	Figurative sense emphasizes regaining composure or not letting something keep affecting you. 'Brush it off' often emphasizes dismissing it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: shake it off meaning
548	5473	6034	easy prey	/ˌiːzi ˈpreɪ/	noun phrase / figurative idiom	A person, animal, or target that is easy to attack, exploit, deceive, manipulate, or defeat.	easy target, sitting duck, soft target, vulnerable target	A sick animal can become easy prey for predators. / Scammers look for people they think will be easy prey. / The inexperienced team looked like easy prey, but they won comfortably.	Literal predator-prey sense and figurative exploitation/competition sense are both common. Sitting duck emphasizes exposed vulnerability; easy target is broader and less predatory.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: easy prey meaning
548	5474	6044	pander / pandering	/ˈpændər/; /ˈpændərɪŋ/	verb / noun	To cater excessively or cynically to someone's desires, prejudices, tastes, or expectations in order to gain approval, support, attention, or advantage.	cater to, play to, indulge, appease, suck up to (person; informal)	The sequel feels like it's pandering to nostalgia instead of telling its own story. / The politician was accused of pandering to the crowd's fears. / Not every bit of fan service is pandering; the word implies compromised sincerity or principle.	Usually pander to an audience/base/tastes/prejudices. Strongly negative: catering becomes pandering when it seems cynical, excessive, manipulative, or beneath one's standards. 'Pander to' is standard; 'pander someone' is not.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: pandering meaning
548	5475	6756	preempt criticism	/priˈempt ˈkrɪtəˌsɪzəm/	verb phrase	To anticipate likely criticism and address, neutralize, acknowledge, or act on it before others raise it.	head off criticism, forestall criticism, anticipate criticism, defuse criticism	The reviewer preempted criticism by acknowledging the obvious counterargument at the start. / The company announced the limitation upfront to preempt criticism.	Existing preempt card gives base sense. This collocation is common in argument/media/PR contexts and does not necessarily mean the criticism is invalid.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: preempting criticism meaning
548	5476	6305	more than just a scuff	/ˌmɔːr ðən ˌdʒʌst ə ˈskʌf/	contextual phrase	A way of saying damage, injury, conflict, or impact is more serious than a superficial scrape or minor mark; 'scuff' is the baseline image of slight surface damage.	more than superficial damage, not just a scrape, more serious than it looks	At first I thought the suitcase had a scuff, but the corner was actually cracked—it was more than just a scuff. / Their argument wasn't a minor scuff in the relationship; it exposed a deeper problem.	Preserves the searched contrast. A scuff is usually a superficial mark caused by rubbing/scraping; figurative extension is creative rather than a fixed idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: more than just a scuff
548	5477	6462	titillation	/ˌtɪtəˈleɪʃən/	noun	Excitement or stimulation, especially of a mildly sexual, sensational, or teasing kind intended to arouse curiosity or pleasure.	stimulation, arousal, teasing excitement, sensationalism	The article seemed designed more for titillation than serious reporting. / The film uses suggestive imagery for cheap titillation.	Often critical, implying shallow/sensational stimulation. From titillate. Can be nonsexual, but sexual/sensational undertones are common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: titillation
548	5478	6427	flippant	/ˈflɪpənt/	adjective	Not showing appropriate seriousness or respect, especially by making casual, glib, or joking remarks about something serious.	glib, frivolous, facetious, dismissive, irreverent	His flippant response made it sound as if he didn't care about the consequences. / I was joking, but I realized afterward that the comment came across as flippant.	Usually negative. Facetious = deliberately humorous about something serious; flippant emphasizes insufficient seriousness/respect. Irreverent can be positively rebellious depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0120 | search: flippant meaning
548	5479	6519	fit the bill	/ˌfɪt ðə ˈbɪl/	idiom	To have exactly or sufficiently the qualities needed for a particular purpose, role, requirement, or description.	meet the requirements, suit the purpose, be just right, qualify	We needed a lightweight app that worked offline, and this one fit the bill. / If you're looking for a short comfort anime, that show fits the bill.	Very common. 'Bill' here historically relates to a written notice/program/description; synchronically learn the idiom as 'meet the need/description.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: It fits the bill.
548	5480	5516	enticement	/ɪnˈtaɪsmənt/	noun	Something that attracts or tempts someone into doing something; the act of enticing.	incentive, lure, temptation	The promise of a bonus was an added enticement to take the job.	Often implies attraction by a reward, pleasure, or appealing prospect.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: enticement meaning
549	5481	6733	pub crawl	/ˈpʌb ˌkrɔːl/	noun	A social outing in which a group visits several pubs/bars in succession, usually having a drink at each or many of them.	bar crawl, pub tour, bar-hopping	We joined a pub crawl that stopped at five pubs. / They were already three stops into the pub crawl when I met them.	Especially BrE/Irish context for pub crawl; bar crawl common in AmE. Alcohol-centered activity; 'crawl' humorously suggests the condition one might be in by the end.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: pub crawl meaning
549	5482	6798	be more vigilant about something	/bi ˌmɔːr ˈvɪdʒələnt əˌbaʊt/	phrase	To pay closer, sustained attention to a possible risk, mistake, threat, or problem and be more careful to detect/prevent it.	be more watchful, be more alert, keep a closer eye on	After finding that mapping error, we became more vigilant about checking WordIDs. / Travelers should be vigilant about their belongings in crowded places.	Existing 'stay vigilant' covers the base adjective; this preserves the productive 'vigilant about' construction and comparative use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: being more vigilant about something
549	5483	6236	piggyback ride	/ˈpɪɡiˌbæk raɪd/	noun phrase	A ride in which one person is carried on another person's back, typically with arms around the carrier's shoulders and legs around their sides/waist.	ride on someone's back, carry piggyback	The kid got tired halfway home, so his dad gave him a piggyback ride. / The anime turns an ordinary piggyback ride into a surprisingly intimate scene.	Also 'give someone a piggyback' (especially BrE) and 'carry someone piggyback.' Figurative piggyback on X = use an existing system, success, signal, idea, etc. as support.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: piggyback ride
549	5484	5548	defamation	/ˌdefəˈmeɪʃən/	noun	The act of damaging someone's reputation by making false statements about them.	slander, libel, character assassination	The actor sued the magazine for defamation.	Umbrella legal concept. Traditionally, libel refers to written/published defamation and slander to spoken defamation, though legal definitions vary by jurisdiction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0114, voca_0117 | search: define defamation
549	5485	5607	distinction	/dɪˈstɪŋkʃən/	noun	A difference or contrast between similar things; also excellence, importance, or a mark of special achievement.	difference, differentiation; excellence	There's an important distinction between being quiet and being shy.	Common pattern: make/draw a distinction between A and B. Other senses include achieve distinction and graduate with distinction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: distinction meaning
549	5486	5521	pervasive	/pərˈveɪsɪv/	adjective	Present throughout a place, system, or situation and difficult to avoid or ignore.	widespread, prevalent, ubiquitous	A pervasive sense of distrust had spread through the organization.	Often used for influences, attitudes, problems, smells, technology, or social conditions that seem to permeate everything.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define pervasive
549	5487	6560	pitiable	/ˈpɪtiəbəl/	adjective	Deserving or arousing pity because of suffering, weakness, inadequacy, or miserable condition; sometimes contemptuously, pathetically poor.	pitiful, pathetic, wretched, lamentable	The abandoned animal was in a pitiable condition. / His attempt at an excuse was almost pitiable. / She felt compassion rather than contempt for his pitiable situation.	Pitiable focuses on deserving/arousing pity. Pitiful overlaps but can more readily mean contemptibly inadequate ('a pitiful excuse').		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: pitiable
549	5488	5632	feral	/ˈferəl/	adjective	Living in a wild state after domestication, or resembling a wild animal in behavior; informally, extremely unruly, chaotic, or unrestrained.	wild, untamed, savage	A colony of feral cats lives near the old warehouse.	Modern informal use can humorously describe people, children, behavior, or energy as wildly uncontrolled.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111, voca_0114 | search: feral meaning
549	5489	5691	market something to someone	/ˈmɑːrkɪt/	verb pattern	To promote, position, or sell a product, service, or idea to a particular audience.	promote to, advertise to, target	The company markets the app to college students.	Pattern highlights the target audience: market X to Y.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: market something to someone
549	5490	6282	rim job / rimming	/ˈrɪm dʒɑːb/; /ˈrɪmɪŋ/	noun, explicit sexual slang	Oral stimulation of the anus; 'rimming' is the activity and 'rim job' is an informal/vulgar noun phrase for an instance of it.	anilingus (clinical/formal), rimming	The term appeared in explicit internet slang; 'anilingus' is the more clinical term. / 'Rimming' is common informal sexual vocabulary, while 'rim job' can sound cruder.	Explicit sexual slang. Hygiene and STI transmission can be relevant in real-world sexual-health contexts. Lexically, distinguish from ordinary 'rim' = outer edge of something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: rim job
550	5491	5704	inclement	/ɪnˈklemənt/	adjective	Severe, harsh, stormy, or unpleasant, especially when describing weather.	harsh, severe, stormy	Flights were delayed because of inclement conditions.	Most strongly associated with weather. Opposite root sense appears in clement = mild/merciful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define inclement
550	5492	5698	whether A or B	/ˈweðər/	grammar pattern	Used to introduce two or more alternatives, often when the statement applies regardless of which alternative is true or chosen.	whether X or Y	We need a decision whether we go today or tomorrow.	Can introduce an indirect choice/question or a regardless-of-which contrast: whether A or B, the result is the same.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: whether a or b meaning
550	5493	5896	tramp	/træmp/	noun / verb	Traditionally, a person who travels on foot from place to place and may be homeless; also a heavy walking step or a long walk. As an insult, especially for a woman, it can mean sexually promiscuous and is dated/derogatory.	vagrant, drifter; trek; slut (derogatory sense)	The old story follows a tramp wandering from town to town. / We tramped through the mud for hours. / Calling a woman a 'tramp' in the sexual sense is insulting and dated.	Important for titles such as Lady and the Tramp: Tramp is the male stray dog's name, drawing on the roaming/drifter sense—not the sexual insult. Compare bum, hobo, vagrant: overlapping but culturally/register-specific.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define tramp
550	5494	5715	ingest	/ɪnˈdʒest/	verb	To take food, drink, medicine, or another substance into the body, especially by swallowing; more broadly, for a system to take in data or material.	consume, swallow, take in	The tablets should not be ingested on an empty stomach.	Technical computing use: ingest data = import/take data into a system for processing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define ingested
550	5495	6472	thrice	/θraɪs/	adverb, archaic/literary	Three times; on three occasions; to three times the degree/amount.	three times	He knocked thrice on the old wooden door. / The prophecy says the bell will ring thrice before dawn.	Archaic/literary in ordinary modern English; 'three times' is normal conversation. Common in fantasy, scripture-like prose, jokes, and deliberately elevated language.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: Thrice
550	5496	6829	onomatopoeically	/ˌɑːnəˌmætəˈpiːɪkli/	adverb	In a way that imitates, evokes, or is formed from a sound associated with what is being named or described.	by sound imitation, mimetically	The comic represents the impact onomatopoeically with a huge 'BANG.' / The bird was named onomatopoeically after its call.	Derived from onomatopoeic. Rare/formal-looking adverb; often clearer to rewrite as 'using onomatopoeia' in ordinary prose.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: ONOMATOPOEICALLY definition | Cambridge English Dictionary
550	5497	6179	atelier	/ˌætəlˈjeɪ/	noun	An artist's, designer's, or craftsperson's studio or workshop, especially one associated with fine art, fashion, architecture, or traditional craft.	studio, workshop, design studio	She trained in a small painting atelier before opening her own studio. / The fashion house invited journalists into its Paris atelier.	Borrowed from French and can sound cultured/art-world/fashion-specific compared with ordinary studio/workshop. Historically an atelier may include a master and apprentices.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: atelier meaning
550	5498	6919	self-sealing argument	/ˌself ˈsiːlɪŋ ˈɑːrɡjəmənt/	epistemology / argumentation	An argument structured so that objections or counterevidence are absorbed, dismissed, or reinterpreted as further confirmation, making the position difficult or impossible to falsify from within its own logic.	unfalsifiable argument, self-immunizing argument, closed belief system	'Anyone who denies the conspiracy is part of it' makes the claim self-sealing. / A self-sealing argument protects itself by redefining disagreement as evidence.	Specific application of self-sealing. Not every robust theory is self-sealing: the key issue is whether genuine counterevidence is allowed to count against the claim.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: self-sealing argument
550	5499	6643	bait-and-switch	/ˌbeɪt ən ˈswɪtʃ/	noun / adjective	A deceptive tactic in which an attractive offer, product, premise, or expectation draws someone in, then is replaced by something worse/different; also used broadly for misleading changes after commitment.	deceptive substitution, switcheroo, false advertising tactic	The cheap advertised laptop was unavailable, and the salesperson pushed a pricier model—a classic bait-and-switch. / Viewers complained that the trailer was a bait-and-switch about the story's real focus.	Originally/legally associated with deceptive sales practices, but everyday figurative use is broader. Not every disappointing change is legally a bait-and-switch.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: bait-and-switch
550	5500	6625	contend with	/kənˈtend wɪð/	phrasal/prepositional verb	To deal with, struggle against, or cope with a difficult problem, force, person, or situation.	deal with, face, grapple with, cope with, battle	Small businesses must contend with rising costs. / The hikers had to contend with wind, rain, and poor visibility.	Distinct from contend that = assert/argue that. 'Contend for' can mean compete for.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: contend with meaning
551	5501	6288	out of your gourd	/ˌaʊt əv jər ˈɡɔːrd/	idiom, informal AmE	Crazy, irrational, wildly mistaken, or extremely intoxicated, depending on context; gourd humorously stands for one's head.	out of your mind, nuts, crazy, wasted (contextual)	You paid how much for that? You're out of your gourd. / He was so sleep-deprived he sounded completely out of his gourd.	Playful/old-fashioned-sounding American idiom. Can refer to insanity metaphorically or intoxication. 'Gourd' literally is a hard-shelled fruit such as a squash/pumpkin relative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: out of your gourd meaning
551	5502	5525	intact	/ɪnˈtækt/	adjective	Whole and undamaged; still complete, with no essential part missing or destroyed.	undamaged, whole, unbroken	The package was crushed, but the contents were still intact.	Often used for objects, structures, systems, traditions, reputations, and abstract things that survive unchanged.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: intact
551	5503	5886	headline alchemy	/ˈhedˌlaɪn ˈælkəmi/	figurative / media phrase	The skill or process of transforming ordinary, messy, or ambiguous material into a compelling headline, often by reframing, simplifying, or sensationalizing it.	headline magic, framing, headline engineering, clickbait craft (critical)	Through a bit of headline alchemy, a cautious interview answer became a dramatic culture-war story. / Good headline alchemy can make a dry subject instantly clickable without misrepresenting it.	Not a fixed dictionary idiom. Alchemy figuratively suggests an almost magical transformation of base material into something more valuable or striking; tone may be admiring or skeptical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: headline alchemy
551	5504	6049	self-mythologize	/ˌself mɪˈθɑːlədʒaɪz/	verb	To construct and promote a larger-than-life, simplified, heroic, tragic, or otherwise compelling story about oneself and one's past.	build one's own legend, cultivate a persona, romanticize one's own story, self-aggrandize (often harsher)	He constantly self-mythologizes as a lone outsider who succeeded without anyone's help. / The memoir is compelling, but you can feel the author self-mythologizing in places.	Often critical: implies selective storytelling that turns biography into personal legend. Self-aggrandize focuses on increasing one's importance/status; self-mythologize focuses on narrative/persona.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: self-mythologize
551	5505	6658	get a feel for something	/ˌɡet ə ˈfiːl fər/	idiom	To gradually develop an intuitive sense, familiarity, understanding, or practical grasp of how something works or what it is like.	get the hang of, become familiar with, develop a sense of	After a week, I started to get a feel for the neighborhood. / You need to hear lots of examples to get a feel for when the phrase sounds natural.	Emphasizes intuitive/practical understanding rather than explicit rules. 'Get the hang of' often focuses more on ability/skill.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: get a feel for
551	5506	6244	sarcophagus	/sɑːrˈkɑːfəɡəs/	noun	A stone coffin, often large, decorated, or monumental, especially one associated with ancient civilizations and burial chambers.	stone coffin, burial chest, tomb coffin	The museum displayed an elaborately carved Egyptian sarcophagus. / In the game, opening the sarcophagus obviously triggers the boss fight.	Plural sarcophagi /sɑːrˈkɑːfəɡaɪ/ or sarcophaguses. Not every ancient Egyptian coffin is technically a sarcophagus; the term especially suggests an outer stone container.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sarcophagus meaning
551	5507	5633	Schenectady	/skəˈnektədi/	proper noun	A city in eastern New York State, northwest of Albany.		The train stopped in Schenectady before continuing toward Albany. / Schenectady is part of New York's Capital Region. / The spelling looks intimidating, but the pronunciation is roughly skuh-NEK-tuh-dee.	Pronunciation target from the original search: roughly skuh-NEK-tuh-dee. Schenectady is a city in eastern New York State, northwest of Albany, historically associated with General Electric. This is mainly a proper-name pronunciation/memory card, so synonyms are not applicable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: schenectady pronunciation
551	5508	6359	rebuke	/rɪˈbjuːk/	verb / noun	To express sharp disapproval of someone's behavior or words; a strong verbal correction or criticism.	reprimand, admonish, scold, reprove, censure	The judge rebuked the lawyer for interrupting repeatedly. / She gave him a sharp rebuke for mocking the new employee.	Stronger/more formal than scold. Rebuke emphasizes direct expression of disapproval. Compare reprimand = official/formal correction; admonish can be warning/counsel as well as criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define rebuke
551	5509	6283	find something off Reddit	/ˌfaɪnd ˌsʌmθɪŋ ɔːf ˈredɪt/	informal source construction	To find or obtain something from Reddit. In casual English, 'off Reddit' can mean 'from/on Reddit as the source,' similar to 'I got it off YouTube/the internet.'	find on Reddit, get from Reddit, come across on Reddit	I found this off Reddit last night. / More neutrally, I'd probably say, 'I found this on Reddit.' / She got the image off a forum and didn't know the original source.	'On Reddit' is the most straightforward location wording; 'off Reddit' is informal and emphasizes source/acquisition ('got it off Reddit'). Not the same as 'off of Reddit' meaning away from the platform in every context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: found this off reddit meaing
551	5510	6808	jest / jesting	/dʒest/	noun / verb	A joke or playful remark; to speak or act jokingly rather than seriously, often with a slightly literary or old-fashioned flavor.	joke, quip, banter; joke, tease	'Surely you jest,' she said. / They were only jesting, but the comment still landed badly.	Jest is less common in ordinary modern conversation than joke/kid and can sound literary, theatrical, or deliberately playful. 'In jest' = as a joke, not seriously.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: define jesting
552	5511	5578	overwrought	/ˌoʊvərˈrɔːt/	adjective	Too emotional, agitated, elaborate, or excessively worked-up in style or expression.	overemotional, overexcited, overblown	The final speech felt overwrought compared with the rest of the film.	Can describe a person emotionally, or writing/art that feels overdone and excessively elaborate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: overwrought
552	5512	6927	canonical	/kəˈnɑːnɪkəl/	adjective	Accepted as authoritative, standard, officially recognized, or belonging to an established canon; in technical contexts, selected as a standard/normalized representative form.	authoritative, standard, official, orthodox, normalized (technical)	That novel is part of the canonical Sherlock Holmes stories. / The database stores one canonical form for each entry.	Sense depends on domain: religion/literature/media canon, mathematics/computing canonical form, data systems canonical record. 'Canonical' does not simply mean popular.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: canonical meaning
552	5513	6689	ledge: synonyms and near-synonyms	/ledʒ/	vocabulary contrast	Depending on context, a ledge may be a shelf, projection, sill, lip, rim, edge, or narrow shelf-like rock formation; these are not interchangeable in every setting.	shelf, projection, sill, lip, rim, edge	The bird perched on a rock ledge. / Put the plant on the window ledge—or windowsill. / A tiny lip around the tray isn't normally called a ledge.	Search-intent card: choose by geometry/context. Shelf often supports objects; sill is architectural; lip/rim = raised/bounding edge; ledge projects horizontally from a surface/cliff.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ledge synonym
552	5514	5960	sterility	/stəˈrɪləti/	noun	The state of being sterile: free of viable microorganisms; unable to reproduce; or figuratively lacking creativity, warmth, vitality, or productive results.	sterileness, infertility, barrenness; lifelessness (figurative)	The instruments must be checked for sterility before surgery. / The treatment can affect fertility and, in some cases, cause sterility. / Critics complained about the emotional sterility of the design.	Three distinct senses matter: microbiological cleanliness, reproductive inability, figurative barrenness. Sterility is stronger/more absolute-sounding than infertility in reproductive contexts, although real medical usage depends on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: sterility
552	5515	6899	gland	/ɡlænd/	noun, anatomy/biology	An organ or group of specialized cells that produces and releases substances such as hormones, enzymes, sweat, saliva, or other secretions.	secretory organ, endocrine/exocrine gland	The thyroid is an endocrine gland that releases hormones into the bloodstream. / Sweat glands release sweat through ducts to the skin.	Endocrine glands secrete hormones into blood/interstitial fluid; exocrine glands release through ducts or onto surfaces. 'Swollen glands' often colloquially refers to swollen lymph nodes, which are not glands in the strict secretory sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define gland
552	5516	5968	sludge	/slʌdʒ/	noun	Thick, wet, muddy, semi-solid material, especially industrial/sewage residue; figuratively, something dense, unpleasant, low-quality, or difficult to move through.	muck, slurry, slime, sediment; dreck (figurative)	Workers removed toxic sludge from the bottom of the tank. / The snow at the curb had turned into gray sludge. / I had to wade through pages of corporate sludge to find the actual announcement.	Slurry is a flowable mixture of solids and liquid, often technical; sludge tends to sound dirtier/thicker and is common in sewage/industrial contexts. Figurative sludge is vivid but informal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: sludge
552	5517	6945	universal shorthand	/ˌjuːnəˈvɜːrsəl ˈʃɔːrtˌhænd/	noun phrase	A symbol, phrase, image, trope, or convention assumed to communicate a complex idea immediately across a very broad audience; often rhetorical rather than literally universal.	widely recognized shorthand, common cultural symbol, shared shorthand	A red heart is often treated as universal shorthand for love, though symbols are never truly culture-free. / The montage became visual shorthand for training and progress.	Useful critical nuance: 'universal' often means widely legible within the speaker's assumed audience, not genuinely understood identically by every culture/person.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: universal shorthand
552	5518	6175	drawbridge	/ˈdrɔːˌbrɪdʒ/	noun	A bridge that can be raised, lowered, or moved to permit or block passage, especially the classic hinged bridge across a castle moat.	movable bridge, bascule bridge (specific modern type)	The guards raised the drawbridge as the riders approached the castle. / Modern drawbridges may open to let boats pass beneath them.	Castle image is common, but drawbridge can also refer to modern movable bridges. A bascule bridge uses a counterweighted lifting design; not every movable bridge is technically the same type.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: drawbridge
552	5519	6293	diffuse	/dɪˈfjuːz/ (verb); /dɪˈfjuːs/ (adj.)	verb / adjective	Verb: to spread or cause something to spread widely through an area or among people. Adjective: spread out, not concentrated, or lacking a clear focus/structure.	spread, disperse, disseminate; scattered, unfocused, diffuse	The scent diffused through the room. / Social media can diffuse an idea rapidly across communities. / The essay has interesting observations, but its argument feels diffuse.	Key confusable: diffuse vs defuse. Diffuse has two pronunciations: verb ends /z/; adjective ends /s/. It concerns spreading/scattering, not removing danger from a bomb or conflict.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: diffuse meaing
552	5520	6534	repentant	/rɪˈpentənt/	adjective	Feeling or showing sincere regret, remorse, and a desire to change after wrongdoing.	remorseful, contrite, penitent, sorry, apologetic	He sounded genuinely repentant rather than merely embarrassed that he'd been caught. / The story gives the villain a repentant final scene.	Stronger/more moral than apologetic: repentant suggests regret for wrongdoing and often a wish to reform. Opposite: unrepentant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: repentant meaning
553	5521	6810	tween	/twiːn/	noun, informal	A child in the transitional years between childhood and adolescence, commonly around ages 9–12 or 10–12 depending on context.	preteen, preadolescent	The show is aimed at tweens rather than very young children. / Tween fashion sits between kids' and teen styles.	Shortened from 'between.' Age boundaries are approximate, not official.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: tween meaning
553	5522	6568	take the liberty of doing something	/ˌteɪk ðə ˈlɪbərti əv/	idiom	To do something without first asking permission, often because you believe it will be acceptable/helpful; frequently used as a polite acknowledgment that you acted on your own initiative.	take it upon oneself, presume to, go ahead and	I took the liberty of fixing a few formatting errors. / I've taken the liberty of booking us a table. / He took the liberty of inviting two more people.	Polite/formal formula, but depending on context it can signal presumptuousness. Common: 'I took the liberty of + -ing.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: take the liberty
553	5523	6839	irrespective of	/ˌɪrɪˈspektɪv əv/	preposition phrase, formal	Without being affected or limited by the thing mentioned; regardless of.	regardless of, no matter, independent of	Everyone can apply irrespective of age. / The rule should be enforced irrespective of who made the mistake.	Standard construction is 'irrespective of.' 'Irregardless' is nonstandard/controversial though recorded in usage; 'regardless of' is the everyday alternative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: irrespective meaning
553	5524	6934	satirize	/ˈsætəˌraɪz/	verb	To criticize, expose, or ridicule a person, institution, behavior, or idea through satire—using humor, irony, exaggeration, parody, etc.	mock, lampoon, parody, ridicule, spoof	The show satirizes corporate jargon and office politics. / The novel satirized the social pretensions of its era.	Satire usually has a critical target/purpose; not every joke or parody is satire. Raw 'satirizes' normalized to base form.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define satirizes
553	5525	6074	seal of disapproval	/ˌsiːl əv ˌdɪsəˈpruːvəl/	playful phrase / wordplay	A humorous reversal of 'seal of approval,' meaning a figurative mark or clear signal that someone disapproves.	thumbs-down, mark of disapproval, vote of no confidence (stronger/formal)	His one-star review was basically a seal of disapproval. / The cat took one sniff, walked away, and gave dinner its seal of disapproval.	Not a standard formal idiom; productive wordplay on 'seal of approval.' Often humorous because it treats rejection as though it were officially certified.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: seal of disapproval meaning
553	5526	5654	unalive	/ˌʌnəˈlaɪv/	adjective / verb-like internet euphemism	An internet euphemism for dead, killed, or die/kill, often used to avoid platform moderation or soften direct wording.	dead; kill (euphemistic)	The creator used 'unalive' instead of 'dead' in the caption.	Strongly associated with social-media euphemistic language. It can sound jocular, evasive, or insensitive outside that context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: u n a l i v e
553	5527	5813	blow something out of proportion	/ˌbloʊ ˌsʌmθɪŋ aʊt əv prəˈpɔːrʃən/	idiom	To make a problem, event, or issue seem much more important, serious, or extreme than it really is.	exaggerate, overstate, make too much of	It was a minor mistake, but the story blew it out of proportion.	Can be active or passive: blow X out of proportion / X was blown out of proportion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: blow out of proportion meanig
553	5528	6944	preempt / preempting	/priˈempt/	verb	To act before someone/something else in order to prevent, forestall, replace, or gain advantage over it; also to interrupt/displace scheduled programming or another action.	forestall, head off, anticipate, prevent, supersede	She addressed the obvious objection early, preempting the audience's question. / The network preempted the show for breaking news.	Existing base preempt card covers criticism. This card preserves productive 'preempting' in communication: anticipate and answer before it arises. Not identical to merely predict.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: preemptingmeaning
553	5529	6484	complicity	/kəmˈplɪsəti/	noun	Involvement in, contribution to, or shared responsibility for wrongdoing, especially by assisting, enabling, supporting, or knowingly going along with it.	involvement, collusion, participation, connivance, shared responsibility	The investigation examined whether senior officials had any complicity in the cover-up. / Silence can be described as complicity when it knowingly enables abuse.	Strong moral/legal word. Mere proximity or awareness does not automatically establish complicity; the relevant degree of knowledge, assistance, intent, or responsibility depends on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: complicity meaning
553	5530	6094	aquarium	/əˈkweriəm/	noun	A transparent tank or container for keeping aquatic animals/plants; also a public facility where aquatic organisms are displayed.	fish tank, marine exhibit, public aquarium	We spent most of the afternoon at the aquarium watching the jellyfish. / The apartment has a small freshwater aquarium with live plants.	Plural aquariums or aquaria. A fish tank is usually the container; an aquarium can mean either the tank or the public institution. Raw 'acquarium' corrected.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: acquarium meaning
554	5531	6497	urna	/ˈɜːrnə/	noun / proper-name element, context-dependent	A Latin/Italian-derived form meaning an urn, jar, vessel, ballot box, or similar container; in English it is uncommon outside names, technical/classical contexts, or borrowings.	urn, vessel, jar, ballot box (contextual)	In Latin-derived terminology, urna can refer to an urn or vessel. / If 'Urna' appears capitalized in a name or title, context may matter more than the dictionary sense.	Not an ordinary everyday English headword. Preserve because the raw search explicitly targeted it; likely encountered as a borrowed/classical/name element. Do not automatically replace every Urna with English 'urn.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: urna meaning
554	5532	6299	go in on someone	/ˌɡoʊ ˈɪn ɑːn/	phrasal verb, informal	To attack, criticize, mock, question, or pressure someone intensely and persistently; in some contexts, to engage aggressively with an opponent.	lay into, tear into, go after, come down on, roast (contextual)	The hosts really went in on him for that terrible take. / Once the comments noticed the mistake, everyone started going in on the creator. / The defender went in hard on the tackle, a related but different construction.	Compare go off on someone = suddenly explode angrily; go in on someone often emphasizes sustained/intense attack or criticism. Also 'go in on something with someone' can mean jointly contribute money—different construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: going in on someone
554	5533	5579	carry on something	/ˌkæri ˈɑːn/	phrasal verb	To continue an activity, process, tradition, discussion, or piece of work.	continue, keep up, proceed with	Let's carry on this discussion tomorrow.	Also intransitive: carry on = continue. Another informal sense is to behave in an excited or uncontrolled way; context distinguishes them.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: carry on something meangn
554	5534	5630	pore over something	/ˌpɔːr ˈoʊvər/	phrasal verb	To study, read, or examine something very carefully and attentively.	study closely, scrutinize	She pored over the contract before signing it.	Common spelling trap: pore over, not pour over, when the meaning is examine carefully. Pour over is literal pouring, as in pour water over something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: poured over meaning
554	5535	6541	flirt / flirt with someone	/flɜːrt/	verb / noun	To behave playfully or suggestively in a way that signals romantic or sexual interest; also figuratively, to toy with an idea, danger, or possibility without fully committing.	tease, make advances, come on to; toy with, entertain	They were obviously flirting at the party. / He flirted with the idea of moving abroad but never made a plan. / She wasn't sure whether he was being friendly or flirting.	Flirting ranges from subtle banter to overt signaling and is highly context/culture dependent. Figurative 'flirt with danger/disaster/an idea' is common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: flirted meaning
554	5536	6400	dart / darting	/dɑːrt/	verb / noun	To move suddenly and quickly in a particular direction; as a noun, a small pointed missile thrown by hand or a quick sudden movement.	dash, shoot, zip, bolt, flit	The cat darted under the sofa. / His eyes darted toward the door when he heard footsteps. / A lizard darted across the path. / She threw a dart at the board.	Movement sense is highly useful: eyes glance rapidly; people/animals move quickly. Compare scamper = run quickly with light small steps; dart emphasizes sudden directional speed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define darting
554	5537	6738	tamper with someone	/ˈtæmpər wɪð ˌsʌmwʌn/	rare/context-sensitive phrase	Tamper normally takes a thing, evidence, system, witness process, etc. With a person, the intended meaning may be improper influence/manipulation—especially 'tamper with a witness'—but ordinary English usually chooses manipulate, interfere with, mess with, or pressure depending on context.	manipulate, interfere with, pressure; tamper with a witness	The defendant was accused of tampering with a witness. / For ordinary interpersonal manipulation, 'he tampered with her' sounds unusual without special context.	Do not generalize object-style tamper to people. Legal phrase witness tampering / tamper with a witness is established; other person objects require context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: tamper with someone meaning
554	5538	6781	leave at the last possible moment	/ˌliːv ət ðə ˌlæst ˈpɑːsəbəl ˌmoʊmənt/	conversational phrase	To delay departure until the latest time one believes will still allow arrival or completion, leaving little or no buffer for delays.	leave at the last minute, cut it fine, leave no margin for error	I always leave at the last possible moment, so one delayed train can ruin everything. / He cuts it fine every morning and reaches the gate just before boarding closes.	'At the last minute' is broader; 'last possible moment' emphasizes deliberately using nearly all available buffer. BrE 'cut it fine' is a useful near-equivalent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: always leave at the last possible moment meaning
554	5539	5981	bicker / bickering	/ˈbɪkər/; /ˈbɪkərɪŋ/	verb / noun	To argue repeatedly about small, petty, or relatively unimportant things, often in an irritated back-and-forth way.	squabble, quarrel, wrangle, snipe at each other	The siblings bickered over who got the front seat. / I like their chemistry—the constant bickering feels more affectionate than genuinely hostile.	Not specifically slang. Bickering often sounds smaller/pettier than arguing or fighting. Squabble is close; banter is playful and need not involve genuine irritation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: bickering meaning slang
554	5540	5593	bedhead	/ˈbedˌhed/	noun, informal	Messy, flattened, or tousled hair caused by sleeping.	messy morning hair, sleep-mussed hair, tousled hair	I woke up with terrible bedhead and had no time to fix it. / His bedhead was sticking up in every direction. / I joined the video call with full-on bedhead.	Also styled 'bed head.' Usually humorous/informal and refers to the messy hairstyle caused by sleeping, not the person. 'Tousled' can be deliberately stylish; bedhead specifically evokes having just gotten out of bed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: bedhead meaning
555	5541	5758	too dead to care	/ˌtuː ˌded tə ˈker/	darkly humorous phrase	So dead—or figuratively so exhausted, numb, detached, or beyond concern—that caring is impossible.	beyond caring, too exhausted to care	After the all-nighter, I joked that I was too dead to care.	Not a fixed idiom. Preserve the searched phrase because its hyperbolic/dark-humor force is the point.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: too dead to care meaning
555	5542	6140	stray away / stray from	/ˌstreɪ əˈweɪ/; /ˈstreɪ frəm/	verb phrase	To move or drift away from a place, group, path, subject, rule, or expected course. 'Stray from' is usually more idiomatic for departing from a path, topic, plan, or norm.	wander away, drift away, deviate from, depart from	Try not to stray from the marked trail. / The adaptation strays pretty far from the original novel. / One sheep strayed away from the rest of the flock.	For abstract departures, prefer stray from: stray from the topic/plan/path/tradition. 'Stray away' is possible, especially physical movement, but often sounds less idiomatic than wander away or stray from.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: stray away meaning
555	5543	5764	inhabitant	/ɪnˈhæbɪtənt/	noun	A person or animal that lives in or occupies a particular place.	resident, occupant, dweller, denizen	The island has fewer than a thousand permanent inhabitants. / The documentary looks at the forest and its animal inhabitants. / In the fantasy novel, the cave's inhabitants rarely come to the surface.	Used for people, animals, fictional beings, or populations occupying a place. 'Resident' is the most neutral for people; 'dweller' often appears in compounds (city-dweller); 'denizen' can sound literary and often means a characteristic inhabitant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define inhabitants
555	5544	5643	backtrack	/ˈbækˌtræk/	verb	To go back along the same route; figuratively, to reverse or modify a previous statement, decision, or position.	retrace, reverse course, walk back	The company backtracked after customers objected to the change.	Often used for retreating from a public claim or policy.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0118 | search: backtrack meaning
555	5545	6068	feel crummy	/ˌfiːl ˈkrʌmi/	informal phrase	To feel physically unwell, emotionally bad, guilty, low, or generally lousy.	feel lousy, feel awful, feel rough, feel under the weather (physical), feel bad	I slept badly and feel pretty crummy today. / I felt crummy about canceling at the last minute, even though I had a good reason.	Crummy is informal and mild-to-moderate. Physical 'feel crummy' is broader than under the weather; emotional use can mean guilty/down. Crummy can also describe poor-quality things: a crummy hotel.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: feel crummy meaning
555	5546	5992	epistemically	/ɪˌpɪstəˈmɪkli/	adverb, philosophy / academic	In a way relating to knowledge, justification, evidence, belief, or what one is rationally entitled to know/believe.	with respect to knowledge/evidence, evidentially (contextual), epistemologically (not exact)	The two claims may be morally similar but epistemically very different: one is supported by direct evidence and the other is speculation. / We should stay epistemically humble when the available evidence is weak.	Epistemic = relating to knowledge/justified belief. Epistemically justified = justified as a belief. Epistemological often describes the theory/study of knowledge itself, so the adverbs are not always interchangeable. Common phrase: epistemically irresponsible/humble/justified.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: what does epistemically means
555	5547	5750	wrinkle (complication)	/ˈrɪŋkəl/	noun, figurative	An unexpected complication, variation, subtlety, or new feature that makes a situation or idea less straightforward.	complication, twist, wrinkle	The new rule adds an interesting wrinkle to the debate.	Distinct from a skin/fabric wrinkle. Common review/analysis phrase: an interesting/new wrinkle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: interesting wrinkles meaning
555	5548	5903	draining	/ˈdreɪnɪŋ/	adjective / gerund	Causing a strong loss of energy, patience, emotion, money, or other resources; literally, removing liquid from something.	exhausting, tiring, taxing, depleting, wearing	That conversation was emotionally draining. / Long meetings are surprisingly draining when you're concentrating in a second language. / The plumber is draining the tank before replacing the valve.	As adjective, emotionally/mentally/physically draining are very common. Compare exhausting (often stronger physical/general fatigue) and taxing (demanding effort/concentration).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: draining
555	5549	5806	sound like a broken record	/ˌsaʊnd laɪk ə ˌbroʊkən ˈrekərd/	idiom	To repeat the same point, complaint, advice, or statement over and over.	repeat oneself, harp on	I know I sound like a broken record, but please back up your files.	From a damaged vinyl record repeatedly playing the same groove.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: sound like a broken record meaning
555	5550	5721	nominal	/ˈnɑːmənəl/	adjective	Existing in name only or very small in amount; also relating to a name, noun, stated face value, or unadjusted monetary amount depending on field.	in-name-only, token, stated	He is the nominal head of the organization, but someone else makes the decisions.	Common senses: nominal leader, nominal fee, nominal value/GDP. Meaning varies substantially by domain.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define nominal
556	5551	6386	My English could still use a lot of work.	/maɪ ˈɪŋɡlɪʃ kʊd stɪl ˌjuːz ə ˈlɑːt əv wɜːrk/	natural correction / sentence pattern	A natural way to say that one's English still needs substantial improvement. 'Could use some/a lot of work' means would benefit from improvement, repair, practice, or attention.	My English still needs a lot of work; I still have a lot to improve in my English	My English could still use a lot of work, especially when I have to explain something spontaneously. / The pronunciation is pretty good, but the rhythm could use some work.	Corrects raw 'My English still use a lot of work.' The subject takes 'could use,' not 'still use.' This construction is softer and more idiomatic than saying something is simply bad.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: My English still use a lot of work meaning
556	5552	5728	overturn	/ˌoʊvərˈtɜːrn/	verb	To turn something over; or to reverse, invalidate, or abolish a decision, law, judgment, or established situation.	reverse, invalidate, topple, upset	The appeals court overturned the earlier ruling.	Useful synonyms depend on sense: reverse/strike down a ruling; topple a government; tip over a vehicle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: overturn synonym
556	5553	5843	take a pass	/ˌteɪk ə ˈpæs/	idiom, informal	To decline an offer, opportunity, activity, or question; to choose not to participate.	pass, decline, sit this one out	I'm taking a pass here—I don't know enough to comment responsibly.	Often softer/more casual than refuse. 'I'll pass' is even more common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: I’m taking a pass here
556	5554	6320	show someone into a room	/ˌʃoʊ ˌsʌmwʌn ˈɪntuː ə ruːm/	verb construction	To politely lead, guide, or direct someone into a room, especially as a host, receptionist, servant, or staff member.	usher in, escort in, lead in, see someone in	The receptionist showed me into a small meeting room. / Please show our guests into the lounge while I finish setting the table.	Here show = guide/escort, not display. Related: show someone out = escort them to the exit; show someone around = give a tour; show someone to their seat/room.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: show someone into a room meaning
556	5555	6004	ocular migraine	/ˈɑːkjələr ˈmaɪɡreɪn/	noun, informal/medical usage	A loosely used term for migraine-related visual symptoms. It may refer to migraine with visual aura, while 'retinal migraine' is a rarer condition involving temporary visual disturbance in one eye.	visual migraine, migraine with visual aura (often intended), retinal migraine (not always equivalent)	She described flashing zigzags before the headache and called it an ocular migraine. / Because 'ocular migraine' is used inconsistently, a clinician may ask whether the visual symptoms affected one eye or both.	Terminology is imprecise in everyday use. Migraine aura often produces visual phenomena perceived in both eyes/visual fields; retinal migraine is rare and monocular. New or sudden vision loss needs medical assessment rather than assuming migraine.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: ocular migraine
556	5556	5815	larva vs lava	/ˈlɑːrvə/; /ˈlɑːvə/	pronunciation/meaning contrast	Larva is an immature stage of an animal such as an insect; lava is molten rock that reaches Earth's surface.		A caterpillar is a larva; molten lava flowed from the volcano.	Useful near-sound contrast. Plural of larva is larvae /ˈlɑːrviː/ (also larvas in some nontechnical use).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: larva vs lava
556	5557	6425	implode	/ɪmˈploʊd/	verb	To collapse violently inward; figuratively, for an organization, relationship, plan, career, argument, etc. to collapse or destroy itself from internal pressure/problems.	collapse inward, cave in; collapse, unravel, self-destruct	The damaged container imploded under external pressure. / The team imploded after weeks of internal conflict. / His argument imploded once the contradictory evidence appeared.	Opposite image of explode, though real physical usage is more specific than simply 'collapse.' Figurative implode strongly suggests internal failure rather than destruction imposed entirely from outside.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: implode
556	5558	6356	agapanthus	/ˌæɡəˈpænθəs/	noun	A flowering plant genus native to southern Africa, known for tall stems topped with rounded clusters of blue, purple, or white trumpet-shaped flowers; commonly called lily of the Nile or African lily.	lily of the Nile, African lily	Agapanthus lined the walkway with large blue flower clusters. / Despite the common name 'lily of the Nile,' agapanthus is not a true lily and is native to southern Africa.	Botanical genus Agapanthus. Common names can mislead geographically/taxonomically. The name derives from Greek elements meaning love + flower.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define agapanthus
556	5559	6231	I'm fuming	/aɪm ˈfjuːmɪŋ/	informal expression	I'm extremely angry or furious, often with the image of anger so intense that one is metaphorically giving off smoke or fumes.	I'm furious, I'm livid, I'm seething, I'm pissed off	I'm absolutely fuming—they cancelled it after we'd already traveled two hours. / He looked calm in the meeting, but afterward he told me, 'I'm fuming.'	Very common especially in BrE, but understood broadly. Fuming usually means intense anger, stronger than annoyed. 'Seething' often suggests anger being contained rather than openly expressed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: i'm fuming meaning
556	5560	6875	aloof	/əˈluːf/	adjective	Emotionally or socially distant, reserved, detached, or unwilling to become friendly/involved; sometimes seeming cold or superior.	distant, reserved, detached, standoffish, unapproachable	He seemed aloof at first, but he was actually just shy. / Her aloof manner made the group think she disliked them.	Aloof describes outward social distance; it does not tell you the cause. Can be intentional, shy, self-protective, or arrogant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define aloof
557	5561	5583	set out to do something	/ˌset ˈaʊt tə/	phrasal-verb pattern	To begin an activity with a particular goal or intention.	aim to, intend to, embark on, undertake, begin with the goal of	We set out to build a simpler system, not a larger one. / She set out to prove the theory wrong but ended up supporting it. / The documentary sets out to explain how the industry changed.	Focuses on the intention at the beginning; it does not guarantee the goal is achieved. Very useful narrative pattern: 'set out to X, but ended up Y.' Distinguish 'set out for + place' = begin a journey toward a destination.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: set out to do meaning
557	5562	6453	pipsqueak	/ˈpɪpˌskwiːk/	noun, informal/insulting	A small, insignificant, weak, young, or unimpressive person, especially one regarded as having more confidence or attitude than their status warrants.	small fry, runt, nobody, twerp, squirt	Who does that little pipsqueak think he is, ordering everyone around? / The villain dismisses the young hero as a pipsqueak.	Comic/old-fashioned insult. Often attacks size, age, status, or perceived insignificance rather than intelligence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: pipsqueak
557	5563	6605	go off on a tangent / get sidetracked / digress	/ˌɡoʊ ɔːf ɑːn ə ˈtændʒənt/	phrase cluster	To leave the main subject, route, or task and move into a side topic or distraction—Japanese 脇道にそれる depending on context.	digress, get sidetracked, go off track, veer off, wander off-topic	Sorry, I went off on a tangent about pronunciation. / We got sidetracked and never returned to the original question.	Recent cards already cover several members individually; this search-intent card maps the Japanese concept to natural English choices. Digress is more formal; tangent emphasizes a side topic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: 脇道にそれる 英語
557	5564	5603	ornithology	/ˌɔːrnɪˈθɑːlədʒi/	noun	The scientific study of birds.	study of birds, avian science	Her interest in migration eventually led her to study ornithology. / Ornithology covers bird behavior, ecology, evolution, physiology, and classification. / A field guide can be useful to both birders and students of ornithology.	A person who studies birds scientifically is an ornithologist. Distinguish ornithology (scientific discipline) from birding/birdwatching (recreational observation), though the interests can overlap.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define ornithology
557	5565	6371	aggravated	/ˈæɡrəˌveɪtɪd/	adjective / past participle	Made worse or more severe; annoyed or irritated (especially informal AmE). In legal compounds, aggravated marks a more serious form of an offense because of specified circumstances.	worsened, exacerbated; irritated, annoyed; more serious (legal)	His knee pain was aggravated by running downhill. / I was getting aggravated by the constant interruptions. / 'Aggravated assault' is a legal category whose exact definition depends on jurisdiction.	Important polysemy: aggravate originally/standardly = make worse; 'annoy' is now common, especially AmE. Legal aggravated is technical and should not be inferred from ordinary 'angry.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: aggravated
557	5566	6090	slither	/ˈslɪðər/	verb / noun	To move smoothly with a twisting, sliding motion, especially like a snake; figuratively, to move stealthily, unpleasantly, or insinuatingly.	snake, slide, glide, creep, wriggle	A snake slithered across the warm pavement. / He somehow slithered through the crowd without anyone noticing. / The villain slithers his way into positions of influence.	Literal snake-like motion is core. Figurative use can make a person's movement/behavior sound sneaky, slimy, or morally repellent. Existing slithering card covers participle; standalone verb is useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: slither meaning
557	5567	5510	jilt	/dʒɪlt/	verb	To suddenly reject or abandon a romantic partner, especially after having seemed committed to the relationship.	dump, abandon, leave	He was devastated after being jilted just weeks before the wedding.	Often appears as jilted: a jilted lover, bride, or fiancé(e).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: jilt meaning
557	5568	6925	prestige vs prestigious	/preˈstiːʒ/ vs /preˈstɪdʒəs/	contrast	Prestige is primarily a noun meaning status/esteem; prestigious is an adjective meaning having or conferring prestige.	status/esteem vs high-status/respected	The award carries prestige. / It's a prestigious award. / A prestige brand is possible, but 'prestigious brand' is the ordinary adjective construction.	Prestige can function attributively before nouns ('prestige television/brand'), but do not generally replace prestigious after linking verbs: 'the school is prestigious,' not *'is prestige.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: prestige vs prestigious
557	5569	6739	for hours on end / for several hours on end	/fər ˌaʊərz ɑːn ˈend/	idiom	Continuously for many hours, with little or no interruption; 'on end' emphasizes an extended uninterrupted stretch.	for hours straight, continuously, for hours at a time	He can talk about old anime for hours on end. / It rained for several hours on end.	Also days/weeks on end. 'On end' can separately mean upright ('hair stood on end'), but this duration idiom means continuously.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: for several hours on end
557	5570	6479	groin	/ɡrɔɪn/	noun	The area where the lower abdomen meets the upper inner thigh, on either side of the body; in everyday speech it may loosely refer to the genital/pubic region.	inguinal region, crotch (less anatomical), pelvic/genital area (loose context)	He pulled a muscle in his groin during the match. / The ball hit him in the groin. / A groin strain often involves muscles of the inner thigh.	Anatomically groin is not simply a synonym for genitals, though everyday usage can blur them. 'Crotch' refers more to the fork of the body/clothing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: groin meanig
558	5571	6363	bezoar	/ˈbiːzɔːr/	noun	A compact mass of indigestible material that accumulates in the digestive tract, especially the stomach; historically, bezoars were also believed to have magical or antidotal powers.	gastrolith-like mass (not exact), stomach concretion	The doctors discovered a bezoar in the patient's stomach. / In fantasy stories, a bezoar may appear as an antidote because of the old belief that it could counteract poison.	Medical types include phytobezoars (plant material) and trichobezoars (hair). The historical antidote belief is important in literature/fantasy but is not modern medical treatment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define bezoar meaning
558	5572	6395	convulsion	/kənˈvʌlʃən/	noun	A sudden episode of violent, involuntary muscular contractions or body movements; figuratively, a violent social/political disturbance or upheaval.	violent spasm, fit, seizure-related movement; upheaval	His body went into convulsions during the medical emergency. / The revolution sent the country into political convulsions.	Medical nuance: convulsion describes the motor phenomenon; it is not identical to seizure, because seizures can occur without convulsions and convulsions can have different causes. Figurative plural 'convulsions' = violent upheavals.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define convulsion
558	5573	6573	bound by stereotypes	/ˌbaʊnd baɪ ˈsteriəˌtaɪps/	phrase	Restricted, defined, or constrained by oversimplified expectations about what a person/group/character should be like.	boxed in by stereotypes, constrained by clichés, typecast	The character feels less like a person and more like someone bound by stereotypes about masculinity. / Good writing lets characters exist without being bound by stereotypes.	Natural compositional extension of 'bound by.' Often useful in media/social analysis; stereotype can constrain portrayal, expectations, or self-conception.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: bound by stereotypes meaning
558	5574	5894	pull the rug out from under someone	/ˌpʊl ðə ˈrʌɡ aʊt frəm ˌʌndər ˌsʌmwʌn/	idiom	To suddenly remove support, security, assumptions, resources, or plans that someone was relying on, leaving them destabilized.	undermine, destabilize, yank away support, blindside	They approved the project and then pulled the rug out from under us by cutting the budget. / The final reveal pulls the rug out from under the audience's entire interpretation of the story.	Raw search 'put the rug from under' normalized. Often used for abrupt policy/funding changes; in storytelling, a reveal can overturn what the audience thought was true.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113, voca_0114 | search: put the rug from under
558	5575	6410	duff	/dʌf/	adjective / noun / verb, chiefly BrE slang; polysemous	Slang with several unrelated senses: bad, useless, or defective ('a duff battery'); the buttocks ('get off your duff,' chiefly AmE informal); a type of pudding/dough in older usage; and other niche slang senses depending on context.	faulty, useless, rubbish; backside (contextual)	The remote stopped working because the battery was duff. / Get off your duff and help me move this table. / 'Duff' is highly context-dependent, so don't assume every slang occurrence means the same thing.	Chiefly BrE 'duff' = no good/faulty is common. 'Up the duff' = pregnant is BrE slang and is treated separately through the pregnancy search cluster. Also appears in names/brands, so source context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define duff
558	5576	6831	in a pinch	/ɪn ə ˈpɪntʃ/	idiom	When necessary in a difficult, urgent, or less-than-ideal situation; as an emergency substitute or fallback.	if necessary, in an emergency, when push comes to shove, as a fallback	In a pinch, I can use my phone as a hotspot. / This sauce isn't ideal, but it'll work in a pinch.	Common AmE. Implies not the preferred solution, but adequate when options/time/resources are limited.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: in a pinch meaning
558	5577	6858	envoy	/ˈenvɔɪ/	noun	A representative or messenger sent on a diplomatic or special mission, especially by a government or leader.	emissary, diplomatic representative, messenger, delegate	The president sent a special envoy to negotiate a ceasefire. / She served as the UN envoy to the region.	Especially diplomatic/formal. Emissary overlaps strongly; ambassador is normally a formally accredited diplomatic representative, while an envoy may be appointed for a specific mission.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: envoy definition
558	5578	6150	clasp	/klæsp/	verb / noun	To hold something or someone tightly with the hand(s) or arms; to fasten/join with a clasp. As a noun, a fastening device such as on a necklace or bag.	grip, grasp, clutch, embrace; fastener, catch	She clasped both hands around the mug. / They clasped hands before stepping onto the stage. / The clasp on my necklace broke.	Clasp often suggests hands closing firmly around something or two hands joining. Clutch can imply anxiety/desperation; grasp is more neutral. Noun clasp = small fastening mechanism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: clasp meaning
558	5579	6085	wolf down	/ˌwʊlf ˈdaʊn/	phrasal verb	To eat food very quickly and greedily or hungrily.	gobble up, devour, scarf down, inhale (slang), bolt	I wolfed down a sandwich before the meeting. / He was so hungry after the hike that he wolfed down dinner in five minutes.	Image of a wolf eating voraciously. Scarf down is informal AmE; gobble/devour are broad; bolt food can imply swallowing dangerously fast.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: wolf down meaning
558	5580	6893	do/make an about-face	/ˌduː ən əˌbaʊt ˈfeɪs/	phrase	To reverse one's position, policy, attitude, or course completely.	reverse course, make a U-turn, change tack completely	After months of opposition, the mayor did an about-face and supported the proposal. / The company made an about-face on pricing.	Both do an about-face and make an about-face occur; 'make' is especially transparent/common. Preserves raw 'doing an about-face.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: doing an about-face meaning
559	5581	6355	mutineer	/ˌmjuːtəˈnɪr/	noun	A person who takes part in a mutiny—organized rebellion or refusal to obey lawful authority, especially by sailors or soldiers against their officers.	rebel, mutinous sailor/soldier, insurgent (not exact)	The captain ordered the mutineers confined below deck. / Pirate stories often blur the line between a crew overthrowing its captain and a formal naval mutiny.	Especially nautical/military. A mutineer rebels from within an existing crew/unit against its authority; a pirate/privateer describes different legal/occupational relationships.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: mutineer meaning
559	5582	6002	break one's neck	/ˌbreɪk wʌnz ˈnek/	idiom / literal phrase	Literally, to fracture the neck; figuratively in 'break one's neck to do something,' to make an extreme or urgent effort, often more effort than is sensible or appreciated.	go to great lengths, bend over backward, bust one's ass (vulgar), rush	I'm not going to break my neck trying to finish this tonight when nobody needs it until Friday. / She broke her neck to help them, and they barely thanked her. / He could break his neck if he falls from there.	Figurative pattern usually break one's neck to do X. It can imply excessive effort or haste, often with resentment. 'At breakneck speed' is related imagery but means dangerously/very fast.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: break one's neck meaning
559	5583	6424	fold / folded	/foʊld/	verb / adjective-participle	To bend something over itself; to close/collapse by folding; figuratively, to give in, withdraw, fail under pressure, or stop operating. In poker, to surrender one's hand.	bend, crease, collapse; give in, cave, capitulate; withdraw a hand	Fold the paper in half. / He talked tough online but folded the moment his boss challenged him. / The company folded after losing its main investor. / She folded before the river card.	A recent existing card already develops the pressure/poker sense; this card broadens the raw 'folded' search to the literal base verb and business 'company folded.' 'Fold' and 'crease' are related but not identical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define folded
559	5584	6170	vice	/vaɪs/	noun	A bad, immoral, or unhealthy habit or character trait; also criminal activity involving prostitution, illegal gambling, drugs, etc. In titles, vice can mean deputy/subordinate rank (vice president). A vise is a clamping tool in AmE spelling.	bad habit, moral failing, wickedness; illicit activity	Coffee is probably my mildest vice. / The detective worked in the vice squad. / Greed is traditionally described as a vice. / She was promoted to vice president.	Several unrelated-looking uses. Moral vice contrasts with virtue. Vice squad targets certain illicit activities. Prefix-like vice- = deputy/in place of. Tool spelling: AmE vise, BrE vice.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0117 | search: vice meaning
559	5585	6882	tangible vs intangible	/ˈtændʒəbəl/ vs /ɪnˈtændʒəbəl/	contrast	Tangible = physically touchable or concretely perceptible/definite; intangible = not physical/touchable, often abstract, hard to quantify, or nonmaterial.	concrete vs abstract, physical vs nonphysical	The laptop is a tangible asset; brand reputation is an intangible asset. / The trip gave me tangible results but also intangible benefits like confidence.	Tangible can be figurative: tangible progress/evidence = clear and real. Intangible does not mean unreal; trust, goodwill, rights, knowledge, and atmosphere can be real but nonphysical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: tangible vs intangible
559	5586	6107	relentlessly	/rɪˈlentləsli/	adverb	In a persistent, continuous, or intensely determined way that does not let up or give respite.	persistently, tirelessly, unremittingly, incessantly, doggedly	She relentlessly practiced the same passage until it sounded effortless. / The villain relentlessly pursues them across the entire film. / The app relentlessly pushes notifications until you disable them.	Can praise determination or criticize oppressive persistence. Tirelessly often sounds positive; incessantly often sounds annoying; relentlessly emphasizes not easing or stopping. Existing relentless adjective card remains distinct.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: relentlessly doing something meaning
559	5587	5703	inclement weather	/ɪnˌklemənt ˈweðər/	noun phrase	Severe, unpleasant, or unsuitable weather, especially rain, snow, storms, cold, or other conditions that disrupt normal activity.	bad weather, severe weather	The event will be moved indoors in case of inclement weather.	Very common formal collocation in closures, travel notices, policies, and event announcements.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: what is inclement weather
559	5588	6468	ephemeral	/ɪˈfemərəl/	adjective	Lasting for a very short time; fleeting or short-lived.	fleeting, transient, momentary, short-lived, evanescent	Internet fame can be incredibly ephemeral. / The beauty of cherry blossoms is often associated with their ephemeral nature.	More literary/formal than short-lived. Can describe art, trends, emotions, organisms, experiences. Ephemera = items originally meant to be short-lived, such as tickets/posters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: ephemeral
559	5589	5929	get a bad rap	/ˌɡet ə ˌbæd ˈræp/	idiom, informal	To acquire or be given an unfairly negative reputation or criticism.	get a bad reputation, get unfairly blamed, be unfairly maligned	Frozen vegetables get a bad rap, but they can be just as nutritious as fresh ones. / Dubs get a bad rap from some anime fans even when the performances are excellent.	Usually written bad rap, not bad rep, although speakers may associate it with reputation. Bad rep is a separate shortening of bad reputation. Related bum rap = false accusation/unfair blame.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: get a bad rap meaning
559	5590	6594	diverge	/daɪˈvɜːrdʒ/; /dɪˈvɜːrdʒ/	verb	To separate and move in different directions; to become different in opinion, development, pattern, or result.	branch off, separate, differ, deviate	The two paths diverge after the bridge. / Their interpretations begin to diverge at this point.	Natural contrast with converge. Diverge from = depart from a path/norm; diverge on/over = differ about an issue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: diverge
560	5591	6345	nay	/neɪ/	adverb / noun / interjection, formal/archaic	No; a negative vote. Also used rhetorically to correct and intensify what was just said: 'or rather—even more strongly...'	no, negative vote; indeed/rather (rhetorical correction)	The motion passed with ten ayes and two nays. / It was difficult—nay, almost impossible—to finish in time.	Modern everyday 'no' is far more common. In voting: aye vs nay. Literary/rhetorical 'nay' revises upward: 'good, nay, brilliant.' Strong old-fashioned/pirate/fantasy flavor as a standalone response.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: Nay
560	5592	6538	constant dopamine hits	/ˌkɑːnstənt ˈdoʊpəmiːn hɪts/	popular/internet phrase	A loose popular phrase for repeated small bursts of rewarding, novel, or attention-grabbing stimulation—notifications, short videos, game rewards, rapid content changes, etc.	constant stimulation, repeated rewards, novelty hits, quick gratification	Short-form feeds are often criticized as providing constant 'dopamine hits.' / The phrase is useful for understanding internet discourse, but it shouldn't be treated as a literal clinical description of what the brain is doing each second.	Common pop-neuroscience shorthand. Dopamine is not simply 'the pleasure chemical,' and everyday claims about exact dopamine spikes are often much more confident than the evidence warrants. Learn the discourse meaning separately from the neuroscience.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: constant dopamine hits
560	5593	6528	the gears are turning (in my head)	/ðə ˌɡɪrz ɑːr ˈtɜːrnɪŋ/	metaphorical phrase	I'm actively thinking, processing, connecting ideas, or starting to work something out; mental machinery is figuratively beginning to move.	the wheels are turning, I'm thinking it through, my brain is working	I can see the gears turning in your head—you've just thought of something. / Once he mentioned the timeline, the gears started turning.	'The wheels are turning' is more conventional, but gears turning is transparent and common enough as a mental-mechanism metaphor. Often used visually of someone's expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: the gears are turning in my head meaning
560	5594	6078	make a point of doing something	/ˌmeɪk ə ˈpɔɪnt əv ˌduːɪŋ ˌsʌmθɪŋ/	idiom	To deliberately and consistently make sure that one does something because one considers it important or wants it noticed.	make sure to, deliberately do, take care to, go out of one's way to	I make a point of writing down new expressions when I hear them in the wild. / She made a point of thanking everyone individually before she left.	Implies conscious intention, often habitual or emphatic. 'Make a point' can also mean state an argument; 'make a point of X' is the deliberate-action construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: make a point of doing meaning
560	5595	6483	atrophy	/ˈætrəfi/	noun / verb	A wasting away or reduction in size, strength, function, or vitality from disuse, disease, poor nourishment, or other causes; figuratively, a skill, institution, relationship, etc. can weaken through neglect.	waste away, shrink, deteriorate, wither, weaken	Muscles can atrophy after long periods of immobilization. / If I stop speaking English for years, some active vocabulary will atrophy. / The institution's capacity slowly atrophied.	More technical than 'wither.' Medical atrophy has specific causes/types; figurative use is common for skills, abilities, institutions, and relationships.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: atrophy meaning
560	5596	5520	get to do something	/ɡet tə duː/	verb construction	To have the opportunity, privilege, or chance to do something, often something desirable.	have the chance to, be able to	I got to meet the director after the screening.	Different from have to do: get to do often frames the action as an opportunity rather than an obligation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: get to do meaning
560	5597	6938	NLP	/ˌen el ˈpiː/	abbreviation, context-dependent	Most commonly in computing/AI, natural language processing: computational methods for analyzing, understanding, or generating human language. Also used for neuro-linguistic programming, a distinct self-help/communication approach.	natural language processing; neuro-linguistic programming	Modern language models are part of the broader NLP landscape. / In a self-help seminar, NLP may instead mean neuro-linguistic programming.	Always resolve by context. In AI/computational linguistics, NLP overwhelmingly means natural language processing. Neuro-linguistic programming is unrelated to that technical field.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: NLP
560	5598	5637	rickety	/ˈrɪkəti/	adjective	Poorly made, weak, shaky, and likely to collapse or break.	shaky, unstable, ramshackle, flimsy, decrepit	We climbed a rickety wooden staircase to the attic. / The old table was so rickety that my coffee kept wobbling. / A rickety bus carried us up the mountain road.	Suggests physical instability plus age, poor construction, or disrepair. Common with chairs, stairs, bridges, buildings, carts, vehicles. 'Ramshackle' stresses dilapidated construction/appearance; 'flimsy' stresses lack of strength; rickety strongly evokes wobbling/shakiness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: what is rickety
560	5599	5798	dunk on someone	/ˈdʌŋk ɑːn/	verb, slang	To mock, defeat, criticize, or show someone up decisively, especially in public or online.	roast, own, humiliate	People kept dunking on the ad for its awkward slogan.	From basketball dunk imagery; online discourse often uses dunk on for performative public ridicule.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: dunk meaning
560	5600	5854	throw in	/ˌθroʊ ˈɪn/	phrasal verb	To add or include something, often as an extra; to contribute a comment/idea; or to give something as a bonus.	include, add, toss in	They threw in free delivery with the purchase.	Distinct from throw in the towel = give up. Also literal sports senses exist, e.g. a soccer throw-in.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: throw in meaning
561	5601	5584	ticklish	/ˈtɪklɪʃ/	adjective	Sensitive to being tickled; figuratively, delicate, awkward, or difficult to handle without causing trouble.	sensitive, delicate, tricky	Money can be a ticklish subject in some families.	The figurative sense is useful: a ticklish issue/question/situation needs careful handling.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: ticklish meaning
561	5602	6715	live off vs live up to	/ˌlɪv ˈɔːf/ vs /ˌlɪv ˈʌp tə/	contrast	Live off X = survive/support oneself using X as a source of money, food, resources, or support. Live up to X = meet an expectation, standard, reputation, promise, or ideal.	subsist on/from vs meet expectations	He lives off freelance income. / Some animals live off insects. / The sequel didn't live up to the hype. / She tries to live up to her own standards.	Completely different phrasal verbs despite similar form. Also live off someone can imply financial dependence/freeloading; live on can overlap for food/income.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: live off vs live up to
561	5603	5690	appeal to someone	/əˈpiːl tə/	verb phrase	To attract, interest, please, or seem desirable to someone; in other contexts, to make an earnest request to someone.	attract, interest; plead with	The simpler design appeals to younger customers.	Raw 'appeal A to B' is not the usual pattern for attractiveness: A appeals to B. Another pattern is appeal to someone for something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: apeal a to b meaning
561	5604	6144	have no business doing something	/ˌhæv noʊ ˈbɪznəs ˌduːɪŋ/	idiom	To have no legitimate reason, right, qualification, or justification to do something; also used hyperbolically when something is unexpectedly good, strong, difficult, etc.	have no right to, shouldn't be, be in no position to; be absurdly/unexpectedly X (hyperbolic)	He has no business telling you how to spend your money. / That cheap little speaker has no business sounding this good. / I had no business trying that trail in ordinary sneakers.	Very productive modern hyperbole: 'X has no business being this good/funny/beautiful.' It literally sounds like prohibition/unsuitability but often means 'surprisingly, almost unfairly X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: has no business meaning
561	5605	5602	cardinal	/ˈkɑːrdɪnəl/	adjective / noun	Most important or fundamental; also a senior Roman Catholic cleric, a red North American bird, or a term used in sets such as cardinal directions and cardinal numbers.	fundamental, chief, principal	One cardinal rule of the job is to protect confidential information.	Polysemous word. Core adjective sense is 'fundamental/primary.' Context distinguishes the cleric, bird, number, and direction uses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define cardinal
561	5606	5770	tourist (slang)	/ˈtʊrɪst/	noun, slang	A dismissive label for someone seen as a temporary, superficial, inexperienced, or inauthentic participant in a scene, hobby, subculture, profession, or risky environment.	poser, dilettante, outsider	Veterans of the scene dismissed the newcomers as tourists.	Extends literal tourist = temporary visitor. Slang force depends on community and can be gatekeeping or contemptuous.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: tourist meaning slang
561	5607	6653	work wonders for something	/ˌwɜːrk ˈwʌndərz fər/	idiom	To produce remarkably good results or a strong beneficial effect.	do wonders for, greatly improve, be highly effective	A few days off worked wonders for her mood. / That small UI change worked wonders for readability.	Can also be 'work wonders on X.' Hyperbolic but common; doesn't literally imply a miracle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: it works wonders for meaning
561	5608	6598	race against the clock	/ˌreɪs əˌɡenst ðə ˈklɑːk/	idiom	To work or act very quickly because there is little time before a deadline or bad outcome.	race against time, be under severe time pressure	Doctors raced against the clock to identify the cause. / We were racing against the clock to finish before the upload deadline.	Clock emphasizes a visible/fixed time limit. 'Race against time' is near-equivalent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: racing against the clock
561	5609	6692	character arc	/ˈkærəktər ˌɑːrk/	storytelling term	The trajectory of a character's internal development or change across a story—beliefs, values, goals, identity, relationships, behavior, etc.; some arcs are positive, negative, flat, or deliberately incomplete.	character development, developmental arc, transformation	His character arc moves from isolation to trusting other people. / A flat arc can still be compelling if the character changes the world rather than changing fundamentally.	Not every sequence of events involving a character is an arc; arc emphasizes development/trajectory. Common review/analysis term.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: character arc
561	5610	6367	minutiae	/maɪˈnuːʃiˌiː/; /mɪˈnuːʃiˌiː/	plural noun	Small, precise, or trivial details, especially details that can absorb attention despite not being central to the main issue.	fine details, tiny details, particulars, trivialities	We spent an hour arguing over the minutiae of the formatting instead of finishing the project. / Fans love analyzing the minutiae of background animation.	Plural of Latin-derived minutia, though singular minutia is rare in ordinary English. Do not confuse pronunciation with minute /ˈmɪnɪt/ (60 seconds). Often 'the minutiae of X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define minutiae
562	5611	6742	rubric	/ˈruːbrɪk/	noun	A set of criteria or instructions used to evaluate or categorize work; especially an assessment scoring guide showing dimensions and performance levels. Historically, also a heading/instruction in a text or liturgy.	scoring guide, assessment criteria, evaluation framework	The teacher shared the grading rubric before the presentation. / Our review rubric scores accuracy, depth, and naturalness separately.	In education, rubric usually means structured assessment criteria, not simply a vague rule. Useful for systematic QA/review workflows.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: rubric meaning
562	5612	5696	out of frustration	/ˌaʊt əv frʌˈstreɪʃən/	prepositional phrase	Because of, or driven by, a feeling of frustration.	in frustration, from frustration	He slammed the drawer shut out of frustration.	Out of + emotion/motive expresses the feeling or motive that caused an action: out of fear, curiosity, kindness, frustration.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: out of frustration meaning
562	5613	6834	Versailles	/vərˈsaɪ/	proper noun	The French city and, by association, the Palace of Versailles; in historical contexts, 'Versailles' may also refer metonymically to the royal court or the Treaty of Versailles context.	Palace of Versailles, Versailles court (contextual)	The Palace of Versailles became a symbol of French royal power. / The Treaty of Versailles was signed after World War I.	English pronunciation commonly /vərˈsaɪ/, unlike the French pronunciation. Context determines city, palace, court, or treaty reference.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: VERSAILLES Definition & Meaning | Dictionary.com
562	5614	6396	fasciculation	/fəˌsɪkjəˈleɪʃən/	noun, medical	A small, involuntary contraction of a bundle of muscle fibers, often visible under the skin as a localized muscle twitch.	muscle twitch, fascicular twitch	A brief fasciculation appeared in the calf after exercise. / Persistent fasciculations can have many possible causes, so the word describes the movement rather than diagnosing why it happens.	More technical than twitch. Fasciculations may occur in healthy people as well as in medical conditions; context, persistence, weakness, and other signs matter clinically. Card is lexical, not diagnostic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define fasciculation
562	5615	6859	bolster	/ˈboʊlstər/	verb / noun	Verb: strengthen, support, reinforce, or improve something such as confidence, an argument, a reputation, demand, or a structure. Noun: a long firm pillow/cushion used for support.	strengthen, reinforce, support, shore up, boost	The new evidence bolstered her argument. / Positive feedback helped bolster his confidence. / The bed had a cylindrical bolster.	Productive figurative verb. 'Bolster an argument/claim/confidence/support.' Noun sense is physical support/cushion and explains the metaphor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: bolster definition
562	5616	6242	puella	/puˈɛlla/	Latin noun / learned reference	Latin for 'girl' or 'young woman.' In English-language contexts it most often appears in Latin study, titles, names, or references such as Puella Magi Madoka Magica.	girl, maiden, young woman (Latin)	In elementary Latin, puella is a standard first-declension noun meaning 'girl.' / Anime fans may recognize puella from the Latin-styled English title Puella Magi Madoka Magica.	Latin first declension: puella, puellae, etc. It is not an ordinary English synonym for girl; using it in English sounds learned, title-like, or deliberately Latin.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: puella meaning
562	5617	6469	snick	/snɪk/	noun / verb	A small cut or notch; a light clicking/snapping sound; in cricket, a glancing hit off the edge of the bat. As a verb, to nick/cut slightly, click, or edge the ball.	nick, notch, click; edge (cricket)	The lock closed with a quiet snick. / The batter snicked the ball to the wicketkeeper. / He made a tiny snick in the edge of the material.	Rare/context-heavy outside cricket and sound-description. Dictionary sources support cut/notch, click, and cricket senses. citeturn0search0turn0search1		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define snick
562	5618	6791	conform to social norms	/kənˌfɔːrm tə ˌsoʊʃəl ˈnɔːrmz/	phrase	To adjust one's behavior, appearance, expression, or choices so they fit the expectations and conventions of a social group or society.	fit in, follow social conventions, adhere to norms	People sometimes conform to social norms even when they privately disagree with them. / She felt less pressure to conform to the group's expectations after college.	Conform emphasizes matching group patterns/expectations, which may be informal and unwritten. Distinct from comply with, which more strongly suggests a request/rule/requirement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: conform to social norms meaning
562	5619	6234	white-savior fetish / white-savior fantasy	/ˌwaɪt ˈseɪvjər ˌfetɪʃ/	critical social-discourse phrase	A fixation on a scenario in which a white person imagines themself rescuing, enlightening, protecting, or elevating nonwhite people, often centering the rescuer's virtue and flattening the agency of those supposedly being helped.	white-savior complex, rescue fantasy, paternalism (overlapping)	The film was criticized for turning local characters into props for a white-savior fantasy. / In dating or travel discourse, a rescue narrative can become fetishized when one partner is imagined as 'saving' the other from their culture.	Not a clinical diagnosis or fixed technical term. 'White savior complex' is the more established phrase; adding fetish highlights repetitive fascination/desire. Critique concerns paternalism, stereotyping, and displaced agency.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: white savor fetish
562	5620	5623	big time	/ˈbɪɡ ˌtaɪm/	informal noun / adverbial intensifier	At a high level of success or importance; informally, to a very great degree.	major success; seriously, a lot	After years of small gigs, the band finally hit the big time. / I messed up big time.	Existing 'screw up big time' shows the intensifier in one phrase; this standalone card captures the broader construction and noun sense hit/make the big time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: big time meaning
563	5621	5834	United Kingdomistan	/juːˌnaɪtɪd kɪŋdəmɪˈstæn/	political/internet coinage, derogatory	A mocking or hostile nickname for the United Kingdom formed with the suffix -stan, typically used to imply that Britain has become culturally or politically alien, especially through immigration or Islamization.		The commenter used 'United Kingdomistan' as a polemical nickname for Britain.	Nonstandard, ideologically loaded coinage rather than neutral vocabulary. Preserve as recognition/context vocabulary; it can carry anti-immigrant or anti-Muslim insinuation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: United Kingdomistan
563	5622	5900	circumstantial series	/ˌsɜːrkəmˈstænʃəl ˈsɪriːz/	uncertain contextual phrase	A source-specific phrase involving circumstantial, likely describing a series shaped by circumstances or a sequence of circumstantial events/evidence; not a standard fixed term by itself.		The phrase 'circumstantial series' needs its original sentence to determine whether series means a TV series, sequence of events, or evidentiary chain.	Preserve because the exact search may encode source context, but do not invent a specialized meaning. See the separate circumstantial card for the adjective's stable core meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: circumstantial series meaning
563	5623	6341	vermilion	/vərˈmɪljən/	noun / adjective	A vivid red to reddish-orange color; historically, a bright red pigment traditionally made from mercury sulfide (cinnabar).	scarlet, vivid red, red-orange, cinnabar red	The shrine gates were painted a brilliant vermilion. / The sunset turned the clouds vermilion. / Traditional vermilion pigment has a long history in art and ritual.	Often associated visually with Japanese torii/shrine architecture, Chinese art, and historical pigments. Color boundaries vary. Historical mercury-based vermilion is chemically/toxically distinct from modern safer substitutes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: vermillion meaning
563	5624	6103	situationship	/ˌsɪtʃuˈeɪʃənʃɪp/	noun, informal dating slang	A romantic and/or sexual relationship that has some features of dating or partnership but lacks clear definition, commitment, labels, or mutually established expectations.	undefined relationship, unlabeled relationship, casual relationship (overlapping), more-than-friends situation	We were seeing each other every weekend, but neither of us knew whether it was a relationship or just a situationship. / Situationships can feel comfortable until the two people realize they have very different expectations.	Not simply 'friends with benefits': a situationship may involve emotional intimacy and dating-like behavior, and sex is not required. Core idea = ambiguity/undefined status.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: situationship meaning
563	5625	5551	GLAAD	/ɡlæd/	proper noun	A U.S.-based LGBTQ media advocacy organization that works for fair, accurate, and inclusive representation in media.		GLAAD criticized the coverage and called for more accurate LGBTQ representation.	Formerly expanded as Gay & Lesbian Alliance Against Defamation; since 2013, GLAAD has been used as the organization's primary name rather than as a current acronym.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: glaad meaning
563	5626	5586	set out	/ˌset ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To begin a journey; to arrange or present something clearly; or to state ideas, rules, or plans in an organized way.	depart, lay out, present	We set out before sunrise, and the report later set out exactly what we had found.	Polysemous. Keep separate from set out to do something because the journey/presentation senses are independently common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: set outmeaning
563	5627	6552	not for the weak	/ˌnɑːt fər ðə ˈwiːk/	informal phrase	Used to say that something is difficult, intense, disturbing, demanding, spicy, frightening, exhausting, or otherwise requires toughness/tolerance.	not for the faint of heart, not for the squeamish, not easy	That horror movie is not for the weak. / Ten hours of spreadsheet cleanup in one sitting is not for the weak. / This hot sauce is definitely not for the weak.	Internet/casual intensifier. Often humorous exaggeration. 'Not for the faint of heart' is the more established idiom; choose 'not for the squeamish' for gore/disgust.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: this is not for the weak meanig
563	5628	6905	attire	/əˈtaɪər/	noun, somewhat formal	Clothing, especially considered as a particular style or appropriate dress for an occasion.	clothing, dress, clothes, outfit, garb	The invitation says formal attire. / His everyday attire is much more casual than his stage outfits.	Usually uncountable in modern use: attire, not *an attire. Common collocations: formal/business/wedding/traditional attire.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define attire
563	5629	6563	don't judge a gun by its holster	/ˌdoʊnt ˌdʒʌdʒ ə ˈɡʌn baɪ ɪts ˈhoʊlstər/	creative proverb variant	A nonstandard metaphorical variation on 'don't judge a book by its cover': don't judge the quality, nature, or capability of something by its outward container/appearance.	don't judge a book by its cover, appearances can be deceiving	The line 'don't judge a gun by its holster' uses the familiar proverb structure to say appearances don't tell you everything.	Not a standard established proverb. Preserve as source-specific wordplay. Raw search had 'it's'; possessive is 'its': by its holster.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: don't judge a gun from it's holster meaning
563	5630	6333	throw a spanner in the works	/ˌθroʊ ə ˌspænər ɪn ðə ˈwɜːrks/	idiom, chiefly BrE	To cause a problem or unexpected complication that disrupts, delays, or prevents a plan or process from working smoothly.	throw a wrench in the works, disrupt, derail, complicate, create a snag	The sudden visa change threw a spanner in the works of our travel plans. / Everything was ready until the supplier pulled out and threw a spanner in the works.	Classic BrE expression; AmE equivalent is 'throw a wrench in the works.' 'The works' means the mechanism/system as a whole. Usually an unforeseen disruptive factor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: throw a spanner in the works meaning
564	5631	5539	tied up	/ˌtaɪd ˈʌp/	adjective / phrasal-verb form	Busy or occupied and therefore unavailable; also literally bound, or committed/locked into something such as money or resources.	busy, occupied, unavailable	I'm tied up until three, but I can call you after that.	Several senses share the image of being bound so you cannot move or use something freely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: tied up meaning
564	5632	6118	afflicted	/əˈflɪktɪd/	adjective / past participle	Suffering from or seriously affected by an illness, problem, hardship, or harmful condition.	suffering from, stricken with/by, troubled by, affected by, beset by	The region was afflicted by years of drought. / He plays a detective afflicted with chronic insomnia. / The afflicted communities received emergency aid.	Often formal/literary and usually negative: afflicted with/by X. 'Affected' is neutral/broader; afflicted implies suffering. As noun phrase 'the afflicted' = people who suffer from a condition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define afflicted
564	5633	5646	weird flex (but OK)	/ˌwɪrd ˈfleks/	internet slang	A joking response to an odd, unnecessary, or questionable boast or display of pride.	strange boast, odd brag	You memorized every fast-food menu in town? Weird flex, but OK.	Often appears as 'weird flex but OK/okay.' Flex = boast/show off. Usually teasing or dismissive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: weird flex meaning
564	5634	6861	pay someone no mind	/ˌpeɪ ˌsʌmwʌn noʊ ˈmaɪnd/	idiom	To ignore someone or not give them attention, consideration, or concern.	ignore, disregard, pay no attention to, take no notice of	Pay the hecklers no mind and finish what you were saying. / She paid him no mind and kept walking.	Also 'pay no mind to someone/something.' Informal and somewhat folksy/regional in feel. Raw 'pay her no mind' is a normal instance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pay her no mind meaninmg
564	5635	6478	inhuman vs inhumane	/ɪnˈhjuːmən/ vs /ˌɪnhjuːˈmeɪn/	contrast	Inhuman = not human, lacking normal human qualities, or shockingly cruel/monstrous. Inhumane = cruel and causing unnecessary suffering; not compassionate or humane.	inhuman: nonhuman, monstrous; inhumane: cruel, merciless, brutal	The creature had inhuman strength. / The prisoners were kept in inhumane conditions. / Calling the workload 'inhuman' can mean impossibly harsh; 'inhumane treatment' specifically emphasizes cruelty/lack of compassion.	They overlap in cruelty contexts but are not interchangeable everywhere. An alien can be inhuman without being inhumane. Humane ↔ inhumane concerns compassion; human ↔ inhuman concerns humanity/human qualities.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: inhuman vs inhumane
564	5636	5573	take someone through something	/ˌteɪk ˌsʌmwʌn ˈθruː/	phrasal-verb pattern	To explain or demonstrate a process, event, argument, or set of steps to someone in sequence.	walk someone through, guide through	Can you take me through what happened after the meeting?	Very common in interviews and explanations: 'Take me through that' asks for a step-by-step account.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: take me through that meaning
564	5637	5874	disrepute	/ˌdɪsrɪˈpjuːt/	noun	A state of having a bad reputation or losing public respect.	disgrace, dishonor, ill repute, bad name, infamy	The scandal brought the organization into disrepute. / Years of low-quality sequels brought the franchise into disrepute with longtime fans.	Very common fixed pattern: bring X into disrepute / fall into disrepute. Compared with infamy, disrepute focuses on loss/lack of respect rather than spectacular evil or notoriety.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: define disrepute
564	5638	6900	breadcrumbs / breadcrumbing (relationships)	/ˈbredˌkrʌmz/; /ˈbredˌkrʌmɪŋ/	dating / relationship slang	Small, intermittent signals of attention or interest that keep someone engaged without offering consistent effort, clarity, or commitment; breadcrumbing is the behavior of sending such signals.	mixed signals, intermittent attention, stringing someone along (overlap)	He kept sending flirty messages every few weeks—just enough breadcrumbs to keep her interested. / She realized she was being breadcrumbed and stopped responding.	Existing WID6120 covers breadcrumbing; this card preserves the plural 'breadcrumbs' as the individual signals in relationship context. Different from web-navigation breadcrumbs.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: breadcrumbs meaning relationship
564	5639	6465	apologetic	/əˌpɑːləˈdʒetɪk/	adjective	Showing that one is sorry for having caused trouble, offense, inconvenience, or harm; expressing regret.	sorry, remorseful, contrite, regretful, sheepish (contextual)	She sounded genuinely apologetic about missing the meeting. / He gave me an apologetic smile after stepping on my foot.	Contrite/remorseful are stronger and more morally weighted. 'Apologetic tone/look/smile' can communicate regret without an explicit apology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: apologetic meaning
564	5640	6814	anthropomorphism	/ˌænθrəpəˈmɔːrfɪzəm/	noun	The attribution of human traits, emotions, intentions, behavior, or form to nonhuman animals, objects, gods, natural forces, machines, etc.	humanization, personification (overlaps)	Giving the robot a jealous personality is anthropomorphism. / We should be careful about anthropomorphism when interpreting animal behavior.	Personification is a literary device giving human qualities to nonhuman things; anthropomorphism can be broader and more literal/systematic, including treating animals or AI as humanlike agents.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: anthropomorphism
565	5641	5707	demisexual	/ˌdemiˈsekʃuəl/	adjective / noun	Describing a person who typically experiences sexual attraction only after forming a strong emotional bond with someone.		She describes herself as demisexual and says emotional closeness is important before attraction develops.	A sexual-orientation label on the asexual spectrum. The exact experience varies by person; it does not simply mean choosing to wait before having sex.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: demisexsual
565	5642	6166	flop (film / entertainment)	/flɑːp/	noun / verb, informal	A work, event, product, or performance that fails badly, especially commercially or with audiences; to fail in that way.	bomb, failure, dud, turkey (informal), tank	The film flopped at the box office despite strong reviews. / It was a notorious flop when it opened, then became a cult favorite years later. / A financial flop isn't necessarily an artistic failure.	Entertainment use often means commercial failure, but speakers may mean critical/audience failure too. 'Bomb' is close; 'tank' often emphasizes performance/results dropping badly.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: film flops meaning
565	5643	6540	flattered	/ˈflætərd/	adjective	Pleased and honored because someone has praised you, shown interest in you, or treated you as special; sometimes politely used when declining an offer.	pleased, honored, gratified, complimented	I'm flattered that you asked me. / She looked genuinely flattered by the compliment. / 'I'm flattered, but I'm not interested' softens a rejection.	Usually positive, but 'flattered' can coexist with embarrassment or refusal. Related verb flatter can also mean praise insincerely/excessively.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: flattered meaning
565	5644	6775	literary agent	/ˈlɪtəˌreri ˌeɪdʒənt/	noun	A professional who represents authors and their work, often pitching manuscripts to publishers, negotiating contracts/rights, and advising on publishing strategy.	author's agent, publishing agent	She signed with a literary agent who submitted the novel to editors. / Many traditional publishers accept fiction submissions mainly through agents.	Roles vary; agents typically earn commission rather than charging ordinary reading fees. 'Literary' here covers commercial genres too, not only literary fiction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: literary agent meaning
565	5645	5934	hot goss	/ˌhɑːt ˈɡɑːs/	noun, informal/slang	Fresh, juicy, interesting, or scandalous gossip.	juicy gossip, tea, dirt, scoop, dish	Okay, what's the hot goss? You said something happened at the party. / I don't even watch the show, but somehow I know all the hot goss about the cast.	Goss = clipped playful form of gossip. Hot goss often sounds knowingly silly/campy rather than serious. Compare tea = gossip/information, especially juicy inside information; dirt emphasizes damaging information.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: hot goss meaning
565	5646	5642	pedestrian	/pəˈdestriən/	noun / adjective	A person traveling on foot; figuratively, ordinary, dull, or lacking imagination.	walker; mundane, uninspired	The design is functional but a little pedestrian.	The figurative adjective is often mildly critical: pedestrian prose, ideas, design.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: pedestrian meaning
565	5647	6717	acumen	/əˈkjuːmən/	noun	The ability to make quick, accurate judgments and understand a particular area well, especially business, politics, finance, or practical affairs.	shrewdness, insight, astuteness, savvy, discernment	Her business acumen helped turn a tiny shop into a profitable company. / Technical skill alone doesn't guarantee good managerial acumen.	Often appears as business/financial/political acumen. Implies sharp practical judgment, not just accumulated knowledge.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: acumen meaning
565	5648	5862	do fuck all	/duː ˌfʌk ˈɔːl/	idiom, vulgar informal	To do absolutely nothing, or virtually nothing useful/effective.	do nothing, do jack shit, do bugger all, accomplish nothing	I've been staring at this file for an hour and done fuck all. / They promised to fix the problem, then did fuck all about it.	Fuck all can also mean 'nothing/almost nothing': I know fuck all about cars. Very blunt/vulgar; BrE and other varieties use it readily in frustrated speech. Compare do bugger all (BrE, somewhat milder) and do jack shit (AmE, vulgar).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: done fuck all meaning
565	5649	5768	turn on	/ˌtɜːrn ˈɑːn/	phrasal verb	To activate something; to suddenly attack or betray someone; to excite or interest someone, including sexually; or to depend on a particular factor.	switch on; attack; excite; depend on	Turn on the lights. / The crowd suddenly turned on him.	Existing turn on someone covers the attack/betray sense; keep the broader base phrasal verb because activation and attraction senses are independently common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: turn on meaning
565	5650	5570	elect someone something	/ɪˈlekt ˌsʌmwʌn ˈsʌmθɪŋ/	verb pattern	To choose someone for an official position by voting, with the position used as an object complement.	choose, vote in, appoint by election	The members elected Maya chair of the committee.	Pattern: elect + person + position: elect someone president/chair/treasurer. No 'as' is required in this construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: elect someone something meaning
566	5651	6695	booted	/ˈbuːtɪd/	adjective / past tense, informal	Past of boot: kicked; forced/removed from a place, group, game, server, job, etc.; started a computer/system ('booted up'); also wearing boots.	kicked out, ejected, removed, expelled; started up	He got booted from the server for cheating. / The laptop booted normally after the update. / She was booted and ready for the snow.	Context decides. 'Get booted' commonly = be kicked out/removed. Computing boot is unrelated in modern meaning to footwear.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: booted meaning
566	5652	5738	bounce off the walls	/ˌbaʊns ɔːf ðə ˈwɔːlz/	idiom, informal	To be extremely energetic, excited, restless, or hyperactive.	be hyper, be bursting with energy, be wired, be bouncing around	The kids were bouncing off the walls after all that candy. / I had so much nervous energy before the trip that I was practically bouncing off the walls. / The puppy had been indoors all day and was bouncing off the walls.	Usually figurative, evoking frantic movement around a room. Can describe excitement, restlessness, excess energy, or hyperactivity. 'Wired' often suggests stimulation/caffeine/nervous arousal; 'bouncing off the walls' is more vivid and playful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: bounced off the walls
566	5653	6665	take someone up on that / take someone up on an offer	/ˌteɪk ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌp ɑːn/	idiom / correction	To accept someone's offer, invitation, challenge, or suggestion. Raw 'take it up on that' is usually a malformed version; say 'take you/him/her/them up on that.'	accept the offer, accept the invitation, take up the offer	You offered to help me move—I might take you up on that. / She took him up on his offer of a ride.	Important object pattern: take PERSON up on OFFER/that. Alternatively, 'take up an offer.' Do not normally say *take it up on that for this meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: take it up on that meaning
566	5654	6176	swing aside	/ˌswɪŋ əˈsaɪd/	verb phrase	To pivot or move to one side in an arc, or cause something to do so, clearing the way.	swing away, pivot aside, move aside, swing open	The gate swung aside to reveal the courtyard. / He swung the lamp aside so I could reach the shelf.	Usually compositional rather than a fixed idiom. Aside emphasizes movement out of the way; 'swing open' emphasizes opening. Can be transitive or intransitive depending on structure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: swing aside meaning
566	5655	6825	imaginative	/ɪˈmædʒənətɪv/	adjective	Showing creativity, originality, or a strong ability to form new ideas, images, stories, solutions, or possibilities.	creative, inventive, original, resourceful, fanciful	The film has an imaginative visual style even when the plot is simple. / That was an imaginative solution to a boring technical problem.	Imaginative = possessing/using imagination; imaginary = not real/existing only in imagination. Important contrast.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: imaginative meaning
566	5656	6904	shorthand	/ˈʃɔːrtˌhænd/	noun / adjective	A rapid abbreviated writing system; more broadly, a concise word, label, symbol, or expression used to stand for a larger, more complex idea.	abbreviation, shortcut, compressed label, stand-in	In the article, 'the West' is used as shorthand for a much more complicated set of institutions and societies. / She learned shorthand for taking notes quickly.	Figurative 'X is shorthand for Y' is extremely common. Shorthand can be useful compression but may hide distinctions or nuance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: shorthand
566	5657	6547	peppy	/ˈpepi/	adjective, informal	Lively, energetic, cheerful, enthusiastic, and full of pep.	energetic, upbeat, perky, lively, bubbly	I'm not very peppy in the morning. / The host has a relentlessly peppy delivery. / She chose a peppy song to wake everyone up.	Often describes personality, voice, music, or mood. Can be affectionate or mildly annoying if the energy feels excessive. 'Pep' = energy/enthusiasm.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: I'm not peppy meaning
566	5658	6220	baddie / baddy	/ˈbædi/	noun, slang / informal	Traditionally, a villain or bad person ('the baddie' in a story). In modern social-media slang, especially 'baddie,' an attractive, confident, stylish person—often a woman—with a polished, glamorous image.	villain, bad guy; attractive confident woman, hottie, it girl (overlapping)	The cartoon baddie reveals his plan in the final episode. / Her makeup and outfit are pure Instagram-baddie aesthetic. / Context completely changes whether 'baddie' means villain or attractive person.	Modern beauty/fashion sense usually spelled baddie. Traditional BrE-style baddie = villain. Social-media sense often implies confidence, makeup/fashion polish, and curated aesthetic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: baddy meaning
566	5659	6096	lingering feelings	/ˌlɪŋɡərɪŋ ˈfiːlɪŋz/	noun phrase	Emotions that remain after the event or relationship that caused them has ended or should seemingly be over.	residual feelings, remaining feelings, unresolved feelings, emotional residue	She still had lingering feelings for him years after they broke up. / The ending left me with lingering feelings of sadness and unease for the rest of the night.	Often romantic, but not necessarily: lingering feelings of guilt, resentment, affection, unease, etc. 'Unresolved feelings' implies they have not been psychologically settled; lingering simply means they persist.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: lingering feelings
566	5660	5966	catch someone in a lie	/ˌkætʃ ˌsʌmwʌn ɪn ə ˈlaɪ/	phrase	To discover or expose that someone is lying, often because their story contradicts evidence or something they said earlier.	catch someone lying, expose a lie, catch someone out, bust someone (informal)	She said she'd never met him, but the photo caught her in a lie. / I wasn't trying to interrogate him; I just happened to catch him in a lie when the dates didn't match.	Catch someone in a lie focuses on discovering a specific falsehood. Catch someone out (especially BrE) can mean expose a mistake/deception more broadly.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: catch someone in a lie meaning
567	5661	6586	negligent homicide / criminally negligent homicide	/ˈneɡlɪdʒənt ˈhɑːməsaɪd/	legal term, jurisdiction-dependent	A homicide offense based on causing another person's death through legally culpable negligence rather than an intent to kill. Exact offense names, definitions, and required mental states vary by jurisdiction.	criminally negligent homicide, negligent killing; manslaughter (not equivalent in every jurisdiction)	New York law uses the offense name 'criminally negligent homicide'; other jurisdictions may use different labels. / 'Negligent murder' is not a safe universal synonym for negligent homicide.	Raw search 'negligent murder' should not be normalized into a universal offense name. In New York, homicide categories include criminally negligent homicide separately from murder. Legal terminology is jurisdiction-specific.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: negligent murder meaning
567	5662	5794	say the quiet part out loud	/ˌseɪ ðə ˌkwaɪət pɑːrt aʊt ˈlaʊd/	idiom	To openly state an underlying motive, belief, or implication that is normally left unstated because saying it explicitly would be embarrassing or revealing.	state the implicit motive, reveal the subtext	He said the quiet part out loud when he admitted the rule was meant to discourage newcomers.	Raw search 'said the quiet part loud' normalized to the standard phrase with out loud.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: said the quiet part loud meaning
567	5663	6556	march someone somewhere	/ˌmɑːrtʃ ˌsʌmwʌn/	causative verb construction	To make, order, or force someone to walk somewhere in a purposeful, controlled, or brisk way, often suggesting authority or lack of choice.	escort, lead, frog-march (stronger), make someone walk	The teacher marched him to the principal's office. / Security marched the protesters out of the building. / She marched the kids upstairs to bed.	Transitive march can imply control: march someone out/in/over to X. 'Frog-march' is stronger and can imply physical force/restraint.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: marching someone meaning
567	5664	6768	dead on	/ˌded ˈɑːn/	adjective/adverb, informal	Exactly correct, accurate, or precisely aligned/aimed; in some dialectal contexts, also excellent/fine.	exactly right, spot-on, accurate, bang on	Your guess was dead on. / The timing has to be dead on or the joke won't land.	Common AmE 'dead-on accurate'; 'dead on' = exactly. In Northern Irish usage, 'dead on' can also mean okay/good/nice, so context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: dead on meanng
567	5665	6439	cause issues to bubble to the surface	/ˌbʌbəl tə ðə ˈsɜːrfɪs/	figurative construction	To cause previously hidden, suppressed, unnoticed, or underlying problems to become visible and demand attention.	bring issues to the surface, expose underlying problems, make problems emerge	The sudden workload increase caused old communication problems to bubble to the surface. / The controversy didn't create every tension; it caused several existing issues to bubble to the surface.	Raw 'caused issues to bubbles to surface' needs grammar repair: cause + object + to + base verb → caused issues to bubble to the surface. Strong metaphor of submerged material rising into view.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: caused issues to bubbles to surface meaning
567	5666	6349	buccaneer	/ˌbʌkəˈnɪr/	noun / verb	Historically, one of the hunters and later sea raiders active around the Caribbean in the 17th century, especially against Spanish shipping; more broadly, a pirate/adventurer. Figuratively, a bold or unscrupulous operator.	pirate, sea raider, freebooter, adventurer	The story turns a historical Caribbean buccaneer into a romantic pirate hero. / The company was run by a group of financial buccaneers who loved risky deals.	Historically more specific than generic pirate. The word is linked to French boucan/boucanier: hunters smoked meat on wooden frames called boucans before some became raiders.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: buccaneer meaning
567	5667	6016	talk at someone	/ˌtɔːk æt ˈsʌmwʌn/	verb phrase	To speak to someone in a one-sided way without genuinely engaging, listening, or allowing a real exchange.	lecture, monologue at, talk over (overlapping), preach at	He didn't really talk with me; he talked at me for twenty minutes about what I was doing wrong. / Some video essays feel conversational, while others feel like the host is talking at the audience.	Contrast talk to/with: neutral interaction; talk at is critical and emphasizes one-way delivery. Talk over someone = keep speaking while they are trying to speak or drown them out.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: talk at someone
567	5668	6075	demolition	/ˌdeməˈlɪʃən/	noun	The act or process of deliberately destroying a building or structure; figuratively, a crushing defeat or thorough dismantling of an argument/performance.	destruction, razing, teardown; rout, dismantling	The old stadium is scheduled for demolition next year. / Her rebuttal was a complete demolition of his argument. / The champion delivered a one-sided demolition in the final.	Literal construction sense is core. Figurative sports/debate use is vivid and emphatic. Demolish = verb. Raw 'demolistion' corrected.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: demolistion meaning
567	5669	5954	intellectually deranged	/ˌɪntəˈlektʃuəli dɪˈreɪndʒd/	creative evaluative phrase	A deliberately extreme way to describe reasoning, beliefs, or an intellectual position as wildly irrational, distorted, or detached from sense.	intellectually unhinged, wildly irrational, intellectually incoherent, outlandish	The essay starts with a fair criticism and somehow ends up somewhere intellectually deranged. / I don't merely disagree with that reading; the chain of reasoning feels intellectually unhinged.	Not a fixed idiom or clinical description. Deranged literally has mental-illness associations and is strong/stigmatizing when applied to a person; applied to an argument/worldview it works as hyperbolic rhetorical criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: intellectually deranged
567	5670	5945	suffocate / suffocating	/ˈsʌfəˌkeɪt/; /ˈsʌfəˌkeɪtɪŋ/	verb / adjective	Literally, to die or cause someone to be unable to breathe because air is cut off; figuratively, to make someone feel trapped, oppressed, overwhelmed, or deprived of freedom/space.	choke, asphyxiate, smother; stifle, overwhelm, constrain	Smoke can suffocate someone before the flames reach them. / The room was hot and suffocating. / His parents meant well, but the constant monitoring felt suffocating. / Too many rules can suffocate creativity.	Compare smother: can literally block breathing/cover completely and figuratively overwhelm with attention. Stifle often means suppress expression, growth, creativity, laughter, etc. Suffocating emphasizes lack of breathing room, literally or emotionally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: suffocating meaning
568	5671	6045	through the roof	/ˌθruː ðə ˈruːf/	idiom	Extremely high or rapidly increased, especially prices, costs, levels, emotions, ratings, or measurements.	sky-high, extremely high, off the charts, soaring	Hotel prices were through the roof that weekend. / My stress level went through the roof when the file disappeared. / After the trailer, expectations were through the roof.	Be through the roof = already extremely high; go/shoot through the roof = rise dramatically. Existing example uses fury level, but standalone idiom has broad high-frequency use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: something is through the roof meaning
568	5672	6473	pull women if you're chopped	/ˌpʊl ˈwɪmɪn ɪf jər ˈtʃɑːpt/	internet slang phrase	In contemporary dating/looks slang, 'pull women' means attract or successfully get dates/sexual or romantic attention from women; 'chopped' means unattractive/ugly. Thus: how to attract women if you're considered unattractive.	get women, attract women, have dating success; ugly/unattractive (chopped)	The video title 'how to pull women if you're chopped' basically means 'how to attract women if you're ugly.'	Preserve the full search phrase because the slang combination is the learning target. Existing WID4985 covers chopped; 'pull' in this sense is especially common in BrE/internet dating slang and can sound objectifying depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: how to pull women if you're chopped meaning
568	5673	5844	God forbid	/ˌɡɑːd fərˈbɪd/	expression	Used to express a strong hope that something bad will not happen; also used sarcastically to criticize the idea that an ordinary or desirable thing would somehow be unacceptable.	heaven forbid, perish the thought (related)	God forbid anything happens to the kids. / God forbid we take one day off. / 'Maybe he could admit he was wrong—God forbid.'	Can be sincere (strong hope that a feared event does not occur) or sarcastic (mocking the idea that some ordinary/reasonable thing would be terrible). Tone and context completely change the effect. 'Heaven forbid' is a milder/euphemistic alternative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: god forbid meaning
568	5674	6626	grindset	/ˈɡraɪndˌset/	noun, internet slang	A mindset centered on relentless work, hustle, self-discipline, productivity, and pursuing success—often used sincerely or mockingly/critically.	hustle mentality, hustle culture, work-hard mindset	His whole feed is sigma-grindset content about waking up at 4 a.m. and never taking days off. / 'Grindset' often parodies extreme productivity culture.	Blend of grind + mindset. Strongly associated with hustle-culture and sigma-male memes; tone is frequently ironic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: grindset meaning
568	5675	6430	the point still stands	/ðə ˌpɔɪnt stɪl ˈstændz/	conversational argument phrase	The main claim or argument remains valid or relevant despite a correction, objection, exception, or flaw in some supporting detail.	the argument still holds, that doesn't invalidate the point, the core point remains	I got the year wrong, but the point still stands: the policy changed before the second season. / You can criticize his example, but his broader point still stands.	Useful in debate, but don't use it as a magic escape from a rebuttal: whether the point really stands depends on whether the challenged detail was essential to the argument.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: the point still stands meaning
568	5676	5757	sucks to be you	/ˌsʌks tə bi ˈjuː/	expression, informal/rude	A mocking or unsympathetic response meaning that someone's situation is unfortunate and they are the one who has to deal with it.	too bad for you, tough luck	You have to work all weekend? Sucks to be you.	Usually sarcastic and insensitive rather than genuinely sympathetic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: sucks to be you meaning
568	5677	6392	concede	/kənˈsiːd/	verb	To admit that something is true or valid, often reluctantly; to accept defeat or yield a point; to give/grant something; in sports, to allow the opponent to score.	admit, acknowledge, yield, surrender, grant; allow (a goal/point)	I'll concede that his first point was fair. / She conceded the election after the final count. / The team conceded two goals in the second half. / He refused to concede defeat.	Highly productive: concede a point, concede that..., concede defeat, concede an election, concede a goal. 'Admit' lacks the competitive/yielding flavor; concession often comes after resistance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: conceded meaning
568	5678	6508	look (slang/interjection uses)	/lʊk/	discourse marker / noun / slang, context-dependent	Besides the ordinary verb, 'Look,' can introduce a blunt explanation or redirect attention ('Look, here's the problem'). 'A look' can mean a distinctive style/outfit or facial expression; slang meanings vary by community.	listen, here's the thing (discourse); style, outfit, expression	Look, I get what you're saying, but I still disagree. / That jacket is a whole look. / She gave me a look that said, 'Don't even try it.'	Raw 'look meaning slang' is too broad for one secret slang definition. Preserve the productive modern uses: discourse-marker Look; 'a look' = style/aesthetic; 'give someone a look' = meaningful facial expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: look meaning slang
568	5679	6452	smoosh / smooshed	/smuːʃ/	verb / adjective-participle, informal	To squash, press, mash, or push things together, often messily or affectionately; smooshed = flattened, compressed, or crowded together.	squish, squash, mash, smush, press together	The sandwich got smooshed at the bottom of my bag. / The puppies were all smooshed together on the couch. / She smooshed the clay into a rough ball.	Informal and expressive. Smush is a common variant. Often conveys soft compression rather than violent crushing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: smooshed meaning
568	5680	5682	weigh one's options	/ˌweɪ wʌnz ˈɑːpʃənz/	idiom	To carefully consider the advantages and disadvantages of the available choices before deciding.	consider one's options, evaluate alternatives	I'm still weighing my options before accepting either offer.	Raw search misspelled weighing. Weigh metaphorically = assess relative value or consequences.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: weighning your options meaning
569	5681	6228	blank stare	/ˌblæŋk ˈster/	noun phrase	A facial expression in which someone looks fixedly without showing an obvious emotion, reaction, recognition, or understanding.	vacant stare, blank look, expressionless gaze, thousand-yard stare (not identical)	I explained the joke and got nothing but a blank stare. / She answered the shocking news with a blank stare that made it impossible to tell what she was thinking.	Can suggest confusion, shock, dissociation, boredom, nonrecognition, or deliberate lack of reaction; the expression alone does not tell you which. 'Vacant' more strongly suggests absence/disconnection.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: blank stare meaning
569	5682	6942	be well-versed in something	/bi ˌwel ˈvɜːrst ɪn/	idiom / adjective phrase	To be very knowledgeable, experienced, or skilled in a subject, field, system, or body of knowledge.	be knowledgeable about, be proficient in, be conversant with, know well	She's well-versed in Japanese animation history. / You don't need to be well-versed in linguistics to notice the pattern.	Usually well-versed in + subject/field. Hyphenated before/after noun commonly. 'Versed in' alone is more formal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: be well-versed in something
569	5683	6102	supervillain / super villain	/ˈsuːpərˌvɪlən/	noun	A highly powerful, extraordinary, or larger-than-life villain, especially in superhero fiction; figuratively, someone portrayed as extravagantly evil or malicious.	archvillain, comic-book villain, big bad (informal)	The movie spends more time developing the supervillain than the hero. / The internet turned him into a cartoonish supervillain who was supposedly responsible for everything.	Usually one word 'supervillain' in modern usage. Figurative use often mocks exaggerated moral framing: 'treated like a supervillain.' Not every main antagonist is necessarily a supervillain.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: super villain meaning
569	5684	6344	rowboat	/ˈroʊˌboʊt/	noun	A small boat propelled with oars by a person rowing.	rowing boat, dinghy (not exact), skiff (type/contextual)	They crossed the lake in a little wooden rowboat. / He tied the rowboat to the dock before the storm arrived.	AmE rowboat; BrE often rowing boat. Not every small boat is a rowboat: the defining feature is propulsion by rowing with oars.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: rowboat meaning
569	5685	6038	rig	/rɪɡ/	verb / noun	As a verb, to arrange/manipulate something dishonestly for a desired result, or to equip/set up something with gear. As a noun, equipment/apparatus, a vehicle setup, or an oil-drilling structure, among other senses.	fix, manipulate, tamper with; equip, set up; setup, apparatus	They were accused of rigging the election. / We rigged a temporary light above the desk with a clamp. / His gaming rig takes up half the room.	Very polysemous. Rig an election/game = dishonestly predetermine/manipulate. Rig up = improvise/set up equipment. Gaming rig = computer setup; oil rig = drilling structure. 'Rigged' online often means unfairly fixed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: rig meaning
569	5686	6837	home of the melon	/ˌhoʊm əv ðə ˈmelən/	context-dependent phrase	Literally, a place presented as especially associated with melons—their origin, production, fame, or local identity. Without source context, it is not a standard English idiom.	melon-producing region, place famous for melons	The town markets itself as the 'home of the melon.'	Preserve exact search. 'Home of X' is a productive slogan meaning a place strongly associated with X. The definite article can suggest a specific/local emblematic melon.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: home of the melon meaning
569	5687	6763	legible	/ˈledʒəbəl/	adjective	Clear enough to read or decipher, especially handwriting, print, text, labels, or visual information.	readable, decipherable, clear	His handwriting is messy but still legible. / The subtitles need to remain legible on a small phone screen.	Legible focuses on whether symbols/text can be read. Readable can also mean pleasant/easy to understand stylistically; intelligible focuses on comprehensibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: legible meaning
569	5688	6848	contrite	/kənˈtraɪt/	adjective	Feeling and showing sincere remorse, guilt, or regret for wrongdoing.	remorseful, repentant, apologetic, penitent, sorry	He looked genuinely contrite after realizing how much his comment had hurt her. / Her apology sounded contrite rather than defensive.	Stronger and more morally weighted than apologetic. Often implies genuine recognition of fault.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: contrite
569	5689	6641	swatting	/ˈswɑːtɪŋ/	noun, internet/crime slang	Making a false emergency report intended to send armed police or other emergency responders to an innocent person's location, often as harassment or intimidation.	false emergency-report attack, malicious police hoax	The streamer was targeted by swatting after someone falsely reported a violent emergency at his home.	Dangerous criminal harassment that can cause injury or death. Named from SWAT, though actual responding agencies vary. Recognition/safety term; not ordinary 'swat' = hit an insect.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: swatting meaning
569	5690	6362	admonish	/ədˈmɑːnɪʃ/	verb	To warn, advise, or mildly-to-seriously rebuke someone about behavior, often with an implication of correction or guidance.	warn, caution, rebuke, reprimand, reprove, counsel	The teacher admonished them to be more careful with the equipment. / She admonished him for speaking so rudely. / The guide admonished visitors not to touch the paintings.	Patterns: admonish someone for X; admonish someone to do/not do X. Compared with rebuke/reprimand, admonish more readily includes warning or corrective advice, not just condemnation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0120 | search: define admonish
570	5691	5575	lift someone up	/ˌlɪft ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To make someone feel happier, more hopeful, or more confident; also to improve someone's social or economic condition.	encourage, uplift, support	A good mentor should lift people up along the way, not just advance alone.	Raw search included 'along the way'; the independently useful idiom is lift someone up.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: lift someone up along the way meaning
570	5692	5824	skew toward something	/ˌskjuː təˈwɔːrd/	verb phrase	To lean, be distributed, or be biased disproportionately toward one side, group, result, or characteristic.	tilt toward, lean toward, bias toward	The survey sample skews toward younger respondents.	Existing skew covers distortion; this searched construction highlights the common intransitive/distribution pattern skew toward.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: skew to meaning
570	5693	5549	libel	/ˈlaɪbəl/	noun / verb	A published or written false statement that damages someone's reputation; as a verb, to defame someone in this way.	defamation, written defamation	The newspaper denied that the article was libelous or that it had libeled him.	Traditionally contrasted with slander (spoken defamation). Exact legal rules vary by jurisdiction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0117, voca_0121 | search: define libel
570	5694	5920	minute	/maɪˈnuːt/	adjective	Extremely small, slight, or precise in degree or detail.	tiny, minuscule, microscopic, negligible, fine	There was only a minute difference between the two versions. / She notices minute changes in pronunciation that I would completely miss.	This adjective is pronounced my-NOOT /maɪˈnuːt/, unlike minute '60 seconds' /ˈmɪnɪt/. Common: minute detail/difference/amount/trace. 'Minutely' can mean in very fine detail.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: minute definition
570	5695	6042	let's go vs go!	/ˌlets ˈɡoʊ/ vs /ˈɡoʊ/	contrast card	Let's go proposes or urges that speaker and others leave/start/do something together; go! directly commands or urges another person/people to move, act, or continue. 'Let's go!' also functions as an excited cheer meaning roughly 'yes!/come on!'	come on, let's move; go!, move!; yes! (cheer)	We're going to miss the train—let's go. / The light's green—go! / He hit the winning shot and everyone yelled, 'Let's go!'	Raw 'let's go vs go let's go' points to pragmatic difference. 'Go, let's go!' can occur as stacked urging, but not as a separate fixed grammar. Intonation/context determines whether let's go means departure, encouragement, impatience, or celebration.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: let's go vs go let's go
570	5696	5959	ferment	/fərˈment/ (verb); /ˈfɜːrment/ (noun)	verb / noun	To undergo or cause chemical breakdown by microorganisms, often producing alcohol, acids, or gases; figuratively, to develop or stir over time, especially ideas, unrest, or social change.	brew, culture; develop, foment, stir	Let the dough ferment overnight for more flavor. / Political discontent had been fermenting for years before the protests began. / The city was a place where new artistic ideas could ferment.	Do not confuse ferment with foment. Foment is specifically to stir up/encourage trouble or unrest; ferment can describe the broader process of active development. Noun ferment = agitation/excitement, pronounced with first-syllable stress.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: ferment meaning
570	5697	5567	get past the wall	/ɡet ˌpæst ðə ˈwɔːl/	phrase	To get beyond a literal barrier or, figuratively, overcome a point of resistance, blockage, exhaustion, or limitation.	break through, get beyond, overcome the barrier	Once I got past the wall of self-doubt, the writing became much easier.	Not a single fixed idiom with one universal meaning; 'the wall' is supplied by context. In endurance sports, the wall can be the sudden point of extreme fatigue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: get past the wall meaning
570	5698	6287	contravention	/ˌkɑːntrəˈvenʃən/	noun, formal/legal	An act of violating, conflicting with, or failing to comply with a law, rule, agreement, or requirement.	violation, breach, infringement, noncompliance	The construction was carried out in contravention of local regulations. / Sharing the data would be in contravention of the agreement.	Typical formal pattern: in contravention of X. Related verb: contravene. More legalistic than violation and uncommon in casual conversation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define contravention
570	5699	5744	trope vs troupe	/troʊp/; /truːp/	spelling/pronunciation contrast	Trope is a recurring storytelling convention or figurative device; troupe is a group of performers.		The reluctant hero is a familiar trope, while the theater troupe performs it onstage.	Both words already exist separately in Master, but the original search explicitly contrasts the confusable spellings, so retain the contrast card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: tropes vs troupes
570	5700	5628	not know X from Y	/nɑːt ˌnoʊ eks frəm ˈwaɪ/	idiom template, informal	To be unable to distinguish one thing from another, implying extreme ignorance or incompetence in that area.	not know the difference between, be clueless about	He doesn't know talent from toilet tissue, but he still judges every performance.	The searched line 'doesn't know talent from toilet tissue' is a playful nonce variation on the template. A famous vulgar version is 'not know shit from Shinola.' Preserve the user's exact contrast in the example.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: doesn't know talent from toilet tissue meaning
571	5701	5866	stitch someone up	/ˌstɪtʃ ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌp/	phrasal verb, chiefly British informal	To deliberately put someone in a bad position, trick or frame them, especially so they take blame or suffer consequences; literally, to close a wound with stitches.	frame, set up, trick, shaft; suture	He swears the evidence was planted and that somebody stitched him up. / You stitched me up by volunteering me for the presentation without asking. / The doctor stitched him up after the accident.	Strong BrE colloquialism. Figurative sense ranges from serious 'frame for a crime' to joking 'put me in an awkward situation.' Compare set someone up, which can also be positive (arrange a date/opportunity).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: stitch someone up meaning
571	5702	6677	implicit processing vs explicit processing	/ɪmˈplɪsɪt ˈprɑːsesɪŋ/ vs /ɪkˈsplɪsɪt ˈprɑːsesɪŋ/	contrast, cognitive/learning terminology	Implicit processing occurs relatively automatically or without conscious awareness of the rule/process; explicit processing involves conscious attention, deliberate reasoning, or accessible knowledge about what is being processed.	automatic/unconscious processing vs conscious/deliberate processing	A learner may implicitly pick up a word-order pattern from repeated exposure, while explicitly processing a grammar explanation requires conscious attention. / Native speakers often judge a sentence before they can state the rule explicitly.	The boundary is theory/task-dependent; implicit does not simply mean 'unimportant' and explicit does not guarantee verbalizable perfect knowledge. Useful in language learning and cognitive psychology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: implicit processing vs explicit processing
571	5703	5792	can't fault someone for something	/kænt ˈfɔːlt ˌsʌmwʌn fər/	phrase	To acknowledge that someone's action, choice, or reaction is understandable or not reasonably blameworthy.	can't blame, understand	I can't fault them for choosing the safer option.	Often conveys reluctant or fair-minded acceptance even if the speaker dislikes the outcome.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: I can't fault them for that meaning
571	5704	5671	cobbler (drink)	/ˈkɑːblər/	noun	A style of mixed drink traditionally served over crushed ice, often with wine or spirits, sugar, and fruit garnish.	cobbler cocktail	She ordered a sherry cobbler packed with crushed ice and fruit.	Distinct from cobbler = a person who repairs shoes and cobbler = a baked fruit dessert.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: cobbler drink meaning
571	5705	6499	go off without a hitch	/ɡoʊ ˌɔːf wɪˌðaʊt ə ˈhɪtʃ/	idiom	For an event, plan, launch, trip, ceremony, etc. to happen successfully and smoothly without problems, delays, or complications.	go smoothly, go perfectly, run without problems, go according to plan	The import went off without a hitch. / Somehow the wedding went off without a hitch despite the storm. / We tested everything twice so the launch would go off without a hitch.	Existing hitch card mentions 'without a hitch,' but this preserves the very common full construction 'go off without a hitch.' Here go off = happen/proceed, not explode or become spoiled.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: go off without a hitch meaning
571	5706	6007	disc golf	/ˈdɪsk ˌɡɑːlf/	noun	A sport in which players throw flying discs toward elevated metal basket targets, aiming to complete each hole in as few throws as possible.	frisbee golf (informal/trademark-sensitive), Frolf (informal)	We played nine holes of disc golf at the park. / Disc golf uses golf-like ideas such as tees, holes, par, drives, and putting, but you throw a disc instead of hitting a ball.	'Frisbee golf' is commonly understood, but Frisbee is a trademark; disc golf is the standard sport name.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: disc golf
571	5707	6855	dearth	/dɜːrθ/	noun, formal	A scarcity or serious lack of something; an insufficient supply.	scarcity, shortage, lack, paucity	There's no dearth of opinions online, but there is a dearth of careful evidence. / The town faced a dearth of affordable housing.	Usually 'a dearth of X.' Formal/literary. Opposite idea: abundance, glut (depending context).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: dearth definition
571	5708	6429	tu quoque	/tuː ˈkwoʊkwiː/	noun / argumentation term	A response to criticism that deflects by accusing the critic of the same or similar wrongdoing instead of addressing whether the original criticism is true.	appeal to hypocrisy, whataboutism (overlapping), 'you too' fallacy	'You shouldn't pirate games.' 'But you streamed that movie illegally last year.' That's a tu quoque response if it is offered instead of answering the criticism.	Latin 'you too.' Often classified as an ad hominem fallacy. However, pointing out hypocrisy can be relevant to credibility, consistency, enforcement, or fairness; it becomes fallacious when hypocrisy is treated as disproving the underlying claim.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: tu quoque meaning
571	5709	5849	kippered	/ˈkɪpərd/	adjective / past participle; British informal/contextual	Prepared as a kipper by splitting, salting, and smoking a herring; figuratively in some British uses, thoroughly defeated, ruined, exhausted, or caught out.	smoked; done for, exhausted (contextual)	After the overnight shift, he joked that he was completely kippered.	Slang force is context-dependent and less universal than the literal fish sense. Do not assume it always means drunk/exhausted without context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: kippered meaning  uk slang
571	5710	6095	aftertaste	/ˈæftərˌteɪst/	noun	A taste that remains in the mouth after food or drink is swallowed; figuratively, a lingering emotional impression left by an experience, story, conversation, etc.	lingering taste, finish; lingering impression, residue, aftereffect	The coffee has a slightly bitter aftertaste. / The finale is hopeful, but it leaves a strangely melancholy aftertaste. / His apology fixed the immediate problem, yet the argument left a sour aftertaste.	Literal food/drink sense is core. Figurative use is especially useful in reviews: bitter/sour/unpleasant/strange aftertaste = lingering negative or mixed feeling. 'Afterglow' is usually positive; aftertaste can be positive, negative, or mixed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: aftertaste
572	5711	6736	tamper-proof / tamper-resistant / tamper-evident	/ˈtæmpər ˌpruːf/	adjective cluster	Tamper-proof = intended to be impossible or extremely difficult to tamper with; tamper-resistant = designed to resist interference; tamper-evident = designed so interference leaves visible evidence.	secure, tamper-resistant, tamper-evident	The bottle has a tamper-evident seal. / No system is perfectly tamper-proof, so 'tamper-resistant' may be more precise.	Important distinction: tamper-evident does not prevent opening; it reveals it. 'Tamper-proof' is often marketing language and may overstate absolute protection.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: tamper-proof meaning
572	5712	5755	gay chicken	/ˌɡeɪ ˈtʃɪkɪn/	slang / social-game term	A provocative game or joke in which two same-sex participants perform escalating romantic or sexual behavior and the first to pull away is treated as the loser.		The scene plays like a game of gay chicken, with neither character willing to back down.	Informal and culturally loaded; can reinforce stereotypes or trivialize sexuality. Meaning comes from chicken as a contest of who backs down first.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: gay chicken meainng
572	5713	6896	broach a subject	/ˌbroʊtʃ ə ˈsʌbdʒɪkt/	collocation	To introduce or bring up a topic—especially a difficult, sensitive, or potentially awkward one—for discussion.	bring up, raise, introduce, mention	I wasn't sure how to broach the subject of money. / She waited until dinner was over before broaching the topic.	Broach commonly collocates with subject/topic/issue. Do not confuse brooch /broʊtʃ/ = decorative pin; same pronunciation in many accents.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: broach a subject
572	5714	5972	tendinitis / tendonitis	/ˌtendəˈnaɪtɪs/	noun, medical	Inflammation or irritation of a tendon, often causing pain and tenderness near a joint.	tendon inflammation	The repetitive motion aggravated the tendinitis in his wrist. / Shoulder pain can have many causes, so 'tendonitis' is not a label to infer from soreness alone.	Tendinitis is the more etymologically regular spelling; tendonitis is extremely common. Tendinopathy is a broader term for tendon disorders and may be preferred when degeneration rather than acute inflammation is involved.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tendonitis
572	5715	5797	based AF	/ˌbeɪst eɪ ˈef/	internet slang, vulgar intensifier	Extremely based—strongly approved of as bold, unapologetic, authentic, or correct.	very based, based as fuck	Refusing to apologize for that harmless opinion was based AF, according to the comments.	AF = as fuck, a strong vulgar intensifier. Meaning of based remains context-dependent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112, voca_0113 | search: based AF meaning
572	5716	5805	gloat	/ɡloʊt/	verb	To show excessive or smug satisfaction about one's own success or another person's failure.	boast, crow, revel smugly	He couldn't resist gloating over the victory.	Common pattern: gloat over/about something. More specifically smug than ordinary boast.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: gloat meaning
572	5717	6457	whew	/hwuː/; /wjuː/	interjection	An exclamation expressing relief, surprise, exhaustion, amazement, or being impressed/overwhelmed.	phew, wow, whoa	Whew, I thought I'd deleted the whole file. / Whew—that was a long day. / Whew, that price is higher than I expected.	Very conversational and sound-symbolic. Phew overlaps strongly with relief/exhaustion; whew can also express impressed surprise.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: whew meaning
572	5718	6767	I couldn't bounce ideas off anyone about that	/aɪ ˌkʊdənt ˌbaʊns aɪˈdɪəz ɔːf ˈeniwʌn əˌbaʊt ðæt/	usage repair / conversational phrase	A natural way to say you had nobody with whom you could discuss an issue and test your ideas. Raw 'I couldn't bounce off about that' is incomplete because the idiom normally requires an idea/topic object and a person.	I had no one to run it by, I had no one to discuss it with	Back then I couldn't bounce ideas off anyone about that, so I had to work through it on my own.	Grammar: bounce something off someone. You can also say 'I didn't have anyone to bounce that idea off.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: I couldn't bounce off about that meaning
572	5719	5673	factorization	/ˌfæktərəˈzeɪʃən/	noun	The process of expressing a mathematical quantity, expression, or object as a product of factors.	factoring, decomposition into factors	Prime factorization expresses 60 as 2 × 2 × 3 × 5.	Common in arithmetic and algebra; related verb factor/factorize.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: factorization
572	5720	6266	flip someone	/ˈflɪp ˌsʌmwʌn/	verb phrase; context-dependent	To turn someone over physically, cause them to rotate, or—in certain slang contexts—change/recruit someone's allegiance or cooperation. It does NOT by itself normally mean 'give someone the middle finger'; that is flip someone off.	turn over, roll over; convert/recruit, turn (informant)	The instructor flipped him onto his back during the demonstration. / Investigators tried to flip one member of the group into cooperating. / If someone gives you the middle finger, they 'flip you off,' not normally 'flip you.'	Raw search likely tests the missing particle. Important contrast: flip someone off = insult with middle finger; flip someone = turn them over or, contextually, persuade/recruit them to the other side.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: someone flips you meaning
573	5721	5701	toward	/tɔːrd, təˈwɔːrd/	preposition	In the direction of; closer to a time or goal; or in relation to one's attitude, behavior, or contribution concerning someone or something.	in the direction of, regarding	She walked toward the station. / His attitude toward the proposal changed.	Towards is a common variant, especially in British English. Toward is common in American English.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: toward meaning
573	5722	5952	a full-blown cultural autopsy	/ə ˌfʊlˈbloʊn ˌkʌltʃərəl ˈɔːtɑːpsi/	figurative phrase	An exhaustive, often critical dissection of a cultural work, trend, event, community, or phenomenon to explain what happened, what it reveals, and why it succeeded, failed, or decayed.	deep-dive postmortem, cultural postmortem, exhaustive dissection, forensic analysis	What started as a review turned into a full-blown cultural autopsy of the franchise and the fandom around it. / The video isn't just mocking one bad ad; it's practically a cultural autopsy of the era that produced it.	Autopsy is literal examination of a body after death to determine cause; figuratively it suggests forensic, retrospective dissection. Full-blown intensifies it: fully developed, not merely a passing analysis. Particularly natural for long-form video essays/criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: a full-blown cultural autopsy
573	5723	6076	rip (slang: do extremely well / tear through)	/rɪp/	verb, slang/contextual	In some informal contexts, to perform with great energy or effectiveness, move very fast, or tear through a task/opponent; exact meaning depends heavily on construction.	crush it, tear it up, go hard, shred, blaze through	He absolutely ripped on guitar during the last song. / She ripped through the exam and finished early. / 'Go rip!' can work as encouragement, roughly 'go crush it,' but rip by itself does not universally mean 'do my best.'	Raw query asks whether rip means beat/do my best: there is no single slang meaning. 'Rip through X' = move/do quickly; 'rip on guitar' = play intensely/well; 'let it rip' = go ahead at full force; 'rip into someone' = attack verbally. Preserve construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: rip means beat or do my best?
573	5724	6212	swag	/swæɡ/	noun / verb, informal	Stylish confidence, coolness, or fashionable presentation; promotional/free merchandise; historically, stolen goods or a bundle/garland. As a verb, to hang/droop in a curve (older/specialized).	style, swagger, drip (modern slang); merch, freebies	He walked in with ridiculous confidence and somehow made the outfit look like pure swag. / The conference gave everyone a bag of branded swag. / In current fashion slang, 'drip' often sounds newer than 'swag.'	Very polysemous and era-sensitive. 2010s slang swag ≈ style/cool confidence, related to swagger but not identical. Corporate/event swag = branded freebies. Older meanings survive in specialized/historical contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: swag meaning
573	5725	6760	chug away	/ˌtʃʌɡ əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To continue working, moving, or progressing steadily and persistently, often slowly and without drama; literally, an engine/machine may make repetitive chugging sounds while operating.	keep going, plug away, chug along, work steadily	The old train chugged away into the distance. / I wasn't making spectacular progress, but I kept chugging away at the vocabulary project.	'Chug along' is more common for steady progress; 'chug away' emphasizes continued action/sound. Separate from chug a drink = swallow quickly in large gulps.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: chugged away meaning
573	5726	6380	contempt	/kənˈtempt/	noun	A strong feeling that someone or something is beneath respect, worthless, despicable, or deserving of scorn; also, deliberate disregard for an authority, especially in legal 'contempt of court.'	scorn, disdain, disrespect, derision, loathing (overlapping)	She looked at the scammer with open contempt. / He spoke with contempt about anyone he considered weak. / The judge held him in contempt of court for refusing to comply.	Stronger than dislike: contempt combines negative judgment with a sense of superiority or lack of respect. Existing cards use contempt in definitions/synonyms, but this standalone card develops the core concept and legal collocation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define contempt
573	5727	5507	be stumped / I'm stumped	/stʌmpt/	adjective / passive expression	To be unable to think of an answer, explanation, or solution; completely puzzled by a question or problem.	be baffled, be puzzled, be at a loss	I'm stumped—I can't figure out why the program keeps crashing.	Stumped comes from stump as a verb meaning to puzzle or defeat intellectually.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: I'm stumped meaning
573	5728	6666	how you show up day to day	/haʊ ju ˌʃoʊ ˈʌp ˌdeɪ tə ˈdeɪ/	figurative phrase	How you consistently behave, participate, relate to others, perform your role, and embody your values in everyday life—not merely whether you physically arrive.	how you conduct yourself, how you engage, everyday behavior	Character isn't just what you say you value; it's how you show up day to day. / A manager's culture is shaped by how they show up for the team every day.	Modern coaching/workplace/therapy-style use of 'show up.' 'Show up for someone' specifically emphasizes being present/supportive; 'show up as X' emphasizes the manner/identity you bring.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: how you show up day to day
573	5729	5533	strike someone as something	/ˈstraɪk ˌsʌmwʌn æz/	verb construction	To give someone a particular impression; to seem a certain way to someone.	seem to, come across as, appear to	His explanation struck me as unusually careful.	Pattern: X strikes A as B. The thing/person causing the impression is the subject; A is the person receiving it; B is the impression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: strike A as B meaning
573	5730	6223	sputter	/ˈspʌtər/	verb / noun	To make a series of small explosive, spitting, or irregular sounds; to speak in a halting, shocked, angry way; or for an engine/process to run unevenly and falter.	splutter, stammer, cough, choke, falter	The old engine sputtered and died. / He sputtered in disbelief when he saw the bill. / Oil sputtered in the hot pan.	AmE sputter and BrE splutter overlap. Speech sense suggests words coming out brokenly because of emotion. Engine/flame sense = irregular bursts, often before stopping.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sputtered
574	5731	6650	something is a lifestyle	/ˌsʌmθɪŋ ɪz ə ˈlaɪfstaɪl/	figurative/conversational pattern	To say an activity, attitude, aesthetic, habit, or identity is not just occasional but a characteristic, ongoing way of living—often emphatic or joking.	a way of life, part of how one lives, not just a hobby	For him, cycling isn't a hobby; it's a lifestyle. / 'Being late is a lifestyle for her' is humorous exaggeration meaning it's habitual.	'Lifestyle' can be neutral marketing/sociology vocabulary or casual hyperbole. Don't assume deep identity in every joke use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: something is lifestyle meaning
574	5732	6274	pursuant to	/pərˈsuːənt tuː/	preposition phrase, formal/legal	In accordance with, as authorized or required by, or following from a law, rule, agreement, request, or other formal basis.	in accordance with, under, according to, following	The records were released pursuant to a court order. / Pursuant to our agreement, payment is due within thirty days.	Strongly formal/legal/administrative. Usually 'pursuant to X,' not bare pursuant in ordinary conversation. In plain English, 'under' or 'in accordance with' is often more natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pursuant
574	5733	6431	pseudo-intellectual	/ˌsuːdoʊ ˌɪntəˈlektʃuəl/	noun / adjective, disparaging	A person—or style of speech/writing—that tries to appear learned, profound, or intellectually sophisticated without the depth, rigor, understanding, or substance to justify that appearance.	poseur, pretentious thinker, faux intellectual, intellectual show-off	The character throws around philosophers' names to sound deep, but the argument is deliberately written as pseudo-intellectual nonsense. / Calling someone a pseudo-intellectual is itself a strong dismissive judgment.	Disparaging and subjective. Better analysis identifies what is shallow—misused terminology, unsupported claims, name-dropping, fake profundity—rather than using the label alone as an argument.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: pseudo intellectual meaning
574	5734	6098	call upon someone for something	/ˌkɔːl əˈpɑːn ˌsʌmwʌn fər ˌsʌmθɪŋ/	formal verb phrase	To formally ask, appeal to, or rely on someone to provide help, action, expertise, support, or another needed thing.	ask, appeal to, request, turn to, rely on	We may need to call upon outside experts for advice. / In a crisis, the town can call upon volunteers for additional support. / The chair called upon each member for a brief response.	Call upon/on someone to do X = formally request that they act. Call upon someone for X = seek X from them. Also 'call on someone' can mean visit them or choose them to speak, so construction/context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: call upon A for B meaning
574	5735	5883	sanitize (figurative)	/ˈsænəˌtaɪz/	verb	Literally, to clean or disinfect; figuratively, to remove offensive, disturbing, controversial, sensitive, or identifying material so something appears cleaner, safer, or more acceptable.	clean up, scrub, bowdlerize, censor, whitewash, redact (contextual)	The adaptation sanitized the darker parts of the original story. / The report was sanitized before publication to remove identifying details. / You can sanitize user input before storing or displaying it.	Nuance: sanitize a story/history can imply making it artificially clean or harmless; whitewash implies concealing wrongdoing; bowdlerize removes supposedly offensive material; redact obscures specific information; in computing/security sanitize can mean clean/validate data or securely remove data.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: sanitize something meaning slang
574	5736	6912	mammography	/mæˈmɑːɡrəfi/	noun, medicine	Medical imaging of the breasts using low-dose X-rays, especially for screening or diagnostic evaluation.	breast X-ray imaging, mammogram screening	Mammography is used for breast-cancer screening in many health systems. / A mammogram is an image/exam produced through mammography.	Lexical distinction: mammography = imaging technique/process; mammogram = resulting image or commonly the examination itself.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: mammography
574	5737	6946	futsal	/ˈfʊtˌsæl/	noun	A fast-paced form of association football (soccer) played mainly indoors on a hard court, typically with five players per side and a smaller, lower-bounce ball.	indoor five-a-side football, five-a-side soccer (not exact)	We played futsal at the gym because the outdoor field was closed. / Futsal rewards close control, quick passing, and movement in tight spaces.	From Portuguese/Spanish fútbol/futebol + sala/salón ('hall/indoor'). It is a distinct codified sport, not simply any indoor soccer game. Commonly five-a-side including the goalkeeper.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: futsal meaning
574	5738	5656	insuperable	/ɪnˈsuːpərəbəl/	adjective, formal	Impossible to overcome, solve, or surmount.	insurmountable, unconquerable, unbeatable, impossible to overcome	The funding problem proved insuperable. / At first the language barrier seemed insuperable, but it gradually became manageable. / They faced what looked like an insuperable obstacle.	Formal and much less common than 'insurmountable.' Typically modifies obstacle, difficulty, barrier, problem. It means impossible to overcome, not merely very difficult; in ordinary conversation 'insurmountable' is usually more natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: insuperable
574	5739	6526	chances are	/ˌtʃænsɪz ˈɑːr/	idiomatic clause opener	It is likely or probable that something is true or will happen.	probably, most likely, odds are, there's a good chance	Chances are, I've already looked that word up once. / If the restaurant is packed every night, chances are the food is pretty good.	Usually followed by a clause: chances are (that).... Informal and natural. 'Odds are' is close.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: chances are meaning
574	5740	6152	Mesozoic	/ˌmezəˈzoʊɪk/	proper adjective / noun	The geologic era from about 252 to 66 million years ago, comprising the Triassic, Jurassic, and Cretaceous periods and popularly known as the age of dinosaurs.	Mesozoic Era, age of reptiles/dinosaurs (informal)	Dinosaurs first appeared during the Triassic and diversified throughout the Mesozoic. / The Cretaceous is the final period of the Mesozoic Era.	Order: Triassic → Jurassic → Cretaceous. The era ends at the K–Pg mass extinction about 66 million years ago. 'Age of dinosaurs' is convenient but many other organisms flourished too.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define mesozoic
575	5741	6200	send chills down one's spine	/ˌsend ˈtʃɪlz daʊn wʌnz ˈspaɪn/	idiom	To cause a sudden shivery physical/emotional sensation because of fear, awe, excitement, disgust, beauty, or another intense reaction.	give someone chills, make one's skin crawl (negative), send a shiver down one's spine	That final line still sends chills down my spine. / The sound of footsteps in the empty hallway sent a chill down her spine. / Her performance gave me chills in the best possible way.	Raw 'chills down me' is incomplete/nonstandard. Standard patterns: send chills/a chill down someone's spine; give someone chills. The reaction can be positive or negative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: chills down me meaning
575	5742	6716	automated scraping	/ˈɔːtəˌmeɪtɪd ˈskreɪpɪŋ/	computing / web term	Using software to automatically extract data or content from websites, documents, interfaces, or other sources rather than collecting it manually.	web scraping, automated data extraction, crawling (overlaps, not identical)	The research pipeline used automated scraping to collect publicly displayed product prices. / A site may rate-limit or block automated scraping.	Scraping = extracting content/data; crawling = systematically discovering/fetching pages, often as part of scraping/indexing. Legality, terms of service, privacy, copyright, and technical permissions depend on context; the vocabulary term itself is neutral.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: automated scraping
575	5743	5606	beta reader	/ˈbeɪtə ˌriːdər/	noun	A person who reads a draft before publication and gives feedback from a reader's perspective on clarity, pacing, characters, plot, and overall effect.	prepublication reader, feedback reader	I sent the second draft to three beta readers before revising it again.	Common in fiction and fanfiction communities. A beta reader is not necessarily a professional editor or proofreader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: beta reader
575	5744	6578	marketplace of ideas	/ˌmɑːrkɪtpleɪs əv aɪˈdiːəz/	noun phrase / metaphor	The metaphor that ideas should compete openly in public discussion, with people able to compare, challenge, accept, or reject them rather than having truth determined solely by authority.	free exchange of ideas, open debate, intellectual marketplace	Supporters framed the forum as a marketplace of ideas where bad arguments could be challenged openly. / Critics argue that a marketplace of ideas does not automatically reward truth when attention and power are unequal.	Important concept in free-speech discourse, especially U.S. legal/political rhetoric. It is a metaphor, not a guarantee that true ideas inevitably win. Often associated historically with arguments for open discussion and later U.S. jurisprudence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: marketplace of ideas meaning
575	5745	6532	do-gooder	/ˈduːˌɡʊdər/	noun, often disparaging	A person who tries to help others or improve society, especially when the speaker regards them as naive, self-righteous, meddling, or performatively virtuous.	busybody (overlapping), bleeding heart (political/dated), moralizer, well-meaning reformer	The villain mocks the heroes as a bunch of meddling do-gooders. / 'Do-gooder' can turn objectively helpful behavior into something the speaker frames as sanctimonious or naive.	Often pejorative despite literally sounding positive. Tone reveals more about the speaker's attitude than about whether the person's actions are actually good.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: do-gooders meaning
575	5746	6167	Mary Jane (marijuana slang)	/ˌmeri ˈdʒeɪn/	noun, informal slang	A dated/playful slang term for marijuana/cannabis, based largely on wordplay with the Spanish-derived word marijuana.	marijuana, weed, cannabis, pot	The lyric uses 'Mary Jane' as a euphemistic nickname for marijuana. / 'Occasional Mary Jane' would normally mean occasional cannabis use if the context is drugs.	Context matters: Mary Jane is also an ordinary female name and a style of shoe. Cannabis sense can sound retro or euphemistic rather than current neutral terminology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: occasional mary jane meaning
575	5747	6114	accept the weight of someone's feelings	/əkˌsept ðə ˈweɪt əv ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈfiːlɪŋz/	figurative phrase	To recognize the seriousness, emotional force, responsibility, or burden carried by another person's feelings rather than dismissing or trivializing them.	take someone's feelings seriously, acknowledge their emotional weight, honor someone's feelings, hold space for someone (contextual)	She wasn't ready to return his feelings, but she accepted the weight of what he had confessed. / The scene works because he doesn't try to fix her grief; he simply accepts the weight of it.	Not a fixed idiom, but a natural metaphor: weight = seriousness/burden. 'Accept someone's feelings' may mean acknowledge them; 'reciprocate' means feel the same way and is NOT implied. 'Hold space' is more therapeutic/wellness-register.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: accepting the weight of someone’s feelings
575	5748	6207	longback	/ˈlɔːŋˌbæk/	internet/body-shaming slang	A mocking slang label for someone perceived as having a long torso or little visible waist/hip/buttock definition, creating the insult-image of the back continuing unusually far downward.	body-shaming insult, long torso (neutral description)	Commenters called the celebrity a 'longback,' turning an ordinary body proportion into an insult. / 'Long torso' is the neutral description; 'longback' is mocking internet slang.	Pejorative/body-shaming, not an anatomical term. Definitions vary across online communities and often target women. Useful mainly for understanding social-media insults, not for describing people neutrally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: longback meaning
575	5749	6370	prefect	/ˈpriːfekt/	noun	A person appointed to a position of authority or supervision; especially, in some British/Commonwealth schools, a senior pupil given limited disciplinary or organizational responsibilities. Historically, also an administrative official.	student leader, monitor; administrator, governor (historical/contextual)	She was made a school prefect in her final year. / In Harry Potter, prefects are students with extra responsibilities and privileges. / Roman administration also used titles translated as prefect.	School sense is especially familiar from British fiction. A prefect is not simply 'class president'; powers and selection vary by school/system. Latin root relates to someone placed in charge.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: prefect
575	5750	6754	audience retention	/ˈɔːdiəns rɪˌtenʃən/	media / analytics term	The degree to which viewers/listeners continue watching or listening over time; often measured as percentage remaining or average percentage viewed across a video/episode.	viewer retention, watch retention, retention rate	The first thirty seconds had poor audience retention, so the creator shortened the intro. / A retention graph shows where viewers tend to drop off.	Common YouTube/video/podcast analytics term. Distinguish audience retention (how long they stay) from reach/views (how many arrived) and returning audience (whether they come back).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: audience retention meaning
576	5751	6262	that's not really how things are	/ˌðæts nɑːt ˌrɪəli haʊ ˌθɪŋz ˈɑːr/	conversational correction / phrase	A soft, natural way to say that someone's description, assumption, stereotype, or model does not accurately reflect reality.	that's not quite how it works, that's not really the case, reality is more complicated	People sometimes imagine everyone in Tokyo behaves that way, but that's not really how things are. / The video makes dating sound completely rule-bound, and that's not really how things are in practice.	Really softens the correction: it often means 'there may be something to that, but the picture is inaccurate/oversimplified.' Useful alternative to a blunt 'that's wrong.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: this is not really how things are meaing
576	5752	6542	jumble	/ˈdʒʌmbəl/	noun / verb	A confused, disordered mixture; to mix things together in a messy or confused way.	muddle, mess, mixture, scramble, mix up	My notes were a jumble of half-finished ideas. / The app jumbled the cards into the wrong order. / All the dates got jumbled in my head.	Jumbled = mixed/disordered. 'Jumble sale' is BrE for a sale of donated secondhand goods. Compare scramble, muddle, garble.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: jumble meaning
576	5753	6194	Pocky game	/ˈpɑːki ɡeɪm/	noun / Japanese pop-culture game	A game in which two people bite opposite ends of a Pocky stick and move toward the middle, often with the possibility of their lips touching; commonly portrayed as flirtatious or embarrassing.	Pocky challenge, kissing game	The anime episode turns the Pocky game into an awkward romantic challenge. / They stopped halfway because neither of them wanted the joke to become an actual kiss.	Associated with Japanese youth/pop culture and anime/manga portrayals. Similar visual setup to a spaghetti kiss, but deliberately played as a game/challenge using a snack stick.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pocky game
576	5754	6554	get hitched to someone	/ɡet ˌhɪtʃt tə ˌsʌmwʌn/	idiomatic construction, informal	To marry someone; to become joined to them in marriage.	marry someone, get married to someone, tie the knot with someone	She got hitched to her college sweetheart. / He joked that he never expected to get hitched to anyone.	Natural but 'get married to someone' is more neutral. 'Hitched to' can also literally mean physically attached, so context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: got hitched to someone meaning
576	5755	6507	look someone in the eye / look someone in the eyes	/ˌlʊk ˌsʌmwʌn ɪn ði ˈaɪ/	phrase	To make direct eye contact with someone, especially while speaking seriously, honestly, confidently, or confrontationally.	make eye contact with, meet someone's gaze	Look me in the eye and tell me you didn't know. / She couldn't look him in the eye after lying to him. / 'Look me in the eyes' is also natural, especially when emphasizing both eyes/direct gaze.	Singular 'look someone in the eye' is the conventional idiom; plural 'in the eyes' is also grammatical/natural but often feels more literal or emphatic. Compare 'look someone in the face.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: look someone in the eye or eyes
576	5756	6440	bubble to the surface	/ˌbʌbəl tə ðə ˈsɜːrfɪs/	idiom-like metaphor	For a hidden feeling, problem, memory, conflict, fact, or tension to gradually emerge and become noticeable or openly expressed.	surface, emerge, come to light, rise to the surface, resurface	Old resentments began bubbling to the surface during the reunion. / As the story progresses, details about his childhood slowly bubble to the surface.	Often suggests something was already present underneath rather than newly created. 'Come to light' emphasizes discovery; bubble to the surface often feels gradual, organic, and emotionally charged.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: bubbling to the surface meaning
576	5757	6922	recursive structure in an argument	/rɪˈkɜːrsɪv ˈstrʌktʃər ɪn ən ˈɑːrɡjəmənt/	argumentation / conceptual phrase	An argument whose reasoning, premises, objections, or justifications reproduce a similar pattern at another level, or whose justification calls for the same kind of justification again.	nested reasoning, recursive justification, regress-like structure	The debate became recursive: every demand for evidence generated another demand to justify the standard of evidence itself. / His reply embedded the same objection inside a new version of the argument.	Not a single standardized logic term. Preserve as conceptual search. Depending on structure, related ideas include regress, circularity, self-reference, or nested argumentation—but these are not identical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: recursive structures in an argument
576	5758	6785	stodgy	/ˈstɑːdʒi/	adjective, informal	Dull, old-fashioned, overly conventional, heavy, or lacking imagination; of food, heavy and filling.	stuffy, dull, old-fashioned, conservative, heavy	The institution has a stodgy image that it is trying to modernize. / The meal was warm but a bit stodgy.	Can criticize people, writing, organizations, design, traditions, or heavy food. BrE especially common for food/institutional dullness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: stodgy meaning
576	5759	5963	fertility	/fərˈtɪləti/	noun	The ability or capacity to reproduce; also the capacity of land/soil to support abundant growth.	reproductive capacity, fecundity; soil productivity	Age can affect fertility, but it is only one factor. / Farmers monitor soil fertility before deciding what to plant.	Fecundity can mean biological reproductive capacity/potential and is more technical; fertility often concerns actual reproductive capacity/outcomes. Common: fertility rate, fertility treatment, fertility window, soil fertility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define fertility
576	5760	6099	flick	/flɪk/	verb / noun	To make a quick, light, sharp movement or strike, often with a finger, wrist, or small object; to move or switch something quickly. As a noun, a quick movement/strike; informally, a movie ('a flick').	snap, flip, twitch, swat lightly; movie (informal)	She flicked the crumb off the table with one finger. / He flicked his eyes toward the door. / I flicked through the channels for a few minutes. / We watched an old horror flick.	Very productive: flick something away/off; flick through pages/channels; flick a switch; flick one's hair/eyes; flick someone lightly. Existing 'flick someone' is only one construction, so standalone card is broader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: flick meaning
577	5761	5555	as in	/æz ˈɪn/	phrase	Used to clarify what you mean by giving an example, restatement, spelling cue, or more specific sense of a word.	meaning, for example, namely	I mean 'light' as in 'not heavy,' not 'bright.'	Very common clarification pattern: X as in Y. It points to the intended example or sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: as in meaning
577	5762	6147	Anatosaurus	/əˌnætəˈsɔːrəs/	proper noun / historical dinosaur genus name	A historical genus name once used for certain hadrosaur fossils, especially material now generally classified within Edmontosaurus; the name is largely obsolete in modern dinosaur taxonomy.	hadrosaur; Edmontosaurus (modern classification context)	Older dinosaur books may label a duck-billed dinosaur 'Anatosaurus,' while newer references usually place the relevant fossils in Edmontosaurus. / The name survives in older pop-culture and educational material.	Useful as historical vocabulary rather than a current standard genus. Dinosaur taxonomy changes as fossils are re-evaluated; older names can persist long after scientific classification shifts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: anatosaurus meaning
577	5763	5800	emotional contour	/ɪˌmoʊʃənəl ˈkɑːntʊr/	noun phrase	The shape, progression, or changing pattern of emotion across a story, scene, performance, conversation, or experience.	emotional arc, emotional shape	The scene keeps the same plot beats but changes the emotional contour completely.	Analytical/metaphorical phrase: contour treats emotional rises, falls, transitions, and intensity as a shape.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: emotional contour meaning
577	5764	6246	droop	/druːp/	verb / noun	To hang, bend, or sink downward limply; to lose firmness, energy, or spirit. As a noun, a downward sag or hanging shape.	sag, hang down, wilt, slump, sink	The flowers drooped in the heat. / His shoulders drooped when he heard the result. / The ears droop naturally on that breed. / By midnight my eyelids were starting to droop.	Useful literal-to-emotional mapping: plants/body parts hang downward; posture can droop with tiredness/disappointment. Existing droopy adjective is related but not a duplicate of the productive verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: droops
577	5765	6279	coitus	/ˈkoʊɪtəs/	noun, formal/clinical	Sexual intercourse, especially penile-vaginal intercourse in formal, medical, biological, or deliberately clinical language.	sexual intercourse, intercourse, copulation	The medical questionnaire uses the formal term coitus rather than everyday sexual vocabulary. / In ordinary conversation, 'sex' or 'sexual intercourse' is much more natural than coitus.	Formal/clinical and somewhat old-fashioned outside technical contexts. It can sound comically clinical in casual speech. Do not assume it covers every kind of sexual activity; traditionally it refers specifically to intercourse.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define coitus
577	5766	6824	lift someone off their feet	/ˌlɪft ˌsʌmwʌn ɔːf ðer ˈfiːt/	literal phrase	To raise a person so their feet leave the ground, physically by force, embrace, impact, etc. Figuratively, 'sweep someone off their feet' is the established romantic idiom.	pick up, hoist, raise; sweep off one's feet (figurative)	He hugged her so hard he lifted her off her feet. / The blast lifted him off his feet.	Raw 'lifted off one's feet' is most naturally literal. Do not automatically substitute 'swept off one's feet,' which means suddenly charm/romance someone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120, voca_0121 | search: lifted off one's feet
577	5767	6183	psyop	/ˈsaɪˌɑːp/	noun, slang / military abbreviation	Originally shorthand for psychological operation: organized activity intended to influence attitudes, emotions, beliefs, or behavior. Online, jokingly or conspiratorially, 'psyop' labels something suspected of manipulating public perception or taste.	psychological operation, influence operation; manipulation campaign (broader)	The military sense refers to planned psychological operations aimed at influencing an audience. / Online, people jokingly call an aggressively promoted trend a psyop. / 'This movie is a psyop' may be pure hyperbole, not a factual allegation.	Internet use has broadened dramatically and is often unserious. Calling something a psyop does not establish coordinated manipulation. Distinguish actual documented influence operations from meme/conspiracy usage.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: psyop meaning
577	5768	6759	emo cosplay	/ˈiːmoʊ ˌkɑːzpleɪ/	informal / context-dependent phrase	Dressing or presenting oneself in an exaggerated emo aesthetic as a costume, imitation, or temporary persona; can be neutral description or a dismissive accusation that someone's emo identity/style is performative.	emo-style costume, performative emo aesthetic	The outfit looked less like everyday emo fashion and more like emo cosplay. / Calling someone's style 'emo cosplay' can imply you think they're only performing the identity.	Not a fixed technical term. Cosplay literally means costume play, but figurative 'X cosplay' often criticizes someone for imitating an identity/role without authenticity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: emo cosplay meaning
577	5769	6203	a discerning eye	/ə dɪˌsɜːrnɪŋ ˈaɪ/	noun phrase	An ability to notice fine differences, quality, flaws, authenticity, or significance and make good judgments about what one sees.	good eye, critical eye, trained eye, perceptive judgment	A discerning eye can spot when an expensive-looking set is actually cheaply dressed. / She has a discerning eye for typography and tiny spacing problems.	Extends existing discerning adjective into a common collocation. 'Critical eye' can emphasize scrutiny; 'trained eye' emphasizes expertise; discerning eye emphasizes selective good judgment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: discerning eye meaning
577	5770	6797	be more on alert about something	/bi ˌmɔːr ɑːn əˈlɜːrt əˌbaʊt/	usage repair	To be more watchful or ready to notice a possible problem. Raw 'more on alarm about' is unnatural; use 'more alert to,' 'more vigilant about,' 'more on guard against,' or 'on higher alert for.'	be more alert to, be more vigilant about, be on guard against	After that scam, I'm more alert to messages that create artificial urgency. / We're on higher alert for duplicate entries now.	'On alert' is natural, but 'on alarm' is not the usual expression. Choose preposition by phrase: alert to, vigilant about, on guard against, on alert for.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: being more on alarm about something
578	5771	6032	give off a vibe	/ˌɡɪv ɔːf ə ˈvaɪb/	informal phrase	To create or convey a particular intuitive impression, atmosphere, personality, or emotional feeling.	give the impression, come across as, exude, radiate, have an X vibe	He gives off a friendly, slightly awkward vibe. / The empty hotel gives off a creepy 1980s-horror vibe even in daylight.	Existing give off WID2740 covers the general phrasal verb; this collocation is independently useful because 'vibe' is an impression rather than literal emission. 'Come across as X' focuses more directly on how a person seems.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: give off the vibe that meaning
578	5772	5909	monotony	/məˈnɑːtəni/	noun	The tiring sameness that comes from little or no change, variety, or interest.	sameness, repetitiveness, tedium, dullness, uniformity	Working from the same room every day can become monotonous, so I sometimes go to a café just to break the monotony. / The show's visual style is beautiful, but the pacing eventually settles into monotony.	Common: break/relieve the monotony; the monotony of daily life/work. Monotony emphasizes repetitive sameness; boredom is the feeling that may result from it; tedium emphasizes how tiresome something feels.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: monotony meaning
578	5773	6792	comply with social norms	/kəmˌplaɪ wɪð ˌsoʊʃəl ˈnɔːrmz/	usage contrast / phrase	Grammatically possible, but often less natural than 'conform to social norms' because comply usually suggests explicit rules, demands, requests, or requirements; use it when norms are treated as obligations.	obey, adhere to, conform to (more idiomatic for norms)	Employees must comply with the dress code. / People tend to conform to social norms. / 'Comply with social norms' works when the norms are framed as rules one is expected to obey.	Search-intent contrast: comply with rules/laws/requests; conform to norms/standards/patterns. Overlap exists, but collocational preference matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: comply with social norms meaning
578	5774	6390	Double Dutch	/ˌdʌbəl ˈdʌtʃ/	noun	A jump-rope activity using two long ropes turned in opposite directions while one or more people jump between them; historically/informal BrE, 'double Dutch' can also mean incomprehensible language or gibberish.	two-rope jump rope; gibberish (dated/BrE)	The kids practiced Double Dutch in the playground. / The instructions sounded like double Dutch to me—an older expression meaning they were incomprehensible.	Capitalize when treating the sport/activity name. The 'gibberish' sense belongs to a family of old English expressions using Dutch stereotypically and may sound dated; the jump-rope sense is culturally important in U.S. urban communities.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: double dutch meaning
578	5775	5553	know something inside out	/ˌɪnsaɪd ˈaʊt/	idiom	To know something extremely thoroughly, including its details and how it works.	know thoroughly, know backwards and forwards	She knows the software inside out after using it for ten years.	Inside out literally means with the inner surface turned outward; figuratively it suggests complete knowledge from every angle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: know something inside out meanign
578	5776	6924	prestige	/preˈstiːʒ/	noun	High status, respect, admiration, or social esteem associated with a person, institution, occupation, product, cultural form, etc.	status, esteem, distinction, cachet, standing	The university's prestige attracts applicants from around the world. / The job pays less but carries considerable prestige.	Usually uncountable. Prestige can be institutional/social and need not reflect intrinsic quality. Attributive use: prestige brand, prestige project.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define prestige
578	5777	6525	a slippery slope	/ə ˌslɪpəri ˈsloʊp/	idiom / argument term	A process or situation in which one step is said to lead to a chain of increasingly serious, difficult, or undesirable consequences; also the argument that allowing A will eventually lead to Z.	thin end of the wedge (BrE, related), dangerous progression, chain of escalation	Skipping one workout isn't a slippery slope by itself. / Critics called the policy a slippery slope toward broader surveillance.	A slippery-slope argument is not automatically fallacious: the key question is whether the proposed causal/institutional chain is supported. Often used loosely for habits that may escalate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: a slippery slope
578	5778	5695	how best to do something	/ˌhaʊ ˈbest tə/	grammar pattern	Used to ask or consider the most effective, suitable, or advantageous way to do something.	best way to, how to most effectively	We discussed how best to explain the change to customers.	Natural compact alternative to 'what is the best way to...'.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: how best to do meaning
578	5779	6428	fair-weather fan	/ˌfer ˌweðər ˈfæn/	noun	A fan who supports a team, person, franchise, or cause mainly when things are going well, but loses interest or loyalty during failure or difficulty.	bandwagon fan (overlapping), fickle fan, glory supporter (BrE sports)	He only started wearing the jersey after they made the finals, so everyone called him a fair-weather fan. / A fair-weather fan disappears as soon as the team starts losing.	From fair-weather friend: someone dependable only in good times. Bandwagon fan emphasizes joining because something becomes successful/popular; fair-weather emphasizes abandoning support in bad times.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: fair weather fan
578	5780	6245	hieroglyph / hieroglyphs	/ˈhaɪrəˌɡlɪf/	noun	A picture-like written symbol, especially one in ancient Egyptian hieroglyphic writing; more loosely, an unreadable or mysterious-looking symbol or mark.	hieroglyphic character, pictorial symbol, glyph	The wall was covered in Egyptian hieroglyphs. / His tiny handwritten notes looked like hieroglyphs to me.	Egyptian hieroglyphs are a full writing system using signs that can represent sounds, words, or semantic categories—not simply 'pictures that mean objects.' Informal plural can mean indecipherable marks.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: hieroglyphs
579	5781	5639	take a beat	/ˌteɪk ə ˈbiːt/	informal phrase	To pause briefly before continuing, often to think, calm down, or let a moment land.	pause, take a moment	Let's take a beat before we respond to that email.	From beat as a brief unit/pause, especially in performance and conversation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: take a beat meaning
579	5782	6651	pedigree	/ˈpedɪɡriː/	noun	An animal's recorded ancestry/lineage; by extension, a person's, institution's, product's, or idea's distinguished background, origins, qualifications, or track record.	lineage, ancestry, bloodline; credentials, provenance, background	The dog has an impressive pedigree. / The director has a strong comedy pedigree. / The theory has an intellectual pedigree stretching back centuries.	Figurative pedigree often evaluates credibility/status from origins or past associations. Can sound elitist when applied to people/social background.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: pedigree meaning
579	5783	6060	gooner	/ˈɡuːnər/	noun, internet sexual slang; also proper fandom term	In current internet sexual slang, a person who engages heavily in 'gooning'—prolonged, highly absorbed pornography/masturbation sessions. Separately, Gooner is a longstanding nickname for an Arsenal F.C. supporter.	gooning participant; Arsenal fan (separate sense)	Online, 'gooner' may be thrown around jokingly as an insult for someone perceived as porn-brained. / In football context, 'a Gooner' can simply mean an Arsenal supporter.	Two unrelated senses can collide. Sexual-slang sense is explicit and often insulting/memetic. Arsenal sense comes from the club's 'Gunners' nickname and should not be confused with it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: gooners meaning
579	5784	6027	blend in with	/ˌblend ˈɪn wɪð/	phrasal verb	To look, behave, or fit sufficiently like the surrounding people/environment that one does not attract attention or seem separate.	fit in with, merge with, camouflage oneself among, disappear into	Wear something neutral if you want to blend in with the crowd. / The new character blends in with the group so naturally that you forget she joined halfway through the season.	Blend in emphasizes not standing out visually/socially; fit in emphasizes social acceptance/compatibility. A color/object can blend in with surroundings without any social meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: blend in with meaning
579	5785	5688	at cost	/æt ˈkɔːst/	phrase	At the amount it cost the seller to obtain or produce something, with no profit added.	at cost price, without markup, at the seller's cost	Employees can buy the equipment at cost. / He sold me the spare part at cost and didn't make a cent on it. / The nonprofit provides the books at cost.	Means for the seller's purchase/production cost, with no profit markup. Crucial contrast: 'at a cost' = with a sacrifice, disadvantage, or expense ('Success came at a cost'). 'Cost price' is common in BrE.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: at cost
579	5786	5826	grift	/ɡrɪft/	noun / verb, informal	A scheme for obtaining money, attention, or advantage through deception, manipulation, or opportunistic exploitation; to engage in such a scheme.	scam, con, hustle	Critics accused the influencer of turning the controversy into a grift.	Modern online use can broaden beyond literal fraud to cynical monetization or opportunism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: grift meaning
579	5787	6562	make a fool out of someone	/ˌmeɪk ə ˈfuːl aʊt əv/	idiom	To make someone look stupid, ridiculous, gullible, or embarrassed, especially in front of others; 'make a fool out of me' can imply humiliation or deception.	humiliate, embarrass, make someone look stupid, play someone for a fool	Are you trying to make a fool out of me? / He felt the prank had made a fool out of him in front of everyone.	Also 'make a fool of someone'—often more concise and idiomatic. 'Make a fool of yourself' = behave in a way that makes yourself look ridiculous.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: making a fool out of me meaning
579	5788	6130	coast	/koʊst/	verb	To move without much power or effort, especially using momentum; figuratively, to progress or succeed with little effort, often because the hard work is already done or one's advantage is large.	glide, roll, cruise; sail through, breeze through	Take your foot off the pedal and let the bike coast downhill. / The team built a huge lead and coasted to victory. / He coasted through the class on natural ability and barely studied.	Figurative coast can be neutral ('win easily') or critical ('stop trying and rely on past success/talent'). 'Coast along' often suggests easy progress; 'coast through' = complete with little effort.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: coasted
579	5789	6885	uncalled	/ˌʌnˈkɔːld/	adjective, uncommon outside specific uses	Not called, summoned, requested, or selected; much less common in everyday speech than the idiom 'uncalled for.'	unsummoned, unsolicited, not called	Several numbers remained uncalled in the lottery. / For 'inappropriate or unnecessary,' say 'uncalled for,' not simply 'uncalled.'	Search likely tests distinction. 'Uncalled-for' may also be hyphenated before a noun: an uncalled-for remark.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: uncalled
579	5790	6675	wishy-washy	/ˈwɪʃi ˌwɑːʃi/	adjective, informal	Weak, indecisive, vague, lacking firm conviction, or insufficiently definite/forceful.	indecisive, vague, noncommittal, weak-kneed, equivocal	His answer was so wishy-washy that I still don't know what he actually supports. / The ending felt wishy-washy instead of committing to either interpretation.	Usually critical. Can describe opinions, people, language, policy, storytelling, colors/flavors that feel weak or diluted.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: wishy washy meaning
580	5791	6513	tutelage	/ˈtuːtəlɪdʒ/; /ˈtjuːtəlɪdʒ/	noun	Instruction, training, guidance, or protection provided by a teacher, mentor, or guardian; especially in 'under someone's tutelage.'	instruction, mentorship, guidance, training, supervision	She studied under the tutelage of a master animator. / Under his coach's tutelage, her technique improved rapidly.	More formal than teaching. Fixed collocation 'under the tutelage of.' Historically also guardianship/protection.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: tutelage
580	5792	6106	dynamics	/daɪˈnæmɪks/	plural noun	The forces, patterns, interactions, and relationships that shape how a group, relationship, system, or situation behaves and changes.	interactions, interplay, relationships, power structure, forces at work	The show becomes much more interesting once the group dynamics start to shift. / Their friendship has a strange power dynamic because one of them is also the other's boss. / Adding a third character completely changes the dynamics of the scene.	Often plural: family/group/team/relationship/power dynamics. 'Dynamic' can be a single pattern/relationship; dynamics emphasizes the interacting forces as a whole. Not the same as 'dynamic' = energetic/changing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: dynamics meanig
580	5793	5931	spring up	/ˌsprɪŋ ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To appear, arise, grow, or develop suddenly or quickly.	pop up, crop up, emerge, arise, mushroom	New cafés have sprung up all over the neighborhood. / Fan theories sprang up almost immediately after the trailer dropped. / Wildflowers spring up here after the first warm rains.	Can describe buildings/businesses, problems, movements, plants, rumors, ideas. Past: sprang up; past participle: sprung up.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: spring up meaning
580	5794	6611	paltry	/ˈpɔːltri/	adjective	Very small, meager, inadequate, or contemptibly insignificant in amount, quality, or value.	meager, pitiful, trifling, negligible, miserly	They offered a paltry $20 for a full day's work. / The update added a paltry two features after a year of development.	Often conveys contempt: not merely small, but embarrassingly insufficient. Raw companion search 'paultry' was a misspelling.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: paltry definition
580	5795	5858	beat the rap	/ˌbiːt ðə ˈræp/	idiom, informal	To avoid conviction or punishment for a criminal charge, especially despite having been accused.	escape conviction, get off	The defendant beat the rap when the key evidence was excluded.	Rap here = criminal charge/accusation in older slang. Often implies escaping legal consequences.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: beat the rap meaning
580	5796	5988	bit vs troupe	/bɪt/ vs /truːp/	contrast card	A bit is a short routine, joke, comic premise, or recurring piece of performance; a troupe is a group of performers. The two are not equivalent.	routine, gag, sketch (bit) vs company, ensemble, group (troupe)	The comedian did a bit about airport security. / The improv troupe performed three shows that weekend. / Their fake argument became a recurring bit on the podcast.	Likely raw question 'a bit is like a troupe?' Answer: no. Bit = piece of material/performance; troupe = people performing. Sketch is usually a more developed scripted comic scene; bit can be much smaller or improvised.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: a bit is like a troupe?
580	5797	6490	poise	/pɔɪz/	noun / verb	Calm, graceful self-control and confidence, especially under pressure; physical balance/elegance. As a verb, to hold or position something ready for action.	composure, self-possession, grace, balance, aplomb	She answered the hostile question with remarkable poise. / The dancer moved with poise and precision. / He stood poised to leave as soon as the meeting ended.	Poise often combines composure + graceful confidence. 'Poised to do X' = ready/in position to do it, often because conditions are favorable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: poise meaning
580	5798	5572	the flower of one's youth	/ðə ˈflaʊər əv wʌnz ˈjuːθ/	literary phrase	The prime, freshest, or most beautiful period of one's youth.	prime of youth, bloom of youth	He remembered those summers as the flower of his youth.	Literary/old-fashioned metaphor: flower = the finest or most flourishing stage of something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: the flower of my youth
580	5799	6943	crack the code	/ˌkræk ðə ˈkoʊd/	idiom	To decipher a code; figuratively, to discover the key principle, pattern, method, or solution needed to understand or succeed at something difficult.	decipher, solve, figure out, unlock the secret, get the hang of	After months of listening practice, I finally felt like I was cracking the code of fast casual speech. / Researchers tried to crack the enemy code.	Figurative use can be playful/hyperbolic: crack the code of dating, marketing, pronunciation. It implies a hidden underlying pattern.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: crack the code meaning
580	5800	5816	lava	/ˈlɑːvə/	noun	Molten rock that has erupted onto Earth's surface, and the rock formed when it cools and solidifies.	molten rock at the surface, volcanic melt	Lava flowed slowly down the side of the volcano. / The lava cooled into dark volcanic rock. / Thick, viscous lava can move very differently from more fluid lava.	Magma is molten or partially molten rock beneath Earth's surface; once erupted at the surface it is called lava. 'Lava' can refer to the molten material and, in geology, the rock formed from a lava flow after cooling. Do not confuse lava /ˈlɑːvə/ with larva /ˈlɑːrvə/.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define lava
581	5801	6229	cuck	/kʌk/	noun / verb, vulgar internet slang	Short for cuckold: traditionally, a man whose spouse/partner is sexually unfaithful. Online, a broader insult implying weakness, humiliation, submissiveness, or being dominated; also used in consensual sexual-fetish terminology with a more specific meaning.	cuckold; weak/submissive man (insult), beta (related insult)	The forum uses 'cuck' as a generic insult for any man it considers weak. / In the traditional sense, cuckold refers specifically to a man whose partner is unfaithful.	Highly derogatory and often associated with manosphere/political trolling. Internet insult sense has drifted far from the literal relationship meaning. Do not assume consensual fetish context unless context supports it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: cuck
581	5802	6902	ressentiment	/rəˌsɑːntɪˈmɑːn/	noun, philosophy	A deep, persistent resentment associated especially with perceived powerlessness, in which hostility may be redirected into moral judgments or value systems that condemn what one cannot attain or confront directly.	resentment, rancor, grievance (partial)	Nietzsche's discussion of ressentiment is not simply about feeling annoyed; it concerns resentment becoming psychologically and morally productive. / The critic interpreted the movement through the lens of ressentiment.	French loanword used especially in Nietzschean/philosophical discourse. Do not flatten it to ordinary resentment: the theoretical sense involves reactive value-formation and powerlessness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: ressentiment
581	5803	6796	virtuous cycle	/ˌvɜːrtʃuəs ˈsaɪkəl/	noun	A self-reinforcing sequence in which one positive development causes another, which feeds back and produces further improvement—the positive counterpart of a vicious cycle.	positive feedback loop, reinforcing loop, upward spiral	More listening improves comprehension, which makes listening more enjoyable, which leads to more listening—a virtuous cycle. / Better service increased trust and repeat business, creating a virtuous cycle.	Common economics/business/policy term. 'Virtuous' here means beneficial, not morally pure. Opposite: vicious cycle.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: virtuous cycle
581	5804	5848	hot take	/ˈhɑːt ˌteɪk/	noun, informal	A strongly stated, provocative, surprising, or quickly formed opinion, often designed to attract attention or spark argument.	provocative opinion, controversial take	Hot take: the sequel is better than the original.	Existing take (noun) mentions hot take, but the compound has its own internet/media connotations and is useful independently.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: hot take meaning
581	5805	6104	dating-app fatigue	/ˈdeɪtɪŋ æp fəˌtiːɡ/	noun phrase	Mental or emotional exhaustion, frustration, boredom, or cynicism caused by prolonged use of dating apps and repeated swiping, messaging, matching, rejection, or shallow interactions.	dating burnout, swipe fatigue, app burnout	After months of repetitive small talk and ghosting, I developed serious dating-app fatigue. / She deleted the apps for a while because swipe fatigue was making dating feel like a second job.	A contemporary descriptive term rather than a clinical diagnosis. Burnout is stronger and broader; fatigue can describe simple weariness/disillusionment. Related phenomena include choice overload and dating-app burnout.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: dating app fatigue
581	5806	5751	catty competition	/ˌkæti ˌkɑːmpəˈtɪʃən/	noun phrase	Competition marked by spiteful, snide, gossipy, or cutting interpersonal behavior.	snide rivalry, spiteful competition	The show turns the friendly contest into catty competition between the leads.	Existing catty card gives the adjective; preserve this searched collocation because it captures how the adjective colors a rivalry/competition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: catty competition meaning
581	5807	5536	on a roll	/ɑːn ə ˈroʊl/	idiom	Experiencing a continuing series of successes or doing something very well repeatedly.	on a winning streak, in the zone	She has won five matches in a row—she's really on a roll.	Usually positive momentum: one success seems to lead naturally into the next.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: on a roll meaning
581	5808	5782	disingenuity	/ˌdɪsɪndʒəˈnuːəti/	noun	Lack of candor or sincerity; the quality of being disingenuous or pretending not to know, understand, or intend something.	insincerity, bad faith, evasiveness	The apology's careful wording gave it an air of disingenuity.	Less common than disingenuousness, but established. Do not confuse with ingenuity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: disingenuity
581	5809	5558	stick around (with someone)	/ˌstɪk əˈraʊnd/	phrasal verb	To remain in a place, situation, or relationship instead of leaving; with someone, to continue staying near or associated with them.	stay, remain, hang around	I decided to stick around for another hour and see what happened.	The core idiom is stick around. 'Stick around with someone' is possible when the context is staying with that person, but the particle normally belongs to stick around, not *stick around with* as one fixed phrasal verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: stick around with someone
581	5810	5569	here we go again	/ˌhɪr wi ˌɡoʊ əˈɡen/	expression	Said when an annoying, familiar, or predictable situation is starting to happen again.	not this again, same old story	Here we go again—another argument about whose turn it is to clean up.	Usually expresses weary recognition, irritation, or amusement at repetition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: here we go again meaning
582	5811	6707	continue down the path	/kənˌtɪnjuː daʊn ðə ˈpæθ/	literal + figurative phrase	To keep moving along a route; figuratively, to keep pursuing the same course of action, development, behavior, or decision trajectory.	stay on the course, keep going in that direction, proceed	If we continue down this path, the app will become harder to maintain. / The story continues down a darker path in season two.	Common conceptual metaphor: decisions/development as travel along a path. Often used as warning: 'If we continue down this path...'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: continue down the path meaning
582	5812	6828	trinket	/ˈtrɪŋkɪt/	noun	A small ornament, piece of jewelry, souvenir, or decorative object, usually of little monetary value.	bauble, knickknack, ornament, gewgaw, keepsake	The market stalls sold cheap trinkets and souvenirs. / She kept a few childhood trinkets in a box.	Can be affectionate or dismissive. Unlike keepsake, trinket often suggests small/decorative and not especially valuable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: trinkets meaning
582	5813	6509	miasma	/maɪˈæzmə/; /miˈæzmə/	noun	A heavy, unpleasant, or oppressive atmosphere, vapor, smell, or influence; historically, a supposed disease-causing 'bad air.' Figuratively, a pervasive cloud of corruption, fear, gloom, confusion, etc.	haze, pall, cloud, atmosphere, vapor	A miasma of smoke hung over the valley. / The story is soaked in a miasma of dread and decay. / Nineteenth-century miasma theory wrongly attributed many diseases to poisonous bad air.	Excellent gothic/literary word. Figurative 'a miasma of X' suggests something diffuse and pervasive. Historical miasma theory is obsolete but culturally important.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: miasma meaning
582	5814	6393	tampering	/ˈtæmpərɪŋ/	noun / gerund	Unauthorized, improper, or secret interference with something in order to alter, damage, manipulate, influence, or compromise it.	interference, manipulation, meddling, alteration	The seal is designed to reveal evidence of tampering. / Witness tampering is a serious legal offense. / The results were investigated for possible data tampering.	Common compounds: evidence tampering, witness tampering, product tampering, data tampering. Base pattern: tamper with X. Usually implies improper/unauthorized interference, stronger than ordinary adjustment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define tampering
582	5815	6639	as we know it	/ˌæz wi ˈnoʊ ɪt/	idiom	In the familiar/current form in which we understand or experience something; used when imagining major change or destruction of that form.	in its present form, as it currently exists	The internet changed publishing as we knew it. / That discovery could transform medicine as we know it. / 'The end of the world as we know it' means the end of the familiar world/order, not necessarily literal planetary destruction.	Tense often follows viewpoint: as we know it now; as we knew it then. Common dramatic phrase: X as we know it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: as we know it meaning
582	5816	6161	put all one's eggs in one basket	/ˌpʊt ɔːl wʌnz ˈeɡz ɪn wʌn ˈbæskɪt/	idiom	To risk all one's resources, hopes, money, time, or plans on a single option so that one failure could cause a major loss.	bet everything on one thing, concentrate all risk, go all-in (overlapping)	Don't put all your eggs in one basket—apply to more than one program. / I wouldn't put all my savings into one stock.	Usually appears as advice not to concentrate risk. 'Go all-in' can be deliberate/positive; eggs-in-one-basket framing emphasizes vulnerability from lack of diversification.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Putting all your eggs in one basket
582	5817	6576	same with something	/ˌseɪm wɪð ˌsʌmθɪŋ/	comparison pattern	Used to say that the same statement, pattern, problem, rule, or observation also applies to another thing.	the same goes for, likewise with, similarly for	The app is slow on my phone, and it's the same with the desktop version. / People overuse that phrase in reviews; same with 'masterpiece.'	Very productive pattern. 'The same goes for X' is often smoother when extending a claim. Compare 'same as' for direct equivalence/comparison.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: same with something meaning
582	5818	5962	infertility	/ˌɪnfərˈtɪləti/	noun	Difficulty or inability to achieve pregnancy/reproduction; medically, definitions use specified periods of regular unprotected intercourse and vary with age/context.	subfertility, reproductive difficulty, impaired fertility	The couple sought medical advice after struggling with infertility. / Some conditions affect fertility without making pregnancy impossible.	Infertility does not necessarily mean absolute sterility. Fertility = capacity/likelihood of reproduction; subfertility = reduced fertility; sterility can imply inability to reproduce. Existing cards mention fertility issues, but the standalone term is useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define infertility
582	5819	5711	dawg	/dɔːɡ/	noun, slang	A stylized slang form of dog used as a friendly way to address a person, similar to dude, bro, or friend.	dude, bro, homie	What's up, dawg? I haven't seen you in forever.	Strongly associated with informal American speech and hip-hop/pop culture. Tone and appropriateness depend on speaker/context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: dawg meaning
582	5820	6864	pang	/pæŋ/	noun	A sudden sharp physical pain or brief intense feeling, especially guilt, regret, sadness, jealousy, hunger, or longing.	twinge, stab, ache, pang of emotion	She felt a pang of guilt after reading the message. / A sudden pang of hunger reminded me I'd skipped lunch.	Usually 'a pang of X'; plural pangs common for recurring/intense feelings: pangs of conscience, hunger pangs.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pangs meaning
583	5821	5990	comatose	/ˈkoʊməˌtoʊs/	adjective	In a coma and unable to be awakened; informally/hyperbolically, extremely sleepy, inactive, or unresponsive.	unconscious, in a coma; inert, dead to the world (figurative)	He remained comatose after the accident. / After the overnight flight I was practically comatose on the couch.	Medical sense is serious and specific. Casual hyperbole is common but should not be confused with actual coma. 'Comatose market/room' can figuratively mean lifeless/inactive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define comatose
583	5822	5511	overstatement	/ˈoʊvərˌsteɪtmənt/	noun	A statement that makes something seem greater, stronger, more important, or more extreme than it really is.	exaggeration, hyperbole	Calling the minor delay a disaster was an overstatement.	Natural opposite of understatement; can be deliberate or simply excessive wording.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: overstatement meaning
583	5823	6422	loads of	/ˈloʊdz əv/	informal quantifier	A large amount or number of something; much/many, especially in casual speech.	lots of, tons of, heaps of, plenty of, a load of	I've got loads of bookmarks left to process. / There were loads of people at the festival. / We still have loads of time.	Very common informal BrE and widely understood elsewhere. Works with count and uncount nouns. 'A load of' can also mean a large amount, but 'a load of rubbish' has its own dismissive flavor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: loads of synonym
583	5824	5786	dull and snide	/ˌdʌl ən ˈsnaɪd/	adjective pairing	Both boring/uninteresting and slyly contemptuous or mean in tone.	boring and sneering, tedious and cutting	The dialogue felt dull and snide rather than witty.	Not a fixed idiom. Preserve the searched pairing: dull describes lack of interest/liveliness; snide describes contemptuous indirect meanness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: dull and snide
583	5825	6488	a total wash	/ə ˌtoʊtəl ˈwɑːʃ/	contextual informal phrase	A complete failure, null result, or situation in which gains and losses cancel out; exact nuance depends on context. More standard related forms are 'a wash' (= no net advantage / roughly even) and 'a washout' (= complete failure).	a wash, a washout, a total loss, a bust (contextual)	If the extra revenue is exactly canceled by the extra cost, it's basically a wash. / If the speaker said 'a total wash' to mean complete failure, 'a total washout' would often be more idiomatic.	Raw phrase is understandable but not a strongly fixed standard idiom. Preserve search provenance while distinguishing 'a wash' from 'a washout' instead of pretending they are identical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: total wash meaning
583	5826	6432	sapient	/ˈseɪpiənt/	adjective, formal/literary	Wise or possessing wisdom; in science fiction/philosophy, capable of higher reasoning, reflective intelligence, or human-like intellectual understanding, often contrasted with merely sentient.	wise, intelligent, rational, self-aware (overlapping, not identical)	Humans are named Homo sapiens from a Latin root associated with wisdom. / The novel distinguishes sentient animals that can feel from sapient species capable of complex reasoning and civilization.	Everyday English rarely uses sapient outside literary/SF/philosophical contexts. Sentient = capable of sensation/experience; sapient = wise or higher-order reasoning. Real scientific/philosophical boundaries are not perfectly captured by the SF shorthand.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: sapient meaning
583	5827	5680	fuzzy	/ˈfʌzi/	adjective	Covered with soft fibers; blurred or indistinct; or unclear, imprecise, and difficult to define or remember.	fluffy; blurry; vague	My memory of that conversation is a little fuzzy.	Common figurative senses: fuzzy memory, fuzzy logic/boundaries, warm and fuzzy. Existing warm and fuzzy is a specific idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define fuzzy
583	5828	6286	poaching	/ˈpoʊtʃɪŋ/	noun / gerund	Illegally hunting, catching, or taking wildlife; by extension, improperly recruiting someone else's employees/clients or taking resources/opportunities regarded as belonging to another party.	illegal hunting, wildlife trafficking; talent poaching, client poaching	The reserve increased patrols to stop elephant poaching. / The rival company was accused of poaching engineers from the team. / Restaurants sometimes complain about competitors poaching staff.	Core wildlife sense involves illegal taking. Business/sports sense is figurative and may be legal but viewed as aggressive/unethical. Also poach = cook gently in liquid, an unrelated common verb sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define poaching
583	5829	6670	when it comes down to it	/wen ɪt ˌkʌmz ˈdaʊn tə ɪt/	idiom	When the situation reaches the essential point, final decision, or moment when action really matters; ultimately, in practical terms.	ultimately, when all is said and done, at the end of the day, when it really matters	When it comes down to it, I'd rather have a smaller deck I actually use. / He talks confidently, but when it comes down to it, will he actually do it?	Related but different from 'X comes down to Y' = X is essentially determined/reduced to Y. This phrase is a clause-level discourse marker.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: when it comes down to it is this
583	5830	5617	psychopomp	/ˈsaɪkoʊˌpɑːmp/	noun	A being, deity, spirit, or figure believed to guide the souls of the dead to the afterlife.	guide of souls	Hermes is sometimes described as a psychopomp who escorts souls to the underworld.	From Greek roots meaning roughly 'soul guide.' Appears in mythology, religion, fantasy, and literary criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: psychopompとは
584	5831	5580	in line with	/ɪn ˈlaɪn wɪð/	prepositional phrase	Consistent or in agreement with a rule, expectation, policy, trend, value, or standard.	consistent with, in accordance with, aligned with	The change is in line with our long-term strategy.	Often used in formal/business contexts to express conformity or alignment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: in line with meaning
584	5832	6770	no fuss, no frills	/ˌnoʊ ˈfʌs noʊ ˈfrɪlz/	informal slogan-like phrase	Simple and straightforward, with neither hassle/ceremony ('fuss') nor unnecessary extras/luxuries ('frills').	simple and straightforward, hassle-free and basic, no-nonsense	The place is no fuss, no frills: good food, quick service, nothing fancy.	Not a rigid fixed idiom, but a natural rhythmic pairing. 'No fuss' emphasizes ease/lack of bother; 'no frills' emphasizes lack of extras.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: no fuss no-frills meaning
584	5833	5823	price in	/ˌpraɪs ˈɪn/	phrasal verb, finance/economics	To incorporate an expected event, risk, or piece of information into the current price or valuation of an asset, product, or market.	factor into the price, discount	Markets had already priced in the expected rate cut.	Very common in finance: X is priced in / investors price in X.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: price in meaning
584	5834	5522	turn up	/ˌtɜːrn ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To appear or arrive, often unexpectedly; to be found after being missing; also to increase the level of something such as volume or heat.	show up, appear, surface; increase	My missing keys finally turned up under the sofa.	Highly polysemous. Core useful senses: appear/arrive, be found, and increase a setting.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: turn up meaning
584	5835	5574	keep that in mind	/ˌkiːp ðæt ɪn ˈmaɪnd/	idiom	To remember and continue considering a fact because it may affect what you think or do next.	bear that in mind, remember that	The road gets icy after dark, so keep that in mind when you leave.	That can refer back to an entire previous statement or warning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: keep that in mind meaning
584	5836	5718	viscous	/ˈvɪskəs/	adjective	Thick and sticky in consistency, resisting flow; in physics, having relatively high viscosity.	thick, syrupy, sticky, glutinous, high-viscosity	Honey is more viscous than water. / The sauce becomes more viscous as it cools. / Lava can range from relatively fluid to extremely viscous depending on composition and temperature.	Physics/materials sense: a viscous fluid strongly resists flow; viscosity is the property. Everyday 'thick' is close but less precise. Do not confuse viscous /ˈvɪskəs/ with vicious /ˈvɪʃəs/.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111, voca_0121 | search: define viscous
584	5837	6485	complicit	/kəmˈplɪsɪt/	adjective	Involved in or helping to enable wrongdoing, especially knowingly or through cooperation, support, or acquiescence.	involved, implicated, collusive, accessory to, enabling	The report alleged that several managers were complicit in hiding the problem. / He worried that staying silent would make him complicit.	Common pattern: complicit in X / complicit with someone. Related noun complicity. Stronger than simply 'involved' because wrongdoing is implied.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: complicit meaning
584	5838	6272	inflict	/ɪnˈflɪkt/	verb	To cause someone to experience something painful, harmful, unpleasant, or damaging; commonly used with injury, pain, suffering, damage, punishment, or a defeat.	cause, impose, administer, deal, wreak	The attack inflicted serious damage on the building. / She didn't want to inflict that kind of pain on him. / The team inflicted its first defeat of the season on the defending champions.	Typical pattern: inflict X on/upon someone/something. Usually the object is negative. Compare 'afflict': a disease/problem afflicts a person; a person/action inflicts harm on someone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: inflict
584	5839	6326	pompously	/ˈpɑːmpəsli/	adverb	In an overly self-important, grand, formal, or dignified manner that seems affected or ridiculous.	pretentiously, grandiosely, self-importantly, affectedly	He pompously announced that only he truly understood the film. / The waiter character speaks pompously about an extremely ordinary sandwich.	Usually critical. Pompous describes inflated dignity/self-importance; pretentious more broadly means trying to appear more cultured/important than one really is.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0117 | search: pompously
584	5840	6628	balance scale	/ˈbæləns ˌskeɪl/	noun	A weighing instrument that compares an unknown mass with known masses by balancing two sides/pans around a central pivot; also a common symbol of justice/fairness.	beam balance, two-pan balance, scales	The old laboratory used a balance scale with weights. / The scales of justice are conventionally shown as a balance.	Often simply 'balance' or 'scales.' Distinct from a modern bathroom/kitchen scale that directly displays weight/mass.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: balance scale
585	5841	5833	full-blown	/ˌfʊl ˈbloʊn/	adjective	Fully developed, complete, or at the most serious/intense stage.	fully developed, complete, outright, full-scale, fully fledged (contextual)	A small disagreement turned into a full-blown argument. / What started as a hobby became a full-blown business. / By evening, the mild headache had turned into a full-blown migraine.	Means something has developed to a complete, unmistakable, or intense stage. Common with crisis, war, panic, addiction, illness, investigation, obsession. Often marks escalation: 'turn into/become a full-blown X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: full-blown
585	5842	6928	canonical order	/kəˌnɑːnɪkəl ˈɔːrdər/	noun phrase	The standard, conventional, officially defined, or normalized ordering of items in a system, representation, sequence, language, or dataset.	standard order, normalized order, conventional order	The serializer puts the fields into canonical order before comparison. / The language has a canonical word order, though other orders are possible.	Technical meaning varies by field. Canonical = chosen/recognized standard representation; it need not mean the only possible order.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: canonical order meaning
585	5843	6539	deets	/diːts/	plural noun, informal/playful	Details; the important or interesting information about something.	details, info, scoop, particulars	Send me the deets when you know the time and place. / Okay, I saw the post—now give me the deets.	Very informal/playful shortening of details. Existing 'hear the deets on something' card contains it, but the raw definition search justifies a clean standalone base card. Common: get/give/send the deets.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: deets meaning
585	5844	6709	come to the forefront	/ˌkʌm tə ðə ˈfɔːrfrʌnt/	idiom	To become especially prominent, important, visible, or central in attention/discussion.	come to prominence, move to center stage, become salient	Privacy concerns came to the forefront after the data leak. / A minor character comes to the forefront in the final episodes.	Existing forefront card covers 'at the forefront'; this preserves the change-of-state construction come to the forefront.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: come to the forefront meaning
585	5845	5775	venture into something	/ˈventʃər ˌɪntuː/	verb phrase	To enter a place, field, subject, or activity that is unfamiliar, uncertain, or potentially risky.	enter, branch into, explore	The company ventured into the streaming market.	Can be literal or figurative: venture into the woods / venture into politics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: venture into meaning
585	5846	5675	preposition + -ing / participial phrase	/ˌprepəˈzɪʃən/	grammar pattern	A structure in which a preposition is followed by an -ing form functioning as a gerund, or a participial phrase is used to modify or frame a clause; the two structures should not be conflated.		After finishing the report, she went home. / He left without saying goodbye.	Important distinction: after/without + -ing can be preposition + gerund; 'After finishing...' can also be analyzed as a supplement/participial clause depending on grammar framework. Preserve as a grammar-comparison card rather than force one label.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: preposition + ing  participle phrases
585	5847	6719	let up on someone	/ˌlet ˈʌp ɑːn/	phrasal verb	To reduce or stop pressure, criticism, demands, punishment, harassment, or intensity directed at someone.	ease up on, go easier on, stop pressuring, give a break	The coach finally let up on him after he improved. / Can you let up on her a little? She already knows she made a mistake.	Person-target construction makes the pressure sense especially clear. Compare weather/pain 'let up' = become less intense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: let up on someone meaning
585	5848	5906	scrounger	/ˈskraʊndʒər/	noun, chiefly British informal	A person who habitually obtains things—especially money, food, or help—from others rather than providing for themselves; someone who scrounges.	freeloader, moocher, sponger, parasite (stronger)	He's always scrounging cigarettes off people; everyone thinks he's a bit of a scrounger. / The tabloid portrayed benefit claimants as scroungers.	Often derogatory and socially loaded, especially in phrases like 'benefit scrounger.' Scrounge can also neutrally mean search around to obtain something cheaply or from scraps.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: scrounger
585	5849	5799	jack someone up	/ˌdʒæk ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌp/	phrasal verb, informal	To injure, damage, upset, agitate, or otherwise affect someone badly; in other contexts, to raise something or sharply increase a price.	mess up, injure, upset	That fall really jacked me up for a few days.	Very context-dependent. 'Jacked me up' can be physical injury, emotional disturbance, or strong stimulation; jack up prices = raise sharply.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: jacked me up meaning
585	5850	5754	lack thereof	/ˌlæk ðerˈʌv/	formal phrase	The absence or insufficiency of the thing just mentioned.	absence of it, want of it, deficiency of it	Success depends on preparation—or the lack thereof. / The problem wasn't bad data so much as a complete lack thereof. / Experience, or the lack thereof, can change how the scene is interpreted.	Formal/compact phrase that refers back to the previously mentioned noun and avoids repetition. 'Thereof' = of that/of it. Common construction: 'X—or the lack thereof.' It can sound slightly legalistic or wry in casual prose.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: lack thereof meaning
586	5851	6809	neurodivergence	/ˌnʊroʊdaɪˈvɜːrdʒəns/	noun	Variation in neurological/cognitive functioning that diverges from what is treated as typical; often used as an umbrella concept in neurodiversity discourse for conditions or traits such as autism and ADHD.	neurological difference, cognitive divergence (contextual)	The discussion focused on neurodivergence and how different people process sensory input. / Neurodivergence is broader than any one diagnosis.	Usage and scope vary by community/context; it is not itself a specific medical diagnosis. Related: neurodivergent (adj/person descriptor), neurodiversity (variation across a population).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: neurodivergence meaning
586	5852	6582	butcher something	/ˌbʊtʃər ˌsʌmθɪŋ/	verb, informal figurative	To perform, pronounce, translate, edit, adapt, explain, or otherwise handle something very badly, damaging or mangling it.	mangle, botch, massacre, garble, ruin	I'm probably going to butcher his last name, so correct me if I say it wrong. / The adaptation butchered the ending. / The subtitles completely butchered the joke.	Very common figurative hyperbole. Literal butcher = slaughter/cut up meat. Figurative use is especially common with names/pronunciation, songs, translations, stories, and performances.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: butcher something meaning
586	5853	6667	isekai slop	/ˌiːsəkaɪ ˈslɑːp/	fandom slang, disparaging	A dismissive label for formulaic, low-effort, derivative, or mass-produced-feeling isekai media; 'flop' instead means a work that fails commercially/critically or is simply mocked as unsuccessful.	generic isekai, formulaic isekai, low-effort content	He called the show 'isekai slop' because it recycled the same overpowered-protagonist setup. / A show can be slop without being a flop if it is formulaic but commercially successful.	Raw search asks slop or flop: use slop for perceived low-quality/formulaic content; flop for failure/poor performance. Both are subjective/informal judgments.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: isekai slop or flop?
586	5854	6771	bells and whistles	/ˌbelz ən ˈwɪsəlz/	idiom, plural noun	Extra features, enhancements, decorative touches, or impressive add-ons beyond the basic function of something.	extras, add-ons, fancy features, frills	The app has lots of bells and whistles, but I mainly care whether the core study mode works. / We chose the basic model without all the bells and whistles.	Often contrasts with no-frills/bare-bones. Can be positive (useful extras) or mildly dismissive (flashy but unnecessary).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: bells and whistles meaning
586	5855	6712	a major industry player	/ə ˌmeɪdʒər ˈɪndəstri ˌpleɪər/	business phrase	A company, organization, or sometimes individual with substantial influence, market presence, resources, reputation, or strategic importance within an industry.	major player, industry leader, key competitor, heavyweight	The company grew from a niche startup into a major industry player. / Being famous does not automatically make a firm a major player if its market share is tiny.	Player is figurative: an actor/participant with influence in a field. 'Major player in the industry' is also very common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: to be a major industry player meaning
586	5856	6029	have a point	/ˌhæv ə ˈpɔɪnt/	idiom	To have a valid, reasonable, or persuasive argument or observation, even if it is not the whole story.	be right about something, make a valid point, have a case	I don't agree with his conclusion, but he has a point about the pacing. / You may have a point—doing it now would save us another trip tomorrow.	Often useful for partial agreement: 'You have a point, but...' Stronger 'have a case' often implies evidence/argument supporting a position; 'point taken' acknowledges the other person's point.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: have a point meaning
586	5857	5923	a menace to society	/ə ˌmenɪs tə səˈsaɪəti/	phrase	Someone portrayed as dangerously or seriously harmful to the public; jokingly, a chaotic person whose behavior is comically antisocial or troublesome.	public menace, danger to society; absolute menace (joking)	The prosecutor called him a menace to society. / You put pineapple on all three pizzas? You're a menace to society.	The dramatic phrase is often hyperbolically reused online for trivial behavior. This joking register is worth recognizing separately from genuinely serious legal/social condemnation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: menace of society meaning
586	5858	6260	pew	/pjuː/	noun	A long bench with a back, traditionally arranged in rows for people to sit on in a church or other place of worship.	church bench, bench	They sat in the back pew during the service. / Sunlight from the stained-glass windows fell across the empty wooden pews.	Not related to internet/comic 'pew pew' gun-sound imitation. In many churches pews are fixed wooden benches, though seating arrangements vary.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pew meaning
586	5859	6171	shudder	/ˈʃʌdər/	verb / noun	To shake or tremble suddenly, especially from fear, disgust, cold, or a strong emotional reaction; figuratively, a machine/building can shake violently.	tremble, quiver, shiver, recoil	I shudder every time I remember that scene. / She shuddered at the thought of doing it again. / The old train shuddered to a stop.	Common patterns: shudder at the thought; shudder with fear/disgust; make someone shudder. Shiver is strongly associated with cold; shudder often conveys a brief involuntary reaction to something disturbing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: shuddered
586	5860	6516	hang-up / hangups	/ˈhæŋˌʌp/	noun, informal	An emotional inhibition, insecurity, persistent worry, prejudice, fixation, or psychological difficulty that interferes with relaxed behavior; also, a problem/obstacle.	insecurity, inhibition, complex, fixation, issue	He has a hang-up about his height. / I used to have a lot of hangups about speaking English in front of people. / Don't let one technical hang-up derail the whole project.	Often 'have a hang-up about/over X.' Plural hangups common. Distinct from 'hang up' verb (end a phone call) and 'a hang-up' can also mean a delay/problem.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: hangups meaning
587	5861	6930	gait	/ɡeɪt/	noun	A person's or animal's manner or pattern of walking/moving on foot; in animals, also a particular mode such as walk, trot, canter, gallop.	walk, stride, manner of walking, locomotion pattern	I recognized him from his distinctive gait before I saw his face. / The horse changed gait from a walk to a trot.	Medical/biomechanical uses: gait analysis, abnormal gait. Do not confuse gate /ɡeɪt/; they are homophones.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define gait
587	5862	6047	ruminate / ruminating	/ˈruːməˌneɪt/; /ˈruːməˌneɪtɪŋ/	verb / gerund	Literally, for a ruminant animal to chew cud; figuratively, to think repeatedly and deeply about something. In psychology, rumination often means repetitive, difficult-to-stop focus on distressing thoughts or feelings.	ponder, mull over, reflect on, dwell on, brood over	I've been ruminating on what he said about language learning. / She kept ruminating over the mistake long after everyone else had moved on. / Cows ruminate by regurgitating and rechewing partially digested food.	Neutral/literary ruminate on/about = contemplate deeply. Psychological rumination is often negative and repetitive, closer to dwell/brood than productive reflection. The figurative sense comes from 'chewing over' thoughts like cud.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: what does ruminating means
587	5863	5731	bungle	/ˈbʌŋɡəl/	verb / noun	To handle or perform something clumsily or incompetently, causing mistakes or failure; also an instance of such mishandling.	botch, mishandle, mess up	They bungled the rollout by sending customers the wrong instructions.	Existing cards mention bungle as a synonym, but this base verb is independently useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: bungle meaning
587	5864	6524	gauge the audience	/ˌɡeɪdʒ ði ˈɔːdiəns/	verb phrase	To assess the audience's mood, knowledge, interest, expectations, reactions, or likely response so you can adjust what you say or do.	read the room, assess the audience, take the audience's measure	A good speaker gauges the audience before deciding how technical to get. / I told one joke first to gauge the audience's reaction.	Gauge = estimate/measure. 'Read the room' is more idiomatic for immediate social mood; gauge the audience can be broader and more deliberate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: gauge the audience
587	5865	5743	extrapolate	/ɪkˈstræpəˌleɪt/	verb	To estimate or infer something beyond known data, observations, or experience by extending an existing pattern or trend.	project, infer, extend	It's risky to extrapolate national trends from such a small sample.	Contrast interpolate, which estimates within the range of known data.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111, voca_0112 | search: extrapolate meaning
587	5866	6597	light as a feather, stiff as a board	/ˌlaɪt æz ə ˈfeðər ˌstɪf æz ə ˈbɔːrd/	chant / cultural phrase	A traditional children's party/sleepover levitation game and chant in which participants try to lift a person while repeating the phrase.	sleepover levitation game, party chant	At sleepovers they used to play 'light as a feather, stiff as a board.'	Cultural phrase, not a normal comparison idiom as a whole. The two halves individually use similes: light as a feather; stiff as a board.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: light as a feather stiff as a board
587	5867	6602	teacher-facing vs student-facing	/ˈtiːtʃər ˌfeɪsɪŋ/ vs /ˈstuːdənt ˌfeɪsɪŋ/	contrast	Teacher-facing materials/interfaces are intended for instructors; student-facing ones are intended to be directly viewed or used by learners.	instructor-facing vs learner-facing	The answer key is teacher-facing, while the quiz itself is student-facing. / We need separate teacher-facing controls and a simpler student-facing screen.	Useful design/education distinction. 'Facing' identifies the primary user/audience, not physical direction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: teacher facing vs student facing
587	5868	6142	RTA	/ˌɑːr tiː ˈeɪ/	initialism; context-dependent	An abbreviation with many possible expansions; the intended meaning depends entirely on context. Common examples include road traffic accident (especially BrE/medical), real-time attack in gaming, and regional transit/transport authorities.	road traffic accident; real-time attack; regional transit authority (contextual)	The chart listed 'RTA' as the cause of injury, meaning road traffic accident. / In gaming discussion, RTA may refer to a real-time attack or a real-time speedrun category depending on the community.	Do not memorize RTA as one universal meaning. Preserve the source context whenever possible. This card is intentionally context-sensitive because the raw search supplied only the abbreviation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: rta meaning
587	5869	6624	end up crying wolf	/ˌend ʌp ˌkraɪɪŋ ˈwʊlf/	usage / nuance card	To end up behaving like someone who repeatedly raises false alarms. The phrase is grammatical, but whether it fits depends on whether the repeated behavior actually involves exaggerated/false warnings.	end up raising false alarms	If you exaggerate every minor problem as a catastrophe, you'll end up crying wolf. / Merely 'doing that repeatedly' is not enough unless 'that' is false/exaggerated alarming.	Raw sentence 'If you keep doing that, you'll end up crying wolf' needs antecedent context. Preserve the semantic condition: crying wolf is specifically false/exaggerated alarm, not generic repetition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: If you keep doing that, you’ll end up crying wolf.
587	5870	6841	jump out at someone	/ˌdʒʌmp ˈaʊt æt/	idiom	To be immediately noticeable, striking, surprising, or attention-grabbing to someone.	stand out, catch one's eye, leap out, be conspicuous	The typo jumped out at me as soon as I opened the page. / Nothing in the first paragraph really jumps out at me.	Figurative visual/mental salience. Also literal 'someone jumped out at me' = suddenly emerged/attacked/startled, so context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: jump out at me meaning
588	5871	5850	Scouser	/ˈskaʊsər/	noun, informal	A person from Liverpool, England; sometimes also someone who speaks with the Liverpool/Scouse accent.	Liverpudlian	He's a Scouser born and raised near the city centre. / The comedian's unmistakably Scouse accent immediately gave away where he was from. / Not every person from Merseyside necessarily identifies with the label in exactly the same way.	Informal demonym for a person from Liverpool; 'Scouse' also names the Liverpool accent/dialect and historically a type of stew. Usually neutral/in-group but, like many regional labels, tone can make it affectionate, stereotypical, or derogatory. 'Liverpudlian' is the more formal demonym.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: scouser meaning slang
588	5872	5562	claw one's way up	/ˌklɔː wʌnz weɪ ˈʌp/	idiom	To advance to a higher position or better situation through persistent, difficult, often fiercely competitive effort.	fight one's way up, struggle upward	She clawed her way up from an entry-level job to senior management.	Vivid image of climbing by gripping with claws; can suggest ruthless competitiveness depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: claw one's way up meaning
588	5873	5996	settle for friendship / settle for being friends	/ˌsetəl fər ˈfrendʃɪp/	phrase	To accept a friendship when one wanted a romantic relationship or something more, often implying compromise or disappointment.	accept being friends, remain just friends, settle for less (contextual)	He wanted to date her, but eventually decided he'd rather settle for friendship than lose her completely. / 'Settle for being friends' can sound as though friendship is a consolation prize, so the framing matters.	Natural forms: settle for friendship / settle for being friends. Settle for X generally means accept something less than what one ideally wanted. Compare 'decide to stay friends,' which is more neutral and does not imply second-best.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: settle for friends meanng
588	5874	6137	go off on a tangent	/ˌɡoʊ ɔːf ɑːn ə ˈtændʒənt/	idiom	To suddenly start discussing or pursuing a topic that is only loosely related—or unrelated—to the main subject.	digress, go off-topic, wander from the point, branch off	I asked about the ending and somehow went off on a tangent about 1990s anime dubbing. / He tends to go off on tangents, but half the time they're the most interesting part.	Especially common for speech/writing/thought. Tangent is a side direction from the main line. Can be mildly critical or affectionate; not necessarily a problem.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: go off on a tangent
588	5875	6784	abhorrent	/əbˈhɔːrənt/	adjective, formal	Extremely offensive, morally repugnant, hateful, or disgusting.	repugnant, detestable, appalling, odious, loathsome	She described the abuse as morally abhorrent. / I find that kind of cruelty abhorrent.	Strong moral condemnation, more formal than disgusting/awful. Common pattern 'abhorrent to someone' or simply 'morally abhorrent.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: abhorrent meaning
588	5876	6206	rizz / rizzing skills	/rɪz/	slang concept / noun phrase	Flirting charm or ability to attract someone romantically; 'rizzing skills' means one's skill at flirting, talking, presenting oneself, or creating attraction.	game, flirting skills, charm, charisma (overlapping)	His rizz is mostly confidence and good timing rather than pickup lines. / She joked that his rizzing skills disappeared the second he actually liked someone.	Existing rizz cards cover the word/action; this contextual card preserves the searched idea of rizz as a learnable 'skill set.' Rizz is playful Gen-Z/internet slang and can already sound self-conscious/ironic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: rizzing skills meaning
588	5877	6852	churn out	/ˌtʃɜːrn ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To produce large quantities of something continuously or rapidly, often routinely and sometimes with an implication of formulaic or mediocre quality.	mass-produce, crank out, pump out, turn out	The studio churns out several low-budget sequels a year. / He can churn out competent articles quickly, but they sometimes feel formulaic.	Can be neutral about high output, but often mildly critical: quantity/regularity over care or originality. Common with content, products, graduates, reports.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: churn out definition
588	5878	6911	hoarseness	/ˈhɔːrsnəs/	noun	A rough, breathy, weak, strained, or raspy quality of the voice, often caused by irritation or problems affecting the vocal folds/larynx.	raspiness, rough voice, huskiness (partial), dysphonia (clinical broader term)	After shouting all night, he woke up with noticeable hoarseness. / Persistent hoarseness can make speaking tiring.	Everyday symptom word. Hoarse = adjective. Dysphonia is a broader clinical term for impaired voice quality/function.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: hoarseness
588	5879	6681	invariance	/ɪnˈveriəns/	noun	The property of remaining unchanged under specified changes, transformations, conditions, or variation.	constancy, stability, invariability	Researchers look for perceptual invariance across different speakers. / In mathematics and physics, an invariant property remains unchanged under a defined transformation.	Technical meaning always depends on 'invariant with respect to what?' In speech perception, the problem of invariance concerns stable linguistic categories despite variable acoustic signals.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: invariance
588	5880	5713	moor	/mʊr/	noun / verb	An open area of uncultivated upland, especially in Britain; as a verb, to secure a boat or vessel in place with ropes, anchors, or similar means.	heath; berth, secure	They walked across the windswept moor. / We moored the boat near the pier.	Two major unrelated senses. Related existing unmoored is figurative 'not anchored/stable.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: moor meaning
589	5881	5842	that's telling	/ðæts ˈtelɪŋ/	expression	That fact, detail, reaction, or omission reveals something significant or revealing about the underlying situation or person.	that's revealing, that's significant	He praised everyone except his own manager. That's telling.	Telling as an adjective = revealing or significant, as in a telling detail/sign.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: That’s so telling
589	5882	6346	dismay	/dɪsˈmeɪ/	noun / verb	A feeling of distress, disappointment, alarm, or discouragement caused by something unexpected or unwelcome; as a verb, to cause that feeling.	distress, alarm, disappointment, consternation; discourage	To my dismay, the file was corrupted. / Fans reacted with dismay when the cancellation was announced. / She was dismayed to discover that the train had already left.	Common pattern 'to someone's dismay.' Stronger/more formal than disappointment and often includes alarm or discouragement. Dismayed = distressed/disappointed by an unwelcome development.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: dismay
589	5883	6543	in the rearview mirror	/ɪn ðə ˌrɪrvjuː ˈmɪrər/	idiom / metaphor	In the past and left behind, especially a problem, period, event, or experience one has moved beyond.	behind us, in the past, over and done with	That stressful launch is finally in the rearview mirror. / By then, college was already disappearing in the rearview mirror.	Based on seeing what is behind a car in its rearview mirror. Often 'put/leave X in the rearview mirror.' Can also literally refer to the mirror.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0119 | search: rearview mirror phrase
589	5884	5857	fiddle with something	/ˈfɪdəl wɪð/	phrasal verb, informal	To touch, adjust, experiment with, or make small changes to something, often repeatedly or without a clear plan.	tinker with, mess with, tweak	I fiddled with the settings until the picture looked right.	Can imply minor, fussy, or potentially unhelpful adjustments.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: fiddle with meaning
589	5885	6907	up my neck	/ˌʌp maɪ ˈnek/	usage note / nonstandard fragment	Not normally a standalone English idiom for irritation or burden. Natural expressions include 'up to my neck in X' = deeply involved/overwhelmed, 'a pain in the neck' = annoying, and 'get on my nerves' = irritate me.	up to my neck in, pain in the neck, get on my nerves	I'm up to my neck in work this week. / That constant buzzing gets on my nerves. / *It's up my neck* is not the normal idiom for 'it's annoying me.'	Preserves a previously discussed confusion. 'Up my neck' can occur literally or inside larger phrases, but do not substitute it for 'on my nerves/under my skin.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: up my neck
589	5886	5772	prevail	/prɪˈveɪl/	verb	To prove stronger or more successful; to be widespread or dominant; or, with upon/on, to persuade someone.	triumph, win; predominate	After a long debate, common sense prevailed.	Common patterns: prevail over/against; conditions that prevail; prevail upon someone to do something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: prevail meaning
589	5887	6216	whip out something	/ˌwɪp ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb, informal	To produce, pull out, or bring out something suddenly and quickly, often dramatically or unexpectedly.	pull out, produce, bring out, brandish (weapon-specific/stronger)	He whipped out his phone and showed me the screenshot. / The character suddenly whipped out a gun, changing the whole tone of the scene. / She whipped out a notebook and started taking notes.	Not inherently weapon-related; object can be phone, card, cash, tool, etc. With a weapon, 'brandish' adds threatening display, while whip out focuses on sudden production.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: whip out a gun meaning
589	5888	6687	ledger	/ˈledʒər/	noun	A book, file, or record in which financial transactions, credits, debits, or accounts are systematically recorded; figuratively, an imagined record of favors, harms, obligations, or moral credits/debts.	account book, register, record, books	The accountant entered the payment in the ledger. / Relationships become exhausting if every favor goes into a mental ledger.	Key contrast with ledge /ledʒ/: ledger has /-ər/ and is an accounting record. Digital accounting systems still use ledger terminology.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ledger meaning
589	5889	5837	copyright strike	/ˈkɑːpiˌraɪt straɪk/	noun, online platforms	A formal copyright-enforcement action against online content or an account, often following a rights-holder complaint and potentially carrying platform penalties.	copyright enforcement action, infringement strike	The channel received a copyright strike after uploading the full clip.	Platform-specific rules differ. A strike is not necessarily the same as an automated claim or simple takedown notice.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: copyright strike
589	5890	6566	strait-laced	/ˌstreɪt ˈleɪst/	adjective	Very strict, conventional, morally proper, or easily shocked by behavior outside accepted rules.	prim, prudish, puritanical, proper, conventional	His strait-laced parents hated the show's crude humor. / She looks strait-laced at work but is completely different with close friends.	Historically also straight-laced spelling occurs, but strait-laced is the traditional form; strait = narrow/restricting. Often mildly critical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: Strait-laced
590	5891	6310	fan the flames	/ˌfæn ðə ˈfleɪmz/	idiom	To make an already tense, angry, controversial, or dangerous situation worse by encouraging, intensifying, or adding fuel to it.	inflame, exacerbate, stir up, fuel, aggravate	Posting the rumor only fanned the flames of the controversy. / Instead of calming everyone down, his sarcastic reply fanned the flames.	Literal image: fanning a fire supplies oxygen and makes it burn harder. Common objects: flames of anger, hatred, conflict, controversy. Opposite idea: defuse/de-escalate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: fanning the flames meaing
590	5892	5940	Commonwealth (international use)	/ˈkɑːmənˌwelθ/	proper noun / political term	Usually the Commonwealth of Nations: a voluntary association of independent countries, many but not all with historical links to the British Empire.	Commonwealth of Nations	Canada, India, Australia, and many other countries are members of the Commonwealth. / The Commonwealth is not a single country or government.	Internationally, capitalized 'the Commonwealth' commonly refers to the Commonwealth of Nations. Commonwealth can also be part of official state names or describe a political community; U.S. states such as Massachusetts, Virginia, Pennsylvania, and Kentucky use 'Commonwealth' in their formal names without a different constitutional status from other states.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: commonwealth meaning internationaly
590	5893	5683	not up for discussion	/nɑːt ˌʌp fər dɪˈskʌʃən/	phrase	Not open to debate, negotiation, or reconsideration.	nonnegotiable, not open for debate, settled, not up for negotiation	The safety requirement is not up for discussion. / Whether we back up the Master first is not up for discussion. / He made it clear that the deadline was not up for discussion.	Firm, often authoritative wording that closes debate rather than merely expressing a preference. Compare 'not up for debate' (very close) and 'not up for negotiation' (especially terms/deals). Can sound controlling if used in interpersonal contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: not up for discussion meaning
590	5894	6807	jocular	/ˈdʒɑːkjələr/	adjective, somewhat formal	Fond of joking or characterized by joking, playful humor, especially in manner or tone.	jovial, joking, playful, humorous, facetious	He made the comment in a jocular tone, so nobody took it as a serious insult. / Her usually formal emails became surprisingly jocular.	More formal than funny/jokey. Describes manner/tone/person; can coexist with teasing or mild derision.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: Jocular
590	5895	6267	rug pull	/ˈrʌɡ ˌpʊl/	noun, finance/crypto/internet slang	A scheme or sudden betrayal in which people attract trust, money, or participation and then abruptly withdraw support, liquidity, funds, or the promised project, leaving others with losses; from 'pull the rug out from under someone.'	exit scam, sudden withdrawal, betrayal, pull the rug out	Investors accused the token creators of a rug pull after the liquidity vanished. / Fans joked that cancelling the project after months of hype felt like a rug pull.	Especially common in cryptocurrency/NFT contexts, where it can describe creators draining liquidity or abandoning a project. Broader internet use is metaphorical and does not necessarily allege a literal financial crime.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: rug pull meaning
590	5896	5702	beckon	/ˈbekən/	verb	To make a gesture inviting someone to come closer or follow; figuratively, to attract or seem to invite someone toward an opportunity or destination.	signal, summon, entice	She beckoned me over with one finger. / A new career beckoned.	Common patterns: beckon someone over/in; opportunity/adventure beckons.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define beckons
590	5897	5614	compliant	/kəmˈplaɪənt/	adjective	Willing to obey or cooperate; also meeting specified rules, standards, or requirements.	obedient, cooperative; conforming	The device is fully compliant with the new safety standard.	Two important shades: a compliant person readily yields/cooperates; a compliant product/system conforms to requirements.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: compliant meaning
590	5898	5723	inferable	/ɪnˈfɜːrəbəl/	adjective	Able to be inferred or concluded from evidence or reasoning even if not stated directly.	deducible, implied, derivable	Her disappointment was inferable from the abrupt change in tone.	Also spelled inferrable, with double r, though inferable is common. Related verb infer.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: inferable
590	5899	6613	index (verb)	/ˈɪndeks/	verb	To create or add to an index; to organize information so it can be searched/retrieved; to adjust/link a value to an external measure; or to indicate/serve as an indicator of something.	catalog, list, organize, register; link, track	The search engine indexes new pages automatically. / Benefits are indexed to inflation. / The measure is intended to index changes in consumer confidence.	Computing/search sense is very common. Financial/economic 'index X to inflation.' Do not reduce it to the noun stock-market index.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define indexing as verb
590	5900	6014	she/her/hers	/ʃiː hɜːr hɜːrz/	pronoun set	A conventional way of listing the feminine third-person singular pronouns someone uses: she (subject), her (object/determiner), hers (independent possessive).	she/her pronouns	She said she uses she/her pronouns. / 'I spoke to her about her book; the copy on the desk is hers' shows the three grammatical forms.	Profiles often abbreviate the set as she/her rather than she/her/hers. Listing pronouns communicates how someone wishes to be referred to; it does not by itself tell you every detail of that person's gender identity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: she/her/hers meaning
591	5901	6031	project confidence and assertiveness	/prəˌdʒekt ˈkɑːnfədəns ən əˈsɜːrtɪvnəs/	verb phrase	To outwardly communicate an impression of self-assurance and willingness to state or defend one's needs, boundaries, or position.	exude confidence, convey self-assurance, appear assertive, carry oneself confidently	Speak clearly and make eye contact if you want to project confidence without sounding aggressive. / The character projects confidence and assertiveness even when she's privately uncertain.	Project here = cause others to perceive a quality. Assertive ≠ aggressive: assertiveness is direct/self-assured expression while respecting others; aggression pushes or attacks.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: project confidence and assertiveness meaning
591	5902	6607	Polaris / North Star / guiding star	/poʊˈlerɪs/	proper noun / metaphor	Polaris is the star currently located close to the north celestial pole, commonly called the North Star or Pole Star; figuratively, a 'North Star' or 'guiding star' is a principle or goal that provides direction.	North Star, Pole Star; guiding principle, lodestar	Polaris appears nearly fixed in the northern sky and has long aided navigation. / Simplicity became the team's North Star when redesigning the app.	Polaris is not the brightest star in the night sky. Figurative North Star is very common in strategy/product language. Lodestar is a literary/formal related metaphor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: polaris north star guiding star
591	5903	6777	in the trenches	/ɪn ðə ˈtrentʃɪz/	idiom	Doing difficult, practical, frontline work directly rather than observing or managing from a distance; immersed in the demanding day-to-day reality of a field.	on the front lines, doing the hands-on work, in the thick of it	People in the trenches know which parts of the workflow actually waste time. / After years in the trenches as a teacher, she moved into policy.	From military trench warfare. Common business/work metaphor; can sound dramatic when applied to trivial work, sometimes intentionally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: in the trenches meaning
591	5904	6188	command respect	/kəˌmænd rɪˈspekt/	verb phrase	To naturally inspire, earn, or compel respect because of one's ability, character, authority, presence, or achievements.	earn respect, inspire respect, merit respect, carry authority	She rarely raises her voice, but she commands respect the moment she enters the room. / Years of careful work made him a critic who commands respect even from people who disagree with him.	Not 'order someone to respect you.' Command here means possess qualities that strongly elicit respect. 'Demand respect' can sound more assertive/explicit; 'earn respect' emphasizes gaining it over time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: command respect meaning
591	5905	6041	like pulling teeth	/ˌlaɪk ˌpʊlɪŋ ˈtiːθ/	idiom	Extremely difficult, frustrating, or laborious because someone is reluctant to cooperate, speak, provide information, or do what is needed.	an uphill struggle, painfully difficult, hard work, like getting blood from a stone (BrE/contextual)	Getting him to tell me what happened was like pulling teeth. / Trying to get everyone to agree on a date was like pulling teeth.	Especially common for extracting answers/cooperation from reluctant people. 'Getting blood from a stone' similarly emphasizes impossibility/reluctance, while uphill battle is broader sustained difficulty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: pulling teeth synonym
591	5906	5638	spineless	/ˈspaɪnləs/	adjective, disparaging	Lacking courage, determination, or strength of character.	cowardly, weak-willed, gutless	He was criticized as spineless for refusing to defend his own decision.	Figurative insult based on the image of having no backbone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0116 | search: spineless
591	5907	5737	inflectional	/ɪnˈflekʃənəl/	adjective, linguistics	Relating to grammatical inflection: changes in word form that express features such as tense, number, person, case, or comparison without creating a fundamentally new lexeme.	grammatical, inflective	The -ed in walked is an inflectional ending marking past tense.	Contrast derivational morphology, which forms a new word/lexeme or changes word class more readily.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define inflectional
591	5908	5506	civvy street	/ˈsɪvi striːt/	noun phrase	Ordinary civilian life, especially as contrasted with military service or another uniformed/service life.	civilian life, civilian world	After twenty years in the army, adjusting to civvy street took some time.	Chiefly British and informal. Often used in the pattern return/go back to civvy street.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: civvy street meaning
591	5909	5542	mangle	/ˈmæŋɡəl/	verb	To damage, distort, or ruin something badly; figuratively, to perform, pronounce, quote, or explain something very poorly.	butcher, mutilate, garble	I completely mangled his name when I tried to pronounce it.	Existing Master has mangled; keep the productive base verb because the figurative 'butcher/garble' use is independently useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: mangle meaning
591	5910	6154	flap out	/ˌflæp ˈaʊt/	verb phrase; context-dependent	To move outward or open while flapping, or to make something extend outward with a flapping motion; in some contexts a loose/nonstandard phrase whose exact sense comes from the physical action.	spread/flap outward, flutter out, open out	The loose fabric flapped out behind him in the wind. / She flapped the blanket out before laying it on the grass.	Not a major fixed phrasal verb in everyday English. Interpret compositionally from flap + out unless the source gives a special slang meaning. Do not confuse with 'flip out' = lose emotional control.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: flap out meaning
592	5911	5890	sand off the edges	/ˌsænd ɔːf ði ˈedʒɪz/	idiom / metaphor	To remove the rough, extreme, distinctive, abrasive, or challenging qualities of something, making it smoother, safer, more conventional, or broadly palatable.	smooth out, soften, tone down, round off, domesticate, sanitize	The remake sands off the edges that made the original so strange and memorable. / Corporate messaging tends to sand off the rough edges of what people actually said.	Literal image: sanding sharp/rough edges smooth. In criticism it can be negative: increased accessibility at the cost of personality, bite, specificity, or risk.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: sand off the edges meaning
592	5912	6734	pub crawl: synonyms and alternatives	/ˈpʌb ˌkrɔːl/	usage contrast	Near-equivalents include bar crawl, bar-hopping, pub-hopping, and pub tour, but nuance differs: crawl suggests an organized/multi-stop route; hopping emphasizes moving between venues; tour can sound more organized/touristic.	bar crawl, bar-hopping, pub-hopping, pub tour	We went bar-hopping downtown. / The hostel runs an organized pub crawl every Friday.	Search-intent card. 'Bar crawl' is the closest AmE counterpart. 'Bar-hopping' is usually uncountable/activity wording rather than a named organized event.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: pub crawl synonym
592	5913	5838	nuke	/nuːk/	verb / noun, informal	Literally, to attack with a nuclear weapon; figuratively, to destroy, delete, reset, or overwhelm something completely or drastically.	obliterate, wipe out, delete	I accidentally nuked the whole folder instead of deleting one file.	Informal figurative use is common in tech/gaming: nuke a server, nuke an account, nuke a build.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: nuke meaning
592	5914	6198	OTPHJ	/ˌoʊ tiː piː eɪtʃ ˈdʒeɪ/	internet sexual slang / initialism	A vulgar internet initialism referring to a sexual act involving manual stimulation through or around clothing/pocket area; exact expansions and usage can vary across slang communities.	sexual slang; context-dependent initialism	The acronym appeared in an Urban Dictionary-style slang discussion, not in ordinary formal English. / If you encounter OTPHJ in a sexual context, treat it as explicit internet slang rather than a standard abbreviation.	Highly niche, explicit slang with unstable crowd-sourced definitions. Preserve provenance/context rather than treating it as a standard lexical item; not appropriate for neutral/formal conversation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Urban Dictionary: OTPHJ
592	5915	5765	thereof	/ˌðerˈʌv/	adverb, formal/archaic	Of that or of it; referring back to something already mentioned.	of it, of that, from that (in some compounds)	The agreement and any amendment thereof must be kept on file. / The report describes the policy and the consequences thereof. / Preparation—or the lack thereof—made the difference.	Formal/legal/literary anaphoric word meaning 'of that/of it.' Rare in ordinary conversation except set phrases such as 'lack thereof.' Related legalistic forms include therein, thereto, hereby, whereof; avoid them when plain English is clearer.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define thereof
592	5916	5662	disband	/dɪsˈbænd/	verb	To break up or cause an organized group to cease operating or existing.	dissolve, break up, disperse	The committee disbanded after completing its work.	Common for bands, military units, committees, organizations, and groups.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: disband meaning
592	5917	6336	commandeer	/ˌkɑːmənˈdɪr/	verb	To take possession or control of something for official/military use, often by authority; more loosely, to seize or appropriate something for one's own use without permission.	requisition, seize, appropriate, take over	The soldiers commandeered a truck to evacuate the area. / My friends commandeered the living room for their all-night movie marathon.	Official/military sense can involve lawful requisition under authority. Informal humorous use exaggerates ordinary taking over: commandeer the couch, TV, meeting room, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define commandeer
592	5918	5863	how's this for irony?	/ˌhaʊz ðɪs fər ˈaɪrəni/	rhetorical expression	Used to introduce a situation the speaker sees as strikingly ironic, contradictory, or unexpectedly fitting.	talk about irony, here's the irony, ironic, isn't it?	How's this for irony? The fire station had to be evacuated because of a fire. / I spent all week fixing my backup system, and then the backup drive died—how's that for irony?	Productive pattern: How's this/that for X? = 'this/that is a remarkable example of X.' Existing irony card explains that irony is not merely any unfortunate coincidence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: how's this for irony meaning
592	5919	5936	salacious	/səˈleɪʃəs/	adjective	Having or appealing to an excessive interest in sexual matters, especially in a lurid, scandalous, or gossip-oriented way.	lurid, prurient, racy, lewd, titillating, scandalous	The article focused on the most salacious details of the celebrity's private life. / The series sells itself as serious history, but the marketing leans heavily on salacious gossip.	Salacious often describes stories/details/reporting, not simply anything sexual. Prurient emphasizes an unhealthy/voyeuristic sexual interest; lewd is more directly obscene/crude; racy is milder and can be playful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: SALACIOUS Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster
592	5920	6243	mook	/muːk/	noun, slang	A foolish, contemptible, or insignificant person; in action/gaming/media discussion, a generic low-level henchman or disposable enemy serving a more important villain.	goon, henchman, minion, lackey, disposable enemy; idiot (contextual)	The hero plows through a hallway full of mooks before reaching the boss. / Games often use weak mooks to teach you a new combat mechanic. / Calling a real person a mook is insulting and somewhat old-fashioned/slangy.	Especially useful in TV Tropes/gaming/action-media vocabulary: unnamed rank-and-file enemies. Broader insult sense predates that specialized fandom use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define mooks
593	5921	6558	figurine vs figure	/ˌfɪɡjəˈriːn/ vs /ˈfɪɡjər/	contrast	Both can refer to small representations of people/characters, but figurine strongly means a small statue/ornament, while figure is broader and is the standard hobby term in phrases such as action figure, scale figure, anime figure.	statuette vs collectible/model figure	I'd call a porcelain angel a figurine, but an articulated Spider-Man toy an action figure. / Anime collectors usually say 'figure' rather than 'figurine.'	Figure has many unrelated senses (number, shape, person, body). Figurine is narrower. In collector communities, product-category conventions matter more than size alone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: figurine vs figure meaning
593	5922	6496	throw a game	/ˌθroʊ ə ˈɡeɪm/	sports idiom	To deliberately lose a game or intentionally play badly so that one's team does not win, often for strategic, corrupt, gambling, or other reasons.	deliberately lose, tank (overlapping), fix a match (related), take a dive (individual/contextual)	The player was accused of taking money to throw the game. / Fans suspected the team had thrown the match, though there was no proof.	Throw a game implies intentional loss. Tank can mean intentionally lose for strategic incentives (e.g. draft position) but also simply perform terribly. Match-fixing is broader manipulation of a contest/result.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: throw the game meaning in sports
593	5923	6017	edgewise / edgeways	/ˈedʒwaɪz/; /ˈedʒweɪz/	adverb	With the edge facing forward/sideways rather than the broad surface; chiefly encountered in the idiom 'get a word in edgewise/edgeways.'	edge-on, sideways	Turn the table edgewise and it might fit through the doorway. / In everyday speech, most people encounter edgewise in 'I couldn't get a word in edgewise.'	Edgewise is common in AmE idiom; edgeways is common in BrE. Literal adverb exists but is much less frequent than the idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define edgewise
593	5924	5667	by grunts	/baɪ ˈɡrʌnts/	context-dependent phrase	A literal prepositional phrase meaning 'by means of grunts' or 'by low-ranking workers/soldiers,' depending on context.	through grunting sounds; by rank-and-file workers	They communicated mostly by grunts and gestures.	Not a fixed idiom. Keep because the search isolates how by combines with the plural noun grunts; existing grunt card supplies the noun senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: by grunts
593	5925	5977	low blow	/ˌloʊ ˈbloʊ/	noun / idiom	Literally, an illegal or unfair strike below the waist in combat sports; figuratively, an unfair, cruel, or unnecessarily personal attack, especially exploiting a vulnerability.	cheap shot, unfair attack, below-the-belt remark, dirty hit	Punching below the belt is a low blow. / Bringing up his divorce just to win the argument was a low blow. / I expected criticism, but attacking her family felt like a cheap shot.	Figurative low blow and hit below the belt are close. Cheap shot can be any unfair/opportunistic attack. Low blow often emphasizes crossing a moral/personal line.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: low blow
593	5926	5676	no fluff, just stuff	/ˌnoʊ ˈflʌf dʒʌst ˈstʌf/	informal slogan-like phrase	A playful way to say that something contains only useful, substantive content and no filler, padding, or unnecessary extras.	no filler, straight to the point, all substance	The guide promises no fluff, just stuff you can actually use.	Fluff = nonessential filler; stuff provides the rhyme and means the actual material/content. Not a fixed universal idiom, but readily understandable wordplay.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: no fluff just stuff meaning
593	5927	6867	humility vs humiliation	/hjuːˈmɪləti/ vs /hjuːˌmɪliˈeɪʃən/	contrast	Humility is modesty and freedom from excessive pride; humiliation is the painful experience/state of being made to feel ashamed, degraded, or foolish.	modesty vs shame/degradation	Humility helped him admit he was wrong; public humiliation would have been something entirely different. / Choosing humility is not the same as accepting humiliation.	Crucial lexical distinction despite shared humil- root. Humility can be voluntary/positive; humiliation is typically imposed or experienced as degrading.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: humility vs humiliation
593	5928	5748	defy expectations	/dɪˌfaɪ ˌekspekˈteɪʃənz/	phrase	To turn out differently from what people expected, often in a surprising or impressive way.	surprise, confound expectations, buck expectations	The sequel defied expectations and became the strongest film in the series.	Can be positive or negative; it simply means the expected pattern/prediction did not hold.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: defy your expectations meaning
593	5929	5734	sovereign	/ˈsɑːvrən/	adjective / noun	Possessing supreme or independent authority; as a noun, a monarch or other supreme ruler.	independent, autonomous; monarch, ruler	The country is a sovereign state with authority over its own laws.	Related existing sovereignty = the state/right of having sovereign authority.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define sovereign
593	5930	5908	polite rigmarole	/pəˌlaɪt ˈrɪɡməˌroʊl/	contextual phrase	A drawn-out set of polite formulas, social niceties, formalities, or conversational steps that feel obligatory before getting to the real point.	social formalities, polite dance, ritual niceties, ceremony	After the usual polite rigmarole about the weather and the journey, they finally started discussing the contract. / Sometimes I'd rather skip the polite rigmarole and just ask what someone actually means.	Not a fixed idiom, but a natural creative combination. Rigmarole adds mild impatience: the politeness is experienced as elaborate procedure rather than meaningful content.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: polite rigmarole meaning
594	5931	6010	cringe	/krɪndʒ/	verb / adjective / noun, informal	As a verb, to recoil or feel embarrassed/discomforted. In modern slang, adjective/noun uses describe something painfully embarrassing, awkward, try-hard, or socially embarrassing.	wince, recoil; cringeworthy, embarrassing, awkward, try-hard	I cringed when I heard my old recording. / That dialogue is kind of cringe, but I still love the show. / People sometimes call any open enthusiasm 'cringe' just because it isn't detached or ironic.	Traditional verb predates slang. 'Cringe' as adjective ('that's cringe') is common internet/youth usage. Existing cringe-worthy card overlaps, but standalone cringe has broader modern grammar and cultural use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: being cringe meaning
594	5932	6069	in the gutter	/ɪn ðə ˈɡʌtər/	phrase / idiomatic	Literally in the drainage channel along a street/roof; figuratively, in a degraded, disreputable, vulgar, or very low condition. 'Mind in the gutter' specifically means interpreting things sexually.	down-and-out, degraded; dirty-minded (mind in the gutter)	The ball rolled into the gutter. / His reputation was already in the gutter after the scandal. / Get your mind out of the gutter—I wasn't talking about sex.	Multiple idioms: reputation/economy 'in the gutter' = very low; 'mind in the gutter' = sexual/vulgar interpretation; bowling gutter is another literal sense. Context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: in the gutter
594	5933	6030	be taken for weakness	/bi ˌteɪkən fər ˈwiːknəs/	phrase	To be interpreted or mistaken as a sign of weakness, lack of resolve, or inability to resist.	be mistaken for weakness, be seen as weakness, be read as weakness	His patience shouldn't be taken for weakness. / She worried that apologizing too quickly would be taken for weakness rather than maturity.	Common pattern: 'Don't mistake my kindness/silence/patience for weakness.' Passive 'be taken for weakness' is natural but somewhat formal. Take X for Y = mistake/interpret X as Y.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: be taken for weakness meaning
594	5934	6342	lubber / landlubber	/ˈlʌbər/; /ˈlændˌlʌbər/	noun, old-fashioned/nautical	Lubber: a clumsy, awkward, or inexperienced person, especially at sea. Landlubber: someone unfamiliar with ships/seafaring, stereotypically a landsman among sailors.	landlubber, clumsy sailor, novice, awkward person	The pirate captain sneered at the new deckhand as a useless landlubber. / 'Lubber' by itself sounds archaic or deliberately nautical today.	Strong pirate/nautical flavor in modern media. Historically lubber was a broader insult for a big clumsy/lazy fellow. 'Landlubber' remains recognizable largely through pirate fiction and jokes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: lubbers
594	5935	5914	fat-ass / fatass	/ˈfætˌæs/	noun / adjective, vulgar insult	A crude insult for a person perceived as fat; sometimes used adjectivally or jokingly/intensifying in compounds.	fatso, lard-ass (insulting), overweight person (neutral description, not synonym in tone)	The character calls him a 'lazy fat-ass' during the argument. / Among close friends it may be used jokingly, but from a stranger it can be openly hostile.	Strongly insulting/body-shaming in many contexts. Spelling varies fat-ass/fatass. Do not use it as a neutral term for someone's body size.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: fatasses
594	5936	6360	reprimand	/ˈreprɪˌmænd/	noun / verb	An official or serious expression of disapproval for misconduct, especially from someone in authority; to give such a warning/censure.	rebuke, censure, admonishment, dressing-down; discipline	The employee received a formal reprimand for violating the policy. / The coach reprimanded the players for ignoring the safety rule.	Often institutional/workplace/military/school context and can be formally recorded. More official than scold; rebuke can be sharp without being an administrative sanction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define reprimand
594	5937	6652	do wonders for something	/ˌduː ˈwʌndərz fər/	idiom	To have a very beneficial, impressive, or restorative effect on someone or something.	work wonders for, greatly improve, be excellent for	A short walk can do wonders for my concentration. / Better lighting did wonders for the room.	Very natural with health, mood, appearance, performance, confidence, etc. 'Do wonders' and 'work wonders' are near-equivalents.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: doing wonders for meaning
594	5938	6604	-tude	/tuːd/	noun-forming suffix / word element	A suffix forming nouns that express a state, condition, quality, or extent, as in attitude, aptitude, latitude, magnitude, solitude.	state/condition suffix	Latitude and magnitude both end in -tude, but modern speakers usually learn the whole words rather than actively deriving them from *-tude.	From Latin -tūdō via French/Latin formations. Not normally a free English word 'titude.' 'Attitude' is not synchronically 'at + titude.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: titude meaning
594	5939	6592	priming	/ˈpraɪmɪŋ/	noun	Preparing or influencing a system/person so that a later response is easier or more likely; meanings occur in psychology, learning, pumps/engines, painting, and other fields.	preparation, preconditioning, cueing	Seeing one word can sometimes prime recognition of a related word. / The opening scene primes the audience to expect a tragedy. / The wall needs priming before painting.	Psychology: prior exposure can influence later processing. Popular claims about behavioral priming can be broader/stronger than evidence supports; context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: priming meaning
594	5940	5615	acquiescent	/ˌækwiˈesənt/	adjective	Ready to accept something without protest or resistance, often reluctantly or passively.	submissive, yielding, compliant	His acquiescent response suggested that he disagreed but would not fight the decision.	From acquiesce. Often implies passive acceptance rather than enthusiastic agreement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define acquiescent
595	5941	5763	deficiency	/dɪˈfɪʃənsi/	noun	A shortage, lack, or inadequacy of something needed; also a weakness or defect.	shortage, insufficiency, deficit	Iron deficiency can cause fatigue.	Common in medicine/nutrition, systems, skills, and formal evaluation: nutrient deficiency, deficiency in knowledge, design deficiency.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define deficiency
595	5942	6351	brute	/bruːt/	noun / adjective	A cruel, violent, stupid, or physically forceful person or animal; as an adjective, based on raw physical force rather than skill, subtlety, or reasoning.	thug, beast, bully, savage (often problematic), brute-force	The villain is portrayed as a huge brute who solves every problem with violence. / We used brute force rather than a clever algorithm. / 'Brute strength' means raw physical power.	As a noun, insulting/dehumanizing. Productive compounds: brute force, brute strength, brute fact. Existing 'brute force' card covers the computing phrase; this card supplies the underlying broader word.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define brute
595	5943	5672	deviant	/ˈdiːviənt/	adjective / noun	Departing from accepted norms or standards; as a noun, a person characterized as behaving outside such norms.	abnormal, nonconforming; transgressive	The study examined behavior the community considered deviant.	Can be neutral/technical in sociology or strongly judgmental/insulting when labeling a person.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: deviant meaning
595	5944	6575	same with me	/ˌseɪm wɪð ˈmiː/	conversational phrase	The same thing is true of me / I am in the same situation or have the same reaction, preference, problem, etc.	same here, me too, likewise for me	'I can't concentrate when I'm hungry.' 'Same with me.' / 'I loved the first season but dropped the second.' 'Same with me.'	Grammatical and understandable, but 'same here' or 'me too' is often more idiomatic in short replies. 'Same with me' is useful when explicitly mapping a previously stated fact onto oneself.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: same with me meaning
595	5945	6495	schmooze	/ʃmuːz/	verb / noun, informal	To chat and socialize, especially in a friendly, flattering, or strategic way to build connections, network, or gain advantage.	network, mingle, chat up, hobnob, butter up (stronger)	He spent the reception schmoozing with producers and journalists. / I hate events where you're expected to schmooze for two hours.	From Yiddish. Can be neutral ('socialize/network') or mildly cynical, implying calculated charm. 'Schmooze with someone'; 'schmooze one's way into...'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: schmooze meaning
595	5946	6199	peck on the cheek	/ˌpek ɑːn ðə ˈtʃiːk/	noun phrase / verb phrase	A quick, light kiss on someone's cheek, usually affectionate, friendly, familial, or mildly romantic depending on culture and relationship.	quick kiss, light kiss, kiss on the cheek	She gave him a quick peck on the cheek before leaving. / In some cultures, cheek kisses are routine greetings and carry little romantic meaning.	Peck emphasizes brevity/light contact. A cheek kiss can be romantic, familial, friendly, or conventional greeting behavior; context matters. 'Peck' also literally means a bird striking with its beak.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: peck on the cheek meaning
595	5947	6222	flail	/fleɪl/	verb / noun	To wave or swing the arms, legs, or an object wildly and uncontrollably; figuratively, to struggle ineffectively or chaotically. As a noun, a traditional threshing tool/weapon-like implement with a swinging part.	thrash, wave wildly, struggle, flounder	He slipped on the ice and flailed his arms trying to stay upright. / The company spent months flailing without a clear strategy. / The monster's tentacles flailed in every direction.	Physical flailing is uncontrolled swinging. Figurative flail = struggle without effective direction, close to flounder. Distinct from fail despite similar spelling.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: flail verb meaning
595	5948	5629	take a hike	/ˌteɪk ə ˈhaɪk/	idiom, informal	A dismissive way to tell someone to go away or leave you alone.	get lost, beat it, go away, scram, buzz off	If you're only here to complain, take a hike. / He told the salesman to take a hike after the third pushy call. / We literally took a hike through the hills the next morning.	Dismissive idiom = 'go away / leave me alone,' often rude but milder/playful in some contexts. The same words also have the fully literal meaning 'go hiking,' so context and tone matter.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: take a hike
595	5949	6629	one's opinion counts for about the weight of a feather	/wʌnz əˈpɪnjən kaʊnts fər əˌbaʊt ðə ˌweɪt əv ə ˈfeðər/	creative metaphor	A humorous/nonstandard way to say someone's opinion has almost no influence or importance in a discussion.	count for very little, carry little weight, matter hardly at all	In this argument, his opinion counts for about the weight of a feather.	Understandable wordplay, but English normally says an opinion 'carries weight,' not literally 'counts for the weight of.' Preserve as a creative source/search sentence and contrast with the idiomatic version.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: In this argument, his opinion counts for about the weight of a feather.
595	5950	5779	derangement	/dɪˈreɪndʒmənt/	noun	A state of severe disorder or disturbance; historically/colloquially, mental disturbance or irrationality.	disorder, disturbance, disorientation	The essay describes the character's growing emotional derangement.	Can sound clinical or stigmatizing when applied to mental health; often appears rhetorically in phrases like 'X derangement syndrome.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: derangement
596	5951	5882	bona fide + insult/noun	/ˌboʊnə ˈfaɪd/	intensifying adjective pattern	Bona fide before a noun means genuine, real, or unquestionably qualifying as that thing; with an insult such as 'bona fide asshole,' it emphatically means a genuine/undeniable one.	genuine, real, certified, dyed-in-the-wool (contextual), full-fledged	He wasn't just being rude; he was acting like a bona fide asshole. / What started as a hobby turned into a bona fide business. / She's a bona fide expert, not just someone with a big following.	Existing bona fide WID5770 covers the dictionary meaning; preserve this contextual card because the search tests how it behaves before a strongly evaluative noun. It can intensify positive, neutral, or negative labels.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: bona fide asshole meaning
596	5952	6414	a dime a dozen	/ə ˌdaɪm ə ˈdʌzən/	idiom, AmE	Very common, easy to find, and therefore not especially valuable, special, or impressive.	commonplace, ubiquitous, ten a penny (BrE), nothing special	Generic isekai premises are a dime a dozen; execution is what makes one memorable. / Cafés like that are a dime a dozen downtown.	Literally 'twelve for ten cents' → cheap because abundant. Often mildly dismissive. BrE counterpart 'ten a penny.' Contrast one-of-a-kind = unique.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: Dime a dozen:
596	5953	6025	career criminal	/kəˌrɪr ˈkrɪmənəl/	noun	A person who repeatedly engages in crime over a long period and treats criminal activity as an ongoing way of life rather than an isolated offense.	habitual offender, repeat offender, professional criminal	Police described him as a career criminal with a long record of burglaries. / The phrase implies a sustained pattern of offending, not merely someone who committed one serious crime.	Repeat offender is more legal/neutral and simply means repeated offending; professional criminal can imply skill/occupation; career criminal is strongly evaluative and may appear in policing/media rhetoric.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: career criminal meanig
596	5954	6013	tut and yap	/ˌtʌt ən ˈjæp/	creative phrase / informal	To combine disapproving tutting with prolonged chatter or complaining; a context-built phrase rather than a fixed idiom.	tsk and complain, grumble and chatter, tut and ramble	They sat in the corner tutting and yapping about how everything used to be better. / The phrase paints a comic picture: little noises of disapproval plus nonstop talk.	Not a standard lexicalized expression. Tut = click of disapproval; yap = talk noisily/at length, often dismissively. Existing yapping/yapper cards cover modern yap slang.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tut and yap meaning
596	5955	6856	staunch	/stɔːntʃ/	adjective / verb	Adjective: very loyal, committed, firm, or strong in support/opposition. Verb: to stop or check the flow of blood or another liquid.	steadfast, loyal, committed, unwavering; stem, stop	She's a staunch defender of free expression. / They tried to staunch the bleeding with pressure.	Adjective is common: staunch supporter/ally/opponent. Verb may also be spelled stanch, especially AmE; both forms occur. Existing 'staunchly' is the adverb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: staunch definition
596	5956	6421	you said a mouthful	/juː ˌsed ə ˈmaʊθfʊl/	idiom, informal	A response meaning 'what you just said is very true, important, or says a great deal,' often agreeing emphatically with a concise observation.	exactly, you can say that again, that's for sure, well said	'Working from home saves commuting time, but it can blur every boundary.' 'You said a mouthful.'	Idiomatic mouthful here = a statement containing a lot of truth/meaning, not literally many words. Somewhat old-fashioned but still recognizable. Different from 'give someone a mouthful.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: you said a mouthful meaning
596	5957	5710	awestruck	/ˈɔːˌstrʌk/	adjective	Filled with a powerful feeling of awe, wonder, admiration, or reverence.	amazed, wonderstruck, overwhelmed	We stood awestruck beneath the enormous night sky.	Awe can combine admiration with fear or overwhelming grandeur; awestruck is stronger than merely impressed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: awestruck meaning
596	5958	5982	Anthropocene	/ˈænθrəpəˌsiːn/	proper noun / geological-cultural term	A proposed term for a period in which human activity has become a dominant influence on Earth's climate, ecosystems, geology, and environment.	human-dominated epoch, age of humans (informal)	Plastic pollution and fossil-fuel emissions are often discussed as signatures of the Anthropocene. / The novel imagines what ruins our civilization might leave behind for observers deep in the future.	Widely used in science/humanities, but as of the mid-2020s it was not formally ratified as an official geological epoch by the relevant stratigraphic bodies. Capitalization is common. The proposed start date has been debated.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: anthropocene meaning
596	5959	6752	unyielding	/ʌnˈjiːldɪŋ/	adjective	Not giving way under pressure; firm, rigid, stubborn, relentless, or physically resistant to bending/movement.	inflexible, uncompromising, relentless, rigid, stubborn	She remained unyielding in her refusal to apologize. / The material felt hard and unyielding. / They faced unyielding pressure for months.	Can be positive (steadfast) or negative (inflexible). Literal physical resistance and figurative attitude/conditions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: unyielding meaning
596	5960	6464	get over yourself	/ˌɡet ˈoʊvər jərˌself/	idiom / imperative, informal	Stop being so self-important, self-absorbed, dramatic, offended, or preoccupied with your own feelings/image; gain perspective.	stop taking yourself so seriously, lose the ego, get a grip (overlapping)	You made one small mistake—get over yourself and move on. / He acts like everyone is watching him; someone needs to tell him to get over himself.	Usually dismissive and potentially rude. Not the same as 'get over something' = recover from an event/illness/relationship.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: get over yourself meaning
597	5961	5509	settle on something	/ˈsetəl ɑːn/	phrasal verb	To make a final choice or decision after considering different possibilities.	decide on, choose, opt for	After comparing several designs, we settled on the simplest one.	Search was 'settled on meaning'; normalize to the base form settle on.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: settled on meaning
597	5962	5706	begrudgingly	/bɪˈɡrʌdʒɪŋli/	adverb	Reluctantly and with resentment or unwillingness, even though one does or admits something.	reluctantly, resentfully, unwillingly	He begrudgingly admitted that her idea had worked.	Often implies giving credit, agreement, help, or compliance while not being happy about it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: begrudgingly
597	5963	5856	break the mold	/ˌbreɪk ðə ˈmoʊld/	idiom	To do something in a new, unconventional, or distinctive way rather than following an established pattern.	defy convention, innovate, be different	The director broke the mold with a quiet, character-driven superhero film.	Also BrE spelling break the mould. Existing synonym mention does not replace the idiom card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: break the mold meaning
597	5964	5608	dissimilarity	/ˌdɪsɪməˈlerəti/	noun	The state or quality of being unlike or different.	difference, unlikeness	The dissimilarity between the two versions becomes obvious when you compare the endings.	Opposite of similarity; more formal and less common than difference.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define dissimilarity
597	5965	6241	arthouse / arthouse-y	/ˈɑːrtˌhaʊs/; /ˈɑːrtˌhaʊsi/	adjective / informal adjective	Associated with independent, experimental, auteur-driven, unconventional, or aesthetically ambitious cinema aimed more at artistic expression than mass-market formulas; arthouse-y means having that kind of feel.	art-film-like, experimental, auteurist, indie, unconventional	The movie starts like a thriller and then becomes increasingly arthouse-y and abstract. / I like slow arthouse films, but this one felt self-consciously obscure.	Also art-house. 'Arthouse-y' can be neutral, admiring, or mildly dismissive ('trying hard to seem artistic'). Not synonymous with simply low-budget or foreign-language.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: art housey meaning
597	5966	5746	nifty twist	/ˌnɪfti ˈtwɪst/	noun phrase	A clever, neat, stylish, or pleasingly inventive twist, especially in a story, idea, design, or solution.	clever twist, neat twist	The final reveal adds a nifty twist to an otherwise familiar plot.	Existing nifty card covers the adjective; preserve the searched collocation because it captures the common evaluative use in reviews.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: nifty twist meaning
597	5967	6015	catch on	/ˌkætʃ ˈɑːn/	phrasal verb	To understand or realize something, often gradually; also, for an idea, fashion, product, or practice to become popular.	get it, figure it out, cotton on (BrE); become popular, take off, gain traction	It took me a few episodes to catch on to what the show was doing. / Don't explain the joke—he'll catch on. / The trend caught on surprisingly quickly.	Two core senses: person catches on = understands; thing catches on = becomes popular. 'Catch on to X' = realize/understand X. Related but different catch someone on = physically catch, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114, voca_0115 | search: catch on
597	5968	5796	bantery	/ˈbæntəri/	adjective, informal	Characterized by playful teasing, joking back-and-forth, or banter.	playfully teasing, jokey	Their conversation was light and bantery rather than serious.	Informal derivative of banter; less standard/common than 'full of banter' or 'bantering.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: bantery meaning
597	5969	6743	locus of the problem	/ˈloʊkəs əv ðə ˈprɑːbləm/	formal phrase	The place, point, system, factor, or conceptual center where a problem is located or concentrated—the part regarded as its main site/source.	center of the problem, site, focal point, source	The UI isn't the real locus of the problem; the underlying data model is. / Researchers debated whether the locus of the difficulty was perception or memory.	Locus is formal/technical Latin-derived noun meaning place/site. Plural loci /ˈloʊsaɪ/ or /ˈloʊkiː/ depending convention.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: locus of the problem meaning
597	5970	6748	distinct	/dɪˈstɪŋkt/	adjective	Clearly different or separate from something else; clearly recognizable or noticeable; definite/unmistakable.	different, separate, discrete, distinctive, unmistakable, clear	These are two distinct meanings of the same word. / I heard a distinct click from the other room. / The character has a distinct speaking style.	Distinct = separate/clearly different; distinctive = characteristic in a way that makes something stand out. 'A distinct possibility' = real/definite possibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: distinct
598	5971	6178	stable	/ˈsteɪbəl/	noun / adjective / verb	As a noun, a building where horses or other livestock are kept; also a group of horses or, figuratively, a group of people under one management. As an adjective, steady and unlikely to change/fail. As a verb, to house a horse.	steady, secure; horse barn; roster/group	The horses were brought back to the stable before dark. / Prices have remained relatively stable. / The agency has a stable of well-known actors and directors.	This card broadens beyond existing emotional-stability uses. Figurative noun 'a stable of writers/talent' = group managed/associated together. Do not confuse stable adjective with static: stable can still change gradually.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: stable
598	5972	6133	Triceratops	/traɪˈserətɑːps/	proper noun / genus name	A genus of large herbivorous ceratopsian dinosaurs from the Late Cretaceous of North America, famous for three facial horns and a large bony frill.	three-horned dinosaur, ceratopsian (broader)	Triceratops lived alongside Tyrannosaurus near the end of the Cretaceous. / The skull's three horns make Triceratops one of the easiest dinosaurs to recognize.	Name means roughly 'three-horned face.' Technically a genus, but everyday English often treats Triceratops as the animal name. It was not from the Jurassic despite common dinosaur mashups.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: triceratops meaning
598	5973	5669	looksmaxxing	/ˈlʊksˌmæksɪŋ/	noun / verb-like internet slang	Trying to maximize one's physical attractiveness through grooming, fitness, fashion, skincare, cosmetic procedures, or other appearance-focused practices.	appearance optimization, glow-up practices	He started looksmaxxing by changing his haircut, clothes, and workout routine.	Internet slang associated especially with male appearance-rating communities. Spelling varies: looksmaxing / looksmaxxing. Can range from ordinary grooming advice to obsessive or toxic appearance discourse.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: looks maxxxing meaning
598	5974	6235	adamant	/ˈædəmənt/	adjective	Completely firm and unwilling to change one's decision, opinion, or demand; strongly insistent.	unyielding, insistent, resolute, firm, steadfast	She was adamant that she would pay her own share. / He remained adamant about keeping the original ending even after everyone objected. / I'm not sure why, but she was absolutely adamant.	Common patterns: be adamant that + clause; be adamant about X. Stronger than 'sure' or 'firm': it highlights refusal to be persuaded. Historically adamant was also a legendary extremely hard substance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: adamant
598	5975	6059	psych! / psyche someone out	/saɪk/	interjection / slang verb	Psych! is a teasing reveal meaning 'just kidding / fooled you.' To psych someone out means to intimidate, unsettle, or manipulate them mentally so they lose confidence or composure.	just kidding, gotcha; intimidate, rattle, mess with someone's head	I told him the tickets were sold out—psych! I already bought them. / Don't let the other team psych you out before the match even starts.	Interjection is commonly spelled psych! (sometimes psyche! informally, though psych reflects the slang). 'Psych oneself up' is different: mentally prepare/excite oneself. 'Psych out' = unsettle/deceive psychologically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: psych meaning slang
598	5976	6447	business as usual	/ˌbɪznəs əz ˈjuːʒuəl/	idiom / noun phrase	Normal operations or behavior continuing despite disruption, controversy, change, or unusual circumstances; sometimes used critically to imply nothing meaningful has changed.	normal operations, same old routine, back to normal, status quo	The storm passed, and by Monday it was business as usual. / After a brief apology, the company went straight back to business as usual.	Can be reassuring ('operations continue normally') or critical ('nothing changed despite the problem'). Often used without article: It's business as usual.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: just business as usual meaning
598	5977	6915	harboring the energy	/ˈhɑːrbərɪŋ ði ˈenərdʒi/	context-dependent figurative phrase	Holding, containing, or carrying a particular emotional force, mood, attitude, or potential. Understandable metaphorically, though 'harbor' more naturally collocates with feelings/thoughts such as resentment, suspicion, or hope.	carrying the energy, holding that energy, harboring a feeling	The scene feels like it's harboring this nervous energy beneath the surface. / More idiomatically: she was carrying a lot of anxious energy.	Preserve search, but flag collocation. 'Harbor resentment/suspicion/feelings' is conventional; 'harbor energy' is creative/metaphorical rather than a fixed collocation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: harboring the energy meaning
598	5978	5860	give it one's all	/ˌɡɪv ɪt wʌnz ˈɔːl/	idiom	To put all of one's effort, energy, or determination into an attempt.	do one's best, give 100 percent, go all out	We didn't win, but everyone gave it their all.	Raw search 'give it all' normalized to the usual idiom give it one's all / give it your all.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: give it all meaning
598	5979	5991	undiverse	/ˌʌndaɪˈvɜːrs/	adjective	Lacking diversity or variety; composed of people, things, or perspectives that are relatively homogeneous.	homogeneous, lacking diversity, uniform, unvaried	The panel was criticized for being unusually undiverse. / The recommendations felt undiverse—almost everything came from the same genre and era.	Understandable and used, but 'not very diverse,' 'lacking diversity,' or 'homogeneous' may sound more natural depending on context. For people/groups, diverse refers to representation/variety, not an inherent moral quality of individuals.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: undiverse
598	5980	6281	meat (slang)	/miːt/	noun, slang; context-dependent	Beyond literal animal flesh/food, 'meat' can slangily refer to a person's body or flesh, genitals (especially penis), a muscular person/body, or the substantial/core part of something ('the meat of the argument').	body/flesh; penis (vulgar); substance, core, main part	The discussion used 'meat' as crude sexual slang, not as food vocabulary. / Let's get to the meat of the argument. / Gaming and internet communities also create highly local 'meat' jokes, so the surrounding sentence matters.	Urban Dictionary-style searches are especially context-sensitive. Do not assume the sexual sense whenever 'meat' appears. Common standard figurative sense: the meat of X = the most substantial/important part.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Urban Dictionary: meat
599	5981	5941	crinoline	/ˈkrɪnəlɪn/	noun	A stiff fabric or structured underskirt/hooped framework used to hold a skirt out from the body, especially associated with 19th-century fashion.	hoop skirt, petticoat framework	The costume department built a crinoline to give the Victorian dress its enormous bell shape. / Walking through a narrow doorway in a full crinoline would have required some care.	Historically crinoline first referred to stiff fabric, then especially the cage crinoline/hooped underskirt. Useful in period fashion, costume design, historical fiction, and visual analysis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: crinoline
599	5982	6805	cursive / cursive handwriting	/ˈkɜːrsɪv/	noun / adjective	A style of handwriting in which letters are often joined in flowing strokes; Japanese 筆記体 is commonly translated as cursive or cursive handwriting.	script, joined-up writing (BrE), cursive writing	I learned cursive in elementary school but rarely use it now. / Can you read her cursive handwriting?	Cursive is broader than one specific school alphabet style. 'Script' can also mean handwriting/typeface and is more ambiguous.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: 筆記体 英語で
599	5983	6307	have bigger fish to fry	/ˌhæv ˌbɪɡər ˌfɪʃ tə ˈfraɪ/	idiom	To have more important, urgent, or worthwhile matters to deal with than the one currently being discussed.	have more important things to do, have higher priorities, have bigger problems	I'm not going to argue about one typo—we've got bigger fish to fry. / The detective ignored the petty dispute because she had much bigger fish to fry.	Often dismisses a smaller issue in favor of priorities. Can sound slightly brusque. Also 'other fish to fry' = other matters to attend to, without necessarily ranking them as bigger.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: bigger fish to fry meaning
599	5984	6691	agree with vs agree on	/əˈɡriː wɪð/ vs /əˈɡriː ɑːn/	usage contrast	Agree with a person/opinion = share or accept that person's view; agree on something = two or more parties reach/share the same decision, plan, terms, or point.	share someone's view vs reach agreement about	I agree with you about the ending. / We agreed on a date for the meeting. / They agree on most issues but disagree on how to solve this one.	Also agree to a proposal/request/terms; agree that + clause; agree to do something. 'I agree on you' is generally wrong when meaning 'I share your opinion.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: agree with vs on
599	5985	6866	humble vs humility	/ˈhʌmbəl/ vs /hjuːˈmɪləti/	contrast	Humble is primarily an adjective describing a person, attitude, origin, or thing as modest/not self-important; humility is the noun for the quality or state of being humble.	modest (adj) vs modesty/humbleness (noun)	She remained humble despite her success. / Her humility made it easy for her to admit mistakes.	Also humble as verb = make someone less proud / lower in status. 'Humbleness' exists but humility is the standard abstract noun in most contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: humility vs humble
599	5986	6201	apologetic vs agreeable	/əˌpɑːləˈdʒetɪk/ vs /əˈɡriːəbəl/	contrast card	Apologetic = showing regret, remorse, or awareness that one has caused inconvenience/offense. Agreeable = pleasant, willing to agree/cooperate, or acceptable/pleasing. They can coexist but describe different things.	sorry/remorseful vs pleasant/cooperative/amenable	He sounded apologetic about being late. / She was agreeable and happy to change the meeting time. / Someone can be apologetic but not agreeable: 'I'm sorry, but I still won't do it.'	Apologetic concerns regret; agreeable concerns pleasantness or willingness. 'Accommodating' is closer to cooperative; 'contrite' is stronger remorse. Do not infer apology merely from a soft/agreeable tone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: apologetic vs agreeable
599	5987	6921	recursive structure (linguistics)	/rɪˈkɜːrsɪv ˈstrʌktʃər/	linguistics	A grammatical structure in which a constituent can contain another constituent of the same type or a rule can apply repeatedly, enabling potentially unbounded embedding.	syntactic recursion, embedding, recursive syntax	The phrase 'the book [on the table [in the room]]' illustrates repeated embedding. / Clauses can be embedded inside clauses: 'I think [she said [he left]].'	Recursion is a major concept in syntax, though theoretical claims about its universality and role in human language are debated. Distinguish recursion from simple iteration.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: recursive structure linguistics
599	5988	5660	let someone in on something	/ˌlet ˌsʌmwʌn ˈɪn ɑːn/	idiom	To tell someone private, secret, or previously unknown information or allow them to participate in a plan.	confide in, reveal to, clue in, include, bring into the loop	I'll let you in on a little secret. / They finally let me in on the plan after the details were settled. / Can you let me in on what everyone is laughing about?	Two related ideas: reveal information/secret to someone, or allow someone to participate/share insider knowledge. Pattern: let + person + in on + noun/pronoun. 'Clue someone in' is close and informal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: let you in on it meaning
599	5989	6750	demographics	/ˌdeməˈɡræfɪks/	noun, plural / adjective-related field	Statistical characteristics of a population or audience, such as age, sex/gender categories, income, education, location, occupation, etc.; also population groups defined by such characteristics.	population characteristics, audience segments, population profile	The app is popular across several age demographics. / We need speakers from different demographic backgrounds, not just different accents.	Often plural 'demographics' for data/profile; singular demographic can mean a population segment ('a younger demographic'). Avoid treating personality or speaking style themselves as demographics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: demographics
599	5990	6189	pout	/paʊt/	verb / noun	To push the lips forward, often to show displeasure, disappointment, or playful sulking; more broadly, to sulk visibly. As a noun, the protruding-lips expression.	sulk, mope, make a face; pursed/protruding lips	She pouted when they said dessert was finished. / He was clearly disappointed, but instead of arguing he just sat there pouting. / She did an exaggerated pout for the photo.	Can be genuinely childish/sullen or deliberately cute/playful, especially in photos. Sulk emphasizes withdrawn bad mood; pout highlights visible facial/lip expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pout
600	5991	5907	rigmarole	/ˈrɪɡməˌroʊl/	noun	A long, complicated, tedious, or unnecessary procedure; also a lengthy, rambling story or explanation.	fuss, palaver, hassle, song and dance, bureaucratic runaround	I just wanted to change my address, but it turned into a whole rigmarole. / We had to go through the usual airport-security rigmarole.	Often 'go through the whole/usual rigmarole.' BrE also pronounces it roughly /ˈrɪɡmərəʊl/. Usually conveys annoyance at unnecessary steps.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: rigmarole
600	5992	6585	I'm totally floored	/aɪm ˌtoʊtəli ˈflɔːrd/	idiom / adjective phrase	I'm extremely surprised, stunned, impressed, shocked, or emotionally overwhelmed.	I'm stunned, blown away, gobsmacked, amazed	I'm totally floored by how good the finale was. / When she told me the actual price, I was floored.	Floor as a verb can mean knock someone down physically; figuratively, information/events 'floor' you by overwhelming your expectations or composure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: i'm totally floored meaning
600	5993	6265	cash in on something	/ˌkæʃ ˈɪn ɑːn/	phrasal verb	To exploit an opportunity, trend, reputation, event, or situation in order to make money or gain some other advantage.	capitalize on, profit from, exploit, monetize, take advantage of	Studios rushed to cash in on the sudden popularity of the genre. / The brand cashed in on nostalgia with a deliberately retro campaign. / He tried to cash in on his brief viral fame.	Often mildly critical because it suggests opportunism, though it can be neutral business language. Distinguish cash in X = exchange chips/coupons/investment for money; cash in on X = profit from an opportunity.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: cash in on
600	5994	6663	obfuscate	/ˈɑːbfəˌskeɪt/	verb	To make something unclear, confusing, difficult to understand, or harder to discover, whether deliberately or as an effect; in computing, to transform code/data to make it harder for humans to interpret.	obscure, cloud, muddle, conceal, confuse	The jargon obfuscates a fairly simple idea. / The company was accused of obfuscating the true cost. / Developers may obfuscate code to make reverse engineering harder.	Often implies deliberate concealment but not always. Noun: obfuscation. Stronger/more formal than confuse.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: obfuscate meaning
600	5995	6394	NDE (near-death experience)	/ˌen diː ˈiː/	initialism / noun	Near-death experience: a vivid subjective experience reported by some people who are close to death or believe they are, sometimes involving sensations such as leaving the body, moving through a tunnel, intense light, peace, or life review.	near-death experience	The memoir describes an NDE after a medical emergency. / Researchers study near-death experiences without assuming that every reported feature occurs in every case.	Raw 'nde' is highly ambiguous, but near-death experience is a common expansion in general discourse. Reports vary widely; interpretations can be neurological, psychological, spiritual, religious, or mixed. The term itself describes the reported experience, not its cause.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: nde meaning
600	5996	6701	weaponry	/ˈwepənri/	noun, collective	Weapons collectively, especially the range, stock, or systems of weapons used by a military, group, character, vehicle, etc.	weapons, armaments, arsenal	The documentary examines medieval weaponry. / The mech's weaponry is visually impressive but wildly impractical.	Usually uncountable collective noun: 'advanced weaponry,' not normally 'a weaponry.' Broader/formal compared with weapons.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: weaponry
600	5997	5689	provided / provided that	/prəˈvaɪdɪd/	conjunction	On the condition that; if and only if the stated requirement is met.	provided that, as long as, assuming that	You can borrow the car, provided you bring it back by ten.	Distinct from provided as the past participle of provide. As a conjunction, provided (that) introduces a condition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: provided meaning
600	5998	6001	dysphoria	/dɪsˈfɔːriə/	noun, clinical / psychological	A state of profound unease, dissatisfaction, or distress. In 'gender dysphoria,' distress related to incongruence between experienced gender and assigned sex/sex characteristics, as defined clinically.	distress, unease, dissatisfaction; gender dysphoria (specific term)	The medication can sometimes cause anxiety or dysphoria. / Gender dysphoria refers to distress, not simply to being transgender; not every transgender person experiences it.	Opposite-root concept euphoria = intense well-being. Dysphoria can occur in multiple clinical contexts. Do not use 'dysphoric' as a casual synonym for merely sad without context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: dysphoria
600	5999	6081	cross-examine	/ˌkrɔːs ɪɡˈzæmɪn/	verb	In court, to question a witness called by the opposing side; figuratively, to question someone intensely and skeptically as if testing their story.	question, interrogate, grill; challenge a witness	The defense attorney cross-examined the witness about the timeline. / I only asked where he'd been—there was no need to cross-examine him for twenty minutes.	Legal sense is specific: cross-examination follows/directly challenges testimony, usually by the opposing party. Figurative use can sound humorous: aggressive detailed questioning. Grill is less legally specific.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: cross-examine meaning
600	6000	6780	on the off chance	/ɑːn ði ˌɔːf ˈtʃæns/	idiom	Because there is a small or unlikely possibility that something may happen or be true; just in case, despite low odds.	just in case, on the slight chance, in case	I brought an umbrella on the off chance it rained. / I messaged him on the off chance he was still awake.	Often followed by that-clause or used before an action taken as a precaution. 'Off chance' = remote possibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: on the off chance meaning
601	6001	5644	AI girlfriend	/ˌeɪˈaɪ ˈɡɜːrlfrend/	noun phrase	An AI chatbot or virtual companion designed or used to simulate aspects of a romantic-girlfriend relationship.	virtual girlfriend, AI companion	The video tests how an AI girlfriend reacts when another AI character joins the call.	Reconstructed from the YouTube title search. The useful lexical target is AI girlfriend rather than preserving the entire video title as a headword.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: My AI Girlfriend Got Jealous When I Added Another Girl to the Call (Grok AI) - YouTube
601	6002	6630	carry as much weight as a feather	/ˌkæri əz ˌmʌtʃ ˈweɪt əz ə ˈfeðər/	creative idiom extension	To have extremely little importance, authority, persuasive force, or influence; a playful extension of 'carry weight.'	carry little/no weight, count for little, have little influence	In this argument, his opinion carries about as much weight as a feather. / Without evidence, that claim carries very little weight.	'Carry weight' is established: have influence/credibility. Adding 'as a feather' is creative humor, exploiting literal vs figurative weight.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: In this argument, his opinion carries about as much weight as a feather.
601	6003	6350	boucan	/buːˈkɑːn/	noun, historical	A wooden framework/grill used in the Caribbean for smoking or drying meat; by extension associated with the hunters called boucaniers, from whom English 'buccaneer' developed.	smoking rack, barbecue frame (historical)	Hunters smoked meat over a boucan before the term boucanier became associated with the Caribbean raiders later called buccaneers. / The word gives a useful etymological bridge from smoked meat to 'buccaneer.'	Historical French/Caribbean term with Indigenous Caribbean roots often traced through French. Valuable mainly for understanding buccaneer etymology rather than modern everyday use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: boucans
601	6004	6259	suffice	/səˈfaɪs/	verb	To be enough or adequate for a particular purpose or need.	be enough, be sufficient, do	A short explanation will suffice for now. / One example should suffice to show the difference. / If all you need is a quick backup, the free version may suffice.	Often somewhat formal. Patterns: X will suffice; suffice for X; suffice to do X. Existing card 'suffice it to say / suffice to say' is a fixed discourse construction derived from this verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: suffice meaning
601	6005	6070	chuck	/tʃʌk/	verb / noun, informal	To throw something casually or forcefully; to discard/get rid of something; in BrE/AusE, also to quit/leave or sometimes vomit in slang. As a noun, a chuck is also a clamping device on a drill.	toss, throw, fling; ditch, dump, throw away	Just chuck the keys over here. / I finally chucked all the cables I hadn't used in ten years. / He chucked the job and went traveling.	Very informal and especially common in BrE for throw/discard. 'Chuck it!' can mean stop it/give it up. Do not confuse with chuckle. Mechanical drill chuck is a separate noun sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: chuck meaning
601	6006	6818	last-ditch	/ˌlæst ˈdɪtʃ/	adjective	Done as a final desperate attempt after other options have failed or are nearly exhausted.	final, desperate, last-resort, eleventh-hour	They made a last-ditch effort to save the project. / The medication was a last-ditch option after other treatments failed.	Raw 'with last ditched' repaired. Usually before noun: last-ditch effort/attempt/measure. Related 'last resort.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: with last ditched meaning
601	6007	6331	Columbina / Columbine-like	/ˌkɑːləmˈbiːnə/	proper noun / descriptive reference	Columbina (also Columbine) is a witty female servant/maid stock character in commedia dell'arte, often clever, lively, and involved in romantic intrigue. 'Columbine-y' is a playful nonce adjective meaning reminiscent of that character/style.	Columbina-like, commedia maid/servant archetype	Her costume has a very Columbina-like, commedia feel. / Calling the performance 'Columbine-y' would be an improvised way to say it evokes the playful maid character.	Raw 'columbiney' is not a standard dictionary adjective. Preserve the search intent by anchoring it to Columbina/Columbine. Do not confuse with Columbine High School or the columbine flower when context is theatre.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: columbiney meaning
601	6008	6284	incursion	/ɪnˈkɜːrʒən/	noun	A sudden or brief entry into another territory or area, especially a hostile military entry; figuratively, an unwanted intrusion into a domain or activity.	raid, invasion, intrusion, foray	The army reported a cross-border incursion overnight. / The company viewed the startup's move into its market as an unwelcome incursion.	Usually smaller/shorter than a full invasion and often military/political. 'Foray' can be exploratory and non-hostile; incursion normally implies intrusion or violation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define incursion
601	6009	6518	shtick / schtick	/ʃtɪk/	noun, informal	A characteristic gimmick, routine, comic persona, recurring bit, style, or behavior associated with a performer/person; sometimes an annoying habitual act.	routine, gimmick, bit, act, persona	His whole shtick is pretending to be painfully cringe on purpose. / The fake arrogance is part of her online schtick, not necessarily how she acts off camera.	Raw 'being shtik for cringe' appears malformed; likely the key word is shtick/schtick. From Yiddish. 'That's his shtick' = that's his characteristic routine/gimmick.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: what does being shtik for cringe mean
601	6010	6089	Knickerbocker	/ˈnɪkərˌbɑːkər/	noun / adjective / proper-cultural term	Historically, a New Yorker descended from early Dutch settlers; more broadly an old New York cultural label. 'Knickerbockers/knickers' also refers to loose knee-length trousers. The New York Knicks name derives from Knickerbockers.	old New Yorker; knee breeches / knickerbockers	The word Knickerbocker became strongly associated with old New York and its Dutch heritage. / Vintage illustrations often show boys wearing knickerbockers fastened below the knee.	Multiple cultural senses. Washington Irving's fictional historian Diedrich Knickerbocker helped popularize the New York association. British 'knickers' now usually means women's underwear, so do not equate the modern words blindly.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define knickerbocker
602	6011	6197	aerobics	/eˈroʊbɪks/	noun	Exercise designed to improve cardiovascular fitness through sustained rhythmic movement that raises heart rate and breathing, especially group routines performed to music.	cardio, aerobic exercise, fitness class	My mother used to do aerobics videos in the living room in the 1980s. / The gym offers dance aerobics as well as strength classes.	Grammatically usually treated as singular as a field/activity: aerobics is good exercise. 'Aerobic' means involving oxygen or cardiovascular endurance; not all cardio is the stereotypical choreographed aerobics class.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: aerobics meaningg
602	6012	5947	ergative verb (English)	/ˈɜːrɡətɪv vɜːrb/	noun, grammar	In many English-learning grammars, a verb that can be transitive with an affected object and intransitive with that same participant as subject, without an overt passive marker.	labile verb (overlapping term), causative/inchoative alternation	I broke the glass. → The glass broke. / She opened the door. → The door opened. / Heat melted the ice. → The ice melted.	The intransitive sentence is not passive: 'The door opened' has no was/be + past participle. Linguistic terminology varies; many linguists call these labile verbs or discuss the causative–inchoative alternation rather than labeling the English verb itself ergative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: ergative verbs
602	6013	5861	work something out	/ˌwɜːrk ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To solve, understand, calculate, plan, or reach a satisfactory arrangement concerning something.	figure out, solve, resolve	We need to work out why the upload keeps failing.	Existing work out card focuses on exercise and 'turn out successfully'; transitive work something out is common enough to preserve separately.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: work stuff out meaning
602	6014	6590	grating	/ˈɡreɪtɪŋ/	adjective / noun	Harshly irritating or unpleasant, especially a voice, sound, manner, or personality; also the act/result of shredding with a grater or a grid-like structure.	irritating, jarring, abrasive, rasping	His forced laugh became grating after a while. / The metallic noise was incredibly grating.	Figurative adjective is common. From grate: scrape harshly / irritate.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: grating meaning
602	6015	5899	circumstantial	/ˌsɜːrkəmˈstænʃəl/	adjective	Relating to the circumstances surrounding an event rather than direct observation; also dependent on or shaped by particular circumstances.	indirect, contextual, situational	The case was based largely on circumstantial evidence. / Their success was partly circumstantial—it depended on unusually favorable timing.	Most familiar legal collocation: circumstantial evidence = indirect evidence from which a fact is inferred. Not synonymous with weak evidence; circumstantial evidence can be strong.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: circumstantial meaning
602	6016	6493	beat one's meat	/ˌbiːt wʌnz ˈmiːt/	slang, vulgar	A crude slang expression meaning to masturbate, typically referring to a male masturbating.	jerk off, jack off, masturbate	The phrase 'beat your meat' is vulgar joking slang for masturbating. / It's the kind of expression you may hear in crude internet humor rather than polite conversation.	Vulgar and often juvenile/comedic. Use neutral 'masturbate' in medical/formal contexts. Preserve because raw search explicitly asked for the slang meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: beat meat meaning slang
602	6017	6634	polymathic	/ˌpɑːliˈmæθɪk/	adjective	Having broad, deep knowledge or ability across multiple different fields; characteristic of a polymath.	multidisciplinary, wide-ranging, broadly learned	Her polymathic mind jumps easily from linguistics to art history to programming. / A 'polymathic brain' is an informal way to praise unusually broad intellectual interests.	Less common than polymath (noun). Broad curiosity alone does not necessarily make someone a polymath; the word usually implies substantial competence/knowledge across fields.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: polymathic brain meaning
602	6018	5692	upon request	/əˌpɑːn rɪˈkwest/	formal phrase	If or when someone asks for it; available or done when requested.	on request, if requested	Additional documentation is available upon request.	Formal/common in notices, policies, and business writing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: upon request meaning
602	6019	6184	aneurysm	/ˈænjəˌrɪzəm/	noun, medical	An abnormal bulging or ballooning of a weakened area in the wall of a blood vessel, most often an artery; rupture can cause life-threatening internal bleeding.	arterial aneurysm, vascular bulge	The scan revealed a small cerebral aneurysm. / A ruptured aneurysm is a medical emergency.	Aneurysms can occur in different vessels, including the aorta and cerebral arteries, and many unruptured aneurysms cause no symptoms. Sudden severe symptoms require emergency medical care; the word is also hyperbolically misused online ('this gives me an aneurysm').		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: aneurysm 意味は
602	6020	6023	under the guise of	/ˌʌndər ðə ˈɡaɪz əv/	prepositional phrase	While presenting something as if it were a particular legitimate, harmless, or different thing, often concealing the real purpose or identity.	under the pretense of, pretending to be, masquerading as, in the name of (sometimes)	He collected personal information under the guise of conducting a survey. / The villain enters the town under the guise of a traveling doctor.	Usually implies deception/concealment, unlike neutral 'in the form of.' Existing example in Master already uses this construction; standalone phrase retained because it is highly productive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: under the guise of
603	6021	5645	unequivocally	/ˌʌnɪˈkwɪvəkəli/	adverb	In a way that is completely clear, definite, and leaving no room for doubt or ambiguity.	unambiguously, categorically, definitively, unmistakably, without reservation	She unequivocally denied the accusation. / The test results do not unequivocally prove the claim. / He said unequivocally that he would not support the proposal.	Related adjective: unequivocal. Strong formal word meaning there is no ambiguity or room for competing interpretation. Common with state, condemn, reject, support, demonstrate/prove. Stronger and more formal than simply 'clearly.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: unequivocally meaning
603	6022	6722	the pot calling the kettle black	/ðə ˌpɑːt ˌkɔːlɪŋ ðə ˌketəl ˈblæk/	idiom	A criticism of hypocrisy: someone faults another person for a flaw that the critic also has.	that's hypocritical, look who's talking, pot-kettle	He called me disorganized? That's the pot calling the kettle black—his desk is worse than mine.	Raw search omitted 'pot.' Standard form is 'the pot calling the kettle black.' Often shortened jokingly to 'pot, meet kettle.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: calling the kettle black meaning
603	6023	6397	eyelid twitching	/ˈaɪlɪd ˌtwɪtʃɪŋ/	noun phrase	Small involuntary contractions around the eyelid, commonly called an eyelid twitch; often temporary and associated with factors such as fatigue, stress, caffeine, or eye strain.	eyelid twitch, eye twitch, eyelid myokymia (common benign form)	My eyelid starts twitching when I've been staring at a screen too long. / The twitch disappeared after a few days of better sleep.	Common everyday phrase worth retaining as a concrete body-context card. 'Myokymia' is a more specific medical term often used for benign eyelid twitching; not every twitch should be self-diagnosed from vocabulary alone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: eyelid twitching
603	6024	5983	Chicxulub	/ˈtʃiːkʃəˌluːb/; also approximations vary	proper noun	The name of the large impact crater buried beneath the Yucatán Peninsula, associated with the asteroid impact about 66 million years ago linked to the end-Cretaceous mass extinction.	Chicxulub crater, K–Pg impact crater	The Chicxulub impact released an enormous amount of energy and had global environmental effects. / The crater itself is mostly buried, so you don't see a giant open hole when visiting the region.	Name comes from Chicxulub in Yucatán; English pronunciations vary. The impact is strongly linked to the K–Pg extinction event that eliminated non-avian dinosaurs and many other organisms.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: Chicxulub meaning
603	6025	6388	game a test / game the test	/ˌɡeɪm ə ˈtest/	verb phrase	To exploit the rules, scoring system, predictable patterns, loopholes, or test-taking strategies in order to maximize a score without necessarily improving the underlying ability the test is meant to measure.	game the system, exploit the test, teach to the test (related), optimize for the metric	Students can sometimes game a standardized test by learning its predictable tricks. / If an AI benchmark becomes too predictable, developers may optimize for it in ways that game the test rather than improve general ability.	Game as a verb = manipulate/exploit a system's rules for advantage. 'Game the test' is more natural than 'game a test' when referring to a specific assessment. Good example of Goodhart-style metric optimization.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: to game a test meaning
603	6026	6746	How many stops in are you?	/haʊ ˌmeni ˌstɑːps ˈɪn ɑːr ju/	conversational construction	How far into a multi-stage route/activity are you, measured by completed/current stops? Natural for a pub crawl when context is already clear.	How far in are you?, how many stops have you done?	'How many stops in are you?' 'We're at the fourth pub.' / How many episodes in are you? uses the same X-in construction with a different unit.	Raw 'How many stops are you in crawling pub?' is unnatural. For pub crawl: 'How many stops in are you?' or 'How many pubs have you hit so far?'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: how many stops are you in crawling pub meaning
603	6027	6676	preachy	/ˈpriːtʃi/	adjective, informal	Too eager to tell people what is morally right or what they should believe/do, especially in a self-righteous, didactic, or heavy-handed way.	moralizing, didactic, sanctimonious, sermonizing, self-righteous	The movie has a clear message, but it rarely feels preachy. / His advice became preachy once he stopped listening and started lecturing.	Common media criticism. A work can be political/moral without being preachy; preachy criticizes delivery/tone, especially overt lecturing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: preachy meaning
603	6028	6123	conscientious	/ˌkɑːnʃiˈenʃəs/	adjective	Careful, responsible, diligent, and guided by a desire to do one's work or duties properly and ethically.	diligent, responsible, scrupulous, thorough, careful, dutiful	She's a conscientious editor who checks every citation before publication. / He was conscientious about replying even when he couldn't solve the problem immediately.	Conscientious ≠ conscious. Conscientious = responsible/careful; conscious = awake/aware. Scrupulous can emphasize moral correctness or meticulous care. Often 'conscientious about/in doing X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: conscientious
603	6029	5720	get down with something	/ɡet ˌdaʊn wɪð/	informal phrase	To enjoy, approve of, understand, or enthusiastically participate in something; older slang can also mean dance energetically.	be into, be cool with, enjoy	I can get down with that idea.	Do not confuse with get down to = begin serious work. 'Get down with it' often means join in/enjoy it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: get down with it meaning
603	6030	6617	move on to the next target	/ˌmuːv ɑːn tə ðə ˌnekst ˈtɑːrɡɪt/	phrase	To stop focusing on the current person, task, objective, or object of attention and proceed to the next one.	proceed to the next objective, switch targets, move to the next item	Once this batch is finished, we'll move on to the next target. / The predator abandoned the chase and moved on to the next target.	Compositional rather than a fixed idiom. Target can be literal, strategic, sales/work-related, or figurative; tone changes with context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: move on to the next target meaning
604	6031	6033	keep your hand over the top of your glass	/ˌkiːp jər ˈhænd ˌoʊvər ðə ˌtɑːp əv jər ˈɡlæs/	practical phrase	To physically cover the opening of a drink glass with your hand as a precaution against spills or someone adding something to the drink.	cover your drink, keep your drink covered	In a crowded club, she kept her hand over the top of her glass while moving through the room. / If you leave a drink unattended, getting a fresh one is safer than assuming it is fine.	This is literal safety advice, not a fixed idiom. 'Cover your drink' is much more compact/natural. The exact phrase may arise in discussions of drink-spiking precautions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: keep your hand over the top of the glass meaning
604	6032	6232	exotification	/ɪɡˌzɑːtəfəˈkeɪʃən/	noun, social/cultural discourse	The process of treating a person, culture, or group as fascinatingly 'exotic' or fundamentally other, often reducing them to stereotypes, fantasy, novelty, or aesthetic difference rather than seeing them as full individuals.	exoticization, othering, fetishization (overlapping), romanticization	The essay criticizes the exotification of Japanese women in Western dating fantasies. / Travel media can celebrate cultural difference without turning people into exotic scenery.	Often critical. Exotification can sound positive on the surface ('mysterious,' 'special,' 'exotic') while still objectifying. Fetishization adds stronger obsessive/sexual or reductive fixation; othering is broader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: exotification
604	6033	6339	LitRPG	/ˌlɪt ɑːr piː ˈdʒiː/	noun / genre label	A fiction genre—'literary role-playing game'—in which RPG/game mechanics such as levels, stats, skills, quests, classes, experience points, or system messages are explicit parts of the story/world.	game-system fiction, progression fantasy (overlapping), RPG fiction	The novel is LitRPG: the protagonist can literally see stats, gain levels, and unlock skills. / Not every isekai is LitRPG; explicit game mechanics are the key clue.	Often overlaps with progression fantasy and isekai but is not identical. Progression fantasy focuses on measurable growth even without game UI; isekai focuses on transport/reincarnation to another world.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: lit rpg
604	6034	5942	provocative	/prəˈvɑːkətɪv/	adjective	Intended or likely to provoke a strong reaction, thought, controversy, anger, or sexual interest.	thought-provoking, challenging, inflammatory, controversial, suggestive, sexually suggestive	The documentary asks a provocative question without pretending there is an easy answer. / His deliberately provocative post was obviously designed to start an argument. / The ad was considered provocative because of its sexual imagery.	Provocative is not automatically sexual. Thought-provoking is usually positive/intellectual; inflammatory emphasizes anger/conflict; suggestive often implies sexual innuendo; provocative can cover all of these depending on what reaction is being provoked.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: provocative
604	6035	5665	unc	/ʌŋk/	noun, slang	Short for uncle; in contemporary slang, a joking label for an older man or someone perceived as old, out of touch, or 'uncle-aged.'	uncle, old head	They started calling him unc because he complained about the music being too loud.	Can be affectionate, teasing, or insulting depending on context. Often used beyond literal family relationships.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111, voca_0116 | search: unc meaning
604	6036	5568	sure as hell	/ˌʃʊr əz ˈhel/	intensifying phrase, informal	Certainly; definitely; used to emphasize strong confidence, determination, or contradiction.	definitely, certainly, damn sure	I'm sure as hell not paying for something I didn't order.	Informal and mildly vulgar. Often appears as be sure as hell or sure as hell + clause.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: sure as hell meaning
604	6037	6257	neurodivergent women	/ˌnʊroʊdaɪˈvɜːrdʒənt ˈwɪmɪn/	context phrase / topic card	Women who are neurodivergent; the phrase often appears in discussion of how autism, ADHD, and related traits may be recognized, masked, stereotyped, or diagnosed differently in women because research and diagnostic expectations historically centered other populations.	neurodivergent females (less preferred in ordinary reference), autistic/ADHD women (when specific)	The article focuses on neurodivergent women who learned to mask social difficulties for years. / Discussions of late diagnosis often mention women whose traits did not match older stereotypes.	Not a separate diagnosis. Avoid assuming all neurodivergent women share one presentation. 'Masking' and diagnostic disparities are common discussion themes, but individual conditions and experiences vary widely.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: neurodivergent women meaning
604	6038	6843	have someone to bellyache to	/hæv ˌsʌmwʌn tə ˈbeliˌeɪk tuː/	informal phrase	To have a person available to complain, grumble, or vent to.	have someone to complain to, vent to, gripe to, moan to	Sometimes you don't need advice; you just want someone to bellyache to for five minutes. / He called his brother because he needed somebody to gripe to.	Bellyache as verb = complain persistently, often about relatively minor things; humorous/negative compared with neutral 'vent.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: have someone to bellyache to meaing
604	6039	6139	wander off	/ˌwɑːndər ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To move away from a place, person, group, path, or subject without a clear plan or often without noticing; figuratively, for attention/thought/speech to drift away.	stray, drift away, roam off, walk away, get distracted (figurative)	Don't wander off—we're leaving in ten minutes. / The child wandered off while everyone was looking at the parade. / My mind wandered off halfway through the explanation.	Off highlights departure from a reference point/group/path. Can be literal movement or figurative attention/topic. Stronger safety implication than simply wander around.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: wander off meaning
604	6040	6115	muster	/ˈmʌstər/	verb / noun	To gather or summon people, strength, courage, energy, enthusiasm, or other resources; as a noun, an assembly or formal gathering, especially of troops.	summon, gather, marshal, scrape together, rally	I finally mustered the courage to ask the question. / She couldn't muster much enthusiasm for another sequel. / The commander mustered the troops before dawn.	Very common figurative collocations: muster courage/strength/energy/enthusiasm/support. 'Pass muster' = meet an acceptable standard (different idiom). As noun, military muster = assembly/roll call.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: mustering
605	6041	6398	fanny (AmE vs BrE)	/ˈfæni/	noun, informal	In American English, fanny is a mild/childish word for the buttocks. In British English and some other varieties, it is vulgar slang for the female genitals/vulva, so the same word can sound dramatically different across dialects.	butt, backside (AmE); vulva/female genitals (BrE vulgar)	An American parent might tell a child to sit on their fanny. / In Britain, that sentence can sound startling because fanny has a much more vulgar anatomical meaning there.	Classic transatlantic false-friend. This dialect difference explains why AmE 'fanny pack' sounds funny or rude to many British speakers, who normally say bum bag.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: fanny meaning america
605	6042	6131	rhinoceros / rhino	/raɪˈnɑːsərəs/; /ˈraɪnoʊ/	noun	A very large, thick-skinned herbivorous mammal with one or two horns on its snout, native to Africa and Asia.	rhino	We saw a white rhinoceros resting near the water. / Despite their heavy build, rhinos can move surprisingly fast.	Plural rhinoceroses or rhinoceros; rhinos is far more common conversationally. Five living species remain. The 'white' in white rhinoceros is not a color contrast in ordinary appearance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: rhinoceros meaning
605	6043	6580	pound someone	/ˌpaʊnd ˌsʌmwʌn/	verb, informal; context-dependent	To hit or beat someone repeatedly/heavily; in vulgar sexual slang, to have vigorous penetrative sex with someone. Context determines the intended sense.	beat, pummel, hammer; have sex with (vulgar slang)	The boxer pounded his opponent with body shots. / In crude sexual dialogue, 'pound you' can be a blunt reference to vigorous sex.	Highly context-sensitive. The ordinary violence sense is common; the sexual sense is vulgar/objectifying. Also pound = strike repeatedly, pound on a door, heart pounds, British currency unit, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: pound you meaning
605	6044	5599	extraterrestrially	/ˌekstrətəˈrestriəli/	adverb, rare	In a manner relating to or suggestive of something beyond Earth; a rare adverb formed from extraterrestrial.	alienly, otherworldlily	The landscape was lit so strangely that it looked almost extraterrestrially beautiful.	Rare and awkward in ordinary prose. It can suggest 'alien / from beyond Earth,' but otherworldly is usually the more natural adjective for an eerie or unearthly quality.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: extraterrestrially can mean other worldly?
605	6045	6886	go on to do vs go on doing	/ɡoʊ ˈɑːn/	grammar contrast	Go on to do = proceed to a new/subsequent action after finishing or discussing something else. Go on doing = continue the same activity.	proceed to do vs continue doing	After explaining the theory, she went on to give three examples. / She went on talking for another twenty minutes.	Classic gerund/infinitive meaning contrast. 'Go on with + noun' also means continue: go on with the story/work.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: go on to do or doing
605	6046	5730	in limbo / a state of limbo	/ɪn ˈlɪmboʊ/	idiom	In an uncertain, unresolved, or suspended state where progress or a final decision cannot be made.	in uncertainty, in suspension, stuck	The project remained in limbo while funding was reviewed.	Natural expressions are in limbo and in a state of limbo; 'limbo state' is understandable but less idiomatic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: limbo state meaning
605	6047	6233	yellow fever (racial fetishization)	/ˌjeloʊ ˈfiːvər/	offensive slang / social-discourse term	A critical or mocking label for a racialized sexual/romantic fixation on East Asian people, especially women, based on stereotypes rather than individual attraction. Separately, yellow fever is the name of a viral disease.	Asian fetish, racial fetishization, fetishization of Asian women/men	She disliked being approached by men whose comments suggested an Asian fetish rather than interest in her as a person. / In discussions of dating, 'yellow fever' can refer critically to racial fetishization; in medicine it means an unrelated viral disease.	The phrase uses 'yellow' as a racial label and can itself be offensive. Distinguish ordinary attraction to an individual from stereotyped preference/objectification. Medical yellow fever is a completely separate literal sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: yellow fever fetish
605	6048	5787	fixation	/fɪkˈseɪʃən/	noun	An unusually strong, persistent focus, attachment, or preoccupation with someone or something; also the act of fixing something in position.	obsession, preoccupation; fastening	His fixation on minor details slowed the entire project.	Psychological/everyday sense often implies excessive focus: fixation on/with something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: fixation meaning
605	6049	6261	here's the kicker	/ˌhɪrz ðə ˈkɪkər/	idiom / discourse phrase	Used to introduce the surprising, decisive, ironic, especially important, or most striking part of a story or situation.	here's the catch, here's the twist, and the crazy part is, most importantly	The hotel looked cheap—and here's the kicker: breakfast alone cost fifty dollars. / He apologized for being late. Here's the kicker: he'd been sitting in the wrong café for an hour.	Kicker = the detail that changes how you see the whole story. 'Here's the catch' specifically emphasizes a hidden drawback/condition; kicker can be good, bad, ironic, or simply surprising.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: here is a kicker meaning
605	6050	6684	representation	/ˌreprɪzenˈteɪʃən/	noun	Something that depicts, stands for, expresses, or models something else; the act of representing; also advocacy on someone's behalf. In cognition/linguistics, a mental or internal encoding/structure used to represent information.	depiction, portrayal, model, symbol, encoding; advocacy	A map is a representation of physical space. / The study asks how words are represented in memory. / The lawyer provided legal representation.	Highly polysemous. In cognitive science, 'representation' is theory-laden: researchers may disagree about what is represented and in what format.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: representation
606	6051	5528	prodigal	/ˈprɑːdɪɡəl/	adjective / noun	Wastefully extravagant with money or resources; as a noun, a person who leaves and later returns, especially in the phrase the prodigal son.	wasteful, extravagant, spendthrift	After years abroad, the prodigal son finally returned home.	Do not confuse with prodigious. The returnee sense comes from the biblical parable of the Prodigal Son, whose defining trait was wasteful spending.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: prodigal meaning
606	6052	6482	gilded cage	/ˌɡɪldɪd ˈkeɪdʒ/	idiom / metaphor	A situation that looks luxurious, privileged, or desirable from the outside but restricts freedom, autonomy, or happiness.	golden cage, luxurious prison, comfortable captivity	The palace became a gilded cage: she had every luxury except the freedom to leave. / A high-paying job can feel like a gilded cage if the money makes you afraid to quit.	Raw search misspelled 'guilded.' Gilded = covered with a thin layer of gold / made outwardly splendid. The metaphor contrasts attractive exterior with confinement.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: guilded cage meaning
606	6053	5724	EFL learner	/ˌiː ef ˈel ˌlɜːrnər/	noun	A person learning English as a foreign language, typically in a setting where English is not the main language of everyday life.	English-as-a-foreign-language learner	The textbook is designed for intermediate EFL learners.	EFL = English as a Foreign Language. Traditionally contrasted with ESL, though EL/ELL and other terms are also used depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: efl learners
606	6054	6589	toss and turn	/ˌtɔːs ən ˈtɜːrn/	idiom	To keep moving restlessly in bed, especially because you cannot sleep.	sleep restlessly, lie awake, fidget in bed	I tossed and turned all night before the interview. / The room was too hot, so I kept tossing and turning.	Usually describes sleepless restlessness rather than ordinary repositioning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: toss and turn definition
606	6055	6138	veer off track	/ˌvɪr ɔːf ˈtræk/	verb phrase	To change direction away from the intended physical route, plan, subject, or course, often gradually or unexpectedly.	go off course, drift off track, deviate, stray, swerve away	The conversation veered off track once everyone started arguing about terminology. / The project began well but veered off track after the requirements changed.	Veer emphasizes a change of direction; off track emphasizes departure from intended course. Existing veer is broad; this collocation is useful figuratively for discussions/projects.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115, voca_0119 | search: veer off track meaning
606	6056	5864	the fish is off	/ðə ˈfɪʃ ɪz ˈɔːf/	phrase, chiefly British-style food use	The fish has gone bad or is no longer fresh enough to eat; more broadly, off can mean spoiled or stale of food.	spoiled, gone bad, not fresh, turned	Don't eat that—the fish smells off. / I think the fish is off; it's got a strange sour smell.	For food, be off / smell off / taste off commonly means spoiled or suspiciously unfresh. Separate idiom 'something is off' = something seems wrong, strange, or not quite right.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: the fish is off meaning
606	6057	5766	unto	/ˈʌntuː/	preposition, archaic/literary	To; toward; or in relation to, especially in biblical, archaic, or deliberately elevated language.	to, toward	Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.	Existing unto itself is a specific phrase; bare unto is useful for recognizing older/biblical constructions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define unto
606	6058	6237	make something easy / make it easy for someone to do something	/ˌmeɪk ˈiːzi/	construction / usage card	To reduce the difficulty, effort, friction, or complexity of an action or situation; commonly framed as make X easy or make it easy for someone to do X.	simplify, facilitate, streamline, make easier, lower the barrier	The app makes it easy to review ten cards at a time. / Clear examples make difficult vocabulary easier to remember. / He didn't exactly make things easy for himself by arriving unprepared.	Raw search 'something made easy' may appear in titles such as 'grammar made easy,' meaning explained/simplified so it becomes easy to learn or do. 'Facilitate' is more formal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: something made easy meaning
606	6059	6268	unsung king / unsung hero	/ˌʌnˈsʌŋ ˈkɪŋ/	creative phrase / adjective collocation	Unsung means deserving praise or recognition but receiving little of it. 'Unsung hero' is the established phrase; 'unsung king' is a creative extension meaning an underrecognized person who is exceptionally good or dominant in some area.	unrecognized, underappreciated, overlooked, underrated	He's the unsung king of background reaction shots—half the comedy comes from his face. / The editor was an unsung hero of the production.	Preserve 'unsung king' because the search may come from a specific praise context, but note that unsung hero is the fixed/common collocation. Unsung literally evokes someone whose praises are not sung.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: unsung king meaning
606	6060	6868	anticipate the listener's information needs	/ænˈtɪsəˌpeɪt ðə ˈlɪsənərz ˌɪnfərˈmeɪʃən niːdz/	communication principle	To predict what background, clarification, context, definitions, or connections a listener will need in order to follow you, and supply them before the listener has to ask.	preempt confusion, provide context proactively, audience-design one's explanation	Good speakers anticipate the listener's information needs before they've expressed them. / If I mention a niche anime term, I should anticipate what context the listener needs to understand the point.	Preserves exact conceptual search with typos repaired. Closely related to audience design and common ground: tailoring an utterance to what the listener likely knows/needs.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: You're anticipating the listner's information needs before thery've expressed them.
607	6061	6438	retroactively	/ˌretroʊˈæktɪvli/	adverb	With effect applied backward to an earlier date, event, period, or already-completed action; by reinterpreting or changing something after the fact.	with retrospective effect, after the fact, retrospectively	The pay increase was applied retroactively to January. / The platform changed the rule but said it would not punish users retroactively. / Later episodes retroactively changed how I understood the first scene.	Legal/admin sense = effect reaches backward in time. Narrative/everyday sense can mean later information changes one's interpretation of earlier material. Retroactive ≠ merely retrospective: retrospective looks back; retroactive changes/applies to the past.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: retroactively
607	6062	5563	acute	/əˈkjuːt/	adjective	Very serious, severe, sharp, or intense; also, in medicine, beginning suddenly and often lasting a relatively short time.	severe, intense, sharp	The hospital is dealing with an acute shortage of nurses.	Common senses include acute pain, acute awareness, acute shortage, acute angle, and acute illness. Contrast medical acute with chronic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: acute definition
607	6063	6192	stop on a dime	/ˌstɑːp ɑːn ə ˈdaɪm/	idiom, chiefly AmE	To stop extremely quickly and within a very short distance; figuratively, to halt or change immediately.	stop dead, stop instantly, halt abruptly, turn on a dime (related)	The dog can sprint across the yard and stop on a dime. / The actor's expression can go from manic to blank on a dime. / The project couldn't simply stop on a dime once contracts were signed.	A dime is tiny, creating the image of stopping within a tiny space. Compare 'turn on a dime' = change direction very quickly. Often used for vehicles, athletes, animals, behavior, or processes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: stop on a dime
607	6064	6786	loaded word	/ˌloʊdɪd ˈwɜːrd/	noun phrase	A word carrying strong emotional, ideological, evaluative, historical, or cultural associations beyond its basic descriptive meaning, so its use can frame a discussion.	emotionally charged term, value-laden word, loaded term	Calling the policy 'radical' can be a loaded word choice depending on the context. / 'Natural' is often a loaded word in debates about behavior.	Loaded does not mean false or forbidden; it means the term brings connotations/framing that should be noticed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: loaded word meaning
607	6065	6548	get out of the sticks	/ɡet ˌaʊt əv ðə ˈstɪks/	idiomatic phrase	To leave a remote, rural, sparsely populated area and go somewhere more urban or connected.	leave the countryside, get out of the boonies, move to town/the city	He couldn't wait to get out of the sticks and move to New York. / We finally got out of the sticks and found a café with decent Wi-Fi.	Existing 'in the sticks' = in a remote rural area. This card preserves the searched motion construction 'get out of the sticks.' Informal; can sound dismissive toward rural places.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: to get out of the sticks meaning
607	6066	6454	scram	/skræm/	verb / imperative, informal	To leave or go away quickly, especially when ordered to; 'Scram!' means 'Get out of here!'	get lost, beat it, clear out, run along, leave	Scram before the guard sees you. / 'You kids, scram!' the old man shouted. / We heard sirens and decided to scram.	Strongly informal, often comic/old-fashioned in modern dialogue. As an imperative it can sound rude. Not related to scramble despite superficial resemblance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: scram mean ing
607	6067	6686	voyeuristic	/ˌvɔɪjəˈrɪstɪk/	adjective	Characterized by voyeurism—taking intrusive or sexualized interest/pleasure in observing others' private, intimate, vulnerable, or sensational experiences; more broadly, uncomfortably prying as a viewer.	prying, intrusive, peeping, prurient, exploitative	The camera lingers in a way that feels voyeuristic rather than empathetic. / Critics called the coverage voyeuristic because it turned private grief into spectacle.	Can be literally sexual or broader media criticism. Broader use still implies an ethically uncomfortable spectator relationship, not simply 'watching closely.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: voyeuristic meaning
607	6068	5973	tenosynovitis	/ˌtenoʊˌsɪnəˈvaɪtɪs/	noun, medical	Inflammation or irritation of the synovial sheath surrounding a tendon, causing pain and sometimes swelling or impaired movement.	tendon-sheath inflammation	De Quervain tenosynovitis can cause pain near the thumb side of the wrist. / Trigger finger is a form of stenosing tenosynovitis affecting a finger's flexor tendon sheath.	Tendinitis concerns the tendon itself; tenosynovitis concerns its sheath, though they can coexist. Spelling raw search 'tendosinovitis' corrected to tenosynovitis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tendosinovitis
607	6069	6656	what made you feel that way?	/wʌt ˌmeɪd ju ˌfiːl ðæt ˈweɪ/	conversational question	What caused or led you to have that feeling, impression, reaction, or belief?	what gave you that impression?, why did you feel that way?	You said the conversation felt awkward—what made you feel that way? / What made you feel that the ending was rushed?	Often gentler/more exploratory than blunt 'Why?' because it asks about the cause/evidence behind the feeling. Can refer to emotion or impression/judgment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: what made you feel that way meaning
607	6070	5846	dox vs dunk on	/dɑːks/; /dʌŋk ɑːn/	slang contrast	Dox means reveal someone's private identifying information online; dunk on means publicly mock, criticize, defeat, or show someone up. They are not interchangeable.		Doxing someone exposes private information; dunking on someone is public ridicule or one-upmanship.	Preserve because the original search explicitly asks whether two internet-slang verbs are interchangeable. Existing individual cards cover each term.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: dox and dunk are interchangeable in slang?
608	6071	6557	figurine	/ˌfɪɡjəˈriːn/	noun	A small ornamental or collectible statue representing a person, animal, character, deity, etc.	statuette, miniature statue, collectible figure	She has a shelf of ceramic cat figurines. / The limited-edition anime figurine sold out immediately.	Usually emphasizes a small statue/decorative object. In hobby/anime contexts, 'figure' is often more natural for character collectibles; figurine can sound decorative or traditional.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: figurine meaning
608	6072	6527	get salty	/ˌɡet ˈsɔːlti/	slang	To become annoyed, bitter, resentful, defensive, or upset, especially over a loss, criticism, teasing, or perceived slight.	get bitter, get annoyed, get mad, get butthurt (vulgar-ish slang), get sore	Don't get salty just because you lost one match. / He got salty when people pointed out the contradiction in his review.	Internet/gaming slang broadened into general informal speech. 'Salty' often implies visible bitterness or sore-loser energy, not just ordinary anger.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: get salty meaning
608	6073	5716	constant motion	/ˌkɑːnstənt ˈmoʊʃən/	noun phrase	Continuous or nearly continuous movement, with little or no pause.	continuous movement, perpetual activity	The toddlers seemed to be in constant motion all afternoon.	Transparent phrase, but useful as a common descriptive chunk: be in constant motion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: constant motion meaning
608	6074	6109	thug	/θʌɡ/	noun	A violent, intimidating, or criminal person, especially one used to threaten or attack others; also used loosely as an insult for someone perceived as brutish or lawless.	brute, hoodlum, goon, heavy, enforcer, gangster (not exact)	The gang sent two thugs to intimidate the shop owner. / The film's villain surrounds himself with anonymous thugs. / Calling protesters 'thugs' can carry strong political and racialized implications depending on context.	Historically from Hindi ṭhag via British colonial usage. Modern English often means violent criminal/enforcer. In U.S. public discourse, applying 'thug' selectively to Black people has been criticized as racially coded; context and speaker matter.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define thug
608	6075	6895	libel someone	/ˈlaɪbəl ˌsʌmwʌn/	verb, legal/general	To publish a defamatory statement about someone in a form treated as libel; more loosely, to defame someone in writing/publication.	defame, smear, malign, publish defamatory claims about	He threatened to sue, claiming the newspaper had libeled him. / Be careful: calling something 'libel' is a legal claim, not merely saying it was insulting.	Traditional libel/slander distinction is written/published vs spoken, but exact defamation law and categories vary by jurisdiction. Existing noun libel card remains useful.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: libel someone meaning
608	6076	5949	labile vs stable	/ˈleɪbaɪl/ vs /ˈsteɪbəl/	contrast card	Labile means liable to change, unstable, reactive, or easily altered; stable means resistant to change and remaining relatively constant.	unstable, changeable, reactive vs steady, fixed, resistant	The compound is chemically labile at room temperature, whereas the comparison compound is stable. / His mood was emotionally labile rather than stable.	Labile is used in chemistry, biology, medicine/psychology, and linguistics. In medicine 'emotionally labile' means emotions shift rapidly/easily. In linguistics a labile verb alternates transitive/intransitive. Stable is the broad everyday opposite only in the 'changeable' sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: labile vs stable
608	6077	5993	under one's belt	/ˌʌndər wʌnz ˈbelt/	idiom	Already achieved, completed, learned, or gained as experience; sometimes, already eaten/drunk.	to one's credit, completed, accumulated, in one's experience	With a few presentations under my belt, I stopped getting quite so nervous. / Once you have a couple of long trips under your belt, packing gets much easier. / He had a good breakfast under his belt before the hike.	Common: have/get X under one's belt. Experience/achievements sense is highly productive. Food sense is more literal-humorous and less central.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: under one's belt
608	6078	5741	tetchy	/ˈtetʃi/	adjective, chiefly British	Easily annoyed, irritable, and somewhat touchy.	irritable, touchy, testy, cranky, prickly	The interview became tetchy when the subject of money came up. / Everyone was getting a bit tetchy after hours of delays. / His tetchy reply made it obvious I'd hit a nerve.	Especially common in British English. Close to 'testy': easily irritated and showing it in speech/behavior. 'Touchy' can also mean sensitive about a particular subject; 'tetchy' more directly describes an irritable mood/manner.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: tetchy
608	6079	6740	lop off / lopping off	/ˌlɑːp ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To cut, chop, or remove a part, especially a branch, limb, end, or unnecessary section, often in a quick/rough way; figuratively, remove a chunk from a price, runtime, budget, etc.	chop off, cut off, trim off, remove	They lopped off several dead branches. / The editor lopped ten minutes off the runtime. / A sale lopped $50 off the price.	Often implies a substantial piece rather than delicate trimming. 'Lop' historically associated with pruning branches; productive figurative extension.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: lopping off meaning
608	6080	6813	treat someone as a liability	/ˌtriːt ˌsʌmwʌn æz ə ˌlaɪəˈbɪləti/	phrase	To regard or handle a person as a risk, burden, disadvantage, or source of potential trouble rather than as an asset.	see as a burden, regard as a risk, treat as dead weight	After the scandal, the party began treating him as a liability. / She hated being treated as a liability just because she needed extra training.	Builds on figurative liability = person/thing likely to cause loss or problems. Often organizational/political, but also interpersonal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: treat someone as a liability
609	6081	6375	coercion	/koʊˈɜːrʒən/	noun	The use of force, threats, pressure, intimidation, or other means to make someone do something they would not freely choose to do.	compulsion, force, intimidation, duress, pressure	Consent obtained through coercion is not freely given. / The confession was challenged on the grounds that it had been obtained by coercion. / Economic pressure can become coercive depending on the circumstances.	Stronger than persuasion or ordinary social pressure: coercion undermines meaningful freedom of choice. Legal definitions vary by context. Related: coerce, coercive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: coercion
609	6082	6661	drop the ball	/ˌdrɑːp ðə ˈbɔːl/	idiom	To make a mistake, fail to do one's responsibility, or mishandle something through inattention, poor follow-through, or negligence.	mess up, fail, let something slip, fumble	I dropped the ball and forgot to send the file. / The company really dropped the ball on customer support.	From sports image of failing to hold/control the ball. Often implies responsibility: 'I dropped the ball on X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: drop the ball meaning
609	6083	6186	dashing	/ˈdæʃɪŋ/	adjective / participle	As an adjective, stylishly attractive, confident, energetic, and adventurous in a somewhat old-fashioned or romantic way; as a participle, moving/running quickly.	debonair, gallant, handsome, stylish; rushing, darting	He plays a dashing young pilot in the old adventure film. / She looked dashing in the tailored suit. / He came dashing down the stairs when he heard the crash.	Attractive-person sense has a classic/romantic flavor and historically skews masculine, though it can describe anyone. 'Dashing' motion comes from dash = move quickly. Debonair is polished/charming; dashing adds energy/adventure.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: dashing
609	6084	6328	sublime	/səˈblaɪm/	adjective / verb	Adjective: extraordinarily beautiful, impressive, elevated, or awe-inspiring, often beyond ordinary beauty. Verb: in chemistry, to change directly between solid and gas without passing through the liquid state.	magnificent, transcendent, awe-inspiring, exquisite; sublimate (chemistry)	The final movement is sublime—quiet, overwhelming, and almost impossible to put into words. / Dry ice sublimes directly from solid carbon dioxide into gas.	Important contrast with WID6110 sublimate. Sublime adjective is aesthetic/philosophical; sublime can also be the chemistry verb. 'Sublimation' additionally has the psychological redirection sense. Do not confuse sublime with subliminal.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sublime meaning
609	6085	6389	crease	/kriːs/	noun / verb	A line, fold, ridge, or wrinkle made by folding, pressing, or bending material or skin; as a verb, to make or develop such a line. In sports, 'crease' can name a marked area near a goal/wicket.	fold, wrinkle, line, crinkle	The shirt had a deep crease down the sleeve. / Try not to crease the poster. / He smiled and the skin around his eyes creased. / Hockey goalies defend the crease in front of the net.	Useful physical distinctions: wrinkle is often irregular/natural; crease often comes from folding/pressure and can be sharper. Sports senses vary by sport.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define crease
609	6086	6157	lay an egg	/ˌleɪ ən ˈeɡ/	idiom, informal	Literally, for a bird/animal to produce an egg; figuratively, especially in AmE, to fail badly or give a poor performance.	flop, bomb, fail, fall flat	The comedian laid an egg with a set that got almost no laughs. / The sequel laid an egg at the box office despite a huge marketing campaign.	Figurative use is informal and somewhat old-fashioned/colorful. Related 'goose egg' = zero. Do not confuse with 'lay the groundwork' etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: To lay an egg
609	6087	5985	strenuous	/ˈstrenjuəs/	adjective	Requiring great physical or mental effort, energy, or exertion; also vigorous or forceful.	arduous, demanding, taxing, exhausting, vigorous	The hike is beautiful but fairly strenuous, especially in the last mile. / She made strenuous efforts to keep the project alive.	Common: strenuous exercise/activity/work/effort. Arduous emphasizes difficulty and sustained labor; vigorous emphasizes energetic intensity and can be positive; strenuous emphasizes exertion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: strenuous
609	6088	5785	data breach	/ˈdeɪtə ˌbriːtʃ/	noun	An incident in which protected, confidential, or sensitive data is accessed, exposed, stolen, or disclosed without authorization.	security breach, data leak	The company notified customers after a data breach exposed account information.	Existing examples mention data breaches, but no standalone card covered the term.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: data breach meaning
609	6089	6545	is that offer still on the table?	/ɪz ðæt ˌɔːfər stɪl ɑːn ðə ˈteɪbəl/	phrase	Is the previously discussed offer, option, proposal, or possibility still available for consideration or acceptance?	is the offer still open?, does the offer still stand?	Is that job offer still on the table? / You mentioned I could stay at your place— is that offer still on the table?	'On the table' = available for discussion/consideration. Contrast 'off the table' = no longer being considered/available.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: is that offer still on the table? meaning
609	6090	6352	spout	/spaʊt/	noun / verb	Noun: a projecting tube/lip through which liquid pours; a jet/stream of liquid. Verb: to send liquid or other material out forcefully; figuratively, to talk at length, especially glibly or dogmatically ('spout nonsense').	nozzle, outlet; jet; gush, spew; ramble, pontificate	Steam spouted from the pipe. / The kettle has a narrow spout. / He spent twenty minutes spouting conspiracy theories he had just read online.	Existing easy-pour-spout card is narrow. Figurative spout + ideas/nonsense/opinions is often critical, implying effortless/unreflective talk. Compare spew, which is more forceful/disgusting.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: spout meaning
610	6091	5950	labile vs volatile	/ˈleɪbaɪl/ vs /ˈvɑːlətəl/	contrast card	Both can suggest instability, but labile emphasizes ease of change/reaction; volatile can mean rapidly changeable/unpredictable and, in chemistry, readily evaporating.	changeable/reactive vs unstable/unpredictable/evaporative	The molecule is labile and reacts readily with water. / Oil prices have been extremely volatile this month. / Ethanol is more volatile than water because it evaporates more readily.	Do not use volatile as a general chemistry synonym for labile: volatility specifically concerns vaporization tendency. For mood, emotionally labile = rapidly shifting affect; volatile person = liable to sudden, potentially explosive anger/change, often stronger and more dangerous-sounding.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: labile vs volatile
610	6092	6863	audience	/ˈɔːdiəns/	noun	The people who watch, listen to, read, or otherwise receive a performance, speech, broadcast, publication, work, or message; also a formal meeting granted by an important person.	viewers, listeners, readers, spectators, public, readership	The video found an audience outside its original fandom. / Know your audience before choosing the level of explanation. / The ambassador was granted an audience with the king.	Audience can be collective singular/plural depending dialect. Modern media: target audience, audience retention, audience demographics. Formal 'an audience with X' = official meeting.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: audience meaning
610	6093	6595	decease / deceased	/dɪˈsiːs/; /dɪˈsiːst/	noun/verb (formal/legal, rare) / adjective-noun	Decease means death or to die in formal/legal language; deceased means dead and is commonly used formally for a person who has died.	death, die; the late, dead	The property passed to his heirs upon his decease. / The deceased was identified the next morning.	As a verb/noun decease sounds legal/archaic. In normal conversation say die/death. 'The deceased' is a formal noun phrase.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: decease
610	6094	6280	cack	/kæk/	noun / verb / adjective, chiefly BrE slang	Slang with several senses: excrement or defecation; rubbish/nonsense or something poor-quality; in expressions such as 'cack-handed,' awkward or clumsy. Exact sense depends strongly on context.	shit, crap, rubbish, nonsense (slang)	That printer is absolute cack—it jams every ten minutes. / The word can also refer crudely to excrement, so context matters. / 'Cack-handed' means clumsy or awkward.	Chiefly British/Irish informal slang and vulgar in its bodily sense. Do not confuse with 'cackle.' Because raw search lacks context, retain the polysemy rather than forcing one definition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define cack
610	6095	6374	dismember / dismembered	/dɪsˈmembər/	verb / adjective-participle	To cut, tear, or remove the limbs or major body parts from a person or animal; figuratively, to break a country, organization, or system into separate pieces.	cut apart, sever limbs, mutilate; break up, partition (figurative)	The horror film shows a dismembered body. / The empire was gradually dismembered by war and political settlements.	Graphic literal meaning. Existing 'dismembering' is a gerund/context card; this preserves the base verb and figurative historical/political use. Dismantle is much less bodily/violent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: dismembered meaning
610	6096	6164	pimp out	/ˌpɪmp ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb, slang	To customize, decorate, or upgrade something in an extravagant, flashy, or attention-grabbing way.	trick out, deck out, customize, bling out	He pimped out the old car with huge wheels, custom lights, and a ridiculous sound system. / They pimped out the gaming room with LEDs and wall-to-wall displays.	Popularized strongly by makeover/car culture such as 'Pimp My Ride.' The underlying word pimp has sexual-exploitation meanings, so this slang can sound dated or insensitive; 'trick out/deck out' are safer alternatives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pimp out meaning
610	6097	5745	titular	/ˈtɪtʃələr/	adjective	Named in the title of a work or holding the title of a position; sometimes having a title without the full power normally associated with it.	title, eponymous; nominal	Hamlet is the titular character of Shakespeare's play.	Titular character = character named in the title. Do not automatically equate titular with main/protagonist; a title character need not be the central protagonist.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: titular meaning
610	6098	5901	thematic	/θiːˈmætɪk/	adjective	Relating to a theme, central idea, recurring subject, or organizing motif.	theme-related, motif-based, conceptual	The two episodes are very different in plot but thematically connected. / The final image gives the story a neat thematic payoff. / I like the character arc, but I'm not sure it fits the film's thematic focus.	Common criticism vocabulary: thematic depth, thematic resonance, thematic throughline, thematically consistent. Theme is the underlying idea; motif is a recurring element that may express a theme.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: thematic meaing
610	6099	6463	skip a beat	/ˌskɪp ə ˈbiːt/	idiom	Of the heart, to seem to miss or interrupt a beat; figuratively, to feel a sudden jolt of fear, surprise, excitement, or attraction.	miss a beat; heart leap, startle	My heart skipped a beat when I saw the message. / For a second, her heart seemed to skip a beat when he walked into the room.	Often figurative/hyperbolic in romance or suspense. Compare 'without missing a beat' = without pausing or losing composure, a different idiom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: skip a beat me4aning
610	6100	5697	render	/ˈrendər/	verb	To cause something to become a particular state; to provide or give; to represent artistically; or in computing, to generate a visual/audio output from data.	make, cause; provide; depict; generate	The damage rendered the machine useless. / The software renders the scene in real time.	Highly polysemous. Common pattern render + object + adjective: render something useless/impossible. Also render assistance, render a verdict, render an image.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: render meaninng
611	6101	6105	courtesy	/ˈkɜːrtəsi/	noun	Polite, respectful, considerate behavior; a polite act or favor. 'Courtesy of' can mean provided by, thanks to, or—sometimes ironically—caused by.	politeness, civility, consideration, graciousness, favor	He had the courtesy to call and say he'd be late. / As a courtesy, the hotel waived the fee. / The photos are courtesy of the museum. / We spent two hours in traffic, courtesy of a crash downtown.	Common patterns: common courtesy; have the courtesy to do; as a courtesy; courtesy of. 'Courtesy of' can be neutral attribution or ironic blame. Raw 'cautesy' corrected.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: cautesy meaning
611	6102	5508	gilly (silly/goofy; nonstandard slang)	/ˈɡɪli/	adjective / noun (nonstandard slang)	A rare or highly local/nonstandard slang form used by some speakers for someone or something silly, goofy, or foolish.	silly, goofy, foolish	You're being so gilly today.	Not standard contemporary English. The search 'gilly means silly?' is supported mainly by user-generated slang; older dictionaries also record gilly as 'a fool' and standard dictionaries give unrelated senses such as a variant of gillie. Preserve because the search itself was memorable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: gilly means silly?
611	6103	5958	juncture	/ˈdʒʌŋktʃər/	noun	A particular point in time, process, or sequence—especially one at which an important decision or change occurs; also literally a joining point.	point, stage, crossroads, critical moment, junction	At this juncture, changing the entire plan would cost more than finishing it. / The story reaches a crucial juncture when she finally learns who betrayed her.	Very common formal phrase: at this juncture. Compared with crossroads, juncture need not imply a choice, though 'critical juncture' often does. Literal joining sense is less common in everyday speech.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: juncture
611	6104	6409	undertones	/ˈʌndərˌtoʊnz/	plural noun	Subtle qualities, meanings, feelings, or attitudes present beneath the obvious surface; literally, quieter/lower tones. Often used for emotional, political, sexual, racial, ominous, or comic implications.	subtext, hints, overtones, undercurrents, subtle notes	The scene has ominous undertones even before anything bad happens. / His supposedly neutral comment carried political undertones. / The perfume has warm woody undertones.	Raw 'undertories' appears to be a typo/search slip. Undertone is subtler/background quality; overtone is also commonly figurative and can overlap. 'Undercurrent' often suggests a hidden force/feeling running through a situation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: undertories
611	6105	6511	welcome to the club	/ˌwelkəm tə ðə ˈklʌb/	idiom / conversational response	A response meaning 'I and/or many others have the same problem, experience, feeling, or situation as you.' Often sympathetic, teasing, or dryly sarcastic.	join the club, you're not alone, same here	'I can't keep up with all these new slang words.' 'Welcome to the club.' / 'I'm exhausted.' 'Welcome to the club—we all stayed up too late.'	Not necessarily about a real club. 'Join the club' is a near-equivalent, often more dismissive/sarcastic: your problem is common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0120 | search: welcome to the club meaning
611	6106	6865	be giddy about something	/bi ˈɡɪdi əˌbaʊt/	phrase	To feel extremely excited, delighted, or light-headedly enthusiastic about something.	be thrilled about, be excited about, be over the moon about	She was giddy about finally seeing the band live. / I was embarrassingly giddy about a tiny feature working correctly.	Existing base giddy covers excited/dizzy; this preserves the natural 'giddy about' collocation and positive anticipation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: being giddy about meaning
611	6107	6451	disillusionment	/ˌdɪsɪˈluːʒənmənt/	noun	The feeling or process of becoming disappointed after discovering that a person, belief, institution, dream, or situation is less good, true, admirable, or ideal than one had believed.	disenchantment, disappointment, loss of faith, disenchantment	His early idealism gradually turned into political disillusionment. / Dating-app fatigue can include disillusionment with the idea that endless choice makes dating easier.	More specific than disappointment: an illusion/idealized belief is stripped away. Related: disillusion (verb), disillusioned (adj).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define disillusionment
611	6108	6039	off they went	/ˌɔːf ðeɪ ˈwent/	narrative expression	A compact narrative way to say that they departed, set out, or immediately began moving/doing something.	away they went, they set off, they went off	The doors opened, the kids grabbed their bags, and off they went. / I gave them the address and off they went to find the restaurant.	Inversion/fronting with directional particle: off + subject + verb. Similar: off we go!, off he ran, away they went. Often lively/storytelling in tone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: off they went meaning
611	6109	6749	expose oneself to a wide range of speakers	/ɪkˈspoʊz wʌnˌself tə/	language-learning phrase	To deliberately get experience hearing/interacting with speakers from varied ages, backgrounds, accents, personalities, and speaking styles, so comprehension does not overfit to a narrow set of voices.	get exposure to diverse speakers, listen to a wide range of speakers	I want to expose myself to people from different demographics—different ages and backgrounds, with various accents, personalities, and speaking styles.	Important pragmatic caution: 'expose myself to people' can momentarily evoke the sexual sense of expose oneself. In language-learning contexts, 'get exposure to a wide range of speakers' or 'expose myself to a wide range of voices/speakers' is safer/natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: I want to expose myself to people from different demographics — people of different ages, backgrounds, with various accents, personalities, and speaking styles.
611	6110	6475	sawed-off / sawn-off	/ˌsɔːd ˈɔːf/	adjective	Having had part cut shorter with a saw; especially used in the fixed weapon term 'sawed-off shotgun' (AmE) / 'sawn-off shotgun' (BrE). More broadly, shortened or truncated.	cut short, shortened, truncated	The prop looked like a sawed-off piece of pipe. / AmE commonly says sawed-off; BrE commonly says sawn-off.	The adjective itself is broader than weapons. Weapon collocations are culturally common but legally regulated; card focuses on language/variant forms rather than operational details.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: sawed-off meaning
612	6111	5902	take something around back	/ˌteɪk ˌsʌmθɪŋ əˌraʊnd ˈbæk/	contextual phrase / euphemistic pattern	Literally, to take someone or something to the area behind a building; context can make it a dark euphemism for taking something away to destroy, kill, discard, or deal with privately.	take out back, deal with privately, put down (contextual)	The broken chair was taken around back to be thrown out. / Joking about taking an old computer out back can imply it's so hopeless it deserves to be 'put out of its misery.'	Raw search 'took it around back' lacks source context. The culturally familiar darker pattern is take X out back, sometimes evoking taking an animal away to be shot/put down. Around back can also be completely literal: 'The delivery driver took it around back.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: took it around back
612	6112	5969	trigger finger	/ˈtrɪɡər ˌfɪŋɡər/	noun	A condition in which a finger catches, locks, or snaps during bending/straightening because the flexor tendon and its sheath are irritated or narrowed; also literally the finger used to pull a firearm trigger.	stenosing tenosynovitis; trigger digit	My ring finger started clicking and locking, and the doctor said it was trigger finger. / In the literal phrase, keep your trigger finger away from the trigger until appropriate.	Medical name: stenosing tenosynovitis. Symptoms can include pain, stiffness, clicking, catching, or locking. Distinct from carpal tunnel syndrome, which involves compression of the median nerve at the wrist.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tirgger finger
612	6113	6083	sing your heart out	/ˌsɪŋ jər ˈhɑːrt aʊt/	idiom	To sing with maximum enthusiasm, emotion, volume, or wholehearted effort, without holding back.	sing passionately, belt it out, give it everything	We drove home singing our hearts out to old anime openings. / She may not have hit every note, but she sang her heart out.	Pattern X one's heart out also occurs with cry/laugh/dance/play, meaning intensely/to one's fullest. 'Belt out' specifically suggests loud, powerful singing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: sing your heart out meaning
612	6114	6295	leech off someone / something	/ˈliːtʃ ɔːf/	phrasal verb, informal/critical	To depend exploitatively on another person or resource, taking money, support, labor, status, or benefits while giving little back.	mooch off, sponge off, freeload on, exploit, parasitize	He spent years leeching off his parents without contributing anything. / Scam accounts leech off the popularity of real creators by reposting their work.	From leech, a bloodsucking animal, so the metaphor is strongly negative. 'Mooch off' and 'sponge off' are more everyday; leech off suggests parasitic exploitation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: leech off of meaning
612	6115	5636	rackety	/ˈrækəti/	adjective	Noisy, rough, disorderly, or shaky; sometimes suggesting a loud rattling quality.	noisy, rattling, ramshackle	The old train made a rackety clatter as it pulled into the station.	Less common than rickety. Rackety emphasizes noise/disorder; rickety emphasizes weak, shaky construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: rackety
612	6116	5875	ragebait / outrage marketing	/ˈreɪdʒˌbeɪt/	noun / strategy	Content, messaging, or marketing deliberately designed to provoke anger or outrage because strong negative reactions drive attention, sharing, engagement, or sales.	outrage bait, anger bait, controversy marketing, outrage marketing, engagement bait	The ad felt less like a sincere campaign than ragebait designed to dominate the conversation. / Some creators build an audience by posting increasingly obvious outrage bait.	Useful translation for 炎上商法 depends on context: ragebait/outrage bait for content engineered to anger; outrage marketing/controversy marketing for a deliberate promotional strategy; publicity stunt if controversy itself is the stunt. Related: outrage economy WID5829.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: 炎上商法　英語で
612	6117	6640	slide/pass money under the table	/ˌslɑɪd ˈmʌni ˌʌndər ðə ˈteɪbəl/	literal + idiomatic phrase	Literally, secretly move cash beneath a table; figuratively/idiomatically, make a concealed, unofficial, often illegal payment such as a bribe or undeclared payment.	pay secretly, bribe, make an under-the-table payment, pay off	He slid an envelope of cash under the table. / The contractor was accused of making under-the-table payments to officials.	'Under the table' commonly means secretly/illegally, especially payment or employment. A literal physical action can evoke that idiom but is not automatically a bribe.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: sliding money under the table meaning
612	6118	5810	substantiate	/səbˈstænʃiˌeɪt/	verb	To provide evidence or facts that prove or support a claim, allegation, or statement.	support, verify, corroborate	The journalist could not substantiate the allegation.	Formal. Usually substantiate a claim/allegation/account with evidence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define substantiate
612	6119	6883	uncalled for	/ˌʌnˈkɔːld fɔːr/	idiom / adjective	Not justified, necessary, appropriate, or deserved; especially rude, excessive, or unfair behavior/comment.	unnecessary, unwarranted, inappropriate, out of line, gratuitous	That personal insult was completely uncalled for. / The extra sarcasm felt uncalled for.	Often moral/social judgment: 'That was uncalled for.' Different from 'uncalled' alone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: uncalled for
612	6120	6357	haggis	/ˈhæɡɪs/	noun	A traditional Scottish savory dish made from sheep's offal (classically heart, liver, and lungs) minced with oats, onion, suet/fat, seasoning, and traditionally cooked in a sheep's stomach or casing.	Scottish offal pudding (rough description)	We tried haggis with neeps and tatties in Edinburgh. / Modern haggis is often sold in an artificial casing rather than an actual sheep's stomach.	Strongly associated with Scotland and Burns Night. 'Neeps and tatties' = turnips/swede and potatoes. Recipes vary, including vegetarian versions; the traditional description is not every modern product.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define haggis
613	6121	5922	menace	/ˈmenɪs/	noun / verb	A person, thing, or situation that threatens harm or causes serious trouble; informally, an annoyingly troublesome or chaotic person. As a verb, to threaten.	threat, danger, nuisance, pest; threaten, intimidate	Drunk driving is a menace to public safety. / That kid is an absolute menace—he hid every remote in the house. / The villain menaced the town for years.	Tone ranges from serious threat to affectionate/joking 'you little menace.' Common pattern: a menace to society/public safety.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: menace meaning
613	6122	5987	take two	/ˌteɪk ˈtuː/	noun / expression	A second attempt at recording, filming, performing, or doing something after the first attempt failed or was unsatisfactory.	second take, second attempt, try again, round two	I stumbled over the first line—take two. / The first version of the app was messy, so this redesign is basically take two.	From film/recording terminology: a take is one recorded attempt. Informally 'take two' can announce any redo. 'Take two pills' is unrelated ordinary verb + number.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: take 2 meaning
613	6123	5627	Shinola	/ʃaɪˈnoʊlə/	proper noun / cultural reference	A former American shoe-polish brand remembered especially through the expression 'not know shit from Shinola,' meaning to be extremely ignorant or unable to distinguish the obvious.		He acts like an expert, but he doesn't know shit from Shinola.	The expression relies on the visual contrast between dark shoe polish and excrement and is vulgar. Modern Shinola is also the name of a Detroit-based lifestyle brand; context distinguishes it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define shinola
613	6124	6195	qi / chi vs psyche vs ki	/tʃiː/; /ˈsaɪki/; /kiː/	etymology / concept contrast	Chinese qì/chi and Japanese ki are historically related East Asian terms: Japanese ki is the Sino-Japanese reading of 気/氣, associated with breath, vital energy, spirit, mood, etc. English psyche comes from Greek psykhē ('soul, breath, life, mind') and is not etymologically related, despite some conceptual overlap.	qi/chi, ki; psyche, mind, soul (conceptual only)	Japanese 'ki' appears in words such as genki and can refer broadly to spirit/energy/mood. / English 'psyche' refers to the mind or soul. / Similar 'breath/life/spirit' metaphors across cultures do not by themselves prove a shared word origin.	Important distinction: qi/chi ↔ Japanese ki have a real historical linguistic relationship through Chinese characters/borrowings; psyche is Greek and unrelated etymologically. The resemblance is conceptual, not evidence of common origin.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: che in chinese and psyche in english and ki in japanese have some relations?
613	6125	6264	pummel	/ˈpʌməl/	verb	To hit someone or something repeatedly and forcefully, especially with fists; figuratively, to overwhelm or batter with criticism, attacks, bad news, weather, etc.	pound, batter, beat, hammer, bombard	The boxer pummeled the heavy bag for three rounds. / The coast was pummeled by wind and rain. / The film got pummeled by critics on opening weekend.	Usually repeated blows/pressure, not one strike. Figurative passive 'be pummeled by criticism/questions/storms' is common. Stronger and more sustained than hit.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pummeled meaning
613	6126	5967	blast furnace / smelting furnace	/ˈblæst ˌfɜːrnɪs/; /ˈsmeltɪŋ ˌfɜːrnɪs/	noun	A furnace used to extract or process metals at very high temperatures. A blast furnace specifically produces molten iron from iron ore using a hot air blast; smelting furnace is the broader category.	furnace, smelter (facility/equipment contextual)	Iron ore, coke, and limestone are fed into a blast furnace to produce molten iron. / The fantasy city is built around enormous smelting furnaces glowing beneath the foundry.	Japanese 溶鉱炉 is commonly blast furnace when referring to the ironmaking structure; 溶解炉 can be melting furnace. 'Smelter' can mean the industrial plant or furnace operation, not always the individual furnace.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: 溶鉱炉 英語で
613	6127	6504	be racked with worry	/bi ˌrækt wɪð ˈwɜːri/	phrase	To be intensely tormented, distressed, or consumed by worry.	be consumed with worry, be tormented by anxiety, be beside oneself with worry	She was racked with worry while waiting for the test results. / He spent the whole night racked with worry about whether he'd made the right decision.	Rack here evokes torture/strain: racked with guilt, pain, grief, anxiety, doubt. Also spelled wracked in some modern usage, but racked is traditional/common in this sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: be racked with worry meaning
613	6128	6062	with all due respect	/wɪð ˌɔːl duː rɪˈspekt/	discourse phrase	A polite formula used before disagreement, criticism, or contradiction to signal respect—or, depending on tone, to soften or ironically sharpen what follows.	respectfully, with respect, no disrespect, I mean no disrespect	With all due respect, I think that interpretation ignores the last two episodes. / With all due respect, that's simply not what the data says.	Often heard as a warning that disagreement is coming. Sincere in formal settings, but can sound passive-aggressive or sarcastic if followed by a harsh attack. 'No offense' is more casual and similarly may fail to soften what follows.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: With all due respect…
613	6129	5653	shy away from something	/ˌʃaɪ əˈweɪ frəm/	phrasal verb	To avoid something because of fear, discomfort, reluctance, or lack of confidence.	avoid, steer clear of, recoil from	She doesn't shy away from difficult conversations.	Common with challenges, controversy, responsibility, attention, or difficult topics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: shy away from meaning
613	6130	6794	undermine / undermining	/ˌʌndərˈmaɪn/	verb / gerund	To weaken, damage, or erode something gradually or indirectly—confidence, authority, an argument, trust, stability, health, etc.; literally, historically, weaken from beneath.	weaken, erode, sabotage, compromise, undercut	Constant interruptions undermined her confidence. / That exception risks undermining the whole rule. / His joke was funny without undermining the seriousness of the scene.	Often indirect/gradual rather than openly attack. Distinguish undercut, which overlaps strongly and can also mean sell more cheaply.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: undermining
614	6131	6338	chrysalis	/ˈkrɪsəlɪs/	noun	The hard or protective pupal stage/casing of a butterfly or moth during metamorphosis; figuratively, a protected transitional state in which something is changing into a new form.	pupa, pupal case; cocoon (commonly confused)	The caterpillar formed a chrysalis and later emerged as a butterfly. / The character spends the middle of the story in a kind of emotional chrysalis before re-entering the world.	Strictly, chrysalis is the pupa of a butterfly/moth; cocoon is a silk casing spun around the pupa by some insects, especially many moths. They are often casually confused. Plural chrysalises or chrysalides.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: chrisalis
614	6132	6073	be a complete nuisance	/bi ə kəmˌpliːt ˈnuːsəns/	phrase	To be extremely annoying, troublesome, inconvenient, or disruptive to other people.	be a pain, be a pest, be a bother, be a real nuisance	The construction noise has been a complete nuisance all week. / Sorry to be a complete nuisance, but could you send that file one more time?	Complete is an intensifier here. 'Nuisance' is milder/less insulting than menace and often applies to situations as well as people. 'Sorry to be a nuisance' is a common polite BrE-style request softener.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: being a complete nuisance meaning
614	6133	5829	mask-off	/ˌmæsk ˈɔːf/	adjective / internet slang	Openly revealing attitudes, motives, prejudice, or hostility that were previously concealed or presented more politely.	openly revealing, unmasked	The reply was a surprisingly mask-off admission of what he really believed.	From 'the mask comes off.' Often used critically in online political/cultural discourse.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: mask-off meaning
614	6134	6679	speaker variability	/ˈspiːkər ˌveriəˈbɪləti/	linguistics / speech term	Differences in speech patterns across speakers—or sometimes within the same speaker—such as pronunciation, pitch, timing, accent, voice quality, lexical choices, or realization of linguistic contrasts.	inter-speaker variation, talker variability, speaker variation	Listeners learn to cope with speaker variability: the same vowel can sound quite different across people. / A robust model should handle accent and speaker variability.	In speech research, talker variability is also common. Inter-speaker = between speakers; intra-speaker = within one speaker across situations/time.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: speaker variability
614	6135	6247	homogeneous	/ˌhoʊməˈdʒiːniəs/; also /ˌhɑːməˈdʒiːniəs/	adjective	Consisting of parts or members that are all the same or very similar in kind, composition, character, or distribution; uniform throughout.	uniform, consistent, similar, undifferentiated	The mixture should be homogeneous before you pour it into the mold. / The school was far more culturally homogeneous than the city around it. / A homogeneous group is not necessarily identical in every respect.	Opposite: heterogeneous. In science, a homogeneous mixture has uniform composition. In social description, homogeneous means relatively similar along the dimension being discussed, not literally identical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: homogeneous
614	6136	6358	en suite	/ˌɑːn ˈswiːt/; /ˌen ˈswiːt/	adjective / noun, chiefly BrE	Connected directly to and for the private use of a bedroom, especially a bathroom; as a noun, an en-suite bathroom.	private attached bathroom, ensuite bathroom	The room has an en-suite bathroom with a shower. / We booked a double room with an en suite rather than a shared bathroom.	From French 'in sequence,' but English hotel/property usage is specialized. Often written en suite or ensuite. In AmE, 'private/attached bathroom' is more ordinary.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define en suite
614	6137	5524	off (core image)	/ɔːf/	preposition / adverb / adjective	A highly flexible word whose central image often involves separation, movement away from contact or a normal position, removal, disconnection, or being outside an active/allowed state.	away, separated, disconnected, not operating	The lid came off, the lights are off, and that room is off-limits.	Not one single meaning. The recurring image is being separated from a surface/path/state or moved away from it. This helps connect take off, cut off, turn off, off-limits, day off, etc. The off in top off/cap off is lexicalized and should be learned with the whole phrasal verb.		"voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: core idea of the preposition ""off"""
614	6138	6936	impel vs compel	/ɪmˈpel/ vs /kəmˈpel/	contrast	Impel = drive/urge someone toward action, often through motivation, feeling, or circumstances. Compel = force or strongly oblige, often leaving little real choice; it can also mean powerfully attract/convince in 'compelling.'	urge/drive vs force/oblige	Curiosity impelled her to investigate; the court order compelled her to testify. / Fear may impel action without literally compelling it.	Overlap exists. Impel foregrounds impetus/motivation; compel foregrounds necessity/coercion. Both commonly take object + to-infinitive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: impel vs compel
614	6139	6565	prim	/prɪm/	adjective / verb	Very proper, neat, formal, restrained, or easily shocked by behavior considered improper; sometimes disapprovingly prudish or stiff.	proper, prudish, strait-laced, demure, stiff, prissy	She sat with a prim expression and corrected everyone's table manners. / The costume makes the character look deliberately prim and old-fashioned.	Often suggests excessive propriety/restraint. As a verb, 'prim one's lips/mouth' = draw them into a tight disapproving expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: define prim
614	6140	5878	connective tissue	/kəˈnektɪv ˈtɪʃuː/	noun / figurative phrase	Literally, body tissue that supports, binds, or connects other tissues and organs; figuratively, material or relationships that link separate parts into a coherent whole.	binding tissue; glue, linking material, connective thread	Ligaments are a type of connective tissue. / The quiet character scenes are the connective tissue holding the bigger action sequences together. / Those recurring jokes become connective tissue between otherwise separate episodes.	The figurative use is especially useful in criticism, storytelling, organizations, and systems: not necessarily the flashy 'main content,' but what makes the pieces connect and function together.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: connective tissue meaning
615	6141	6169	lash out	/ˌlæʃ ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To suddenly attack someone physically or, much more commonly, speak/act toward them angrily and aggressively, often as an emotional reaction.	snap at, attack, strike out, vent at, go off on	He lashed out at everyone who tried to help him. / She later apologized for lashing out when she was overwhelmed. / The cornered animal lashed out with its claws.	Usually lash out at someone/something. Emotional sense often suggests reactive anger rather than planned aggression. 'Vent' can be less targeted/hostile; 'snap at' is usually verbal and briefer.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: lash out meaning
615	6142	6156	have egg on one's face	/ˌhæv ˈeɡ ɑːn wʌnz ˈfeɪs/	idiom	To look foolish or embarrassed because something one said, predicted, or attempted turned out to be wrong or failed.	look foolish, be embarrassed, be humiliated, lose face	He confidently predicted a landslide victory and ended up with egg on his face. / If the rumor turns out to be false, a lot of commentators will have egg on their faces.	Usually result-oriented embarrassment after being publicly wrong. 'Lose face' is broader and culturally loaded; egg on one's face is colloquial and often mildly comic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: To have egg on your face
615	6143	6593	dishonorable	/dɪsˈɑːnərəbəl/	adjective	Not deserving honor or respect because it is shameful, unethical, disloyal, or lacking integrity.	shameful, disgraceful, ignoble, disreputable	He considered betraying his friend for money dishonorable. / The officer received a dishonorable discharge.	US military 'dishonorable discharge' is a specific severe punitive discharge; ordinary adjective use is broader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118, voca_0120 | search: dishonorable meaning
615	6144	6860	virtuoso	/ˌvɜːrtʃuˈoʊsoʊ/	noun / adjective	A person with exceptional technical skill and artistry, especially in music; more broadly, someone highly accomplished in a particular craft. As adjective, displaying such mastery.	master, expert, prodigy (not exact), highly skilled performer	She's a virtuoso violinist. / The sequence is a virtuoso display of animation and timing.	Traditionally strongly associated with musical performance, but figurative extensions are common: virtuoso performance, a virtuoso of negotiation. Plural virtuosos/virtuosi.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: virtuoso meaning
615	6145	5594	take your like and get out	/ˌteɪk jər ˈlaɪk ænd ɡet ˈaʊt/	internet expression / meme phrase	A joking way to say that something was annoyingly funny, clever, or effective: the speaker gives the post a 'like' while mock-telling its creator to leave.	take my upvote and get out	That pun was awful. Take your like and get out.	Internet-comment phrasing. Often playful rather than genuinely hostile; variants use upvote, award, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: take your like and get out meaning
615	6146	6735	tamper with something	/ˈtæmpər wɪð/	phrasal verb	To interfere with, alter, damage, manipulate, or meddle with something without authorization, especially in a way that may compromise its proper state, evidence, safety, or function.	interfere with, meddle with, manipulate, alter improperly	Someone tampered with the lock. / The investigation found no evidence that the data had been tampered with.	Tamper is normally used with 'with.' Common: tamper with evidence, equipment, packaging, data. Often implies improper/unauthorized interference.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: tamper meaning
615	6147	6084	sneer	/snɪr/	verb / noun	To smile, curl the lip, speak, or look in a way that shows contempt, scorn, or mocking superiority.	smirk contemptuously, scoff, jeer, mock, curl one's lip	He sneered at the cheap costume, which immediately made him less likable. / 'You call that an apology?' she sneered.	Sneer is contemptuous; smirk is a self-satisfied smile and need not show contempt; scoff is verbal disbelief/mockery. Existing sneering card is adjectival; standalone verb/noun is productive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: sneer
615	6148	6761	grifter	/ˈɡrɪftər/	noun, informal	A person who obtains money, influence, or support through deception, scams, opportunistic persuasion, or cynical exploitation; online, often someone accused of monetizing an audience or cause insincerely.	con artist, scammer, hustler, fraud, charlatan	Critics dismissed him as a political grifter who changes positions whenever there is money in it. / Online, 'grifter' is often thrown around more broadly than 'con artist.'	From grift = swindle/petty fraud. Modern political/media use often implies cynical monetization or bad-faith opportunism, but it remains an accusation, not proof of fraud.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: grifter meaning
615	6149	6745	nouns followed by to + gerund	/tə + ˈdʒerənd/	grammar pattern	Some nouns commonly take 'to' as a preposition followed by a noun or gerund, e.g. approach to doing, key to doing, solution to doing, obstacle to doing, objection to doing, commitment to doing.	approach to, key to, solution to, objection to, commitment to	Consistency is the key to building active vocabulary. / They found a practical solution to reducing waste. / She has no objection to working late.	The crucial point is whether 'to' belongs to a prepositional construction rather than an infinitive. Not every noun + to uses a gerund: 'ability to do,' 'decision to do,' 'attempt to do' take infinitives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: noun word like approach that requires gerund phrase like approach to doing something
615	6150	5729	LARPmaxxing	/ˈlɑːrpˌmæksɪŋ/	internet slang	Trying to improve one's identity, image, lifestyle, or perceived status by intensely adopting or performing a chosen persona, aesthetic, role, or subculture.	persona-maxxing, role-playing one's ideal self	He jokingly called his new medieval wardrobe LARPmaxxing.	Nonstandard internet formation: LARP + maxxing. Often humorous or mocking, implying that self-improvement has become performative role-play rather than something fully authentic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: larpmaxxing meaning
616	6151	6387	could use something	/kʊd ˈjuːz/	modal construction / idiom-like usage	Would benefit from, need, or welcome something; often used to express a mild need or to say that something needs improvement.	could do with, would benefit from, need, could really use	I could use a coffee. / This paragraph could use another example. / You look like you could use some sleep. / The app could use a little polish.	Very common conversational softener. 'I could use X' ≈ I would welcome/need X. 'X could use some work' = X needs improvement. Often gentler than direct 'need.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: could use something meaning
616	6152	6491	umbilical cord prolapse	/ʌmˈbɪlɪkəl ˌkɔːrd ˈproʊlæps/	noun, medical	A childbirth emergency in which the umbilical cord descends through the cervix/vagina ahead of or alongside the presenting part of the baby, creating a risk that the cord can be compressed and reduce blood/oxygen flow.	cord prolapse, prolapsed umbilical cord	The obstetrics lesson described umbilical cord prolapse as an emergency because cord compression can compromise fetal oxygenation.	Medical vocabulary card. It describes a specific obstetric emergency; management is time-sensitive and belongs to trained medical care, not self-treatment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: umbilical cord prolapse
616	6153	6671	build on something	/ˌbɪld ˈɑːn/	phrasal verb	To use an earlier success, idea, foundation, result, or development as the basis for further progress; to develop or extend it rather than starting over.	develop, expand on, capitalize on, extend	The pilot had a strong start, but the series never really built on it. / Let's build on what worked in the first version instead of redesigning everything.	In 'it never really built on that strong start,' the subject failed to turn the promising beginning into sustained development. Distinct from literally building on land/a foundation.		"voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: it never really built on that strong start. in this sentence, what ""built on"" means?"
616	6154	6567	prim and proper	/ˌprɪm ən ˈprɑːpər/	fixed phrase	Extremely neat, correct, respectable, restrained, and concerned with proper manners or behavior.	very proper, decorous, strait-laced, prudish, buttoned-up	She acts prim and proper around her grandparents. / The prim-and-proper school uniform contrasts with the character's chaotic personality.	Can be admiring, teasing, or critical. Often implies stiffness or excessive concern with appearances/manners.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: prim and proper meaning
616	6155	5544	twirl	/twɜːrl/	verb / noun	To spin or rotate quickly and lightly, or to make something do so; as a noun, a spinning movement.	spin, whirl, rotate	She twirled the pen between her fingers.	Raw search 'TWIRLE' is most plausibly a spelling variant/typo for modern twirl. Historical spelling twirle exists, but the normal modern headword is twirl.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: TWIRLE MEANIG
616	6156	5774	venture	/ˈventʃər/	noun / verb	A risky or uncertain undertaking, especially a business project; as a verb, to dare to go, say, or do something despite risk or uncertainty.	enterprise, undertaking; dare	The new venture could be profitable. / Few hikers ventured beyond the marked trail.	Existing venture a guess is one specific construction; this card captures the broader noun and verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: venture meaning
616	6157	6561	register = be noticed / mentally processed	/ˈredʒɪstər/	verb	For something to be noticed, recognized, understood, or produce a mental reaction; if something 'doesn't register,' the person fails to notice, process, or treat it as significant.	sink in, be noticed, be processed, click, land	I heard the words, but they didn't register until a few seconds later. / If 'native English doesn't register' was the source wording, it likely means native/natural English isn't being recognized or mentally registering as such, though the exact intended subject needs source context.	This is the cognitive sense of register, not sign up/record. Existing cards mention 'sink in/register'; this search is useful because 'X doesn't register' is highly productive. Raw sentence is semantically incomplete/odd, so preserve uncertainty.		"voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: what does the word ""register"" mean in the following sentence, ""native english doesn't register."""
616	6158	5974	arthritis	/ɑːrˈθraɪtɪs/	noun, medical	Inflammation or disease of one or more joints, causing symptoms such as pain, stiffness, swelling, or reduced movement; an umbrella term covering many different conditions.	joint disease, joint inflammation	Her knee stiffness is caused by osteoarthritis. / Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease and is very different from ordinary wear-and-tear osteoarthritis.	Arthritis is not one single disease. Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, psoriatic arthritis, etc. differ substantially in cause and treatment. Existing gout card is one specific form.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: arthritis
616	6159	5605	ascendance / ascendancy	/əˈsendəns/; /əˈsendənsi/	noun	A position or period of increasing or dominant power, influence, or control.	dominance, supremacy, rise	The party's rapid ascendance changed the political landscape.	Ascendance is valid, though ascendancy is also very common for a state of dominant influence. Do not confuse with ascent = the act of rising/climbing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: ascendance meaning in english
616	6160	6857	entrant	/ˈentrənt/	noun	A person, team, animal, product, or other competitor that enters a contest, race, examination, market, or field.	competitor, participant, contestant, newcomer (contextual)	The race attracted more than 500 entrants. / The new company is a strong entrant in the streaming market.	Entrant emphasizes entering/being entered. In business, 'new entrant' = a firm newly entering a market. Not every participant is necessarily an entrant in all contexts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define entrants
617	6161	6789	neuter	/ˈnuːtər/	verb / adjective / noun	Verb: remove an animal's reproductive capacity; figuratively, deprive something of power, force, effectiveness, or distinctive character. Adjective/noun: a grammatical gender/category neither masculine nor feminine in languages that use it.	spay/castrate (context-specific); weaken, emasculate, defang, render ineffective	The cat was neutered at the clinic. / The compromise neutered the proposal by removing its strongest provision. / German has neuter nouns.	Raw 'neutere' repaired. In animal care, neuter can be generic, though castrate/spay are sex-specific. Figurative use can be forceful/negative.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: neutere meaning
617	6162	6072	absolute cinema	/ˌæbsəluːt ˈsɪnəmə/	internet meme / evaluative phrase	A meme phrase used to praise a moment as extraordinarily dramatic, beautiful, satisfying, ridiculous, or 'cinematic'; often sincere and ironic at the same time.	peak cinema, cinema, masterpiece (hyperbolic), kino (internet slang)	The villain entered exactly as the music dropped—absolute cinema. / The cat knocked over the cup, stared into the camera, and walked away. Absolute cinema.	Associated online with a reaction image of filmmaker Martin Scorsese raising his hands. Often applied humorously to things that are not films at all. Hyperbolic internet praise rather than literal film criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: absolute cinema meaning
617	6163	6481	pry it out of my cold, dead hands	/ˌpraɪ ɪt aʊt əv maɪ ˌkoʊld ded ˈhændz/	hyperbolic idiom / cultural phrase	A dramatic way to say one will never willingly give something up: the only way to take it is after the speaker is dead.	you'll never get it from me, over my dead body, I'm never giving it up	You can pry this old controller out of my cold, dead hands. / I know the app is outdated, but you'll have to pry it from my cold, dead hands.	Often humorous when applied to trivial possessions/preferences. Related to the famous 'from my cold, dead hands' formulation in U.S. gun-rights rhetoric, but now widely repurposed as hyperbole.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: pry it out of my cold dead hands meaing
617	6164	6723	the opposite of a double whammy	/ði ˌɑːpəzɪt əv ə ˌdʌbəl ˈwæmi/	concept / expression choice	There is no single fixed opposite of 'double whammy.' Depending on context, say a double win, two wins at once, a one-two boost, a double benefit, a win-win, or 'two birds with one stone.'	double win, double benefit, win-win, two birds with one stone	The new schedule saves money and gives us more free time—a double win. / It's the opposite of a double whammy: we got two benefits at once.	Double whammy = two bad blows/problems together. 'Win-win' emphasizes benefits for multiple sides, while 'two birds with one stone' emphasizes achieving two goals with one action; neither is an exact lexical antonym.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: double whammy opposite
617	6165	6657	have trouble / difficulty / a problem / struggle / a hard time with something	/hæv ˈtrʌbəl/	usage contrast	All express difficulty, but nuance differs: have trouble doing X is very natural/general; have difficulty is more formal; have a problem with X may mean difficulty OR objection/disapproval; struggle with emphasizes ongoing effort; have a hard time is conversational and can include emotional difficulty.	have difficulty, struggle with, find difficult	I have trouble remembering names. / She had difficulty understanding the legal wording. / I have a problem with that policy (= I object to it). / He struggles with pronunciation. / I had a hard time adjusting after the move.	Grammar: trouble/difficulty/a hard time + -ing; struggle with + noun/-ing; have a problem with + noun/-ing. 'I have a problem doing X' exists but 'with' is often clearer.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: have trouble vs difficulty vs problem vs struggle vs a hard time with something
617	6166	6633	the million-dollar question	/ðə ˌmɪljən ˈdɑːlər ˌkwestʃən/	idiom	The crucial, difficult, or especially interesting question whose answer would resolve the main uncertainty.	the big question, key question, crucial question	Why did the system fail in the first place? That's the million-dollar question. / The million-dollar question is whether users will actually pay for it.	Hyperbolic; not literally worth a million dollars. Variants use other large amounts ('$64,000 question') historically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: million dollar question meaning
617	6167	5530	busted	/ˈbʌstɪd/	adjective / past participle, informal	Broken or not working; caught doing something wrong or illegal; in some contexts, exposed or found out.	broken, caught, exposed	The printer is busted, and I got busted trying to fix it without permission.	Very informal. Multiple common senses derive from bust: broken/damaged and caught/arrested/exposed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: busted meanig
617	6168	5559	just so you know	/ˌdʒʌst soʊ ju ˈnoʊ/	discourse phrase	Used to give someone information you think they should be aware of, often as a warning, clarification, or heads-up.	FYI, for your information, heads-up	Just so you know, the last train leaves at eleven.	Tone ranges from helpful to mildly pointed or defensive depending on delivery.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111, voca_0117 | search: just so you know meaning
617	6169	5995	game (verb)	/ɡeɪm/	verb	To manipulate or exploit a system, rules, metrics, or process to gain an advantage; also to gamble/play games in other contexts.	exploit, manipulate, work, rig (stronger), optimize around	Companies learned how to game the ranking algorithm. / If a test rewards memorizing one pattern, students will naturally learn to game it.	Very common construction game the system. Unlike cheat, gaming a system can exploit loopholes while technically following rules. 'Game' can also mean play video games ('I game on PC'), a separate verb sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: game verb meaing
617	6170	5581	come down to something	/ˌkʌm ˈdaʊn tə/	phrasal verb	To ultimately be determined by, reduced to, or depend on one essential factor or choice.	boil down to, depend on, hinge on, amount to, ultimately be about	In the end, the decision comes down to cost. / Whether I keep the card comes down to one question: will I actually use it? / A lot of the disagreement comes down to different assumptions.	Often used after stripping away complexity: 'It all comes down to...' The complement is usually a noun, noun phrase, or wh-clause. 'Boil down to' emphasizes reduction to essentials; 'come down to' can also emphasize the decisive factor/choice.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: come down to meaning
618	6171	6301	name-calling	/ˈneɪm ˌkɔːlɪŋ/	noun	The act of insulting, attacking, or belittling someone by applying offensive, mocking, or derogatory labels instead of addressing the substance of an issue.	insults, mudslinging, verbal abuse, labeling	The debate collapsed into name-calling within ten minutes. / Calling everyone who disagrees an idiot is name-calling, not an argument.	Can range from childish insults to politically loaded labels. In argument analysis, name-calling is often treated as ad hominem-style rhetoric, though not every negative descriptive label is automatically fallacious.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: name-calling meaing
618	6172	5769	turn against someone/something	/ˌtɜːrn əˈɡenst/	phrasal verb	To change from supporting, liking, or favoring someone or something to opposing or rejecting them.	oppose, become hostile to, abandon support for	Public opinion turned against the proposal.	Unlike turn on someone, this often emphasizes a change in allegiance or attitude rather than a sudden attack.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: turn against meaning
618	6173	5801	stan someone	/stæn/	verb / noun, slang	To be an extremely enthusiastic or devoted fan of someone or something; as a noun, such a fan.	adore, be a superfan of	I've stanned that singer since her first album.	Originated from Eminem's song 'Stan' and broadened in internet/fandom language. Can be playful/self-aware or imply excessive devotion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: stan someone meaning
618	6174	6506	that's fair	/ðæts ˈfer/	conversational phrase	A casual way to acknowledge that another person's point, reaction, preference, limitation, or criticism is reasonable or understandable, even if you do not fully agree.	fair enough, that's reasonable, I can see that, valid	'I stopped after three episodes because the pacing was too slow.' 'Yeah, that's fair.' / 'I don't want to spend $30 on an app.' 'That's fair.'	Often functions as low-key concession/validation rather than literal judgment of fairness. 'Fair enough' is very close and sometimes sounds slightly more final.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: that's fair meanig
618	6175	6601	teacher-facing	/ˈtiːtʃər ˌfeɪsɪŋ/	adjective	Designed to be seen, used, or managed by teachers rather than directly by students.	for teachers, instructor-facing, educator-facing	The platform has a teacher-facing dashboard for assignments and analytics.	Product/education jargon built on X-facing = oriented toward/intended for X.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: teacher facing meaning
618	6176	6892	about-face	/əˌbaʊt ˈfeɪs/	noun / interjection	A 180-degree turn, literally in drill/marching; figuratively, a complete reversal of opinion, policy, plan, or behavior.	U-turn, reversal, volte-face, turnaround	The company made an abrupt about-face on remote work. / About-face! the drill instructor ordered.	Hyphenated noun. Figurative 'do/make an about-face.' Compare 'U-turn,' especially BrE/politics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: about-face meaning
618	6177	6407	Physalia	/faɪˈseɪliə/	proper noun / biological genus	The siphonophore genus that includes the Portuguese man o' war (Physalia physalis) and related bluebottle forms.	Physalia genus, Portuguese man o' war genus	Physalia physalis is the scientific name commonly used for the Portuguese man o' war. / Physalia floats at the ocean surface and trails long tentacles below.	Useful taxonomy hook for カツオノエボシ. Physalia is a hydrozoan siphonophore genus; the organism is colonial, with specialized zooids rather than one ordinary jellyfish body.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: Physalia
618	6178	6698	visage	/ˈvɪzɪdʒ/	noun, literary/formal	A person's face or facial appearance, especially when described dramatically, artistically, or in older/literary language.	face, countenance, features	A grim visage appeared in the doorway. / The statue's serene visage contrasts with the violence of the scene.	Much more literary than face. Often used for striking expressions/appearances or personified objects. Compare countenance, also formal/literary.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: visage meaning
618	6179	6871	hammer something out	/ˌhæmər ˌsʌmθɪŋ ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To work through details, disagreements, or difficulties through sustained discussion/effort until an agreement, plan, wording, or solution is produced.	work out, thrash out, hash out, negotiate, iron out	We spent an hour hammering out the final wording. / The two sides are trying to hammer out a deal.	Metaphor of shaping metal by repeated blows. Common: hammer out an agreement/deal/compromise/details. Suggests effort and back-and-forth.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: hammer things out meaning
618	6180	6006	have one's full get-up on	/ˌhæv wʌnz ˌfʊl ˈɡetˌʌp ɑːn/	informal phrase	To be wearing one's complete outfit, costume, uniform, or distinctive set of clothes/accessories.	be fully dressed up, have the whole outfit on, be in full costume	He showed up with his full cowboy get-up on—boots, hat, belt buckle, everything. / Once I had my full get-up on, the costume suddenly looked much better than it did piece by piece.	Get-up = outfit, often conspicuous, themed, old-fashioned, humorous, or costume-like. 'Full get-up' emphasizes the whole ensemble, not merely being dressed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: have my full get up on meaning
619	6181	6933	suck up to someone	/ˌsʌk ˈʌp tuː/	phrasal verb, informal, disparaging	To flatter, please, or act excessively deferential toward someone—especially a superior—in order to gain favor or advantage.	brown-nose, butter up, curry favor with, ingratiate oneself with	He's always sucking up to the manager before promotion season. / She refused to suck up to people just because they had status.	Object usually person/authority. Butter up can be a one-off attempt; suck up to often suggests habitual servility/opportunism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: suck-up to someone meaning
619	6182	5733	progeny (fictional lineage/offspring)	/ˈprɑːdʒəni/	noun, contextual use	Descendants or offspring; in supernatural fiction, can refer to beings created, sired, or descended from another being.	offspring, descendants	The vampire's progeny were expected to show loyalty to their maker.	Existing progeny card gives the general sense; preserve this contextual card because the original Anne Rice search asks how the kinship/lineage term functions in a fictional social system.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: do progenies pledge allegiance in Anne Rice's books?
619	6183	5943	grotesque	/ɡroʊˈtesk/	adjective / noun	Distorted, bizarre, ugly, unnatural, or disturbingly exaggerated; in art, a fantastic ornamental form combining strange human, animal, or plant elements.	hideous, distorted, monstrous, bizarre, macabre	The creature has a grotesque smile that is almost human but not quite. / The contrast between the cheerful music and the grotesque violence makes the scene deeply unsettling. / Medieval buildings sometimes feature carved grotesques.	Grotesque can describe appearance, behavior, inequality/excess ('a grotesque waste of money'), or an artistic mode mixing comic and horrifying. A grotesque is not necessarily a gargoyle: gargoyles specifically function as waterspouts.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: grotesque
619	6184	5828	pendulum	/ˈpendʒələm/	noun	A weight suspended so it can swing freely back and forth; figuratively, something that shifts repeatedly between opposing positions or extremes.	swinging weight; oscillation	Public opinion often seems to swing like a pendulum between the two extremes.	Common metaphor: the pendulum swings back/toward X.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: pendulum meaning
619	6185	5650	tank	/tæŋk/	verb, informal	To fail badly, decline sharply, or deliberately perform poorly; also to cause something to fail or plunge.	plummet, bomb, fail, sabotage	The stock tanked after the announcement.	Existing 'tank it' focuses on intentionally losing; standalone tank also commonly means fall/fail dramatically without intentionality.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: tank verb meaing
619	6186	5852	top dog	/ˌtɑːp ˈdɔːɡ/	noun, informal	The most powerful, successful, dominant, or important person or group in a situation.	boss, leader, number one	After the merger, everyone wondered who would emerge as top dog.	Existing cards use top dog as a synonym, but no standalone card covers the expression.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: top dog meaning
619	6187	5788	based (slang)	/beɪst/	adjective, internet slang	Expressing approval of someone or something seen as confidently authentic, unapologetic, bold, or correct despite social pressure.	bold, unapologetic, respectable (context-dependent)	She said exactly what she thought and the comments called her based.	Highly context-dependent internet slang. It can be sincere, ironic, political, or simply mean 'I approve.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112, voca_0113 | search: based meaning slang
619	6188	6153	flap your jacks	/ˌflæp jər ˈdʒæks/	nonstandard / playful phrase	A playful or context-built expression, likely wordplay around flapjack rather than a widely established idiom; its exact intended meaning depends on the source scene.	context-dependent wordplay, flapjack pun	'Flap your jacks' sounds like deliberately silly wordplay rather than ordinary English. / If a character says it as a command, the humor may come from splitting 'flapjack' into flap + jacks.	Not a standard dictionary idiom. Preserve rather than invent a fixed meaning. Nearby searches for 'flap out' and 'flapjack' strongly suggest the original context involved wordplay or misunderstanding.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: flap your jacks meaning
619	6189	6806	modular (figurative)	/ˈmɑːdʒələr/	adjective	Organized into relatively independent components that can be combined, replaced, rearranged, reused, or developed separately; figuratively used for ideas, workflows, software, courses, writing structures, etc.	component-based, composable, plug-and-play, flexible	A modular workflow lets us improve the enrichment stage without rebuilding the whole pipeline. / The course is modular, so students can take units independently.	Existing modular-vs-single-unit card covers structural contrast. Figurative use preserves the same core metaphor: separable/recombinable components.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: modular meaning figuratively
619	6190	6821	reticular	/rɪˈtɪkjələr/	adjective, technical	Forming, resembling, or relating to a network or netlike structure.	netlike, networked, reticulate	The tissue has a reticular pattern. / The reticular formation is a network of neurons in the brainstem.	Technical anatomy/biology adjective from Latin reticulum 'small net.' Common in reticular formation, reticular fibers, reticular pattern.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: reticular
620	6191	6523	have it both ways	/ˌhæv ɪt ˌboʊθ ˈweɪz/	idiom	To try to enjoy the advantages of two incompatible alternatives or positions while avoiding their disadvantages; often used critically.	have the best of both worlds (not always critical), want both sides, eat one's cake and have it too	You can't demand total creative freedom and then blame everyone else for every decision—you can't have it both ways. / He wants privacy but also constant public attention; he can't have it both ways.	Usually critical because the two desired positions conflict. 'Best of both worlds' can describe a genuinely compatible combination and is more positive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: have it both ways
620	6192	6329	façade of grandeur	/fəˌsɑːd əv ˈɡrændʒər/	figurative phrase	An outward appearance of magnificence, importance, wealth, dignity, or greatness that hides a less impressive, insecure, ordinary, or flawed reality.	pretense of grandeur, grand exterior, veneer of magnificence	The hotel maintained a façade of grandeur even as the rooms behind the lobby fell apart. / His pompous language created a façade of grandeur around a fairly simple idea.	Not a fixed idiom, but a natural metaphor. Façade = front/appearance concealing reality; grandeur = magnificence/greatness. Related 'delusions of grandeur' concerns an exaggerated belief about one's own importance, not merely appearance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: facade of grandeur meaning
620	6193	6050	domineering	/ˌdɑːməˈnɪrɪŋ/	adjective	Trying to control other people in an overbearing, bossy, or authoritarian way.	bossy, controlling, overbearing, authoritarian, imperious	Her manager was so domineering that nobody felt comfortable making decisions without permission. / The character can be protective without becoming domineering.	Strongly negative and specifically interpersonal. Dominant can be neutral/descriptive; domineering implies excessive control over others.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define domineering
620	6194	5870	provocateur	/prəˌvɑːkəˈtɜːr/	noun	A person who deliberately provokes strong reactions, controversy, anger, or conflict; agent provocateur can specifically mean someone who incites others into illegal acts, sometimes as an infiltrator.	agitator, instigator, firebrand, troll, shit-stirrer (vulgar/informal)	He's less a serious commentator than a professional provocateur who knows exactly what will make people angry. / The character acts as a provocateur, constantly pushing the others into confrontation.	Existing cards use provocateur as a synonym, but the word itself has useful nuance: provoking reaction may be the person's role/strategy, not merely incidental offensiveness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: provocateurs
620	6195	6572	bound by tradition	/ˌbaʊnd baɪ trəˈdɪʃən/	phrase	Constrained, obligated, shaped, or strongly limited by inherited customs, conventions, or traditional expectations.	constrained by tradition, tied to custom, tradition-bound	The family felt bound by tradition to continue the ceremony. / The genre isn't bound by tradition; creators can reinvent the format.	Bound by = restricted/obligated by. Can be neutral (connected through shared tradition) but often implies reduced freedom.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: bound by tradition meaning
620	6196	6193	spaghetti kiss / Lady and the Tramp kiss	/spəˈɡeti kɪs/	cultural reference / noun phrase	A romantic-comedy image in which two people eat opposite ends of the same strand of spaghetti/noodle and unexpectedly meet in a kiss, strongly associated with Disney's Lady and the Tramp.	noodle kiss, shared-spaghetti kiss	The scene parodies the classic Lady and the Tramp spaghetti kiss. / They tried the spaghetti-kiss bit as a joke and immediately started laughing.	Not a formal idiom; it is a recognizable visual trope/cultural reference. Useful for describing recreations/parodies without quoting copyrighted dialogue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: lady and the tramp style spaghetti kiss
620	6197	5917	slow on the uptake	/ˌsloʊ ɑːn ði ˈʌpˌteɪk/	idiom	Slow to understand, notice, or grasp what someone means, especially a joke, hint, or situation.	slow to catch on, not quick on the draw, dense (harsher), a bit slow	It took me a minute to realize she was joking—I'm a little slow on the uptake today. / He's not stupid; he's just sometimes slow on the uptake with sarcasm.	Can be mildly self-deprecating/playful, but calling someone else slow on the uptake may sound insulting. Uptake here = understanding/mental reception.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: slow on the uptake meaning
620	6198	5831	outrage economy	/ˈaʊtˌreɪdʒ ɪˌkɑːnəmi/	noun phrase	A media and attention environment in which anger, controversy, and moral outrage are repeatedly produced or amplified because they drive clicks, engagement, influence, or revenue.	attention economy driven by outrage, ragebait ecosystem	The creator argued that the outrage economy rewards the most inflammatory interpretation.	Analytical phrase rather than a single fixed technical term; closely related to ragebait and engagement incentives.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: outrage economy
620	6199	6790	conform	/kənˈfɔːrm/	verb	To behave, look, or develop according to a rule, standard, pattern, convention, or group expectation; also to match/be consistent with something.	comply, fit in, adhere, correspond	He felt pressure to conform at school. / The design must conform to accessibility standards. / The results conform to the predicted pattern.	Patterns: conform to a standard/norm; conform with can mean be consistent with, but 'conform to' is very common. Conformity can be voluntary or pressured.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: conform meaning
620	6200	5541	straight up	/ˌstreɪt ˈʌp/	adverb / adjective, informal	Honestly or directly, without exaggeration or disguise; also used as an intensifier meaning genuinely or simply.	honestly, seriously, genuinely	Straight up, I didn't expect the movie to be that good.	Informal. Other literal senses exist ('vertically upward', drinks served without ice depending on context), so context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111 | search: straight up meaning
621	6201	6705	unique to someone/something	/juːˈniːk tə/	phrase	Existing only in, belonging specifically to, or characteristic of one particular person, thing, group, place, system, etc.	specific to, peculiar to, exclusive to, distinctive of	That pronunciation pattern isn't unique to you; lots of learners do it. / The feature is unique to the paid version.	Natural pattern unique to X. Strictly, unique means one-of-a-kind, but modern usage also commonly means distinctive/specific to a limited entity/group.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: unique to you meaning
621	6202	6308	degrade	/dɪˈɡreɪd/	verb	To reduce in quality, condition, status, dignity, or performance; to humiliate or treat someone as less worthy; scientifically, to break down or deteriorate chemically/physically.	deteriorate, decline, erode, break down; demean, humiliate, debase	Image quality degrades each time the file is recompressed. / The plastic slowly degrades in sunlight. / He felt degraded by the way they spoke to him in front of everyone.	Can be intransitive ('the signal degraded') or transitive ('compression degraded the image'). Human/dignity sense = demean. Scientific degradation is neutral process language, unlike moral/social degrading.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: degrade meaning
621	6203	6614	indexing	/ˈɪndeksɪŋ/	noun / gerund	The process of creating/searching an index or organizing data for retrieval; in finance/economics, linking values to an index or following an index-based investment strategy.	cataloging, data indexing, indexation	Database indexing can make queries much faster. / Wage indexing links pay adjustments to a price index. / Passive index investing aims to track a market index.	Meaning varies strongly by domain: databases/search, publishing, economics, investing, cognition. Related verb index.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define indexing
621	6204	5818	roll with it	/ˈroʊl wɪð ɪt/	idiom, informal	To accept an unexpected situation and adapt to it rather than resisting or becoming upset.	go with the flow, adapt, take it in stride	The schedule changed again, so we just rolled with it.	Often suggests flexibility in the face of uncertainty or inconvenience.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: roll with it meaning
621	6205	6155	flapjack	/ˈflæpˌdʒæk/	noun	In North American English, a pancake. In British English, a baked oat bar made with oats, butter, and syrup/sugar.	pancake (AmE), oat bar (BrE)	The diner served a stack of flapjacks with maple syrup. / In Britain, a flapjack is usually a chewy baked oat square rather than a pancake.	A classic AmE/BrE lexical difference. Context can radically change the food image. Also useful for understanding playful compounds/wordplay such as the nearby raw search 'flap your jacks.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: flapjack
621	6206	6020	draw on	/ˌdrɔː ˈɑːn/	phrasal verb	To use a supply, resource, experience, knowledge, or ability; also, for time to pass/approach, or literally to pull clothing on.	draw upon, tap into, rely on, make use of	She drew on years of teaching experience to explain the concept simply. / The film draws heavily on 1970s horror. / As evening drew on, the streets became quieter.	Highly polysemous. Resource sense often takes experience/knowledge/reserves/tradition. 'Draw on/upon' can mean use as a source. Time sense: night/evening/winter draws on. Do not confuse draw on paper = make a picture on a surface.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: draw on meaning
621	6207	5663	play it off	/ˌpleɪ ɪt ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb, informal	To act as though an embarrassing, awkward, suspicious, or important event is insignificant, intentional, or not worth noticing.	brush off, laugh off, pretend it was nothing	I tripped onstage but tried to play it off like it was part of the joke.	Often means conceal embarrassment or significance through casual behavior.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: play it off | WordReference Forums
621	6208	5566	acutely aware	/əˌkjuːtli əˈwer/	adjective phrase	Very strongly or keenly conscious of something, especially a problem, risk, difference, or uncomfortable fact.	keenly aware, highly conscious	He was acutely aware that everyone in the room was watching him.	Stronger than simply aware; often used when the awareness is impossible to ignore.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: acutely aware meaning
621	6209	5927	ward off evil	/ˌwɔːrd ɔːf ˈiːvəl/	phrase	To keep evil, harmful forces, bad luck, or danger away through protection, ritual, magic, belief, or metaphorical preventive action.	repel, avert, keep at bay, protect against, drive away	The villagers hung charms above the doors to ward off evil. / In the story, the repeated line functions like an incantation meant to ward off evil spirits.	Ward off is broader: ward off illness, attack, criticism, danger. Incantation WID153 = spoken magical formula; apotropaic describes something whose purpose is to avert evil/harm.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: it's an incantation to ward off evil
621	6210	6136	get sidetracked	/ˌɡet ˈsaɪdtrækt/	phrasal verb / adjective pattern	To be distracted or diverted from the main subject, task, route, or goal by something secondary.	get distracted, get diverted, go off track, lose the thread	I opened the article to check one word and got sidetracked for forty minutes. / We got sidetracked talking about the soundtrack and never answered the original question.	Often unintentional. Sidetrack someone = divert them; get sidetracked = become diverted. Compared with go off on a tangent, sidetracked can involve activity/tasks as well as conversation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115, voca_0121 | search: get sidetracked
622	6211	6065	manosphere	/ˈmænoʊˌsfɪr/	noun, internet/cultural term	A loose network of online communities and ideologies focused on men, masculinity, dating, gender relations, or men's grievances, including groups such as men's-rights activists, pickup artists, incels, and red-pill communities.	male-oriented online ecosystem, men's-rights sphere (partial), red-pill ecosystem (partial)	The documentary traces how several influencers moved from dating advice into the broader manosphere. / 'Manosphere' is an umbrella label, so two communities placed under it may disagree sharply with each other.	The term is broad and often critical; it does not mean every online space for men. Some parts focus on men's issues, while others are associated with misogyny, anti-feminism, or extremist rhetoric. Use specific subgroup labels when precision matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: manosphere
622	6212	6559	articulation	/ɑːrˌtɪkjəˈleɪʃən/	noun	The clear expression/formulation of ideas; the production of speech sounds by positioning/moving speech organs; a joint or connection permitting movement; in figures/models, movable joints and their range.	expression, enunciation; jointing, movable joints	Her articulation of the problem was clearer than mine. / Good articulation makes consonants easier to understand. / This action figure has excellent articulation in the shoulders and hips.	Important polysemy for the surrounding figurine/figure search context: collectors use articulation for movable joints. Existing 'articulation disorder' is only the speech-clinical compound.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: articulation meaning
622	6213	6168	pathologize	/pəˈθɑːlədʒaɪz/	verb	To interpret or frame a behavior, trait, emotion, identity, or social phenomenon primarily as a disease, disorder, or psychological abnormality, sometimes excessively or inappropriately.	medicalize (overlapping), diagnose, treat as pathological, problematize (not exact)	Not every awkward social habit needs to be pathologized as a disorder. / Critics argue that the framework pathologizes normal grief. / Medicine can identify genuine pathology without pathologizing every variation in human behavior.	Often critical in psychology/social discourse. Medicalize = bring something under medical framing/treatment; pathologize more specifically casts it as abnormal/diseased. It does NOT mean merely 'study pathology.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pathologize
622	6214	6316	humanity has fallen so low	/hjuːˈmænəti hæz ˌfɔːlən soʊ ˈloʊ/	rhetorical expression	A dramatic statement of moral or cultural disappointment meaning that people or society seem to have declined to an embarrassingly, shockingly, or morally degraded level.	how far we've fallen, society has sunk so low, what has humanity come to?	I opened the comments, saw people cheering for the scam, and thought, 'Humanity has fallen so low.' / It's often used half-seriously or hyperbolically about something absurdly disappointing.	'Fall/sink so low' is the productive idiom; 'humanity has fallen so low' applies it to society as a whole. Tone can range from genuine moral despair to melodramatic internet humor.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: humanity has fallen so low
622	6215	5778	liable	/ˈlaɪəbəl/	adjective	Legally responsible for something; or likely to do, experience, or suffer something, often undesirable.	responsible, accountable; likely	The company may be liable for the damage. / He's liable to forget if you don't remind him.	Two key patterns: liable for = legally responsible; liable to = likely/prone to.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112, voca_0114 | search: define liable
622	6216	6908	adapt vs adopt vs adept	/əˈdæpt/; /əˈdɑːpt/; /əˈdept/	contrast / pronunciation	Adapt = change/adjust to fit a new use or situation. Adopt = take up, accept, choose, or legally become the parent of. Adept = highly skilled (adjective/noun).	adjust vs take up vs skilled	We adapted the workflow to the new format. / The team adopted a new policy. / She's adept at spotting subtle wording differences.	Pronunciation matters: adapt /ə-DAPT/, adopt /ə-DOPT/ (AmE), adept /ə-DEPT/. Patterns: adapt to/for/from; adopt a policy/method/child; adept at + noun/gerund.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: adapt vs adopt vs adept meaning and pronunciation
622	6217	6240	volcel	/ˈvɑːlˌsel/	noun, internet slang	Short for 'voluntary celibate': a person who is not having sex or pursuing sexual relationships by choice, often contrasted with incel ('involuntary celibate').	voluntary celibate, celibate by choice	He described himself as a volcel because dating was simply not a priority for him. / In forum slang, volcel is often contrasted with incel, though people argue about where the boundary lies.	Internet coinage from voluntary + cel(ibate). May be sincere, ironic, or used accusatorily ('you're a volcel, not an incel'). Do not assume religious celibacy; motivation varies.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: volcel meaning
622	6218	5712	in awe	/ɪn ˈɔː/	prepositional phrase	Feeling deep wonder, admiration, reverence, or amazement toward someone or something.	amazed, full of admiration	I was in awe of how calmly she handled the crisis.	The natural phrase is in awe, not normally 'in an awe.' Common: be in awe of someone/something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: in an awe meaning
622	6219	5819	the best thing since sliced bread	/ðə ˌbest θɪŋ sɪns ˌslaɪst ˈbred/	idiom, humorous	An exaggerated way to call something an excellent, impressive, or very useful invention or development.	wonderful innovation, greatest thing ever	He thinks the new app is the best thing since sliced bread.	Raw search 'since the slice of bread' likely points to this fixed expression. Usually playful or ironic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: since the slice of bread meaning
622	6220	5736	homonymy	/həˈmɑːnəmi/	noun, linguistics	The relationship between words that have the same form or sound but unrelated meanings, depending on the linguistic definition being used.	lexical ambiguity, homonymous relationship	Bank meaning 'financial institution' and bank meaning 'river edge' are a classic example of homonymy.	Terminology varies: homonym can be used broadly or more narrowly alongside homophone and homograph.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: HOMONYMY Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster
623	6221	6596	a weight off one's shoulders	/ə ˌweɪt ɔːf wʌnz ˈʃoʊldərz/	idiom	A feeling of relief after a worry, burden, responsibility, or uncertainty is removed.	a load off one's mind, relief, burden lifted	Finishing the project was a huge weight off my shoulders. / Hearing that the test was negative took a weight off her shoulders.	Common forms: be a weight off my shoulders; take a weight off someone's shoulders. Metaphorical burden.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: weight off my shoulders
623	6222	5727	node	/noʊd/	noun	A point at which lines, routes, branches, or connections meet; a distinct point or unit in a network, structure, anatomy, or data system.	junction, connection point, vertex, hub, network point	Each computer on the network acts as a node. / In a graph, an edge connects one node to another. / The city's central station is a major transportation node.	Meaning varies by field: network node, graph/tree node, lymph node, plant node, transportation node. Core image is a distinct point in a larger connected structure. In graph theory 'vertex' is the precise counterpart; in networks 'hub' usually means a particularly connected/important node.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define nodes
623	6223	5859	early doors	/ˌɜːrli ˈdɔːrz/	British informal phrase	At an early stage or early in a period of time; sometimes simply early.	early on, at an early stage	It's early doors, but the new manager already looks promising.	Distinctive British expression, especially common in sports commentary and informal speech.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: early doors meaning
623	6224	5565	perceptive	/pərˈseptɪv/	adjective	Good at noticing, understanding, or interpreting subtle details, feelings, or situations.	insightful, discerning, observant	That was a perceptive observation about why the scene feels unsettling.	Usually complimentary: it suggests noticing more than is immediately obvious and understanding its significance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: perceptive
623	6225	6503	window-shopping (figurative/slang uses)	/ˈwɪndoʊ ˌʃɑːpɪŋ/	figurative usage	Browsing possible partners, jobs, homes, options, or opportunities without serious commitment or immediate intention to choose/buy; a metaphorical extension of literal window-shopping.	browsing options, looking around, keeping one's options open	He was on the dating app mostly window-shopping rather than trying to meet anyone. / Employees sometimes start window-shopping for other jobs before they actually decide to leave.	Not one fixed slang definition: the metaphor transfers 'look but don't buy/commit' into other domains. In dating it can sound objectifying, so tone/context matter.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: window-shop meaning slang
623	6226	5664	mog	/mɑːɡ/	verb / slang	In online appearance slang, to greatly outclass someone, especially in looks, physique, height, or another status trait.	outclass, overshadow	People in the comments said the model mogged everyone else in the photo.	Modern internet slang, especially in looks-focused communities. Related: mogging. Tone can be objectifying or hypercompetitive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: mog meaning
623	6227	5651	take a dreamer	/ˌteɪk ə ˈdriːmər/	context-dependent phrase	A non-fixed phrase whose meaning depends on the surrounding sentence, typically 'consider/choose/use a dreamer' or in a construction such as 'it takes a dreamer to...'	consider a dreamer; require a dreamer	Sometimes it takes a dreamer to imagine what everyone else thinks is impossible.	The raw search lacks enough context for one fixed idiomatic meaning. Preserve the searched chunk rather than inventing a phrasal verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: taking a dreamer meaning
623	6228	6423	mouthful: expression family	/ˈmaʊθfʊl/	usage cluster	Mouthful has several literal and idiomatic patterns: a mouthful of food/drink; 'X is a mouthful' = long/difficult to say; give someone a mouthful = angrily tell them off; 'you said a mouthful' = what you said contains a lot of truth.	bite/sipful; tongue-twisting phrase; earful; well said (contextual)	That's a mouthful of a title. / He took a mouthful of soup. / She gave him a mouthful for being late. / 'You said a mouthful' means strong agreement, not criticism.	Existing WID2477 covers the core noun senses. This card exists because the search explicitly asked for 'mouthful expressions' and links the idiom family without collapsing their distinct meanings.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: mouthful expressions meaning
623	6229	5621	hold oneself out to be/as something	/ˌhoʊld wʌnˈself ˈaʊt/	formal / legal phrase	To represent or present oneself to others as having a particular identity, status, qualification, or authority.	represent oneself as, present oneself as	He held himself out as an expert despite having no formal training.	Common in legal/formal contexts, especially when discussing how a person represented their qualifications or status.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: hold oneself out to be meaning
623	6230	5980	spar	/spɑːr/	verb / noun	To practice fighting with controlled blows, especially in boxing/martial arts; figuratively, to exchange arguments, teasing, or verbal attacks in a lively competitive way.	practice fight, box; trade barbs, joust, banter, clash	The two fighters sparred for three rounds without going full force. / The hosts spent half the podcast sparring over which sequel was worse.	Figurative spar often suggests back-and-forth rather than one-sided hostility. Spar with someone over/about an issue. A spar is also a pole/beam in nautical/structural contexts—a separate sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: spar meaning
624	6231	6021	uphill battle	/ˌʌphɪl ˈbætəl/	idiom	A difficult struggle in which success requires sustained effort because circumstances are unfavorable.	hard struggle, difficult fight, steep challenge, fight an uphill battle	Convincing people to switch after years of habit is an uphill battle. / The anime had almost no marketing, so finding a large audience was always going to be an uphill battle.	Metaphor of fighting while moving uphill. Often 'face/fight an uphill battle.' Does not mean impossible—just significantly disadvantaged/difficult.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114, voca_0115 | search: uphill battle meaning
624	6232	6711	ascertain	/ˌæsərˈteɪn/	verb, formal	To discover, determine, or establish something with reasonable certainty, especially by investigation, checking, or evidence.	determine, establish, find out, verify, learn	Investigators could not ascertain the cause of the failure. / I checked the workbook to ascertain which WordID was actually correct.	More formal than find out. Common objects: facts, identity, cause, extent, whether/what/how. Does not mean merely guess or assume.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ascertain meaning
624	6233	6066	adopt vs adapt	/əˈdɑːpt/ vs /əˈdæpt/	contrast card	Adopt = take something up, accept it, begin using it, or legally become a parent. Adapt = change something—or oneself—so it fits new conditions, purposes, or media.	take up/embrace vs adjust/modify	The team adopted a new workflow, then adapted it to fit the smaller project. / The film adapts the novel rather than adopting it unchanged.	Memory hook: adopt = take it as yours/use it; adapt = alter to fit. A company adopts a policy; it adapts the policy to local law. An organism adapts to its environment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: adopt vs adapt
624	6234	6278	the boulder of Sisyphus / Sisyphus's boulder	/ðə ˌboʊldər əv ˈsɪsəfəs/	mythological metaphor / noun phrase	The huge stone Sisyphus must repeatedly push uphill in Greek myth; figuratively, a burden or task that must be struggled with again and again without permanent resolution.	Sisyphean burden, endless burden, recurring task	Every morning the backlog returned like Sisyphus's boulder. / For him, maintaining the old system became a boulder he pushed uphill only to watch it roll back down.	Not a fixed idiom as such; 'Sisyphus's boulder' is a recognizable allusion. The key image is not merely heaviness but repetition: apparent progress is repeatedly undone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: boulder of sisyphus meaning
624	6235	6758	sour grapes	/ˌsaʊər ˈɡreɪps/	idiom / fable concept	Disparaging something you cannot have or failed to obtain, often as a way to protect your pride by pretending it was undesirable anyway.	rationalization, dismissing what one can't have, resentment disguised as contempt	He didn't get the promotion and suddenly started saying the job was terrible anyway—classic sour grapes.	From Aesop's fable 'The Fox and the Grapes': the fox cannot reach the grapes and concludes they must be sour. Japanese 酸っぱい葡萄 maps directly to this concept.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: 酸っぱい葡萄とは
624	6236	6365	hex	/heks/	noun / verb	A magic spell or curse intended to cause harm or bad luck; as a verb, to cast such a spell on someone or something. In computing/mathematics, hex can also abbreviate hexadecimal.	curse, spell, jinx, bewitch; hexadecimal (technical abbreviation)	The witch put a hex on the prince. / He joked that the team must be hexed after losing five finals in a row. / In programming, FF is a common hex representation of 255.	Magic sense is common in fantasy. Hex vs curse: hex often suggests a deliberately cast magical spell; curse is broader. Technical hex = base-16/hexadecimal is unrelated in meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: hex meaning
624	6237	6551	DSA	/ˌdiː es ˈeɪ/	initialism, context-dependent	An abbreviation with many unrelated expansions. Common modern examples include Democratic Socialists of America (U.S. politics), Digital Services Act (EU law), Digital Signature Algorithm (cryptography), and digital subtraction angiography (medicine).	context-dependent acronym	The article says the DSA requires certain online platforms to follow EU rules—there DSA means Digital Services Act. / In a cryptography textbook, DSA may mean Digital Signature Algorithm.	There is no single context-free definition. Preserve the acronym card because the raw search lacks context. NIST uses DSA for Digital Signature Algorithm; other fields use the same letters differently.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: dsa meaning
624	6238	6731	idiolect	/ˈɪdioʊˌlekt/	noun, linguistics	The distinctive language habits and linguistic system of an individual speaker, including pronunciation, vocabulary, grammar, preferred expressions, and stylistic patterns.	individual speech variety, personal dialect, individual language use	Everyone has an idiolect shaped by where they've lived, what they watch, and whom they talk to. / Her idiolect includes expressions she picked up from several regions.	Raw 'ideolect' repaired to idiolect. Contrast dialect/sociolect, which characterize groups; idiolect focuses on an individual.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: ideolect meaning
624	6239	6101	get something off one's chest	/ˌɡet ˌsʌmθɪŋ ɔːf wʌnz ˈtʃest/	idiom	To say something that has been worrying, burdening, irritating, or emotionally weighing on you, so that you feel relieved afterward.	unburden oneself, vent, confess, air something, get it out	Can I tell you something? I just need to get it off my chest. / She finally got years of resentment off her chest. / Writing the message helped me get the whole thing off my chest, even though I never sent it.	Raw 'get the load off your chest' blends this idiom with 'take a load off.' Standard is get something/it off your chest. Vent = express frustration; confess = admit something hidden/wrong; this idiom covers many emotional burdens.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: get the load off your chest meaning
624	6240	5585	feel shy about doing something	/ˌfiːl ˈʃaɪ əˌbaʊt/	phrase	To feel hesitant, embarrassed, or reluctant about doing or discussing something because of shyness or self-consciousness.	feel hesitant about, be embarrassed about	Don't feel shy about asking for clarification.	Natural pattern: be/feel shy about + noun or -ing form.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: feel shy about meaning
625	6241	5971	fiend	/fiːnd/	noun	Originally an evil spirit or devil; also a cruel/wicked person. Informally, someone intensely devoted or addicted to something, especially in compounds such as coffee fiend.	demon, devil; monster; addict, fanatic, junkie (contextual)	The legend describes a fiend that haunts the forest. / She's a coffee fiend—she knows every café in the neighborhood. / The villain is portrayed as a sadistic fiend.	Existing fiending WID4515 derives from fiend. Modern playful compounds X fiend often mean someone who craves/loves X intensely; 'drug fiend' is harsher and historically stigmatizing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: fiend
625	6242	6494	make a commitment	/ˌmeɪk ə kəˈmɪtmənt/	collocation	To firmly decide, promise, or agree to devote oneself, one's time/resources, or future action to a person, plan, responsibility, relationship, or goal.	commit, pledge, promise, dedicate oneself, undertake	Before buying the annual plan, I wanted to be sure I could make the commitment. / They weren't ready to make a long-term commitment to the relationship. / She made a commitment to practice every day.	Common patterns: make a commitment to someone/something; make a commitment to do something. Commitment can be practical, moral, financial, or romantic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: make a commitment
625	6243	6022	grueling	/ˈɡruːəlɪŋ/	adjective	Extremely tiring, demanding, and difficult, especially over a sustained period.	exhausting, punishing, arduous, strenuous, taxing	The last section of the hike was grueling. / Editing a two-hour video on a tight deadline turned into a grueling week of late nights.	Grueling emphasizes exhausting ordeal. Strenuous emphasizes effort/exertion; arduous emphasizes difficulty and sustained labor; punishing suggests exceptionally harsh demands.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define grueling
625	6244	6377	dissipate	/ˈdɪsəˌpeɪt/	verb	To gradually disperse, fade, or disappear; to cause something such as heat, energy, smoke, tension, fear, or clouds to spread out and vanish. Also, to waste money/energy through careless living.	disperse, scatter, fade, evaporate, dissolve; squander	The morning fog dissipated by noon. / The heat is dissipated through the metal fins. / Her anxiety began to dissipate once she understood what had happened. / He dissipated much of his fortune.	Can be intransitive or transitive. Scientific use: dissipate energy/heat. Emotional/atmospheric use: tension, fear, doubts dissipate. 'Dissipated' can also mean dissolute/self-indulgent, a less common literary sense.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define dissipates
625	6245	6141	wander off vs wander away	/ˌwɑːndər ˈɔːf/ vs /ˌwɑːndər əˈweɪ/	contrast card	Both mean move away while wandering. Wander off more strongly frames leaving a specific place/person/group or going where one should not; wander away emphasizes increasing distance or gradual movement away.	stray off/away, drift away, roam away	The toddler wandered off in the store, so we had to look for him. / We watched the deer wander away into the trees. / My attention wandered off is possible, but 'my mind wandered' is usually more natural.	Often interchangeable physically, but off commonly carries a 'left the group/place' event boundary; away highlights direction/distance. For abstract deviation from rules/topics, stray from or drift away from may be more natural.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: wander off vs wander away
625	6246	6897	pore	/pɔːr/	noun	A very small opening in skin, plant/animal tissue, rock, or another material through which substances may pass; especially openings associated with hair follicles and sweat/sebum on skin.	opening, tiny hole, aperture	Sweat reaches the skin surface through pores. / The material's tiny pores absorb moisture.	Plural pores common in skincare. Do not confuse pore over = study/read closely, or pour = cause liquid to flow; all can sound similar depending accent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pores meaning
625	6247	6417	time out of mind	/ˌtaɪm aʊt əv ˈmaɪnd/	idiom, literary/old-fashioned	A time so far in the past that human memory cannot reach it; since an unimaginably ancient time.	time immemorial, beyond living memory, since ancient times	The custom has existed from time out of mind. / In fantasy narration, the phrase can give a place the feeling of having been ancient beyond memory.	Literary/archaic flavor. Closely related to 'time immemorial,' which is much more common as 'since/from time immemorial.' Bob Dylan also used Time Out of Mind as an album title, but the phrase predates it by centuries.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: time out of mind
625	6248	6036	stay vigilant	/ˌsteɪ ˈvɪdʒələnt/	verb phrase	To remain watchful, alert, and attentive to possible danger, problems, deception, or changes.	stay alert, remain watchful, keep one's guard up, stay on the lookout	Travelers were advised to stay vigilant in crowded stations. / Even after the obvious scam message is deleted, stay vigilant for follow-up attempts.	Vigilant implies sustained attention to possible threat/problem. 'Stay alert' is broader and more everyday; 'keep your guard up' suggests defensive caution.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: stay vigilant meaning
625	6249	5832	virtue signaling	/ˈvɜːrtʃuː ˌsɪɡnəlɪŋ/	noun, often critical	Publicly expressing values or moral positions in a way perceived as intended mainly to display one's own virtue, social alignment, or moral standing.	moral posturing, performative virtue	Critics dismissed the company's statement as virtue signaling.	Usually pejorative and often contested: the label implies performative motive rather than proving that motive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: virtue signaling
625	6250	5640	have no stakes in something	/hæv noʊ ˈsteɪks ɪn/	phrase	To have no personal interest, investment, risk, or potential gain or loss tied to an outcome.	have nothing at stake, have no vested interest	I have no stakes in either company, so I don't care which one wins.	More idiomatic alternatives include have no stake in something and have nothing at stake. Preserve the searched plural wording while noting the usual patterns.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: do not have stakes meaning
626	6251	6847	whitewash / whitewashed	/ˈwaɪtˌwɑːʃ/	verb / adjective-participle	To conceal, minimize, sanitize, or gloss over wrongdoing/unpleasant facts; also to alter representation in a way that erases or replaces nonwhite identity/casting; literally, coat with whitewash. In sports, defeat without allowing a win/score in a series/context.	cover up, sanitize, gloss over; erase racial identity (contextual)	The documentary was accused of whitewashing the regime's abuses. / Fans criticized the adaptation for whitewashing the character. / The team whitewashed its opponent in the series.	Several distinct senses share 'make white/clean.' Racial/media sense requires context and should not be collapsed into generic sanitizing. Sports sense varies by sport/region.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: whitewashed meaning
626	6252	5596	nothing to sneeze at	/ˌnʌθɪŋ tə ˈsniːz æt/	idiom, informal	Significant, respectable, or valuable enough that it should not be dismissed as unimportant.	not insignificant, respectable	A ten-percent raise is nothing to sneeze at.	Usually used to emphasize that an amount, achievement, or advantage is better or more important than it may first sound.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: nothing to sneeze at
626	6253	5634	backwater	/ˈbækˌwɔːtər/	noun	A place, community, or area considered isolated, undeveloped, or uninfluential; literally, a quiet body of water away from a main current.	remote place, out-of-the-way place	He grew up in a rural backwater far from the country's cultural centers.	Figurative use can sound dismissive or insulting when applied to a place or community.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: backwater meaning
626	6254	6471	on the snick	/ˌɑːn ðə ˈsnɪk/	regional / context-dependent phrase	A regional British expression reported for a door or latch left in a state where it can be pushed/opened without fully locking it; usage varies regionally and may overlap with 'on the snib/sneck.'	on the snib, latch not fully locked (regional)	Leave the door on the snick while we're carrying the boxes in. / The phrase is regional enough that many English speakers will not recognize it.	Not a broad standard idiom. Search evidence shows regional variation such as on the snick/on the snib/sneck for latch states. Preserve as niche provenance rather than overgeneralizing. citeturn0reddit36		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define on the snick
626	6255	6674	IDGAF-er / someone who doesn't give a fuck	/ˌaɪ diː dʒiː eɪ ˈef ər/	internet slang / nonce formation	A person characterized by an 'I don't give a fuck' attitude—unconcerned with others' opinions, rules, expectations, or consequences. The -er form is understandable but not a stable standard word.	someone who doesn't give a fuck, DGAF type, carefree/reckless person (contextual)	He presents himself online as an IDGAF-er who never worries about what anyone thinks.	Raw 'idgafer' is internet-style morphology from IDGAF + -er. Tone can suggest admirable independence or careless/rude disregard depending on context. Preserve as nonstandard/source-specific.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: idgafer meanng
626	6256	6574	call someone out	/ˌkɔːl ˌsʌmwʌn ˈaʊt/	phrasal verb	To publicly or directly challenge, criticize, confront, or identify someone's bad behavior, false claim, hypocrisy, mistake, etc.; in some contexts, to summon/challenge someone.	confront, challenge, criticize, expose, hold accountable	She called him out for taking credit for someone else's work. / Fans called the article out for misquoting the interview. / If I'm wrong, call me out on it.	Common patterns: call someone out for X; call someone out on X. Can mean accountability or public shaming depending on context/tone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: call someone out meaning
626	6257	6177	moat	/moʊt/	noun	A deep, broad ditch, often filled with water, surrounding a castle, fortification, or property for defense; figuratively, a durable competitive advantage protecting a business from rivals.	defensive ditch; competitive advantage, barrier to entry	The castle was surrounded by a wide moat and reached by drawbridge. / A powerful network effect can create an economic moat around a platform.	Business/investing metaphor popularized by 'economic moat': brand, network effects, switching costs, scale, etc. that make competition harder. Literal moat need not always contain water.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: moat meaning
626	6258	5821	polemical	/pəˈlemɪkəl/	adjective	Strongly critical or argumentative, especially in writing or speech that attacks a position and advances a controversial one.	controversial, argumentative, combative	The essay is deliberately polemical rather than neutral.	Related noun polemic = a forceful written/spoken attack or controversial argument.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: polemical
626	6259	6648	shed / throw off / cast off	/ʃed/	verb cluster	Shed means let something fall off, lose, discard, or get rid of it; depending on context, useful synonyms include throw off, cast off, slough off, discard, lose, molt, or emit.	throw off, cast off, slough off, discard, lose, molt	Snakes shed their skin. / The company is trying to shed its old image. / Exercise helps me shed some of the stress from the day.	Raw search asks for a synonym of throw, apparently targeting shed. They overlap in 'throw/shed off' but are not general synonyms: you can throw a ball, not *shed a ball.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: throw synonym shed
626	6260	5546	go with something/someone (choose)	/ˈɡoʊ wɪð/	phrasal verb	To choose or select a particular option, person, plan, style, or course of action.	choose, select, opt for	After comparing all three designs, we decided to go with the second one.	Normalized from the context-dependent search 'tough competition vs go with'. The independently useful lexical target is go with = choose/select; 'tough competition' is a transparent combination and does not need its own card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: tough competition vs go with
627	6261	5789	localizer	/ˈloʊkəˌlaɪzər/	noun	A person who adapts translated content, software, games, or media for a target language, region, and culture.	localization specialist, adapter	The localizer rewrote the joke so it would work for the target audience.	In media/game discourse, localizer can include translators, editors, and adaptation staff depending on workflow.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: localizers
627	6262	6052	overbearing	/ˌoʊvərˈberɪŋ/	adjective	Unpleasantly forceful, controlling, demanding, or intrusive in dealing with others.	domineering, bossy, controlling, oppressive, pushy	I liked the advice, but his tone was so overbearing that I immediately wanted to resist it. / An overbearing parent may make even ordinary decisions for an adult child.	Domineering strongly emphasizes desire to control; overbearing emphasizes the oppressive effect of someone's forceful personality/behavior. Pushy is more everyday and often milder.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define overbearing
627	6263	6011	feel like everyone's looking at me	/ˌfiːl laɪk ˈevriwʌnz ˈlʊkɪŋ ət miː/	conversational pattern	To have the subjective feeling that everyone around you is watching, noticing, or judging you, whether or not they actually are.	feel self-conscious, feel watched, feel like all eyes are on me	I know nobody probably cares, but I feel like everyone's freaking looking at me in this outfit. / When I walked into the quiet room late, I felt like everyone was looking at me.	Feel like + clause marks perception/impression, not a factual claim. 'Freaking' adds emotional emphasis. 'All eyes are on me' is stronger/more idiomatic and can describe actual attention too.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: I feel like everyone is freaking looking at me meaning
627	6264	5613	rampart	/ˈræmpɑːrt/	noun	A defensive wall or embankment built around a fort, city, or other place; figuratively, a protective barrier.	fortification, defensive wall, bulwark	Soldiers watched the valley from the old stone ramparts.	Often plural when referring to the defensive walls of a fortress.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: rampart meaning
627	6265	6163	flip someone off / get flipped off	/ˌflɪp ˌsʌmwʌn ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb, informal/vulgar gesture	To show someone the raised middle finger as an insulting or hostile gesture; get flipped off = receive that gesture.	give someone the finger, give the middle finger, make an obscene gesture	A driver flipped me off even though he was the one blocking the intersection. / I got flipped off for honking when the light turned green.	Common in North America. Strongly rude/hostile but not necessarily accompanied by words. 'Flip out' is unrelated = become extremely upset/excited.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: get flipped off meaning
627	6266	6948	skeleton	/ˈskelətən/	noun	The internal or external framework of bones/supporting structures of a person or animal; by extension, the bare framework, outline, or minimal supporting structure of something.	bony framework, framework, structure, outline, bare bones	The museum displays a complete dinosaur skeleton. / I wrote the skeleton of the argument first, then filled in the details. / The building's steel skeleton was visible during construction.	Literal anatomical sense is core. Figurative uses emphasize a stripped-down supporting framework: skeleton crew = minimum staff; skeleton key = key designed to open multiple locks; skeleton of a plan/argument = basic structure. Distinct from 'skeletons in the closet' = hidden embarrassing secrets.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: skeleton meaning
627	6267	6757	anticipate an objection	/ænˈtɪsəˌpeɪt ən əbˈdʒekʃən/	argumentation phrase	To predict a counterargument, concern, or challenge that someone is likely to raise and consider/address it in advance.	foresee a counterargument, preempt an objection, address a likely objection	A strong essay anticipates the objection that correlation does not prove causation. / She anticipated his objection and explained the cost before he asked.	Useful rhetoric/argumentation skill. Anticipating ≠ automatically refuting; you can anticipate an objection and then concede, qualify, or answer it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: anticipating an objection meaning
627	6268	6276	Sisyphus	/ˈsɪsəfəs/	proper noun / mythological figure	A king in Greek mythology condemned in the underworld to push a huge stone uphill forever; each time he nears the top, it rolls back down, making his punishment an image of endless futile labor.	mythological figure; symbol of endless labor	The essay compares modern bureaucracy to Sisyphus pushing his stone uphill. / Once you know the myth of Sisyphus, the adjective Sisyphean becomes instantly memorable.	The myth has many ancient variants. In modern philosophy/literature, especially Camus, Sisyphus can also symbolize confronting absurd, repetitive existence rather than merely 'failure.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sisyphus meaning
627	6269	6225	emasculate	/ɪˈmæskjəˌleɪt/	verb	Literally/historically, to castrate or remove male reproductive power; much more commonly figurative, to make a man feel less masculine, powerful, confident, or effective, or to weaken something's force/power.	make feel less masculine, humiliate, disempower, weaken, neuter (figurative)	He felt emasculated by the constant jokes about his masculinity. / Critics argued that the revised law had been emasculated by so many exceptions that it could no longer work effectively.	Figurative use reflects cultural ideas about masculinity and power, so it can reinforce gender stereotypes depending on framing. Non-person figurative sense = deprive of strength/effectiveness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: emasculate
627	6270	6802	be on high alert	/bi ɑːn ˌhaɪ əˈlɜːrt/	idiom	To be in a state of heightened watchfulness and readiness because a threat, risk, or important event may occur.	be highly vigilant, be on guard, be on heightened alert	The airport was on high alert after the security warning. / After the first suspicious email, I was on high alert for follow-up scams.	Stronger than simply 'be alert.' Often institutional/security language, but everyday figurative use is common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: being on high alert
628	6271	6502	window-shop	/ˈwɪndoʊ ˌʃɑːp/	verb	To look at goods in store windows or browse shops without intending to buy anything immediately.	browse, look around, shop without buying	We spent an hour window-shopping in SoHo without buying anything. / I like window-shopping for watches even when I know I won't get one.	Usually literal shopping/browsing. Can extend metaphorically to dating, jobs, houses, etc., but that figurative use depends on context and can sound cynical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: window-shop meaning
628	6272	5670	channel / channeling (automotive)	/ˈtʃænəl/	verb / gerund, technical	In automotive customization, to lower a vehicle body over its frame by modifying the floor/body so the body sits lower relative to the chassis.	body drop, lower the body over the frame	The custom shop channeled the body several inches over the frame.	This is a specialized hot-rodding/custom-car sense, distinct from the existing general/spiritual 'channeling' card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: channeled meaning in a automotive industry
628	6273	5620	put off	/ˌpʊt ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To postpone something; to make someone dislike, distrust, or lose interest; or to distract/discourage someone.	postpone, defer; repel, discourage	We put off the meeting until Friday. / His rude tone really put me off.	Important contrast: put something off = postpone; put someone off = repel/discourage/distract.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: put off
628	6274	5912	heritable	/ˈherɪtəbəl/	adjective	Capable of being inherited genetically; in quantitative genetics, describing variation in a trait that is statistically attributable to genetic differences within a particular population/environment.	inheritable, genetic, transmissible (contextual)	Some disorders have a strong heritable component. / Height is highly heritable, but that does not mean an individual's height is fixed entirely by genes.	Important nuance: heritable ≠ inevitable/genetically determined. Heritability is population- and environment-specific. In everyday nontechnical use, inheritable is often used for property/rights as well as traits; heritable strongly suggests biological genetics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define heritable
628	6275	6322	come to a halt	/ˌkʌm tə ə ˈhɔːlt/	idiom / verb phrase	To stop moving, operating, progressing, or continuing, often completely or after slowing/disruption.	stop, grind to a halt, come to a stop, cease	Traffic came to a halt after the accident. / The conversation came to a sudden halt when his name was mentioned. / Without funding, the project gradually came to a halt.	'Grind to a halt' emphasizes slow/difficult stopping; 'come to a screeching halt' emphasizes abruptness. Can describe physical motion or processes/activities.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: came to a halt
628	6276	6048	mythologize	/mɪˈθɑːlədʒaɪz/	verb	To turn a person, event, place, or story into a myth or legend; figuratively, to portray it in an idealized, simplified, heroic, or larger-than-life way.	romanticize, idealize, lionize, turn into legend, mythicize	Biopics often mythologize their subjects by smoothing away contradictions. / Fans have mythologized that production disaster so much that the real story is almost beside the point.	Can be neutral in scholarship (create/treat as myth) or critical when it means distort through heroic/romantic storytelling. Unlike simply fictionalize, mythologize stresses symbolic/legendary status.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: mythologize
628	6277	6696	despondent	/dɪˈspɑːndənt/	adjective	Very discouraged, dejected, or without hope, especially after disappointment or failure.	dejected, downcast, dispirited, hopeless, crestfallen	He looked despondent after hearing the project had been canceled. / The ending leaves her exhausted but not completely despondent.	Stronger/more literary than sad. Usually a temporary emotional state; does not by itself imply a clinical diagnosis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120, voca_0121 | search: define despondent
628	6278	5626	allegory	/ˈæləɡɔːri/	noun	A story, image, or work in which characters and events systematically represent broader moral, political, religious, or abstract ideas.	symbolic narrative, extended metaphor	The novel can be read as an allegory of political revolution.	Unlike a single symbol, an allegory typically sustains a larger pattern of correspondences across a work.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0115 | search: allegory
628	6279	6337	stakes of none / no stakes	/ˌnoʊ ˈsteɪks/	nonstandard/context phrase	A context-dependent phrase likely expressing that nothing important is at risk or that an event has little/no consequence. Standard English would normally say 'there are no stakes,' 'the stakes are nonexistent,' or 'nothing is at stake.'	no stakes, nothing at stake, no consequences, low stakes	If nobody can lose anything, there are basically no stakes. / 'Stakes of none' sounds stylized or nonstandard; I'd normally say 'nothing is at stake.'	Preserve raw search provenance but do not teach 'stakes of none' as a standard fixed phrase. Stakes = what can be gained/lost; high-stakes/low-stakes are standard compounds.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: stakes of none
628	6280	6306	scuffle	/ˈskʌfəl/	noun / verb	A short, confused, relatively small physical fight or struggle involving pushing, grabbing, or blows; as a verb, to engage in such a struggle.	tussle, struggle, skirmish, altercation, grapple	A scuffle broke out near the entrance, but security separated them quickly. / The two players scuffled after the whistle.	Usually smaller/less organized than a fight or battle. Also historically can mean move with shuffling feet. Do not confuse scuffle (minor fight) with scuff (surface scrape/mark).		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: scuffle
629	6281	6612	EVT activity	/ˌiː viː ˈtiː ækˌtɪvəti/	abbreviation phrase, context-dependent	An activity labeled 'EVT'; the abbreviation is too context-dependent to assign one reliable expansion without the original source.	context-dependent	The schedule lists an 'EVT activity,' but the surrounding program is needed to know what EVT stands for.	Preserve ambiguity rather than invent an expansion. EVT can stand for many unrelated terms across education, engineering, medicine, events, etc. Source context should control.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: evt activity meaning
629	6282	5978	family jewels	/ˌfæməli ˈdʒuːəlz/	plural noun, humorous euphemism	A humorous euphemism for a man's testicles/genitals; literally, valuable jewelry or heirlooms belonging to a family.	balls, nuts (vulgar/informal), testicles (neutral anatomical)	The ball hit him right in the family jewels. / The museum displayed the royal family's jewels—literal family jewels, not the euphemism.	Usually humorous and old-fashioned/euphemistic in the anatomical sense. Context disambiguates literal heirlooms from the genital joke.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: family jewels meaning
629	6283	6660	sponge off someone	/ˌspʌndʒ ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb, informal	To live at another person's expense or repeatedly get money, food, housing, etc. from them without contributing fairly.	mooch off, freeload on, leech off, scrounge from	He spent months sponging off his parents instead of looking for work. / I don't mind helping once; I just don't want anyone sponging off me.	Negative and stronger than casually 'bum X off someone' for a small item. Often suggests an ongoing dependent/freeloading pattern.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: sponge off meaning
629	6284	6055	raw-dog life / rawdog life	/ˌrɔː ˌdɔːɡ ˈlaɪf/	vulgar slang / internet phrase	To go through life without a usual aid, buffer, distraction, medication, entertainment, or preparation—deliberately extending raw-dog from its sexual slang sense to mean 'without protection/help.'	do it with no buffer, go without, white-knuckle it (contextual), go in unassisted	My headphones died, so I had to raw-dog the entire flight with no music or podcast. / He's raw-dogging life without social media, caffeine, or a calendar.	Originally vulgar sexual slang for sex without a condom; modern internet usage productively means doing something with no usual comfort/protection/distraction. Still crude, so register matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: raw dog life meaning
629	6285	6067	academic prose	/ˌækəˈdemɪk ˈproʊz/	noun phrase	The style of continuous written language used in scholarly articles, books, essays, theses, and similar academic work, as distinct from verse, dialogue, notes, or informal speech.	scholarly writing, academic writing, formal expository prose	The argument is interesting, but the academic prose is so dense that I keep rereading each paragraph. / Clear academic prose can still be precise without stuffing every sentence with jargon.	Prose simply means ordinary written/spoken language not organized as verse; academic prose is a register/style, not necessarily 'good' or 'bad' writing. It can range from lucid to highly technical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: academic prose
629	6286	6920	recursive structure	/rɪˈkɜːrsɪv ˈstrʌktʃər/	noun phrase	A structure that contains or refers to another instance of the same kind of structure, allowing patterns to be nested or repeatedly applied.	nested structure, self-similar structure, recursion	A folder can contain another folder, creating a recursive structure. / The definition points to a rule that can apply again inside its own output.	General concept across computing, mathematics, linguistics, logic, and organization. Recursion is repeated self-embedding/application, not merely repetition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: recursive structure
629	6287	6939	be at ease	/bi ət ˈiːz/	idiom	To feel relaxed, comfortable, calm, and free from anxiety or awkwardness; in military drill, 'at ease' is also a command/posture allowing limited relaxation.	feel comfortable, be relaxed, feel at home, be unworried	I felt more at ease once the conversation became informal. / Her warm tone put everyone at ease. / The soldiers stood at ease.	Patterns: be/feel at ease; put someone at ease. Opposite: ill at ease. Military sense is distinct but related to relaxation from strict attention.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: be at ease
629	6288	6009	being cringe is being free	/ˌbiːɪŋ ˈkrɪndʒ ɪz ˌbiːɪŋ ˈfriː/	internet maxim / phrase	The idea that accepting the possibility of looking embarrassing, uncool, or overly earnest frees you to enjoy things and express yourself without excessive self-consciousness.	embrace the cringe, be unapologetically yourself, kill the part that cringes (related meme)	I almost didn't post it because it felt cheesy, but being cringe is being free. / The phrase pushes back against ironic detachment: sometimes sincerely loving something is worth looking uncool.	Not a traditional proverb; contemporary internet culture. Often used sincerely-but-playfully to defend enthusiasm, fandom, hobbies, fashion, dancing, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: being cringe is being free
629	6289	6609	learn about other cultures vs learn other cultures	/ˌlɜːrn əˌbaʊt ˌʌðər ˈkʌltʃərz/	usage contrast	Learn about other cultures = gain knowledge/information about them, the normal phrasing. Learn a culture can mean acquire/internalize its practices and norms through experience, but 'learn other cultures' is usually less natural for general study.	study other cultures, learn about cultures; become familiar with a culture	Travel can help you learn about other cultures. / Living with a host family helped her learn the local culture from the inside.	Use learn about for knowledge as topic. Learn X directly is normal for languages/skills and can work for a culture when emphasizing acquisition/enculturation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: learning about other cultures vs learning other cultures
629	6290	5932	polysemous	/pəˈlɪsəməs/	adjective, linguistics	Having multiple related meanings or senses.	having multiple senses, semantically multifaceted	'Head' is highly polysemous: it can refer to a body part, a leader, the top/front of something, and more. / 'Off' is so polysemous that memorizing one Japanese translation won't get you very far.	Polysemy = multiple related senses of one word. Homonymy = same form associated with historically/semantically distinct words/meanings. The boundary can be debatable, which is why dictionaries may analyze senses differently.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: polysemous
630	6291	6903	self-sealing	/ˌself ˈsiːlɪŋ/	adjective	Able to seal itself automatically; figuratively, structured so that apparent counterevidence is reinterpreted as support, making a belief or argument resistant to disconfirmation.	self-closing; unfalsifiable, self-immunizing (figurative)	The tire uses a self-sealing layer around small punctures. / A self-sealing belief can treat every objection as further proof of the conspiracy.	Literal technical uses exist in tires, containers, materials, etc. Figurative epistemic use is especially useful with 'self-sealing argument/belief.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: self-sealing
630	6292	6647	free loam	/ˌfriː ˈloʊm/	source-dependent phrase	Literally, loam (fertile soil with a balanced mixture of sand, silt, and clay) that is free/no-cost or unrestricted; 'free loam' is not a standard fixed English expression.	free soil, loam (contextual)	The listing advertised free loam for anyone willing to haul it away.	Preserve because it was explicitly searched, but no special idiomatic meaning should be invented without source context. Possible typo/mishearing is also possible. Loam itself is a common soil term.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: free loam meaning
630	6293	6498	unscathed	/ʌnˈskeɪðd/	adjective	Without suffering injury, damage, harm, or serious negative consequences; unharmed despite danger, attack, criticism, or difficulty.	unharmed, uninjured, undamaged, untouched, intact	Everyone escaped the crash unscathed. / The character somehow came through the scandal relatively unscathed. / Very few businesses emerged from the crisis completely unscathed.	Often 'escape/come through/emerge unscathed.' Can be physical or figurative. Scathe is rare as a modern verb, but scathing and unscathed remain common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: unscathed meaning
630	6294	6079	telltale	/ˈtelˌteɪl/	adjective / noun	Revealing or indicating something that is otherwise hidden or not immediately obvious; as a noun, historically a person who tells on others, and in technical contexts an indicator/device.	revealing, indicative, unmistakable, giveaway	There was a telltale pause before he answered. / The telltale smell of smoke made us check the kitchen.	Common collocations: telltale sign, telltale signs, telltale mark/smell. As adjective it means 'giving the game away.' Do not confuse with 'tell a tale.' Existing cards mention telltale as synonym but not standalone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: telltale meaning
630	6295	5693	strut (car suspension)	/strʌt/	noun	A structural suspension component that supports and locates part of a vehicle's wheel assembly and often incorporates a shock absorber.	suspension strut, MacPherson strut	The mechanic found a leaking front strut.	Automotive struts are load-bearing suspension members; they are not simply another name for every shock absorber.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: what is car struts
630	6296	6292	nooner	/ˈnuːnər/	noun, informal slang	An activity occurring around noon; especially, a sexual encounter during the middle of the day. In some contexts it can also mean a midday event, drink, meal, or broadcast.	midday quickie (sexual sense), lunchtime encounter; midday event	The sitcom joke was about sneaking home for a nooner. / Outside sexual slang, local contexts sometimes use nooner for a lunchtime event or gathering.	The sexual sense is the best-known informal meaning. Formation: noon + -er. Context is necessary because organizations/events sometimes independently use 'Nooner' as a name for midday programming.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: nooner meaning
630	6297	5776	that tracks	/ðæt ˈtræks/	informal expression	That makes sense or fits with the known facts, pattern, or person's usual behavior.	that makes sense, that checks out, figures	He forgot the deadline again? Yeah, that tracks.	Contemporary conversational use of track = align/correspond with evidence or expectations.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: that tracks.
630	6298	5925	go hands-off	/ˌɡoʊ ˌhændz ˈɔːf/	phrase	To stop intervening closely and allow a person, process, team, or system to operate with greater independence.	step back, back off, stop micromanaging, take a hands-off approach	Once the team understood the workflow, I went mostly hands-off. / The game goes hands-off during some story sequences and just lets the scene play out.	Hands-off can describe management, parenting, policy, technology, etc. Contrast laissez-faire: often specifically an ideology/policy of minimal intervention; hands-off is broader everyday English.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: go hands-off meaing
630	6299	5761	the shit vs shit	/ðə ˈʃɪt/; /ʃɪt/	slang contrast, vulgar	Calling someone/something 'the shit' means excellent, impressive, or the best; calling someone/something 'shit' usually means terrible, worthless, or contemptible.	awesome/excellent vs terrible/worthless; the bomb (positive, dated-ish) vs crap	That band is the shit. / That band is shit. / This ramen is the shit means high praise; this ramen is shit means the exact opposite.	The definite article completely reverses the slang evaluation: 'the shit' = excellent/top-tier; bare 'shit' as a predicate adjective-like noun = terrible. Highly informal/vulgar. Compare 'my shit' which can mean my stuff/music/work depending context and is different again.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: you are the shit vs you are shit
630	6300	5881	come out of the closet	/kʌm ˌaʊt əv ðə ˈklɑːzət/	idiom	To openly disclose a previously hidden sexual orientation or gender identity; by extension/jokingly, to reveal another identity, preference, or affiliation one had kept secret.	come out, disclose one's identity, reveal	He came out of the closet to his family in college. / The phrase is sometimes extended jokingly to tastes or fandoms, but that can trivialize the LGBTQ+ meaning depending on context.	The central modern meaning is LGBTQ+ coming out. 'Come out' is usually the more natural shorter form when context is clear. Distinguish from skeletons in the closet: that phrase concerns shameful/hidden secrets generally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: come out of the closet
631	6301	6618	take a step back	/ˌteɪk ə step ˈbæk/	idiom	To temporarily distance oneself from a situation or activity in order to reconsider it more calmly/broadly; also literally move backward.	pause and reassess, gain perspective, pull back, reconsider	I took a step back and looked at the whole workflow instead of fixing one cell at a time. / We need to take a step back and ask what problem we're actually solving.	Very common figurative management/conversation phrase. Can also mean reduce involvement/responsibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: take a step back meaning
631	6302	6116	accelerate	/əkˈseləˌreɪt/	verb	To increase speed; to cause a process, rate, growth, change, or development to happen faster.	speed up, quicken, hasten, expedite, intensify (contextual)	The car accelerated as it entered the highway. / Remote work accelerated changes that were already happening in the industry. / The final episodes accelerate the pace almost too much.	Can be transitive or intransitive: the car accelerates; X accelerates change. Expedite means actively make a process faster; intensify means become stronger, not necessarily faster. Raw 'accerlerate' corrected.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: accerlerate meaning
631	6303	5659	stickiness	/ˈstɪkinəs/	noun	The quality of being sticky; figuratively, the ability of a product, idea, website, habit, or experience to keep people engaged or be remembered.	adhesiveness; retention, staying power	The app's daily streak feature increases user stickiness.	Figurative business/product use concerns retention or staying power; context determines the specific metric.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: stickiness meaning
631	6304	5793	dogpile on someone	/ˈdɔːɡˌpaɪl ɑːn/	verb, informal	For many people to attack, criticize, mock, or pressure one person at the same time, especially online.	gang up on, pile on	Hundreds of accounts dogpiled on her after the clip went viral.	From literal dogpile = a heap of bodies. Online sense often concerns mass criticism/harassment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: dog piled meaning
631	6305	5946	ergative	/ˈɜːrɡətɪv/	adjective / noun, linguistics	Relating to a grammatical alignment in which the subject of a transitive verb is marked differently from the subject of an intransitive verb; in English grammar discussions, 'ergative verb' is also used for certain verbs that alternate between transitive and intransitive uses with the affected participant as object/subject.		Basque is often described as having ergative-absolutive alignment. / In 'I opened the door' and 'The door opened,' open shows the alternation often called ergative in English teaching materials.	Two related but distinct uses of ergative appear in linguistics. Typological ergative-absolutive alignment is not the same thing as the English 'ergative verb' alternation, also called causative/inchoative or labile alternation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define ergative
631	6306	5749	cozy up to someone	/ˌkoʊzi ˈʌp tə/	phrasal verb, informal	To become deliberately friendly or ingratiating toward someone, often to gain favor, influence, or advantage.	curry favor with, ingratiate oneself with	The lobbyist spent months cozying up to influential officials.	Can imply insincerity or strategic friendliness. Also literal cozy up = get physically comfortable/close.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: cozy up to meaning
631	6307	6512	benign / being benign	/bɪˈnaɪn/	adjective / phrase repair	Benign = harmless, gentle, mild, or not dangerous; medically, noncancerous/not malignant. Raw 'been benigning' is not standard English; likely intended 'being benign' or 'has been benign.'	harmless, mild, nonmalignant, innocuous, gentle	The tumor turned out to be benign. / What looked threatening was actually fairly benign. / If the intended sentence was 'it's been benign,' use benign as an adjective—not *benigning as a normal verb.	Benign is normally an adjective, not a productive verb. Medical benign ≠ guaranteed irrelevant: a benign condition can still cause problems depending on location/size. Contrast malignant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: been benigning
631	6308	5681	snatch up	/ˌsnætʃ ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To take, buy, obtain, or seize something quickly, especially before someone else can.	snap up, grab, seize	Fans snatched up the remaining tickets within minutes.	Often implies speed and limited availability; snap up is especially common for purchases/opportunities.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: snatch up meaning
631	6309	6408	vocal	/ˈvoʊkəl/	adjective / noun	Relating to the voice or singing; also, openly and forcefully expressing opinions or feelings. As a noun, especially in music, a sung part or recorded singing track.	outspoken, expressive; voice-related; singing part	She's been very vocal about wanting the adaptation to stay faithful. / The song's lead vocal is almost completely dry. / Vocal cords are more precisely called vocal folds.	'Vocal about X' = outspoken about it. Musical plural 'vocals' is very common. Do not confuse vocal = voice-related/outspoken with verbal = relating to words/language.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: vocal meaning
631	6310	6080	telltale sign	/ˌtelteɪl ˈsaɪn/	noun phrase	A characteristic clue that clearly reveals the presence, cause, identity, or true nature of something.	dead giveaway, revealing sign, giveaway, clear indication, red flag (often warning-specific)	The tiny bubbles are a telltale sign that the water is beginning to boil. / Avoiding every specific question can be a telltale sign that someone is hiding something.	Telltale sign is often diagnostic/revealing, not necessarily negative. Red flag specifically warns of danger/problem; giveaway emphasizes revealing a secret/answer.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: telltale sign meaning
632	6311	5649	feel foreign	/ˌfiːl ˈfɔːrən/	phrase	To feel unfamiliar, unnatural, strange, or outside one's usual experience.	feel unfamiliar, feel alien, feel strange	Speaking in such a formal style still feels foreign to me.	Foreign extends metaphorically from 'from another country' to something that feels unfamiliar or alien to one's habits or experience.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: something feels foreign meaing
632	6312	6619	lose sight of something	/ˌluːz ˈsaɪt əv/	idiom	Literally, to stop being able to see something; figuratively, to forget, neglect, or stop focusing on an important goal, fact, principle, or bigger picture.	forget, overlook, lose track of, neglect	We lost sight of the boat in the fog. / Don't lose sight of why you started learning English in the first place.	Figurative use is extremely common: lose sight of the goal/point/bigger picture. Opposite: keep sight of / keep X in sight.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: lose sight of somehting meaning
632	6313	6054	new DLC unlocked	/ˌnuː ˌdiː el ˈsiː ʌnˈlɑːkt/	meme / internet phrase	A joking gaming metaphor meaning that a new trait, problem, ability, obsession, chapter, or piece of life experience has suddenly appeared, as though downloadable game content had been unlocked.	new feature unlocked, new lore unlocked, achievement unlocked (related meme)	I started getting back pain at thirty—new DLC unlocked. / After learning that detail about the character, it felt like new lore DLC had unlocked.	DLC = downloadable content added to a game. In meme use, life/personality is treated like a game receiving expansion content. Usually playful, absurd, or self-deprecating.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: new DLC unlocked
632	6314	6202	have someone on a string	/ˌhæv ˌsʌmwʌn ɑːn ə ˈstrɪŋ/	idiom	To have strong control or influence over someone, often by manipulating their emotions, attention, hope, or dependence so they remain responsive to you.	have someone wrapped around one's finger, string someone along, control, manipulate	She knows he'll cancel any plan the moment she calls—she has him on a string. / The villain keeps both rivals on a string by feeding each of them different information.	Image of a puppet controlled by strings. 'String someone along' specifically means keep them waiting/hopeful without committing; 'have someone on a string' emphasizes control.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: have someone on a string
632	6315	6196	take a leap of faith	/ˌteɪk ə ˌliːp əv ˈfeɪθ/	idiom	To make a decision or take action despite uncertainty and without having enough evidence or guarantees, because you trust, hope, or believe it will work.	take a chance, make the jump, trust and go for it, take a risk	Moving abroad without knowing exactly how it would work was a leap of faith. / At some point you have to take a leap of faith and submit the application. / The relationship requires a leap of faith from both of them.	Often positive/hopeful but inherently acknowledges uncertainty. Not necessarily religious. 'Blind faith' can sound uncritical; leap of faith emphasizes the decision to cross an evidential/psychological gap.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: take a leap of faith
632	6316	6747	How many stops into the pub crawl are you?	/haʊ ˌmeni ˌstɑːps ˌɪntuː ðə ˈpʌb krɔːl ɑːr ju/	conversational question	A natural way to ask how far someone has progressed through a pub crawl, measured by venues/stops visited.	How far into the pub crawl are you?, how many pubs have you hit?	How many stops into the pub crawl are you? / We're three pubs in and taking a food break.	Also very natural: 'How far into the pub crawl are you?' and 'How many pubs have you been to so far?'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: how many stops are you in pub crawl
632	6317	6366	shunt	/ʃʌnt/	verb / noun	To move, divert, or redirect someone or something to another place, track, route, or function; medically, a shunt is a passage/device that diverts fluid or blood.	divert, redirect, reroute, sidetrack, transfer	The train was shunted onto a side track. / The company shunted the complaint from one department to another. / A surgical shunt can redirect fluid to relieve pressure.	Often implies being pushed aside or redirected rather than simply moved. BrE railway use is especially common. Figurative 'shunt someone aside' can suggest marginalization.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: shunt
632	6318	5611	klutzy	/ˈklʌtsi/	adjective, informal	Clumsy or awkward, especially in physical movement or handling things.	clumsy, awkward, bumbling	I'm so klutzy that I knocked over my glass twice.	Related noun: klutz, from Yiddish.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0112 | search: klutzy meaning
632	6319	6122	appraise vs apprise	/əˈpreɪz/ vs /əˈpraɪz/	contrast card	Appraise = assess or estimate the value, quality, condition, or significance of something. Apprise = inform someone about something.	assess/evaluate vs inform/notify	We hired someone to appraise the painting. / Please apprise me of any changes to the schedule. / I appraised the situation first, then apprised the team of the risk.	Classic confusable pair. Appraise usually takes an object being evaluated; apprise commonly appears as 'apprise someone of something.' Appraiser is a person who values property.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: appraise vs apprise
632	6320	6209	blue balls	/ˌbluː ˈbɔːlz/	vulgar slang / noun	A slang term for temporary aching or discomfort in the testicles associated with prolonged sexual arousal without orgasm; also used figuratively for frustrated anticipation.	epididymal hypertension (technical label sometimes used), sexual frustration; frustrated anticipation	He complained of 'blue balls' after making out for a long time. / People jokingly described the cliffhanger as cinematic blue balls because the promised reveal never came.	Vulgar/informal. The discomfort does not create an obligation for another person to provide sexual activity; it typically resolves on its own. Figurative use = prolonged buildup without payoff.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: get blue balls meaning
633	6321	6500	off the hook	/ˌɔːf ðə ˈhʊk/	idiom	Freed from responsibility, blame, obligation, punishment, or a difficult situation; no longer required to answer for or do something.	in the clear, excused, released, absolved, no longer responsible	Since Maya volunteered to present, I'm off the hook. / The new evidence doesn't necessarily let him off the hook. / Don't think one apology gets you off the hook for fixing the problem.	Common patterns: be off the hook; let/get someone off the hook. Originally associated with a fish escaping a hook; modern figurative use is extremely common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: off the hook meaning
633	6322	6026	size someone up	/ˌsaɪz ˌsʌmwʌn ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To quickly assess someone's character, abilities, intentions, threat level, or suitability, often by observing them.	assess, evaluate, appraise, take someone's measure, scope someone out	I could tell he was sizing me up the moment I walked into the room. / The interviewer spent the first few minutes sizing up how confident each candidate was.	Can sound strategic, wary, competitive, or judgmental. 'Check someone out' may mean look at/evaluate but can also imply attraction; size up emphasizes assessment.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: size up peopl meaning
633	6323	5884	paywalled	/ˈpeɪˌwɔːld/	adjective	Accessible only after paying, subscribing, or passing through a site's paywall.	behind a paywall, subscriber-only, paid-access	I found the article, but it's paywalled. / Half the sources I wanted to read were stuck behind a paywall.	Very common internet/media adjective. Paywall = access barrier requiring payment/subscription. Hard paywall allows no free access; metered/soft paywall allows some free content.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: something is paywalled meaning
633	6324	5657	doomscrolling	/ˈduːmˌskroʊlɪŋ/	noun	The habit of compulsively scrolling through a stream of distressing, alarming, or negative online news and content.	compulsive negative-news scrolling, compulsive bad-news consumption	I lost an hour to doomscrolling before bed. / I opened the app to check one headline and ended up doomscrolling until 2 a.m. / Doomscrolling can make a bad news cycle feel inescapable.	Also 'doomscroll' as a verb. Refers to compulsively consuming distressing/negative online content, especially news and social feeds, even when it worsens one's mood. Related 'doomsurfing' exists but is less common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: doomscrolling meaning
633	6325	6467	talk down to someone	/ˌtɔːk ˈdaʊn tə ˌsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb	To speak to someone in a condescending, patronizing way, as if they are less intelligent, mature, knowledgeable, or important than you.	patronize, condescend to, speak condescendingly to	I hate it when customer-service scripts talk down to people. / Explain it simply, but don't talk down to me.	Distinct from 'talk someone down' = persuade someone to calm down, lower a price, or come down from a dangerous place. Word order changes meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: talk down to someone meaning
633	6326	6703	slit-mouthed / mouth slit open	/ˌslɪt ˈmaʊðd/	translation / descriptive phrase	Having a mouth cut or split unusually wide; Japanese 口裂け is context-dependent and may be translated descriptively as slit-mouthed, a slit mouth, or a mouth slit/cut open.	slit-mouthed, split-mouthed, mouth cut open	Kuchisake-onna is commonly rendered 'the Slit-Mouthed Woman.' / The creature was drawn with a mouth slit unnaturally wide.	For 口裂け女, established English rendering is Slit-Mouthed Woman. For a generic injury/design, choose descriptive wording rather than assuming the folklore reference.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: 口裂け 英語で
633	6327	5933	give someone butterflies	/ˌɡɪv ˌsʌmwʌn ˈbʌtərˌflaɪz/	idiom	To make someone feel a fluttery nervous excitement in the stomach, often from attraction, anticipation, or anxiety.	make someone's heart flutter, make someone nervous/excited, give someone butterflies in their stomach	He still gives me butterflies even after all this time. / I always get butterflies right before I have to speak in front of a group.	Commonly romantic, but not exclusively. Get/have butterflies (in one's stomach) = experience the feeling; give someone butterflies = cause it. Can be pleasant excitement or nervous anxiety.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: give someone butterflies meaning
633	6328	6412	to oblivion	/tu əˈblɪviən/	intensifying phrase	To a state of complete destruction, disappearance, unconsciousness, forgetfulness, or extreme excess; used after verbs such as bomb, blast, drink, beat, grind, or consign depending on context.	into nothingness, completely, into unconsciousness, beyond recognition (contextual)	The base was bombed into oblivion. / He played the game until three in the morning and ground the same mission to oblivion. / The old scandal was eventually consigned to oblivion.	Oblivion literally/abstractly means being forgotten or unknown, or unconsciousness. 'Verb + into/to oblivion' is hyperbolic and productive; the exact result comes from the verb.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: to oblivion meaning
633	6329	6654	I can only do so much	/aɪ kən ˌoʊnli duː soʊ ˈmʌtʃ/	conversational phrase	There is a limit to how much I can accomplish, help, control, or compensate for given my time, ability, resources, or role.	there's only so much I can do, I have limits	I can help you practice, but I can only do so much—you still have to use the language yourself. / With two hours left, I can only do so much.	Often sets a boundary or explains limitations without saying 'I won't.' Tone can be resigned, defensive, realistic, or empathetic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: i can only do so much meaning
633	6330	6649	lucid	/ˈluːsɪd/	adjective	Clear and easy to understand; mentally clear and rational; in 'lucid dream,' aware that one is dreaming while the dream continues.	clear, coherent, intelligible, rational, clear-headed	She gave a lucid explanation of a complicated idea. / He was tired but still lucid. / I realized I was dreaming and briefly became lucid.	Major senses: clear expression; clear mental state. 'Lucid interval' has medical/legal uses. Lucid ≠ vivid, though lucid dreams can be vivid.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define lucid
634	6331	5557	whether to do something or not	/ˈweðər tə/	grammar pattern	Used when considering a choice between doing something and not doing it, or between alternative courses of action.	whether or not to, decide if	I haven't decided whether to go or not.	Whether can introduce alternatives; 'or not' may be omitted when the yes/no contrast is already clear.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: English Phrase: whether to (do something) or not | PhraseMix.com
634	6332	5771	prevalence	/ˈprevələns/	noun	The state of being widespread or common; in statistics/epidemiology, the proportion of a population having a condition at a given time or over a period.	commonness, frequency, widespread occurrence	The study measured the prevalence of the condition among adults.	Often used formally: prevalence of X; high/low prevalence. Distinct from incidence, which concerns new cases/events.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: prevalence define
634	6333	6419	give someone a mouthful	/ˌɡɪv ˌsʌmwʌn ə ˈmaʊθfʊl/	idiom, chiefly BrE informal	To angrily tell someone off, criticize them, or say a lot of harsh words to them.	give someone an earful, tell someone off, bawl someone out, give someone a piece of one's mind	When he finally arrived two hours late, she gave him a mouthful. / The driver leaned out the window and gave the cyclist a mouthful.	Chiefly British/Irish/Australian-style usage; 'give someone an earful' is more familiar in AmE. Distinct from literal a mouthful of food/drink and 'a mouthful' = long hard-to-say phrase.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: Give someone a mouthful
634	6334	6521	make the rounds	/ˌmeɪk ðə ˈraʊndz/	idiom	To circulate among people/places; to visit a series of people or locations; for news, rumors, images, jokes, etc. to spread widely.	circulate, go around, spread; visit in turn	The clip started making the rounds on social media. / The doctor made the rounds before breakfast. / I made the rounds at the party and talked to everyone for a few minutes.	Major senses: information circulates; a person visits a sequence of places/people. Medical 'rounds' is especially common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: make the rounds meaning
634	6335	5984	in one piece	/ɪn ˌwʌn ˈpiːs/	idiom	Safe and unharmed after danger or a difficult journey; also physically whole, not broken into separate pieces.	safe and sound, intact, unharmed, whole	Text me when you get home in one piece. / Somehow the fragile figure arrived in one piece despite the terrible packaging.	Raw 'in piece' normally needs one: in one piece. Compare 'piece by piece' and 'in pieces' = broken apart or, figuratively, emotionally devastated.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: in piece meaning
634	6336	6466	can't be bothered	/ˌkænt bi ˈbɑːðərd/	idiom, especially BrE informal	To be unwilling to make the effort to do something because it feels too troublesome, uninteresting, or not worth one's energy.	can't be arsed (vulgar BrE), don't feel like it, can't be fussed, won't make the effort	I know I should cook, but I can't be bothered tonight. / He couldn't be bothered to read the instructions before complaining.	Usually 'can't/couldn't be bothered to do X.' It often means lack of motivation rather than literal inability. Very natural BrE; widely understood elsewhere.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: i can't be bothered eanig
634	6337	6820	sift through	/ˌsɪft ˈθruː/	phrasal verb	To examine a large amount of material, information, evidence, files, etc. carefully in order to find what is useful or important; literally, pass material through a sieve.	sort through, comb through, go through, screen	We sifted through hundreds of bookmark searches to find useful candidates. / Detectives sifted through the evidence.	Literal sifting separates fine/coarse material; figurative sense implies patient filtering of a mass of information/items.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: sift through meaning
634	6338	6146	search vs search for	/sɜːrtʃ/ vs /ˈsɜːrtʃ fɔːr/	contrast / usage card	Search + place/person/system means examine it in order to find something; search for + target means try to find that target. Search can also be intransitive or take a database/web object.	look through vs look for	Police searched the house for evidence. / We searched for the missing file all morning. / I searched the website for the old review. / 'Search the answer' is usually unnatural if you mean 'look for the answer.'	Core frame: search [AREA] for [TARGET]. Compare look for X = seek X; look through X = examine X. In web usage, 'search Google/the database' is normal; 'search for X' names the query target.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: search vs search for
634	6339	6489	a total slog	/ə ˌtoʊtəl ˈslɑːɡ/	informal phrase	Something that is thoroughly tedious, exhausting, slow, or laborious to get through.	a grind, an ordeal, hard going, a chore	The middle ten episodes were a total slog for me. / Cleaning thousands of messy rows by hand would be a total slog.	Built from existing noun slog = long difficult tiring effort. 'Total' intensifies it conversationally; useful especially in reviews of games/books/shows/work.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: total slog meaning
634	6340	5921	phraseology	/ˌfreɪziˈɑːlədʒi/	noun	The particular choice, arrangement, or style of words and phrases used by a person, group, text, or field.	wording, phrasing, diction, formulation, turns of phrase	The meaning is fine, but the phraseology sounds oddly formal for casual conversation. / Legal phraseology can make a simple idea feel much harder than it is.	Useful distinction: wording = exact words; phrasing = how something is expressed; diction = choice/style of words, often literary; phraseology can refer broadly to characteristic expressions of a language/person/field.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: phraseology
635	6341	6127	harsh nose contour	/ˌhɑːrʃ ˈnoʊz ˌkɑːntʊr/	cosmetics phrase	Nose contour makeup with lines, shading, or contrast that looks too strong, sharp, obvious, or insufficiently blended rather than creating a subtle sculpted effect.	heavy nose contour, severe contour, unblended contour, strong contour	The lighting made her nose contour look much harsher on camera than it did in person. / If the contour lines are too dark and close together, the result can look harsh rather than sculpted.	Harsh describes visual effect, not a formal makeup technique. Existing contour card notes cosmetics generally; this preserves a useful beauty-review collocation and evaluative nuance.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: harsh nose contour
635	6342	6255	social script	/ˈsoʊʃəl skrɪpt/	noun phrase, sociology/psychology/everyday discourse	A learned or culturally shared expectation about how people are supposed to behave, speak, or sequence actions in a familiar social situation.	social norm, behavioral script, expected routine, cultural script	A first date comes with a social script about who asks questions, when to flirt, and what counts as progress. / Moving between cultures can make invisible social scripts suddenly obvious.	Useful for dating/culture discussions: scripts are not literal written rules and individuals can reject or improvise them. 'Norm' is broader; script emphasizes expected sequence/roles in a situation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: social script meaning
635	6343	6082	disarming	/dɪsˈɑːrmɪŋ/	adjective / participle	Literally removing weapons or the ability to attack; figuratively, reducing suspicion, hostility, or defensiveness through charm, openness, humor, vulnerability, etc.	charming, winning, reassuring, endearing; neutralizing, disarming	She has a disarming smile that makes even skeptical people relax. / His willingness to admit he was nervous was unexpectedly disarming. / Officers began disarming the militia.	Figurative disarming does not merely mean charming; it suggests lowering another person's guard. 'Disarmingly honest' = so candid that resistance/suspicion drops.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: disarming meaning
635	6344	6570	can you go one day without ...?	/kæn ju ˌɡoʊ wʌn ˈdeɪ wɪˌðaʊt/	rhetorical complaint pattern	A frustrated or teasing way to ask whether someone can refrain from a repeated behavior even for a single day; usually implies the speaker thinks they do it constantly.	do you have to keep...?, can you stop... for once?	Can you go one day without complaining? / Can you go one day without turning every conversation into an argument?	Usually rhetorical, not a literal request for information. Tone ranges from playful teasing to sharp criticism.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: can you go one day without complaining meaning
635	6345	5647	grim	/ɡrɪm/	adjective	Very serious, gloomy, harsh, unpleasant, or depressing; also stern or forbidding in appearance.	bleak, gloomy, dire, harsh, forbidding, stern	The latest economic forecast looks grim. / The movie paints a grim picture of life after the collapse. / She gave me a grim smile and went back to work.	Broad adjective: grim news/prospects/conditions = very bad; grim expression = stern/gloomy; grim determination = serious and resolute. 'Dire' focuses on severity/urgency; 'bleak' often adds hopelessness. The malformed search 'in a grim' normally requires a following noun.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define grim
635	6346	6205	NGL (not gonna lie)	/ˌen dʒiː ˈel/	internet/text slang	Initialism for 'not gonna lie,' used to introduce a candid opinion, admission, reaction, or sometimes simply to add conversational emphasis.	not gonna lie, honestly, to be fair (sometimes), I have to admit	NGL, that ending hit me much harder than I expected. / I was skeptical, but ngl, the food was great. / NGL can function almost like a discourse marker rather than a literal claim that one normally lies.	Very informal, common in texting/social media. Usually written ngl/NGL. Can signal frankness, soften an admission, or just add casual emphasis. Avoid in formal writing.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: ngl
635	6347	6673	opening act	/ˈoʊpənɪŋ ˌækt/	noun	A performer or performance that appears before the main/headlining act; figuratively, an earlier event that serves as a prelude to something bigger.	support act, opener, warm-up act; prelude	The opening act played for thirty minutes before the headliner. / The scandal was only the opening act of a much larger political crisis.	Raw 'openinng' repaired. In concerts/comedy, 'opener' is common. Figurative use frames the first event as setup for the main event.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: openinng act meaning
635	6348	6772	skittish	/ˈskɪtɪʃ/	adjective	Easily startled, nervous, wary, or unpredictable; originally/common with animals, especially horses, and figuratively with people, markets, investors, etc.	nervous, jumpy, edgy, wary, flighty	The horse became skittish around the traffic. / Investors are skittish after weeks of bad news.	Suggests readiness to react or bolt, not just generalized anxiety. Figurative 'skittish market/investors' is common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: skittish
635	6349	6644	a glimmer of hope	/ə ˌɡlɪmər əv ˈhoʊp/	idiom	A very small but noticeable sign or amount of hope in an otherwise discouraging situation.	ray of hope, faint hope, spark of hope	The final test result gave them a glimmer of hope. / Even one positive reply was a glimmer of hope after weeks of silence.	Glimmer = faint light, hence a small sign of something positive. Common 'glimmer of hope/recognition/understanding.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: glimmer of hope meaning
635	6350	6778	dry-ish humor	/ˈdraɪ ɪʃ ˈhjuːmər/	informal phrase	Humor that is somewhat dry: understated, restrained, deadpan, or delivered without obvious signals that a joke is being made.	somewhat dry humor, understated humor, deadpan-ish humor	His humor is dry-ish rather than fully deadpan—you can usually hear a little smile in his voice.	-ish deliberately softens categorization: 'kind of dry.' Dry humor often relies on understated delivery and incongruity rather than big emotional cues.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: dry-ish humor meaning
636	6351	5674	tic	/tɪk/	noun	A sudden, repetitive, involuntary movement or sound; figuratively, a habitual mannerism or recurring verbal/behavioral quirk.	involuntary movement; mannerism, habit	Saying 'you know' every few seconds had become a verbal tic.	Keep broad lexical sense. Existing 'verbal tic' from Batch 01 is a specific phrase; this base noun also covers physical/vocal tics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define tics
636	6352	6872	truck along / trucking along	/ˌtrʌk əˈlɔːŋ/	phrasal verb, informal	To continue moving, working, or progressing steadily, usually without dramatic speed; similar to 'chug along' or 'keep plugging away.'	chug along, keep going, plug along, carry on	The project is trucking along nicely now. / I wasn't moving fast, but I kept trucking along.	Informal, especially AmE. Often 'keep on truckin'' in older/pop-cultural usage. Raw 'trucking along' is not necessarily about literal trucks.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: trucking along meaning
636	6353	6126	hustle bro	/ˈhʌsəl broʊ/	internet slang / noun	A mocking label for a man who aggressively promotes hustle culture—constant work, entrepreneurship, optimization, wealth-building, discipline, and productivity as identity or moral virtue.	grindset bro, hustle-culture guru, entrepreneur bro (overlapping)	My feed suddenly filled up with hustle bros telling me that sleeping eight hours is a character flaw. / The video starts as business advice and gradually turns into full hustle-bro grindset rhetoric.	Usually pejorative/mocking. 'Bro' marks a stereotype, not literally a brother. Related to grindset, entrepreneur-influencer, manosphere/self-improvement ecosystems, but not identical to them.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: hustle-bro
636	6354	5616	complaisant	/kəmˈpleɪzənt/	adjective	Willing or eager to please others; obliging, sometimes excessively so.	obliging, accommodating, eager to please	He was so complaisant that he rarely challenged anyone's requests.	Do not confuse with complacent = self-satisfied and insufficiently concerned. The words are pronounced differently.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0117 | search: define complaisant
636	6355	6005	GRWM (get ready with me)	/ˌdʒiː ɑːr ˌdʌbəljuː ˈem/	initialism / social-media genre	Get Ready With Me: a social-media video/post in which someone talks to the audience while getting dressed, doing makeup/hair, or preparing for an event/day.	get-ready-with-me video, routine vlog	I put on a GRWM while I was making breakfast and ended up listening to the whole story. / Her GRWM is less about makeup than about chatting through what happened on her date.	Common on TikTok, YouTube, Instagram, etc. Often functions as a conversational/storytime format rather than purely a beauty tutorial.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: grwm
636	6356	6221	toot / blow one's own horn	/ˌtuːt wʌnz ˌoʊn ˈhɔːrn/; /ˌbloʊ wʌnz ˌoʊn ˈhɔːrn/	idiom	To praise, advertise, or boast about one's own achievements, abilities, or qualities.	brag, boast, sing one's own praises, self-promote	I don't mean to toot my own horn, but I finished the whole project in one day. / She's talented but uncomfortable blowing her own horn in interviews.	'Not to toot my own horn, but...' is a common humorous preface before self-praise. Toot is especially AmE; blow one's own trumpet is common BrE equivalent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: toot my own horn meaning
636	6357	6315	rampant	/ˈræmpənt/	adjective	Spreading, occurring, or existing unchecked and very widely, especially something undesirable such as corruption, disease, rumors, abuse, or speculation.	widespread, unchecked, rife, pervasive, out of control	Rumors were rampant after the announcement. / Corruption had become rampant throughout the organization. / Speculation is rampant whenever a show ends on a cliffhanger.	Usually negative and stronger than widespread: suggests lack of control. Common patterns: rampant corruption/speculation/inflation; X is rampant. Heraldry has a separate technical sense of an animal standing upright on hind legs.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: rampant
636	6358	5877	GP	/ˌdʒiː ˈpiː/	noun, chiefly British	General practitioner: a doctor who provides primary/general medical care and is usually the first point of contact for non-emergency health problems.	general practitioner, family doctor, primary-care doctor/physician	I couldn't get a GP appointment until Friday. / If the pain doesn't improve, you should probably speak to your GP.	In UK English GP is extremely common. U.S. speakers more often say primary-care doctor/physician or family doctor, though general practitioner is understood. GP can mean other things in specialist contexts, so context matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: GP
636	6359	6058	ROI (return on investment)	/ˌɑːr oʊ ˈaɪ/	initialism / noun	Return on investment: a measure of how much benefit, profit, or value an investment produces relative to its cost; figuratively, the payoff obtained for time or effort spent.	return, payoff, cost-effectiveness, value for money/time	The campaign had a strong ROI because sales increased far more than the ad spend. / Spending two hours polishing that tiny detail has terrible ROI for me.	In finance, ROI is commonly calculated as net gain relative to investment cost, though exact definitions vary. Casual figurative use is common for time/effort: 'low-ROI task.' Do not assume every organization calculates it identically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: roi meaning
636	6360	6275	Sisyphean task	/ˌsɪsəˈfiːən tæsk/	idiom-like literary phrase	A task requiring endless or repeatedly renewed effort while producing little lasting progress, like Sisyphus eternally pushing a boulder uphill only for it to roll back down.	endless task, futile labor, never-ending struggle, uphill battle	Trying to keep every inbox permanently at zero can feel like a Sisyphean task. / Fixing one bug only to create two more turned the cleanup into Sisyphean labor.	From the Greek myth of Sisyphus. 'Sisyphean' emphasizes repetitive, seemingly futile effort; an 'uphill battle' is difficult but can still clearly end in success.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: sisyphean task
637	6361	6704	miko / Shinto shrine maiden	/ˈmiːkoʊ/	noun / translation	A female attendant at a Shinto shrine who assists with rituals, sacred dance, purification, charms, shrine duties, etc.; commonly called miko in English, with 'shrine maiden' as an accessible translation.	shrine maiden, Shinto shrine attendant	The anime character is a miko who works at her family's Shinto shrine. / Visitors watched a miko perform a ceremonial dance.	Japanese 巫女. 'Shrine maiden' is conventional but can evoke fantasy/maiden imagery; miko is often preferable in Japan-specific contexts. Role/history vary by period and shrine.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: 巫女 英語で
637	6362	6300	jump through hoops	/ˌdʒʌmp θruː ˈhuːps/	idiom	To perform many difficult, annoying, arbitrary, or bureaucratic requirements in order to achieve something or satisfy someone.	go through hoops, navigate red tape, bend over backwards (related), meet endless requirements	I had to jump through five different hoops just to reset the account. / International paperwork can make you feel like you're constantly jumping through hoops.	From trained animals/circus performers jumping through hoops on command. Usually implies the requirements feel excessive or unreasonable, not merely that there are several normal steps.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: jump through hoops
637	6363	6208	MeToo / #MeToo as a verb	/ˌmiː ˈtuː/	proper-name-derived verb, informal	To publicly accuse or expose someone in connection with sexual harassment, assault, or misconduct in the cultural context of the #MeToo movement; sometimes used loosely or polemically.	accuse publicly of sexual misconduct, expose, call out (broader)	The commentator claimed the producer had been 'MeTooed' after multiple allegations became public. / Using 'MeToo' as a verb can compress very different allegations and processes into one loaded label.	Informal and politically/socially loaded. #MeToo is primarily a movement/hashtag; verbal uses such as 'get MeTooed' can sound dismissive, sensational, or imprecise. Prefer precise description of allegations when factual accuracy matters.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: metoo verb meaning
637	6364	6931	reefer	/ˈriːfər/	noun, slang / nautical	Slang, dated: a marijuana cigarette/joint, or marijuana. Nautical: a refrigerated ship/container/truck used to transport perishable goods.	joint, marijuana (dated slang); refrigerated container	The old movie uses 'reefer' as slang for marijuana. / The fruit was shipped overseas in refrigerated reefers.	Very context-dependent. Drug sense is strongly associated with older 20th-century slang ('Reefer Madness'). Logistics sense remains current industry vocabulary.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: reefer meaing
637	6365	6645	game of chicken	/ˌɡeɪm əv ˈtʃɪkɪn/	idiom / game-theory metaphor	A confrontation in which two sides keep escalating or refusing to yield, each hoping the other will back down first; if neither yields, both may suffer serious consequences.	brinkmanship, showdown, test of nerve	The two companies are playing a game of chicken over licensing fees. / In the classic driving image, two drivers head toward each other and the first to swerve is labeled 'chicken.'	Used in politics, negotiations, relationships, and game theory. Key structure: mutual danger + incentive to make the other side yield first.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: the game of chicken
637	6366	5888	flattening (figurative)	/ˈflætənɪŋ/	noun / gerund	The act of reducing complexity, depth, variation, hierarchy, or nuance so that different things are treated as simpler or more uniform than they really are.	simplification, leveling, reduction, homogenization	The adaptation's biggest problem is the flattening of morally complicated characters into heroes and villains. / Compressing the data caused some flattening of the differences between groups.	Literal flattening = making physically flat. Figurative criticism often means loss of nuance, dimensionality, contrast, or hierarchy.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: flattening meaning
637	6367	5597	get out of one's head	/ɡet ˌaʊt əv wʌnz ˈhed/	idiom-like phrase	To stop being trapped in one's own thoughts, overthinking, or self-conscious internal focus; in other contexts, to stop thinking about someone or something.	stop overthinking, get out of one's own thoughts	I needed to get out of my head and just enjoy the conversation.	Context matters: 'I can't get it out of my head' instead means one cannot stop thinking about it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: getting out of my head
637	6368	6906	court chic	/ˌkɔːrt ˈʃiːk/	context-dependent fashion phrase	A style or aesthetic associated with the elegance, dress, ornament, or visual codes of a royal/aristocratic court; not a single standardized fashion category.	courtly style, regal aesthetic, aristocratic chic	The costumes mix modern tailoring with a kind of court chic—ornate fabrics, jewelry, and rigid silhouettes.	Preserve exact search. 'Court' likely means royal court, but without source context the phrase is descriptive rather than a fixed technical term. Chic = stylish/elegant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: court chic
637	6369	6555	parade someone/something	/pəˈreɪd/	verb	To display, show, lead, or move someone/something publicly and conspicuously, often to impress, celebrate, shame, or make a point; sometimes critically, to show off.	display, show off, flaunt, march, exhibit	The company paraded its new executives in front of the press. / He kept parading his credentials as if they settled the argument. / Prisoners were paraded before the cameras.	Tone depends on object/context. 'Parade someone around' can imply treating a person as a trophy or spectacle. Literal parade = move ceremonially in procession.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: parade someone meaning
637	6370	6182	bust one's ass	/ˌbʌst wʌnz ˈæs/	vulgar idiom	To work extremely hard or make an intense effort; in other contexts, to fall hard and hurt oneself, or 'bust someone's ass' can mean punish/catch/defeat them.	work one's ass off, grind, work extremely hard, go all out	I busted my ass getting this finished before the deadline. / She busted her ass for years to build the business. / Be careful on the ice or you'll bust your ass.	Vulgar but common. Work-one's-ass-off sense is usually positive/self-emphatic. Object changes meaning: bust someone's ass may mean discipline, arrest, beat, or harass depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: bust my ass meaningi
638	6371	6448	concede defeat	/kənˌsiːd dɪˈfiːt/	verb collocation	To acknowledge that one has lost or can no longer succeed in a contest, struggle, argument, campaign, or effort.	admit defeat, surrender, give up, accept loss	After three failed attempts to repair it, I finally conceded defeat and called a technician. / The candidate conceded defeat after the results became clear.	More formal than 'give up' and emphasizes acknowledgment of loss. One can concede defeat without literally surrendering to an enemy; useful figuratively for stubborn tasks/problems.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: concede defeat meanign
638	6372	6702	countenance	/ˈkaʊntənəns/	noun / verb, formal	Noun: a person's face or facial expression, especially literary/formal. Verb: to accept, tolerate, approve of, or permit something, often in negatives.	face, visage, expression; tolerate, sanction, permit	His countenance remained calm. / The school will not countenance bullying of any kind.	Important polysemy: the verb is pronounced the same and often appears as 'will not countenance X.' More formal/literary than face.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: define countenance
638	6373	6727	pollinate	/ˈpɑːləˌneɪt/	verb	To transfer pollen to the reproductive part of a flower, enabling fertilization; figuratively, ideas can cross-pollinate between groups or fields.	fertilize (not exact), transfer pollen; cross-pollinate	Bees pollinate many flowering plants. / Conferences can help ideas cross-pollinate between different disciplines.	Pollination precedes fertilization; they are related but not identical biological processes. Nouns: pollination, pollinator.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: pollinate meaning
638	6374	6289	course correction	/ˈkɔːrs kəˌrekʃən/	noun	An adjustment made to bring a journey, project, strategy, behavior, model, or process back toward its intended direction or goal after drift or new information.	adjustment, recalibration, corrective change, change of course	The team made a small course correction after the first round of user feedback. / AI systems may need course corrections when evaluation reveals a recurring failure mode.	Originally/navigation metaphor: correct the course of a vessel/aircraft. Figuratively usually implies adjustment rather than total abandonment. 'Pivot' often suggests a larger strategic change.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: course correction AI
638	6375	6529	rep vs reps	/rep/; /reps/	usage contrast	Rep can mean reputation ('a bad rep'), representative ('sales rep'), or one repetition of an exercise. Reps is commonly the plural exercise repetitions and can also mean representatives. Context determines the sense.	reputation; representative; repetition(s)	He has a good rep in the industry. / Ask your sales rep. / I did three sets of ten reps. / You need more reps speaking spontaneously if you want the phrasing to become automatic.	Very polysemous abbreviation. In gym/skill-training talk, 'get your reps in' = accumulate repeated practice attempts. Don't confuse 'bad rep' (reputation) with 'bad rap' (unfair criticism/blame), though speakers sometimes blur them.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0120 | search: rep vs reps
638	6376	6929	provenance	/ˈprɑːvənəns/	noun	The origin, source, history, and chain of custody or derivation of an object, work, data item, claim, artifact, etc.	origin, source history, lineage, pedigree, chain of custody	The museum documented the painting's provenance. / We preserve each card's search provenance so we can remember why it entered the vocabulary list.	Especially art/archives/data/research. Data provenance records where information came from and how it was transformed. Distinct from provenience in some archaeological usage.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: provenance
638	6377	5550	bitch about someone/something	/ˈbɪtʃ əˌbaʊt/	verb phrase, informal/vulgar	To complain repeatedly or make unkind, critical remarks about someone or something.	complain about, gripe about, bad-mouth	They spent the whole lunch break bitching about their boss.	Raw search 'bitching someone' is not the usual standard pattern for this sense; normally bitch about someone/something. Distinguish bitch someone out = scold someone harshly.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: bitching someone meaning
638	6378	6218	PvP zone	/ˌpiː viː ˈpiː zoʊn/	gaming noun phrase	An area in a game world where player-versus-player combat is enabled, encouraged, or governed by special rules.	combat zone, contested zone, PvP area	Once you cross the bridge, you're in a PvP zone and other players can attack you. / I accidentally wandered into the PvP zone with a gathering build.	Game-specific mechanics vary: PvP may be automatic, opt-in, faction-based, full-loot, etc. Zone emphasizes geographic/area-based activation rather than a whole-game mode.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pvp zone meaning
638	6379	6769	no-frills	/ˌnoʊ ˈfrɪlz/	adjective	Simple, basic, and inexpensive or practical, without extra features, decoration, luxury, or unnecessary services.	basic, bare-bones, simple, stripped-down, utilitarian	We stayed at a no-frills hotel near the station. / I want a no-frills flashcard app that loads quickly.	Usually positive/neutral when simplicity/value is desired, but can imply austere/basic. Frills literally = decorative extras.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: no-frills meaning
638	6380	6584	hickey	/ˈhɪki/	noun, informal	A temporary bruise-like mark on the skin caused by sucking or kissing strongly, usually on the neck; also has a few unrelated regional/technical slang senses.	love bite (BrE), kiss mark	She wore a scarf to hide a hickey on her neck. / A hickey is basically a superficial bruise caused by broken tiny blood vessels under the skin.	Common informal AmE; BrE 'love bite.' Usually associated with romantic/sexual activity. Do not confuse with hiccup.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: hickey meaning
639	6381	6456	tug-of-war	/ˌtʌɡ əv ˈwɔːr/	noun	A contest in which two teams pull opposite ends of a rope; figuratively, a prolonged struggle between opposing people, forces, desires, or interests.	struggle, contest, power struggle, push-pull	We played tug-of-war at the school festival. / She felt a constant emotional tug-of-war between staying and leaving. / The negotiations became a tug-of-war over control.	Usually hyphenated as the noun. Figurative use often describes competing motives or power. 'Tug' itself = pull with force.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117, voca_0118 | search: tug of war
639	6382	6450	self-inflicted	/ˌself ɪnˈflɪktɪd/	adjective	Caused by one's own actions, choices, or behavior, especially harm, injury, difficulty, or suffering.	self-caused, brought on oneself, of one's own making	The injury was self-inflicted. / Missing the deadline was largely a self-inflicted problem—we kept changing the scope. / The phrase can describe literal injury or figurative trouble.	Common collocations: self-inflicted wound/injury/damage/problem/crisis. Literal self-injury contexts can be serious; figurative use is extremely common in commentary and analysis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: self-inflicted
639	6383	6040	motor	/ˈmoʊtər/	noun / adjective / verb	A machine that converts energy into motion; relating to bodily movement; as a verb, to travel by motor vehicle or move along quickly.	engine (overlapping), electric motor; movement-related; drive, travel	The fan uses a small electric motor. / The injury affected fine motor control in his hand. / We motored down the coast before sunset.	Motor vs engine: an engine often converts fuel/heat into mechanical power; an electric motor converts electrical energy into motion, though everyday usage overlaps. Existing motor skills card covers one adjective use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: motor meaning
639	6384	5677	try doing something vs try to do something	/traɪ/	grammar contrast	Try doing something means experiment with an action to see what happens; try to do something means make an effort or attempt to achieve it.	experiment with; attempt	Try restarting the app. / I tried to restart it, but the button wouldn't respond.	Applied to the raw search: 'try messing up something' would mean experiment with messing it up, while 'try to mess up something' means attempt to mess it up.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: try messing up something or to mess up something?
639	6385	6256	neurodivergent	/ˌnʊroʊdaɪˈvɜːrdʒənt/	adjective / noun-like identity term	Having a brain or cognitive style that differs from what is considered neurologically typical; commonly used for autism, ADHD, dyslexia, and other forms of neurological/cognitive difference, with boundaries varying by community and framework.	neurologically atypical (descriptive), ND (informal abbreviation)	The group was designed by and for neurodivergent students. / She describes herself as neurodivergent and says noisy open offices drain her quickly.	Originates in the neurodiversity framework and is not itself a medical diagnosis. Usage boundaries vary: some use it broadly for any meaningful neurological divergence; others use narrower community conventions. Neurotypical is the common contrast.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: neurodivergent meaning
639	6386	6549	give someone a backhanded compliment	/ˌbækˌhændɪd ˈkɑːmpləmənt/	phrase	To give praise that also contains, implies, or smuggles in an insult, criticism, stereotype, or demeaning assumption.	damn with faint praise, give a left-handed compliment, compliment-insult	'You're surprisingly articulate' can be a backhanded compliment because the praise implies low expectations. / Telling someone 'you look great for your age' may land as a backhanded compliment.	Existing backhanded card mentions the phrase; this preserves the productive construction with a recipient. Whether something feels backhanded depends on implication and context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: backhanded compliment to someone meaning
639	6387	6238	I'm hosed	/aɪm ˈhoʊzd/	informal slang	I'm in serious trouble, stuck, ruined, or unlikely to succeed because the situation has gone badly.	I'm screwed, I'm toast, I'm done for, I'm in trouble	If that backup is corrupted too, I'm hosed. / I missed the last train and my phone's at one percent—I'm completely hosed.	North American informal slang. Usually less vulgar than 'I'm fucked/screwed' but still casual. Hose can also mean cheat/overcharge or spray with a hose; context distinguishes them.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: I'm hosed meaning
639	6388	5986	province	/ˈprɑːvɪns/	noun	A major administrative region in some countries; figuratively/formally, a person's proper area of knowledge, responsibility, or activity.	region, administrative division; domain, sphere, remit	Ontario is a province of Canada. / Budget decisions are outside my province; I only handle the technical side.	Political meaning varies by country. Figurative 'the province of X' is formal: 'That question is the province of philosophy.' Do not assume province equals U.S. state in powers/status.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: province meaning
639	6389	6348	Caribbean	/ˌkærɪˈbiːən/; also /kəˈrɪbiən/	proper adjective / noun	Relating to the Caribbean Sea, its islands and surrounding coasts, or the peoples/cultures of that region; the Caribbean = the region itself.	West Indian (overlapping/historical-regional), Caribbean region	Piracy and privateering are strongly associated in popular imagination with the Caribbean. / Caribbean cultures are highly diverse in language, history, ethnicity, and colonial experience.	Both common English pronunciations are standard, with regional/speaker variation. The Caribbean is not culturally homogeneous; avoid treating it as one single island culture.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define caribbean
639	6390	6415	one-of-a-kind	/ˌwʌn əv ə ˈkaɪnd/	adjective / noun phrase	Unique; unlike anything or anyone else; having distinctive qualities that make it the only one of its type.	unique, singular, unparalleled, distinctive, sui generis (formal)	The handmade jacket is genuinely one-of-a-kind. / She has a one-of-a-kind voice that I can recognize immediately.	Hyphenate before a noun: a one-of-a-kind design. After be, hyphens are still common: the design is one-of-a-kind. Natural conceptual opposite of 'a dime a dozen.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: one-of-a-kind
640	6391	5905	annals	/ˈænəlz/	plural noun	Historical records arranged by year; more broadly, the recorded history of a field, place, institution, or human activity.	chronicles, records, history	The upset went down in the annals of sports history. / It may be one of the strangest production decisions in the annals of anime.	Usually plural. Common grand/formal phrase: in the annals of history / go down in the annals of X. Do not confuse annals /ˈænəlz/ with anals.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define annals
640	6392	6737	meddling	/ˈmedəlɪŋ/	noun / adjective	Unwanted or inappropriate interference in other people's affairs or in matters that are not one's responsibility.	interference, intrusion, nosiness, officiousness	She resented his constant meddling in her relationships. / The villain complains about 'meddling kids.'	Usually negative and interpersonal/social. Meddle in/with. Compared with tamper, meddle often concerns affairs/decisions; tamper more often objects, systems, evidence, packaging, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: define meddling
640	6393	6335	bracer	/ˈbreɪsər/	noun	A protective guard worn on the forearm, especially by archers to keep the bowstring from striking the arm; historically, also a stimulating drink or something that braces/strengthens.	arm guard, vambrace (not identical), archer's guard	The archer tightened the leather bracer on his forearm before shooting. / Fantasy costumes often use decorative bracers even when the design is not historically functional.	In archery, a bracer protects the inside forearm. A vambrace is armor for the forearm and is not exactly the same thing. Older 'a bracer' can mean a strengthening/stiff drink, but that sense is uncommon today.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: bracer meaning
640	6394	6753	unyielding exposure	/ʌnˌjiːldɪŋ ɪkˈspoʊʒər/	nonstandard / context-dependent phrase	A comprehensible but unusual phrase suggesting exposure that does not let up, relent, or give the learner relief—continuous, relentless exposure. More idiomatic choices are relentless, sustained, constant, or intensive exposure.	relentless exposure, sustained exposure, constant exposure, intensive exposure	Months of relentless exposure to fast conversational English changed what sounded 'too fast' to me. / 'Unyielding exposure' is understandable, but 'relentless exposure' is more idiomatic.	Preserve exact search while correcting naturalness. Unyielding usually modifies people, positions, pressure, surfaces, resistance—not exposure itself.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: unyielding exposure meaning
640	6395	6577	same with yesterday?	/ˌseɪm wɪð ˈjestərdeɪ/	usage / correction card	Raw 'same with yesterday' is context-dependent and often less natural than 'same as yesterday' when asking whether today's situation/order/schedule is identical to yesterday's. 'Same with yesterday' works only when extending a prior statement to yesterday as another case.	same as yesterday; the same goes for yesterday (contextual)	'Same order as yesterday?' is natural. / 'It rained Monday, and the same with yesterday' is possible but 'the same was true yesterday' sounds smoother.	Key contrast: same as X = comparison/equivalence; same with X = the same statement applies to X. Preserve the raw search because this is exactly the sort of preposition choice that causes uncertainty.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: same with yesterday meaning
640	6396	6383	menstruate	/ˈmenstruˌeɪt/	verb	To undergo menstruation: to discharge blood and tissue from the uterus as part of the menstrual cycle.	have a period, menstruate	Some people menstruate regularly while others have irregular cycles. / The medical article distinguishes ovulation from menstruation—they are related parts of the cycle but not the same event.	Neutral medical/biological verb. In everyday conversation, 'have/get one's period' is more common. Not everyone who menstruates identifies as a woman, and not every woman menstruates.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: menstruate meaning
640	6397	5773	deviation	/ˌdiːviˈeɪʃən/	noun	A departure or difference from an expected, normal, planned, or standard course, value, or behavior.	departure, divergence, variation	Any significant deviation from the plan must be reported.	Also technical/statistical: standard deviation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: define deviation
640	6398	6832	in the flesh	/ɪn ðə ˈfleʃ/	idiom	Physically present in person rather than seen in a photo, video, recording, imagination, or description.	in person, face-to-face, physically present	I'd only seen the painting online, so it was amazing to see it in the flesh. / After years of video calls, they finally met in the flesh.	Can refer to people or impressive objects/art seen physically. Slightly vivid/dramatic compared with ordinary 'in person.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: in the flesh meaning
640	6399	6914	morph / morphing	/mɔːrf/	verb / noun	To change gradually or smoothly in form, appearance, character, or function; in digital media, transform one image/shape into another through a continuous transition.	transform, change, evolve, shift, metamorphose	The conversation slowly morphed into an argument. / The animation shows one face morphing into another.	Informal/productive figurative verb. Often morph into X. Digital-image sense helped popularize modern usage.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: morphing meainig
640	6400	5514	burlesque	/bɜːrˈlesk/	noun / adjective / verb	A comic or exaggerated imitation of a serious style; also a stage-entertainment tradition combining comedy, theatricality, and often striptease.	parody, caricature, revue	The show was a playful burlesque of old Hollywood melodramas.	Two important senses: literary/theatrical parody, and the entertainment tradition now strongly associated with glamorous striptease.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: burlesque
641	6401	6303	own up to something	/ˌoʊn ˈʌp tuː/	phrasal verb	To admit that one did something wrong, embarrassing, or previously hidden, and accept that it is true.	admit, confess to, acknowledge, come clean about, fess up to	Who broke the mug? Just own up to it. / He finally owned up to lying about where he'd been. / She owned up to the error before anyone else noticed it.	Pattern: own up to + noun/gerund. 'Confess' can sound more serious/legal/religious; own up is conversational and strongly suggests taking responsibility.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: owning up to meaning
641	6402	6533	skirt around / skirt over an issue	/ˌskɜːrt əˈraʊnd/	verb phrase	To avoid dealing directly with a difficult, sensitive, or inconvenient subject; 'skirt around the issue' is standard. 'Skirt over' is less idiomatic and may blend skirt around with gloss over.	avoid, evade, sidestep, dance around, gloss over	He kept skirting around the real issue instead of answering the question. / If you mean 'treat it too briefly,' gloss over the issue is more natural than *skirt over it.	Useful correction card from raw 'skirt over.' Skirt = move along the edge of something, hence figuratively avoid the center/core. Standard collocations: skirt the issue, skirt around the issue.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: skirt over meaning
641	6403	6901	apex	/ˈeɪpeks/	noun	The highest point, peak, tip, or culmination of something; literally the topmost point of a shape/object and figuratively the highest level of achievement, power, development, etc.	peak, pinnacle, zenith, summit, acme	At the apex of his career, he was directing two major films at once. / The apex of the triangle points upward.	Often 'at the apex of.' In anatomy, apex can mean the pointed/end part of an organ, e.g. apex of the heart/lung. Figurative apex emphasizes highest point.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: apex meaning
641	6404	6804	go running your mouth	/ˌɡoʊ ˌrʌnɪŋ jər ˈmaʊθ/	idiom/slang, confrontational	To start talking too much, boasting, gossiping, insulting, revealing things, or speaking recklessly—especially when the speaker thinks you should have kept quiet.	shoot your mouth off, talk shit, blab, mouth off	Don't go running your mouth about something you don't understand. / He went running his mouth to everyone about the argument.	Confrontational and often threatening/accusatory in tone. 'Run your mouth' = talk recklessly/excessively; 'go running your mouth' adds a sense of setting off and doing it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: go runnning your mouth meaning
641	6405	6134	Pteranodon	/təˈrænədɑːn/	proper noun / genus name	A genus of large flying pterosaurs from the Late Cretaceous of North America, notable for a long cranial crest and lack of teeth.	pterosaur (broader), flying reptile (broad informal description)	Pteranodon was a pterosaur, not a dinosaur. / Reconstructions often emphasize the animal's long backward-pointing head crest.	Important distinction: pterosaurs are flying reptiles closely related to dinosaurs, but are not dinosaurs themselves. Pteranodon means roughly 'wing without tooth' and was toothless.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: PTERANODON Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster
641	6406	6845	cooked haters	/ˌkʊkt ˈheɪtərz/	internet slang / context-dependent phrase	A nonstandard slang combination likely meaning haters who are doomed, defeated, exposed, exhausted, or otherwise 'cooked'; exact nuance depends on source context.	doomed haters, defeated critics (contextual)	After the comeback, fans joked that the haters were cooked. / 'Cooked haters' is understandable meme language, but not a stable dictionary phrase.	Cooked is highly context-sensitive internet slang: doomed, finished, exhausted, mentally fried, exposed, etc. Preserve uncertainty rather than invent one exact source meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: cooked haters meaning
641	6407	6071	next thing you know	/ˌnekst ˌθɪŋ ju ˈnoʊ/	discourse / narrative phrase	Used to jump quickly to a surprising or rapid next development: before you realize it, suddenly the situation has changed.	before you know it, suddenly, and then, one thing led to another	We stopped for one drink, and next thing you know it was two in the morning. / I watched one episode, and next thing I knew I'd finished the whole season.	Tense often shifts: 'next thing I knew' when narrating past events; 'next thing you know' for vivid/general storytelling. Suggests rapid progression or omitted intermediate steps.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: next thing you know meaning
641	6408	6458	zany	/ˈzeɪni/	adjective / noun	Amusingly strange, wildly unconventional, absurd, or clownishly energetic; historically, a comic fool or clown.	wacky, goofy, madcap, eccentric, absurd, offbeat	The show has a zany sense of humor without becoming completely random. / He plays a zany inventor who solves every problem in the worst possible way.	Usually playful rather than harsh. 'Wacky' and 'goofy' are close; madcap suggests hectic comic energy. Historical noun sense connects to comic theatre.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define zany
641	6409	6664	denominate / denominated in	/dɪˈnɑːməˌneɪt/	verb	To express, designate, or issue an amount/value in a particular unit or currency; 'denominated in dollars' means stated/payable in dollars.	express in, state in, price in, designate	The bond is denominated in U.S. dollars. / Prices were denominated in euros. / The notes are issued in several denominations.	Especially finance/economics. Don't confuse denomination = religious subgroup, although both share naming/category sense historically.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: denominated meaning
641	6410	6330	commedia dell'arte	/kəˌmeɪdiə delˈɑːrteɪ/	noun, theatre/history	A form of Italian popular theatre that flourished from roughly the 16th to 18th centuries, featuring professional performers, improvised scenarios, masks, physical comedy, and recurring stock character types.	Italian improvised comedy, masked comedy, commedia	Harlequin, Pantalone, and Columbina are stock figures associated with commedia dell'arte. / The production borrows commedia-style masks, exaggerated movement, and comic archetypes.	Literally Italian 'comedy of the profession/art.' Performances were structured around scenarios and stock roles rather than being completely improvised from nothing. Influential on European comedy, pantomime, clowning, and character archetypes.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Commedia dell'arte
642	6411	6891	allow doing vs allow someone to do	/əˈlaʊ/	grammar contrast	Allow + object + to-infinitive is standard when naming who performs the action: 'allow users to edit.' Allow + gerund can describe a permitted activity generally: 'The rules don't allow smoking.'	permit doing; permit someone to do	The app allows users to switch modes. / The building doesn't allow smoking. / This setting allows editing without an account.	Do not say *allow someone doing* for the ordinary permission meaning. Passive: users are allowed to edit; smoking is allowed.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: allow doing something vs allow someone to do something
642	6412	6721	call the shots / be calling the shots	/ˌkɔːl ðə ˈʃɑːts/	idiom, informal	To be the person who makes the important decisions and controls what happens.	be in charge, run the show, call the tune, be in the driver's seat	She owns the company, but her investors are really calling the shots. / Once you're the director, you get to call the shots.	Informal control/authority idiom. Can imply actual power differs from official title.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: calling the shots meaning
642	6413	6046	horseplay	/ˈhɔːrsˌpleɪ/	noun	Rough, noisy, energetic play or fooling around that can become unsafe or disruptive.	roughhousing, fooling around, messing around, rowdy play	The teacher told them to stop the horseplay before someone got hurt. / What started as harmless horseplay ended with a broken lamp.	Usually uncountable. Roughhousing is very close and often physical; horseplay commonly appears in safety rules/workplace warnings: 'No horseplay.' Not related to horses literally.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define horseplay
642	6414	6347	privateer	/ˌpraɪvəˈtɪr/	noun / historical verb	A privately owned armed ship—or its commander/crew—authorized by a government through a commission such as a letter of marque to attack and capture enemy shipping during wartime.	licensed raider, corsair (overlapping), state-authorized sea raider	A privateer could legally attack enemy merchant ships under authorization from its government. / To the enemy, the distinction between privateer and pirate could feel rather academic.	Key distinction: pirate operates without state authorization; privateer is privately operated but officially commissioned against designated enemies. Legal status depended on period, jurisdiction, and commission.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define privateer
642	6415	6615	pruning	/ˈpruːnɪŋ/	noun / gerund	Cutting away unwanted branches/parts to improve growth or shape; figuratively, removing unnecessary elements; in computing/AI, eliminating branches, parameters, connections, or possibilities to simplify or speed a system.	trimming, cutting back, thinning, elimination	After pruning, the tree produced healthier growth. / We pruned redundant cards from the candidate list. / Model pruning can reduce computational cost by removing less useful parameters or connections.	Useful literal→technical metaphor. Exact technical method depends on field: decision trees, neural networks, search algorithms, gardening.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: what is pruning
642	6416	5739	testy	/ˈtesti/	adjective	Easily irritated, impatient, or quick to become annoyed.	irritable, touchy, cranky	He got testy when the same question was asked for the third time.	Often describes a temporary mood or interaction rather than a permanent personality.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: testy definition
642	6417	6173	rite of passage	/ˌraɪt əv ˈpæsɪdʒ/	noun phrase	A ceremony, event, ordeal, or experience marking a transition from one social status or life stage to another; figuratively, a common formative experience people in a group are expected to go through.	initiation, milestone, coming-of-age ritual, formative experience	The ceremony functions as a rite of passage into adulthood. / Getting hopelessly lost on your first solo trip can feel like a travel rite of passage.	Anthropological term associated with transitions such as birth, adulthood, marriage, death, initiation. Figurative everyday use is broader and often humorous. Rite = ritual; right/write are unrelated.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: rite of passage definition
642	6418	5601	cardinal direction	/ˌkɑːrdɪnəl dəˈrekʃən/	noun	One of the four primary compass directions: north, south, east, and west.	compass point, primary compass direction	The map marks each cardinal direction with a large letter. / North is a cardinal direction; northeast is an intercardinal direction. / The trail signs show all four cardinal directions.	The four cardinal directions are north, east, south, and west. Intercardinal (ordinal) directions lie between them: northeast, southeast, southwest, northwest. 'Cardinal' here means principal/fundamental, not the bird or church rank.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: cardinal direction meaning
642	6419	6311	sweeping	/ˈswiːpɪŋ/	adjective	Very broad, extensive, comprehensive, or far-reaching, often affecting many people/things or making a claim with little qualification; also the participle of sweep.	broad, wide-ranging, extensive, far-reaching, comprehensive	The law introduced sweeping changes to the tax system. / That's a sweeping claim to make from three anecdotes. / The review makes several sweeping judgments about an entire generation.	Common collocations: sweeping changes/reforms/powers/victory/generalization/statement. With claims/generalizations it often implies overbreadth; with reforms it can simply mean extensive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define sweeping
642	6420	6599	sweat bullets	/ˌswet ˈbʊləts/	idiom, informal	To be extremely nervous, anxious, frightened, or under intense pressure, often visibly sweating.	be extremely nervous, panic, be on edge	I was sweating bullets waiting for the interview result. / He started sweating bullets when the teacher asked to see his homework.	Hyperbolic, not literal. Common progressive: 'I was sweating bullets.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: sweating bullets meaning
643	6421	6253	indigent	/ˈɪndɪdʒənt/	adjective / noun, formal	Very poor and lacking the money or resources needed for basic necessities; as a noun, an indigent person.	impoverished, destitute, needy, poor	The program provides legal representation for indigent defendants. / The hospital historically served indigent patients who could not afford private care.	Formal and common in legal/administrative contexts, especially 'indigent defendant.' Stronger/more institutional than simply low-income. Do not confuse with Indigenous.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define indigent
643	6422	6000	passing	/ˈpæsɪŋ/	noun / adjective / gerund, context-dependent	The act of going by, transferring, or dying; temporary/brief as an adjective; in identity contexts, being perceived as belonging to a social category, sometimes one different from an assigned or known identity.	brief, fleeting; death/decease; being perceived as (identity context)	I only made a passing comment about the ending. / We were saddened by his passing. / In some identity discussions, 'passing' means being perceived by others as a member of a particular group.	Highly polysemous. Common: passing interest/remark = brief; someone's passing = death (euphemistic); passing grade = sufficient to pass; passing in race/sports; identity 'passing' has specific social histories and can be sensitive. Context is essential.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: define passing
643	6423	5564	acutely	/əˈkjuːtli/	adverb	To an intense, severe, or highly perceptive degree.	keenly, intensely, sharply, severely, extremely	She was acutely conscious of the awkward silence. / I became acutely aware of how different the two accents were. / He was acutely ill and needed immediate treatment.	Two useful clusters: (1) intensely/keenly aware or sensitive—acutely aware/conscious/embarrassed; (2) medically/severely—acutely ill, acute pain. It often signals high intensity rather than merely 'very.' Contrast chronically in medical duration.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: acutely definition
643	6424	6793	wily	/ˈwaɪli/	adjective	Clever at gaining an advantage through tricks, strategy, deception, or resourcefulness; crafty and hard to outmaneuver.	crafty, cunning, sly, shrewd, tricky	The wily negotiator never revealed his real limit. / A wily fox is a classic storybook image.	Often admiring and suspicious at once: intelligence plus craftiness. More literary/playful than simply clever.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: wily meaning
643	6425	6713	shore up	/ˌʃɔːr ˈʌp/	phrasal verb	To support, reinforce, strengthen, or prevent something from weakening, collapsing, or failing—literally with supports or figuratively with resources/actions.	reinforce, strengthen, bolster, prop up, support	The company raised cash to shore up its balance sheet. / The rewrite shores up the weak middle of the story. / Workers shored up the damaged wall.	From shore = prop/support with a brace. Figurative collocations: shore up finances, support, defenses, confidence, a position.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: shore up meaning
643	6426	6185	foul play	/ˌfaʊl ˈpleɪ/	noun phrase	Criminal, violent, dishonest, or suspicious wrongdoing, especially when investigating an unexplained death/disappearance; in sports, unfair or illegal play.	wrongdoing, crime, misconduct; cheating, dirty play	Police said there was no evidence of foul play in the disappearance. / Investigators began to suspect foul play after finding the door forced open. / The referee penalized the team for foul play.	Common investigative collocation: 'no foul play suspected.' It does not name a specific crime; it signals possible malicious/criminal involvement. Sports sense is more literal rule-breaking/unfair conduct.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define foul play
643	6427	5592	calisthenics	/ˌkælɪsˈθenɪks/	noun	Physical exercises using body weight and little or no equipment, often to develop strength, flexibility, and coordination.	bodyweight exercise	Push-ups, squats, and pull-ups are staples of calisthenics.	Usually treated as an uncountable/plural-form noun: do calisthenics.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: calisthenics meaning
643	6428	6762	Substack	/ˈsʌbˌstæk/	proper noun / platform name	An online publishing platform associated especially with subscription newsletters, essays, podcasts, and independent writers; by metonymy, 'a Substack' can mean someone's publication/newsletter hosted there.	newsletter platform, subscription newsletter (contextual)	She publishes a weekly essay on Substack. / I subscribed to his Substack after reading one free post.	Brand/proper noun. 'A Substack' is common conversational shorthand for a publication hosted on the platform, though the generic item is a newsletter/publication.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: substack meaning
643	6429	6213	weeaboo / weeb	/ˈwiːəˌbuː/; /wiːb/	noun, internet slang	A usually mocking label for a non-Japanese person perceived as obsessively idealizing Japanese culture, especially anime/manga, in an exaggerated, shallow, or identity-consuming way; 'weeb' is the common shortened form and can be self-deprecating.	weeb, obsessive Japanophile (pejorative), anime nerd (broader)	He jokingly calls himself a weeb because half his shelves are manga. / 'Weeaboo' is harsher when it implies fetishizing Japan rather than simply enjoying Japanese media.	Existing Master references the term but lacks a standalone card. Historically emerged from internet replacement/filter culture around an earlier slur. Modern 'weeb' ranges from affectionate self-label to insult; Japanophile is much more neutral.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: weeaboo
643	6430	6381	up to one's ears in something	/ˌʌp tə wʌnz ˈɪrz ɪn/	idiom	Extremely busy, deeply involved, or overwhelmed by a large amount of work, trouble, debt, paperwork, etc.	swamped, buried in, snowed under, overwhelmed, knee-deep in	I'm up to my ears in vocabulary cards this week. / By Friday she was up to her ears in paperwork. / They were up to their ears in debt.	Image: submerged so deeply that only the head/ears remain above the pile. Commonly negative/overwhelmed, though playful exaggeration is frequent. Usually 'up to my/your/his ears in X.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: up to my ears
644	6431	6285	raid / be raided	/reɪd/	noun / verb	A sudden attack, entry, or search carried out to seize, damage, arrest, steal, or obtain something; as a verb, to conduct such an action against a place/group or to take resources from it.	attack, storm, search, bust, plunder, loot	Police raided the warehouse before dawn. / The village was repeatedly raided for supplies. / In games, our guild raids the same boss every Friday.	Wide range: police raid, military raid, robbery raid, game raid, pantry/fridge raid. 'Be raided' focuses on the target. Gaming raid is a coordinated group encounter and may be semantically specialized.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: define raided
644	6432	6353	whale spout / blow	/ˈweɪl spaʊt/	noun phrase	The visible plume above a whale when it exhales forcefully through its blowhole; it consists mainly of warm moist breath condensing in cooler air, often mixed with seawater/mucus droplets.	whale blow, blowhole plume, exhalation plume	We spotted the whale first by its tall spout on the horizon. / Different whale species can have characteristically shaped blows, though distance and weather affect what you see.	Whales do not simply shoot stored seawater from their lungs. The visible 'spout' is primarily exhaled warm moist air condensing, plus droplets around the blowhole.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: whale spout meaning
644	6433	6043	stand out to someone	/ˌstænd ˈaʊt tə ˌsʌmwʌn/	phrasal verb pattern	To be especially noticeable, distinctive, memorable, or significant to a particular observer.	stick out to someone, catch someone's attention, strike someone, be noticeable	One line in the review really stood out to me. / What stands out to you most about the animation? / Her pronunciation immediately stood out to me as unusually natural.	Existing WID4376 notes this as the standard alternative to raw 'stock out to someone'; retained because this exact productive pattern is highly useful. 'Stand right out to me' simply intensifies it.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: stand right out to me meaning
644	6434	6364	raucous	/ˈrɔːkəs/	adjective	Very loud, rough, disorderly, and energetic, especially of voices, laughter, crowds, parties, or entertainment.	rowdy, boisterous, noisy, unruly, loud	A raucous crowd spilled out of the bar after the game. / The episode ends with a raucous party scene full of shouting and laughter.	Often combines loudness with rowdy energy. Can be positive ('raucous fun') or negative ('raucous disturbance'). Existing appearances as a synonym do not replace a standalone card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: raucous
644	6435	6418	time immemorial	/ˌtaɪm ˌɪməˈmɔːriəl/	idiom / noun phrase	The indefinitely remote past, beyond human memory or record; used especially in 'since/from time immemorial.'	time out of mind, since ancient times, beyond living memory	People have told stories around fires since time immemorial. / The village claimed the right had existed from time immemorial.	Immemorial literally means extending beyond memory. The fixed phrase 'time immemorial' is much more common than using immemorial alone. Often rhetorical rather than a literal historical claim.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define immemorial
644	6436	5641	project	/ˈprɑːdʒekt/ (n.); /prəˈdʒekt/ (v.)	noun / verb	A planned piece of work or undertaking; as a verb, to estimate or predict, to display outward, to cast an image, or to attribute one's own feelings to someone else.	undertaking; forecast, display, attribute	The team projected higher sales for the new project.	Stress changes in common AmE pronunciation: noun PRO-ject, verb pro-JECT. The verb is highly polysemous, including psychological projection.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: project
644	6437	5679	slide in the direction of something	/slaɪd ɪn ðə dəˈrekʃən əv/	phrase	To move gradually toward a physical direction, state, tendency, position, or outcome.	drift toward, move toward	The discussion slowly slid in the direction of politics.	Not a fixed idiom; slide adds a sense of smooth or gradual movement/change.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: slide in the direction meaning
644	6438	6669	hit the beat	/ˌhɪt ðə ˈbiːt/	music/dance phrase	To land a movement, note, accent, edit, or action exactly on the musical beat or rhythmic accent.	land on the beat, match the beat, strike on the beat	The dancer hits the beat perfectly with each shoulder movement. / The cut hits the beat right as the chorus starts.	Compositional but common in dance/music/editing talk. Contrast 'miss the beat' and 'keep the beat.' 'Beat' also has police/reporting/story senses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: hit the beat meaning
644	6439	5913	garish	/ˈɡerɪʃ/	adjective	Unpleasantly bright, showy, colorful, or ornate in a way that feels excessive or lacking in taste.	gaudy, flashy, loud, lurid, tacky	The hotel lobby was decorated in garish gold and purple. / I like colorful animation, but that scene's neon palette was so garish that it distracted me from the characters.	Garish is usually negative. Gaudy emphasizes tasteless showiness; lurid can suggest shockingly vivid color or sensational content; flashy can be neutral/positive depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: garish meaning
644	6440	6470	have an inflated sense of self	/hæv ən ɪnˌfleɪtɪd ˌsens əv ˈself/	phrase	To have an exaggerated view of one's own importance, ability, status, attractiveness, virtue, or uniqueness.	have an inflated ego, be self-important, have an exaggerated self-image, think too highly of oneself	Social media can encourage some people to develop an inflated sense of self. / He has an inflated sense of his own importance and assumes every disagreement is about him.	Not a clinical diagnosis. 'Sense of self' can be neutral; inflated marks excessive self-evaluation. Related: grandiose self-image, ego inflation.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: people have an inflated sense of self meaning
645	6441	5694	opposite	/ˈɑːpəzɪt/	adjective / noun / preposition	Completely different or contrary in a relevant respect; also a person or thing on the other side or at the other extreme.	contrary, reverse, inverse	Her reaction was the exact opposite of what I expected.	Broad core word useful across opposite direction, opposite effect, the opposite of, and sit opposite someone.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: opposite meaning
645	6442	6088	Knickerbocker glory	/ˌnɪkərˌbɑːkər ˈɡlɔːri/	noun, British	A tall layered ice-cream sundae, traditionally served in a long glass with fruit, sauces/syrups, cream, and other toppings.	ice-cream sundae, parfait-like dessert	I ordered a Knickerbocker glory mostly because I'd heard the name in British TV. / The dessert arrived in a tall glass layered with ice cream, fruit, sauce, and whipped cream.	Classic British dessert; recipes vary. The name is culturally distinctive and sounds old-fashioned/retro. Not simply any sundae, though sundae is the closest broad category.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: knickerbocker glory meaning
645	6443	6840	report doing something	/rɪˈpɔːrt ˈduːɪŋ/	grammar / reporting-verb pattern	To state or say that one did/experienced something, using report + gerund when the subject of report is also the understood subject of the -ing action.	say one did, state having done, report that one did	Several users reported seeing the same error. / She reported feeling dizzy after the ride.	Common pattern: report doing/having done something; also report that + clause. Not normally 'report to do' for this meaning.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: report doing meaning
645	6444	6751	depth with a few voices, breadth with different ones	/depθ wɪð ə ˌfjuː ˈvɔɪsɪz bredθ wɪð ˌdɪfrənt wʌnz/	learning principle / coined formulation	A listening-learning strategy: repeatedly listen to a small number of familiar speakers for deep adaptation and detailed comprehension, while also rotating through many different speakers for robust generalization across voices/accents/styles.	deep repeated exposure + broad speaker variability	For listening practice, I want depth with a few voices and breadth with different ones: learn a handful of speakers deeply, but keep testing myself on unfamiliar voices too.	Preserve Jin's/search-source formulation as a useful conceptual contrast. 'Depth' supports adaptation to individual idiolects; 'breadth' counters overfitting and builds tolerance for speaker variability.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: Depth with a few voices, and breadth with different ones
645	6445	6937	pink cloud / pink-clouding	/ˌpɪŋk ˈklaʊd/	recovery slang	A period of intense optimism, euphoria, confidence, or relief sometimes experienced early in recovery from addiction, when life may feel unusually hopeful and problems seem easier than they are.	early-recovery euphoria, honeymoon phase (partial)	He felt amazing during the first weeks of sobriety, but his counselor warned him about the 'pink cloud.' / Pink-clouding can make someone underestimate the work of long-term recovery.	Recovery-community term, not a formal diagnosis and not experienced by everyone. Often discussed as temporary; avoid treating later difficulty as inevitable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: pink cloud meaing
645	6446	6486	stoppage time	/ˈstɑːpɪdʒ ˌtaɪm/	noun, football/soccer	Extra time added at the end of a half to compensate for time lost to substitutions, injuries, delays, goal celebrations, video reviews, and other stoppages.	added time, injury time	They scored the winner in the fifth minute of stoppage time. / The board showed seven minutes of added time.	Football/soccer term. Not the same as extra time, which is an additional period played in some knockout matches after regulation ends tied.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: stoppage time meaning
645	6447	6317	squawk	/skwɔːk/	verb / noun	To make a loud, harsh cry like a bird; figuratively, to complain loudly or noisily. As a noun, the cry or a loud complaint.	screech, cry, shriek; complain, gripe, protest	The parrot squawked every time someone entered the room. / Customers started squawking about the new fee. / One angry squawk from the bird woke everyone up.	Bird sense is primary. Human complaint sense is informal and can sound dismissive. In aviation, 'squawk' also means transmit/set a transponder code: 'squawk 7700' for an emergency.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116, voca_0118 | search: squawk
645	6448	5666	qualm	/kwɑːm/	noun	An uneasy feeling of doubt, guilt, or concern about whether something is right or wise.	misgiving, scruple, reservation, compunction, doubt	She had no qualms about rejecting the offer. / I have a few qualms about giving the app that much access. / He accepted the deal without a qualm.	Most common in plural: 'have qualms about + noun/gerund,' 'no qualms about doing.' A qualm is a moral/practical uneasiness, not simply uncertainty. 'Scruple' leans moral; 'reservation' is broader and often milder.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: define qualms
645	6449	6672	being sigma	/ˌbiːɪŋ ˈsɪɡmə/	internet slang / meme phrase	Behaving in a way associated with the 'sigma' meme archetype: independent, self-contained, cool, dominant, unfazed, or performatively unconcerned with social approval—often used ironically.	acting like a sigma, sigma behavior, lone-wolf meme behavior	He ignored the drama and walked away; the comments called it 'being sigma.' / 'Being sigma' is often more of a joke than a serious personality category.	Existing sigma card defines the label. This preserves the productive phrase 'being sigma.' Meaning is meme-driven and unstable; frequently ironic/parodic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: being sigma meaning
645	6450	6477	you're such a handful	/jər ˌsʌtʃ ə ˈhændfʊl/	idiom / conversational phrase	You are difficult to manage, control, supervise, or keep up with—often because you are energetic, mischievous, demanding, unpredictable, or emotionally intense.	hard to handle, a lot to deal with, demanding, mischievous	That puppy is adorable, but he's such a handful. / 'You're such a handful,' she said, half annoyed and half amused.	Can be affectionate/playful or genuinely critical depending on tone. Distinct from 'a handful of people' = a small number, covered in Batch21.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: you're such a handful meaning
646	6451	6403	learn by heart / rote memorization	/ˌlɜːrn baɪ ˈhɑːrt/; /ˌroʊt ˌmemərəˈzeɪʃən/	contrast / learning vocabulary	Learn by heart = memorize something so thoroughly that you can reproduce it from memory, often word for word. Rote memorization = memorizing through repetition, often with little emphasis on understanding.	memorize, commit to memory; learn by rote, drill	We had to learn the poem by heart for class. / Flashcards can become rote memorization if you repeat definitions without learning how the words actually behave in context. / I know the dialogue by heart.	They overlap but focus differently: by heart describes the result (known from memory); by rote describes the method (mechanical repetition). Rote can be critical when contrasted with understanding, but repetition itself is not inherently useless.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: learning by heart by rote memorazation
646	6452	5930	offer prayers	/ˌɔːfər ˈprerz/	verb phrase	To pray for someone or something, or formally present prayers as an act of worship, remembrance, gratitude, or supplication.	pray, say prayers, send prayers, pray for	People gathered to offer prayers for the victims. / At the shrine, visitors offered prayers for good health and success.	Offer prayers sounds more formal/ceremonial than simply pray. In religious contexts 'offer' conceptualizes the prayer as something presented to God/deity. 'Thoughts and prayers' is a separate modern phrase and can be sincere or criticized as formulaic.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: offer prayers meaning
646	6453	5891	oncologist	/ɑːnˈkɑːlədʒɪst/	noun	A doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer.	cancer specialist, oncology specialist	Her GP referred her to an oncologist for further tests. / The oncologist explained the treatment options and what to expect from each one.	Oncology = study/treatment of cancer. Medical oncologists, surgical oncologists, and radiation oncologists have different roles.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0113 | search: oncologist
646	6454	6878	setback	/ˈsetˌbæk/	noun	A problem, reversal, delay, or difficulty that interrupts progress or makes success harder, usually without necessarily ending the effort.	reversal, obstacle, hitch, blow, delay	Losing a week of work was a setback, but the project recovered. / She treated the rejection as a temporary setback.	Usually implies progress can resume. Stronger 'defeat' may imply losing; setback is often temporary/partial.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: define setbacks
646	6455	5687	I have a feeling (that...)	/aɪ hæv ə ˈfiːlɪŋ/	phrase	Used to express an intuition, impression, or expectation that something is true or will happen, without claiming certainty.	I suspect, I have a hunch	I have a feeling that you're going to love it.	A feeling here means intuition, not necessarily emotion.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: I have a feeling that you love it meaning
646	6456	5809	on the nose	/ˌɑːn ðə ˈnoʊz/	idiom / adjective phrase	Too obvious, direct, literal, or unsubtle, especially in writing, symbolism, dialogue, or interpretation; in other contexts, exactly correct.	overly obvious, unsubtle; exactly right	The symbolism works, but the dialogue is a little too on the nose.	Raw phrase 'be a little more on the nose with it' means make the intended point more explicit/obvious. Context distinguishes 'too obvious' from 'exactly right.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: be a little more on the nose with it
646	6457	6361	admonitory	/ədˈmɑːnəˌtɔːri/	adjective, formal	Giving or expressing a warning, caution, reproof, or moral advice; intended to admonish.	warning, cautionary, reproving, admonishing	She gave him an admonitory look when he reached for his phone during the ceremony. / The story ends on an admonitory note about pride and greed.	Formal/literary and much less common than cautionary or warning. Can combine warning + mild reproof rather than pure anger.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define admonitory
646	6458	6616	empathetic	/ˌempəˈθetɪk/	adjective	Showing or involving empathy—the ability/willingness to understand and share or imaginatively enter another person's feelings or perspective.	empathic, understanding, compassionate, sensitive	She was empathetic without pretending she knew exactly how he felt. / An empathetic response can acknowledge someone's frustration before offering advice.	Empathic is a common variant, especially technical/professional. Empathetic ≠ sympathetic exactly: sympathy is concern/pity; empathy emphasizes understanding another perspective/feeling.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: define empathetic
646	6459	6890	long to be	/ˌlɔːŋ tə ˈbiː/	verb phrase	To strongly desire or yearn to be in a particular state, role, place, relationship, etc.	yearn to be, ache to be, deeply wish to be	As a teenager, he longed to be taken seriously. / She longed to be somewhere quiet.	Long as verb is literary/emotional compared with want. Pattern: long to do; long for + noun/person; long for someone to do.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: long to be meaning
646	6460	6219	wither away	/ˌwɪðər əˈweɪ/	phrasal verb	To gradually become weaker, smaller, thinner, less vigorous, or less significant until nearly disappearing; literally of plants/bodies and figuratively of institutions, hopes, skills, etc.	waste away, shrivel, fade away, dwindle, atrophy	Without water, the plant slowly withered away. / The old organization withered away after its funding disappeared. / Skills can wither away if you never use them.	Wither alone often means dry/shrivel or lose vitality; away emphasizes gradual disappearance/decline. 'Waste away' is especially associated with bodily emaciation; atrophy can be literal or figurative and more technical.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: wither away meaning
647	6461	6461	officiate	/əˈfɪʃiˌeɪt/	verb	To perform the official duties at a ceremony, especially a wedding or religious service; or to act as an official/referee in a sporting event.	preside over, conduct, referee, umpire	Her uncle officiated at the wedding. / A licensed celebrant officiated the ceremony. / She has officiated college basketball games for ten years.	Common patterns: officiate at a wedding/service; officiate a wedding (also common); officiate a game. Officiant = person conducting a ceremony.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: define officiated
647	6462	5979	inflection pattern	/ɪnˈflekʃən ˌpætərn/	noun, linguistics	The systematic way a word changes form to express grammatical information such as tense, number, person, case, gender, or comparison.	inflectional paradigm, conjugation pattern, declension pattern, morphological pattern	Sing–sang–sung follows a different inflection pattern from regular verbs like walk–walked–walked. / Learning common inflection patterns makes unfamiliar forms easier to recognize.	Existing inflection WID374 is primarily illustrated with voice/pitch. Here the target is grammatical morphology. Conjugation is usually for verbs; declension for nouns/adjectives/pronouns in languages with case; inflection is the umbrella term.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: inflection patterns
647	6463	5598	take the wheel	/ˌteɪk ðə ˈwiːl/	idiom	To take control of driving; figuratively, to assume control, leadership, or responsibility for a situation.	take control, take charge	I was exhausted, so she took the wheel for the last hour of the drive.	Literal driving sense readily extends metaphorically to taking control.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: take the wheel meaning
647	6464	5604	bunting	/ˈbʌntɪŋ/	noun	Decorative flags or strips of material hung for celebrations; also the name of several small seed-eating birds.	festive flags, decorative pennants	Red, white, and blue bunting hung across the porch.	Two common unrelated senses: decoration and a type/group of bird. Context from nearby ornithology/cardinal searches may make the bird sense especially relevant.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: bunting
647	6465	6108	black coffee vs coffee black	/ˌblæk ˈkɔːfi/ vs /ˌkɔːfi ˈblæk/	contrast / usage card	Black coffee is the normal noun phrase for coffee served without milk or cream. 'Coffee black' is chiefly used after a verb or in ordering patterns such as 'I take/drink my coffee black,' where black functions as a complement describing how the coffee is served.	coffee without milk/cream, plain coffee	I usually drink black coffee in the morning. / I take my coffee black, no sugar. / 'A black coffee, please' is natural; 'a coffee black' is generally not the normal standalone noun phrase.	Black does not necessarily mean no sugar; it specifically means no milk/cream, though speakers may infer plain coffee contextually. Compare 'I like my steak rare': adjective complement after object.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: coffee black and black coffee
647	6466	6445	metal ingot	/ˈmetəl ˌɪŋɡət/	noun	A solid block, bar, or standardized mass of metal cast into a shape convenient for storage, transport, further processing, or trade.	metal bar, billet (not exact), cast metal block	The smelter poured molten metal into molds to make ingots. / In crafting games, iron ingots are a standard material for making tools and armor.	Ingot is a broad cast-metal form; billet, bloom, slab, and bar have more specific industrial meanings/process histories. Gaming often simplifies these distinctions.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: metal ingot
647	6467	6862	get someone off their feet	/ˌɡet ˌsʌmwʌn ɔːf ðer ˈfiːt/	context-dependent phrase	To cause or help someone to stop standing or to lift/remove them from a standing position; exact meaning depends on context. For romantic enchantment, the fixed idiom is 'sweep someone off their feet.'	sit someone down, lift someone, get someone to rest (contextual)	After standing all day, we finally got her off her feet and into a chair. / If you mean 'charm someone completely,' say 'sweep someone off their feet.'	Raw search likely contrasts several constructions. Existing cards cover knock/lift someone off their feet; 'get someone off their feet' itself is compositional and context-sensitive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: get someone of their feet
647	6468	6571	fanfiction / fan fiction	/ˈfænˌfɪkʃən/	noun	Fiction written by fans using characters, settings, worlds, relationships, or other elements from an existing work or real-person fandom rather than an entirely original canon.	fanfic, fic	She started writing fanfiction after finishing the series. / The pairing is popular in fanfic even though it never becomes canon.	Both fanfiction and fan fiction occur; fanfic/fic are common community terms. Fan works vary enormously in genre, quality, canon-compliance, and transformative intent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: faniction meaning
647	6469	6553	get hitched	/ˌɡet ˈhɪtʃt/	idiom, informal	To get married.	get married, tie the knot, wed	They got hitched in Vegas. / After dating for six years, they finally decided to get hitched.	Informal, often lighthearted. Hitch literally means fasten/connect, which motivates the marriage metaphor. Do not confuse with hitch a ride.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: got hitched meaning
647	6470	6730	don't hate—let her cook	/ˌdoʊnt ˈheɪt ˌlet hər ˈkʊk/	Gen-Z / internet slang combination	A playful defense meaning stop judging or criticizing her and give her room to continue—she may prove the idea/style/action works.	don't judge her; give her a chance; let her do her thing	The outfit looks wild, but don't hate—let her cook. / 'She's changing the whole ending?' 'Don't hate. Let her cook.'	Combines two independently productive slang expressions. Not a single fixed proverb, but a natural meme-like sequence.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: don't hate let her cook gen-z slang
648	6471	5587	come into	/ˌkʌm ˈɪntuː/	phrasal verb	To enter; to acquire or inherit something; or to become involved or relevant in a situation.	enter, inherit, acquire, become involved	She came into some money unexpectedly, but money didn't come into the decision.	Several common senses: physically enter; inherit/acquire; 'where does X come into it?' = what role does X play?		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: coming intomeaning
648	6472	5658	wherewithal	/ˈwerwɪðˌɔːl/	noun	The money, resources, ability, or means needed to do something.	means, resources, capacity	We had the idea but not the financial wherewithal to build it.	Common pattern: have the wherewithal to do something. Often refers to money, but can be broader.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: wherewithal meaning
648	6473	5685	everything is on the table	/ˌevriθɪŋ ɪz ɑːn ðə ˈteɪbəl/	idiom	All options, proposals, or possibilities are open for consideration or negotiation.	all options are open, nothing is ruled out	At this stage, everything is on the table.	On the table can also mean formally presented for discussion; this expression emphasizes unrestricted options.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: everything is on the table meaning
648	6474	6117	chrysanthemum	/krɪˈsænθəməm/	noun	A flowering plant of the genus Chrysanthemum, widely cultivated for colorful daisy-like flowers; culturally significant in many countries, especially in East Asia.	mum (especially AmE), chrysanthemum flower	The garden is full of chrysanthemums in yellow, white, and deep red. / In Japan, the chrysanthemum is strongly associated with the Imperial House, while funeral associations are prominent in parts of Europe.	Often shortened to mum in North American gardening. Cultural symbolism varies greatly by country, so a chrysanthemum can suggest longevity/nobility, autumn, mourning, etc. depending on context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: chrysanthemum meaning
648	6475	6145	enculturation	/enˌkʌltʃəˈreɪʃən/	noun, anthropology/social science	The process by which a person learns and internalizes the norms, values, behaviors, language, symbols, and practices of the culture in which they live.	cultural socialization, cultural learning, socialization (broader)	Children learn turn-taking, politeness, food customs, and countless unspoken rules through enculturation. / Moving between Japan and the U.S. can make normally invisible processes of enculturation much easier to notice.	Enculturation = learning one's surrounding culture, often from childhood. Acculturation usually concerns changes through contact with another culture; assimilation is stronger movement toward absorption into a dominant culture.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: define enculturation
648	6476	6313	grumpy-bumpy	/ˈɡrʌmpi ˌbʌmpi/	playful rhyming expression / nonce phrase	A cute, childish, or teasing rhyming extension of 'grumpy,' used for someone in a bad mood or to make grumpiness sound less serious; not a standard fixed expression.	grumpy, cranky, grumpypants (playful), grouchy	Who's a grumpy-bumpy this morning? / The phrase sounds like playful baby-talk or an improvised nickname rather than ordinary adult vocabulary.	Preserve source flavor. English often creates playful reduplicative/rhyming forms (grumpy-bumpy, silly-billy, easy-peasy). Meaning comes mainly from grumpy; bumpy supplies rhyme/playfulness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: grumpy bumpy meaning
648	6477	5699	familiarize oneself with something	/fəˈmɪliəˌraɪz/	verb pattern	To learn about something or become accustomed to it so that it is no longer unfamiliar.	acquaint oneself with, get to know	Please familiarize yourself with the emergency procedures.	Most common pattern is familiarize oneself with something; also familiarize someone with something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: familiarize meaning
648	6478	6795	riff / riffing on something	/rɪf/	verb / noun, informal	To improvise freely around an idea, theme, joke, musical phrase, or another person's contribution; figuratively, to develop playful variations rather than follow a fixed script.	improvise, play off, elaborate on, spin variations on	We spent an hour riffing on possible titles. / The movie riffs on old monster films without directly parodying one specific scene.	From music, especially repeated/improvised phrases. In conversation/writing, 'riff on X' suggests loose, creative development.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: riffing meaning
648	6479	6230	pickle-nosed	/ˈpɪkəl noʊzd/	playful/nonstandard adjective	A comic or nonce description meaning having a nose likened to a pickle in shape, size, color, or general ridiculous appearance; not a standard fixed English expression.	pickle-shaped nose, funny-looking nose (contextual)	The cartoon gives the villain a huge, pickle-nosed profile. / 'Pickle-nosed' sounds like an improvised visual insult rather than an established adjective.	Preserve the exact searched form because its value is likely source-specific humor. Interpret compositionally: pickle + nosed. Compare hook-nosed, button-nosed, red-nosed, etc.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: pickle nosed meaning
648	6480	6788	arbiter	/ˈɑːrbɪtər/	noun	A person or authority regarded as having the power to judge, decide, settle, or determine what is correct, acceptable, tasteful, etc.	judge, authority, referee, adjudicator	No single dictionary is the ultimate arbiter of how people are allowed to speak. / The court became the final arbiter of the dispute.	Often figurative: arbiter of taste/style/truth. Related arbitrate, arbitration, but an arbiter need not always be a formal arbitrator.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: arbiter meaning
649	6481	5719	get down to it	/ɡet ˌdaʊn tə ˈɪt/	idiom	To stop delaying or preparing and begin seriously doing the task or dealing with the matter.	get started, get to work, get down to business	We've talked enough—let's get down to it.	Related existing down to card includes get down to work, but this pronoun chunk is a common conversational command/transition.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0111 | search: get down to it meaning
649	6482	5552	the crap out of something/someone	/ðə ˈkræp aʊt əv/	intensifying phrase, informal	Used after many forceful verbs to mean doing the action to an extreme or very intense degree.	the hell out of, the heck out of	The storm scared the crap out of me, and the crew had to pump the water out of the boat like crazy.	Pattern: verb + the crap out of + object, e.g. beat/scare/kick/clean the crap out of something. Raw search 'crap out of it' likely came from this intensifier construction.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110, voca_0111, voca_0112 | search: crap out of it meaning
649	6483	6680	discourse focus	/ˈdɪskɔːrs ˌfoʊkəs/	linguistics term	The part of an utterance or discourse that is highlighted as especially informative, contrastive, new, or currently central to the communicative point.	information focus, focus, focal information	In 'JOHN bought the book,' stress on John can mark discourse focus when contrasting who bought it. / Prosody helps listeners identify discourse focus.	Exact definitions differ across information-structure theories. Focus interacts with givenness, topic, contrast, syntax, and prosody; not merely 'what the conversation is about.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: discourse focus
649	6484	5944	-turvy	/ˈtɜːrvi/	bound element / playful word formation	An element best known from topsy-turvy, sometimes echoed in playful formations to suggest inversion, disorder, or reversal; not normally a productive standalone suffix in standard English.	upside-down, reversed, chaotic (idea)	Topsy-turvy is the established word; a writer might coin a playful X-turvy formation for comic effect, but readers would hear it as an echo of topsy-turvy.	Existing topsy-turvy WID5144 covers the standard adjective. Raw search 'something-turvy' suggests curiosity about the reusable-looking second half. Treat novel -turvy formations as wordplay, not a normal suffix you can freely attach to anything.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: something-turvy
649	6485	6916	LARPer	/ˈlɑːrpər/	noun	A person who participates in LARP (live-action role-playing), physically portraying a character in an improvised or structured fictional setting; online, sometimes used derisively for someone seen as performatively pretending to be a type of person.	live-action role-player; poser/pretender (slang extension)	The park was full of LARPers in fantasy costumes. / Online critics called him a 'revolutionary LARPer,' implying the persona was performative.	Capitals vary: LARPer/larper. Internet extension can be insulting and often means 'someone acting out an identity/ideology without genuine stakes.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: larper meaning
649	6486	5812	fearmongering / fear-mongering	/ˈfɪrˌmʌŋɡərɪŋ/	noun / adjective	The deliberate use or spread of frightening claims, warnings, or rumors to influence people's attitudes or behavior, often by exaggerating a threat.	scaremongering, alarmism	Critics accused the campaign of fearmongering about immigration.	Often strongly evaluative: calling something fearmongering implies the fear is being exploited or exaggerated.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: fear-mongering
649	6487	6603	amplitude	/ˈæmpləˌtuːd/	noun	The maximum extent or magnitude of a varying quantity measured from its central/equilibrium value; in waves, related to the size/strength of the oscillation.	magnitude, extent, wave height (informal/contextual)	A louder sound generally corresponds to greater pressure-wave amplitude, though perceived loudness is more complex. / The signal's amplitude decreased.	Technical word in physics/math/electronics. Do not simply equate amplitude with 'volume.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: amplitude meaning
649	6488	5957	indoctrinate	/ɪnˈdɑːktrəˌneɪt/	verb	To teach someone a set of beliefs or ideology in a way intended to secure unquestioning acceptance, especially by discouraging criticism or alternatives.	brainwash, condition, inculcate, propagandize	Critics accused the group of indoctrinating children from an early age. / Teaching a viewpoint is not automatically indoctrination; the accusation usually implies that questioning and competing views are suppressed.	Strongly negative in modern use. Inculcate can simply mean instill ideas/habits through repetition and need not imply manipulation; brainwash is stronger and suggests coercive psychological control.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: indoctrinate
649	6489	6113	put one's feelings on the line	/ˌpʊt wʌnz ˈfiːlɪŋz ɑːn ðə ˌlaɪn/	figurative phrase	To openly expose one's genuine feelings in a situation where they may be rejected, hurt, embarrassed, or otherwise emotionally at risk.	make oneself emotionally vulnerable, risk rejection, open one's heart, put oneself out there	Telling her how he felt meant putting his feelings on the line. / A sincere review can feel like putting your feelings on the line when the work means a lot to you.	Creative extension of 'put X on the line' = risk X. More idiomatic alternatives often focus on the person: put yourself out there, make yourself vulnerable. Preserve exact emotional framing because it is intuitive/productive.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: putting your feelings on the line
649	6490	5777	amendment	/əˈmendmənt/	noun	A change, correction, or addition made to a document, law, agreement, or proposal; in U.S. law, a formal change/addition to the Constitution.	revision, modification, addition	The committee proposed an amendment to the bill.	Raw misspelling amendament normalized. Common pattern: amendment to something.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0112 | search: amendament
650	6491	6706	in-house	/ˌɪn ˈhaʊs/	adjective / adverb	Done, produced, handled, or employed within an organization by its own staff/resources rather than outsourced to an outside provider.	internal, internally, on staff, not outsourced	We built the tool in-house instead of hiring an agency. / She joined the company's in-house legal team.	Hyphenated before noun ('in-house team'); often written in house adverbially too, though in-house is common in both uses.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: in-house meaning
650	6492	6294	defuse	/diːˈfjuːz/	verb	Literally, to remove the fuse from an explosive and make it safe; figuratively, to reduce the tension, danger, anger, or explosiveness of a situation.	de-escalate, calm, neutralize, disarm, ease tension	The technician defused the bomb. / A joke helped defuse the tension in the room. / She tried to defuse the argument before it turned personal.	Commonly confused with diffuse. DEFUSE = make less dangerous/tense (think remove a fuse); DIFFUSE = spread out. Figurative 'defuse a situation/tension/crisis' is extremely common.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: defuse meaning
650	6493	6564	CP (ambiguous abbreviation)	/ˌsiː ˈpiː/	initialism, highly context-dependent	An abbreviation with many meanings. Online, 'CP' can be used as an abbreviation for child pornography/child sexual abuse material; current professional/safety terminology generally prefers CSAM. In other contexts CP may mean cerebral palsy, checkpoint, combat power, Canadian Pacific, etc.	CSAM (for abusive-material sense); context-dependent acronym	If someone says 'making CP' in an online safety/criminal context, they may be referring to creating child sexual abuse material. / In a medical context, CP may instead mean cerebral palsy.	Raw search is ambiguous and potentially refers to abusive illegal material. Learn the recognition meaning only; CSAM = child sexual abuse material is clearer and avoids framing abuse as pornography. Do not infer this expansion without context.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0118 | search: making cp meaning
650	6494	6003	ocular	/ˈɑːkjələr/	adjective	Relating to the eye or vision.	eye-related, visual, ophthalmic (technical)	The doctor checked her ocular movement and vision. / The creature's oversized ocular structures make it look almost insectlike.	Ocular is formal/technical. Ophthalmic is especially medical/anatomical and often relates to ophthalmology; visual can refer more broadly to sight or things seen.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: ocular
650	6495	6332	a spanner in the gears	/ə ˌspænər ɪn ðə ˈɡɪrz/	creative idiom variant	A problem, obstruction, or unexpected complication that disrupts a system or process; a blended/creative mechanical variant of established 'throw a spanner in the works.'	obstacle, snag, wrench in the gears, disruption	The licensing issue put a spanner in the gears just as the project was gaining momentum. / One incompatible file format can throw a spanner into the gears of an otherwise smooth workflow.	Less fixed than 'spanner in the works.' The image is mechanically intuitive: a tool caught in gears stops the machinery. AmE often uses 'throw a wrench in the works.'		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: spanner in the gears meaning
650	6496	6764	a swirl of something	/ə ˈswɜːrl əv/	figurative noun phrase	A moving, mixed, or confusing mass of things—colors, emotions, thoughts, rumors, activity, etc.—evoking substances rotating together in a swirl.	mixture, whirl, jumble, rush	I had a swirl of conflicting emotions after the finale. / The painting is a swirl of blue and gold.	Productive metaphor, not one fixed idiom. 'A swirl of emotions/thoughts/colors/activity' suggests mixture plus motion or instability.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: swirl of thing meaning
650	6497	5919	epic	/ˈepɪk/	noun / adjective	Originally, a long narrative work about heroic or legendary events; as an adjective, grand in scale or scope. Informally, extremely impressive, exciting, or memorable.	grand, monumental, sweeping; awesome, amazing (informal)	The film is an epic spanning three generations. / The final battle has an epic sense of scale. / That road trip was epic—we ended up seeing three national parks in four days.	Informal 'epic!' was especially prominent in internet/youth slang and can sound playful. Epic scale ≠ necessarily high quality: a movie can be epic in scope but poorly made.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: epic
650	6498	5994	tourist (slang: outsider/dilettante)	/ˈtʊrɪst/	noun, slang / derogatory contextual	A person who participates superficially or temporarily in a scene, subculture, political cause, hobby, or risky environment without deep commitment or understanding.	dilettante, poser, casual, outsider, fair-weather participant	Hard-core fans dismissed the newcomers as tourists who would disappear once the trend cooled off. / Calling someone a 'tourist' in a community usually questions their commitment or understanding, not their literal travel status.	Slang meaning is context-dependent and often gatekeeping/derogatory. Similar to poser, but tourist emphasizes temporary/surface-level participation rather than necessarily pretending to be something one isn't.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: tourists meaning slang
650	6499	5939	common wealth vs commonwealth	/ˌkɑːmən ˈwelθ/; /ˈkɑːmənˌwelθ/	contrast card	Common wealth as two ordinary words means wealth/resources shared by or belonging to a community; commonwealth as one word is a political community/state or an organized association of states.	shared/public wealth vs political community	The debate was about protecting the common wealth of natural resources. / Massachusetts is officially a commonwealth. / The Commonwealth of Nations includes many countries with historical ties to the British Empire.	Historically commonwealth literally developed from 'common weal' = public good/welfare. Modern Commonwealth with capital C often means the Commonwealth of Nations. Do not assume the spaced phrase and political term are interchangeable.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: common wealth meaning
650	6500	6835	Visine	/vaɪˈziːn/	proper noun / pronunciation	A brand name associated with over-the-counter eye drops; pronounced approximately 'vie-ZEEN' in American English.	eye drops (generic category)	He asked how to pronounce Visine: vie-ZEEN. / She reached for eye drops after staring at the screen all day.	Brand name, not the generic word for eye drops. Preserve mainly as pronunciation/search-memory card.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0121 | search: visine pronunciation
651	6501	6662	you're my rock	/jʊr maɪ ˈrɑːk/	metaphor / affectionate phrase	You are a steady, dependable source of emotional support, strength, stability, or reassurance for me.	my anchor, my support, someone I can rely on	You've been my rock through this whole mess. / She called her sister her rock because she could always rely on her.	Warm relational metaphor. Can be romantic, familial, or platonic. Rock suggests stability/strength, not emotional coldness.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119 | search: you're my rock meaning
651	6502	6406	Portuguese man o' war	/ˌpɔːrtʃəˈɡiːz ˌmæn əv ˈwɔːr/	noun	A floating marine colonial organism with a blue-purple gas-filled float and long venomous tentacles; Japanese カツオノエボシ. It resembles a jellyfish but is a siphonophore colony rather than a single jellyfish.	bluebottle (related regional name), Physalia physalis	A Portuguese man o' war washed ashore, so we kept well away from its tentacles. / カツオノエボシ is usually called a Portuguese man o' war in English.	Not a true jellyfish: each visible animal is a colony of specialized zooids working together. Detached/dead tentacles can still sting. Scientific name commonly Physalia physalis.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: カツオノエボシ 英語で
651	6503	6160	egghead	/ˈeɡˌhed/	noun, informal	An intellectual, academic, expert, or highly cerebral person, especially one stereotyped as impractical, nerdy, or detached from ordinary concerns.	intellectual, brainiac, nerd, academic (contextual)	The panel was full of policy eggheads arguing over tiny methodological differences. / He jokingly calls himself an anime-history egghead.	Can be teasing, admiring, or dismissive. Historically prominent in U.S. political/cultural discourse. More about intellectualism than simply being intelligent.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: Egghead
651	6504	6627	the only OP thing about him is his sigma grindset	/ði ˌoʊnli ˌoʊ ˈpiː θɪŋ əˌbaʊt hɪm ɪz hɪz ˌsɪɡmə ˈɡraɪndset/	meme / slang sentence	A joking line meaning the person is not overpowered in ability; the only 'overpowered' trait they have is an exaggerated sigma-style work/hustle mentality.	his only overpowered trait is his hustle mentality	The only OP thing about him is his sigma grindset—he's not stronger than anyone; he just never stops grinding.	OP = overpowered in gaming/anime slang. Sigma grindset combines sigma-male meme culture with relentless hustle. Preserve full sentence because the joke depends on the collision of gaming and internet-masculinity slang.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0119, voca_0120 | search: The only OP thing about him is his sigma grindset. meaning
651	6505	5519	hiatus	/haɪˈeɪtəs/	noun	A pause or break in an activity, series, process, or period of work.	break, pause, interruption	The band returned after a three-year hiatus.	Often used for shows, careers, projects, publications, or relationships that temporarily stop and may later resume.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: define hiatus
651	6506	6514	I didn't think you had it in you	/aɪ ˌdɪdənt θɪŋk ju hæd ɪt ɪn ˈjuː/	idiom / conversational phrase	I did not expect you to have the courage, ability, toughness, creativity, cruelty, or other quality needed to do what you just did.	I didn't know you were capable of that, you surprised me	You stood up to him? I didn't think you had it in you. / That was a genuinely moving speech—I didn't know you had it in you.	'Have it in you to do X' = possess the capacity/nerve/quality to do it. Can be praise, surprise, teasing, or insult depending on what 'it' refers to.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: i did not think you had it in you meaing
651	6507	6819	chip off	/ˌtʃɪp ˈɔːf/	phrasal verb	To break, cut, knock, or remove a small piece from a larger object; or for a small piece to break away.	break off, flake off, knock off, splinter off	A corner chipped off the mug. / I chipped some ice off the windshield.	Distinguish 'a chip off the old block' = a person resembling a parent. Physical chip off emphasizes a small piece.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: chip off meaning
651	6508	6725	two plus two equals five	/ˌtuː plʌs ˌtuː ˌiːkwəlz ˈfaɪv/	rhetorical / cultural phrase	A deliberately false equation used to symbolize accepting an obvious falsehood, especially under ideological, social, or authoritarian pressure; also used jokingly for bad reasoning.	obvious falsehood, forced belief, absurd conclusion	The essay uses 'two plus two equals five' as an image of being pressured to deny obvious reality. / If your assumptions are wrong, your argument can make two plus two equal five.	Famously associated with George Orwell's *Nineteen Eighty-Four*, though the phrase predates the novel. Contrast 2+2=4 as objective/basic truth.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: two plus two equals five meaning
651	6509	5600	otherworldly	/ˌʌðərˈwɜːrldli/	adjective	Strange, beautiful, eerie, or ethereal in a way that seems to belong to another world rather than ordinary reality.	ethereal, unearthly, supernatural	The blue light gave the cave an otherworldly atmosphere.	Broader and more idiomatic than extraterrestrial when the point is a surreal, eerie, or ethereal impression rather than literal aliens.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0110 | search: otherworldly
651	6510	6787	top-down pronouncement	/ˌtɑːp daʊn prəˈnaʊnsmənt/	noun phrase	An authoritative statement, rule, or decision issued from leadership to people below, with little participation, consultation, or bottom-up input.	decree from above, executive directive, edict, top-down declaration	The change felt like a top-down pronouncement rather than a decision the team had helped shape.	Pronouncement is a formal/authoritative declaration. 'Top-down' highlights direction of power/process; phrase often carries criticism of centralized decision-making.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: top-down pronouncement meaning
652	6511	6827	beckoning	/ˈbekənɪŋ/	adjective / participle	Calling, signaling, or inviting someone to come nearer or proceed; figuratively, attractively inviting or promising.	inviting, alluring, calling, summoning	She made a beckoning gesture with her hand. / The open road looked strangely beckoning.	From beckon. Can be literal gesture/call or figurative attraction. Existing base 'beckon' card covers the verb; this preserves adjectival/participial use.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0120 | search: beckoning meaning
652	6512	6269	quit / drop the charade	/ˌkwɪt ðə ʃəˈreɪd/	idiom-like phrase	To stop maintaining a false appearance, pretense, performance, or obviously insincere story and acknowledge what is really happening.	drop the act, stop pretending, end the pretense, cut the bullshit (vulgar)	Come on, quit the charade—we both know you don't actually want to go. / After months of pretending the launch was on schedule, management finally dropped the charade.	Charade here = elaborate or transparent pretense, not the guessing game. 'Drop the act' is more conversational; quit the charade can sound dramatic/accusatory.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0116 | search: quit the charade
652	6513	6343	growl / growled	/ɡraʊl/	verb / noun	To make a low, rough, threatening sound like an angry animal; of a person, to speak in a low harsh angry voice; of machines/thunder, to make a similar rumbling sound.	snarl, rumble, grumble; say harshly	The dog growled when I reached toward its food. / 'Get out,' he growled. / Thunder growled in the distance.	Dialogue tag 'growled' conveys low, rough hostility, not literally animal speech. Snarl can be sharper/more openly aggressive; grumble is usually complaint rather than threat.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0117 | search: growled
652	6514	6086	hold someone's arms behind their back	/ˌhoʊld ˌsʌmwʌnz ˈɑːrmz bɪˌhaɪnd ðer ˈbæk/	literal action phrase	To restrain someone by keeping their arms positioned behind their back, limiting their movement.	restrain, pin someone's arms back, hold someone	One guard held his arms behind his back while the other searched him. / In the scene, she twists his wrist and holds his arm behind his back.	Not a fixed idiom. Grammar: hold someone's arms behind their back; singular if one arm. Raw searches 'one's arms behind backs/on their backs' normalized. Can describe restraint in fights, arrests, or physical staging.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0115 | search: hold one's arms behind backs meaning
652	6515	5895	satiated	/ˈseɪʃiˌeɪtɪd/	adjective	Fully satisfied, especially after eating or after a desire/appetite has been fulfilled.	satisfied, full, sated, replete	After the huge meal, I was completely satiated. / The ending gave me answers, but I still didn't feel emotionally satiated.	More formal than full/satisfied. Sated is a close, often more natural synonym. Figurative: satiate curiosity, appetite for drama, desire for revenge.		voca_0110–0121 | folders: voca_0114 | search: satiated meaning
652	6516	6971	circumvent vs qualify vs hedge vs carefully frame	/ˌsɝːkəmˈvent/; /ˈkwɑːləfaɪ/; /hedʒ/	usage contrast	To circumvent is to avoid or get around a rule, obstacle, or issue; to qualify a statement is to limit or modify its force; to hedge is to make a claim less direct or certain; to carefully frame something is to deliberately choose how it is presented.	circumvent = get around; qualify = limit/modify; hedge = soften uncertainty; frame = present strategically	He tried to circumvent the restriction by using another account. / I'd qualify that claim: it applies mainly to beginners. / She hedged by saying 'in some cases' and 'I think.' / He carefully framed the criticism as a suggestion.	These are not interchangeable. Qualifying changes the scope or strength of a claim; hedging signals uncertainty/caution; framing concerns presentation and perspective; circumventing means bypassing something rather than wording it cautiously.	academic / contrast	voca_0122
652	6517	6988	end-to-end encryption	/ˌend tə ˌend ɪnˈkrɪpʃən/	noun	A communication-security design in which data is encrypted so that only the communicating endpoints possess the means to read the message content, rather than intermediate service providers being able to read it in transit.	E2EE, encrypted endpoint-to-endpoint communication	The messaging app uses end-to-end encryption, so the service is not supposed to be able to read the message content in transit.	Usually abbreviated E2EE. It protects message content between endpoints, but it does not automatically hide all metadata, protect a compromised device, or guarantee the identity of the person at the other endpoint.	technology / security	voca_0122
652	6518	6951	excommunicate / excommunicated	/ˌekskəˈmjuːnəkeɪt/; /ˌekskəˈmjuːnəkeɪtɪd/	verb	To officially exclude a person from participation in the sacraments or membership privileges of a church; figuratively, to cast someone out of a community or group.	expel, exclude, cast out, ostracize	He was excommunicated from the church after openly rejecting its doctrine. / Jokingly, they said he'd be excommunicated from the fandom for admitting he hated the finale.	The literal sense is specifically religious and formal. Figurative uses are often humorous or exaggerated. Do not confuse with communicate.	religion / figurative	voca_0122
652	6519	6949	get down to the nitty-gritty	/ɡet daʊn tə ðə ˈnɪti ˈɡrɪti/	idiom	To stop dealing in generalities and begin discussing or working on the most important practical details of something.	get down to business, get into the details, get to the heart of the matter	We've talked about the overall plan; now let's get down to the nitty-gritty. / Once we got down to the nitty-gritty of the contract, several problems appeared.	Usually takes of + noun: get down to the nitty-gritty of the budget/deal/problem. More specific than simply get down to it: nitty-gritty emphasizes the concrete, often messy details.	idiom	voca_0122
652	6520	6950	work for the man	/wɝːk fər ðə mæn/	idiom, informal	To work for a large company, institution, government, or other conventional authority, especially as viewed from an anti-establishment or independent-minded perspective.	work for a corporation, work for the establishment, have a conventional job	After years of freelancing, he joked that he was finally going to work for the man. / She left her corporate job because she was tired of working for the man.	the Man can mean institutional authority or the establishment. The phrase often carries joking, countercultural, or mildly rebellious overtones; it does not simply mean 'work for a male boss.'	culture / idiom	voca_0122
653	6521	6979	epistemic standards	/ˌepɪˈstemɪk ˈstændərdz/	plural noun phrase	The criteria or level of evidence and justification a person or field requires before accepting a belief or claim as warranted.	standards of justification, standards of evidence, belief criteria	Scientific and everyday decisions may operate with different epistemic standards. / We should apply roughly comparable epistemic standards to competing claims.	Broader than evidentiary standards: epistemic standards can include evidence quality, source reliability, coherence, inference rules, and acceptable uncertainty. Useful when discussing double standards in belief formation.	epistemology	voca_0122
653	6522	6985	sequester	/sɪˈkwestər/	verb	To isolate or separate someone or something from others; in law, to take possession of property by authority; in trials, to isolate a jury from outside information.	isolate, separate, quarantine; impound, seize	The jury was sequestered during the high-profile trial. / The researchers sequestered the samples from potential contaminants. / The policy aims to sequester carbon underground.	Broad verb with domain-specific uses. Carbon sequestration = capturing/storing carbon. Existing adjective/past participle sequestered describes something kept apart; this card centers the active verb.	general / legal / science	voca_0122
653	6523	6966	snare	/sner/	noun / verb	A trap, especially one made with a loop or wire for catching animals; as a verb, to catch or trap, literally or figuratively.	trap, noose trap, catch, entrap	The ranger found an illegal snare in the woods. / The scam was designed to snare inexperienced buyers. / His foot was snared by a loop of rope.	A snare is a kind of trap; it may use a tightening loop, but snare is broader than noose. Snared = caught/trapped. Also snare drum is a completely separate common compound.	literal / figurative	voca_0122
653	6524	6981	kitchen-sinking / kitchen sinking	/ˈkɪtʃən ˌsɪŋkɪŋ/	noun / gerund, relationship communication	Bringing many old, unrelated, or already resolved grievances into a current argument so that the original issue gets buried under a pile of complaints.	gunnysacking (related), piling on grievances, bringing up everything at once	We started arguing about the dishes, but within five minutes we were kitchen-sinking and fighting about things from three years ago.	Named after 'everything but the kitchen sink.' Often discussed in relationship/conflict communication. Gunnysacking is related but emphasizes storing grievances up and unloading them later; kitchen-sinking emphasizes throwing many grievances into the present fight.	relationships / communication	voca_0122
653	6525	6983	keep something at bay	/kiːp ˈsʌmθɪŋ æt beɪ/	idiom	To prevent something threatening, unpleasant, or unwanted from approaching, worsening, or having its full effect.	hold off, fend off, keep away, stave off	Regular exercise helps keep stress at bay. / The medication kept the symptoms at bay for several months. / They lit a fire to keep the insects at bay.	Often used with illness, fear, hunger, danger, attackers, insects, or problems. It usually means control or delay rather than permanently eliminate.	idiom	voca_0122
653	6526	6968	agreeable vs disagreeable	/əˈɡriːəbəl/ vs /ˌdɪsəˈɡriːəbəl/	adjective contrast	Agreeable can mean pleasant, acceptable, or willing to agree; disagreeable means unpleasant, objectionable, or difficult to deal with—not simply 'a person who disagrees.'	agreeable: pleasant, amenable; disagreeable: unpleasant, objectionable	The weather was surprisingly agreeable. / She was agreeable to the proposed change. / He became increasingly disagreeable as the meeting dragged on.	Agreeable to a plan = willing to accept it. Disagreeable often describes unpleasantness in temperament, sensation, or conditions; it does not normally mean 'holding a different opinion.' For that, use disagreeing, opposed, or in disagreement.	contrast / usage	voca_0122
653	6527	6962	give someone the shake	/ɡɪv ˈsʌmwʌn ðə ʃeɪk/	idiom, dated/informal	To get rid of, escape from, or shake off someone who is following, accompanying, or bothering you.	ditch, shake off, lose, get rid of	We took a side street and finally gave the guy the shake. / She was trying to give her unwanted companion the shake.	A relatively uncommon/dated idiom. It is different from give someone a fair shake = give someone a fair chance. Modern speakers are more likely to say shake someone off, lose someone, or ditch someone.	idiom / dated	voca_0122
653	6528	6957	cross-pollination	/ˌkrɔːs ˌpɑːləˈneɪʃən/	noun	The transfer of pollen between different plants; figuratively, the exchange and mixing of ideas, methods, styles, or influences between different people, fields, or cultures.	exchange, interplay, hybridization, cross-fertilization	Bees help with cross-pollination between plants. / The project benefited from cross-pollination between linguistics and computer science. / Anime has long involved cross-pollination with games, music, and online fan culture.	The figurative sense is common in discussions of culture, research, business, and creativity. It suggests productive transfer across boundaries, not just random mixing.	literal / figurative	voca_0122
653	6529	6952	irreverence	/ɪˈrevərəns/	noun	A lack of the respect or solemnity normally considered appropriate toward a person, institution, belief, or serious subject.	disrespect, impiety, cheek, flippancy	The show's irreverence toward authority is part of its appeal. / His jokes mix affection with a deliberate irreverence toward tradition.	Can be negative ('disrespect') or approving when it suggests refreshing refusal to treat something too solemnly. Adjective: irreverent. Contrast reverence = deep respect.	attitude / register	voca_0122
653	6530	6984	not as much as I should	/nɑːt æz mʌtʃ æz aɪ ʃʊd/	phrase	Less often, less intensely, or to a smaller degree than the speaker believes would be appropriate or desirable.	less than I ought to, not enough, less often than I should	I read in English, but not as much as I should. / I call my parents, just not as much as I should.	The omitted action/degree is recovered from context. Very natural self-evaluative phrasing. Compare not as much as I used to = a comparison with the past, not with an obligation or ideal.	everyday phrase	voca_0122
654	6531	6967	noose	/nuːs/	noun	A loop in a rope or cord formed with a sliding knot that tightens when pulled, historically used for hanging or for catching things.	slipknot loop, tightening loop	The rope had been tied into a noose. / The image of a tightening noose is also used metaphorically for mounting pressure or danger.	A noose describes the tightening loop itself, whereas a snare is a trap and may or may not involve a noose. Because of its strong association with execution and lynching, the word can carry heavy historical and emotional weight.	object / sensitive history	voca_0122
654	6532	6972	qualify a statement	/ˈkwɑːləfaɪ ə ˈsteɪtmənt/	verb phrase	To add a limitation, condition, exception, or clarification that makes a statement less absolute or more precisely accurate.	limit, modify, nuance, add a caveat to	I'd qualify that statement by saying the effect is strongest in younger learners. / She qualified her criticism with 'at least in the cases I've seen.'	This is the academic/discourse sense of qualify, not 'be eligible.' A qualifier narrows or modifies a claim. Qualifying can overlap with hedging, but a qualification may be precise and confident rather than uncertain.	academic / discourse	voca_0122
654	6533	6990	be in someone's corner	/biː ɪn ˈsʌmwʌnz ˈkɔːrnər/	idiom	To support, encourage, defend, or stand by someone, especially when they face difficulty or opposition.	support, back, stand by, have someone's back, root for	Even when the project was going badly, I knew she was in my corner. / It's easier to take risks when you have someone in your corner.	From boxing, where a fighter's corner team advises and supports them between rounds. 'Someone is always in your corner' means they consistently support you; it does not require physical presence.	idiom	voca_0122
654	6534	6959	Clannad	/ˈklænəd/	proper noun	The name of an Irish musical group, later used as the title of Key's visual novel/anime CLANNAD; in the game context it is strongly associated with the theme of family.	—	I finally looked up why CLANNAD is called Clannad. / The title fits the story's focus on family, even though the linguistic history is more complicated than 'Clannad means family.'	The Irish band says its name was formed from Clann as Dobhar ('family from Dore'), shortened into Clannad. The visual novel's creators treated CLANNAD as meaning 'family' in Gaelic, but Irish 'clann' is the actual ordinary word related to family/children; CLANNAD itself is a proper name, not simply the Irish common noun for 'family.'	proper noun / anime	voca_0122
654	6535	6973	portend	/pɔːrˈtend/	verb, formal/literary	To be a sign or warning that something, usually important or unpleasant, is likely to happen in the future.	foreshadow, presage, herald, augur, bode	The sudden drop in sales may portend a difficult year ahead. / Dark clouds portended the storm.	Portend describes a real-world sign indicating what may come. Foreshadow is especially common in storytelling, where an earlier detail anticipates a later event. 'Bode well/ill' is closely related. Portend often sounds formal or ominous.	formal / literary	voca_0122
654	6536	6953	sacrilegious	/ˌsækrəˈlɪdʒəs/	adjective	Showing disrespect toward something sacred or religious; by extension, outrageously disrespectful toward something people treat as almost sacred.	blasphemous, profane, irreverent, desecrating	Defacing the shrine would be considered sacrilegious. / Anime fans might jokingly call skipping the opening theme sacrilegious.	Correct spelling: sacrilegious, not *sacrelegious*. Despite its connection to sacrilege, the word is pronounced with /-lɪdʒ-/. Figurative use is often playful hyperbole.	religion / figurative	voca_0122
654	6537	6987	end-to-end	/ˌend tə ˈend/	adjective / adverb	Extending across an entire process, system, route, or workflow from the initial starting point all the way to the final endpoint.	complete, full-process, from start to finish, comprehensive	We tested the workflow end to end. / The company offers an end-to-end service from design through delivery.	Hyphenate before a noun: an end-to-end test/solution. Often unhyphenated after the verb: test the system end to end. It does not mean 'done entirely by one person'; the focus is coverage from one endpoint of a process to the other.	business / technology	voca_0122
654	6538	6955	for all I/we know	/fər ɔːl aɪ noʊ/; /fər ɔːl wiː noʊ/	idiomatic phrase	Used to emphasize that the true situation is unknown and that a possibility, sometimes an extreme one, cannot be ruled out.	as far as I/we know, possibly, maybe for all anyone knows	For all I know, he could already be on the train. / For all we know, this may have been planned months ago.	For all I know foregrounds the speaker's own lack of knowledge; for all we know frames the uncertainty as shared by a group. Often followed by could/may/might. It can sound skeptical, speculative, or slightly dismissive depending on tone.	idiom / epistemic	voca_0122
654	6539	6963	give someone a fair shake	/ɡɪv ˈsʌmwʌn ə fer ʃeɪk/	idiom, informal	To give someone a fair opportunity, fair treatment, or an unbiased chance to prove themselves.	give a fair chance, treat fairly, give a fair hearing	I didn't love the first episode, but I want to give the series a fair shake. / New employees deserve a fair shake before they're judged.	Usually positive: give someone/something a fair shake. Also 'not get a fair shake' = be treated unfairly. Distinct from give someone the shake = get rid of/escape someone.	idiom	voca_0122
654	6540	6960	gash	/ɡæʃ/	noun / verb	A long, deep cut or wound; also a deep cut or split in a surface. As a verb, to cut deeply.	deep cut, slash, laceration	He had a nasty gash on his leg after the fall. / The collision gashed the side of the boat.	Stronger than cut: gash typically suggests a relatively long, open, or deep wound. In some varieties of British slang, gash also has crude/offensive senses; context matters.	body / general	voca_0122
655	6541	6977	evidentiary standards	/ˌevɪˈdenʃieri ˈstændərdz/	plural noun phrase	Rules or thresholds governing what counts as acceptable evidence and how strong evidence must be to justify a conclusion, claim, or legal finding.	standards of evidence, proof requirements, evidential criteria	Different courts apply different evidentiary standards. / A scientific claim should be judged by evidentiary standards appropriate to the field.	Legal contexts may distinguish admissibility rules from burdens/standards of proof such as preponderance of the evidence or beyond a reasonable doubt. In broader academic use, the phrase concerns what evidence is considered sufficient and reliable.	law / epistemology	voca_0122
655	6542	6961	sneakily (not *sneakishly*)	/ˈsniːkɪli/	adverb	In a secretive, stealthy, or sly way intended to avoid being noticed.	secretly, stealthily, slyly, furtively	He sneakily checked his phone under the table. / The cat sneakily crept into the room while the door was open.	Sneakily is the standard adverb from sneaky. *Sneakishly* is rare/nonstandard-sounding and is usually not the form a native speaker would choose.	usage / correction	voca_0122
655	6543	6970	maelstrom	/ˈmeɪlstrəm/	noun	A powerful whirlpool; figuratively, a chaotic, violent, or overwhelming situation involving many forces, events, or emotions.	whirlpool, vortex; turmoil, chaos, whirlwind	The small boat was pulled toward the maelstrom. / The scandal dragged the company into a maelstrom of lawsuits, media attention, and internal conflict.	The figurative pattern maelstrom of + plural/uncountable noun is common: a maelstrom of emotions/events/controversy. Stronger and more dramatic than simple confusion or busyness.	literal / figurative	voca_0122
655	6544	6965	ensnare / ensnared	/ɪnˈsner/; /ɪnˈsnerd/	verb	To catch in a trap or snare; figuratively, to trap or entangle someone in a difficult situation, scheme, obligation, or relationship.	trap, entangle, ensnarl, catch	The animal became ensnared in wire. / He found himself ensnared in a web of debt and legal problems.	Often figurative and somewhat literary: be ensnared by/in something. Compared with trap, ensnare paints a stronger image of becoming caught in something difficult to escape.	literal / figurative	voca_0122
655	6545	6980	one-off coinage	/ˌwʌn ˈɔːf ˈkɔɪnɪdʒ/	noun phrase	A word or expression invented for a single occasion or narrow context rather than established as a conventional part of the language.	nonce word, ad hoc coinage, occasionalism	The reviewer called it a 'plotquake,' probably a one-off coinage rather than established slang.	In linguistics, nonce word/nonce formation is the more technical term for an item coined for a particular occasion. A one-off coinage may be transparent and memorable without ever becoming conventional.	linguistics	voca_0122
655	6546	6975	continuum	/kənˈtɪnjuəm/	noun	A continuous range in which adjacent points differ only gradually and there are no clear-cut breaks between categories.	continuous range, spectrum, gradation	Political views do not always fit into two boxes; they can lie on a continuum. / Language proficiency is better thought of as a continuum than a simple beginner/advanced split.	Plural: continua /kənˈtɪnjuə/ or continuums. Often used in academic discussion when categories shade gradually into one another. Similar to spectrum, though continuum emphasizes continuity.	academic	voca_0122
655	6547	6976	continuum hypothesis	/kənˈtɪnjuəm haɪˈpɑːθəsɪs/	noun, mathematics	A statement in set theory proposing that there is no size of infinity strictly between the cardinality of the integers and that of the real numbers.	CH (abbreviation)	The continuum hypothesis asks whether there is a cardinality between that of the natural numbers and the real numbers.	A famous result in mathematical logic: within the standard ZFC axioms of set theory, the continuum hypothesis can neither be proved nor disproved if ZFC is consistent. This is a specialized mathematical term, not the ordinary continuum sense.	mathematics	voca_0122
655	6548	6964	attrition vs depletion	/əˈtrɪʃən/ vs /dɪˈpliːʃən/	noun contrast	Attrition is gradual reduction through repeated losses, departures, wear, or pressure; depletion is the reduction or exhaustion of a stock, supply, or resource through use or loss.	attrition: gradual loss, erosion; depletion: exhaustion, reduction, drain	The team lost staff through attrition as people resigned over several months. / Heavy pumping caused depletion of the groundwater supply.	Attrition often emphasizes a process of wearing down or losing members over time—employee attrition, war of attrition. Depletion emphasizes that a finite resource is being used up—battery depletion, resource depletion. They can overlap, but the conceptual focus differs.	contrast / academic	voca_0122
655	6549	6969	pontoon boat	/pɑːnˈtuːn boʊt/	noun	A broad, stable recreational boat supported by two or more hollow pontoons rather than by a conventional single hull.	pontoon, party boat	They rented a pontoon boat for a lazy afternoon on the lake. / Pontoon boats are popular for cruising, fishing, and group outings.	Pontoons are buoyant tubes or floats underneath the deck. Pontoon boats prioritize stability and deck space over high-speed performance.	vehicle / recreation	voca_0122
655	6550	6989	induce	/ɪnˈduːs/	verb	To cause or bring about a result; to persuade or lead someone to do something; in medicine, to deliberately initiate a physiological process such as labor.	cause, bring about, provoke; persuade, prompt	The drug can induce drowsiness. / Nothing could induce him to change his mind. / Doctors decided to induce labor the following morning.	Common patterns: induce something; induce someone to do something. More formal than cause in many contexts. Medical induce is often understood as induce labor when context is clear.	general / academic	voca_0122
656	6551	6958	watermark	/ˈwɔːtərmɑːrk/	noun / verb	A faint design or identifying mark embedded in paper or superimposed on an image, video, or document; as a verb, to mark content in this way.	embedded mark, identifying mark, logo overlay	Hold the bill up to the light and you can see the watermark. / The preview images were watermarked with the photographer's logo.	Traditional paper watermarks are formed in the paper itself and become visible in transmitted light. Digital watermarks may be visible overlays or hidden signals used for attribution, tracking, or authenticity.	media / technology	voca_0122
656	6552	6982	emotional bankruptcy	/ɪˈmoʊʃənəl ˈbæŋkrʌptsi/	metaphorical noun phrase	A metaphor for a state in which someone seems to have exhausted their emotional resources, empathy, resilience, or capacity to keep investing in a relationship or situation.	emotional exhaustion, depletion, burnout, emotional insolvency (metaphorical)	After months of conflict, he described himself as being on the edge of emotional bankruptcy. / The phrase suggests there is simply nothing left in the emotional account.	Not a precise clinical diagnosis or standardized psychological term; it is a financial metaphor. Depending on context it may mean burnout, inability to give more emotionally, or a perceived lack of empathy/inner resources.	metaphor / psychology	voca_0122
656	6553	6986	treatise vs thesis vs dissertation	/ˈtriːtɪs/ vs /ˈθiːsɪs/ vs /ˌdɪsərˈteɪʃən/	academic-writing contrast	A treatise is a systematic, often book-length work on a subject; a thesis is either a central proposition or an academic research work, especially a master's/undergraduate thesis in AmE; a dissertation is a substantial degree research work, especially a doctoral dissertation in AmE, though usage varies by country.	treatise = systematic scholarly work; thesis/dissertation = degree research works	Darwin's book is a scientific treatise, not a student dissertation. / She wrote a master's thesis on bilingual education. / He defended his doctoral dissertation last spring.	Terminology varies internationally. In U.S. usage, master's thesis and doctoral dissertation are common; British universities often use dissertation for substantial undergraduate or master's projects as well. Japanese 卒論 often maps naturally to undergraduate/senior thesis depending on institution, not automatically dissertation.	academic / contrast	voca_0122
656	6554	6978	epistemic consistency	/ˌepɪˈstemɪk kənˈsɪstənsi/	noun phrase	Consistency in the standards, assumptions, or reasoning one uses when deciding what to believe or regard as justified knowledge.	consistency of reasoning, consistent epistemic standards	If you demand strong evidence for claims you dislike but accept weak evidence for claims you favor, your epistemic consistency is questionable.	A philosophical/critical-thinking phrase rather than a fixed technical test. Epistemic = relating to knowledge, belief, justification, or evidence. Often invoked when comparing how a person evaluates different claims.	epistemology / critical thinking	voca_0122
656	6555	6954	counterintuitive	/ˌkaʊntərɪnˈtuːɪtɪv/	adjective	Contrary to what instinct, common sense, or an initial guess would lead you to expect.	surprising, unexpected, contrary to intuition	It sounds counterintuitive, but slowing down can make you finish the task faster. / The result was counterintuitive: the smaller group remembered more.	Does not mean merely complicated or wrong. A counterintuitive result specifically conflicts with an intuitive expectation. Opposite: intuitive.	academic / general	voca_0122
656	6556	6956	crisis / crises	/ˈkraɪsɪs/; plural /ˈkraɪsiːz/	noun (singular/plural contrast)	Crisis is the singular form meaning a time of intense difficulty, danger, or decisive change; crises is its irregular plural.	emergency, critical situation, turning point	The company is facing a financial crisis. / The report compares several economic crises from the last century.	Spelling and pronunciation change: crisis /ˈkraɪsɪs/ → crises /ˈkraɪsiːz/. Compare analysis → analyses, basis → bases. Do not pronounce crises like 'crisis-es.'	grammar / academic	voca_0122
656	6557	6974	parched	/pɑːrtʃt/	adjective / past participle	Extremely dry from heat or lack of water; very thirsty; dried out or withered because of insufficient moisture.	bone-dry, dried out, desiccated, thirsty	After the hike I was absolutely parched. / Weeks without rain left the soil parched and the trees visibly stressed. / Her lips felt parched in the dry air.	Can describe a person/throat/mouth ('very thirsty/dry') or land, plants, and surfaces. Do not confuse with perched /pɝːtʃt/ = sitting or positioned on a raised/narrow place.	general / contrast	voca_0122
